\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Interview With \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  General President Sean O`Brien\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 2,223 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I`m proud, as Rox said, to be labeled the most pro-union president in American history.\par \par \uc2 You have heard me say it before. Wall Street`s important, a lot of good folks there, but they didn`t build America. The middle class built America. And unions built the middle class.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: They`re killing unions. They`re killing unions, because the unions are not able to survive. They`re not able to survive this onslaught. It`s making them impossible.\par \par \uc2 They have worked hard. They have worked long to get their salaries up a little bit. They`re not able to do it. Virtually 100 percent of the new jobs under Biden have also gone to illegal aliens.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, well, both of those gentlemen are trying hard to woo my next guest.\par \par \uc2 Sean O`Brien is the president of the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 . They have still not committed to a presidential candidate. Sean has the unique idea of going to each of their conventions to pitch his case for unions and, more to the point, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 . He`s kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 How is that going? What response have you gotten, Sean, from either candidate about coming to their respective conventions?\par \par \uc2 SEAN O`BRIEN, GENERAL PRESIDENT, TEAMSTERS: So, we put the ask in recently.\par \par \uc2 And we haven`t got a commitment either way. But we`re hopefully optimistic that we will get the opportunity to speak at both conventions and to make certain that they hear our voice loud and clear on what we`re demanding on behalf of not only Teamster members, but working people in America.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If one of them doesn`t invite you, is that the one you say, all right, forget it, I`m not endorsing you?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No, I mean, I don`t see why they don`t want to hear from one of the most powerful unions in the country.\par \par \uc2 We`re very transparent and inclusive. But, more importantly, our members vote, 1.3 million members nationwide. So I don`t know why anyone would not take the opportunity to hear us and listen to our wants and needs moving forward. I mean, we are the greatest country in the world...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: ... built by union workers. There`s no reason why we shouldn`t have a voice.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not about politics with us. We`re -- we don`t want to be utilized as political rhetoric or who`s going to help working people. We want to make sure people understand that, regardless if you have a D or an R, we need your help. We need you to work with us to solve problems in this country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it wasn`t a debate for your counterpart at the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 . Shawn Fain there quickly endorsed \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , said that it really was no debate.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes. No.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Why are you still -- why are you still waiting?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, again, because like I said, we are the most transparent union and inclusive.\par \par \uc2 We did presidential forums at our union, where we invited each and every candidate to speak to our rank-and-file members. Now we`re doing straw polling at each -- 331 locals throughout the United States, where we are having debate, dialogue, and, more important, rank-and-file participation.\par \par \uc2 And then we`re doing straw polling and we`re doing all kinds of polling, because we want to make the best decision on behalf of our members. We want to hear our members` voice. Whatever any other union did, that`s their politics, but we are going to be inclusive and we are going to be transparent in this process.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you have a history of preferring Democratic candidates than Republican. I believe George H.W. Bush in 1988 was the last Republican you supported.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, your members like a lot of Republican candidates, we`re told, but not so much the leaderships, and that`s why that happens.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, for far too long, we have just contributed to the Democrats, supported Democrats. And I`m a lifelong Democrat, always will be. However, it`s unfortunate where people just assume they get our support because they have a D next to their name.\par \par \uc2 We want action. We want results. And the one thing that we are committed to is being -- whatever choice we make, being that person`s conscience moving forward on behalf of our members.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think of this latest dust-up with Shawn Fain over at the \uc2 UAW\uc2 ? He`s being investigated right now for a vendetta campaign on those who are not cooperating or not doing what he says.\par \par \uc2 You have sort of modern whistle-blowers in that union. I`m simplifying it here. But he does seem to be drawing a lot of attention, and not in a good way. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, that`s good for me because it`s not focused on me.\par \par \uc2 But the reality of it is, look, there`s three sides to every story. I don`t know the facts well enough. Obviously, the good thing is, there`s always due process with -- in any investigation, and the facts are going to come out. And I guess you just got to stay tuned to that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, but Neil Barofsky, who is the monitor for the union and looks at what they do, has accused the union of not cooperating in this investigation.\par \par \uc2 Generally, when someone`s conducting and you`re not cooperating, if true, that`s a big no-no. That could be a problem.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes. No, I mean, look, when you have government oversight, there are policies and procedures that you`re mandated to follow.\par \par \uc2 And as everybody knows, the Teamster union has been under a consent decree. And I can only speak for my union. We do pay attention to what was required of us. And if there`s a mistake that`s being made, hopefully, it`s something that can be corrected and not diminish all the good work that he`s done.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So let me step back from this. And we talk about unions and politics.\par \par \uc2 And, obviously, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  raised some eyebrows speaking in Las Vegas and maybe, in an outreach to service workers -- the service workers union is very big out there -- saying that he would have the \uc2 IRS\uc2  end the tax on tips, for example. It`s not such a slam dunk, but that was very popular with members, even though the head of that union belittle the ploy, as he called it, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  offered.\par \par \uc2 But it worked with a lot of members, who like that idea. What do you think of it?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 No, I mean, look, I`m optimistic on anybody that wants to support unions. And every union has different wants and needs. People can say whatever they want, make these promises.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: The thing that matters the most is, they have to deliver on what they`re going to -- what they say they`re going to do.\par \par \uc2 And that`s part of this process right now and this confusion is, historically, politicians, and not all of them -- there`s a lot of good ones that have great credibility. But politicians have made hopes and promises, and never deliver on them. And that`s why there`s a lot of skepticism.\par \par \uc2 And, hopefully, he`s true to his word, if that`s what he said he was going to do, if elected.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sean, I ask you a little bit about the environment that unions are in right now.\par \par \uc2 And, sometimes, you`re not a typical union leader. You speak very softly. You don`t get into many arguments. But we do remember that one with Markwayne Mullin, the senator. And I just want to refresh people`s memories in case they forgot, where it got a little heated. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARKWAYNE MULLIN\uc2  (R-OK): Let me read what the last one said. It said: "Greedy CEO who pretends like he`s self-made."\par \par \uc2 Sir, I wish you was in the truck with me when I was building my plumbing company myself and my wife was running the office, because I sure remember working pretty hard and long hours.\par \par \uc2 "Pretends like he`s self-made. What a clown, fraud. Always has been, always will be. Quit the tough guy act in these Senate hearings. You know where to find me. Any place, any time, cowboy."\par \par \uc2 Sir, this is a time, this is a place. You want to run your mouth, we can be two consenting adults, we can finish it here.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: OK, that`s fine. Perfect.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: You want to do it now?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I would love to do it right now.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Well, stand your butt up then.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: You stand your butt up, big guy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): Hold on. Stop it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Is that your solution to every problem?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: No, no, sit down.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: That`s why you`re a clown. Look at you.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: You know, you`re a United States senator.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Bernie Sanders might have saved the day there. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 But I -- every time I look at that, I`m always like, what -- how close did it get? I mean, you never came to blows.\par \par \uc2 But you both had promised to get together sometime, maybe at a diner. Remember, I was going to broker something at a Denny`s or an \uc2 IHOP\uc2 . You never called me. Did you ever get together?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, I think you helped facilitate a cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 And we did -- we actually did meet and get together. And, look, there`s a lot of things we agree to disagree on. I think the one thing we agree on is that we`re both in positions where maybe we could have acted a little differently in those hearings.\par \par \uc2 But, at the end of the day, I have a constituency to serve, and he does as well. And it was very cordial. And, hopefully, we can work together.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Hmm.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: And that`s one of the focus we have been working on as an organization, building relationships.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, it was amicable. I mean, you didn`t come to blows at wherever you got together.\par \par \uc2 Was it a diner or...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No, it was just a little -- small, little restaurant.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: It was nothing fancy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Were you recognized? You`re both recognizable people.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes, I`m not sure. It`s a pretty small place. So I don`t think the folks that run it pay attention.\par \par \uc2 But it`s no secret that we met.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: And, look, I mean, it`s like anything else. Sometimes, you take a step back and think about it.\par \par \uc2 And we can probably -- hopefully, he can support some of our issues moving through the Senate. And he has a lot of union members that are \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  in Oklahoma. So, it helps him as well to be working with us on certain issues.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If Senator Sanders had not intervened, do you think it would have come to blows?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes, I...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Or was that was all like some say professional wrestling could be, that it was all staged?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I mean, look, I think -- I think -- well, no, I don`t think it would have escalated.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: There was too many people around.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: And I can`t jump over the boards like I used to. So it might have took me a little while to get over the dais, you know?\par