. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: Fleet Week in its 36th year, will honor nearly 2300 military personnel from today through Monday, it's a great time. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Tomorrow, it's going to be a good show. The Ingraham Angle is next.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. A big and overdue declaration from \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , more on that with Newt Gingrich in moments. But first, Biden's judicial snake. That's the focus of tonight's Angle.\par \par \uc2 Now, from day one, the Biden administration has put the Left's demand for diversity and activism above competence and merit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: As you know, one of the biggest priority of the President is nominating and confirming extraordinarily qualified men and women who are dedicated to our Constitution and who represent the diversity of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. Well, from the open borders for their push for transgenderism. The Democrats have relied on their handpick judges to validate these policies, regardless of the Constitution, the rule of law or just plain common sense of biology, which takes us to today's \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  hearing to consider the nominations of several Biden appointees to key federal judgeships.\par \par \uc2 Now, Democrats were hoping, I think to squeak these nominees through, all of them women, knowing that Americans don't really see much of these hearings. But today was a doozy and it was illustrative of the Democrat's perversion of Article 3 courts, because the public has a right to know how radical these nominees are, these clips are going to run a little bit longer than normal tonight, but this judicial nominee, Sarah Netburn was questioned by Senators \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  and Ted Cruz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): You went to Brown University. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 SARAH NETBURN, BIDEN'S JUDICIAL NOMINEE: I did.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: And then you went to law school in California.\par \par \uc2 NETBURN: That's correct.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: And you've worked for the Brennan Center.\par \par \uc2 NETBURN: I interned. I was a law student intern.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: OK. And you worked for the Center for Reproductive Law and Policy?\par \par \uc2 NETBURN: I was a law student intern there for eight weeks during my 1L summer.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: OK. And you are a political activist, aren't you?\par \par \uc2 NETBURN: I am not. I am a sitting United States magistrate judge.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: OK. All right. Do you remember an inmate named William McClain, also known as July Justine Shelby?\par \par \uc2 NETBURN: Yes, I know who you're referring to.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: OK. Mr. McClain raped a child. Didn't he?\par \par \uc2 NETBURN: 30 years ago, the petitioner and the habeas case before me --\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Did he rape a child?\par \par \uc2 NETBURN: He pled guilty to that crime. Yes, it's abhorrent.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: And then he raped a 17 -- he raped a little boy, right?\par \par \uc2 NETBURN: I believe that's correct. I believe he pled guilty to two acts of sexual violence.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: And then he raped a 17-year-old girl, right?\par \par \uc2 NETBURN: Again, this case was handled by the state court in Indiana, but I believe --\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: I'm just asking you the 