You Swap Seats? The Four Most Divisive Words On A Flight\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeanine Pirro, Richard Fowler, Jesse Watters Greg Gutfeld, Dana  Perino \par \uc2 8,269 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: But thank you, George. All wise advice. George Kamel on that. So the spending continues and so does it in Washington. We'll explore that tomorrow. Right now, here's THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Hello, everyone. I'm Dana Perino along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Richard Fowler, Jesse Watters and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City. And this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 One of the most controversial Americans of our time, O.J. Simpson dead at 76 after a battle with prostate cancer. He's regarded as one of the greatest running backs of all time and use his sports fame to turn himself into a household name. He starred in Hertz commercials and many movies.\par \par \uc2 But everything changed the night of June 12, 1994 when Simpson's ex-wife Nicole Brown Simpson and her friend Ronald Goldman were found brutally stabbed to death in the courtyard outside her home in L.A.\par \par \uc2 O.J. charged with their deaths, he was tried and eventually acquitted in the trial of the century.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This point is it is a pursuit. And unfortunately, it has a lot of mitigating circumstances because of the high profile of the proposed suspect that's inside the vehicle which could possibly be and appears to be at this time O.J. Simpson.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was the most dramatic and heartstopping moment of this trial. In front of the jury. O.J. Simpson tried on the gloves prosecutors say he was wearing the night he murdered his ex-wife. He tried to show the gloves do not fit.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It doesn't fit. If it doesn't fit, you must acquit.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We the jury in the above entitled action find the defendant Orenthal James Simpson not guilty of a crime of murder in violation of Penal Code Section 187. A felony upon Nicole Brown Simpson.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: O.J. was sued separately by the Goldman and the Browns in civil court for wrongful death where a jury found him liable. The Goldman family speaking out about his death, saying, the only thing I have to say is it's just for the reminder of Ron being gone all these years. It's no great loss to the world. It's a further reminder of Ron's being gone.\par \par \uc2 Judge Jeanine, I mean, it's interesting to listen to some of the younger producers today who didn't live through it and know about it, they might have watched the documentary about it or the reason that show that was on it.\par \par \uc2 But for those of us who live through it, and I'm sure from your lens as a lawyer and a judge.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: I was one of the talking heads.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, that's why we have you here today.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, I was the sitting D.A. at the time and I'll never forget June 12th. I just been elected the D.A. in Westchester County in New York. And one of the guys I have my whole office at my house for a barbecue. One of the guys came out, he said, boss, O.J. Simpson is in a white Bronco and the police are chasing him.\par \par \uc2 So, a few -- a few of us went in to the kitchen, we couldn't figure out what was going on. And then we realized we went into the den because there were a lot more of us that there -- he was supposed to show up at the police department and turn himself in, he refused to do so.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we're all these prosecutors together and saying, you know, this consciousness of guilt, this is flight. But what we didn't realize that night was that this was the beginning of one of the most sensational trials, if not the most sensational trial of the se -- of the century, the media was fascinated. The public was fascinated. The public had never seen a courtroom, they had never seen a trial, people would run home from work and say I've got to watch the trial. I've got to see what was going on.\par \par \uc2 And I and so many others. I mean, I stayed as D.A. and I was a judge before that. But I didn't go into television from there. But so many people got their -- got their jump into television from this.\par \par \uc2 But one of the most significant parts of this is the United States and people in this country. We're all involved in this shared experience of what the criminal justice system is like, and the new DNA, what did it mean? How significant was it?\par \par \uc2 What about, you know, the PCR? What about all of the other different types, and then there was the undercurrent of racism in this trial, that because of Mark Fuhrman was brought to a head. And there were so many people who were very critical of the -- of the decision by the jury here.\par \par \uc2 In this trial that ran for nine months, it ran for nine months. I mean, this was -- people made careers out of this. And the jury made the decision in less than four hours.\par \par \uc2 And I remember being on live television at the time, and they came in and focus on all of us. And you know, this was a jury of eight blacks and two mixed race and I believe two whites, and there were many who believe that because of the racism, because of the issues that Mark Fuhrman was involved in, that the Goldmans and that Nicole Brown Simpson were literally paying for the sins of the fathers.\par \par \uc2 And there's always been the question about is he guilty or isn't he? And you know, for those of us who understood the forensics and the -- and the DNA and all of the other stuff going on, you know, the idea that the glove didn't fit, give me a break, that glove was covered with blood, the blood shrunk, of course, he couldn't get his hands in the gloves.\par \par \uc2 But you know, you accept the decision of the jury. And of course, he got burned later in the civil case, where he barely paid any of the money. But the public fascination with this, it began a whole new line of people with crime stories, and court T.V., and all of us who love this and made this part of our careers. We were happy that from now on we have juries who understood what DNA was, they understood direct circumstantial evidence, anyway.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I could ask you a 20,000 more questions.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, me too.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But Richard Fowler, from your perspective.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Listen, I want to pick up where the Judge left off, because I remember this case well, I was in the third grade when this verdict came out. And I remember sitting in the -- in class when the teacher played it on a red radio. And I remember the reaction that I had, because to the Judge's point, this case was rife with race, because there was so much --\par \par \uc2 I mean, to understand the prism of the O.J. Simpson trial, you have to understand what's happening in L.A. at that time, right? Two years prior to this, there was the Rodney King beating that was caught on tape in 1981. Those four officers got a change of venue to Simi Valley, and they were acquitted after beating Rodney King. And so, there was a just tension in the air.\par \par \uc2 And if you go back -- and we were talking to producers in the green room before the show, like, you could literally watch the footage and see two Americans, people who are neighbors and one vote -- black people saying this is great, and white people crying at the same time watching this verdict, despite the evidence just because of where we were as a country, and just how rife the racial tension was around the \uc2 LAPD\uc2  in fume. I think that was sort of made worse by the Mark Fuhrman tapes that had come out in the middle of the trial.\par \par \uc2 And it's just an interesting moment in history. But to the Judge's point too, it also created the cable news cycle, the 24 hour media, all this idea that you can follow a case and these true crime stories that this narrative that we now live in was created by this case, by the O.J. Simpson trial. And here we are. A lot of years later.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Here we are prime time.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Just a little bit older than Richard, I was 16 when this happened, I used to come back from practice and watch the case. And I do remember exactly what you said and realizing how divided the country was.\par \par \uc2 I think, as a white guy, I was like, wow, this guy is a killer. He just got away with murder. The evidence is obvious. And then you see images of black America celebrating and I'm not saying all black America felt that O.J. was innocent. I'm saying that when you see people cheering and then explaining that good. You know, finally we have someone who looks like us use the justice system in their favor.\par \par \uc2 And it was almost like a wakeup call. People realized, wait a second, maybe this system isn't always about justice. Maybe it's about money.\par \par \uc2 And O.J. Simpson was so wealthy and to the Judge's point, I finally started admiring these defense lawyers. These guys they called the dream team, you pay these guys so much money. And it was almost like they had superpowers. I was dazzled with Johnnie Cochran. I was just hanging on every word he said, I couldn't believe it. And you know, not only do the gloves shrink because of the blood, Judge, he had advised O.J. to stop taking his arthritis medication. So his knuckles had swollen.\par \par \uc2 And so, and then you're looking at the DNA (ph) and it's in you're looking, wait a second, this stuff can get contaminated. And thankfully, years later, the chain of custody has now gotten perfect where you don't even let anybody breathe near this thing.\par \par \uc2 I just want to just say my heart goes out to the Goldman family, the Brown family. It does look like the estate now owes them almost $100 million. Because O.J. how -- I don't know how he did it. He avoided somehow not paying these people almost a dime. I hope they collect.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Are you going to say how old you were?\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I wasn't born. So it's nice to hear this from all these old people. I got to say this one really hurts. It's funny. I always think of that car rental commercial when I'm running through the airport after stabbing two people. Did you know that Steve Doocy's wife was in that commercial. She's the one who tosses the car keys to O.J. I think she really dodged a bullet or at least a knife. I think O.J. is real crime was making Jeffrey Toobin famous.\par \par \uc2 A little -- some facts about O.J., he left behind five children and other people he didn't murder. He dies holding the -- I guess the record for the average rushing yards per game and most stabs in a Brentwood driveway. Do we really know he's dead though? Maybe we should ask an L.A. jury they might declare him alive. Who's going to search for the real killer now? I hear Jussie Smollett is free. He's been searching for those other Trumpers. Maybe he could pack this in.\par \par \uc2 And then one note to \uc2 Khloe Kardashian\uc2 , you may have lost a dad, but you still have two moms. So, now, justice -- my lesson here is justice is wholly fallible. If it can be throttled from outside forces, you can't get a fair trial. And that wasn't a fair trial to the victims.\par \par \uc2 So, you need to apply that lesson to now. We are in a political and a social and emotional climate, where getting a fair trial is hard to get. And as much as you hate Derek Chauvin, right? You still know that trial wasn't fair, thanks to the documentary The Fall of Minneapolis. And \uc2 Michael Avenatti\uc2  agrees that there's no chance for a fair trial for Trump in New York, or else the proponents wouldn't mind if you moved it outside of New York.\par \par \uc2 So, what I learned, it's not about the evidence in the trial. It's where you present it. It's not about what's inside the courtroom. It's about what's outside the courtroom and how it applies pressure to the judge and the jury, whether it's the threat of public chaos or media outrage. The O.J. trial showed us what would and what could come when the court of public opinion, Trump's the actual court of law, that jury made a political decision and emotional decision but it certainly wasn't a legal one.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: -- Justice.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So I could make a programming note tonight, we have Kato Kaelin on "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME".\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Great booking. All right --\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: He knew the pump on the wall.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: O.J. Simpson dead at 76 years old today.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, President Biden hobnobbing with celebrities at a state dinner while Americans skip meals to afford housing.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Just call him Joe Antoinette (ph) at the White House rolling out the red carpet for a swanky state dinner with the Japanese Prime Minister. While regular Americans are feeling the pain from yet another devastating inflation report.\par \par \uc2 Washington got to schmooze with big tech billionaires like \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  and Hollywood celebs like Robert De Niro. Bill and Hillary were walking around like they own the place even though they're never getting the keys back.\par \par \uc2 And boy, was it a feast, the elites gorging on House cured salmon dry aged ribeye steak and fava beans. And while they got to fill their bellies, a new report claims that an alarming number of Americans are skipping meals just so that they can afford their home.\par \par \uc2 And this has got a sting. A voter panel on Biden's favorite cable news show "Morning Joe" is slamming Bidenomics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKA BRZEZINSKI\uc2 , MSNBC NEWS HOST: We hear from voters in Michigan, Wisconsin and Pennsylvania undecided voters. Here are their answers when they're asked about how President Biden is doing on the economy. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Raise your hand if you think President Trump's policies on the economy would be better for your family, personally. Raise your hand all right, so that is everybody.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I feel like he doesn't even take accountability.\par \par \uc2 OMAR, MICHIGAN VOTER: When he's talking about the economy doing stellar. He's talking about the stock market. He's not looking at homelessness or joblessness. He's not (INAUDIBLE) thinking about how much it costs to go to the grocery store. He's gaslighting literally everyone in the process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But Biden's defenders are doing their best to brainwash voters into thinking that the economy is doing great.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN (D-SC): Social media, and the misrepresentation, disinformation all of those things are out there and that's the battle that we have to fight. And we've got to do a better job of fighting it more effectively.\par \par \uc2 BRZEZINSKI: We just got breaking news. The Consumer Price Index increased at a faster than expected pace last month, a signal that inflation remains stubbornly high.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, Jesse, I'll start with you. Even the undecided voters on that panel with "Morning Joe" were complaining and basically saying Joe's in denial, he doesn't understand how much we're hurting. Do you think that a Joe of "Morning Joe" and Mika were trying to send a message to Biden, they know we watch this every morning?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It depends on if they knew what they were going to hear first. I don't know if they knew what the answers were first, but that looked like to me the first time they didn't interviewed anybody besides themselves.\par \par \uc2 We have Johnny. Now Johnny goes out to the streets and he gets the pulse of the people. I don't think they've gotten the pulse. I think they're only pulse is the studio, and that's not going to cut it.\par \par \uc2 This information, they actually put out a report that said inflation is a good thing last year. And the expectation is, is that inflation is going to cool to a point where he's going to come and have a little runway going into November, but that's not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 So, I have a little advice for the libs and Richard, because you can't bring inflation down fast enough. You have to say this, you have to say \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will make inflation worse, you have to say \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will let Russia just rip through Ukraine and make Putin's price hike even bigger. You have to say Trump's tax cuts are going to make inflation worse, you're going to say Trump's greedy CEO buddies are going to keep gouging you and this billionaire doesn't even know what the price of milk is anyway, so don't fall for it. That's what they have to do.\par \par \uc2 Now, why did I just say that? Because I am so bored of saying the same thing for four years.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, all right, Richard, would you like to answer that? You know, Clyburn, \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  says, you know that people are in denial that they're not really seeing how Bidenomics is helping them? I mean, people are every day at the grocery store is seeing it.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Look, I think there's a couple of things. I agree with Jesse's point that there is a clear contrast to be made between the current occupant of the White House and the former occupant of the White House, right?\par \par \uc2 I think when you hear \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  talk about the economy that he wants for the American people, he reminisces about 2018 or 2017. But you rarely ever, ever -- rarely ever hear him talk about 2025 or 2026 2027, or the plan what he's going to do when he wins, he just reminisces about the past.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think all those arguments are true, Jesse, thanks for that. But I also --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Don't think they're going to work because everybody remembers the inflation was so low during the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: But I also think that there has to be some contrition from this White House, and saying, listen, the inflation is not exactly where we want it to be. Right? But here are the things we're doing to make your life easier at home.\par \par \uc2 One thing that we're doing is this, we are capping the cost of late fees on credit cards to $8.00 a month, which means that will save the American people and working families up to $12 billion every year, we've already capped insulin payments to $35.00 a month for senior citizens, not to mention the fact that we had the longest -- the longest -- the longest sprint of job growth, we've seen the GDP grow.\par \par \uc2 And while you're not feeling it, yet, these impacts are coming your way because we're working on it. And we're actively working on it.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, back at the ranch, we have a candidate that's talking about the 2020 election, or how the economy was doing in 2017. Or like you said, he doesn't know the price of milk or the price of egg.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Those are stealing my line, Richard.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, all right, you know what Dana? Richard is saying that, you know, they're just not feeling those bene