nc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240410ek4900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Aftershocks Reported From Rare 4.8 Northeast Quake; White House On Israel Support After Aid Workers Killed; Israel: Strike Killing Aid Workers "Grave Mistake"; White House: Israel Has Obligation To Protect Civilians; Biden Speaks From Baltimore Bridge Collapse Site; Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  War Impact On 2024 Election; Rare 4.8 Earthquake shakes Up Northeast; 1st US Total Eclipse Since 2017 On Monday; Influential Black Radio Host Slams DEI Programs\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Nick Kosir, Kellyanne Conway; Howie Kurtz;  Alexis McAdams; Charles Payne \par \uc2 8,808 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum, and this is THE STORY. Moments from now, John Kirby will join me live here on set in Washington, D.C. First, though, the aftershocks keep coming this afternoon from a rare 4.8 magnitude earthquake in the northeast, shaking up lots of homes and the people and the pets inside them. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 Center near White House station New Jersey outside of New York City but homes also shook 50 miles away in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're having an earthquake, holy (BLEEP)! What the (BLEEP! Did you feel that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I did.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What the (BLEEP)?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, plenty of people also felt it in New York City. Look at this video from EarthCam as the quake rattled some of the city's most famous monuments. You can see, you can see the old Statue of Liberty going back and forth a little bit there. Fox Weather Meteorologist Nick Kosir reporting live from New York City. Pretty amazing shots today. This is not something we normally see in New York, and New Jersey, and Pennsylvania, Nick.\par \par \uc2 NICK KOSIR, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: Yes, a little bit uncommon, right? And I got to tell you, the epicenter of this earthquake was about 45 miles west of New York City. But people here could feel it. And it's really been a mixed bag of experiences. Some people, I just talked to a food vendor who was on the ground level when the earthquake happened. He said, he didn't feel a thing.\par \par \uc2 I've also talked to somebody who works on the 34th floor of their building, and they said, yes, they felt it, their chair was rattling, the windows were shaking a little bit. So that's what it's been, kind of a 50-50 bag. I've got the French family right next to me. They're in town from Georgia. Raise your hand if you could feel the earthquake today. Raise your hand if you couldn't. And you guys were in the same hotel room, right? Next to him. So, what did you feel when the earthquake came through?\par \par \uc2 TARA FRENCH, RESIDENT: On the 19th floor there was a little vibration and some waving back and forth. A little unsteadiness.\par \par \uc2 KOSIR: So, you guys, you had your feet on the floor, you guys were on the bed, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOSIR: Gotcha. And so, when the earthquake did happen, were you scared at all?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, just like confused.\par \par \uc2 KOSIR: And that's another sentiment that I've heard a lot. People were just confused trying to figure out what was going on. And from what I've heard, it sounded like a truck was rolling by, right?\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: Well, yes, we were kind of caught off guard. I was putting on my makeup, so why is this unsteady?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You didn't know what was happening.\par \par \uc2 KOSIR: There you go. Well, thank you guys. I appreciate your time. Best of luck in New York City. Hopefully, no more earthquakes happen. And yes, Martha, no major issues reported here throughout New York and New Jersey. So, everybody's seemingly safe at the current moment. And there have been five aftershocks since the initial earthquake. And officials are urging people to, if you do feel an aftershock, to drop, cover, and hold.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Drop, cover, and hold. Got it. Thank you very much, Nick. With that, let's bring in Steve Holler, who is Associate Professor of Physics at \uc2 Fordham University\uc2 , home of one of the country's oldest seismic observatories. I have to admit that I did not know that \uc2 Fordham University\uc2  had this technology and it's been there since 1924. So, tell me a little bit, Steve, about what happened from a scientific perspective. What fault this is and why it has been quiet for so long and why you think it, it perked up today?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HOLLER, ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR OF PHYSICS AT FORDHAM UNIVERSITY: Well, the East Coast is geologically it's been pretty stable for a really long time. So, but what happens is the plates these plates that we're sitting on, right, we're sitting on these massive rock plates that kind of move all around the earth. And when, you can, you can see this look at the map and say, oh well, South America fits into Africa.\par \par \uc2 And so, we've had this idea of plate tectonics and continental drift for most of the century now. But these plates are still moving, they're still pushing up against each other. There's little stresses that build up and eventually they release. And when they release, it produces an earthquake. And so, we feel, that's what we feel when, when the stress is relieved on these, on these plates, on these fault lines and that's what happened today.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, so there have been five documented aftershocks. Will, are there likely to be more? How do you know that this is just the beginning or whether that was the whole event?\par \par \uc2 HOLLER: I think that was the whole event. I think everything is now settling down. There'll be some aftershocks, maybe for a few days, maybe a couple weeks. But those, those events are going to be at a much lower magnitude than what we experienced with the primary event. This big earthquake was a 4.8, and that was substantial in terms of earthquakes, especially for the East Coast, but in other parts of the country and even in the world, that is sort of moderate. They're familiar with these types of earthquakes. They happen more routinely. It's just a rare event for the East Coast to have something of this magnitude strike just because we have been stable for so long.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: While I ask you this question, if you guys could show the video of the earthquake shaking the United Nations and Yankee Stadium and Morristown, New Jersey backyard not too far from where I live. So, Steve, as I understand it, are happening all the time in the earth, right? So, give us a sense of how much activity is out there all the time. And also, are you surprised when you see some of this shaking in these images that there wasn't more damage, or was this not strong enough to produce that kind of damage?\par \par \uc2 HOLLER: Well, I think we engineer and we build for a whole range of different effects. Mostly, we're concerned on the East Coast about things like hurricanes and high winds. And so, our buildings are given some flexibility to them to withstand any of the shaking that we have. Even our skyscrapers, if you're just on a skyscraper on a windy day, not even a strong windy day, but just moderate, you will actually feel the skyscraper move.\par \par \uc2 It's disconcerting to some people, but it's completely normal and built into the engineering specifications. And so, it doesn't surprise me that given this magnitude of earthquake that we didn't have substantial damage to buildings. These are rare, it's a rare event for the East Coast, but there are hundreds of earthquakes every day around the world.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Every day, yes.\par \par \uc2 HOLLER: This is a normal situation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what, it's very cool science that you study, to look at the Earth, to see how it's moving, to know that it has settled down, looking at the equipment that you see and look at every day. So, Steve Holler, thank you very much from Fordham. Great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 HOLLER: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks a lot, Steve. Have a good day. So, coming up, John Kirby joins me live here in studio from Washington as critics warn President Biden's pivot on Israel may play right into \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's hands.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, are you abandoning Israel? Are you abandoning Israel? Are you abandoning Israel?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Is that a serious question?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you trust Netanyahu?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why not hostages before the ceasefire?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Israel firing two senior military officers after calling it strike on Gaza that killed seven aid workers a "grave mistake". They are also reopening a crucial border crossing in northern Gaza just hours after the White House called for an immediate ceasefire which was yesterday actually and issuing this ultimatum.\par \par \uc2 "If there are no changes in Israel's policy, there will have to be changes in ours."\par \par \uc2 Here's retired Four-Star General Jack Keane.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Now, what have we done? We just strengthened \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's hand, rather dramatically, and leveraged them in those negotiations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bringing John Kirby, National Security Communications Advisor, and I should mention, welcome, John, first of all, and we're waiting for your boss, who is at the site of the Baltimore Bridge collapse, and we'll go there when that gets underway. But thank you very much for being here. So, you hear General Jack Keane, he says we strengthened \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's hand by saying to Israel, either you find ways to carry this out, we're losing far fewer civilians, or we're going to change our policy in terms of support for you. He says it's quite appalling and we should stay out of it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: Well, with all due respect to the general, who I know and like for a long, long time, I would just have to disagree. Number one, you can't look at all the things that we've been doing for Israel since the 7th of October and suggest that we are somehow walking away from their ability to defend themselves.\par \par \uc2 And frankly, in the call yesterday, the president made it very clear to Prime Minister Netanyahu that we know they have a right and responsibility to go after that threat, and we're going to continue to help them do that. It's how they do it that matters because one thing that does strengthen \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ' hand, Martha, is for them to capitalize on the imagery of civilian infrastructure and capitalize on the imagery that's out there of innocent Palestinians being killed and wounded as a result of this conflict.\par \par \uc2 So, we want to get that ceasefire in place temporarily, six weeks or so, to get those hostages out, get more humanitarian assistance in.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Hamas\uc2  started this war.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: They did, indeed.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They crossed the border. They slaughtered families and children. None of this would be happening if it weren't for what happened on October the 7th. And here, I want to play a sound bite from President Biden and also Vice President Harris, back in October and December on what the -- what's the reaction should be. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY, CORRESPONDENT, \uc2 CBS\uc2  NEWS: Do you believe that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  must be eliminated entirely?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We support Israel's legitimate military objectives to eliminate the threat of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Does the White House still believe that \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , as the president and vice president said, needs to be completely eliminated?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Of course. \uc2 Sure\uc2 . Absolutely. And they can't have any control. And you know, no more governance in Gaza. Whenever this war is over, of course, we want that threat taken away from the Israeli people. And you're right on the 7th of October. People are forgetting, there was a ceasefire in place on October 6th, and Mr. Sinwar decided to break that ceasefire by slaughtering 1,200 Israelis.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the Wall Street Journal said today, President Biden seems to have forgotten that errors are a tragic and inevitable part of war. The president pandered to the anti-Israel faction in his party on Tuesday by harshly condemning Israel, lecturing it, and then blaming it, not \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , for the larger humanitarian disaster in Gaza. Isn't it \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's fault that these people are suffering?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: \uc2 Hamas\uc2  started this war, as you said, there is no question about that. This conflict wouldn't exist without Mr. Sinwar's decision to violate that ceasefire. But what we've also said is that Israel, in addition to having the obligation to go eliminate that threat, they also have an obligation to do everything possible to protect civilians and to allow humanitarian assistance, and we just haven't seen them meet those obligations to the degree that's needed.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you know, we have tens of thousands of dead and injured Palestinians. Not some of them are \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , no question about that. But the toll has just been too high. We want Israel to succeed here. Of course we do. But how they do it matters as well for the long term, for their own security.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I mean, we all have seen over the course of history, that when war is provoked by an aggressor, the retaliation, sadly ends up with tens of thousands of people killed in many cases.\par \par \uc2 When we left Afghanistan, we had a similar tragic hellfire missile attack that landed and killed 10 people. A man who was carrying water -- bringing water as part of the humanitarian aid. And at that point, when you were asked, what are we going to do about that? Who's accountable for that? This is what you said, John.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: None of their recommendations dealt specifically with issues of accountability. So, I do not anticipate there being issues of personal accountability to be had with respect to the August 29th airstrike.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, there were seven children that were killed by that U.S. hellfire missile.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No accountability, you said, was part of the plan. So, why is this so different than what we did there?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, look, of course, these are events that happened three years apart, two different geographic locations, two different countries, two different sets of circumstances, two different types of threat that were being evaluated, and completely two different militaries that were involved with two different chains of command.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we got to be careful comparing both events too closely. We, too, had an independent investigation, Martha, of that incident. And that independent investigator found that there was no need for personal accountability to be had. But I did find that the U.S. military needed to make some systemic changes, procedural changes, in how we looked at intelligence and acted on that intelligence.\par \par \uc2 The Israelis similarly, have said the same thing about this event, this week with the -- with the WCK strike, that they are going to make some systemic changes.\par \par \uc2 Now, we're glad to hear that. And we're going to be watching to see what those changes are and how they can put them into effect.\par \par \uc2 But these are two different events --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They are not that different, though, John.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I know, I understand that the outcome --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I understand -- yes.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I get -- the I get the outcome, but --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But why? So, this was a trigger moment, it appears for the White House because obviously that the momentum has been building to take a harder line against Israel and their tactics.\par \par \uc2 But this was the event that really pushed things over the edge. And so, why is it so different in terms of what Israel carried out in the midst of war, and you know, these aid workers, and my heart breaks for them as it does for the seven children here with this man underneath this hellfire missile.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But the aid workers know that they are in a war zone and that they are taking a tremendous risk, at risk of their own safety.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes. Well, and first, again, two different incidents. I understand that there is similarities here, but it would be, I think, imprudent to try to compare two things that happened three years apart and two different conflicts.\par \par \uc2 That said, the Israelis have said that they made a mistake here. They have come clean about what happened. And we're going to be looking at their report to see what we think of it as well. And they said that they are going to improve -- they need to improve the deconfliction processes with the aid workers, and that's going to be important going forward.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  yesterday, speaking about the -- saying that Israel risks -- talking about what distinguishes us from terrorists like \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and I'll let him say it. We can talk about it after. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: As has been said, whoever saves the life, saves the entire world. That's our strength. It's what distinguishes us from terrorists like \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . If we lose that reverence for human life, we risk becoming indistinguishable from those we confront.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Does he believe that Israel is now less distinguishable from \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: No, of course, not. You know --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that sounds like what he is saying, John.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I think all he's refreshing there is our idea, as I said earlier, that a modern military, like Israel's, democracy -- when you go to war like this, there is a concomitant obligation to do everything possible to minimize civilian casualties and damage to civilian infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Nobody wants to see innocent life killed, and the \uc2 Israeli Defense Force\uc2 , again, they have apologized for this, they recognize they made a mistake. And I don't believe, and the president doesn't believe that Israeli soldiers are waking up every day, putting their boots on the floor, and saying, hey, we're going to go kill some civilians today. They are not trying to do that. Yet, it's still happening. And it's happening at a rate that's just unacceptable. And we are just urging them to be more careful, and we're going to continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 You know, we're hoping to have a conversation with them in coming days about Rafah. Because we still don't believe a major ground operation, when you have more than a million people there is a smart idea. We're not saying don't go after the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  battalions, nobody has ever told them not to do that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Don't you think, Iran looks at this development and says, great, the U.S. is urging Israel to cool this whole thing down. And that's going to provide a great opportunity for us.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: If I was in Iran and the supreme leader, and that's what I thought, then I think I need to read a little bit closer, because that's not what we're saying.\par \par \uc2 They will be wrong to come to that conclusion, Martha. We're not saying that Israel can continue to prosecute this fight. We're just saying that the how, the way they are doing it needs to change.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We have President Biden jumping in here from the bridge in Baltimore, where we watch that horrible crash of the bridge. And the president is on the scene there. Let's listen to what the commander in chief had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: By the way, folks, I say to my dad, dad, they mispronouncing Baltimore, my dad and the Biden -- please sit down.\par \par \uc2 The Biden family goes all the way back to being waterman in this bay for a long, long time back in the mid-1800s. And my father born and raised here in Baltimore. And there is a strong, strong connection, still have family in the region as well.\par \par \uc2 Governor Moore, Senator Cardin, Senator \uc2 Van Hollen\uc2 , Congressman Mfume, Mayor Scott, County Executive Johnny O. -- I like that. Johnny O. --\par \par \uc2 To all the military members and first responders and, most importantly, to the people of Maryland, I'm here to say: Your nation has your back, and I mean it. Your nation has your back.\par \par \uc2 And you've got, without exaggeration, one of the finest delegations in the Congress of any state in the Union. And they know how to get things done, and we're going to get this paid for. Aren't we?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: All right. I was just briefed by the United -- Unified Command about the ongoing impact of this tragic collapse of Francis Scott Key Bridge last Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The damage is devastating, and our hearts are still breaking. Eight, eight construction workers went into the water when the bridge fell. Six lost their lives. Most were immigrants, but all were Marylanders -- hard- working, strong, and selfless.\par \par \uc2 After pulling a night shift fixing potholes, they were on a break when the ship struck.\par \par \uc2 Just seconds before, one of the men named Carlos, who was only 24, left a message for his girlfriend. Here's what it said: "We just poured cement, and we're waiting for it to dry," he said.\par \par \uc2 Well, to all the families and loved ones who are grieving, I've come here to grieve with you. We all are.\par \par \uc2 It's not the same, but I know a little bit about what it's like to lose a piece of your soul -- to get that phone call in the middle of the night to say family members are gone. I've been there.\par \par \uc2 It's feeling like having a black hole in your chest, like you're being sucked in, unable to breath. The anger, the pain, the depth of the loss is so profound.\par \par \uc2 And we know it's hard to believe, and you're probably not going to believe me, but I can tell you now from personal experience: The day is going to come when the memory of your loved one, as you walk by that park or with the church or something that you shared together, it's going to bring a smile to your lips before it brings a tear to your eye.\par \par \uc2 It's going to happen. It's going to take a while, but I promise you, it will happen. And that's when you know you're going to be able to make it. I promise you it will come. And our prayers for you is that time comes sooner rather than later -- but it will come.\par \par \uc2 We'll also never forget the contributions these men made to this city. We're going to keep working hard to recover each of them.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, my vow is that we will not rest, as Carlos said, until the cement has dried on the entirety of a new bridge -- a new bridge.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this afternoon, we took an aerial tour to survey the wreckage. You know, from the air, I saw the bridge that's been ripped apart. But here on the ground, I see a community that's been pulled together.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank you all -- the first responders, the port workers, state and local officials -- who sprang into action before dawn, who've been here ever since.\par \par \uc2 And we did talk at, I think it was, 2:00 or 3:00 in the morning. You were out here. You were here.\par \par \uc2 Within minutes of the collapse, the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  arrived on the scene. Within hours, I ordered personnel from the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2 , the Navy, the Department of Transportation to assist in every way possible.\par \par \uc2 Within a day --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, we're going to keep a close eye on that. Remember the $1.2 trillion infrastructure bill, only about 40 percent of that money has been committed. When we talk about where the money is going to come from? A lot of questions about that. It seems like it is already out there for roads and bridges. So, we'll see where that goes.\par \par \uc2 And when we come back, Howie Kurtz and Kellyanne Conway on the political headwinds the president is facing. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Boy, pretty incredible. We are now seven months to the day away from the presidential election. This is going to go very fast now, folks. So how much impact is this issue that we were just talking about with John Kirby, the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war, going to have on your vote?\par \par \uc2 Recent polling shows that Americans are split when asked if they would support a candidate who continues military aid to Israel. And this past February, more than 100,000 Democrats in the crucial state of Michigan fired a warning shot over their support for President Biden come November.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would say a lot of folks who have been typically blue, Democrat voters are voting uncommitted today in an attempt to persuade President Biden to do more, to speak out more about the pain, about the atrocities in Gaza, knowing that it hits so close to home, especially in Michigan, knowing that we have such a large Arab and Muslim community.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think it's a good way to send a message like, hey, we as a country need to start doing better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Clearly an issue in Michigan, which has been a very tight state in the past couple of presidential elections. Let's bring in Howie Kurtz, host of MediaBuzz, and Kellyanne Conway, former senior counselor to President Trump, now president of KA Consulting and a Fox News contributor. Great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HOWIE KURTZ, MEDIABUZZ HOST: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you so much for being here today. You know, Howie, let me start with you. In terms of this as an election issue, when I look at that very divided number --\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- 47-48, to me, it screams for an articulation of the mission and an understanding of why the country is committed or not committed to doing something. Do you see a shift coming here from the White House --\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: I think this might be a turning point. And I would start by saying, look, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  started this war and could end this war today. But I don't think Bibi left Biden much choice because all of Biden's public demands to have Israel do something to reduce civilian casualties and provide more aid have gotten nowhere. Now, as you were saying with John Kirby, of course, mistakes, tragic mistakes are made in wartime. Of course, the United States has been in that situation as well. But I think just something about the killing of these aid workers in the clearly marked convoy, Jose Andres' World Food Kitchen has just had a tremendous impact.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , your former boss, said to me recently that Israel has to finish this up and get out. And now he's saying this is a PR debacle for Israel. And I think even \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would agree with that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, speaking of that, let's play that from the \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2  interview. Here's the former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER US PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You got to get it over with and you have to get back to normalcy. And I'm not sure that I'm loving the way they're doing it because you got to have victory. They're losing the PR war. Israel is absolutely losing the PR war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Kellyanne, it sounds like the current president and the last president are pretty much on the same page on this.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Well, maybe that little part, Martha. But let's not forget who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been to Israel as commander in chief and president, let's review. There's been no more pro-Israel president than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Seven presidents, seven made a promise to move the US embassy to Jerusalem and to recognize Jerusalem as the capital of Israel. One President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  kept that promise. The Abraham Accords, the Golan Heights, the list goes on and on. And then, of course, even keeping in check a nuclear capable Iran.\par \par \uc2 You've got \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in his very first week in office saying to Houthis, you're not really that dangerous. Let's get back in the JCPOA and all sorts of nonsense and look where we are.\par \par \uc2 I think that President Biden is keeping no one pleased or happy about his positions. They're ever changing. When he's going to Marine One today, he looks almost glib and disinterested, completely disengaged that a reporter dare ask him, are you abandoning Israel? He should have turned around as the US president and said, of course not.\par \par \uc2 But here's what I think and here's what needs to happen. You see what's happening on the ground. Young people, voters of color in some of these states don't really care what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has to say in making excuses. They're standing with a 31 or so Democrats in the House right across the street here, who refused to condemn \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in a resolution. The resolution was very straightforward.\par \par \uc2 This is not just happening on the college campuses, it's happening in the halls of Congress where we have some of the youngest, most prominent Democrats in their party refusing to condemn \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Last point, if you look at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's handling of issues, the two where he does the worst right now, where his disapproval is well above 60%, the southern border, which he knew coming in was going to be a powder keg for him and Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war, which is newer, six months old. He's not handling either of those in a way that I think suggests he's going to make any kind of course correction between now and early voting five months from now, Martha, so that he can win back some of these voters. They can go to RFK K Jr., they go to Trump, they can stay home.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. And there's no doubt that this is weighing on him in a big way, Howie. Here's a little way backside, as we like to call them, from November 4, 2023, not that long ago but this is still very much in the minds of the White House. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (Inaudible) Joe needs to halt his actions immediately and realize that he's going to face massive opposition from Democrat voters next election.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You have a whole generation of voters coming up, who are going to be able to vote in this next election. And if you don't stop this genocide now, you will not get these votes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: There is no question, Martha, that the prosecution of this war has hurt \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  politically. And so, it would be easy to say, well, he's doing this now in order to repair the damage, and particularly with the left wing of his party whichever much liked Israel anyway.\par \par \uc2 But I don't think that's right. I think that Biden continues. I don't think it's fair to say that he's abandoned Israel, but what is the alternative? And again, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , I still can't get over the brutality and the barbarism of the October 7th attacks or the taking of civilian hostages, which we now seem used to deal.\par \par \uc2 The alternative would be that American taxpayers pay hundreds of millions of dollars for military aid and the president of the United States doesn't have anything to say about it. And Israel does whatever it wants, even if it's hurting Israel with world opinion. And I think that's what's happening.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, it's a tough thing to navigate and you feel world opinion, but then what I feel is not articulated, Kellyanne, we only have a few seconds here, but just for a quick thought, but is the why.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right. And what happens to Iran, Iran no doubt watches this and says, oh, the US is backing off. This is fantastic news for Iran.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Absolutely. And of course, they're watching all the bullies and strongmen in the world are watching what happens. The word that encapsulates what's happening right now in this country is weakness. And people want strength. The opposite of strength is weakness. The opposite of strength is failure. And by the way, five of those hostages are American.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's right.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: And I did not hear the commander in chief and American president even say that today --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He didn't mention them.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: -- from the south lawn.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, it's incredible. And 31 Americans died in this initial attack that was begun by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , who started this war that they are now in deep into Gaza now. So thank you very much, Kellyanne and Howie. Seven months to go --\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- and this is going to continue to be a very hot button issue. Thank you both for being here today. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 So a collision of two incredibly rare events as millions across the country prepare for a total solar eclipse, a powerful earthquake jolts the northeast.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't know. I'm not sure what's happening there in Morristown during the earthquake this morning. You can see a little bit of wave in the hill there. A lot of people on the east coast say that they did not believe that this was actually happening, a rare 4.8 earthquake centered in New Jersey, of all places, but rattling homes 50 miles away in Pennsylvania. And it was also felt by some in New York City. Correspondent Alexis McAdams reporting live from Times Square. Did you feel it, Alexis?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. I didn't feel it there at Fox News headquarters but people I've talked to here across Manhattan, specifically in Times Square, sure did. So we'll check in with people in a second on that.\par \par \uc2 But we're talking about this earthquake. As you said, you can check around here. You can see kind of what's going on in this area. It's kind of business to usual here going on even though the epicenter is about 40 miles away from where we are right now. At least 42 million people felt this earthquake here across the country. So we want to check in with some people who said they felt it, too.\par \par \uc2 You guys were in New Jersey this morning, right?\par \par \uc2 EDWARD SON, FELT THE EARTHQUAKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And you woke up from the earthquake. Tell me about what happened.\par \par \uc2 SON: We were in bed and the earthquake hit. We didn't know what hit at first, we just thought it was the bed shaking, but it -- was from the dog. But it was an earthquake. We realized after, because it just kept shaking.\par \par \uc2 McAdams: Talking about the shaking, how long did it last and like how intense was it?\par \par \uc2 SON: It didn't last long. Maybe like a minute or two.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It didn't last.\par \par \uc2 SON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It wasn't strong enough to make anything fall.\par \par \uc2 SON: Yes. Just things were shaking, the TV was shaking, the desk was shaking. It wasn't too scary but --\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: But you knew right away it was an earthquake?\par \par \uc2 SON: Yes, we assumed.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Did you get all those alerts then sent to your phone?\par \par \uc2 SON: Not at the moment. Yes, we got an Amber alert a little after when we're on the -- when we're on the car to get to New York, but before that we didn't get anything.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Kind of a wild morning there for you to wake up to. Thanks for talking to us. So that's just kind of similar what we're hearing, Martha. But, oh, again, we want to just kind of show you around here in a live look in New York City. The mayor told everybody, hey, go back to doing everything as usual on this Friday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And that's what people are doing in the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2 , walking around in Times Square after that 4.8 magnitude earthquake hit near White House Station, New Jersey. You could feel from Baltimore, in Massachusetts to the border on New Hampshire, feeling that ground shaking. No major damage reported, though, in the area.\par \par \uc2 So the quake interrupted the daily commute, though, in New York City for hours and in New Jersey, you can check this out. Long lines of transit customers in New Jersey waiting there for Penn Station trains while the bridge, the tunnel and the tracks were all inspected. All three airports, Newark, JFK and LaGuardia are back open, even though they had ground stops across the New York area.\par \par \uc2 So prepare for the aftershock is what we're hearing now from authorities. And we have heard reports of that aftershock in New Jersey, so we'll keep you posted on that. But it's either -- I felt it and things were falling off my desk or people are like, what earthquake are you talking about? So depends on who you talk to, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Thank you very much, Alexis. Alexis McAdams in Times Square, New York City.\par \par \uc2 So for the first time in seven years, we are about to see a total solar eclipse here in the United States. \uc2 NASA\uc2  says more than 30 million people live in the path of totality. Lots more are traveling to get to the path of totality for a firsthand look on Monday, others will be watching from planes in the sky. Let's bring in Corey Powell, American scientist, editor at large and founder of OpenMind Magazine. Hi, Corey.\par \par \uc2 COREY POWELL, AMERICAN SCIENTIST: Hello.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great to see you again. It's great to have you back on the show.\par \par \uc2 POWELL: Nice to be here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You got your eclipse t-shirt. You're ready to go.\par \par \uc2 POWELL: That's my eclipse t shirt from 2017.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, I'm glad you kept it. I'm glad you kept it. So give us three scenarios. So these people who are going to the path of totality, what exactly will they see when they look up into the sky and for how long?\par \par \uc2 POWELL: Well, so first of all, you hope for clear skies. I wish you all clear skies. So if you're right in the path, you'll get between three and four minutes of the sun completely covered by the moon. So you have a long lead up. Over a period of about an hour, you'll notice the sun getting into a thinner and thinner crescent. Use your eclipse glasses for that part. You don't want to be looking straight at the sun until it's completely covered. When it's completely covered, it goes from being a weird looking sun to something like you've never seen before. It is a glowing circle in the sky and it is very hard to believe it's real.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wow.\par \par \uc2 POWELL: So that's -- for the people who are right in the path, they get the best of all experiences.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So what are we going to see in New York City because it's going to happen during THE STORY, during the 3:00 hour on Monday. So, what's the best case scenario for what we might see, Corey?\par \par \uc2 POWELL: Right. So for the people who aren't right in the path, you don't get that really cool -- you don't get the ring, you don't get the corona, you don't get that totality. What you do get is the long period of the sun getting eaten away into this little crescent as the moon passes in front of it.\par \par \uc2 So again, you put on your solar eclipse glasses. They don't cost very much. They're easy to get. Everybody's selling them. And you can watch this. You can actually kind of see this dance. You can see the moon passing in front of the sun.\par \par \uc2 One of the coolest things to me is if you're standing anywhere where there are trees around, you look down at the shadows, and the shadows of the trees turn into little crescents. It's the strangest thing. You look down at the sidewalk and the sidewalk is just covered with these little crescents. It's very beautiful.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's -- yes, that's cool. I'm glad you brought that up. What about if you're in an airplane? What would you see?\par \par \uc2 POWELL: Well, if you're in an airplane, what they're trying to do is they're trying to basically fly you along with -- carrying you with the movement of the earth so the eclipse lasts longer. And I've never had that experience. I've seen photos and videos from it. It's pretty spectacular. First of all, you just -- you see more, it lasts longer. You're chasing the shadow, but you also get to see the shadow projected down onto the ground. You can really see this -- you feel this whole geometry of, there's a shadow from space and it's hitting the earth and you can kind of watch it moving through, sweeping over the landscape. It's quite spectacular.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So I just had to go through this to get it straight myself. But a solar eclipse, we're going to get the moon in between the sun and the earth, right? Now, a lunar eclipse is when the Earth is in between the moon and the sun?\par \par \uc2 POWELL: Exactly. If the moon is blocking the sun for us, then you get a solar eclipse. It's the other way around. If the earth is blocking the sun for the moon, we look back and we see a lunar eclipse. If you happen to be standing on the moon at that time, you would see a solar eclipse. So that's something to look forward to.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And in 1967, there was a solar eclipse viewed from the surface of the moon, which you can look up pictures of. That's really cool too.\par \par \uc2 POWELL: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Corey --\par \par \uc2 POWELL: The Surveyor 3 probe, yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, exactly. It's fascinating. So we'll see what we see on Monday. We hope for a nice clear day so that everyone gets a brilliant view of this. And the images that we're looking at are just really spectacular. So it's a great thing to kind of stop and think about how small we are on this day. Corey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 POWELL: It's amazing. It really is. You know, we've got earthquakes, we got this majesty of nature.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, pretty powerful stuff. Corey, thank you very much. Corey Powell, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 POWELL: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So influential radio host Charlemagne the God sounds off on what he says is driving DEI, diversity, equity and inclusion, in corporate America. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLEMAGNE THE GOD, "THE BREAKFAST CLUB" RADIO HOST: it's just corporate PR. They want good vibes, and also they want to cover their -- okay? Did you know that if a company gets sued for civil rights violations, just having a DEI program will be counted as evidence in their favor, even if the program doesn't do -- okay? It's the I have a black friend of the legal system.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No kidding, right? "Making Money" host Charles Payne joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking right now, new video from the border in New Mexico. Correspondent Matt Finn telling us that he and his crew spotted two men trying to get through one of the many holes in the wall. Matt says the men turned around and took off, then screamed, let us cross, why are you so racist?\par \par \uc2 So that is just a little bit of a slice of life at the border. You can see the hole in the wall right there, that coming in from our own Matt Finn covering the border for us right now.\par \par \uc2 And new backlash against "diversity, equity and inclusion" efforts, also known as DEI, this time from popular black radio host Charlemagne the God. He says, yes, we want diversity, equity and inclusion, but watch what else he said on the "Daily Show."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLEMAGNE THE GOD: The truth about DEI is that although it's well intentioned, it's mostly garbage. In fact, maybe the only thing that DEI has accomplished is giving racist white people cover to be openly racist. Did you know that if a company gets sued for civil rights violations, just having a DEI program will be counted as evidence in their favor, even if the program doesn't do -- okay? It's the, I have a black friend of the legal system.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. With that, we bring in Charles Payne, host of "Making Money" on Fox Business. Charles, good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 Do you agree with what you heard there from Mr. Charlamagne the God?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX BUSINESS HOST: I agree with most of it in a sense that it's lip service, it's virtue signaling. It's not results-oriented. And I think that the real problem from the very beginning was the e part, right? Because we sort of use to talking about equality, the equity part, threw a lot of people off or threw businesses off. It just -- it's -- and it's just one of these things where a company says, yes, we do this and we got -- we've got the program, but there's no results.\par \par \uc2 You see Silicon Valley almost every year they say, oh, they put out these reports, we don't have any black workers, but we're going to do this in the next year. We don't have any black workers, but we're going to do that.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's not all on the companies, right? It's also on a society that's not generating the kind of opportunities because we don't have the right education for most Americans, to be quite frank with you. But this program, so many of these programs, I'm tired of them, Martha. They're just lip service, and they are -- and they really are BS.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: One more soundbite from him on the "Daily Show." Let's watch this real quick.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLEMAGNE THE GOD: Over 900 studies have shown that DEI programs don't make the workplace better for minorities. In fact, it can actually make things worse because of the backlash effect. The biggest failure of DEI is that the number of black people in power at big companies is basically the same as it was five years ago. In fact, maybe the only thing that DEI has accomplished is giving racist white people cover to be openly racist.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, Charles, as we all covered, we saw a lot of these kinds of programs and efforts after the killing of George Floyd and that followed a number of events that kind of culminated in this pushback where corporations were challenged, you know? What's your program? What are you doing about this? And they -- most of them seem to have buckled to this pressure.\par \par \uc2 And what he's saying about these studies that he cites is that they show that they didn't accomplish any goal at all.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So I guess the question for us now is, you know, how do you accomplish that? And I think you touched on it a moment ago when you talked about education.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. I mean, the big -- quite frank with you, Martha. I wouldn't say they buckled to it. It was such an easy box to check, you know, to be quite frank, you know? Okay, start this program. Bring in somebody, you know, and also create this title, right? You had all these sort of chief diversity officer kind of titles.\par \par \uc2 But if you ever looked at an organizational chart, none of them led to the CEO's office, right? Like, you know, other real officers of the company. It was all speeches, it was all BS, and it was all checking a box kind of thing. And then that's not what we want, right? We don't want fake results. We don't want fake efforts, and that's what that is.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, you know what? Some corporations need to change the culture. But I know a lot of people in business and a lot of my white friends who run big companies, and they say, listen, I'm trying, Charles, you know, but we also would love to see more black candidates who are prepared.\par \par \uc2 Now, honestly, in life, there are times when someone takes a shot, you know? Not everyone who got a job in this building was 100% "qualified." Someone saw something in them. When I got my first job on Wall Street, Martha, I was not the best candidate in terms of academically, but the guy interviewed a whole lot of people. He saw something in me.\par \par \uc2 But overall, our country better do a whole lot better, not just for black people or Hispanic people, but for the nation in general. Because we are falling so far behind that this conversation is a moot point from where we all could be. Listen, we had a jobs report out today. Native Americans are not getting jobs, they're losing jobs. That's the warning signal.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And I can't help but think about the areas that were the most damaged during COVID learning loss. And, you know, in many cases, minorities were the hardest hit in that equation. And I think if we had a nationwide effort to make sure that these kids caught up, you give them the tools that they need, and it's barely discussed from the halls of power in this country. And I think we really need to.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It's got to start in kindergarten. It's got to start in kindergarten.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Give them the hardest, most rigorous curriculum --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: -- out there, period.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know. It's all about days off and shortened schedules, so that's not going to get us there. Charles, thank you so much. Always good to see you, my friend. Thank you\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So that is THE STORY for today, for Friday, April 5th. But as always, THE STORY goes on. I'm going to see you on "Fox News Sunday" this weekend. I will be in for Shannon. Texas Governor Greg Abbott will join me and Senator Chris Coons. Join us then on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020240406ek4500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 White House Relays Frustrations To Israel; Illegal Immigration And Sanctuary City Impact On United States; Migrants Accused Of Squatting And Damaging Residence; Some Cities Spend Billions For Illegal Migrant Services; "No Labels" Will Not Field Third-Party Ticket; Chinese National Attempted To Run Gate At Marine Base; Town Revolts Against Mayor Dubbed "Dolton Dictator;" Classroom Building In \uc2 North Carolina State University\uc2  Being Inspected For Potentially Carcinogenic Chemicals; Ultrarich Building Shelters In Preparation For End Of World; Rep. John James (R-MI) And Rep. Don Davis (D-NC) Interviewed On Bipartisan Pushback Against Biden Administration's Green Agenda; President Biden Threatens To Revise U.S. Policy Toward Israel After Israeli Strike Kills Humanitarian Aid Workers For \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 ; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Clarifies His Recent Comments Urging Israel To Finish Up War In Gaza\b0\par\par\uc2 John Robertson, Peter Doocy, Nate Foy, Madison Alworth, Mike  Emanuel, Mike Tobin, Gillian Turner, Bret Baier, Jonathan Serrie, Griff  Jenkins \par \uc2 7,866 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY BELISARIO, ASSOCIATE PRODUCER ON JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME: What kind of car do you drive?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I have a Corvette.\par \par \uc2 BELISARIO: Do you keep classified documents in your Corvette?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, is -- that's like -- not actually I can't tell you where I put them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: 8:00 --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Judge. They said save your OMT.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes. They just said to me, save it from tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It must be a good one.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's a clueless criminal.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Because she is a gifted --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And they're all over.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: She is gifted at "ONE MORE THING." All right. That's it for us have a great --\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Hey, good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm John Roberts, in for Bret Baier this evening.\par \par \uc2 New information on the suspected migrant squatters who have been in and out of custody in New York, and how they may have gotten into that property in the first place.\par \par \uc2 Our "COMMON GROUND" segment deals with bipartisan opposition to some of President Biden's green agenda.\par \par \uc2 And how much would you spend to survive the apocalypse? A look at the growing market for doomsday bunkers. All of that just ahead.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, President Biden says an immediate ceasefire between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is essential. The White House says the president told Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  today, future U.S. support for the Gaza war depends on new steps to protect civilians and aid workers.\par \par \uc2 The administration is denying this represents a change in policy. White House correspondent Peter Doocy starts us out this evening. He's live on the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, John. For half an hour today, President Biden and Prime Minister Netanyahu had what officials here are calling a good and direct conversation.\par \par \uc2 But officials here are admitting publicly there has been growing frustration with Israel in the West Wing and they are calling here for a ceasefire in Gaza immediately.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL We need to see certain changes. And if we don't, then, we'll have to consider changes to our own policy.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): According to the White House, President Biden told Prime Minister Netanyahu, the strikes on humanitarian workers and the overall humanitarian situation are unacceptable."\par \par \uc2 And U.S. military aid may dry up if Israel does not publicly provide concrete steps to alleviate suffering in Gaza in coming hours or days.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: He made clear that U.S. policy with respect to Gaza will be determined by our assessment of Israel's immediate action on these steps.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): This call was a direct result of the Israeli strike on \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  aid workers. Chef Jose Andres, claims his staff were targeted.\par \par \uc2 JOSE ANDRES, FOUNDER, \uc2 WORLD CENTRAL KITCHEN\uc2 : They airstrikes on our convoy -- I don't think were an unfortunate mistake. It was really a direct attack on a clearly marked vehicles, whose movements were known.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The president has seen that clip.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: But he's been briefed and he's aware by the comments.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Biden allies have been imploring him to shift his approach to Israel.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: The strategy that the United States is implementing at this point is not working to change the behavior of Prime Minister Netanyahu. It is not working to end the war.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): And while officials won't outline a definite policy change, things appear to have changed. Since this Biden statement, October 7th, "My administration's support for Israel's security is rock solid and unwavering."\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: How is the support unwavering, but you're also reconsidering policy choices?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Both can be true. Not going to waiver, but -- will there, perhaps, be some policy changes we might have to make if we don't see policy changes out of Israel? Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: No matter what words they use, things here are changing. In fact, President Biden, according to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  shared privately with a small group of Muslim community leaders here at the White House earlier this week that he has been told about the war in Gaza to "Stop it." Stop it now by his most trusted adviser, the first lady. John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All depends too on what your definition of the word, waiver, is. Peter Doocy tonight at the White House. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And "BREAKING TONIGHT", the southern border crisis and the revolving door for migrants accused of occupying a Bronx home illegally, along with more serious crimes.\par \par \uc2 One of them is now at large after arrests and re-arrests earlier this week. We're also learning how sanctuary city status or the absence of it is contributing to the situation.\par \par \uc2 We've got "FOX TEAM COVERAGE" Madison Alworth in New York, as Democrat-run big city start to tabulate the billions of taxpayer dollars being spent on dealing with migrants.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with correspondent Nate Foy. He is in the Bronx where it all went down. Hello, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, John.\par \par \uc2 One of the eight Venezuelan migrants, who police say squatted at this Bronx, New York home behind me is still on the run tonight, as the homeowner returns to assess the damage left behind.\par \par \uc2 He gave us a tour of the home he wants to remain nameless, because he still fears for his life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: New York law is not favor to the safety.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): This landlord took us inside his Bronx, New York house, after an eight-month battle to evict eight Venezuelan migrants, who police say broke into his basement and made it their home.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Did they ever threaten you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, they threatened me. Of course, last time, even the officer was there and they were sitting down against us. And they were showing me something like this.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): He tells Fox News he rented the basement to an Ecuadorian woman and her husband, who moved out abruptly and left the unit for her brother. But after failing to finalize the new lease, the apparent migrant squatters moved in, broke his security cameras and cost him thousands of dollars.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What are you going to do as a landlord, like me?\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Three of the migrants accused of squatting here are in ICE custody, pending removal operations. After a raid made more difficult by New York City's sanctuary status.\par \par \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  tells Fox, "We received no assistance from \uc2 NYPD\uc2 ." One of the migrants, Hector Desousa-Villalta, crossed the border illegally last May. Police arrested him three months later for assault and attempted murder.\par \par \uc2 JOHN CHELL, CHIEF OF PATROL, NEW YORK POLICE DEPARTMENT: This gentleman here is not here for the American dream. Most of the migrants in New York City had to work, pursue that dream. Others are not.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Two of the migrants arrested last week now face additional shoplifting charges in nearby Nassau County, which is not a sanctuary County and will cooperate with ICE.\par \par \uc2 BRUCE BLAKEMAN, EXECUTIVE, NASSAU COUNTY, NEW YORK: What we have to do is work with ICE and work within the framework of a very, very bad law that the state legislature and the governor have imposed upon us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Two additional migrants remain at Rikers Island, while ICE is still looking for Jefferson (INAUDIBLE). The Bronx D.A. requested a $45,000 bond for him, but the judge let him walk free. John?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: It is curious how all of this suddenly became a priority for ICE now. Nate Foy for us in the Bronx. Nate, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, to the financial toll of the migrant crisis in places both near to and far from the border.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Madison Alworth, takes a look at the staggering numbers tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Cities across America are spending billions of dollars on benefits for migrants who have crossed the border illegally.\par \par \uc2 CASCIA JONES, IMMIGRANT: Yes. I do feel like I slap in the face. I slapped in the face, as me, as a legal immigrant.\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH (voice over): How many benefits each asylum seeker is getting is a story better told by smaller numbers. In the sanctuary city of New York, housing is guaranteed with tens of thousands of migrants flooding the system, some have been housed in hotel rooms that go for $300 a night.\par \par \uc2 Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  is also running a $53 million debit card program. $1,400 for a family of four. The mayor defending the move.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), MAYOR OF NEW YORK CITY: I'm not hearing from my colleagues down in Ecuador, Colombia, Mexico, that hey, everybody is running to New York, because they are going to get a food card.\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH (voice over): In Los Angeles, migrants are given full coverage health insurance, and soon, could get $1,000 a month through the city's guaranteed income pilot program, which is immigration status blind.\par \par \uc2 And Chicago has spent $300 million since 2023 on migrants on things like rental assistance. If you're a migrant there, you could get $15,000 annually to help pay for your housing. But some places now cutting back on migrant housing.\par \par \uc2 MIKE JOHNSTON, MAYOR OF DENVER, COLORADO: That still be a city that's welcoming. And we can be a city that realizes we have to be conservative with our fiscal resources.\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH (voice over): Denver has closed for hotels that were being used to house migrants. Saving the city roughly $60 million. Each new migrant that arrives to the Mile High City costs it anywhere from $1,600 to $2,000.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH (on camera): Illinois is also making cuts. Starting May 1st, migrants who have been in the U.S. for less than five years are losing their health care coverage.\par \par \uc2 That will be $13 million in savings for the state. John?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: It is a big expense for everybody out there. Madison Alworth for us. Madison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And now, to "DEMOCRACY 24". In the campaigns of both President Biden and former President Trump, breathing a sigh of relief tonight.\par \par \uc2 Chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel is here to tell us why. Hi, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: John, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Centrist group, No Labels announced today, it's giving up on its plan to create a unity ticket for this year's presidential election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE CUNNINGHAM, NATIONAL DIRECTOR, NO LABELS: No Labels was looking for a hero. And a hero never emerged.\par \par \uc2 And that's where we ran to the trouble is at the end of the day, we weren't able to find candidates that we felt had a straightforward path to victory in this.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): No Labels tried recruiting some big names, including former Republican press presidential candidates \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  and \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , former Republican Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  of Maryland, who decided to run for the Senate instead, and West Virginia Democrat Senator Joe Manchin, who is retiring from the Senate, but all declined.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: What I will tell you is I'm a conservative Republican. I have said many, many times, I would not run as an independent, I would not run as No Labels, because I am a Republican.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): I will not be seeking a third-party run. I will not be involved in the presidential run.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): No Labels did have some achievements, saying, it regained ballot access and 21 states, including battlegrounds like North Carolina, Arizona, and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 It also said it had a $70 million budget to launch a unity ticket campaign to attract voters who didn't want to support Trump or Biden in 2024.\par \par \uc2 This announcement coming after the passing of its founding chair, former Connecticut Senator Joe Lieberman, who died last week.\par \par \uc2 Today's news does not mean it will just be Biden versus Trump round two, independents \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  and Cornell West, plus libertarian, and Green Party candidates could all be in the mix in November.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And one of the big questions, will RFK managed to get the criteria to be included in debate -- debates?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: That would be fascinating.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: That way, wouldn't it? Mike, thank you, appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 A judge in Georgia is rejecting former President Trump's bid to dismiss criminal charges in the States 2020 election interference case.\par \par \uc2 Trump says his free speech rights are being violated. But the judge ruled the indictment alleges statements by the former president and 14 others charged in the case were made of what he calls furtherance of criminal activity. He says the statements are not protected by the First Amendment to the U.S. Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Another judge this one in Florida is refusing to dismiss the former president's classified documents case. Mr. Trump says he was allowed to have the materials under the Presidential Records Act. The Special Counsel argues the law does not apply the classified documents.\par \par \uc2 Stocks took heavy losses today over the increasing tension in the Middle East. The Dow fell 530 points. The S&P 500 was off 64. NASDAQ was down 228.\par \par \uc2 Up next, why some Chinese nationals are trying to sneak into one U.S. territory. But first, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 WFXT in Boston, as a deadly late season nor'easter pounds the region. It brings damaging wind gusts, rain, and yes, heavy snow. It has knocked out power to more than 600,000 customers from Pennsylvania all the way up to Maine.\par \par \uc2 Police in Collegeville, Pennsylvania say an 82-year-old woman died when a tree crushed her car.\par \par \uc2 FOX11 in Los Angeles, as investigators tried to solve one of the largest cash heists in the city's history.\par \par \uc2 They say a burglary crew broke into a money storage facility on Sunday and got away with, listen to this. $30 million. No arrests had been announced.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at State Farm Stadium in Glendale, Arizona from Fox 10 there. One of the big stories there tonight, the defending national champion Connecticut Huskies at a tough time getting to Phoenix where they will seek to win their second straight title. They are ranked number one.\par \par \uc2 The teams' plane encountered mechanical issues, The take-off delayed from 6:00 in the evening, until 1:30 In the morning. The group arrived in Arizona at 3:15 local time this morning. The National semi-finals will be played Saturday evening.\par \par \uc2 And that's tonight's live look, "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: A federal appeals court has upheld a lower court judge's ruling putting a temporary halt on New Jersey's primary ballot design. The state uses what is called the county line system. The group's candidates who have won the county party's backing in a single column, then lists others in discrete places elsewhere on the ballot. Critics say that gives the party back candidates an unfair advantage.\par \par \uc2 This ought to be interesting, Fox News Digital reporting \uc2 EcoHealth Alliance\uc2  President \uc2 Peter Daszak\uc2  will testify publicly before the House Select Subcommittee on the coronavirus pandemic next month. Daszak testified behind closed doors in November that House Republicans said his testimony contained many discrepancies they want to air in public.\par \par \uc2 Tonight we're learning about another immigration problem as Chinese nationals are caught tried to sneak not across the southern border but into the U.S. territory of Guam.\par \par \uc2 State Department Correspondent Gillian Turner reports why this could be a serious national security issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): As the number of Chinese nationals crossing into the U.S. illegally is surging, some are finding a new route in via Guam. A Us territory that's home to three strategic military bases and over 20,000 American troops, a key outpost for America's defense against China.\par \par \uc2 118 Chinese nationals have been apprehended trying to enter Guam since 2020 prompting national security concerns.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: There's no chance, Laura, that this is accidental, that these folks are actually seeking asylum.\par \par \uc2 TURNER (voice over): Senator \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2  says many are exploiting illegal loophole that allows visa free entry to the Northern Mariana Islands for 14 days, just a boat ride away from Guam.\par \par \uc2 This policy has made the island vulnerable to a plethora of problems including drug trafficking, illegal immigration and organized crime.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O'BRIEN, FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: They use our spies to drive into the military bases to test our readiness, to test our security because they're planning for a future actions against U.S. bases in a war.\par \par \uc2 TURNER (voice over): A Pentagon official tells Fox News they're working on plans for a "Guam defense system that will deter aggression and reassure our allies and partners".\par \par \uc2 Guam's customs agency says it's paramount that all arrivals to Guam are screened and authorized for entry and properly accounted for in accordance with the law.\par \par \uc2 Days ago, a Chinese national attempted to run the gate at 29 Palms Marine Corps Base in California. O\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Obviously, that's something that we take very seriously. We work with folks on the ground when we see those types of cases pop up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): Well, at least some Chinese nationals are making it through Guam's defenses. And we know this, John, because earlier in the year, Guam's Labor Department sanctioned four companies operating on the island for using undocumented Chinese laborers, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And of course, we've seen them come across the southern border here in the continental United States by the thousands. Gillian Turner for us at the State Department. Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, she is called the worst mayor in America and she is fighting to stay in office. You will meet her when we come back.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, rescuers search for missing people and try to reach hundreds still stranded from Taiwan's strongest earthquake in 25 years. At least 10 people died, more than a thousand were injured.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's President meets the leader of a strike by thousands of junior doctors. \uc2 Yoon Suk Yeol\uc2  says the government is open to talks about its contentious push to sharply increase medical school admissions.\par \par \uc2 The meeting was the first since more than 90 percent of the country's 13,000 doctor trainees walked off the job in February.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Amsterdam from Earth Cam one of the big stories there tonight, \uc2 NATO\uc2  marks 75 years of collective defense across Europe and North America. The top diplomats of the alliance are vowing to stay the course in Ukraine. The anniversary comes as the now 32 nation alliance considers a plan to provide more predictable long term military support to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: A woman that critics call the worst mayor in America, Tiffany Henyard of Dolton, Illinois is accused of massive corruption and ethical issues. And she is allegedly taking extraordinary steps to stay in power. Our Senior Correspondent Mike Tobin is in Dolton tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY HENYARD, MAYOR OF DOLTON, ILLINOIS: I'm here at the White House.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Pictured here with President Biden, Tiffany Henyard calls herself the super mayor. With her image on posters all over town, critics call her the Dolton dictator and accused her of embezzling public money, taking lavish trips on the public's dime, pulling police officers from patrols for her security detail and retaliating against an employee who claims sexual harassment.\par \par \uc2 Now, even more outrage after Henyard locked most of the public out of a village hall meeting on Monday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE:\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is egregious.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): The meeting came after Henyard vetoed a resolution asking agencies like the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to investigate her use of public funds.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For her to lock us out is deplorable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why can't we have transparency what's going on with our tax dollars?\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): The trustees who approved the resolution walked out of the meeting because of the mayor's decision to lock the public out. Henyard dismissed that as a stunt.\par \par \uc2 HENYARD: They didn't come here to have the business. They came here to do a political theater stunt which you just saw.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): In a town where boarded up homes and store stand on nearly every block. The median income is less than $55,000. Its mayor earns more than five times that. The Illinois Policy Institute says Henyard earns $224,000 as supervisor of the township, then $49,000 as mayor of Dolton.\par \par \uc2 So, her combined income is greater than 49 of 50 governors, including Illinois Governor \uc2 JB Pritzker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She's scary. She's absolutely scary.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): The village explained the locked doors as a response to threats of violence. As a result of the continued misinformation portrayed by the media and internet bloggers who are using Dolton as a means to generate ratings and revenue, i.e. clickbait.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): The doors to village hall are locked now even during business hours, someone looking to get something like a city sticker or permit needs to wait in a vestibule for a security guard to let them in. Only then that person can approach the window and do business with the village, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: That's quite the payday the mayor's got there. No wonder she wants to stay in power. Mike Tobin with quite the story for us tonight. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is close to finalizing highly anticipated standards targeting menthol cigarettes. The regulations would come despite heavy opposition from small businesses, civil rights, law enforcement, and free market consumer groups. The Food and Drug Administration has repeatedly missed target dates for finalizing the proposed ban, the most recent of was earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 There are serious concerns tonight about dozens of instances of cancer at \uc2 North Carolina State University\uc2  that date back decades. \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  is investigating. Correspondent Jonathan Serrie is in Atlanta tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: This classroom building at N.C. State has been closed since November after the university discovered the presence of PCBs. The potentially carcinogenic chemicals were commonly used in construction materials and electrical systems when Poe Hall was built in 1971. Local station WRAL says it has 152 reports of cancer in people who worked or studied in Poe Hall.\par \par \uc2 RANDY WOODSON\uc2 , \uc2 NORTH CAROLINA STATE UNIVERSITY\uc2  CHANCELLOR: At this time, the university is not offering health testing or reimbursement for health testing relating to Poe Hall because we're still in the evaluation phase of the building's environment.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: A CDC spokesperson tells FOX News "The \uc2 National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health\uc2  is conducting a health hazard evaluation at \uc2 North Carolina State University\uc2 's Poe Hall. However, NIOSH does not provide details about ongoing evaluations." At this point, public health officials are not calling this case a cancer cluster, a designation that could create a tailored government response. Houston, Texas, is relocated residents from a cancer cluster the city blames on multiple chemicals from a nearby industrial site. In the case of PCBs, toxic effects have been studied extensively in laboratory animals but less so in humans with casual exposure in a building.\par \par \uc2 FREDERICK GUENGERICH, VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY BIOCHEMIST: Frankly, I think it's still pretty fuzzy even after all these years in terms of how much it would take to cause cancer in people if this ever really happens.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (on camera): Experts say cancer clusters are difficult to prove because the disease so common. According to the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2 , more than 40 percent of all-Americans will be diagnosed with some type of cancer at some point during their lifetimes. John?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: A lot of concern for folks there in North Carolina. John Serrie for us. John, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Call it paranoia or prudent planning. Tonight, we show you some of the doomsday plans for the ultrarich. Many of them are paying big bucks for state-of-the-art bunkers in hopes of surveying the apocalypse in style and keeping the rest of the world out. Correspondent Griff Jenkins has that story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 AL CORBI, SAFE PRESIDENT: The \uc2 Ritz Carlton\uc2  is underground.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: How much are you willing to spend on a doomsday bunker? Want a ring of fire protecting you like a moat, or watercannons to repel invaders? Modern day bunker builders say, if you've got the dough, the sky is the limit.\par \par \uc2 RON HUBBARD, ATLAS SURVIVAL SHELTERS CEO: Wealthy people in the U.S., they don't want to suffer if they go in a bomb shelter. They want basically an extension of their lifestyle in their bunker.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Ron Hubbard, the CEO of Atlas Survivor Shelters, saw a spike in demand when the Ukraine war started, and again when war broke out in Gaza. Other fears driving this trend, World War III, a terror attack, and a power grid collapse. But builder Al Corbi says his biggest concern is something entirely different.\par \par \uc2 CORBI: Every geopolitical jurisdiction should be concerned about is civil unrest. It isn't a foreign invasion anymore. That idea the Russians are coming, the Russians are coming. No, they are not.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Hubbard builds bunkers across the economic spectrum, from the average buyer to the billionaire. This one in Texas ran about $400,000. The inside, a far cry from the Cold War era shelters. For $20,000 you can get the basic bomb shelter. Dig a little deeper in your wallet, and you can get the $100,000 big boy bunker, which works just fine for Alexander Kozlowski.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER KOZLOWSKI, ATLAS SURVIVAL SHELTERS CLIENT: It's worth it. It's worth it 100 percent just to have, just to know that, hey, just in case, we have it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): And we've come a long way, John, from these old fallout shelters of the 1960s. And a Pew poll from the end of 2022 found that roughly four in 10 Americans believe we are living in the end times. So you better start building your bunker now, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Or if you're a multi-billionaire like \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , you can have a very plush bunker on your Hawaiian island. Griff Jenkins for us. Griff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Bret Baier has our "Common Ground" segment as lawmakers from both parties fight against the Biden administration's green agenda.\par \par \uc2 But first, on this day in history 56 years ago, Martin Luther King Junior was assassinated in Memphis, Tennessee. Reverend King was preparing for a march on behalf of striking sanitation workers. He stepped out on his balcony of his hotel when he was shot dead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: The \uc2 Ford Motor Company\uc2  is delaying production of new electric pickups and SUVs as sales growth slows despite the Biden administration's push for an all E.V. future. Tonight's "Common Ground" segment focuses on bipartisan efforts to curb President Biden's green agenda, specifically his plans to send gas powered vehicles the way of the dodo. Here's Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, John.\par \par \uc2 It is time for our "Common Ground" segment, Democracies and Republicans coming together on different things. Joining me tonight, Michigan Republican Congressman John James and North Carolina Democratic Congressman Don Davis. Gentlemen, thanks for being here. I know you work a number of different thing, but the issue we're talking about tonight, electric vehicles, and this mandate about the crackdown on tailpipe emissions. We have writeup that President Biden finalized a crackdown on gas cars, forcing more than half of new car sales to be electric by 2030. Officials said automakers would be forced to ensure 56 percent of light duty car sales are battery electric, another 13 percent are hybrid by 2032.\par \par \uc2 There are real questions about the feasibility of that. Congressman Davis, let me start with you. Are you concerned?\par \par \uc2 REP. DON DAVIS, (D-NC): I am concerned, and I would say that we shouldn't go too far too fast, and I would only imagine putting the trust in Washington D.C. to ultimately determine the winners and losers. I have great concerns here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman James, what are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 REP. JOHN JAMES (R-MI): Well, the automotive industry is not just responsible for 50, five-zero, percent of Michigan's GDP, but all industries in all states rely on the automotive industry to provide a reliable, economical transportation. We all believe in clean air and clean water, but we have must progress and make sure that the federal government is never in a position to remove choice and freedom from the American people. We absolutely must make sure that if you want an E.V., get an E.V. If you want a hybrid, get a hybrid. If you want a gas powered vehicle, get a gas powered vehicle. But these are decisions that should be made by the consumer.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here's a Republican senator and the transportation secretary on this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 KEVIN CRAMER\uc2 , (R-ND) SENATE ENVIRONMENT COMMITTEE: The model is broken because even with big subsidies only rich people can buy an electric vehicle. And we don't have a grid that can energize them. We don't have enough transmission. We don't have enough generation.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: The number one way that we have supported E.V.s is by making them more affordable. That's why those tax credits are about. And that's one of the reasons why you've seen the prices get closer and closer to parity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I guess, Congressman James, the issue is about how feasible it is. We had the infrastructure bill, and $5 billion was allocated for bipartisan effort to create charging stations. Of the $5 billion, as of a month ago is has resulted in four charging stations across the country opening so far. Obviously, there are big plans for things, but again, to Congressman Davis's point, is this getting ahead of things?\par \par \uc2 JAMES: That's $1.25 billion per charging station when we are supposed to have hundreds by now. In places like Iowa where it got to 40 below zero, in places like Michigan where we have winters. These are not solutions. And what this leaves us open to is a China that is coming up with these vehicles fully subsidized, competing on their strong turf for vehicles that they can sell below what it costs us to make.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman Davis, how are you working on this in the common ground way with Congressman James or others who see the same way? And what do you tell the administration about this? We have automakers scaling back or delaying E.V. plans, a number of them have kind of rolled back their plans in recent weeks and months.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Of course, there are those that are very supportive and are moving towards E.V. However, there are others who have grave concerns just like I have. And that we've already heard charging stations, we're just not there on the infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is the mandate. The electric trucks, "Wall Street Journal" editor board, "Let there be electric trucks. The \uc2 EPA\uc2 's rule will require electric models to account for 60 percent of new urban delivery trucks, 25 percent of long-haul tractor-trailer sales by 2032. The harm is predictable in return for no climate benefit." Again, this is the editorial board of "The Wall Street Journal". "Some 1.4 million chargers will have to be installed by 2032 to achieve the EPAs mandate, about 15,000 a month." And the realism there, Congressman James, is really the question.\par \par \uc2 JAMES: The realism is extremely in question. When you take a look at the natural resources that would be required to make these batteries in the first place, you're relying on a supply chain that needs critical minerals that we don't have yet because we don't have the regulatory environment in the Biden administration that is permitting more quickly. You have unreliable supply chains which could cause food shortages, chip shortages on levels where you don't have these deliveries, on top of the fact that these are heavier trucks. OK, you say that you want to scale back the impact on the environment. What's going to happen when you have heavier trucks that are on the road. You're going to require more trucks to carry the same payload. And so you're going to have more trucks damaging the road and having more of a demand on our grid system, which is going to make worse our climate issues.\par \par \uc2 It is not based in reality, and somebody needs to come to common sense, which is why we are working across the aisle to push back on this administration to say enough is enough.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing, Congressman Davis. When you talk to other colleagues on your side of the aisle who are pushing for this and are siding with the administration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ED MARKEY, (D-MA) SENATE TRANSPORTATION COMMITTEE: We know that United States has a goal of 50 percent all new electric vehicles by 2030. A move away from the all-electric vehicle future is to destroy our planet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do they say?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Obviously, there's a greater concern about the environment, and we get that. But again, I come also from an academic background, and sometimes we can talk all day about theory, but theory may not wholly align with what's practical and what's taking place on the ground.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gentlemen, we appreciate the time. This is a conversation that clearly is going to last for a long, long time as we see this battle play out. Thanks for coming on "Common Ground."\par \par \uc2 You can see all of the "Common Ground" segments on my podcast "Common Ground." Find that and the "All-Star Panel" podcast under the Bret Baier podcasts at FOXNewspodcast.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts. You can also see the whole discussion on the FOX News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page. John, I'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Bret, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden's threats to revise U.S. policy toward Israel. Later, the stage is set for this weekend's final four.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: President Biden spoke a short while ago with Prime Minister Netanyahu. He made clear that U.S. policy with respect to Gaza will be determined by our assessment of Israel's immediate action.\par \par \uc2 TAL HEINRICH, NETANYAHU SPOKESWOMAN: \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , they want international pressure to be applied on Israel instead of on them, and again, let them live another day and stay in power.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Big news coming out of that phone call between President Biden and the Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 . Let's chew on that for a while with our panel, syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2  is with us here tonight, former \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  communications director Douglas Heye, and Julia Manchester, national politics reporter for "The Hill."\par \par \uc2 Julia, start us off. This would seem to be a stunning change in direction for the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICS REPORTER, "THE HILL": You're absolutely right, John, and I think a lot of this started with that Israeli strike on the \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  workers. That's when we really saw a shift in tone in how the U.S. was really viewing this. I would say that American public support of Israel has been starting to ticker down for quite some time. But this being a well-known charity organization and being aid workers, some of those aid workers from western countries, including the United States, definitely played a role in changing this.\par \par \uc2 And I think Biden very much trying to be firm with \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , but also understanding, I think at the same time, that there is still a push for the U.S. to support Israel.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And Hugh, this comes at a time when Israel is concerned that Iran may launch some sort of retaliatory strike for the killing of that IRGC --\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: Very much so. They are running all sorts of different GPS jamming signals. They are very concerned about what's going on form the Mullahs. But I've got to say, Secretary Blinken today declaring that Israel could become indistinguishable from \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , that's obscene. It's jolting, it's jarring, and it's obscene. And he ought to retract that, because Israel is waging perhaps the most civilized war in the most difficult urban warfare environment in history, and they are losing hundreds of \uc2 IDF\uc2  casualties as they do it. It's tragic what happened to civilian aid workers, but this gang in Washington of the same people that droned 10 innocents as we were abandoning Afghanistan 30 months ago, I think maybe they should sit down.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: To your point, just for the -- I'm going to jump around a little bit here, Congressman Michael Waltz was on "America Reports" earlier today, and he addressed that point. He said the Biden administration better be careful about the outrage that it has directed toward Israel over that unfortunate tragic incident because of what you mentioned happened in Afghanistan. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ, (R-FL): Under President Biden, we had a civilian aid worker strike during the disgraceful withdrawal from Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Let's save a bit of the public righteous indignation when it comes to our ally clearly making a mistake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: In the drone strike, Doug, which the Pentagon initially said was a righteous strike, that aid worker was killed along with nine members of his family, seven of them children. So is there a lesson about glass houses and stones here?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE, FORMER REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: There's always a lesson with that. And look, what happened in Afghanistan was really the bloom coming off the rose for the Biden administration. It showed that its core promise of we've got this and we are going to be completely competent wasn't really true.\par \par \uc2 But also who and what matters in this situation. Whatever happened, and we're going to obviously investigate this, is a tragedy, but the reality is it also happened to the wrong aid workers. And what I mean by that is Jose Andres isn't just an internationally known chef. He is a commodity in Washington. He knows everybody in every newsroom. He knows a lot of members of Congress and a lot of senators, called the president himself. Not every celebrity chef gets to do that.\par \par \uc2 That's part of why this has changed that conversation. And it's why I reached out to my friend Matt Brooks today, who is the head of the Republican Jewish Coalition, to see where are you all on this? And they realize the same thing that a lot of voters do, is that time is not on Israel's side here, especially then when you consider the very real threat from Iran.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: In fact, there was an interview that the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did with Israel Hiyom in which he said pretty much the same thing. Let me play that. Oh, sorry. We don't have the video. I'll read it for you here. He said "You have to finish up your war, to finish up, you've got to get it done, and I'm sure you'll do that. And we've got to get to peace. We can't have this thing going on. And I will say Israel has to be very careful because you're losing a lot of the world. You're losing a lot of support. You have to finish up. You have to get the job done, and you have to get on to peace, get on to a normal life for Israel and for everybody else."\par \par \uc2 Julia, the former president, who says that he is probably the most stalwart president ever when it comes to Israel, is saying it's time to get this wrapped up.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Yes, Israel is not only fighting a war against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , they are fighting a public relations battle. And right now in this moment they are losing. And to Doug's point, I think it was a turning point with the Israeli strike on the world central bombing -- or \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 , yes.\par \par \uc2 But going forward for not only President Trump, but I think also \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , he is also someone who is fighting this public relations battle not only from the world but from Israelis themselves. So it's a very complicated situation.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: John, I brought up that very quote to the president, former President Trump this morning, and he wanted to clarify it and he wanted to make sure people understood. He wants Israel to achieve victory and to do it quickly, as opposed to the Biden administration which wants sort of standing in place, cease-fire, very slowly. I think the former president is lightyears ahead in his support of Israel over the current president.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, in fact, let me play an excerpt of your interview with the former president this morning. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: You are still standing 100 percent with Israel. You achieved the Abraham Accords, which was the first peace deal since Sadat.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Right.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: And are you still 100 percent with Israel? And what's your advice to Netanyahu beyond get it over with in a hurry.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's all the advice you can give. Every night they are releasing tapes of a building falling down. They should be releasing tapes like that. They're doing -- that's why they're losing the P.R. war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Is he still 100 percent behind Israel.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Yes, because he said victory, victory, victory, but do it quickly. Biden has been holding them back, and I think they ought to listen to Trump and not to Biden.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: What I thought was interesting in everything that was said today, Doug, was there was a large degree of ambiguity in terms of not what the Biden administration was looking for from Israel, but what the Biden administration would do if Israel doesn't comply.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: I think that goes directly to domestic politics here in America. As we had so many conversations of weaknesses that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has with the electorate, so does \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And we look at Michigan. We hear all this conversation about the problem that he has with what Israel is doing, supporting Israel, and how that might hurt him in Michigan with Muslim voters, especially around Dearborn, a lot of Jewish Democrats as well who are looking with a very cast eye on this. A lot of uncertainty for just where is the Biden administration today, much less last week or next week.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Right. One quick question to you, Julia. With \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on the ropes and Israel saying they have to eradicate \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , will that put pressure on them to back off?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: I don't think Israel will ever back off, but it definitely puts pressure on them to really reevaluate their strategy and maybe pay more attention to more civilian risks.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Folks, great to have you with us today. Really appreciate it. Great to see you, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The Final Four is also bringing in volunteers who will be in the airport to answer questions about all the festivities as well as provide information.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Phoenix Sky Harbor Airport has never been more ready for basketball fans. The airport expecting a massive surge of people similar to what it saw during the 2023 Super Bowl. The airport has put up, get this, a Final Four countdown clock in preparation for this weekend's college basketball national championship event. It has also, and my son would love this, redesigned recycling bins into basketball hoops and increased the number of volunteers. A lot of people will be trying to play Steph Curry with those trash bins.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, what we can learn from America's greatest presidents and what they'd say to Americans now. Thanks for watching SPECIAL REPORT. "THE INGRAHAM ANGLE" with Kid Rock is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240405ek4400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  Compares E.V. Skeptics To Landline Lovers; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Tanking In Swing State Polls Against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ; Just Say No: Oregon Recriminalizes Drugs After Policy Caused Chaos; $20 Minimum Wage Chaos! California Law Causing Layoffs, Confusion, Price Hikes; Ghosted By Gen Z: Young Workers Not Showing Up For Job Interview\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Dana Perino, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 7,942 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ANDY TILLETT, NEW YORK POST NATIONAL EDITOR: McDonald's and \uc2 Burger King\uc2  and various fast food locations.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Incredible. It was great to see you, great to chat with you, and good stuff from the New York post. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Neil will be back tomorrow. And you can catch me tomorrow alongside John Roberts 1:00 p.m. Eastern Time on "AMERICA REPORTS". For now, here is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everyone, I'm Judge Jeanine Pirro along with Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Dana Perino and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is now openly mocking Americans who aren't buying into their electric car scam. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's fantasy of replacing your gas guzzler with an E.V. is short-circuiting. Sales are nowhere near what the administration promised and most people don't have the green to go green.\par \par \uc2 But America's hard hat-wearing transportation secretary is keeping the faith. \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  is dismissing anyone not on board with the E.V. revolution as some kind of tech Neanderthal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: Let's be clear that the automotive sector is moving toward E.V.s, and we can't pretend otherwise. Sometimes when these debates happen, I feel like it's the early 2000s and I'm talking to some people who think that we can just have landline phones forever.\par \par \uc2 The reality is that the automotive sector is moving toward E.V.s and the U.S. can either fall behind China, or we can claim the lead. President Biden wants to make sure that those E.V.s are made in America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Buttigieg going on to brag about his at home charger, which is a luxury that he is afforded, but not for the rest of us. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has spent $7 billion of your tax money and has produced only seven stations in two years.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: There are more ways to move electricity than there are to move electric -- than there are to move liquid fuel. You know, that's why my house in Michigan, I control our hybrid plug in electric with electricity. Obviously, I can't do that with gas at my house.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Truckers are bashing Biden's E.V. push in their industry, saying, electric big rigs are a pipe dream. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEWIE PUGH, INDEPENDENT DRIVERS ASSOCIATION EVP: There was a company in Joliet, Illinois, that was going to put a terminal in for 30 trucks. The city said, 'you can't do it, you're going to use more electricity than the entire city at Joliet, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 ALEX "THE TRUCKING GUY", TRUCKER AND TIKTOKER: Our newer trucks will have to have a longer than usual warranty. They're going to add that in into the pricing of the trucks, which, for smaller trucking companies, they're not going to be able to afford it. And so, they're just going to have to extend the life of their old trucks and come less efficient, and it ends up creating more pollution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, Jesse, you know, it seems the administration is in a big rush for this when the infrastructure 