 was listening to the Start Here podcast. And they want to talk about the $20.00 minimum wage in California. So, they have on a guest who is an economist from your alma mater, from Berkeley, and I wanted to scream.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Because he said this doesn't affect small businesses at all. There's no problem. Everybody is fine. It's like no effect at all. They I go, why do it?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: This is -- yes, that is wrong and I'm a deeply ashamed to be an alumnus of Cal Berkeley 19 -- just 2005 I graduated.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Minimum wage jobs are not designed to make a living. You aren't supposed to live comfortably on a minimum-wage job. A minimum wage job was intended as the first rung of the employment ladder. You get started with some work experience, you get some money in your pocket to get -- put gas in the car and take Betsy Sue to the old drive-in and maybe get her some flowers and, you know, maybe have some money left over for a dime bag of candy.\par \par \uc2 But now, those are the people that are going to lose their jobs because you can't raise minimum wage without raising all the wages above it. An assistant manager who made $20.00 now is going to have to make $25.00 or $30.00 and so on. So, you either slice off the bottom of the ladder or you slice off the top of the ladder. Either way, you get a tiny little step ladder which Dana has.\par \par \uc2 So, if minimum wage was $7.00, that's $56.00 bucks a day. If it's $10.00, it's $80.00 bucks a day. For a high school kid, that's good money. For a retiree, it's good for cigars and beer. But for a dad with a family, it's absurd. So, it's not about the wage, it's the mismatching of worker to wage by so-called economists.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So-called economists indeed. Judge, what do you think of all of this?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, I think it's -- I think it's the unions that were able to get the passage of the bill. I think that