e'll just pay the workers $1,000 an hour. Well, does the bridge work? It doesn't matter. An economist says the bridge will be fine. If you just build it like this, you just slide everybody down. And at the end of the bridge, there's a bowl of soup.\par \par \uc2 Everybody says that's not going to work. And the Democrat is like, no, we're going to do it. We're going to make it work. And everyone is like, I'm -- I swear to God it's not going to work. And they start building this bridge out of spaghetti, and everybody gets tangled up. I'm sure it tastes great while you're driving over it.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And you got fat though, right?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Spaghetti makes you fat?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Carbs.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Carbs don't make you fat.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: What?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's another hoax, Jessica. The fine people, dictator on day one, and carbs.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Oh, OK.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I love this show. Coming up, the latest taking trend at your workplace.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Gen Z now treating employers as if they were on a bad date. 93 percent of the young workers admit to not showing up for an interview and 87 percent couldn't even be bothered to grace the office with their presence on day one of the job. Greg?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: These are employment heroes. Can I tell you how many people that I've hired I wish had had the guts and poor thoughts not to have shown up? It would have saved me so much time and suffering. You think it's hard to hire? It's way harder to fire. And the hardest people to fire are the incompetence because they figure out how to game the system, and they're so bad you just avoid them.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think Kilmeade is on "FOX AND FRIENDS" for 24 years?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That was a mean one. Dana, what you got?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I am wondering if Generation X really screwed up raising these kids because this is not -- there's just like -- they can't even order at restaurants. This is -- it's a -- it's a problem. I'm just -- I'm going to blame Generation X and watch my mentions go crazy.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Judge?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I don't -- I don't know whether or not I should say what I'm thinking so I'm not going to.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, yes, definitely do it though.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Do it. Do it. Do it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Hold her hands.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Are they stupid? I mean, I don't get it. They -- first of all, they can't get anything accomplished. You say to someone in the store, a young person, do you have this? Yes, it's over there. Where? I don't know. It's over there. Show me. And they're looking at their phone while they're talking to you. It's like they're doing you a favor. And then I feel like the bad guy because I want to give this place my money. I'm done with them.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Let me give you my money. I always think Jessica when you're going to an interview, they're not interviewing you, you're interviewing them. That's how I approach interviews. I'm interviewing -- is this place going to be a good place for me to work? What kind of people do they have here? What is this interviewer all about? Maybe I don't like this guy. Maybe I do. But you need that face-to-face to determine whether or not you're going to shake them down and never get fired because you're a DEI hire like Kilmeade.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's time now for "ONE MORE THING," and they say I go because I didn't go yesterday, Greg. Sometimes you have to take matters into your own hand. Check out this preschooler who was determined to make a shot. The young boy traveled up and down and up and down the court without dribbling once for over a minute straight, ignoring his teammates' please to pass the ball.\par \par \uc2 He goes back and forth. He goes we're not going to do it for the whole minute. But I love this kid. Look at this. He's doing a dance now. You know, he never gives up the ball except there. Go ahead, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's pretty funny. All right, the Fox News shop has slashed prices. Thank you, Bidenomics. We are here now, beautiful tote bag. You can take this to the beach with your family. You could take it to the airport. You could take it anywhere. You can put Dana in it. This thing is great, very fashionable. Go to shop.foxnews.com to browse.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME," the Diddy \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  connection. No relation.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Love it. Greg.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No relation.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Tonight, I have Matt Whitaker, Jamie Lissow, Kat Timpf, and Tyrus. That's tonight at 10:00 p.m. Watch it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's it?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. That's it for us, everybody. I hope you enjoyed the show. We have another six seconds. Do you want to say anything, Greg?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's it for us tonight. Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020240404ek4300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Trump Loses Bid to Delay Hush Money Trial; Total Eclipse Set to Spark Tourism Boom?; New California Minimum Wage in Effect; Severe Weather Pounds Country; Earthquake in Taiwan; U.S. Attorney General Touts Crime Prevention Efforts; Appeals Court Hears Arguments Over Texas Border Law; Gas Prices Rising\b0\par\par\uc2 Andy Tillett, Steve Bender, Rick Reichmuth, Greg Palkot, Gianno  Caldwell, Garrett Tenney, David Spunt, Peter Doocy, Jeff Flock, Sandra  Smith \par \uc2 7,993 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 FOX on top of prices still on the top of voters' minds and still tapping drivers wallets. We're on top of it all.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 And a new set of swing state polling showing inflation is still hitting home, despite signs it's coming down. We have got FOX team coverage with Peter Doocy at the White House on who's got the edge and Jeff Flock in Philadelphia, where prices at the pump are putting drivers on edge.\par \par \uc2 We begin with Peter.\par \par \uc2 Busy day there, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: It is, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden needs to hope that this upcoming election will be decided by voters worried about abortion access. He's doing great with that set. But on the issues that traditionally decide elections, he is not doing nearly as well, including handling the economy.\par \par \uc2 Trump there has a 20-point advantage over Biden in a new Wall Street Journal poll 54 to 34, and now President Biden is hoping to broaden his appeal by getting cozier with the progressive hero Bernie Sanders.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Bernie, you and I have been fighting this for 25 years. Finally, finally we beat big pharma, finally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: This Journal poll also has Trump with a 20-point advantage over Biden when it comes to handling immigration and border security, but the Biden brain trust remains confident, according to the president's top adviser.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: These polls like The Wall Street Journal one land in the White House and he's losing all the battleground states that...\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY: No, he's not losing in all the battlegrounds.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: All but one.\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN: He's coming up, and he's even or doing better. So you know what? Once people start to focus in and they see their two choices, it's obvious that Joe will win this election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: They have got real problems with key constituencies, though.\par \par \uc2 Case in point, last night, the president invited a small group of Arab, Muslim and Palestinian leaders over for a small private meeting and a doctor who was just back from treating patients in Gaza walked out on President Biden -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Quite a development.\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy live at the White House for us.\par \par \uc2 Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The price pressures extending to drivers getting hit at the pump.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Flock is at -- in Philadelphia for us. He's got more on that and a very rainy Philadelphia, I should add.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Price is at the pump getting about as unpleasant as the weather here in Philadelphia today. Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Yes, maybe you see that number over my shoulder, $5.99 for premium gas. That's pretty high, the average gallon of regular, though, nationwide $3.54. Not horrible, maybe, you say, up just a penny in the last week, but up 20 percent -- 20 cents in the last month and slated to go higher.\par \par \uc2 That, of course, is underpinned by the price of oil, and, today, the Biden administration announcing oil prices are so high they can't afford to replenish the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. That, of course, was famously run down by the Biden administration to bring down the price of gas when it got high the last time.\par \par \uc2 Now there is about 363 million barrels in the Strategic Reserve. That sounds like a lot, but there was 638 million barrels when President Biden took office. And it's not a great time to have the reserve run down. Why? The oil bulls tell us, that is to say, the traders who say that oil prices are going up, they point to \uc2 OPEC\uc2  production costs, which they affirmed today at a meeting overseas the bombing of the Iranian consulate in Syria, new sanctions on Venezuelan oil by the U.S. and Ukraine hitting Russia's oil infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 All of that driving up prices. And \uc2 Andy Lipow\uc2 , the oil analyst, telling FOX Business, here's his big concern.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW LIPOW\uc2 , PRESIDENT, \uc2 LIPOW OIL ASSOCIATES\uc2 : The greatest fear in the oil market is if the Straits of Hormuz moves should be shut, where 20 percent of the world's oil is transiting every day. Should that happen, we certainly could see oil prices spiked $30 to $40 per barrel, which is a significant 75 cent to $1 per gallon increase in gasoline prices.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: That would be a major increase, Sandra, and make things even more unpleasant than the weather is here, where I stand in Philadelphia -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And battling right through it, Jeff Flock, thank you very much for that report from Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Most other prices are cooling, however, but why aren't most folks feeling it?\par \par \uc2 To economist Steve Moore and political watcher Tiana Lowe Doescher.\par \par \uc2 Well, Steve, my guess is it's not the prices they're cooling. It's the rate of inflation. The rate those prices keep going up is getting smaller, right?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: Yes, the way I put it, Sandra, is that we just haven't slayed the inflation dragon. And all of this kind of premature euphoria that inflation was behind us, we're not seeing it.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, if your report is right, we're looking at potentially $5- a-gallon gasoline again.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Oh.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Remember, it was $2 and I think 49 cents a gallon when Trump left office.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the key indicators of where inflation is headed, if you look at the gold price, if you look at commodity prices, if you look at what's happened to things like Bitcoin, which are an alternative to the dollar, they're all shooting up in price, which means we have got a -- still got a lot of work to do.\par \par \uc2 Basically, today, the price of virtually everything you buy on average is about 20 percent more expensive. And that's why people aren't feeling the love for this economy.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, Tiana, we were just showing a moment ago on the screen polling on who voters think is best suited to handle the economy. And most voters give that to former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He gets the highest marks there. And all of a sudden, if we're in a world of, gosh, hope that's not the case, Steve, but $5 at the gallon and oil prices, to \uc2 Andy Lipow\uc2 's point, back above $100 a barrel, that changes the political landscape heading into November.\par \par \uc2 TIANA LOWE DOESCHER, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Because, unlike 2016, we now have two candidates at the top of the ballot that we have now lived under one term of Trump and one term of Biden.\par \par \uc2 And, under Trump, we had 50-year-low unemployment while inflation was still low, while interest rates were near zero, while you had some of the fastest real wage growth in about 20 years. Under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , prices have increased about 18 percent on average.\par \par \uc2 And even though Biden has claimed that inflation is slowing, look, in just the last month alone, CPI inflation, wholesale inflation, which is a leading indicator, both have increased, PCE inflation, which is what the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  cares about.\par \par \uc2 And that's why you have these bond markets collapsing, because investors understand that, as Steve said, the dragon is not tamed. And, look, Biden even has understood this. The rest of congressional Democrats, last July, they used the term Bidenomics 500 times. Now they don't use it at all, except you know who does use it in the hundreds?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Republicans.\par \par \uc2 LOWE DOESCHER: Congressional Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We think we knew the answer to that one, Tiana, but good point.\par \par \uc2 Fed Chief \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , meanwhile, is stressing the need today to see more evidence inflation is indeed cooling before cutting those rates. This affects everyone, Steve. When do you think the first rate cut may be coming, if at all, this year?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: I don't see a case for it right now, Sandra. I wish I did. Look, I want to see this economy improve.\par \par \uc2 But as I was just mentioning, every single forward-looking indicator is suggesting now inflation is getting worse, not better. Now, how do you handle that inflation? The most important thing is not what \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  does. It's getting the federal -- in my opinion, it's making major cuts in government spending and debt, and that's not going to happen any time soon in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the other big factor of the economy, Sandra, that's really affected by the inflation and the higher interest rates is the housing market. And these new reports are showing the average middle-income family today cannot afford a median family home because the mortgage payments are so much higher.\par \par \uc2 They're up 60 percent since Trump left office.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, and those house prices are still big. They're not coming down. So how can we possibly be in an environment where we can even discuss cutting rates?\par \par \uc2 So, and, by the way, the only reason people were even talking about it or the market was pricing it in is because \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  suggested it. So it'll be really interesting to see what happens here.\par \par \uc2 Thank you to both of you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Oh, by the way, one quick thing before you go, drug prices, Steve, Tiana, if I could ask you about this. Senator Bernie Sanders hosted an event today along with President Biden to tout their fight against high-priced prescription drugs and a cap on the price of asthma inhalers.\par \par \uc2 Tiana, to you on this. How will this play out on the campaign trail?\par \par \uc2 LOWE DOESCHER: So, look, something like Symbicort that's been on the market for almost 20 years, they have had time to recoup that research and development cost that they put into that drug.\par \par \uc2 But then everyone knows the next step that Bernie Sanders takes, which calls \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  greedy for charging a lot for Ozempic. But we know that these drugs take billions of years per year just to get them ready for market. So if they want to brag about a 20-year-old inhaler finally being $35, that's OK.\par \par \uc2 But if you're paying $5 to fill up your tank of gas, it doesn't offset too much.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Steve, do you have a five seconds on that?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, I don't see drug prices coming down. Do you, Sandra?\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think he's -- again, a premature kind of celebration, but drug prices have not come down.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 No, in fact, one of our FOX News reporters today was reporting from a pawn shop in Chicago where she said she saw customers coming in to offer items in -- to go pay for medicines. That is a real economic situation that is happening right now.\par \par \uc2 Tiana, Steve, thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LOWE DOESCHER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, did Texas just get some good news in its border brawl with the White House? Find out what happened in court today.\par \par \uc2 Plus, fierce storms wreaking havoc for fliers. We will have the very latest.\par \par \uc2 All that and new proof the California minimum wage is taking a bigger bite out of consumers' budgets.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That border battle taking center stage in the Big Easy today, the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals in New Orleans hearing arguments as the Biden administration fights that Texas immigration law.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' David Spunt is outside that courthouse. He's got the very latest from there for us at this hour.\par \par \uc2 Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Sandra. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 Interesting. In federal court today, there were three federal judges on this panel. Two of them seemed somewhat skeptical over the argument the United States is making to completely throw out this controversial Texas law. One of those judges didn't even speak at all.\par \par \uc2 The reason I'm in New Orleans is because the Fifth Circuit, where the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals is, covers the Lone Star State and the state of Texas. Outside of the building earlier today, there was a protest by a pro- immigration group that was actually anti-Senate Bill 4.\par \par \uc2 Senate Bill 4 is a Texas law signed by Governor Greg Abbott that would allow any Texas law enforcement officer to arrest people for illegally crossing the border. The \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  argued the border is federal territory, and border agents should therefore be doing the arresting.\par \par \uc2 Governor Abbott argues the Border Patrol is overwhelmed and understaffed and is not doing enough. Therefore, Texas officials should be able to do so. Here is the Texas solicitor general. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE PRISCILLA RICHMAN, U.S. FIFTH CIRCUIT COURT OF APPEALS: If the answer is just we don't have the resources, why hasn't the federal government taken Texas up on its generous offer to say, hey, here are resources, we will do it for you? What have they -- they're not saying yes. They're saying no.\par \par \uc2 AARON NIELSON, TEXAS SOLICITOR GENERAL: They're not just saying no. Respectfully, Your Honor, they're destroying our Texas property. They're saying -- suggesting awful things about Texas at the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , which are demonstrably untrue. And they're suing us.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Sandra, today, lawyers for the Justice Department argued that Texas has no right to patrol the border.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANIEL TENNY, DEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE ATTORNEY: Opposing counsel was talking about how extraordinary our position would be, but it's really their position that would be extraordinary and unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 They have not cited a single case in which the government has endeavored to bring an action of this kind, and the court has refused to allow it, not one from any court.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Now we await the ruling, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Could take a couple of weeks, but the bottom line, whoever loses here will go directly back to Washington, D.C., to the steps of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . And those nine justices will have to settle this, not to mention in the middle of an election year --