 New York Post national editor, Andy Tillett.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, Andy.\par \par \uc2 This is incredible. Like, does anybody look back at history and go, this doesn't work? I mean, every business owner said, if you do this to us, we will probably have to lay off workers, right? And we will probably have to raise prices to the consumer.\par \par \uc2 ANDY TILLETT, NATIONAL EDITOR, THE NEW YORK POST: Yes, indeed, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Well, we found out on the very first day of this $20 minimum wage going into effect prices rose. So, at \uc2 Burger King\uc2 , one burger was $1.80 more than it had been two days previously. And a fish sandwich had gone up by $4 already. So, that's on the first day of this wage coming to effect.\par \par \uc2 If you want to think about how, in four weeks, six weeks, two months, as the cost of having more workers -- having more wages for workers really starts to kick in, those prices could go higher. And I don't think ordinary Americans want to be paying $12, $13, $15 just for a burger and fries.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: These are substantial prices increases. And then what happens is, this happens and then you have the administration, the president himself will step out and say that this is price gouging.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what did they expect would happen? I mean, don't you look back and say, OK, the market's always going to determine? I interviewed someone this week out in California. First of all, she said she spent months figuring out if this law applies to her, right? That's a lot of time, a lot of money away from improving your business and managing your employees, who basically said that her employees were able to basically make this anyway in tips and otherwise.\par \par \uc2 But now this mandate changes the game. She won't be able to hire as many as those, like, college-age, younger workers. She's going to have to slim down her work force. She's already raising prices. I mean, time and time again, this is what we see.\par \par \uc2 TILLETT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 The most obvious effect, to me, seems to be that, with $20 minimum wage, there's going to be, the fast-food companies, which spend multimillions of dollars on research and development, are going to pivot towards A.I. The first fully automated \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's in the country was opened in Fort Worth in December 2022.\par \par \uc2 I'm pretty sure that the next place they will be looking to roll that out will be California, based on this particular thing. And the people that this affects are the normal working people, the hardworking people who go to jobs and then want to get a burger after work.\par \par \uc2 It's not the kale-smoothie-drinking Hollywood elite who are going to be affected by this.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TILLETT: Because the first knock-on effect, as studies or research showed, was that the prices go up and it's passed straight onto the consumer.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That franchise owner that we just played the sound of there a minute ago, \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's franchise, such a good point that he said, I can't waste or spend my time lamenting bad policies. I have to look forward.\par \par \uc2 And, boy, do so many business owners understand that.\par \par \uc2 TILLETT: Sure. I'm sure.\par \par \uc2 And it's the first time that California has singled out one particular industry, the fast-food industry, and said this wage will apply to workers in that area and not to everyone else. It doesn't seem to make sense that they would want to single things out in that way on one particular -- one particular, you know...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 You're starting to wonder, when you walk into a fast-food restaurant, if 20 bucks is going to cover your meal.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TILLETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I mean, that's happening.\par \par \uc2 TILLETT: Yes, and I'm sure most people don't want that.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes. I mean, it is unbelievable. It's very common now on social media to see people taking pictures of their fast-food receipts and posting them, because they're in sticker shock. So...\par \par \uc2 TILLETT: We have done numerous stories about how the prices at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's, of \uc2 Burger King\uc2 , of various fast-food locations...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It's incredible.\par \par \uc2 TILLETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It was great to see you. Great to chat with you.\par \par \uc2 TILLETT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And good stuff in The New York Post. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Neil will be back tomorrow. And you can catch me tomorrow alongside \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  1:00 p.m. Eastern time on "America Reports."\par \par \uc2 For now, here is "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240403ek4300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Hits Biden Border Failures In Michigan, Wisconsin; Trump Back On The Campaign Trail; JK Rowling And Trump Are Threats To The Left; Tonight, We're Learning More About The Beating Death Of 16-Year-Old Preston Lord, And The Group Of Mostly High School Thugs That Were Charged In His Murder; California's War Against Restaurants Continued Yesterday When Newsom's $20 An Hour Minimum Wage Went To Effect For Fast-Food Chains; Shack Attack, Alleged Squatters Use \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2  Receipt As Evidence\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Trace Gallagher, Jimmy Failla \par \uc2 7,969 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 