ds of thousands of dollars to actually get these people out. And it takes sometimes up to a year.\par \par \uc2 Now, Jimmy, we just talked about restaurant costs with a restaurant owner because of the $20 minimum wage here in California. But it looks like there are other fees still showing up on receipts. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Went to a restaurant last night and I got the bill and they charged me a COVID fee. And I asked the guy, what's the COVID fee? He goes, well, we got to wipe down the menus. Wipe down the menus. You're charging $3 to wipe down your own menus?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's one of your buddies. I know he's funny.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Yeah, I've worked with Sebastian a lot at Gotham Comedy Club down the block. Great dude. So, yes, first of all, it's amazing that the restaurants are basically telling you they weren't wiping down the menus before, OK. Number one.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Gross.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: But number two, he also alluded to this being a coffee house during the video. Now, that would make sense because most of the men who hang out at coffee houses drive there with a mask on in the car, OK. Most of the women who hang out at coffee houses drive there with a mask on their cat.\par \par \uc2 So, if it was a coffee house, I'm not shocked. But Sebastian's right to say it's a scam.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, we flew here today and people had their children still in masks, Jimmy, on a five-hour flight. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Child abuse.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. Really quickly. Everyone's heard about Shakira said, you know, the Barbie movie. She took her sons and, you know, really kind of trashed the movie. They felt that it was emasculating and, you know, et cetera, et cetera, because she's raising two boys.\par \par \uc2 Thank God someone's actually talking about this. Thank goodness we have celebrities who actually have some common sense. And I didn't see the movie. I'm not going to see it. But, yeah.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Congratulate -- congratulations to Shakira on never being booked in Hollywood again. But she's missing the whole point of Barbie, which is to find out if your son is straight, OK. That's why you take them. If he likes it, he's not. If he doesn't --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We're getting in trouble.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240403ek4200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 California Bill Would Ban After-Hours Contact With Employees; Severe Weather Threat; U.S. Military Racing to Remove Francis Scott Key Bridge Debris; Gas Prices Rising as Mideast Tensions Grow; Iran Vows Response After Alleged Israeli Strike; Judge Rejects Bid to Dismiss Hunter Biden Tax Charges; Trump Targets Biden Over Immigration\b0\par\par\uc2 Rikki Schlott, Mark Tepper, Adam Klotz, Jennifer Griffin, Griff  Jenkins, Phil Flynn, Jack Keane, Trey Yingst, Eric Shawn, Jonathan Hunt,  Mark Meredith, Sandra Smith \par \uc2 7,904 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  putting the border front and center today in the race for the White House, Trump just wrapping those remarks in Michigan before heading to Wisconsin, two states that he won in 2016, but then lost in 2020.\par \par \uc2 So how is this issue resonating in these key battleground states this time around?\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 We have got FOX team coverage kicking off a new hour for you with Mark Meredith on how this border battle is playing out and Griff Jenkins on how the White House is digging in.\par \par \uc2 We begin with Mark -- to you first, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Within the last hour, former President Trump surrounded himself with law enforcement officials in Michigan. He vowed, if reelected, to tackle two big issues, crime and illegal immigration. Those two issues could be a game changer in Michigan and Wisconsin, two states Democrats consider to be their blue wall.\par \par \uc2 And Trump's visit today to Grand Rapids comes amid a story which is getting a lot of attention, police recently finding the body of 25-year-old Ruby Garcia. She was found on the side of a highway and the man who she was romantically linked to has been charged with her murder. The suspect is here in the U.S. illegally from Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Trump and the RNC today launched a new Web site to highlight similar cases nationwide, which Trump calls a national security threat.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We have a new form of crime. It's called migrant crime. They're having fist fights with our police officers right in the middle of streets. They're sending prisoners, murderers, drug dealers, mental patients and terrorists, the worst they have in every country, all over the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Polling shows Americans are increasingly concerned about border security, but Democrats insist Trump has no real solutions to these real- world problems.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL TYLER, BIDEN CAMPAIGN COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: This man is not a person who actually cares about crime, who actually cares about solving issues. He's only interested in absolving himself.\par \par \uc2 So, this campaign is going to make sure the American people understand that every single day between now and November.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Now, Trump is going to be rallying supporters tonight in Green Bay, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 And we learned today that President Biden, he's planning to head to Wisconsin next week as well, an indication, Sandra, how much both men are going to be chasing each other in these swing states.\par \par \uc2 Also want to mention that Trump made some news at the end of his remarks, saying he's going to talk about abortion next week, given some of the developments we have seen in Florida -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We will be watching all of it, Mark Meredith in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Mark, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So what is the White House saying about this?\par \par \uc2 For that, we turn to Griff Jenkins. He's live at the White House. He has the latest for us.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good afternoon, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 And the White House appears to be settling into a standard pivot on border questions, simply blaming congressional Republicans for failing to take action. A little earlier this afternoon, things got pretty heated between John Kirby and our own Peter Doocy. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: To the folks that are concerned about border security, the president will be the first one to stand up here and say he agrees that the border does need some security capabilities, that we do need more Border Patrol agents.\par \par \uc2 And all that has to happen is for the speaker to do his job, put that supplemental on the floor. Let's get a vote. Let's get those 1,300 additional Border Patrol agents down there to do their jobs.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: But everybody in this room knows that the bill that you guys keep talking about as a solution is dead at the moment and...\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Says you. It doesn't need to be dead, does it?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The bill's dead.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Says you.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: When's the vote?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: You -- ask Speaker Johnson that. It doesn't need to be dead, Peter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: This comes as former President Trump is blasting Biden over the border in Grand Rapids, as Mark just laid out.\par \par \uc2 Now, Trump is striking, Sandra, at the issue that voters cite most often as Biden's biggest failure, according to FOX's latest poll. You see here 67 percent disapproving of his handling on immigration, worse than on the economy or inflation.\par \par \uc2 Now, part of the sentiment may be stemming from the flow of fentanyl across our southern border, which was part of the call today between President Biden and China's President Xi, which the White House called a candidate and constructive call. They also talked about renewed ties between the two militaries, the need to address A.I. risk and maintaining peace in the Taiwan Strait.\par \par \uc2 There was also a pledge made between the two presidents to talk as regularly as needed. And we learned finally, Sandra, that Secretaries Yellen and Blinken will make trips to China in the coming days and weeks -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Griff Jenkins live at the White House on that for us.\par \par \uc2 Griff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So is this border issue resonating in key battleground states?\par \par \uc2 With us now, GOP pollster \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2  and Democratic pollster Carly Cooperman.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to both of you, ladies, for joining us.\par \par \uc2 So, Lee, tell us what you're seeing on that front.\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN POLLSTER: Look, I think it matters a lot.\par \par \uc2 We're seeing a huge shift in polls. Immigration has become the number one issue to Americans over the economy for the first time over the last several weeks. And we're seeing the severity of people's concern about immigration has really shifted; 32 percent of Americans say that immigration poses a major risk to them personally.\par \par \uc2 And that's an increase from when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president. Only 17 percent said the same thing. So we're seeing a real shift in how people are viewing this. Now, the question is, is Trump going to be able to capitalize? Obviously, Biden is trying out there and the Democrats are trying to say that he doesn't have real solutions, although 55 percent of Americans felt safer -- or feel safer with Trump's policies over 33 percent to Biden.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You know, Carly, if we put up on the screen those presidential results going back to 2016, as John would say, the way back machine, you look at the 47.5 to 40 -- the 0.2 margin of victory that Trump pulled off in that state, in Wisconsin as well.\par \par \uc2 And you see him focusing on this issue of immigration in these key battleground states. And you wonder if this is resonating with voters, Carly.\par \par \uc2 CARLY COOPERMAN, DEMOCRATIC POLLSTER: I do think it's resonating voters. The polling has consistently showed that immigration has come to the top in terms of what's most important to voters, and it consistently shows that they disapprove of how President Biden has handled the border issue thus far.\par \par \uc2 And that is why we are hearing time and time again now the Biden administration is talking about wanting to take on this issue. They're advocating to get this border bill passed that was a bipartisan effort put forward by the Senate, which congressional Republicans killed once Trump said he didn't approve of it.\par \par \uc2 And that's despite the fact that 59 percent of voters approved of that bipartisan bill. And they're also talking about considering things like executive actions. The Biden administration knows that they have to address this issue if they want to be successful in these states.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And you look at the 2020 results and how Biden turned things around and won by a small margin of victory there in Michigan and Wisconsin as well. And you wonder what happens this time around in the battle for Michigan right now.\par \par \uc2 RCP polling for the 2024 election has Trump at 47.4 percent to Biden's 43.9 percent. In Wisconsin, that margin is smaller, 48.3 percent to 47.5 percent for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . But if, Lee, you look at the performance that voters are giving \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on immigration, it is worse than inflation. It is worse than the economy.\par \par \uc2 Griff just showed these numbers from the White House. And as we saw in the primaries, this is where the focus was at for voters. They're worried about what they're seeing at the southern border and they are experiencing more and more every day what that crisis at the southern border means for them all over this country, Lee.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Yes, I think it's really going to be telling.\par \par \uc2 And this is an issue that a lot of Democrats have tried to push and say this is now the Republicans' fault because they are not making a deal. This is on Speaker Johnson. That's not the way the American people view it right now. The American people right now are very much saying they're blaming Democrats.\par \par \uc2 They have had power for the last 3.5 years and they haven't done anything to make things better. In fact, they feel much worse. And so I think that blame-shifting really does hurt the Democrats and only helps the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 I think the issue that's going to be hardest for Republicans to navigate right now, because everything else is in their favor, the biggest issue -- and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said that he's going to address it next week -- is, where are we going to land on abortion?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Gotcha. So we will see what more we hear about that. And we are hearing that former President Trump is going to be making remarks soon that front.\par \par \uc2 Carly, I want to end with this on a separate topic. \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 's campaign is saying that it has enough votes to get on the ballot in another key battleground state, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 So, Carly, how could that impact this race?\par \par \uc2 COOPERMAN: Yes, look, R.F. Kennedy's ability to be impactful in this election 100 percent corresponds to how many ballots he can get on across the country.\par \par \uc2 North Carolina, in particular, is a state that the Biden administration has been targeting as a pickup state. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  lost by fewer than two points in that state in 2020. And if RFK ultimately is successful in getting on that state, it is something that is going to be -- it will be difficult for the Biden campaign, because RFK is pulling from voters for both sides.\par \par \uc2 But the Biden administration is really concerned about this. And so there is an effort to counter this narrative, but it certainly will make things troublesome for Democrats in North Carolina if he's successful.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Lee, how about you? How do you see this? How does this impact Trump and Biden, RFK getting on another ballot?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: I think it actually impacts in meaningful ways. I think one of the big things that I have been looking at is, yes, RFK Jr. is pulling from both sides, but, in particular, he's pulling from the left.\par \par \uc2 And now he's got a V.P. pick who is very anti-war. I think he's going to pick up a lot of those pro-Palestinian, free Palestinian Democrats who are out there protesting President Biden. I think it's going to be a big issue for him.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Very interesting.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to both of you, Carly, Lee. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COOPERMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, did you see the markets today? Stocks falling on new worries about higher energy prices. That could send inflation even higher. You have got oil and gasoline prices up. And there's a big question whether that could delay the Fed's plan for an interest rate cut this year.\par \par \uc2 Remember, they were suggesting there could be even three? Well, now the markets are questioning that. All three markets fell into the red, the Dow down almost a full 400 points, 1 percent drop there.\par \par \uc2 All right, a judge smacking down \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's request to dismiss those tax charges.\par \par \uc2 And what happens now that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has posted that $175 million bond? The surprising new twist that could come from both developments just ahead.\par \par \uc2 Then, how that suspected Israeli strike on an Iranian target over there could soon be hitting drivers already paying higher prices here, as Iran vows revenge. We will have the latest.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right FOX News Alert.\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's legal team is responding now after a federal judge says no way to dismissing those tax charges.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Hunt has the details from Los Angeles for us -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 And as a result of that decision, we look to be back on track for a trial of Hunter Biden beginning June 20 after Judge Mark Scarsi, having said he'd need a couple of weeks, instead ruled against every one of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's eight motions to dismiss the charges within just a few days of hearing them.\par \par \uc2 Biden's lead attorney, Abbe Lowell, argued in court here in L.A. last Wednesday that the tax charges should never have been brought. Chief among his arguments was that the case amounts to political persecution of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and is motivated more by his client's last name than by actual wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 Judge Scarsi gave short shrift to that argument, saying in his ruling -- quote -- "The motion is remarkable in that it fails to include a single declaration, exhibit, or request for judicial notice. Instead, defendant cites portions of various Internet news sources, social media posts, and legal blogs. These citations, however, are not evidence."\par \par \uc2 In essence, the judge ruled that there is indeed a case to answer over allegations that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  evaded more than $1.5 million in taxes, spending his money instead, according to prosecutors on drugs, escorts, and girlfriends, exotic cars, and clothing.\par \par \uc2 Biden's attorney said Judge Scarsi's decision not to dismiss the charges was flat wrong, saying in his statement -- quote -- "We strongly disagree with the court's decision and will continue to vigorously pursue Mr. Biden's challenges to the abnormal way the special counsel handled this investigation and charged this case."\par \par \uc2 It's worth noting the language there, Sandra, vigorously pursuing challenges. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's legal team seems intent on appealing this decision and doing everything in their power to push that June 20 trial date back, with a clear hope of ultimately ensuring a trial never happens - - Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, Jonathan Hunt live on that out of Los Angeles for us.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 To former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's courtroom battles now. What's next after he just posted up $175 million bond in the New York civil fraud case?\par \par \uc2 Eric Shawn has those details for us -- Eric.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Well, he's got the money. New York Attorney General Letitia James now has to keep hands off of former President Trump's properties, at least for now. Trump has secured that $175 million bond that stemmed from his real estate fraud trial. He turned to an insurance company headed by California billionaire and Trump supporter Don Hankey.\par \par \uc2 Hankey and his family owned the large company Knight Insurance. Hankey told FOX Business that politics had nothing to do with his backing Trump's bond.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON HANKEY, CHAIRMAN, KNIGHT INSURANCE GROUP: It's a big bond for us to underwrite, but Trump -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came up with all cash. So at the end, it was an easy decision and with almost no risk.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Meanwhile, back in New York, Trump's lawyers are also apparently trying to get Judge Juan Merchan, who's overseeing that Stormy Daniels cover-up trial, well, thrown off the case.\par \par \uc2 In court papers, his lawyers say Trump is -- quote -- "submitting a pre- motion letter seeking leave to file a recusal motion based on changed circumstances and newly discovered evidence."\par \par \uc2 His lawyers for now are not yet publicly saying exactly what that newly discovered evidence is, but it does turn out that Merchan's daughter is president of a large progressive political consulting firm that works for Democrats, among them, President Biden and his campaign, Vice President Harris, the Democratic House minority leader, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , among many other prominent Democrats.\par \par \uc2 But the New York State Advisory Committee on Judicial Ethics has already declared there is no conflict for the judge. It issued a ruling saying -- quote -- "A relative's independent political activities do not provide a reasonable basis to question a judge's impartiality."\par \par \uc2 Well, Trump is also now waiting to see if New York state's appellate court will completely overturn the full amount of that judgment against him -- it was $454 million, before the appellate court cut that by less than half -- or, if in the end, the former president will finally be on the hook for that full amount -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Got it, Eric Shawn, live out of our New York City newsroom for us.\par \par \uc2 Eric, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So what is next for the former President Trump after posting this bond in the civil fraud case?\par \par \uc2 Let's ask former Deputy Assistant Attorney General Tom Dupree. He's here now on that for us.\par \par \uc2 So, what is next, Tom? Hello.\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE, FORMER JUSTICE DEPARTMENT OFFICIAL: Sandra, the next step is the Appellate Division, which is the appeals court that supervises the trial court in New York. And that's where former President Trump will go to fight the battle as he tries to get this judgment overturned.\par \par \uc2 The appellate court will consist of three brand-new judges to the case who look at what happened in the trial court. They will look at the penalties that the trial judge imposed. They will look at the nature of the sanction, the amount of the punishment, and then they will decide whether or not it was lawfully imposed or whether it needs to be set aside.\par \par \uc2 So the battle will continue, but now it shifts to the Court of Appeals.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What's the timeline look for all of this? Obviously, we are in an election year.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Yes, it's probably going to move fairly slowly.\par \par \uc2 Typically, unless there's some reason to decide things quickly -- and I'm not sure I see a reason here -- it can take months. It can sometimes take up to a year for an appellate court to reach a decision. You're going to have to have briefs filed. You're going to have oral arguments. So there will be a hearing.\par \par \uc2 And then the courts will often take months to decide the issue. They will have to write an opinion. So it's a fairly long process. But the significance of Trump posting a bond means that the attorney general can't come and grab Trump Tower or his other properties while the former president's appeal moves ahead.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Because we have been highlighting that portfolio that would have been up for grabs, Trump Tower, the Trump Park Avenue, 40 Wall Street. We remember covering all this leading up to this moment.\par \par \uc2 So, considering that, Tom, New York Attorney General Letitia James must wait for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's appeal to play out before she can take any further action now. And George Washington Law Professor Jonathan Turley says that appeal could go all the way to the Supreme Court. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: There were no victims here. There wasn't a single penny that was lost by anyone. The banks involved testified that they wanted more business from Trump.\par \par \uc2 So to come up with this astronomical figure really shocks the conscience. Now, on appeal, he's going to have trouble with this New York law. It is unique. It doesn't require victims. It doesn't require losses, but they still have to justify the amount. And that could go all the way to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thoughts on that, Tom?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Yes, I agree with that.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is a complicated case. I think, as Professor Turley correctly points out, there are questions about how this law is applied. And there are also questions about, even if this law was fairly applied to former President Trump, what about the amount of the penalty? I mean, it really is a shocking amount of a penalty.\par \par \uc2 And I think the appellate judges are going to very, very carefully scrutinize the record. They will take a fresh look at all of the evidence, at all of the legal arguments and they will come to their own conclusion about whether any portion of this penalty can stand.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Very interesting.\par \par \uc2 And, Tom, switching gears for a moment here to the Hunter Biden case, a federal judge has just rejected his team's request to have those tax charges thrown out. Now a trial is expected to start in June. So was that the right call?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: I think it absolutely is.\par \par \uc2 Look, Sandra, I am a big fan of creative legal arguments, don't get me wrong, but \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's arguments were terrible. And you saw the judge actually called out his lawyers for, well, basically bad lawyering. He said he found their arguments very unpersuasive. He didn't see any evidence that would cause him to throw out these charges.\par \par \uc2 And he's telling \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , look, it's time to face the music. You are going to be standing trial in my courtroom in a few months. I don't think there's any way for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  to postpone this day of reckoning.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Tom Dupree on the news for us.\par \par \uc2 Tom, thanks so much for joining us. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, severe weather we are watching, it is threatening states from the Gulf Coast to the Great Lakes. Right now, more than 75 million Americans are at risk. We're going to have the latest for you.\par \par \uc2 Plus, some signs of progress in Baltimore, as the nation's economy is seeing impacts from that closure of the vital waterway. How much longer until shipping is free-flowing again? We're on it.\par \par \uc2 But, first, Iran vowing revenge today after that suspected Israeli strike on its embassy in Syria. What is Israel now saying? And what could it mean for our forces in the region, as protesters in Tehran lash out?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Tensions escalating in the Middle East, as protesters in Iran burn Israeli and American flags. General Jack Keane on where this is heading.\par \par \uc2 Plus, oil surging on that news. The impact for you here at home -- next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Iranian protesters burning American and Israeli flags in response to the killing of a top military commander in a suspected Israeli strike, Iran vowing retaliation for that strike.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Trey Yingst is in Tel Aviv, Israel, for -- with the very latest from there for us.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sandra. Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 I do want to start with some breaking news out of Jerusalem. We understand tonight that police are clashing with protesters outside of Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 's private residence. I spoke with one person in the city who indicated police are on horseback and they are using water cannons to push away these anti-government demonstrators.\par \par \uc2 All of this comes as we are learning more about that strike yesterday in Damascus, Syria. Reports indicate seven members of Iran's IRGC were killed, including two top Iranian generals. Protesters took to the streets overnight of Tehran, burning both American and Israeli flags. They chanted "Death to America" and called on their own government to respond.\par \par \uc2 Iranian officials echoed these calls today, with the country's supreme leader posting to X, saying -- quote -- "We will make the Zionists regret this crime of attacking the Iranian Consulate in Syria and other crimes like this by God's power and strength."\par \par \uc2 Iranian President \uc2 Ebrahim Raisi\uc2  also said the attack would not go unanswered. In the past, Iran has threatened harsh responses and then done nothing, but given the current state of affairs, Israeli forces are on high alert.\par \par \uc2 We're also following one other developing story today out of the Middle East. Overnight, a series of Israeli strikes killed seven members of the \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 . Among the dead are a number of foreign citizens, including one American. Graphic videos circulated showing the bodies of these aid workers, some wearing protective gear.\par \par \uc2 They were inside of an armored car when the explosions occurred. The Israelis are taking credit for what they call a tragic and unintentional incident. Israel says they will conduct an independent and thorough investigation and then release the findings -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Trey Yingst from Tel Aviv for us at this hour.\par \par \uc2 Trey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So what does the U.S. need to do to be prepared in the wake of this suspected Israeli strike?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is FOX News strategic analyst and retired Four-Star General Jack Keane.\par \par \uc2 General, great to see you tonight. Thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 So what do we need to do to be prepared for this?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Well, certainly, this -- if there's going to be retaliation -- and we're not certain of that yet -- it's largely going to be focused on Israel, for all the obvious reasons.\par \par \uc2 For our audience to understand, for the last few years, 3.5 about, the Israelis have been conducting close to 300 airstrikes in Syria against Iranian proxies and IRGC targets. And why is that? Because they're preventing them from establishing missile bases and the transit of missiles into Lebanon through Syria.\par \par \uc2 Some of these targets, they have hit right at the Damascus International Airport as they begin to load missiles into warehouses. So this is part of a campaign that they have been conducting for some time. Obviously, there's war in Gaza that's accelerated all of the concern about this.\par \par \uc2 And the target they selected is someone that they have been trying to get for some time. And they happen to be able to have the right intelligence and they acted on it very decisively. Iran will likely focus on them. They could attack using their own resources from Iran to do that, much like they did when we took down the \uc2 Qasem Soleimani\uc2  in January 2020.\par \par \uc2 Or they could use their proxies in the reason, the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  or the Houthis, to do it, who have the capability certainly to do that. In terms of us, I think likely our forces have been told to protect themselves even more so, given the potential of an attack against our bases in Iraq and Syria, would be the most likely if they were going to do something.\par \par \uc2 And they would more likely choose the proxies that operate and been delivering those attacks for so long. Now, our audience needs to know that there have not been attacks on our bases in Iraq and Syria for weeks after we killed the leader of the militia group operating in Iraq that Iran was backing.\par \par \uc2 And Iran ordered the militias not to attack our bases as a result of that. So we will see what happens here. The likely focus will be on Israel. And, listen, a lot of talk and wringing of hands that this could escalate into World War III. That is not going to happen. Iran does not want a direct confrontation with the United States and go to war with the United States.\par \par \uc2 All of their objectives in the Middle East that they're trying to achieve, domination and control, are gone because they lose their regime. They know that. And they understand that. And their actions have been along those lines.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: When we take action against them, they take measured action against us. And I think that's what we will see here.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I have only -- General, thank you for your thoughts on that. I have only got about 30 seconds left or so, but I want to get this in here, because President Biden has just put out a tweet on the official White House account about his call he had today with China's Xi Jinping.\par \par \uc2 He says: "Today, I had a candid conversation with President Xi of the People's Republic of China on a range of issues, including areas of cooperation and areas of difference. I emphasized the importance of maintaining peace and stability across the Taiwan Strait and in the South China Sea and raised concerns over the PRC's support for Russia's defense industrial base, their unfair trade practices, and economic and technological practices that harm American workers and families. I look forward to responsibly managing our relationships in the weeks and months ahead."\par \par \uc2 I wanted to get your quick response to that, General.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, well, certainly talking -- these two leaders having a dialogue makes sense. And we have always done that in the past with other adversaries.\par \par \uc2 But, also, you have got to deal with a grain of salt here. I mean, President Xi is an aggressive adversary. He's increased the repression at home, and he's increased the aggression abroad. Our sources are telling us with some credibility that he doesn't want any kind of increase in aggression that would lead to some kind of confrontation in the next couple of years, which is very different from what he said in the past, because of the economic challenges he has at home.\par \par \uc2 And -- but he has made an exception to that, and that's what's going on with the Philippines.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: He is completely frustrated by the Philippines giving us four military bases to use. And he's going after Marcos, because Marcos is completely different than his predecessor.\par \par \uc2 Marcos has aligned wholly with the United States. And that is one of the issues that the president was talking to him about in his aggression towards the Philippines.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: General, always appreciate a discussion with you. Appreciate you joining us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, great talking to you, Sandra. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 New signs a growing conflict between Israel and Iran over there could be hitting drivers here.\par \par \uc2 Plus, 75 million Americans bracing for severe weather across a wide swathe of the nation this hour. We have got the latest on its track.\par \par \uc2 And, later, the same state now mandating a $20 fast-food minimum wage could soon be making it illegal for most bosses to contact their workers after hours. Is it needed or is it a step too far?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Stocks rocked, as oil rises on new worries Israel and Iran could enter into a direct conflict, and it's coming as drivers are already seeing higher prices as we enter the busy driving season, all this reigniting inflation fears.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is oil analyst and FOX News contributor Phil Flynn.\par \par \uc2 Phil, I don't watch the oil markets as closely as you do, but all of a sudden the other day I looked, and I said, whoa, oil's back at $85 a barrel? Do you see this going even higher? And does that mean we're going to see prices go up at the pump?\par \par \uc2 PHIL FLYNN, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: You know, I'm afraid so, Sandra. You're absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we saw oil hit $85 a barrel, and it's one of the concerns that I have had. Not only do you have this rising geopolitical risk around the world. You really have a situation where global demand is exceeding supply.\par \par \uc2 And all this talk that electric vehicles and the change in our way of life would reduce oil demand isn't happening. In fact, global oil demand is going to hit a record high, and we have been selling America a bill of goods, and we're not prepared for it. And I'm afraid we're going to pay for it at the pump.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Really interesting.\par \par \uc2 Just had a discussion with the transportation secretary, \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 . It happened earlier today on "America Reports." And I asked him, Phil, if the market is ready for this White House push for electric vehicles. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: They're extremely expensive. I mean, the electricity obviously uses fossil fuels anyway to power these vehicles. So, I mean, is this just premature, this push right now?\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, U.S. TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: Yes, these are all really interesting and important questions.\par \par \uc2 But I would say, we know it's not premature because more Americans every single year choose electric than the year before. And if you look at the surveys, it is extremely rare for anybody who has gone electric to want to go back to the old gas car technology.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, to be clear, it's not for everybody and it's not happening overnight. Our goal is, by the end of this decade, we're about half and half with new car sales. But the reason we can't just sit back and let this happen on its own pace is that there is a race going on.\par \par \uc2 Either China or the United States will dominate the E.V. future.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But, hello, the difference is that we have got the resources, right? We have got the fossil fuel-rich country.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But it's a question we keep asking, Phil, and one that we talked to Bjorn Lomborg a lot about, he writes in The Wall Street Journal, is, if they're so popular and people love these vehicles so much, why would we have to mandate them?\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: That's absolutely correct.\par \par \uc2 And he makes this false assumption that we're in a race somehow with China to control the electric market. And, Sandra, if that's the case, we're losing that race only because China has an electric Trojan horse, because they control the rare earth minerals that you're going to need to build these electric cars.\par \par \uc2 So you're absolutely right. And the thing is, is that there would be no electric -- we wouldn't even be talking about electric car if it wasn't the private sector, guys like \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  that they used to laugh at 20 years ago and now they're looking at electric cars as a savior.\par \par \uc2 And it's the private sector that drove this. Make no mistake about it. China is laughing at the way the United States is rushing into this, because it's making our economy less efficient. It's making us less stable. And they would love -- because they look at the big picture. They'd love for their economy to overtake us.\par \par \uc2 And our push towards electric vehicles is going to be a major mistake that I think is going to set our economy back and give them an edge.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes. And I just saw a statistic the other day. About 75 percent of these charging stations are actually efficient and up and running and working properly. I mean, you get to some of these charging areas and they're just not ready to go. And that's a problem for these drivers.\par \par \uc2 Phil, it was great to see you.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And, also, we want to thank the secretary for joining us. We will continue the discussion in this country, for sure.\par \par \uc2 Phil, thanks. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 To Baltimore now, where the U.S. military is racing to clear bridge debris and fully reopen that waterway. Why officials say the process is more complicated than expected.\par \par \uc2 Plus, severe weather storming through the heartland and set to disrupt travel. What you need to know. We will have the latest.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The first vessel to leave the Port of Baltimore since the Key Bridge collapse last week took off Monday using a temporary channel to bypass the wreckage.\par \par \uc2 Now the U.S. military is working as fast as possible to remove the massive debris and reopen the waterway.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Jennifer Griffin has the details. She's live from the Pentagon for us.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHIEF NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 The Coast Guard has joined the U.S. military and they are working as fast as possible to reopen the Port of Baltimore. A tugboat, as you mentioned, pushed a fuel barge through a newly opened alternate channel late Monday. And, today, some more good news from Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. WES MOORE (D-MD): We have opened two temporary channels that can support commercial traffic. We are still a long way from being able to get the size and the cadence of the commercial traffic back to where it was before the collapse.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. SHANNON GILREATH, \uc2 U.S. COAST GUARD\uc2 : We have reopened that northern channel that we spoke about yesterday with our first two barges to traffic through there. And we just reopened that southern channel today at a draft of 14 feet.\par \par \uc2 We're going to continue working and planning for a third alternate channel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  has provided 1,100 soldiers who specialize in construction to assist in the clearance effort. The Navy has provided 12 crane and support vessels, tugs, survey and dive crew boats, four massive barges, including one with a 400-ton lift capacity, another capable of lifting 1,000 tons.\par \par \uc2 During its first crane operation, military crews removed 200 tons of steel from the now-ruined Key Bridge, a piece of that steel almost the size of the Statue of Liberty. It took 10 hours to remove the first 200 tons. Up to 4,000 tons of steel sit on top of the Dali cargo ship that took down the bridge.\par \par \uc2 President Biden plans to visit the site of the wreckage on Friday, Sandra. Adding to the horror, the 21 crew members of the MV Dali remain on board the freighter that crashed into the Key Bridge exactly a week ago. That freighter remains trapped by a tangle of steel wreckage -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Jennifer Griffin live on that from the Pentagon for us.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And to the severe weather threat across the heartland and into the Northeast tomorrow. It's expected to wreak havoc on travel.\par \par \uc2 FOX meteorologist Adam Klotz has the very latest for us.\par \par \uc2 Some crazy stuff expected out there, Adam.\par \par \uc2 ADAM KLOTZ, FOX NEWS METEOROLOGIST: Yes, you're absolutely right, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Already seeing some of these severe storms as it works its way across portions of the Midwest, stretching back across the Southeast. We have seen big storms and tornado watches in place, largely covering Kentucky, down into most of Tennessee. That's running all the way until the evening hours because the ingredients are present.\par \par \uc2 Even though it's a bit of a bullseye there across Northern Kentucky and portions of Ohio, the tornado threat is fairly widespread. We're looking at EF-2 tornadoes or greater from the Midwest from Ohio, stretching all the way down close to the Gulf Coast, so a really big area where we could see tornadoes this evening.\par \par \uc2 Now, this entire system continues to push its way off towards the east,not really a tornado threat so much tomorrow, but by the time you get late to tomorrow and then the next couple of days, kind of shocking, but a low- pressure system sits off the Northeast Coast. And then, suddenly, some of these interior areas, we're talking about feet of snowfall coming down for days, particularly at some of these higher elevations.\par \par \uc2 Don't be shocked if areas across New Hampshire or Vermont see several feet of snow. Now, it's this moving system that's going to be the concern for any kind of travel trouble here the next couple of days. You're seeing these spots in orange or yellow. Those are areas where we see at least some risk of slowdowns for airports.\par \par \uc2 Not a surprise. This is a big system, Sandra, impacting a whole lot of people.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Wow. We will be watching that for many hours to come.\par \par \uc2 KLOTZ: Yes, we will.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Adam, thank you for the update.\par \par \uc2 All right, a new proposal in California would guarantee workers are off the hook when they're off the clock. But could this bill to keep bosses at bay backfire?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Going after bosses for contacting their workers after hours, it's a new push in California.\par \par \uc2 The bill would allow employees to ignore calls and texts from their boss after they're off the clock. We have reached out to the lawmaker behind this, but is something like this needed or does it go too far?\par \par \uc2 Let's ask \uc2 Strategic Wealth Partners\uc2  president Mark Tepper and New York Post columnist Rikki Schlott.\par \par \uc2 Mark, is this necessary?\par \par \uc2 MARK TEPPER, \uc2 STRATEGIC WEALTH PARTNERS\uc2 : No. No. This is definitely a step too far.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these are arrangements and agreements that should be between employers and employees. We all have free will. If you have an overbearing boss, there's 8.7 million jobs. You can go and get a job elsewhere. And I would have to say most small business owners are very reasonable.\par \par \uc2 Most of them aren't going to have expectations that you work way after hours or on weekends. However, as a small business owner myself, while I don't have those expectations, it certainly doesn't go unnoticed. It's very much appreciated when someone responds after hours.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So they would have the right -- this bill would force them to have the right to investigate and fine companies that show a pattern of barging in on their employees' personal time, Rikki. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 RIKKI SCHLOTT, THE NEW YORK POST: I mean, I think that there are bigger fish to fry and problems to solve out in California than this.\par \par \uc2 And it certainly feels like it's not the place of an over-busy politician to, as Mark said, basically iron out something that should be interpersonal. But, I mean, the only -- only argument that I can really think of in this sense is that the Zoom era and the work from home era has allowed, what, home life and work life to blend together in a way?\par \par \uc2 But we need to talk through that and what that looks like in a hybrid situation going forward, not have legislators do it for us.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I think Rikki has a great point, Mark. I mean, isn't there just a reasonable expectation? And can't you still send the text and say get back to me at your availability or perhaps in the morning?\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Mark, the writer of the bill said, to Rikki's point, that the return -- these -- this bill would return working standards from before the smartphone era, which has -- quote -- "blurred the boundaries between work and home life."\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Sandra...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I think most people listening at home, they -- maybe that resonates with them a little bit, right? We don't just close the door and leave our office.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Correct.\par \par \uc2 But I will tell you what, Sandra. What this really is to me is, their is, just another display of equity in the workplace, right?\par \par \uc2 We can't let the go-getters who go above and beyond, who respond to e-mails at 10:00 p.m., we can't allow them to get ahead. We have to cater to the least motivated group of employees. And that's exactly what you have going on here. That's exactly what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, I guess, Rikki, the question is, and obviously the bigger picture here is that California already has some of the strictest, most stringent small business laws on the books.\par \par \uc2 It's just getting harder and harder for some of these small businesses to stay in business.\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of these regulations often backfire, like their exorbitant minimum wage, which is -- it makes it difficult for teens to get summer jobs. It makes it difficult for small businesses to make ends meet. I think that, really, the government needs to step away from the interpersonal relationship between bosses and employees.\par \par \uc2 And California is basically the test case for why that's a problem.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Mark, do you answer your texts and phone calls after hours?\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: I do, of course. I mean, I own the business, so I got to do it. What if a client has a problem? I got to address it immediately.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: And I would hope that if one of my employees had a solution, they'd respond rather promptly.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Very interesting.\par \par \uc2 I think that the aspect of most small businesses and small business owners, they're reasonable.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Right.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But there are emergencies and there are situations that have to be immediately addressed.\par \par \uc2 I appreciate the conversation, Rikki, Mark. Great to see you both. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And hard to believe an hour has flown by. Thanks so much for joining us. You can catch me tomorrow alongside \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  1:00 p.m. Eastern time on "America Reports." Again, thank you for joining us here.\par \par \uc2 For now, here is "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240402ek4200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 California's $20 Minimum Wage; Biden Makes Appeal to Haley Voters; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Narrowing V.P. Picks; \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  Speaks Out; Senior Iranian Commander Killed in Syria Strike; Border Battle; Driver Rams Gate at FBI Field Office in Georgia; Driver Charged in \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Shooting Appears in Court\b0\par\par\uc2 Trey Yingst, William La Jeunesse, Dan Hoffman, David Spunt, Nicole  Parker, Madison Scarpino, Nate Foy, Sandra Smith \par \uc2 7,821 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PATRICK HENDRY, PRESIDENT, POLICE BENEVOLENT ASSOCIATION OF THE CITY OF NEW YORK: Today is another step towards justice for our hero brother Jonathan Diller and his family.\par \par \uc2 This is a career criminal who's been arrested many, many times, and less than a year ago was arrested for an illegal firearm. Why was he back out in the streets? He should have been remanded. And, last Monday, in his car, he had another illegal firearm.\par \par \uc2 He is just as responsible for the death of our hero brother as the triggerman.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: We are live in New York City, where one of the two suspects arrested in connection with the deadly shooting of officer Jonathan Diller just appeared in court; 41-year-old Lindy Jones is accused of driving the vehicle at the time of the shooting, a judge ordering him held without bail for now.\par \par \uc2 It follows an emotional plea from Officer Diller's widow to get tough on crime.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE DILLER, WIFE OF SLAIN \uc2 NYPD\uc2  OFFICER: My son will grow up without his father, I will grow old without my husband, and his parents have to say goodbye to their child. How many more police officers and how many more families need to make the ultimate sacrifice before we start protecting them?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Critics now asking if we will start protecting these officers by cracking down on criminals. We will have more on that for you in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 First to Nate Foy. He's live here in New York City. He's got the very latest on what we're learning after that suspect appeared in court -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, the man accused of driving the car during \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officer Jonathan Diller's murder exactly one week ago today appeared in a Queens, New York courtroom this afternoon. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 You just mentioned, Lindy Jones is his name. He faces several weapons charges and he faces 15 years in prison if convicted in this specific case, but 15 additional years for a separate open weapons case from last year. His next court date is April 16.\par \par \uc2 The judge deemed him a flight risk, so he's being held without bail. And here's the Queens DA, \uc2 Melinda Katz\uc2 , talking about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELINDA KATZ\uc2 , QUEENS, NEW YORK, DISTRICT ATTORNEY: My office quoted him as saying, "I shoot people."\par \par \uc2 And that is one of the reasons that we do believe he is an amazing flight risk. Once you're held accountable for one gun charge and now the second gun charge, he's facing up to 30 years, and he made it very clear that this is what he does. He shoots people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Sandra, over 100 police officers supported the late \uc2 NYPD\uc2  detective today.\par \par \uc2 Diller leaves behind a 1-year-old son and his widow, who during his funeral this weekend called on New York's elected officials to do more to protect police officers. Guy Rivera's accused of murdering Diller during a traffic stop last week, and police say it could have been prevented.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL DIGIACOMO, PRESIDENT, DETECTIVES' ENDOWMENT ASSOCIATION: These bail reform laws that Albany has enacted has catered to the criminal element and not to the victims of crime. And because of those bail reform laws, we have a dead cop on our hands now.\par \par \uc2 This system is broke. Our legislature has to wake up, fix what's broke, or more people are going to die.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Rivera will appear in court next on May 7.\par \par \uc2 His criminal history includes 21 prior arrest at least, including several felonies -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Nate Foy live here in New York City for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So what can be done to protect police when career criminals are just let back on the streets, as in this case?\par \par \uc2 With us now is former New York City Police Detective Pat Brosnan.\par \par \uc2 Pat, great to see you. Thanks for joining us, horrible circumstances, but, to that New York official a moment ago, the system's broken. We're catering to the criminals. Is that happening?\par \par \uc2 PAT BROSNAN, FORMER \uc2 NYPD\uc2  DETECTIVE: Good afternoon, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 The fact, the fact is, Sandra, that the police need to be more proactive. The reality is that we can -- we can roll back bail reform. We can get judges uncuffed where they have responsibility and discretion for sentencing and bail. We can legitimize the rogue prosecutors, but above and beyond that the overarching change must be that constitutionally lawful searches, Fourth Amendment viable searches of individuals who you believe are carrying a weapon have to be amplified.\par \par \uc2 We have to revisit the value of stop and frisk from the '80s and '90s and early 2000s, because, otherwise, the bad guy feels empowered to carry a weapon, no different than Rivera and his partner. And the other -- by the way, the other most amazing thing is that no one's brought up the three strike rule, the habitual offender rule.\par \par \uc2 Both of these bad guys will -- if they got caught with that loaded gun out there in Far Rockaway, they were going to prison for life with no parole. It's called the three strikes law. That's why he opened fire on Detective First Grade Diller, because he knew he was going in forever and ever and ever.\par \par \uc2 And that's a fact, and that's a great law. But we have to, have to, have to be more proactive. Otherwise, they will continue to carry illegal weapons, and they will open fire when they're looking at a heavy, heavy sentence, as they should and as they will.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, we know it was Guy Rivera who shot and killed, ultimately, Officer Diller. And this suspect who appeared in court today was driving the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 He, as we just learned, faces 30 years, held without bail. Are the punishments going to fit the crimes here that these two suspects carried out?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: Not in my view. Not in my view.\par \par \uc2 Detective First Grade Diller, his 9-month-son, Ryan, and his wife, Stephanie, have received a death sentence for the fact that there's no father around. I am a proponent of the death sentence, and right here you have a textbook case of it. He was murdered, executed, assassinated in cold blood on a lawful car stop, parked illegally in a bus lane on Mott Avenue.\par \par \uc2 It should be death. That's my view on it, absolutely. Thirty years in -- and, by the way, bail reform, the less is more provisional bail reform allowed Guy Rivera to be released a full year early. For every day that -- every 30 days that he does -- ostensibly does not have a violation, Sandra, he gets released 30 days earlier.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: That put him back on the street an entire year earlier. He would not have been on the street capable of assassinating Detective Diller...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: ... if that bail reform that Cuomo had parachuted onto us two years ago.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, look no further than the families who are greatly affected by this, obviously, Stephanie, Officer Diller's now widow and mother of their 1-year-old son, who's now left behind without a father.\par \par \uc2 It was Stephanie who said, this is a city that needs to get tough on crime, not tougher, needs to get tough on crime, period, because she is blaming that for her husband's death.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: One thousand percent. Could not agree more. You have prosecutors who are rogues who do not enforce the law they're sworn to.\par \par \uc2 You have judges who are handcuffs, Sandra, who have no discretion, zero discretion under this bail reform on both sentencing and bail. You have to energize the police. You have to empower them to go out and conduct Fourth Amendment constitutionally legal and lawful searches so that the bad guys will not take the risk of carrying a weapon, because they know they're going to be searched.\par \par \uc2 These guys were sitting -- think about it. They're sitting in plain view illegally parked with two loaded Glocks on their person. Do you want to know why, in my view? Because they never thought they'd be searched.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: But gentlemen -- sorry -- outstanding officers like Detective Diller are proactive and they challenged them, and that's why he opened fire.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It's just a horrific and such a sad, sad story and state affairs in cities like New York, where we are seeing these soft-on-crime policies play out far too often and end like this.\par \par \uc2 Pat Brosnan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And this is another Fox News Alert, live to Chamblee, Georgia, where we are learning more after that car rammed into the gate of an \uc2 FBI\uc2  office.\par \par \uc2 Madison Scarpino is live there in Atlanta. She's got the latest for us.\par \par \uc2 What are we learning?\par \par \uc2 MADISON SCARPINO, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  confirms that the suspect was taken to Grady Memorial Hospital -- that's in the heart of Atlanta -- for a mental evaluation.\par \par \uc2 But they do say that he will be arrested. And the car was actually towed away from the facility about 15 minutes ago. We saw the car being inspected for a while by people with gas masks and hazmat outfits on for protection to just make sure there wasn't anything dangerous in the car.\par \par \uc2 And it must have gotten the all-clear because it is now towed away, as I just mentioned. But it all started around noon, when officials say that the driver slammed into the office's employee entrance gate into a security blockade. And you can see on the video the blockade worked. The driver didn't get very far. The car is wrecked. The airbags went off.\par \par \uc2 And an \uc2 FBI\uc2  source says that the suspect was actually trying to follow employees into the gate. They say he tried to go into the facility, but was unsuccessful. And, fortunately, nobody was injured. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says that he was not associated with the facility at all.\par \par \uc2 And, as for the building, Sandra, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  field office is in Chamblee, Georgia, which is a city just outside of Atlanta. It's near -- just off of I-85 North If you're from Atlanta, you're very familiar with that DeKalb interstate. It's about 10 minutes from DeKalb-Peachtree Airport.\par \par \uc2 And it's one of the 56 field offices nationwide, where \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents carry out investigations and assess local and regional crime threats. Now, the Atlanta field office, in particular, it's known for leading complex investigations, including its cyber program, which is one of the busiest teams at the bureau.\par \par \uc2 But back out here live, as far as a criminal motive or if there was any domestic terrorism involved, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says that they cannot answer that at this point. But this situation is very much so still developing, and we're monitoring everything, and we will keep you updated.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Madison, thank you. We know you will.\par \par \uc2 More on this with former \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent and FOX News contributor Nicole Parker.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, thank you and we had you on the breaking news as we were learning these details as it was happening earlier. What can you add to what we have learned now?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE PARKER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, first of all, that \uc2 FBI\uc2  field OFFICES are extremely secure and they do have these barricades in place to stop individuals of this nature.\par \par \uc2 Having worked in FBI Miami, I can tell you that the employee entrances, there's an armed gate. You have to put in your card, put in a specific code, and then it goes up, and then there's a barrier, and it has to go down in order for you to enter the office.\par \par \uc2 It wouldn't be the first time that a non-\uc2 FBI\uc2  employee tried to tailgate an employee and gain entrance into the \uc2 FBI\uc2  office. Those barricades work. And we saw that happen today.\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  field offices have been the target of threats. This is not the first time. We don't know the details at this point. Was it terrorist-related? What I do know from individuals -- sources that I have is that this potentially may have been someone who the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had been notified of a BOLO, be on the lookout, for a certain individual that may have been threatening that office or people in that field office in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Again, no link or evidence that it is the same individual that they had been warned about, but if -- as the investigation continues, I'm sure that those facts will be coming out. This is something that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  takes extremely seriously because they are a target. We saw what happened in August of 2022 in the FBI Cincinnati field office, where an individual, again, attempted to gain entry into an \uc2 FBI\uc2  office.\par \par \uc2 They were unsuccessful. It ended up turning into a high-speed chase, and that individual ended up being killed by law enforcement. So, when someone attempts to gain access to an \uc2 FBI\uc2  office, the office employees, you are immediately alerted. Agents are alerted.\par \par \uc2 My understanding is that there were agents that helped to take down this individual. There are guard gates. There are guard shacks. There's law enforcement right there at the front gates. There's video surveillance. It is very highly secure.\par \par \uc2 But employees are notified. And we are trained on how to handle situations when someone is trying to gain entry into our office.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Nicole, as far as a motive, which we do not have yet -- they haven't released any sort of motive if they have learned of one -- do you believe that they will make that public?\par \par \uc2 PARKER: You know, at this point, it's too premature to say if they will make that public.\par \par \uc2 I think that, in the environment that we're in, they are going to try to be as transparent as possible because they want to deter other people from acting in the same manner and show, hey, if you go to an \uc2 FBI\uc2  field office and you try to do that, there will be justice served against you.\par \par \uc2 And it's important to note that this person has been sent to a hospital for mental evaluation. We have something called complaint duty, and you're the one that takes in individuals when they come to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  field office. You take down their complaints. You're called the duty agent.\par \par \uc2 I myself have been on duty agent service, and I have had an individual come to the field office and threaten to cause harm to us right there.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: So, this, again, is not the first time. But we will see what happens and I'm just grateful that no one was hurt and that there were no explosive devices found either.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, I mean, look at these pictures. It's hard to think anybody thought they were going to get through that barrier. But to your point about times in the past where you have seen someone trail in another employee after they have keyed through their card and gone through the gate, that would be understandable.\par \par \uc2 But they were on this almost the minute that it happened.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Parker, thank you very much for joining us on the breaking news.\par \par \uc2 All right, well, there are thousands of migrants heading into the U.S. in a new caravan, and now a new court ruling could be rolling out the welcome mat for them. We will explain.\par \par \uc2 But, first, the world on watch as a suspected Israeli strike is said to have taken out a top Iranian commander. Major reverberations from this, we are on it.\par \par \uc2 Then, after that Moscow massacre, a retired U.S. general sounding the warning. Why he says the threat of ISIS attacks here on the homeland is growing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2  (RET.), FORMER COMMANDER, U.S. CENTRAL COMMAND: I think we should expect further attempts of this nature against the United States, as well as our partners and other nations abroad. I think this is inevitable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: To the border battle now.\par \par \uc2 Just as another migrant caravan is making its way to the border between Mexico and Texas, an appeals court panel will hear arguments that week -- this week over that Texas law allowing the rest of illegal border crossers.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' David Spunt is at the Justice Department. He's got the latest on that for us.\par \par \uc2 Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 There has been a lot of back and forth on this law. A federal appeals court, specifically, a three-judge panel, will listen to arguments about the constitutionality or the merits of this law on Wednesday in New Orleans. We will be there following the latest developments.\par \par \uc2 The reason that a court in New Orleans is listening to arguments about this controversial Texas law is because Texas falls within the map of the circuit Court of Appeals. So New Orleans is in charge of the state of Texas. We expect arguments before this panel to take about an hour. The case will be argued from both sides of the government and those representing the state of Texas.\par \par \uc2 You may remember, last month, the Supreme Court allowed the Texas law, giving local Texas law enforcement the ability to arrest anyone who is suspected of crossing the border illegally. The Supreme Court said it could be enforced. Just hours later, Sandra, a lower court put a pause or a stay in place on that law.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department is fighting to make that law unconstitutional, arguing that immigration control is entirely federal power. U.S. Border Patrol agents alone control the border and make arrests. But Texas Governor Greg Abbott has said many times the law is needed because of its state sovereignty and safety, and he says basically the \uc2 United States government\uc2  not doing its job.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Border Patrol is understaffed and undermanned. Border Patrol agents are not doing the proper job, according to Texas Governor Greg Abbott, so he believes that his staff and his team in Texas can do better. The actual merits of the law, meeting the constitutionality, will be argued on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 But, ultimately Sandra, no matter who wins or who loses, one side will likely go back to the Supreme Court, once again putting the justices in the middle of this issue in the middle of election season. So, back we go to the Supreme Court, but Wednesday will be an important day in the life and in the future of this controversial law -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And we will be watching it.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt live on that for us.\par \par \uc2 David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to a suspected Israeli airstrike in Damascus, Syria that reportedly killed a senior commander in Iran's Revolutionary Guard.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Trey Yingst is in Tel Aviv, Israel. He's got the latest and the details on that for us.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sandra. Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 We are learning more about this rare daytime strike taking place in Damascus, Syria, killing top Iranian General Mohammad Zahedi. Zahedi is a man responsible for the weapons shipments from Iran to places like Lebanon and Syria. He's also someone who reports directly to top IRGC leadership.\par \par \uc2 Now, reports do indicate six other members of Iran's Revolutionary Guard Corps were killed in the strikes earlier today. Syria's foreign minister reacting, saying this:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAISAL MEKDAD, SYRIAN FOREIGN MINISTER (through translator): We strongly condemn this criminal act carried out by the Zionist enemy, which led to the death of some innocent civilians who were passing by the street. So, again, we emphasize our position beside our brothers in the Iranian Embassy and the Islamic Republic of Iran, and we assure that Syria and the Islamic Republic don't forget their enemies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The location here is important to talk about. We are talking about a strike inside the capital of Damascus at a building just next to the Iranian embassy.\par \par \uc2 The question now is if and how Iran will respond. Israeli forces remain on high alert across the country -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Trey Yingst on that, thank you.\par \par \uc2 What are the reverberations from this strike?\par \par \uc2 Let's ask former CIA station chief in Moscow and FOX News contributor Dan Hoffman.\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts, Dan?\par \par \uc2 DAN HOFFMAN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, this was a pretty extraordinary tactical success by the Israelis.\par \par \uc2 It's worth noting that they spared the Iranian ambassador. They used their intelligence to find and fix the location of the IRGC commander for Lebanon and Syria, and they used their kinetic capability to finish him.\par \par \uc2 This operation is really important for Israel, both to degrade the IRGC's capabilities, but it's also how you deter Iran, because so much of the weapons that Iran is sending to his \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  go right through Syria. And that's how you do it.\par \par \uc2 We're still not doing much more than shooting down drones from the Houthis. I think we could take a page out of Israel's book in terms of how to deter Iran and degrade their capability in terms of their ability to support their proxy terrorists.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, Dan, the Iranian ambassador warning, the response to the airstrike will be harsh, so we will be watching for where that goes next. Meanwhile, we have heard \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  sound the warning over the terror threat from the southern border. But now add in the Moscow massacre that ISIS-K has taken credit for, and Retired General \uc2 Frank McKenzie\uc2  is worried an attack here on the homeland could be coming.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: ISIS-K, in particular, but ISIS in general, has a strong desire to attack our homeland. We should believe them when they say that. They're going to try to do it.\par \par \uc2 And I -- so I think the threat is growing. It's begun to grow as soon as we left Afghanistan and took pressure off ISIS-K. So I think we should expect further attempts of this nature against the United States, as well as our partners and other nations abroad. I think this is inevitable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Dan, do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: I do. I have agreed with it since the day we left Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Look, General McKenzie is right. We have taken the pressure off of ISIS-K. They enjoy ungoverned space, the same sort of ungoverned space that Al Qaeda enjoyed in Afghanistan before 9/11. What concerns me is that they now have a demonstrated external operations capability. They have launched attacks in Iran and Turkey and now a mass casualty attack in Russia.\par \par \uc2 We're also in their crosshairs. And they have every reason to be looking at where we're most vulnerable. And that's why I think the concerns about our southern border are justified, and take the confluence of events right now with Israel's counteroffensive in Gaza, which Director Wray has also emphasized has proved to be a force multiplier for terrorists to fund-raise and disseminate propaganda, disinformation, and attract followers.\par \par \uc2 So this is a time to be on high alert. It reminds me of when our -- my former Director at CIA George Tenet used to say the lights are blinking red. The question is, what are we going to do about it?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Considering the threats, the warning on the threats, there is almost consensus with our military community. Do you see our \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  taking this seriously enough, Dan?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: Well, I think we need to do more.\par \par \uc2 I think the question is, now that we have left Afghanistan, we don't have the capability we once had to collect intelligence and deliver kinetic strikes on ISIS-K and Al Qaeda in Afghanistan. I think this is a good time for the House and Senate oversight to be looking at the Biden administration and their plan for dealing with these increased threats.\par \par \uc2 We don't want to deal with them here in the United States at the point of attack. What we prefer to do is detect those threats way out what we like to call left of boom, and then preempt them before any harm is caused. But that job has become that much more difficult because we're not there. We're not in Afghanistan anymore.\par \par \uc2 So there has to be some plan going forward. I'd like to hear what it is from the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That's quite a warning from you as well.\par \par \uc2 Dan Hoffman on all of that for us.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Dan.\par \par \uc2 New developments, meanwhile, in the Trump-Georgia case renewing the question if district attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  should stay on the case. Find out why.\par \par \uc2 And, later, California's $20 minimum wage for fast-food workers kicking in. Could that have more workers down and out? Hear from a business owner demanding from California's governor.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: A new development in the wake of that Maryland bridge disaster. It could be a positive sign.\par \par \uc2 And California's fast-food wage hike has officially kicked in. Will it have more consumers shelling out?\par \par \uc2 We're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: To the Trump Georgia case, and lots of questions today after district attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  said this:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FANI WILLIS\uc2  (D), FULTON COUNTY, GEORGIA, DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Recently, they tell me they don't like me to talk about race. Well, I'm going to talk about it anyway.\par \par \uc2 Truth is, there's some challenges that come to being black.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: DA Willis making that comment, despite Judge Scott McAfee's rebuke.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and co-defendants already appealing the decision to keep Willis on the case. So, where is this heading?\par \par \uc2 Good thing we have our legal eagles John Yoo and Katie Cherkasky here.\par \par \uc2 So, John, what say you?\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: I think \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 , unfortunately, is forgetting that this case is not about her. It's not even really about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It's about public faith in the justice system.\par \par \uc2 And when a judge orders a prosecutor not to talk about race, not to throw around racial accusations, and then the DA does it anyway, then she's undermining public faith in the integrity of justice. She's actually demonstrating the kind of disregard for the courts that she was when she was testifying.\par \par \uc2 And the job of the prosecutor ultimately is to make sure that the public has faith in the decisions of the courts and the justice system. And she's undermining that. And that's going to, I think, undermine her ability to carry out this trial. It's only going to help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Considering everything John just said, Katie, why do you think she said this?\par \par \uc2 KATIE CHERKASKY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: I think she's daring the appellate court to disqualify her at this point.\par \par \uc2 As Trump's attorneys pointed out in their appeal, one of the questions for disqualification is whether forensic misconduct is a basis for disqualification and what exactly that entails. And there is argument, based on Supreme Court precedent, that even injecting the idea of race, especially in a case that has nothing to do with race, is grounds alone for that disqualification.\par \par \uc2 So, certainly, it will be interesting if the appellate court takes up this case what they make of that. But I think that alone could even form the basis for disqualification if the court wants to go there.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, now this, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  also pushing the boundaries of his gag order in the hush money case, one ex-judge saying he should be jailed if he violates it.\par \par \uc2 So, John, what's your take on that?\par \par \uc2 YOO: First of all, he has First Amendment rights. In fact, I think the judges in New York City have gone way too far with these gag orders, particularly when the person who's subject to them, the defendant, is also running for president, when he's speaking as part of the most important political process that our Constitution and the First Amendment is designed to protect, which is elections and political speech.\par \par \uc2 I think that the courts have to be very careful, in fact, more than careful. They should err on the side of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in making sure that he has the First Amendment free speech rights to talk about things, even if it involves criticizing the judge, even if it involves criticizing the decisions for making this case.\par \par \uc2 Put it another way, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could say these things in the courtroom. If he could say these things in the courtroom, how can a judge try to prevent him from saying them outside the courtroom?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Katie?\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: I completely agree. I'm not really sure what the basis is for these gag orders. It's always unlawful for anybody to obstruct justice or tamper with witnesses.\par \par \uc2 And, certainly, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does have a First Amendment right to engage in campaign speech. And even in the D.C. court, when they upheld the gag order, they allowed for him to portray the prosecutions against him as political vendetta.\par \par \uc2 So the line in the sand there does need to err on the side of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and on his First Amendment rights. And I certainly don't think that these gag orders have been narrowly tailored enough to meet the standards set by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Just getting a little bit of an update on that story and on the criminal hush money DA Bragg case and some clarity on it.\par \par \uc2 John, just a quick reaction from you on this. The Manhattan DA office has filed a memorandum in support of their March 28 letter seeking clarification on the gag order. They write about "the defendant's dangerous, violent and reprehensible rhetoric fundamentally threatening the integrity of the proceedings and is intended to intimidate witnesses and trial participants alike, including this court."\par \par \uc2 Want to get your reaction to that, just in?\par \par \uc2 YOO: The only ground that this court could have for restricting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's speech is that it actually threatens -- that there's an imminent threat of obstruction of justice.\par \par \uc2 Now, you can't just as a prosecutor throw that red flag out there. You have to have some proof. There has to be evidence, I think, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's statements actually are interfering with the ability of the court to carry out this trial. You can't just accept the prosecution's word for it. We have to see evidence. We have to see examples.\par \par \uc2 And they have to be real. They can't just be crazy people writing things on the Internet.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, John and Katie, I appreciate you breaking us -- staying with us and on the breaking news to you, John, as well. Katie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. More coming up. The race for the White House heating, up as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is reportedly whittling that V.P. list down. So who is in and who is out?\par \par \uc2 Then, find out how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is making a direct play for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  backers. Will it work?\par \par \uc2 Plus, meet the California business owner demanding answers from Democratic Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  as the state's $20 minimum fast-food wage kicks in.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. WES MOORE (D-MD): This is a huge economic impact, not just to Baltimore, not just to the state of Maryland, but the economic impact this is having on the American economy is staggering, when one of the busiest ports, one of the most effective ports in this country has essentially been shut down.\par \par \uc2 We are going to rebuild the Francis Scott Key Bridge. This is incredibly important.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Maryland Democratic Governor \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2  telling Neil over the weekend the state will rebuild the Francis Scott Key Bridge that collapsed after a container ship collided with it last week.\par \par \uc2 Today, he said a temporary channel on the northeast side of the collapse is now open. The White House now saying President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will plan to travel to Baltimore on Friday to assess the damage for himself. We will keep you updated as we learn more on that.\par \par \uc2 And now to the race for the White House. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's search for his next running mate is starting to get serious, we are told. A top Trump adviser reportedly begins the process of narrowing down the list, with plans to draw this out "Apprentice"-style. OK, you have our attention.\par \par \uc2 The Hill national politics reporters Julia Manchester joins us now.\par \par \uc2 What do we think?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE HILL: Well, Sandra, this is no surprise to me, because we know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really likes the drama and he likes the suspense. He knows that his supporters like to watch that. So they will appreciate how this process is going to be drawn out.\par \par \uc2 But, really, we have already seen the process playing out, I think, on cable news, in fact, watching a number of these potential contenders essentially audition really when they do interviews and speak out in public at Trump campaign events and such.\par \par \uc2 So I think we're already seeing this in motion, and former President Trump is watching closely.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The short list for some time now has included Senator Scott -- Senator Scott of South Carolina, Vance of Ohio, Katie Britt of Alabama, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  of Florida, Governors Noem of South Dakota, \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2  of Arkansas, \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  of North Dakota, I mean, just to name a few.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Yes. Yes, it's already a long list.\par \par \uc2 And I think the question that the Trump campaign is going to have to answer and former President Trump is going to have to answer when he's making this decision is, what do these people bring to the ticket that really could make it more of a well-rounded ticket?\par \par \uc2 So if we would go back to 2016, when former President Trump chose Indiana Governor, then-Indiana Governor \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , I think the logic around that was because \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  had huge sway with the evangelical vote and he wanted to solidify that vote. This time around, talking to sources, they say, look, it has to be a woman or a person of color.\par \par \uc2 So, Senator Tim Scott of South Carolina, \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 , \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , Katie Britt, they say that that could help the Republican ticket starting with former President Trump at the top maybe make some headway with black voters or win that very key suburban swing voter, which oftentimes is a woman that ends up playing that deciding vote.\par \par \uc2 So we will have to see what logic really plays out in terms of the Trump team, but they have a big list. And as we get closer to the convention, they're going to have to narrow it down.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, the former president has reportedly said his top criterion is to pick someone who's going to be a good president in case of emergency and also someone who can help expand his appeal in November.\par \par \uc2 Julia, while I have you, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is planning to head out to both Wisconsin and Michigan, this as polling shows President Biden is closing the gap in those key battleground states. So what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: So, look, Sandra over the course of the next several months, as we had to the election, we are going to go through a polling roller coaster. It's going to get closer. Then maybe Trump will start leading and then maybe Trump will fall behind and Biden will start leading.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that he's going to Michigan and Wisconsin is no surprise. We know that former President Biden has shown or -- President Biden -- excuse me -- has shown sign of weakness in those states, particularly in Michigan when we look at the uncommitted vote. We know that these are two states that President Trump flipped in 2016 and that he lost in 2020.\par \par \uc2 So it's very important that he goes there. And, most importantly, they are part of the six major battleground states. So we're going to be in for a rocky ride when it comes to polling, but I think we're just going to see this becoming much more narrow.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 Julia, one other quick question for you. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's reelection campaign has just put out a new ad that makes a direct appeal to \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  supporters. So who has a better shot at getting her supporters, Julia, Trump or Biden?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: I was talking to a number of Republican sources about this today, and, essentially, what they were saying was, look, we don't know how this is going to pan out.\par \par \uc2 They -- some Trump allies say, look, a number -- a large number of \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  supporters voted for Biden in 2020. So it's more on -- the onus is more on Biden to win those voters back. But others say, look, Trump is at risk of potentially losing these voters.\par \par \uc2 And if these are Republicans who voted for Trump in 2016 or 2020 or both and then changed their minds this primary election, if they decide to vote for Biden, that would obviously be bad. But if they decided to stay home and not show up to the polls, that would be a loss for Trump as well.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Julia Manchester from the Hill.\par \par \uc2 Julia, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, business owners nationwide watching as California's $20 minimum wage for fast-food joints kicks in. We have got an employer coming up, but first to William La Jeunesse on what we are finding out.\par \par \uc2 Hi, William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Sandra, this isn't just a small step on a wage ladder. This is a huge leap in employers' single largest expense, labor. So what's going to the impact on workers, consumers, and employers?\par \par \uc2 That story coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, so it's a story that we have been talking about for some time on this California minimum wage hike to $20 for fast-food workers.\par \par \uc2 It has finally kicked in, that $20 minimum wage for fast-food workers, and it could have consumers having to shell out.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' William La Jeunesse is on this for us live in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Wow, business owners, they're just trying to figure out what they need to know and if they're included in this, William.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Well, that's true.\par \par \uc2 But it's almost certain to raise prices for consumers, right? But to Governor Newsom and his union supporters, they say this is as much about money as it is justice for fast-food workers, raising their pay 20 percent beginning today from $16 to $20 an hour, necessary, they say, to compensate for the cost of living in California.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRID VILORIO, CALIFORNIA FAST-FOOD WORKER (through translator): Unfortunately, California is really expensive, so earning $20 in any job, fast-food or not, is really helpful.\par \par \uc2 STATE. REP. ISAAC BRYAN (D-CA): We dehumanize fast-food workers. We act like their labor isn't worthy of respect, isn't worthy of recognition, and damn sure isn't worthy of being paid justly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: But critics say the impact is going to go beyond the chain outlets to every restaurant and employer competing for low-skilled labor.\par \par \uc2 The result, higher consumer prices and some workers will get cut.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WIEDERHORN\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 FAT BRANDS\uc2 : Because if you're an independent operator and the chain operators are paying $20 an hour, you're going to have to raise your wages as well or your employees are going to leave and go to the chains.\par \par \uc2 ALEX JOHNSON, CALIFORNIA RESTAURANT OWNER: We're no longer hiring. We're not backfilling positions. We're not growing in the state anymore. We're not expanding more locations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So already two chains have cut 1,200 drivers and will soon rely, they say, on \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , which are not covered by the law.\par \par \uc2 Governor Newsom justifies the increase this way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): We saw the abuse, we saw the inequities in terms of the wages and the treatment. And we realized we had a responsibility to do more and step up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So the raise applies to roughly about 550,000 workers, Sandra. Their annual wage, if they work a 40 hour week, will rise to about $41,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 And your question implies it will apply to any outlet or brand that has 60 or more locations nationwide, which is basically every fast-food restaurant -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And could be more.\par \par \uc2 I mean, our next guest said that. She had a hard time figuring out if her business even fell under the law. And we will talk to her in a moment.\par \par \uc2 William La Jeunesse, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So we are going to get reaction from a business owner who says she has more questions than answers after meeting with the California governor's office. She will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Our next guest has been raising questions over California's wage hike for six months now. She spoke with the governor's office, Governor Newsom, today to demand answers.\par \par \uc2 Gabriela Campbell is a Handel's Ice Cream franchisee in California. She joins us now. And, by the way, I want to tell our viewers we did reach out to the governor's office. He is welcome to come on the show any time.\par \par \uc2 Gabriela, you had a virtual meeting with his office today. What can you tell us came from that?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELA CAMPBELL, CALIFORNIA BUSINESS OWNER: Hi, Sandra. Thank you for having me on.\par \par \uc2 We met with an adviser to Governor Newsom today, and a little bit disappointing, I would have to say. Although we weren't told as a group of Handel's franchisees that we were included in the bill, we also were passed on again to other agencies, namely, the Department of Industrial Relations and the Fast Food Council, for answers to the question of whether or not we are part of this bill.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What does this fast-food wage hike mean for you and your business? How would it affect you?\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: It would affect us and that we would have to raise our prices. We're considered a snack. And at $5.50 for an ice cream cone, you can't really go much higher.\par \par \uc2 You might be able to get a kid's meal for $10. You can't do an ice cream cone at $10 -- at $8, $9, $10. It's not feasible, and we will get priced out. We will really have to start laying off some of our employees.\par \par \uc2 And, as you can see the photos, they're high-school-aged kids. We have 25 employees.Three are full-time. The rest are full-time students, high school and college students.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And I know that you said that most of the time, some of the time at least, they're able to make more than $20 an hour on their own when you consider tips and other things. So what is your plan?\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I mean, for this level of uncertainty, that is almost impossible for businesses to weather through. What's your plan?\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: It is.\par \par \uc2 We plan to continue to seek the answers that we're looking for. It's just so ambiguous the way the law was written, and collectively as the group of us plan to continue with the Fast Food Council, set up a meeting next week if we can.\par \par \uc2 I have had a call in to the Department of Industrial Relations. I have had a phone call with the attorney with the labor commissioner's office. I have been pounding payment and beating down doors for 5.5 months. And we have a -- we have some recognition from the author of the bill's staff member that ice cream parlors were not the intent of the bill five months ago.\par \par \uc2 We were told from different outlets that ice cream parlors were not the intent and that the bucks have been passed on from \uc2 SEIU\uc2  and \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  as cleanup on the bill...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: ... to Fast Food Council, Department of Industrial Relations.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: And I believe the latest was to access the frequently asked questions.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And I know you -- I know you have been spending six months fighting this and trying to figure out how you're affected. And that takes time away from you and your operation and improving operations for your customers.\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We understand. And we will check back with you.\par \par \uc2 We appreciate you joining us, Gabriela. Our best to you.\par \par \uc2 You can catch me tomorrow, alongside John Roberts, 1:00 p.m. Eastern time on "America Reports."\par \par \uc2 For now, here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240401ek4100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 California Raising Minimum Wage; RFK Jr.'s Third-Party Threat; Recovery Operations Continue After Baltimore Bridge Collapse; Hunter Biden Attorney Argues For Tax Case Dismissal\b0\par\par\uc2 David Spunt, Jacqui Heinrich, Hillary Vaughn, Sol Wisenberg,  Jonathan Hunt, Griff Jenkins, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,090 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 March 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, we have got a lot more on that market.\par \par \uc2 FOX on top of two other big developing stories, however. We are live at Los Angeles, where \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's attorney is trying to fight those tax charges and get him off the hook. And we're live in Baltimore, Maryland, where investigators are looking for answers and they're on the hunt.\par \par \uc2 First to Baltimore, and here's what we know right now. Divers are searching for the bodies of six construction workers, presumed dead, after that bridge collapsed. About a dozen ships are unable to get out of the port as shipping traffic remains pretty much halted. Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  says it is way too soon to tell when the port will reopen and fears major impact on our supply chain.\par \par \uc2 And the costs are mounting through all of this. One estimate has insured losses going up to $4 billion. Wow. And that's just for starters.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to it to Griff Jenkins in Baltimore with the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil. Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 We are on the Patapsco River on a boat here. And we're getting a much closer look at really the magnitude of what happened to this bridge and the challenge of those divers we have watched going in and out. You can see actually here closest to us just a few hundred yards away the little boat with the blue lights.\par \par \uc2 That's \uc2 Maryland's Department of Natural Resources\uc2 . They're part of this recovery effort trying to get the bodies of those six presumed dead to bring closure to those families. You come over here to this open area, Neil, you can see the debris in the middle of that section. There were boats there and divers all morning long there.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, that is an area that they believe possibly some of those workers and their vehicles may have been. And then keep coming over here heading sort of south down along the bridge. You can see that 1,000-foot ship with some nearly 5,000 containers still sitting there, the Dali.\par \par \uc2 And we know now from that White House briefing earlier, Neil, according to the vice admiral of the Coast Guard, that there are some 50-plus containers on that ship with hazardous material, not posing a threat right now, but nevertheless on it.\par \par \uc2 Thankfully, it wasn't near the bow, where you see the section span of the bridge collapsing, because, obviously, that is the worst damage. It appears to be a little farther back towards the pilot house. But it is the first day, Neil, of the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 's really on the ground with boots in their investigation.\par \par \uc2 A lot of questions as to why this happened. We were able to speak with the chairwoman, Jennifer Homendy, about what the investigation is going to tell us, that data recorder that they're now in possession of, and I asked her about a Wall Street Journal exclusive piece that suggested that possibly dirty fuel could have played a part into the ship losing power.\par \par \uc2 Here's what she said, Neil.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: There's -- The Wall Street Journal is reporting exclusively today there may have been issues with dirty fuel. Can you tell us anything about that?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOMENDY, CHAIR, \uc2 NATIONAL TRANSPORTATION SAFETY BOARD\uc2 : Yes, I -- we don't know that right now, but we will look into it. To be honest, I have not seen those reports, so I'd have to get some context, but we would have to look at that. And, of course, having the right fuel, making sure it's clean fuel certainly would contribute to the operation of the vessel, but we have an entire engineering team here that will really look into that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And one final last look, Neil.\par \par \uc2 What really hits home for me being this close is just what a silver lining it was that the harbor Maryland pilots coordinated in real time in a short span of minutes as that ship lost power and began to drift towards this bridge. They coordinated the harbor pilots with the Maryland Transportation authorities and they closed this bridge down, because, as you look at that bridge, where it just stops, imagine being a car driving in the dark at night at 1:30 a.m.\par \par \uc2 You would have just driven right into the river and likely plunged to your death. So it is likely, as Governor \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2  says, countless lives were saved because of the quick coordination between those harbor pilots and the Maryland Transportation authorities that closed that bridge almost instantly when they learned about the danger -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow. Talk about quick thinking.\par \par \uc2 Griff, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 Griff Jenkins in Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 We get any more developments, we will take you back there.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, I want to go to Sal Mercogliano. He's a former merchant mariner and a maritime expert.\par \par \uc2 Sal, very good to have you and thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of what you're learning thus far, Sal? I guess they have this black box off the ship, so they have to analyze that. What would be on that?\par \par \uc2 SAL MERCOGLIANO, FORMER MERCHANT MARINER: So, Neil, that -- thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 That black box would be the vessel data recorder. So it would have all the readings from the engine room, everything that potentially led to the loss of power on the vessel. It would also have all the instrumentation from the bridge. And probably most interesting for everybody is a voice recorder that records the conversations on the bridge, that conversation between the ship's pilots that were on board helping to navigate the vessel and the ship's master.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Sal, a lot of people do some Monday morning quarterback on these things, I think always dangerous, but wondering when the ship was listless and then the power was going out, why didn't they just drop anchor right then and there?\par \par \uc2 Is that something that is powered? You need electricity to make that happen?\par \par \uc2 MERCOGLIANO: No.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you as a former mariner that the sound of silence is the most scariest sound on a ship.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MERCOGLIANO: And so right then and there they would have to think about what to be done. But, fortunately, the anchors do not require power. They're brakes, so you just have to release them.\par \par \uc2 But you have to think about the moment and when did they want to release it? A ship at eight knots, about 10 miles an hour, 115,000 tons, an anchor is going to have a hard time stopping it. It really is not like in the movies, where do you see that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is that right? OK. Well, that answers that. I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 Another thing I wanted to bounce over to you, Sal, is, this is the third ship that has hit a bridge. The two others were in different countries. And you don't want to tie them together. The other two were nothing like this. But does it have something to do with the size? Is it cargo ships that are much bigger now, passenger ships that are much bigger now?\par \par \uc2 Royal Caribbean\uc2 's Icon of the Seas is a monster of a ship. It can't even go to all the ports it's assigned to. And I'm thinking there's going to be a ship next year that's going to be bigger than that one. Now, again, that's a passenger ship versus cargo ships. But these things are getting bigger and bigger, it seems.\par \par \uc2 MERCOGLIANO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you're dealing with a bridge that was built in 1977. A ship the size of the Dali wasn't even envisioned. And Dali is not a big container ship.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Really?\par \par \uc2 MERCOGLIANO: I mean, the Ever Given was twice the size of this.\par \par \uc2 And what we're doing on the oceans right now is increasing the volume of transportation, but we're also increasing the velocity. And that doesn't give you a lot of time and slack in the system for an error, so that, when something goes wrong, it tends to go wrong very quickly and unfortunately in this case very catastrophically.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Sal, another thing I was curious about, maybe you can help me out with this, is that cargo crew members were interviewed today. I don't know if they were all interviewed today. I don't know if the interviews were finished today.\par \par \uc2 What are authorities asking them, the ticktock on what they did, what they were doing over the weekend? What?\par \par \uc2 MERCOGLIANO: Well, I think one of the things that they want to find out, was there systematic problems in the operation of the ship? Maybe it was a command structure. Maybe there was maintenance issues.\par \par \uc2 They really want to get a feel for them to tell them things that may not be in the written documents. And, of course, they want to find out, what were the actions taken during that?\par \par \uc2 While we will have a voice recording of the bridge, we will not probably have a voice recording down in the engine room. And when that power went out, you are down in the engine room of the ship, there is no power. There's no lights. You have got emergency lights, maybe a handheld light.\par \par \uc2 What were the actions that you were taking? What did you do during that period of time to help mitigate the incident that eventually led to the collapse of the bridge?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sal, thank you very much. You answered a lot of very dumb questions on my part, but very interesting.\par \par \uc2 Sal Mercogliano, he is a former merchant mariner, maritime expert, knows of what he speaks.\par \par \uc2 All right, want to draw your attention to what happened at the corner of Wall and Broad today, three losing sessions were finally stopped, as the major market averages all advanced today, in the case of the Dow, a stunning 477-point gain. I guess we have one more day of trading on this month and quarter.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Friday, for Good Friday, the markets are closed. But they are putting in a heady performance, not only for the month, but at this rate for the quarter as well. In fact, dating back to 1970, this looks like it could potentially be the best first quarter for stocks since 1971. So we keep an eye on that.\par \par \uc2 Also keeping an eye on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and what's going on there with him. He's in a Los Angeles courtroom, not he, but his lawyer is, to try to argue with dropping some charges.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Jonathan Hunt, who's there -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 The hearing just got under way without \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . He has decided for some reason to skip what is a critical hearing as he attempts to get all the tax charges against him dismissed.\par \par \uc2 After the break, we will tell you what he's arguing and how the prosecution is responding -- coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, want to take you to Los Angeles right now, where \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's attorney, Abbe Lowell, is filing motions at this hour to remove those tax charges from the various other complaints against the president's son.\par \par \uc2 I don't know where that stands, but I have a feeling that Jonathan Hunt does -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Neil, the hearing got under way just about 12 or 13 minutes ago.\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has decided to skip it, somewhat surprisingly to many legal observers, given that this is a critical hearing in which his attorneys are trying to get all of the nine tax-related charges against him dismissed. His lead attorney, Abbe Lowell, arrived here just about an hour ago, alone this time.\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was here for the first hearing back in January. Now, Lowell is going to argue that this case against \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is -- quote -- "selective and vindictive and is politically motivated." He's also going to argue that a plea deal that Biden had struck with prosecutors that was rejected by a judge last summer should actually still be binding on the prosecution.\par \par \uc2 In his motion, Abbe Lowell wrote -- quote -- "As part of that agreement, he," Biden, "sacrificed valuable rights in exchange for the prosecution's agreement not to prosecute the very sort of indictment that it has brought here. This court should require the prosecution honor its agreement and dismiss the indictment."\par \par \uc2 Now, special prosecutor David Weiss has said that that agreement was rejected and therefore doesn't apply anymore. He wrote in his response to the Biden filing -- quote -- "Because the agreement never went into effect, the government was free to withdraw it, which it did in writing on August 9, 2023. Therefore, the defendant's motion to dismiss the indictment returned by the grand jury is meritless and should be denied."\par \par \uc2 So now it will be up to Judge Scarsi to hear the arguments today. He has just said, according to our producer in the courtroom, Lee Ross, that he is going to consider carefully each one of the eight motions filed by Biden's legal team. So we're likely to be here for a couple of hours at least, I would think, Neil.\par \par \uc2 If ultimately Judge Scarsi throws out those motions to dismiss and rules the trial should go ahead, it is scheduled to begin on June 20, height of election campaign season, and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , by the way, could face up to 17 years in prison -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Jonathan, thank you very much. I look forward to going back to you.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest, though, on the implications of this with John Yoo, back with us, the former deputy assistant attorney general, Sol Wisenberg, the former deputy independent counsel, contributors both.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 John Yoo, to you on what you think goes down here. Let's say those tax charges are not removed. Nothing's changed then, right?\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: I think you're going to see a trial start in June. Hunter's bringing two kinds of challenges.\par \par \uc2 One, he is saying selective prosecution, that all these political pressures on the prosecutors caused them to bring charges against Hunter when he shouldn't have been charged. Neil, that's exactly the same kind of claim that President Trump is trying to raise and losing on repeatedly too. That's because these selective prosecution claims never really work.\par \par \uc2 Judges are very, very reluctant to ask, why did the Justice Department charge this person, but not that person? And then his second claim, Hunter's second claim, that, oh, the plea bargain has to be obeyed, I should be given immunity, I'm not sure that works either because, as we remember, the judge in Wilmington, Delaware, rejected that plea bargain, said there was something fishy about it.\par \par \uc2 And so the judge didn't approve it. I don't see why it should go into effect and why Hunter should get the benefit of that plea bargain.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sol, I assume it's the same judge. It's Mark Scarsi, right?\par \par \uc2 SOL WISENBERG, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No, no, the judge that Jonathan is talking about was the judge in Delaware, because, remember, originally, it was supposed to be a deal, a diversion agreement and a plea agreement that settled everything.\par \par \uc2 But I think it's important to note that the diversion, it's -- what the defense is claiming, what \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's lawyers are claiming is, it's not the original plea agreement that is no longer in effect, but it's the diversion agreement that was signed. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's people say it was in effect, it didn't require the judge to approve it. And, in fact, diversion agreements are typically not approved by the judge.\par \par \uc2 The prosecution is countered and said, yes, but it did depend upon the probation officer approving it and she refused to approve it. That's really the issue there. I think it's probably the best of all of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's motions to dismiss. I don't think it'll win, but I don't think it's a slam dunk for the prosecution by any means.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, John Yoo, the fact that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  isn't there and we're told that his absence wasn't explained, does that mean anything?\par \par \uc2 YOO: I'm sure he's a busy guy. He's got a lot of other investigations to respond to too.\par \par \uc2 I think when you really want to see him, of course, is when the trial actually starts. He has to be there. These kinds of pretrial motions, they don't really have to be there. And, actually, we have seen President Trump go to a lot of these. He didn't have to go to those either. The thing that Trump did is he somehow used those appearances to his own political benefit even while it was legally killing him.\par \par \uc2 Maybe Hunter took the lesson from that and decided I'm not going to show up in court. I don't want to cause trouble. I don't want to make my legal case worse off.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm just wanting to have far this goes then, Sol.\par \par \uc2 One of the key arguments is that, in any of these probes, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has said there's been no evidence that the president, his father, was personally involved.\par \par \uc2 I mean, is anything at stake here bring us to any clarity there?\par \par \uc2 WISENBERG: No, because it's very clear that the assistant U.S. attorney in charge of the investigation didn't want -- as the whistle-blowers have shown and said and supported with some