ong Un\uc2  for a job well done.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, I think they see that people are turning against the abuse of government authority and the use of it against the people. And they want to try to resurrect -- you know, try to protect them. But, Jimmy, we have to get to this story because this is right in your wheelhouse. One of the royals has a new business venture. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  decided it was the right moment to take to \uc2 Instagram\uc2  to launch her new lifestyle brand, American Riviera Orchard.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Now, the trademark registration for Meghan's new brand promises things like tableware, cookware, food, textiles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jimmy, is this the new goop called dupe?\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Do you know that scientists actually have a word for people who buy \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2 's brand? They're called idiots. OK, this is such a grift. American Riviera Orchard. What is that? It sounds like an expensive candle that will upset my dog when he smells it because they've blended some pretentious scent.\par \par \uc2 But there's this part of me that respects her because she's gotten rich off stupid people. She's like the Bernie Sanders of celebrities, and that she's making money playing some sort of a victim.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Jimmy, great to see you as always. And I know you're going to be buying her freshly minted candles. Catch Jimmy on the road. He'll be in Montana. Lots of cool places. Follow me on social media. Jesse is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240320ek3j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Bernie Sanders Pushes For 32-Hour Workweek; Gerald Levin Dies; Potential TikTok Bidders?; Interview With Sen. \uc2 Mark Warner\uc2  (D-VA); Senate Pressure Builds to Pass Potential TikTok Ban; Interview With Latvian Prime Minister Evika Silina; Interview With Fmr. Rep. Bob Barr (R-GA); \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Awaiting Decisions in Multiple Cases\b0\par\par\uc2 Chad Pergram, Gary Kaltbaum, Aishah Hasnie, Charlie Gasparino,  Steve Harrigan, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,616 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 Let us be brief. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is collecting a lot of briefs in Florida, where a judge is being asked to dismiss the classified documents case, in Georgia, where yet another judge is deciding if \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  should be booted from that election interference case, and, in New York, where no less than the district attorney is requesting more time to prepare that hush money case.\par \par \uc2 So many cases, so little time.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Hard to be brief when you have all of this going on.\par \par \uc2 Let's first go to Steve Harrigan in Fort Pierce, Florida, with the latest on what happened in that courtroom.\par \par \uc2 We understand the former president left just a short time ago -- Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That's right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Things here just wrapped up about one hour ago. This is the documents case, those documents taken to Mar-a-Lago, Trump and two of his aides accused of mishandling those federal documents. There's been no ruling yet, but it looks like the judge gave a pretty strong hint, the judge who was appointed by Trump, that it won't be dismissed.\par \par \uc2 It was \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  and former President Trump in court today for several hours really facing off. No exchanges between the two were seen. Trump's attorneys really tried to make the argument that what Trump did with those documents is no different than presidents going back to George Washington and the way they took documents, tapes, records after office.\par \par \uc2 The prosecution said, Trump was an exception,that he knew what he did was wrong, and that he repeatedly failed to return those documents to the National Archives. There's one key moment that happened today, Neil. And that's Judge Cannon, who was appointed by Trump, basically said to the defense attorneys, what you're saying might be strong in a trial, but I can't see how that gets me to dismissal.\par \par \uc2 So a pretty strong hint from the judge that she's not going to go for the dismissal that Trump and his lawyers wanted today -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you for that, Steve Harrigan.\par \par \uc2 Now to Jonathan Serrie in Atlanta and still waiting and wondering about the fate of one \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us, Jonathan?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, we're still waiting to hear a decision from the judge, who has said earlier that he intends to keep himself honest to a self-imposed deadline to render a decision by this Friday.\par \par \uc2 He said that to local radio station WSB in an interview last week. Judge Scott McAfee, who was appointed by Governor Brian Kemp just over a year ago, assured listeners his decision \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  will not be influenced by the contested special election he faces in may to keep his seat.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE SCOTT MCAFEE, SUPERIOR COURT OF FULTON COUNTY, GEORGIA: I have had a rough draft and an outline before I ever heard a rumor that someone wanted to run for this position. So the result is not going to change because of politics.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Several defense attorneys are calling on the judge to disqualify district attorney Willis from prosecuting the case, claiming that her romantic relationship with special prosecutor Nathan Wade created financial and professional conflicts of interest.\par \par \uc2 Wade and Willis claimed the relationship started after the appointment of the special prosecutor and had no bearing on the case. Again, Neil, we are still awaiting the judge's ruling, which will come down no later than Friday evening if he holds himself to his own deadline -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, got it.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Serrie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 That third case I just wanted to allude to concerns the Manhattan district attorney, who has asked for a delay in the start of the trial in the hush money case involving \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because of new evidence, apparently, we're told, thousands of documents. That is again coming from the U.S. attorney's office in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Here to weigh in on all of this is Bob Barr, the former Georgia Congressman, former U.S. attorney.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 That's a lot of cases, a lot of moving and juggling around of those cases. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. BOB BARR (R-GA): The one thing that the Republicans can always count on that they can take to the bank regarding Democrats is, Democrats will overreach.\par \par \uc2 And that's what they have done over and over again here in their effort to get Trump. The case in New York, the case in Florida, the case here in Georgia most especially is an overreach that never should have reached this particular stage.\par \par \uc2 And I think the prosecutor here, Ms. Willis, yesterday and Judge McAfee's ruling, that sort of was overshadowed by the conflict of interest matters, but his ruling striking six counts in the indictment that was brought against President Trump and others, because it is on its face defective.\par \par \uc2 It does not put the defendants properly on notice so that they can defend themselves. This is pretty much basically criminal law 101, but darned if she didn't do it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In the meantime, we're learning a little bit more about this New York situation where prosecutors said they were open to delaying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's New York hush money trial. It could be upwards of a month.\par \par \uc2 And some of the reasons are striking, Bob, that they wanted to give the former president's lawyers time to review evidence that's only recently been turned over. We're told it's quite a few documents' worth of evidence, potentially in the thousands. So what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 BARR: It's unusual at this late stage in a criminal case for the prosecution to all of a sudden discover thousands of documents. It raises questions about where they came from, their veracity, their validity, and so forth.\par \par \uc2 So, certainly, under basic rules of criminal procedure, the defense needs adequate opportunity to review those documents. I don't think that this is the sort of thing that is going to help Mr. Bragg, because it inevitably will delay this case, which is sort of a mess in and of itself, even further.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, in the Florida -- in the documents case there, the judge, of course, a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appointee, indicated that there could be a delay, and likely will be, but it's not going to be a trial canceled or called off or shut down. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 BARR: That's not surprising.\par \par \uc2 Federal courts often will sort of telegraph, federal judges, that is, telegraph their view of a case at a preliminary motions phase. And it's unusual for a judge to completely dismiss a well-crafted, which this is, a well-crafted indictment...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARR: ... simply because the defense says, well, the laws are being improperly applied or the espionage law doesn't apply or whatnot.\par \par \uc2 So I suspect that that case at least will go forward. And the main problem, as I see it for President Trump there, is not so much the case in chief as it is the obstruction charges.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We were just showing, Congressman, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leaving the courthouse in Florida.\par \par \uc2 He's attended a few of these events, more of the civil cases, the one involving E. Jean Carroll. He was there for the finance ones that came up in New York. So he picks and chooses where he can go. Obviously, it's pressing when you have so many cases to go to.\par \par \uc2 But his presence, at least in some of those other cases, didn't help at all. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Well, overall, though, it's not really hurting him. He is a master of, if anything, the art of the deal and the publicity that one can turn from detrimental to positive.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, I'm sorry I wasn't clear. You're right. Politically, he's capitalizing on all of this, as people sense, even those who might not flip over him, that this does look like a pile-on.\par \par \uc2 But it didn't mitigate any of the financial punishments thrown at him that he's now having to deal with. That was whether he was there in the courthouse or not. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Judges are not swayed, at least every judge that I have ever been before, judges are not swayed by whether or not a party shows up or not.\par \par \uc2 Now, if a party is in the middle of a trial and all of a sudden exits themselves in the middle of a trial, that can be problematic for the court and for the jury. But simply showing up at different phases, a motions hearing or whatnot, or a punishment phase, a judge isn't going to be swayed by that.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump is really playing to the camera than doing it very well, but it's not going to affect the judges' decisions.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Bob Barr, very good catching up with you on all these myriad of cases.\par \par \uc2 They were fast-moving parts today. We will keep you posted on those, especially if we get any decision out of Atlanta on the \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  situation.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, you have probably heard that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is featured in an election this week that you might find stunning. He's heavily favored to win and get another six-year term. But the prime minister of Latvia has had some rather choice things to say about \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , including one comment that caught my attention, that he's like an unpredictable alcoholic.\par \par \uc2 She states her mind, and she's not afraid to do so. And she's here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, well, stunner of stunners, it looks like \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has just won another six years in office. If he completes his full term, that would be 30 years as the most powerful figure in Russia, and some say the most threatening figure in the world right now.\par \par \uc2 Evika Silina joins us, the prime minister of Latvia, a frequent critic of the Russian president. Of course, Latvia, a former Soviet republic, is now a \uc2 NATO\uc2  member, a member of the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . The prime minister kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister, thank you.\par \par \uc2 EVIKA SILINA, LATVIAN PRIME MINISTER: Hello.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you make of this? \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  back in power. It seems he's got a lock on that power, and now he is intimidating with comments on going nuclear if you or any of your fellow \uc2 NATO\uc2  members dare send troops into Ukraine. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SILINA: I believe that we, as \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries, have to stick together, and that's so important that the United States can make a very brave decision of helping Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And I'm here actually to express my gratitude to Ukrainians who are not just fighting for us, but they're fighting for Europeans and Americans as well. And Russia is stealing Ukrainian babies, stealing Ukrainian children. So we just can't ignore \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's attitude towards democratic countries.\par \par \uc2 And he doesn't like democracies. And, yes, I have been quoted that I have expressed my opinions that to live with Russia and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's autocratic regime in neighborhood, it's like living with an alcoholic in your neighbor.\par \par \uc2 And it's very dangerous...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SILINA: ... because you never know what will be next.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think of the rants he has made and that you liken to an angry alcoholic, I mean, or being at least unpredictable, because, a number of times now, he has mentioned this nuclear response to an aggressive \uc2 NATO\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 You share about a 133-mile border with Russia. Does that concern you?\par \par \uc2 SILINA: Sure. It always will concern us, because we know for years how it is to live near, in neighboring country who is threatening us every day.\par \par \uc2 We have been threatened by weaponization of migration. We have built a fence with Russia and Belarus. We actually will finish the fence with Russia. And three -- both states have agreed on military defense line within all the borderline with Russia and Belarus. So we are preparing ourselves.\par \par \uc2 We understand that we have to fight for each centimeter of our \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I just -- but I wonder, because, when you said that, I think of you and Estonia and Lithuania. You could be next in Moscow's agenda here.\par \par \uc2 He's been particularly critical of you. And I just wonder. If he were to succeed in Ukraine, is it your concern, is the message you're sending here in the United States and elsewhere that \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries could be next, countries like yours?\par \par \uc2 SILINA: We cannot afford him to win Ukraine, because, if he wins Ukraine, then Russia will see democratic states as much weaker power.\par \par \uc2 And that's why I'm here trying to negotiate and discuss and explain to our good international and transatlantic strategic partner U.S. that we cannot let Russia win. And this war can still be win over. And I believe that's why it's very important now act very fast and to help Ukraine to win this.\par \par \uc2 Because, yes, he is threatening us, but we all are \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries. And it can affect each of us. We don't know who can be next, because those threats are not very particular. Those threats are too \uc2 NATO\uc2 , as such.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now what do you make of -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has talked about this. He has praised \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in the past. But he has also intimated that \uc2 NATO\uc2  must do more of its part and especially beef up its commitment to defense.\par \par \uc2 And I'm wondering, when he added, and he said it was more for effect, or that was the impression, that Russia should go ahead and invade those countries that don't put up that 2 percent, what did you make of that? Are you worried, if he becomes president, he will stick to that and be loyal more to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SILINA: I believe that each European country has understood that the situation in our geopolitical territory has changed, and we have to do more in our military to strengthen our military capabilities.\par \par \uc2 And I believe, after elections in Europe, there will be a new defense commissioner, because we, as Europeans, we have to defend ourselves. We cannot just rely on the United States. And that has been on our table for months already that we will do more.\par \par \uc2 And we, as Latvians and Baltics, we are spending almost 3 percent on our defense capabilities. But, without you, without our international allies, there is no easy way to defend, to have those military artillery or military equipment, what we really need in case what will happen next, what can happen next.\par \par \uc2 That's why we need to stick together. And if we are together and we have united decisions made, then we are very strong. We have more capabilities of defense than Russia has now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Prime Minister, you weren't at all troubled by some of the comments that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has made here?\par \par \uc2 And, again, they might have been for effect, to your earlier point, but that he could end this in a day, that if he came into office, he could wrap this up immediately? Some interpreted that as a sign that he would let \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  win or keep the land that he's already gotten. What did you think?\par \par \uc2 SILINA: I believe that we wish good results in your elections. We understand that this is very turbulent times before elections, and there are many different public opinions coming out of candidates.\par \par \uc2 But I believe that United States has been an example to Latvia, to Baltic states. And you always have been a strong power of holding democracy and liberties alive. I don't think any president of United States can turn down those values that you have been holding for hundreds of years.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, rhetorics can be harsh, but I believe those values will prevail.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, real quickly, the pope kind of sounded a lot like, let's get on and move on, and Ukraine maybe should make peace with Russia. What did you make of that?\par \par \uc2 SILINA: I think that Russia understands the language of power. And we have to speak with Russia out of sense, out of position and platform of power.\par \par \uc2 And there cannot be very polite diplomatic negotiations going on, because Russia will use that as a weakness. That's why it's very important for us to be united, coming out of \uc2 NATO\uc2  with united decisions. And we have to speak as powerful democratic nations.\par \par \uc2 I understand the pope is always wanting the peace, but there could be peace only when Ukraine wins this war.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Prime Minister, thank you very much. I know you have a busy visit here and we appreciate your stopping by.\par \par \uc2 Evika Silina, the prime minister of Latvia, a \uc2 NATO\uc2  member and part of the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We have a lot more coming up, including the fight over TikTok. You know how it was overwhelming in the House to go ahead and send a message to the Chinese, divest, the parent company of TikTok, at least, or we shut this puppy down. Now what happens in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, TikTok's CEO is making the rounds here in the Senate, trying to kill this bill. I got to chat with him as he left the building. Wait until you hear what he tells me when I asked why ByteDance won't sell to save the app.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Looks like the third time was a charm for \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 's Starship, completing its third test flight, its most successful yet, even though it did fall into the atmosphere and didn't survive reentry.\par \par \uc2 The impact of this -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the latest on the future of TikTok and what happens now.\par \par \uc2 We will be talking to Senator \uc2 Mark Warner\uc2  about all of that.\par \par \uc2 First to Aishah Hasnie on the push now in the Senate to do what the House just did.\par \par \uc2 But it's not that easy is it, Aishah?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: No, it's not, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the bill isn't going anywhere. Leader Schumer has basically said that they're going to review the text, which really gives TikTok an opportunity right now to come in and try to stall or kill the bill altogether.\par \par \uc2 And CEO \uc2 Shou Chew\uc2  was actually making the rounds here on Capitol Hill today talking with senators. He was visiting with Senator Fetterman's office. I caught wind of that and I was standing outside the office. He saw me, came outside and made his pitch to try to kill the bill. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHOU ZI CHEW, CEO, TIKTOK: There's a lot of misinformation out there, and I intend to clarify it. This is a ban bill.\par \par \uc2 In this form, if it's passed into law, this is a ban on the app in this country. It's going to impact 170 million Americans who use our app. It's going to impact seven million small businesses, and I hope their voices are heard.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The lawmakers are saying that this is not a ban, that you're spreading misinformation, sir.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, again, this bill does not ban TikTok. It's merely requiring TikTok to divest, basically asking ByteDance to sell.\par \par \uc2 And so I chased him out into his car and I asked him, why won't ByteDance just sell?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Sir, why won't ByteDance just sell the company? That would avoid a ban. Why wouldn't you just sell?\par \par \uc2 CHEW: The bill is 12 pages' long. We have looked at it. It is not feasible to do whatever the bill thinks it does within the parameters set down in the bill.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: To divest the company?\par \par \uc2 CHEW: So, this bill, in all the details -- you can go through the details -- this is -- this will lead to the banning of the app in the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, again, he says it's simply just not feasible.\par \par \uc2 Now, Senator Fetterman says that he's inclined to vote for the House bill. He told us this about his conversation with Chew.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): I expressed my frustrations and concerns and how that's become more personalized for me and within my family. And I addressed my concerns, and I do believe he heard them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, again, right now, it's not going anywhere in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And the longer it stalls in the Senate, Neil, really, the chances of it passing or even being put on the floor go down -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Aishah, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, if they do eventually find a way to find a buyer for a TikTok, it would be a big, big takeover and probably the richest of all time. Right now, that honor, if you call it that, is held by \uc2 Time Warner\uc2  and AOL when they got together.\par \par \uc2 The architect of that megamerger -- nothing has been bigger since -- Jerry Levin, has passed away. The message from that time and Jerry Levin -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: At the time, it was the biggest merger in American history and later on would be called the biggest debacle. And now there's talk that TikTok could break that record if someone bids for that company.\par \par \uc2 But what I'm talking about is the merger Time Warner at America Online, or AOL, and it was supposed to be a marriage of the future. It was anything but.\par \par \uc2 Gerald Levin put that together, the key architect of that undertaking, where his message was, this is a new age. He would later say it wasn't quite the age he envisioned. He passed away at 84, but not before leaving an imprint on corporate America and just how far you can go to pair industries that on paper might look good, but oftentimes are anything but.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you regret that?\par \par \uc2 GERALD LEVIN, ARCHITECT OF AOL-TIME WARNER MERGER: No, I don't regret the concept underlying the transaction.\par \par \uc2 I obviously regret the aftermath. But there was a guiding principle, which was really a digital makeover of an older media company. And that's still going on today. It's interesting how you can go from the deal of a century to a disaster.\par \par \uc2 What I would say is that, and not in any form of defense or reformed legacy, but what was happening then and what really undermined that transaction was the rolling thunder coming from the Internet on demand entitlement for lots of programming basically free. That thunder is still rolling through the media environment from music to television to publishing at this point.\par \par \uc2 The most important thing, and particularly when we have a lot of the chaos in our financial institutions over the last several years, is to recognize that, when you are the CEO, there's -- there is no blame. There's no blame for exogenous circumstances, no blame for predecessors, successors, or bankers, lawyers, colleagues.\par \par \uc2 It's just to stand up and say, I really did do it and I regret what happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Jerry Levin, of course, the head of Time Warner and the architect of what was at the time the largest merger in American history and it has been since.\par \par \uc2 Of course, a lot of eyes on TikTok. If it were put up for sale and any company -- and they mentioned a bunch of them, from \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , to \uc2 Meta\uc2 , to \uc2 Oracle\uc2  -- all could make very, very lofty, heady bids that would eclipse that.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 I don't know quite how the TikTok thing goes, but I do know how this one went down and how it was viewed. And you knew and wrote about all the principal players. What did you think?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: I really liked Jerry Levin.\par \par \uc2 I got to know him better after the deal imploded and he was a board member of the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2 . And what I really -- what I found great about Jerry Levin, when he left business, the media business -- and he was the king of all media for a time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Let's not -- let's be real clear.\par \par \uc2 He got into wellness. I mean, he had some tragedy when his son was murdered, tragically murdered, this deal huge impact on his life. And instead of doing something bad to himself, he got into self-help and meditation and yoga and believing in how to repair broken people through holistic measures.\par \par \uc2 And I got to know him a little bit, and he was still a tough guy. And when Eliot Spitzer called him in to take a deposition from him, because, remember, Eliot Spitzer was suing Dick Grasso for making too much money. It was one of the dumbest suits against the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2 , because it kind of fell into a nonprofit category, even though it paid all its taxes. It was a really dumb suit.\par \par \uc2 Jerry Levin looked him in the eye and said, let me tell you something. You're only doing this for a higher office. He went in there. He lambasted Spitzer's prosecutors. He went in there without a lawyer and not wearing a suit and told them basically to go you-know-what themselves.\par \par \uc2 I saw it. I read the transcripts. I saw the deposition. And that said something to me. Like, this guy was a real player and a straight shooter. Now, the deal itself, let's be real clear here. This deal is the template for the future. He was just wickedly early. And we had...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is it a template for a TikTok deal, if it's forced to sell? There's no guarantee that would even happen, by the way.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: OK.\par \par \uc2 Neil, it's a template for TikTok. It's a template for, which has to sell itself to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to survive.\par \par \uc2 It's a template for Paramount that would die to be bought by \uc2 Apple\uc2  right now. Big media -- big tech has the platforms, needs the content and, right now, it's at a stage where it's way beyond where AOL was. It can deliver the content and make money off it.\par \par \uc2 The only thing stopping this is essentially the Biden administration, which is -- hates mergers. But it is the template for everybody. And it was interesting that when -- after -- when AOL Time Warner happened, Jack Welch -- I know this for a fact -- called everybody in his office, including Bob Wright, former head of \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2 , which was then part of GE, and said, we just got slammed, the future is here.\par \par \uc2 When the deal didn't work out, it basically scared everybody from doing deals. \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2  was looking to buy \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . Bob Wright didn't press the button on that because of this deal.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: But this sort of merger and consolidation is the only way our business survives in the long term.\par \par \uc2 And I'm saying that not just about Discovery, about all of us.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Because that's the way it's going.\par \par \uc2 And Jerry Levin early, haphazardly set the template.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well said. Charlie Gasparino, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We don't know the details and exactly how Mr. Levin died. We do know that he had been battling Parkinson's for some time, but a visionary who just got this particular vision on this particular record takeover slightly out of step. He would say later on it wasn't a matter of being wrong. It was a matter of my underappreciating just what wasn't right.\par \par \uc2 Jerry Levin gone at 84.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you know what happened in the House.\par \par \uc2 In an overwhelming vote that was bipartisan, the move was, TikTok cannot be what it is right now, controlled by China, and that means ByteDance, the parent company of China, must unload it, divest it, as they say on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 But it isn't getting the same reaction in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . Again, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has not even detailed if or even when the Senate will take it up.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Mark Warner\uc2  joins us right now. He is the Senate Intelligence Committee chairman.\par \par \uc2 Senator, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 Do you think the Senate should take up this issue?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARK WARNER\uc2  (D-VA): Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 Neil, I have been on your show many, many times talking about the national security threat that is posed by having a platform that 170 million Americans use on average 90 minutes a day. China is collecting this data about lots of Americans.\par \par \uc2 And what is even more problematic for me is, the genius of TikTok is, it knows what you like before you know what you like. And a lot of young people get all their news. They could switch the algorithm a little bit and suddenly all the TikTok videos will be promoting that Taiwan ought to be part of China, or that Putin's right...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: ... on getting Ukraine. And I think...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, all these examples you raised, you obviously eloquently put the key arguments here.\par \par \uc2 But it doesn't look like \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  either agrees or sees the need to do something right now.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, that could change. Is it your understanding that it will and the Senate will take up the matter?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, listen, Neil, I know Senate never moves quickly on anything.\par \par \uc2 But my friends in the House, that was a huge vote, 352 votes. It was just yesterday. I think, Schumer, I have had preliminary conversations. Chair Cantwell on the Commerce Committee is going to have views. There may be things that need to be slightly altered or amended.\par \par \uc2 But I think anyone who cares about -- we have plenty of divisions in our country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: We ought to be able to argue amongst ourselves, left and right, Republican, Democrat. We don't need the Chinese Communist Party dominating or influencing.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, the sheer size of that vote, the sheer size of that vote in the House would maybe -- has maybe changed the thinking in the Senate, as far as you...\par \par \uc2 WARNER: I think so.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: I would say so.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So let me ask you about that then, Senator.\par \par \uc2 One other idea that's been bandied about, if ByteDance were to go ahead and divest itself of TikTok, no sure thing, that TikTok would essentially be for sale one way or the other. A lot of American names have come into play here. \uc2 Oracle\uc2 's name comes up, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 , of course, the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  parent.\par \par \uc2 Do you have any concerns with any of those names?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, I have concerns about too much concentration, if this was acquired by another social media company.\par \par \uc2 And, frankly, that's all of our preference. If you like TikTok, if you're a social influencer on that, you want to be, and you make your living that way, that's great with me. It just ought to be a company that's not controlled by China.\par \par \uc2 So I was really glad to see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Treasury Secretary \uc2 Steve Mnuchin\uc2  put out word today that he was trying to put together a group of investors that could potentially buy this application. I think that he'd be great. He was one of the guys that first educated me on this issue.\par \par \uc2 And I know I have said this. I don't say this often, even on FOX, but, on TikTok, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was right years ago in saying it was a national security threat. Now, he's changed his tune a little bit now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: But his initial indication on this as a national security threat was right. And I think it would be great if a group of investors were to buy this.\par \par \uc2 So the service could still be extended. People could still get to see all the crazy and fun videos, but, ultimately, it would be with American or European or somebody other than Chinese ownership.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, it doesn't quite cut black and white, right, Senator? I mean, you mentioned \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  changing his mind on this, that maybe we don't get rid of it for the time being or push to get rid of it.\par \par \uc2 But it is a hot political issue, or could be, right? Because 170 million Americans use this.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Lopsidedly, they're young, and they don't want it to go away.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that they might get ticked off and take it out on politicians who do push to have it go away.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: I hear it. And that's why I say, let's not have it go away. Let's just not have the \uc2 Communist Party of China\uc2  pulling its strings.\par \par \uc2 I think...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what do you -- how do you react when young people say, they don't care, Senator?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: But...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They figure that everyone spies on them when they're online. It's not forgivable, don't get me wrong, but that they don't draw the distinction China doing it versus an American company doing it, as you're still being spied on.\par \par \uc2 How do you react to that? How do you talk to them?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, I would react a couple of ways.\par \par \uc2 One, that funny or inappropriate video two, five years from now, if somebody's trying to blackmail you from the Chinese spy services, I don't think you're going to want that to happen. And even if they don't care about the propaganda purposes, we would never let the Chinese Communist Party buy FOX News or MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 The idea that they have this propaganda channel that can affect Americans' views, again, we got plenty to fight about amongst ourselves.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Let's not turn the reins over.\par \par \uc2 And one of the reasons that I think that something will happen is that we have done nothing on social media for years. I mean, the fact that we don't even have any kids online safety, again, broad bipartisan support for that, if we can't at least start with something that is this pervasive, controlled by an adversary of the United States, then all the things that folks think about Washington are true.\par \par \uc2 But I got a lot of hope; 352 people in the House, I didn't think you would get 352 House members to agree on anything.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, you're quite right about that. You're quite right about it.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you. You were mentioning the possibility how would we react to the Chinese where -- you first mentioned FOX News and MSNBC? Which would worry you more, if the Chinese bought FOX or they bought MSNBC?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: You know, I'm an equal opportunity abuser of the extremes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WARNER: And you're not on one of the extremes.\par \par \uc2 But the extreme voices on either end of the political agenda scare the heck out of me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you that about -- well, finally, I was mentioning \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  before, Senator, but he did mention and surprised some people, urging that there be new elections in Israel, doesn't like what he's seen and heard out of \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A lot of people interpreted that, even on your side of the aisle, that's a bit intrusive and a bit much for a U.S. senator to call for elections in a sovereign country. Do you think he went too far?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Oh, listen, I'm a lifelong supporter of Israel.\par \par \uc2 I am horribly concerned, because of some of the activities of the current Israeli government, that we could be losing a generation of Americans in terms of support for Israel. And Israel needs American support for its survival and for its success.\par \par \uc2 I'm not going to weigh in on who the Israelis should choose as their leader, but I would point out that \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2  was never shy about trying to interfere in American politics, particularly when \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was president.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So you don't have a problem with what \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  did?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Listen, I'm not going to tell a foreign nation who their leader should be.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: I do think, if the polls in Israel show that he would not get voted back in -- but I'm not going to do that.\par \par \uc2 But I would say that the way that Prime Minister Netanyahu dealt with Obama and, frankly, the way he's dealt with Secretary Blinken, Israel's strongest ally in the last couple of months, has not been with respect.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will watch it very closely.\par \par \uc2 Senator Warner, very good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Mark Warner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Well, forget weekend at Bernie's. How about enjoying a longer weekend because of Bernie? What he wants to do that you might like, maybe your boss might not like.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: It seems like Democrats want businesses to be taxed more, pay their workers...\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): Really? Is that what you think?\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Pay their workers...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Excuse me.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: I didn't get to ask a question.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Excuse me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right work 32 hours in a week, get paid for 40, that's pretty much the idea coming from the Vermont Senator Bernie Sanders.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram has more.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon. Less work, but the same pay.\par \par \uc2 Sanders argues that people should get more time off. He claims automation and A.I. will increase productivity. Sanders says people are working more for less pay.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: How are businesses going to survive that? That's the question. How can businesses survive all of those proposals?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: When Mr. Bezos pays an effective tax rate lower than the average worker, I think we have a real problem in our tax system.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: One witness testified that workers are sharper and more refreshed after a three-day weekend. But Republicans say the government should not tell private companies what to do.\par \par \uc2 The top GOP member on the labor committee, \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 , said the plan is like throwing -- quote -- "napalm on inflation."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): Government should not be in the business of undermining an employer's ability to keep their doors open with unreasonable and perhaps unconstitutional mandates.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Those opposed to a 32-hour week say companies must backfill for the remaining eight hours. That drives up costs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROGER KING, SENIOR LABOR AND EMPLOYMENT COUNSEL, HR POLICY ASSOCIATION: If you can't fill the productivity gap by cutting back hours or making some adjustments to your business, you pass on added costs to the consumer. You have to pay for it some way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Now, King said the market should decide what's the best number of hours on the job. Democrat Chris Murphy said there's a decline in people going to church because they're spending too much time at work -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Chad Pergram, thank you.\par \par \uc2 I wonder what Gary Kaltbaum, a good read of all things economic and financial, thinks about the impact of that?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Gary, what do you think of this?\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: And to think these same people are against shrinkflation at the supermarket, but not in hours.\par \par \uc2 Look, just to take the business side, you're telling those greedy business owners who don't pay enough that you only have 32 hours a week now, but you're going to pay them the same amount. Simple equation, there's going to be less employees, less productivity, less profits, and away we go the other way.\par \par \uc2 The other side of the equation, what do you tell the employees? Work less. Instead of reaching for the sky, reach for the sidewalk instead. And all I can tell you, just nothing good comes of this. You're dealing with one man who basically has never been in the business world, he's been in politics his whole life, trying to mandate what business has to pay and what to do.\par \par \uc2 Just absolutely crazy, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I will put you down as a maybe on this plan.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Gary, the one thing I thought, when I first heard about this, I said, oh, they want to mandate or look at mandating four-day workweek, so the 40 hours would be spread out over four days, and then I heard about the 32-hour thing, and I immediately went to my boss and said, sign me up. He threw me out of the office.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So I'm just wondering how companies would deal with this, because you might have a lot of people in a four-day workweek, but they're only working 32 hours.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Cost goes up, productivity goes down, and profits go down.\par \par \uc2 As I said, it's just -- it's the worst of all worlds. You have got to let market forces do their job. I think the businesses of this country are doing pretty darn well, Neil. I mean, the growth of the GDP over the last 20 years have been fantastic without any interference.\par \par \uc2 Just leave it well enough alone. I still remember when our total GDP was $15 trillion. It's like $25 trillion right now. Just stay out of the way, leave us be, all will be fine, go away.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, and, by the way, that $25 trillion is dwarfed by the $33 trillion, almost $34 trillion in debt we have, so we're in knee-deep in this stuff. That would get worse, wouldn't it?\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Don't get me started on the debt, Neil. This latest spending proposal will take us to $2 trillion to $3 trillion of debt every year going forward and grow.\par \par \uc2 And just remember, that's with the prediction that we're going to grow 3 percent every year. If we ever go into recession again, I can't imagine what the numbers are going to be. And my biggest worry, and I have said this to you before, I'm worried one day the bond market's going to wake up and look out, yields are going to spike, and then all heck breaks loose. I hope that does not happen.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, well, we will see. It's still early on. It's what's thrown out there. But, again, I mean, the idea for a lot of companies that are doing well, you can have a Bernie Sanders say you're doing so well, you can afford to do this. So we will watch it closely.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Gary Kaltbaum.\par \par \uc2 Want to draw your attention to the corner of Wall and Broad, where the Dow was selling off today. We had a pretty stubborn inflation report. But, by and large, we also have some other stubborn numbers that have people thinking, maybe we got too far ahead of ourselves.\par \par \uc2 We're going to explore all of this and worker demands with the \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  co-founder \uc2 Ken Langone\uc2 . I wonder what he thinks, back then in those days in the '70s, when he was coming up with \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , if he had the mandate to pay for 40 hours for workers who were actually going to work only 32 hours. Wonder what he would think of that. He's pretty frank, has some saucy language.\par \par \uc2 We will talk to him tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240314ek3e0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Jon Taffer; Biden, Trump Head to Georgia; Interview With Former U.S. Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 ; Biden Delivers Fiery State of the Union Address; Interview With U.S. Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 ; Strong Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Green, Bryan Llenas, Mike Pompeo, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,818 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, the campaign season ramping up and taxpayers now buckling up as President Biden is teeing up some more spending.\par \par \uc2 Why Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  says it will keep the economy humming. He is here. And so is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's former Secretary of State, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , remember him, who could be in the running, maybe, maybe, maybe for a new title, a bigger title.\par \par \uc2 And we're on it, all of it.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. Glad to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 Let's go first to Grady Trimble at the White House on these fast-moving developments and the president's fast-moving plans to take advantage of what was a widely received and well-received State of the Union address.\par \par \uc2 To you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It was, Neil.\par \par \uc2 At a time when many Americans are not happy with the president -- President Biden's handling of the economy, he used last night's address to try to change those perceptions, touting billions of dollars of student loan debt cancellation and unveiling plans for more social programs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We enacted tax credits of $800 per person per year, reduced health care costs for millions of working families.\par \par \uc2 Restore that child tax credit. No child should go hungry in this country.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I want to provide an annual tax credit that will give Americans $400 a month for the next two years as mortgage rates come down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: The president and his administration make the case. Policies like those are good for lower- and middle-class Americans, building the economy from the bottom up and middle out, as the president likes to say frequently.\par \par \uc2 But his critics in the GOP says these freebies are expensive. They argue spending on these types of programs have caused higher prices and higher interest rates that are making the American dream less attainable.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JODEY ARRINGTON (R-TX): He's doubling down. He's pouring gasoline on the fire of inflation. He's going to make our -- the quality of life and standard of living even worse.\par \par \uc2 REP. JASON SMITH (R-MO): He talks about this tax credit to purchase a home. The reason why mortgage interest rates are the highest they have been in more than two decades is because of Biden's economic policies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: If you're wondering how President Biden plans to pay for these programs, he wants to raise taxes, Neil, but he says only the wealthy and corporations will have to pay more.\par \par \uc2 He's going to try to make his pitch to voters with two stops this weekend, campaign events in Pennsylvania -- that one starts in about an hour -- and tomorrow in Georgia -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very much for that, Grady Trimble.\par \par \uc2 Mark Tepper with us right now, \uc2 Strategic Wealth Partners\uc2  president and CEO.\par \par \uc2 We can always play politics with this stuff, Mark. When we look at Republicans, they're hardly saints when it comes to fiscal seriousness. So for them to start questioning the piling on of debt under this president versus the prior president versus the president before that guy, and on and on and on, does seem a little hypocritical.\par \par \uc2 Having said that, though, whoever you cast your lot with, they're casting a lot more money. Does that concern you? Should it concern the markets? Very,\par \par \uc2 MARK TEPPER, \uc2 STRATEGIC WEALTH PARTNERS\uc2 : very concerning.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, we are on an unsustainable fiscal path right now. And all we continue to do is just stick it to future generations. I mean, Neil, we're $34 trillion in debt. We're actually running an additional deficit of a trillion dollars every 100 days.\par \par \uc2 I mean, to your point, we haven't run a budget surplus since 2001. So both sides of the aisle are to blame. But I will say that, I mean, it is unprecedented to run a 6 percent of GDP deficit with the unemployment rate under 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 And I got to tell you, one of the things I heard yesterday out of President Biden is that he said we are the strongest economy in the world. And let me just put something in perspective. In the fourth quarter, GDP grew by about $330 billion. It cost us $830 billion in debt to pay for it. That is unsustainable.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The president made an interesting comment when talking and dealing with hecklers on this spending. This was from last night, Mark.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Thanks to our Bipartisan Infrastructure Law, 46,000 new projects have been announced all across your communities.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, I notice some of you who strongly voted against it are there cheering on that money coming in.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I like it.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm with you.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm with you.\par \par \uc2 And if any of you don't want that money in your district, just let me know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: "Just let me know."\par \par \uc2 Now, I think what he was referencing is, you don't want it, give it back to me. But it's not his to give back one way or the other. But it was an interesting little insight into the thinking in Washington that, whether you're a Republican or Democrat, I think would give you pause.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 And I was just eating lunch a couple hours ago with my dad. And I was talking about why President Biden kept provoking and antagonizing the right and kind of poking the bear. But I got to tell you, with everything that we have seen from President Biden, obviously, Bidenomics is supposed to be growing from the bottom up and middle out.\par \par \uc2 I mean, all he's really done, with all of his policies, is, he's grown the government from the top down while the middle class is bottoming out. It has been horrific for the middle class. It's been even worse if you're lower income, because inflation is the most regressive tax in the world. So these policies, they're just straight-up not working.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I think the White House might make the argument about it is working in terms of the job gains we have seen.\par \par \uc2 We saw a very, very big surge in them in the latest month, 275,000. They argue retail sales are looking better. More people are out there buying. I think you have mentioned in the past that a lot of them putting out on credit. That might be in fact the case, but they argue that, if they're doing so bad, you wouldn't see companies hiring the workers they are.\par \par \uc2 So this is just a messaging issue. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Well, first of all, so the 275,000 jobs, great headline number, but these numbers are -- I will use a Biden term. They're malarkey, right?\par \par \uc2 So, look, I mean, first things first, there's a lot of guesswork that goes into these numbers. They're not looking at actual table data.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Did you say that when these had...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, wait a minute, Mark. Did you say that -- I'm not an apologist of any way -- when these kind of numbers were popping up in the Trump administration?\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They are what they are.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Correct. That is true.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: But what we have seen out of this administration is, I think nine out of the 12 months last year and two out of the first three months this year, they come out with a really excellent headline number.\par \par \uc2 And then the next month, when no one's paying attention anymore, there's a steep downward revision. We just saw a downward revision of negative- 167,000. Beyond that, Neil, if you look at what happened last month, we lost 187,000 full-time jobs last month. All the job gains are coming from part-time jobs.\par \par \uc2 So, from my standpoint, that is not truly indicative of a booming labor market right now. And that's probably part of the reason you saw the unemployment rate actually tick up to what's, I think, a two-year high at 3.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know, Mark. I respect the heck out of you. But if we had these exact same numbers and it was under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , let's say he becomes president again, same numbers, I would hasten to add you might be reacting differently, maybe not, because you're a good sport and you take a look at the data.\par \par \uc2 But I'd rather have jobs increasing than not. So that's all I'm saying, but final thoughts for you.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Yes. Look, as would I. I mean, positive numbers are positive numbers. And maybe this part-time gig economy where you got an \uc2 Uber\uc2  or \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  or whatever it might be to make ends meet, maybe that's changed the game a little bit.\par \par \uc2 Maybe the landscape has changed and it's not all that bad.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: But when you're losing full-time jobs at the expense of part-time, I don't know. Not the best, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we shall see, my friend. Very good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Likewise.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Mark Tepper following all of this and the wealth effect or however you want to look at it.\par \par \uc2 The markets, by the way, had their own sort of drill they were following. They were confused about whether tech had overrun its path. Bottom line, they were down collectively, but off the worst levels of the day.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, a lot of attention being played to how the administration is fanning out all the support it can get to crow about what we're seeing, all of this, though, with the backdrop of some scary airline incidents.\par \par \uc2 A good time to be talking to the U.S. transportation secretary. \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  is here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you are looking live at Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, the Philadelphia area, I should say.\par \par \uc2 The president is going to a number of events there. And since his State of the Union to address, both he and many of his biggest Cabinet stars have been fanning around the country to try to get the good word out that things are looking better.\par \par \uc2 The president widely praised last night for a pretty strong State of the Union speech, the one that was considered -- I think feisty was a word I heard a great deal about.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, the transportation secretary of the United States is one of those stars out on the hustings here talking the good talk, \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Secretary, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, U.S. TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: Same here. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 Secretary, let me ask you a little bit about the president's view. He says the economy is in better shape. But, unfortunately it's not in the shape that a lot of people seem to agree. In other words, he is crowing about an economy they don't see why he's crowing about. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, look, I think a lot of energy out there is going into talking down the economy.\par \par \uc2 The president's job is to point out what's going right and, just as importantly, what we're seeking to do for the future to continue lowering costs and growing the economy. The numbers obviously are remarkable, all- time record high in terms of job creation under one president, lowest unemployment, certainly the longest it's been below 4 percent since before I was born, all-time record high on the stock market too.\par \par \uc2 But not every American is the median American. And just because I can point to statistics saying that, on average, wages have gone up faster than prices doesn't mean every single American is seeing that at home. And that's part of why you're going to continue to see a relentless focus on lowering those everyday costs.\par \par \uc2 The president adamant about the $35 cap on insulin, which he was able to get for seniors. He could get it for the entire country if just a handful of congressional Republicans would cooperate. He renewed that call last night, in addition to issuing his call for there be an out-of-pocket cap on prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm sorry, sir.\par \par \uc2 But wouldn't they need more immediate help with things like the grocery store and getting cheaper cereal prices, cheaper -- a host of prices? Because that's where people are feeling the pain. And this is welcome news, like you say, on prescription and related drugs, but they're hurting, right, every day they go to the supermarket.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, look, inflation is down. Eggs are cheaper than they were a year ago. So are airline tickets and a lot of things in between.\par \par \uc2 But, look, we know there's more work to do in the fight against inflation. The whole world faced enormous inflation after COVID, although the U.S. has the lowest rate of inflation of any of the G7 countries. And I believe that has a lot to do with the president's leadership.\par \par \uc2 But nobody's spiking the ball. Nobody's saying our work is done.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wait, wait, wait. You don't think it has a lot more to do with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  hiking interest rates and really bringing that under control?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: You know, we're not -- yes, we're not the only country with central banking and monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: True. Very true.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: What I would say is a lot of things, including, by the way -- and I know that we're just one piece of this, but I would say that the work we did to try to unstick supply chains played a role, because we know that shipping costs were one of the things that did put upward pressure on prices in the late part of 2021 and early 2022.\par \par \uc2 So, look, my point is, there's a lot of credit to go around for the many good results that have happened, and yet we know there needs to be more. And the president laid out an agenda that's much more focused on, as he always likes to say, an economy that grows from the bottom up and the middle out.\par \par \uc2 He believes strongly that a tax code where the tax rate paid by a billionaire or corporation being less than a firefighter just isn't right. He called on Congress to change that and is going to continue taking steps that I think would continue down the path of deficit reduction that he has achieved in his presidency, while growing the economy and trying to make everyday life just more affordable for people, partly by keeping prices under control and partly by growing wages.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Secretary, we have more debt than we did when he assumed office. The debt has grown under this president. By the way, it's grown under the last one, the one before him, one before him. So no one's making real progress here.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, but, again, more meaningful deficit reduction than we have seen in quite a while.\par \par \uc2 Yes, you're right., more work to be done, which is why he put out a real -- look, you're never supposed to even use the T-word, tax, in an address, but he pointed out a way to do it that would not touch anybody making less than $400,000 a year, which is something that most Americans, if not most members of Congress, support.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, that's not necessarily brave to talk about going after billionaires and corporations.\par \par \uc2 I think the issue, though, gets back to, why can't we just control spending, right?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, yes, but if it isn't, why won't want any congressional Republicans cooperate with him, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, no, you're quite right about that. But there's no cooperation on anything.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: I mean, look, the investments that we have done...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There's no cooperation on anything, which brings me to my bigger point here, that, when you step back and say that things are improving and getting better -- and you're quite right, we're well off to our highest levels when inflation is running at about a 9 percent rate, now close to 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 But the fact of the matter is, for a lot of people who buy general groceries and a lot of their everyday life items, they're still stuck and they think the administration is trying to say, you're imagining it. What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Look, anybody who has been in a marriage or a relationship knows that you never approach somebody by saying that you're wrong to be feeling what you're feeling.\par \par \uc2 Americans are having many different economic experiences, their family budgets, their personal economic situation. But what we are seeing is each passing month more and more encouraging news about inflation coming under control, but, just as importantly, wages going up.\par \par \uc2 And for the first time in a long time, it's been true on a sustained basis that just about anybody who wants a job can get a job. Now, we're working to make sure those are good-paying jobs, especially the ones we're creating with infrastructure and manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: We're really excited about these often six-figure jobs, whether you have got a college degree or not, that we're creating through the work on roads and bridges, the semiconductor manufacturing that the president has worked to bring back on our shores.\par \par \uc2 We're by no means saying mission accomplished. What we're saying is we're proud of the progress we have made on the president's watch and pushing hard to make even more.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 I could go into that a little bit more, but, Secretary, I did want to talk, as you are the transportation secretary of the United States, we have had a number of scary airline incidents, this \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  flight that skidded off a Houston airport runway. You had another one that lost a wheel as it was taking off, earlier, of course, the \uc2 Alaska Air\uc2  flight where the door came off, could have been a lot -- serious had this happened much higher altitude.\par \par \uc2 Do you think flying is safe today? A lot of people see these incidents, isolated though they are, but they are hearing them more frequently.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: It remains the case that flying is by far the safest way to travel in America, but that doesn't mean that we're satisfied with the way things are.\par \par \uc2 It's going to take a lot of work to keep it that way. It's why the \uc2 FAA\uc2  has conducted a deep dive into practices at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , met with senior officials, pointed out a number of concerns around their quality control, and gave them 90 days to put forward a comprehensive plan on how to get a handle on that, and will not permit \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to increase their production rate until they demonstrate that to the \uc2 FAA\uc2 's satisfaction.\par \par \uc2 Safety is not a destination. It's a journey. And maintaining the remarkable civilizational achievement that flying in a tube through the air at nearly the speed of sound is by far the safest way to travel in America is not something that can happen if we ever rest, which is why we're going to continue pushing, whether it's on \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , whether it's on our own work, funding the \uc2 FAA\uc2  properly to move into the future.\par \par \uc2 But not just the secretary of transportation, but, as a frequent flier who gets on an airline every few days, I see the extraordinary work that goes into that safety record. And, by the way, I only wish that we could have a comparable safety standard when it comes to roadway safety, where we lose more than 100 Americans a day.\par \par \uc2 We're finally seeing those rates start to move in the right direction, but still a ton of work to do there as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You mentioned \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , and the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  has been very critical of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  for its lack of cooperation in the 737 MAX 9 probe.\par \par \uc2 Are you disappointed in \uc2 Boeing\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Definitely concerned about what we have seen.\par \par \uc2 I have communicated that directly to the CEO of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . And I think really the proof is going to be in how they respond. I think that they need to demonstrate better results and really need to demonstrate that they have achieved a culture that puts safety first.\par \par \uc2 Production is important. Pace is important. Them being successful as a company is important, but none of that matters if you don't have 100 percent, 1000 percent assurances around safety.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Secretary, very good seeing you again. Thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Same here. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 , the transportation secretary of the United States.\par \par \uc2 All right, we're going to have \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  joining us, of course, someone very, very interval in the last Trump administration. Some people are saying maybe in another Trump administration?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, President Biden says he has no regrets using the term illegal last night in his State of the Union address, but, boy, oh, boy, oh, boy, do many in his own party.\par \par \uc2 The dust-up from within the Democratic Party with their leader -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you looking, for example, at \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 FMR. SEN. JOE LIEBERMAN (I-CT): Well, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  is really -- I mean, I think if she had expressed interest in the No Labels nomination, there would have been a lot of support in our group, but she's chosen otherwise.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Could Sinema, could the independent Arizona Senator, former Democrat, who opted out of running again for that office -- have you talked to her about maybe a bigger office?\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: She's not interested. But I look forward to coming on the show when I can tell you the names, but we're not going to be a long way from that. We're ready to go...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: ... if the delegates authorize today. And that will happen, in my guess, the next couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the co-chair of No Labels saying, no kidding, we are very serious about launching a third-party group to go ahead and challenge what would be a repeat of the last election.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Llenas has been toiling away right now in Palm Beach, Florida, with the latest on this startling development -- Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well there was a great interview with Joe Lieberman, who just refused to tell you anything about those candidates, try as you did.\par \par \uc2 But, anyway, 800 no-labels delegates met virtually today and nearly unanimously voted to move forward with their bipartisan presidential unity ticket. Now, they're going to compete in November. That's what they're saying. And the party says they're going to begin the process of choosing who will be their president and vice presidential picks.\par \par \uc2 The No Labels chair writing this -- quote -- "It was apparent that the citizens believe this is a just cause and that No Labels should provide Americans with the additional choice that they so clearly want."\par \par \uc2 And it's not just No Labels getting out, trying to get in the way of the Trump-Biden rematch. So too is independent presidential candidate RFK Jr., who pitched himself to voters in his own State of the Union address last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 ., PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Our nation seems more divided than ever, but Americans everywhere want to heal that divide. Our nation has become artificially divided by political forces that can survive only when we the people are at war with each other.\par \par \uc2 People are tired of being manipulated by fear.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Former President Trump and President Biden will host dueling campaign rallies tomorrow in Georgia a week after they both held dueling stops at the southern border.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's campaign says this is all part of a big campaign push in battleground states post his State of the Union address. And Trump's Georgia visit comes as the Fulton County DA \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  court drama continues and as Trump ramps up his attacks on Biden for his age and mental acuity.\par \par \uc2 Trump reacting to Biden's State of the Union speech last night, telling FOX Digital -- quote -- "He was angry, mentally disturbed and misrepresenting a lot of the facts concerning almost every subject he discussed."\par \par \uc2 Two big questions facing the Trump campaign now, Neil, one, all about how he's going to afford the legal battles, as well as what's expected to be the most expensive general election ever. He just posted a $92 million dollar bond while he appeals the $83 million judgment that was awarded to author E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 And then the other question is, who will be his vice presidential pick, Neil?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, my friend. Great reporting, as always.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Llenas in Florida toiling away there.\par \par \uc2 We have got the -- \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , the former secretary of state, former CIA director, FOX News contributor, joining us now. Always enjoy having him on, as much for his service to this country as just the fact that, well, he's an Italian-American. And we like to see that continue.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Secretary, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me ask you a little bit about this No Labels thing. I know you like to eschew politics and all of that, but obviously the entrant of a third candidate or third candidate team, if you will, complicates the picture here.\par \par \uc2 But Joe Lieberman says it's on and they are going to get on all the state ballots, even if they don't have candidates yet for those state ballots. But what do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Well, look, in the end, the political party system we have had for a long time has put every president into office for decades. I suspect this won't be any different.\par \par \uc2 One of the reasons I suspect they're having trouble getting folks to step forward is because it's not likely to be a successful outcome at either getting the presidency or, frankly, reshaping how Americans are thinking about the political process. And so, in the end, you can't win with nothing, Neil. You have got to have a candidate. It sounds like they don't have one yet.\par \par \uc2 And I suspect it will be President Trump or President Biden that is ultimately elected.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There's more focus this time than even there was back in 2016 when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  first ran and won for the presidency on who he picks to be with him. Your name does come up. Are you interested in that?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: I mean, look, it's flattering that my name comes up, that I had a chance to serve for four years, and didn't completely blow it. So people still think, hey, it might be possible he'd get another swing.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: I don't often comment on jobs I have not been offered.\par \par \uc2 But, Neil, you have known me long enough to know that if I get a chance to serve and think that I can make a difference, I'm almost certainly going to say yes to that opportunity to try and deliver on behalf of the American people.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think it would be different in a second Trump administration, that is returning to the Oval Office, recruiting people?\par \par \uc2 He had had sometimes some difficulty with early picks for secretary of state, defense, et cetera, you know that far better than I, and that he might be scaring off people who would otherwise be honored to be part of a White House senior staff and Cabinet.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: You know, Neil, there were folks who didn't want to serve in the last Trump administration too, or folks who came in and then punched their ticket and went on and left pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 I'm confident that, when President Trump is ultimately successful and is back in the White House in January, that he will be able to field an incredibly capable group of people who are patriots working to deliver on the things he promised the American people he would deliver on, the border, on national security, to get the economy backtracking.\par \par \uc2 I'm confident he will find a good group of men and women who will want to serve in that way.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, he demands, we're told, intense loyalty, that the one reason why I think he had some of the mistakes that he had in some hires were that they were not loyal.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Oh, goodness, I think every administration wants to have people that are delivering against what they said they would do. So call it what you might, call it loyalty, call it mission-focused. That's how I thought about it.\par \par \uc2 I was laser-focused every day on, whether I was at the CIA or State Department, trying to say, what is it the president told the American people we do, what's the ask, and how do we execute that? How do you actually implement that as a practitioner, not someone who's out giving speeches or pontificating in a classroom?\par \par \uc2 We had to actually get it done. I'm confident President Trump will be looking for people who will faithfully execute what it is he asks them to do. I think, as a president, you should always want that from everyone. I must say, as secretary of state, I certainly wanted my team to do what I was asking them to do and was enormously frustrated when I found that I couldn't get them to do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Your take on -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has raised some eyebrows, particularly in Europe, with his criticism of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and those countries that don't pay up. It might have been exaggeration, but even offered in one speech for Russia to go ahead and invade that country that doesn't keep a minimum of 2 percent for defense.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that and whether a Trump administration, if it were to return, would even be loyal to \uc2 NATO\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Neil, I think the best predictor of future performance -- it's kind of a financial idea, right -- is past performance. It's not always indicative, but it often tells you a lot about at least the ideas that will animate how someone invests or in the case of a president how they will deliver.\par \par \uc2 And I look at what we did for those four years. We made a lot of European countries uncomfortable and as a result of that they did the right thing, what they should have done without being made uncomfortable, I think Secretary-General Stoltenberg, the general secretary of \uc2 NATO\uc2  still to this day, would say that some $400 billion flowed into \uc2 NATO\uc2 's defense operations because of what President Trump and our team did.\par \par \uc2 I have every expectation that the fact that it was the Javelin missiles that the Trump administration provided to Ukraine that actually saved Kyiv in the moment. I'm confident that the next Trump administration will do the right thing for America and \uc2 NATO\uc2  will be a central part of that. And I hope the Europeans will take up their responsibility and do it well and do it right.\par \par \uc2 I think President Trump will ask for that too.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think he will be as keen on being supportive of Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: You know, what we always had in mind was make sure we get it right for America, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: It got shorthanded as America first.\par \par \uc2 When we did that well, when we did the things that mattered to America, it was often the case that, while we didn't send a lot of American troops to far-off places to risk their lives, we often found the American power tool, right, the capacity to deliver American economic power, the capacity to get trade rules right.\par \par \uc2 Those things were really good for these countries. And I'm confident that the things that we did for Ukraine in those four years would continue under a Trump administration in the second term as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Secretary, always good seeing you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Thank you, Neil. Bless you. Have a good day, sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You too.\par \par \uc2 We always go back and forth, and some people are at loggerheads, in this divisive, polarizing era, who's going to be our next president? Who's going to be on -- always worried about, who's our next president?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That's a quote.\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now maybe the focus -- maybe now the focus should be not on maybe who the next president is and who our leaders are and frustration with that, but maybe focusing on a higher authority still.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Green after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Not really.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): Laken Riley.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I...\par \par \uc2 Oh, you don't like that bill, huh? That conservatives got together and said it was a good bill? I'll be darned.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I have been really looking forward to do this interview with my buddy Lauren Green.\par \par \uc2 Besides just being a wonderful person, Lauren, like me, we're one of the FOX originals, right, when FOX News started in 1996. We were in the beginning.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN GREEN, FOX NEWS CHIEF RELIGION CORRESPONDENT: Yes, right, 1996, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And look at us now. There we go.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Amazing. You haven't aged a bit.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I was just saying that. I was waiting. Ba-dum-bum.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, anyway, one thing is great, as our religion editor and so much more, "Light for Today: 365 Daily Devotions from the Lighthouse."\par \par \uc2 Lauren practices what she preaches and relays what may be some famous biblical passages, the Old and New Testament over the thousands of years have been preaching. And that is to just step back from this craziness, right?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think the one thing that's really important I learned is that, of course, you can't put God in a box. We like in this modern day to say, God, you can have control over this part of my life, but not this part of my life.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: And that's not the way God works. God works...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A lot of people don't even want God now.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Well, exactly. I mean...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I mean, look at this craziness on Capitol Hill and all.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Well, this is very interesting. It's very interesting, because a lot of the polls out and the religious polls that you see in Pew and other places, they want to talk about how many people believe in God.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's around 70 percent say -- that's not even the issue. The devil believes in God.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: I mean, it's really, what are you worshiping? And that becomes the core of what is causing the divisiveness. What are you really worshiping? Are you worshiping God or your identity, your identity politics, your gender identity, your tribal identity?\par \par \uc2 Anything you put before God becomes a false idol and becomes -- and what we don't realize is, sometimes, we have these identities that become our fundamental trust, the thing that really believe that this is going -- the thing -- this is the thing that is going to save me. This is the source of my salvation.\par \par \uc2 If it's not the living God, then this is what devolves into a lot of divisiveness.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I do think, as we have lost sense of that, Lauren -- and you and I have talked about this.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's no mystery. We lose a lot of other things.\par \par \uc2 But one thing I have always known and loved about you from the very first time we met, you always seem so calm.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Really?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So you're clearly not Italian.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I just say, how did she stay so calm?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: I am content.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: I think that's what...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's the whole concert pianist thing, right?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Well, that's part of it.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that reading Scripture every day, listening to sermons, reading devotionals, other than mine -- I do read other devotionals -- has created a sort of contentment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 But you picked all of these out, a devotion for each day?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So the ones that caught you and you elaborate on them a little bit.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Well, these are actually thoughts for the day I began writing after my first book, "Lighthouse Faith."\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And I just amassed about 450 of these thoughts for the day. And then that turned into the devotional, because God works in the natural. God works in the every day. He created it, soul, mind, body.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: He has dominion over all of it. And we should probably go to him in all those areas.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In other words, you were on a higher place. See, I'm thinking like, boy, these tech stocks overrun their -- overrun themselves.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You think of more important things.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Well, you know what?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But we are such an obsessed society on all this other stuff, the parties that don't talk to each other. I get even e-mail, it might surprise you, Lauren, from born-again Christians who really say very nasty things to me and make me feel vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But not across the board. But I'm just saying, that's the vernacular these days.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Well, this is hard, because it's one of the -- and I hate to say this to the head of business channel here, but it's all God's money.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: And that's I guess -- I think that -- when you start thinking in terms of that way...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But he doesn't want it back, does he? Or...\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Well, he wants a portion of it back, you know?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. No, I hear you.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: But it's not for his sake. It's for our sake.\par \par \uc2 When God asks you a question, it's not for information. It's really to open...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He knows the answer.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: He knows the answer.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: It's for us to actually start understanding our own hearts.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If you had a message for people who just, they're under pressure, they got these higher prices, they're dealing with this, they feel forsaken, some of them see tragedy almost on a daily basis, wars that don't end, and they say, where's God? Where's God?\par \par \uc2 What does Lauren say?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: God is always here. The problem is, is that we -- like I say, we want to put God in a box.\par \par \uc2 We have not gone to him for all of our troubles and all our pains. And some of them are self-inflicted, actually.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: So we have to understand what is self-inflicted, what God has allowed us to actually suffer through in order to change our hearts.\par \par \uc2 C.S. Lewis talks about God gets us. Pain is like the foghorn or the bullhorn for us in God getting us, getting our attention. I would say, read Scripture, pray, understand that God is here. His love is as close as the air we breathe.\par \par \uc2 In him, we move and have our being. This is not a God who is distant. This is a God who is right present with us right now, every day, 24/7.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That is beautiful.\par \par \uc2 "Light for Today: 365 Daily Devotions from the Lighthouse." And she walks her talk and her preaching, because she doesn't preach. She just talks.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: She makes you feel really guilty because you don't feel the same way and you don't act the same way.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And big star and all that.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, we will have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Lincoln (sic) Riley, an innocent young woman who was killed by an illegal.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): He should have said undocumented, but I -- that's not a big thing, OK?\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): He should have used the word undocumented. I don't believe that the president's heart is with the word illegal.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Do you regret using the word illegal to describe immigrants last night, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, not -- I don't regret -- technically, not supposed to be here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I think the president said it best right there. They're technically not supposed to be here. But they are, by the millions.\par \par \uc2 Mark Dannels is the Cochise County, Arizona, sheriff.\par \par \uc2 Sheriff, great seeing you.\par \par \uc2 I couldn't believe, Sheriff...\par \par \uc2 MARK DANNELS, COCHISE COUNTY, ARIZONA, SHERIFF: You too, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... how people had such a major dust-up over this, illegal, undocumented. You're not supposed to be here. That's what it comes down to. What did you make of the hissy fit over it?\par \par \uc2 DANNELS: Well, it's sad. It shows you how deep politics are, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Ask the Riley family. Ask Lakely's (sic) mom and dad and family if they care about the word illegal or technically not supposed to be here. She's not with us because, under the leadership of President Biden and his policies, it's sad, it is, that we're fighting over the words, instead of the reality of a young inspiring American that died.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, where do we go now, Sheriff?\par \par \uc2 I mean, the president was bemoaning, Republicans are pulling the rug out from under even fellow Republicans who have this bipartisan border deal. It's a moot point now. It's not happening. He was hinting at taking actions on his own. Apparently, that's been shelved.\par \par \uc2 So where do we stand now?\par \par \uc2 DANNELS: Well, we have seen, for a last 3.5 years, Neil, there's been no political will. There's been no political action.\par \par \uc2 All we have seen over the last 3.5 years is political blame between Congress all the way up to the White House with this president. And what I didn't see last night, I think it was really important, and I say that sums it all up with what you're asking me is, I saw no responsibility from the president and I saw no apology from the president.\par \par \uc2 It's got to start there. We're at a point of this crisis that there's been so much tragedy, whether it's fentanyl poisoning, whether it's crime, whether it's death as a result of this open border. Somebody's got to take some responsibility. I would love to see an apology from somebody in Washington, D.C., to include the president.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, for the president, his knee-jerk and more emotional immediate response was to use the term illegal. And I understand others in the party, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and these others, are saying, no, no, no, they're undocumented immigrants.\par \par \uc2 But that has a sense -- the latter has a sense it's just a paperwork issue. But it's not a paperwork issue.\par \par \uc2 DANNELS: No.\par \par \uc2 And this is what's -- this is how far off we are on this, is the fact that when you come across the border without permission, you are breaking a federal law. That is illegal. And we have set aside Title 8. Last night, the president asked for funding. Give me the funding, and I can fix this.\par \par \uc2 You have more than the funding, Neil. He has the power and he has the law behind him under Title 8 that says you come across the border illegally, you're automatically supposed to be expelled. They used that discretion. They set it aside. And that's why it's catch-and-release right here on the border. He has the power of the people.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Mark Dannels, always good seeing you, my friend. It's the same old issue and we don't make progress. But you and your men and women are trying to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 The Cochise County, Arizona, sheriff on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Our next step is recommending that maybe Jon Taffer should be put at the border to chase this craziness away, because, judging from his show and the new season, he's just the guy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JON TAFFER, HOST, "BAR RESCUE": Are you going to share your drink? I think if we sit here until tomorrow morning you still won't have cocktails.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think you're right.\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: And to put you through this and put them through it is just torture.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: We're now about 45 minutes in. Food came out, but more drinks have gone in the trash than have crossed the bar.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We need to collect money from these guys too.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I haven't.\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: This is a total failure.\par \par \uc2 Folks, listen up. I think, if we sit here until tomorrow morning, you still won't have cocktails.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think you're right.\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: And to put you through this and put them through it is just torture. This is about as bad as I have seen in "Bar Rescue." You have no skills and at this moment no chance of success! And let's get out of here so I can go to work.\par \par \uc2 Good night!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I'm thinking of myself, we have got to get Jon Taffer in with a session with Lauren Green.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, the "Bar Rescue" host extraordinaire, love his show, just love him as a person, because you know exactly where you stand or, God forbid, don't stand, joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 Jon, how you doing?\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: Always good to see you, Neil. Just another day in the office for me, that shot.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's -- oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 How do they react? I mean, when those cameras go down and all that, are they like, whoa?\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: You know what's amazing, Neil, is I try to trick them. I come a day early, I come a day late. They think I'm going to another bar.\par \par \uc2 But if there's a 20 percent chance that Jon Taffer was going to come to your bar and put you on national television, don't you think you would clean it up?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's a good point.\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: They don't even do that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And it illustrates the fact that business owners that are failing, they don't see the cracks in the walls. They walk right by it. And when I was in a hotel business, I had general managers that walked right by that crack in the wall. Business owners that are failing are sometimes blinded by that failure.\par \par \uc2 And, Neil, after the pandemic and all the beating up that the industry has been through, they're not seeing as well as they should, candidly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: And prices are higher. And I just read an article in The Wall Street Journal just the other day, selling a $15 hamburger in New York City, you still can't make money at $15.\par \par \uc2 So, as an industry, we're not struggling to find customers as much as to find profitability. And that's a challenge.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And a lot of them mail it in, no matter the business.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you seem to police that the most aggressively, if they have the passive issue or it comes to service or they take their sweet time getting food to customers or drinks. You don't let them get away with it.\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: No, I don't.\par \par \uc2 Think about this, Neil. A man 50 years old living in his parents' basement, he blew their retirement money. They have nothing. And they're about to lose their house. I show up at his business and he won't close the business. They have a few thousand left, and he's not going to stop until it's all gone.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: Now, when I show up, it's not about a bar. I'm trying to save their parents' house.\par \par \uc2 So it's intense to me, Neil. And it's a big responsibility. So I go at him hard because I'm fighting for that house. And every failing business has a broken wallet and sometimes a broken family.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Jon, I always wonder. And there's a lot we don't see, but are there some people you just can't help, don't listen to your help, and you just say, the hell with it, you're a lost cause?\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: Yes. Unfortunately, that's the case.\par \par \uc2 But nobody's a lost cause when there's a family at home, when there's a house on the line. So it's interesting, Neil. When I don't like the owner, when I say, this guy's a jerk, I don't even want to help him, I think of the employees. Well, I'd like to help them. I think of the wife at home. I'd like to help her. I think of the family.\par \par \uc2 So, there's always somebody to fight for in "Bar Rescue," even if it isn't the owner.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So when you're in contract negotiations and the show's a hit, are they afraid of you? Whatever you want, Jon, whatever you want. It's OK.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: You know, it's funny. In negotiations, I remember saying a few years ago, you book me because I'm a lion, and then you don't like it when I roar.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, that's a very good point. That's a very -- but they let you do what you do because what you do is very popular, and people love it, right?\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Neil, at the end of the show, I get that hug. And we're wearing microphones in our chests, so people don't hear what's said to me. But, Neil, they tell me, I changed their lives. They're talking to their wives again. They feel they got their future back.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: I'm the father they never had.\par \par \uc2 The things they say to me when I hear that hug. And then I realize the ones that fight with me the most, Neil, are the ones that give me the biggest hugs in the end.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's beautiful.\par \par \uc2 TAFFER: So it makes me fight even harder next time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Beautiful, beautiful. I get words whispered too. They're not repeatable.\par \par \uc2 Jon, thank you.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240309ek3800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 SCOTUS Rules 9-0 To Keep Trump On Colorado Ballot; Trump Returned To Colorado Ballot Ahead Of Super Tuesday; United States Envoy: "Reasonable Grounds" To Believe \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Committed Rape, "Sexualized Torture"; Biden Prepares For State Of The Union As Polls Lag; Kirby On Push For Biden To Call For Gaza Ceasefire; Haley Looks Ahead To Super Tuesday After Win In D.C.; Haley On Backing Trump If He's GOP Nominee; Haley Reacts To Growing Third-Party Speculation; Biden Faces Polling Slump Ahead Of Super Tuesday; Rep. Burgess Owens (R-UT) Is Interviewed About Border; Ex-Border Patrol Chief: Never Spoke To Biden Or VP\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, James Freeman, Trey Yingst, Peter Doocy, Bret  Baier \par \uc2 8,274 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: But still, 450,000 people, that's a significant number of people. And, I mean, the percentages, too, are really quite striking. Well, that's something I think that everybody needs to look at for the future, considering how much widespread that is. Thank you, Alexandra. Appreciate it. Sandra?\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . We'll see you tomorrow. "THE STORY" with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much, you guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. On this Monday, this is THE STORY. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  will join us live on this day before Super Tuesday. Lots to speak with her about today. And also, John Kirby is here as the White House faces huge challenges electorally, at the border, and on Israel. So, we will speak with John Kirby in moments away later this hour. But first, Former President Trump celebrating the Supreme Court's unanimous decision rejecting state efforts to keep him off the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , 45TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You cannot take somebody out of a race. The voters can take the person out of the race very quickly, but a court shouldn't be doing that, and the Supreme Court saw that very well. And I really do believe that will be a unifying factor because while most states were thrilled to have me, there were some that didn't, and they didn't want that for political reasons. They didn't want that because of poll numbers, because the poll numbers are very good. We're beating President Biden in almost every poll.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, we're going to talk about a lot of these polls. These are some of the numbers that he has been referring to beating President Biden by five in the New York Times Siena poll. Very big numbers in this Siena poll that came out over the weekend. He's up by two in Fox News polling. Same at The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 And those last two results are within the margin of error on those two comparisons. So, the Wall Street Journal's James Freeman is here to talk about this, and pollster \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2  standing by as well. Obviously big implications for 2024 electoral politics, but first let's go to Republican West Virginia Attorney General Patrick Morrissey.\par \par \uc2 He led a coalition of states in writing the brief to the Supreme Court, supporting the former president in this case. He's also running for the governor of West Virginia. General, good to have you here. Thank you very much for joining us today. Tell me your reaction to this and tell me what impact your amicus briefs may have had on the thinking of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MORRISEY (R), WEST VIRGINIA ATTORNEY GENERAL: Absolutely. So, thank you, Martha, for having me on. All along, we had believed that the actions on the states to disqualify President Trump from the ballot would always be found invalid because this was essentially a federal decision.\par \par \uc2 We know that the states have been trying to put out some extra criteria that's not included under the Constitution, that's not part of the categorical criteria where you exclude someone from office, let's say it's natural born citizen or the number of terms you've been in office.\par \par \uc2 So, we thought all along, in order for someone to be vulnerable to being thrown off the ballot, you needed to have a federal decision by Congress. That obviously didn't occur, and so we thought that we were going to be correct in our arguments that we put forth, that the Supreme Court would say the states lack the power to do it plain and simple.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I mean, we listened to the fascinating discussion on all of this at the Supreme Court when the audio was played and you know you heard some of the arguments that there's just no precedent for a state kicking about someone off the ballot. This is the leading contender for the GOP nomination as we, as we mentioned.\par \par \uc2 So, one of the interesting things here, I think one of the prominent things here is that this was a unanimous 9-0 decision. So, tell us what you think went into the thinking of for example Justices Kagan or Brown-Jackson, who may have been inclined to vote against this?\par \par \uc2 MORRISEY: Well, I think if you heard the Supreme Court argument, you got a sense that even some of the more liberally leaning judges were going to be sympathetic to Trump's argument because they were asking about what might happen. And I think all of the justices concluded that chaos would ensue if you could allow individual states to step on and rule that someone's disqualified. That would be the worst form of election interference.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we started to get that sense during the oral argument. And that makes sense, right? Because we're talking about a uniquely federal office, a national office. This is different than running for governor or a statewide office. This is the presidency of the United States that have specific rules and if you're going to let a Secretary of State who's unelected make that decision or a court in Colorado without actually having a criminal finding of insurrection to make that decision, that would be very problematic.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right. That's a little problematic. Yes, exactly. He hasn't been charged in any of these counts with insurrection, and these states independently decided that he was guilty of it, and therefore used a tenant of the Constitution to try to kick him off their state ballot. And it seems that cooler heads have prevailed here in the Supreme Court today. Attorney General Morrisey, thank you very much for joining us today from West Virginia. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MORRISEY: Hey, thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. So, let's bring in James Freeman, Wall Street Journal Editorial Page Assistant Editor and Fox News Contributor; and \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 , Pollster and President of Maslansky and Partners. James, you know, your take on this and the impact on electoral politics.\par \par \uc2 JAMES FREEMAN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, I think this kind of abuse of the legal system, the judicial system to try and get Trump out of the race is part of why he's actually becoming a little more popular, which is a little weird because you think of him as someone people know well, they either love him or hate him, but his negatives in the Journal Poll and some others are shrinking.\par \par \uc2 And I think people look at cases like this, just an appalling abuse of our system. Obviously, the idea that a single state could decide who can run for a federal office was ridiculous. Also, you mentioned, no due process. He's never been convicted. Even some of the dissenters on the Colorado Supreme Court talking about it.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is a code meant to decide disputes like whether someone's met the residency requirement for a school board race. The idea that you think you're going to use this to intervene in the presidential race is really, really outrageous. I hope it dissuades other states from following soon.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, and there are some questions about post-election in terms of the impact of this could have a fail out of the taken off the ballot anyone anyway what some of the states might have pursued in that, James, just quickly and then I'll get --\par \par \uc2 FREEMAN: Well, I think that does raise a question we've seen. This is where the Supremes, the justices did have a disagreement is they, they agree the state can do this but they disagreed on whether Congress has to act to pass legislation to say, here's how you disqualify someone. And it does raise, unfortunately, the specter of, are they going to have different opinions if there's an effort at the federal level after the fact to intervene here?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Lee, let's talk about, welcome, and let's talk about some of these polls, because they got a lot of attention over the weekend. And some of the numbers in these polls are quite stunning. I just want to pull this one up. We showed the headline number already. This is black registered voters in this recent polling -- 66 percent, Biden; 23 percent, Trump. Now, going back to 2020, 91 percent for Biden and eight percent for Trump. That is a huge move for black voters towards former President Trump, Lee.\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , POLLSTER: It's a huge move. And there's a huge move across a number of key segments that were traditionally always hugely going for the Democratic candidates. And it doesn't seem to be the case now. You see that with black voters, you see it with females now who are tied, who went by a huge margin to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . You're seeing with Hispanic voters as well.\par \par \uc2 The gap is narrowing across the board. And I think it's fascinating. I think if I were a Democrat right now, I'd be very, very concerned because in every place where they had that margin of error covered or where they had the surge, they're not seeing it. And again, also in those key swing states those seven states in a matter the most you see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  taking it away and no matter what the Democrats are saying right now, I think it's a really big issue for them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, thank you very much guys good to see you both, \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2  and James Freeman. Thank you for coming by today. So, coming up we have John Kirby live from the White House on the message President Biden plans to deliver at this week's State of the Union address to convince voters that he is fit for the job. We've seen the polling going against that category, so we'll talk to John about that and much more.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, breaking moments ago, a \uc2 United Nations\uc2  envoy says that there are "reasonable grounds" to believe that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  committed rape on October the 7th against Israelis, and "sexualized torture" beginning on that day as the attack under Israel was underway, and that the sexual violence "may still be happening" according to their investigation. Here's what the leader of the group \uc2 U.N. Women\uc2  had to say about the accusations of that right after this happened in early November. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And just to provide a little bit of context in terms of \uc2 U.N. Women\uc2 's role, \uc2 UN Women\uc2  specifically provides and has extensive knowledge on gender-based violence and provides and supports investigations as we do with all \uc2 U.N.\uc2  investigations.\par \par \uc2 And so, consequently, in this context 