n, to $371 million last year. I mean, it kind of shows you.\par \par \uc2 And it all kind of harkens back to whether or not there was so much resistance to go after \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  because of the bias at the United Nations against Israel. Your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 SALES: Well, the United Nations has shown its systematic bias against Israel for many years. And it's only gotten worse since October 7th. The \uc2 UN Human Rights Council\uc2 , the so called \uc2 Human Rights Council\uc2 , they have issued dozens of statements condemning Israel over the years, guess how many they've issued about Russia or Iran, or China or Cuba? You can kind of probably on one hand.\par \par \uc2 UN Women\uc2 , it took them months to issue a statement about \uc2 Hamas\uc2  as systematic use of rape and sexual abuse against Israeli women. And then guess what? They got so much heat from Israel's critics, they walked it back. So nobody should look at the \uc2 UN\uc2  and think here is some sort of neutral independent or trustworthy arbiter when it comes to Israel. They are biased against Israel and have been for decades.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. I mean, we were going to do a story and a little bit on the fact that Iran is executing peaceful protesters, and nobody at the \uc2 UN\uc2  is saying anything about this. I know these 12 staff members have been fired by the United Nation. They are clearly facing bigger consequences. This is clearly going to become a much bigger deal. I've got about 30 seconds left for you, Nathan.\par \par \uc2 SALES: Yes, I guess you know, what do you call 12 fired unrest staffers, a good start. You've got a clean house. It's not limited to just 12 people. They may have engaged in the most egregious conduct, but let's look even more closely at the policy priorities or where's the money going, look at the textbooks.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SALES: Because I think when you peel back the curtain, you're going to find a lot of stuff that makes American taxpayers really uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Nathan Sales, good to have you on as always. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 SALES: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, THE STORY exclusive with activist \uc2 Masih Alinejad\uc2 , now on a hunger strike to protest executions of political prisoners by Iran's Islamic Republic, excuse me. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MASIH ALINEJAD, ACTIVIST: Imagine you wake up one day early morning, with the news of the execution of your own beloved one for the crime of peacefully protesting, for the crime of demanding democracy, dignity, freedom. We the people of Iran are angry or mourning, but we want to take action.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: While dozens of women going on hunger strike protesting the execution of a young man in Iran, 23-year-old Mohammad Ghobadlou accused of killing a police officer with his car during demonstrations back in 2022. His mother said he had bipolar disorder and was not taking his medication. Iran claims its Supreme Court upheld his death sentence after a psychological exam. He was killed this week by hanging.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in \uc2 Masih Alinejad\uc2 , an Iranian American journalist and activist taking part in the hunger strike. This really is one of those things where the whole world needs to get involved and be aware of what's happening here, Masih. I want to put this quote up because this is from one of the women who is in the prison, Narges Mohammadi. Her \uc2 Instagram\uc2  post reads the following, the imprisoned women to keep the names of the executor to live and survive. Hundreds of individuals in the prisons of the Islamic Republic awaiting execution will resist.\par \par \uc2 And apparently for now that resistance, Masih, and tell me if I'm wrong, is a hunger strike. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: It is, it is, indeed. The political prisoners, 