 at 34%, \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2  at 2%, you at 1%. I guess the good news, congressman, is the top -- the bottom to categories, none 22% and don't know 34%. That's 56%. Looks like there's a lot of room to grow in the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: Right say so, Trace. In fact, I come from the private sector and the business world, and you look at the numbers. I love those numbers because it says there's a lot of market share to be grabbed. Every single voter in this country, Democrat, Republican, Independent, they know \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and if they're not supporting him now that means they're looking for options. And the fact that we got to 20% market share in just 10 weeks in New Hampshire is a pretty good sign that if I keep going, introduce myself, show up, actually debate answer questions appear on T80% market share in just 10 weeks in New Hampshire is a pretty good sign that if I keep going introduce myself, show up, actually debate, answer questions, appear on TV like yours, and actually face the press, you know what, anything's possible.\par \par \uc2 And I know one thing, Trace, most of this country's ready for change. I think they actually want to move on from both of these men. And it's time for a new generation. And I've got some bold propositions, some common sense ideas that this country could use, and most of all, time to be respectful to one another, Democrats to Republicans, and (inaudible) Democrats.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And, congressman, it seems to me that you're not just running against the incumbent, the current president, but there are a lot of people. The perspective seems to be that you're also running against the DNC, that they're not really taking kindly to your campaign, and might in some capacity be targeting you. Is that a fair assessment or -- or unfair?\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: That's fair, and that's how this pretty nauseating political game works. It's a political industrial complex, that likes to coordinate incumbents no matter what the numbers say, no matter what voters want. And I think people should recognize the GOP and the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  are private enterprises. That work for their own needs and their own interests, not necessarily for the public interest.\par \par \uc2 And I think both parties right now really have to do some self reflection, look in the mirror. But it's also an opportunity for voters, especially the exhausted majority, Trace, center right, center left, decent Americans, if you want to change this nonsense, go vote in your primary, don't hand the keys to the far right 10%, the far left 10%, seize this back, return power to people.\par \par \uc2 And it is not something that takes an act of Congress, or an act of God, or an act of the president. It's just an act of participate (inaudible) hope people vote in the primaries.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. We're on the verge of kind of losing here, congressman, but I want to very quickly ask you what you think about the Biden strategy, the Biden campaign strategy to kind of go after not just \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and -- but yet the MAGA Republicans as a whole. Your last comments.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: Yes, Trace. Well, first of all, we obviously need better internet in this country. That's the issue right now. I'll tell you, I went to a Trump rally. I went to a Trump rally the other night in New Hampshire, in Rochester, just to say hello to voters. Just to say hello to voters because they're Americans. And it was a beautiful experience, kindness, warmth, friendliness.\par \par \uc2 And I'll tell you it was a an epiphany to me and I think it should be to all politicians show up, meet fellow Americans. You might just be surprised.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Congressman, best of luck to you. Thank you for coming on the show. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: Hey, sorry for the bad connection. Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Well, meantime explicit photos of popstar Taylor Swift sweeping the internet bringing new attention to deep fakes and whether anything we see online can be believed, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: While these are apparently real pictures of Taylor Swift, the X- rated photos getting millions of views online or not. And she is not the only victim of the high tech fakes. The chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt live with more on this story that's gaining a lot of traction. Jonathan?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Good afternoon, Trace. Politicians have obviously been abused through AI, ordinary people have been abused through AI, many celebrities have been victims of AI-generated images. But when you come for Taylor Swift, the world takes notice.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OREN ETZIONI, UNIVERSITY OF WASHINGTON: I think we are going to have a pandemic of this kind of AI-generated porn and a tsunami of disinformation. It's not just video, it's also audio, it's images, it's text. Unfortunately, it's become all too easy to just forge reality.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: There is a more lighthearted side to AI fakes. This AI-generated picture of the Pope in a Balenciaga jacket for instance, made a lot of people smile. But the Taylor Swift AI pictures which we will not show for very obvious reasons demonstrate the dark side of AI a dark side that the high school students also across the country are finding they can become victims of.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA, VICTIM OF AI PORN: I never thought being like a student, like I never nothing AI didn't even come to my mind. I just thought would be like creeps on the internet.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And on the political side, both President Biden and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have already been targets of AI fakes and there is real concern. AI could be used to try to influence this year's election. Experts point out that AI for the moment at least isn't in and of itself the problem, human nature is.\par \par \uc2 SHOMRON JACOB, ITERATE.AI: A human has to do it. So it's a problem in a way where humans are figured out how to misuse the advancement in the technology world, especially internet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Now tech companies are, as you would imagine, rushing to find ways to more easily identify AI-generated fake pictures and video. But in the meantime, we're going to see a lot more of this and in this heated political atmosphere of 2020 portraits, the dangers are obvious and really disturbing.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Right. It is such as Taylor and her camp that are upset about this, her fans are also very upset by this whole targeting and Taylor Swift with this AI stuff. So it's, yes --\par \par \uc2 HUNT: They're making their feelings known online for sure.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. It is a problem, Jonathan Hunt. Jonathan, great to see you. Thank you as always.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is THE STORY of Friday, January 26, 2024. But as always, THE STORY goes on too. Tonight on "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT," America's late news, we will have the lieutenant governor of Texas \uc2 Dan Patrick\uc2  on because there is a standoff between the feds and Texas. We will deal with that tonight, 8:00 in the west, 11:00 in the east.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020240127ek1q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Border Battle; Interview With \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  President Shawn Fain; \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  Union Endorses President Biden; Trump Attacks Haley After New Hampshire; GOP Presidential Primary Shifts to South Carolina\b0\par\par\uc2 Danamarie McNicholl, Sandra Smith, Alexis McAdams,Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,363 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right to put it mildly, the heat is on as the presidential raise heads now to South Carolina. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  says she is not going anywhere. And that seemed to have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  going nuts, ramping up the rhetoric against an opponent who was refusing to leave.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Who the hell was the impostor that went up on the stage before and, like, claimed a victory? She did very poorly, actually.\par \par \uc2 She had to win. The governor said, she's going to win, she's going to win, she's going to win. Then she failed badly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, but did she fail badly?\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  currently has 32 delegates to \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's 17. Now, you need 1,215 to win. So, we did the math for you and said, all right, well, they both have a long way to go, and it's still early.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to it, to Alexis McAdams in Manchester, New Hampshire, with the very latest -- Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil. Yes, I think I have moved to Manchester, New Hampshire. So if you need any restaurant recommendations when you come back, I will keep you posted on that.\par \par \uc2 But this has been an ongoing fight, right, between Trump and \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  said she wanted this to be a two-person race here on the ground. She got it, but, last night, she did not come out on top here, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sending yet another warning shot to \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's campaign, saying she should drop out, she's not presidential material.\par \par \uc2 Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She was up. And I said, wow, she's doing, like, a speech like she won. She didn't win. She lost.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This race is far from over. There are dozens of states left to go.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HALEY: And the next one is my sweet state of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's team believes she's got the cash and the momentum to keep going there to South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 But what do voters think about the most important issues facing the country right now? Well, it comes down to the immigration issue, the economy, jobs, foreign policy and abortion sitting there at 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Back out here live in New Hampshire, Neil, we can tell you that, how confident is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Well, he's talking about vice presidential picks already, right? A few of them were up on the stage last night. Their names have been floated. That includes \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  and Senator Tim Scott.\par \par \uc2 So I asked them, do you want to beat Trump's V.P.? Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: So, look, I'm going to do whatever's right for the country. And in the next step, as long as I'm able to be helpful in this campaign, I will be.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: So is it going to be Vice President Tim Scott?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): It's going to be Tim Scott working for -- on behalf of the American people. That means getting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be the next president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: I thought, if they would tell anybody, right, it would be me. I told him I wouldn't tell anybody, Neil, but no answer just yet at this point. So we will keep an eye posted on that.\par \par \uc2 And we know you're going to have a special guest coming up to talk about some other issues, including the \uc2 UAW\uc2  backing President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . But this comes after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had another win here in the Granite State, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Indeed. We look forward to that. That will be Shawn Fain, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  chief. He will be here to talk about why he decided the way he did.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, to Sandra Smith of "America Reports" fame. She just works around the clock to make it a lot easier. Sleep is just an afterthought.\par \par \uc2 But, Sandra, I know you have been looking into sort of the warning signs for both major-party candidates.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Fair point.\par \par \uc2 We asked the question to Republican voters on the ground in New Hampshire, Neil, would you vote for Trump if he is indeed the eventual nominee? The results were pretty revealing. Those who said they would not vote for him at all if he becomes the nominee, 35 percent of New Hampshire Republicans. That number is 11 percent who say they're dissatisfied if he becomes the nominee, but they would still vote for him.\par \par \uc2 But more than half said that they would be satisfied if Trump is the eventual nominee. We put the same question to Republican voters about \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  if she were to become the eventual nominee. Well, there was a far less number who said they would be satisfied with that outcome, 39 percent.\par \par \uc2 But even more say than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , even though they're dissatisfied she's the nominee, they would still vote for her among Republicans in that state. Those who said they would not vote for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  at all, it's very similar to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , 32 percent.\par \par \uc2 One other thing I want to highlight here, Neil, as you just heard immigration once again, just like Iowa, in New Hampshire top the list of concerns for Republican voters on the ground. We asked the question about the president's -- the current president's approval rating among New Hampshire Democrats, OK, Democrats in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 His overall approval rating, the president, with those voters, 74 percent, Neil. OK, on the economy, that number is even bigger for New Hampshire Democrats. Eighty percent approve of this handling of the economy, on student debt, 73 percent.\par \par \uc2 I want to get my tricky pen over here so I can highlight what it looks like for immigration. On immigration, that number, Neil, drops to 54 percent approval rating among New Hampshire Democrats, 49 percent on Israel- Palestine. So that's a really, really interesting outcome when you go back to that top finding that our reporter just showed us a moment ago on the national priorities.\par \par \uc2 Turn off my tricky pen here, Neil. When you look at the national priority being immigration, once again, 41 percent. So this is the top issue for them, for Republicans, facing the party today.\par \par \uc2 So, among Democrats, this is a bipartisan issue, it's becoming, Neil, their overall approval rating much lower on that issue than things like the economy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The marvelous thing about that is how easily you handle that big screen.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you and Hemmer. I mean, they gave me an Etch A Sketch to practice with and they said, no, Neil, we will have Sandra come in.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I don't know. That pen works a little differently on your screen here. The Etch A Sketch, I am no good on, though.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know. You got to shake it real hard.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sandra, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: She's worked a long day. A lot of people have.\par \par \uc2 Now, in the meantime here, a lot of people talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and how he reacted last night to the fact that at least one key opponent is staying in the race and not going anywhere, very different from this reaction you might recall a little more than a week ago in Iowa. Take a look at that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I want to congratulate Ron and Nikki for having a good -- a good time together. We're all having a good time together.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And I think they both actually did very well. I also want to congratulate the Vivek, because he did a hell of a job. They all did. They're very smart, very smart people, very capable people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that was after Iowa.\par \par \uc2 And after New Hampshire, a bit of a different side of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , some say going back to his more familiar side to a lot of folks when it turned out that \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  wasn't going away anytime soon and was celebrating her performance in New Hampshire. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Who the hell was the impostor that went up on the stage before and, like, claimed a victory? She did very poorly, actually.\par \par \uc2 And when I watched her in the fancy dress that probably wasn't so fancy...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: ... come up, I said, what's she doing? We won.\par \par \uc2 I think that she actually appointed you, Tim.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And think of it, appointed, and you're the senator of the state, and she endorsed me. You must really hate her.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that went over well, but the fact of the matter is two very different Donald Trumps there, the same guy. Of course, he can keep it measured or keep it feisty. It works, no matter what, it would seem.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Bedford of The Washington Examiner, Julia Manchester, The Hill and national politics correspondent.\par \par \uc2 Julia, to you first on the two different Donald Trumps we saw there. He was getting wide praise over the last few days about how diplomatic he's been, how very gracious he's been. So there's something obviously that \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  did that stuck in his craw, the fact that she is staying in this race, the fact that she came out of this telling her supporters she did a great job.\par \par \uc2 He obviously disagrees with that and it stuck with him. What did you think?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE HILL: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is frustrated, Neil. I think that's what you saw last night.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to be able to call himself the presumptive Republican nominee and essentially coast to the finish line at the Republican National Convention this summer.\par \par \uc2 But it appears he's very much on that path, but the problem is, you still have \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . You still have \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  getting some of this limelight when it comes to the attention surrounding the primary. So that's really starting to get under his skin.\par \par \uc2 I think there's also pressure to want to wrap up the nomination in time for the day before Super Tuesday, which is the first day of the federal trial and his alleged efforts to overturn the 2020 election results, so definitely a frustration there with \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, look, we have, what, 30 or so days to go until South Carolina, so a lot can happen in between now and then.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Sarah, let's say that \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  stays in the race right through South Carolina a month from now, and her point is to just continue chipping away against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and to be that default candidate should something happen to him. Now, some of her people seem to indicate to me that is part of her strategy. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SARAH BEDFORD, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Well, I think that her claim to stay in the race, right, is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in both Iowa and New Hampshire really only got around 50 percent of Republican voters, which means about half of the Republican primary base in two states that looked very different support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and about half don't.\par \par \uc2 And so \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , now that she is really the only opponent left in the race, has a chance to consolidate that I don't want to say anti-Trump vote, but the non-Trump vote. The last thing that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has ever wanted was for this to turn into a two-man race.\par \par \uc2 And it's doing so at a time when he really hasn't been able to break away and start hitting 60, 70 percent or the kind of margins that suggest he is the runaway favorite of the whole base. So the worry for him is that, well, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  has all these fund-raising advantages, she's heading into her home state and she is consolidating that non-Trump vote, that she will give him more of a run for his money than he might have gotten otherwise if other so-called spoilers had stayed in the primary.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm also wondering, ladies, and I want to show you some examples of this, where the age issue, which could be a predominant one for both \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , assuming they are eventually each party's nominee, and how it plays out.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, Republicans are fond of stringing together these images of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Some people said the blood of liberty -- or, excuse me, the -- excuse me -- the tree of liberty is watered with the blood of patriots.\par \par \uc2 And we brought Israelis and Palestinians together at a political level on the -- in -- at the -- in Aqaba and Sharm.\par \par \uc2 America is a nation that can be defined in a single word. Excuse me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, Republicans have been fond of bashing the president on that.\par \par \uc2 Then along comes \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  bashing her party's front-runner for essentially the same thing, and these are some of the examples that her campaign at least has been citing. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: By the way, they never report the crowd on January 6. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  is in charge of security. We offered her 10,000 people, soldiers,\par \par \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 , did anyone ever hear of him? He's probably one of the strongest leaders anywhere in the world. He's the leader of Turkey.\par \par \uc2 Sioux Falls, thank you very much, Sioux Falls.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we all make mistakes under pressure like that.\par \par \uc2 But, Julia, the one thing that certainly \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  was pouncing on in my last interview with her is this mistaking her for \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  in January 6 and all of that, that this is what you get, she says, when you get a -- two 80-year-old guys going at it. The president, that is, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , is 77.\par \par \uc2 But what did you make of that?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Yes, look, with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , I think there's a perception among voters that he seems older than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and he is older than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by a few years, but I think he acts older in a number of ways.\par \par \uc2 I think it's easier to pull those clips if you're going to go after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to sort of draw that contrast. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , at least from a public- facing view, still appears to have a lot of energy. Yes, he's mixed up those names in those comments, and it was easy for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  to pounce on that.\par \par \uc2 But I think it works, it's probably a better strategy with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , though I think it's fair to ask. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would be 80 if he were elected president. So that's a fair question.\par \par \uc2 But also on the ground in New Hampshire, Neil, and in Iowa, I was asking Republican voters, undeclared voters about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's age and these attacks from \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . And to a lot of these voters, it didn't really seem to resonate in a way. I mean, they acknowledged there was concern there, but it's not their top concern when they're going to the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 So if they're unhappy, for example, with the economy under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , they're not necessarily going to take a pause when thinking about voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because of his age.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, Sarah, obviously, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  and her forces are going to say, you string this stuff together and you focus on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the fact that he is an older guy, 77 years old, and he suffers some of the same kind of flubs that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  does, obviously trying to link it to that, it's open season on the nastiness.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: That's right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's kind of to be expected the vibe of a primary campaign. But I don't think there's any real comparison of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's abilities versus \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's abilities right now in terms of the minds of voters. And what's -- the problem for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is that when you look at footage of him now, relative to footage of him even just 10 years ago, there's a pretty noticeable difference in his energy levels, in his ability to string a sentence together, frankly.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  now is the same \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of 10 years ago, of 20 years ago. He's always been zany. He's always gone off on these tangents. He's not declining relative to what the public knows of him, whereas \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is just demonstrably less youthful and less energetic than he was when he was a senator, when he was a vice president.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: And that's what voters are picking up on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We all get old. I don't even remember what I had for breakfast this morning.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, ladies, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 But on, we go. We're going to nastier as we go as well.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, \uc2 Andy Sabin\uc2 , remember of the billionaire backer of \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 ? And he made very, very clear, Neil, if she loses, that's it, I'm not giving another penny.\par \par \uc2 Well, apparently, the \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  folks caught that and said, take your money and -- well, they didn't say that, but I will let him explain, because he's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDY SABIN\uc2 , GOP DONOR: You know what Custer's last stand is? She must win in New Hampshire, not come in second by two points or one point. In order to be a viable candidate, she must clearly show that she's a winner. She has to win, or I don't think she should go on.\par \par \uc2 I think it's political suicide to continue for her unless she's a clear winner in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that's a political donor \uc2 Andy Sabin\uc2 , very big in political circles, as much for his influence and power as his money.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  folks saw that exchange I had with Andy and immediately said, you can keep your $6,600 or whatever it is. They had some other choice things to say. We have been trying to get them to come on, have not heard back.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Andy Sabin\uc2  is back with us.\par \par \uc2 Andy, you still stick by what you said. She had to win. She didn't. So you're not giving her any more financial support. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SABIN: Right. Neil, thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 Neil, do you know Kenny Rogers is?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, of course.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: You have to know when to fold them. You have got to know when to fold them and you have got to know when to walk away. It's time for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  to walk away.\par \par \uc2 I will be the first person to write her a check for president in 2028. There's absolutely no upside to her going to South Carolina. And there's a tremendous downside. She's down by 31 points now, and that's the last I heard, in South Carolina. She's got two senators and a governor supporting Trump.\par \par \uc2 Going to South Carolina...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So what? But so what, Andy? So what?\par \par \uc2 I mean, you're very good with numbers. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has 32 delegates, and \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  has 17. Last time I checked, you need 1,215 to get the nomination. You're quite right. It would look prohibitive for her in South Carolina, where she does trail in these polls.\par \par \uc2 But if we're to knock someone out this early in the game because it doesn't look good, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  would have never had a shot. \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  would have never had a shot.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: She never -- look, if you can't win your own state, there's no reason to go on. If I were her...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, she hasn't lost it yet. She hasn't lost it yet. You had faith in her going into New Hampshire, and you're quite right, and you're within your right to say, now that she's lost it, you think her odds are winning or next to nil. I get that.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But do you think that that -- there's any value at all to staying in a race? It might be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's to lose, but she could give him a run for his money, and she could test his mettle, and all benefit.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: Look, Neil, there's no upside. She's surrounded by consultants and staff. They don't want to lose their paychecks. So they tell \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  what she wants to hear, not what she should hear.\par \par \uc2 And what she should hear, there's no viable path to continue. You can only harm yourself by going to South Carolina and losing by 30-plus points in your home state. It's political suicide.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now -- so now you're putting your support behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as the presumptive nominee, but you're no huge fan of his. You're not going to give him any money, but he's your default.\par \par \uc2 So what's so different about your approach versus hers?\par \par \uc2 SABIN: What's different about my approach? I'm sorry. Could you repeat...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, you don't -- last time we chatted, you're not a big fan of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's, but he's -- he looks like the nominee.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: No, I'm still -- look...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So explain why you're backing him.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: Look, I told you it was either my dog or Biden, and I chose my dog over Biden.\par \par \uc2 As long as he's the Republican nominee, I'm going to support him. There is no reason -- I hope Donald's listening -- where he couldn't be the best president we ever had. He's got great policies, OK?\par \par \uc2 But, for example, what -- he doesn't need to attack Haley. He won. Why attack? What does he gain by attacking these people? He should focus..\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So what did you make of what he did last night? He was pretty vitriolic in his response to her staying in the race. And that showed kind of like the old \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He had been very gracious prior to that and maybe he returns to the gracious thing, usually saves that from when candidates leave, and then he's fine with them.\par \par \uc2 But I'm just wondering, what do you make of that? Does that part bother you?\par \par \uc2 SABIN: Yes, he -- I don't understand his logic.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: And I said this before. He sticks his foot in his mouth. He's got everything going for him. Why not be gracious?\par \par \uc2 Go ahead, worry about the country. Worry about winning. We have so many issues. He doesn't need to knock people. He's already beaten Nikki. Everybody knows that. On. Onward.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 But you didn't get to be a wealthy guy and a good businessman accepting the consensus and the standard advice. So what's to stop \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  from trying to buck the consensus, challenge the system and keep in the fight?\par \par \uc2 SABIN: Well, look, she's going to lose her money train. She's going to lose her donors.\par \par \uc2 Why not save that money in the event -- she's got 2028. But suppose something unknown happens to Trump over the next six or seven months.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: She has the money to step in again if she had to.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So stay in the race.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: Why not save your money, instead of giving it to these consultants and her staff...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: ... for a rainy day, whether it be 2028 or whether it be stepping in because Trump has a legal problem?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. All the more reason for her just to stick around and see what happens.\par \par \uc2 But, Andy, you raise a lot of great points. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Andy Sabin\uc2  has withdrawn support for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . You heard what he had to say about that. We did reach out to the Haley folks if they wanted to come on and talk about this a little bit more. They did not. But hope springs eternal. We always would love to have them on.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, some very big news, the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  placing its bets on one \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and will fully support and work hard for his reelection.\par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain, the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  president, will be joining us. And now people wait for what the Teamsters might do. \uc2 Sean O'Brien\uc2 , the president of the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 , will be joining us as well.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, this was the scene.\par \par \uc2 If we can go wide here just to see how bizarre this was, this guy, this migrant caught in this wire mesh that they're now trying to remove against the governor's wishes, tried to talk him out of this. And they got him out.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, as endorsements go, they don't get any bigger than this if you're running for president of the United States, the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  coming out for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, that's a lot of personnel and power to commit to the president's reelection. The guy behind it kind enough to join us, Shawn Fain, the head of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Shawn, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN, PRESIDENT, UNITED AUTO WORKERS: Hey, thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You could have considered \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You did not. Why not?\par \par \uc2 FAIN: Well, I mean, if you look at just the facts and the body of work of both candidates and both of them in their own words, nowhere in history has \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ever stood for the American worker.\par \par \uc2 He stands against pretty much everything that we stand for.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you committed to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Many people are noticing it seems very late to commit to him. Were there any doubts? Were there any concerns?\par \par \uc2 FAIN: No, never a doubt.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, we have a process we follow. And our leadership, as we said, our endorsements are going to be earned and not freely given anymore. And so we had to look at a lot of things. And, overall, we just had our contract fight with the Big Three, and our most successful contract in history.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden was -- stood there with us on the picket line, unlike President Trump back in '19 when GM was on strike for 40 days, and he was completely nonexistent and silent on the issue.\par \par \uc2 I can go down through the list of things, the difference in the candidates, but it's very clear to us who stands with working-class people in this country and who stands against them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Your counterpart of the Teamsters, \uc2 Sean O'Brien\uc2 , has met with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and, of course, the president, all the major candidates when they were still in the race. You opted not to go that route. And I'm just curious as to why.\par \par \uc2 FAIN: Well, you look at the body of work.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I'm not going to speak for President O'Brien, but I will speak for the \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 FAIN: And in 2008-2009, the economic recession, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  blamed the workers for what was wrong with these companies.\par \par \uc2 In 2015, he talked about doing a rotation of good-paying jobs in the Midwest, somewhere where they'd pay less and have people begging for their jobs back at lower wages. In '19 and also in '15, when \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  workers voted to organize, he put an \uc2 NLRB\uc2  in place that killed the organizing -- that killed the organizing the contract for those workers.\par \par \uc2 In '19, when he was president, he didn't support the strike. He told workers at \uc2 Lordstown\uc2  assembly plant, which was closing, don't sell your houses. And then he did nothing to support them, versus President Biden, who in 2023, when a plant was going to close in Belvidere, Illinois, for \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , he stood with those workers.\par \par \uc2 He helped us save a community and helped bring not one plant, but two plants back to life. And he stood with our members on the picket line in our fight for economic justice.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He's also been a big proponent, Shawn, as you know, far better than I, for E.V.s, electric vehicles. Many automakers that said this has sort of been the bane of their existence, that the market just isn't there to support what they call the heavy hand of government to force the issue.\par \par \uc2 Sure enough, we learned just a few minutes ago, Shawn, after the bell ended for the day, that Tesla shares are falling after the carmaker forecast slower growth because of weak E.V. sales or weaker-than-expected.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that's a problem for the automakers? You did -- you're quite right. You scored this historic and very generous labor package for your members, but that it's imperiled by this industry push for E.V.s.\par \par \uc2 FAIN: Look, we have to have an environment where we can all live in, where we can breathe clean air and have clean water.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has always led the way in environmental concerns, going back to our founding. And wherever the industry goes, we're going to be a part of it. Our workers are going to be part of it. We're going to fight for those jobs and we're going to fight for it to be a just transition, where it pays good wages, good benefits, so that people can afford to live off that job.\par \par \uc2 And...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think it's being pushed too quickly, though? I guess that's what I'm asking you...\par \par \uc2 FAIN: Well...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... that it comes with the president doing so.\par \par \uc2 FAIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But he has to juggle, I understand, the wishes of environmentalists. And you're an environmentally concerned union chief. I get that.\par \par \uc2 But that too much so, and it could hurt your members going forward, especially if these sales just languish.\par \par \uc2 FAIN: Well, I mean, look, we still make ICE engines and ICE vehicles and we're in both markets.\par \par \uc2 And however this goes, we're going to protect our workers. But being concerned about -- there's infrastructure that has to be put in place still. All those things are being worked. As far as the timetable goes, adjustments may have to be made. Businesses always make adjustments when they launch new plants or create new businesses.\par \par \uc2 They have a deadline target, and a lot of times those get moved. So it's just going to be a matter of how things develop and how the market goes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There's always a dichotomy. I mean, the union membership in the past was very strong for \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  in both elections. George Bush Sr. benefited from the union vote. Many of your own members now, Shawn, are very big Trump supporters.\par \par \uc2 I don't know the breakdown. You know that far better than I, but that a good many of them are at Trump rallies and are MAGA enthusiasts. How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 FAIN: Look, every -- that's what makes this country a beacon for other countries. It's democracy in action.\par \par \uc2 Look, let me be clear about this. A great majority of our members will not vote for President Biden. Yes, some will. But that's the reality of this. The majority of our members are going to vote their paychecks. They're going to vote for an economy that works for them, and they're going to vote for a president -- when you look at these two presidents, the choice is very clear about which one stands up with the working class and stands up for labor and which one stands for the billionaire class.\par \par \uc2 And that's his base.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We shall watch very closely.\par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain, thank you very much for taking the time. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 FAIN: Oh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the head of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  now making the push for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to get reelected president, Shawn Fain, the head there.\par \par \uc2 Another Sean, \uc2 Sean O'Brien\uc2  of the Teamsters, still holding off support for candidates, but he has met with quite a few, including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the S&P 500 has never been higher, my friends.\par \par \uc2 Even though the Dow had a tough day of it, the Nasdaq did not. The S&P 500, in case you're counting, that's the fourth straight record for this average that is more representative of America, 500 companies from all sorts of industries. A lot of people use that as sort of a Good Housekeeping stamp of approval for how America is doing. And, today, it was doing very well, people buying in like crazy.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, we told you what the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is going to do now in supporting \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to get reelected president. The Teamsters have not made a decision this issue, \uc2 Sean O'Brien\uc2  doing his homework, he says, talking to all the candidates, I mean, even those who have since left the race. And that includes \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This was from my chat with him earlier.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEAN O'BRIEN\uc2 , GENERAL PRESIDENT, TEAMSTERS: We met with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  two weeks ago, and we are meeting with our general executive board and rank- and-file members at the Teamsters headquarters on January 31.\par \par \uc2 We have extended the invitation to President Biden. We're talking to the White House now to try and schedule them both on the same day. And we think it's important that we're meeting with all candidates. We have a very diverse membership, 1.3 million members. So we owe it to our members to do our due diligence and make the recommendation what's in the best interest of Teamster members nationwide.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sean, the other Shawn, S-H-A-W-N, Fain of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , had said of a meeting with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that it would be pointless.\par \par \uc2 Quoting him here: "I see no point in meeting with him because I don't think the man has any bit of care about our workers and what they stand for, what the working class stands for." He went on to say: "He serves a billionaire class and that's what's wrong with this country."\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, look...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, meeting a waste of time, what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: I mean, everybody has their opinions.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure. Sure.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: My opinion is, I think everybody has their own structure and opinion in doing things.\par \par \uc2 But dialogue is very important. It's been very important to our organization. It's been very important. And, look, we have conversations with billionaires every single day, corporate America, who we negotiate against, who we have to deal with. And a lot of times you find common ground when you have a dialogue.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I'm not speaking for any other union. I'm speaking for the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 . We're not going to be influenced by any pressure to endorse one candidate or the other. We understand that there's a lot of pressure out there to make an endorsement. We're going to do what we have historically done. We're going to make our decision after we get input from our members and our general executive board.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's been quite a while since you considered a Republican. What would make you switch now?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, look, there's a lot of issues that are important to us.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of issues that -- we have got to deal with national right to work, which we are not for. We have got to deal with making organizing members that much easier without threats of retaliation. We need bankruptcy reform. We need a lot of commitments. There's a lot of boxes that need to be checked off.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has done great work on behalf of the unions. He's done real well fixing the pensions. But at the end of the day, what we have done in the past doesn't make it guaranteed for the future. And we have got a very diverse membership. And our members vote. We know what our members -- how they vote, and they can influence an election.\par \par \uc2 So we need to make sure that all the boxes are checked off on behalf of our members.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sean, they certainly vote, and a lot of them are in the MAGA crowd. They love \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So they don't often mimic union bosses.\par \par \uc2 I don't want to paraphrase you that way, but that there is a difference between the higher-ups and the rank and file. What do you notice at the Teamsters?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, that's the perception.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: I know who I work for every day. I have bosses. I have got 1.3 million bosses that give me -- elect me to lead this great organization.\par \par \uc2 So, look, at the end of the day, everybody has their opinions. It's a different time. To your point, we endorsed Nixon, we endorsed Reagan, we endorsed George Bush Sr. Historically, over the last several years, the Democrats have gotten the endorsement consistently.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: But it's like anything else. We have got to take a step back, look what we did in history, and see if that's going to help us moving forward.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of the day, the decision that's going to be made is going to be an inclusive decision. It's not going to be one-sided. We're not going to take a hard line and say, we're not meeting with certain people. I will meet with anybody any time, especially if it's in the best interest of our members.\par \par \uc2 And I can't speak for anybody else's thought process, but I can only speak on behalf of the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: When you did meet with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , how did it go?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: It went fine. I mean, we put our cards on the table. It was a very matter-of-fact meeting.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: He claimed he was 100 percent supportive of unions, but history, obviously, take a look back, and there's certain issues that we have with him.\par \par \uc2 We told him what our issue is moving forward is and what we want to see a president of the United States...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A lot of workers didn't like working with him. They said he wouldn't pay them. He wouldn't pay them overtime, that he reneged on contracts. I don't know how true a lot of that is, but there's a lot of burnt bridges with his own former workers.\par \par \uc2 Did enter your thinking process?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Yes. I mean, we had a lot of dialogue on that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: We said, look, there's a lot of information out there that contradicts what you said.\par \par \uc2 And he assured that, moving forward, that he's pro-worker, pro-creating job, just like everybody else says. And the one thing that we are good at, regardless of who wins this presidential election, we are going to be a conscience to any candidate, whether we support them or not, to remind them of what they said and what they are doing. I think that's important.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Sean O'Brien\uc2  of the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He's not given any indication as to how soon he will make a decision on who he's supporting for president, but he says it will be with a lot of homework and a lot of time. So we shall see.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, an interesting incident at the border today concerning all of that razor wire that now judges are saying, you have to remove it, and you better remove them pretty fast.\par \par \uc2 Danamarie McNicholl in the middle of all of that in Eagle Pass -- Danamarie.\par \par \uc2 DANAMARIE MCNICHOLL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It's been a busy day at the border with a high river and a fast current migrants are trying to cross. We will have all of that coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it's all about fixing the mess at the border, but good luck fixing it.\par \par \uc2 The two parties remain far apart, and then, lo and behold, a bizarre incident in Eagle Pass, Texas, that sort of brought all of this to light.\par \par \uc2 Dana -- Danamarie McNicholl -- I'm sorry -- with more from there -- Danamarie.\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL: Neil, Texas is not backing down, reinforcing areas with even more razor wire in areas that migrants have frequently climbed over, just like we saw this morning.\par \par \uc2 A FOX News drone captured 30 migrants climbing onto a shipping container using some of that clothing to protect themselves from the razor wire. Texas DPS waited on the other side to arrest the group for criminal trespassing.\par \par \uc2 Today, Texas Governor Greg Abbott released a statement on the Lone Star States right to defend itself, saying in part: "President Biden has enticed illegal immigrants."\par \par \uc2 Now, the recent Supreme Court decision gave legal authority to Border Patrol to cut the razor wire. Nothing here has been cut. The Texas National Guard told me Border Patrol has not tried to access Shelby Park where hundreds of yards of razor wire line the Rio Grande. The river, it's fast, it's dangerous right now after officials released water from a damn upstream.\par \par \uc2 FOX cameras captured a male trying to cross the strong current, swept him downstream, but ultimately he managed to come ashore. The Texas National Guard did pick him up. And Texas DPS says the Rio Grande averages a drowning every single day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. CHRIS OLIVAREZ, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : Texas is doing every measure, putting every measure in place to prevent that, to try to discourage that. But yet, at the federal level, the federal government has not done anything as far as trying to discourage illegal immigration, especially those that want to cross a dangerous river.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL: And this area in Shelby Park is secure, but hundreds of people are crossing a day illegally into the United States where there aren't any barriers or razor wire -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Danamarie, thank you very much. Great report.\par \par \uc2 So, more on that and more on history being repeated here, this particular candidate has no chance, thinking that a comeback was in the offing. I'm not talking about the woman you probably hear who's still in the race. I'm talking about \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . He was the comeback kid back in 1992. Could history repeat itself?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think we know enough to say with some certainty that New Hampshire tonight has made \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  the comeback kid.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We learned later on that \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , who came in second in New Hampshire, he came up with the comeback kid thing, and it worked. It glided him even after losing Iowa and then stumbling in New Hampshire, but good enough for second place finish.\par \par \uc2 He was well on his way. I thought of that in getting \uc2 Douglas Brinkley\uc2  on the show, one of my favorite historians, great presidential historian on the moonshot and everything else.\par \par \uc2 But, Doug, we forget that it's still early in the process. I think it takes a little over 1,200 delegates to determine the Republican nominee. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's got 32 delegates. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  has about 17. So we have a long way to go. \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  was kind of acknowledging the same, I guess, in 1992.\par \par \uc2 So what do you make of that, that it's still a long haul here?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS BRINKLEY\uc2 , PRESIDENTIAL HISTORIAN: Well, you're right, Neil, that the comeback kid was a great line by \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and New Hampshire gave him new life.\par \par \uc2 He was besieged with personal problems...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRINKLEY: ... \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , the deal with Gennifer Flowers, and overcome that, draft dodging of Vietnam, marijuana, these issues.\par \par \uc2 And he famously went on "60 Minutes" after a Super Bowl and got emotive with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and then ran well in New Hampshire, being very emotive to people by asking for forgiveness. And the person that he came in second to was Paul Tsongas from Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRINKLEY: And by getting rid of -- coming in, in two Paul Tsongas, that was good news for Clinton, because Paul Tsongas had no outreach in the South. So by the time \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  hit South Carolina, he was looking to be a front-runner.\par \par \uc2 And in New Hampshire, he beat heavyweights like Tom Harkin and \uc2 Mario Cuomo\uc2  and...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, no doubt. No doubt. And we forget. Now, the comparison to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is wildly off base. And I readily admit that.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is running away with this thing. He's the only other competitor, was the only other competitor in this particular state. He's won Iowa and New Hampshire right now. So he's the prohibitive favorite.But there are a lot of people who've been on this show and on my FOX Business show, Doug, who said, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's wasting her time. There's no benefit to staying in the race.\par \par \uc2 What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 BRINKLEY: I don't agree with that. She is not wasting her time.\par \par \uc2 Look, automatically, if she sticks through to Super Tuesday, she's going to be number two everywhere. That's not a bad legacy. She's beaten so many powerhouse figures, including DeSantis and Scott and others.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what if she loses South Carolina, her home state, by what could be 20, 30 or more points?\par \par \uc2 BRINKLEY: I think it's over then.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRINKLEY: I think because of just luck of the draw, she's from South Carolina. She might as well give it a try down there.\par \par \uc2 The downside for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is if he starts attacking her personally more or going after her children, because you could get her very inflamed. And if it's a close loss in South Carolina, she might, in a mode of spite, continue to go onwards. And, remember, the No Labels party is scouting for somebody to lead it.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  is on their short list, although I don't at this moment think she would do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think of his different reaction to her staying in the race versus right after Iowa, when so many were quitting the race? He was very statesmanlike, a little less so last night.\par \par \uc2 What did you think?\par \par \uc2 BRINKLEY: I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  likes to annihilate his opponents.\par \par \uc2 And he did beat \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  handily in New Hampshire. Probably, if it wasn't going to South Carolina, her home state, meaning someplace that Trump was just going to wallop her, maybe she would have dropped out. But I think it makes sense. She's got the money. She's got a donor base. Give it a whirl in South Carolina, see what happens.\par \par \uc2 But it's garnering the ire of Trump. And the question is what he will do or Vivek or one of his surrogates will do to try to smear Haley more and more. And that gets ugly, like it was with \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and \uc2 John McCain\uc2  many moons ago.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will have to watch it closely.\par \par \uc2 Douglas, I wish we always had more time with all this breaking news and labor leaders back and forth. I hope to get you back.\par \par \uc2 Douglas Brinkley\uc2 , a presidential historian, so many great books that really make you think. Sometimes, we repeat history or, at our peril, ignore it.\par \par \uc2 All right, just a reminder, again, we had a record on the S&P 500, so the bull market is still going on, the Dow a little less fortunate, Nasdaq doing just fine.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240125ek1o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 White House Blames Texas For Drowning Three Migrants; Democrats Challenger Mocks President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  For Not Campaigning; Shoplifting Lunacy: Big Stores Are Now Locking Up Underwear As Theft Surges; Student Debt Strike: Some Borrowers Refusing To Pay Out Of Protest; Because My Child Is Screaming? \uc2 Panera Bread\uc2  Drive-Thru Refuses To Take A Mom's Order\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters, Dana Perino, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,357 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 January 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I was there, too. But will I remember though, Meridith, is how cold it was. That's all I remember.\par \par \uc2 Meridith McGraw, Politico reporter on those developments here.\par \par \uc2 So, they're talking, that's promising. They're not saying overly nasty things about each other. That's kind of promising. We'll see. Here's THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Judge Jeanine Pirro, along with Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters, Dana Perino and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 The White House is doubling down on a new border hoax. Two years after falsely accusing border agents of whipping migrants. The administration is now falsely claiming Texas Governor Greg Abbott for the tragic drowning deaths of three migrants.\par \par \uc2 Last week, a mother and her two children drowned in the Rio Grande while attempting to cross the border. The White House not waiting for the facts, instead blasting out a statement that claims that Texas officials "blocked border agents from attempting a rescue by shutting down a part of a public park".\par \par \uc2 And of course, the liberal media ate up every word.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In Texas, the Republican governor prevents federal officials who are trying to rescue a woman and her two children who drowned trying to cross a river in Texas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Agents were physically barred by Texas authorities from entering the area.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He's been talking about how Texas has done everything except shoot these migrants and it almost sounds as though he was lamented, so he let them drown.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, FORMER LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR OF MARYLAND: His troopers, his forces are preventing federal officials law enforcement from saving a life or lives in this case.\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, MSNBC HOST: These are families, these are mothers, children. I just -- I am actually just disgusted with where we have landed here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But it was all a lie. Border Patrol was never blocked from saving them. Biden's own Department of Justice confirming the three migrants drowned one hour before Border Patrol even arrived at the gate to inform Texas and Texas officials are asking the Biden administration to correct the record about the migrant drownings, but the White House is standing by this smear.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Our statement is consistent with DOJ's filing. As the DOJ filing said there was an ongoing emergency situation that Border Patrol was blocked from accessing.\par \par \uc2 There were other migrants in the water as well.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Then, why wasn't that included in the statement? That's what you were referring to.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: There were other migrants in the water as well.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: If that's what you were referring to?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Our statement does -- is very much consistent with DOJ filing. Anything else specific you want to know about that? I would certainly refer you to DOJ.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right. Jesse, the White House is lying here like they lied about whip gate a couple of years ago and they get away with it and there are no consequences. And most of the print media won't even write a correction.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: No, they recycle the propaganda. That's the game. There's so many hoaxes just in the last couple of weeks. Remember dictator on day one. The SEAL Team 6 assassination squad, Mar-a- Lago is only $18 million. Biden talks to Hunter's clients about the weather. Trump wants the economy to crash.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's just the last couple of weeks off the top of my head. Now you have that these people were drowning and Abbott blocked Biden's lifeguards from a rescue.\par \par \uc2 We are going to face just such a torrent of disinformation this election year, it's going to be a struggle. And as they recycle the propaganda, they all tell us in the media, don't do your own research.\par \par \uc2 Remember Phil Bump \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , the guy that scrambled out of the podcast when he didn't know what the heck he was talking about with the laptop. He just prints this piece today. Doing your own research is a good way to end up being wrong.\par \par \uc2 Now, this guy's a journalist who believes everything the government tells them and then writes it all down. Philip Bump is always wrong because he never does his research.\par \par \uc2 Now as a journalist, I am constantly issuing clarifications. When I get new information, like if something is said by Harold or Jessica and then in the commercial break, we find out they're wrong. Next block, issue a clarification for the sake of the show because we don't want any disinformation out here.\par \par \uc2 If Biden, Judge, really wanted to secure the border and stop the deaths, he would stop the asylum craziness. He would stop handing out free iPhones, bus tickets, and asylum to everybody in the third world.\par \par \uc2 What they want to do and this is not me saying this. This is the Democrats saying this, they want people to pick the crops, they want people to have babies and they want to turn Texas blue. Again, not me saying it, that's them.\par \par \uc2 And why are they attacking Greg Abbott? Because he's in a wheelchair. That's probably why they're doing this, ableists.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, Harold doesn't having an open border and automatic catch and release with a bus and plane and a ferry available and extra service, doesn't that end up promoting illegal immigration and the deadly consequences that we're seeing here?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: It's good to be here. Two things, one, it does all those things. I was pleased to see -- I think the most important thing for me, there's no excuse for when things -- when you can get things wrong. But when you're confronted with the facts, you should -- you should say the truth. Just like I think about Texas, that most horrible thing has happened in Texas, one of the things nothing to do with politics, it was that shooting at that school. And they didn't get the facts right at first, they kept telling things and commenting about and Uvalde, Texas.\par \par \uc2 So, the White House should be candid about what they got right, what they didn't get right. What I'm most -- and hope you don't have to correct me on this. What I'm most concerned about in the coming hours, it looks as if Republicans and Democrats met at the White House in the last hour, and the Republican leadership in the House came out. And they generally speak. I think sometimes politicians do in superlatives. They didn't this time, they came on to look, we stood our ground, we share with the president what we wanted and they made it sound like something positive might have happened, they didn't say anything negative happened.\par \par \uc2 And then the Democrats came out and said something similar. If we want these things to stop, if we want a different policy, all the things we've talked about at this table, it's got to be our elected officials who come around the proverbial table at the White House and in Congress and get something done.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm encouraged about what they've done. I'll tell you, there's a guy though who got my book by blood rolling a little bit earlier. Well, I think does not have the right to be around a table in Congress. He's a congressman from Texas named Congressman Nehls. I hope I'm pronouncing his name correctly.\par \par \uc2 He was quoted a few days ago saying, let me tell you, I'm not willing to do too damn much right now to help a Democrat and to help \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's dismal approving ratings. I will not help the Dems try to improve this man's approval ratings. Why would i?\par \par \uc2 Sir, you got elected to Congress, any Democrat that utters this nonsense or Republican. Judge, you can (INAUDIBLE) all you want. I don't think this is what people in politics should be doing, you should always be trying to solve problems.\par \par \uc2 I hope he comes to the table with his Republican friends and Democrat friends and Democrats who make equally asinine comments like we saw, someone not wanting to correct the record here at the beginning of the show. You have to call balls and strikes everywhere. We want answers to our border. Congress has to act and the president has to act. Hopefully, they will.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Are you saying that no Democrat ever said that he was going to criticize and stop \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from doing everything he could do during the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  years? And don't act like it's just one side and not -- and not the other.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Well, Judge, not only that, not only am I not saying that. I'm criticizing Democrats. I criticize \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I'm just -- I call balls and strikes. And I think we all should.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right. Dana 18 percent approval rating Biden has on immigration, all he's got to do is enforce the law. He doesn't need Congress and when Iowa's 40 percent immigration is the biggest issue, how much trouble is he in?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And also there was an informal poll taken amongst the Republican conference yesterday, absolute number one issue is the border way more than the economy, but they're not alone. The Democrats are joining them in that.\par \par \uc2 And one thing you could point to, so \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was named the border czar 1029 days ago. She goes -- she goes on "The View", like we're going to talk about that. This is not an issue that she's ever asked about or has to deal with. And it was -- it is the main issue driving their approval numbers down and everything else is coming down along with that. Of course, immigration being a big issue as well.\par \par \uc2 The only thing you have to do at the White House is acknowledge there's a problem and try to fix it. Why did you run to -- run for Congress anyway.\par \par \uc2 But they have incentivized the migrants to come and that is being helped also by blue state governors like Governor Newsom who says, OK, well, free health cares here for you as well.\par \par \uc2 Then you start to read some of these stories about how there's migrants standing outside in this cold with no coats. I have a family that is helping in Denver, Colorado at nonprofits trying to figure out a way how can we get them some warm enough clothes, because they care about the human condition.\par \par \uc2 And the Biden administration is so willing to lie about their own citizens. You think about the Texas State Patrol or Border Patrol that the state folks there that they were accusing of letting these people die. They're American taxpaying citizens.\par \par \uc2 And you think about Governor Abbott, he's put up with so much especially being attacked because he's in a wheelchair, that's a little bit too far.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, I would just say that these poll numbers 18 percent, imagine just trying to get it to 30 percent. That would just be like a little drop in the bucket, but they're not willing to do anything.\par \par \uc2 If I were the White House, I would have said, our bad. Sorry. Got that information, and then you can move on. Now they won't be able to.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, and they won't. Go ahead, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, if I didn't know any better I think the White House has a pretty low opinion of Americans who actually everyday tried to save the lives that are put at risk by the White House.\par \par \uc2 So, I go back and I listen to this story. And it's obviously we go through a lot of these stories. But it reminds me that the two most consequential words of the last eight years was fake news. Because I think that you can't listen to these stories again the same way. When you hear them, you hear them differently.\par \par \uc2 When I see the montage of them, feigning outrage, I know they don't care, that MSNBC crew of clowns, they don't care about those victims, right? Those are just products for their script. And they're grateful that they've got it and they can create a script around it.\par \par \uc2 The good news is, I don't think the lie could travel around the world before the truth comes out. I think right now, we have an educated public that can see this, even though it seems like the Dems have hired Jussie Smollett as a campaign adviser, because every single one of their stories is a hoax. It's a hate crime hoax.\par \par \uc2 And if it's -- if you accuse somebody of a crime that has some kind of racial component, wouldn't a hoax also be a hate crime? What about the people that are accused of this stuff? What about the whip gate guys? They never got a formal apology.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you could conceivably ruin people's lives by saying those three people died on your watch. That could ruin somebody's life. Why is there no punishment for that? I don't get it.\par \par \uc2 But these stories repeat themselves because like you said, there's no disincentive. You rely on the media to amplify the story while bearing the debunking. So these scumbags that MSNBC could destroy a person's life, call them a murderer. But when they're exposed, they don't even make a retraction. Or if they do, it's days away, or it's on page 23, B7, whatever.\par \par \uc2 And yet, if any of us said anything, anything or any politician, Republican said anything that would be slightly problematic. They would ruin you in an instant. They would ruin you in an instant.\par \par \uc2 In this case, they say some really horrible things. I am heartened though that this isn't going to fly anymore this year. You look at X's community notes, you see how quickly people respond. They can't get away with this. I couldn't watch that montage with going no one's taking that seriously. No one believes that.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The only reason that travelled so quickly was because it blew up over the weekend. Because they would have asked them the next day at the White House briefing.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Also, shifty shifts, fundraise off it immediately.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Of course.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Very good. All right. Coming up, the Biden reelection campaign continues to be a total disaster. Democrats are now mocking Joe as Bigfoot and Kamala is running to her safe space on "The View".\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: President Biden is so absent from the campaign trail, and from his own mind that the Democrats are starting to mock him, calling him Bigfoot. The big guy's top Dem challenger launching this political ad that pokes fun at the president for ducking the Democratic primary race.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I thought I was good at hiding. So I asked around. Have you seen Joe? I mean, how can you have tens of thousands of people looking for you all the time and not one person finds you? I'd looked for him everywhere, even the Democratic primary debate. No, Joe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And while the White House keeps Biden locked up in the cellar, the White House is now backing on Kamala to be able to put more than two words together. The Veep sharing her deep thoughts with the ladies of "The View".\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY BEHAR, ABC NEWS HOST: What are you going to do to stop the crazies?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I am scared as heck, we should all be scared. But as we know, and certainly this is a table of very powerful women, we don't run away from something when we're scared, we fight back against it.\par \par \uc2 We done a lot of good work, we need to let people know who bring it to them. There's a split screen that you can throw up and see. And it's going to be the choice between what is about respecting our democracy, what is about competence versus chaos.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Dana, interesting. So, Biden gets compared to Bigfoot and Kamala gets to see Bigfoot up close at "The View".\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, really? I didn't see. I couldn't see Bigfoot.\par \par \uc2 A couple of things, the new -- there's another yet again \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  makeover and she's going to come out and she's going to have a new full- court press. They decided to go with the ABC contingent, they got to spend a whole day with her yesterday and they got the -- she got the full treatment online, the podcasts, the nightly news. And of course, "The View".\par \par \uc2 I hated that she said, I'm scared to death. Here's a vice president of the United States say, I'm scared, we're going to win, it's going to be fine. Like, where's the confidence and then she tries to back that off.\par \par \uc2 Then she talks about chaos. What's interesting is that in Iowa, and I know they don't care about these people, but in Iowa, 35 percent of the Republicans who voted said they're -- they want disruption in the country.\par \par \uc2 So, they're not looking for the stability that the Biden administration says that they can continue on for another eight years.\par \par \uc2 And last point on Dean Phillips, a guy who's running against Biden, this creates an interesting pain point for Biden a little bit in New Hampshire, because Biden's name is not on the ballot. Dean Phillips name is on the ballot and many other Democrats are running in that primary. And Biden is nowhere to be found.\par \par \uc2 You can write his name in and they're trying to do that to have to spend like $4 million, I think, to try to get people to write in Biden's name on this ballot. It doesn't -- it doesn't exude confidence.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Not at all. Harold, you know why he's called Bigfoot?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I do not.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: He's got big feet. You know, what do you think of Dean Phillips anyway? I love screwdrivers.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Look, I think if you're going to run against somebody in a primary, especially the president of your own party, I wouldn't be running commercials with Bigfoot, I'd be -- I'd be contrasting myself with him, what I'd be doing differently, whatever the issue might be.\par \par \uc2 But I think it's funny, but I think you got to be serious. If you're running for a lesser office, maybe you get a chuckle. But if you're going to run for the president in your own party, you got to do that.\par \par \uc2 Now, if I were the president, I'd go to New Hampshire. I'd campaign for a day or so and made clear to Democrats there what's at stake.\par \par \uc2 Two or three, with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , it amazes me and Dana's talked about this before, you can't get the three or four bullet points down. I mean, you have to be able to say, our campaign is going to be about touting how we've invested more in America's ability to build innovative things from medicines, to chips, to cars, to ensuring that America is a leader.\par \par \uc2 Two, we're going to make it where every woman and doctor don't have to fear healthcare choices and talking about them, we're going to make sure that we cut taxes for those earning (INAUDIBLE). These are the basic things. I don't know how you're not able to articulate and ramp off these points one by one by one by one and then talk about this, the other stuff.\par \par \uc2 As I've said -- we said in Iowa, when you talk about threats to democracy, it cuts both ways. Republicans think of it as you're saying you're attacking \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And Democrats think of it as saying you should attack \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, this is not -- this is not an on the level kind of a number here. So, I think the economy and border security will continue to be the foremost issues on voters minds.\par \par \uc2 And if the White House and the president and Congress able to address those issues, that'll not only will help the country, but it will help the party, help his party in the process, and I think even help Republicans as well.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Judge, Dean Phillips, Kamala, you pick?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, I agree with Dana, I think -- I think that Kamala maybe she's had some media training. There's something different about her now.\par \par \uc2 But what amazes me is she's kind of like Jill, when Jill says he's got more energy than anybody I know. She says \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can see round corners, really? Could he see around the corners with the baby formula disaster? Could he see around the corner with Afghanistan? Could he see around the corner with the classified documents that he had for 20 years that he wasn't even supposed to take out of this SCIF?\par \par \uc2 I mean, and the whole idea that she's scared and she says, "We should all be scared". She's the Vice President in the United States. She shouldn't be saying, you know, we are -- we've got your back. We've got you. This is America, the Biden administration is working for you.\par \par \uc2 Instead, I mean, they just watch your numbers go lower and lower. And you know what, he's got 18 percent on the -- on immigration, and now he wants to deal with it, now the nine months before the election, now they want to deal with it. We had the biggest month in history last month. I mean, Americans all over consider themselves part of a border state when they're not.\par \par \uc2 And then she says the media is against us. Are you kidding? The media is carrying their water. The media is carrying those stories, we just did it in last block. They won't even correct it when they get something wrong.\par \par \uc2 And finally, this idea that she says the Republicans have nothing to run against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  except his age. Well, 74 percent of Americans think he shouldn't run because of his age. So, nothing she says is saving or helping them.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Jesse, what have you to say about this?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I think the Biden campaign is getting a facelift. And I think Kamala might be the new face of the campaign. Biden probably is going to let Obama take the reins of the campaign. And it's because he's shy, he can't walk, he can't talk, he can't campaign.\par \par \uc2 Every time he goes out there, he fake jogs. He does events, they call him in and he doesn't even deliver a speech. And then he disappears for a few days and then he pops up and screams democracy and then just go back to Delaware and hides.\par \par \uc2 I'm kicking myself, I didn't think of the Bigfoot analogy, that is a sticky analogy. An honest press would gobble that up and run with it. These people make their living on, on content, on conflict, on buzz, and the Bigfoot thing, it's beautiful. I was going to say the White House is like the Bermuda Triangle. Joe goes in there and doesn't see him for a week.\par \par \uc2 But again, these are the kinds of things that if you were covering a campaign, you'd put right out there, but they're covering up a campaign and that's why it's a sin.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All righty then. Ahead, criminals are coming for your underpants. \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and Target are locking up boxers and socks.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Blazing crime is getting so bad in our nation's capital, drivers are now wishing for a snowstorm to stop carjackings.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Washington D.C. is getting devastated by a dramatic spike in robberies and vehicle thefts. And it's got this woman praying for a blizzard to stop the bad guys.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We need at least 10 to 15 inches.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, come on.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It will slow people down. Just think, carjackings will probably go to zero because they can't run.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And America's shoplifting epidemic is getting even more insane. \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and Target have begun locking up underwear and socks and I just don't know what Greg's going to do with that information.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, a friend of mine was at like a \uc2 Walmart\uc2  or CVS in D.C., security guards tackled a guy as they're tackling him because he was shoplifting. A gun falls out of his pocket.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And everybody just kept shopping like this is just the -- what the world we live in.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Why isn't CVS locking up their guns?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: By the way, it's -- I'm forced to go to comm -- go commando now, so it's --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Good to know.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Just with my socks.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Or not.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: So, what's left to steal, right? What is left -- it makes you think what are they not locking up? Like, Chia Pets, greeting cards, Brian Kilmeade's book. At some point though, if there's no commerce and there's not going to be any community, a