r \uc2 PERINO: Watching people eat is disgusting.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It should have been in slow motion.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Why isn't that in slow motion?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It is in slow mothing. It's a sloth.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No, it should have been in slower motion. Anyway --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Gross.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: See you tonight, Luna.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: See you tonight.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You better wait up and wear that thing I like.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. OK, it's time for --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, Fabulous Fashion Finds. OK, everybody knows I'm all about style. And this girl definitely has style. Check out this funky new hairdo she created using nothing but the elements. I call it the Medusa.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, Harold.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: The Tampa Bay the \uc2 Tampa Bay Buccaneers\uc2  are headed to Detroit to play the Lions in a dome. Listen to this -- listen to this question a reporter asked the Tampa coach.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Any special plans to acclimate the team to not only endure, but perform in those kinds of frigid temperatures should you face them in Detroit.\par \par \uc2 TODD BOWLES, HEAD COACH, \uc2 TAMPA BAY BUCCANEERS\uc2 : You do know we play indoors right in the yellow dome? We have to be outside for 20 seconds getting off the bus going under the thing, so we'll be OK.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: They've only played the dome for a couple of decades now. Hopefully, the reporter will study a little harder. And Greg will get into this more tonight.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Do you like gossip?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Do you like learning about dysfunctional people's personal lives - -\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: -- and what makes people tick? Well, buy my book, Get It Together. Preorder it. It's on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  right now.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You tricked us.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020240118ek1h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Push For Votes In Iowa Enters Final 3 Day Stretch; Houthi Rebels Pledge Retaliation For U.S. Strikes; Fierce Winter Storm Snarling Iowa Campaigning; Final Campaign Push 3 Days Out From Iowa Caucuses\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Mike Pompeo, Pete Hegseth, Jack Keane, Aishah  Hasnie, Dana Perino, Peter Doocy \par \uc2 8,875 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 January 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: Cue the Billy Joel music. President Biden there in a coffee shop in Allentown, Pennsylvania. Couple of people said some unkind things on the way in but got to have a thick skin to be president.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: That does it for us. Thanks so much for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm John Roberts. "THE STORY" with Martha in Iowa starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum, and this is the story live in freezing snowy Des Moines, Iowa. It's the center, of course, of the political world for the next few days during the Iowa caucuses, which happen on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Today, my campaign trail interviews with Nikki Haley and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  coming up here on THE STORY. But first, this.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon says that the United States does expect Iran-backed Houthis will retaliate. That, after the United States and allies carried out massive strikes last evening against the group in Yemen. Take a listen now to this new threat as articulated a short time ago. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ABDULRAMAN AL-AHNOUMI, HOUTHI MEDIA OFFICIAL: This rally is the first to condemn this aggression against the Republic of Yemen last night. It is also a message to Britain and the United States of America in particular that they will pay a heavy price for this aggression. These crowds today authorize the leader of the revolution and authorize the military leadership to respond to this aggression at high levels at sea and on land at several levels.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That was the seen in Yemen's capital, Sana'a, as people poured. Look at this -- this is a massive, massive demonstration in the streets of Sana'a, Yemen, the capital there. There's an increasing risk of escalation and expanding the war based on all of this. The Houthis say that their strikes are really motivated against Israel and against the United States and in support of the Palestinians. So, where is all of this headed? General Jack Keane will join us. Pete Hegseth is standing by.\par \par \uc2 But first to former Secretary of State, former CIA Director and Fox News Contributor, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 . He is on Iran's target list, has been since his time as Secretary of State due to the maximum pressure campaign that was instituted under the Trump administration against Iran. Secretary Pompeo, good to have you here, sir. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Thanks, great to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What was your reaction when you heard about this massive strike last night? A lot of U.S. assets involved, including the USS Florida. What did you make of the retaliation? Did you think it was the right tone and right strength?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Well, Martha, I'm glad that we have finally taken a response that is serious, not like the previous ones, which were way too tepid and way too weak. So, this is real. This was a serious response. But my judgment ultimately is you have to cause the Iranians themselves to have real harm done to them, a real cost imposed on them. The Iranians are willing to fight to the last Houthi, right? They won't see this as something that impacts them directly. Every missile, every rocket that's fired on an American is an Iranian-made, Iranian intellectual property, Iranian trained soldier.\par \par \uc2 This is all Iran-backed. And the Houthis are no more fighting to avenge what happened in Gaza than a man in the moon. They are fighting because the Iranians have put them up to this and so that that's the place we're going to have to go. That's who will have to impose Gaza or, Martha, to your point as you open the show, we'll likely see continued attacks on American interests in the region.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, what do you think the nature of that is likely to look like? What would you be wanting the country and our military to be prepared for here?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Well, we experience this as well. We watch the Houthis. You'll remember, the Houthis have been firing missiles into Saudi Arabia and into the Gulf for years. And I can't tell you how many Democrat senators screamed at me because we were supporting the kingdom of Saudi Arabia trying to defend itself from these very Iranian-backed terrorists. And then the first move the administration takes is to delist them to say, nope these aren't terrorists, these are people that the United States can work with.\par \par \uc2 So, we ought to both mount a diplomatic campaign, we ought to re-designate them as terrorists, we should be prepared for more missile launches. And our \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  is good. But even if we have a 99 percent catch rate, even if we stop all of those missiles, we'll eventually have some American hurt. And the president put our soldiers, sailors, and airmen and marines at risk because he was unprepared to defend the things that really mattered and to deter Iran seriously.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I want to ask you about this January 1st meeting with all of the heads of the national security team and President Biden, who was in Saint Croix at the time on New Year's Day. He, apparently, did not realize, according to all of the reports, that the Secretary of Defense \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 , as they were really planning a retaliatory attack against the Houthis, what would it look like, what kind of assets would be used in this. He didn't realize that he had just been in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Austin headed back to the hospital in an ambulance hours later. You know, the more you learn about this, we got a lot more detail on this from some reporting in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  today. What stands out to you and what's your concern now as we see this attack carried out while Austin's in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Well, Martha, we have a deep bench. We have a capable organization, the White House counsel, but this is incomprehensible to me that no one knows who's in charge of that, right? It's not that there can't be planning take place without the president there or the secretary himself. That happens all the time. But the fact that the two most senior leaders, one's on vacation, the other is in the hospital unbeknownst to the other, and apparently the Secretary of Defense's assistant gets some authority but doesn't really even know why.\par \par \uc2 I must say, it's disturbing as someone who was a soldier and then served at the highest levels in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  to see this level of incomprehensible incompetence. And know that our adversaries are seeing this too, it may not well be a coincidence that these attacks have increased in the last few days as a result of them witnessing the incapability of an administration to simply do the blocking and tackling of nowhere their most senior leaders, the most senior decision-makers are located.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, especially, since senior leaders like yourself are also under threat from Iran. I know they just re-upped your security detail. They said the threat still is, it still exists against you and it may be increased based on the escalation that we're seeing. So, Secretary Pompeo, thank you for your service and good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Yes, maam. You have a good day. Enjoy Iowa, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, I always do. It's good to be in Iowa. All right, thank you very much, sir. We'll see you soon. So, several progressive Democrats have slammed President Biden for making the move and carrying out these strikes. Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  from California posted: "The president needs to come to Congress before launching a strike against the Houthis in Yemen." Squad member, \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 , agreed, she said: "The people do not want more of our taxpayer dollars going to endless war and the killing of civilians.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in Pete Hegseth, "FOX AND FRIENDS" weekend Co-Host and Iraq and Afghanistan War Veteran. Pete, thank you very much for being here. Do they have a point? Do you think that the president should have gone to congress in order to carry out these strikes last night?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS HOST: They do have a point, Martha. I actually, I rarely agree with the members of the Squad but in this particular case, they're talking about something that over the last, especially the last 20 years. An authority in Congress that's been completely undermined and eroded by an open-ended AUMF that effectively said, anyone who's in that region or really around the world who we deem to be an enemy we can strike at any time.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are big advantages to that when you have a lot of enemies and a lot of radical Islamists who need to be killed because they're committed to your destruction. But what you see in these strikes which are not enough to deter Iran, and \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  is completely right, is a possibility of strikes that lead to escalations that could lead to wider conflict that our elected representatives are not given a voice on. And the executive should be ringed back by the Constitution in what we execute and what we do. That should be bipartisan. It's good to see these far-left members calling them out on it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, does it remove the element of surprise though in terms of last night's attack? I mean that's one of the complicating factors.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Well, yes, and the reality here is if we were at war with anyone, they're certainly at war with us. It would be the Islamic Republic of Iran, and there's no appetite for a declaration on that front. So, again, this fits into that ambiguous gray zone of counter-terrorism backing our allies also they say we're their enemy, but what -- the question goes back to, the top line question: what's the strategy and who's in charge? Because we don't know what the strategy is right now.\par \par \uc2 We're not calling them terrorists and we're letting them get close to a bomb and we're not really backing Israel. And who's in charge? The secretary was kind. Is it Austin? Is it the chief of staff? Is it \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? We don't know. And so, things can go wrong quickly. Congress should assert itself if the White House is not capable.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, one person who is spending a lot of time out in the snow today in Iowa who would like to be that leader is the governor of Florida, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . I spoke with him this morning at an event, a campaign event. I asked him about what happened last night in terms of this retaliatory attack against the Houthis and here's what he said, Pete.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We can't allow terrorists, basically the modern-day barbary pirates, which our founding fathers fought, to commandeer shipping routes or to interrupt that type of activity. And so, you have every right to defend U.S. interests with that. He needs to be working with Congress to ensure that Congress approves if he does anything beyond that. I think the problem is probably worse than it would have been if he had just taken swift action at the beginning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Boy, he's right, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Go ahead. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: That kind of swift action is exactly what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did with \uc2 Qasem Soleimani\uc2  hitting the head of their terror organization and then put them on their heels. That kind of action is not what we're seeing, so we're going to get more of this. The governor's right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: One last question to you in terms of where you think this goes next because you make a great point about the lack of definition of what the goal is exactly here. What do you see next, Pete?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Well, it could go any number of ways. This administration is trying to tell us that these actions are meant to de-escalate but they said they wanted to de-escalate in Ukraine. They said they wanted to de-escalate the conflicts around the world, which have only escalated. So, by not taking the necessary steps, small actions lead to miscalculations, lead to retribution, lead to hotter rhetoric, lead to inevitable, or what looks like more likely, conflict.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't want it to be this way, but I think it's going to get more dangerous and more violent in that region before it gets safer, because the clock is ticking under this administration to make those bold moves because the world is rudderless because America is rudderless, and this president looks weak. So, I'm not bullish on peace in the Middle East at this point unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And no doubt they know our time clock, we're in the middle of an election year and there may be some effort to get some things accomplished in that window. So, we'll see what happens. Pete, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Always good to see you, Pete Hegseth. So, as we mentioned, the United States now expects that Iran-backed Houthis will retaliate in some way after the strikes last night. What that's going to look like? We don't know. Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  engages in this escalating conflict in the Middle East from his hospital bed at Walter Reed. We hope that he is improving quickly and can get back on the job full-time soon. General Keane joins us on where he sees all of this, very high consequences when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDATE: Where is the president in all this? It's like he is not -- he is not in touch with his duties on a daily basis. And so, I think our adversary see that, I think that they see that there is a deficit of leadership. And ultimately, when America is weak, you invite more problems.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We went out to Ankeny, Iowa earlier this morning. That was from my discussion with a Florida governor, who obviously has a lot of campaigning to do in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 But he was talking about the Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  who remains in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 The White House has defended the defense secretary and they did so again just a short time ago, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: He was involved actively and intimately all day yesterday with the national security team here at the White House, and of course, with the commander in chief, with President Biden, tracking all the developments and write in on all the discussions and the decisions that were made as we approach the hour where we -- where we knew we were going to go ahead and launch these strikes. He was fully engaged, even from his hospital bed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. That was John Kirby earlier on another network.\par \par \uc2 Now, joining me, retired four-star General Jack Keane, Fox News senior strategic analyst, former vice chief of staff for the Army, and chairman of the Institute for the Study of War.\par \par \uc2 General, always good to have you with us. Thank you for joining me this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 I want to pull up a headline from the Wall Street Journal, sir, how Lloyd Austin's deputy ended up running the Pentagon from the beach. And here is a quote, "For four days in January, most of Washington, including President Biden, didn't know who was running the Pentagon."\par \par \uc2 Not to belabor this, but this is a significant story. I know, General, you have spoken about it a lot in the last few days. What's your reaction to what Kirby had to say today? Is that pass muster with you?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Well, I think I mean, look, the administration has admitted that this was a major snafu. We had a systematic failure here. Certainly, the secretary of defense, if he is going in for cancer surgery should have disclosed that -- it to the chain of command, but also to the American people.\par \par \uc2 And the same thing when he contacted an infection and required hospitalization again.\par \par \uc2 I think we've actually covered this story pretty well, Martha. I'd like to move on myself if we could.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. No, I hear you. We just -- I just want to add that President Biden just commented on it. He said it was clearly a lapse of judgement that just happened while you and I were speaking in Allentown. That's why we're circling back around that.\par \par \uc2 So, let's get to the retaliation. And the Pentagon today just a little while ago saying that they do expect that the Houthis will retaliate against the United States.\par \par \uc2 And Kirby also said earlier that the United States is not looking for conflict with Iran. What's your reaction to that discussion from the Pentagon about expected retaliation?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes. Yes, that's a great point. Let me just clear something that happened in in the last segment. The president has complete authority to take the action he took, authorized by the \uc2 Congress of the United States\uc2 , as a result of a joint resolution in the War Powers Act of 1973 that gives him the authority to respond in an emergency crisis situation when we're being attacked.\par \par \uc2 So, he does not have to go to the Congress and ask permission. He is not starting a war. He is reacting to attacks that have already been taken place by an adversary.\par \par \uc2 In terms of retaliation. Yes, certainly the Houthis and Iranians directing them will attempt to do something here. But let's give the administration some credit here. This is long overdue. We've been talking about this for weeks, Martha. And we finally have a comprehensive attack that appears to have done some damage.\par \par \uc2 The bomb damage assessment is likely being completed. And if there are more targets to re-attack, then, I think we should do that. And take as much of the Houthis capabilities away as we possibly can. That should be phase one.\par \par \uc2 Phase two is reset the strategy with the Iranians, put the sanctions back on them the way Trump had administered to them. Work with the Arabs and the Israelis in dealing overall with resetting the strategic strategy with Iran.\par \par \uc2 And also, recognize that an important ingredient in that is take -- make sure we get a Iran's attention by conducting kinetic strikes. CENTCOM, the Central Command has given the administration plenty of options here. The proxies will not stop until Iran tells them to stop. And what we need to do is take Iran on in a limited measured way, but nonetheless serious enough to get their attention.\par \par \uc2 And I think IRGC bases and targets would be a very good start point to do that. There are plenty of options that the administration has. But you can just see by the statement that you just mentioned, is that we're avoiding escalation.\par \par \uc2 The fear of escalation, in other words, that Iran would respond and we would wind up going to war with Iran is what is paralyzing us from taking any substantive action against them.\par \par \uc2 And it just doesn't make any sense. Iran uses the proxies to avoid direct confrontation. They don't want a war with the United States. They know full well, they would lose their regime as a result of a war with -- war with the United States.\par \par \uc2 And as we continue to be reluctant and timid about this, they will continue to push the proxies forward. And that's the reality of what we are facing.\par \par \uc2 Good action last night. We need more of it. But we need to focus on the center of gravity, which is Iran.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Given everything that's happening, General, it strikes me that the American people don't have a clear understanding of the Biden doctrine in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Excuse me, we just got some brand-new sound from the president, I'm told. So, let's listen to this. And then, we'll circle back. Let's hear it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We will make sure that we respond to the Houthis if they continue this outrageous behavior along with our allies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you (INAUDIBLE) Secretary Austin?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I do.\par \par \uc2 I'm sorry, for God's sake. Come on --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Was the last (INAUDIBLE) I think, (INAUDIBLE) did not to tell you earlier.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are we, in the fact, proxy war with Iran?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Say it again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are we in the fact of proxy war?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No. Iran does not want a war with us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you willing to call Houthis a terrorist group, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think they are.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Will the strike (INAUDIBLE), sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes. We've gone very -- I don't think there's any civilian casualties. That's another reason why it's a success.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Quite a bit there.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the Houthis are a terrorist group. Yes, he has faith and Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 , he said we'll continue to keep pressure on the Houthis as long as our allies stand with us. And there may have been one other point in there. But that was -- that was the gist of it. He was sort of leaning down looking in bookcases in a classroom, while these questions were fired at him. But they were fairly direct answer, sir. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, all of that is sort of typical way we find out about U.S. policy. It's kind of bizarre to be frank about it, Martha. And you're right. When you just -- before the you broke for the president, what is our strategy, and that is something that the American people deserve to hear when it comes to the Middle East. I don't expect him to go up and say, our strategy has failed in the Middle East, because we removed the sanctions that Trump imposed.\par \par \uc2 And as a result of that Iran is flush with money, and they become more aggressive, that we rushed into the nuclear deal. And Iran now is closer to actually having nuclear weapons. And we let expire ballistic missile development, which they have, and we removed the designation of Houthis as a foreign terrorist organization. I don't expect him to lay all of that out.\par \par \uc2 But I do think, looking at what has happened as a result of those failed policies that the United States and the people are do an explanation of what is the strategy going forward.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: With Iran is the major aggressor, using six or seven proxies to dominate and control the Middle East, to disrupt the flow of shipping and oil in the Middle East, and also to drive the United States out of it, and weaken, and eventually destroy the State of Israel.\par \par \uc2 Those are the objectives. And what are we doing to counter those strategic objectives that Iran has?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: We deserve an answer and a comprehensive one. You're absolutely right about that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. And I think in an election year, we hope that those questions will become more forceful, whether it's in sit down interviews or debate environments. What is the goal here?\par \par \uc2 How strong an ally is Israel? Are you going to work with the Arab states in the Middle East? How much do you want to minimize the power of Iran? And how do you plan to do it? All fair game as we head into this. And as you point out, it is a little bizarre, that we're getting this foreign policy update, you know, sort of spoken into a bookcase in a classroom. And I have to say, sort of mumbled during the course of this visit about something else.\par \par \uc2 So, General, thank you. Thank you very much for being with us today. We'll be speaking soon. I'm sure. There is a lot going on and we always count you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Great, great talking to you, Martha. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much, sir.\par \par \uc2 All right. So, it feels like we've been saying this a lot. So, I hope people aren't tired of hearing about how cold it is in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 But the only reason we're talking about it so much is what's the impact right, on the caucuses?\par \par \uc2 We know how hard the people are here, but will they turn out if it feels like 30 below on Monday?\par \par \uc2 We're going to get campaign trail reaction from Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . And Dana Perino, joins me next as well.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're back here from Iowa. Cold snowy, a blizzard on the way, you've seen Chicago, the airports are shut down. So what -- here's the question about what to come.\par \par \uc2 Here's a map of the Republican presidential candidates and where they are today. Weather has forced some of these events to be canceled, some of them are being done by Zoom. Earlier today, I talked with Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . He was out and about very early this morning. He was with \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , the governor in Ankeny, which is just outside of Des Moines. We talked about how the weather may become part of this story on caucus night. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: These are negative temperatures to an extreme that we have never seen on a caucus day before. How concerned are you about the impact of that? And, you know, 116, 120, how many thousands of people do you think you're going to show up?\par \par \uc2 RON DESANTIS (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Nobody knows. I mean, anyone that tells you they know anyone that says, Oh, I can model this electorate, here's what -- no. We don't know. What I think we know, though, is because we've gone at this set on from a very mechanical organizational type way where we have our people that have committed to caucus for us. But, I mean, you know, this is -- they've never seen anything even close to this. I mean, this is a situation.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I think that, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says he has all the votes he needs. I mean, how does that impact some people that were leaning his way, I don't know. But I think our folks are going to turn out, nobody knows what the total turnout will be.\par \par \uc2 KIM REYNOLDS (R), IOWA GOVERNOR: It's going to be cold, but I was ready. We do cold weather and winter here in Iowa, it's who we are. And I think to be really honest, they know how important this is, you know? We got to get somebody that can win, somebody that can step in and get this country back on track. And they know that it's serious.\par \par \uc2 And so, I, you know, I'm hoping, I'm encouraging, be safe but show up. And, you know, I've said it before, and I sincerely mean it, he is probably one of the most effective leaders that I have seen in a long, long time. And this country needs that they need a leader that can restore just the greatness of this country and what it was founded on.\par \par \uc2 And I've got 11 grandchildren, and that's why I got engaged. You know, I want them to have the same opportunities that I had growing up in this country. And I think it's important.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I had asked the governor if she had any regrets about backing DeSantis and perhaps staying out of this. Ad she said she absolutely did not have any regrets about it. She has a tremendous amount of support here in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 So let's take a look at how the caucuses work. Registered Republicans gathered more than 1,600 sites across the state schools, churches, even neighbors living room in some of the more rural places, representatives step in front, they make the pitches for the candidates and then the caucus goers write their pick for President on a blank piece of paper, those ballots are publicly counted. The final tally is then reported to the state party at stake in Iowa for the primary process is 40 delegates, divided among the candidates based on the result. You have to get at least 15% of the room or then that person drops out and people move towards other candidates.\par \par \uc2 So there's Iowa's 99 counties, of course, then you've got the big college towns as well. And that's how some of them voted back in 2020. So Dana Perino is going to join me in just a moment before she heads out here to Iowa, and congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie is live in Urbandale, Iowa today just outside Des Moines. Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. I'm going to turn into a weather reporter here really quickly and show you what Iowans are dealing with. This is a regular sidewalk just outside of Des Moines here and just look at how far my feet go into this snow. This is what Iowans are we're going to be dealing with the next couple of days, but this is also a door knockers are dealing with today. Yes, they are out in full force.\par \par \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 's team has been out all morning and so has a pro Nikki Haley, super PAC canvassers. We weren't just out with them. Take a look at this video. And they were knocking on a lot of doors, talking to those last minute undecided voters trying to get that face to face contact, eager or even joking about how they might even get the sympathy card right now convinced some of those folks just because they're out there in the middle of this snowstorm, that's a blizzard really. Take a listen to just how many folks are going to have out there, hundreds today, trying to get those last minute votes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So we could we could switch to phones but the best conversations are had at people's doorsteps. And so, we only have a finite amount of time, we want to make the most of that. And people are still making up their mind. There's still room to move voters and, more importantly, there's still room to make sure that people understand how important it is that they show up on Monday night.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And, Martha, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , he stopped by his campaign headquarters in Urbandale as well to visit volunteers. They are working the phone banks for him. He says your vote matters even more because of the whether. He's asking those that have committed to him to bring three, four people along with them.\par \par \uc2 Former president Trump is set to arrive to Iowa tomorrow. His son, Don Jr., he's asking those that have committed to him to bring three four people along with them. For President Trump is set to arrive to Iowa tomorrow. His son Don Jr. was here last night telling folks don't get cocky. You know, I've been talking with people here big Trump fans, Martha, who are so confident he's going to win. They're not going to caucus.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP JR\uc2 ., FORMER PRESIDENT TRUMP'S SON: The deep states preferred candidate in the billionaire donor class. Nikki Haley is surging rapidly. Well, 'cause everyone is like, oh, (inaudible) it's what it is for. They don't think that way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, 2016 had a big historic voter turnout but we were looking at 30 to 40 degrees. We're talking about negative 15 now. So yes, Iowans are tough but a lot of folks here are saying the weather is going to impact caucus night. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Aishah, thank you very much. We will see. Let's bring in Dana Perino, co-anchor of "America's Newsroom," co-host of "The Five." Dana, great to have you with us. You know, obviously it's about turnout so I think all of these campaigns at this point are thinking is, you know, can we make some sort of advantage out of this, do we face some of the Trump voters are going to sit back because they think they have such a big lead. And there's always a little bit of surprise at the end but we see obviously a very big lead for the former president, very quickly.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes. And I think Governor DeSantis is right. Nobody really knows how the weather will affect it. But you can imagine -- for a few days, I've been thinking, OK, here we come. We're all coming to Iowa. And we're complaining about the weather and the Iowans are going to say, yes, it gets cold here in the winter.\par \par \uc2 But then listening to Janice Dean, I'm realizing this is different. This is the kind of weather when I grew up in Colorado, if it was this cold, they would cancel school because it was just too cold to let kids stand out for the bus. You have people getting to 1,600 different sites in the dark, in this kind of temperature, in the cold. So I do think it might have a little bit of a factor we'll have to see. Of course, it always does come down to turnout.\par \par \uc2 But this added element of surprise is going to be something that the candidates will have to contend with. I think that Trump Jr, is very interesting, following on what his dad has said from the beginning, which is he did not like losing in 2016. And he has been determined that will not happen to him again.\par \par \uc2 What could happen is, if Trump's voters do turn out, he could get over 50%. And that will be the first time ever a Republican has done that in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And if he gets that head of steam coming out of Iowa, it's going to be very powerful. Let's put up some of the recent polling. This is \uc2 Suffolk University\uc2 . This is before \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  dropped out. It's got Trump at 54. That was from 2016. Let's take a look at the new numbers.\par \par \uc2 We know what happened ultimately in 2016. Ted Cruz was a by a hair winner and, of course, Trump went on to get the nomination. Before Christie dropped out, you see that Trump at 54, Haley at 20, and then you see the other screen with him out of the race. Trump 54, Hayley 22, still 30 some points behind, Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I'm sure she has a lot of hope for New Hampshire but we'll see.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think the real story there is the DeSantis number, right, because he has very strong support from evangelicals, but not as strong as President Trump's. His support -- President Trump's support in rural America and Iowa has a lot of rural communities is super strong. Also \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , he has traveled that state. He has dedicated so much time and effort. His numbers of these in the polls don't move.\par \par \uc2 They think the polls are wrong. Of course, we'll see about that. But they're fairly consistent in Iowa. And there's been slightly more movement in New Hampshire where Haley is a bit closer to Trump. But still Trump with that commanding and enduring lead.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's interesting because we showed at the top of that, what happened in 2016. And Ted Cruz was able to galvanize the evangelical vote in Iowa. But interestingly, that vote really is galvanizing it appears, at least in the polling, behind the former President Trump and you look at it in contrast to a more progressive move for the country in the last four years. And you can see in a way that's firing up.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: When I think that's just based on experience and for good reason, right? Let's just take one issue. If you really cared about pro life issues, you can look at President Trump and say he was the one who was able to get the Supreme Court justices that ultimately roll back Roe v. Wade. They are happy about that, they are satisfied with that, they are not as worried as some are about the political fallout from that. They wanted to support life. They think President Trump did, they've stuck with him. And that's been a difficult thing for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to try to tap into.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And that's the cool thing about the primaries, because that's Iowa, right? New Hampshire is a whole different story. And we'll see you here this weekend, Dana, and then we'll all be on to New Hampshire. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I can't wait. OK, bye. Bye.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll see you soon (inaudible), all right.\par \par \uc2 So if you're just joining us, President Biden making some big headlines this hour, calling the Houthis a terrorist organization, regardless of the fact that he took them off the terrorist designation list. He says he does expect that the group will retaliate, that coming from the Pentagon. We'll show you those comments when we comeback.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Some news breaking just a short time ago. And we want to go back to that now. President Biden addressed the conflict in the Middle East, this aspect of it for the first time really. He also commented on the hospitalization of his Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 . White House correspondent Peter Doocy reporting live from the North Lawn on what the President had to say just a short time ago on his trip to Pennsylvania. Hi, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Martha, this is the first time President Biden has talked about his hospitalized defense secretary since we found out that when President Biden called Austin on Saturday, when he found out that he was in the hospital, Austin didn't tell him why he was in the hospital. He didn't tell him that he was there for complications from prostate cancer surgery. President Biden says he has confidence in Austin but he did kind of gently ribbed him a little bit. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Did you (inaudible) Secretary Austin?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: I do. I'm sorry --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (Inaudible) earlier?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So he says it's a lapse in judgment for us to not to have told him earlier what was going on. Very uncommon for a president to criticize a cabinet secretary, particularly since in this case, President Biden says he is not going to ask for Austin's resignation. And his staff is telling us that he would not accept a resignation if one was offered.\par \par \uc2 Something else really big that came out of the President's little chat, very quick chat with reporters there. It's a change in US policy, potentially, because on the Air Force One right up to Pennsylvania, John Kirby was talking about how a few years ago, the US delisted the Houthis as a terrorist organization. We know at the time they did that for humanitarian reasons, to get humanitarian aid into Yemen.\par \par \uc2 Kirby said on Air Force One today that no decision has been made about making them an official tier organization again. But listening to the President, it sounds like a decision has been made.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (Inaudible) terrorist group, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think they are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Something else the President said is that he thinks Iran, who back the Houthis, got the message from this 60 targets at 16 location strike late last night. But a few minutes ago, during the commercial break, we got word that the Houthis fired another ballistic missile into those shipping lanes that is consistent with what the Pentagon told us that there was going to be some kind of retaliation. But it's different than what the President predicted that Iran got the message. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very interesting. Peter, thank you very much. Peter Doocy at the White House. And \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  facing a contempt of Congress vote next week. Now he says that he will testify in private. He has been insisting on a public hearing, certainly made a big public event, why walking into his contempt hearing the other day. Then he got up and walked out when he didn't like some of what he was hearing.\par \par \uc2 His lawyer says now that the House has authorized an impeachment inquiry into President Biden. And that because that has changed the situation, Hunter will now testify behind closed doors and send -- because of a -- he said -- the attorney said you need to send a "new proper subpoena." He's also set to go on trial in June, right in the middle of his father's reelection campaigns. So there's going to be a lot of overlap between court and 2024 presidential politics.\par \par \uc2 He was in court, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , in LA yesterday. He was pleading not guilty to tax charges that could potentially add up to 17 years in jail for the president's son. Prosecutors say that instead of paying his taxes, Hunter spent millions living the high life including hundreds of thousands of dollars for "various women and adult entertainment" as part of those expenditures.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in former Deputy Assistant Attorney General John Yoo, now a UC Berkeley Law Professor. John, thank you very much for being with us today. We know that Hunter and his team have defied this subpoena process. They said happy to do it in public, don't want to do anything behind closed doors. But now Abbe Lowell is saying that the -- that the game has changed, and I just want to quote from Abbe Lowell's letter, obviously a very prominent attorney who represents \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . He said, I write to make you aware if you are not already that your subpoenas were and are legally invalid, and you cannot form a legal basis to proceed with your misdirected and impermissible contempt resolution. And you two, of all people, should know that that's the case. That's the earlier letter.\par \par \uc2 Now he's saying that because there is an impeachment inquiry underway, John, that it can be valid. He wants them to issue a new subpoena. Sorry for that big intro but just wanted to lay that out. What do you think about all this, John?\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: One way to look at both what's happened now in the subpoena and what happened yesterday in Los Angeles, is that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and his team now realize that the best way to proceed is to accept that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has to be treated the same way that all other Americans are treated, that you shouldn't try to get a special sweetheart plea deal to escape not paying your -- $1.4 million in taxes over four years.\par \par \uc2 If you did that, Martha, if I did that, Martha, we'd be going to jail and other Americans are sitting in jail right now who committed that level tax evasion. Similarly in the House, what they're saying is, OK, we're going to obey a subpoena, just like everybody else has to obey a subpoena.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , you mean, Martha, we don't have the right to tell the Congress how we're going to testify how we're going to be deposed. We're going to obey a legally valid subpoena and show up, and be questioned by lawyers behind closed doors, and then in a public hearing just like everybody else.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. John, you -- sorry for the quick discussion. We had a little breaking news there from Pennsylvania. We look forward to seeing you next time. I always love having you on. Thank you very much, John Yoo --\par \par \uc2 YOO: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- for joining us today. So out on the campaign trail, this was from last night actually. We went a couple of hours east to Cedar Rapids this morning. We were in Ankeny, on the trail with the Republican presidential candidate Nikki Haley last night, and then today with Governor DeSantis. We'll show you some of that too, coming up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So what's in your win? I mean, I don't know how you keep it straight all the time. We are --\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We are in Cedar Rapids, I think, but at the end of the day, this is what counts. These town halls, touching every hand, answering every question, making sure that they get FaceTime with you, that's what this is about. And I love the process. And so it's been 11 long months of trying to touch as many people as we can but we feel good about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So these candidates have just three days to go. They are in the homestretch in Iowa. They're very busy making their final pitches across the state as Monday quickly approaches. Last night, we went to Cedar Rapids for a countdown to caucus event to cover the Haley campaign a bit. As I said, we were with DeSantis this morning, we talked to her, we talked to the voters, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think Nikki Haley's going to be the first woman president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In 2024?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: 2024, she's going to be -- a year from now, we're beginning -- we're going to be getting ready to call her Madam President.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Tracy, you're here tonight to see Nikki Haley. They're playing Tom Petty's "American girl." Do you like the idea of having a woman for president?\par \par \uc2 TRACY, VOTER: I absolutely love it. Because to me, it's not about what your gender is, it's about who's going to do the best for our country. My wife is a lifetime voting Democrat and she just switched hers to Republican, because I think she can pull votes from both sides. And maybe, maybe we can actually have a country that doesn't have to argue and fight.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you so much. It's great to meet you. And thank you so much for your service. Good to see you tonight. Good luck with your vote on Monday, we'll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I came from Virginia to volunteer for Nikki, because I care about this country. And like he said, we need to make sure the country is on the right track. And we don't want to repeat a Biden-Trump again because that'll be a disaster for the country.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We had a discussion with President Trump as you know. I asked him who he thought his VP would be. And he said, well, I know who I would pick, but I'm not going to share it. I said, can you imagine yourself with any of the people who are running against you right now? And he said, possibly, maybe.\par \par \uc2 I know, you've answered this before, and I asked a couple people here, would they be interested in a ticket with both of you on it? And they nodded their heads and said yes. What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I think that he's not going to even have to worry about picking a VP. I know that I will in six months, that's our whole goal. I think he was the right president at the right time. I agree with a lot of his policies. But it is time for us to go forward, not backwards. And so I'm determined to do that. I have said over and over again, I'm not interested in being vice president. I'm interested in saving this country. I don't want my kids to live like this. We can do better. And it's time that we do better.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I saw your kids in the front row. They are working hard here in the crowd. They're getting everybody to sign up to caucus. What about that? What does that mean to you to have their support, especially since Michael is not here?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I'm just so proud of them. You know, they grew up in public life, and we always strive to shelter them from it. Now that they're adults and they want to be a part of it, is just a very sweet thing as a mom to know that they care and that they love this process. And that they're competing right now to see who gets the most commit to caucus cards. It's fun to watch.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  dropped out. I know, you talked, you know, what was your reaction, first of all, to the off mic things that he said?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I mean, look, I mean, the fellas take it all personally. It's not personal for me. They've been saying this when I had -- there were 14 people and I had 2%, it doesn't matter. I've gone through this all my life. I don't take it personally because it's their issue, not my issue. We've got a country to say, that's my focus and that's what I'm going to say.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Did you talk to him today?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I did. I talked to him this morning.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And how did that go?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I told him that I appreciated what he did in the race. I appreciated his commitment. And I knew that it was personal to get into the race. And I knew it was more personal to get out of the race and I wished him well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Did he apologize?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: He did not.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting, all right. That was my discussion with Nikki Haley last night.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining us, everybody. That's "The Story" for today, Friday. "The Story" goes on as we continue to cover all of the Iowa caucus lead up. We're going to be on Sunday afternoon, the day before the caucuses. We will do our show live here from Des Moines at 3:00 PM on Sunday. And then, of course, we've got a big night with all of our team back in Iowa to cover the caucuses on Monday night.\par \par \uc2 So stick around for Your World. It gets started in just a minute or so as we watch this process from a very cold Iowa where people are super hardy. They are out and about today. There's a live shot from the place that we are broadcasting from here in Des Moines. I've been hearing a lot over the years. I have to say 