a breaks a little bit late. You know, we've got 10 days left. I don't know how late something can break, but it's a caucus system, so it is open to some surprises, some potential for surprise. Will we see any in Iowa?\par \par \uc2 MATT TOWERY, POLITICAL ANALYST AND POLLSTER: Well, you're right, Will. In fact, in the past, I've polled Iowa for news organizations since 2004. I went back and looked at the results. And typically, the pollsters have a bigger gap between the leader and the final result, and then sometimes it inverts and someone ends up behind. So, it can always narrow a little bit more because it is a caucus type of participation.\par \par \uc2 That said, right now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does have a big lead and our polling shows he's doing well with evangelicals and he also is doing well with general GOP members who usually would attend caucuses. So, it seems like his organization this time is much better than in 2016. So, this lead, he has, may actually hold.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Well, Iowa is also be about who stays in the race and who begins the process of dropping out. So, how does it look down there at second, third, fourth place?\par \par \uc2 TOWERY: Well, most of the polls, including my own, show the two other candidates, main candidates, DeSantis and of course Haley, trading places, just trading votes back and forth. Haley wants to come in second because everyone knows New Hampshire, most pollsters, a lot of us at least, sort of view New Hampshire as an aberration because it's a state where undeclared voters are allowed to vote, there's no democratic action going on, Democrats could become undeclared voters up until like October.\par \par \uc2 So, we think in that state, that may be more of an anti-Trump, more moderate crowd, not typical like South Carolina that will follow. And so, Nikki Haley wants to come out of Iowa with some sort of bump so she can then take advantage of whatever the sort of unusual makeup of the New Hampshire primary this time might give her.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Governor DeSantis was a little testy when he was asked whether or not he actually thinks there is hope for victory in Iowa. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Can you admit you found yourself in a fight for second place?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: First of all, Trump has always been leading in the race. I mean, he's the former president. He's one of the most famous people in there --\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: But you're not even the top challenger to him now.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We are the top. I'm the only one that has a chance to beat Trump and win the general election. Nikki Haley can't get conservative voters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Is he right? What's your reaction, Matt?\par \par \uc2 TOWERY: Well, I think he's on to something. And, you know, he's been down there, or up there rather, for quite a while, and he does have an organization. So, I'm not discounting DeSantis as having a better performance in the Poll Show.\par \par \uc2 As I said, Will, it's really hard with these caucuses to pin the tail right in the right place because you don't know who's going to turn out that day and that night, what it's going to be like, what the weather's going to be like, and what the behind the scenes. And DeSantis has put a lot of money down there.\par \par \uc2 So, it's a battle in which Haley is trying to come up late on the momentum she gained from the debates and her rise in the polls nationally, whether she can do it and then move on to New Hampshire. The main thing is South Carolina will decide the whole thing. And right now, Nikki Haley is trailing by 30 points in her home state.\par \par \uc2 If she can't overcome that, then after that, if Trump wins at that margin, then these other states just fall in line and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  becomes a nominee without any problem.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: You'll watch South Carolina. Matt, thank you so much. Coming up, you won't believe how much the Biden administration has spent on DEI training. But first, another batch of Epstein documents have been unsealed. Nate Foy has the details, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: We've got our hands on the third batch of Epstein documents unsealed today and there's more coming. So here with the details is Fox News correspondent Nate Foy. I understand, Nate, you just got a fresh new batch of names associated with Epstein.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yeah, we've had four separate drops throughout the day. The newest one, 36 documents, 381 pages. So, we have the entire newsroom going through that right now. But what we learned in the newest batch is released today, for the first time, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's name is included. The former Secretary of State is on a list of names and phrases that lawyers wanted searched on \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 's confidant, Ghislaine Maxwell's electronic devices.\par \par \uc2 This is all part of a 2015 defamation lawsuit that Epstein accuser Virginia Giuffre brought against Maxwell. Former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  is on that same list. In a separate document, Maxwell discusses taking a trip with Epstein and Clinton, but said she couldn't remember the date. Neither of the Clintons are accused of any wrongdoing. A Clinton spokesperson says the former president last spoke to Epstein almost 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Another new name to report tonight is \uc2 Harvey Weinstein\uc2 . The disgraced Hollywood producer is in prison for sexual assault and rape, and he's noted in Epstein's message book. Take a look here. The phone log shows a handwritten note reading, quote, "she had on the phone Mr. \uc2 Harvey Weinstein\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Prince Andrew and attorney \uc2 Alan Dershowitz\uc2  are again mentioned tonight in these newest documents. They're on that same list as the Clintons. Dershowitz repeatedly calls for all the documents to be released. He said last night there are smoking guns yet to be revealed and he has nothing to hide.\par \par \uc2 Many of the documents released tonight show Epstein's recruiting process and how he paid Giuffre's ex-boyfriend $200 for every girl that he brought to his home.\par \par \uc2 So, with today's drop now, including this newest drop, Will, we're about 80 percent of the way through the documents that we've been told will be coming, 240 in total. The rest are set to come on Monday.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: And just as a point of clarification, these names are not necessarily alleged to have committed crimes --\par \par \uc2 FOY: Correct.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: -- yet. They are simply, to this point, people who had a relationship or alleged to have a relationship with \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FOY: Correct. They're people that were part of the questioning process in a 2015 defamation case. They simply were people that lawyers asked witnesses, hey, did you ever hear anything about this person? And the answer could have been no, but their names still came up, so they're in these documents.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: All right, Nate, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 FOY: You got it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Truth is, we all know it, discrimination still exists in America. Discrimination still exists in America. Discrimination still exists in America. We need to remember that diversity is our strength. We have to find a way forward. That's the work in my administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Apparently, that so-called work costs at a minimum $16 million, because that's how much the Biden administration spent on brainwashing federal government employees with so-called DEI training this past year. Joining me now is Pastor Corey Brooks, CEO of Project Hood.\par \par \uc2 Pastor, thanks so much for being with us. Why do you think the federal government feels the need to re-educate their administration?\par \par \uc2 COREY BROOKS, PROJECT HOOD CEO: You know, we've had over 60 years of diversity training and diversity, all in the name of helping black people, so to speak. And what has it done? It hasn't helped us one bit. It hasn't helped us educate our children. Diversity doesn't create jobs in our community. It doesn't move us out of poverty.\par \par \uc2 But what diversity training does is it enriches the racial essentials of those who are enhancing themselves and benefiting from those policies like the Biden administration is trying to do. But at the end of the day, it does nothing to uplift black people.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: You know, when you say diversity doesn't help us in the black community get jobs, you know, there's this -- obviously, diversity is, can be a virtue, diversity of thought, diversity of background to any organization. But I think what we're talking about, I think what you're talking about, and I know what I'm talking about, is this formalized DEI, diversity, equity, and inclusion.\par \par \uc2 I got in a debate with \uc2 Dallas Mavericks\uc2  owner \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2  about this. It's not simply this abstraction, this feel-good idea, hey, everyone should be included. It's an ideology that is taught and apparently costs $16 million to be taught by federal employees.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Yeah, it's this idea that, somehow, we can create equal outcomes. But what we need in America is not equal outcomes. We need equal opportunities. We need to focus on true uplift that only comes from hard work family values, merit, and good American principles. Those are the things that uplift people on the south side of Chicago where I'm from.\par \par \uc2 That's what we're focusing on every single day. Accountability, responsibility, uplifting people, giving them the tools that are necessary for them to live their lives. But that DEI is doing nothing to uplift our community.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Let's take a look, Pastor, the Daily Caller is reporting on some of the examples of the types of things that were involved in this training. Quote, "past government diversity trainings have instructed federal workers that asking an Asian colleague for help with a math problem, well that could be racist. And men can, of course, become pregnant now." That's of course par for the course in modern day DEI.\par \par \uc2 And that social pain can be the same thing as physical pain, Pastor. That's what we're learning within the federal government.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Yeah, you know, what's so heart-wrenching is that instead of spending all this money to teach these principles, I can teach them real simple. It's love your neighbor as you love yourself. It's treat people the way you want to be treated. It's investing in all of Americans, making us the best that we can possibly be.\par \par \uc2 And I think when we focus on practical principles instead of focusing on forced diversity and inclusion, that's the way we're going to uplift the American society, and that's the way we're going to become a better people.\par \par \uc2 So many times, all we're doing is focusing on these racial issues, these racial issues, and it's not bringing us together. It's only driving us further apart.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Yes, the whole point is to see past our superficial differences --\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: -- to look into our internal character. Pastor Brooks, great to hear from you. Thank you. It's like a scene out of Parks and Rec, how a progressive mayor is making sure she stays rich. Plus, 100 Biden documentaries in the works. Jason Rantz and Garrett Ventry react to the week's culture whip, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: It's time for the week's Culture Whip with my panel Seattle radio host Jason Rantz, and Republican strategist Garrett Ventry. Thanks for being here, gentlemen. A progressive Democrat mayor of a small town on the outskirts of Chicago is being accused of a wild spending spree and making herself rich.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You can't turn your head in Dalton or Thornton Township without seeing Henyard's picture or name. It's on all the government-owned vehicles, buildings, even on the rugs in the township. The Township Board passed an ordinance that allows Henyard to continue collecting her $224,000 a year salary. But if anyone other than Henyard gets the job, the ordinance resets that salary to $25,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: So, Mayor Tiffany Henyard is also accused of using taxpayer money for a $3,000 trip to Vegas, flying first class, of course. And she has her own personal makeup artist. I don't know that many mayors that have a makeup artist. She also produced two music videos featuring city workers. So, Jason, she's making more than the governor.\par \par \uc2 JASON RANTZ, SEATTLE RADIO HOST: She is. I mean, think about it this way. The Dolt of Dalton, Illinois, makes more than the median income in that town of $24,000. There's something almost impressive about how audacious she is. I mean, you don't generally see this level of audacity coming from any lawmaker, let alone someone in such a small town.\par \par \uc2 But I have to imagine that this is not a new personality, that when she went around asking for votes, she probably acted this way, brand focused. So, the people put her in power, and now they're going to have to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Well, Garrett, this is how Henyard acted at one of her board meetings, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: All y'all do is keep losing. Y'all making a fictitious play world of y'all run stuff and y'all don't stop. I am the executive branch, y'all are the legislative branch, that's it. Know your place, learn your seat. Every night I got to show you all what it is, is saying. They lie because they can't beat me. I can tell you they try to run a campaign on last, it ain't getting you nowhere. Nowhere.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: You know, I don't know. That's one thing, Garrett. There is -- another -- this salary thing that sets it back if she's out of office it goes back to twenty-five thousand. I want to know how she pulled that off.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT VENTRY, FORMER SENATE JUDICIARY GOP SENIOR ADVISER: No, it's an absolutely remarkable growth. I mean, it's like, no one could write this if this is a \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series. It's like Veep meets Anna Delvey. I've never seen anything like it here. It's, you know, she's using an Amex like she's a, you know, nineteen-year-old trust fund kid in Montauk over the summer, but she's using with the government's money.\par \par \uc2 I've never seen anything like it, moving a salary down to, you know, back out of twenty thousand from over two hundred thousand. I guess, you know, in this economy baby scares away challengers. But it's the most brazen thing I've ever seated politics and I don't know how it's happening.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Again, I love to interview the mayor.\par \par \uc2 VENTRY: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Yeah, it'd be an incredible interview. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is getting a Hollywood makeover, it's documentary style but don't get your hopes up for us, you know, as a tell-all of Hunters life.\par \par \uc2 The L.A. Times reporting it will show a softer Hunter, painting, selling his art, raising his son, navigating everyday life as a sober adult, with the ongoing criminal investigations and in the crosshairs of president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his supporters. So, I'm sure this is going to be a hit, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 VENTRY: Yeah, I mean, speaking of Anna Delvey, I guess we have \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  now. So, I mean, it's just very interesting here. I mean, everyone knows, I mean, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  dropped his laptop off. He was dropping off a crime scene and opened up, you know, trolls of investigation, illegal activity that led to him getting indicted and his father on the verge of being impeached by the House of Representatives here.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think it's very interesting showing the soft side of Hunter. I read in that same article that those cameras were there when he defied a congressional subpoena as well. So clearly, he's not taking this very seriously or anything here.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Jason, new media sure has a way of supporting these, you know, hangers on. I mean, Harry and Meghan, I mean, post-Career Obama, what was his? He's got a production deal and a podcast deal. Here's a documentary on Hunter.\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: Yeah, look, if it was an actual documentary on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , I might actually be interested in that. I mean, it would be rated X, and it would include drug use and prostitutes. But I mean, it does sound like there's a lot of potential material there.\par \par \uc2 But obviously, this is just a propaganda video. They're trying to do this and twist it into, it's not just about Hunter, but it's about his father, whose only crime is loving his son too much.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Yeah, so here's the bet as we go, Jason. What does better, this documentary or the market for Hunter's art?\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: I mean, there's a good -- oh, man. It's probably the art because you can so easily purchase. Yeah, right?\par \par \uc2 CAIN: It's got a subjective market. All you need is one stupid buyer. This is -- Garrett, this is going to require -- you can get at least a couple thousand people to watch.\par \par \uc2 VENTRY: I think so. Yeah, I mean, it's going to be very -- it's going to be very interesting. You could get his finger paintings for $250,000, so that market is probably better.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: All right, Jason, Garrett, thank you so much for being with us tonight. That's it for us tonight. I'm Will Kane in for Laura Ingraham. Don't forget to tune in tomorrow and Sunday morning when I co-host Fox and Friends Weekend, and check out my podcast, the aptly named Will Kane podcast. Big things in store coming up in the next week on that podcast. Thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240106ek1500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Holiday Stress; Minimum Wage Hikes in Multiple States; Republican Candidates Make Final Push in Iowa; Stock Market Outlook; Holiday Debts\b0\par\par\uc2 Mike Emanuel, Scott Martin, Edward Lawrence \par \uc2 8,296 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 January 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Investors seeing green in 2023? Will they be popping more champagne in the new year?\par \par \uc2 Hello, everyone. I'm Edward Lawrence, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is a special edition of "Your World."\par \par \uc2 And it was a special year for stocks in 2023 with all three major averages surging, this coming despite rate hikes from the Fed and price hikes that had customers scrambling. But with inflation now cooling and rate cuts possibly coming, could this rally keep going?\par \par \uc2 Let's ask our new -- New Year's money panel, Lee Munson from Portfolio Wealth Advisors and Kathryn Rooney Vera from chief market -- chief market strategist at \uc2 StoneX\uc2 , which I love that name, by the way.\par \par \uc2 OK, Lee, though, let's start with you.\par \par \uc2 Where does the market go in 2024?\par \par \uc2 LEE MUNSON, PORTFOLIO WEALTH ADVISORS: Well, let's look at where earnings are going, right?\par \par \uc2 Even after all the estimates have been brought down, we still have about, depending on who you ask, 8 percent to 10 percent earnings growth going into next year. So if we just look at where the S&P is now, if we can really grow at another 10 percent, I think we can get well into the 5000s, maybe 5100, 5200.\par \par \uc2 I'm not here to make an end-of-next-year prognostication, but I think we're going to see more up next year. Just remember, it's going to be a little rocky. I think investors aren't really aware that if we get any little hot prints of inflation or any little things that doesn't show inflation is going -- sinking like a rock, you're going to get more volatility and people are going to lose hope.\par \par \uc2 But I think any lost hope, that's a chance to buy low.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. So, this is on tape. We have this for next year.\par \par \uc2 So, Kathryn, in 2023, we saw the rise of A.I. No, I'm not talking about in the Terminator way. I think one of the movies was "The Rise of the Machines." But the A.I. boom for big tech stocks like \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , even \uc2 IBM\uc2 , will that continue?\par \par \uc2 KATHRYN ROONEY VERA, CHIEF MARKET STRATEGIST, STONEX: Well, let's hope it continues, because 30 percent of the S&P 500 is comprised of those technological companies.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I think the best bet for the S&P next year, if it continues to move higher, is a broadening out of what we have seen in 2023, which is really the Magnificent 7 really running the table in terms of the equity move higher.\par \par \uc2 So if we get a broadening out, and if the Fed is able to nail this perfect landing scenario, which is that they end inflation with 525 basis points, 5.25 percentage points of hikes, without inducing recession, without sending the unemployment rate higher, then I think equities could move higher this year.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: That would be very interesting.\par \par \uc2 And do you think A.I. still leads the way? I mean, you say A.I. -- and, Kathryn, I will ask you this. You say A.I., and the company stock pops, it seemed, in 2023.\par \par \uc2 ROONEY VERA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Do you think that continues, or do there have to be results now, this is how A.I. is growing my business?\par \par \uc2 ROONEY VERA: Yes, A.I. has to deliver. It's been the buzzword.\par \par \uc2 Every company in their earnings reports and their guidance talks about A.I., because it's the cool thing to talk about right now. And it gets your stock price moving higher. And we have seen productivity gains do very well. But for them to -- productivity to continue to move higher, I think we need to see execution, not just talk about adoption of A.I.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROONEY VERA: And up to this point, Ed, what I would tell you is that large -- large-magnitude companies talk about it, but they really haven't deployed it in its full magnitude.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 So, Lee, let's stay with artificial intelligence. We have the first now lawsuit from \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  suing \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  for using thousands of its intellectual property to teach ChatGPT to learn. Will there be an A.I. reckoning in 2024, you think?\par \par \uc2 MUNSON: I'm sure there will, because this thing is going very fast.\par \par \uc2 I woke up this morning not that hung over.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MUNSON: I didn't drink that much champagne last night, but when I started thinking about what are the things that are going to surprise people and cause stock prices to be volatile, it's not the Fed, right?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MUNSON: The number one thing is, what if we get a major lawsuit with A.I.? What if Congress tries to get in there and monkey around?\par \par \uc2 I think those lawsuits are going to be weak. I don't think that we're going to have -- we don't -- we do not have the same type of totalitarian commerce system that Europe has. Now, if we're in Europe, I would be a little bit concerned, but here in America, the courts have always backed up big tech, big business.\par \par \uc2 I'm not saying that that's good. I'm saying that is the most likely thing to happen going forward. And A.I. can get around a lot of that.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MUNSON: But the idea of being able to scrape the Internet, being able to take all human knowledge, these are incredibly fast things, and it doesn't matter. It's going to be a cat-and-mouse game. So A.I. is always going to be faster than regulators.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right. I want to move quickly. We're going to go to inflation now.\par \par \uc2 Kathryn, I want to ask you about inflation. So where do you think inflation -- do you think it gets back -- well, the Fed doesn't think it's going to get to 2 percent until 2025. But do you think we see softening in inflation, core inflation specifically, without food and gas prices?\par \par \uc2 ROONEY VERA: The Fed is going to cut next year. The question is when and why.\par \par \uc2 The why, I think, is the most important. So if there's immaculate disinflation, which is inflation moving lower, not actually deflation, prices aren't dropping, they're just rising at a slower pace, if that happens without any rollover in the labor market, the fabled soft landing, I think that's less likely than some sort of deceleration activity, which would bring inflation back to the 2 percent target.\par \par \uc2 So I doubt we get to the 2 percent target next year or the following year. The Fed cuts next year because inflation is moving closer to the target or because we're in recession. There's a few things that I think we have to take into account. Right now, the markets are priced to perfection.\par \par \uc2 But all of that yummy money that I like to call it from the federal government during COVID, that is drying up. That's going to be gone in the first half of next year. Rates are high, and they're going to start to pinch. So the U.S. consumer is feeling the brunt of still-high inflation, a labor market that's rolling over...\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROONEY VERA: ... having to repay their student loans that they took out. That started in October of last year.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So -- right.\par \par \uc2 ROONEY VERA: Yes, it's going to be tough.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: I want to bring Lee in here, though. We have 30 seconds left, and I want to get this from Lee.\par \par \uc2 I have $100 to put on a stock. What sector, what company do you like a year from now, Lee?\par \par \uc2 MUNSON: Believe it or not, you're still going to have to be invested in some of these big mega-cap tech stocks.\par \par \uc2 I hate it. I'm a value investor. I don't like it. But if I had to have a buck, I know I'm hedging. I would put 50 cents in the Nasdaq 100. I would put the other 50 cents in small cap value. There's a ton of ETFs that track U.S. small value, because you're going to have those banks and tanks and cranks, real economic things moving upward.\par \par \uc2 But still, at the end of the day, the big growth, the big thing that's moving the needle on earnings per share are still these big overpriced tech companies. Nothing we can do about it.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Kathryn, Lee, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Lee, I have to tell you, I'm feeling a little awkward with the signs back there sort of sideways. Is that me? Is that what I have been drinking last night? It's kind of off...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But, Kathryn, Lee, great debate.\par \par \uc2 MUNSON: It's New Year's cheer, Edward. It's New Year's cheer.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Thank you, guys. Thank you, guys, for this. I appreciate it. We will talk later, I'm sure.\par \par \uc2 ROONEY VERA: Thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, with two weeks to go, it's almost go-time for these Republican presidential candidates with the Iowa caucuses closing in, and money moves could tell us where the momentum's heading.\par \par \uc2 And walking, texting, and hurting -- why your next trip to the E.R. might not happen -- or might happen during that next group chat. We report, you might hide.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Two weeks until voters start deciding, and we're on the campaign and money trail in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Well, with Iowa, New Hampshire closing in, GOP presidential candidate \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  is pulling cash out of TV ads and putting it elsewhere. Meanwhile, Nikki Haley's team is said to be revving up last minute door knocking in Iowa with an influx of new cash, something \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ' campaign has been doing for a long time.\par \par \uc2 But is any of it enough to match front-runner \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Let's discuss with Democratic strategist, pollster Doug Schoen, Patrice Onwuka of the Independent Women's Forum, and Politico's Daniel Lippman.\par \par \uc2 Doug, let's start with you.\par \par \uc2 How do you break through as these number twos crisscross, Iowa?\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FORMER BILL CLINTON POLLSTER: With great difficulty.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has done a great deal of door knocking, and all that's happened is his vote has stayed the same or gone down. Nikki Haley has a late infusion of cash from \uc2 Americans for Prosperity\uc2 . They're doing something similar. Her message is very tentative.\par \par \uc2 I don't see her breaking through in Iowa. I don't see Governor DeSantis. I see Iowa 2024 going for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , probably not with a majority, but with a healthy victory.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And so, Daniel, if former President Trump does win Iowa and then let's say New Hampshire, it's over, right?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL LIPPMAN, WHITE HOUSE AND WASHINGTON REPORTER, POLITICO: I think the other candidates will stay in the race for a while, at least until South Carolina for Nikki Haley, since that's her home state.\par \par \uc2 But if she doesn't win South Carolina, then I think the money will start to dry up, and people in the media and her consultants and her donors will say, where's the path? Are you just taking money away from Republicans that could be used for making the Senate blue or keeping the House red?\par \par \uc2 That's going to be a big question for these low-polling candidates and also people like Asa Hutchinson. Why are you even in the race anymore if he's going to try to go to Super Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, Patrice, saved the best for the last of this first round. Do you feel like any one of these candidates trying to unseat former President Trump and making the case is making the case to be vice president or is it too early to think like that?\par \par \uc2 PATRICE ONWUKA, INDEPENDENT WOMEN'S FORUM: Good to see you. Happy new year.\par \par \uc2 And I think, certainly, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , he has certainly been doing his best to be the pro-Trump candidate in the race. We have seen him pull his TV ad money because he's strategically trying to go after the youth vote, particularly in Ohio, where one in four of those caucus voters are under the age of 30.\par \par \uc2 And he's going to be hitting them through social media, pairing that social media campaign with text messaging, very similar to what President Trump did in 2016 and 2020. So I think that he certainly sees himself as a great shadow for the president. Whether he would end up on the ticket is another story.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of the day, this has always been a race for President Trump to lose. The only person who's going to come close, I think, is Nikki Haley in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. And she has been rising.\par \par \uc2 But, Doug, why do you think that the message from all of these other candidates cannot eclipse the popularity of the former president?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Well, because voters really feel that, with the criminal prosecutions that have occurred, along with the decision of the Colorado Supreme Court to remove Trump from the ballot, that he's being targeted, indeed, scapegoated.\par \par \uc2 It's taken the air out of the room. It's taken the energy out of the process. And Republicans have rallied around Trump. And neither Haley nor Governor DeSantis, as the two and three running candidates, has come up with a credible alternative message. And, indeed, Trump is getting stronger, with the exception of New Hampshire, which could go to Haley, but I don't see any place else where she is at this point competitive.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Daniel -- Daniel, let's look out down 2024. Let's say that these candidates don't break through. Do you see any one of these Republican candidates trying to break off and maybe make an independent run?\par \par \uc2 LIPPMAN: I don't expect it, because, if you're \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  and you're trying to stop \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from winning a reelection, basically, having a second term, then, if you had an independent run, you would only be basically ensuring his election, because you would be taking away those moderate Republican voters that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is targeting.\par \par \uc2 And so if you're Nikki Haley and you want to stay in Republican politics, you're not going to do an independent run either. And so I think this basically means Trump is continuing to defy the laws of political gravity and that he is kind of Teflon Don still.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, Patrice, what do you think? Do you see any of these candidates trying to vie for a place in the Cabinet? Or do you think that there could be an independent run? Or are we looking at this race just being over and everybody just kind of fading away?\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: Everyone is going to fade away. I do think that, come Super Tuesday, it will be a done deal. And very likely it's going to be for President Trump, unless there is some sort of Hail Mary.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: And I do think the hangers-on right now are hoping that something -- in Colorado, that's probably not likely going to be the case anymore -- but maybe he doesn't end up on a ballot in one of these different key states, or there's some other unexpected happening.\par \par \uc2 Listen, in the start of 2020, I think we all thought it was a done deal for President Trump, and we saw what happened in 2020 in March of that year. So there's never a completely 100 percent guarantee that he's going to win, but it looks pretty good for him.\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to the issues, I'm not just limiting it to the prosecutions against President Trump, I actually think that the bread-and- butter issues, the economy, where people are at, and they remember a president that -- a President Trump presidency when they could actually afford to go to the grocery store, where the price of everyday life was so much lower.\par \par \uc2 And I think that they're ready for -- people are ready to go back to a time where normal actually helped their pockets. It didn't hurt their pockets. So I think things are good for him, but there's no guarantee.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, you might be seeing some people hold their nose when they cast the ballots next year, because it's just the way things are.\par \par \uc2 But Doug, Patrice, Daniel, I appreciate your help on all this. Have a great 2024. We're starting the year off right. All of us are here. Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Happy new year to all.\par \par \uc2 LIPPMAN: Happy new year.\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: Thank you. Happy new year. . LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Yes, 25 states with minimum wage hikes this year. That already has one company telling workers to take a hike.\par \par \uc2 And who are you more excited to see over the holidays, mom, dad, or their dog? Hope the answer doesn't send you the doghouse.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: I'm Mike Emanuel from FOX News in Washington. Happy new year. "Your World" continues in a moment, but, first, this "Special Report" newsbreak.\par \par \uc2 Israel's military announces the first significant drawdown of troops since its war against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  began. Thousands of soldiers are being pulled out of the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  returns to Washington tomorrow after his Caribbean vacation.\par \par \uc2 Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  rang in the new year in Iowa. Other candidates will return to the campaign trail tomorrow with less than two weeks to go before the first presidential contest.\par \par \uc2 And with the new year comes new laws. The minimum wage has increased for 22 states. In California, illegal immigrants are eligible for health insurance. And Florida is requiring elected officials to reveal more details about their net worth.\par \par \uc2 I will be back for "Special Report" live at 6:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 Now back to "Your World."\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Ready, set, hike, 25 states boosting their minimum wages in the new year, including California, where Pizza Hut is laying off more than 1,200 delivery drivers at its shops there.\par \par \uc2 Now, so this -- as hikes kick in,more companies will tell workers maybe to take a hike? We will have to see.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from Brandon Arnold of the national Taxpayers Union.\par \par \uc2 So, Brandon, happy new year, first of all.\par \par \uc2 And is this a natural consequence of a hike of minimum wage, a big hike in minimum wage?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON ARNOLD, EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT, NATIONAL TAXPAYER UNION: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 You're going to see reduced employment in all the states, essentially, where this is taking place. Job numbers remain fairly strong, but they're starting to show signs of weakness. In fact, if you move out of the health care industry and education and hospitality and leisure, we're actually seeing a reduction in jobs right now.\par \par \uc2 So this is just going to pour more gasoline on that fire as labor markets start to weaken.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But do you think other companies will follow suit then?\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 This is the tip of the iceberg. And I think what happens, particularly in small businesses who may not have these elaborate accounting firms and CPAs and so forth, they operate more on a field basis. So as they start to see these labor costs increase, they may not lay people off immediately, but when times get tough, they're going to have to make changes.\par \par \uc2 They're going to either have to raise prices, start to reduce those labor costs, or combination of both. And that's not fair to those employees that are getting laid off, nor is it fair to the customers that are all of a sudden paying $12, $15 for a Big Mac.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, and, in some cases, so, the layoffs -- well, so I'm wondering if you think the layoffs will matter to Democratic candidates who are -- or lawmakers who are pushing minimum wage?\par \par \uc2 Because they will just blame the companies. They will say, hey, those companies are still making profits. They should turn some of those profits back into workers' wages.\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD: They will.\par \par \uc2 And, listen, so many of these small businesses, or even large businesses, have been hurt really bad in these Democratic strongholds. You look at California in particular. Crime has become a huge problem, retail theft, the safety of their customers, the safety of their employees. And so we have started to see this trend where they have left these large cities, where things are a little out of control, to say the least.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD: This is, again, just pouring more fuel on that fire. We're going to see those big businesses lay off people. Pizza Hut made that announcement already that sent shockwaves to a lot of folks.\par \par \uc2 And those small businesses, those are the ones that I think that are really going to take it on the chin, because they don't have the economies of scale. They don't have the access to capital that these bigger businesses do. That's where the problem is really going to be acute.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And the push is not ending. Senator Bernie Sanders this year was saying that he would like to see $17-an-hour minimum wage across the nation.\par \par \uc2 Now, Washington, D.C., already has that, but some other countries, smaller cities, Des Moines, Iowa,some -- those cities can't handle $17 an hour, right?\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD: Yes, I mean, minimum wage increases are almost always problematic, because politicians can't get that number exactly right where you're raising them to the level where you're helping people, but not hurting the small businesses affected.\par \par \uc2 But when you do that on a national basis, it's going to be an absolute disaster.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD: Like you said, those lower-cost jurisdictions, those small businesses are not going to be able to afford those huge labor costs that are going to flow from such a hike. So you're going to see 10, 12, 15 percent reduction in the number of jobs available.\par \par \uc2 All of those people would much rather be working for $8, $9, $10 an hour than to be on the unemployment line.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So is there a way to get wages up without mandating?\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD: Yes, I mean, there's several ways. The first thing is to have a good business climate. That means reducing crime. That means having reasonable tax rates, reasonable levels of regulation.\par \par \uc2 But I think a more direct approach ought to be fixing the EITC. And that is a tax credit that is intended to help the working poor. It provides benefits to people that are in the labor force that are doing their best, but might be on the lower rungs of the economic ladder. Right now, it's kind of a disaster, very high improper payment rates, money not flowing to the people that need it most.\par \par \uc2 It needs to be fixed. I'd much rather see politicians devote their time to that than to putting these job-killing policies into place.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Brandon Arnold, great discussion to begin the new year. Happy new year to you, first of all.\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD: Same to you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. Yes, I appreciate it. And the discussion will continue with minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 So, are the holidays more taxing than tax season? Find out why this time of year has folks so stressed out -- coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: The most wonderful time of year, or maybe not, a new survey revealing 63 percent of Americans find the holidays more stressful than tax season.\par \par \uc2 Are my next guests feeling that stress? Joining me now, FOX News contributor Scott Martin and comedian Alli Breen.\par \par \uc2 So, Scott, let's start with you first, the financial expert. What do you think? More stress than tax season?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MARTIN, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Probably.\par \par \uc2 And that's also, Edward, because of expectations. When I go into tax season, I don't really have high expectations. I expect things to go wrong, and, therefore, they typically do on April 15 and afterwards. But if you look at Christmas, you look at the holidays just in general, there's a lot of expectations of things to go right, and seeing that loved one, your loved ones, and the food being great and everybody acting very nice to each other.\par \par \uc2 And when something goes wrong, which is because everybody's expecting things to go so well, that's why it might feel so bad.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Ali, you stressed out about the holidays? Is it the family gatherings? Is it what you guys are talking about?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ALLI BREEN, COMEDIAN: I mean, it's everything. Thank God for eggnog. Nothing disguises heavy drinking like the nog.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREEN: Just sip that and relax.\par \par \uc2 I don't know. Christmas is less stressful. New Year's is the one that's really stressful, I think, because it starts out stressful. You don't know what to do. You don't know who you're going to kiss. We're heading into a new year. They tell us it's the beginning of World War III. It's the last year of democracy, global -- I mean, it's like, yes, it's complete stress.\par \par \uc2 You don't know what you're headed into. You have to find a good resolution. Very stressful.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Finding that resolution, that's tough for me.\par \par \uc2 BREEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Scott, do you have -- speaking of which, do have your New Year's -- Scott, do have your New Year's -- New Year's resolution? I can talk.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Just to not act like myself again, I guess, the next year,\par \par \uc2 Edward, I do have people to kiss. That's not my problem, actually. But I agree with Ali too.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: The presents too. When you think about Christmas and giving people gifts and receiving gifts, that's a lot of pressure and certainly things that typically go wrong, because everybody expects things to go right.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Now, speaking of stress, you might want to be careful the next time you're walking and texting.\par \par \uc2 That's because injuries from twalking -- I guess that's a thing, twalking - - are rising.\par \par \uc2 So, Ali, have you ever found -- fallen victim to twalking?\par \par \uc2 BREEN: Yes, I mean, that actually sounds dirty. I'm glad you defined it.\par \par \uc2 I didn't know what that was at first. Oh, God, yes. I have walked into trees. I have walked into doors. I have walked into everything. I'm surprised I haven't gone straight into a lake or the Hudson River for -- it's dangerous. Yes, you got to pay attention to your surroundings. But it's hard. You got to text.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREEN: You got to let people know what's going on.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, and it's like one of those zombie -- oh, wait, I'm sorry. It's like one of those zombie things.\par \par \uc2 BREEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: As you're walking, everybody's looking down at their phone as you're walking down.\par \par \uc2 And how about you, Scott? Have you run into things?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: All the time. I mean, I run into things when I don't have a phone in my hand, Edward. You know that.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you that what's funny is everybody out there should take a little survey or take a little observation of how many people are actually walking in traffic, crossing the road, getting into their car with their phones in their hands, throwing bags around, whatnot, and not paying attention to things, only to their phone, and how much of a detriment that actually is for their lives.\par \par \uc2 Because, as Ali said, you're walking in the middle of traffic, you're running into trees, telephone poles, whatever it may be, and you're risking dubious injury there.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So what is it with the phone that people just can't let it go? Ali, what is it?\par \par \uc2 BREEN: It's so addictive. It's so addictive.\par \par \uc2 But no matter how addicted we are, like Scott said, we will do it all the time, but then whoever's doing it that we're seeing do it, we're angry at. You're like, get off your phone, pay attention, look around, you know?\par \par \uc2 But it's so -- even in the car, like, I worry about teenagers starting to drive. I think it's more dangerous than drinking and driving.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREEN: I would be so, like, scrolling through TikTok when I was a teenager. It's horrible.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So man's best friend for a reason, a new survey finding nearly half of Americans believe that their loved ones are more excited to see their pets than see them over the holidays.\par \par \uc2 What do you think, Scott? More excited to see -- when you go somewhere, you more excited to see the pet or the relatives?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Well, my family's watching this, Edward, so let's just say I'm excited to see both, but the dogs and the cats are great. I mean, there's no judgment. They're always happy to see.\par \par \uc2 Sure, they maybe make a mess when they're eating, but, hey, that's a small price to pay versus your relatives.\par \par \uc2 But a quick story on Christmas last week for us. Our dog bit a couple of our guests that came over, so that's something to kind of put in peril as well, because our guests, the humans, didn't do that, but the dog did.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Oh, yes, my pet rattlesnake, that would be hard too to go -- but, Ali, so what about you? You more excited to see the pets?\par \par \uc2 BREEN: I -- last holiday that I spent with my family just this past Christmas, all of my pictures are of their dog.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREEN: That's all that I have. I realized I took like one picture of the family, 25 pictures of the dog. This dog didn't bite. It's all full of love.\par \par \uc2 We did have one human who bit people though, so we were -- unfortunately, the children need to calm down.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: That's where the eggnog comes in, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREEN: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Ali, Scott, I appreciate it. Thanks for this. This has been a great debate. Happy -- happy 2024, New Year's Eve. Happy year.\par \par \uc2 BREEN: To you too.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: It's a good way to start it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Happy new year. See you.\par \par \uc2 BREEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: It's a good way to start it.\par \par \uc2 BREEN: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Until debt do us part. It's a new year, and if you racked up holiday debt, you're going to have to start paying it soon.\par \par \uc2 Find out how to tackle those bills on a special edition of "Your World" next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Taking on debt in the new year, and it could be a lot based on holiday shopping and the surge we're seeing, a survey showing that half of shoppers will incur some kind of debt this holiday season.\par \par \uc2 And get this; 37 percent say it will take months to pay it off.\par \par \uc2 So, how can you tackle that debt in 2024?\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from personal finance expert Derrick Kinney.\par \par \uc2 Derrick, it just a factor of people making bad choices, buying too much from what they have, just one of those spur-of-the-moment-type things?\par \par \uc2 DERRICK KINNEY, GOOD MONEY FRAMEWORK: Most people don't set out to really mess themselves up financially. They tend to buy gifts for the people they love.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, their budget doesn't love them back and they dig themselves a big hole that's going to be hard to get out of without a game plan and a strategy for the new year.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, so give me that game plan. How do you crush the debt, holiday debt that, you're racking up?\par \par \uc2 KINNEY: Well, step number one, I want to make this simple for our viewers, is, arrange your debt on a piece of paper, on your notes app on your phone by highest interest to lowest interest, and you want to knock out every extra dollar you have got to the highest interest debt that is holding you back.\par \par \uc2 I want your money to be working as hard as you did to earn it. Don't worry about the lowest interest rate debt. Make sure you're attacking the highest interest rate. That's going to help save you money and get you better on track and make progress.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And I see on there you have, set goals. You want to make goals as you go along, right, that you can achieve.\par \par \uc2 KINNEY: Yes, ideally with the person you love the most, you want to have an accountability partner if you can. It's going to make it a whole lot less lonely, sacrificing, living on ramen noodles, if you will. Just joking about that.\par \par \uc2 But, as you make progress, set quarterly goals and say, every quarter, we want to target this amount of debt payoff, but also important, Edward, determine what's at stake if you don't achieve the goal. For example, will I not be able to retire or crawl on the floor chasing my grandkids or maybe my son or daughter can't go to the college they want to?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KINNEY: That can cause you to say, I will not let that happen. I'm going to make financial progress.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, you need to make it real.\par \par \uc2 And I also like the last one where you talk about giving yourself a little treat.\par \par \uc2 KINNEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Give yourself something that you can hold on to or look forward to when you make that goal.\par \par \uc2 KINNEY: That's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 Think about when we were kids. Our parents would incentivize us to do the hard things. If you do well in school, here's a treat. And, really, as adults, we're all kids at heart. So what you want to do is every quarter say, when I pay down this amount of debt, I'm going to go to a concert with my best friend or with my spouse,or I'm going to get a pedicure, or we're going to do something fun together.\par \par \uc2 Because if you're living on, as I joked earlier, ramen noodles, you're barely making it, you have taken all the joy out of your life, you're not going to have much fun. And it's going to be even harder to achieve those goals.\par \par \uc2 By the way, though, Edward, if you do incentivize yourself, pay cash for it. Don't put it on the credit card.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. Yes, that's a good idea.\par \par \uc2 And in the last 30 seconds we have, what do you recommend for people buying gifts in these economic conditions going forward?\par \par \uc2 KINNEY: Well, you want to separate it from needs versus wants.\par \par \uc2 And right now, being frugal is cool. And it's important to let people know, look, if you have a tight budget, say, you know what, this year, things are tight for me financially, but, instead of a gift, I want to give you the quality time. Let's have an experience together.\par \par \uc2 Because, oftentimes, the gift, they forget. The experience, they never forget.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 KINNEY: And it costs you a whole lot less money.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, I appreciate it. The tips are very insightful. Thank you for -- thank you for your insight there, Derrick Kinney. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KINNEY: My pleasure. Thank you, Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: As we begin the new year, one final look back at all of the best interviews, events and insights from Neil Cavuto and "Your World" last year.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: As we start the new year, we take another look back at the best moments that made "Your World" in 2023.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 