Plus, whatever happened to the Russian military contractor, the \uc2 Wagner group\uc2 ? And Bret Baier explains what additional infrastructure the U.S. may need to meet the Biden administration's electric vehicle goals?\par \par \uc2 But first, "BREAKING TONIGHT", President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  prepares to win over voters after what many say was a tough year. The president is on a family vacation in the Caribbean amid the expanding conflict in the Middle East, the war in Ukraine and as the crisis at the border swells to record numbers, these challenges will remain flashpoints in 2024.\par \par \uc2 As a new Fox poll shows how voters think this year has gone, just 24 percent think 2023 was a good year. While that number has steadily risen since 2020, it is still 13 percentage points lower than it was in 2019.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Tomlinson is traveling with the president and reports from the Virgin Islands on how President Biden plans to get back into the race. Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: Trace, President Biden faces a number of political battles when he returns to Washington.\par \par \uc2 Impeachment Inquiry looms on Capitol Hill, as well as passing that massive spending bill to fund the southern border, Israel, and Ukraine, and avoid a government shutdown. His son Hunter has a January court date.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): More bad news. A new Fox poll shows 39 percent of Democrats think it was a good year for the country. 10 percent of Republicans and 20 percent of independents agree.\par \par \uc2 But a majority of Democrats think it was a good year for their families. One in four Republicans and a third of independents say the same.\par \par \uc2 The oldest sitting president in U.S. history has a grueling reelection campaign ahead. While Biden has refused to file for the Democratic primary in New Hampshire, the Republican front runner is fighting to stay on the ballot in several