in a civil lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Well, there a startling new data from the southern border, there have now been more than 276,000 migrant encounters in December so far.\par \par \uc2 That's the highest monthly count on record. And since the new fiscal year began on October 1st, there have already been more than 760,000 encounters at the southern border, also a record for the highest quarterly count.\par \par \uc2 As migrants enter the U.S., many are traveling north, where cities and now suburbs are overwhelmed by the rapid population increases.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE" Fox Business correspondent Lydia Hu looks at the impact in New York. But we begin with correspondent Garrett Tenney, who explains how the crisis is spreading outside of major cities.\par \par \uc2 Garrett, good evening.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Trace, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 The migrant crisis is no longer just a border city problem or a sanctuary city problem. It is now a growing concern for the suburbs as well.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There are some doors that we should not open.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's a physical security threat. It's a health threat.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don't even know how we could consider taking this island because the city of Chicago cannot handle it.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): This week, the village of Elburn, outside Chicago, became one of a growing number of suburbs taking action to try to mitigate the fallout of the migrant crisis.\par \par \uc2 JOHN NEVENHOVEN, VILLAGE ADMINISTRATOR, ELBURN, ILLINOIS: When they show up at our station, fine, let's get them on the train. Let's get them downtown.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): At least, 10 Chicago suburbs have passed new rules to prevent migrants from being dropped off in their towns, after Democratic Mayor Brandon Johnson crackdown on migrant busing to control the flow of migrants coming to his sanctuary city.\par \par \uc2 Now, groups like this one today from Venezuela are being dropped off in the suburbs and taking trains into the city.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Crisis.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Venezuela crisis.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's crisis, yes. Crisis.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Officials in Grundy County about 60 miles southwest of Chicago, say, Mayor Johnson is creating chaos for the suburbs, and they now have signs like this one along the highway, saying, "No migrant buses."\par \par \uc2 Other sanctuary city suburbs are bracing for impact as well. In the town of Wheat Ridge, Colorado, more than 100 people attended a meeting to protest, after hearing Denver officials were considering using one of the suburbs hotels to shelter migrants.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If Denver wants to be a sanctuary for these immigrants, then, Denver needs to be the sanctuary.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): And tonight, another Chicago suburb, McHenry is expected to join the growing list of suburbs restricting migrant buses and we're told many more putting together plans to announce in the new year. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Garrett Tenney in Chicago tonight, Garrett, thank you. The migrant crisis is also having an economic impact on cities throughout the U.S. Some businesses have closed as shelters and housing become overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Lydia Hu is at the New York Roosevelt Hotel, where many retail spaces are now vacant as the migrants have moved in. Hey, Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Trace, the ones iconic Roosevelt Hotel in midtown Manhattan, closed during the pandemic and reopened as an 800 room shelter for migrants about seven months ago.\par \par \uc2 Now, small businesses that operate on the same block say they are suffering.\par \par \uc2 The owner of this coffee shop says, before the shelter opened, coffee sales were growing,\par \par \uc2 but now, fewer customers stop in, revenue is down 20 to 25 percent over last year, he says.\par \par \uc2 The owner of a shoe store tells the New York Post, her sales are down too. She wants the landlord to reduce her rent.\par \par \uc2 Small businesses used to thrive along the upscale street of Madison Avenue here in midtown Manhattan. But now, about half of the retail spaces that occupied the perimeter of the shelter appear vacant like this, boarded up, signs advertising, retail space for rent.\par \par \uc2 More than 7,000 migrants arrived in New York City in just the last two weeks. currently more than 68,000 remain in this cities care.\par \par \uc2 The president of a nonprofit representing 300 city business leaders said in a statement, "Conditions around the Roosevelt Hotel reflect the impact of the migrant crisis on many communities across the city. Clearly, this has delayed or reversed small business recovery from the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 New York City remains a top destination for migrants as they can get shelter at places like the Roosevelt Hotel under local law for now.\par \par \uc2 New York's democratic governor supports New York City's mayor's efforts to suspend the right to shelter law as the city is nearing a breaking point. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Lydia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 An Iranian national with ties to terrorism has been arrested at the northern border and returned to Canada, after he tried to illegally cross near Niagara Falls.\par \par \uc2 The suspect is a permanent Canadian resident and wanted by authorities there on assault charges. The man had attempted to enter the U.S., at least, two other times in October.\par \par \uc2 A New Jersey man is facing charges over his alleged efforts to provide material support to al Shabaab. The group is a designated foreign terrorist organization. Federal officials say the suspect was motivated by the October 7th terrorist attacks in Israel. Devoted himself to waging violent jihad against America and its allies, and had planned to join and train with the \uc2 al-Shabaab\uc2  in Somalia.\par \par \uc2 While the U.S. is now authorizing another nearly $150 million in weapons support for Israel that is the second time this month, the Biden administration is bypassing Congress to approve an emergency weapon sale. It comes as some countries call for Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to reach a peace deal.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Matt Finn takes a look tonight from London.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): For the first time since Israel launched ground operations, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  says it's attacking the exact kibbutz that served as the base for \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants before they crossed into Israel for the October 7th terror attack.\par \par \uc2 Israel says it's intensifying its southern Gaza operations named Oz and Nir in honor of the Israeli kibbutz that was savagely attacked. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  says it's eliminated significant terrorists, tunnels, and targets, like rocket launch positions.\par \par \uc2 New images show tens of thousands of Palestinian refugees seeking shelter in the overwhelmed town of Rafah in the southernmost part of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Israel's unprecedented air and ground strikes have now reportedly displaced an estimated 85 percent of Gaza is 2.3 million people. Today, a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  delegation is reportedly considering Egypt's peace plan.\par \par \uc2 The three states proposal calls for a ceasefire, hostage swamps, and for \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to relinquish power in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  says it's no deal unless Israel ends attacks. Israel has made it clear \uc2 Hamas\uc2  will not control a post war Gaza.\par \par \uc2 And we're hearing for the first time from one of the hostages taken from the Nova Music Festival on October 7th.\par \par \uc2 Mia Shem says she was kept in a dark room for 54 days, monitored by a terrorist in a civilian house.\par \par \uc2 MIA SHEM, RELEASED HOSTAGE (through translator): Its families under \uc2 Hamas\uc2  control. Suddenly, I started asking myself questions. Why am I at a family's house? Why are there children here?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN (on camera): And today, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, emphasize how critical it is for third parties to stay out of this war, state affiliated or not, making note that the United States and Israel are strong partners committed to peace in the Middle East. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Matt Finn in London tonight. Matt, thank you. The Treasury Department is targeting the flow of Iranian money to Houthi forces with new sanctions. Those are imposed on a group of money exchange services from Yemen and Turkey that allegedly helped provide funding for Houthi attacks on commercial ships in the Red Sea.\par \par \uc2 The militant group has stepped up attacks against commercial vessels, tracking data shows that, at least, four tankers transporting diesel and fuel from the Middle East are taking longer routes to avoid the Red Sea -- sparking concerns about rising costs and supply disruption.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we look at "WHATEVER HAPPENED TO", the Russian military contractor, the \uc2 Wagner group\uc2 , following the death of its longtime leader.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight, Ukraine reports what had caused Russia's biggest aerial bombardment since the beginning of the invasion.\par \par \uc2 At least 30 civilians were killed with more than 140 people injured. And others buried in rubble during the 18-hour attack that claimed at least 30 lives. President Biden condemned today's attacks.\par \par \uc2 France is increasing security efforts ahead of New Year's Eve with 90,000 officers set to deploy, around 6,000 will be stationed in Paris, where more than 1.5 million people are expected to attend celebrations on the Champs- Elysees.\par \par \uc2 France's defense minister warns, there is a very high terrorist threat, in part, because of the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war.\par \par \uc2 And the live look now at Rome. One of the big stories there tonight, several climate change protesters smear a Gucci Christmas tree with washable paint in Milan.\par \par \uc2 Activists say they blame the luxury fashion industry for exploiting natural resources and widening the economic wage gap.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. And we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHIEF BREAKING NEWS CORRESPONDENT: China is appointing a new defense minister as the country's military attempts to assert its dominance in the Indo Pacific region. Former Navy Commander Dong Jun replaces previous Minister Li Shangfu , who has not been publicly seen or heard from since August. It is also unclear why Li was fired. Dong's naval background is significant amid increased Chinese aggression in the South China Sea.\par \par \uc2 North Korea's new reactor at its main nuclear complex is expected to be formally operational by next summer. South Korea's defense minister says the light water reactor appears to be in a trial stage. The new reactor would be an additional source for bomb fuels and could allow North Korea to produce more plutonium.\par \par \uc2 While the Russian military contractor the \uc2 Wagner group\uc2  once played a significant role in the invasion of Ukraine, now, with its longtime leader dead, there are questions over the group's future.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's Whatever Happened To, Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin takes a look at where mercenaries are regrouping and who is leading the charge.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): When the plane carrying \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 , the onetime ally of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and founder of the \uc2 Wagner group\uc2  exploded in midair near Moscow on August 23rd, killing him and his top associates, many thought the \uc2 Wagner group\uc2 's days were numbered.\par \par \uc2 DMITRI ALPEROVITCH, AUTHOR OF WORLD ON THE BRINK: I think it's highly unlikely that it will reconstitute its former glory.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Dmitri Alperovitch was born in Russia and is the author of world on the brink.\par \par \uc2 ALPEROVITCH: It doesn't have access to the recruitment of prisoners, of convicts, whatever is left of it, I think is going to be a shadow of its former self.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): The plane crash came two months after Prigozhin staged a mutiny against \uc2 Russia's Defense Ministry\uc2  and began driving an armored column toward Moscow. And just days after the disgraced Wagner chief spoke during a visit to Africa.\par \par \uc2 ALPEROVITCH: The core of the convicts that Wagner was so famous for recruited from prisons have been taken over by the Ministry of Defense.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): The Wagner fighters who went to Africa to extract diamonds, gold and raw materials, and recruit mercenaries for Putin's wars are regrouping under new leadership, including Prigozhin's own 25-year-old son Pavel.\par \par \uc2 ALPEROVITCH: There is a feeling that the family and Wagner fighters have learned their lesson, you do not cross the regime.\par \par \uc2 RAMA YADE, SENIOR DIRECTOR, ATLANTIC COUNCIL AFRICA CENTER: Without Wagner, Putin would lose a substantial part of his African foothold. You have all these Russian state owned companies very active in mining, in hydrocarbons.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Founded by Prigozhin in 2014. Putin used the \uc2 Wagner group\uc2  to carry out coups in Africa. He exchanged weapons for mining rights, especially in Mali, Sudan, Libya and the Central African Republic.\par \par \uc2 YADE: He feeds the war in Ukraine. For Putin himself, the trading of mining concessions allowed him to avoid the Western sanctions.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): At the Pentagon, Jennifer Griffin, Fox News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And up next, several states are raising the minimum wage in the new year as some companies warn the move comes with consequences for businesses and consumers.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 2 in San Francisco where sheriff's deputies found a homemade pipe bomb during vehicle search. The San Joaquin County Sheriff's Office says deputies searched the vehicle parked outside a home while serving a search warrant.\par \par \uc2 Fox 28 Columbus where Ohio's Republican Governor vetoes a measure that would have banned transgender athletes from participating in girl's sports and gender affirming care for minors.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  determined the bill would impact a small number of children and would create circumstances where state law overrules medical decisions made by both parents and physicians.\par \par \uc2 And a live look at Times Square from Earth Cam, one of the big stories there tonight, New Year's Eve organizers test out the confetti drop. According to the Time Square Alliance, more than one ton of confetti will be dropped. Some pieces will include wishes from people around the world for what they are hopeful for in the New Year.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT and we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Breaking tonight, a federal judge has temporarily blocked most of an Iowa law that bans some school library books and forbids teachers from discussing sexual orientation and gender identity with students. The judge says both bans are incredibly broad and has resulted in the removal of history volumes and award winning novels. That law was set to take effect January 1st.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, massive waves still flooding towns along most of California's coast. At least eight people were hospitalized in Ventura County as waves crashed over beach barriers sending a surge of water inland. The National Weather Service has issued flood warnings and high surf warnings for waves breaking between, no kidding here, 28 and 33 feet.\par \par \uc2 While the minimum wage will be higher in 22 states and dozens of cities and counties in the New Year but small businesses and major companies say the increase in wages could come with unintended consequences.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Kelly Saberi is in Chicago tonight to tell us about it. Kelly, good evening.\par \par \uc2 KELLY SABERI, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Trey. Some business owners say they recognize the need for better wages as inflation persists. The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  explains that $1.00 in 2023 can buy roughly 70 percent of what it could buy in 2009. That's the last year the federal minimum wage increased.\par \par \uc2 But business owners tell us they would rather make that call at their own discretion. Federal minimum wage remains at $7.25. Illinois is one of 22 states along with 38 cities and counties that will increase the minimum wage in the New Year. So on January 1st, all workers in Illinois will see a $1 increase for a minimum wage of $14 an hour. But that's not even among the highest wages around the country. As of next week, Washington state and California will be in the $16 an hour range, while Connecticut, New Jersey, and New York all have increases to $15 an hour or more. For new and small businesses, the wage increase threatens their ability to grow.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUSS NYGAARD, OUR JOURNEYS CAFE AND BOBA SHOP: The costs involved with it is really a detriment to us right now to what can do just in order to maintain our costs the way we