y EVs.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 In our last segment looking at electric vehicles, we told you about the oversupply manufacturers and dealerships are reporting. Tonight, we see what additional infrastructure would be needed to help reach President Biden's emissions goals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CHATTERJEE, FORMER FEDERAL ENERGY REGULATORY COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: They are promoting policies that will drive the sale of electric vehicles but necessarily the use of electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The number of electric vehicle charging ports in the U.S. has nearly doubled in past three years. Public charging ports account for 88 percent of all E.V. charging ports.\par \par \uc2 With E.V. charges, does that add, obviously, more stress on the grid, more need for electricity?\par \par \uc2 JIM MATHESON, NATIONAL RURAL ELECTRIC COOP ASSOCIATION: Yes, the increased demand itself adds stress, and on top of that, it's a different kind of load. People want to charge their cars really fast. And so that's a lot of electricity in a short amount of time. It's not the way the grid is set up to operate today.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are three different levels of charging stations. Only about 200 are level one. Those can take more than 50 hours to charge a vehicle from empty to 80 percent full. Most public stations are level two. Those can charge a vehicle to 80 percent in four to ten hours. Just 15 percent of public chargers are fast charging, level three. Those take the least amount of time, but not as quick as a trip to the gas station. It takes between 20 minutes to an hour to get a vehicle to 80 percent charged at those level three stations.\par \par \uc2 MATHESON: Every one has its own set of complexities, and it's hard to get these things built. These things don't just happen overnight. You've got get permitting and cited. So it's not as easy as people make it out to be.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: OK, here we go.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In 2021 when President Biden signed the $1 trillion infrastructure bill into law, about $7.5 billion of that was allocated for building electric vehicle charging stations. It took more than two years for the first fast charging stations to open.\par \par \uc2 That's a major -- it's a major drawback.\par \par \uc2 MATHESON: It tells you how tough it is to make this system work. And every situation is different. So you have these national programs as one-size- fits-all approach. It doesn't work. And in this case, the uptake for this has been really slow.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: that's a problem.\par \par \uc2 MATHESON: Yes, that is a problem. And I've got to say I represent a lot of the rural areas where there is not as big of an uptake on electric vehicles because the range people have to drive in their vehicle every day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Energy Department is tracking locations across the U.S. where an electric vehicle road trip would be possible using what the department calls fuel corridors. But most are still in planning stations. Those currently in place are mostly along the east and west coast. A 2,400-mile road trip along Route 66 is not yet possible in an electric vehicle.\par \par \uc2 And forcing Americans to do it in some kind of transition that the car dealers and car manufacturers are doing, it just does not seem to be taking. The market drives the thing, usually.\par \par \uc2 MATHESON: More electricity sounds good in a lot of ways for the people I represent. But, on the other hand, we want to make sure it's delivered in a reliable, affordable way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Wireless charging roads are also being installed around the world. Swedish company \uc2 Electreon\uc2  completed the first project stateside in Detroit last month. Coils are laid beneath the asphalt and a receiver is connected to the vehicle. As the car drives the battery unit is powered directly.\par \par \uc2 CHATTERJEE: And if the market through innovation can have these technological breakthroughs that enable to us have this futuristic kind of advancement in how our transportation section functions, that's amazing. And I hope that that happens. But I want the market to drive that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): \uc2 Electreon\uc2  is working on several wireless charging projects across eight countries. Those include bus terminals in Israel, delivery truck loading docks in Sweden, and trucks at ports in China.\par \par \uc2 We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Bret, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the 2024 election, Biden's year-end review, and what's ahead. Plus, the panelists share their New Year's resolutions.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: The idea that one bureaucrat in executive position can simply unilaterally disqualify someone from office, that turns on its head every notion of constitutional due process that this country has always abided by for over 200 years. It opens up pandora's box.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This should be decided by the voters of the United States. It should not be decided by courts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: With that, let's bring in our panel, Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, and Daniel Lippman, "Politico" White House and Washington reporter. Welcome to all of you. I mean, you heard the intro there, Mollie Hemingway, and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  makes a fair point there because it really does kind of blow up due process. Who needs voters when you can just stick a bureaucrat in there?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": Yes. When the United States expresses concern with other countries about whether their elections are free or fair, usually what they're referring to is that the ruling party has removed opposition candidates from the ballot. This is a humongous threat to the legitimacy of the 2024 election. It's not the only threat. You have a Democrat Party that has engaged in basically using any tool at the hand, whether it's criminal prosecution, civil litigation, removal from the ballot and other efforts to take out their most formidable opponent in the 2024 election. It's a very big threat to the republic and one I'm not sure people are taking as seriously as they need to be.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And we keep putting up this map, Juan, of all these states that are considering the same kind of ideas about kicking Trump off the ballot. It just seems to me like, look, the Supreme Court hasn't yet weighed in. We expect that to happen. But this is going to kind of be this trickle until the election.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I don't think it's any question about it, Trace. It makes for a very uncertain future here. The reality is you have like 14 states right now looking at just this issue. And you already have had California say no, you will stay on the ballot, but Colorado, Maine, Oregon is soon to make a decision. The reality is in Maine that the law allows the secretary of state to make this call, as unfair as some may feel. So it's within her power to do so. And she wrote an extensive documentation as to why she decided. She said she felt Trump had incited the insurrection, the effort to overthrow the government, the refusal -- effort to stop the Congress from certifying the election.\par \par \uc2 But this is going to go to the Supreme Court. And guess what? Once it gets to the court, it's not going to be over because a lot of people don't trust the Supreme Court to be nonpolitical in their thinking given recent controversial decisions and the conservative supermajority on the court.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, and due process does not mean that a secretary of state gets to decide if you're guilty of insurrection. If you have never been -- if you have never been found guilty and you've never been charged with it, you can't have bureaucrats decide what you are guilty and not guilty of, Daniel.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL LIPPMAN, "POLITICO": Yes. And you haven't seen the Democratic politicians in Maine back the secretary of state. They have mostly said this is not a good idea.\par \par \uc2 But I think this is basically good for Trump's short-term in the primary because it helps Republicans go to his cause. But long term it reminds general election suburban swing voters that this is a risk they are taking if they take on Trump, that he is facing a lot of criminal trials. No other candidate, like \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  or Nikki Haley, would even be dreaming of getting off the ballot like this.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Meantime, here is "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ." They are talking about Trump-Biden rematch. They say, quoting here, "Unlike 2020 there is no guarantee most voters will see President Biden as the safer bet between the two men to bring order back to America. In no small part because Mr. Biden was elected to do so and has not delivered it." Doesn't really leave a lot to run on for the Biden campaign, Mollie.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Yes. It's a very difficult, challenging campaign for Biden that is he facing. It almost is reminiscent of 1980 when you had widespread malaise in the economy, rising prices. You had problems with international situations with conflicts abroad. And that is the situation that Biden finds himself in, in addition to all sorts of other things, like a manmade disaster at the border through his policies of opening up the border. And so it's going to be really difficult for him to make the case in 2024, and he won't have the ease that he had in 2020.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And to kind of illustrate Mollie's point there, "Newsweek" writes the following headline, "\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  less popular than last four presidents who failed to win second term," going on to say, "Biden's approval rating is about 10 points behind former Democratic President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  at this point in their presidencies." I would like to couple that with the new FOX News poll, 2023 for the country, good year, bad year -- good year, 24 percent, bad year, 66 percent. I mean, how do you recover from those kinds of numbers, Juan?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I mean, remember even in the FOX poll you see a surge in terms of where the country is thinking and feeling about itself was just a few years ago. You see some improvement even among Republicans there who are, of course, less likely to want to say anything good about an administration, a Democratic administration.\par \par \uc2 But I think that when you look at Biden's problems. A lot of it has to do with governing, Trace. And we are talking about the international crises, Ukraine and Russia, the Middle East, China, things that could turn around, could have a different look in a few months. And certainly, when you look at the economy, you could see attitudes shift pretty quickly. We have just closed out a year in which, as you reported earlier, the stock market was up 14 percent, unemployment is down, wages going up. So, it could be that the perception of people's economic well-being could shift in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, and Juan makes a fair point there, because with all the bad news, all the bad Biden scenarios, Quinnipiac says in the latest poll here, Biden 47 percent, Trump 46 percent. Daniel, the bottom line is we all know it's going to be raiser close. Razor close.\par \par \uc2 LIPPMAN: It is. And there's a lot of Democrats who think that Biden can increase his numbers by attacking Trump more and not having as many positive ads about Biden's economy, which is not really clicking. I think the problem that Biden faces is that he has focused a lot of his long-term investments like climate change, Chips, infrastructure, not as much on the short-term stuff that have people struggling to pay their bills.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, it's a fair point.\par \par \uc2 Mollie, I have got 10 seconds for you to wrap us up in this whole thing. Razor thin, we all know it's going to come down to six, maybe seven states.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: It's good to remember, though, too, that four years ago Biden was leading Trump by a huge percentage, and still that election ended up razor close, that even this latest poll shows them tied, it shows the strength Trump has relative to Biden this year.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Now, really what we need to find out is what your New Year's resolutions are, because that is key to all of us. Juan Williams, 2024, what's it going to be for you?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Ignore conventional wisdom is what I say as a resolution, Trace, because I think between a record amount of political spending in this coming year and the advance of artificial intelligence, it's going to be a unique political year, a stormy, rollercoaster ride.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Daniel, is it as stormy on your side of 2024?\par \par \uc2 LIPPMAN: I hope not so much. But I know we are on TV, but my resolution is to read more books, particularly fiction, to expand my mind and not just focus on the swamp here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Listen, as long as you are reading while you are watching TV, watching Fox news, then we don't care. Mollie, your resolution?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Yes. My resolution is to read scripture every day. I started reading more in 2023. I loved it. I got a lot out of it. And so now I'm going to make sure I start every day with a healthy dose of scripture, and everyone is welcome to join me.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Good for you. Mollie, Daniel, Juan, happy New Year to all of you. Thank you for coming on. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Happy New Yearbook, Trace.\par \par \uc2 LIPPMAN: You too.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Up next, a very special extended edition of "Notable Quotables.".\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It the is final Friday before the new year. That means it's time for a special edition of "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: What would it take for you to agree to normalize relations with Israel?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMED BIN SALMAN\uc2 , SAUDI CROWN PRINCE: For us, the Palestinian issue is very important. We need to solve that part.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you close?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: To borrow a phrase, I think we are getting closer to peace every day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nowhere is safe in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The mistaken killing last week of the three hostages, soldiers thought the three were \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fighters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How many more innocent civilians and children have to be killed?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Abigail is a miracle.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They have to come home alive, and that means now.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Free, free Palestine!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We embrace a commitment to free expression.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The eyes of the world have moved elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It doesn't help us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Planet earth is big enough for the two countries to succeed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you still refer to President Xi as a dictator?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Look, he is.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: He says, you are not going to be a dictator, are you? I said, no, no, no, other than day one.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Picture in your mind, it's Election Day. He will be convicted of felonies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We weren't allowed to ask questions about that. We weren't allowed to ask about the "Big Guy."\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You interacted with so many of your son and brothers foreign business associates?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I did not. And it's just a bunch of lies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Their lies have become the false facts believed by too many people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We had President Biden's promise to bring Evan back. Evan is not here.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I haven't had the occasion to go to East Palestine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He sees this community not as a Biden community. He is not doing anything for it. It is shameful conduct.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We need to just get rid of him and move forward.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This will haunt them in the future.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The voters said, look, the government should not be telling a woman what to do with her body.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Break the law. We give you everything free.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I love you \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is harming our country in ways that are hard to describe.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They are poisoning the blood of our country.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I am willing to make significant compromises on the border.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What we saw was likely the largest natural disaster in Hawaii state history.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The debris is consistent with the catastrophic loss of the pressure chamber.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We tracked, we detected, we took the steps to protect ourselves.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I am here to protect women, girls. Why do I have to apologize for that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They were hardworking women. These are women that should not be forgotten.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There is a common misconception that reads don't get scared.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In a second they can come and destroy your life.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, what a tangled web we weave. Once I told a lie, I had to keep lying.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There were some very influential, smart people in bed with FTX, Sam Bankman-Fried, from day one.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We can be two consenting adults. Do you want to do it now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'd love to do it right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Stand your but up, then.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You stand your butt up.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS, (I-VT): Hold on. Stop it.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK: Go -- yourself.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I never give up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: To the American people on behalf of all of us here, we hear you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think it's time for guys like me to get out of the way.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN, (D-WV): I want to mobilize what we'll call the radical, moderate, centrist middle.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Were they, I guess, human or nonhuman biologics.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nonhuman.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He's just a one of a kind player. This is never before seen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I do not play golf for the money.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Wages are now moving up more than inflation. That might help improve the mood of people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This union has found something worth fighting for.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: The mighty, mighty union.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our union partners like to mention how much money we make, as if it's a bad thing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The first sitting president to walk a picket line.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's exciting to be the first to do something, but I did not want to be the last.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Vision, courage, and some faith.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Goodbye, darling, until tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Crisis narrowly averted on the ellipse.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was toppled by a big gust of wind.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In the holy city of Bethlehem, Christmas this year was effectively canceled.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our hearts are in Bethlehem, where the prince of peace is once more rejected by the futile logic of war.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Do you want a leader from a different generation, or do you want \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  in three-inch heels?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Honestly, every time I hear you I feel a little bit dumber.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's the real world.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: To Barbies world, over half-a-billion dollars at the global box office.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Taylor Swift is the apex predator in the entertainment ecosystem.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have only one enemy, this is Putin. And that's it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This country is a powder keg. And this court is just throwing matches at it.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She served for 22 years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alice Johnson?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Alice.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But she would be killed under your plan.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, no, no, no. Under my -- oh, under that?\par \par \uc2 On Inauguration Day 2025, the Biden dictatorship will be over.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Trump just talks the talk. We walk the walk.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: You have the freedom to defecate in public in California.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Folks say they are just cleaning up this place because all those fancy leaders are coming into town. That's true.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am suspending my campaign.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is not my time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The race is over.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No! Yes! Boy, I almost fell off.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Think about 2023. Coke in the White House, now sex in the Senate. Can't wait for 2024.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And that is it for SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Trace Gallagher in Los Angeles. Do not forget that on Tuesday night, FOX NEWS AT NIGHT returns to its normal timeslot, 8:00 in the west, 11:00 p.m. in the east. And for all of us here at SPECIAL REPORT, FOX NEWS AT NIGHT, and the FOX News family, we would like to wish you and your family a very happy and healthy New Year. I'm Trace Gallagher in Los Angeles. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020231230ejct00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Many States Boosting Minimum Wage; New Year's Eve Preparations; Maine Blocks Trump From Ballot; Migrant Surge; Interview With Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  (R-FL); Stock Market Outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 Kelly Saberi, Alexis McAdams, William La Jeunesse, Madison  Alworth, Edward Lawrence \par \uc2 8,364 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 December 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Wall Street finishing out 2020 three with a bang, as we prepare to ring in the new year in Times Square.\par \par \uc2 The markets posting big gains for 2023, the Dow up nearly 14 percent, the Nasdaq surging more than 40 percent, and the S&P 500 up more than 24 percent. But will it continue in 2024? Our market experts will weigh in.\par \par \uc2 And with stock soaring, despite high interest rates to bring down inflation, what impact will that have on the 2024 presidential race? We will ask Republican presidential candidate and Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . He's here.\par \par \uc2 A busy "Your World" starts now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Edward Lawrence, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 First to FOX Business' Madison Alworth with a banner year for stocks -- Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Edward.\par \par \uc2 The last trading day of 2023 has come to a close. And while today's numbers weren't as strong, the end of the year saw a strong rally and major indices were up big across the board for all of 2023, take a look, ending the year with double-digit returns, the S&P finishing the year up 24 percent, Dow Jones industrial average up 13 percent, and the Nasdaq also seeing those big gains, up over 43 percent, a huge success there.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq soaring thanks to artificial intelligence. That boom really had an impact on that sector, big-tech stocks really driving those big jumps.\par \par \uc2 So, looking at the specific stocks, A.I. chip leader \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  ended the year up by more than 240 percent; 2023 was really a breakout year for artificial intelligence. Any company that was creating products in that space saw some benefit from it.\par \par \uc2 Another tech winner, \uc2 Meta\uc2 , AKA \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . That was up over 194 percent this year. But it's not just tech that won out. We saw another big winner in the retail space. Now, this was a brand that was cool when I was in middle school, and it has made quite the comeback, \uc2 Abercrombie & Fitch\uc2  up nearly 300 percent this year. It was the brand's best annual performance since going public in 1996.\par \par \uc2 Something that is down slightly to end the year is the 30-year mortgage rate. We're closing out the year with a rate of 6.61 percent. That is after the 30-year mortgage rate hit 7.79 percent in October. Another rate we saw come down, the rate of inflation. The peak for 2023 in year-over-year inflation was 6.4 percent. Right now, we are at 3.1 percent inflation.\par \par \uc2 That's a lot better, but still well above the Fed's goal of 2 percent inflation. Even still, the Fed has now signaled that they will start to cut rates in 2024, making Wall Street very happy and helping to drive this end- of-year rally.\par \par \uc2 Will it continue into 2024? That is the question, Edward, and I guess we will have to come back to work on Tuesday to find out.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, we will find out right here from the market guests. We ask the big panel here to see if this market wave will continue. We have got the market pros right.\par \par \uc2 There, you see it, new York Stock Exchange trader Peter Tuchman and market watcher \uc2 Kenny Polcari\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kenny, let's start with you. But, wow, look at those glasses.\par \par \uc2 Always the optimist here. So is there one thing that led the market or was it a confluence of things, a storm of events that led the markets?\par \par \uc2 KENNY POLCARI\uc2 , FOX NATION HOST: I think, at this last six weeks or so, it was a confluence of events that led to the market.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, it's everyone feeling better, the soft landing, all that stuff. But then it was how the narrative changed from higher for longer and we're going to keep rates there to suddenly we're talking about a couple of rate cuts to suddenly it's going to be six or seven rate cuts, which makes no sense to me. It's completely illogical.\par \par \uc2 And investors need to be careful what they wish for. If the Fed starts cutting rates by six or seven times next year, that means we're going off the edge. It's not because it's all coming up roses. So you got to be careful what you wish for. And that's my concern.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: You do have to be careful, yes.\par \par \uc2 So, Peter, I want to go to you now. So, you know the ins and outs of trading. So let's say I had $100 right now. What sector or company would be flashing in your mind?\par \par \uc2 PETER TUCHMAN, NYSE TRADER: You know what? To be perfectly honest, I like -- I love the -- I like the overall exposure of the S&P 500.\par \par \uc2 I think at this point, look, part of -- the bigger part of this rally this year was obviously the fabulous seven. And in the last six weeks, we basically went -- in the Russell 2000, we went from a 52-week low to a 52- week high literally in 46 days.\par \par \uc2 That meant that the breadth of the rally started to pick up. And that's significant in a lot of different ways. I think, at this point, it's hard to pick a sector.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: There's so many moving parts.\par \par \uc2 Let's think about, 2023, we had two wars. We had 18 consecutive rate hikes. We had, every possible challenge that a market could have, we had.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: And yet we're going to close out this year less than a percent away from all record time highs.\par \par \uc2 So I put 100 bucks into the S&P 500.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: Disclaimer: I'm not an adviser.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, right, exactly. Not an adviser, but, man, you look good still.\par \par \uc2 So, Kenny, we have seen in the past what's good for Wall Street doesn't always mean it's good for Main Street. Their expectations, the consumer will pull back this next year for 2024. Are we headed for that disconnect again?\par \par \uc2 POLCARI: So I think we are potentially headed for disconnect, because my fear is that the economy is slowing down faster, which is why we're hearing that narrative.\par \par \uc2 And that's going to affect the consumer. That's going to hit you and me right in the pocketbook, right? And so I think we're going to see -- that's what we're going to see in the first half at least of 2024 until we get through first-quarter earnings and we start to really see what the Fed is thinking.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Peter, we got 30 seconds left. I want to ask you about A.I.\par \par \uc2 A.I. was the buzzword in 2023. If a CEO mentioned it, the stock went up. What do you think's going to happen with artificial intelligence moving forward in 2024 from a financial perspective?\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: I think we know -- we don't know as much as we think we know about this story. We're going to have to watch it unfold.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, it's what took \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  from $108 last February to $400, $500. That's significant. It was the biggest contributing factor to the fabulous seven. I think there are a lot of moving parts in that whole sector, in tech, per se. And I think we need to let the market -- there's so much chatter about A.I.\par \par \uc2 And I think the important thing is, think about how quickly that it went to Congress and people started to evaluate it and the potential dangers of it. I think we don't know a lot about the story. That's -- we will watch that unfold.\par \par \uc2 But, look, the market seems really strong right now. I don't see -- look, the economy and the markets don't always trade in concert with each other, right? So, yes, there must be people struggling. Yes, there are things that are a lot more expensive than they were a year ago. But, net-net, at the end of the day, the market's strong.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Kenny and Peter, I appreciate your insight on all of this. Those glasses win the day. OK, happy new year to both of you guys.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: I miss you, Kenny. We miss you on the floor, Kenny.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So...\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 POLCARI: I miss you. Peter, I miss you too.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, with stocks up for the year and inflation coming down, it might affect the 2020 politics.\par \par \uc2 Now, with me now is Republican presidential candidate and Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who just wrapped up a town hall in Edgewood, Iowa, moments ago.\par \par \uc2 Governor, thank you for joining me.\par \par \uc2 Let's start with something positive here.\par \par \uc2 First, happy new year.\par \par \uc2 But your stand against \uc2 Disney\uc2  for wokism sort of started the beginning of the end for a lot of companies focusing on imposing certain values on customers. We saw the culmination of that with Bud Light. Were you concerned that that stand could have backfired?\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Ultimately, it was about standing up for kids.\par \par \uc2 It's just inappropriate. And I'm a dad of young kids, first grader, kindergartner and pre-K 3. To be injecting things like sexuality and transgenderism into elementary school curriculum, it's just unacceptable. And so we took a very clear stand on that. Parents supported us.\par \par \uc2 And it was interesting, because my election, the \uc2 Disney\uc2  employees, by and large, backed us in Central Florida. I won those areas. I did much better than most Republicans. And so I think people are sick of having agenda shoved down their throat. I think there's also the business issue of, you have a fiduciary obligation to your shareholders.\par \par \uc2 Is trying to get involved in these issues, is that honoring that fiduciary obligation or is it violating? And so I think this whole thing of things like ESG and all the things that have happened in the corporate world over the last few years ultimately is a dead end.\par \par \uc2 We took action in Florida against ESG. We banned the use of it in our pension funds.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We're solely focused on return on investment.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: And I think what we were able to do across the board has been the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 We want American businesses just focused on doing what they do best...\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: ... and not trying to change policy in this country.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, Governor, I want to get into Iowa now.\par \par \uc2 You have a large door-to-door network. What's the message you're trying to pull off to upset former President Trump?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, I'm the guy that can get this done, just like we did in Florida, winning a big victory, but, even more importantly, delivering on 100 percent of my promises.\par \par \uc2 We need somebody, a president, that's going to fight. And a lot of Republicans haven't even been willing to do that. But we need a president that's going to win these big fights. And I have beat the left on issue after issue, and we need somebody that's going to be able to lead, lead this country into a different direction, make sure that compass is set to true north...\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But -- yes.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: ... and get the job done.\par \par \uc2 And I have shown -- I have been more effective down here in Florida than just about anybody in the country in modern memory. And you're talking about the economy. In Florida, I have paid down 25 percent of our debt since I have been governor.\par \par \uc2 We have number one for new business formation and our economy is ranked number one of all 50 states by CNBC. So we're showing how it's done on the economic and fiscal front. And if Washington did even half as good as Florida has done, we'd be in a much better spot nationally.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, the latest FOX Business poll, though, shows, among Iowa caucus-goers, you still are at 18 -- 18 percent. That's two points ahead of Nikki Haley, but 34 points behind former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Can you really make that up?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, I think, if you look historically, the polling has not accurately predicted a caucus result, because it's just a different beast. It's not a typical election. You're going there for three hours.\par \par \uc2 People give speeches. It's a process. So what we have done is, we have worked really hard to sign up tens of thousands of Iowans. And we add more every day. And we're accelerating the number of people who are committed to turning out for us and bringing a friend, bringing a neighbor. That's how you do well here.\par \par \uc2 And so we have done it the right way, and I think you're going to see that on caucus night.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: So, we're excited about what -- the direction we're going.\par \par \uc2 And we're going to be working hard all the way through caucus night.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: I want to get your reaction to the Maine secretary of state pulling former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  off the ballot there.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, obviously, to have one executive branch official unilaterally striking someone off the ballot is not an appropriate interpretation of the 14th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 We could have -- in Florida, what, are we going to have Biden struck off the ballot because he's allowed an invasion of eight million people, including enemy nations have sent people in? This could just be -- end up being a tit for tat. So I think it'll get reversed by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . I think it's more of a stunt that you have a very liberal person in that position who's just trying to play for cheap clicks.\par \par \uc2 But, ultimately, I don't think it's grounded in a proper interpretation of the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Nikki Haley in recent days clarified what some call a gaffe, omitting slavery when answering what caused the Civil War.\par \par \uc2 Do you think -- what do you think about her clarification that?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: It was an incomprehensible word salad, and then she continues to really not make a lot of sense in what she's saying.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's just an indication she's been very much fluffed by the media for the last six to eight weeks. Let's see how she does when she actually faces scrutiny. And I think, when she gets off her talking points, I think she has a much more difficult task at that point.\par \par \uc2 And part of it is, when you get into these races and you're an elected official, what is your core foundation? And, with me, my core foundation are the enduring principles that have made this country unique, our constitutional system. And that's just in my DNA as a candidate, as a governor, and will be as a president.\par \par \uc2 I think, for her, I don't think she has those core convictions. I think she's trying to tell people what she thinks they may want to hear in the moment, rather than just say what the truth is. And you even hear this in some of her explanations now, where she's saying that she's talking about her truth.\par \par \uc2 No, no, no, no, it's not your truth or my truth.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: It's the truth.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Governor...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: And we need to be grounded in the truth.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We got to throw this postmodernism out the window.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, Governor, you're still two points ahead of her. Her message resonates with some folks.\par \par \uc2 If you don't finish second in Iowa, there's going to be pressure for you to bow out of the race. Would you do that?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Oh, we're going to do -- we're going to -- we're going to take Iowa. I'm not worried about that. We're in this for the long haul.\par \par \uc2 You're talking about you need 1,300, 1,400 delegates. There's a lot of real estate here, and we're built for the long haul, and that's what we're going to do.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, should Governor Christie, then, or \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  drop out now, do you believe?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: That's up to them. I mean, they can do what they want.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you I'm running to win. That's the only reason why I'm running. I know Nikki Haley will not rule out being vice president, because she spends a lot of money attacking me. So, is she trying to defeat President - - former President Trump, or is she trying to be his vice president?\par \par \uc2 She will not rule that out. But she's gotten a lot of support from these never-Trumpers. They're viewing her as like the darling of never-Trump. Yet she's positioning herself to potentially be in the running for that.\par \par \uc2 I have categorically ruled out being V.P. I think she should do that, so her supporters know where she stands.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Governor DeSantis, I know you have something to go to. I appreciate your time on this. Thank you for talking with us.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, border crossings shattering another record, and now the Biden administration is issuing a warning. But it's not for the migrants coming. We will explain.\par \par \uc2 And then, looking at -- live at Times Square, where security preps are under way, and more protests could be on the way also. Hear what officials just said that they are looking for.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: A new record at the border but not one to celebrate, as migrants' surge is pushing border officials and the major U.S. cities to the brink.\par \par \uc2 To FOX's William La Jeunesse in Los Angeles with the latest -- William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Ed, 276,000 encounters in a single month, that's a new record, not counting the got-aways, and yet that's almost the population of Pittsburgh apprehended at the border in 30 days?\par \par \uc2 As an issue, it's a beast, which is why I typically break it down into four problems, not one. Why do people leave? That's the root cause. What are the choke points in transit? Say Mexico. What about our policies at the border, asylum, parole protocols? And after that, you have apprehension. What do we do? Detain, deport, or release.\par \par \uc2 Now, each one offers an opportunity to reduce illegal immigration, if there's a will, the money and political consensus to do it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW RUDMAN, WILSON CENTER: Neither country, neither the U.S., nor Mexico, really has the capability to manage that flow of people, particularly when it's 10,000 people perhaps appearing at the border every day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Now, Texas is trying.\par \par \uc2 Yet, yesterday, the Biden administration promised to sue should Texas implement a law allowing police to arrest, jail, prosecute and deport migrants who enter Texas illegally. This week, President Biden did agree to keep Mexican imports flowing north in exchange for troops on their southern border, though, yesterday, you can see they made no attempts to stop this group.\par \par \uc2 Mexico also wants the U.S. to invest more in Central America, but critics say U.S. taxpayers already are getting shortchanged.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAUL ORTIZ, U.S. BORDER PATROL CHIEF: We're writing blank checks to these countries. Our financial aid has to come with some strings attached to it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Now, Texas says Biden is deliberately -- his inaction is leaving it no choice, and they will appeal to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  to stop illegal immigration in their state.\par \par \uc2 Now, in a separate, but related story, the Border Patrol arrested an Iranian national with ties to terrorism sneaking into the U.S. near Niagara Falls two days after being turned away here at the Rainbow Bridge. Now, he is a legal resident of Canada, wanted on assault charges there. ICE held him for two months before a release last week -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Oh, wow. Thanks, William. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 So with the new record crossings, the Biden administration issuing a warning, not for the migrants who keep coming and making their way here, but for the state of Texas and its new law to arrest and remove them from here.\par \par \uc2 Joining us on the phone is Chris Cabrera, the national Border Patrol Council vice president.\par \par \uc2 So, Chris, why does the new Texas law help?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CABRERA, VICE PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: It's unfortunate what the government is trying to do and not let us get the help that we need as far as arresting those that we're by law supposed to arrest, but, instead, we're releasing by policy.\par \par \uc2 It makes no sense. It kind of shows you what the Biden administration really wants to do. And it's to let these folks into the country.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, this new law allows local law enforcement to arrest folks, right, and get them deported back across the country -- I mean, across the border?\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: Yes, exactly. Yes, back across the border.\par \par \uc2 That's the way we're supposed to be doing it. Unfortunately, we're not. And the only folks that are actually trying to help us, they're threatening to sue them for helping us do our job. It actually makes it -- it defies logic.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Do you think -- as the enforcement gets more aggressive in Texas, do you think it's pushing more numbers to Arizona?\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: Yes, I do believe it is.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we still have a lot of issues in some parts of Texas, mainly Eagle Pass, but the Rio Grande Valley has slowed tremendously. And you look at the footprint that the Texas DPS puts out in the Rio Grande Valley. So yes, it is pushing that traffic further west. And, hopefully, these other governors or other states will get on board the way Texas is doing it. And if the federal government won't do it, maybe the states individually can get it done.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, if you add it all up, it's 7.1 million people crossing the border illegally since President Biden has come into office. And that's including the February numbers that the CBP sources are telling FOX News -- I mean, the December numbers.\par \par \uc2 Why doesn't the federal government step in here?\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: I have no idea. I think it just defies logic, like, they're wanting people to come in for some reason.\par \par \uc2 It's -- they're always trying to address the root factors as some type of push factor, but, in actuality, it's a pull factor. If we're letting everybody come in, of course, they're going to try and come in. They know they're going to get released.\par \par \uc2 So...\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: What do you think...\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 No, no, I was asking you -- I just want to get in real quickly too, what do you think about this caravan that's coming in, 8,000-strong now, it looks like? And it's not stopping. Mexico and the U.S. had a meeting yesterday, but it doesn't look like Mexico is making any moves to stop it.\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: Yes, the caravans, they're a dime a dozen. We see those all the time. It's not surprising that I -- quite, frankly, if I didn't work down here, I probably wouldn't believe it. But since I see it so often, it's just kind of an everyday thing for us.\par \par \uc2 And I think other countries around the world are seeing right now that we're not very strong with our enforcement. We're actually very weak, and they're taking advantage of us.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, Chris, I want to ask you. ICE has released their fiscal year 2023 report. It says 142,000 deportations, 142,000 deportations in 2023. What is that telling you?\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: That tells me we're not deporting nearly enough people.\par \par \uc2 I haven't seen too many deportations down here. Usually, we see quite a bit, but, yes, we're way behind the curve right now.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Chris Cabrera, I appreciate it. Overwhelming. And we will -- this conversation will continue as these pictures will also continue.\par \par \uc2 So, I appreciate it. Thank you for your help.\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: All right, thank you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not just Republicans blasting Maine's decision to boot \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from the ballot. What a key Democrat in the state is saying.\par \par \uc2 And, later, how much would you pay to watch the ball drop in Times Square, from a $800 seat at Applebee's to $12,000 grand package at the \uc2 Marriott\uc2 ? Find out how folks are paying up to party up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Getting ready to say goodbye to 2023 and hello to minimum wage hikes.\par \par \uc2 And the speaker of the new -- and speaking of the new year, how much money would you drop to see the ball drop?\par \par \uc2 We're coming back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: It's not just \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Republican rivals attacking the Maine ballot ruling. Even Maine Democrat Jared Golden, who voted to impeach Trump, says it's wrong and he should be allowed on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 As we await possible news from the Supreme Court on this, former federal prosecutor Katie Cherkasky joins me now.\par \par \uc2 So, Katie, from a legal perspective, the decision from the Maine secretary of state came without the former president participating in a hearing of -- any way. What does that say to you?\par \par \uc2 KATIE CHERKASKY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Well, the decision from Maine was even more egregious than the one out of Colorado, where they held some sort of sham trial. In Maine, it was a unilateral decision of an executive.\par \par \uc2 And, obviously, I think the Supreme Court will inevitably get involved with this to clarify the contours and the parameters of Section 3 of the 14th Amendment, which has never been analyzed by a court. So the idea that there's any sort of clear outcome that can be analyzed under that section is really something that's just never been adjudicated.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, let's take a step back. How dangerous do you think that the ruling by the secretary of state in Maine, as well as the Colorado Supreme Court decision, how dangerous is that, do you think, for the state of our democracy?\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: I think it's just a terrible precedent.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's clear, based upon the fact that all of these states are coming out inconsistently and differently on this issue, that this section of the Constitution is not meant to apply to the presidential -- to the presidential race, which is a national race. It simply can't be, because you end up with this preposterous split among the states.\par \par \uc2 And so, certainly, that is why the Supreme Court's involvement is vital, crucial and necessary to stop this from continuing, because it cannot be sustained coming up to an election like this.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden has made the case that voting for him would save the democracy. But now you have government, different government agencies, maybe state or local or federal too, going after the top opponent. That would get condemned, if the U.S. saw it happening in other countries, be condemned by the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Is this saving democracy?\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: Well, I think that the irony of all this cannot be overstated.\par \par \uc2 The idea that this all started because there was some sort of attack on democracy by Trump, which is now being combated by very egregious and overt attacks on democracy quite literally, I think it's quite clear to everyone across the aisle, whether they're a Trump supporter or not, this is simply not the process. And that has nothing to do with even getting into the factual analysis under whether he could even be disqualified.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there are so many constitutional issues, that it is very clear that the court will have to clarify that very, very quickly, I'm sure. But certainly, it's...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. Well, I want to get this in. In the last 30 seconds, I want to get this in.\par \par \uc2 How quickly should the Supreme Court weigh in on this and what message should they send, in the last 15 seconds?\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: They need to send a message that this section of the Constitution is not something that the states can individually decide. And I think that will alleviate most of the concerns here. I don't think they will get into the facts of whether he committed an insurrection.\par \par \uc2 I simply think it does not apply to the office of the presidency and the states cannot individually make those determinations without due process.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Katie, happy new year. I appreciate your insight on all of this. Very interesting debate.\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: Happy new year. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, coming up: Want to party in style in Times Square for New Year's Eve? Prepare to pay up.\par \par \uc2 But first to Alexis McAdams, where the ball will drop. Talking about security, right, Alexis McAdams?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Edward.\par \par \uc2 Yes, you can either pay hundreds of dollars to sit at the Applebee's in Times Square, or you can be out here with thousands of other people in the middle of it all to see the ball drop. Right now, they say there are no credible threats to the New Year's Eve celebrations or New York City.\par \par \uc2 I will have more coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  announcing increased security measures around Times Square, shown here live via EarthCam.\par \par \uc2 Transportation hubs will also see a boost. The move comes after pro- Palestinian demonstrators disrupted the Thanksgiving Day Parade. They tried to interfere with the Rockefeller Christmas tree lighting earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Alexis McAdams is in Times Square, where preparations are under way -- Alexis.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Hey, Edward.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there's been lots of preparation that's gone into this to get all ready and make sure the security is in place for the Times Square celebrations on New Year's Eve. We can tell you the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  just a short time ago telling us there are no credible threats against New York City or the New Year's Eve celebrations that are planned, but, hey, they do expect pro- Palestinian protesters to possibly show up.\par \par \uc2 Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), MAYOR OF NEW YORK: The \uc2 New York City Police Department\uc2  did an amazing job during the tree lighting with individuals trying to disrupt the tree lighting. They executed a well-thought-out plan and they're going to do so this time as well.\par \par \uc2 People are going to come here, ring in a new year, enjoy themselves in a safe way. And those who want to peacefully protest, they have the right to do so, but you're not going to do that at the expense of disrupting.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Now, there's a live look that we want to show you here at Times Square, as the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  is preparing for what they say is expected to be safe and on track as usual, but there is going to be an extended security perimeter here in the area.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of thousands of people are expected here on New Year's Eve, and the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  has all hands on deck. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY MADDREY, NEW YORK CITY POLICE CHIEF: We have to be prepared for everything.\par \par \uc2 So, with lone wolf attacks, it's really about our training, how we address our officers to make sure that they're alert and prepared. No backpacks, no duffel bags, large packages. No alcohol. These are all prohibited items. But our officers will be there, and they will be there to protect you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: So, last New Year's Eve, we can tell you a man was arrested, Edward, after police say he tried to kill a group of \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers.\par \par \uc2 I was out here when it happened. They said he came in from Maine and was armed with a machete-style knife and hit at least two or three officers on the head. He's now been federally charged. He will be in court here in the new year.\par \par \uc2 Now, one of the other things \uc2 NYPD\uc2  is telling everybody is, again, there's no credible threats, but they want anyone to report suspicious activity, whether they see it on the \uc2 MTA\uc2  or on any of the city subway systems, to authorities immediately -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, for me, the best way to just watch this is at home.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, thank you, Alexis McAdams. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 One million people expected to cram in to Times Square to watch the ball drop on New Year's Eve. How do you keep everyone safe?\par \par \uc2 Well, former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  inspector Paul Mauro joins me now.\par \par \uc2 So, Paul, we have seen pictures of the past couple of days of those protesters blocking access to airport in New York also in Los Angeles. They likely will try something New Year's Eve. How do you be everywhere and watch everything at once?\par \par \uc2 PAUL MAURO, FORMER \uc2 NYPD\uc2  INSPECTOR: Well, obviously, that's a very tough task.\par \par \uc2 But, fortunately, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  has a tremendous amount of experience with this. The P.D. here is really probably the most experienced department in the world regarding handling large-scale events. We actually had a couple of years not in the too distant past where there was literally no arrests for the entire New Year's Eve event.\par \par \uc2 Mind-boggling, when you consider we had over a million visitors. But this year is a very different year. We have seen these protests proliferate. There's been no real enforcement against them, thousands of people blocking traffic, blocking transportation hubs, and yet, what, five or six people arrested, immediately cut loose.\par \par \uc2 There hasn't been much of a disincentive. And, furthermore, there is a new law or agreement, I should say, relative to a civil suit that the New York Civil Liberties brought against New York City that really proscribes the way the police department can engage with protesters.\par \par \uc2 And as bad luck would have it, that dovetails with the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  incursion into Israel, which caused all this recent unrest.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: So, you have a lot of crosscurrents coming together this year for New Year's Eve.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Now, Paul, I want to remind folks that there is no specific threat, though, against the New Year's Eve celebration in New York.\par \par \uc2 But, because of those protests, how does the security compare to last year? You said, some years, there were no arrests, but how does the security compare?\par \par \uc2 MAURO: So, this year, I think is going to be an even more all-hands-on- deck situation, which is generally is. But now you see Governor Hochul weighing in because they need the bodies.\par \par \uc2 I think it's going to be more diffuse. I mean, I think, this year, you have to start to look at things like the airports, obviously, the Oculus downtown, Penn Station, Grand Central Station, transportation hubs that these protesters have been targeting in the past.\par \par \uc2 There is a lot more chatter than maybe you have heard in the past regarding an event, but this chatter, it's low-level. This is the stuff that happens every New Year's Eve, when a lot of people are together.\par \par \uc2 But when I want to ask, to just enlarge the canvas here a little bit, is, where is the federal government? They're doing a lot of stuff prophylactically for New Year's Eve, as they always do, sure. There's going to be a federal government engagement.\par \par \uc2 But where is the federal government engagement regarding these protesters? They're shutting down interstate transportation hubs. Where are the sort of cases, conspiracy cases, RICO, the stuff that they love to bring against Catholics and against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Where is that kind of stuff against Antifa and other groups that have made common cause with these protesters?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: That's interesting, yes. And that's something we will be looking for in 2024.\par \par \uc2 So, Paul Mauro, I appreciate your time on this. Thank you very much. Hoping you have a safe New Year's Eve. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: And to you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, looking live at Times Square, courtesy of EarthCams coming right now. There it is. You want dinner drinks and a bird's-eye view of the ball dropping, it's going to cost you.\par \par \uc2 And once that ball comes down, minimum wage in 22 states goes up. Not everyone's celebrating.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Ready, set, hike, 25 states boosting their minimum wages in the new year, with most of the increases kicking in on New Year's Day.\par \par \uc2 To Kelly Saberi in Chicago digging deeper into the numbers there, -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY SABERI, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Edward, Illinois is one of 22 states that will increase the minimum wage in the new year.\par \par \uc2 On January 1, all workers in the land of Lincoln will see a $1 increase to the minimum wage, and it will become $14 an hour, but that's not even among the highest wages around the country. As of next week, Washington and California will be in the $16-an-hour range, while Connecticut, New Jersey and New York all have increases to $15 an hour or more.\par \par \uc2 The federal minimum wage has not changed since 2009. It remains at $7.25. For people like Russ Nygaard, a minority co-owner of a bubble tea shop about two hours outside of Chicago, the wage increase could affect their ability to grow their new store.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUSS NYGAARD, OUR JOURNEYS CAFE AND BOBA SHOP: We understand the need for a good wage for people. I don't think anybody in business that is hiring is looking to not pay somebody a quality wage.\par \par \uc2 It is a detriment when the increases continue to come and you're trying to absorb at the same time you're trying to establish and grow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SABERI: On the other hand, supporters of the hikes say this will help businesses hire and retain employees, saying -- quote -- "They help workers put food on the table and keep a roof overhead and they boost the consumer spending that businesses depend on. Fair wages help businesses hire and retain employees and deliver the reliable customer service that leads to repeat customers, instead of lost customers."\par \par \uc2 Over in California, two Pizza Hut franchises will lay off drivers as a result of the state increasing fast-food wages to $20 in New York City. \uc2 Uber\uc2 , \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  and \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  drivers will be paid $18 an hour. So while many workers will see a raise in pay, businesses could be seeing that hit to their bottom line, Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Kelly, so, as we see the pace of inflation cooling now, could these minimum wage hikes start sending prices higher as business compensates for higher costs?\par \par \uc2 Now, let's ask economist and former director of the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  Doug Holtz-Eakin.\par \par \uc2 So, you heard Kelly say that, in California, Pizza Hut says that they're going to lay off more than 1,200 delivery drivers ahead of the minimum wage hike. Why? And could this be bad? Or could this be the start of something, more layoffs for other companies?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, FORMER CONGRESSIONAL BUDGET OFFICE DIRECTOR: Well, you can change the law and say you have to pay people more, $16, $20 an hour. That doesn't mean there's more money.\par \par \uc2 So businesses have to adjust. They're going to adjust in two primary ways. Number one, they will hire fewer people. In extreme cases, they will lay them off. That's what you hear about with Pizza Hut. But they will hire fewer. Hurts growth of those businesses.\par \par \uc2 And they will pass the cost along to their customers. That's a one-time hit on the inflation front. So, it's really an enduring myth....\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But why can't you just -- but...\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But here's what I want to know. Then why can't you just mandate higher wages?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Well, businesses don't have the money, right? You just say, pay them more. With what? They didn't hand out the money to pay them more, so they're going to have to go find it, find it with higher prices, find it by cutting other workers, giving other people fewer raises.\par \par \uc2 This is why it's an enduring myth that raising the minimum wage is an act of economic justice. When the CBO took a look at a proposal by Senator Sanders to raise the federal minimum wage to $17 an hour, they found out that 700,000 people did not get jobs they otherwise would have gotten.\par \par \uc2 So, essentially, those people gave up their incomes so that those who stayed employed could get a raise. Of those who stayed employed, only 400,000 got pulled out of poverty. So for every person that pulls out of poverty, two get consigned to zero income. That's not fair.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Doug, we only have about 30 seconds left.\par \par \uc2 I wanted to get your -- what do you think the effects of this move will be at a time when unemployment rate, which is low now, but it's rising, and consumers might be pulling back next year?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Well, I certainly think that the great progress that's made on the inflation fund has been done by cooling the labor market and taking off the table some really sharp wage increases.\par \par \uc2 This puts them back on the table, at least in January. And so it's not good news from the inflation-fighting front. And if you're a consumer trying to adjust to a fixed paycheck and higher prices, the problem's obvious.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, Doug Holtz-Eakin, I appreciate your help and insight on this, very smart economist, smarter than me.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: I appreciate it. Thank you for your help. Happy new year.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Thank you. Happy new year.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, looking live at Times Square, it's coming soon. Yes, there it is. How much are you willing to pony up to whoop it up? Why $800 a person is looking like a bargain now.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: How much would you drop on New Year's Eve to see the ball drop? Some people shelling out $800 for a seat at a Times Square Applebee's, just one of the many pricey packages available.\par \par \uc2 Would my next guests buy in? Let's find out.\par \par \uc2 The Daily Mail U.S. associate editor Charlie Lankston and travel expert Lee Abbamonte.\par \par \uc2 So, Charlie, first to you. What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE LANKSTON, U.S. ASSOCIATE EDITOR, DAILY MAIL: I mean, honestly, I think it's absolutely ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 I live in New York and I would pay that much to avoid Times Square at all costs, especially on New Year's Eve.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: It is a nightmare zone to be around. And the idea that anyone would pay thousands of dollars in order to endure the crowds, the insane policing that goes on, I just think it's absolutely bonkers.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Lee, it does sound like -- it doesn't sound like a great travel deal. I mean, I think you're getting, what, drinks, dinner, a view, probably paying for a bathroom if you're getting that Applebee's deal. Is it worth it?\par \par \uc2 LEE ABBAMONTE, TRAVEL EXPERT: Listen, I'm a New Yorker, and, no, it's absolutely not worth it.\par \par \uc2 She kind of stole my thunder there. You couldn't pay me $799 to sit in the Applebee's in Times Square to watch the ball drop. And I have actually stood outside in Times Square before to watch the ball drop. And it's an absolute nightmare.\par \par \uc2 If you have had a couple beers, let me tell you how awful it is to try to find a place to go to the bathroom, by the way. So, Times Square is just avoided all costs. You will never find New Yorkers there. It's only people from out of town. And I totally understand. They see it on TV every year.\par \par \uc2 But is it worth it? Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And those pens are not very big there. So I guess that's what the people are -- they think, oh, well, spend $12,000 for a package at \uc2 Marriott\uc2 , and you get inside. You can see the ball drop. You have heat.\par \par \uc2 Lee, I guess, what do you think? I mean...\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: I mean, I have stayed in that Marriott Marquis before. They renovated it. It's OK. But is it worth like $6,000 a person or $12,000 for two people?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: Absolutely not. So, it's -- so it's hot. Big deal. I mean, you still have a weird view. It takes 60 seconds. It goes down and then you're like, that's it? And it's not even that big anyway.\par \par \uc2 I mean, honestly, if you have never seen it before, it's very underwhelming. So, I mean, I don't mean to sound negative for people who are traveling all across the country, but it's not that great.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: In defense of -- in defense of \uc2 Marriott\uc2 , Charlie, it's still a full package. Your $12,000, it's not just looking at the ball. You get food. You get drinks. I mean...\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: Yes, but there are so many other places in New York City that offer better food, better drinks, no crowds.\par \par \uc2 And, to be quite frank, if you are going to get the true Times Square ball drop experience, then you should experience it properly. Get in there with the crowds, get smushed in the pen, and do it like a real tourist.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: Paying $12,500 in order to experience it from the comfort of a \uc2 Marriott\uc2  just seems like a waste of money, no matter how you look at it.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, I want to get this in. As we get ready to say goodbye to 2023, most Americans are saying goodbye to those New Year's resolutions. A new survey finds half of Americans think it's time to drop the tradition.\par \par \uc2 Lee, you think it's time to drop it?\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: Well, listen, I was actually in the gym this morning. I have never seen it as crowded, right, people getting an early start on their New Year's resolutions.\par \par \uc2 Come Super Bowl Sunday, or I should say Super Bowl Monday, 90 percent of them won't be there anymore. So, yes, I think it's time to drop. I think it's a stupid tradition. And nobody ever adheres to them anyway. If you need to make a change in June, make it in June. Don't wait until New Year's.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Charlie, we have 30 seconds left.\par \par \uc2 What would you put in place, then, of the resolutions? What should we add as a tradition?\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: I think people are actually focusing more on goals these days, rather than resolutions.\par \par \uc2 They're trying to set new lifestyle changes for themselves without that restriction and that pressure of having to adhere to a certain thing, making more time for exercise, drinking more water, rather than trying to overhaul your life, as, you know, was just pointed out, because it's January.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't make any sense. So, live for yourself, have fun, do good things.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And before we move on, I just can't get over this.\par \par \uc2 The Marriott Marquis, it does offer a VIP party on the eighth floor, a live deejay, five-hour premium open bar, buffet, available options to watch the ball. And then it's formal attire though, black and white. So you get to dress up.\par \par \uc2 Well, I appreciate, you guys. Thank you so much for this debate. It's been very fun. Happy new year to both of you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, if you're making New Year's resolution for yourself, that's no fun.\par \par \uc2 How about making one for Neil? Let's look forward to reading your resolutions. He's going to read them on Tuesday, when he gets back. So, e- mail us. Hit us up for a resolution for him.\par \par \uc2 Don't forget to watch "Cavuto Live" tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 "The Five" is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020231229ejct0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Liberal Mayors Whine About Migrants They Once Welcomed; Pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  Lunatics Are Ready To Ruin New Year's Eve; Tech Giant Slash Woke Diversity Programs; Woke Backlash: Major Companies Slashing DEI Programs And Budgets; Pizza Hut Lays Off Workers Before CA $20 Min Wage; Reacher Star: School Bully Asked Me For Selfie; God Of Chaos: \uc2 NASA\uc2  Launches Mission To Intercept Asteroid\b0\par\par\uc2 Kennedy Montgomery, Jessica Tarlov, Charlie Hurt, Katie Pavlich,  Jimmy Failla \par \uc2 8,288 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 December 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: So, it seems like -- it's just it seems like it's almost done. We'll see what happens in Iowa and New Hampshire. So, I appreciate it, thank you for your help, Tiana.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, "YOUR WORLD" will have a lot more, the 2024 Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  will be here. Keep it here for THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, hello, everyone, I'm Kennedy along with Jessica Tarlov, Charlie Hurt, Katie Pavlich and big Jimmy Failla. It is 5:00 here in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Give us your poor, your tired and your federal money. The leaders of three sanctuary cities are begging President Biden to shower them with some cold hard taxpayer-funded cash as they whine their hearts out over the big bad Texas governor busing illegals to their doorsteps.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS, MAYOR OF NEW YORK CITY: You cannot continue to do the federal government's job, the federal government must take responsibility and lead on this humanitarian crisis instead of leaving it for cities and localities to handle.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JOHNSON, MAYOR OF CHICAGO: We need the federal government to lean in and provide more financial assistance. All of our cities have reached a point where we are either close to capacity, or nearly out of room.\par \par \uc2 MIKE JOHNSTON, MAYOR OF DENVER: We need more federal support to be able to manage this amount of inflow. It will crush city budgets around the country as we know. We're looking at about $160 million of potential costs going next year's budget. That's almost 10 percent of our entire city budget.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: Oh, you do know the definition of sanctuary, don't you? But maybe they are better off not seeking help from Team Biden. That's because this White House is acting like a bunch of nincompoops on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Secretaries Blinken and Mayorkas coming back from a meeting with Mexico's president empty handed with no big border deal to announce.\par \par \uc2 Blinken did come up with a strange new term for the crisis, irregular migration. And Mayorkas thought the meeting was productive. That's a fun word, doesn't apply here.\par \par \uc2 But border state Democrats doesn't think productive is cutting it, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): The border has been ignored. They're listening too much to the immigration activists. The White House and a lot of members of Congress in the Senate, listen to the immigration advocates. But who's listening to the border communities?\par \par \uc2 We're losing Democrats. They feel that the Democratic Party that the president is not doing enough.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: Well, clearly that message is not breaking through to Democrats. So, the party is just praying that voters live under a rock.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Take a look at the immigration slash border security. It was just 11 percent in August, look where it's hopped to now, 19 percent. So, it's closing in on the economy.\par \par \uc2 The most \uc2 Google\uc2  searches for migrants by state, look at all these states, Phil (PH). They're all blue states. Illinois is one, New York is two, Massachusetts is three, New Jersey is four, Colorado is five.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: What? You mean people actually care about immigration, Jimmy?\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FAILLA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Imagine. First of all, good to see everybody and bear with me. You are the first sober people I've spoken to. This is going to be doozy. Yes, your uncle Jimmy. Wish him luck.\par \par \uc2 Well, this is essentially what happened. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took the border and turned it into the college bar that doesn't check I.D. Do you remember that bar in college where once the word got out, you were going to get in no matter what? OK, they incentivized this.\par \par \uc2 But the idea that they went down there with no deal and had the I guess hutzpah? Is that a fair word?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Hutzpah.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: I'm trying not to say balls on T.V. Bear with me. I apologize.\par \par \uc2 But the idea that they came back and said that, OK, it was productive. OK, this is a now problem. We just broke a record for fentanyl poisoning deaths, people are dying. Infrastructure is overwhelmed in inner cities, the idea that you call it productive, OK. It's such an insult and an indifference to the people being harmed by this.\par \par \uc2 And this is the only point I want to make up, OK, bring up, is that the Democrats and everyone should remember this, when the election rolls around, and they start telling you no, no, we're the party of struggling minorities in inner cities. OK. They are crushing struggling minorities in their cities right now by overwhelming schools, obviously, and infrastructure and facilities and it is the biggest screw you to the people they purport to care about the most. So, if I am a little hopped up, it's not just the tequila sodas.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: All right, but we'll be serving plenty of those in your white coffee mug throughout the show.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: I was promised.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: Mark my word. But Jimmy brings up a point that you sort of discussed yesterday and that is, you know, these mayors are saying we only have so many resources. So, what are they supposed to do if they have declared themselves sanctuary cities?\par \par \uc2 So, they put the bright neon open sign at the -- at the front door and now people are going in. How do you solve this?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, I think that you have to let city council meetings like the one that was shut down in Chicago where the people wanted to talk about voting to make Chicago not a sanctuary city anymore, you have to let those go through. And you have to hear what the people say, it doesn't mean that the policy is automatically going to be overturned. But you can take that information, and you can go to the federal government, or you can go to your governors and say, the people do not want this anymore.\par \par \uc2 The people got this in the first place, because they did, it is working differently than it used to. You just look at the people who were advocates for sanctuary city policies. chief amongst them, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , right, who, obviously in a very different state of mind and position in his life, was talking about how important it is that people could come out of the shadows and work with law enforcement, to talk about what's going on in their communities to make us safer, and understood that the people who are coming here were here for a better life to work hard, we're paying taxes into accounts, just in case the government checked on them, want their kids to go to school, and to pay it forward as it were.\par \par \uc2 That is very different to what's going on right now. And it is becoming a quality-of-life issue. And that's the same thing as the economy. And that's what people vote on.\par \par \uc2 And I always say this, this does not mean that a bunch of Democrats are going to become a bunch of Republicans. That's not this, but it doesn't mean that you have --\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: But it does mean a bunch of New Yorkers are going to become a bunch of Floridians.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: Yes, they are going to leave and go places where maybe they don't advertise themselves as sanctuary.\par \par \uc2 But Charlie, are they just trying to get money from the federal government?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE HURT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: Is that this play? Or are they really trying to shift policies in a more rational direction?\par \par \uc2 HURT: Well, I think primarily, they're just trying to get money, because that's what they do. But the larger argument is the -- you know, what happens when ideology meets reality. They sit around, and they talk about this stuff in ideological terms and it all sounds nice and rosy.\par \par \uc2 And they also go even further and tell other people what it is that, you know, their policy solutions for problems that they're not personally facing.\par \par \uc2 And of course, you know, the border state governors, border states have been dealing with this for 20 years, and have been screaming about it saying, we need to do something about it. And all of these sanctuary city people sitting around and say, oh, I've got all the answers. This is what you do, you need to make it.\par \par \uc2 And then of course, when that happens, all hell breaks loose, and it's a catastrophe. And now we're going to have to dig them out of the ideological hole that they've gotten themselves into.\par \par \uc2 Luckily, I think that -- you know, I do think that maybe they don't become permanent Republicans, but they will vote, this is a very powerful issue that people do vote on.\par \par \uc2 And when they look around and they see things today, as you point out, that don't work, whether it's the economy, or you know, getting on an airplane and seeing, you know, your tax dollars paying for illegals to fly around the country, it makes people mad.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Because this issue doesn't -- it's not necessarily just an illegal immigration issue, it becomes an education issue, it becomes a sanitation issue, it becomes a public safety issue.\par \par \uc2 When you're cutting budgets for education, for example, it becomes a health care issue when emergency rooms are completely overrun with people who are not paying to the system who are using the system.\par \par \uc2 You know, the mayor of Chicago and the mayor of New York City continue to ask \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to do something about this. They're not doing anything.\par \par \uc2 If you read the readout between the State Department and the president of Mexico that was issued today, it's paragraphs and paragraphs of gobbly gook diplomatic nothingness.\par \par \uc2 But the good news is, is they're going to meet again in January, a couple of weeks from now to go over how this issue can be solved. We know how it solved, there's nothing in that meeting, at least publicly they put out about any kind of asylum reform, any kind of Remain in Mexico policy that can be put into place. That's what's needed here.\par \par \uc2 This actually is not complicated. And these mayors, there are things they can do, they can say, we will not shelter you. We will not give you benefits, we will not