 women.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Not long ago, many of us marched together for black and brown lives. In the eyes of some, this principle does not extend to the Jewish people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, the Israeli government is now prodding the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  to formally investigate these reports of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  sexual atrocities.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.N.\uc2 , for its part, this week launched a P.R. campaign aimed at eliminating violence against women.\par \par \uc2 Mike?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Gillian Turner, live at the state department. Gillian, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, embattled New York Republican Congressman George Santos says his expulsion from Congress would establish a new precedent that would be the undoing of many others.\par \par \uc2 Both Democrats and Republicans have offered resolutions to remove Santos, a vote is expected tomorrow. Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill tonight. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Mike, good evening. Santos was defiant as lawmakers debated whether to make him only the sixth House member ever expelled.\par \par \uc2 Santos torched the ethics committee, but offered no rebuttal to allegations that he built campaign donors.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. GEORGE SANTOS (R-NY): They decided to stop short of completing the process, going ahead, and putting out a slanderous report.\par \par \uc2 These reports littered, littered in hyperbole.\par \par \uc2 All right. Good morning, everybody.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Santos refused again to resign. He said, if he left, "the bullies would win." Expulsion skeptics say, historically, the House has only ousted Confederates or felons.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): Neither apply to George Santos. And so, I rise not to defend George Santos, whoever he is, but to defend the very precedent that my colleagues are willing to shatter.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): House Speaker \uc2 Mike Johnson\uc2  is conflicted.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): I personally have real reservations about doing this. I'm concerned about a precedent that may be set for that.\par \par \uc2 I didn't want the speaker to have to deal with this. He has bigger issues to deal with.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Some Republicans worried that expelling Santos trims the GOP's narrow majority, but other Republicans want Santos out.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARC MOLINARO (R-NY): Dear God, Mr. Speaker, my future former colleague is divorced from reality. He has manufactured his entire life to defraud the voters of his district.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: User or a crook.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Democrats say \uc2 Santos\uc2  reflects what they say is wrong with the GOP.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): George Santos is a malignant distraction. But the problem within the extreme MAGA Republican House majority will still remain. (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Santos claims he is at peace. If he is expelled, it takes two-thirds to expel a member. Mike?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We'll follow it. Chad Pergram, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats are renewing their criticism of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's new code of conduct. \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  Chairman, Dick Durbin says without an enforcement mechanism, the code cannot restore the public's faith in the Court. The committee is considering subpoenas for billionaire Republican donor Harlan Crow, a benefactor of Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , and conservative activist \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2 , who helped former President Trump compile a list of potential Supreme Court nominees.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  performed the Heimlich maneuver on Senator \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2  during a Senate lunch today. Ernst was choking on food.\par \par \uc2 She later posted on X, "Can't help a choke on the world policies Democrats are forcing down our throats."\par \par \uc2 Paul is a graduate of \uc2 Duke University\uc2 's medical school and is a medical doctor.\par \par \uc2 The Dow hit a new high for the year today after surging 520 points. The S&P 500 finished ahead 17. NASDAQ was down 32.\par \par \uc2 Up next, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has a profane suggestion for advertising these -- advertisers boycotting his X social media platform.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 10 in Phoenix as the woman convicted of murdering her children, and accused of killing one man and conspiring to kill another is extradited to Arizona.\par \par \uc2 So-called Doomsday Mom Lori Vallow was in an Idaho prison after the guilty verdict this past May.\par \par \uc2 Fox 29 in Philadelphia, as the city council passes legislation banning facial coverings. The bill would prohibit people wearing skeet from wearing ski masks in parks, schools, and on public transit.\par \par \uc2 And now, moves on to Mayor James Kenney for his potential signing.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Tampa. One of the big stories there tonight from Fox 13, a distillery in New Port Richey will begin bottling a new signature rum for the Rolling Stones. It is called Crossfire Hurricane referring to Mick Jagger's birthplace in the song jumping Jack Flash.\par \par \uc2 The process is expected to start early next month with the rum on the market early next year.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: New Rules proposed by the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  would require most U.S. cities to replace lead water pipes within 10 years. The agency says tighter standards would improve I.Q. scores of children and reduce high blood pressure and heart disease in adults.\par \par \uc2 Former Chief White House medical adviser Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  is expected to testify before Congress early next year. It is part of Republicans years' long investigation into the origins of COVID-19 and the U.S. response. Fauci will sit for transcribed interviews in early January in a public hearing at a later date. It'll be his first appearance before the Republican controlled House.\par \par \uc2 Documents obtained by Fox Business indicate the Biden administration work together with employees of \uc2 Google\uc2  owned \uc2 YouTube\uc2  in 2021 to target alleged misinformation relating to the COVID-19 virus and its vaccinations. The documents reveal a level of correspondence previously unknown to the American public as President Biden and his aides sought to promote coronavirus vaccinations in efforts to quell the raging pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has a message for businesses that are pulling advertisements from his X social media platform. Unfortunately, we can't tell you exactly what he said because it involves profanity. Suffice to say he claims the boycott could kill the company.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business Correspondent Kelly O'Grady has the latest from Los Angeles. Hello, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Mike. Well, no letup in the showdown between \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Disney\uc2 's \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 . Thousands of X users are posting screenshots of canceled \uc2 Disney\uc2  Plus subscriptions. The outpour of frustration comes as Musk is telling off advertisers who are abandoning X, the billionaire called out Iger directly alleging the company's actions are akin to blackmail.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, CEO, TWITTER: If somebody's going to try to blackmail me with advertising, blackmailing with money? Go (INAUDIBLE) yourself. Go (INAUDIBLE) yourself, is that clear? I hope it is. Hi, Bob, if you're in the audience.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY (voice over): Now, those comments come after over 200 companies including \uc2 Disney\uc2  Plus spending on X. At that same summit, Iger defended the choice, citing Musk's inflammatory tweet many perceived as anti-Semitic.\par \par \uc2 BOB IGER, COMPANY CEO, \uc2 WALT DISNEY\uc2 : We just felt that the association with that position and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and X was not necessarily a positive one for us.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY (voice over): Musk did apologize for the message in question, sharing it was the dumbest social media post he's ever made.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, the billionaire is not giving in despite facing a $75 million hit on advertising revenue by year's end. Now, he predicts that while the boycott may bankrupt the company, the public will blame advertisers for the platform's collapse, not him.\par \par \uc2 We are starting to see advertisers trickle back. In fact, the \uc2 Anti- Defamation League\uc2  which started the boycott has resumed spending on X and The Babylon Bee, which is a conservative satire site is even increasing spending now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY (on camera): None of the major advertisers have responded to Musk's inflammatory comments, though few seem to have an issue advertising on TikTok, which is also rife with anti-Semitic content, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Kelly O'Grady live in L.A. Kelly, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 America's crime crisis focuses tonight on Washington State. Workers at three \uc2 Macy's\uc2  stores held a strike and one of the issues was the company's policy regarding theft. Correspondent Dan Springer is in Lynnwood, Washington tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAN SPRINGER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): On the busiest shopping day of the year, hundreds of workers at three \uc2 Macy's\uc2  stores in western Washington walked the picket line instead of helping shoppers in line. Without a contract since June, the workers represented by the \uc2 United Food and Commercial Workers\uc2  say they rejected the company's offer of a $0.50 per hour pay raise. But the union says that's not the only issue.\par \par \uc2 SARAH CHERIN, CHIEF OF STAFF, \uc2 UFCW\uc2  3000: You're hearing, you know, economics is always going to be a priority, right? Workers are dealing with the cost of living. But sure, safety is an important part of that and they really are asking their employers, what are you going to do to keep me safe at work?\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER (voice over): In the last few years, retail theft has become a constant problem across the country. And according to Forbes magazine, it is the worst in Washington State where for every resident, a stunning $347 worth of goods is stolen. The Washington Retailers Association reports $3 billion worth of merchandise was taken last year alone.\par \par \uc2 LIISA LUICK, \uc2 MACY'S\uc2  EMPLOYEE: Not too many smash grabs but that happens as well. A lot of them are repeat offenders. That didn't used to happen but now they come in regularly. (INAUDIBLE). Some of them just walk in, pick something up and walk out and they do this multiple times a week.\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER (voice over): Liisa Luick says \uc2 Macy's\uc2  suspended her without pay for three weeks after she called police on a repeat shoplifter. The company has since backed down and given her back pay, but she wants protections written into any new contract.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER (on camera): \uc2 Macy's\uc2  wouldn't respond to our questions but did issue a pretty bland statement saying safety is always a top priority. The three day workers strike did prompt a new round of negotiations between the workers and the company. But now, worker safety will be on the table, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Dan Springer live in Lynnwood, Washington. Dan, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Police here in Washington have recorded 250 homicides so far this year. That's the highest number the nation's capital has seen in two decades, with a month left until the year's end.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has released a wanted poster for the suspects in yesterday's carjacking of an agent's vehicle. The agent was not harmed in the -- the vehicle was recovered.\par \par \uc2 Federal watchdog will investigate how the Biden administration chose a site for a new \uc2 FBI\uc2  headquarters following a contentious competition marked by allegations of conflict of interest. Inspector General for the \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2  is probing the decision to locate the facility in Greenbelt, Maryland oversight and Virginia. The new building would replace the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's crumbling headquarters here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Up next, two governors from opposite sides of the country and the political spectrum go after each other tonight. And later, we'll look back at the life of \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: A federal judge is temporarily blocking Montana's first of its kind state ban on the use of short video sharing app TikTok from taking effect January 1st. The judge says the ban overstepped state power and infringes on the constitutional rights of users. TikTok says more than a third of Montana residents use that platform.\par \par \uc2 An unusual political event tonight here on Fox. Florida Republican \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  will debate California Democrat \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  on the "HANNITY" program. Correspondent Bill Melugin has a preview tonight from Alpharetta, Georgia. Hello, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Mike. Well, this is going to be a clash of political Titans and it is happening live on Fox News tonight. These are two of America's most high-profile governors, their states and their policies are polar opposites of each other. Tonight, they're coming face-to-face for the very first time after months of hurling insults at each other.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GAVIN NEWSOM\uc2 , (D) CALIFORNIA: He is just weakness masquerading as strength. He's flailing.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: I think his hair gel is interfering with his brain function.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to clean his clock.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Stop pussyfooting around. Are you going to throw your hat in the ring and challenge Joe? Are you going to get in and do it, or are you just going to sit on the sidelines and chirp?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: A lot of frostiness there. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  has previously called DeSantis a, quote, "small, pathetic man," and said his presidential campaign has, quote, "bellyflopped." DeSantis, on the other hand, has said that Newsom ruined his state with his liberal progressive policies and is responsible for the mass exodus of people out of the state of California.\par \par \uc2 Tonight's debate is dubbed the great red versus blue state debate. It's going to present two visions of America to a national audience. And these governors' policies could not be further apart. DeSantis expected to go after Newsom on topics like homelessness, crime, taxes, COVID-19 lockdowns and mandates, and California's cost of living. Newsom expected to target DeSantis over his positions on abortion, transportation of illegal immigrants to blue cities around the country, healthcare, and social issues.\par \par \uc2 And back out here live, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  has said he is sitting out the 2024 presidential campaign while Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is currently trailing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the polling for the GOP primary. So that's opening up speculation that this matchup we're going to watch tonight could be a potential preview of a 2028 presidential matchup. Mike, one thing we can all expect probably going to happen tonight, a serious amount of fireworks, face to face for the first time. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Talk about must-see TV. Bill Melugin, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 A New York appeals court has reinstated a gag order that barred former President Trump from commenting about court personnel after he disparaged a law clerk in his New York civil fraud trial. The decision from a four judge panel came two weeks after an individual appellate judge had put the order on hold while the appeals process played out.\par \par \uc2 As former President Trump continues to dominate the Republican presidential primary polls, this evening we take a look at his time in office and what that means for the future. My colleague Bret Baier talks with \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2  of ABC news.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, Mike.\par \par \uc2 Joining us tonight is \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2 . He is the chief Washington correspondent for ABC News. He's also the author of a new book called "Tired of Winning," about former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . John, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2 , AUTHOR, "TIRED OF WINNING": Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to talk about the book. I want to ask you about your day job and what you're hearing in Democratic circles about the Biden campaign. I have talked to a bunch up in Washington and other places about their level of concern right now about where they stand about the age issue, and about if anything is going to change with him as the nominee.\par \par \uc2 KARL: There's two ways to answer that. First, from the Biden campaign itself, they express confidence both behind the scenes and publicly, especially at the prospect that they will be running against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They think Biden has beaten Trump, that Trump is somebody that he can beat rather easily again. But, beyond the confines of the Biden campaign, there is real concern in Democratic circles. They look at Biden's approval rating. It is at historically low levels. It's at the kind of level that you see an incumbent president heading towards a losing reelection campaign. There is concern if not about his age and whether or not he can go through with it, clearly the perception among that he is too old.\par \par \uc2 And look at the polls. They see Democratic voters practically screaming for an alternative, Democratic voters saying they would like to see somebody other than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  running as the Democratic nominee.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But as far as that shoe ever dropping, it's just not logistically how that happens.\par \par \uc2 KARL: Look, he is the nominee. Barring some major health event, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the Democratic nominee.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to talk about the book. You have this chapter called "Team of Sycophants" in which you write that a lot of the criticism is really from top Republicans, some of them who are in the White House of former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 KARL: It's across the board. And I want to say one thing important about this book. I would say more than 90 percent of the people that I spoke to for this book were Republicans. The vast majority of them people that served for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , supported \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , some of whom still support him to this day.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at the people that he put in the most important positions in his administration, secretary of defense, chief of staff, the Department of Justice, senior people in every one of those positions have expressed, publicly in most cases, real concerns about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and what it would mean if he returned to the White House, and searing criticism of what he was likes a president.\par \par \uc2 So this is not coming from weak-kneed RINO Republicans on the Hill or certainly not from Democrats. They echo that. That's irrelevant as far as I'm concerned. That's just politics. But these are people that saw \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  up close who are very worried about what it would mean if he returned to the White House.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Not surprisingly the former president put out a Truth Social post right after your book came out, saying, among other things, this, "Failed ABC fake news reporter \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2  just wrote another bad book. He worked so hard but has so little talent. Some people have it. Some people don't." He goes on to say, "Many other false stories in Karl's very boring book, but nothing worth mentioning." Has he had a good review of any of these books?\par \par \uc2 KARL: He was quite positive about my first book, "Front Row at the Trump Show," actually publicly on multiple occasions said that I treated him fairly, which he said surprising me, but you treated me fairly. And he has acknowledged that I have known him for a long time. Look, Bret, I have known \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  since the early 1990s. I went into the White House as a White House correspondent first under Obama. Democrats criticized me as being too tough on Obama. When Trump came in, I felt it was my duty to treat him fairly and with respect. And I believe that he knows that I did so.\par \par \uc2 So I would say at least in that long statement that you showed, at least acknowledge that I work hard.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KARL: But I have had a good relationship with him over the years because I, you know, I treated him fairly. I called it as it was. And that's what I'm doing in this book.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What we have seen so far each indictment, each time he has been in a trial, his poll numbers have either stayed the same or gone up. His fundraising either stayed the same or gone up. Do you think as we go along that that dynamic is going to change as he is facing different court cases, some that many legal experts say have a little bit more bite than the early ones?\par \par \uc2 KARL: We don't know what happens in it's a conviction. I think the case that is most likely to get to a possible conviction before the election would be the election interference case, the federal case. And how does the public react to a conviction?\par \par \uc2 But I also think there is something else important here. Yes, his poll numbers have gone up with each indictment. He has gotten a message. The message is one of retribution. He says in 2016 I said I am your voice. Now I say I am your retribution. He really means his retribution, going after the people that are going after him.\par \par \uc2 I think there has been far too little attention paid to what he is actually talking about as a candidate for president and far too little attention paid not to the legal cases -- we know those cases chapter and verse -- but what a second Trump term would look like. I argue in this book that it would be quite different than what we saw in the first term, and I think it's something that people need to confront and to deal with.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, we have talked about it on this show with a lot of punditry, including Brit Hume and others. Jonathan, as always, thank you. The book is called "Tired of Winning." Thanks for coming on.\par \par \uc2 KARL: Great, thanks a lot, Bret. Great to talk to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Mike, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Bret, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we remember the legendary American diplomat \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2 , and later the panel with the latest on the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war and hostage for prisoner exchange.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Crews in Chicago are building a winterized base camps for migrants in Brighton Park. Protesters have fought against it for weeks. The structures for the heated tents will house 500 to 2,000 migrants. The facility will have bathrooms, showers, a kitchen, and security. Neighbors say it will be bad for the migrants, the neighborhood, and for taxpayers. Meanwhile, the mayor says the city will not pursue a similar facility at the Emmerson Fieldhouse. He says it is no longer needed.\par \par \uc2 Japan is suspending all flights by its Osprey aircraft after a U.S. Air Force Osprey based in Japan crashed in the sea during a training mission Wednesday. The body of one crew member has been recovered. Seven others are missing.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, global leaders are paying tribute to legendary American diplomat \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2 . The former secretary of state died yesterday at the age of 100. President Biden has just put a statement. He recalled his first meeting with Kissinger and said, "Throughout our careers we often disagreed. But from that first briefing, his fierce intellect and profound strategic focus was evident." Correspondent Lauren Green attended one of Kissinger's final public appearances last month. She takes a look at his life and legacy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HENRY KISSINGER\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: There is no country in the world where it is conceivable that a man of my origins could be standing here next to the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN GREEN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2 , the 56th secretary of state, will be remembered as one of the most admired, controversial, influential, and, yet, respected men in the political world. Kissinger advised 12 presidents from JFK to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but the nexus of his power came under President \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RICHARD NIXON\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Kissinger is a man who is known to all people who are interested in foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: His realpolitik method pushed the practical rather than the ideological.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Few people were better students of history. Even fewer people did more to shape history.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Kissinger's diplomacy laid the groundwork for President's Nixon historic 1972 visit to China, leading to normalizing U.S.-China relations.\par \par \uc2 KISSINGER: It's culminating still through eight American presidents and four generations of Chinese leaders.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: A controversy to this day, he was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize for negotiating the ceasefire agreement ending American involvement in Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 Born to an orthodox Jewish family in German, Kissinger and his family fled to the United States as the Nazis gained power, becoming an American citizen and serving in the U.S. army during World War II, studying at Harvard and later teaching there. He was the first person to serve as both secretary of state and national security adviser at the same time. He remained influential even at 100.\par \par \uc2 KISSINGER: A society does not grow by victories of one faction over another, but by reconciliations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREEN (on camera): He is survived by wife Nancy, two children, and five grandchildren. He will be interred at a private family service and later a memorial service in New York. Mike?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Lauren Green, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with the latest on the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war, plus tonight's debate between Governors \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Ukrainian officials say Russian missiles tore through apartment buildings in the Donetsk region. They say one person was killed and families were buried under the rubble.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London. One of the big stories there tonight, visitors to the Edinburgh Zoo get their final chance to see and bid farewell to a pair of popular giant pandas. The animals are returning home to China after more than a decade in Scotland.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: This was the seventh day of the humanitarian pause. It's going to be up to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  whether they will continue to do this.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: We do not support a move to the south unless or until they have adequately accounted for the protection of innocent human life.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): To the Biden administration, quit handcuffing Israel, quit trying to restrict their ability to destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2  as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: So hostages are coming out. There was also violence today in Jerusalem, which \uc2 Hamas\uc2  took credit for, killing some innocent Israelis. So it feels like a very delicate situation in the Middle East as Israel and the United States try to get hostages out, and Israel is eager to get on with its military operation against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, Jason Riley, "Wall Street Journal" columnist and senior fellow at the Manhattan Institute, and Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist." Welcome to you all.\par \par \uc2 So Jason, your thoughts on where things stand in the Middle East at this hour?\par \par \uc2 JASON RILEY, COLUMNIST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Everyone likes to see these hostages reunited with family members, every decent person likes to see that. The concern, however, is what if \uc2 Hamas\uc2  can take advantage of this pause to regroup, to plan new attacks, and so forth. And that is the big concern here.\par \par \uc2 And the other reality is that we know the hostages are their only leverage. They are unlikely to ever give up all of these hostages. Israel knows that. And my big concern right now is what we heard from Kirby, some others in the administration, which is trying to micromanage how Israel goes about doing this. And they want to extend this pause as long as possible. And the problem there is that what they really want to do is keep Israel from completing its mission, which is to eliminate \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . And so this ceasefire has to stop at some point, and I think the sooner the better.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's put on the screen a tweet from President Biden. He says "\uc2 Hamas\uc2  unleashed a terrorist attack because they fear nothing more than Israelis and Palestinians living side by side in peace to continue down the path of terror, violence, killing and war is to give \uc2 Hamas\uc2  what they seek. We can't do that." Some have interpreted that, Juan, to mean that the president may be softening in his support for Israel and its military operation. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, two thoughts. One is that I thought the attack today that killed three people that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  took credit for, it seems to me it was a parallel to the original attack on Israel, which, again, took place when Secretary of State Blinken was there and trying to work on a deal to establish some kind of peace. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  clearly does not want peace. They don't want a two-state solution. They want the end of Israel. I mean, it's crazy. But, anyway, that's what they want, and I think that's why you are going to see this kind of ongoing violence.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the political picture here at home, President Biden has been solidly supportive of Israel. I think that has scrambled a lot of the political dynamics here because Republicans are also supportive of Israel in general, and want to, in fact, increase the support. But you see them now saying well, are we seeing any signs of softening of President Biden's support for Israel as he tries to get a deal. But getting a deal and trying to move forward I think is essential, because at some point the fighting is going to be done. And the question is, where do we go from here? What's next? Saudi Arabia, I think Syria, potentially, I hope. But you see Qatar and Saudi Arabia saying we want some kind of deal.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Mollie, you heard Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  basically saying don't handcuff the Israelis, let them do what they need to do. Would it be better for President Biden to say, take 30 days, do what you need to do, get it over with, because we don't want this lingering for 60 days or 90 days or six months?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": Well, one of the things about the ceasefire to Jason's point, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  uses a ceasefire to think about what it wants to do and regroup. Israel is also able to do that during a ceasefire, to reassess how it's pursuing things. Israel seems to want to just be able to do what they need to do to eradicate \uc2 Hamas\uc2  without the U.S. handcuffing it. So that would be a good way to help. It does seem like the Biden administration is trying to dictate a lot about how that goes. And I'm not sure how that would go.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Tonight, on FOX, a unique political event. Two up and coming governors, one a current presidential candidate, one seen as a future presidential candidate. There it is, Newsom, DeSantis, heavyweight bought with Hannity in the middle of it. Jason, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 RILEY: Well, the voters will get to see two alternatives to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which is what they seem to want, at least in terms of what they are telling pollsters. Listen, we are going to see big contrast between two big states with a lot of people. Contrast on everything from covid management to climate management, to school choice and taxes and so forth. I think Florida has the better record there, which is why I think Governor Newsom is going to spend all his time talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and abortion.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's play a sample of these two guys together.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: He is running a shadow campaign. I think what he's done in California, Governor Newsom, causing a mass exodus out of the state. You have really seen cities collapse there. That is what he would want to do for this entire country.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GAVIN NEWSOM\uc2 , (D) CALIFORNIA: I don't like people that demonize other people, or go after vulnerable communities, but I also don't like liars.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Juan, how do you see it? Is this a preview of 2028? Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It could be. I think right now, for 2024, the upcoming election I think \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is basically saying, in case of emergency, here I am, break the glass. And I think what you are seeing for DeSantis is, talk about me. Pay attention. His campaign has been slipping, losing momentum, and he wants people now to focus on him, and this is his opportunity.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We're up against the clock. I want to give Mollie a chance.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: And \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is running a shadow campaign. I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is right about that. In many ways he's running the type of campaign that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  should have run. He is everywhere. He's talking about all of his strengths. But he is not doing a direct challenge to the incumbent. And that's essentially what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 's problem is, is that he is running against the incumbent. Of course, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  has basically the same policies as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , which makes him not politically salient as well.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Do you think some of the audience will look at Newsom and say he is a young, vigorous guy, I wish he were the Democratic nominee?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Definitely. And that makes sense for why he is doing this, to be able to do a debate without actually entering of the race.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Final thought from you, Jason?\par \par \uc2 RILEY: I agree with that. He is going to be Biden's surrogate tonight. He's going to go out there and make the arguments that Biden should be making on the campaign and hope that that will resonate to Biden's effect with voters.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think DeSantis should be debating Haley. That would be something to see at this juncture. And we will see where that goes.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We know some programming people here. Maybe we can pitch that next.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Bring them on. Bring them on.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Thank you, panel.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a special day right here in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Three, two, one.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Beautiful. Tonight, the White House is ushering in the second century of the national Christmas tree lighting ceremony. Tuesday, a strong gust of wind toppled the tree. The crane has been holding up the 40-foot spruce. The beloved tradition of the national Christmas tree lighting ceremony began Christmas eve in 1923, how about that, when President \uc2 Calvin Coolidge\uc2  lit a 48-football balsam fir from Vermont. There you go.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, our "Whatever Happened To?" segment looks at the Wall Street Best, \uc2 GameStop\uc2  saga and how it impacted the future of trading.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 eastern, 3:00 on the west coast. Thanks very much for watching. I'm Mike Emanuel in Washington. "THE INGRAHAM ANGLE" with Laura is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020231201ejbu00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Israel And \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Strike Temporary Ceasefire Deal; Massive Explosion Rocks Rainbow Bridge; Hostage-For-Prisoner Swap Delayed; Biden's Open Border Endanger America; Rainbow Bridge Explosions A Warning Sign To America; Crime Running Rampant In Biden's America; Democrats Smears Become More Ludicrous By The Day; \uc2 Mike Davis\uc2  Responds To Media's Outrageous Smears; Democrats Are Desperate To Get Trump Off The Ballot; Raymond Waded Into Pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  White House Protest; Arab-American Democrats Sour On Biden Ahead Of 2024\b0\par\par\uc2 Jason Chaffetz, Alexis McAdams, Alex Hogan, Caroline Elliott,  Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 7,610 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 November 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS HOST: Watching Special Report. I'm Mike Emanuel in Washington. I'm back tomorrow for a live Special Report. That'll do it for this evening. The Ingraham Angle is up next. Have a wonderful holiday. God bless, America.\par \par \uc2 JASON CHAFFETZ, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Jason Chaffetz, in for Laura Ingraham. And this is a special edition of The Ingraham Angle. Two major breaking stories happening tonight. The first, Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  have come to a temporary ceasefire deal, which includes a hostage and prisoner swap. But late tonight we learned from the Israeli government that the swap has been delayed. We'll go live to Northern Israel for the latest in moments.\par \par \uc2 But first, two people are dead after a massive vehicle explosion at the U.S.-Canada border. Video shows the car flying through the air before ending in a fiery crash. Fox's Alexis McAdams is standing by with all the breaking details, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Jason. So this has been developing throughout the day and with breaking news comes breaking developments. So the latest information we have so far is sources telling us that earlier in the day that explosion after that crash was so loud, they thought there were several explosive devices in that vehicle. So that was the initial reporting that came throughout the day. Now, New York's Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  trying to get out in front of this, because so many people are on edge around Buffalo and in the country. And this is what she had to say, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): We've been on heightened alert since October 7th. That's why it's so important for me to stand here and tell the world based on what we know at this moment. And again, anything can change. There is no sign of terrorist activity with respect to this crash.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Take a look there on your screen as well. You can see the car going at a high rate of speed, right, then it suddenly goes airborne clearing a fence there near the border and smashing to the ground that impact causing what sources tell us was a massive explosion that had people on high alert, so much so that they ended up putting extra security at the airport and evacuating government buildings.\par \par \uc2 And people were really afraid is what was going to happen next. The firing golf that vehicle as crews rushed into help. So authorities say the investigation is ongoing. It's going to be that way for several days, maybe weeks or more. The blaze in the crash injuring one border patrol agent who only had a minor injury. The governor saying at this time, it's not clear if the crash was intentional or if you know this person was driving specifically toward the Rainbow Bridge there.\par \par \uc2 Back out here life, we can tell you the governor once again, right wanting to calm the fears of so many people as we've heard from federal authorities, Jason for several days, that they have a heightened alert with growing issues across the country and they want to make sure everybody is alerted as we head into this holiday weekend where we've seen so much happening. Jason.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Alexis, thank you. And in Israel, a temporary ceasefire deal has been reached with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , which includes the release of 50 hostages, but there's a catch and a delay. For all the details, we go live to northern Israel where Fox News Foreign Correspondent Alex Hogan is standing by. Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Jason. There was hope that these hostages could be released as early as tomorrow. Now we're hearing that that likely will not be happening until earliest Friday morning. Now what this deal includes, it is the release of 50 hostages, women and children in exchange for about 150 Palestinians, women and children in Israeli jails, as well as a four-day ceasefire and that ceasefire could be extended even longer. One extra day for every 10 additional hostages who are released.\par \par \uc2 So families here in Israel are hopeful tonight, their loved ones could be among those released. There's also a lot of concern and fear, of course, about what their loved ones have likely endured over the last six plus weeks. But Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  says that the current deal also gives hope to the hostages who will not be part of this first group.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , PRIME MINISTER, ISRAEL (Via Translator): The Red Cross will be allowed to visit the remainder of the hostages and to give them required medications and I expect the Red Cross to do its work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: The ceasefire has not started yet at in Gaza. The IAF is conducting those targeted raids. They also released new footage of the large tunnels found under the Al Shifa Hospital, the largest medical facility in northern Gaza. Now so far, they say that they have found 400 tunnel shafts built by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  as they work their way through the northern Gaza Strip. Meanwhile, here in northern Israel, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  responded to incoming fire from \uc2 Hezbollah in Lebanon\uc2  by sending in fighter jets to hit military sites.\par \par \uc2 Several people died in Lebanon over the past few days including two Lebanese journalists. \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  says that there will be retaliation here for those deaths. We're hearing from U.S. state officials that there is concern that the conflict could continue to spread out into a much larger conflict. U.S. officials are hoping that the ceasefire with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Israel In the south will also carry over here to the conflict between the \uc2 IDF\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We also are learning that just within the last day today, there was a surprise visit, it was unannounced by Iran's foreign minister visiting Lebanon. Jason.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Interesting. Alex, I have one question for you, the people on the ground there in Israel. I mean, this attack -- this -- this fighting has been going on for weeks. Is this a negotiated deal? Is it giving them hope? Do they think that this is their -- the end is in sight? Or do they know that, hey, this might be a slight pause, but the fighting will continue on.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: I think there's definitely a sense of hope for the families as far as those who are hopeful that in the next coming hours, maybe 48 hours, they could be among the lucky few to see their family members again. But there's not the expectation among the general Israeli public that this by any means, means a stop in the war. This is what we have heard repeatedly being called as just a temporary ceasefire, pausing the fighting right now, to be able to free up some of these hostages by no means calling this an end to the war.\par \par \uc2 Here alone in the north, these towns have been evacuated and while this is very, very far from the conflict that we're seeing in the south, it is just not safe for people to come back here because of the growing conflict with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . Same thing in the south towns along Gaza, in Israel have been evacuated, because it's just not safe to return there. So there's about 280,000 Israelis that are currently displaced in the country, because of the regional conflicts that we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 There's 1.7 million people displaced in Gaza, but here on the ground in terms of those concerns right now and those feelings of, is this almost over? No, this is just a temporary pause to free up some of those families. Jason,\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Alex, stay safe. You're doing some amazing reporting. And I know you're away from family this Thanksgiving. Thanks for, for what you're doing and bringing us the latest from Israel. And I really do appreciate it. Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISOR: Between creating the time and space to get hostages out, which is a huge win first for the families, but also for Israel, for the United States and for other countries.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: So should the White House really be exploiting this temporary ceasefire to take a victory lap? Joining me now is Richard Goldberg, former Director for Countering Iranian Weapons of Mass Destruction at the National Security Council under President Trump. Thanks for being here. What do you think -- what do you make of the national security advisor's comments and the general comments coming out of the White House at this point?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GOLDBERG, FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL OFFICIAL: Well, Jason, we're seeing tonight already this pause, this 24-hour delay and a deal. We thought was going to happen, families waiting with bated breath by their phones, to see if they get the call, a Hunger Games type psychological warfare, to see if their loved ones are coming home tomorrow. And now they find out, the rug has been pulled out of them. It'll be another day delay.\par \par \uc2 So these are all psychological warfare games and extension of the horror show that started on October 7th. Nobody should be trying to deliver press releases and victory laps right now. We should remain very focused in understanding of who \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is, what this evil is that continues to play out in front of us and make sure we have Israel's back to restart operations and destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2  when this is over.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yes, but it does sound Richard like the White House was very encouraging, very supportive, and probably very involved in this -- in this -- this hostage negotiation. And it's, -- hey, if you're going to be able to bring any of those hostages home, if you're one of those families, you want it to be your loved one. So trying to find that proper balance. That's a tough call.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Well, absolutely, but I do think the administration has gotten this wrong from the very beginning, who started this hostage negotiation. It was one of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's chief sponsors that hosts them, the Qataris in Doha where \uc2 Hamas\uc2  still has an office, their leaders are still negotiating right now at this hour on this hostage deal. We should have been putting pressure on Qatar from day one, we still should be putting pressure on Qatar, to say close down your sponsorship of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and deliver all of the hostages unconditionally.\par \par \uc2 Let's remember what's going to happen in this deal. Children and women are going to be taken away from fathers. The families will be divided. Think of all the families that won't get any loved ones when this is over. And think of, again, all the dead, the 1200 people who will never come home because they've already been mass murdered by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So understand this is not a time for victory laps. It's great to see people come home, every life saved is valued. That's why Israel is doing this deal. But let's remain very focused on who \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is, who their sponsors are, what their game is in this negotiation. And make sure we don't allow this to become a permanent ceasefire. Make sure we have Israel's back to finish the job.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: So Richard, we know a bit about the hostages that were just taken. I mean they were literally just came across the border and took them and use them as human shields in a place of negotiation. But in this swap, a three to one swap, they would be releasing -- Israel would be releasing prisoners, tell us about the makeup of these prisoners that would be going back over to the Palestinian side of the equation?\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Well, that's right, not just this operational pause by the Israelis that we've talked about as far as the ceasefire, but also the release of the three to one ratio of people who are in Israeli jails, because they were convicted of committing terror-related crimes. These are people who tried to kill Jews in Israel, whether by knife attack, explosives, smuggling for terrorist, plotting attacks, they did not commit those, thankfully, casualty causing terrorist attacks in the end. They were caught beforehand or in the process.\par \par \uc2 And so what the Israeli say is these people don't have blood on their hands, as they say in Israel. Those are the ones that are going to be on a list to be released in exchange for the hostages. But remember, these are going to be people who are involved in terrorism, going right back into the West Bank, going into East Jerusalem, free to rejoin the battlefield. So Israel is paying a high price for this, but they're doing so out of the humanitarian wish to get their people home. Because in the end, Israel values life, their enemies value death.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Richard, thank you for your perspective. Have a wonderful Thanksgiving. We do appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right. Joining me now is Ambassador Robert O'Brien. He's the former National Security Advisor for President Trump. Ambassador O'Brien, thanks so much for joining us. What's your general take on what's happening and what's going on right now? And what do you make of this pause for another 24 hours?\par \par \uc2 AMB ROBERT O'BRIEN, FORMER TRUMP NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISOR: Well, the first thing is, I want to say it'll be great if the hostages actually come home. I'd be careful if I was the administration about talking about the victory that's been attained because so far, no hostages have come home. So we need to get them home. We don't want to be premature. This has happened in the past with the Biden administration where they've touted a deal for Brittney Griner or for someone else and then it's been delayed.\par \par \uc2 So let's make sure the hostages get home. But if they get home, it's a victory for the families and a victory for Israel. But once that's done, we need to make sure that Israel goes back to the task of eliminating \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , root and branch and taking them out, so they can't commit these kinds of terrorist activities again in the future.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: You played such a pivotal role there in the Trump administration, explain to us on the macro level, what's going on with Qatar, what's going on with Iran, because nothing's happening with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  for that matter. But nothing's going on with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  without the Iranians being involved. Correct?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, this is an Iranian operation from start to finish. And \uc2 Hamas\uc2  would not shoot a BB gun over the border with Israel without the approval of the IRGC and the Kurds force. So Iran's behind this. Iran's the funder of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Iran trained \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . They pay bounties to suicide bombers. So this is an Iranian operation. They've attacked -- they've essentially attacked Israel. They've used \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  or caspases (ph), as proxies to bring the war to Israel's borders. And we need to hold Iran accountable for it.\par \par \uc2 And, look, we just paid $6 billion in ransom for five Americans held by Iran. And what did they do with that money and money is fungible, it may not be that specific money, but the funds that the Iranian government has, and that they've achieved during the Biden administration. They funded \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , they've taken terrorists, they've taken hostages, and they've committed terrorist acts by killing 1300 Israelis on the border. So we've totally got to hold Iran responsible at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Now, there's been some fighting up north. We just heard a report from northern Israel about what \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is doing. Is that the exact same equation with Iran involvement with the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  as well?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: It is, Jason. The \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has been supplied by over 100,000 missiles, short-range, medium-range, long-range missiles by the Iranians, and they present an extreme danger to the state of Israel. The Israelis have had to evacuate people from the border to make sure that what happens -- what happened down south, the Sderot area, it doesn't happen up north in Galilee, and the border with Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 So look, but \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  needs to be careful. And I think \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is watching closely as Israel eliminate \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . And we've got to make sure as Rich said, we support the Israelis. When this pause is over, the Israelis have to go back to the business of eliminating \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . That's the main goal here.\par \par \uc2 We want to get the hostages home and then we want to eliminate \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . And \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is watching that wondering if we attack Israel, are we next and I think the answer is yes. So, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is being far more careful than I think a lot of people may be anticipated.\par \par \uc2 Our administration is so concerned about escalation. We want the other guys to be concerned about escalation. We want Iran to be concerned about escalation. We want \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  to be concerned about escalation and not always us being so worried about escalation.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Robert O'Brien, the former National Security Advisor for President Trump, thank you so much for joining us and hope you and your family have a wonderful Thanksgiving. Appreciate it joining us on The Ingraham Angle. Alright, new details about the vehicle explosion at our northern border. We go live to the scene next. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: A fiery crash at our northern border sending shockwaves through the country. Who was behind this and what was the motive? For more we go live to Niagara Falls where Fox Multimedia Reporter Caroline Elliott is standing by. Caroline.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE ELLIOTT, FOX MULTIMEDIA REPORTER: Well, Jason, there have been some conflicting reports today. But tonight, we want to focus on the latest information about the driver in this case. Now, tonight \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  tweeted out that he was just briefed by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  on the incident at Rainbow Bridge in Niagara Falls. They have now confirmed there's no reason at present to believe this was terrorism. And right now they believe they have the identity of the driver.\par \par \uc2 Now, here on the ground, there are still several roads and the border entry behind us is closed. And talking with border agents here on the ground, they tell us that this will likely take some time before it's reopened. And officials are just telling us right now they're thankful this wasn't worse. Jason\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Caroline, thank you very much. Do appreciate it. All right. Joining me now is Paul Mauro, former \uc2 NYPD\uc2 , Head of Intel Operations. Paul, thanks for joining us tonight on The Ingraham Angle. What's the latest? What are you hearing? You've got a good network of people in law enforcement in New York, what are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 PAUL MAURO, FORMER NYPD HEAD OF INTEL OPERATIONS: So they've been pouring water on this as being a terrorist attack from probably hour and a half, two hours ago, maybe even earlier. Some of it just didn't check out.\par \par \uc2 Now look, you always have to investigate it as if it's terrorism. And that has to continue until you really know this positively. But there are certain things here that just kind of didn't fly. For instance, if it were a terrorist attack and the car was ostensibly loaded with heavy explosives, A, you didn't get much of a blast. OK, because fortunately, the only to deal ways are the driver and the passenger.\par \par \uc2 But secondly, what would have the plan have been that the crash of the vehicle was supposed to be the triggering mechanism. That doesn't really make a whole lot of sense. First -- and secondly, the car is supposedly stolen. When was it stolen? And by track -- backtracking that way, you could say, OK, the car has only been in these people's possession for a certain amount of time. Did they have the time to wire it up for a detonation, which is a fairly technical thing, I couldn't do it.\par \par \uc2 So I think that there was a lot of skepticism about it from the get go. But that said, and you know, in defense of some of the reporting that came out, there were reports coming from the scene that there was a loud explosion. And what I put that down to is the fact that the car was apparently going incredibly fast. And a car of that size hitting anything is going to make a lot of noise. And I think the bystanders, it sounded like an explosion.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Well, yes, and especially if you don't hear those things from -- on a daily basis. Here's what Governor Hochul said about how big the crash was. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: If you can imagine, this vehicle, basically, incinerated, nothing is left. But the engine, the pieces, they're scattered over 13, 14 booths. So it is a large scene. And it's going to take a lot of time for our federal law enforcement partners to be able to piece together the real story.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: I do hope, Paul that they were able to identify who the drivers were and much swifter than what it sounds like. But I would guess with the debris so big and so far, and so wide, it does take a little while to put this together, even though it may have been a stolen vehicle.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Right. So the stolen vehicle is actually what works against you because the plate and even the VIN is not going to come back to the owner, because the car has been stolen. So then you're left with two mystery bodies that you have to figure out how to track and if they're not in possession of ID, if the crash was so cataclysmic that you don't have an intact wallet or something to go through or they purposely didn't have their wallets on them, then you really don't have a lot to go on.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like they've ID them, I assume they managed to get some kind of a license or something at the scene. And now what they'll be doing is just reverse engineering that going through all the data points that come off of that as in social media, phones, the financials, credit card usage, if there is, prior criminal history, they'll begin to get a feel for what they're dealing with here and who they're dealing with.\par \par \uc2 And you know, the car reportedly was going 100 miles per hour. Again, if you were looking to make a terrorism statement and you wanted to potentially kill people, hurt people, CBP officers or something like that. This was either the most low rent terrorism act in American history or this wasn't a terrorist act and I think that's how it's panning out now. But you don't know and then a context of what we have in Gaza, you've got to treat it that way.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yes, I mean, the person could have been having a medical seizure or something, we don't know. But that's why good law enforcement over the Thanksgiving holiday are giving up their time to dive into it. Paul, we appreciate it and hope you have a wonderful Thanksgiving as well.\par \par \uc2 And what happened at the border today sent chills through most Americans, it was scary. People didn't know if it was a terrorist attack. We didn't know exactly what was happening or what the motive was. We've made us ask ourselves, could it have been related to the open border policies of the administration? Was it simply an accident? We don't know. But it caused us all to think. Congressman \uc2 Dan Meuser\uc2  is here with the reaction. That's coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: There's 280 something people on the terrorist watch list that we know of. Where are the people? The individuals whom we encounter that are on the terrorist screening --\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Are they out of the country --\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Or screened --\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Are they out of the country or in the country?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Senator, they very well may be out of the country.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: But you don't know. There're people on the terrorist watch list and you can't tell me where they're at.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Today's explosion at the border, regardless of the motive behind it, is a chilling reminder that we're all on high alert and living in a post-9-11 mindset, which means that our borders need to be secure. We shouldn't be living in fear. But that's what the cluelessness in D.C. is forcing us to do. It's having dire consequences on the safety of our nation and the psyche of the average American.\par \par \uc2 But at the bare minimum, we need to know who's crossing into our country? Why are they here? Where are they going? What are they doing? These are not difficult questions if you have the political will to actually ask them. But unfortunately, the leaders of today, including Secretary Mayorkas, they aren't willing to do it because they don't have the political will to actually shut down the border.\par \par \uc2 So, we don't know exactly what happened at the explosion by Niagara Falls. And we need to know sooner rather than later. And law enforcement's working on it. But it's evident that our nation's borders, our nation's border, both the north and the south, they are not under operational control.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Pennsylvania Congressman \uc2 Dan Meuser\uc2 . Congressman, thanks so much for joining us tonight. When you first got word of what was happening in Niagara Falls, what ran through your mind?\par \par \uc2 CONG. DAN MEUSER (R-PA): Jason, great to be with you. Myself, like most Americans, unfortunately, we're not surprised just due to how poor is our southern border is, and as well as our northern border. We will see how this develops. But this certainly should be a very minimum, a gut punch to the Biden administration, that we have to do just what you said, close the borders, know who's coming through. We need we need high fences and we're a country with wide gates.\par \par \uc2 But moving forward, we just have to maintain -- understand that they've been -- what we do know is this, excuse me, that there's been a hundred and sixty-seven though illegals coming across our border on the terror watch list that is six times more that has occurred in the year -- two years ago and that is a danger to our nation.\par \par \uc2 So again, seeing a scene like this, an attack on our northern border. Unfortunately, it was -- the dot come as any surprise.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Well, and that's the mindset that Americans are having to live with. And that's what terrorism does, right? It creates terror in people's everyday living. I think that one of the main impetuses of what's going to happen here in the election is going to be about safety. It's going to be about economic safety, and safety, you know, in your wallet, safety overseas, safety at our borders.\par \par \uc2 But I want you -- I interviewed you on Fox News Radio, and I want you to tell people what happened to you personally and your staff over the last multiple months and years of what you've had to deal with in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 MUESER: And it is all related. We're not enforcing the laws on the books. And that's exactly why Mayorkas, and I support the impeachment of Mayorkas, because Article II of the Constitution states you need to enforce the laws that Congress passes. And he's not, not in the slightest. It's a complete disaster. And it runs into it's very similar to the situation that exists in our streets.\par \par \uc2 We have prosecutors that are not enforcing the laws with cities like Washington D.C. and Philadelphia that have, at literally, defunded the police. Now, D.C. over the last four years, it's been decreased -- reduced into the police funding by over a hundred million dollars, so it's almost one-sixth of the budget.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not a surprise that crime has skyrocketed. We have carjackings in D.C. up over a hundred percent. Yet just the other day, my car, couple of windows smashed in the morning, but the police saw the criminals inside my car thinking they were trying to rob it and they've ran off.\par \par \uc2 But in discussing with my staff afterwards, and I knew -- I certainly knew some of this. I have my eight staffers in Washington, three of them were held up a gunpoint in the last two years. We started making some calls around the other offices, interns, guests coming in, are running into armed robbery, are running into all kinds of harassment.\par \par \uc2 And just clearly, you know, these largely Democrat-run cities are an absolute disaster. I mean, the worst crime since the early 90's or even the 70's.\par \par \uc2 And I'll just add this. San Diego, for instance, stands out. \uc2 San Diego\uc2  has a Republican district attorney. Last name is Stefan, a woman, Summer Stefan. The city is almost the size, a little bit smaller than Philadelphia, and they have about one-tenth the level of crime. So, it can be done as long as it's done right. And we put those who are committing crimes, we put them away. And yes, we fund the police properly.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, there was a recent study that showed that 40 percent of Americans, 40 percent are scared to leave their home within a one-mile radius. They're scared about going outside, particularly at night. Congressman Meuser, have a wonderful holiday, a great Thanksgiving, and I really appreciate you joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 All right. The radical left has a new villain and he's ready to respond to the latest smear campaign against him. You're familiar with him. It's \uc2 Mike Davis\uc2 . He's coming up next. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: The media has been fixated on disqualifying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  before a vote has even been cast. But they also want to frighten off anybody who might be thinking of working for him, like my next guest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: \uc2 Mike Davis\uc2 , a conservative lawyer and activist with a serious inside track to a top job under President Trump 47 at the Justice Department. \uc2 Mike Davis\uc2  is on their radar, not in spite of, but almost certainly because of his unhinged rhetoric. One whose seem keen to transform Trump's darkest fascist impulses into an actual policy agenda.\par \par \uc2 UNK