par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: I mean, is there any more irony than that? I mean, these people are actually saying this.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Yeah, they turned the \uc2 FBI\uc2  against then-candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016 when they falsely accused him of Russian collusion. They turned the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the intel agencies against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2020 over \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's laptop.\par \par \uc2 And now they've turned the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Biden Justice Department against President Trump, his top aides, his supporters on January 6th, parents outraged at school board meetings, Christians protesting outside of abortion clinics while they give amnesty to their BLM and Antifa and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  base along with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his corrupt family.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: \uc2 Mike Davis\uc2 , the irony is just dripping, just dripping tonight. So, \uc2 Mike Davis\uc2 , thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: What Trump would do, what we now know, concentration camps in effect for millions of immigrants, you've got to make this case and you have to make it that it is a choice, and it's a choice, as you guys have been saying eloquently, do you want fascism?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Right.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Or do you want an imperfect democracy?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Democrats and the media are trying to scare you over what Trump might do and how he will destroy democracy. Meanwhile, they are actively trying to bar you from even being allowed to vote for him. Case in point, Colorado. After a judge ruled that Trump can stay on the ballot, they actually went to court to try to make sure he couldn't even be on the ballot. A liberal group filed an appeal and the case is now headed to the state's Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Brett Tolman, former U.S. attorney. Brett, they don't want these people to be -- they're all for democracy, they just don't want there to be a Republican candidate.\par \par \uc2 BRETT TOLMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: That's exactly right, Jason. I mean, this is unprecedented activism by lawyers who want to try to manipulate the law, manipulate facts, try to utilize the courts to eliminate a candidate they're afraid of. This is something that, you know, I guess many of us might have speculated would happen if someone was resentful enough and fearful enough of a potential candidate.\par \par \uc2 But we are a republic. We have checks and balances on our government. We have a former president, and President Trump, who has a record that shows a stronger economy, a much more peaceful international scene because of his efforts. You have a track record that flies in the face of what Biden has been doing.\par \par \uc2 And so, what they're left with are words, and are, you know, rhetoric to try to convince those and to be afraid of Trump. And if they're afraid of him, then maybe they'll be able to eliminate him as their political rival.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Well, that's their playbook. They keep going to it. You know, the problem is we have four years, like you said, of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , where the world was safer. We were safer at home. The border was closed. We didn't have the rapid inflation. We didn't have two wars going on. And yet, these people go up and will actually say out loud on camera, that if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is reelected, that suddenly they're going to be concentration camps.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's so offensive. Millions of people were killed in concentration camps by, you know, in what happened with the Nazis. And they're out there trying to compare him to having concentration camps. It's just so infuriating.\par \par \uc2 TOLMAN: Yeah, Jason, you're right. The hyperbole and the rhetoric that's being used is really actually dangerous rhetoric. It's the stuff that wants to divide the country. You know, they compare and contrast him. You know, I saw a reference to him being like an insurrectionist like, you know, General Robert E. Lee that led an army against the Union.\par \par \uc2 The irony of that is \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 , the president at the time, was trying to unify this country. He brought his rivals into his cabinet. Instead, we see the leadership of the Democrats wanting to put their rivals in jail, or to prosecute them, or to malign them to such a degree to divide this country.\par \par \uc2 It's sad. There's little leadership on the left. And what I see of, you know, the former party that wanted to unify this country, there's no semblance of that left.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah. It's amazing. The people that preach the tolerance, they're usually the least tolerant among us. Brett, wonderful Thanksgiving for you and your family. Appreciate you joining us.\par \par \uc2 All right. Biden's foreign policy may cost him the next election. And the government issues a new warning for you and your Turkey. Raymond Arroyo has all the details in Seen and Unseen, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: It's time for Seen and Unseen, where we reveal the story behind the headlines. For that, we turn to Fox News contributor Raymond Arroyo. Raymond, I hear there was some commotion at the White House this week.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Jason, we were covering the turkey pardon for Fox and Friends, which will be airing tomorrow morning. I'll be joining them. But when I left the White House, we encountered a group of pro-Palestinian protesters outside the White House. They were less than happy with the president and his foreign policy. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We want ceasefire now. We want to stop this genocide.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: What would you say to those who say \uc2 Hamas\uc2  provoked this?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: That's absolutely lie. There is no evidence that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacked.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Israel killed their own people?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: The decision makers like the fool in this office that decides that it's OK to kill civilians. He's a war criminal himself.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: If \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is a terrorist organization, then \uc2 IDF\uc2  is a terrorist organization. \uc2 IDF\uc2  is a bigger terrorist organization if you ask me.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You believe the Israeli retaliation to the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack is a genocide?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: It is. Let's not even talk about October 7th, because what about October 8th, October 9th, October 10th, October 11th? Let's keep going.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: There are over 6,000 children that have been genocide, massacred by our own president here. He's the one that's funding Israel and backing them up and giving them the green light to kill us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You know, Jason, you watch this, and when I heard those folks and you listen to the protests, it is a poisonous idea, you know, invoking history, whether real or imagined, to justify barbarity, and then turn the tables and say, no, you're practicing genocide here.\par \par \uc2 Well, the Israelis didn't ask for their civilians, women and children, to be slaughtered in the morning, unprovoked. That's what happened here. And you see this reaction. But they are angry. And they're angry at this president.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, it's interesting to see the divide among the Democrats and what's going on there. But that lady, she did not want to talk about October 7th, that's for sure.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yeah. No, no, that's what I'm saying. They just dismiss the barbarity. And how about, well, didn't \uc2 Hamas\uc2  start this? And she said, no, it's a lie. Oh, OK. The latest polling shows that Biden's support among Arab Americans, by the way, has fallen to 17 percent. That's a 42- percentage point drop from 2020.\par \par \uc2 Now, I talked to some of these protesters about why they're turning on the president and who they'll support next time. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Did you vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Unfortunately, I did, and that was the biggest mistake of my life.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Will you vote for him again?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Never.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Did you support President Biden in the last election?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I did, and I will never, never support him again.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Did you support President Biden in the last election, and will you support him now?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: No, I will not support him. Last time, yes, I did.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Why not this time?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I will not vote for Biden. I will not vote for anyone who supports genocide.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Did you support President Biden in the last election?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I did, unfortunately. I did.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And are you going to support me?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Hell no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)]\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Hell no. Yeah, look, Jason, as you know better than I, Virginia, Michigan, there are key states where you have a large Arab population. They are not happy with this president, and they may stay home this time out.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, I don't know that they have anybody on their ballot except, you know, \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2 , I guess, is their hero, but I don't know where they go.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, and as the White House is losing support with Arab Americans, the president seems more interested in speaking to Americans that are too young to vote.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: And by the way, I like kids better than people. And I love your ears. I love them. They're really cool. What's your name?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Catherine.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Catherine, what a beautiful name. That's my mommy's name. Well, nice to see you. How old are you, 17?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: She's six.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Six.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You know, Jason, anytime he's around children, it's always awkward, uncomfortable and weird. I mean, who talks to children that way?\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: It's a little scary, a little spooky because there's so many videos like that. But Raymond, I hear the government is urging the public to be cautious with their meal preparations this Thanksgiving. What are they telling us?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, Jason, they should have issued a warning to those children about Biden coming close. But the \uc2 U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  issued their annual public service announcement about the dangers of frying a turkey. This time they set it to music.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, you know, Jason, look, here's the reality. These accidents of home fires, kitchen fires, they rise three times during the holidays -- three times as much during this holiday season. That's mostly due to these friers. So beware, keep your eyes on those friers and don't overfill them, ladies and gentlemen. Happy Thanksgiving. Hope it saves you.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Oh, good Thanksgiving to you as well, Raymond. All right, that's it for tonight. I thanks to Laura Ingraham for allowing me to sit in. I want to give a quick plug to my book, the Puppeteers, the people who control the people that control America. It's good holiday gift. I'm just saying. Thanks for watching. Kaylee's in next for Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020231123ejbm00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Meets With \uc2 UAW\uc2 ; Interview With Rep. \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2  (R- PA); Israel Agrees To Four-Hour Humanitarian Pauses; Fireworks At Republican Debate; Interview With Georgia Secretary Of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 ; Interview With Gov. \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  (R-NH); Interview With Former U.S. Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 ; U.S. Issues New Warning To Iran; Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  Not Seeking Reelection\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Mark Meredith, Mike Tobin, Madison Alworth, Chad  Pergram, Jennifer Griffin, Jacqui Heinrich \par \uc2 8,549 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: The U.S. senator who is leaving and the U.S. fighter strikes that keep on coming, FOX on top of both very fast-moving developments.\par \par \uc2 First, U.S. fighter jets striking an Iranian weapons storage facility in Syria again, the growing call this time to respond harder to at least 46 Iranian-sponsored attacks on U.S. soldiers, this time to expand the counterattacks, not to proxies of Iran outside Iran, but Iran itself and inside Iran.\par \par \uc2 We have got FOX team coverage with Jennifer Griffin on the growing Iranian threat, Jacqui Heinrich on the U.S. ramping up its response to that threat and former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  on if enough is being done to counter that threat.\par \par \uc2 Then, \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  calls it quits, won't seek reelection as a senator, but hinting he could be setting his sights on something a little higher.\par \par \uc2 Busy day, busy show, and it all starts now.\par \par \uc2 All right. Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Glad to have you.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Jennifer Griffin first at the Pentagon on this precision airstrike and what it might mean as to what comes next -- Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Neil, a pair of American F-15s struck an Iranian weapons storage facility last night, the second U.S. airstrike in two weeks following 46 attacks on U.S. bases since mid-October.\par \par \uc2 But Iran and its proxies apparently are not getting the message or being deterred.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH, PENTAGON DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: Since our strike last night, we did see four additional attacks against our troops at varying bases in Iraq and Syria. But I can't predict what other attacks could be coming.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Pentagon Deputy Press Secretary Sabrina Singh says there are now 56 U.S. service members who have reported injuries since October 17, half of them traumatic brain injuries.\par \par \uc2 Three U.S. troops received minor injuries at Green Village in Syria last night from a multirocket attack. Today in Iraq, U.S. troops traveling in an armored personnel carrier hit a roadside bomb, an IED, near Mosul. There were no U.S. injuries.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. has not yet responded to the downing of a U.S. MQ-9 Reaper drone yesterday off the coast of Yemen by Iranian-backed Houthis. The $32 million surveillance drone went down over the Red Sea. The U.S. airstrikes on that weapon storage facility in Maysalun, Eastern Syria, targeted a weapon storage facility used by Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps, the same drones and rockets used to constantly target U.S. troops and bases in Iraq and Syria.\par \par \uc2 No one was killed in the U.S. airstrike, according to the Pentagon. The intent of the Iranian proxies is different, according to a senior military official who briefed reporters after last night's strike -- quote -- "They're taking shots at what they believe to be very large numbers of U.S. personnel, with the intent of killing them."\par \par \uc2 Many of the rockets and drones fall short of their target or are shot down by U.S. forces. Pentagon officials said they have ramped up the defense of U.S. bases in Iraq and Syria, sending Patriot missile defense batteries and other defensive capabilities -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jennifer Griffin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So what is the administration's attack plan on these constant attacks on U.S. soldiers?\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 The president said the U.S. will strike back again if U.S. forces continue to come under attack, but he didn't say whether his response so far has been adequate enough, especially given the four new attacks on U.S. forces since Biden ordered the U.S. retaliatory strike last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Mr. President, are the retaliatory strikes working?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes. I mean, they're working in the sense that we're hitting the target.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: We're now up to 46 proxy attacks on U.S. forces since October 17, with the U.S. responding twice.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon is still doing a battle damage assessment to determine whether any IRGC members or proxy forces were killed or injured in the latest U.S. response. But the National Security spokesman -- National Security Council spokesman -- excuse me -- defended the U.S. against criticism that Biden's response so far has been too weak.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: I mean, we have sent a strong signal not just to Iran, but to other actors in the region, that we don't want to see this conflict with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  widen or deepen.\par \par \uc2 And thus far, so far, we haven't seen that happen. We haven't seen another actor jump all in to try to help \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in their fight against Israel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Meantime, the president spoke today to tensions between the U.S. and Israel on its response in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 The president announced, the White House announced that Israel has embraced humanitarian pauses that the president has been calling for. They're going to be happening daily, we're told a pause of four to five hours each day, and they will be announced about three hours in advance.\par \par \uc2 But this took a lot of convincing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Mr. President, are you frustrated with Prime Minister Netanyahu that he has not listened more to some of the things you have asked him to do?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Taken a little longer than I hoped.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The president confirmed reports that he did ask Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  to embrace a three-day pause in fighting in order to get the hostages out.\par \par \uc2 And he added that he has asked Netanyahu for an even longer pause for some parts of that, but he did not elaborate on what that meant. And officials here maintain that the U.S. is still not for a general cease-fire, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui at the White House.\par \par \uc2 To \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  right now, the former defense secretary under \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr., Secretary, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 Do you think we're doing enough to counter these constant attacks by Iranian-based proxies, 46 to date?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 , FORMER U.S. DEFENSE SECRETARY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 No, Neil, I don't think we're doing enough. As you just said, 46 attacks. We have responded twice. To me, the metric is not what the -- President Biden said, that we're hitting the target. The metric by which you measure success is, are we deterring them? And, clearly, we are not deterring them.\par \par \uc2 I think you have to increase the frequency and the scale of the bombings, the counterstrikes we impose upon them. And, at some point, they will stop. And, to me, at that point is when you have restored deterrence. So, to me, that's the metric.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I just wonder, Secretary, if I'm Iran, right, and I know that there's great reluctance and anxiety in the U.S. to expand this to hitting Iran itself, then I'm going to continue working through proxy. And I don't care if a tent gets blown up in a desert or whatever. It's not a tent in my country. So I'm going to keep doing what I'm doing, aren't I?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Yes, look, they don't care, obviously, because we attacked them last night, and there were four more attacks in the hours that followed.\par \par \uc2 So, in -- I assume that, in their mind, that the -- net-net, it's good for them to continue what they're doing. It shows faith to the cause. It shows support for \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . It continues to chip away at our credibility.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I get concerned that, if we don't respond more forcefully and more frequently, that they will misinterpret that, and, at some point, they may push \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  to enter the fight or support \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's actions. And then that is going to be -- that will be an escalation that could have been deterred. But, right now, I just don't think we're pushing back forcefully enough.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is that third rail actually avoiding attacking Iran itself? It's OK we go after the proxies, maybe destabilize them, but by no means attack Iran, that that would lead, to here some tell it, to World War III.\par \par \uc2 Would it? What do you think would happen if we did that, Secretary?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Well, it would not lead to World War III, that's for sure.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I don't know what's in the administration's mind right now. I don't think we want a front with Iran at this point in time. I don't think we want to make sure that cover, support...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, why? Why, though, Secretary? They're the ones fighting with us.\par \par \uc2 They know that economic sanctions aren't going to anything.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: No, I understand.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They're sitting on tens of billions of dollars worth of cash. So, even if you froze cash outside of Iran, they have got tens of billions of dollars, I believe $28 billion, at the ready now.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: I think we want to give Israel space to do what it needs to do to eliminate, defeat \uc2 Hamas\uc2  first. What we don't want to do is open another front in the north and another front in the east with Iran.\par \par \uc2 That said, I do think -- I have been arguing this for weeks -- we should be talking with our allies and partners about what happens at that point in time when we're prepared to take on Iran and for once and for all take care of this problem, beginning with diplomacy, financial sanctions, economic sanctions, and the threat or use of military force as need be.\par \par \uc2 But that -- I don't think now is the time to open that front. That said, I don't also believe that responding forcefully to these attacks by Iran and its proxies is going to lead to that either. They don't want a conflict. And I just don't think we have achieved deterrence yet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Secretary, Dr., 