ded into a reelection bid that has happened before for presidents who have then been reelected. So, obviously, a poll is just a snapshot in time.\par \par \uc2 That is also, though, the why of Manchin of it all is concerning to Democrats. Democrats have been very up front about the fact that a third- party bid for a Manchin worries them, especially if that bid is on a No Labels ticket. Now, No Labels is the bipartisan dark money group, doesn't disclose their donors, but are trying to get on ballots across the country.\par \par \uc2 They actually held a call with reporters yesterday saying that they were on 12 ballots, 12 state ballots across the country, the most you could be on so far, and they are on track to be on all 50.\par \par \uc2 If Manchin, for some reason, becomes the nominee for that party -- we don't know whether that will be the case. He's flirted with running for president, and they have certainly not tamped down any speculation around whether he was going to be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 If he is on that ballot, Democrats are very concerned and they have been up front about that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Then to say nothing of \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , who is polling at 24 percent...\par \par \uc2 MERICA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... as a possible third-party candidate, if he gets on a lot of state ballots. He's going to have a tougher road there. But it's a reminder it could be a crazy Election Day event, right?\par \par \uc2 MERICA: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 You don't really know what's going to happen. And if you listen to Manchin's video, he announces his retirement about halfway through, and the second half, he talks all about national issues, why Americans need to take back their country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MERICA: All messages you would hear in a presidential campaign, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Dan, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 The Messenger national politics reporter Dan Merica.\par \par \uc2 