ery fluid and dynamic.\par \par \uc2 There have been three-way talks with the CIA, \uc2 Israel's Mossad\uc2  and the Qatari prime minister, and they're talking about a substantial cease-fire in exchange for hostages. The Israeli leadership has said time and again there won't be any cease-fire without release of the hostages.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Islamic Jihad, not \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , has put a couple of the hostages in front of cameras. They said things denouncing the Israeli leadership. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  called it the worst form of psychological terrorism. And Islamic Jihad says they will be released when appropriate measures are met.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the exodus of Palestinians continues, as it has now substantially for five days, when there is a window, a four-hour window in which the humanitarian corridors open up. That's a main drag, Salah al-Din, that stretches from the north to the south of the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 Now that four-hour pause is going to be backed by the United States.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We understand that Israel will begin to implement four-hour pauses in areas of Northern Gaza each day, with an announcement to be made three hours beforehand.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have been told by the Israelis that there will be no military operations in these areas over the duration of the pause and that this process is starting today.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Now, Israel is still bringing tremendous pressure from the ground forces and from the air.\par \par \uc2 Just after sundown tonight, we saw a substantial airstrike go in, substantial sustained airstrike going into Beit Hanoun, which is in the northeast corner of the Gaza Strip here. And Beit Hanoun has been subject to fighting and combat really from the first day that Israeli forces went into the ground -- went in on the ground almost two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 Now, to the very south, far south of the country and the coast of the Red Sea, the town of Eilat was the subject of a missile strike. The air defense took out the missile but a drone got through the defenses and struck the town of Eilat. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  won't say where these particular attacks originated from at this point, but in the past we have seen the attacks that target Eilat, they have been fired by the Houthi rebels and they come up the Red Sea, the Iran-backed Houthi rebels -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It doesn't end.\par \par \uc2 Be safe, Mike. Thank you, \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  in Israel on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Congressman \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2  of Pennsylvania and what he makes of this four-hour pause. It's going to be lasting a while.\par \par \uc2 What do you think, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BRIAN FITZPATRICK\uc2  (R-PA): Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, it just goes to show you that Israel is trying to do the right thing here. True to their word, they said they wanted to limit to the greatest extent possible civilian casualties. They still need to defend themselves. They still need to confront \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and they still need to free the hundreds of prisoners that remain at this very moment.\par \par \uc2 So I give them credit for trying to do the right thing here. I don't think that Israel's adversaries or our adversaries would ever extend the same courtesy. But I think it's commendable that they're doing so.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In the meantime, the president has made it very clear, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , that is, that the fact of the matter is, we have got a war here. We don't want to provide any opportunities to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  here.\par \par \uc2 So, it's sort of like a Faustian choice for him. Are we pushing this on him too much?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Well, I think I think President Biden ought to make clear that his suggestion is just that.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, President Netanyahu has the responsibility to defend Israel, to defend his people. He doesn't have to listen to any dictates from any other country. He has the right and responsibility to do that. And I think he will. I think he also understands that he doesn't want to lose public opinion.\par \par \uc2 So how he goes about prosecuting this defense of his nation is very, very important to make sure the world stands behind him.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Congressman, I know you had an opportunity to speak and sit down with \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He is very concerned, as are you, sir, I know, about bad elements who might have already slipped through the border, and maybe inspired by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and doing something similar here. How real do you think that threat is?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: It's very real, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Director Wray told us never has the terrorism threat been higher in the past decade, and maybe even dating back since 9/11, than it is right now. We know that close to 200 people on the terror watch list have been interdicted at the Southern border. We know what's going on overseas with multiple wars raging right now.\par \par \uc2 And our Southern border is very porous. It's a very, very dangerous time, all the more reason why, Neil, we have to reauthorize Section 702 of FISA, which is the single greatest tool we have to protect our country, which expires at the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Congressman, thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2  of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we will keep you posted on some stunning political developments here. It may be a little bit of a surprise, may be one to the present itself, getting to meet Shawn Fain, again, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  chief. It was meant to talk about that big deal and the big pay raises that came with it for the \uc2 UAW\uc2  working with the automakers.\par \par \uc2 But if the president was expecting an outright endorsement today, he didn't get it -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, relief for the president of the United States no more auto strike, but no endorsement from the guy who pushed that strike and got what he wanted for his workers.\par \par \uc2 Mark Meredith has more from Belvidere, Illinois, with what happened today - - Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon to you from beautiful Belvidere.\par \par \uc2 The president got a very warm welcome from the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , but you're right, no formal endorsement. The union says it's still waiting for its tentative deal to be signed, sealed and delivered before talking about 2024.\par \par \uc2 The president had a lot to say to those autoworkers, but perhaps the most notable moment came as he was taking the stage. He took off his jacket, put on a big red \uc2 UAW\uc2  shirt, wore it throughout his entire speech, where he talked not only about electric vehicle productions, but also the need to raise pay in the American auto industry.\par \par \uc2 And he says he hopes more companies will follow the lead of GM, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and Ford.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, I want to thank you for your commitment to the solidarity, for exercising your right to bargain collectively.\par \par \uc2 You made this happen. That's no -- by the way, it's a bigger thing, I think, than even you all realize. You have changed -- you're changing the face of the country economically.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Speaking of the economy, the latest \uc2 New York Times\uc2  poll shows only 2 percent of voters believe that the economy is in excellent condition, with 52 percent ranking it as poor.\par \par \uc2 Among those not happy with the state of the economy, the Illinois Republican Party, which put this today, with the president visiting today. They said: "If the president's idea of success is high taxes, excessive government, debt and spending, a crime crisis and a governor putting political ambition ahead of our residents, Illinois is a great place to promote Bidenomics."\par \par \uc2 Now, the president's likely in no danger of losing the state in his reelection bid. He won by more than a million votes last cycle. But there is going to be the focus here on raising money. In fact, the president, he's already left Belvidere to go raise some money. He's out in Chicago tonight, Neil, for those fund-raisers. And he's campaigning with the governor of this state, who's been floated as a possible candidate in the years ahead -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 All right,thank you for that, Mark Meredith.\par \par \uc2 Well, the bottom line is, the auto companies could afford to pay those pretty eye-popping wage increases of 24 to 25 percent because their fossil fuel-related car business is doing just fine, not so their E.V. business. In fact, many of the big companies, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and Ford, are sort of curtailing increasing production of these vehicles because they just say the path is too aggressive and the customers aren't there.\par \par \uc2 Enter something that's interesting going on in Michigan right now, Gotion, where they were planning a battery plant. But that battery plant, E.V. battery plant, got a collective thumbs down from a lot of folks.\par \par \uc2 This person leading that charge, Lori Brock, is a Green Township, Michigan, resident opposed to the Gotion battery plant. She's not alone.\par \par \uc2 Very good to have you, Lori.\par \par \uc2 Why your opposition here? It has more to do with just the fact that it's an E.V. plant, but the fact that there's a China connection, right?\par \par \uc2 LORI BROCK, GREEN TOWNSHIP, MICHIGAN, RESIDENT: Absolutely, and the fact that they lied to us completely. There is no transparency.\par \par \uc2 They kind of just sprung this on us in March after the local officials had kept it in a private -- privacy for almost a year. And we're not going along with it. They lied to the Appropriations Committee to get money. They said that 95 percent of the community was for it, when we really didn't know anything about it.\par \par \uc2 So when we did find out about it, we have been striking, we have been petitioning, and then we had this recall. And thank God we got a all-the- way-across-the-board recall on the entire Green Charter Township Board. That just happened on Tuesday night's election.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So it's not going to happen, right? This plant is not going to happen?\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Well, I don't know that the plant won't happen. We're going to try.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: I mean, we have got control of the township now, and we're going to put into force things that will protect us, like environmental protection acts and \uc2 CFIUS\uc2  reviews and all the things that our government didn't do for us.\par \par \uc2 So we're going to continue to fight them. We have got lawsuits against them right now. We don't want them here. Ever since they came here, they have lied to us, and they have threatened us, and they have manipulated everything that they have asked for.\par \par \uc2 So we're definitely against them here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So the ones who argue for it, that it's going to create a lot of jobs, and your town needs a lot of jobs, you say?\par \par \uc2 BROCK: That's the thing. They keep saying that we need a lot of jobs.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, I went through, and went on Indeed, and went all through our job market, and there's 1,800 jobs right now that are not being taken.\par \par \uc2 So I don't know where these people -- the (INAUDIBLE) has been lying to people. There are a lot of jobs that are available that our community just isn't using right now. And the Gotion has lied. They said that they were going to be paying the people $62,500 a year.\par \par \uc2 Now that's down to $24 an hour average with the Chinese nationalists. We haven't gotten the truth from them one time. We just don't feel this is the right fit for our community. We don't like being lied to. We don't like being manipulated.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: So we fought back. And we're going to continue to fight back.\par \par \uc2 So...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 All right. We will see what happens, Lori Brock.\par \par \uc2 Now, we did reach out to the Gotion pro-factory folks. We have yet to hear back, but hope springs eternal. We will be watching this one very closely.\par \par \uc2 Also watching something that's just occurring around the block from us here in our FOX News studios. This is a pro-Palestinian protest, started at Bryant Park. It has extended to Fifth Avenue and other areas. It's closing off traffic.\par \par \uc2 New York traffic, folks, is horrific on any day. This is adding to that. So to my New Yorker friends -- by the way, that includes me -- it's going to be a nightmare getting home.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRAD RAFFENSPERGER (R), GEORGIA SECRETARY OF STATE: This is domestic terrorism, and it needs to be condemned by anyone that holds elected office and anyone that wants to hold elected office anywhere in America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Very scary stuff going on in Georgia today, officials there detailing a suspicious letter that was sent to some Fulton County election office workers. It was laced, apparently, with fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , the gentleman you just saw there, is the Georgia secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 Secretary, what happened here?\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: Haven't received the letter yet, but the postal authorities have told us to be on the lookout for it.\par \par \uc2 What happened actually out in Washington state is that several county election offices received envelopes. And several of those did have fentanyl in them. And they also know -- at the same time, they realized that one of those envelopes was actually mailed to Georgia, Fulton County election office.\par \par \uc2 So it hadn't been received yet. And perhaps it'll be picked up in the local processing facility, mail facility here in Atlanta, but it could end up in the office. So we were meeting with election officials today to prepare them for that.\par \par \uc2 And we had law enforcement there. We had great support from our governor, \uc2 GEMA\uc2 , the Georgia Emergency Management Association, and also our Department of Health. Toomey has reached out and made sure that everyone is going to be secure and making sure we have Narcan -- Narcan on -- ready if that's what it comes to.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I understand it was sent -- or the target was to send it to the Fulton County election office.\par \par \uc2 Do you know whether it was addressed to anyone in particular?\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: Don't know yet. We haven't been informed of those details.\par \par \uc2 But we just note that there is something headed towards that address. We will just see if it arrives there or if it gets intersected somewhere in the mail sorting system.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you know or does your office know whether you were a target?\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: I don't believe so, because it's -- I'm not Fulton County elections -- county front of elections.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I see.\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: So it came to that. So I don't believe I was.\par \par \uc2 But, regardless, it's really distressing, like I said earlier today, that someone would actually do this. It's domestic terrorism at its worst.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But it's coming...\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: Well, not at its worst. People weren't killed yet, but...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's coming from Washington state. Is that -- do I understand that correctly?\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: Yes, it came from the West Coast. And that's what we have been led to.\par \par \uc2 And from what I understand, they have an ongoing, a very active investigation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 Now, obviously, you start piecing things together, Secretary, and, of course, Georgia no stranger to controversy, with the recount after the Georgia election. You yourself received a lot of death threats, as did the election officials, who essentially stamped in the end after multiple recounts that Georgia did go to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It might have nothing to do with any of that. But are you curious about that?\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: Well, it is distressing also from -- you look at, back on Tuesday, in Georgia, we had a very quiet, efficient, well-run election for all 159 counties, very few issues at all. Results were posted quickly, and the winners graciously accepted their victories, and the losers graciously accepted their loss, and we moved on.\par \par \uc2 And that really was what happened virtually nationwide. There's -- the newspapers the next day, on Wednesday, were really just talking about how it was a smooth election nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, some people were excited about the results. Others were disappointed. And then this happens. Yesterday is when we were first notified. And it's just -- that's kind of like a kick in the stomach, we shall say.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: But it wasn't something that we -- obviously, we deal with it, like we have dealt with other situations.\par \par \uc2 And we hate to see that our election offices and our election workers are being targeted in this way.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Quickly, sir, you know, you have probably been following polls.\par \par \uc2 I know you don't obsess over them. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is leading by a pretty significant amount in your state right now, in four other battleground states. And it could be a very different result this time. I'm sure you're focusing on just making sure the elections are fair and there's no curious developments there, but just your thoughts on that.\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: Well, it's interesting. That poll came out. Then another poll a few days later came out, it also had Governor DeSantis on there virtually the same as President Trump.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: And then it showed Nikki Haley in one state getting 9 percent-plus and President Trump was at plus-three.\par \par \uc2 So, anyway, those are just polls. But, at the end of the day, what happens really is, people vote and make these decisions.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: And we support protecting the rights of every voter to have free, accurate, secure elections.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. And it's not a sure thing until Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , Georgia secretary of state, thank you, Secretary. Good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020231110ejb900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 3 Arrested For Operating High-End Brothel Network; New Attacks From Iran Proxies After U.S. Strikes; Devastated Families Of Hostages Pleading For Help; Now: New York College Students Stage Pro-Palestinian Walkout; New Attacks From Iran Proxies After United States Strikes; Sen. Manchin Not Running For Re-Election In 2024; Israeli PM Netanyahu Talks To Bret As War Rages; NYC Students Stage Pro-Palestinian Walkout\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Gillian Turner, Jeff Paul, Brian Brenberg,  Jennifer Griffin, Jack Keane, Bret Baier, Kellyanne Conway, Douglas Murray \par \uc2 8,277 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, Jeff Flock, for some amazing work that that organization, Tunnel to Towers, does. Thanks so much. Sandra?\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It really is. John, great to be with you. Another jam-packed show. Set your DVR, never miss us. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I'm John Roberts. We will see you again tomorrow. Right now, stand by for THE STORY with Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thanks, guys. Breaking right now, Democrat West Virginia Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  will not seek re-election, but will he seek another office? We'll talk to Bret Baier coming up and a live look now at pro-Palestinian students walking out of school and rallying to basically shut everything down for Palestine. Let's listen to this for a moment.\par \par \uc2 All right, they've got some pretty big numbers down there. We're keeping a close eye on it. We have a reporter down there. Very big crowd gathering in New York City. So, we'll keep a close watch on that situation. Also breaking right now, here's an interesting story. The Department of Justice has just announced that they have busted an alleged sex ring operating in Massachusetts, Boston area, and in Virginia that is said to have serviced many politicians, members of the military, and other prominent corporate executives. So, we're keeping a close eye on that as well. We'll bring you more as soon as we get it later this hour. That's coming up.\par \par \uc2 But first, our lead story. Iran keeps striking our bases. Remember when the president said don't? They are not listening. We have had four more attacks in the last several hours. Among them, our Al-Asad air base in Iraq. Now, 46 in total, and it seems clear that the message that we sent with the two strikes on these military outposts that are overseen by Iran proxies really has not slowed this down at all so far.\par \par \uc2 We've got more than two dozen brain injuries of varying degrees and several other minor injuries. Overall, fortunate that no one has been killed in all of these attacks and we hope that continues. So, as they continue to test us, the White House is jockeying with Qatar and Egypt trying to get a three-hour pause or four-hour pauses over the course of several days.\par \par \uc2 We're getting, you know, sort of mixed messages on how all this would work, but it would be designed to bring home hostages. Some of them reportedly under discussion right now in this deal would be American hostages. And obviously all of these distraught families want to bring their loved ones home. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LIRAN BERMAN, BROTHERS ZIV AND GALI TAKEN HOSTAGE: We are pleading you, the world, to help us, to speak to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . We have no clue what's happened to them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There's uncertainty when you're trying to fall asleep at night and then the nightmares come and you don't know, she's a woman, what's happening to her.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What is happening to her, who knows, right? So General Jack Keane is going to join us in, with his thoughts on the strategy in all of this in moments, but we begin live at the State Department with Correspondent Gillian Turner. Hi, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha, the short pauses that have been agreed to today are not sadly in exchange for any American hostages as hard as the Biden team might be pushing behind the scenes for that to happen. These short pauses are really, we are told, about getting humanitarian aid into Gaza. The State Department told us just a few moments ago they are still, however, even now, holding out a lot of hope that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  might soon release more Americans. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VEDANT PATEL, STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: The announcement that was today is about ensuring the flow of humanitarian aid as well as allowing the ability for civilians to move safely and depart areas where there might be hostilities taking place. But again, we've not, we'll continue to keep our eye on the ball when it comes to release of these hostages.\par \par \uc2 TURNER (voice-over): Now, as for the specifics, the White House confirmed a bit ago that the pauses in Northern Gaza announced today are going to last four hours at a time and that civilians on the ground will be given a three-hour warning before each pause begins. Listen.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: We've been told by the Israelis that there will be no military operations in these areas over the duration of the pause and that this process is starting today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): Now, yesterday at the G7, the Secretary of State interestingly said that these short pauses are an important first step to ultimately, the longer pauses you talked about and getting the hostages freed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We all agreed that humanitarian pauses would advance key objectives to protect Palestinian civilians, to increase the sustained flow of humanitarian assistance, to allow our citizens and foreign nationals to exit, and to facilitate the release of hostages.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Now, Martha, separately, the talks about this bigger deal that are ongoing between the U.S. and Israel on the one side, Qatar and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on the other side are continuing. We heard from Trey Yingst about an hour ago that there has been some breakthrough progress there. Nobody at the State Department is sharing any details about that this hour. They have not all along. They've been very short on information. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, Gillian, thank you very much. Let's go to Israel, to the southern part of Israel with our correspondent Jeff Paul, where thousands of Israelis are calling for more humanitarian aid into Gaza. Hi, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Martha, as Israeli forces continue their ground assault in and around Northern Gaza, very little progress has been made in the release of the more than 200 hostages that were taken back on October 7th. You see their faces on the posters, but their families have very little information about where they are, let alone how they're doing. So, tonight, here in downtown Tel Aviv, that frustration boiling over, and we were right there to capture the moments.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice-over): Thousands gathering here in downtown Tel Aviv, many of them doctors and nurses. They are pointing out that here we are more than a month into the war, and very little is known about the health condition of the innocent people who were taken hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Obviously, they want everyone to be returned home, but at the very least, they are pleading with organizations like the Red Cross to do their job, get into Gaza and help the most vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 HAGAI LEVINE, HOSTAGES AND MISSING FAMILIES FORUM: We have to realize every moment could be the last moment. So, if the Red Cross cannot help, why should it exist? What is the purpose of existence if in such humanitarian case, regardless of anything else, you don't need to be pro-Israeli, you need to be pro-human.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): Now, there are some high-level talks right now between the U.S. and Israeli officials along with Qatari officials about this possible exchange of hostages for a pause in the fighting, but the \uc2 IDF\uc2 , Martha, has been very clear there will not be a ceasefire until every single one of those hostages is back home. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Jeff, thank you very much. And with that, we go to Tel Aviv and Dr. Ophir Falk, former Foreign Policy Advisor, I should say, to Prime Minister Netanyahu. Dr. Falk, good to have you back on the program. Thank you very much. You know, we've heard from our reporters about these high-level ongoing talks between the United States and Israel and some of these other factors as well. You've got Qatar and Egypt involved. What is Israel's understanding of what is supposed to happen during these pauses and are you in favor of them?\par \par \uc2 DR. OPHIR FALK, FORMER FOREIGN POLICY ADVISOR: Well, thank you for having me, Martha. Let's make it clear there is no ceasefire and there will not be any ceasefire without the release of our hostages. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  in Israel is currently dismantling \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . And again, there will be no ceasefire without release of hostages. What we are doing, Israel is setting humanitarian corridors that enable safe passages for civilians to get out of harm's way, to get into safer zones, to go from the north to the southwest of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 And today, as well as yesterday, there were about 50,000 Gazans that got out of harm's way, all right. And Israel once again, urges Gazans to evacuate, to evacuate and go to the south where there is a safe-zones. Now, asking for a ceasefire with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is like asking for, I don't know, for a ceasefire after Pearl Harbor or after 9/11 with al-Qaeda. That's not going to happen. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  is dismantling \uc2 Hamas\uc2  right now and they will do so until \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is destroyed and we release our hostages.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let me ask you this, what does \uc2 Hamas\uc2  get out of these pauses? Because we've seen the video that you just talked about with thousands of Gazans leaving and they're holding white flags, and I've also read reports about \uc2 IDF\uc2  members going from building to building and coordinating with residents there to make sure that everyone knows that they have this opportunity to get out. So, what is the point of the pause if you're allowed to deliver humanitarian assistance and get people out without the pause?\par \par \uc2 FALK: Well, Israel is all in favor of providing humanitarian aid, providing civilians with food, water, and medical attention. Israel seeks to save civilian lives while \uc2 Hamas\uc2  seeks to kill civilians, our civilians, and their civilians. They hide behind and below their civilians while we target these terrorists. And we are going to continue to target these terrorists. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  is going to continue to dismantle \uc2 Hamas\uc2  until \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is destroyed. The day after \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , once \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is destroyed, the day after \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , we will make sure that Gaza is demilitarized, de-radicalized, and once that happens, rebuilt, and then there will be a future of hope.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, we have to go, and I know you have to go, I should say, but just very quickly, how close are we to seeing some hostages released, do you believe?\par \par \uc2 FALK: I can't elaborate on how close we are. What I can say is that the only thing that has facilitated hostage release to date is pressure, pressure on \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , massive pressure on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and that pressure is just going to grow and grow and grow until our hostages are released and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is destroyed.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much. Good to have you with us as always, Dr. Falk. We'll speak with you soon, I hope. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, General Jack Keane is going to be here moments away and we'll talk to him about the concerns about a wider war because \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has said that the reason for the assault, the barbaric assault on October the 7th, was really to shake things up in the Middle East, and that's what we have seen. So, we're going to talk to General Keane about what comes next, what their goals are, and whether or not we are doing anything to deter Iran at this point. So, we'll speak with him in moments.\par \par \uc2 We'll also go back to Bryant Park, where we're watching a fairly large gathering. When you look up and down the streets you can see that there are a lot of people down there right now. We have a reporter on the scene. Students who have walked out of class was the back story of this particular protest. They say they want to shut it down for Palestine.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We are watching this scene downtown in New York City. We have seen so many scenes like this across the country since October 7th.\par \par \uc2 In this case, right now, this is live. The students have walked out of their high school and college classrooms as part of nationwide protests to "shut it down" for Palestine.\par \par \uc2 Brian Brenberg is down there, co-host of "THE BIG MONEY SHOW" on Fox Business. And he is covering this for us this afternoon in midtown Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Brian, what can you tell us? What are you seeing down there?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BRENBERG, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK HOST: Martha, it is quite a scene down here. So, we've been here for about 20 minutes. I would say the crowd size has at least doubled in that time.\par \par \uc2 You can hear and see the chanting behind me. I'm right in front of the \uc2 New York Public Library\uc2 , and you have a mix of people here. This is supposed to be a high-school student and teacher walkout event. But you actually have, I think, a huge variety of people.\par \par \uc2 We've seen high school students, we seen college students, we seen college professors from area schools. They are here they say to shut it down. They seem to be calling for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 Although it's interesting, when I talk to people here, they have different interpretations of what shut it down mean. Some of them are talking about military relationships. Some of them are talking about what's happening in the streets around us.\par \par \uc2 There was a group earlier at \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  (PH) talking about shutting it down. So, it's a wide interpretation. But the point is, there is a ton of energy here.\par \par \uc2 This crowd is growing very fast. They have not stopped chanting since I've been here. We're trying to get some takes from folks on the ground about why they are here. I can tell you it's already getting heated. We've seen what looked like some close altercations, some calls for police.\par \par \uc2 One young man who seemed to be counter kind of getting moved out into the street. So, we'll watch it, Martha. But things are just getting heated up here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, keep us posted, Brian. We'll keep a close eye on it. We've got that live camera down there and you as well. So, we'll keep watching it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Brian Brenberg of "THE BIG MONEY SHOW", Fox Business.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Thanks, Martha. Bye, bye.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to have him in the crowd. Thank you, Brian. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, another attack against the United States on our bases in the Middle East. Today, it happened in Iraq at al-Asad. A one-way drone that was -- that was active in that area. So, we're going to keep a close eye on all of these attacks.\par \par \uc2 General Jack Keane will be here with us after the break on the appropriate action from the United States, and whether the world is on the cusp of something much wider when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The threat of a much wider conflict in the Middle East picking up steam as Iranian-backed proxies launch new attacks against American forces in the region. The total of these attacks is now at 46. And just hours after a pair of U.S. airstrikes hit a weapons facility in eastern Syria that is part of Iran's proxies, weapons caches, a senior defense official says that these militants "have an intent" to kill American personnel.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, retired four-star General Jack Keane. But first, to chief national court security correspondent Jennifer Griffin live at the Pentagon. Hi, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well since the U.S. carried out airstrikes in eastern Syria last night, there have been four new attacks by Iranian proxies against U.S. bases.\par \par \uc2 Three attacks on U.S. troops in Syria and a fourth in Iraq. A pair of F-15 struck in Iranian weapons storage facility last night, the second U.S. air strike in two weeks following 46 attacks on U.S. bases since mid-October.\par \par \uc2 But Iran and its proxies are not getting the message or being deterred. Three U.S. troops received minor injuries at Green Village in Syria last night from a multi rocket attack.\par \par \uc2 Today in Iraq, U.S. troops traveling in an armored personnel carrier hit a roadside bomb and IED near Mosul. There were no U.S. injuries were told.\par \par \uc2 Pentagon Deputy Press Secretary Sabrina Singh says there are now 56 U.S. service members who have reported injuries since October 17th. Half of them, traumatic brain injuries.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH, DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON (via telephone): What we assess is 25 of our forces or of our troop members have been diagnosed with TBI. The other additional injuries are very minor.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we're talking like headache, rolled ankle, cuts. But that is it. All have returned to duty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The U.S. has not responded yet to the downing of a U.S. MQ-9 Reaper drone yesterday off the coast of Yemen by Iranian-backed Houthis.\par \par \uc2 The $32 million surveillance drone went down over the Red Sea. The U.S. airstrikes on a weapon storage facility in Maysalun, eastern Syria targeted a weapons storage facility used by Iran Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps.\par \par \uc2 The same drones and rockets used to constantly target U.S. troops and bases. No one was killed in the U.S. air strike, according to the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Defense officials said they have ramped up the defense of U.S. bases in Iraq and Syria, sending Patriot missile defense batteries and other defensive capabilities to the U.S. troops. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jennifer, thank you very much, from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 So, let's bring in retired four-star General Jack Keane. Fox News senior strategic analyst, former vice chief of staff for the army, and the chairman of the Institute for the Study of War.\par \par \uc2 General, good to have you here. Thank you very much. You know, when I listened to Jennifer's report, I'm reminded of President Biden, staring down the camera, and saying, my message for Iran is don't. Is it wrong listening based on what we just heard?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Heck, no. I mean, Iran strategic objective in the Middle East is dominate and control it, and also take control of Persian Gulf oil.\par \par \uc2 They have had that as their aspirational goals since the early 80s. Then they have two objectives to achieve it. And they state this every single year. Drive the United States military presence out of the region. And that's what these attacks are all about -- break American will at home.\par \par \uc2 Why are we still in Iraq and Syria, is they want the American people to be asking the administration, why are we putting our troops at risk over this issue? That's what they want, and that is playing out in the United States as they speak.\par \par \uc2 Their second objective to achieve domination is to destroy the State of Israel. So, what is -- what their instrument \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is using potentially \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , the Houthis a little bit are all towards reducing Israel's influence or reducing their political, geopolitical stature inside their own country, get the people lose confidence in them, worldwide condemnation, which they're getting, as a result of it.\par \par \uc2 Isolate Israel, more geopolitically. Some of that is happening. But the second objective, the one that you are focusing on here, is to drive the United States out of the region. And these are complimentary things to Iran achieving his overall goal.\par \par \uc2 So, when the administration tries to parse them, and separate them, that is wrong. That's all part of the aspirational goal. What should we do? You have seen it. These measured deliberate attacks, they were going to continue to attack us, and they're trying to kill us, and we're going to wait until somebody gets killed. And then, we got to look that family in the face and try to explain why we didn't take tougher action? Come on, this is America here.\par \par \uc2 Here is the issue. When Iran punches us using our proxies, we've got to go back and hurt them, where they recognize that hurt. Reagan learned this in the mid-80s, after he lost the Marine barracks, two embassies, Lebanon, Kuwait, hundreds kill.\par \par \uc2 Later on, when Iranians were interfering with the flow of oil out of the Gulf. What did he do? He struck their oil platforms. This is Iran struck the IRGC bases. What happened as a result of it, they shut down.\par \par \uc2 President Trump, second president learned a lesson dealing with Iran. It took a while. But in January 2020, after the Iranians are punching at us as they are doing right now, he takes down the leader of the IRGC Quds Force who is directing those attacks and we kill him.\par \par \uc2 They came back and hit us with a measured response and shutdown. And they were back on their heels when the Biden administration takeover.\par \par \uc2 What should we do about this? We have to recognize that the tie, the connection here to the Iranian proxies is the IRGC. They train those proxies at the IRGC training center in Iran, as they had \uc2 Hamas\uc2  was in there in September. 500 plus in them training there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Practicing with the paragliders, right?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, practicing there with the paragliders. Take that facility down. Tell them that's what's going to happen., if you continue to do this, they do it one more time. Take it down.\par \par \uc2 Now, why don't they want to do that? Their concern is that's going to escalate the war, and we're going to be at a war with Iran.\par \par \uc2 I don't know. Anybody who studies this with any analytical ability would actually think that that's what's going to happen. The Iranians want no war with the United States. That is why since the early 18 -- 1980s, they have been using proxies to give them the distance from that.\par \par \uc2 They are not going to go to war with the United States. Why not? They lose their regime over that. That's not what they want. They want to drive us out of the region using their proxy. So, hit them where it hurts.\par \par \uc2 Tell them also, what's at risk is IRGC bases, as Reagan held it at risk, and their IRGC headquarters. Let these guys know that we're serious and we're going to prevent the loss of American life.\par \par \uc2 We're not going to act after the loss of American life.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, hit them where they function.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just attack their ability to carry out these kinds of attacks, set them back on their heels.\par \par \uc2 I thought this was very interesting, you know, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's goal, right was more than just this horrific brutal barbaric attack.\par \par \uc2 The barbarity of it had an intention to provoke the kind of response that we're seeing right now. And they say that they hope that the state of war with Israel will become permanent on all borders, and that the Arab world will stand with us. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes. I mean, the strategic goals here are very interesting because they focused on killing civilians and then doing it savagely. That's not what they've done in the past. The reason -- and also taking civilian hostages. The reason is, they wanted a very violent response from Israel. They knew they were going to get it.\par \par \uc2 And they weaponize their people. They exposed their people. They wanted the women and children to be killed. This is how diabolical and sinister this is. The people on the streets out here in New York City and other places, they don't understand the logic of what I'm talking about right here. This is how diabolical it is.\par \par \uc2 A government elected by the people will not protect them. Quite the contrary, they serve them up to be used as casualties. Where is the bunker complex in Gaza Strip --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: To protect them.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: To protect the civilians.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, they don't care.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Who does the government, who's there to protect them, who do they protect? They protect themselves. We got to see the truth of it.\par \par \uc2 So what did they get out of this, a positive thing. They got 