d. And I salute the Congress for censoring her.\par \par \uc2 But it's beyond that. I think the protests that you're seeing, I'm sure it includes some naive people, but there are a lot of people who know exactly what they're saying.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And he went on to say that, you know, this is a fight that is really at the heart of what Iran wants to do, and that's erase Israel. So we wanted some specific details about that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, but what about the effort to release these hostages? It almost feels like -- it's clearly it doesn't get lost but it feels like that sometimes, covering all of this is happening so, so quickly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And it's really amazing that in all the talk about humanitarian pauses and ceasefires, and all of the protests talk about occupation that there isn't a talk and a pressure on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to say, you first release all of the hostages, move forward and do that part, or where is the ceasefire on that side. That does get lost in the conversation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed. Bret, thank you very much. We'll look forward to the full interview with Prime Minister Netanyahu tonight on special report. Good to see you, Bret. I'll see you later.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. See you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, President Biden feeling the heat from all sides this week. Watch what happened at an event with the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  as they were kind of celebrating the end of that strike in Illinois just a short time ago. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Biden, you need to call for a ceasefire in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: No, no, no. Let her go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Protest in the crowd there. Another new poll showing the President trailing former President Trump in the key swing states of Arizona, Georgia, Nevada and Pennsylvania. This one has them tied in Wisconsin. It's the Emerson Survey. We saw similar results from the New York Times Siena Poll earlier this week, and the President had a slight lead in Wisconsin. So there's two polls that show that leading or very tight, tight or very tight in Wisconsin as well.\par \par \uc2 So let's bring in two very experienced pollsters to talk about all this. Kellyanne Conway, former senior counselor to President Trump, now president of KA Consulting and a Fox News contributor and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , former Clinton advisor and pollster, now CEO of \uc2 Stagwell\uc2 . Welcome to both of you.\par \par \uc2 Kellyanne, let me get your quick take first on that Emerson poll. What's your takeaway?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: This is the third poll in a week along with CNN and New York Times Siena showing former President Trump's strength against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in a cage match rematch. And I think it's pretty remarkable as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  fluctuates anywhere between 39% in one of the states to 43%. So his floor and ceiling are so dangerously close together.\par \par \uc2 The way Biden could pull it out is, if you have all these third party candidates and they're somehow taking away more from Trump and Biden. And, of course, as we found out in 1992 when \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  was a formidable third party challenger, it doesn't matter how popular you are. And in his case, if you get 19% of the popular vote nationwide, if you get, as he did, zero electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 So the question for many of these challengers, Jill Stein, RFK Jr., Cornel West, maybe some crypto or tech billionaire none of us are thinking of, Martha. The question for them is, which of the states could you actually win? Not could you be the spoiler but which of the states could you actually win so that you're taking electoral votes away from one of them.\par \par \uc2 One last point, I think what's remarkable in all three of these polls, and they come from outlets, \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , Siena College, Emerson College, CNN, none of which one \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to win again, let's be frank. They show that Biden is really struggling against Trump among working class voters and among young people. Those are two. And, of course, you see the Democratic Party is eating itself alive over this Israel-Gaza-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  issue, so you put all of that toxic brew together. And it's very difficult right now for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to cobble together the constituencies that helped him win in the first place.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very interesting. So, Mark, you look at these numbers and as Kellyanne points out, there's a stack of them now that seems to be in the same direction. But it's also -- it just feels difficult to buy some of this because you look at the last several elections, and Republicans have felt good going into them and haven't seen the success that is spelled out potentially in these polls.\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 STAGWELL\uc2 : Look, the Harris poll has shown Trump ahead for the last couple of months nationally, so I'm not surprised by these swing state polls. But there was a lot of territory between here and there. Look, I think on Israel, actually, Biden has more support there. It's about by ask are you for Israel or \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ? It's about 85 to 16, 15. And so, he has more support.\par \par \uc2 And if I asked you to prove it more a majority approves of his Israel policy. So actually, he is affecting swing voters with it. What Biden has to do, though, is address inflation, immigration, crime. Those core issues that are affecting how people are living their day to day lives. And he's got a year to do that. And as I know from working in 1995 and '96 with President Clinton, a year is a long time politically.\par \par \uc2 So he's in hot water now but I think there is a path to whether or not that's going to be deeper hot water or whether or not who's going to be comfortably ahead. And remember I wouldn't worry about these polls that show young people defecting because, look, against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , trust me, they are going to come back to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We will see. Kellyanne Conway, \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , thank you very much. Great to have both of you with us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So coming up, we have connected with author Douglas Murray, who is covering the war in Israel right now after this scary scene while he was reporting on the front lines.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS MURRAY, AUTHOR: So that -- incoming, incoming.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's safe, Douglas.\par \par \uc2 PIERS MORGAN, JOURNALIST: Which way was that rocket coming from? Is it coming from Gaza or from Israel?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Yes, seem to be coming from Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Let's check back in on the student walkout that is going on in New York as part of a nationwide protest to show -- to "shut it down for Palestine" as the name of it. Brian Brenberg, co-host of the "Big Money Show" on Fox Business joins us now. Hi, Brian.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BRENBERG, FOX BUSINESS HOST: Hey there, Martha. Yes, things have changed pretty significantly since I just last spoke to you. We've got police barricades now. I'm looking right at police standing right behind me. The crowd has probably tripled since I last saw you, so things are growing here fast.\par \par \uc2 We've got, again, we've seen more high school students show up now but we have a lot of college professors. It's been really interesting to try to have conversations. The conversations are not coming easily. It is loud. It is a lot of shouting. I talked to one gentleman to try to get his perspective on what happened on October 7th.\par \par \uc2 He said I don't even want to talk to you about it. He called \uc2 Hamas\uc2  freedom fighters. He said your question is biased. I'm not interested in the conversation. So it's been really interesting to try to have the conversation.\par \par \uc2 But I got to tell you just the energy here is very, very high. And it just seems like things are going to grow more before they die down, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Brian, I mean that that's one of the questions, right? How much do people understand about what happened in on October the 7th? And you're basically saying it doesn't -- the people you've spoken to say it doesn't matter to them?\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: You know what's fascinating, Martha, I'll tell you this. They don't want -- nobody wants to talk about October 7th. They want to talk about the next day and the next day because they want to talk about what's happening in Gaza now. So there's almost a, I don't want to call it an outright denial, but it's just they don't want to go to October 7th. They're not even sure that the numbers that we got for October 7th in terms of Israeli lives last are true.\par \par \uc2 So this is really not about October 7th at all, it's about what's happened after. And there's an unwillingness to talk about. That to me is what's really standing out here. We're not even having a conversation on the same plane.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It feels like that a lot lately. Brian, thank you very much. Brian Brenberg in Midtown at the Shut It Down for Palestine rally. My next guest is journalist who has been reporting live from Israel. And we showed you a clip before and there were rockets flying over his head. Douglas Murray joins us now, Fox News contributor, National Review Institute fellow, and he is in Israel right now. Good to have you with us. Thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 I watched some of your reporting the other day when you did have rockets flying over your head. Tell me a little bit about -- I know you've been in these situations before. Unfortunately, it's not something new for you but you want to cover the story from the ground. Tell me about that.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: That's right. Well, actually, as you just heard, I mean, one of the obscenities of this conflict has been the way in which the completely unprovoked \uc2 Hamas\uc2  massacre of the 7th of October has been just passed over. And you see these protests around the world shamefully in my own city of New York and elsewhere, of people pretending that it was the Israelis who somehow called it upon themselves and now retaliating. And everyone is on the question of the Israeli retaliation.\par \par \uc2 And I think this is just obscene. You saw just in the background there, Bryant Park, somebody with a placard saying "Free All the Palestinian Prisoners." Well, sorry. The Palestinian prisoners in Israeli jails are there because they stabbed Israelis or shot Israelis, or plotted to blow up Israelis or parts of terror groups. And these people walk around Bryant Park saying release them. It's like going around saying let's release everyone implicated in 911 in America. It's absolutely obscene.\par \par \uc2 And I've been down to the Gaza-Israel border, I've been watching the shelling. There's rockets coming all ways. At the moment I'm in Tel Aviv two nights ago in Tel Aviv once again, you know, just during a normal dinner. Three times we had to go down to the bomb shelter. That's in Tel Aviv. You very often hear the crump of rockets falling not far from where I am into the sea here, fired from the Gaza. It seems to have alleviated a little today because I think \uc2 Hamas\uc2  now so preoccupied with fighting in what they regard as being their own terrain in the Gaza that even \uc2 Hamas\uc2  have stopped firing rockets as much as they have been.\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is this amazing organization that can claim to be running out of water, electricity and everything else apart from rockets.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, they've said they don't really care about providing water and electricity, it's about shaking things up. You can't see it but right now, in this protest, Susan Sarandon is there. And she's, you know, preaching to the crowd as the Hollywood movie star.\par \par \uc2 And I know that you witnessed the video of October 7th, the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Go Pro and the surveillance video. I watched it as well here at the Israeli consulate. What would you like to tell these people in Bryant Park right now, Douglas, about that experience? And what impact has it had on your reporting?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, I was also in one of the kibbutz yesterday, Nir Oz. It was had 400 people living in it, more than 80 were -- was stolen, including children. Another 40 or so were murdered. Old men burnt alive in their homes. Children massacred where they were. The world hasn't spent very much time focusing on that.\par \par \uc2 But I say one thing to Susan Sarandon and all of the other adults who are doing this. They think in their weird warped view of the world in which they know almost nothing, that they are somehow on the side of the sort of anti-Nazis, Antifa, anti-fascism on the side of the underdog. And I want to tell them something very clearly. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has said it will do October the 7th again and again, and again, which means that they will not stop till they have wiped out every Jew.\par \par \uc2 Now, here's the challenge to Susan Sarandon and her friends. They think they're being anti-Nazi. If they had their way, and I hope to God they never do, if they had their way, they will wake up the next morning, they will realize they were the Nazis. They were the SS, they were the apologists for the Holocaust in real time. I hope they never have to learn that lesson but they would have it coming if they ever had their wishes, which thankfully, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  and others will make sure they never do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Strong words from Douglas Murray, who is reporting live from Israel this week. Thank you very much, Douglas. Good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: It's a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So that is THE STORY as we continue our coverage. We can take a live shot here at Bryant Park right now where the crowd is gathering. Brian Brenberg has told us that the take on what's going on in the world very different from these protesters, from what you just heard from Douglas Murray, who is live on the scene in Israel watching it all.\par \par \uc2 So we continue our coverage of this entire story here on THE STORY, and we thank you very much for joining us this afternoon as we watch the crowd gather here. We will see you back here tomorrow. Have a good evening, everyone.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020231110ejb900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Threat From Iranian Proxies to U.S. Forces?; Interview With Sen. \uc2 Jerry Moran\uc2  (R-KS); Protests Continue Over Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  War; \uc2 UAW\uc2  Reaches Historic Agreement With Big Three Automakers; Will Republicans Separate Israel and Ukraine Aid?; Interview With Rep. Scott Perry (R-PA); White House Holds Daily Press Briefing\b0\par\par\uc2 Jennifer Griffin, Alex Hogan, Peter Doocy, Alexis McAdams, Grady  Trimble, Aishah Hasnie, Mike Tobin, Sandra Smith \par \uc2 9,726 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: And with a lot of hard work and continuing to build on issues of trust and being able to verify what's in these trucks, we're confident that we can get there in coming days.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: James in the back, you have the last question.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: That's very kind. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Two questions. First, on the hostages, more than three weeks have passed now since the original \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack and the seizure of hostages. How many Americans have you confirmed in that time are actually hostages?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Less than 10.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: That's not a number. I want a number from you.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Less than 10.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Why can't you give a number?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: James, I have given you an answer. What's your next question?\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: You have given an answer. You haven't been responsive, let the record show.\par \par \uc2 The other question has to do with Israeli war aims. The Israelis have said that their objective -- and they have been very explicit about it -- is to destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . From you and other administration officials, we hear that the objective is to ensure that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  can never terrorize Israel again.\par \par \uc2 Those are two different objectives. Are you having a problem with the Israelis in terms of a clear communication here of their objectives or have their objectives, to your eyes and ears, appeared to change over time?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: No, and no.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: So, how does -- does the United States support the goal of destroying \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We support what Israel is trying to do to protect their citizens from the threat of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, James, I think you know this. We aren't on the ground fighting in this war. There's no intent to do that. This is -- these are Israeli military operations. They get to decide what their aims and strategy are. They get to decide what their tactics are. They get to decide how they're going to go after \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We're doing everything we can to support them, including providing our perspectives, including asking them hard questions about their aims and their strategy and the kind of questions we'd ask ourselves. But the question you asked me is a question better put to the \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thanks, everybody.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Thanks, Admiral.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Thank you, Admiral.\par \par \uc2 Just because of the events happening on the South Lawn, we don't have that much time.\par \par \uc2 Zeke, you just want to...\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE) to Israel this week, they're seeking spending offsets. That's also moving to decouple that from Ukraine and on the other supplemental request from the administration.\par \par \uc2 What's the White House position that? Would the White House -- would the president's sign an Israel-only supplemental request, or is the White House still insisting that they all move together?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: So, Look, just I do want to do a little bit of a lay-down here, because this is incredibly important.\par \par \uc2 When you think about \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and you think about Putin, they represent different threats, right? And both want to annihilate a neighboring democracy. And we have been very clear about that. And so we can't afford to let that happen. And that's what we have been very clear about. So making sure both Israel and also Ukraine succeed in their fights for democracy against terrorism and tyranny, it is vital.\par \par \uc2 It is vital to America's own national security. So we must help innocent civilians devastated by Putin's war in Ukraine and the conflict Israel -- and between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , including by providing lifesaving humanitarian assistance in Gaza. That is important as well.\par \par \uc2 And there is a bipartisan consensus. We have seen it. We have seen a bipartisan consensus in the Senate. You have heard me say how the director of OMB and also our Office of Leg Affairs have been giving briefings in a bipartisan way to members of both the Senate and the House.\par \par \uc2 And it is important. We see a strong majority of Republicans and Democrats in the House and the Senate, as I just mentioned, who both have been very vocal on the record. Just last week, I listed out a bunch of House members, a few House members and senators, and quoting them and saying what they have said on the record in supporting -- in supporting both Ukraine and Israel, Israel supplemental, this urgent national security needs.\par \par \uc2 And so that's what we want to see. There's no reason, there's no reason why that should not happen. There's no reason why we, as America, the United States of America, cannot make sure that Israel and also Ukraine has the funding that they need.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: And is the president open to considering any spending offsets for that funding?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: So, look, I'm certainly not going to get into hypotheticals from here. But what I can say is...\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE) Republicans are calling for that, right?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: No, I understand that. I hear you.\par \par \uc2 What we have been very clear about is that we want to see both move forward. We want to see both of the requests that we have made in the supplemental for the national security, we want to see both move forward, because it is vital, vital as those two countries are fighting, fighting for their democracies.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: But the spending offsets, is that on the table?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Look, these are emergency funding requests, right? We have been very clear about that. Like other emergency funding that Congress has passed with bipartisan support, they do not require offsets. They just don't. They don't require offset, what we suggested, what we asked for, because they are emergency requests.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Well, I am telling you that they don't require offset. These are emergency -- emergency requests. That's not how it goes.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, I haven't called on you.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thanks, Karine.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about the new executive actions that the administration is taking place in response to antisemitic threats. I saw the list, the DOJ involved, Homeland Security involved, \uc2 CISA\uc2  involved.\par \par \uc2 Given the skyrocketing number of threats, is all of that going to be enough?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: So, look, we have been very clear. The president has been very, very focused, not just now, but since the beginning of his administration on dealing with -- and unequivocal, right, when we talk about hate and there not being room for hate against Jews, not being room for hate against Muslims.\par \par \uc2 And this is something that the president has been certainly steadfast on. Look, the announcement that we make -- we made, we're going to have DOJ and other agencies certainly be part of this, have conversations with the campuses and universities, and to see what else -- this is the beginning -- to see what else.\par \par \uc2 Maybe there will be other things that are needed. But this is how the president is showing how important this is, how critical this is, and how we have to take this very seriously.\par \par \uc2 Just to list out a couple of things, the second gentleman today, as well as the education secretary, \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2  and other Biden/Harris administration officials, will discuss action the Biden administration, Biden/Harris administration, is taking to counter the alarming uptick in reported instances of antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 So they're going to have those conversations with the Conference of Presidents of Major American Jewish Organizations. That's important. We're going to continue to have other conversations here on the senior level. As well, later this week, you have Secretary Cardona and Domestic Policy Adviser Neera Tanden will attend a site visit at a university and hold a roundtable with Jewish students.\par \par \uc2 And in recent weeks, the Department of Education has conducted site visits in San Francisco, in St. Louis and in Maine to address and learn more about the antisemitism at schools and college campuses. And so that, we're going to continue to hear directly from these Jewish organizations.\par \par \uc2 We want -- I said at the top, if folks have concern, I have listed out how they should be able to flag those concerns for us. And so we believe what we announced just today is incredibly important. It's just a first step.\par \par \uc2 And there is no place for antisemitism. There is no place for it.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: And does the White House believe that college administrators are doing enough to deal with this problem?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: That is for them to speak to.\par \par \uc2 Honestly, what we have been very equivocal about, antisemitism has no place, has no place in this country, no -- there's no hate. There's no place or no room for hate. We have been very, very clear about that. I would refer you to them to speak to what they're doing on campuses.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: OK.\par \par \uc2 And this just recently happened, so you may not have any information about it yet, but there was an individual who was arrested for making antisemitic threats against a U.S. senator. Do you have any information?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: That is the first time I'm hearing of it. Obviously, we're going to condemn any form of hate, including antisemitism, including hate against Muslim community, Islamophobia. We have been very clear about that.\par \par \uc2 We will denounce any form of hate, any form of violence, and we will continue to do that from here. I have just not -- haven't heard -- I have not heard -- This is the first time I'm hearing about that, particularly incident.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Thank you, Karine.\par \par \uc2 Does President Biden think the anti-Israel protesters in this country are extremists?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: What I can say is what -- we have been very clear about this. When it comes to antisemitism, there is no place. We have to make sure that we speak against it very loud and be very clear about that.\par \par \uc2 Remember, what the president decided to -- when the president decided to run for president is what he saw in Charlottesville in 2017, when he saw neo-Nazis marching down the streets of Charlottesville with vile antisemitic just hatred. And he was very clear then, and he's very clear now. He's taken actions against this over the past two years.\par \par \uc2 And he's continued to be clear. There is no place, no place for this type of vile and despite -- this kind of rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: We hear you guys, though, talk about extremists all the time. It is usually about MAGA extremists. So what about these protesters who are making Jewish students feel unsafe on college campuses? Are they extremists?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I have been very, very clear. I have been very, very clear. We are calling out any form of hate, any form of hate.\par \par \uc2 It is not acceptable. It should not be acceptable here. And we are going to continue to call that out. And let me be very clear. This is a president that has continued to have that fight in this office, in this administration when he repealed Trump's Muslim ban on his very first day in office. That is something that this president did.\par \par \uc2 He also established an inter-policy committee to counter Islamophobia, antisemitism, and related forms of bias and discrimination. We have taken this very, very, very seriously from the president all the way on down.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Does President Biden look at these anti-Israel protests on college campuses and think it's nice to see that the country's youth are so involved, or does he think the next generation is doomed?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Here's the thing. There's no place for hate in America.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But I'm curious what he thinks.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: No, no, no, I'm telling you, there's no place for hate in America.\par \par \uc2 And we condemn any antisemitic threat or incident in the strongest terms. And you heard me say at the top I gave a message to students who are feeling, who are feeling under threat right now, right? We're tracking these threats very closely. We are there for them.\par \par \uc2 No student should have to be able to go to class, live on campus in fear. And these incidents, these reported antisemitic incidents at schools and on campuses, that should not be. We have to condemn them. We have to condemn them.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Karine, can you tell us more about the president's meeting...\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, the White House wrapping its briefing there.\par \par \uc2 We are hearing from \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  taking questions from reporters in the room, including our own Peter Doocy, asking, are those anti-Israel protesters, are they extremists? She did not answer that question, although she did go on to confirm the president and the White House stands on their support for Israel and against all antisemitism that we are seeing pop up in this country coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 She also confirmed that senior administration officials are indeed going to be meeting with Jewish leaders and universities to discuss what is happening on college campuses in America right now, and also John Kirby, the NSC coordinator who also briefed in that room just a short time ago, talking about the incident at the airport in Russia, when a plane was landing from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 It was rushed with people chanting "Kill the Jews." He called it a chilling demonstration of hate, bigotry and intimidation. He also said, by the way, "We do not believe a cease-fire is the right answer now," as he talked about humanitarian aid going into Gaza, so a lot of news out of the White House just a few moments ago.\par \par \uc2 All of this is happening as you are looking at the night sky at the Israel- Gaza border. It is 10:12 p.m. there. Israeli really forces are pushing deeper into Gaza at this hour, and the Pentagon bolsters its presence into the region to prevent all sides from entering a deeper conflict.\par \par \uc2 So where is this all heading? Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 First, let's get right to \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2 . He's on the Southern Israel -- in Southern Israel for us on ground forces getting a kidnapped Israeli soldier out.\par \par \uc2 Mike, what are we learning about how that all played out?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: A remarkable development, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Private Ori Megidish is back with her family tonight, having been kidnapped by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on October the 7th. What we know now, that it was a result of intelligence generated by \uc2 Shin Bet\uc2  and the actions of elite Israeli forces on the ground.\par \par \uc2 We don't have all the details of exactly how Ori Megidish was rescued, but we know that she's been checked out by doctors and she is reunited with her family in Israel tonight.\par \par \uc2 In light of this, in the light of Israeli forces on the ground, Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is resisting all calls for a cease-fire, saying now is a time for war and saying civilized nations should be supporting Israel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: Just as the United States would not agree to a cease-fire after the bombing of Pearl Harbor or after the terrorist attack of 9/11, Israel will not agree to a cessation of hostilities with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  after the horrific attacks of October 7.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Due to observations or accounts we have from observations, Israeli forces are on three sides of the sprawling population center of Gaza and the surrounding neighborhoods and pushing in.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli forces tell us that they have made contacts with pockets of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  resistance and removed anti-tank positions. In the meantime, Israeli forces have been in the Gaza Strip. They are now there for a fourth night. As you can hear behind me, the cannons are thundering away in support of them -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  on the ground there for us.\par \par \uc2 Mike, we will check back in with you as the news breaks soon. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Iran, meanwhile, accusing Israel of crossing red lines in this battle with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and warning it may get involved in the conflict. So, how should the U.S. respond?\par \par \uc2 With us now is Lieutenant General \uc2 Jerry Boykin\uc2 , former deputy undersecretary of defense and former Delta Force commander.\par \par \uc2 Sir, thank you, and welcome to you.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. \uc2 JERRY BOYKIN\uc2  (RET.), \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2 : Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: First off, what about -- what Iran accusing Israel of crossing these red lines? How should we be responding to that?\par \par \uc2 BOYKIN: Well, first of all, that is preposterous. And they