e the airport there in Russia with those that are landing from Tel Aviv, rushed, with people chanting "Kill the Jews," and acts of violence?\par \par \uc2 The White House just calling that a chilling demonstration of hate, bigotry, intimidation. What do you think when you see that happening as this war rages on?\par \par \uc2 BOYKIN: Well, first of all, those are the same terms I would use. That's what it is. It's just pure hatred. And I'm sorry to see that.\par \par \uc2 I'm sorry that the world hadn't come to a point where we can understand and accept that the Jews are people too, and they have been chosen for many things, most of which has been -- they have been chosen for extinction on more than one occasion.\par \par \uc2 What do I think? I think that, in America -- and let me just speak for a second on that -- any school that's taking government money that has people out on that campus protesting the fact that the Jews are defending their homeland, which is the only homeland they have, I think we ought to take their money.\par \par \uc2 I think we ought to take every penny that they have and not let them get away with that. And when this is all over with, I think that they should be held accountable. I hope somebody's taking names right now, because they should be held accountable. And that school is irresponsible if it doesn't get rid of those people and make a point and show the world that we are serious about standing with an ally like Israel.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Really interesting, General, to hear that.\par \par \uc2 I'm looking at my notes from \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 . He spoke just a short time ago. It was about two hours ago, out with a very firm message to the world, he and his defense secretary saying, we will achieve absolute victory. This war will not be short, he said, or easy.\par \par \uc2 He said the future of our civilization is at stake as he detailed their ongoing ground invasion. The Israeli prime minister saying, it is either us or \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . We have to be willing to fight the barbarians, because the barbarians are willing to fight us.\par \par \uc2 He then called this, General, a turning point for leaders and nations and said, we will fight, we have to fight for hope and promise.\par \par \uc2 I want to get your reaction to the prime minister's words.\par \par \uc2 BOYKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I think partially, at least partially, what he's saying is, you're going to have to make a choice. You're going to have to choose. You got to choose between good and evil. He used that term in his speech, good and evil. And I think that he's saying, you're going to have to decide what you got to be on the side of what's right, or are you going to be on the side of what's wrong? Good versus evil.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: General, really appreciate you joining us. Thank you so much, sir.\par \par \uc2 BOYKIN: Yes, sir. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right.\par \par \uc2 Demonstrations against Israel continuing today on the heels of large protests this weekend. We're going to have the very latest for you.\par \par \uc2 But, first, fresh moves to decouple Ukraine aid from Israel are coming.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie on the Hill and how that's going.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Well, House Republicans just dropped the text of their stand-alone Israel aid package, but it's going to run into a lot of troubles not only with Democrats, but also with some conservatives. I will explain next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It is a showdown on Capitol Hill over aid to Israel and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 New House Speaker Mike Johnson planning a Thursday vote for a stand-alone aid package for Israel, at odds with both the White House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 To FOX's Aishah Hasnie now. She's live on the Hill with the very latest from there.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Hey there, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Well, you're right. Speaker Johnson is in for a big spending battle with the Senate, but, first, he has to get this stand-alone aid package through the House. And he's going to face some stiff opposition from not just Democrats, but even conservatives in his own party.\par \par \uc2 Johnson is going to bring a $14 billion stand-alone aid package for Israel to the floor this week. To offset the cost of that $14 billion, he wants to cut $14 billion from the \uc2 IRS\uc2  funding in the Democrats' Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 And here he is, Sandra, just moments ago explaining exactly that in an exclusive sit-down interview with "Outnumbered" host \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , HOST, "OUTNUMBERED": How's it going to be paid for?\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): We're going to be releasing that here shortly.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: OK.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: But we -- my intention and my desire in the first draft of this bill is to take some of the money that has been set aside for the -- building and bulking up the \uc2 IRS\uc2  right now. They have about $67 billion in that fund, and we will try to take the $14.5 necessary for this immediate and urgent need. We will deal with the rest of that issue later.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But some House conservatives may still not be interested in this.\par \par \uc2 Before the bill text even came out, Sandra, Congressman \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  posted on X that he's a hard no, even with cuts, because he says: "Today, when Congress says their new spending is paid for with cuts, what they really mean is they're replacing spending on a project that's itself not paid for with new spending."\par \par \uc2 Now, if Johnson can get this done without colleagues like Massie, he will face a bitter battle with the Democrat-controlled Senate, where you have got not just Senate Democrats, but also GOP Leader \uc2 