ed terror attack on their soil.\par \par \uc2 I think it's an easy choice, quite honestly.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, so let's get to the numbers involved here and see where you stand.\par \par \uc2 Johnson says: "My intention and my desire in the first draft of this bill, which we now have, is to take some of the money that's been set aside for the rebuilding and bulking up of the \uc2 IRS\uc2 ," which you say you will gladly take money from that. He says: "We have about $67 billion in that fund. We will try to take the 14.5 necessary for this immediate urgent need."\par \par \uc2 Is that the right number?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Well, if Israel needs that to conduct their offensive operations, they're a sovereign nation. We ought to be supportive of that.\par \par \uc2 Of course, they use our munitions in Iron Dome and other things. If they need this to be successful, I'm in full support of it. And I'm also in full support of taking it from an unneeded, unwanted, unasked-for expansion of the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . This was not to rebuild the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . This was to expand the \uc2 IRS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 No American citizen said, we can fix all America's problems by putting more \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents out there bothering American citizens that are just trying to hang on to as much money as they earn. And so, if this is what it takes, I think this is a really good first start. And, again, it acknowledges, at the same time, we want to help our friend, our ally in Israel, we're also acknowledging that we're $33 trillion in debt.\par \par \uc2 We have to make difficult choices. And, again, I don't know that having more \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents really solves our spending problem. And so we shouldn't be spending on that, if we're spending on anything. But since some of my friends on the left think that's important, let them make the choice. I will make that choice.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Got it. Sounds like it has your support on all levels there.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, Congressman. Appreciate you joining us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Thank you very much. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We have got to get to this breaking news. \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and the \uc2 UAW\uc2  have reached a deal, a tentative deal. It's the final Detroit automaker to do so following more than six weeks of autoworker strikes against the Big Three. The GM deal following accords reached with both Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , with the union winning record pay hikes.\par \par \uc2 To Grady Trimble on the news.\par \par \uc2 Grady, what do we know?\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this deal with \uc2 General Motors\uc2  effectively ends the strike. And just in the last five, 10 minutes or so, I got a statement from \uc2 General Motors\uc2  CEO \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I will read part of it to you. She says: "GM is pleased to have reached a tentative agreement with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  that reflects the contributions of the team, while enabling us to continue to invest in our future and provide good jobs in the U.S." She goes on to say, "We're looking forward to having everyone back to work,' which I'm sure she is after more than 45 days.\par \par \uc2 Our understanding is that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  used the Ford deal, which it struck first last week, as a template for both \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and \uc2 General Motors\uc2 . Here is what's in that Ford deal, a 25 percent pay raise over the four years of the contract, even more when you factor in cost-of-living adjustments, a quicker pathway to the top pay tier, and the right to strike over future plant closures.\par \par \uc2 Today's deal is also a relief for President Biden, the first sitting president ever to join striking workers on a picket line. President Biden says he spoke to \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain on the phone today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They won a 