versity here to do more. They say the silence from the university's president makes them feel unsafe on campus.\par \par \uc2 And it's not just here. Over at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 , also in New York, there's been a lot of incidents there leading to threats and possible violence. Now, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , Sandra, is investigating this after someone posted online saying that they're going to go around campus and shoot up Jewish centers and also kill Jewish students.\par \par \uc2 The governor of New York was there today to try to talk to these students who say they're afraid to even walk around. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): I came here in person with one strong message, that we will not tolerate threats or hatred or antisemitism or any kind of hatred that makes people feel vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And the series of messages that was posted in an online discussion board read that people should follow around Jewish students, attack them and kill them on campus, and also threaten to shoot up that Jewish dining hall.\par \par \uc2 It's not just on those college campuses. We have seen protests spill out into the streets across the country, in New York, hundreds arrested at Grand Central Station after the group demanding a cease-fire there jammed into the station, blocking that terminal during rush hour.\par \par \uc2 So, back out here live, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  continues to investigate over at Cornell, and we're hearing about a possible lawsuit that could be filed against the university too because they think they're not doing enough to protect those students.\par \par \uc2 Sandra, it was just only the other day that Cornell professor also took a leave of absence, as you remember, because he called the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacks against those Israelis exhilarating, so a lot going on at these campuses -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Alexis McAdams reporting live here in New York City from Columbia.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 Now let's get to Peter Doocy. You saw him a moment ago. Got a question in that Briefing Room.\par \par \uc2 What was your takeaway from that and the president's day today?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Sandra, the administration knows that there's been this nearly 400 percent uptick in antisemitic incidents since the October 7 attack on Israel.\par \par \uc2 And so they are tasking the Justice Department and DHS to try to address the root of these antisemitic incidents. And while they know that these college campus protests are now becoming problematic in some of the things that students are chanting, and in that some of them are devolving into violence, they are not condemning the protests completely.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Does President Biden look at these anti-Israel protests on college campuses and think it's nice to see that the country's youth are so involved, or does he think the next generation is doomed?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Here's the thing. There's no place for hate in America.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But I'm curious what he thinks.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: No, no, no, I'm telling you, there's no place for hate in America.\par \par \uc2 And we condemn any antisemitic threat or incident in the strongest terms.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: They are also condemning the initial Russian response to this video that you see on screen right now. An angry antisemitic mob rushed into an airport to try to intercept a flight that had just landed from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: You all saw what happened in Dagestan, Russia, yesterday, in what can only be described as a chilling demonstration of hate, bigotry, intimidation.\par \par \uc2 But we have heard crickets from the Kremlin, nothing, no condemnation, no calling for a stoppage of hate, discrimination and bigotry towards Jews, nothing. And I think that speaks volumes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Top officials here are also meeting with the Saudi defense minister. They are trying to figure out how to stop the violence from spreading in the Middle East -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Peter Doocy on a very busy afternoon at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Peter.\par \par \uc2 More rockets fired at U.S. forces in Iraq today. Is the U.S. response against Iran proxies forceful enough?\par \par \uc2 We will ask Kansas Republican Senator \uc2 Jerry Moran\uc2 , who will be questioning Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  in a hearing tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Back to Israel now where efforts are ongoing to free the hostages being held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists at this hour.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Alex Hogan is on the ground there reporting from Tel Aviv. She's got the latest.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli government says this soldier that was released today is the first to be freed with the use of ground forces. And that is giving a lot of hope to the hundreds of other families of hostages that were taken on October 7.\par \par \uc2 I sat down with one woman whose little sister was taken, and she shared with FOX the phone call, the final moments of when that sister was taken. I do want to caution this is difficult to hear.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): Romi, everything is going to be OK, honey? I'm with you.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice-over): It's been weeks, but the morning of October 7 keeps replaying for the families of the hostages.\par \par \uc2 YARDI, SISTER OF HOSTAGE: Everywhere I go, I see her face. I try to imagine her taking care of other kids that were abducted as well.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: The family of 26-year-old Romi shared with FOX the phone call of when she was taken.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (through translator): Mom?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): Romi? I need you to tell me the color of the car, so we can find you.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: After hours of her hiding, they had been certain that she was almost home to them safe, until this.\par \par \uc2 (GUNSHOTS)\par \par \uc2 YARDI: Shouting and screaming and panicking because they were shot.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: With no answers yet, like so many other families, it's these final seconds of connection that they're holding onto.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): Romi? My love, we are with you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: It's those moments, those final moments that they shared with her shortly after that, that they heard her crying and voices saying that they were taking her.\par \par \uc2 The family was able to trace Romi's phone to Gaza, and they have not heard from her since -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, the night has certainly fallen there. It is now 10:43 local time for you, Alex.\par \par \uc2 We did hear from Tel Aviv earlier the prime minister, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , saying, we will achieve absolute victory, he said, talking to the world. He said, this war will not be short. It will not be easy.\par \par \uc2 What sort of reaction are you seeing and hearing to that tonight, Alex?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Sandra, we heard a lot of frustration and concern from some of these families, wondering, would this ground invasion mean that they would have fewer chances of seeing the return of their family members back home safely?\par \par \uc2 That was the main concern. And they were bringing those concerns to the prime minister. Is it safe to go in? Will this prevent us from seeing our loved ones again? Well, the prime minister responded to that, saying, no, this is a two-pronged approach, attacking \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and also putting pressure on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to release these hostages.\par \par \uc2 And that is what we are seeing today with the release of this soldier back to her family. I have to tell you, it is a very different sentiment here in Israel tonight. There is a sigh of collective relief that people are breathing together, finally seeing someone come home safely. That was not a deal negotiated. It was not a deal that was brokered.\par \par \uc2 It was again soldiers going in, ground forces being able, according to the \uc2 IDF\uc2 , to bring this person home. That is a major victory for this one family, but it's a major victory for all of the families who, I should note, have been gathering day after day in front of city hall. They have been putting up displays. There's also a headquarters in the city where people, volunteers and these families come together.\par \par \uc2 They work for 24 hours a day looking through any footage that they could find, making phone calls, working collectively together to try to trace these people. It's really a grassroots effort that the government is partnering with them to be able to find some of these people that intelligence -- it would be interesting to see in the future if we can get some information about if they were able to use, the military was able to use some of that grassroots information.\par \par \uc2 But this is an all-hands-on-deck effort and a major win for families here tonight -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Alex, thank you for your reporting and for telling their stories live on the ground in Tel Aviv for us at this hour.\par \par \uc2 Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And with Israeli forces able to secure the release of one soldier during these ground operations, does our next guest think more hostages could be brought to safety?\par \par \uc2 Our next joining us -- next guest joining us, retired Navy SEAL Don Mann.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, sir, for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Will we see more hostages come out safely?\par \par \uc2 DON MANN, FORMER U.S. NAVY SEAL TEAM SIX MEMBER: Hello, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 I do hope so. I believe so. The hostage rescue teams, the \uc2 IDF\uc2 , the hostage rescue teams, the U.S. support, Navy SEALs, the hostage rescue team of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , they're over there supporting the Israelis. And they have a very, very difficult road ahead, because it used to be, you would get some intel, you would fly into the target, you would surround the target, you would go in, eliminate the threats, grab the hostage and come out.\par \par \uc2 Now it's a whole different story. Now you have all these \uc2 Hamas\uc2  animals waiting for the \uc2 Israeli Defense Force\uc2  to come in to rescue hostages. But now this is another one that's been rescued. It's a second attempt. And then I do have hope that there's more that are going to come out safely.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You say animals. \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  spoke earlier saying these are barbarians. We have to be willing to fight them because the barbarians are willing to fight us.\par \par \uc2 Can you give us some idea, based on your experience, what these hostage rescues are like?\par \par \uc2 MANN: Yes, a hostage rescue is a lot, lot more emotional, in a sense, than just going into battle, because human lives are at stake, not just friend and foe, enemy and unknowns.\par \par \uc2 Now they're going in to try to save a hostage. Through the years I was in, it was normally one or two hostages. It's a whole different story now. Now there's 230 hostages in tunnels, and the enemy is set up. We don't have the element of surprise going in after these hostages. We don't -- we have lost that.\par \par \uc2 So we're basically going into an enemy situation where the enemy is set up for the hostage rescue teams. They're hidden somewhere, 300 miles worth of tunnels, most likely. We don't know about the booby traps and the IEDs, but I do know -- I really do know this. The Israelis are doing everything they can.\par \par \uc2 Their number one priority is to get these hostages out. The number two priority is to get rid of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Both have to be done, but their number one priority is hostage rescue. And they have the best in the world supporting them with the U.S. and others.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And that is...\par \par \uc2 MANN: So, I do think there's hope.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And that is what we heard from John Kirby, the NSC coordinator, from the White House a short time ago, that that is still the priority, is getting these hostages out.\par \par \uc2 Also, earlier, we did hear when it came to administration officials that the less said the better. And that's difficult. That's difficult for everyone who wants more communication, rather than less. But that's part of it, right? These hostage rescue operations are top secret, the way they're going in and how they need to get in.\par \par \uc2 MANN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And the forces going in know that. And they don't talk. They know they get the mission, they get the warning order, they know they're going into a target, and it doesn't go anywhere else but their little small team that they're going in. It has to be that way.\par \par \uc2 The times where news has leaked out that there's a mission going forward, it's really hurt the troops and U.S. attacks as well. So, yes, it's got to be kept quiet until afterwards.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Don, if I could just take this big picture with you and your experience, we heard from the Israeli defense minister say, we will achieve absolute victory, at a time when many are wondering what exactly that endgame looks like and how that will ultimately be determined, as \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  said a short time following his defense minister that we have reached a turning point for leaders and nations.\par \par \uc2 Real quick, wanted to get your final response to that.\par \par \uc2 MANN: Yes, absolute victory.\par \par \uc2 We have -- \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , who is a very, very strong wartime leader, he has to say that. He has to feel 