agher, Brit Hume,  Leslie Marshall \par \uc2 7,769 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: The colorful drones ebbed above the lake in three separate ten minute showing somewhat organize said was a culmination of five years of coordination and planning. Isn't that beautiful?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Not as beautiful as American man.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: American Man looked good, all right.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'm going to get two copies.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's it for us, have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, Dana, so it was really nice being on Perino on Politics.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, Perino on Politics, quick 25 minutes. Really good information. Thanks for coming on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You bet. Have a good night. All right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier live from the Fox News Los Angeles Bureau.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, two elderly Israeli women have been released by their \uc2 Hamas\uc2  kidnappers and senior U.S. officials confirmed the U.S. is in fact privately urging Israel to wait on its expected ground incursion to allow for more time to arrange the release of additional hostages.\par \par \uc2 Senior Correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  starts us off tonight live from southern Israel. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Two Israeli women Nurit Cooper and Yocheved Lifshitz are free from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity. A slow drip of hostage releases as Israeli military leaders say they're ready to begin the ground invasion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY\uc2 , PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: I'm not going to sit here and assume that there's going to be some sort of a ground invasion.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Yet, for days, Israel's defense minister has told forces to be ready, today speaking of the country's Navy.\par \par \uc2 YOAV GALLANT, ISRAELI DEFENSE MINISTER (through translator): We are preparing thoroughly for the next step. A multilateral operation in the air, ground and sea.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): The number of confirmed hostages has risen almost every day. Today, Israel said 222. The White House says that includes a handful of Americans.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We still have about 10 unaccounted for Americans and it's not exactly clear to us where those 10 people are.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Reports today signaled ongoing negotiations between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to release dozens of dual citizen hostages.\par \par \uc2 However, the implementation of that deal is facing challenges. The Israeli defense forces say they are working to release all of the hostages.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI, \uc2 ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES\uc2  (Through translator): We're not looking at who holds what kind of passport. What interests us is not what race, religion or anything else, it is to bring everyone home.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is reportedly willing to negotiate the release of all those held captive except for Israeli soldiers.\par \par \uc2 For now, Israel is ramping up air strikes on Gaza where the death toll is rising rapidly. And many are still in desperate need of aid. Convoys have delivered a small percentage of the food, medicine and water that is needed. Israel is still preventing any fuel deliveries.\par \par \uc2 SAMER ALDELJABER, COUNTRY DIRECTOR IN PALESTINE, WORLD FOOD PROGRAMME: The truck crossing in Rafah border are a small window of hope.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Amid rising fear that the war could spread beyond Gaza and Israel, Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  repeats a message, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is ISIS.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: I've been through wars. I've seen horrible things. I've never seen things this horrible. This is a battle of our common civilization.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Why is it important to hammer that particular thing?\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. RICHARD HECHT, \uc2 ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES\uc2 : It wasn't sporadic freedom fighters fighting as a welfare organization. That's how \uc2 Hamas\uc2  portrays themselves to the world. But what you saw here today is brutal, ruthless tactics even worse.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): The Israeli Prime Minister's office notes the freed women were kidnapped with their husbands on October 7th, their husbands are still hostages in Gaza, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  live in southern Israel. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  has visited troops stationed near the border with Lebanon as sporadic fighting along that boundary continues. And concern is on the rise f a possible second front in this war, an effort funded intensely by Iran.\par \par \uc2 Senior Foreign Affairs Correspondent Greg Palkot is at the Israel Lebanon border again tonight. Good evening, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Bret. Yes, we're right up against that border where we have been tracking into this evening clashes between the Israeli military and the next door Iranian backed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  militants.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Israeli Defense Force\uc2  claims it has struck 10 \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terror cells just in the past 24 hours. That casualty toll for the last two weeks, 20 \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fighters killed, seven Israeli soldiers plus civilians killed and injured.\par \par \uc2 All this leading to evacuations on both sides of the border. Residents in 43 towns and villages here in Israel have been told to leave, that means as many as 100,000 civilians have fled. Some are still here though and that is a risk. Take a look at what we saw and heard earlier.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: This is where a \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  missile hit just on the other side of the border from Lebanon here in northern Israel. Damage done to this private house, police, military in place.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): It's believed to have been an anti-tank missile, which is small in size, but when it's fired from a short distance away, it can pack a real punch.\par \par \uc2 Along with the destruction of the house, two children huddling in a shelter inside were hurt from flying glass. The busy mayor of the town of Kiryat Shmona at the scene again.\par \par \uc2 AVICHAI STERN, KIRYAT SHMONA, ISRAEL MAYOR: It's not nice, it's not good. Very scary. But we are OK, we are strong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): We are OK, the mayor says, we are strong. Now, you have to remember \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is now tying up tens of thousands of Israeli soldiers in this area as the ground incursion against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and the hostage drama both loomed down south. Probably what Iran the benefactor of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has in mind, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg Palkot live now on Israel's northern border. Greg, thanks.\par \par \uc2 President Biden cutting short a speech today on Bidenomics to head to the White House Situation Room. He briefed on the latest on Israel's response to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacks.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, he told reporters any talk of a ceasefire is premature. The priority the president's saying is getting hostages out.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has that part of the story live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The president's only public event today did not focus on any of the big issues that he's facing, like getting hostages out of Gaza, keeping this war from widening or getting a huge aid package through Congress for Israel and Ukraine. Behind the scenes, much more activity than they're willing to speak to, prompting Biden to cut his speech short.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I apologize, I have to go to Situation Room. We have another issue that I have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The White House never divulged what drew the president away from his Bidenomics event, but shared a photo from the meeting with the V.P. and the national security team.\par \par \uc2 Biden only briefly addressing Israel today, speaking to but not confirming rumblings that the U.S. is engaged in talks about a temporary ceasefire in exchange for hostage releases.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is the U.S. supporting the hostages for a ceasefire deal?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We should have a ceasefire -- not a ceasefire. We should have those hostages released and then we can talk.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Officials have been reluctant to share exactly what they're advising including over the timing of a possible Israeli ground invasion. But they are disclosing some questions. They're asking Israel to think over.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Have you thought through the branches? Have you thought through the sequels? Have you thought through the unintended consequences? And so, we are in active conversation with them about that.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): While also pressing Congress for funding.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Are you concerned that not being clear with folks about what the U.S. is advising Israel to do will hamper our support in Congress for this aid?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I think we've been very clear and consistent throughout, we've got enough appropriations available to us for a while longer.\par \par \uc2 Well, how long is that while? It's going to depend on the expenditure rate and the kinds of operations that they're conducting.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Meantime, swift backlash over this response when asked if the administration is concerned about the rise in anti-Semitism.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We have not seen any credible threats. I know there's been always questions about credible threats.\par \par \uc2 And so, just want to make sure that that's out there but look, Muslim and those perceived to be Muslim have endured a disproportionate number of hate fueled attacks.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  tweeting in part, to be clear, the president and our team are very concerned about a rise in anti- Semitism, especially after the horrific \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorist attack in Israel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Karine went on to say that this concern prompted the president to have his Homeland Security team keep an eye out for any threats to the Jewish community, as well as Muslims, Arab and Palestinian American communities.\par \par \uc2 But Karine also drew criticism for refusing to condemn the anti-Semitic rhetoric that we're hearing at some of these pro-Palestinian protests on campuses across the U.S. citing the First Amendment and right to peaceful protest, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Defense Department is sending more American weaponry to the Middle East, troops are also being told to prepare to deploy within 24 hours if needed. And the Defense Secretary warns that a possible expansion of fighting throughout the region is a real threat. Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson reports from the Pentagon tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The Eisenhower Carrier Strike Group is sailing to the Persian Gulf.\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2  (RET.), FORMER CENTCOM COMMANDER: That actually significantly increases pressure on Iran.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Pentagon initially assigned the Eisenhower to the eastern Mediterranean but plans changed as Iran backed militias intensified drone, rocket and missile attacks against American forces.\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: We know that Iran responds to pre-posture of U.S. forces and our capabilities now are in fact designed to deter them from attacking.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): The United States military still has a significant presence across the region, including 900 troops in Syria and about 2500 and in Iraq. U.S. Central Command says overnight the military intercepted two one way attack drones heading towards the base and southwest Syria housing American troops.\par \par \uc2 Last week, drone and rocket attacks aimed at Al Assad airbase in Iraq and U.S. troops stationed closer to Baghdad.\par \par \uc2 Across nine hours last week, the USS Carney downed 15 drones and four cruise missiles. The Carney was in the Red Sea when it intercepted these missiles. Iran backed Houthis fired them from Yemen.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PAT RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: We don't necessarily see that Iran has explicitly ordered them to take these kinds of attacks. The fact that they are supported by Iran, we will ultimately hold Iran responsible.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  says the Defense Department is activating more Patriot missile defense systems in the Middle East and a thad battery, that's a high tech missile defense system.\par \par \uc2 GEN. LLOYD AUSTIN, DEFENSE SECRETARY: We're going to do what's necessary to make sure that our troops are in the right good position. They're protected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): An Iraqi Army spokesperson says Iraq will go after the militants who fired on bases that house U.S. troops. The Defense Department says that Secretary Austin thanked the Iraqi Prime Minister today for reaffirming the commitment to protect coalition forces, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich Edson live at the Pentagon. Rich, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll take a closer look at what's happening in the Middle East with new attacks on those U.S. bases on this, the 40th anniversary of the Marine barracks bombing in Beirut conducted by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We'll be talking with Fox News Senior Strategic Analyst retired General Jack Keane live in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 17 in Nashville with the manhunt for the person accused of shooting two La Vergne police officer Saturday outside a \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  store. 38-year-old John Drake Jr. is a convicted felon and estranged son of Metro Nashville Police Chief John Drake. Both officers had been released from the hospital.\par \par \uc2 Fox 13 in Portland, Oregon as an \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  flight bound for San Francisco is diverted to Portland after an off duty pilot inside the flight deck attempted to disable the aircraft engines. 44-year-old Joseph David Emerson was subdued by the crew. He was booked on 83 counts of attempted murder and endangering that airplane.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Orlando from Fox 35, our affiliate there. one of the big stories there tonight. A new study on rocks collected by the last man to walk on the moon is helping scientists to understand its formation and age. They now believe the Moon formed more than 4,460,000,000 years ago. That is about 40 million years older than previously believed. Apollo 17 was \uc2 NASA\uc2 's final mission to the moon in 1972.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We're outside the Beltway too, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , 40TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There was nothing in its appearance to suggest it was any different than the trucks or cars that were normally seen on and around the airport. But this one was different.\par \par \uc2 At the wheel was a young man on a suicide mission. The truck smashed through the doors of the headquarters building, in which, our Marines were sleeping, and instantly exploded.\par \par \uc2 And now, many of you are asking, why should our young men be dying in Lebanon? Why is Lebanon important to us? The Middle East is a powder keg. Four times in the last 30 years, the Arabs and Israelis have gone to war. And each time, the world has teetered near the edge of catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Like, they're teetering now. President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  40 years ago, reacting to the Marine barracks bombing in Beirut that killed 220 Marines, 18 sailors, three soldiers, 58 French troops and six civilians. 309 fatalities in all, 40 years ago tonight.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Senior strategic analyst retired general, Jack Keane.\par \par \uc2 General, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 Where were you back then, 40 years ago?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Yes, I was commanded an infantry battalion at Fort Lewis, Washington. I think I was in my second or third year of command, you know.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the reaction to that, and what happened after that, you know, obviously, Iran-backed attack. And here we are, again, with, you know, U.S. bases getting attacked today.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, that's very interesting what took place there, because the Marine barracks was bombed. And it was Iranian proxy that did it. There was a full rundown on \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , something referred to at the time as Islamic Jihad, which we have a Palestinian version of today.\par \par \uc2 But the use of proxies like that, by a nation state to do something like that, I think, took us by surprise. And we had difficulty coming to grips with the nation state that actually done it. Because there was no reaction in terms of, we did eventually pull the troops out. You can make a case whether the troops should have been there or should not have been there.\par \par \uc2 But I thought it was extraordinary that we didn't take kind of action and hold someone accountable for killing so many of our troops. But this proxy that began then by Iran, to use proxies to deflect themselves from responsibility and accountability turned out to be a strategy that work.\par \par \uc2 They blew up our embassy in Lebanon, they blew up our annex in Lebanon, they blew up the Kuwait-U.S. embassy also within a year. They blew up Khobar Towers the next decade in Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 And, by and large, that strategy has worked for them you know through the years and we have been very reluctant to hold them accountable. Now, certainly, more recently we have.\par \par \uc2 But even today, Bret, look at -- they have attacked the U.S. facilities in Iraq and Syria close to 90 times in the last three years. And we've only reacted a half dozen times.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, General, let me put up the maps. Let me put up the maps. The U.S. has bases all over the Middle East, obviously, and a number of different places. But you look at these strikes, these are just recent strikes on U.S. bases, believed to be, as you mentioned, from Iran-backed proxies.\par \par \uc2 Now, fortunately, there hadn't been massive fatalities, and there haven't been -- there were very few people injured, but they are getting attacked increasingly.\par \par \uc2 KEANE:" Yes. I'm stunned by it. That we're not doing anything about it. We are defending against some of it, if they're shooting a missile, we shoot it down, if they shoot a drone down, we get most of them, we don't get all of them. We haven't had fatalities. But that's not the issue. The issue is, we have Iran using the proxies that are attacking U.S. bases, whether you're U.S. troops or bases that our hosts are running, and we have our troops on those bases. And the reason is to try to kill Americans.\par \par \uc2 What is the strategy here? The Iranians have announced their strategy every year for 40 plus years, dominating control of the Middle East controlled the flow of oil out of the Persian Gulf.\par \par \uc2 Two things, they say have to be done to accomplish that mission, drive the United States out of the region by forcing military to turn tail and leave, and destroy the State of Israel.\par \par \uc2 If we want to stop the Iranians from attacking these bases, we have to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And General, there's a lot of people --\par \par \uc2 KEANE: And we should (INAUDIBLE) them for it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's a lot of people that listen to you and say, we're on the brink here of getting drawn into something, like a World War III something. If you look at the U.S. forces, just the naval forces that are now positioned around this region, it is significant, and it's only growing.\par \par \uc2 The Eisenhower is going to be on station -- the carrier strike group very soon. And there are people who look at this and listen to you and understand the need for a response to these attacks, but worry about getting drawn into some bigger regional conflict that spills out all over in. What do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well, I think what you do here is certainly sent enough of a message against those proxies. I'll let the -- certainly, let the Pentagon and others on a national security team figure out what the targets should be.\par \par \uc2 But send a message loud and clear to them that we're not going to put up with this. And things are just going to get worse if you continue to do that. To sit back there, and just defend and not reattack makes no sense to me. That is how we're going to get into a much larger war.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is all calculated here. Iran is taking this action, because they perceive appeasement and weakness on a part of the United States. They think they can get away with it.\par \par \uc2 The savage attack that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has made is perceived weakness they believe was inside Israel in terms of their political and social division that's taken place, but also the way this administration has dealt with Iran for the last three years; wanting to go back into the nuclear deal, backing off on a Trump-set sanctions.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Iran is flush with money now, because we have not enforced the sanctions. They see that as weakness. So, when they see weakness, they get more aggressive. I don't know why we have to keep learning the same lesson over and over again.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you one thing quickly about this. Axios has the story. "Marine Corps Three-Star General Advising Israeli Military on Gaza Ground Operation: Marine Corps officers sent include Lieutenant General James Glynn, according to a senior military Israeli official. Glynn, previously headed up the marine special operations, was involved in operations against ISIS in Iraq," reportedly advising the \uc2 IDF\uc2  on this ground operation. What do you think about that quickly?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Is he active duty? Retired? I don't -- I don't have a feel for it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. I mean, they are saying it's a current three-star.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: OK. Well, I think that's appropriate. I mean, look at that you got a three-dimensional war on our hands; tall buildings, roads, and alleyways to deal with on the ground, and then, third dimension underneath.\par \par \uc2 Very complicated, infested with civilians who live in the area. Hostages somewhere. This is a complicated in an urban setting. I think that we can recall in recent -- in recent history here.\par \par \uc2 And certainly, they're going to have to take their time and exercise some patience to be able to work their way through it. And obviously, complicated now by how \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is using the -- using the hostages as a strategy likely to delay the offense and certainly to minimize the Israeli use of their offensive power because of these hostages.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: General, as always, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes. Great talking to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, why nation supporting the Palestinians refused to allow refugees into their country?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  has once again escalated its strike against big Detroit automakers, this time adding a factory that makes Ram pickup trucks for \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . The union said that 6,800 members walked out this morning at the Sterling Heights Michigan assembly plant, a huge profit center for that company.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed as Treasury yields retreated. The Dow lost 191, the S&P finished down seven, the Nasdaq was up 35.\par \par \uc2 While protests supporting Palestinians continue around the world, neighboring countries are refusing to allow refugees into their nations. Lucas Tomlinson looks at that situation tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sitting next to the British Prime Minister \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  in Cairo, Egypt's president \uc2 Abdel Fattah El-Sisi\uc2  says he will not allow any Palestinians to enter his country as refugees.\par \par \uc2 ABDEL FATTAH 