2 Back here in the U.S., a Customs and Border Protection memo is warning foreign terrorist fighters engaged in the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  conflict could soon attempt to enter the U.S. through the southern border or may already have. The memo by the agency's San Diego field offices intelligence unit says individuals, quote, "inspired by or reacting to the current Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  conflict may attempt to travel to or from the area of hostilities in the Middle East via circuitous transit across the southwest border of the U.S."\par \par \uc2 Long time New Jersey Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  was arraigned again today over new corruption charges. Correspondent Bryan Llenas shows us from New York.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Senator, did you betray your country?\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Tonight, Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  pleading not guilty to conspiring to act as a foreign agent. It's the latest charge accusing Menendez of secretly promising to take actions that benefit the government of Egypt in exchange for hundreds of thousands in cash and gold bars. New photos show the New Jersey senator meeting with Egyptian government and military officials alongside co-defendants, his wife Nadine and an Egyptian businessman Wael Hana. Prosecutors say thousands of text messages reveal a corrupt triangle in which Egyptian military and intelligence officials made requests to Senator Menendez through Hana and Nadine.\par \par \uc2 As former chairman of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, Menendez had unique influence on foreign military arm sales and financing to Egypt. Prosecutors say Menendez, the former chairman of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, wielded his influence to ensure military arms and financing flowed to Egypt, gave Egypt highly sensitive information on the number and nationalities of staff working at the U.S. embassy in Cairo, and secretly ghostwrote a letter 