 talked about earlier, on the 40th anniversary of the marine barracks bombing inside Beirut.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, FOX News chief political analyst Brit Hume, Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist, and FOX NEWS AT NIGHT anchor Trace Gallagher from Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 Trace, I'm in your seat. You are very far, far from this seat. What's your sense of things on the ground there now getting settled in Jerusalem and how things are going ahead of what is expected to be this ground operation?\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I think the ground operation has kind of slowed to an almost halt, Bret. And what you are seeing is a good point that "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " made in the full screen that you called for, which is that things are heating up in the north. And today we saw a rocket attack in Kiryat Shmona, and those may recall back in 2006, we were all up in Kiryat Shmona because it was the war between \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and Israel, and we were watching rockets come in and out for about 30 days. That's the big fear, is they keep shooting rockets. And the \uc2 IDF\uc2 , the \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2 , keeps going after these military points in Lebanon where these rockets are coming from. And it sounds and looks like that northern front is going to become an active front as soon as this ground invasion begins.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, nobody knows when the ground invasion is going to begin. Maybe that's a good thing. Maybe the more time that the \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  have, the more they can get an idea of what they're facing in what would be a very ugly urban warfare, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Brit, "The Wall Street Journal" had a poll out, "Americans weary of getting pulled into Middle East conflict." Use of U.S. troops if Israel was attacked by its neighbors, favor 38 percent, oppose 57 percent. I think that tracks with what you see nationally about people worried about getting into something broader in that region. But it's real. It's a real concern.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, and I don't think the United States could sit by and let Israel be attacked from all directions, really, and not respond militarily, in which point the people who are resisting that would be majority, as the poll suggests.\par \par \uc2 But I think, Bret, there's a deeper strain in American public opinion about all this. And what people are resistant to -- yes, they don't want the United States involved in military actions if they can be avoided. But what they really don't like is indecisive military actions, which explains why public opinion soured on Iraq, why public opinion soured on Afghanistan, and why public opinion has soured now on even Ukraine, where we have nobody fighting on the ground over there, none of our soldiers. And it's merely a matter of money and material. But even that, if people don't think there's a plan to win it, they want to end it. So that's, I think, something to keep in mind as we move forward here and keep an eye on American public opinion.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Leslie, as you bounce around these campuses all over and these different protests we are seeing in the U.S. and around the world, there's a bipartisan delegation from Capitol Hill, bicameral as well, as Senator Katie Britt, Republican from Alabama, had just launched these videos shown by the \uc2 IDF\uc2  in Israel and was emotional. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KATIE BRITT, (R-AL): When we watched the videos and heard the stories today, the things that happened were unthinkable. It's disgusting, it's despicable, and it is pure evil.\par \par \uc2 I do not believe that good can exist with evil. And when evil rears its head, we must look it in the eyes, and we must take it down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Your thoughts on that, and contrast what we hear from the White House about some of this stuff sprouting up, and whether there's an equivocation there or not?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: There's emotions, I think, not just when we see people that are dragged and murdered and raped and beheaded and held hostage. And that, Bret, I would say, is a huge issue. What Israel decides to do with Gaza, with Lebanon, with Syria, what the United States and our allies decide to or not to do, is definitely going to be taken out on, if you will, by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on these hostages. And I'm sure that the officials making these decisions know that far better than I do.\par \par \uc2 But politically, we see these images and we get outraged. But there are more and more people, and not just in the Arab world, that are also seeing numbers, like over 2,000 children that have been killed in Gaza, 1,100 women. Now we have infants that are in NICU, in incubators, that are dying because there's been blackouts there.\par \par \uc2 And I do know that most people I have spoken to, and you guys know my background, lived in Israel, Jewish parent, married into a Muslim family, and have been to the West Bank and Gaza when I lived in Israel. There is definitely, especially among the youth, they don't want to live this way anymore. They don't want their children and children's children to live this anymore. But everybody is saying, we've to get rid of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , but the casualties. We're not seeing \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in these coffins. We are seeing thousands of innocent women and children. I don't believe these women and children, these pregnant women, these babies in NICUs are part of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . So the emotion we are hearing not just from Republican legislators but from Democratic legislators, we are just so upset about what's happening to the children. And that's why when Israel decides to do a ground offensive is essential.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Let's keep in mind here that the information that we see so widely quoted and reported on the casualties in Gaza are coming largely from the Palestinian Health Authority, or Health Ministry, which is \uc2 Hamas\uc2  controlled. And I think we have to be very much on guard to not exaggerate those casualties because they come from sources we simply cannot rely on and should not rely on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To that point, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " came out with an editor's note on the Gaza hospital coverage, saying that given the sensitive nature of the news during the widening conflict and prominent promotion it received, "Times" editors should have taken more care with the initial presentation and been more explicit about what information could be verified."\par \par \uc2 Trace, last word here quickly. Israeli defense officers and a whole bunch of officials coming out saying we need to be careful about getting out there and calling --\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Bret, "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " didn't just get this wrong and report it wrong. They screamed it wrong with the biggest headlines. And they called it a dispute. And they only took evidence from one side of this, and that was \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . And \uc2 Hamas\uc2  was given them phony information. And then Israel said initially, look, we are not doing anything. We are going to investigate this. And then they gave all the proof they needed, and it turned out that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  was wrong, and they are wrong, Leslie, about the numbers in most cases.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will talk --\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: But, Trace, \uc2 Doctors Without Borders\uc2  has specifically said that the children in the incubators because of the blackouts are dying. And I believe those \uc2 Doctors Without Borders\uc2  who have no political game in this and they are international, physicians from the United States and elsewhere in the world.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel stand by, if you would. We've got this House speakership thing happening on Capitol Hill. We'll bring some perspective on that.\par \par \uc2 And later, perspective from above as the war breaks down below.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Looking live on Capitol Hill there, we can take the live shot again. And as the candidates for speaker, they are giving two-minute speeches followed by a Q and A segment, and then closing speeches. There are now nine speaker candidates. We'll put those up on the screen. And they've got to get the numbers. They've got to get out of the caucus and then they have to get -- there they are -- to 217, the magic number, depending on who is in the chamber.\par \par \uc2 We're back with the panel. Brit, this has become a debacle. The question is how long it lasts.\par \par \uc2 HUME: There's no way to know that at this point, Bret. When we talked about this the other night, it occurred to me this could go on for a very long time indeed. It was interesting to hear Pete Sessions say something which was unmistakably true but hadn't been talked about that much, which was the need for a speaker, or an important function of a speaker, is to be a good fundraiser. He suggested he could be one. Perhaps he can.\par \par \uc2 But who is a world class fundraiser by any measure? Kevin McCarthy was. And they discarded him. But it's come down to the fact that the way they set the rules and the way McCarthy set them, allowed them to set them, it only took eight people to unhorse him, and he got unhorsed. Now they ought to regret it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To your point, former House Speaker \uc2 Newt Gingrich\uc2  this weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH\uc2 , FORMER SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: There's a very real danger that they'll elect somebody, and three or four or five weeks from now, you're going to have a group of people blow up and decide to go back into the same mess. So they need to pick somebody to get stability.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trace, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. I flew over a lot of political people are just kind of indifferent. If the opposite of love is indifference, that's where they are. Maybe it's time they got in the basement and did some paper, rock, scissors or a secret straw polling to see if any of the nine can come up with 217?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Leslie?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Did you say me? Yes. OK, I would agree with Brit that it's going to go for a very long time. If you can't decide between two, now you've got nine. Even if you whittle that down to two, this, I fear, is going to go on a long time. I think I'm liking the rock, paper, scissors idea right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Maybe so. From overseas, that sounds like a good idea.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Because you see or experience the planet as a whole, you realize we're all humans living side by side.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Astronauts aboard the International Space Station share their views on unity amid the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  conflict. Israel going back against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  after those attacks. The vantage point of the international crew provides a unique look at the earth without borders as a single planet that we all share together. That was the point the astronauts were making, saying the overview effect, experiencing the earth from a different perspective.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, our "Common Ground" segment features Senators Chris Coons and John Thume who have just returned from that bipartisan trip to Israel.\par \par \uc2 Please remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. out here in the west coast. I'll be back out in Los Angeles tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is coming up right now.\par