Gutfeld along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford Jr., Bill Hemmer and Dana Perino, THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  promising to make \uc2 Hamas\uc2  pay for their barbaric assault on Israel. The Israeli death toll now topping 1,300 and at least 27 Americans have been killed. Netanyahu telling the world that there's no difference between \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and the most radical Islamic terrorists.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is ISIS and just as ISIS was crushed, so too will \uc2 Hamas\uc2  be crushed. And \uc2 Hamas\uc2  should be treated exactly the way ISIS was treated. They should be spit out from the community of nations. No leader should meet them. No country should harbor them. And those that do should be sanctioned.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Israel continues to hammer \uc2 Hamas\uc2  targets in Gaza with precision airstrikes. The Israeli forces promising to root out every single terrorist in response to them butchering innocent men, women and children.\par \par \uc2 But no matter what Israel does, progressives like AOC will still accuse the country of ethnic cleansing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): Our responsibility is to the stability and the security of the region. That means being able to support -- not support, yes, Israel in its defensive capacities, right, in its ability in that context.\par \par \uc2 But it also means that the United States has a responsibility to ensure accountability to human rights, to prevent the ethnic cleansing of Palestinians, and to ensure that horrors do not happen in the names of victims who do not want their tragedy used to justify further violence and injustice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And while the likes of AOC tried to claim Israel can't respond to the most gruesome terror attack in its history, the country's president is slapping down a reporter who tried to push the same narrative.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You seem to hold the people of Gaza responsible for not trying to remove \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ? No, let me finish.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HERZOG, PRESIDENT OF ISRAEL: Do you hold a missile in your kitchen, in your living room? A missile that is sent on our head here and if there wouldn't be a siren, it's a missile from that kitchen.\par \par \uc2 All due respect, if you have a missile in your kitchen, and you want to shoot it at me, am I allowed to defend myself? Yes, that's the situation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well done. Dana, I want to go to you first but permission to rant.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: OK, so AOC brings up ethnic cleansing. Why does she do that? She's introducing a rhetorical method to prevent Israel from seeking justice from their tormentors.\par \par \uc2 Again, it's enabling the free punch from the perpetrators while applying the stain of the oppressor on the victim.\par \par \uc2 I would like to know from AOC what the proportionate response should be, maybe she could draw up a handy chart. When \uc2 Hamas\uc2  does X, you do one percent of X. I think that would be really, really helpful. You know, when they behead your children, you can write an angry letter to the local paper. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And you can march down to the boss's office and complain?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Put a little letter in the recommendations box. A couple of things, well, she talks about ethnic cleansing. Well, what do you think \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is doing? \uc2 Hamas\uc2  literally wants the extinction of the Jewish state.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's their charter.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That is actually the definition of ethnic cleansing. Also, for the reporter that was asking President Herzog those questions. Try doing that in Iran or to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , or to the Egyptians or -- and speaking of the Egyptians, why isn't anybody in the Arab world willing to take the Gazans who are being told to get out so that they can have safety? Who cares about them?\par \par \uc2 Oh, what about \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ? Does \uc2 Hamas\uc2  care about them? \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has not done anything with the money that they've been given to provide electricity or water? Why is that Israel's responsibility? I don't think that is necessary.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is, the thinking is, so a terrorist kills a baby. And in response to that, you try to kill the terrorists, but he's holding his baby in front of them as a human shield.\par \par \uc2 So, the study of the laws of war would be helpful from some of these reporters because Israel is literally fighting for its very existence. Its invulnerability was shattered and they are going to need the space and time.\par \par \uc2 Just think about it, we are six days from babies being beheaded. And already you have the press and Democrats in academia saying, oh, gosh, maybe we really should be, please. Like, let's back off.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, the narrative.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I can think of something else that ends in off, but I will not say it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Good for you, Dana. We'll save it for the break. Bill, you've spent a lot of time in Israel in the Middle East, it feels like this happens over and over again, where they exploit this David versus Goliath myth. That somehow terrorists are David because they're not a nation state, right? And so, then, but they can operate independent of any moral norms, which actually makes them Goliath.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: We woke up this morning to \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  giving what I thought was a pretty stunning press conference. It was 6:00 in the morning, \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  was there, U.S. Secretary of State.\par \par \uc2 And what Netanyahu said is that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is ISIS, right? And \uc2 Hamas\uc2  must be eliminated. I don't know if Israel can do that.\par \par \uc2 You know, we took out Raqqa in eastern Syria and claimed victory over ISIS but ISIS still exists today. I'm not sure Israel can do this job. I'm not sure we can do this job.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  also stepped to the microphone with Netanyahu this morning. And he said, I am Jewish. And let me tell you about my grandparents who fled Russia, former Soviet Union during the Second World War. Let me tell you about my grandparents who survived the concentration camps. It was a very powerful message.\par \par \uc2 I thought it was \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 's strongest moment that we have seen as a public servant for our country. He then went on late today to Amman, Jordan, he's going to go to the UAE, Qatar, Saudi Arabia and Egypt.\par \par \uc2 Now, we know in a big picture scene, that a lot of these leaders want this region to excel economically. And they know they cannot convince international businesses to do business with them if you have fanatics like \uc2 Hamas\uc2  underneath the surface who lie in wait for a period of six years, and make you think that they want peace with you.\par \par \uc2 What we don't know is beneath the surface in these other countries. I don't think the United States, I don't think Israel can wipe this out. I think you need a regional effort coordinated with public statements of will backed by a military response to try and get some progress here.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I would add is we really don't know where we are in this war as of tonight. You say day six, Dana, correct on that. But what happens next? Does \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  act up in the north? We don't know. Does Iran pull another shoot and get more action within Israel? I don't know.\par \par \uc2 You know, there's turmoil within the West Bank and has been for weeks. I don't know what the second chapter is in the story. And I don't -- and no one knows how much longer this war will last.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know, Judge, he -- Bill brings up this coordinated idea of a coordinated effort. We've had 27 Americans killed, they'll probably be more. We've waged war for less. Is it time to consider, you know, what we do? Never mind the, "escalation".\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: You know, Greg, that was exactly where I was going. But before I get there, I want to make one comment about AOC. AOC to talk about ethnic cleansing in relation to the, you know, Palestine, the Jews from the beginning of time have been the subject and the target of ethnic cleansing. I mean, we don't have to go through history to know that that's their target. And as you say, that's the mission.\par \par \uc2 And I also want to say one thing about AOC. You know, she's a political idealogue. What she said is -- says is small potatoes, this is a bigger issue.\par \par \uc2 But in 2021, when Congress allocated money for more interceptors for the Iron Dome, who was crying in Congress at the time? She said, I wept at the complete lack of care for human beings being impacted by these decisions. Wept over the fact that Israel had an Iron Dome where they could defend innocent Israelis.\par \par \uc2 Put that aside, let's talk about the fact that the Arab world is not accepting these Palestinians, all right? Egypt is not accepting them. The Arab world from the creation of the -- of the State of Israel, the nation state in 1948, wanted no part of the Palestinians, what does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Hamas\uc2  does not take their power or their -- whatever leverage they have with Iran, or the other Arab countries to say, you know what, take in these Palestinians. Why? Because they want to use them as leverage. They want to use them to be able to say to the world, look, Israel is killing these Palestinians, all right, who by the way have been given noticed we're going to take out Gaza at this point.\par \par \uc2 Now, to the final question. There was a question asked today of Kirby. 27 Americans were killed. That's a very large number, larger than the number of Americans killed by terrorists during the withdrawal from Afghanistan, larger than the number of Americans killed at the USS Cole, larger than those killed in Benghazi, or beheaded by ISIS.\par \par \uc2 And 27 Americans killed. Why is it the posture of the Biden administration to just let Israel handle this job, instead of U.S. forces going in and getting the guys that not only killed Americans, but that are holding them hostage?\par \par \uc2 And I know people, I spoke to a doctor friend of mine, who on Sunday was flying in people to Israel. I mean, it's the private sector, again, the former government sector, or people who were in the government who are taking this on.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Harold, that's a good question. Welcome back to the table. Feel free to talk about anything regarding this issue since you haven't been here for three days.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR\uc2 ., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: A couple of things and I'm glad to be back. As it relates to the congresswoman from New York, I think you've all said it well, she talked about the framework that the U.S. has committed to ensuring the stability and security of the region. I can think of no other way, no bigger way to secure or to stabilize the region than to do what the Israelis and the Americans are committed to doing, which is to eliminating the terrorist capabilities and infrastructure of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Congress, those in Congress, please get a speaker. If it takes Democrats to do it, we needed to ensure that we can provide whatever support is needed. But I would say to those Democrats who are dealing with some Democrats who are saying things that I think are against the notion of providing a framework for security and stability in the Middle East, ignore them, and get on with the business of ensuring that America can support Israel and all that it needs to do.\par \par \uc2 Two, I hope that MBS and Mr. Netanyahu do not allow this awful tragic set of events or incident to prevent them from going forward with their plan to chart a new course and a future for freedom and reconciliation and shared growth in the region. This is one of the reasons why I believe this attack happened. And I'm hopeful that you guys won't let that go.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the congresswoman and others who are being critical in some ways of Israel, this is why it happened because Israel and MBS were trying to provide a new framework, a sturdier framework for stability and security in the region.\par \par \uc2 Finally, I'm going to give the White House some benefit of the doubt here. I do think, first, they should say we're freezing the funds, the six billion that Qatar number working on, say you're freezing it. Because if that's what we're doing, America and the world needs to know that.\par \par \uc2 Two, Judge, you're right. If we need to send more planes to help people evacuate, we should. But I don't think the White House should disclose all of their operational plans. We certainly don't want to give that to those who may be opposed to us, or for that matter, opposed to Americans. And we know who some may be in that region.\par \par \uc2 I'm glad to be able to speak on this. I've been -- I've been proud to watch this group around this table over the last several days. This has been a unity that I think the country wants, even though there are few voices out there that in my party and perhaps so many other party that I think don't speak for what's best in America's interests or for that matter the region's interest.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well said. All right, coming up, Israel is preparing a ground assault to crush the mosque. Bill Hemmer, who's that guy? Will head to the big board to show us what that could look like.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: The fighting is intensifying during day six of the war in Israel. Israeli airstrikes continuing to pummel \uc2 Hamas\uc2  targets in Gaza and Israel says there will be no power, water or fuel in the Gaza Strip until all the hostages are freed.\par \par \uc2 Well, Greg Palkot is in Ashdod, Israel with the latest. How does it look tonight, Greg?\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Dana, we're hearing an intensification of battle tonight. In the distance, explosions. They could be two things, one, we believe their Iron Dome defenses of Israel knocking down rockets that are coming from Gaza, from the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants arsenals. But also, they could be hits in Gaza as well.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli Air Force has been pummeling the place as you -- as you noted, hitting \uc2 Hamas\uc2  infrastructure but also hitting a surrounding civilians that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  doesn't really seem to care about. The death toll there is now said to be up to 1,500 as preparations continue in a very visible way for some kind of possible ground incursion.\par \par \uc2 There's a big clearing house for reservists in this city and they are showing up in huge numbers. Everybody's very willing to get into the fight. And we've been observing in the last couple of days a buildup of armor and think tanks in action, blasting away at Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the death toll grows day by day. Right now, it's 1,200 dead in Israel and that's including those 27 Americans. Really, Dana, out one after another. The small kibbutzim, small villages are still being -- the bodies are still being uncovered.\par \par \uc2 Both of the Israelis and other nationalities, there are several many French dead here as well, for example, and that continues to go on. Secretary State Blinken, he is here. He's focusing on a couple of things, meeting with officials but also meeting with families of those killed, those held hostage. Charter flights to get Americans out of Israel begin tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Also tomorrow, Defense Secretary Austin adding a bit of a military touch to the American connection here. Back to you, Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Greg Palkot, thank you so much. Stay safe. And we'll talk to you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Bill Hemmer, you're up at the board and you're going to give us a little bit of a taste of what's going on on the ground in the invasion of Gaza. You've got the map there. One of your favorite things is a map.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: I love geography. And it's important to understand this part of the world. It's a region that is complicated. And it is small when we talk about the area and the size of this neighborhood. You got Lebanon to the north. Remember that's where Iran supports \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  in the north. You got the West Bank here where I mentioned a moment ago, that had been turmoil for several weeks. Egypt to the southwest and Jordan to the east here. But this is the area of concern. That's the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 And let's just go into this area of the Middle East. See all these towns right here, all these villages in Israel, kibbutzim as well. Some of them have like 3,000 people, some of them 30,000 people. They've all been cleared now. Tens of thousands of Israelis have been evacuated out of this area and the Israeli military has moved in, because this is the target space right now for the \uc2 IDF\uc2 , the \uc2 Israeli Defense Force\uc2 , and this is Gaza.\par \par \uc2 What is Gaza? Gaza is a place that is heavily populated, more than two million people. Gaza is a place that does -- well, you got about 24 miles here, let me put this on better. But 24 miles here on the ocean. You got about 36 miles here bordering Israel, you got eight miles down here on Egypt, and there are critical areas to understand about what's happening.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  took over politically years ago, OK. They have never pursued peace with the Israelis. And you see up here in Gaza City and this refugee camp, you're going to hear a lot about it. You just heard the Israeli president a moment ago, remember you have the sound bite, you got a missile in your kitchen, why can't I -- a rocket in your living room, why can't I take action against you.\par \par \uc2 What the Israeli government knows is that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  hides in plain sight in these areas like the refugee camp and they hide among civilians and residents who live there. And a lot of the attacks so far in the bombing have taken place in the northern part of the Gaza Strip. Khan Yunis and other city down here in the south.\par \par \uc2 This Rafah Border Crossing here, I just want to make mention of this. That is the only way if you're a -- if you're a Palestinian living in Gaza, that's the only way you can cross out into Egypt. That's been closed, the Israelis hit it first, the Egyptians said we're going to close it down as well.\par \par \uc2 If you get some sort of negotiation for the Gazans living in there with safe clearance, they could get into Egypt into Sinai through this crossing, and apparently, their negotiations right now are underway.\par \par \uc2 If you guys got questions, fire away, but that's just a little bit about what we -- what we're looking at tonight.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Anybody here got a question for Bill? Go ahead, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: This might be a dumb question. You know, we hear about these tunnels. Where do the tunnels go? Are they combined -- confined to Gaza? Or do they go to other countries?\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Well, for a while, the tunnels went into Israel, so they would cross underneath here. And what the Israelis did is they took this fence and they set it up for this 36 mile stretch here. Then, underneath the ground, they put a concrete wall below the earth, they've never disclosed how deep that concrete wall runs.\par \par \uc2 There was speculation, Greg, among some military minds, that the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants dug tunnels all the way up to the edge of this fence, but not to be detected by all the electronics that are on that fence.\par \par \uc2 Why would they do that? They did not want to alert the Israelis to what their mission was all about. Their speculation that they went under these tunnels to the fence, popped out, blew their explosives and then had access into Israel.\par \par \uc2 But specifically, under this entire area, that Gaza Strip, they estimate 300 miles of tunnels underground that loop around and go to different areas. And that's where they believe many of these 150 hostages are now being held tonight.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Judge?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, Bill, you know, everybody wants the Americans and the hostages to be found. But the problem, it seems to me that they've got to find them first. And if this was an invasion that was planned, the attack on Israel was planned for they say, now it's up to six months. And it was by land, sea and air. These people \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is expecting Israel and the \uc2 IDF\uc2  to come back in, they probably applauded everything out that the \uc2 IDF\uc2  is going to do. How hard does it make it for the \uc2 IDF\uc2  to go in there?\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: I think the Israeli intelligence is very good. I'm certain there were a lot of doubts after this on Saturday morning, believe me. I also think some of the reports are true that they've already taken out some of the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders, so they know where to find them. The question would be obvious about yours, about how you save innocent life of hostages that are now being held down there.\par \par \uc2 This may give them some hope, Judge, maybe. You got the USS Gerald Ford in the region. That's a strike group out here in the eastern Mediterranean, anywhere between six to 10 ships in this group.\par \par \uc2 You got the USS Eisenhower that set to deploy, but it's in Virginia right now. It's going to take about two weeks, but that's going to steam ahead to the eastern Mediterranean as well. With our intelligence, perhaps, you know, we can perhaps enable the Israeli Special Ops to do the job that they need to do. But no one knows this area better than the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants. And the Israelis right now, we're trying to catch up with our own intelligence tonight.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And one of the things that happened is that when Israel left that Gaza area, there's no human intelligence on the ground. And if they're not -- if the if the Palestinians, the \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , terrorists are not going to use signals intelligence, then you'd have no information. So that's part of the intelligence failure. Harold?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Though it's so interesting, your map there. I think for many Americans, the size of this region, belies the discord and the violence and the strife that we hear -- we hear -- we've heard about and we've lived with for decades, just amazing.\par \par \uc2 But to the south there, Egypt, give us a sense of -- Dana touched on in her first remarks about how we're not finding the level of cooperation we might want from some of -- some of our Arab friends. Particularly there at that border. What's causing that challenge? And do we know if indeed, we'll be able to find some agreement of cooperation with them to accept some of the Palestines?\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: I think a couple of things are going on here, Harold, you know, you have to identify who the militants are and are not and the -- and the Egyptians don't want that.\par \par \uc2 And also, you know, if you've got 2.3 million people here, how many people are going to choose to leave this and how long the bombing happens? And then when the Israeli forces move in, what are you left with in your neighborhood? And how many Palestinians is that forced to flee down here into Egypt and then they're responsible for it?\par \par \uc2 I mean, listen, we know the history of Syria, and Jordan and Egypt and whether or not they have embraced the Palestinian people. And I think the record is pretty clear.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the Judge, you pointed out just a few moments ago, a lot of these countries have turned their back when it comes to taking care of the Palestinians. I would argue that Jordan perhaps leads this entire region in terms of countries who have taken care of the Palestinians over time in history.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, what you're saying, Bill, is that the Egyptians want to protect their border and know who's coming into their country. That is a novel concept. Thank you very much, Bill.\par \par \uc2 Up next, as Israel reels from the worst terrorist attack in its history, left-wing student activists are holding a National Day of Resistance for \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: So, now you have leftwing college students all across America holding a National Day of Resistance. The anti-Israel rally showing a shocking display of indifference toward the victims in southern Israel. This after \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists murdered women and children.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If anyone would like to cover their face, do indicate that and we will give you an N-95 mask.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On Saturday, October 7th, the Palestinian resistance in occupied Palestine launched a resistance operation by the name of al- Aqsa Flood across land, air, and sea. The Palestinian resistance has broken free from their cage in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hamaz are Palestinians. OK? All of us \uc2 Hamas\uc2 !\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Resistance is justified when people are occupied!\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Resistance is justified when people are occupied!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: So, there is a one side of it. Now, here's another. Journalist Bari Weiss calling out the cowardice at America's universities like you just saw.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARI WEISS, EDITOR, THE FREE PRESS: you go and look at the \uc2 Instagram\uc2  or the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  of many of the most prestigious and celebrated so-called institutes of higher education in this country, you'll see posts about sustainability week. You will not see posts about the greatest slaughter of Jews since the Holocaust. It tells you something about how deep the ideological rot is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: So, I think it was Brit Hume last night who said a lot of rocks are being turned over in America as we speak and I think he's right about that. Dana, we were on the air this morning on "NEWSROOM." We were talking about the complicated history of this part of the world and the fact that you have to spend a lot of time to learn it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: And you said, and if you don't learn it, you're ignorant about it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Right. I mean it's purposeful because right now there's -- we've talked about this before. If you want to be educated on any topic in the world, there's no better time to be alive because a lot of things you can actually learn for free. You don't even have to go to university. Pick up any podcast. I'll give you a list of all the things I've been listening to this week.\par \par \uc2 And it did take effort and time. I didn't know a lot about the Middle East and the complications of the region before 9/11. I had to learn that afterwards. And I watch documentaries, I read books, I listen to podcasts. I start reading especially the Wall Street Journal editorial page which helped me a lot.\par \par \uc2 So, I -- there is -- there is that. But I also think that this is not a new situation, right? So, it's not just like all of a sudden the freshman class of 2023 is this leftwing nuts. Basically, this has been going on for quite a long time, maybe a few decades. And so, then you have the institutions at the universities at the highest levels somehow conflicted for the most part. I wouldn't say all of them but for the most part.\par \par \uc2 The other thing about these young people that are all, you know, rhyming and feeling good about themselves, if they were in Israel, if they were citizens of Israel, what they would be doing today is not putting on an N- 95 mask, they'd be putting on a uniform because they would have been called up to defend their country.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: That's true. After 9/11, I spent a lot of time with the looming terror trying to understand al-Qaeda. Harold, you work in the world of finance. What do you think about what's happening in Harvard when you have a reaction from a dozen CEOs who are backing \uc2 Bill Ackman\uc2  who is huge in the financial world here in New York not to hire anti-Israel students from \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: So, the market is speaking. Look, I believe everyone has a right to freedom of speech and everyone has a right to be wrong. I think what I just saw there, the University of North Carolina students are, they were people on the campus, I don't know if they were students or not, but it's amazing to me that someone would say we are \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . The second goal, the second stated purported goal of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , their constitution is to destroy Israel, is to kill Jewish people. So, I don't understand how you could -- you could be for that and call yourself s being part of some organized or resistance.\par \par \uc2 But you asked me a basic question. I think what-- my alma mater is the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 . One of our great graduates and friend of mine was on television this morning on another network calling for the senior leadership at Penn to resign.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Right.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Because he felt that they had not denounced strongly -- denounced, let alone strongly enough some of this language. Again, freedom of speech is a hallmark of our nation, but that does not prevent a university from denouncing what is occurring -- what has occurred and what is occurring on their campuses. We did it rightly so when we talked about issues affecting African-Americans and Black Americans after George Floyd's killing. This should absolutely be the case on every campus in the country where these kinds of demonstrations are taking place.\par \par \uc2 And any president or leader that has not do it, I think they're not only going to face the peril of their graduates not getting jobs but they're probably going to face the peril of losing their own jobs.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Quickly, just to Greg and then Jeanine. What Harold is describing as a potential turning point, but it seems like in all -- a lot of big stories that we have, we predict turning points. I'm not so sure that I'm convinced that this is one of them.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, as long as the environment of academia which is basically an assembly line for woke idiots keeps going like this, it will never end. Who are they resisting anyway? I mean, they're embraced by their professors and their peers. I mean, academia is rife with that.\par \par \uc2 You remember after 9/11 there were stories of groups of radical Muslims celebrating the attack on NYC rooftops and there was no proof of that. It ended up being an urban legend. 20 years later, an urban legend is an urban reality except it's not radical Muslims, it's our own radicalized American students in masks endorsing terror.\par \par \uc2 They're -- I mean, this is like incredible. I mean, it speaks to this funhouse mirror of identity politics that twists and perverts all logic. It's a mind virus that turns average people into vile Cretans.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Jeanine?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, I like to think it's a turning point and hopefully I'm not being naive here. But what \uc2 Bill Ackman\uc2  is doing and what he is sending a message to the community -- to the financial community and I -- you know, I wonder where \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  is, I wonder where a lot of these other leaders are, the capitalists in our society. But what they're doing is they're basically saying, look, we have an obligation to -- the shareholders, to the board of directors, to the stakeholders, whatever, to have people of character and common sense as employees. And if you're going to be focused on your microaggression, if you need a corner to cry in, and if you're a wuss, we don't want you in our company.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Pretty quick trigger.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You are -- you're -- you are not someone that we want. And hopefully, this is the moment that these people will recognize that they can't start trashing people whose babies are taken out of their stomach and stabbed, and then the mother is shot, all right. I know it's horrible, but they need to hear this. And they want to say that they're the oppressors. You know what? Get a life.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: \uc2 Hamas\uc2  wants to kill and destroy the state of Israel. This is not calling someone they didn't want to go to work. This calling someone who wants to extinguish a group of people.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: They're saying it's from the river to the sea, meaning the Jordan River to the Mediterranean.\par \par \uc2 We got to buzz here for a moment. The Biden Administration a moment facing backlash after telling Americans trying to get out of Israel to fly commercial. How's that going to work out?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: President Biden taking some heat over what critics say is a slow effort to get Americans out of harm's way in Israel. The Biden Administration is now reversing course after Mr. Kirby told Americans trying to get out of Israel that they should fly commercial.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS COORDINATOR: First of all, commercial flights are still going in and out of Ben Gurion Airport, so that's always an option for those who may want to leave. There are still some viable ground routes. And we're exploring many other options as well to see if there's other things that we could do to help Americans who want to leave.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Starting tomorrow, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  will be offering charter flights for Americans to get out. Judge, your thoughts on Mr. Kirby's the commercial flight line was that, given the benefit of the doubt -- I like to give people the benefit of the doubt, but what are your thoughts on that on whether or not the flights should have been started sooner, the charter flights?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: What do you think my thoughts are?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Based on the look on your face, I think --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Look, there are some airlines that are not flying to Israel now, so I mean, how can you possibly expect the Israelis to get on an airplane, a commercial plane. We should have been in there as my friend was on Sunday with his plane and some of the veterans had been on their planes, and a congressman. Who is it, Cory?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Cory Mills.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Cory Mills, yes. I mean, look, we're Americans. We don't wait for people to ask, we just do it.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Dana?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Permission to give some advice to the comms people at the White House. You have power and there's two things that they've done where I think that they could have done a better job since Saturday. Because basically the people that are above them in terms of leadership or in the Chief of Staff's Office or National Security Council, they are not giving the press people what they need in order to do things that are just logical.\par \par \uc2 For example, on the charter flights, I would never have gone out there. And knowing that I was going to get that question without being able to say yes, there's charter flights. And the way you do that is you go to the Chief of Staff's Office and say I'm going to get this question, the President is going to be criticized just like he was in the Afghanistan withdraw, and I don't want to do that. I want to protect the President and I want to get the Americans out. So, can you call whoever you need and they will them we need the flights? And then I will go out and do the briefing, OK.\par \par \uc2 The second thing they should have done is on Saturday when getting the question about the $6 billion and say I'm not going out there until I can say that those funds will be frozen. Well, guess what? Today, they're saying, yes, actually, the Treasury Department is saying that like, we're not going to give any of that money out. They could have said that Saturday.\par \par \uc2 So the communications people do have the ability and the power to push things if they want to.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I hope someone was listening. Bill, you have strong thoughts about this too?\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  announced that he's going to fly some planes to --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: -- Israel and bring Floridians back. They also said there are more than 20,000 Americans who want to leave Israel. I haven't seen that number quoted anywhere else. The White House did not give out that number and neither did the State Department.\par \par \uc2 Two things here. Three hours ago, India announcement it had its first flight out of Israel with 212 citizens on board and Hungary has had two flights for 325 citizens and one ship to Cyprus already with 65 citizens on board. So, look, I trust the American government to get this done the right way. But I do agree maybe we should have stepped on the gas.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: After Afghanistan, you still have faith?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Has Air Gutfeld flown over, Greg, to get anybody to bring --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Not yet. It's only private, just me. But they -- this administration does seem eternally surprised by things whether it's the $6 billion or the pull-out or the effects of like, defunding the police or abolishing cash bail or legalizing theft which creates more theft or elevate -- elevating Sanctuary Cities to sainthood. To them, cause and effect is like a foreign concept. You know, how did I get this hangover?\par \par \uc2 To them, you know, that fiery ball in the sky every morning is heading our way, but we know it's the sun. They just -- and they just can't understand why things are so expensive when you print more money. It just seems like they're always behind the -- you know, behind the curve on everything. But I hope I'm wrong, but I'll be right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I'm glad the flights are going tomorrow though.\par \par \uc2 Up next, critics blasting some on the media for defending the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Media madness over their reaction to the terror attacks in Israel. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  publishing a story that referred to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  as terrorist, but then they changed it to gunman, and only changed it back after criticism. And an MSNBC anchor had the gull to ask an Israeli mom whose kids were kidnapped this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC HOST: What are your feelings about the attacks against Gaza right now? How can the government --\par \par \uc2 RENANA GOMEH, SONS ABDUCTED BY \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 : I must say -- look -- you -- you're looking for a symmetrical situation, and I must say it isn't. I can't be sympathetic to animal human beings -- well, they're not really human beings -- who came into my house, broke everything, stole everything, took my children from their bedrooms, and took them to the Gaza Strip. Israel never done that and will never do. So, there is no symmetry. I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Greg, you know, Andrea Mitchell, 40 years, and she asked that question.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's amazing the asymmetry is like -- it's like genetic or something among the media. The media also does this trick where your commentary on an atrocity is greater than the outrage they have for the atrocity. For example, when we saw mobs of Cretans with their brand-new signs championing the bloodshed and we speculated or I speculate, how did they get those signs so quickly? And mysteriously, how do they mobilize so quickly?\par \par \uc2 The bottom-feeding media threw a fit over that which was missing after the attack actually occurred. It's asymmetry. And that woman nailed it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But she did it in a very gracious and dignified way, she didn't have to. I'm struggling with something because we are reporting 13 Israelis killed by terrorists. But it might say 1300 Israelis killed 1300 Palestinians killed. No, it's not the same. There were 1300 Israelis killed by terrorists, and the response -- in the response to the terrorist attack, 1300 Palestinians have been killed. I'm struggling with that a little bit to try to figure it out because a baby ripped from its mother's womb is not the same as a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorist meeting his demise.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: I would just add that Israel knows it has to act fast because if it doesn't, it's just a question of time before half the world turns its back on Israel. Keep an eye on the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Does the United Nations during this period of turmoil pass a resolution that condemns Israel?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That'll be interesting. Harold?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I -- look, I've known Andrea Mitchell a long time and I know of her commitment to Israel. I interpret this slightly different. I thought that answer -- I thought the question was maybe not the most artful way to ask it, but I thought the answer was incredibly powerful. What that mother said of a kidnapped Israeli was that the force we will enact and impose on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and on this region, I'm willing to do it even though I have a one there who may be kidnapped, even if that means someone may be harmed.\par \par \uc2 So, I interpreted it a little different. I would not have asked it the way Andrea asked it, but I don't question her commitment to Israel. I've known her for -- I've known her for 30 years, and there's not a journalist who understands that region as well as she does.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, I'll tell -- speaking to the mother of a child of two children taken hostage, that was a tough one to ask.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I agree.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: We're going to switch gears from the breaking news. "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: OMT. Harold.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Mattress Mack, a Texas businessman whose real name is Jim McIngvale and his wife contributed $50,000 to help Mary Lou Retton cover the cost of her medical bills as she fights a rare form of pneumonia. She's a childhood hero of mine, one of the great, great American competitors. God bless you and thank you to the McIngvales.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Nicely done. I'll give it a 10.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Judge.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, a young boy charmed restaurant patrons in New York with his charismatic rendition of Beyond The Sea at his parents' wedding anniversary. They call him Tiny Bennett.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: 47.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: He's pretty cute.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK. There's a cat named Butters. He's been missing for 12 years. But guess what? All was not hopeless because Butters was reported lost in 2011, he was just found 12 years later. He had a chip and his family had moved to Washington state. They never forgot Butters. And now they are united.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Nice, very nice.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Butters.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You can skip me because I have a video. Bill?\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: I got a quick thing. Matt Scott, Paralympian, donated 50 custom adaptive wheelchairs to athletes who have had a real struggle throughout their lives. Five grand each. And John Starks from the \uc2 New York Knicks\uc2  showed up there. He was the surprise guest.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's it. All right --\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Good for him.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Thank you for watching. We'll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020231013ejac00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Gov. \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  (R-ND); Interview With Former \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  Chairman \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 ; \uc2 UAW\uc2  Strike Expands; New York Flooding; Interview With Rep. Michael McCaul (R-TX); Interview With Rep. \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  (D-TX); Interview With Sen. Tim Scott (R- SC)\b0\par\par\uc2 Hillary Vaughn, Rick Reichmuth, Katie Byrne, Jeff Flock, Chad  Pergram, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,315 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: What a day, on the verge of a government shutting down and an autoworker strikes just revving up.\par \par \uc2 FOX on top of twin jolts that could have consumers hitting the brakes and sellers on Wall Street, well, hitting en masse.\par \par \uc2 First to Capitol Hill, where the clock is running out, and now fear of a prolonged shutdown is beginning to set in. Is there anything that D.C. can do to stop it?\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Republican Senator presidential candidate Tim Scott is here. And bipartisan Texas duo Mike McCaul and \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , they're here too. And "Your World" is now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 And we begin with Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill on a countdown to a shutdown that I guess right now, Chad, is looking more and more likely. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon. The government is all but shut down right now. There is no Rescue Plan out of this cul-de-sac.\par \par \uc2 The House can't even approve its own bill to fund the government for a month and bolster border security, the vote, 232-198, with 21 GOP no's.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): One group wants to be more extreme than the other group. And so even their effort to pass a continuing resolution amongst themselves failed spectacularly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The chief economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  estimates there's a 90 percent chance a shutdown could run for three weeks. FOX has told some Republicans they are willing to have a shutdown until mid-October. That's when military paychecks are due.\par \par \uc2 There is also chatter that some Republicans may try to remove Kevin McCarthy from the speakership.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Have you seen the reports that hard-liners might be trying to replace you?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I saw that when I ran for speaker.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: But did you see it yesterday?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Did they ever give up? They never voted for me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Republicans are attacking each other, as GOP Florida Representative \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  talks to Democrats about the prospects of bouncing McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE LAWLER (R-NY): There's only one person to blame for any potential government shutdown, and that's \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 . He's not a conservative Republican. He's a charlatan.\par \par \uc2 And, as far as I'm concerned, when you're working with Democrats to try to vacate the speaker, you're a joke.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The Senate has a bipartisan bill which can pass. It scored the support of three-quarters of all senators on two prior test votes.\par \par \uc2 The conservatives are blocking the Senate from voting on that bill until Sunday or Monday. That is after the shutdown deadline -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, Chad, one of the reasons why we had the big fall on Wall Street today, certainly by day''s end at our lows, down 158 points, awful month, by the way, for stocks -- we will get into that later -- was this notion that we will have a shutdown and it could be a lengthy one.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: That is possible because it's all about the math.\par \par \uc2 There is no matrix right now in the House and Senate that can move this. And I would note that they hit their session lows earlier in the day right at about the same time that the House defeated that interim spending bill.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bingo. You're right.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: That kind of echoes back with the -- with the financial fallout in 2008.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Very good, my friend. Always good having you.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Chad, for that. We will see. We will get a closer idea where we stand on this late tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Michael McCaul joins us right now, the Texas Republican working in concert, here's a concept, fair and balanced, and being bipartisan, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , the Texas Democrat, both kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, we have a lot to get into, including what you have been trying to do on the border and meeting with the foreign minister of Mexico, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 But I did want to first talk about this prospect of a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 And to you, Chairman McCaul, it seems like that's inevitable. The only thing in doubt is how long it might last. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): Well, I think that's right.\par \par \uc2 Neil, I have seen this movie many times before. It doesn't end so well. A shutdown never, I think, benefits the American people or our service members who get their pay docked, members on the border, CBP, Border Patrol agents. I hope we can get this resolved without a long-term shutdown.\par \par \uc2 But, at the end of the day, we get this done in a bipartisan fashion. And you just can't get everything you want just one side of the aisle. And so I think you're going to see a Senate version coming back to the House, and we will see where this goes.\par \par \uc2 But, again, another point, Neil, is that they always blame Republicans for shutdowns. And, politically, I don't see this really working well for my party either, if they don't see that we can govern.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, on that point, it's going to stick to the Republican kind of argument here, that it is four or five who are making it very difficult to wrap this up.\par \par \uc2 And all it takes is one of them disenchanted about Kevin McCarthy and how he's handling this to boot him out. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, I think that would just create more dysfunction, a lack of leadership.\par \par \uc2 If we oust Kevin McCarthy as speaker, then we go into speaker elections. Kevin has been able to surprise everybody, against the, odds and get things done. We have a conference right after this interview where we're going to talk about the path moving forward.\par \par \uc2 So I hope we can work together as a team and not be divided. When we're united, we win when we're divided, we lose. And this C.R. had a lot of border security provisions on it that I personally thought were very good. It came out of the Homeland Security Committee, including my remain-in- Mexico policy, which cut down on these political asylum claims and, quite frankly, shut the spigot off to illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 And yet you had 21 Republicans voting against it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will see where that goes.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Cuellar, on that issue, I know both of you had a chance to meet with the foreign minister of Mexico, went to the border, went to assess the situation.\par \par \uc2 Now, you have heard from a number of your colleagues, Congressman, who said that there is no crisis at the border, and it's not as bad as it's been portrayed. How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): Well, I live on the border. I don't just go visit for a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 We do have a immigration crisis, a crisis. Security, it's a different thing. The border is secure when we talk about crime statistics. But when we talk about immigration, yes, we do have a problem. And I have always said -- and I think Michael McCaul and I are the same page -- that, if you want to play defense, you...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm sorry, Congressman. I didn't understand that. When you say secure, secure to me means people can't come through like a sieve.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Secure like safety. If you look at murder, rape, assaults, using of the latest federal official numbers, you will see that Laredo, for example, is about two, three times safer than Washington, D.C. The murder rate here is higher.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm talking about crime.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, no, no, I understand that, but that's not...\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: No. Well...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I guess your definition security and mine are a tad different.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Well, let's...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't want to argue semantics with you, sir, but I mean, it's being dangerous and out of control, whatever your thoughts on that.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: No.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm just wondering, did your meeting with the foreign minister give you some common ground that you guys found that looks encouraging or not?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Yes. And let me just finish my thought.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: It -- crime statistics, they're lower on the border.\par \par \uc2 Immigration, yes, we do have a problem. And I'm in agreement. We do have a problem with immigration.\par \par \uc2 What we did was talk, Michael McCaul and I, as the co-chair of the U.S. Mexico Interparliamentary Group, what we want to do is to make sure that we push security and immigration policies outside the one-yard line called the U.S.-Mexico border.\par \par \uc2 If we can do it on their 20-yard line, it's going to work a lot better. And that's what we want to do, is to stop people before they get to the U.S. border. Otherwise, it's going to be very difficult. So, let's do the work outside of the southern part of Mexico, Guatemala.\par \par \uc2 Let's work with Panama. Let's work with Colombia. But let's secure those areas, so people don't come over to our U.S. border.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, both of you are more familiar with what's happening at the border than many people who are not even near it.\par \par \uc2 But the rap you hear about Mexico, I don't know about the foreign minister, but much has been said of the president, that the soldiers might be there, Mexican soldiers might be at the border, but they're just watching those migrants flooding into our country and don't do anything.\par \par \uc2 Chairman McCaul, I'm wondering, is that the real problem?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Yes, I think Mexico is not fully cooperating to help secure our border.\par \par \uc2 I think Henry's right. We need to push the border out and not play at the one-yard line at our -- at our goal line, but push this thing out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: I thought the prior administration had this right by stopping all these people coming into the United States by adjudicating their political asylum claims in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 That's what I brought up with the foreign minister. Can we process these migrants in Mexico, rather than in the United States? Because, Neil, as you know, once they touch base, then catch and release comes into play. That's what we have over seven million encounters.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, is that on Mexico, Chairman? Is that -- I want to raise the same point. Is that on Mexico, and we're not doing enough to force that on Mexico?\par \par \uc2 Some candidates like Nikki Haley have advised maybe you just freeze all aid to Mexico, all transactions with Mexico because of this until they change.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, that was a provision I had in H.R.2, the border security bill.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: We passed a foreign operations bill, where I did just that.\par \par \uc2 It defunds the secretary's salary until he puts remain-in-Mexico back in place. We will see if that gets through the Senate.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: But Mexico has a responsibility here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Real quickly, Congressman Cuellar, your point on that, and do you trust Mexico?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Well, look, let's look at 2015 and 2019, President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and President Trump. Why did the numbers go down in 2015 and 2019?\par \par \uc2 The main reasons they went down was because Mexico was actually using their military folks at their Southern border, and they were stopping people before they came in. So, there is a way. Like Michael McCaul and I are saying, there is a way that we can get Mexico to do more.\par \par \uc2 It happened under President \uc2 Obama\uc2  2015, it happened under President Trump 2019. We can do it now, but we got to get Mexico to do its job at that Southern border.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Gentlemen, I want to thank you both.\par \par \uc2 And you're a shining example of what it's like to work together, whatever party differences, that that is still possible. So that's very encouraging.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank you both. Have a safe weekend.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 Now, any regular viewer of this show or show on FOX Business or weekend show, you know this is a theme and something I stress, that you deserve to hear from all sides, all parties all the time.\par \par \uc2 This isn't an echo chamber here, nor is it a bash-a-thon one side versus the other.\par \par \uc2 We're going to continue with that theme tomorrow with the House Budget Committee chair, Texas Republican \uc2 Jodey Arrington\uc2 . We have got Michigan Democrat Dan Kildee. And we will also have \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  joining us tomorrow beginning at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 A lot of concurrent, fast-moving news developments. We like to make sure that you hear from everybody who matters, because it matters to you.\par \par \uc2 All, in the meantime, this matters too, about 30 million people in the Northeast, and a good many of them in the New York metropolitan area. It is flooding out there, so much so that La Guardia had to all shut down for a big chunk of time today. It was like a swampland there; 4,000 flights were delayed and remained delayed as a result of what's hitting that area.\par \par \uc2 And then, in outside areas, including when you get into Brooklyn and Queens and the other boroughs in the greater New York area, floods everywhere, New York in a state of emergency, New Jersey in a state of emergency, and, so far, fears it gets worse before it gets better.\par \par \uc2 We're on it after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, well, the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  warned us that this strike could extend and expand. Now they have got 7,000 more picketers out there.\par \par \uc2 In case you're counting, it's up to about 25,000 strikers. They cited and expanded this with two of the big automakers, but they left out one so far today.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Flock to get into the details right now in Wayne, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: You were warned. Yes, and that's what happened today, \uc2 GM\uc2  and Ford in the crosshairs, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  not so much. And now it is getting nasty out here, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Both sides kind of firing at each other. The CEO of Ford, \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2  -- and I quote him now -- that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is scaring workers about losing jobs and holding these negotiations hostage over battery plants. Shawn Fain from the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , the president, firing back against Mr. Farley, saying -- quote -- "He's lying, probably because he hasn't been showing at the negotiations very much."\par \par \uc2 Huh. And \uc2 GM\uc2  gets involved too, saying that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is not serious about ending the strike. I asked Mr. Fain about that very thing just a short time ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: \uc2 GM\uc2  says you still haven't responded to their last contract offers. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN, PRESIDENT, UNITED AUTO WORKERS: No, that's false. We have responded. We have been meeting. We have been meeting with these people over and over.\par \par \uc2 And the irony of this is, they want to talk about me not being there. I have been there every day. The CEOs of these companies have literally been in two or three meetings in this whole process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Take a look at the numbers. Yes, it's a \uc2 GM\uc2  plant in Chicago, 4,600 workers walking picket lines there. At \uc2 GM\uc2  -- I should have said a Ford plant. I should have said a Ford plant.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 GM\uc2  plant in Lansing, Michigan, 2,300 workers there. We have got pictures from that Ford plant, by the way. That is the Explorer plant. I have been to that one probably more times than any other plant. And that's a big seller, as you know, for Ford. They also make the Police Interceptor, which is favored by a lot of police forces around the country.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 GM\uc2  plant makes SUVs, the Chevy Traverse, the GM Acadia, and the Buick Encore.\par \par \uc2 And I leave you with one final broadside fired today by Ford against Mr. Fain, \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2  saying: "Shawn Fain lately has been on TV more than Jake from State Farm."\par \par \uc2 If he really wanted to hurt him, I would have thought he would have said he's been on TV more lately than Neil Cavuto from FOX. But that, of course, would not have been true.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, that would have been...\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Nobody is on more than you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It would have been insulting to you, who's on TV more than me and them put together. I don't know.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, great reporting, as always, my friend, Jeff Flock.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Over for the last 50 years maybe.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, you get the idea. With the strikers here looking at a pay hike of at least 20 percent, they have turned that kind of money down. Got to think of some of these other generous pay packages we have been seeing, where you get UPS workers, who are seeing 30-plus percent benefits phased in over a number of years.\par \par \uc2 You have seen pilots garner 40 percent wage increases. Now, that is a small part of the overall population, to be sure. But it does have inflationary carryover.\par \par \uc2 Kevin has, following all of that, the former White House chief economist, much more.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, what do you think of this and the reverberating effect? No matter how this ends for the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , there will be copycat strikes down the road, and then each hoping to get the same sort of thing. The inflationary impact, though, is what?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT\uc2 , FORMER CHAIRMAN, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Yes, the inflationary impact is, it creates a spiral.\par \par \uc2 And so what's going on? One of the key asks for the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  is a cost of living adjustment, the COLA, that we used to talk about in the '70s and 80s, which was an important part of every negotiation, because inflation moves faster than wages. And if your wages adjust with inflation in real time, then you don't end up having a big decline in your standard of living, which is what most Americans are seeing right now.\par \par \uc2 And you talk about the \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike, but there are 66,000 people striking in Vegas right now against the casinos.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: There's a big health care strike in California.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  puts out statistics on how many days of work were lost because of stoppages or -- strikes or stoppages. And it was four million in August, four million in the U.S. And so the reason why people are doing this is that they haven't been able to keep up with inflation.\par \par \uc2 And the problem that you have been highlighting on your show since inflation started is, once the wages start chasing the prices back up, then prices have to go up again, and you end up in a 1970s kind of spiral.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I had a chance to talk to \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2 , a voting member of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , the other day on FOX Business, which, Kevin, if you don't get, you should demand, but you already do, and it's nice.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: I do, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But one of the things he was saying is this idea of the Fed having to be in the position of a preemptive strike to hike rates to ward off some of the inflation that might come of some of these strike settlements, not that we're there, but it does complicate things, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Oh, for sure, because what's going to happen, first of all, is that a lot of these people are going to see -- that have power are going to see big wage increases, and then that's going to feed through to prices again.\par \par \uc2 And so the Fed's models, they are actually pretty forward-looking. And so, once there's actually a contract, and they say, OK, we're phasing in these wage increases over time, then that will go into their inflation outlook that they present to the governors when they vote.\par \par \uc2 And the bottom line is that if the cost of living adjustment had been left in the union contract -- it was taken out back when President \uc2 Obama\uc2  was -- quote, unquote -- "saving the auto companies," if it had stayed in, then their wages right now would be 40 percent higher, 40 percent.\par \par \uc2 And so that's about what their ask is. And they're...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: ... making whole.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bottom line, it's sticking. That's the fear, right? That's the fear, that it's sticking.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And it's going to take a while to unstick.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Yes, correct. Correct. That's right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 All right, Kevin, thank you very much. Good catching up with you.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2  on all of that.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Yes. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In the meantime, we are keeping track of this nasty weather in the Northeast. I think it really, frankly, caught a lot of people by surprise, because maybe some of them didn't have the FOX Weather app.\par \par \uc2 But, be that as it may, the fact of the matter is, there's a state of emergency in New Jersey, in New York. Eric Adams says this is a storm we cannot take lightly and we're not taking lightly. But roads are covered in water. You have terminals over at the La Guardia that have been covered in water. You have got train stations that have a fraction of their normal trains running.\par \par \uc2 And you have millions of those in the tristate, New York, Connecticut, and New Jersey, area who are desperate to find a way to get home or a street that's dry.\par \par \uc2 Good luck on that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, no Hail Mary pass yet to avoid a shutdown of the \uc2 United States government\uc2 . This would be the third time in about as many years.\par \par \uc2 What's at stake and what it could mean, what you get and what you don't if that happens -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, from a government maybe on the verge of shutting down to a strike that's going on and on for autoworkers.\par \par \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  is kind enough to join us right now. The North Dakota governor is a candidate for president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 Governor, you had been discussing during the debate the administration's maybe backward role in this, and that, by pushing electric vehicles and the like, they have sort of set their workers up for something not too pleasant. What did you mean?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 DOUG BURGUM\uc2  (R-ND), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, Neil, you're exactly right here.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you're going to pursue the Biden energy policies, which is basically like handing the keys of our economy over to China and \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , it helps both of them, if you're trying to kill the U.S. energy industry here at home, and then you're suggesting that we should somehow have a all-E.V. driven transportation strategy, where China's controlling 85 percent of the rare earth minerals, and then subsidizing with taxpayer dollars.\par \par \uc2 Everybody that's watching, your taxpayer dollars are going to subsidize the batteries and the cars. That also then subsidizes the purchase of those cars, subsidizes -- so it helps out the buyer of the car and helps out the car company.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the workers want a piece of that, because the federal government's paying everybody else. But guess what? You don't need as many workers to build an E.V. as you do an internal combustion machine. And so this is not only -- it's anti-U.S. labor, its anti-U.S. economy, and it's actually destabilizing the world.\par \par \uc2 And somehow it's all under the fallacy that going to E.V. makes the world - - a world that's in an environmentally better spot.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, do you think, then, Governor, do you think these workers are underpaid, that they should get an increase?\par \par \uc2 They turned down, as you know, one north of 20 percent. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I think every worker in the country right now is feeling the crunch of the Biden inflation.\par \par \uc2 When you have got $700 or more just to cover the price of the pump and the food on the table, that's $8,400 a year, depending on what people are -- what their base pay is. That $8,400 is hitting you regardless of your income level. And that's -- people's real income are...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, no, you're quite right on all of the above. And the numbers you use are dead-on accurate.\par \par \uc2 I'm just curious whether you think a 20 percent wage hike demand at a minimum is justified.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, they may be just holding their own. I mean, prices are up by more than 20 percent since Biden got into office.\par \par \uc2 So -- but it's not just the autoworkers. This is going to go across the whole industry, because when you have government policies that inflate everything, because you're chasing ideology, because, if you actually cared about the environment, you would want to have every ounce of energy produced here.\par \par \uc2 By killing internal combustion machines, you don't destroy demand for oil and gas in the world. That demand is just going to be filled by people that don't produce it as cleanly as we do. I mean, this is the most naive economic set of policies you could even possibly imagine, like I say.\par \par \uc2 And it empowers dictators like Putin. And, of course, China loves it, because they can get discount gas from Russia now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, let me ask you something about the debate.\par \par \uc2 There's a great deal of criticism about talk over and you guys couldn't stop that. I know it's difficult. But you went from barely getting a word and edgewise in the first debate to, some say, overdoing it in the second debate. Did you overdo it? Did you talk too much, interrupt too much?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I would -- I reject the interrupt. I would call it in -- I was trying to inject myself into a conversation.\par \par \uc2 When they ask a question about the economy, and I have created more jobs than everybody else on the stage, and I don't get called on, when they talk about energy and I'm the only person leading an energy state -- we produce more energy than most \uc2 OPEC\uc2  nations -- I don't get called on.\par \par \uc2 And then we -- you go down the line and we talk about China. I'm the only guy -- when I was a kid with a software company. You knew me then Neil -- 1989, I walked into a market in China, and there was our software being sold for a buck for a five-and-a-quarter-inch floppy. I'm the only one that's known for 34 years China's stealing all of our I.P.\par \par \uc2 I don't get called on when we're talking about China.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, Governor, I did know your private sector days, and what you did and built was amazing. You're an incredible guy.\par \par \uc2 Does it bug you that you have all this gravitas, all these credentials, and now people focus on you? He's the next guy who's going to leave. He's the next guy who's going to have to step out of the race. Does that bug you?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I think the narrative -- let's go back to Theodore Roosevelt.\par \par \uc2 Theodore Roosevelt in the early 1900s helped establish presidential primaries to take the hands out of the party bosses. So it shouldn't be a set of \uc2 RNC\uc2  rules and a network that decide whether the field gets narrowed. It's the voters in Iowa and New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I understand that.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: The voters in Iowa and New Hampshire...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But he came to office, he came to presidency through the assassination of a president, of President McKinley. Obviously you don't want to go that route.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm only saying it, though, to say that life is not fair.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that this process has been unfair? You have great credentials. You have done a great job in North Dakota. But no one thinks you have a chance in hell.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I don't -- I don't think that. I think I got a great shot, Neil, because if you start on June 7 of this year, granted, nobody knew me, but now we're ahead of two of the other guys on stage. We're ahead of one in Iowa, the other one in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 If you're in the top five running for the presidency of the United States of America after three months, I call that a great start. I don't call that no chance. And I have been told this whole -- my whole life. I was told you couldn't build a global tech company in North Dakota. I was told I couldn't win for governor in North Dakota.\par \par \uc2 Competition is good for the American people. And the ideas we have an energy, economy, national security need to be heard.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: That's the discussion we should be having. And we're going to we're going to keep having it. And the voters in New Hampshire and Iowa are going to get to decide who they want to move forward to the next primaries.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 I know we all have short attention spans today, Governor. I guess I'm guilty of that as well. The media puts enormous pressure on you guys. But another theory that's come up after these two debates is that maybe we need a way to cut the mic on you guys when you're talking too much, no offense to you, or one of those Austin Powers little drop chutes...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... where, if you say too much, you just -- you're no longer on the stage.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that, the people who kick that around?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I think, if you had the ability to stop the mic, there'd be more time to talk on policies.\par \par \uc2 Guys -- you wouldn't have to be trying to inject, because, if you're going to talk about the economy, energy, national security...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: ... again, I'm the only one that's got troops at the border down there flying helicopter missions. But you don't get called on about the border.\par \par \uc2 So, if you want to have more talk on policy and less crosstalk, then control the mics. It's been done in the past.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Governor, always an honor to have you. Thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Likewise, Neil. Always good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 That's Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 . We will be talking to Senator Tim Scott a little later.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, keeping track of this nasty weather in the Northeast right now affecting 30 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 Katie Byrne in the middle of it all with FOX Weather in, I believe Hoboken, New Jersey -- Katie.\par \par \uc2 KATIE BYRNE, FOX MULTIMEDIA JOURNALIST: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, after a messy morning commute, now some people are worried about getting home from work or school tonight. We are live from a flooded street, one of many in Hoboken, as workers try to free some of these storm drains.\par \par \uc2 We will have more coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, it would be cliche to say, in the Northeast, when it rains, it pours, because it keeps raining and it keeps pouring, and more and more trains are unable to move, planes are unable to fly, and people trying to get out of the New York metropolitan area, well, they're going nowhere fast.\par \par \uc2 Katie Byrne following all of this right now in Hoboken -- Katie.\par \par \uc2 BYRNE: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, a big thing now is trying to get rid of some of this water. That's having a hard time receding. You could just see some workers freed up this storm drain, lifted it up to get that going just a little bit, but a lot of streets are looking like this around Hoboken right now.\par \par \uc2 And things are improving a little bit now that we're right around low tide. High tide was around 9:00 a.m. this morning, kind of aligning with the rush hour and the worst of the rain that we saw, but we're not in the clear just yet. It's one of those days where people that we have met say, you can't really get around Hoboken by car.\par \par \uc2 In fact, we're right by the train station. We have met a lot of commuters today who said this morning they ended up not driving because of all the road closures and taking a bike or just walking instead because of how bad the roads were.\par \par \uc2 I met Carter Craft. He says he's used to dealing with flooding in Hoboken, but this storm stands out as one of the worst in a while.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARTER CRAFT, HOBOKEN, NEW JERSEY, RESIDENT: Just another flood in Hoboken. What used to be these five-year storms now seems to happen like three or four times a year. So, when we have too much water in an environment that is overly paved, there's no room for the water to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BYRNE: Now, when you look at New York City, the worst of the flooding there targeted Brooklyn and Queens.\par \par \uc2 Subways there taking on rushing water at L.A. Guardia Airport. A terminal, Terminal A, was shut down because of it. And now we're starting to see those rainfall totals. So, interestingly, Brooklyn was in the lead, places like the Navy Yard picking up more than seven inches of rain.\par \par \uc2 Now Long Island's creeping up there. Valley Stream got almost nine inches of rain today, so a wet one for so many across the Northeast as people now try to make their way home from work or school, if they ever even ended up making it there today, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, try to stay dry. That seems next to impossible, Katie, but great reporting on this just the same.\par \par \uc2 Katie Byrne, FOX Weather multimedia journalist.\par \par \uc2 We have got Rick Reichmuth, FOX chief meteorologist, why we're looking at this and what we have to look forward to.\par \par \uc2 What do we have here, Rick?\par \par \uc2 RICK REICHMUTH, FOX NEWS CHIEF METEOROLOGIST: Yes, this is all really a part of Ophelia, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Last weekend, we had Tropical Storm Ophelia form, hit the Southeast and then brought that four days of gloom and rain across the Northeast over the weekend. That remnant has kind of moved offshore and then reformed a bit, not tropical, but those remnants bringing more moisture back in across parts of the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 And it's caused persistent onshore flow, which has meant the high tides that we have had have caused some flooding all week long. We're also at last supermoon of the year, which is today, by the way. That means our high tide cycles are actually a little bit higher. So that's part of the problem.\par \par \uc2 Then you get rainfall totals like this, Valley Stream almost nine inches of rain from this storm, JFK Airport, your wettest one day ever. In fact, this number right here hasn't been updated just yet. This will be the wettest one day that we have seen in the JFK Airport ever, which is pretty amazing, when you think about all the big rain events.\par \par \uc2 We still have flooding concerns. This is where the flooding concern is. That said, on the west side of this picture, I think things are going to begin to get better. We have one big band of storms that continues to pummel the same areas. It's just to the east of the city, so central parts of Long Island.\par \par \uc2 You see right there that red in that band bringing some really heavy rain continuing. And that's where the most urgent flooding is going on. And then the rest of the areas around Brooklyn and Queens and in towards Manhattan and over towards Jersey and towards Hoboken, that water beginning to recede a little bit, because, you take a look at a radar, and, for the most party, we're drier back here.\par \par \uc2 We're going to get a little more rain from this, but, at this point, it looks like the worst of this is going to remain to the east of the island. And, Neil, as you know, so many people head home on a Friday afternoon out across parts of Long Island. That flooding is going to be dangerous there. You need to make sure you are taking it really slow and don't go driving into any flooded areas -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it is a nightmare out there. Rick, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Rick Reichmuth.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we're following the possibility of a government shutdown. You heard that so many times, but you ever wonder what is shut down when that happens?\par \par \uc2 Hillary Vaughn following it closely in D.C. -- Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, Republicans at this moment are scrambling to find out what their options are to avoid a shutdown that would affect millions of Americans in many ways. We will have the latest on what Republicans will do next to try to make a shutdown that seems inevitable not happen.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you keep hearing we're so close to a shutdown. Actually, we are.\par \par \uc2 But Hillary Vaughn wants to sort of clarify what is shut down in the event of a shutdown. She's on Capitol Hill, first to monitor that possibility, and the other, I guess -- and it really depends how long it goes on -- what gets shut down -- Hillary.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Hey, Neil. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 The longer the shutdown, the worse it will be for many Americans and the more Americans who will start to feel it. Immediately, in the event of a shutdown, federal workers are going to be staying at home, not showing up to work. Many of them are going to be missing out on a paycheck.\par \par \uc2 Most staff on Capitol Hill will not get paid either until the shutdown is over, but members of Congress will. The longer a potential shutdown lingers, people might start feeling it in different ways. Medicare and Social Security recipients might see delays in customer service.\par \par \uc2 People on food assistance programs might feel the pinch as those funds begin to dwindle, and passport and visa processing might be delayed if they run out of money and staffing. It all depends on the timing. Airports should be operating like normal.\par \par \uc2 But TSA agents and air traffic controllers, they will not get paid during the shutdown. They still have to report to work, though, something Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  said on FOX today will be a recipe for chaos at airports.\par \par \uc2 For everyone else outside the government, they may start to notice the shutdown. Tourists, travelers hoping to visit government museums, national parks could see visitor centers, campgrounds closed. A shutdown could also mean D.C.'s goodbye to the giant pandas at the zoo could be delayed.\par \par \uc2 And, also, the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2 's beloved annual Fat Bear Week could also be impacted as well, Neil, not necessarily as serious, but sad for those who want to say goodbye to the pandas or want to vote on whoever is the fattest bear before they -- as they try to fatten up before they go hibernate for the winter -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I don't even like the theme of that, the fattest bear.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Could they -- can't they say the most calorically challenged bear?\par \par \uc2 Anyway, that's a whole separate argument.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Hillary, great reporting through all of this.\par \par \uc2 She never shuts down. She's always on, Energizer Bunny.\par \par \uc2 Hillary Vaughn in the middle of all of that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we are following these developments with this flooding that has now brought state of emergencies in New York and in Jersey. Governor Hochul right now is sort of sending out a message here: We are on top of this. We're going to try to work with you on this. And we will get through this.\par \par \uc2 But for a lot of people under a lot of water, they wonder, OK, Governor, just how soon will that be?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, down day, down week, down month, down quarter. Well, what's down with the markets, as they lose considerable steam of late?\par \par \uc2 A lot of them worried that inflation's real, that generous pay packages could make it more real sticky when it comes to just being with us as a constant.\par \par \uc2 Enter Senator Tim Scott, the presidential candidate, off a pretty-well- praised debate performance this past week.\par \par \uc2 Senator, always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you, Neil. It's always good to be back. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Maybe you wore the red tie today in honor or not so honor of the markets. They're deep in the red right now. A lot of worry out there for investors.\par \par \uc2 What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, there's a lot of consternation.\par \par \uc2 The good news is, we need to get this shutdown over with. Frankly, if it happens, one of the things that we should think about is a long-term implication for the next generation of Americans. The challenge that we're seeing today is that it seems like everybody wants to kick the can down the road and let someone else solve the problems.\par \par \uc2 Real leadership requires us to do the things that are necessary today to make sure that the next generation is better off. As president of the United States, Neil, the one thing I would do -- and I would think that this would help get a deal done in the House today -- it is to finish the construction of the Southern border.\par \par \uc2 Having President Biden sell off the construction parts for pennies on the dollars creates consternation in the House. Not having the remain-in-Mexico policy exacerbates the situation. And then, frankly, seeing six million illegal crossings, with more to come because of the elimination of Title 42, this president could show leadership by reinstating Title 42 because of the fentanyl crisis that's cost us 70,000 American lives.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the government is -- the government is on the verge...\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: And with that kind of leadership, I think we could get a deal.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm sorry, sir.\par \par \uc2 The government is on the verge of shutting down right now. Nikki Haley pointed the guys like you, who have been in the Senate a while, the fact that it's happened where we haven't seen a budget, well, four times out of last 40 years, and that you're part of that institution that created this sort of frozen activity.\par \par \uc2 What do you say?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, you just -- I mean, I like Nikki. I wish she was the same kind of candidate as she was as a person. But now she's kind of flipped the tune a little bit. You really just can't trust her. She offered a tax increase to the South Carolinians, hundreds of millions of dollars of additional taxes for gas.\par \par \uc2 Number two, she had almost a 40 percent increase in spending while she was governor. Number three, she's backing away on one of the fundamental issues, the issue of life. She will not join myself. And \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  joined me on the debate stage for a 15-week national limit on national -- on saving life. She won't do that.\par \par \uc2 So, having her talk about topics is something that is interesting, but it's so inconsistent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Because what happened there, Senator? Because you didn't go at each other like you did in this latest debate.\par \par \uc2 I'm wondering. You're two South Carolinians. It may be that. And you wanted to clarify the one surviving South Carolinian. I don't know what it is. But it was there for world to see.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think you overdid it? Do you think she overdid it?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I will just quote the great philosopher John Rambo. She drew first blood. I did not do that. She did.\par \par \uc2 She started off by making it personal. I, frankly, did not have a negative comment about her as -- until I was responding to what she said. But there's -- it is a target-rich environment. And I'm happy to have a contrast about my record versus her record.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , of course, who didn't show up for the debate, he's running away with this thing in the polls, Senator. And you have reminded me they're only polls.\par \par \uc2 But he's questioning the need for another debate. A lot of other people are as well. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, the debate stage is where we have an opportunity to lay out our vision for our future in this nation.\par \par \uc2 The debate stage is a place where, just this last debate, I asked a question about the 15-week limit. And Ron changed his position. He said he would now support a 15-week limit. That's, frankly, a good position to take. I wish we had more candidates.\par \par \uc2 Vivek and Nikki, please join the 15-week limit. Let's stop radical states like California and Illinois and New York from having abortion on demand.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In other words, don't go to smaller, more limited. No, I want to be clear. You don't believe it should be at six weeks or any smaller period, that to go around that 15-week period was the wise route.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: We should -- yes, so the states have the opportunity to preserve life at a lower level.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Nationally, we should have a 15-week limit to stop the radical states from having abortion up until the day of birth.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right now, with the passing of Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 , a lot of people are wondering, not only who will replace her, but, for now, she was herself an institution.\par \par \uc2 And she hearkened back to a time where both sides could kind of work with each other. Do you fear those days are gone?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Those days are harder than they used to be, but I will say that one of the things I have done, I would sign, as president of the United States, I would take my fend-off fentanyl bill that would freeze the assets and sanction the accounts of the Mexican cartels.\par \par \uc2 And step one is to deny them the oxygen they need to breathe, which is also known as money. My bill that I wrote earlier this year passed through the Banking Committee and the Senate 23-0, of the Banking Committee.\par \par \uc2 You can get things done by focusing on the American people, and not red and blue.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: That is what we need in this country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, have a safe weekend, Senator. Always good catching up with you.\par \par \uc2 Senator Tim Scott, candidate for president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 All right, we're all over these fast-moving developments tomorrow live at 10:00 Eastern time. We will give you the latest on the shutdown, how close we are. Can we avoid it? Doesn't look like we can.\par \par \uc2 That widening strike, the weather, the nasty weather, across much of the country, and, again, how we solve our problems, and this push right now to just end debate's period. Really?\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230929ej9t0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Gov. \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  (R-ND)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,588 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, from a government maybe on the verge of shutting down to a strike that`s going on and on for autoworkers.\par \par \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  is kind enough to join us right now. The North Dakota governor is a candidate for president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 Governor, you had been discussing during the debate the administration`s maybe backward role in this, and that, by pushing electric vehicles and the like, they have sort of set their workers up for something not too pleasant. What did you mean?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 DOUG BURGUM\uc2  (R-ND), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, Neil, you`re exactly right here.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you`re going to pursue the Biden energy policies, which is basically like handing the keys of our economy over to China and \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , it helps both of them, if you`re trying to kill the U.S. energy industry here at home, and then you`re suggesting that we should somehow have a all-E.V. driven transportation strategy, where China`s controlling 85 percent of the rare earth minerals, and then subsidizing with taxpayer dollars.\par \par \uc2 Everybody that`s watching, your taxpayer dollars are going to subsidize the batteries and the cars. That also then subsidizes the purchase of those cars, subsidizes -- so it helps out the buyer of the car and helps out the car company.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the workers want a piece of that, because the federal government`s paying everybody else. But guess what? You don`t need as many workers to build an E.V. as you do an internal combustion machine. And so this is not only -- it`s anti-U.S. labor, its anti-U.S. economy, and it`s actually destabilizing the world.\par \par \uc2 And somehow it`s all under the fallacy that going to E.V. makes the world - - a world that`s in an environmentally better spot.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, do you think, then, Governor, do you think these workers are underpaid, that they should get an increase?\par \par \uc2 They turned down, as you know, one north of 20 percent. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I think every worker in the country right now is feeling the crunch of the Biden inflation.\par \par \uc2 When you have got $700 or more just to cover the price of the pump and the food on the table, that`s $8,400 a year, depending on what people are -- what their base pay is. That $8,400 is hitting you regardless of your income level. And that`s -- people`s real income are --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, no, you`re quite right on all of the above. And the numbers you use are dead-on accurate.\par \par \uc2 I`m just curious whether you think a 20 percent wage hike demand at a minimum is justified.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, they may be just holding their own. I mean, prices are up by more than 20 percent since Biden got into office.\par \par \uc2 So -- but it`s not just the autoworkers. This is going to go across the whole industry, because when you have government policies that inflate everything, because you`re chasing ideology, because, if you actually cared about the environment, you would want to have every ounce of energy produced here.\par \par \uc2 By killing internal combustion machines, you don`t destroy demand for oil and gas in the world. That demand is just going to be filled by people that don`t produce it as cleanly as we do. I mean, this is the most naive economic set of policies you could even possibly imagine, like I say.\par \par \uc2 And it empowers dictators like Putin. And, of course, China loves it, because they can get discount gas from Russia now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, let me ask you something about the debate.\par \par \uc2 There`s a great deal of criticism about talk over and you guys couldn`t stop that. I know it`s difficult. But you went from barely getting a word and edgewise in the first debate to, some say, overdoing it in the second debate. Did you overdo it? Did you talk too much, interrupt too much?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I would -- I reject the interrupt. I would call it in -- I was trying to inject myself into a conversation.\par \par \uc2 When they ask a question about the economy, and I have created more jobs than everybody else on the stage, and I don`t get called on, when they talk about energy and I`m the only person leading an energy state -- we produce more energy than most \uc2 OPEC\uc2  nations -- I don`t get called on.\par \par \uc2 And then we -- you go down the line and we talk about China. I`m the only guy -- when I was a kid with a software company. You knew me then Neil -- 1989, I walked into a market in China, and there was our software being sold for a buck for a five-and-a-quarter-inch floppy. I`m the only one that`s known for 34 years China`s stealing all of our I.P.\par \par \uc2 I don`t get called on when we`re talking about China.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, Governor, I did know your private sector days, and what you did and built was amazing. You`re an incredible guy.\par \par \uc2 Does it bug you that you have all this gravitas, all these credentials, and now people focus on you? He`s the next guy who`s going to leave. He`s the next guy who`s going to have to step out of the race. Does that bug you?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I think the narrative -- let`s go back to Theodore Roosevelt.\par \par \uc2 Theodore Roosevelt in the early 1900s helped establish presidential primaries to take the hands out of the party bosses. So it shouldn`t be a set of \uc2 RNC\uc2  rules and a network that decide whether the field gets narrowed. It`s the voters in Iowa and New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I understand that.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: The voters in Iowa and New Hampshire --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But he came to office, he came to presidency through the assassination of a president, of President McKinley. Obviously you don`t want to go that route.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I`m only saying it, though, to say that life is not fair.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that this process has been unfair? You have great credentials. You have done a great job in North Dakota. But no one thinks you have a chance in hell.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I don`t -- I don`t think that. I think I got a great shot, Neil, because if you start on June 7 of this year, granted, nobody knew me, but now we`re ahead of two of the other guys on stage. We`re ahead of one in Iowa, the other one in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 If you`re in the top five running for the presidency of the United States of America after three months, I call that a great start. I don`t call that no chance. And I have been told this whole -- my whole life. I was told you couldn`t build a global tech company in North Dakota. I was told I couldn`t win for governor in North Dakota.\par \par \uc2 Competition is good for the American people. And the ideas we have an energy, economy, national security need to be heard.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: That`s the discussion we should be having. And we`re going to we`re going to keep having it. And the voters in New Hampshire and Iowa are going to get to decide who they want to move forward to the next primaries.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 I know we all have short attention spans today, Governor. I guess I`m guilty of that as well. The media puts enormous pressure on you guys. But another theory that`s come up after these two debates is that maybe we need a way to cut the mic on you guys when you`re talking too much, no offense to you, or one of those Austin Powers little drop chutes --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: -- where, if you say too much, you just -- you`re no longer on the stage.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that, the people who kick that around?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I think, if you had the ability to stop the mic, there`d be more time to talk on policies.\par \par \uc2 Guys -- you wouldn`t have to be trying to inject, because, if you`re going to talk about the economy, energy, national security --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: -- again, I`m the only one that`s got troops at the border down there flying helicopter missions. But you don`t get called on about the border.\par \par \uc2 So, if you want to have more talk on policy and less crosstalk, then control the mics. It`s been done in the past.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Governor, always an honor to have you. Thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Likewise, Neil. Always good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 That`s Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 . We will be talking to Senator Tim Scott a little later.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230929ej9t0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 GOP Candidates Take Stage For Second Debate; Californians Being Destroyed By High Gas Prices; Are Black Votes Abandoning Biden?; Why Do Elites Want Biden To Win?; Biden Quickly Visits Michigan For Some Face Time; Biden Visits \uc2 UAW\uc2  Strike For 12 Whole Minutes; Kamala Eloquently Speaks To An Audience Of Kids; Why You Should Delete TikTok Right Now; Report Says Chinese Hackers Obtain 60k State Department Emails; What To Expect From Second GOP Debate With Rob Schneider\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Bill Melugin, Mollie Hemingway, Raymond Arroyo,  Rob Schneider \par \uc2 8,224 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: And remember, we'll still be here covering everything. Fair, balanced, and unafraid. The Ingraham Angle starts now from a beautiful West Coast, California.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. In just two hours, seven candidates will take the stage for the second GOP presidential debate of the season. So right off the bat, let's go live to the Reagan Library in Simi Valley where Fox News National Correspondent Bill Melugin is standing by. Bill, so the candidates been to that library before for another presidential debate and primary debate. And you could feel the tension. You can feel the excitement. What are they doing now?\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Laura, good evening to you. They're all getting ready for this debate in their own way right now. And I can tell you the energy is starting to pick up. You can see over my shoulder; people are starting to show up here. They're starting to line up. These candidates have been walking around the property. taking it all in as you mentioned, this is the Ronald Reagan Presidential Library, a spot they all want to be for this huge debate tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now the candidates are getting ready in their own ways. Nikki Haley's camp talked to me earlier. they said she's listening to music. They gave me her playlist, it includes Def Leppard, Post Malone, The Killers and Queen. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 's team tells me he worked out earlier. Now he's playing with his kids. He's getting ready for the debate as well. Both of them are looking to eclipse \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and try to get in that second-place spot behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who's just blowing everybody out of the water in the polling right now.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of him, he is skipping this debate again tonight, citing that huge lead that he has in the polling. He took it a step further and said everybody on stage tonight is essentially wasting their time by running against him. So the bottom-line for these candidates tonight, they have to do something to shake up this race and try to chip into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's huge lead, or else nobody is going to have a chance of being the nominee because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is running away with it right now.\par \par \uc2 The field could get smaller in the coming months. Former Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson, he did not qualify for this debate tonight. He held a press conference today saying if he's not polling at 4 percent by Thanksgiving, he will consider dropping out of the race. So we'll have to wait and see what happens. Is somebody going to have a moment tonight?\par \par \uc2 And Laura, one other interesting to point -- interesting tidbit to point out. California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is here tonight. He's going to be at this debate maybe in the spin room as well. The Biden team sent him out here to essentially do counter programming against GOP messaging coming out of this debate. So very interesting to have one of the most liberal governors in the country out here at this GOP presidential debate. And remember, late November, Fox News hosting a debate between Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Governor Newsom, that'll certainly be something to watch. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Bill, great to see you. There he goes again. Yes. Well beyond the usual debate, intrigued including a jockeying for time, the best one liners as Bill just mentioned. Republicans hope that what happens tonight is actually going to set them on a course to win next year, not by a hair, but by a huge margin.\par \par \uc2 Now things are looking really horrid for Democrats right now. Gas prices skyrocketing again, the average getting close to $4 a gallon again. And it's over $6 in places like California. Did you see the consumer confidence numbers today? They're cratering. The border is an invited invasion. And we warned about it. It's only getting worse. And the most sickening disregard for human life is on display across urban America.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Everybody must eat. Everybody must eat. Everybody must eat. Everybody must eat. Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Apparently, they're eating iPhones. Well, Trump predicted America would fall apart if Biden won and he's pretty much been right, hasn't he been. And as a result, he looks like he'll be very hard to stop. But no matter who the nominee is, showcasing our candidates tonight asking them tough questions. It shows the GOP strength and its depth especially when compared to the decrepit party of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . We know that he's not going to have a real, no holds barred presser anytime between now and November, it's just not going to happen. He's not capable of it.\par \par \uc2 But right now, Republicans look simply don't have enough power to make things better, as bad as we know Biden is. And Republicans are not going to have enough power to make things better unless they can grow their coalition. Now let's face it, there aren't likely to persuade upper class social liberals to work with them or trust them on things like maybe rubber stamping the Pentagon's budget. That's about all the all agree on.\par \par \uc2 So that leaves working class people from across the country, a multi-ethnic coalition, and from time-to-time, we've seen Democrats, they try to go through the motions with working class people, but it's all pretend, it doesn't mean anything. The fact is everything Democrats are doing is on undermining the buying power, the parent power and the safety of working- class voters and boy, do they feel it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's harder and harder to get by. I still work a job because I keep -- most people -- you can't retire 100 percent, because we need to work, groceries are a lot more now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Am I supposed to work until I'm 70, 80 years old.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The economy is not good, inflation is horrible.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Can't even go to the park and play without them shooting.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Stand in front of a restaurant and talk to your friend without losing your life and that's why up to my --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Trump knows their anxiety and the fear, it's real. It can't be dismissed or spun away as much as the White House tries to do this. This is partly why Trump's going to Michigan to address the auto workers. Of course, Biden failed to mention yesterday how much worse off the average Michigander is under his leadership. Real wages in Michigan are down. The state ranks 37th now, on the real median household income scale. It dropped by the way from 32, nice going.\par \par \uc2 But the whole GOP, they also have to get at least in their mind to Michigan. And frankly, this is a test run at the debate tonight. And for the GOP members of Congress fighting over the budget, they have to understand the importance of bringing working class voters into this party, everything else, it's a waste of time. Now some have suggested that they follow more of an establishment friendly approach. Maybe folks like \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  or \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  can teach them a thing or two.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, Nikki Haley is getting some real attention from the old guard and a lot of big donors. Well, they hate Trump, we know that and they don't much like populism. But even if you assume that there are a bunch of voters out there who want more trade with China, liberalized immigration, and war with Russia, those voters are already on Biden side. The Republicans cannot out establishment the Democrats. Remember McCain tried that in 2008. Romney tried it in 2012. It didn't work then and it will not work now. Republicans have to be the party of populism because they certainly aren't going to be the party of Wall Street or Silicon Valley, not going to be the party of Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 They aren't going to be any more pro-Ukraine either than Biden is, they aren't going to be any more pro-China than Biden, they aren't going to be more pro-open borders than Biden. Those avenues are closed. And the only lane open is toward the working class. That's the backbone of our economy, our traditions, and our patriotic spirit.\par \par \uc2 Remember that they along with urban voters are being shafted by the DC Unity Party. And so we got to reach out to them, before my voice goes. Joining us now, -- Executive Editor of the Common Sense Society, Mollie Hemingway, Fox News Contributor. Mollie, where am I wrong here?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, this is a really interesting, an important point. You have people who are talking about being at the Reagan Library, and what they seem to be wanting to emphasize there is foreign policy. And that is an important thing to emphasize the Cold War policies that Reagan used to win the Cold War, those are not the policies of \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , or \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , and the Republican Party does do well without foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 But there was something about the Reagan era that really worked well, where Reagan reached out, reached out to working class voters, and managed to grow the party, he brought in so many Democrats, you call them Reagan Democrats, that's something that really does work well for the Republican Party, caring about those economic issues, caring about also some of these social issues.\par \par \uc2 An auto worker doesn't just care about having good income and having good wages and benefits. He also doesn't want his kid to be taught to trans in the first grade, you know, there are all these issues that do matter to average Americans.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And I can say, oh, \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  is with us as well. Stephen, I mean, those numbers out of Michigan for the average family there and what's happened to them are devastating. And now we're going to apparently allow in millions and millions of other voters over the next year. And if it's next four years, probably what 15 million, Mollie, something like that, Chris. But Stephen, how much damage is this doing to the average family's budget, what they're having to spend every week and every month?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MILLER\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP SENIOR ADVISER: Well, they're getting attacked from all sides. That's why globalization and open borders and inflationary spending are going to be central in 2020. Working families cannot keep up. They have low skilled workers driving down incomes. They have run away out of control spending in Washington, it's making their dollar worthless. And they have trade policies that force American workers in Michigan to compete with actual slave labor in foreign countries.\par \par \uc2 I don't care what kind of economic policy you have, as long as you're competing with child labor. and slave labor, American workers are going to lose and that is the reason why there is a transformational moment that could occur for Republicans if they choose to tap into that public angst, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Chris, we asked Fox Digital to talk to people in San Diego about just the issue of gas prices. Now they're over $6 there. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's crazy, I was just about to take a picture and send it to my friends who are in the Midwest and show them how crazy it is here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I remember when it used to be like, $2 and I could fill up with like, $20, like most of my paychecks only gas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How much does it cost you to fill up your gas tank?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: $88.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: About $75.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I pay some things $100. $120.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: 150.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Have you had to choose between filling up your gas tank and buying other things?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, actually, yes, a couple of times it's been either groceries or gas, and I'm like.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Gas prices have gone up, but what about your pay?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Stay the same.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Chris, this one issue I think could win the whole election, just the issue of gas prices.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BEDFORD, THE COMMON SENSE SOCIETY: That's brutal. I mean, I tried to have a 1994 truck and I got it from California. A lot of people in California drive old vehicles. They keep them up. And it gets them outside of all the restrictions that Governor Newsom has put on them, their ability to drive actual vehicles, they have to go with these older ones. It's cost over $100 every time I fill up, I have to fill up a couple times just driving, dropping my kid off at school, just making it to work. And that hits your wallet every time we stop at the grocery store. It's 150 bucks.\par \par \uc2 But I'm wondering exactly who these GOP candidates on the safe side are going to be talking to. I mean, the voters, the Republican voters seem like they've got a pretty clear preference for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But who doesn't? Is Republican donor class.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And the senators.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: And the senators.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , I mean -- would they rather have Biden be president?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: I think they actually might.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Than Trump.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: Absolutely. And, they're trying to talk to the finance guys, the finance guys and everyone else are trying to back anyone other than Trump. Early on they had DeSantis who -- you're not going to argue with him on his record. It's a great record. But he's not been doing well. They've been losing faith in that. So tonight, they're going to be looking at that screen saying, who's going to agree with me on Ukraine, who's going to agree with the finance class on this or that, and they're going to go with that.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley is out there maybe as someone and you look at \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , he --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Came from nothing --\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: Separating himself from the debate, for being the only person out there with a foreign policy that sounded like the voters. So maybe he's going to be under attack today. But everyone else is going for the --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, Mollie, what of this? I mean, it's nothing personal against anybody. I mean, they're really interesting people and nice people. And they have all had positions of some prestige. But the voters have been very clear on the issues they care about. And it seems like the donors are saying, no, no, no, no, no, can't do it. And senate leadership. no, no, no, no, no. But I mean, we are supposed to be a representative democracy, are we not? Aren't though the people have their say in the end?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: And this does happen in other primary situations as well. It's really pronounced right now. The people could not be more clear about their concern about the direction of the country, they really do think things are serious. They are very worried about the economy. And they're clear also that they do not want to have yet another endless war in Ukraine without -- a war without a strategy.\par \par \uc2 The people who run Washington, DC do not care what the American people think, they're beholden to other interests. But tonight, there's a real opportunity, the candidate who does articulate concern for foreign policy that matches what American voters have wanted, and consistently that they have done well with from the 50s, 60s, 70s --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Two words, two words America First. Aren't those two words, Stephen? Don't those two words capture where people's mindset happens to be now that we feel terrible about all these other problems --\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes, it's America First --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But ours, we're bad, we're in a bad spot right now.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: The voters want America First. The donors want Ukraine first. That's the fight you see right now between \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's Republican Party and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Republican Party, and that is going to be the issue that defines the struggle within the Republican Party for some time to come. And like you, Laura, I hope the populace prevail.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, panel, thank you for putting up with my coughing fit at the beginning. All right. Joining me now Victor Davis Hanson, \uc2 Hoover Institution\uc2  Senior Fellow, Victor, is it time for some of these candidates? I mean, we heard about Asa Hutchinson, who didn't even make the debate stage, they're going to start to drop out and coalesce behind the one candidate who actually has the support of the base and is ahead of Biden now. Is that going to start happening, again, for the good of the country, as they always say?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR DAVIS HANSON, HOOVER INSTITUTION SENIOR FELLOW: Yes, I don't think if this debate degenerates into a melodrama or psychodrama between \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and Ramaswamy and \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , 