 New Jersey Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  committee they believe. The feds also said that he was being cashed in by "giving sensitive U.S. information to Egypt and trying to interfere to block criminal cases against people he knew."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAMIAN WILLIAMS\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY SOUTHERN DISTRICT N.Y.: Bribes in the form of cash, gold, home mortgage payments, a low show or no-show job for Nadine Menendez, a \uc2 Mercedes Benz\uc2 , and other things of value to the Senator and his wife.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is a stunning indictment. Senator Menendez says that the accusations are "baseless." And he goes on to say there's been an active smear campaign of anonymous sources and innuendos to create an air of impropriety where none exists. Senior congressional correspondent, Chad Pergram, live with story today on Capitol Hill. Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon. The feds say, Menendez tried to interfere in this investigation. The Senator is up for re-election and that could put his in a narrowly divided Senate. Listen to what the prosecutor said earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Senator Menendez allegedly provided sensitive, non-public \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  information to Egyptian officials and otherwise took steps to secretly aid the government of Egypt.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): The Fed say, they found a half million dollars stuffed into envelopes in the pockets of a jacket belonging to the senator. Senate Democratic caucus rules require a senator give up their gavel as chairman of a committee if they face a felony indictment. CREW, the Committee for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington, demanded that Menendez step down from office. They're not the only ones. There are also calls from Democrats.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 DEAN PHILLIPS\uc2  (D-MN): There is evidence here. And yes, I'm a Democrat, so is Senator Menendez. But based on what I've seen, I'm disappointed. Yes, I think he should resign.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: This is the second corruption indictment for Menendez in a decade. The indictment alleges Menendez sought information about Egypt in 2018, just months after he was cleared. In that case, a judge declared a mistrial.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB MENENDEZ (D-NJ): I'm so blessed to have two great -- and I love you so much, I can't, I can't truly thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Now, this is about as bad a look as you can have, the chairman of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee indicted over allegedly using his position to help a foreign government. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Chad, thank you very much. Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill. With that, we bring in Richard Painter, former Chief White House Ethics Lawyer. Richard, good to have you with us today. Your reaction to this lengthy 39- page indictment of Senator Menendez? Well, this is shocking.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD PAINTER, FORMER CHIEF WHITE HOUSE ETHICS LAWYER: Well, this is shocking. And we've been around the road here before with Senator Menendez. He was indicted and tried, and he would have been convicted. But for a hung jury in 2017, everybody in Washington has known that he was crooked for a very, very long time. People in the Democratic Party and elsewhere in New Jersey have known that. He never should have been put in charge of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee. He never should have been run for office again. And he should resign immediately. And I'm shocked that the Senate leadership has not called for his resignation from the Senate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that may come strong words. Mr. Painter, thank you. Hang on if you can. We're just going to go to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the President at the White House, making an announcement that is related to guns and gun safety. Let's just listen and dip in here for just a moment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I looked at him. I said, what can I do? And he said, we need psychiatric help. We need help. We need help. Anyone who doesn't think that these kinds of engagements have a permanent effect on young children, in many cases older their entire lives, even if they never had a bullet touch them, misunderstands. These are hard and tough cops asking me, could I get them psychiatric help? To all the state and local leaders and advocates from all across the country and to the survivors and families are with us today, many of whom Jill and I have gotten to know.\par \par \uc2 And by the way our losses may be different circumstances, but I know events like this are really hard to tend. You want to be here to promote the change, but it brings back all the memories as it happened a day ago. And I thank you those of you who are parents for being here, brothers, sisters for being here. It matters. You have absolute courage. You found purpose in your pain. Because of all of you here today, all across the country, survivors, families, advocates, especially young people who demand our nation do better to protect all, who protested, organized, voted, and ran for office, and yes, marched for their lives, I'm proud to announce the creation of the first ever White House office of Gun Violence Prevention, the first office in our history.\par \par \uc2 I created by executive order, I determined to send a clear message about how important this issue is to me and to the country. It matters, and here's why: after every mass shooting, we hear a simple message, the same message all over the country. I've been to every mass shooting -- do something, please do something. Do something to prevent the tragedies that leave behind survivors who will always carry the physical, emotional scars. Families will never quite be the same. Communities overwhelmed by grief and trauma. Do something. Do something. Well, my administration has been working relentlessly to do something.\par \par \uc2 To date, my administration has announced dozens of executive actions to reduce gun violence more than any of my predecessors at this point in their presidencies. And they include everything from cracking down on ghost guns, breaking up gun trafficking, and so much more. And last year, with your help, I signed into law the Bipartisan Safer Communities Act, the most significant gun safety law in almost 30 years. It strengthens background checks, expands the use of red flag laws, improves access to mental health services and so much more. This historic law will save lives. It's really important first step.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, I was the guy along with a woman in California who also, we once banned assault weapons in multiple magazines. We're going to do it again. We're going to do it again. Called action, a reason to hope, because for so long the conventional wisdom was, we'd never get any Republicans to support gun safety legislation, but we did. For the first time in three decades, we came together to overcome the relentless opposition from the gun lobby, gun manufacturers, and so many politicians opposing common sense gun legislation, and we beat them. And we did it through a bipartisan effort that included a majority of responsible gun owners. We're not stopping here.\par \par \uc2 Again, I'll say it again, I'm not going to be quiet until we get it done. It's time again to ban assault weapons with high capacity magazines. If you need 80 shots in a magazine, you shouldn't own a gun. Because look, last time we did it, it worked. We also, last time we established universal background checks require safe storage of firearms. It's time. It's time. Look, while we push, we push for Congress to do more, we're going to centralize, accelerate, and intensify our work to save more lives more quickly. That's why this new White House Office of Gun Violence Prevention is what it's designed to do. It will drive and coordinate a government and a nationwide effort to reduce gun violence in America and from the overseas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. President Biden in the Rose Garden this afternoon and he is establishing a new Gun Safety Office. He said that everywhere he goes he says he's been to every mass shooting in the country, and he says that every time he goes, people ask him to do something. He also talked about work that he did with a woman in California to ban automatic weapons. He says that it's time to bring back the assault weapons ban and put a cap on high magazines. So, we'll continue to monitor the event that's going on at the White House. Joining me now on set here "\uc2 FOX\uc2  AND FRIENDS" Co-Host, Brian Kilmeade, Host of "ONE NATION" on Saturdays at 9:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 Brian, good to have you with us. We invited you here to talk about this stunning indictment against Senator Menendez and this is the second time that he has been brought up on these corruption charges. Back in 2017, he was accused of similar offenses with regard to a Florida doctor and gifts that he was given by him including lots of trips, plane trips to Cuba, I believe, and he was basically had a hung jury in that situation. Play callback number five to remind everybody about how relieved Senator Menendez was when he got off of this the first time. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: To those New Jerseans who gave me the benefit of the doubt, I thank you. To those who have a doubt, I'm going to work harder than ever before so that there is no doubt. To those who were digging my political grave so that they could jump into my seat, I know who you are and I won't forget you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: "I know who you are and I will not forget you." Brian, your reaction to this stunning indictment. It is lengthy and there is a lot in here.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL HOST: First up was Jack Smith that took on Senator Menendez and ended up in a hung jury. \uc2 John Edwards\uc2  blew that one over too. So, they talked about this. It's just crazy because I was reading background to this about a month ago and thinking the indictment has to come if it's happened. They wrote up in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and it was about as really started when he married his wife that he met through somebody else. She was broke. She was busted. Next thing you know, they're exchanging gold bars. He's putting him in his pants and stuffing all kinds of money in his shirt and his suits.\par \par \uc2 And then, you talk about the worst thing that could happen. Chairman of Foreign Relations, before any money is given, I understand the presidency will always check with the committee chairs, say this is what we're doing with this country and this is what we're doing with this country. And here he is with all this power and suddenly he's dealing with these three businessmen who are dealing with the Egyptian government. The Egyptian government is getting all kinds of aid and he seems to be, according to the indictment, financially benefiting from that. And at the same time, I'm brought to some quotes he had on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He says, it's a question, never before have I ever imagined thinking that an American president could be sitting on the Senate floor talking about he's compromised by the Russian government. So, he never thought about that. Evidently, he did think about that, according to the allegations, he might be very much compromised to Egypt and benefiting financially using diplomatic aid. And a lot of people thought just the indictment would destroy his career. He barely gets out on a hung jury, hardly a verdict of innocence, and he was able to get six more years.\par \par \uc2 This guy's up for re-election. I mean, how does the Democratic Party, you already saw some fracturing on other channels of other Democrats. How did the Democratic Party continue to back him or leave him in that spot? He's got to be pulled back from that spot. If you're under indictment, I don't think you can be chairman. So, right away, a Democrat's got to replace him, and you have to wonder how vulnerable that seat is.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I assume we're going to see the same sort of thing with him that we saw with President Trump. He will be at a courthouse, likely a mugshot, fingerprints for Menendez once again and his wife. That's one of the interesting things here because he said my wife knew these people before, before I even met her. So, these are her friends essentially.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, if she's texting with these friends you know what's the problem here?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: She also has a car. She had a car problem.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: She also had a \uc2 Mercedes Benz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, part of the indictment, Menendez searches \uc2 Google\uc2  for price of gold on October 18th, 2021. He performed a Web search. How much is one kilo of gold worth? And then on January 29th, 2022, kilo of gold price. He wanted to see, I guess, if the if the price of gold had gone up, if, if the gold that was sitting in his house was worth more, perhaps. Here he is talking about predicting in April that this whole thing will lead nowhere. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: This inquiry will and up, I believe, in absolutely nothing. If anyone looks at my history on the Egypt, they would know that by both denying aid to Egypt, denying arms sales to Egypt, criticizing its human rights record, I'm not in a position to be helpful to anyone as it relates to Egypt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Well, he was in position to help someone the question is, did he? And that was when the old leader who was, who was with a terror organization was leading that country, ends up in prison. But I will say this, there's also some questions about her. They went to her safety deposit box, found $480,000 in cash. Not bad for a woman who was broke when she got married.\par \par \uc2 And she also got a C300 -- \uc2 Mercedes Benz\uc2  C300 convertible, valued at about $60,000. We don't know where that came from, but that also is in the indictment, as you mentioned, 39 pages. So, he's got to get -- he's got some attorneys' fees, so, hopefully, was able to keep some of that money that was in the sleeve to some of his jackets.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. We'll see where this goes. You hear Democrats calling for him to resign, stepped down. And as Brian points out, he's under indictment, he may not be able to keep his chairmanship of the Foreign Relations Committee -- the Senate, for which is one of the most powerful and important positions, especially given our involvement in Ukraine and what's going on with China and a lot of other very serious situations, they're going to have to evaluate whether or not he can keep that position, given the pressure that he's under now with these legal issues.\par \par \uc2 Brian, thank you. It was good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Thanks for having me, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Great to see you. Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, live look now at the southern border, where around 10,000 migrants. This is just a daily occurrence now, cross without, apparently, without any fear that they are going to be turned away once they leave their country and enter the United States.\par \par \uc2 Think about how difficult that is to get into other points of entry in the United States for anybody who's coming or going from this country, but not in this part of America.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration on track to oversee the largest ever influx of migrants to our country. But the talking points on this have turned from ignoring it to try and to place the blame elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The MAGA Republican in Congress and my predecessor, spent four years gutting the immigration system under my predecessor.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: GOP, the Republicans put out a continue resolution that actually reverses the work that the president is trying to do.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): This gridlock that is driven by the Republicans, who are enjoying the spectacle, who have no intention of helping solve it. So, this lies on their feet as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: First, to former GOP Texas Congresswoman Mayra Flores. In a special election in June of last year, she flipped the blue House District red. She went on to lose the general election in November after redistricting. But she says she's running again in 2024. She is the first Mexican born woman to serve in the House of Representatives and her husband is a border agent.\par \par \uc2 Myra Flores, welcome to THE STORY. Good to have you here this afternoon. We've never seen anything like this on the southern border. You're obviously close to it both geographically. And because your husband is a border agent, give us your reaction to what's going on right now.\par \par \uc2 MAYRA FLORES, FORMER REPRESENTATIVE OF TEXAS: Well, all I see is a huge rant up here in south Texas, and the American people call it a disaster, a complete disaster.\par \par \uc2 I don't know how the Biden administration, how Democrats, Senate Democrats, and Secretary Mayorkas could look at the face of the American people after what they have done to the southern border, and to our country. They are truly the biggest threat to our country.\par \par \uc2 We have seen millions of people cross illegally into our country. Why? Because they continue to reward illegal immigration. And it's a huge slap on the face on migrants like myself, who came here to United States legally, and how so many people right now are waiting in line to come here to the United States legally, but they have to wait in line.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, that's true.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's so frustrating for people from other countries who aren't getting in this way, because they see what's happening. And they say, you know, I've spent all this money on lawyer fees, I've worked on every document they want me to fill out, I'm bending over backward to become an American. And then, they watch what's happening here. And it is absolutely so heartbreaking for those individuals.\par \par \uc2 This is President Biden talking about expanding the legal pathways. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're significantly expanding legal pathways for entry, so businesses can get the workers they need. Families don't have to wait for decade to be together.\par \par \uc2 I've also directed my team to make historic increase in the number of refugees admitted from Latin America. People fleeing violence and persecution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you have millions of people who come into the country illegally, but it sounds like he wants to have even more people come.\par \par \uc2 FLORES: Exactly. If he continues to reward illegal immigration, millions more will continue to come to the United States. Just as fiscal year, we've already surpassed last year's fiscal, total amount of people that have come illegally into our country.\par \par \uc2 This year, we've already had over 2.3 migrants cross illegally into our country, and it's not going to slow down. More people are going to continue to come here to the United States. And I don't understand why he is so obsessed with illegal immigration, and I think it's because he wants these people to be exploited, being taking advantage. Why not focus on legalizing people to come here to United States legally? Why not focus on legal immigration? Why does he wanted to be here illegally?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you know, he blames Congress, and particularly, Republicans, as you just heard in that soundbite. You know, he is saying that it's Republicans who have stood in the way of that. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 FLORES: Well, they have the majority in the House. They have the majority in the Senate for two years.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 FLORES: And they did absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, then -- yes.\par \par \uc2 FLORES: They did absolutely nothing. And they use this issue with Hispanics, every year. They make promises about some type of immigration. Bill never going to fix this problem. Why? Because they want to continue using this issue to continue getting the support from the Hispanic community. But that's why we are walking away in big numbers in 2024. And he is not going to win his reelection.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We'll see what happens. You have your reelection effort underway. Former Congresswoman Mayra Flores, thank you very much. Great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 FLORES: Thank you. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you. You, as well.\par \par \uc2 So, as the border crisis appears to get worse by the day, media coverage seems to be changing a bit on this story. Also, referencing its possible impact for Democrats in 2024, as Ms. Flores just did, watch -- listen to the changing tone here. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIN BURNETT, CNN ANCHOR: You're saying you got 5,000 people in a couple of days, coming into a city of 28,000 to 30,000 people. It's like 15 percent of your population --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BURNETT: I mean, how do you even manage such a thing?\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST, HOST, MSNBC: When there is a crisis at the border, there is data that supports that, that border apprehensions are nearing records again, after kind of ebbing over the last couple of months. So, it is here. It is real.\par \par \uc2 ABBY PHILLIP, ANCHOR, CNN: Does this make you understand better, what border cities have been going through for years?\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: Of course, we do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Of course, we do, says the governor of New York. Let's bring in Katie Pavlich, editor@townhall.com, and Marie Harf, former state department spokesperson under President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . Both are Fox News contributors. Great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Marie, do you sense that politically the reality of what's happening is confronting Democrats in a way that they cannot ignore anymore?\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Martha, I think it is. I mean, look, there's a lot of Democrats, particularly senators or Congress people from border states who've known this for a while, right? Mark Kelly talks about this in Arizona all the time.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that the Biden team knows this is a problem and a crisis. That's why they're surging hundreds of active-duty men and women in uniform down to the border. They've sent additional National Guard folks down there, DHS officers. You know, they are really trying to get a handle on it, but these numbers are out of control. There normal (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me -- let me ask you a question.\par \par \uc2 HARF: That's true, but --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I just want to ask you a question about what you just said, if I may.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, what is -- what is that surge of resources to the border there to do? It's there to help process people into the country more quickly? Because it's not being sent there to block them from entering, is it?\par \par \uc2 HARF: Well, I think some of it is. Right. The Biden team is trying to apprehend folks who are trying to cross the border illegally.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But they are not sending them back.\par \par \uc2 HARF: They are trying -- they are trying to increasingly get the processing time shorter. Then, that is a problem with resources. We need more judges. We need more places to put people. That is something Congress actually has to do.\par \par \uc2 And they are also, you know, they are also trying to work with other countries to get folks to not come to the United States originally.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's not going very well.\par \par \uc2 HARF: I mean, this is a -- this is a -- it's not, it's a really hard problem. But we can't shut our government down in 10 days, either, that won't help solve it. So, not a lot of good ideas coming out of Washington right now, including from Congress, which ultimately has responsibility for this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But, you know, I looked up all of the executive orders, Katie, that the president has done. 122 executive orders, and many of them have to do with immigration, but not one of them is an executive order in an emergency situation to declare an emergency at the border, to say that we are -- we are going to stop all of this traffic coming across the border, and fentanyl, and human trafficking, and lost children. Nothing along those lines in these 122 executive orders from the president.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Well, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  campaigned on bringing in millions of people into this country. You can go back and roll the tape. He says it's not going to hurt the country if we bring in millions of people. And in fact, we should. This administration has no intention, according to the White House press secretary today and yesterday, of stopping or putting a cap on the number of people who can come here illegally.\par \par \uc2 And when they attack Republicans on Capitol Hill for cutting funding for Border Patrol, whose mission they completely destroyed, by turning them into processing agents, instead of protective agents to secure the border. They want more money for their illegal immigration industrial complex.\par \par \uc2 What do I mean by that? They want to spend money through grants to give them to NGOs, who then put these people on buses and planes to go to cities all over the country.\par \par \uc2 You have the mayor of Chicago, the mayor of New York now using millions of dollars to build these tent cities to house these people. When the Biden administration could do things like the Trump administration did, which is force people to wait for their asylum claims in Mexico or in the countries that they're coming from.\par \par \uc2 They're not doing any of that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: They want more money to build facilities for processing.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't understand it. You know what?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: And they act like people are going to leave.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: They're not sending any of these people back. They are letting them stay for years.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I really don't understand it, because politically, it's becoming problematic for them. Marie, I need a real quick answer here.\par \par \uc2 You know, why are they switching gears? I mean, this is obviously blowing up in their faces, you've got Democrat -- you know, New Yorkers, who are freaking out about what's going on in their city.\par \par \uc2 How do they think politically this is going to benefit them? And why don't they just come in front of the cameras and say, time to change gears, we have to stop everything -- all the traffic at the border, and we have to deal with the people that we have here. And then, maybe we can open it up in the future.\par \par \uc2 HARF: I actually think they're trying to solve this problem. If there were one easy solution, they could just flip a switch on, they would do it. I guarantee you, because they know it's a problem.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: What kind barb wire --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What about blocking people? Not allowing anyone to come across that river. What about that?\par \par \uc2 HARF: Because we have --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: They are setting wire, put up by the Texas (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 HARF: They are trying to stop as many people as they can, but we do have certain responsibilities to adjudicate refugee and asylum claims. And they are trying to --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well --\par \par \uc2 HARF: There -- it is a - it is a very, very difficult problem, and none of us here could say (INAUDIBLE) it would get stop.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is a sovereign nation. If you don't have a border -- yes, you don't have a sovereign nation, if you don't have a border. I don't think there's another country that would deal with -- and with this in this way. And I think it's very interesting. It's becoming a hotter and hotter political situation. It's number two now on the issues that voters say they care the most about in our recent polling.\par \par \uc2 So, Marie and Katie, thank you both very much. It's always good to have you both here.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, more than 18,000 workers fighting against the auto industry, we asked the president of a local \uc2 UAW\uc2  chapter, what exactly are they demanding, in order to get back to work?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY RYERSON, TRUCK DRIVER, MICHIGAN: You know, we're fighting for what we feel we deserve. So, we're going to stand with our union 100 percent. And, you know, we want to make sure that we're all treated equal and paid the same way, and have the same benefits.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And how long are all of you going to strike with?\par \par \uc2 RYERSON: As long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  expanding their strike today to dozens more facilities. This is getting very intense to situation. It's across 20 states now, but only \uc2 GM\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  plants are involved right now. The union says that Ford has agreed to some of their demands, including cost of living wages, to strike over plant workers, better profit sharing and improved job security.\par \par \uc2 Tony Totty is president of UAW Local 14, he's standing by, first with the background and both sides of this story, Fox Business correspondent Jeff Flock reporting live from a Ford facility in Wayne, Michigan outside Detroit. Hi, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, \uc2 FOX\uc2  BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Martha, hello to you. Workers here cheered by this news about Ford maybe they're closer than we thought, to reach the some sort of a deal. As you point out though, the folks at \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and \uc2 General Motors\uc2  still in the crosshairs, 38 new facilities named today. These are parts distribution facilities. I think it's 20 of them from \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and 18 of them from \uc2 General Motors\uc2 . These are places where you get parts for cars that are broken down could be a problem.\par \par \uc2 For though issue, this statement about the news that came out today says that they're working diligently with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  to reach a deal although, "we are making progress in some areas, we still have significant gaps to close some key economic issues." Still cheered are there Ford workers like Sandy Kirklin. Come on in here, Sandy.\par \par \uc2 You know, Sandy was 10 years old down there in Texas when you wanted to become an auto worker.\par \par \uc2 SANDY KIRKLIN, STRIKING FORD WORKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Any regrets?\par \par \uc2 KIRKLIN: Not a one.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: How do you feel today about this news about Ford?\par \par \uc2 KIRKLIN: It's a good start. It's a really good happy day. I just want to get it done, suck and go back to work.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: They're kind of playing one against the other, don't you think now? I mean, you've been an auto worker for 25 years.\par \par \uc2 KIRKLIN: 25 plus years, yes.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: They've never done it this way before.\par \par \uc2 KIRKLIN: We've never been on strike since, what, 30 years, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Yes. How do you feel about this coming down? Do you support the union leadership? They've been pretty out there.\par \par \uc2 KIRKLIN: Yes, they have. They're strong, they're strong willed, and that's what we need.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Tell me last, what's your priority? What's your number one priority?\par \par \uc2 KIRKLIN: Cola and tears, everybody needs to make the same amount of money. Even if it takes only two years to get to it, everybody needs to be equal in there. Otherwise, you're pitting against each other.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: You think you've been treated fairly.\par \par \uc2 KIRKLIN: I have the new hires now.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Appreciate it. Thanks so much. Yes, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KIRKLIN: You too.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Glad you get your lifetime dream there. Careful what you wish for sometimes.\par \par \uc2 KIRKLIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Sandy, I wanted to be a TV news reporter. Careful what you wish for, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That was good news for us. Hi, Jeff. Thank you very much Jeff Flock in Detroit. Let's bring in Tony Toddy, president of UAW Local 14. Tony, thank you very much for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 This is obviously a stressful situation that's going on here. It could have an impact on the American economy, even the presidential election some say, depending on how far this goes. This is a statement that we got from \uc2 General Motors\uc2  today. "Today's strike escalation by the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is top leadership is unnecessary. The decision to strike an additional 18 of our facility is affecting more than 3,000 team members plus their families and communities as validity to the blueprint identified in last night's leaked texts -- that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  leadership is manipulating the bargaining process for their own personal agendas."\par \par \uc2 He's referring to some communications from the comms director at \uc2 UAW\uc2  saying, you know, if we can't keep them -- and do. What do you say to \uc2 GM\uc2  about --\par \par \uc2 TONY TOTTY, PRESIDENT, \uc2 UAW\uc2  LOCAL 14: Well, thank you for having me. And if you want to know about or, you know, good look at manipulating things, they've been manipulating our contract for over 10 years where our members like Sandy said in the previous hit, are working under unfair agreement. These temporary employees that hire in at $16.67 an hour in work years on end as temporary employees, not, you know, being able to plan for their future because they don't get to contribute to a 401(k), they don't get the profit sharing that other members enjoy. It's really \uc2 General Motors\uc2  that have been manipulating the situation for quite some time.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How long do those temporary workers work before they get those kinds of advantages that the permanent workers get?\par \par \uc2 TOTTY: Well, I've heard the strike line up to seven years. And \uc2 General Motors\uc2  contractually, it's supposed to be two years. But if you go 30 days with a broken time, you got to start over again. And here at my facility, they did that. They had members that worked 16 months. And right before Christmas, they laid them off for 60 days, and then they brought him back and they had to restart their time.\par \par \uc2 So it's games like that, that they've been manipulating contract.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So what's the hourly --\par \par \uc2 TOTTY: $16.67 are temporary employees and it's about $15.80 at \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Is it true that one of the demands is $32 hour workweek?\par \par \uc2 That's, you know, we'd submit a lot of demands, just like they used to submit a lot of demands. Do we think that that's actually going to play itself into our contract, probably not. Does it really highlight the fact that our members work 6, 7, 8 day -- or 6, 7 days a week, 12 hours a day, yes.\par \par \uc2 Our members are tired and they want some vacation. Do I think 32 hours is it, we don't know. We'll see what comes at the table.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The car companies say they offered a 40% increase in wages. That sounds pretty high.\par \par \uc2 TOTTY: Say that again.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A 40% increase in wages as part of this deal.\par \par \uc2 TOTTY: They have not out for that. \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  received 36% increase in the last four years for a salary of $29 million -- companies have not -- 40% pay increase.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. That's what I'm saying, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is asking for, according to what we found, a 32 hour work week and 40% wage increase.\par \par \uc2 TORRY: OK. So, you know, that's part of negotiations and they'll figure it out at the table. We have positive news coming out of Ford. \uc2 Chuck Browning\uc2 , the vice president, is doing a great job along with Laura Dickerson to make progress. And that's what we want.\par \par \uc2 It's just like Sandy said, we want to get back to work. We're not professional strikers. We're professional auto workers and we want to get back to it. So hopefully, cooler heads prevail. We need \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and \uc2 General Motors\uc2  to get real at the table, so you no more facilities don't go out.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. And then we saw the \uc2 UAW\uc2  ripped up some of those proposals and threw them in the trash on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . So we'll see where all this goes. Thank you very much, Tony. Good to have you with us today and good luck to both sides here. Hope we can all get back to work soon.\par \par \uc2 TOTTY: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. So Saudi Arabia and Israel on the cusp of a historic peace deal, which has the potential to reshape the Middle East. That is the message from Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , who sat down for an exclusive interview with Bret Baier. Here's a preview airing tonight on Special Report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: That's not to minimize the hurdles before -- I think when you have three leaders and three countries - - really want a result. And say it under President Biden, Saudi Arabia under the Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , and Israel under my premiership. I think that really raises the possibility that we'll succeed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So this is a very big development. And we saw Bret's interview with MBS, the Crown Prince in Saudi Arabia. We bring in Israel's Minister of Strategic Affairs, Ron Dermer. Good to have you with us, Ron.\par \par \uc2 RON DERMER, MINISTER OF STRATEGIC AFFAIRS OF ISRAEL: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 DERMER: So, I guess, let me play this soundbite because both sides in these interviews, MBS and then Prime Minister Netanyahu, saying that they are getting closer every day. This is an absolutely historic deal and it really builds on the Abraham Accords deals from the Trump administration, and both sides that they want it to get closer to a Saudi Israeli deal. Here's MBS talking about how he sees it. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMED BIN SALMAN\uc2 , SAUDI CROWN PRINCE: We have a breakthrough of reaching that gives the Palestinians their needs and make the region calm. We're going to work with whoever there.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS ANCHOR: Not to go too far but the concessions Israel wouldn't have to give the Palestinians what would --\par \par \uc2 BIN SALMAN: Yes, I don't have to describe things because I want to see really a good life for the Palestinians, so --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So what kind of good life for the Palestinians do the Israelis see as part of this deal?\par \par \uc2 DERMER: Well, we'd like -- for our own interests, we'd like to have the Palestinians have a good life. And certainly, the Crown Prince of Saudi Arabia would like that, and certainly the United States administration would want that. And we're going to have to work out those details and what does it mean in the context of an overall piece.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of moving parts to this. A lot of different pieces that we have to put together on the table and see if we can make this historic breakthrough, but I don't think people fully understand what this means. If you have a Saudi Israeli peace, it is really the beginning of the end of the Arab Israeli conflict that has gone on for a century. It will bring about, I think, a broader reconciliation between Judaism and Islam. And I think once you bring in the Arab world into peace with Israel, the chances of us getting a political settlement with the Palestinians will go way up.\par \par \uc2 That's the approach the Prime Minister has taken -- time because the approach -- we need to first have peace with the Palestinians and only if we get that peace agreement with the Palestinians will we get a broader peace for the Arab world. That would be great if the Palestinians wanted peace, but thus far they have refused peace offers time and again.\par \par \uc2 So I think his approach has been, let's go and get peace for the Arab world. And the Palestinians should be part of the process, which is what he said today at the United Nations, but they should not have a veto over the process. I'm optimistic, the prime minister is optimistic. The prime minister would like to see this peace, President Biden would like to see this peace.\par \par \uc2 And as you heard, and kudos to Bret for getting an interview both with the Crown Prince of Saudi Arabia and the Israeli prime minister, and you'll hear it later as well. When you have all these three leaders, who are I think, capable of bringing this to a finish line, I think we have a good chance to get there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask you this. President Biden is going to meet with Prime Minister Netanyahu before the end of the year at the White House, that that seems to be the plan. Now they have not met yet, which is very unusual. Do you believe that President Biden will sign off on an agreement between the Saudis and Israel that doesn't include a Palestinian state?\par \par \uc2 DERMER: Well, I think what the Biden administration wants to see, and would like we would like to see, frankly, is that we do not close the door on a political settlement with the Palestinians in the future. As the prime minister said, what we want is to make peace with the broader Arab world, and then use that peace to make peace with the Palestinians as well.\par \par \uc2 So, we don't have an interest in closing that door. The Americans don't have an interest in closing that door. And certainly Saudi Arabia doesn't have an interest. I think it's going to be better for everybody. And it's good, Martha, that the Palestinians have taken a different approach now than they took three years ago when you had the Abraham Accords, where they basically attacked it, and they didn't want to be part of it at all.\par \par \uc2 Now they have in Saudi Arabia, a country that would like to help them, we would like to -- situation for them as well. Americans would so let's figure out -- both have this historic breakthrough and leave open the possibility of a peace agreement with the Palestinians in the future.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Ron Dermer, thank you very much. This is just the beginning of a huge story. So I hope you'll come back.\par \par \uc2 DERMER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And take us through this as it moves forward and that'd be a historic meeting with President Biden. Clearly Israel and Saudi Arabia are open to having some kind of agreement, which is historic, as you say, Ron.\par \par \uc2 DERMER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We look forward to following it. Thank you very much. Good to see you, Ron.\par \par \uc2 So the Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  dress code debacle takes a surprising new twist. There he is, working in his shorts and hoodie. Carley Shimkus is joining us next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TYRUS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: We're seeing this bend and traditions in our country, and it's not just a sentence everywhere. Like there's certain rules and things and decorum that you need to. Listen, I dress the way I dress and I said to you if you're going to wear a hat backwards and you're going to come on TV and represent, you have to have your A game every time. It has have to be a reason that they're like -- oh, this is TV.\par \par \uc2 And he dresses like he just got out of a bad breakup. It's not even casual. I mean, he's really dresses like she left him, you know, and like he's been watching nothing but love movies all weekend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking just moments ago, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent Charles McGonigal has pled guilty to covering up payments from a source, who pled guilty in a separate case last month to working with a Russian oligarch who was under US sanctions. That is not allowed.\par \par \uc2 McGonigal ran the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is Counter Intelligence Division, reportedly had a role in launching the investigation into former President Trump. What a story from this former \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent. We will follow it.\par \par \uc2 The other big story brewing right now in the Atlantic Ocean. Tropical storm warnings in effect for roughly 7 million Americans right now as Ophelia brews off the east coast, about to dump heavy rain and the potential for flash flooding. Fox Weather's Brandy Campbell live in Cape Hatteras, North Carolina right now. Hi, Brandy.\par \par \uc2 BRANDY CAMPBELL, FOX WEATHER REPORTER: Hey, Martha. Well, as you can see, the conditions here are very active out here and Tropical Storm Ophelia is only going to cause them to continue to deteriorate until we get into tomorrow evening.\par \par \uc2 As you can see behind me, we have some humongous waves that have been formed because of this storm. It really looks amazing, whitecap and you have the sea foam that's been blown on shore as well. So that is one aspect of this storm that is really taking over in the other side right now. We're not having any rain for now but the wind speeds, that's the other side of this storm.\par \par \uc2 So you guys come to me, I have the anemometer in my hand. I'm going to give you a quick reading of just how fast the wind is going right now. It's going, I'm getting 20, 31 miles, 33 mile per hour wind speeds right now. But the top wind gusts we could get to be about 60 miles per hour. So it's a windy event and you have high waves but also they're worried about the overwash going into the roads, impacting people, also asking folks to stay off of the roads because it's not going to be ideal.\par \par \uc2 So right now the storm is expected to make landfall sometime early in the morning, Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wow. OK. Thank you very much. Obviously high alert all along the coast and we'll continue to keep an eye on that. Thanks, Brandy.\par \par \uc2 So the top two Senate Democrats currently at odds now over a new policy that lets Pennsylvania Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  show up on the floor of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , the greatest deliberative body in the world, in shorts and hoodies. Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  deciding not to enforce the dress code for Senators anymore. Why you might ask, is number two \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  also asking that question. He says, he's "concerned" about that. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): I think we need to have standards when it comes to what we're worrying on the floor of the Senate. I can't understand exactly what he was thinking at that point. I want to give him the benefit of the doubt and to speak to him. But I think the Senate needs to act on this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, wouldn't it be great to hear from \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  to answer that question? What were you thinking, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 ? "\uc2 Fox\uc2  and Friends First" co-host, Carley Shimkus is here. Carley, always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 CARLEY SHIMKUS, FOX NEWS HOST: Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, there's a lot of pushback. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  pushing back also, I think they're going to reverse this rule.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Yes. Well, I think Durbin speaks for a lot of people when he says it's probably a good idea to have a standard on the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . I don't think that's too much to ask. And sometimes I wonder how someone would react if you take somebody from the past and show them the world today, the country today. Pluck somebody from the 1800s and show them 2023, and I think that they would be very impressed with technological advancements. But they'd also say, well, you've really let yourself go.\par \par \uc2 And I mean that in a lot of ways. You really let yourself go because look at the cities across the country, homeless camps, littering, public parks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: You know, graffiti covering once beautiful buildings. There was cocaine found at the White House this year and, yes, also, sweat pants now allowed on the Senate floor. So there is a sloppiness and a decline, and a lack of respect seeping into this country. And it's sad and I think this is another example of it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And he mocks it, you know? He's like, oh, you're not -- you don't care about, you know, important things. But the problem is that it is exactly as you say, Carley, it's an indication of other things.\par \par \uc2 And Peggy Noonan writes today, great piece in the Wall Street Journal. I highly recommend everybody take a look at it. It's called the "Senator's Shorts and America's Decline." She's drawing a line between these two things. She says we want to be respected but no longer think we need to be respectable. We're in a crisis of political comportment. We're witnessing the rise of the classless. Our politicians are becoming degenerate. This has been happening for a while but gets worse as the country coarsens. We are defining deviancy ever downward. And the question is what changes any of this?\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Yes. You know, Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  posted a tweet and he said, the world is watching the Senate floor. And I thought that was such a good and simple way of summing up why this matters. And the positive of it is that there is major pushback. So, what changes this may be the pushback (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 But I was also thinking about why \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  likes to dress like this. And I think it's because -- I think it's because he's trying to look like a blue collar -- we all know that -- or a lot of Americans out there are saying, wait a second, I'm a waitress, I work at an auto body shop. I have to put a uniform on every morning. I can't wear sweatpants to work and dress how I want.\par \par \uc2 And I do it because I put that uniform on because I respect my job. And I want to dress the part. And Senator Fetterman is no less or more important than they are, so he should dress the part as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, it's really thumbing your nose at other people who feel like you have to dress for the position, you have to respect where you are. You know, another headline of casual new dress code doesn't suit the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , the getup veers so far into the grunge zone that -- the Pennsylvania Democrat probably couldn't wear it to work as a teacher --\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- in many schools --\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: And I think this is getting a reaction to it. He's saying, you know, why should you care about the way I dressed? So the better question is, why don't you -- dress?\par \par \uc2 Yes, you're one of 100 that's represented -- people in America, you should respect the role given by the people of Pennsylvania,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trust him and Zelenskyy because Zelenskyy, you know, comes into the White House in his green, you know, military dress, right? And he's making a statement. He's saying I am a military leader right now of the people in my country and the battle --\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- where people are being killed in the streets and bombed in their homes, right? That's why he takes that different stance as a world leader when he visits our country. Very stark contrast to \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's point.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Carley, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Thank you so much, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: More of THE STORY right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Before we go here on THE STORY, we want to say thank you to our chairman \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2 , who has announced that he's transitioning from chair of our parent company, \uc2 Fox Corporation\uc2 , in November to the chairman emeritus \uc2 Fox Corp\uc2 . His son Lachlan will be chairman of both companies and we congratulate him on his promotion today.\par \par \uc2 And today at the Wall Street Journal, the Editorial Board, saluted \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  in this piece. They write, we're especially grateful that Mr. Murdoch and the other \uc2 News Corp\uc2  and Dow Jones executives have supported us when we've come under political or other pressure to change our views or our coverage. This is true whether the pressure has come from the Chinese Communist Party, business titans, woke journalists or American presidents.\par \par \uc2 As he turns to the helm of the company over to his son Lachlan, he said on Thursday, \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2 , that he will, "stay involved every day in the contest of ideas." That is what our work is all about, we say. So we hope that we hope to see him do just that every day. We couldn't have said it better than the Wall Street Journal Editorial Board did today.\par \par \uc2 So that is THE STORY for today, Friday, September 22nd. Thank you very much for being with us we hope that those hurricanes and tropical storms leave you safe on the East Coast. THE STORY will be back here on Monday. Have a great weekend, everybody.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230923ej9m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Senate Dressing Down; Interview With Rep. Bob Good (R-VA); Tropical Storm Targets East Coast; Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  Charged With Bribery; Border Surge; \uc2 UAW\uc2  Strike Widens\b0\par\par\uc2 Rick Reichmuth, Brandy Campbell, Peter Doocy, Griff Jenkins, Chad  Pergram, Jeff Flock, Charles Payne \par \uc2 7,949 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN, PRESIDENT, UNITED AUTO WORKERS: \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  was trying to...\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: On strike and a tear, the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  targeting \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  with a wider strike, and I mean wider, 38 locations across 20 states, thousands upon thousands, many picket lines today, with more and more walking out.\par \par \uc2 Is the threat to America's economy closing in?\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone, I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is YOUR WORLD.\par \par \uc2 Let's get to the right to FOX Business' Jeff Flock, who's in Wayne, Michigan, with the very latest -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Charles, hello to you.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this never really has been done before, where the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is working and negotiating with all of the companies at once and in some ways playing them against each other. That's what happened today when they decided not to strike anymore at Ford, but instead hit \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and \uc2 General Motors\uc2  hard.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at the numbers, the strike today expanding by 38 additional facilities, 20 at \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , 18 at \uc2 General Motors\uc2 . And these are parts distribution facilities, as you report, 20 states all across the country, everywhere from Oregon to Florida to California to New York.\par \par \uc2 Yes, these are the facilities that actually supply the parts for cars already on the road. We have got a live picture at one of those facilities. It is just not far from where we are here. It's a \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  location in the town of Center Line, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 This is one of the 38 where picket lines are now forming. The thing is, it's not that many workers. There were already 13,000 workers on strike. This is another 5,000. And, also, these are locations without that many workers, and it holds the prospect that maybe management could come in and try to do those workers' jobs.\par \par \uc2 These are important facilities, though. They're cash cows for the auto companies, and that could put a dent in their bottom line real quick. Shawn Fain, the president of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , telling us it's the companies' fault.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAIN: The companies own this. This is on their shoulders. Everything that's happening right now is on their shoulders. They own it, because they chose not to take care of the membership.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: And, Charles, the auto companies, specifically \uc2 GM\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , both not too happy about this.\par \par \uc2 In fact, I will read you one of their statements, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  saying that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  seems "more concerned about pursuing its own political agendas than negotiating in the best interest of the employees and the sustainability of U.S. operations, given the market's fierce competition."\par \par \uc2 I would end with this, Charles. This is not your father's \uc2 UAW\uc2  automakers negotiation, negotiating in public, picket lines in multiple locations, different companies, really putting feet to the fire out there. We will see how it works. As I said, it's a little bit of a -- I don't know. I guess it's a kind of a chancy strategy...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: ... in that you could bring potentially folks from management in to work on these jobs.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. No.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: That doesn't sometimes go very well. We will see -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, it looks like they're making their final shot. That's for sure. Thanks a lot, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 So, with the strike expanding, will the economy -- not will, but how much will it be hurt? Because we know economic losses just from the first week now at $1.6 billion.\par \par \uc2 Folks, let's bring an economist Steve Moore with more.\par \par \uc2 Steve, I have got a report from one firm that says, if all members struck, went on strike, every 10 days would be a loss of $5.6 billion. So, everyone watching this show will be impacted by this from one degree to another.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: Well, that's for sure, Charles.\par \par \uc2 And this is a high-stakes showdown for both the auto companies and the autoworkers, because, now, on the one hand, the autoworkers are saying, look, we have lost so much money in the last couple of years because of Bidenflation. They feel like they're $6,000 down in terms of their real take-home pay.\par \par \uc2 So I feel their pain. And I think a lot of people do. And I think Biden's really to blame for that. On the other hand, these auto companies, I don't think they can sustain a long-term strike like this. And, remember, Charles, right now, there's less than 20,000 -- that's still a lot -- of the employees on strike.\par \par \uc2 But there are 100,000 that could go on strike. And that would cause real damage to the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. And, again, according to Anderson Economics, at least $5.6 billion every 10 days.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: To your point, it's an awkward position, right?\par \par \uc2 Because this is one of these rare occasions -- I think it's Gallup or Pew, one of them, I think Gallup, 75 percent of Americans are behind the \uc2 UAW\uc2 . And I think, Steve, you hit on it. Everyone understands the economic pain, the 40-year high inflation. No matter who you are, your paycheck doesn't go nearly as far.\par \par \uc2 And then they have got the extra incentive, because, with the government forcing everyone to just own E.V.s, at some point, they're going to lose their jobs. These jobs are going to go away over the next five years.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, there's no question about this.\par \par \uc2 And it's interesting, because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has said so many times, as you know, Charles, he says, I'm the best friend the unions ever had. Well, actually, no, he's not the best president for unions. He's been one of the worst president for union workers, because it's not just \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers.\par \par \uc2 It's union workers in all sorts of industrial jobs and service jobs that have really been hit by this inflation. So, that's why they're asking for a 38 percent pay raise, plus a four-day workweek, because they feel like they have to catch up.\par \par \uc2 But you're right. I mean, this is also an industry. I don't get it, Charles. I mean, I went to buy a new car a couple of weeks ago. I asked if you have any gas cars, and all they wanted to tell me was the E.V.s. And I'm not against the E.V.s. But 80 to 90 percent of the people want to buy a gas car.\par \par \uc2 And the car companies, they're making cars that people don't want, Charles. That's not a very good strategy to make money.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And the scary part of it is, they're making cars that people don't want with the approval of the government. Steve, this is the more nefarious part of it.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I believe that if we can't -- if the incentives don't work, if the olive branch doesn't work, the 7,500 bucks or whatever, then, at some point, they're going to make it illegal to drive internal combustion engine cars and we will be left with no choice.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, I think you're right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you don't have to just imagine this happening. You're seeing, in some states, for example, California and Washington state are already trying to abolish gas cars after -- in the next five or six years. And so here's why this story doesn't have a happy ending.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the number -- the percentage of cars today that are E.V.s that are being purchased right now, it's 10 percent or less. And so how do you make money trying to sell cars that consumers have clearly said, we don't want them?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Now look, the hybrids would be a good solution here, I think, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: And when I talk to some car manufacturers and car distributors, they say, that's what people want. I'm just frustrated that the only company right now, Charles, that's making gas cars is \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , and they're a Japanese company. They're not an American company.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. They don't really believe in the E.V. thing that much, to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 Steve, your audio is a little messed up here. We will cut it a little short, but appreciate your wisdom all the time.\par \par \uc2 And, folks, just a reminder. We went through this before. In the 1920s, electric vehicles had a greater market share than gasoline cars. And guess what? Eventually, we picked gasoline cars. If it's up to America, this whole thing would be a moot point. But it's not.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, read them and weep, stocks hit hard, with all the major averages down for the week. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  signals -- signaling that it is not quite done yet with hiking rates. And, of course, that had investors in a selling mood, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 And we have seen the surge on camera, now the reality in the numbers. New record for illegal border crossings for the year. With millions passing through, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  insists passing the blame. We're going to get reaction from Border Patrol union president Brandon Judd coming up.\par \par \uc2 Also pockets full of cash, gold, \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2 , it sounds like a scene from "The Sopranos." Well, it just so happens that a Senate Democrat has been charged for a second time on corruption-related defenses. The personal and political fallout -- coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: New Jersey Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 , the chairman of the powerful Foreign Relations Committee, is being indicted, along with his wife, on bribery charges.\par \par \uc2 To FOX's Chad Pergram with the very latest -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Charles, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The second big indictment for Menendez in a decade, and it's major that the chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee is indicted on charges that he used his position to help a foreign government.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAMIAN WILLIAMS\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY, SOUTHERN DISTRICT OF NEW YORK: The indictment alleges that Senator Menendez used his power and influence, including his leadership role on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, to benefit the government of Egypt in various ways.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The feds accuses Menendez of accepting hundreds of thousands of dollars in an effort to benefit the Egyptian government and help other businessmen.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say they found a half-million dollars in cash stuffed into a jacket and a safe. That raised eyebrows among those who know New Jersey politics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R-NJ), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Where folks were taking cash bribes and stuffed them in suit jackets, blazers, windbreakers in their homes, and when the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched, they found them.\par \par \uc2 I never once heard anybody have a good explanation, especially someone in public office, for having that kind of cash in their home.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Menendez accused the feds of a smear campaign and misrepresented what he does in office.\par \par \uc2 The feds believe the senator was trying to interfere with their investigation. Menendez was defiant when cleared six years ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 ROBERT MENENDEZ\uc2  (D-NJ): I have made my share of mistakes, but my mistakes were never a crime. I have learned through this process a lot about our system of justice. It is truly a system of justice you can afford.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Senate Democratic Caucus rules require a senator give up their gavel if they are indicted for a felony. These problems could put the New Jersey seat in play next year -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Chad, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. attorney's describing the alleged take as pockets stuffed with cash and a lot of gold. So just how serious are these charges?\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from Deputy Assistant Attorney General Tom Dupree.\par \par \uc2 I got to tell you, Tom, as soon as I started reading the article, I'm like, is this the script from "The Sopranos"? It's absolutely amazing, \uc2 Mercedes- Benz\uc2 , 13 gold bars, half-a-million dollars in cash. Just -- we obviously have an initial take on it, but walk us through the legal take on this.\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE, FORMER JUSTICE DEPARTMENT OFFICIAL: Well, Charles, my initial legal take is the same as yours. This is a stunning, breathtaking indictment, if the allegations in this case are true.\par \par \uc2 This indictment paints a story of old-school straight-up corruption. You have got bribery, you have got fraud, you have got extortion. As you know, you have got large amounts of cash stuffed into jacket pockets. You have got luxury cars. You have got gold bars. It's extraordinary that he was able to allegedly get away with this corrupt scheme for so long.\par \par \uc2 And this indictment is just an overwhelming show of force. And I think the senator, if even a fraction of these allegations are proven true, is looking at the possibility of substantial jail time.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It's interesting. I think you just hit upon one of the problems here.\par \par \uc2 If -- again, if he's found guilty of these charges, the fact that he had gotten away with this so long, it feels like the same thing, right? These folks get -- they become more brazen, right? It becomes OK. It's -- you worry. You're probably careful the first time that. But, after a while, you just kind of believe you're above the law, I guess, or just -- I mean, who's going to go to -- particularly after he beat those charges before, he probably felt, if -- again, if he's guilty, that he had some sort of Teflon.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Yes, I think you may be right about that.\par \par \uc2 He may have said, look, I can work the system. I can engage in this sort of behavior and get away with it. And so the second time, he went even well beyond what he was alleged to have done the first time. You would think that having such a close brush with the law and having to defend yourself at trial and be accused by prosecutors of all sorts of crimes might prompt you to walk the straight narrow and turn over a new leaf and not risk your liberty by engaging in the same sort of behavior.\par \par \uc2 But, again, if these allegations are true, -- and we have to keep in mind he's presumed innocent until proven guilty. But if these allegations are true, it's just stunning to me that he thought he could get away with it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, you get on your hands and knees every day and say, OK, I will never make that mistake again.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, fingerprints were found on the gold bar. There was an allegation also of his wife getting a no-work job, one of these jobs where you just get paid, but you never have to show up again, right out of the scene of "The Sopranos."\par \par \uc2 And so this means -- it feels like this indictment is pretty strong. I don't think they would bring it against a sitting senator, and one as powerful, in fact, as \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 , if they really didn't feel there's a very strong case.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 And, look, the fact that the U.S. attorney has alleged so many different criminal actions here -- this is not one simple exchange in the parking lot. I mean, this is a yearslong, corrupt scheme that encompass all sorts of different alleged acts that Menendez did on behalf of his co-defendants, on behalf of the Egyptian government, the things he allegedly got in return cash, gold bars, luxury vehicles, a no-show job.\par \par \uc2 This is a broad, brazen criminal scheme that is alleged in this indictment. Frankly, I'm just surprised that this didn't come to light sooner and this indictment didn't drop earlier.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, let me jump in here because, reportedly, U.S. Senator Menendez has decided to step down temporarily from his position as chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee until the matter of his allegations have been resolved, from \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And so, again, Chad Pergram alluded to this as we were coming into the segment, just the first of many shoes perhaps to drop. What happens now from a legal perspective? What's the next step?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Well, there will be the first typical housekeeping events that we have grown accustomed to. In other words, Menendez will go in.\par \par \uc2 Presumably, he will be booked, he will be fingerprinted. He will enter an initial plea, which I assume will be not guilty. But I think really the first thing that has to happen is, Menendez and his lawyers have to huddle up and decide whether or not they're actually going to try to fight this, in the face of what seems to be overwhelming allegations of criminal activity, or if they want to try to cut a deal, maybe negotiate something that I don't think would allow him to keep his Senate seat, but might keep him out of jail, possibly.\par \par \uc2 I'm not sure the government wants to offer that, given the scope of what's going on here. But if you're Menendez's lawyers, I think you have to swallow hard, level with your client and say, this might be the time to fold and to cut a deal.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 Ironically, Tom, I was having a conversation with someone today about this, because the DOJ has been under a lot of pressure, particularly for favoritism, allegations of favoritism, that failed deal with the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many are saying this would be the ideal case for the DOJ to say, we're not -- we don't show any favoritism. We will go after a senator, even a Democrat, and maybe because of the circus, the situation around the DOJ itself, maybe there won't be a deal offered to Menendez. Maybe this is a good chance for the DOJ to throw the book at someone.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 That may be the attitude they're taking to say that, look, when you have evidence of a criminal scheme like this, we're going to get tough, we're going to throw the book at him.\par \par \uc2 And to your point earlier, I think this is exactly the sort of case that the DOJ would not have brought unless they were 100 percent convinced they could prove guilt beyond a reasonable doubt. They already took one shot at Menendez previously and missed. I don't think they're going to make that mistake again.\par \par \uc2 And you can tell from the thoroughness of this indictment and all the alleged acts that were committed, all the evidence they have gathered, fingerprints, testimony, everything, the DOJ is very confident that it has an overwhelming case against Senator Menendez.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All the T's have been crossed and the I's have been dotted.\par \par \uc2 Tom, thank you very much. I always appreciate your legal wisdom and knowledge.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Thank you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, to the border, where illegal crossings are shifting into overdrive.\par \par \uc2 And the Biden White House is trying to shift the blame, right, Griff Jenkins?\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 We have got breaking new record-shattering numbers here at the border, along with heart-wrenching video of migrants trying to crawl through the razor wire here, along with new video of more migrants on the way.\par \par \uc2 All that's coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Border encounters just topping last year's record high that. Did the blame game in Washington just hit a new low?\par \par \uc2 And the East Coast, bracing for the impact from North Carolina to New York. A tropical storm is coming.\par \par \uc2 And we're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So the same week we see more than 50,000 migrants passing through the border, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is passing the buck.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Peter Doocy is at the White House with details -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And even though they have been in office here at the Biden White House for about 2.5 years, they keep going back to the first day, when President Biden put forward an immigration proposal that Republicans voted down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: MAGA Republican congressmen and my predecessor spent four years gutting the immigration system under my predecessor.\par \par \uc2 They continue to undermine our border security today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So President Biden says Republicans are to blame for the images that you are seeing every day at the border.\par \par \uc2 And White House officials say they have been consistent from the beginning, that, if you come unlawfully, you will be turned around. However, just in the last couple weeks, we have noticed that the words coming out of the folks talking about immigration here don't always match the video that we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: When you spoke last month and you said we are stopping the flow at the border, is 10,000 migrants in a single day stopping the flow?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I just mentioned 250 individuals have been stopped who do not have the legal pathway from coming in. And that's the work of our law enforcement. That's the work of our law enforcement at the border.\par \par \uc2 Remember, House Republicans are trying to cut that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president and the vice president, who is still in charge of addressing the root causes of migration, had an event here at the White House this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 It was not about the border or immigration, instead about gun safety -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Peter, thank you very much. You're doing an amazing job, by the way.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, let's get right to Virginia Republican Congressman Bob Good.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, the president's blaming your party. What's your response?\par \par \uc2 REP. BOB GOOD (R-VA): Well, that's typical of the president. He doesn't take responsibility for any of the harm that he's doing that's literally destroying the country.\par \par \uc2 And top of the heap there is what he's doing at the border. I would submit that never in the history of the country has our own president done more to intentionally harm the United States than what this president has done with the border,