o, three confirmed cases of tuberculosis in the last 24 hours. That makes it incredibly more difficult to mitigate a highly contagious disease while dealing with overwhelming numbers.\par \par \uc2 Charles, we will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Griff, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So how are border agents dealing with all of this?\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in National Border Patrol Council President Brandon Judd to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, I would imagine, as you see those -- that train rapidly approaching, it's just nonstop. Again, I just wonder how -- and we salute you, you and the other folks that -- who are doing this job, but it's -- not only does it seem thankless. It just seems endless.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: It is endless.\par \par \uc2 And what we're doing is complete insanity. We're not actually protecting our borders. We are deploying. You will notice right there you're not seeing any Border Patrol agents out there. And the reason that you're not is because we're all doing administrative work. We're processing, we're transporting, we're doing detention security, we're doing hospital watch.\par \par \uc2 We're doing everything but what we're supposed to be doing, which is protecting the American people out on the front lines. We can't do our jobs because this has been and the administration continues to release people.\par \par \uc2 But when you see them lie to the American people, when you see him say that they are turning people back, that is a complete lie and he knows that. And that's what allows this to continue to happen. He knows darn good and well that we are releasing upwards of 80 percent of the people that cross our borders illegally. We are rewarding them with the release into the United States.\par \par \uc2 And as long as we continue to do that, these people are going to continue to come, the American people are going to be less safe, fentanyl is going to flow into our country, and more of our citizens are going to die. It's monstrous what this administration continues to do, especially when he can change it tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 He could stop this tomorrow with the proper policy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Brandon, you mentioned everyone can see this, and certainly everyone in America, but it feels like everyone around the world can see it.\par \par \uc2 And it seems to act as an invitation of itself. If you are somewhere and you want to get here, it seems like now is the perfect time to make your move.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: They do see it around the world. That's why we're apprehending people from 160 different nations.\par \par \uc2 That's why we're seeing these cartels that are able to generate a record profit. They're able to go throughout the entire world, not just to Central America, not just into Mexico, not just to Venezuela. They're going throughout the entire world, and they're advertising their services. And people are paying upwards of $50,000 to be smuggled to the United States.\par \par \uc2 These individuals are going into indentured servitude. That doesn't include the sex trafficking that is taking place. It doesn't include the deaths that are happening. If this administration truly cared about life, they would do what is necessary to uphold the rule of law. If we uphold the rule of law, more people will be safe.\par \par \uc2 More people will -- less people will die in the rivers. Less people will die in the deserts. Less of our citizens would die of fentanyl if this administration truly cared about people. But because they continue to lie, because they're allowed to lie, that's why we see this chaos existing.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I have got just 30 seconds, but I -- you see now political infighting and some Democrats even calling out the White House. Is that a sliver of hope that maybe sanity will come in and prevail and we really will have true border security?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: It's not -- not until you have the activists pull themselves out, not until this administration stops pandering to the activists that want this open border.\par \par \uc2 Look at the West Wing. Look at what's in DHS, the political appointees in the West Wing. They are open border activists. The political appointees in DHS, they are open border activists. They want this. That's what they know the base wants, and they want to energize their base. They know, if they pull back, that their base won't go out to vote.\par \par \uc2 So they continue to pander to that base.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow, we just -- as you were talking, we're watching a man navigate the razor wire with a child on his shoulders. This is absolutely remarkable. It's just wow. And, of course, the dangers that the young folks and the women face coming up, and just this scene alone, just it should galvanize everyone to make sure we protect our borders.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, we always appreciate you, man. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Thank you, Charles. Have a good one.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: By the way, we're going to be back live at the border tomorrow, as the homeland security secretary says that he's heading down.\par \par \uc2 Kinney County, Texas, Sheriff Brad Coe on that visit and the record search 10:00 a.m. Eastern on "Cavuto Live."\par \par \uc2 And to that Capitol Hill dress code debate. Why Joe Manchin is giving a thumbs-down to dressing down.\par \par \uc2 And folks bracing for a tropical storm set to bear down on the East Coast. The forecast is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: This just in, a source confirming to FOX News that President Biden will be heading to Michigan on Tuesday to speak in support of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There's enormous -- there has been enormous pressure building on him to visit the state in the wake of the strikes. Now, that will put him there a day before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is expected to address union workers in Detroit.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, preparations under way as tropical storm warnings are now in effect along the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 FOX Weather's Brandy Campbell is in Cape Hatteras, North Carolina, with the latest there -- Brandy.\par \par \uc2 BRANDY CAMPBELL, FOX WEATHER MULTIMEDIA JOURNALIST: Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 As you can see, the conditions out here are pretty wild on the island right now. They're only going to continue to deteriorate, as Ophelia, Tropical Storm Ophelia, gets closer to us.\par \par \uc2 I just want to show you what's happening behind me. As you can see, we are on the shore. The waves out there, it is an angry ocean. I checked one of the buoys in the past hour, and it was showing that the wave heights out there were getting close to 20 feet tall, obviously not as high here on the shore, but still very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Thankfully, we haven't seen anyone trying to get in and surf out here on the shore. The other obvious component we are seeing is the wind speeds. It's been nonstop since last night and going into today. That will continue.\par \par \uc2 I do have an anemometer. So I'm going to go ahead and measure some wind speeds right here while we're live to just give you an idea of how gusty it is. Right now, it is measuring about 23, but we have also gotten it up into the 30s, and know that we can get sustained winds at the peak of this up to 45 miles per hour, gusting up to 60.\par \par \uc2 So, this will continue as Ophelia gets closer, making landfall early tomorrow morning. We're also worried about overwash causing storm surge as well in this area, but, again, a beautiful sight behind me, also a little treacherous. So, folks in here need to stay home, avoid travel at this time as well -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, be careful out there.\par \par \uc2 Brandy Campbell in Cape Hatteras, North Carolina, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 So, how bad could things get?\par \par \uc2 FOX chief meteorologist Rick Reichmuth has been tracking