and West Virginia Democrat Senator Joe Manchin just the latest to try to reverse it.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to break it all down, the Hill White House reporter Alex Gangitano.\par \par \uc2 And, Alex, I got to tell you, real quick, Hive & Colony, they sell suits. They say, why would a man wear a suit? Because it shows respect to everyone around them, that they put time and effort, and it's respect around -- about people around you and your circumstance.\par \par \uc2 This is a nut -- nutty thing that they're trying to do here.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GANGITANO, THE HILL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And what's even more kind of unbelievable about all of this is that the Senate has always been such a place of tradition. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  values that tradition so much. And the fact that Schumer would support this move and go forward with this, I mean, the whole thing is fascinating to watch.\par \par \uc2 We actually heard the White House earlier today talk about it, because President Biden was a longtime senator, of course. He's a Senate guy and said, he dresses that than, I think, a lot of us, but it's not for us to step into what the Senate has decided here.\par \par \uc2 So, it's definitely an interesting move, and I don't think we will -- this will be slowing down in terms of criticism any time soon.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, we have heard Senator Collins suggests she may wear a bikini.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, is there a point where we all kind of say, OK, it's going too far? Will we need something that drastic?\par \par \uc2 GANGITANO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it does raise an interesting point. Obviously, Collins was joking. But what women can wear -- if women show up in, I don't know, exercise leggings or whatever, that would obviously be a bit inappropriate. I mean, senators and lawmen -- and House members have sometimes peeked out of the Cloakroom in gym clothes...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GANGITANO: ... but not walked around the halls regularly.\par \par \uc2 So I think there is a point that there has to be some sort of parameters, or else this will turn into everyone can wear whatever they want. But, of course, women are sometimes held to a different standard or whatever else than...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Absolutely, yes.\par \par \uc2 GANGITANO: So, I think Collins was speaking to that point.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, we're really scared, worried about the slippery slope.\par \par \uc2 Well, someone who can afford the suits, Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 , in a lot of hot water right now, a half-a-million in cash, gold bars, a Mercedes. It's -- your thoughts on this?\par \par \uc2 GANGITANO: There's a lot of repercussions here.\par \par \uc2 Menendez has been kind of in this position before, but he has a really powerful post. So he has to now forego his Senate Foreign Relations chairman slot. And that's big for him.\par \par \uc2 I mean -- and what's interesting is that Senator Cardin, who will have to slip in and assume that role, is resigning, or retiring, at the end of this term. So, then we're looking at Senator Shaheen for a chance.\par \par \uc2 And this is a huge chairmanship that is something that Menendez -- he's a foreign policy guy. That's a huge part of his legacy in the Senate. So it really is a huge blow for him to give that up.\par \par \uc2 And Schumer, I think, is right now trying to toe the line of defending somebody in his caucus, but also doing the right thing and taking away his chairmanship.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 Alex, thank you very much. Really, really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GANGITANO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And, of course, folks, don't forget we will be back here tomorrow morning 10:00 a.m. Eastern time with "Cavuto Live." And make sure you check me out.\par \par \uc2 The market had an ugly, ugly day, an ugly, ugly week, but don't despair. I have got your back.\par \par \uc2 But, for right now, I keep you -- I give you to the great hands of the folks at "THE FIVE." They're next.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230923ej9m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Rep. Tony Gonzales (R-TX); Migrant Crisis Continues; Republicans Try to Avert Shutdown; \uc2 UAW\uc2  Strike Continues; Interview With Former Rep. \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2  (R-CA); Attorney General Grilled on Capitol Hill\b0\par\par\uc2 Dan Hoffman, Bill Melugin, Eric Shawn, Aishah Hasnie, Grady  Trimble, David Spunt, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 9,038 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): It's one thing to have a gun charge in Delaware. That doesn't involve the president of the United States. But \uc2 Burisma\uc2 , oh, my. That goes right to the White House. We can't have that.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SHEILA JACKSON LEE\uc2  (D-TX): Does the rhetoric regarding the Biden case have any basis in reality?\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: No, it does not.\par \par \uc2 I don't recollect the answer to that question, but the \uc2 FBI\uc2  works for the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): I'm sorry. I'm sorry. You don't recollect -- you don't recollect whether you have talked with anybody at \uc2 FBI\uc2  headquarters about an investigation of the president's son?\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC SWALWELL (D-CA): I welcome you to the law firm of Insurrection LLP, where they work every single day on behalf of one client, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. JEFF VAN DREW (R-NJ): Political weaponization of the DOJ is on you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All righty, that went well, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  grilled on Capitol Hill, facing all those questions over all of the handling of the investigations into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and then \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and kind of everyone in between, charges that the Justice Department has been weaponized as well.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we're going to be speaking to one of the lawmakers doing that questioning, California Congressman \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. Glad to have you.\par \par \uc2 I want to go first to David Spunt on Capitol Hill, keeping track of this drama. And it was a multihour drama.\par \par \uc2 David, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 The attorney general knew from walking in the building today that he had a tall task ahead of him. And one of those big, tall tasks that I'm talking about is trying to convince skeptical Republicans that there is not a dual system of justice, especially when the hearing opened like this. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: The fix is in. Even with the face-saving indictment last week of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , everyone knows the fix is in. Four-and-a-half years, four- and-a-half years, the Department of Justice has been investigating Mr. Biden, an investigation run by David Weiss, an investigation that limited the number of witnesses agents could interview.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Neil, as predicted, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was the big topic in the room, specifically the investigation being run by special counsel David Weiss.\par \par \uc2 Republicans pushed the attorney general on potential White House contacts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Has anyone from the White House provided direction at any time to you personally or to any senior officials at the DOJ regarding how the Hunter Biden investigation was to be carried out?\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: No.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Each time the attorney general was asked about the Hunter Biden probe, he said he wasn't in charge of it. Ask those questions of David Weiss.\par \par \uc2 He did say, though, this broadly about that so-called dual system of justice.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: Everyone is treated alike. I understand that people may not understand why particular investigations are conducted in particular ways until all the facts come out. That's what we have a court for, and all of the explanations will come out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: And with respect to special counsel David Weiss, Judiciary Chairman Jim Jordan told me today that he fully expects to see David Weiss for a transcribed interview on October 11 with public testimony the following week on October 18.\par \par \uc2 He said that agreement, though, was made before David Weiss was elevated to special counsel. He still hopes that Weiss honors that commitment. No word, though, from David Weiss' team at this hour when I checked -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And it was Garland who elevated him to that role, right?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: It was. Garland appointed him to be special counsel last month when David Weiss asked him to do so.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 All right, are you a lawyer now, David, or are on your way to that?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: I am not a lawyer. Some people say that I should have considered it, but thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I think you...\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: No, I'm not a lawyer. I have great respect for lawyers, though.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I think you have already passed the equivalent of the bar.\par \par \uc2 David, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A man who is, and a very good one, \uc2 John Yoo\uc2 , the former deputy assistant attorney general.\par \par \uc2 John, what came of this today? I mean, you're obviously seeing Republicans try to paint him as a guy who has a double standard, go full-throttle rough on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , take a pass on your present boss, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Did they succeed?\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO\uc2 , FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: First, I'm glad you and David are not lawyers, because that's the only reason most Americans can understand what you're saying these days.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Hardly. Hardly.\par \par \uc2 YOO: Here's what...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 YOO: I think, if you were \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 's aides, you would tell him when he walked out the door of that hearing mission accomplished. You were on the stand essentially for six hours and made no news. He let nothing slip.\par \par \uc2 He answered no questions, right? You can't even put your finger on anything he said. Every -- the answer to every question was, I left it all in the special counsel's hands. I left it all David Weiss' hands.\par \par \uc2 And so, in a way, Garland achieved what he wanted to do. You could see the members of the House on the Republican side were getting very frustrated with him, as you saw in those clips. But the other lesson that comes out of it is that there's still a lot of questions that are hanging over this investigation, both into Trump and into Biden.\par \par \uc2 Are the two cases being treated differently? How does the attorney general explain the testimony from all these whistle-blowers who say that their efforts to get to the bottom of the Hunter Biden scandal were slow-walked or frustrated by higher Justice Department officials?\par \par \uc2 So, to get those answers, what we learn from today's hearing is that the House is not going to get any help from the Justice Department. It's got to conduct the investigation on its own and get to the bottom of it by themselves.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. So they won't have any help from the Justice Department in that.\par \par \uc2 But should that be the way it goes?\par \par \uc2 YOO: Well, here's the interesting thing, because the Justice Department still needs to be helpful, because, as we have talked about before, the House has now converted this from oversight of the Justice Department to find out if there is any obstruction, interference with the investigation into Hunter to see whether there's any political motivation into the investigation into former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And that's turned into an impeachment investigation. That raises a whole lot of other questions. It raises the constitutional stakes. And on this one, the Justice Department would be necessary to help Congress, for example, go after people who refuse to testify.\par \par \uc2 What happens when the House, this committee, calls \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  to testify, as they eventually will, and Hunter gives them the back of the hand and refuses to show up? It's the Justice Department that will have to drag him into court.\par \par \uc2 Are they going to help the House now with their impeachment investigation, or are they going to side with President Biden and refuse to cooperate?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Maybe it bends credulity here, and maybe this is just the way it is.\par \par \uc2 When Garland was repeatedly saying that he is not the president's lawyer, that he didn't share or even discuss, if I heard it correctly, John, the whole Hunter Biden situation with him, is that viable to you? I mean, technically, that's the way it's supposed to work, but I'm sure Weiss and his staff had alerted, certainly, Garland of the indictments, the gun indictments, that were pending are going to be the three of them against \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And it would be tempting to think that Garland would just give the president a heads-up, I hate to break this to you, sir, but your son is going to be facing these three indictments that could land him in jail for up to 25 years. None of that happened?\par \par \uc2 YOO: Once again, you remind us, I wish some of the members of the House had gone to the Cavuto school of law, because they should have asked exactly those questions.\par \par \uc2 They could have said, for example, well, Mr. Garland, you say you're not President Biden's lawyer, but, under the Constitution, you work for President Biden. It's actually President Biden who's in charge of enforcing the law. You're saying not just Garland, but nobody in the Justice Department ever talked to anybody in the White House about these cases?\par \par \uc2 Maybe Garland didn't, but there's a deputy attorney general. There are all these aides to both of them. If they didn't talk about it with them, who's really in charge of making these decisions to keep David Weiss?\par \par \uc2 One thing that came out of the hearings that was interesting is that when Garland showed up for the job, Weiss -- it had already been decided to keep Weiss on to investigate the Hunter-Biden case. Nobody seems to be responsible for that decision.\par \par \uc2 That raises this question, who's really calling the shots within the executive branch about these cases? For Garland to say, it's not me, actually makes you wonder, well, who's doing your job then? Because that's Garland's job, reporting to President Biden, ultimately.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what if Garland is also saying, as the attorney general of the United States and the lawyer for the people, or however you want to interpret it, I don't want to present to my boss that possible conflict, so I'm going to have one of my underlings do it?\par \par \uc2 What about that?\par \par \uc2 YOO: That's exactly what I think very well might have happened, is that Garland might say, I never talked to the president.\par \par \uc2 But as we have seen from some of the earlier testimony with the whistle- blowers...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 YOO: ... they keep pointing a finger at people in the deputy attorney general's office, maybe even the deputy attorney general herself. Maybe they're the ones who are talking to the White House about this.\par \par \uc2 But, if they weren't, that's also troublesome, that's also problematic, because the president ultimately is the one under the Constitution who's in charge of all of this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 Well, John, I love your legal compliments to me, but you're the real deal, my friend, and a brilliant real deal, at that. This doesn't end, my friend, to your point.\par \par \uc2 John Yoo\uc2 , the former deputy assistant attorney general, \uc2 U.C. Berkeley\uc2  law, former Justice Thomas law clerk, you see, that's gravitas right there, not just the Neil Cavuto of "L.A. Law" fame.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have got Congressman \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2  joining us, the California Republican, House Judiciary Committee member, had a chance to do some of the questioning today.\par \par \uc2 How did you come away from all of this, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA (R-CA): Well, Neil, this may come as a surprise to you after the last few minutes, but it came away great.\par \par \uc2 The attorney general telling us he's not in charge, he's not in charge of the prosecution of the former president, he's not in charge of the investigation or even the coordination of the various U.S. attorneys who have to cooperate with the special prosecutor, Weiss, he is not in charge, that leaves us, appropriately, looking downstream to those who are clearly acting.\par \par \uc2 As he defended his own integrity today, he really threw the offices of the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  appropriately under the bus for their mishandling of these cases. By saying he isn't doing it, and, obviously, they're not getting done right, it's clear that there are problems at the Department of Justice, including various political appointees, U.S. attorneys, and, of course, as you mentioned, some of these deputies who are also political appointees.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know the procedure here, Congressman, but the charge of slow-walking the Hunter Biden investigation, everything attached to it, you could make the argument here that that was a deliberate strategy, having little to do with politics, and everything to do with what he perceived his role to be, let others move this, I'm out of this.\par \par \uc2 But you're arguing, by appearing so out of it, to the point of being aloof, gremlins took charge.\par \par \uc2 ISSA: Well, gremlins took charge before he ever got there. Let's remember that U.S. attorney Weiss, he had this case since 2018.\par \par \uc2 It is not the attorney general that let toll agreements lapse, allowed \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  to get out of serious felonies and millions of dollars that he cheated the government and the American people. These are things that happened under a career U.S. attorney, a man who's been very cordially in and out of the top job going back decades.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2007 or 2009, he was the acting U.S. attorney.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 ISSA: This is a career person in Delaware, the home state of the long- serving, simultaneous-serving senator.\par \par \uc2 So the idea that it isn't a cordial relationship and maybe an outright fear of taking on the then-senator, the then-vice president, and the now- president could in fact be ingrained in that Delaware office, that small office with a few judges, and really mostly investigating corporate America in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, if I can switch gears, Congressman, I know there was this Republican powwow that's supposed to take place among all the Republican congress men and women as to what and how to move forward to prevent this budget or government potential shutdown in a couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 What came of it? Do you know where that stands right now?\par \par \uc2 ISSA: Well, Neil, I'm glad you asked. It's ongoing. I'm leaving. When I take the mic off, I'm running downstairs.\par \par \uc2 But let me tell you what we know. We know that the speaker has made it clear that he will work hard and diligently with Republicans as long as it takes, but not to shut down the government. Ultimately, we will either be united to pass this bill and send it to the Senate, or the Democrats will get a major concession to keep the government open.\par \par \uc2 We are not going to shut down the government, or at least Republican majorities are not going to shut down the government, because it's inefficient.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But not all your colleagues are of that mind-set, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, a few of them want his head, the speaker's head. They're disappointed in the way he's handling this. Some are now going to try to take advantage of that feature that would allow just a single one of them to say, you're out of here.\par \par \uc2 ISSA: I have been down this road before of two things, one, people who want to get rid of the speaker, but have no viable alternative, and, secondly, those who say shut down the government who, just a few days after it shuts down, start crying about how people are being hurt in their district.\par \par \uc2 The fact is, no government shutdown is good. No government shutdown accomplishes something for those who shut it down. And that's why the speaker's made it clear, we, the vast majority of Republicans, will not be party to shutting down the government. We will do everything we can to keep it open.\par \par \uc2 That's what the speaker has said. That's what the speaker's downstairs doing as we speak.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, as you know, some of those more conservative members, sir, are the ones saying, this is on principle and we just can't cave on this.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 ISSA: There's nobody more principled than many of the people who are solidly on board to keeping the government open.\par \par \uc2 You are not principled just because you're one of five that won't vote for a rule to move debate forward. You're not one principled just because you hold out endlessly when the vast majority of Republicans have picked a speaker. Those are people I respect with, I serve with.\par \par \uc2 I have pushed hard to help them have meaningful parts of this Congress. But, at the end of the day, they are no more and no less principled than the many people on the other side of the issue.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, so very quickly on that -- I'm sorry to bother you with going to this meeting.\par \par \uc2 ISSA: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I have heard that this idea of a short-term solution to fund the government through October 31, ironically, Halloween, but who is counting dates, but that is dead on arrival, and that this group has said, no, not happening.\par \par \uc2 You say?\par \par \uc2 ISSA: There are 435 potential votes. Only 222 of them are Republicans. So, at the end of the day, the speaker wants to do this with his fellow Republicans.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 ISSA: I support the speaker, even if he has to get votes from the other side, and even if there's a price to pay.\par \par \uc2 But the people that will be at blame for a bigger spending bill will be those who wouldn't take a very good compromise done by Republicans among Republicans as yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 All right, \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2 , I will let you get to that meeting. I appreciate it, sir.\par \par \uc2 ISSA: Of course, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2  of the beautiful state of California.\par \par \uc2 All right, how are things going in Michigan right now, day six of that U.S. strike? Some interesting developments. Grady Trimble is there -- Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, there is a new offer from one of the automakers, but is it a major breakthrough that's going to end this strike, or is it just another proposal for the \uc2 UAW\uc2  to throw in the trash?\par \par \uc2 We will tell you what we know next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, day six, but some positive signs we might be getting closer to dealing with this.\par \par \uc2 I'm talking about the \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike, but I certainly don't want to get ahead of my proverbial schemes.\par \par \uc2 Grady Trimble following the latest developments in Wayne, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Grady, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Neil, I can tell you that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  says it got an offer from \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , but it's not telling us what's in that offer. Neither is the automaker, just saying that they're reviewing that proposal.\par \par \uc2 I can also tell you that \uc2 General Motors\uc2  is firing back today, the company's president penning an op-ed for the Detroit Free Press, in his words, dispelling some of the myths that the union is spreading.\par \par \uc2 Mark Reuss\uc2  says: "Amid the rhetoric of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ' leadership, the flow of misinformation is not fair to anyone." He says, under GM's last proposal for the U.S. from Thursday of last week, about 85 percent of current represented employees would earn a base wage of approximately $82000 a year.\par \par \uc2 So that's what the management says, but what are the workers saying about what they want and what they will do about it? Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: More money, better benefits, shorter work hours, better life.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm hoping Ford does the right thing and they get something worked out and negotiated, and we all get ready to go back to work.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: But if there's no deal?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Then I guess we will be striking a little longer.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From my understanding, it's just going to hit like a domino, basically, so just got to keep doing it, keep doing it, keep doing it until we get what we want.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: The Big Three automakers warned about the ripple effects of this strike, and now we are seeing them.\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  says that it is idling a plant in Kansas, which means they will be laying off around 2,000 workers there. Ford already laid off 600 workers at its plant right here in Michigan. And \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  says it's temporarily laying off about 70 employees in Ohio because of the strike, but there could be more on the way; 300 people, they say, could get laid off in Indiana.\par \par \uc2 So we will see what comes of that deal from \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , as we hear a one- man choir here who seems to be in good spirits, despite this strike now stretching in day six -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He has a great voice. Well, so do you. But, I mean, it's a battle. It's a Godzilla-Rodan going at it here.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Grady Trimble, on that.\par \par \uc2 So, it could be an interesting development. We will see.\par \par \uc2 To Mark Tepper on the financial implications of all of this.\par \par \uc2 Mark, for those who aren't familiar, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  did meet today and, as expected, didn't raise interest rates, hinted at more to come, maybe watching things like this, because there have been some very generous worker settlements over the last six months, if you think about it, dating back to Chrysler's 40 -- I should go more to the point about the UPS deal that rewarded workers with an upwards of 40 percent pay raise, generous pay raises for pilots and the like.\par \par \uc2 Could be a minimum of 21 percent here for workers, and a lot more of them. Do you think that is going to prove where inflation just sticks for a while?\par \par \uc2 MARK TEPPER, \uc2 STRATEGIC WEALTH PARTNERS\uc2 : Yes, and I think that's what the Fed thinks as well.\par \par \uc2 I mean, with, look all of these pay raises, as we have talked about in the past, Neil, they're all inflationary. A lot of these costs are going to get passed on to the consumer. And the Fed has had a really difficult time fighting the inflation battle, because they have had to deal with fiscal policy that was kind of working against them.\par \par \uc2 Now they're dealing with the possibility of some super high wage inflation. I mean, the last I heard is, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  you turn down 20 percent raises. So...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: I mean, I don't know if they're holding out for 30-plus. I don't know where this is going to end up.\par \par \uc2 But the interesting thing here, Neil, is that all economic policies, no matter what it is, they're always going to impact several different groups over a long, short, and medium-term period of time. And that's really what we're looking at right here. I mean, whether they're -- whether this strike continues for 30, 60, 90 days, there will be negative consequences if that happens.\par \par \uc2 But there's also negative consequences if there's a 40 percent raise as well. So you kind of got to look at all these things and say, wow, we are now in a situation where we will realize some consequences from the way this all ends up shaking out, no matter what.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In other words, that those increases are passed along to consumers. And we have seen that in a variety of other products. So that could make the Fed's job more difficult.\par \par \uc2 I have raised this with some of the prominent presidential candidates and what they would do about it. \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  has taken a look at the deal itself, at least what the strikers want. But he's also uncovered another element that could prove worrisome. This is from \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What would you do as president with something like this?\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R-NJ), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, one of the things I'd make clear right away to both sides is this. People deserve to be paid for their level of expertise and skill and for how hard they work.\par \par \uc2 When they ask to be paid for a 40-hour workweek, but only do 32 hours of work, that's a problem, Neil, I mean, and I think it's going to wind up turning Americans off. Everybody would love to be able to get paid for when they don't work. But, in this instance, that's not what it should be.\par \par \uc2 And so I have a problem with the unions, the approach the unions taking. Let's pay people what they deserve for their skill, their expertise and how hard they work. But let's not be paying for people not to work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, sure enough, we are getting hints now out of some auto dealers around the country that the price of vehicles, particularly some of the very popular ones, like the Jeep Grand Cherokee and the Ford Bronco and others, will likely see their sticker prices go up by 10 percent or more.\par \par \uc2 So the spillover is on, right?\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And to address what Governor Christie was talking about, in defense of the autoworkers, I don't think they want to work 20 percent less because they're lazy and they want to sit on the couch and get paid for a full week of work.\par \par \uc2 What's at play here is the Biden administration. The \uc2 EPA\uc2  is rolling out E.V. mandates. You have got California wants 100 percent of new vehicles sold by 2035 to be E.V.s. And it is well-known, given all the research papers I have read on E.V.s, that E.V.s require 30 percent less labor to produce.\par \par \uc2 So you have these autoworkers. They're playing nice in the sandbox, right? They're going to work every day. They're willing to build E.V.s. But at the same point in time, as they build those, they are destroying their jobs.\par \par \uc2 So, from that standpoint, I got to defend them a little bit. However, that's really not the best economic policy. The way an economy grows is, you focus on maximizing output. You focus on maximizing production. When you do that, there are jobs that are created, wages go up. So that's the way we should be approaching this.\par \par \uc2 But I do believe this is not about laziness. This is really about them wanting some job security as our government shoves E.V.s down everyone's throats, including theirs.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we'll see what happens. We're a long way from done on this.\par \par \uc2 Mark Tepper, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We told you about what the industry is already doing. All the Big Three have announced some decision to close or shutter ancillary plants that have nothing to do with being the targets of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , because they are directly affected.\par \par \uc2 In other words, if you make supplies for cars or parts for cars, your facility is shut down. And it is widening.\par \par \uc2 Julie Manchester joins us, The Hill national politics reporter.\par \par \uc2 Julia, the companies are saying here, there's a limit to what we can do. If it's not 21 percent that was offered and the union isn't taking it, there might be a middle ground in the 21 percent to the 40 percent or so that the industry is looking at.\par \par \uc2 But where do you see this going?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE HILL: Yes, it doesn't seem like there's really any compromise in sight.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have heard about some movement. However, in terms of where this goes, look, we're going to be watching to see how these various lawmakers from Michigan, for example, from these states where it's -- the strike is really impacting -- or is really having an impact, what they're going to do on this.\par \par \uc2 And what President Biden eventually does on this, it doesn't seem like the president has a direct hand in those negotiations, but as someone who calls himself the most pro-union president in American history, you would think that there might be some jockeying or lobbying behind the scenes on this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: One of the things that has been very clear in these negotiations thus far is the copycat syndrome.\par \par \uc2 You get while the getting's good, and I get that. But, normally, where there is one generous union deal, others try to emulate that. For example, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  working with UPS for what was a generous pay package that was ultimately organized there, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  picking up on that now.\par \par \uc2 Pilots who feel right now that they were due their return, and they're getting it. I'm just wondering here. That does complicate the inflation picture. And Americans will have to pay more for airline tickets and maybe UPS packages and the like.\par \par \uc2 But to hear the strikers and workers say, not nearly as much as the media has pounded, that the impact of this is not going to be that bad. And they're saying all will be fine, we won't go back to the '70s.\par \par \uc2 But when you look at these pay packages, they're much more generous than the ones in the '70s. So what happened?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 And, I mean, look, it seems like every indicator shows that we will and Americans will feel the direct impact of this. And, look, this isn't the only strike happening here in the U.S. right now. Of course, in California, you have the writers and actors strike going on as well. So the president really has his hands full with dealing with both of these twin strikes at the same time.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I mean, this is going to really complicate, I think, the messaging on the economy for the president, particularly on inflation. I mean, here's someone who's trying to bank on being a very pro-union president, while, at the same time, message on promoting the economy and the growth in the economy.\par \par \uc2 And if Americans aren't feeling that the prices of goods and services aren't going -- aren't going to go down, they're going to blame the president. And it seems like these concessions could eventually have the -- could very much eventually lead to higher inflation, which will complicate matters for him.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, I caught up with Nikki Haley on this very subject.\par \par \uc2 And she echoed what you just said there, that there will be something akin to a taxpayer revolt. This is from Nikki Haley. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: When you have the most pro-union president and he touts that he is emboldening the unions, this is what you get.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you who pays for it is the taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you and would you draw a line, Ambassador, if you were president of the United States for union jobs versus any jobs?\par \par \uc2 This president clearly does prioritize union jobs. And he's made very clear here that union workers deserve more, that their pay increases have not come close to the success and the money that all of these auto companies have enjoyed. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, I tell you in South Carolina, as governor, I mean, we were building planes with \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . We were building more BMWs in any place in the world.\par \par \uc2 I recruited \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2 . I recruited Volvo. We had five international tire companies, but I was a union-buster. I didn't want to bring in companies that were unionized, simply because I didn't want to have that change the environment in our state.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Yes, that's interesting, because that seems to be a very different strategy than the strategy that's been -- being taken by the Republican front-runner, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Of course, the Republican Party isn't all around pro-union. There's definitely some tension there. However, next week, President Trump is doing some counterprogramming with the FOX Business debate by addressing \uc2 UAW\uc2  voters or -- and workers in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 I think that goes to show that he has some sort of a pull with some of these more blue-collar voters in some of these states like Pennsylvania, like Michigan, who may sometimes vote Republican.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. Well, to your point \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , the union leadership at the time didn't flip over him, but the union members certainly did, and he won them in 1980 and 1984.\par \par \uc2 So we will watch closely.\par \par \uc2 Julia, it's so good of you to come by. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime, Republicans are meeting right now to avoid a government shutdown, but there's a division in their ranks. That's probably an understatement.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie has more -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil, that's right.\par \par \uc2 They are huddling right below us right now, trying to figure out some way to fund the government. But, of course, no answers yet. And it looks like they're going to have to work over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you more coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, meet and greet or meet and, well, fight?\par \par \uc2 We don't know. We do know that Republicans are gathering right now behind closed doors, I understand, to find out a way to avert a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie is there with more -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Hey there, Neil. That's right.\par \par \uc2 So that meeting started at 4:00. They have been in there now for about 30 minutes. And I just was told by our Hill team down there that Texas Congressman Chip Roy, who, of course, was one of the authors of the C.R. deal, just ran out of that room, didn't speak to the press.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers are preparing to work over the weekend. They are getting ready to really sit here and talk to each other and try to figure this out. One thing is clear, though. That original C.R. deal is dead. Right now, negotiators working with the holdouts. The holdouts want to move forward with those 12 appropriations bills. And they want a top-line number at pre- COVID spending levels before they consider any kind of short-term C.R.\par \par \uc2 But, Neil, some of them don't want a C.R. at all. And that is a huge problem for Speaker McCarthy. And I asked him what he plans to do about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But how do you convince folks that don't want to extend the budget as is? How do you convince those people?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The same way we did it when we passed the parents bill of rights, the same way we did it when we passed border security, the same way we did it when we -- when Washington wanted to decriminalize all the crime.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Meanwhile, Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  is lobbying Republicans to oppose any C.R. He's also threatening to file a motion to vacate the speaker if McCarthy goes to Democrats for help.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): If it's a coalition that involves using Democrats to roll conservatives to advance \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's spending priorities, then that will likely mark the beginning of a series of votes on a motion to vacate.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: What if he flips enough Republicans? What if he flips enough Republicans?\par \par \uc2 GAETZ: We're not going to have sufficient Republican support for a continuing resolution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes, McCarthy, of course, feels very differently. He thinks he can get 218 Republicans, he told me earlier today.\par \par \uc2 He doesn't know if that's going to happen tomorrow, but he plans to get it done -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you for that, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie following those developments.\par \par \uc2 I just want to go to Eric Shawn, who's been following the president of the United States as he wraps up his trip to New York City right now for \uc2 U.N\uc2 .- related events and a big fund-raiser.\par \par \uc2 Eric, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Yes, well, the big issue here in New York today has to do with Mayor Eric Adams.\par \par \uc2 As you say, Neil, the president's been meeting with foreign leaders. They held the traditional welcoming dinner last night at the \uc2 Metropolitan Museum of Art\uc2 . Scores of international diplomats attended that. I saw the line of cars went for two miles all the way down Park Avenue, but he's not meeting with the mayor.\par \par \uc2 The president's still in the city at this hour, but not meeting with the mayor has raised a lot of questions. Adams is not on his itinerary. The president did sit down with fellow Democrat New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  last night, and they did discuss the city's growing migrant crisis together.\par \par \uc2 She tweeted -- quote -- "Had a very productive conversation with President Biden tonight regarding some of our specific requests for help with the migrant crisis. Also commended him on his leadership at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . today."\par \par \uc2 Well, Mayor Adams, who has been very outspoken and critical about the toll the migrant influx is taking on his city, says, despite that, he does not see the lack of a presidential welcome as a snub.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), MAYOR OF NEW YORK: We are in constant communication with the White House. The goal is to get the items that we have identified, and that includes a decompression strategy at the border, receiving the funding that we deserve, calling the state of emergency, allowing people to work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Well, this comes as there was a rowdy protest against a migrant shelter in the borough of Staten Island last night. Take a look at this video.\par \par \uc2 It resulted in 10 arrests. And the police officer, they tell us, was injured also during this. A crowd of residents tried to block a bus that was delivering migrants to a former senior facility that is now turned into a migrant shelter. The folks in that neighborhood say they just don't want the migrants there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAL MONFORTE, PROTESTER: I live 150 feet away from this building. And I'm concerned, yes. I have a daughter. We have people in our community who have children.\par \par \uc2 People walk up the blocks to the main bust stop here. We don't know who they are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Well, about 10,000 migrants a month continue to come to New York City.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Neil, 60,000 are in the city's care. Mayor Adam says it will eventually cost taxpayers of New York City about $12 billion to take care of the migrants over the next two years -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Incredible.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, my friend, Eric Shawn, in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Very quickly, I want to pass along some news concerning \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . He has already said that he plans to plead not guilty to those three gun charges that could land him, if found guilty on all of them, in jail for as much as 25 years. Having said that, though, he is still scheduled to appear in a federal court in Delaware on September 26, though that would be next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Don't have details on time, but the judge wanted him there. So he's going to be there.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, taking a look at border issues that are going way beyond the border.\par \par \uc2 Bill Melugin has more from Eagle Pass -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: A mass illegal crossing here in Eagle Pass today, Neil, as thousands of migrants from all around the world crashed the border here at once. And now the mayor of Eagle Pass is calling on the White House to change policy.\par \par \uc2 The shocking video coming up right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so much for calm returning to the border right now.\par \par \uc2 Bill Melugin discovering anything but, stunning developments to share with you. He's in Eagle Pass, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Bill.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Today, we witnessed one of the biggest mass illegal crossings we have ever seen during our coverage here at the Southern border. I just said that on Monday because we had a big group. Well, that's been eclipsed about 48 hours later.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to these images from our FOX News drone. Take a look at this. This is earlier today here in Eagle Pass, as a massive line of migrants crossed illegally from Piedras Negras, Mexico, into Eagle Pass, thousands of them from all over the world.\par \par \uc2 And as you watch this footage, I want viewers to understand this wasn't just over like 10 or 15 minutes. This went on for two hours straight, just a mass illegal crossing. We talked to several of these people as they were coming across. Most of them said they were from Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 They all said they arrived in Northern Mexico on a train this morning, then started walking towards the border. Take a look at this ground footage we shot as these people were coming in. They were excited. They were cheering. They were waving to my camera as I was filming them, again, chanting "Venezuela, Venezuela."\par \par \uc2 Thousands of them all coming at the same time, predominantly single adult men. There were some family units as well, but they were predominantly single adult men. The mayor of Eagle Pass, a Democrat, has now declared a state of emergency out here, and he is calling on the Biden White House to change policy and change it fast.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROLANDO SALINAS, MAYOR OF EAGLE PASS, TEXAS: Let these people know that we're a nation of laws. People can't just come in like this. Right now, you see all thousands of people coming in without consequence.\par \par \uc2 And the word is getting out. They cross here. The word gets out back in their countries and they keep coming. Why? Because there's no consequences to come in here illegally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And a lot more migrants may be on the way. Take a look at this video out of Tapachula, Southern Mexico, right on the border with Guatemala.\par \par \uc2 What you're looking at is a mass of Haitian migrants overrunning and almost stampeding a refugee center down there, demanding expedited visas, so they could move throughout the country and get up here to the U.S.-Mexico border. They obviously want to cross illegally into the United States.\par \par \uc2 They caused a bit of a stampede. They ran over some people, and two people ended up being hospitalized as a result of that rush down there in Tapachula.\par \par \uc2 And back out here live, our sources are telling us this big group that crossed today, that ain't it. A lot of the migrants were telling us there's a lot more people in Piedras Negras, across the river from us. You can expect more groups in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican military has been deployed all day long across the river right across from us. They have done absolutely nothing to stop these illegal crossings. We have literally watched them with their soldiers, watching people run by them right into the river. It's essentially a photo of having those soldiers over there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, they do nothing at all. The idea was that they were going to stop them. They're not doing anything like that.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I guess it was supposed to be a show of force because they have got soldiers and Mexican police dotted along the river.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And you would think, oh, it looks intimidating. If I try to go into the river, they might tackle me and arrest me. That's not happening. They're just watching them go into the water.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing, Bill. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Bill Melugin.\par \par \uc2 You don't have to remind my next guest. He sees it right in his district, Congressman Tony Gonzales of Texas.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, what triggered this again?\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): Yes, the cartels control the border, and all hell has broken loose on the Southern border.\par \par \uc2 Two years ago today, we were seeing very similar images that we saw in Del Rio, where there were thousands of Haitians under a bridge. Fast-forward two years, and things have only gotten worse. Now you're seeing Venezuelans. It's pure lawlessness.\par \par \uc2 I'm getting reports that there were nearly 3,000 people that crossed illegally today. They're expecting that same number tomorrow and maybe upwards of 10,000 by Friday. So things continue to get worse. Locally, you have got a lot of American citizens that cross back and forth to Mexico because they have relatives and business and other things.\par \par \uc2 Well, one of those bridges is near closing. So this is where Americans are impacted by this crisis. And, oh, by the way, these people aren't staying in Texas. They're going to all parts of the country. So, today, it's Eagle Pass. Tomorrow, it's your city.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What's wild about this, Congressman, if the cartels are in fact leading this, the presence of all of these Mexican troops along the border, they're not doing anything to dissuade them.\par \par \uc2 GONZALES: That's exactly right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And what I have been told is, the cartel almost carried the Biden administration for a couple rounds in June and July by keeping the numbers down. And now they have turned those numbers all the way up. I'm getting reports that there's another train on the way with thousands of people.\par \par \uc2 It looks like this train is headed to El Paso, which is also in my district. The entire border is absolutely on fire. And until we start -- this is how we solve it. We know the problem. This is how we solve it. And every president has had to do this, enforce the laws that are already on the books by surging immigration judges to the border.\par \par \uc2 And if somebody doesn't qualify for asylum, you send them back via repatriation flights. That's what the administration did two years ago, when there were thousands of Haitians under the bridge. And that's what they have to do again in order to get this situation under control.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The difference right now, Congressman, very clearly seems to be the cartels.\par \par \uc2 And they don't worry about the being videotaped or photographed. They're out there. Their presence is known, and no one is doing anything about it.\par \par \uc2 GONZALES: It's a great point.\par \par \uc2 And, Neil, it's not only -- these cartels aren't only in the Mexican side.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GONZALES: They're in the American side as well. They have included all of this in expanding what is happening.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 As always, we reach out to Homeland Security to get some sort of answer on this. Hope springs eternal that eventually our calls will be returned. That has not happened.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Gonzales, thank you very much. I do appreciate it, sir.\par \par \uc2 GONZALES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime here, Ukrainian President Zelenskyy making a pitch to the United Nations: We need help.\par \par \uc2 He might want to stop by Washington, D.C., maybe powwow with some of those Republicans gathering on all this spending stuff, because they're going to have a much tougher audience -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Boy, so many behind-closed-door meetings, secret or otherwise, so little time.\par \par \uc2 On Capitol Hill right now, senators have entered a classified briefing, presumably on Ukraine. I told you about the separate one going on with House Republicans to deal with budget and spending issues. The two could be linked by this much, money and how much we should commit to that effort.\par \par \uc2 Dan Hoffman is the former CIA station chief in Moscow, FOX News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Dan, President Zelenskyy of Ukraine, of course, has been making the pitch before the United Nations and any Western leader he can find that: We're fighting the good fight and we need the good money to fight the good fight.\par \par \uc2 But he's running into some roadblocks, most notably here in the U.S. among some politicians, particularly in the right, who are saying, enough.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of this growing movement?\par \par \uc2 DAN HOFFMAN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, look, this isn't the first time that President Zelenskyy has found his country intertwined with U.S. domestic politics, and that's a very, very uncomfortable place to be.\par \par \uc2 And it can be a very bad thing for our national security. What I expect right now is that the Biden administration is making its case to senators, and they need to do it to the -- in the House as well for why we need to provide Ukraine with another $24 billion worth of military assistance.\par \par \uc2 Now, Europe has pretty much matched our efforts, but the Biden administration has never really articulated its endgame. How does he see -- how do we see victory? What does victory look like, and what's our strategy for getting there?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, should he concern himself now that the spigot will run dry from the United States, or we don't kick in as much?\par \par \uc2 The rap here from many conservative Republicans and even a couple of the presidential candidates, why doesn't Europe do more? It's in their neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 Where do you see this going?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: Well, I think this is the challenge the Biden administration has faced.\par \par \uc2 They have received criticism, including from Republicans, that we haven't done enough fast enough. We were slow to give Ukraine artillery, and we were slow to give them the Javelin anti-tank weapons and the Abrams tanks. We still haven't given them the ATACMS attack long-range artillery. All of those things were delayed.\par \par \uc2 And that calls into question what the strategy is. If it's to continue to assist Ukraine as long as it takes -- that's what President Biden has articulated -- that's not going to cut it, I don't think, with so many competing priorities, and the fact that we're in a political meat grinder right now, where Ukraine has become fodder for conflict between Democrats and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think that's going to -- potentially, it's not going to end well for our national security.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The argument against providing more aid is, we can't keep track of the money we have already given, and we don't know where it's all going.\par \par \uc2 Do you share that concern? Should there be more accountability here?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: I don't share that concern.\par \par \uc2 I think we know what we're giving them. We're giving them a lot of weapons that are being used on the battlefield. And, look, let's be clear. Ukrainians are fighting and dying as the first line of defense against Russian aggression that would be led to Europe after they're done with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 They have cut the Russian army down to size. And it's a small percentage of our defense budget that is accomplishing that mission. And let's remember Russia is allied with our enemies, North Korea, China and Iran.\par \par \uc2 And so this is a very much worthwhile and righteous fight that Ukraine has taken on. Again, I think the criticism of the Biden administration, which is coming from Democrats and from Republicans, is that there just hasn't been a strategy to get Ukraine what they need when they need it.\par \par \uc2 They lacked what they needed for this counteroffensive. We could have given them F-16s 18 months ago.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: And Ukraine would have had the close air support that they desperately needed. And it's a shame we didn't.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will keep an eye on it.\par \par \uc2 Dan Hoffman, always good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 All right, that will do it here. The Dow down 76 points today, no hike in rates, at least at this meeting.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230921ej9k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Iranian President Addresses United Nations; Interview With Rep. \uc2 Garret Graves\uc2  (R-LA); Interview With Rep. \uc2 Jared Moskowitz\uc2  (D-FL); Can Kevin McCarthy Avoid Government Shutdown?; Tensions Mount in \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  Strike; Interview With Sen. \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  (R-NC); Migrant Crisis; Interview With Fmr. Gov. \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  (D-NJ)\b0\par\par\uc2 Gary Kaltbaum, Eric Shawn, Aishah Hasnie, Grady Trimble, Bill  Melugin, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,920 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: FOX on top of two storms putting the nation edge, thousands of migrants heading north to the border as an auto strike here could end up sending jobs south of the border.\par \par \uc2 First to the migrant crisis, Customs and Border Protection now reporting more than 35,000 encounters in just four days. Go ahead and average that out. It's about 9,000 a day. Senator \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  sponsoring a bill he says would stop these encounters in their tracks. The senator is here. And "Your World" is now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. Glad to have you. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 We will be talking to the senator in just a second. Later on in the show, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , the presidential candidate.\par \par \uc2 First to Bill Melugin in Eagle Pass, Texas, where this crush of migrants is on and all too real.\par \par \uc2 Bill, what's the latest?\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, for over two years now, we have heard the Biden White House telling migrants not to come and that the border is closed.\par \par \uc2 But the migrants just simply aren't buying it. They are coming in record numbers. We want to take a live look at our FOX drone right now. Take a look at this. This is a family that's just trying to cross the river right behind me here in Eagle Pass right now.\par \par \uc2 They have gotten stuck and they're kind of waiting it out on this middle island here. They have got a small child with them. These currents can get incredibly dangerous. A 10-year-old boy drowned right behind where we're standing last week, obviously dangerous for this family.\par \par \uc2 But the migrants, they keep crossing illegally. They're not listening to this messaging of do not come. Take a look at this video our drone team shot from the same area of an even bigger group of migrants crossing illegally in the river. They're having to form a human chain to try to stay afloat in that current.\par \par \uc2 I mentioned how dangerous it is. What's wild about this is, these crossings are happening just a football field away from the port of entry, the legal way to come into the United States, but they are ignoring that. They are deciding to come in illegally. They're not afraid of the consequences, and they believe they're going to be released into the United States if they can just step foot on U.S. soil.\par \par \uc2 Human smuggling also still rampant here at the Southern border. Take a look at this video, Texas DPS Troopers chasing an illegal immigrant from Mexico driving a stolen car full of illegal immigrants. High-speed chase. They spike-strip his tires. He ends up losing control and goes off-road into the brush.\par \par \uc2 Then everybody goes bailing out of that vehicle and runs off in all different directions. Texas DPS Troopers go into that brush. They take down the driver, as well as six illegal immigrants. The rest of them ended up getting away, making them known got-aways.\par \par \uc2 Lastly, take a look at this wild footage out of El Paso, Border Patrol agents out there arresting a group of illegal immigrants they found hiding in the city sewer system and in storm drains. What happens is, once they cross illegally, they go underground in an effort to evade law enforcement and get further into the city without apprehension.\par \par \uc2 And you can see them literally coming out of the ground after they were found and arrested by Border Patrol agents. And back out here live, Neil, you mentioned we just had a huge surge of a group of more than 2,000 illegal immigrants cross right behind us yesterday morning here in Eagle Pass.\par \par \uc2 We're getting reports from our sources another group might be on the way. There is another train heading north to Piedras Negras, Mexico, directly across the river from us right now with masses of migrants on it. If the Mexican government doesn't stop it, that train could be here by this evening, and we could see another mass crossing tonight.\par \par \uc2 Our whole team is out here. We will be keeping an eye out for that. We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You have been that way from the very beginning, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Bill Melugin in Eagle Pass.\par \par \uc2 To Senator \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  of North Carolina, who says, enough is enough, and he's got a plan, and it's a doable one, the senator kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 What do you want to do, Senator?\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC): We have got to stop the illegal flow of immigrants across the Southern border.\par \par \uc2 It's gotten worse and worse. In August, we saw the greatest number of family units in the history of the country. We just saw a report in the prior segment here about a 1-year-old who died at a day care facility. An illegally present person, was apparently involved in the fentanyl trade that was going on in a day care facility.\par \par \uc2 That's a got-away. What we're not talking about are the tens of thousands of people who are not presenting themselves to border security. They are sneaking into this country, and you have to ask yourself, because it's so easy to get released now, why are they doing it?\par \par \uc2 The obvious answer is that they are criminals and they're making neighborhoods, our communities, the Hispanic communities, the communities that they're moving to once they cross the border. It is a disaster, and I lay it all at Biden's feet, because it was under control in the prior administration.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, now the question becomes, what has emboldened migrants lately, Senator?\par \par \uc2 You have seen this infamous footage of migrants coming in a train through San Diego, no one really keeping track of where they are after they get off the train. God knows what the latest is, but it's spreading far and wide, and with little sort of reaction. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Well, I think a part of what you're seeing is the cartels playing this administration like a piano.\par \par \uc2 They advertise in foreign countries that they can get you to, to the United States. They pay a fee to the cartels, to the tune of almost a billion dollars a year, over $800 million, their most profitable line of business. And they're getting people across the border.\par \par \uc2 I think they're probably seeing thousands of people in Central Park, people going to Chicago. They're not hearing the mayor saying it's a crisis and it's unsustainable, but I think that they're seeing an entry on the Southern border that gets them anywhere in this country, and they're responding to that.\par \par \uc2 And they have got an administration that's aiding and abetting the problem. For this administration to say that they have told people not to return, it's disingenuous. It became disingenuous when he started taking down the policies that were working in the Trump administration. And we need to get them back.\par \par \uc2 It's also dishonest to say that it takes a lot to fix it. So much of the problem was created by bad decisions by this president and his administration.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I want to switch gears, if you don't mind, Senator, on -- it seems like a silly topic, but it's ignited a firestorm, a response from actually some on both sides of the aisle concerning this more relaxed dress code on the floor of the Senate. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Well, Neil, actually, there is technically no rule. It's been a tradition.\par \par \uc2 I guess it was a tradition back when they used to wear ruffled shirts and powdered wigs, but that was never a written rule. But I think it's comical when we're worried about whether or not somebody's wearing a tie. Notably, I'm not wearing a tie today. That's not because the rules supposedly changed, but I'd rather be talking about funding the government.\par \par \uc2 I'd rather be talking about the economic situation, getting to the serious work here. And if people get more serious by not wearing a tie or dressing differently, as long as they show respect to the institution, knock yourselves out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: I just don't think we need to spend a lot of time on it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so wearing sweatpants or shorts, I mean, you don't draw a line here. There are more important things to talk about, right?\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Well, I agree.\par \par \uc2 And I think, if people do that, they will see that in a future campaign ad.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I'm seeing if they can apply this to TV anchors, Senator.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And we're off to the races.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you one final thought on this shutdown threat. I know a lot of this is centered in the House right now.\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A number of measures that have come off have gone down to narrow defeat. A shutdown isn't now entirely out of the question, at the rate these test votes are going and procedural votes.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried about that?\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Yes, I am.\par \par \uc2 In fact, I have been concerned since August, because I know the rhetoric does not match anything that I would consider to be productive movement of negotiations. And McCarthy has a difficult time trying to figure out exactly what is satisfactory.\par \par \uc2 If we haven't got to a point where we think we have an agreement, but then the goalposts moved, then, of course, we're more likely to go to a shutdown than not. Hopefully, we're going to see some good discussions over the next week to avert it. But I would say the chances are very high that we may have at least a short-term shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Hopefully, Kevin McCarthy and the members of the House can avoid it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will keep an eye on that. Keeping an eye on your tie-less presentation today, though, Senator. So it's a good look. It's working.\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: And my sweatpants.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I have a feeling. I'm not going to ask.\par \par \uc2 But, Senator, always good seeing you, Senator \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: You too, Neil. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime here, we talk about a lot of migrants coming north. Well, to hear the automakers tell it, a lot of jobs that they're looking at could go south if the union keeps pushing what they call pretty extreme demands in those ongoing labor talks.\par \par \uc2 Grady Trimble following it all -- Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: But, Neil, they are sticking to those demands. No deal still on day five of this strike.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the threat from the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president to ramp it up. We will have that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Day five of this \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike, and no solution in sight, and now the threat from the \uc2 UAW\uc2  itself to target still more locales for possible strikes if progress isn't made by Friday.\par \par \uc2 Grady Trimble in the middle of it all in Wayne, Michigan -- Grady.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 For a long time now, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has been concerned about jobs moving south to Mexico. In fact, that was a key sticking point during the last strike against just \uc2 General Motors\uc2  back in 2019. It's certainly going to come up again in these contract talks, if it hasn't already.\par \par \uc2 Another major factor in these talks that's different from last time around is electric vehicles. They're becoming more and more popular. The automakers are certainly going in that direction, but the workers here are worried that, as the Big Three transition to E.V.s, which takes fewer people to assemble, that these workers will be left behind.\par \par \uc2 I had a chance to speak with the coo for North America of \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  today, and I pressed him on whether they will keep their work force as they transition to electric. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK STEWART\uc2 , COO, STELLANTIS NORTH AMERICA: Our plan is to do our very best to not leave folks behind. We all have to be competitive today at \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , for today's vehicles and ICE and our plug-in hybrids. We definitively have to be just as competitive, if not more so, in tomorrow, in a technology that costs today about 40 to 50 percent more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: The technology costs more.\par \par \uc2 And right now, even without electric vehicles in the picture, the labor costs are a lot higher for the Big Three automakers compared to non-union E.V. makers like \uc2 Tesla\uc2 . So if the automakers get a bad deal from their perspective, that disparity could grow even more.\par \par \uc2 (CARS HONKING)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Now, President Biden said last week -- as you hear the honks of supporters driving by, President Biden said that he was going to send his acting labor secretary and White House adviser to help with the negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  made it clear they didn't really want that help, and now we're learning that those two administration officials are not coming here to Detroit, after all, that they're going to stay involved, but they're going to do it from afar.\par \par \uc2 Someone who is planning a trip to Michigan is former President Trump. And if you thought maybe the \uc2 UAW\uc2  would welcome him with open arms, look at this statement from \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain.\par \par \uc2 He says: "Every fiber of our union is being poured into fighting the billionaire class and an economy that enriches people like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , at the expense of workers. We can't keep electing billionaires and millionaires that don't have any understanding what it's like to live paycheck to paycheck and struggle to get by and expecting them to solve the problems of the working class."\par \par \uc2 Right now, the union is only striking at three plants, one for each of the Big Three automakers. But since there's no deal, and it doesn't sound like too much major breakthroughs are being made in the closed-door meetings, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is saying, if there's no progress or no deal by noon Friday, they are going to ramp this thing up.\par \par \uc2 So, the no threat is out there. The automakers know about it, Neil. We will see how they respond.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And there had been some ancillary layoffs and cutbacks at some of the Big Three companies as a result of this, so they're countering, right?\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: In fact, just at this plant where we are, 600 Ford employees who are not part of the strike, who were still going to work, they have been laid off. They're not going to work.\par \par \uc2 And that's part of the concern is, how are they going to get paid? And the union says they will use some of their strike fund, which is supposed to go towards striking workers for that. Now they're having to cover some of the workers who have been laid off as a result of the strike who are not technically picketing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Grady, thank you very much. And you kept your cool with all that honking going on there.\par \par \uc2 Grady Trimble in Wayne, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 A lot of these strikers are convinced that they can get very generous pay increases, so much so they have opted to turn their noses up at up to 21 percent that some of these auto companies have been offering.\par \par \uc2 We're going to pick \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 's brain on this and what that could be telling us -- right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is skipping the next presidential debate, but something tells me that \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  isn't going to let that go unnoticed.\par \par \uc2 He is coming up, get his thoughts on that, get his thoughts on the strike going on with auto workers and much more -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This almost doesn't warrant a FOX Alert. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has gone ahead and skipped the second presidential debate, this, of course, the FOX Business one next week in California.\par \par \uc2 He says he's going to be delivering a Detroit prime-time speech, presumably to address the strike that's going on, even though the head of the \uc2 UAW\uc2  does not appear to be a big fan of the former president.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  is joining us right now, presidential candidate, the former two-term governor of the blue state of New Jersey. He made it red for a couple of terms.\par \par \uc2 Governor, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R-NJ), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you for having me, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let's talk a little bit about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's decision. I'm sure you're not surprised, but, a number of times, Governor, you have said that you would follow \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Do you still plan to follow him?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 No, look, I said I was going to give him two debates to show up. So he's not going to show up at debate number two. So, after debate number two, we're going to have to go find him, I guess.\par \par \uc2 You know, look, we have got \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who campaigned all these years ago in a basement. We now have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who's hiding behind the walls of his golf clubs, and only going into very controlled circumstances, where he doesn't get to ask -- be asked questions by voters.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think this is incredibly disrespectful to voters, Neil. They have a right to hear from him about his record when he was president, about what his plans are if he were to be the nominee again. And he just is ignoring it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So what would you do if you followed him, if you knew a place where he would be, if he was in a diner or whatever? I mean, what would you do? What would you say?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Neil, I'm going to keep that between me and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when I get there. No reason to give him a preview on that. But he will just never know when I'm going to show up. And I think that'd be a good thing for this process. We need for him to engage, because the voters deserve it. It's disrespectful for him not to.\par \par \uc2 And, quite frankly, it's cowardly. He doesn't want to be on there because he doesn't want to defend some of the things he's done and said, and like accepting \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's endorsement.\par \par \uc2 I have to tell you, if an autocratic murderer like \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  endorsed me, I would reject that endorsement immediately. But, for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he seems to like those things from authoritarian dictators.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, you know, Governor, while Democrats, by and large, like that and your message, you will have a devil of a time getting past Republicans, right?\par \par \uc2 You poll well in New Hampshire, where you're focusing a lot of your efforts. I get that. But the view is that you have ticked off so many Trump voters and supporters that they will never support you.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: I just don't believe that's true, first off.\par \par \uc2 We have just started to engage in this process. I have been in the race for three months. And I don't believe that that's going to be the case, ultimately. And, certainly, after I win the nomination, they're certainly going to vote for me, instead of voting for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who is both mentally and physically proving himself not to be up to the job.\par \par \uc2 So, we're going to go out there and we're going to make the case, because if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's the nominee, Neil, I am very afraid that we are going to wind up with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as president for another four years. He's going to do damage to this country that will be very difficult to repair.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, you have talked about New Hampshire and its importance.\par \par \uc2 I think you have said, Governor, that, if you don't finish in the top four, you quit the race. I think, in 2016, you finished sixth in that state. So, fourth or better, you stay. Fifth or below, you're out.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 No, look, I think you have to do well in either Iowa or New Hampshire. I'm staking it on New Hampshire, and you got to do well there. I'm a realistic guy, Neil, and I know that you got to make the sale to people. And that's what we intend to do over the course of the next number of months, between now and when the primary occurs in late January.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Florida has -- they dispense with the loyalty pledge. In other words, whoever wins supports -- is supported by everyone else and everyone else supports whoever wins.\par \par \uc2 You were among those not raising your hand on that notion of that in the last debate, which I think was a message that, if it were \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you wouldn't be supporting it. Could you explain?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Well -- well, the specific question, Neil, was, would you -- would you support someone convicted of a felony for president of the United States?\par \par \uc2 And, look, I just don't believe that's something that anybody should be doing. If he's convicted of a felony...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What if he's not? What if he's not convicted of a felony?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Well, the -- but that wasn't the question.\par \par \uc2 And I -- look, I have -- Neil, I have gone through this a whole bunch of times before. And the fact is that we are going to win the nomination, and I intend to win it. And, when we do, I have no expectation that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will support me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. That probably might be a safe bet.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you a little bit about this ongoing \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike, Governor. I talked to Nikki Haley about this over the weekend, and she was saying, be careful here. Some of these pay demands and the like are going to be passed along, didn't say exactly what she would do as a president in that.\par \par \uc2 This president has made it very clear where he stands. He supports union workers and offered to send two administration officials to help in the talks. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  chief doesn't want him there, at least at this point.\par \par \uc2 What would you do as president with something like this?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Well, one of the things I would make clear right away to both sides is this. People deserve to be paid for their level of expertise and skill and for how hard they work.\par \par \uc2 And when the business is doing well, folks who help to create the products for that business should be doing well also. I have no problem with that. And I think workers are right to be asking for more money.\par \par \uc2 Here's the problem. When they ask to be paid for a 40-hour workweek, but only do 32 hours of work, that's a problem Neil. I mean -- and I think it's going to wind up turning Americans off. Everybody would love to be able to get paid for when they don't work. But, in this instance, that's not what it should be.\par \par \uc2 And so I have a problem with the union -- the approach the union is taking. Let's pay people what they deserve for their skill, their expertise and how hard they work. But let's not be paying for people not to work.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me switch gears to what's happening in Washington as well, Governor, where a government shutdown seems to look increasingly more likely as the days dwindle by and failed procedural votes still keep happening, as Kevin McCarthy is going to keep offering these matters before a vote, even if it fails.\par \par \uc2 But it looks like we are on a shutdown course. And some are blaming his leadership and, more specifically, intransigent Republican conservatives for this. Where do you stand?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: It's a failure by everybody involved, Neil.\par \par \uc2 If we shut down the government, you're sent down there to operate the government, not to shut it down. And so you have got to find a way to get it done. We have got to recognize that we're in divided government. And in divided government, you're not going to get everything that you want.\par \par \uc2 And I think that the speaker did a good job in terms of what he did regarding the debt ceiling, getting some concessions there in return for raising the debt ceiling. And I think we have got to look to be able to forge an agreement, recognizing that we're in divided government.\par \par \uc2 But everybody gets an F, Neil, if it turns out you closed down the government. That's not what they're there to do. They're there to solve problems, not create more.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Governor, we're just getting reports -- this is from ABC News, I'm sure you have heard -- concerning the classified documents that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had in his possession and an assistant who told ABC News that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would write to-do lists on some of these classified documents.\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: Look, this just shows the level of disrespect he was showing for the documents that were created with American men and women in both the military and in our intelligence community putting their lives on the line to get that information, so the president can be fully briefed.\par \par \uc2 But, to me, Neil, I have read that report. And the worst part of the report is not, believe it or not, in my mind, the to-do lists on the back of classified documents. The worst is when he called that assistant, which she was going to go before the grand jury, and said: You don't know anything about my boxes, my boxes of documents.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that is active witness tampering. That's active obstruction of justice.\par \par \uc2 I mean, so, if she's telling the truth, then we have more evidence that will be presented at the time of trial, and this case will be tried. And this is the guy that we want to make our nominee? We -- this is the person we think can beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 He's going to be spending most of March, all of March and most of April in a federal courthouse in Washington, D.C., defending himself, rather than being out on the trail, taking it to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . We need to nominate someone whose full focus will be on taking it to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and defeating him and restoring conservative Republican values to the White House and to governance in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this is just another example of the drip, drip, drip of information that's going to come out about irresponsible conduct by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that's going to be used by Democrats and others to take him on if he were the nominee. We need to stop that now, thank him for his service, send them back to Mar-a-Lago and Bedminster, and get a nominee who can win this race and beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, you had said of a Biden-Trump possible match that both are past their sell-by date.\par \par \uc2 Now, Governor, I have heard from a lot of older Americans who are curious. What is \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 's definition of a sell-by date? How old do you have to be?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: It's not an age. It's the way you act at that age, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So, for instance, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is now 80 years old. When you fall asleep sitting across from the Israeli president in the Oval Office while there's a whole camera spray in front of you, that shows you're no longer up to the job, also the various fictional stories he tells.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is past his sell-by date temperamentally. I want to just remind voters of the big difference between 2016 \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and 2024 \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . In 2016, he said: I am your voice.\par \par \uc2 In 2023, he's now saying: I am your retribution.\par \par \uc2 We do not need a president who believes the presidency is to be used for retribution. We need a president who is going to use the presidency to lift up the lives, the spirits, and the future of every American. That's why they're both past their sell-by date. It's not a number, Neil. It's the way you act. It's your conduct.\par \par \uc2 And he is -- they are both, in my view, past their sell-by date.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, because the White House spokesman says that 80 is the new 40. I guess you don't buy that.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: No, I don't buy it.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, it doesn't look like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  buys it either. I mean, he's not acting like he's 40. I knew \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  when he was 40. He's certainly not acting that way.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, Governor, very good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , you can catch him, of course, in the big debate next week on the 27th. It'll be on FOX Business, simulcast on this fine network as well. Should be fun stuff.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, not-so-fun stuff in Washington, D.C., where, as we pointed out with the governor, we're in the verge of yet another shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie following all of that.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, tensions are rising here on Capitol Hill, as five Republicans in the House just tanked a pretty popular defense bill in the conference, all to put pressure on this government spending battle.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to tell you coming up just how much closer we're getting to a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, will we or won't we?\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hussein on Capitol Hill on that fear of a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 It looks a little more fearful now, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes, it does, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And that's because of what just happened about an hour ago, so a huge blow for Speaker Kevin McCarthy this afternoon, as House Republicans failed to pass a pretty popular defense bill, a defense bill that pretty much the entire conference actually supports. And that actually created some commotion on the House floor just a short while ago.\par \par \uc2 Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The yeas are 212. The nays are 214. The resolution is not adopted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, five Republicans tanked this defense bill, which really has been dragged into this fight over a short-term spending bill to fund the government.\par \par \uc2 And one of those critics is Representative Ralph Norman. I just spoke to him right before I came on with you, Neil, and he says that he doesn't want to do this piecemeal. He wants a top-line number and he wants to do all of these 12 appropriation bills now. He does not want to do a continuing resolution.\par \par \uc2 Now, right after this vote, a group of veteran members walked over to the House steps and blasted these holdouts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE GARCIA (R-CA): What we just saw with these five individuals was them adding, effectively, their name to that list that are enabling Chairman Xi right now, who's looking at this with a sigh of relief that we didn't just get this DOD package to the floor.\par \par \uc2 I'm disappointed. I am pissed off.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, what does it mean for the government, and will it shut down?\par \par \uc2 Well, it doesn't bode well for the upcoming vote on that continuing resolution. It's supposed to happen this Thursday, Neil. But Speaker McCarthy already pulled the first procedural vote on that today. It was supposed to happen today. Not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 And after this DOD bill just failed, he told FOX it just doesn't make sense to him. Everybody goes back to the negotiating table just two days before they try to take up that C.R. Not looking good, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. Thanks for that update, Aishah. We will see.\par \par \uc2 Hope springs eternal, and my next guests hopes so, a Republican and Democrat who don't think it's crazy to work with each other on things, Congressman \uc2 Jared Moskowitz\uc2 , a Florida Democrat, also \uc2 Garret Graves\uc2  with us, Louisiana Republican.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, welcome to both of you.\par \par \uc2 First off, on the status of this back-and-forth on whether we shut down, Congressman Graves, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 GARRET GRAVES\uc2  (R-LA): Hey, look, I don't think a shutdown is a win for anyone.\par \par \uc2 I think that you had a group of folks that came together. They put together an agreement that would result in an 8 percent reduction in spending. It would allow us to continue funding government, working on the regular appropriations bill and closing the Southern border. I think it's a great solution and buys us more time to continue discussing a path forward on the remaining appropriations bills.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's the solution. Let me say it again. A government shutdown shouldn't be an end zone for anyone.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that might be the case and the hope, but Congressman Moskowitz, it looks like the closer and closer we get to this September 30 deadline, that is exactly what we will be looking at.\par \par \uc2 What do you do in that event? What do you protect or want to save in that event?\par \par \uc2 REP. JARED MOSKOWITZ (D-FL): Well, listen, I agree with my colleague, obviously, that we don't want a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 So I think there's bipartisan agreement on that. How we avoid that, we probably disagree on how we get there. Ultimately, the state of play is this. Right now, the speaker, in order to get something out, he's going to need those Freedom Caucus members, those MAGA members to do it, the ones who have said no, to get something out.\par \par \uc2 And if he doesn't get anything out, then, obviously, we shut down and the Senate's in full control. If he gets something out, it's probably going to be so loaded up with some MAGA policies that the Senate will not take it, and we still shut down.\par \par \uc2 So I think we're headed to a shutdown either way, and then looming over that, of course, is this threat of a vacancy vote if the speaker does anything that a couple of these MAGA members don't like. So, listen, I think the American people better buckle up, because I think we're in -- a shutdown is coming.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, gentlemen, I do want to -- you do agree on a couple of key points, and that is FEMA and keeping it adequately funded and protected and apparently out of the arms of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So it would be a stand-alone, I guess, Cabinet agency.\par \par \uc2 Maybe, Congressman Graves, you can explain.\par \par \uc2 GRAVES: Sure.\par \par \uc2 Look, we were actually meeting and talking about problems with disasters and disaster preparedness and response in our respective states, a Republican and Democrat coming together, finding common ground. Then, having FEMA buried within the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  without the autonomy that they need is not a model that makes sense.\par \par \uc2 Both of us have vast experiencing doing disaster preparedness and response activities. We have seen this failure, this bureaucracy up close and personal. We have seen how it adversely affects the people we represent, effectively, our federal government revictimizing disaster victims.\par \par \uc2 I think it's a great plan, a great path forward. And I want to thank my friend Congressman Moskowitz for coming up with the idea and working together with us on this and a few other disaster solutions.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, of course, Congressman Moskowitz, your state knows a thing or two about the urgency and the importance of FEMA, of course, dealing with storms on a more routine basis than many.\par \par \uc2 But I am wondering what a FEMA stand-alone Cabinet agency offers that it would not under the umbrella of Homeland Security.\par \par \uc2 MOSKOWITZ: No, that's a great point.\par \par \uc2 And I'm happy to stand here with my colleague, who has a ton of experience on this. As you may remember, I was the director of emergency management for the state of Florida for Governor DeSantis a couple years ago. And so I worked with FEMA on a multitude of disasters.\par \par \uc2 And, right now, listen, FEMA is an agency that has to work, government has to work. This is an agency that helps people on its time of need, to help people respond, recover and rebuild. We're having disasters all over the country in blue areas and red areas. This has to be a bipartisan issue.\par \par \uc2 Why does it need to come out of Homeland? Well, FEMA was never intended to be in Homeland. It was always intended to be a Cabinet agency. It got swept up into Homeland after 9/11. But to be one of 20 agencies, this needs to be a direct report to the president. We need to get rid of that red tape, get rid of that bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 This will make FEMA faster, more responsive. And FEMA will not be able to make changes for the next 20, 50 years on how to deal with disasters so long as it sits within Homeland Security.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will watch it very closely.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, it's encouraging to see both you guys standing together talking nice to each other. So, that does -- that does happen now and then. You're both to be commended.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 GRAVES: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 MOSKOWITZ: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime here, a little dust-up outside the United Nations right now.\par \par \uc2 The Iranian president is speaking, and, let's just say, generating some crowds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, he certainly knows how to draw a response and a crowd, Iranian President \uc2 Ebrahim Raisi\uc2  addressing \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A lot of people are angry that he's even there.\par \par \uc2 Eric Shawn in the middle of all of that in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Eric.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, being in the middle is kind of an understatement. Take a look at this. These are anti-regime protesters here right outside the \uc2 U.N\uc2 ., and they are angry today at two presidents. One is Iranian President \uc2 Ebrahim Raisi\uc2  and the other the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALIREZA JAFARZADEH, NATIONAL COUNCIL OF RESISTANCE TO IRAN: Any money that goes to Iran, no matter how you paint it, it's going to end up in the pocket of the Revolutionary Guards to kill people inside Iran and to fund terror abroad.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: That was Alireza Jafarzadeh, who is one of the officials of the group.\par \par \uc2 They are angry that President Biden released $6 billion to release the American hostages. Although these demonstrators are members of the largest resistance group, the National -- Iranian National Congress, they are upset that that money is going to Tehran.\par \par \uc2 They are happy that the hostages are back here in the U.S., but they are saying that there should never have been a deal made, that they should have just swapped hostages instead. The group released a report showing Iran's security spending is way up, $50 billion to prop up the Assad regime and $168 million in pay raises for the mullahs, the regime's religious hard- liners.\par \par \uc2 But you know President Biden and Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  say that the deal was worth it in order to get the Americans back.\par \par \uc2 As you can see, the rally is going on. They have been here, Neil, all day chanting these chants. By the way, one of the research groups did a study on how much money the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  has paid Iran since 1981 to get our American hostages back. And that amount is more than 15 -- $15 billion.\par \par \uc2 And you can see they have been here since early this morning, and they're continuing with fervor and opposition.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Eric, you are unflappable, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Eric Shawn in the middle of that outside the United Nations.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Victoria Coates on all of this, what she makes of all of this, \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2  foreign policy vice president, former deputy national security adviser under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Very good to have you, Victoria.\par \par \uc2 We often see this when top Iranian officials are here in the United States, particularly in the case of the Iranian president when he addresses the \uc2 U.N\uc2 ., nothing new, but particularly now given the timing of this hostage swap deal, the $6 billion that was unfrozen for Iran, what do you make of it all?\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA COATES, FORMER U.S. DEPUTY NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: Well, good to be with you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And God bless Eric for being in the middle of the General Assembly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 COATES: That's a rough neighborhood at the time of year.\par \par \uc2 But, I mean, also God bless the Iranians for coming out and exercising their right of peaceful protest here in the United States against the regime that has been so vicious and cruel to them. And we're marking the one-year anniversary of Mahsa Amini's death, the young Kurdish woman who was basically beaten to death for wearing her hijab wrong, which unleashed extraordinary protests across Iran.\par \par \uc2 So I think it's really unconscionable that we gave, under these circumstances, President Raisi a visa. We -- it is well within the rights of the State Department not to grant that visa to him on grounds that he has participated in terrorist acts. And on top of it all, what's really outrageous is, you and I are footing the bill for his private security while he's here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 He has Secret Service protection.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know what else is a little weird, Victoria? I mean, it's the environment in which we live. I get it.\par \par \uc2 But we're so suspicious of Iran, but Saudi Arabia has been the country leading these oil production cuts. And they're the ones who, in a backdoor way, are kind of gouging us.\par \par \uc2 COATES: Well, I mean, the Saudis view the oil industry as a business. And I think their real issue with the United States right now is the fact that the current administration has been using the Strategic Petroleum Reserve as a piggy bank, artificially trying to prop up -- or tamp down, rather, prices by releases from the reserve that's just not what it's intended for, and then going out and sending signals like canceling the Alaska permits or the New Mexico projects that were canceled over last night, which signals to them, we're not going to ramp up our production domestically.\par \par \uc2 Yet we want them to. And I think that, in their mind, is a disconnect about how you do business.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Whether you're talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or this Republican approach, it seems to be we have plenty of oil in our country. We're going to pursue it again in our country if we take the White House, take the House, the Senate, what have you.\par \par \uc2 And that would mean relying on countries, some of whom like us, most of whom don't seem to flip over us. And that would make it a moot point. How likely do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 COATES: Well, I think it's very likely, because this is an issue that touches all Americans where they live.\par \par \uc2 And I'm from Pennsylvania, so I touch -- task natural gas on that list as well, we being the second largest producer of natural gas, which has -- the conversion to which is really reduced U.S. emissions historically over the last 10 to 15 years. So the notion that we can't do this in an environmentally responsible way is just incorrect.\par \par \uc2 And I strongly agree. I don't want to be dependent on Iranian barrels on the water. I don't want to be dependent on Russian barrels on the water. I would prefer those regimes to be starved from resources, so that they can't oppress their own people, they have a harder time invading Ukraine, and you cut off cheap energy supplies to China.\par \par \uc2 So I do think this would be a very strong strategic play for a president of either party next time around.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Victoria, I know you served under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and served very well in the national security advising role.\par \par \uc2 Some have been a little concerned about his comments concerning \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . Putin has said very nice things about the former president, he of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . We had \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  on a little earlier, who wonders how odd that is, and just the fact that the former president would brag about good relations and good thoughts concerning \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that and whether that could be a risk in a potential future Trump administration?\par \par \uc2 COATES: Well, I mean, it wasn't in the last Trump administration, and there was a lot of the same discussion going on at the time that, oh, boy, was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a Russian asset, and you have had the Steele dossier and all these things.\par \par \uc2 And the fact of the matter is, Neil, the proof is in the pudding, and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  did not invade Ukraine during the Trump administration. The rumor is that...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, he has since -- no, you're quite right. He has since, and all of this bad blood since.\par \par \uc2 COATES: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think that is a constructive way to move forward, or should Putin be isolated on the world stage if the former president becomes president again?\par \par \uc2 COATES: I think it's going to be very difficult to do constructive business with Putin, especially given the extent of the relationship with Xi.\par \par \uc2 I don't mind having open lines of communications with the Russians. There are various things we cooperate with them on from nuclear to space. We might look at changing that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 COATES: But, for the moment, we at least need to keep the lines open. And, as I said, I think we should keep them out of Ukraine. And, somehow, President Trump was able to do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Would you entertain serving in another Trump administration if he gets back in the White House?\par \par \uc2 COATES: I would -- if any president asks you to serve, of course, you have to take that invitation very seriously. It's an honor. I'm also very honored to be at \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So I think that would be a question for the future president.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That is a perfect answer. You know that?\par \par \uc2 Victoria Coates, thank you very much. Good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 COATES: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Victoria Coates, former national -- deputy national security adviser to \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime here, let's get a read about what was going on in the markets today, sold off a little bit. A lot of these worries are building over this strike and the big pay raises attached to it. Now, that is Wall Street's way of saying we welcome workers getting their due. Could this be more than their due, since some of these pay packages they're talking about are at a minimum going to be 20 percent increase in salary and some benefits maybe more than that?\par \par \uc2 There's a goal of getting up to 40 percent, kind of mimics the deal that was crafted for UPS workers by the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 . Airline pilots are seeing big pay raises of 40 percent or more.\par \par \uc2 Gary Kaltbaum watching all of this.\par \par \uc2 Gary, your argument is, not taking sides and whether they deserve it or not, but they get passed along. That's inflation that sticks, right?\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And we already know what happens when inflation hits. And, Neil, something happened at the end of the day today, and we will see whether it carries through tomorrow. The 10-year yield hit a 15-year high at the close today.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: And that's the markets.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  was printing money and taking yields down, that wasn't the real market. So the real market right now is really worried about, A, oil prices above $90, B, wages, C, food. Like, the list goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 And if interest rates keep going higher, that is a headwind for the economy, and you can end up getting into some sort of a vicious cycle. I'm hoping it starts to top out here, but that was a bad finish today going into \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, you're right,that 15-, 16-year high in those yields here, and those are rates that the Fed can't control. But it's the market's way saying, we see inflation continue to stick around for a while.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is wrapping up a two-day meeting tomorrow. What do you think they do?\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: They have already telegraphed they're not doing anything, but with oil prices in the $90s, I happen to think they're going to have to talk a little bit tough going forward, because they can come out with every government statistic they want.\par \par \uc2 If oil prices go into the $100 and higher, that defines inflation. When energy prices are going up and interest rates go up to accommodate, there's no bad -- worse one-two punch than those two things happening for the economy going forward.\par \par \uc2 So I think that's going to be something he's going to have to address, or else. I think, if he doesn't address it, I think interest rates go higher, being worried about it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, mortgage rates have been backing up. If the Fed were to resume tightening, let's say, maybe at the November meeting, by your estimate, they're going to go still higher.\par \par \uc2 But housing's held up. Do you see that continuing to be the case, or are its days numbered?\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: The worry is, housing is held up because who wants to trade in a 3 percent mortgage for a 7.5?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: And I'm just wondering. There's going to be pent-up selling going forward.\par \par \uc2 I don't know what's going to trigger that, but that's something to watch real, real closely, because that is a distortion created by the Fed when they took rates down to zero percent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Gary, I didn't tell you, did I, what my wife and I paid for our first mortgage? It was 13.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Oh, I did 1,000 times.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Way up there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 All right, Gary, always good seeing you, my friend. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Gary is an uncanny read of these markets and economy, been very prescient.\par \par \uc2 All right, at the corner of Wall and Broad, a sell-off, again, before we get that decision on the part of the Fed. As Gary said, the betting is that the Fed is not going to raise rates tomorrow, but they are going to telegraph concern, and they could signal a rate hike coming at the next meeting six weeks later.\par \par \uc2 It's too soon to tell, but that seems to be a sentiment that is building.\par \par \uc2 But what's that about sentiment that builds? It's like a snapshot. Once snapped, it's shot. OK, you have heard that before too.\par \par \uc2 Here's "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230920ej9j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Five Americans Freed In Prisoner Swap With Iran; Lawmakers Scramble To Finalize Funding Deal; Hunter Biden Suing IRS Over Tax Disclosures; Negotiators Resume Talks As Automakers Strike Continues; Trump Criticizes DeSantis Heartbeat Abortion Bill; Candidates Debate 15-Week Limit; Haley: Focus On Late Term Abortions; Global Impact Beyond Fossil Fuels; Schumer Drops Senator Attire Guidelines; Senate Passes New Dress Code Allowing Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  to Dress Less Formally; Video Emerges Of Teen Driver Hitting And Killing Bike Rider; Owner And Tenant Of Daycare Facility Arrested For Exposing Children To Fentanyl Leading To One-Year-Old's Death; Biden Administration Criticizes For Releasing $6 Billion In Frozen Funds To Iranian Republican In Exchange For Five American Prisoners; Former President Trump Criticizes Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  For Signing State Abortion Bill\b0\par\par\uc2 John Roberts, Peter Doocy, Chad Pergram, Grady Trimble, David  Spunt, Rich Edson, Bret Baier, Mark Meredith, Alexis McAdams, CB Cotton,  Brit Hume, Juan Williams, Ben Domenech \par \uc2 7,880 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'm lactose intolerant, Judge. Just looking at this gives me diarrhea.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, but you know what, the truth is that there's more than cheese in that.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Will it be explosives?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It will be explosive.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, good luck out there with the traffic. That's it for us. Have a great night everybody.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome back to Washington. I'm John Roberts. Bret Baier will join us from Saudi Arabia a little bit later on.\par \par \uc2 But breaking tonight, five Americans are on their way home this evening after being released from custody in Iran. It is part of a prisoner swap with that country, one that includes the release of $6 billion of frozen Iranian assets that has led to fierce criticism of the deal.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight from 1600 Pennsylvania. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: John, good evening. When these freed hostages land overnight in the D.C. area, they will be immediately offered help from the Defense Department at adjusting to life on the outside. And so far, we haven't heard anybody say anything but positive things that Americans are heading home. But there are a lot of complaints about the deal to get them here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: This is a ransom payment of about $6 billion, about a billion bucks per prisoner.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): President Biden says, today, five innocent Americans who were imprisoned in Iran are finally coming home after many months of difficult and principled American diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 American Siamak Namazi held since 2015 says, I am dying to find out what gadgets now exist. When I was taken hostage, the iPhone 6s had just come out. You cannot imagine what an eight-year itch feels like.\par \par \uc2 The returning Americans have already spoken to President Biden and Secretary of State Blinken.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: I can tell you that it was for them, for me, an emotional conversation.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): To get the five Americans home, President Biden worked to unfreeze $6 billion in Iranian funds that are only supposed to be used to buy food or medicine.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: We have the means and mechanisms to make sure that that happens.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): That leads to new concerns about an easy workaround in Tehran.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ (R-FL): That frees up 6 billion of Iranian money for terrorism, for supporting Russia and everything else. I worry that this is really greasing the skids to back into a new nuclear deal.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The five Iranians freed from U.S. jails were charged with offenses ranging from failing to register as a foreign agent to buying parts that could be used in missiles, the deal making between D.C. and Tehran appears to be limited to hostages. For now, no plans to try to reenter the Iran nuclear deal.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: It would be great if we could diplomatically get to a place where we were back in 2015. And I'm not saying that diplomacy is completely off the table. It's just not the focus right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): President Biden is already in New York City for the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  but has no public events on his schedule today, including at the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He did just arrived at his first meetings of the day, though, it's a pair of fundraisers, including one that is being billed as Broadway for Biden, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: But we will be watching that big speech tomorrow before the General Assembly. Peter Doocy for us at the White House tonight. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We are now just a week and a half away from a potential partial shutdown of the federal government if Republicans and Democrats cannot agree to legislation to keep offices open and services running.\par \par \uc2 GOP members who control the House are said to be getting jittery at the prospect of being blamed for another negotiating failure. Our Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill tonight. Chad, a lot going on there.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: John, good evening. Lawmakers are no closer to avoiding a shutdown. GOP South Dakota Representative \uc2 Dusty Johnson\uc2  and other centrist Republicans crafted a bill to fund the government for a month and enhance border security. But the plan lacks the votes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. DUSTY JOHNSON (R-SD): Nothing happens easily around here. The reality is that we can't shut down government and we must secure the border.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The plan met a frosty reception from Democrats.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Last night's proposal in the House can be boiled down to two words, slapdash, reckless. slapdash because it's not a serious proposal for avoiding a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The house has only approved one spending bill this year. House Speaker Kevin McCarthy tried to get his members to pass spending bills since July. That failure means the House loses leverage to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Is that part of the idea to demonstrate to them that they're going to get jammed by the Senate if they don't move something over here and strengthen your position?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The best way this system works is we pass our individual bills. Unfortunately, I have some members denying those come up. We're going to keep working through that.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): McCarthy said a government shutdown impedes impeachment. GOP Indiana Representative Victoria Spartz is retiring but she called McCarthy, "weak". McCarthy rebuked Spartz.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: You know, one thing I've learned in life, anybody who criticize you has never worked harder than you. I mean, if Victoria is concerned about fighting stronger, I wish she would run again and not quit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): A cocktail of Democrats and GOP members could renew old funding on a temporary basis to avert a shutdown. But that could draw the ire of the right. McCarthy says he's not worried that opponents could try to remove him as speaker, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: A lot of rhetoric being thrown there on Capitol Hill. Chad Pergram for us tonight. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is suing the \uc2 IRS\uc2  over the release of his tax records. The president's son is facing the prospect of being charged for tax crimes and other violations relating to his business affairs. Correspondent David Spunt is at the Justice Department tonight and has the latest from there for us. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, John. Good evening. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is going on the offense just days after he was indicted on three felony counts for owning a gun back in 2018.\par \par \uc2 This suit specifically takes aim at the two \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblowers who sounded the alarm about his tax case. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's attorney Abbe Lowell also making the claim today in a separate letter to Congress that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is actually owed a refund for overpaying his taxes.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit though challenges whether the two \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblower should even be considered whistleblowers, that argues that Gary Shapley and Joseph Ziegler in public statements and interviews, "Blatantly violated section 6103 of the IRS Code by engaging in a campaign to publicly smear Mr. Biden" but Gary Shapley said on this program, he was just doing his duty.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GARY SHAPLEY, \uc2 IRS\uc2  WHISTLEBLOWER: Every taxpayer deserves to be treated fairly. And it was my oath of office to make sure that that happens.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): The lawsuit says \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has all the same responsibilities as any other American taxpayer. But similarly, Mr. Biden has no fewer lesser rights than any other American citizen and no government agency or government agent has free rein to violate his rights simply because of who he is.\par \par \uc2 Now, one of Shapley's attorneys today said his client did absolutely nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 MARK LYTLE, GARY SHAPLEY ATTORNEY: This lawsuit is just a frivolous smear to try to, you know, block the whistleblowers or any potential whistleblowers from coming forward. There were no violations. The whistleblower has followed the statute.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Mark Lytle's co-counsel said earlier today, he believes U.S. attorney and now Special Counsel David Weiss retaliated against whistleblower Gary Shapley.\par \par \uc2 John, this lawsuit, as I mentioned, comes just days after Hunter was indicted on three counts involving his purchase of a gun in 2018, but may not be his only legal problem in a federal courtroom.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Weiss and a court filing announced that Hunter could face tax charges, perhaps in California where he currently lives or Washington D.C. where he once lived before. We'll follow it, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Every day something new. David Spunt for us from the DOJ. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The White House is getting involved in the strike against Detroit automakers, even if the parties involved do not want the interference. This comes as union leaders and company negotiators continue to talk.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business Correspondent Grady Trimble is in Wayne, Michigan tonight. He's got the update for us. Hi, Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Hi, John. The big three automakers and the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  were back at the bargaining table today. By all accounts though, progress has been slow.\par \par \uc2 At this Ford plant, picketing workers have been blocking semi-trucks from pulling in. At one point, police came to tell striking union members they have to let the trucks through. Workers held some trucks up for a few minutes but eventually let them pass.\par \par \uc2 The picketers have welcomed with open arms several Democratic lawmakers who want to walk with them in solidarity. President Biden says he's sending two administration officials to Detroit to help with the negotiations.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain and the workers we've talked to tell us they don't want it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I say we don't need his help.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Why not? Because we are united through for people. We don't need the president's help.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's our fight. Let us fight our fight. That's all we want. We don't want to hurt the economy. We don't want to be here not getting paid peanuts. But you know, it's our fight. It's fight for our life.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think we can negotiate our contract with the company with out the government getting involved.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice over): In an update this evening, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  says today's discussion was constructive and focused on where we can find common ground to reach an agreement that provides a bridge to the future by enabling the company to meet the challenges of electrification.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (on camera): Right now, the union is targeting three plants, one at each of the big three automakers, but the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is threatening that it could scale up this strike depending on how negotiations are going. But it hasn't said where or when, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Grady Trimble watching the action for us in Wayne, Michigan. Grady, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were up today. The Dow gained six points to start off the week. The S&P 500 finished ahead three, the NASDAQ rose two.\par \par \uc2 Up next, presidential candidates on the Republican side sparring over abortion rights. We will hear from them when we come back.\par \par \uc2 And later, Bret Baier kicks off our reporting from Saudi Arabia this week with a look at what is happening in Riyadh, the capital city.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: From your point of view at the Ministry of Economy and planning, what is 2030 -- vision 2030 mean to you?\par \par \uc2 FAISAL ALIBRAHIM, MINISTER OF ECONOMY AND PLANNING OF SAUDI ARABIA: Vision 2030 is about unlocking potential. That's the main objective of vision 2030.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Well, a mystery no more. The defense department says search crews have found parts and debris associated with the missing F-35 aircraft in South Carolina. The Marine Corps plane went down over North Charleston Sunday afternoon. The pilot ejected safely.\par \par \uc2 That aircraft is part of the Marine Fighter Attack Training Squadron based in nearby Beaufort.\par \par \uc2 What to do about Ukraine is shaping up to be a big issue in the Republican presidential campaign. Another source of contention for the candidates, abortion rights. Here is senior national correspondent Rich Edson.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Former President Trump is condemning Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , for signing a bill prohibiting most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What he did is a terrible thing and a terrible mistake. But we'll come up with a number.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Trump has refused to embrace a number, a specific timeline on when abortion should be legal. The Republican frontrunner says he'd negotiate a compromise.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : For the first time in 52 years, you'll have an issue that we can put behind us.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): DeSantis' campaign says, "We've already seen the disastrous results of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  compromising with Democrats: over $7 trillion in new debt."\par \par \uc2 DeSantis has said states should decide. He says he'd appoint conservative judges and prevent taxpayer money from enabling abortions. Conservative groups have pushed the Republican field further to embrace nationwide restrictions at 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Some candidates are already there.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): President Trump, Nikki Haley, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and Vivek as well, Ramaswamy, they all are the same place, they have not committed to a 15-week limit. We need a president who fights for life from day one.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is also urging the field to back restrictions after 15 weeks. "There's no negotiating when it comes to the life of the unborn."\par \par \uc2 Former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley has criticized any 15-week ban is impossible to get through a divided Senate.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Let's focus on what we do agree on. Let's ban late term abortion. Let's encourage adoptions.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The GOP field is also at odds over funding for Ukraine, with Congress debating an additional $24 billion in assistance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): And Trump has been critical of U.S. funding and is demanding the Europeans spend more. Just giving a speech here in Washington. Pence said that his former running mate and the other Republicans are abandoning American leadership on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 Pence says failing to help Ukraine is a giant flashing green light for China to move against Taiwan. John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: More coming up with the panel a little bit later on in the program. Rich, Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Please join Dana Perino, \uc2 Stuart Varney\uc2 , and Univision's Ilia Calderon, as they moderate the second Republican presidential debate. That will be on Fox Business, September the 27th, from the Ronald Reagan Presidential Foundation and Institute in Simi Valley, California. Don't --\par \par \uc2 Well, SPECIAL REPORT will be coming to you from Saudi Arabia this week. Bret Baier is at the capital city of Riyadh tonight with some background on the rapidly changing kingdom.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hey, John, we're on the outskirts of Riyadh, in a place called Diriyah. It's the birthplace of the first Saudi state, one of six sites on \uc2 UNESCO\uc2 's World Heritage list inside Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 It is also a $63 billion project initiated by \uc2 King Salman\uc2  and his son, Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman, known around the world by his initials, MBS. The de facto ruler has a nonstop leadership style and his ministers follow his goal called Vision 2030. That has initiated sweeping changes in the kingdom in a very short period of time.\par \par \uc2 For women, to drive to participate in sports and entertainment, to allow them to wear what they choose to being able to start businesses just to name a few.\par \par \uc2 Here is the minister of economy and planning on his boss's drive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FAISAL ALIBRAHIM, MINISTER OF ECONOMY AND PLANNING, SAUDI ARABIA: His leadership style is coming from his ownership of the cause. He wants Saudi Arabia to be the best version of itself. He wants Saudi Arabia to be a global player that helps improve of the world, a global change agent.\par \par \uc2 He wants Saudi people and people residing in Saudi to live the best lives and actually to be able to contribute the most to this economy, but also, to the global economy at large.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When you hear critics of the kingdom from the outside, it doesn't seem like you have time to listen to that criticism. You're too busy.\par \par \uc2 ALIBRAHIM: I think we're used to criticism, but we're more focused on getting the job done. It's -- the proof is in the numbers. The proof is in the progress.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): While Saudi is making progress in a goal to make a big green energy transition, oil still drives revenues here. And there are a lot more revenues now after a recent production cut in oil.\par \par \uc2 The energy minister joined me from Calgary and the World Petroleum Congress.\par \par \uc2 PRINCE ABDULAZIZ BIN SALMAN AL SAUD, MINISTER OF ENERGY, SAUDI ARABIA: What we are doing is -- has nothing to do with prices. What we are doing is making sure that we reduce volatility of the market.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As you all know, Your Highness, pump prices affect presidential approval ratings, they go back all the way to Reagan that the higher the prices, the lower the presidential approval rating.\par \par \uc2 So, there are some people inside the U.S. who look skeptically. They understand what you're saying about volatility, but the fact that you and Russia, if you get together on a decision on production cuts, can affect how America works.\par \par \uc2 AL SAUD: I understand but you've got to take -- look at the components. Because a good chunk of what is happening in your gasoline market now or diesel market even more is lack of investment and lack of refining capacity, and lack of infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When asked about the oil production cut and how it may affect Russia's revenues when it's in the middle of a war in Ukraine, \uc2 Prince Abdulaziz\uc2  said there is a such thing as Saudi first, making decisions based on the good of the kingdom.\par \par \uc2 SPECIAL REPORT We'll be live in Saudi Arabia tomorrow night. We'll give you a behind the scenes tour of this kingdom and talking to a lot of different ministers, all setting the table for the big one-hour interview with Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , MBs, Wednesday on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 John, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right. Looking forward to that, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Again, Bret is going to be hosting SPECIAL REPORT from Saudi Arabia about tomorrow, and again on Wednesday with that first major American television news channel interview with the Saudi Crown \uc2 Prince Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , coming to you on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Do not miss that.\par \par \uc2 Up next, It's casual Friday every day for senators. Why the majority leader is suspending the dress code for members?\par \par \uc2 First, beyond our borders tonight. China's military says 103 war planes toward Taiwan in a 24-hour period in what the islands defense ministry calls a recent new high. As is customary, the planes turn back before reaching Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Chinese warplanes fly toward the self-governing island on a near daily basis, but typically in much smaller numbers than today.\par \par \uc2 Climate activists staged another day of protest in Berlin, blocking key roads during rush hour before being removed by police. Members of the Last Generation group glued themselves to the road, causing a traffic jam for commuters driving into the city.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Rome. One of the big stories there tonight. Russia's new ambassador to the Vatican meets with \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  for a protocol visit. This comes as signs emerge that the Vatican's Ukraine peace envoy could soon be taking a second visit to Moscow to try to find peace there.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories BEYOND OUR BORDERS tonight. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has instructed the sergeant at arms to cease enforcement of the Chamber's dress code. This is seen as a concession to Pennsylvania Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who prefers extremely casual attire.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith has got to look for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The Senate will come to order.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): In a place where tradition is celebrated, tonight, a change. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  says instead of a formal dress code, now, "Senators are able to choose what they wear on the Senate floor. I will continue to wear a suit."\par \par \uc2 Schumer has yet to say what sparked the change, but critics blame one man.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Welcome back, Senator.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Pennsylvania's \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is well known for sporting hoodies and shorts instead of suit and ties, except on the Senate floor as was required. The 54-year-old even prides himself on standing out.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): I feel it's, you know, a little more freedom, which should be bipartisanship, you know what I'm saying, so -- but I don't know why the right side seems to be losing their minds over it, like, dogs and cats are living together, and you know, like the world spinning off its axis. But, you know, I think it's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: But Fetterman's fellow senators are outrage over efforts to ease Senate standards.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL HAGERTY, (R-TN): To see it completely downgraded, no dress code now, I just think it's another step in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Don't do it.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Fetterman continues to defend his clothing choices, even last week responding to one Republican lawmaker who criticized him, posting "Instead of crying about how I dress, how about you get your S- together and do your job, bud."\par \par \uc2 In recent years, reporters have spotted lawmakers from both parties sporting a more casual look, but some fear it's a slippery slope.\par \par \uc2 JAMES WALLNER\uc2 , R STREET INSTITUTE: I think the big issue is there is a lack of respect and continuing decline in respect that the members themselves and the people who work there and the people who watch the Senate from afar have for this institution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): The Senate dress code changes do not apply to staffers and any others who may be on the Senate floor, meaning they will still have to dress up. And we don't know if this casual code is going to stick around for senators forever or if another majority leader comes to power and changes it, John. But \uc2 C-Span\uc2  will be more fun to watch.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I think he is going to be an army of one when it comes to that extremely casual wear, but we will see. Mark, good to see you tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Colorado Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  apologized on Friday after getting kicked out of a musical play for disruptive behavior and then falsely denying that she had been vaping in the theater. Surveillance video emerged showing Boebert vaping in her seat at a Denver theater. Her campaign earlier denied the theater's contention. Boebert says her actions were not meant to be malicious or cause harm, quote, "but the reality is that they did."\par \par \uc2 Former South Carolina attorney and convicted murder Alec Murdaugh has agreed to plead guilty to several crimes relating to his business practices. Those charges include wire fraud, bank fraud, money laundering, and conspiracy to commit wire fraud. He's expected to be in federal court Thursday in Charleston.\par \par \uc2 Some very disturbing video tonight. A retired California police chief is hit and killed by a teenager driving a car, and police say it was no accident. Correspondent Alexis McAdams shows us tonight. And we warn you, some of the video may be very distressing to you. Good evening, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: John, good evening. That's right, one teen faces murder charges after that video surfaced, and police are still searching for the passenger who was filming as the duo drove right into this innocent biker. This dangerous joy ride left an innocent dad, husband, and former police chief dead. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ready?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: We stopped that video right before impact there, but you can see the 64-year-old fly up into the air after that, shattering the car's windshield on the way down before he landed lifeless in the roadway. This happened back in mid-August when Andreas Probst was out riding his bike. Probst was only a few miles from his house and thought he would be back within an hour, but he was killed. Probst spent 35 years in law enforcement according to community members who I talked to, serving as a police chief out in California before he moved to Vegas for a remote job.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAT TREICHEL, GHOST BIKES: From talking to his daughter and his wife, as he was just a great, down-to-earth guy who was doing the right things, and maybe the wrong place at the wrong time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Moments before that fatal crash, investigators tell me the teen driver slammed into another car. The duo then posted that now viral clip on social media after driving off, leaving Probst on the road to die. More than 100 community members came to vigil showing support for the Probst family. The group Ghost Bikes Las Vegas left a white bike near the crash site to honor Probst and raise awareness for cyclists safety.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TREICHEL: He was doing -- it was textbook. He was doing everything perfect. I think this particular situation probably is riding on most of us more as to what is going on in society.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And that's what the community members are also trying to figure out. Police tell me it was that social media clip, John, that changed the case to a homicide investigation after about two weeks when it surfaced, and the community and the nation are outraged. John?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: We will see what happens when that goes to court. Alexis McAdams for us. Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Authorities in Los Angeles say they have arrested a person accused in the ambush murder of a sheriff's deputy over the weekend. Eight-year veteran Ryan Clinkunbroomer was gunned down while stopped at a traffic like. A dark gray Toyota Carolla pulled alongside him, stopped, and then you see it speed away. Twenty-nine-year-old Kevin Salazar was taken into custody after an hours long standoff. Deputy Clinkunbroomer was 30 years old.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT LUNA, LOS ANGELES COUNTY SHERIFF: People absolutely loved and adored him. See, he wasn't just one of our deputy sheriffs. He comes -- is he third generation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: The deputy had become engaged to be married just four days ago.\par \par \uc2 The owner of a daycare facility in the Bronx and a tenant in that building are under arrest tonight. They are accused in the death of a one-year-old boy and the exposure of three other children of what is believed to be fentanyl. Correspondent CB Cotton is in the Bronx tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why were their drugs in the daycare?\par \par \uc2 CB COTTON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: As officers searched for Grei Mendez's husband, the daycare owner stayed silent as she walked out of a New York City police precinct Sunday. Her daycare now under investigation. Inside she rented a room to her husband's cousin, Carlisto Acevedo Brito, both now charged with murder, manslaughter, and multiple counts of assault and drug offenses.\par \par \uc2 But her attorney insists she didn't know about the kilo of fentanyl or the drug packaging devices officers found inside. Four children who were dropped off Friday morning would later be showing signs of an opioid exposure or unresponsive. All were given Narcan, but only three survived.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES ARANDA, MENDEZ ATTORNEY: She called the cops. Would you call the cops if you know there was fentanyl in your apartment? Of course not.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: One family is now grieving after one-year-old Nicholas Dominici died. As a makeshift memorial grows for the boy, some wonder, how did the daycare pass inspections, including a surprise one earlier this month?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS, (D) NEW YORK CITY: It is a real wake-up call for individuals who have opioids or fentanyl in their homes. The mere contact is deadly for an adult and is extremely deadly for a child.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: The daycare opened this past January, and photos captured from the inside reveal opened food strewn across countertops and only a few kid supplies jammed into the tight space.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 COTTON (on camera): And late this evening, we learned lab results for three children have, indeed, tested positive for fentanyl. While New York City's Mayor Eric Adams says inspectors never noticed any sort of drug lab here inside this daycare, the city's chief health commissioner admits his inspectors aren't trained to test for this sort of deadly substance. The two suspects in this case have pleaded not guilty and are currently being held without bail. A grand jury is expected to convene sometime this week. John?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Wow, what a tragedy. CB Cotton for us tonight up in the Bronx. CB, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the Iran prisoner swap, the presidential race, and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  suing the \uc2 IRS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 29 in Philadelphia as nine juveniles are back in custody within 12 hours after police say they broke out of a detention center in Berks County. The escape was sparked by a riot that broke out at that facility on Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Dayton, Ohio. One of the big stories there tonight from FOX 19, commercial air taxis are coming to Dayton. They will be built at a half-a-billion dollar manufacturing facility. Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  says the effort will great 2,000 jobs. The all-electric vertical takeoff and landing vehicles will be used mostly for commercial passenger operations.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We will be right back with the panel.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: From day one, our sanctions have clearly and, indeed, always exempt the use of resources for humanitarian purposes. We have absolute confidence in the process and the system that's been set up.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL, (R-TX) HOUSE FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: We all know money is fungible. And then the president of Iran just came out and said I'm going to spend it however I want to. And of course he is. And guess where it's going to go? It's going to go into terror proxy operations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Five American hostages that were held in Iran are on their way back home. They'll be landing soon. But lots of debate over how it all came together.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, FOX News chief political analyst Brit Hume, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, and Ben Domenech, editor at large for "The Spectator" and host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio.\par \par \uc2 So, a lot of debate over whether or not freeing up the $6 billion was a ransom or not. Morgan Ortagus was on with us earlier. She said either way the deal gave Iran access to money they wouldn't have access to. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: They didn't have that money because it was frozen because of sanctions. So you can say, you can argue that I'm not lifting sanctions. And maybe you're not technically lifting them. But if you are not enforcing them and you're giving Iran, the world's leading state sponsor of terrorism, $6 billion, then we're just arguing semantics.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Brit, why don't you start us off there from your undisclosed location on whether or not it was American money, they still got cash for the hostages.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: That's exactly right. A hostage swap is defensible and easily so. This is the kind of thing you have to do to get your people back, which is a matter of some urgency. But throwing in the $6 billion freed up that was frozen which the Iranians have made clear they are going to do with whatever they want, and because, as has been repeatedly pointed out, money is fungible, it's effectively the same thing as giving them $6 billion they didn't have before that they are free to spend on the malign activities in which they are continuously engaged.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Juan, it's great to have five hostages coming back home, but to Brit's point, is this going to encourage more hostage taking? And was there a different way to do this? This administration seemed to be negotiating from a point of weakness.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, two points to be made. One is the United States, in terms of our values, we care about hostages. So that's always going to put us at disadvantage, not only vis-a-vis the Iranians, but you think "The Wall Street Journal" reporter being held by the Soviet Union. Think about our behavior, the deal we made for Brittney Griner.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: The Russians, not the Soviet Union, I will just say, though I agree.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Give it time.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Give it a minute.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Give Putin a little more time. He will be back.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: My point is, we are always at a disadvantage because we care about it, so we are always going to come out on the wrong end of that deal.\par \par \uc2 But, to Brit's point, I don't like the deal if it's in isolation. But my thought is maybe this is part of a larger going on. As you know, the Biden administration is trying to put something together between the Saudis and Israelis. The Saudis and the Iranians are always rivals, if not purely enemies. We have not heard a lot today from Israelis on this deal, which is a little bit of a surprise. Normally they would be quite upset. And so it may be that there is something larger taking place, and maybe we will put it in that context.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Do you want to comment on this bit, or do you want to go right to Republicans and abortion?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: I just would say, I think you are correct, Juan, in your analysis. But I just think that we don't have a lot of faith in this administration, in this White House, and in this president when it comes to his dealings in the past with the Iranian regime and on this point I think as well. I would agree with Morgan that this is something that looks in the aggregate like a very bad move.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Republicans, campaign 2024, and the abortion issue. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on "Meet the Press" yesterday called \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signing the six-week abortion ban a, quote, "terrible mistake." Brit, the candidates are all over the place on this while the Democrats have a pretty coherent message.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, the Republicans having won the big win that they had waited for for years when the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade have now had a terrible time figuring out where to land as a party on the issue of abortion. And Democrats have clearly gained ground on it. Mr. Trump's statement over the weekend seems to me that he is about as purely political as anything you could imagine. I think his stances on this issue all along have been that way, and he's calculated that the issue is hurting the Republicans and would hurt him if he adhered to it as his party's nominee. So he flipped on it, and he is now clearly pro-abortion rights. At least he is against banning abortion at any stage of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 It's going to be interesting to see how people who have stuck with him, people on the religious right, evangelicals and so on, will feel about this, if it's going to hurt him.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So far nothing he has said hurt his standing with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Juan, one of the reasons why Democrats limited their losses in the House in the 2022 midterms was because of the abortion message. Is this going to be a way for them to potentially get the House back?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think it is. Look, we have been going over all the last few days that don't look good for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And one thing we don't factor in is not only \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as a as the opponent in the general election, but the impact of the abortion decision, the Dobbs decision by the Supreme Court, that continue roil and be a defining magnet for Democrats. It energizes them and brings out Democratic voters, especially those famous suburban females.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Does this spell trouble for Republicans?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Every single governor who signed a heartbeat bill, the same kind of bill that the former president criticized, got reelected. All of them. Republicans have passed these bills in states that were even purple states, and they have done so successfully, and they have done so with people who would advocate for them.\par \par \uc2 Him undermining them at this moment, at a moment when the pro-life cause believes that they are very much indebted to the former president for achieving this historical aspect of reversing Roe is something I think that is essentially going to test how much those voters believe that they can get out of him in a second term. If they believe that this term is going to be about building his own legacy or in some ways trying to find some peace with "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " readers that he very much wants to be held in approval by, then I think that there is an actual opening there for conservatives who are willing to stand up on the abortion issue, willing to really take it to the former president on this issue, to basically say you can't trust him another four years the way you invested in him last time around.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Only four months and a little bit left to go before the first contest. We'll see if anybody can make inroads on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us, folks, because coming up next, the spending, the Senate dress code, and jitters about a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Later on, shark bites man, we've heard that before, but man saves shark? That you need to see.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL HAGERTY, (R-TN): The \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  is the world's most important deliberative body. And to see it completely downgraded, no dress code now, I just think it's a step in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARKWAYNE MULLIN\uc2 , (R-OK): There's a side of me that's super excited about it because I hate wearing a tie and I would rather be in blue jeans and a pair of boots and a white t-shirt, but the fact is that you do dress for the job.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And we're back with the panel now as across the street from us they are changing the dress code in the Senate. No longer will you have to wear a coat and tie or suit pants when you are on the floor of the Senate. Brit, there are more than a few people who are saying this is a nod to the casually attired \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  as a man who is always decked out in sartorial splendor. Why don't you start us off here?\par \par \uc2 Fetterman is a man had difficulty because of the stroke that he had, the severe stroke that he suffered, in handling his duties as senator. And you remember he was out and hospitalized for a long period of time with continuing after-effects from the stroke. You would think that he would want people to think he is truly fit for the job. And I don't know why he thinks it's such a great idea for him to show up on the Senate floor dressed like the man, looking like the man who came to fix the air conditioning. These are gym clothes he wears.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HUME: And I don't think it adds to his credibility and I don't think it adds to the Senate's credibility. There is such a thing as decorum. Decorum means fitness, but it doesn't mean fitness in the way that he apparently thinks.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Juan, I want to put up on the screen here pictures of four members of Congress in the Senate and the House, \uc2 Ben Ray Lujan\uc2 , \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 , Mark Kirk, and Tim Johnson. These are all members of Congress who had strokes. Tim Johnson had an aneurysm that led to a massive stroke. And they came back from their strokes, and they continued to wear a coat and tie. Why should \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  be able to go in his gym clothes to the floor of the Senate?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don't make the connection between the fact that he had a stroke and what --\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Because people are saying this is what is he comfortable in. This highlights his recovery.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don't buy that argument. I don't buy that argument.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: That's what his colleagues are saying.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No. I think that they're saying, listen, this is the way he likes to dress. But I don't think it's because of his stroke.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Why should he get a bye?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don't think he should get a bye. In fact, Ben and I were talking about this in the green room, and I said, you know, I don't think all traditions need to be protected, but if you are in the gallery or you've been on the Senate floor, it's a workplace. You expect people to dress in a decent, respectable fashion. So do I sound like I'm an old geezer? OK. But I'm telling you, that's the way I feel about it, John. Sincerely. What's next? Bare feet, you can just walk in there? No, don't do that.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Flip-flops or slides are the next best thing.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: I don't want to see senator in Crocs. Come on, this is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Crocs would be a step up for some people.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: But look, this is completely unacceptable. And look, let's have a higher standard at some point that expects our senators to adopt a certain level of decorum. And I think that this is absurd. The idea that it's being done in order to further the way that he feels about himself, if that's the rationalization, is absolutely ridiculous. The guy could --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I do think we can do away with powdered wigs.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: That's fine. That's fine.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I don't know. I think, Brit, you still have a couple of those in your closet, don't you?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HUME: I do. I do.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: There was time when they looked really good on people.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 HUME: They still wear them in the courtrooms in England.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: That's true, they do. Thankfully they don't wear them across the street anymore. Thank you, all. We'll see you again very soon.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, my. Babe, it's too dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: A massive mako shark washed ashore at a Pensacola, Florida beach. She said hey, that's do dangerous. She was half right. Several men came together to drag the shark back into the water. It thrashed and threw rescuers aside as they tried to help, tried their best to stay away from the business end of the shark. Onlookers watched as the shark flailed at the air, showing his teeth several times during the struggle. It did swim away, though, after a few minutes of being back in the water. And the men received a standing ovation from the crowd. A thing like that could win you a Darwin Award, but thankfully for them it turned out better than that.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, Bret Baier anchors the show from Riyadh, Saudi Arabia, with the inside look at the kingdom. On Wednesday, Bret is going to head the first major American television news channel interview with the Saudi Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230919ej9i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Italy Calls For Naval Blockade to Deal With Migrant Surge; Hunter Biden Sues IRS; Climate Protests; \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  to Visit United States; American Hostages Freed From Iran; \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  Strike Continues\b0\par\par\uc2 Bill Melugin, David Spunt, Chad Pergram, Trey Yingst, Peter Doocy,  Grady Trimble, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,878 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: It is day four, and the union fight continuing today at a Ford assembly plant in Michigan, as the rallying cry against the Big Three automakers continues.\par \par \uc2 And the strikers pitch' comes down to this: What you did for yourselves, you better do for us.\par \par \uc2 That's it. Then there's the whole striking shooting works, \uc2 UAW\uc2  Shawn Fain's simple negotiating strategy for the big automakers. Those big pay raises you gave yourselves over the last four years, give them to us for the next four years.\par \par \uc2 FOX on top of union members going for the gold, not necessarily going for CEO pay, just the same percentage hike that CEOs are seeing in their pay. That's why strikers can reject 20 and 21 percent pay raises. They figure, compared to the boss, that's chicken feet, and this game of chicken is only just begun.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 To Grady Trimble in Wayne, Michigan, right now on how workers are turning some very generous pay packages for their bosses to their advantage -- Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  and the Big Three automakers were back at the bargaining table today. And we just got an update from \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , who says the discussion was constructive and focused on where we can find common ground to reach an agreement that provides a bridge to the future by enabling the company to meet the challenges of electrification.\par \par \uc2 You noted that the workers want better pay, Neil. They also want a guarantee that, as these companies transition to electric vehicles, they will be brought along on that transition. If you think this strike is going to end quickly, listen to how the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president, Shawn Fain, described the talks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN, PRESIDENT, UNITED AUTO WORKERS: Really, things are still status quo, moving slowly. We had some meetings over the weekend, but we still have a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 And that's going to be up to the companies on how this plays out. So we will see how things progress the next few days. And if we have to amp up pressure, that's what we're going to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has welcomed a number of Democratic lawmakers who wanted to walk with picketing workers and stand in solidarity with them.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says he is sending two administration officials to Detroit to help with the negotiations. Biden also says that, as the automakers are earning record profits, they should share those profits with workers. You would think that message would resonate with these striking workers, but listen to what they told me when I asked if they think the Biden administration should be involved in these talks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I say, we don't need his help?\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Why not?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Because we are a united group of people. We don't need the president's help.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's our fight. Let us fight our fight. That's all we want. We don't want to hurt the economy. We don't want to be here not getting paid peanuts. But it's our fight. It's a fight for our life.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think we can negotiate our contract with the company without the government getting involved.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Already, Ford says it has laid off 600 workers at this plant who are not on strike because of these walkouts.\par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2  says it could have to idle a facility in Kansas as a result of the picketing in Missouri. That shows you how interconnected the auto plants are. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  is threatening to ramp up these strikes, depending on how negotiations go. But they're not saying when they might do that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Of course, that is the strategy: Keep the automakers guessing -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And they have this strike fund, right, with about $825 million. That assures they're getting paid on average $500 a week to get them through this. But they could deplete that pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: They do.\par \par \uc2 And they have said that if workers are laid off as a result of the strikes, they would also potentially get money from that strike fund, that $500 a week that you mentioned. So that would deplete the fund even further. But that's part of the reason that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is going about this strike in this way, because, if they spread out the picketing across three plants at each of the Big Three automakers, then that strike fund lasts quite a bit longer.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Grady, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Grady Trimble in Wayne, Michigan, on all of that.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to Dave Dodson, a very good professor, author of the bestseller "The Manager's Handbook," Stanford School of Business lecturer.\par \par \uc2 And, Professor, what's interesting here is, when you hear union members turn up their nose at up to a 21 percent pay raise, you know they're feeling their oats here. And they're confident they can do a lot better than that. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 DAVE DODSON, STANFORD UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF BUSINESS: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 And add to that Neil, the comment there about how they don't feel like they need the president of the United States to come help them. I mean, they really feel like they have got a big hand here. And it's interesting how Fain and the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is handling the strike, which is very different.\par \par \uc2 These -- they call them stand-up strikes. And instead of the sort of gentlemanly way that we used to do it, which is the union would say, we're going to strike and we're going to go after Ford, and we're going to do it on this date, Fain's got a very different strategy. He's just kind of going in and marching in and going into these micro-strikes.\par \par \uc2 And we have not seen that in 20 years. The airline attendants did it 20 years ago. They would go in and strike against a particular flight. And they called it chaos, by the way, and it brought the airline to its knees. This is really interesting. I mean, they have got almost a billion dollars in their strike fund, which is enough money, but not a lot.\par \par \uc2 But when you only have to go strike a few plants at a time, it's cheaper. It's more intimidating. And it's more disruptive, because, as you just noticed, if you strike one plant, that can ripple through to other plants.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, indeed, we're learning as of this hour that \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the parent company for Chrysler, is now on the wire saying it might have to close 18 facilities. Don't know where they all are.\par \par \uc2 So the spillover effect is real. What will the impact be on the economy?\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Well, the -- so labor used to be a huge component of a car. So now it's a distant, distant second to materials. So that will help actually the odds of getting the parties together again, because labor isn't anywhere near as big a component, which, by the way, is part of the reason we're in this situation, is because labor does not have the role that it used to have in the unions.\par \par \uc2 But if the strike goes on for a long time, of course, it's going to have a huge impact on the economy, because, right now, we have this fragile economy that we just kind of feel like we have landed the plane. And we might crash if that happens.\par \par \uc2 But what really matters right now in my mind, Neil, is what the public thinks right now. And it's interesting. In 15 short years, if you can imagine this, the public perception of unions has gone from an all-time low, never lower than this, 48 percent, to the second highest positive perception of unions since 1953, when, by the way, "Dragnet" and "I Love Lucy" was on the airwaves.\par \par \uc2 So we had like this dramatic change in how we think about unions. I got to tell you one other thing, Neil, that's interesting, is that, at major business schools, the MBAs who someday are supposed to be running these companies think that unions have a positive impact on the economy by a 3-1 ratio.\par \par \uc2 I think that's amazing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think, Professor, that's the fault of maybe some selfish CEOs? They are paid some eye-popping sums. We're a free country. We will do whatever the market bears. I get that.\par \par \uc2 But that a lot of these workers have quickly cited the fact that, over the last four years, these CEOs have seen their pay climb to about 40 percent. They want the same. And I'm wondering whether this sort of triggers other workers in other industries and other companies to do the same.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Well, we're seeing, as you and I have talked about on the show before, the biggest set of strikes in the last 20 years.\par \par \uc2 And I really do believe that, certainly, there's a part of it that has to do with economics. But there's also part of it just feeling like, it's not fair. Like, I want my fair share.\par \par \uc2 And it's interesting that these strikes started at places like \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , where the chief executives and the founders were making billion -- literally billions of dollars. So part of this is sort of a sense of fairness.\par \par \uc2 And you have seen that in the electorate, by the way. And you can't pull apart the strike with the upcoming election. They do go together. And you think about what both parties kind of -- the vice both parties are in, because, in Michigan, Michigan was won by 600 -- or -- excuse me -- there are 600,000 union vote -- union members -- excuse me -- in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 And the margin of victory in Michigan was 150,000 votes. You think that's amazing...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: ... in Pennsylvania, there are 700,000 union members. And the margin of victory was only 80,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 So neither political party can go in there and take on the unions or push back on them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. Handle it delicately, right, all right, to put it mildly.\par \par \uc2 Dave Dodson, thank you, Dave. Always good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, the White House reaction has said, we have these two representatives who we're interested in bringing down there to Michigan if and when you need them.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, reaction to the return of those American hostages now out of Iran and into freedom, but not without some controversy.\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy at the White House with more on that -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And, Neil, the plane carrying those hostages is in the air on its way to the D.C. area.\par \par \uc2 Not everybody happy about the way this went down, though. I will explain after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, five Americans who were released in that Iran prisoner swap are now on free soil, not necessarily back in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The latest on the reaction and how the White House is dealing with the fallout. Peter Doocy is there.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Peter.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Yes, Neil, the plane is in the air. It'll land within the next couple hours here in the D.C. area.\par \par \uc2 And officials at the White House are telling us that they think they can guarantee the $6 billion in Iranian money they just unfroze will only be used for food and medicine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: From day one, our sanctions have clearly, and, indeed always exempt the use of resources for humanitarian purposes, because our aim is not to harm the Iranian people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But even if this specific $6 billion only goes for food and medicine, there is new concern the Iranians could just redo their budget and use $6 billion they no longer need to put towards food and medicine back home towards something else.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: This is a ransom payment of about $6 billion dollars, so about a billion bucks per prisoner.\par \par \uc2 For every bad actor, this says, take an American, you will get a billion dollars. This is material. It is terrible policy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: One of the Americans held hostage since 2015, Siamak Namazi, now says: "I am dying to find out what gadgets now exist. When I was taken hostage, the iPhone 6s had just come out. You cannot imagine what an eight- year itch feels like."\par \par \uc2 We understand that they have had emotional conversations as well with the secretary of state and the president. And that's about all that we know about the president's day today. He is in New York, no events on the schedule until some campaign fund-raisers this evening -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Peter.\par \par \uc2 To Lieutenant Colonel Bob Maginnis, \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 ," Divided We Stand" his latest bestseller.\par \par \uc2 Colonel, the deal is done, nothing's going to change. So now what?\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. BOB MAGINNIS (RET.), \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2 : Well, here we are giving $6 billion to a terror organization.\par \par \uc2 Money, Neil, is fungible. The Iranians know that. It's not going to necessarily end up in the stomachs of Iranian people, but in the weapons they give to terrorists that are attacking Israel and our interests around the world. So, this is a bad deal.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I applaud the freedom of these five that are coming home, but, at the same time, the policy is bankrupt. We haven't learned anything since the Obama years, when JCPOA was abandoned by the Trump administration because the Iranians were putting together a nuclear program. And every evidence is that they're continuing.\par \par \uc2 In fact, three European nations this past week declared that the 60 percent inventory of enriched uranium that the Iranians have has no civilian use. It's only for a future nuclear program. And, oh, by the way, they have a fourth-generation missile that can carry 4,000 pounds, a nuclear weapon or two, and could deliver it into the underbelly of Europe, much less all of Israel and many of our U.S. facilities.\par \par \uc2 So this is not a good deal policy-wise. Humanitarian, I understand, but that's not a good justification.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I wonder. We seem to be the ones reaching out to Iran on this deal. We reach out to China rather frequently trying to score any deal, or at least spur talks.\par \par \uc2 It gives the appearance sometimes that we're desperate, Colonel. Should we be?\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: Well, I think we are desperate. And not only will this deal put a dollar price on the backs of Americans traveling into Iran and other places, but I think tomorrow, when the Iranian president is at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . -- and he will declare basically the U.S. is a weak partner in this sort of deal.\par \par \uc2 And it will bring up reflections of our abandonment of Afghanistan. So we do not need to fuel terror. We suffered tremendously on 9/11. Afghanistan, we have given back to Al Qaeda. And now the Iranians who are fueling terrorism -- and I have seen it firsthand, Neil, when I was in Iraq what they did to our American soldiers. They killed a lot of us.\par \par \uc2 And that's not something I think most Americans understand or appreciate, that this $6 billion is just a gift back to the Iranians to continue their sort of treachery that they have done against the West and against especially Americans for many years.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Another thing that's odd about it as well, Colonel, is the fact that Russia now depends on these sort of rogue nations for help. He just met with, of course, North Korea's leader, Kim Jong-un, maybe to get some goodies from them.\par \par \uc2 Already they have gotten a good number of drones that are in use as we speak in Ukraine now. So where is this still going?\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: Yes, Kim Jong-un is happy to sell .155 munitions to the Russians for use in Ukraine, the Iranians, of course, their drones. They have even sent advisers to Crimea to advise the Russians on the employment of these drones.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't appear as if we're doing very well with any of the rogues around the world, whether it's the treachery of the Russians in Ukraine or the North Koreans against the South and elsewhere. The Iranians, of course, are trying to bolster their position in the Middle East. And it appears as if they're doing well with the Chinese help.\par \par \uc2 And, unfortunately, we're going to probably continue to turn in the wrong direction. Policy does matter here, Neil. And, unfortunately, the policy of this administration is not taking us down a track that's going to be safe long-term, not only economically for the U.S., but certainly in terms of security and the threats that are opposing us today.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Colonel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Scary stuff, but I appreciate your insight, as always.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, a lot of attention to \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  coming to the United States. It's his second big visit here. He includes a number of important stops, but, right now, he might be more concerned not so much about how he's dealing with Russia, but how he could deal or hopes to deal with a number of Republicans who are no longer keen on his fight against Russia.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Ukrainian President Zelenskyy coming to the United States. He might have a big tin cup in hand amid talk that some Republicans want to stop giving him $24 billion in more aid.\par \par \uc2 What to do -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Forget Russia. Right now, the Ukrainian president, Zelenskyy, could have more difficulty rallying Republicans in this country, increasingly skeptical of pouring what they say is good money after bad.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst on the impact of all of that in Jerusalem. -- Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 This comes as the war rages on. And with any counteroffensive, there will be ups and downs. But over the weekend, Ukraine seems to have shifted the momentum, recapturing some key territory just to the south of the city of Bakhmut.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian forces say they liberated the villages of Klishchiivka and Andriivka, both areas that were heavily fought over in the Donbass region. Ukraine has also reported some success on the southern Zaporizhzhia front, as its troops try to break through heavily fortified Russian lines.\par \par \uc2 Now, overnight there were Ukrainian strikes in Crimea, as explosions were heard in the port city of Sevastopol. Russia continued its aerial attacks overnight against Ukraine, firing 18 cruise missiles and 17 drones into the neighboring country, according to reports.\par \par \uc2 Now, these developments do come as Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  just landed in the United States, where he will attend the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2  and meet this week with President Biden. The Ukrainian leader will also meet with a variety of U.S. lawmakers amid a push for an additional $24 billion in humanitarian and military aid.\par \par \uc2 The requests come amid a political shakeup that is reshaping the \uc2 Ukrainian Defense Ministry\uc2  after allegations of fraud and corruption. Today, just 12 days after Ukraine appointed Rustem Umerov to be the country's next defense minister, all six of Ukraine's deputy defense ministers have been dismissed from their positions.\par \par \uc2 Now, no reason was given by the new defense minister in Ukraine for these dismissals, other than this was a reboot of the system. But it is certainly of note this week as Zelenskyy is in the United States meeting with U.S. lawmakers -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for that, Trey.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley got together with me on my weekend show, "Cavuto Live," to talk about the pushback from Republicans on this matter. She's not pleased. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I always speak hard truths because I think the American people deserve it.\par \par \uc2 I think that we have to look at this for what it is. America can never be so arrogant to think that we don't need friends. We should never be so arrogant to think that another 9/11 won't happen. What you have is, you have this pro-American, freedom-loving country, democracy, that was invaded by a thug, the same thug who held hands with President Xi prior to the Olympics, and they named themselves unlimited partners.\par \par \uc2 Remember, dictators tell you exactly what they're going to do. They're very transparent. China said they were going to take Hong Kong. They did. Russia said they were going to invade Ukraine. We watched them. China said that Taiwan is next. We better believe them. Russia said, after Ukraine, it's Poland and the Baltics.\par \par \uc2 Neil, that's a war. We're trying to prevent war. My husband's in the military. The last thing I want is for our men and women to go to war.\par \par \uc2 So the way you do that is, you make sure Ukraine wins. That does not mean you give cash to Ukraine. I don't believe in giving straight-up cash to any country. That doesn't believe you -- mean you put troops on the ground.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I understand that. But you know there are some...\par \par \uc2 HALEY: It means we work with our allies to make sure they have the equipment and the ammunition to win.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I apologize.\par \par \uc2 There are many House GOP members who want to strip out that -- I believe the $24 billion targeted for Ukraine in a defense appropriations measure. What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I tell them that we have spent 3.5 percent of our defense budget. Percentage of GDP, 11 European countries have put in more than us. That's a small price to pay to prevent war. That's a small price to pay to prevent my husband and other military members from having to go to war.\par \par \uc2 A win for Russia is a win for China. We can never forget that. If Ukraine wins, it's the biggest message to China in terms of invading Taiwan. Why do the Taiwanese support America helping Ukraine so much? Because they know, if Ukraine wins, China will leave them alone. We have to remember that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, well, that is not a dominant view in the Republican Party, even a dominant view among the Republican presidential candidates.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram following that closely on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Chad, where is all this going?\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, right now, a coalition of Republicans crafted a bill to fund the government for a month, but also bolster border security, but the bill lacks the votes to pass.\par \par \uc2 Dusty Johnson\uc2  of South Dakota crafted the plan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. DUSTY JOHNSON (R-SD): I think this is what always happens in the House. Nothing happens easily around here. If it's not the path forward, then we will find another way.\par \par \uc2 The reality is that we can't shut down government and we must secure the border. Our framework, our deal does that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: House Speaker Kevin McCarthy has tried to get the GOP to approve annual spending bills since July.\par \par \uc2 The House has OKed one. Republicans even failed to pass their own defense spending bill last week. That means the House loses leverage to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The Senate -- is that part of the idea to demonstrate to them that they're going to get jammed by the Senate if they don't move something over here and strengthen your position?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Look, the best way the system works is, we pass our individual bills.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, I have some members denying those come up. We're going to keep working through that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: McCarthy also told me a government shutdown slows impeachment.\par \par \uc2 GOP Indiana Representative Victoria Spartz called McCarthy a -- quote -- "weak speaker" and accused him of not leading. McCarthy took aim at Spartz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: One thing I learned in life, anybody who criticizes you has never worked harder than you. And, I mean, if Victoria is concerned about fighting stronger, I wish she would have run again and not quit.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I'm not quitting. I'm going to continue to work for the American public.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Now, the way to avert a government shutdown is to pass a straight temporary bill with a cocktail of Democrats and GOP members voting yes.\par \par \uc2 However, that could prompt a challenge to McCarthy's speakership. McCarthy says he's not worried about that -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Chad Pergram, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Well, climate protesters are nothing new. Where they have their protests over the last couple of days, today, in particular, kind of new.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, never mind rocketing oil and now gasoline prices, at least a dozen states paying over four bucks a gallon, on average. That continues, but so do protesters looking for an end to fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 Bob Cusack on the fallout from all of that.\par \par \uc2 They picked more original places to have these protests. This is the \uc2 New York Fed\uc2 . They have had them in New York City, in Central Park and elsewhere. And it's gone nationwide. But the backdrop are higher energy prices as a result of what's been happening.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 BOB CUSACK, EDITOR IN CHIEF, THE HILL: Well, I mean, this is a problem for the White House, which has been saying that gas prices have been dropping. And they were, but now they're rising again.\par \par \uc2 And, generally speaking, Neil, Democrats on health care and abortion, generally speaking, they're on the same page. On energy and the climate, they're not. These activists want Biden to go much further to say there's a climate emergency.\par \par \uc2 Biden has not been a Green New Deal guy. He certainly did pass some climate legislation through the Inflation Reduction Act, but that's not good enough. And it's really never probably going to be good enough for this aspect of the left.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Even a lot of those strikers right now targeting the Big Three automakers have been saying that the E.V. push, the electric vehicle push on the part of their bosses, really at the behest of those in Washington, have not really delivered the goods.\par \par \uc2 So they know that whatever contract they can agree on will come thanks to the money being made over traditional gas-powered cars. It is a weird conundrum, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: It is.\par \par \uc2 And as far as the E.V. push, I mean, there are problems with our electricity grid and rolling brownouts or blackouts, and that infrastructure has to be addressed by Congress. So this is going to be difficult. And as you mentioned, there are some things that you think, OK, this is a perfect solution, and maybe not so perfect, at least not yet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so where does it go?\par \par \uc2 Because the administration is quite literally between a rock and a hard place. The same folks it wants to keep behind them, that is, the climate -- the climate advocates and all of that, are not really in sync with some of the auto industry and other union players who say that it hurts them.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes, I mean, most of the Democratic establishment, not all, is behind another Biden what they hope is a victory in 2024.\par \par \uc2 But this is going to be a problem, and I think the White House basically has to say the same message. Well, what's the alternative? If it's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it's a lot worse for your cause, and they're going to have to hammer that home.\par \par \uc2 But that's the real concern, Neil, is that some of these people who came out in 2020 for Biden may not come out in 2024.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Speaking of that, I want to end on this note. There are at least seven battleground states in which \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , for the moment, trails \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I mean, we could have a sort of a flip side of an election where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  maybe easily wins, if he were the nominee, the electoral vote. It could be a fight for the popular vote, but it is a significant development, the likes of which we had not seen since 2016. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: I mean, I think this race right now is a total toss-up. Biden has -- he's the incumbent, and it's always good to be the incumbent, but his poll numbers, as you mentioned, Neil, they're not good.\par \par \uc2 Biden barely won three years ago. And, listen, it's going to be very difficult to replicate that, because now they're going to have to make the case, you're better off than you were four years ago, but most people don't think that, and that's going to be a tough sell, because I think that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's going to have to run more on his record, because he is president of the United States. Last time, he was not.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Indeed, he was.\par \par \uc2 All right, thank you very much, my friend. We will be following it very closely.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, you might not have much sympathy for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , given all of his run-ins with the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , but now he's suing the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , and he has a message, or at least his lawyers do, for you too. What happened to him when it comes to privacy invasion could happen to you -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is going after the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , essentially for going after him, but, more importantly, for violating his privacy here. And he says that there are implications for you as well, that it could happen to you as well.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt with more at the Justice Department -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is coming out swinging, starting a brand-new week, hoping to change the narrative just days after he was indicted on those 2018 felony gun charges. Now, this lawsuit targeting the \uc2 IRS\uc2  starts out with the definition of a whistle-blower.\par \par \uc2 It reads, in part: "A whistle-blower is supposed to uncover government misconduct, not the details of that employee's opinion about the alleged wrongdoing of a private person."\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit argues \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistle-blower Gary Shapley and Joe Ziegler in public statements and interviews -- quote -- "blatantly violated Section 6103 of the \uc2 IRS\uc2  code by engaging in a campaign to publicly smear Mr. Biden."\par \par \uc2 It says: "He has all the same responsibilities as any other American citizen. And the \uc2 IRS\uc2  can and should make certain that he abides by those responsibilities. Mr. Biden has no fewer or lesser rights than any other American citizen, and no government agency or government agent has free rein to violate his rights simply because of who he is."\par \par \uc2 Now, I mentioned those three counts involving purchase of a gun in 2018, but that may not be his only legal problem in a federal courtroom, Neil. U.S. attorney David Weiss in a court filing last month announced that he could go after \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and indict him on those tax charges that were part of the plea deal.\par \par \uc2 We could be watching for indictments in either California, where \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  currently lives, or Washington, D.C., where he once lived before -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you for that,David Spunt.\par \par \uc2 To Katie Cherkasky now, the former federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of all of this, Katie?\par \par \uc2 KATIE CHERKASKY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Well, as a criminal defense attorney myself, I'm very sensitive to claims of governmental violations of rights.\par \par \uc2 And so I think, as a broad-brush topic here, it is important that any government agents follow procedures for disclosing information. Now, pertaining to the specific lawsuit, I didn't really see any allegations in there from \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's attorneys that would tend to show that the \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistle-blowers overstepped their bounds in disclosing private tax information.\par \par \uc2 More so, it was discussion of the deficiencies with the investigative process, which is really kind of a fine line. So the judge is going to have to go through there and see if there was actual overstepping, but I didn't see anything in the written motion specifically.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And maybe my skull is too thick, Katie, but what's the difference? Revealing what's in those returns is revealing what's in those returns, right?\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: Well, that's correct. But a lot of what is alleged in the motion is really the discussions of the fact that there was pushback on some of the investigative staffs that the investigators wanted to take.\par \par \uc2 And there was some insinuations of who the returns may be related to, which I think could be more of an issue. But, certainly, I think that it's a valid point to make that there are procedures for disclosing otherwise confidential and private information. And if those were not followed by government agents, then certainly that needs to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of the actual idea of what whistle-blowers are allowed to disclose, there is allowance for them to get into concerns with the efficiency or deficiencies with the government agency that they're working for. So there's really a fine line there between private taxpayer information and concerns with the overall processing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 But this is not the first, Katie. And, ironically, I can't believe I'm pairing the two together, the grievances \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  might have with the \uc2 IRS\uc2  as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had with the \uc2 IRS\uc2  and the government, et al, this idea that they do leak stuff. And the \uc2 IRS\uc2  has done this before.\par \par \uc2 And you can question their reliability at times, especially armed now with potentially tens of thousands of our agents. The cynics are going to come out and say, here they go. They're revved up and ready to do a lot more.\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: Yes, I absolutely agree.\par \par \uc2 I think that we need to hold our government accountable, regardless of who the defendant is. That's the entire point of the system here. So I think it raises valid questions. And I think that there needs to be processes that are followed, especially when we are talking about potential criminal matters or really any confidential or personal matters.\par \par \uc2 So I'm not sure in this case that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's has a claim that's meritorious, but the idea that these agents might willy-nilly be discussing things outside of allowable channels is in fact something that needs to be looked into closely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't want to sound like the part of the black helicopter crowd here, Katie, but could this be an effort on the part of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and his lawyers to deflect additional charges that could be coming on these tax issues, keep them at the gun issues that are out there, the various charges that could land him in jail for up to 25 years, unlikely though that may be, but a way to make sure, well, these will never come down the pike now?\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: This is not going to kill any sort of tax case on the criminal side, but, certainly, I think \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has plenty of grievances against these \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistle-blowers.\par \par \uc2 For all intents and purposes, their testimony really led to the downfall of that plea agreement in many respects. So, certainly, I'm sure he's not pleased. I'm sure his attorneys are very upset with that, but I do not believe that, even if the judge agreed that these disclosures were unlawful, that that would put the kibosh on any sort of criminal prosecution of that.\par \par \uc2 They're really unrelated in a certain sense.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think more charges are coming?\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: Well, I would anticipate that at least the tax charges will be filed.\par \par \uc2 Now, I guess the big question is, will there be anything other than that, that somehow is able to be brought by David Weiss? I think it looks like he's trying to avoid implicating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And when you start going down any of these paths with Hunter in any of the finances or taxes, it really leads very quickly back to questions regarding \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I'm not sure that he's ready to go down that path, but, again, we're all waiting with bated breath for that one.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, we always are. And you're always there to help us sort it out, Katie Cherkasky.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Katie. Good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we have a big migrant problem. Italy has a big migrant problem. We're trying to deal with the sieve, the people, thousands coming here, Italy trying to deal with thousands coming on rickety boats there.\par \par \uc2 The only difference is, the prime minister of Italy has a plan, a naval blockade, to force the issue and stop the surge.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Italy has a doozy of a problem with migrants. It's not just us. In fact, they came with a rather serious solution to deal with, the prime minister there recommending a naval blockade of North Africa to prevent the thousands who've made their way to Italy to stop coming to Italy.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Union\uc2  is urging the prime minister to keep the calm, but it's hard to do that as, well, the migrants keep coming. We have a similar problem that's gotten very bold, to put it mildly, in this country, in fact, those who find a free train ride here as well at a time when they get easy passage of the United States and no one seems to stop them.\par \par \uc2 Bill Melugin on top of those developments in Mission, Texas -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Early this morning, in Eagle Pass, Texas, we witnessed one of the largest mass illegal crossings we have ever seen in the last 2.5 years. We will get right into this video. Take a look. Border Patrol sources telling us a staggering 2,200 people crossed illegally into Eagle Pass just after midnight this morning.\par \par \uc2 You can see them all streaming through the Rio Grande in this video given to us by a source on the Mexican side of the river earlier this morning, this from Piedras Negras, Mexico, as this mass of illegal immigrants cross into Texas.\par \par \uc2 You take a look at this second piece of video, another perspective showing almost a stampede of these migrants, hundreds upon hundreds of them rushing down to the edge of the river trying to get into the water to cross illegally into Eagle Pass.\par \par \uc2 Then, in the morning, our drone team got on scene. Take a look at this. Once all those illegal immigrants had gotten to the U.S. side of the border in Eagle Pass and were waiting for processing, our drone was overhead, a lot of single adults, some family units, as these migrants continue to have little to no fear of consequences for crossing the border illegally.\par \par \uc2 They are not listening to the Biden administration messaging of do not come. They cross and they expect to be released into the United States. And it appears a lot more may be on the way. Take a look at this video out of Zacatecas, Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Sources providing FOX this video showing a train loaded with migrants heading straight for the U.S.-Mexico border. You can see the migrants cheering and waving to the cameras as they pass, as they go through Mexico, traveling north, trying to get to the U.S.-Mexico border, where they will inevitably cross illegally, as, again, they are not listening to Biden administration messaging that the border is closed and that they should not come. And, Neil, CBP sources are telling us that, as of this morning, they had over 20,000 migrants in federal custody. That's even with all these mass street releases they have been doing. They say they're reaching a breaking point and that things here at the Southern border have never been worse.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right Bill Melugin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 To Brandon Judd, the National Border Patrol Council president.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, it almost makes you feel like we have to do what they're doing or plan to do in Italy, a blockade. A little bit different, of course, when you're talking about a landlocked border here. But I'm just wondering, what do you think of the way Italy is dealing with this?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: Well, I think that what they're doing is absolutely astonishing.\par \par \uc2 I think that when you look at this liberal government recognizing how bad this issue is, the people of Italy realizing how bad this issue is, and they're actually going to start to do something, yet our government isn't going to follow that suit.\par \par \uc2 Now, they're going to put up a blockade because their laws allow them. We can't do a blockade. Even if we stretched our border hand in hand with Border Patrol agents, that group of 2,200 migrants that crossed the border, we can't push them back into Mexico. So we can't stop them from coming. We have to take them into custody.\par \par \uc2 But there are other things that this administration could do to stop this. And we have proven that in the past. President Trump proved what policies are effective. All we have to have from this administration is the proper policy, and we could stop this tomorrow. They just don't want to do it because they're worried about their base supporters.\par \par \uc2 They're worried that they're going to get them out to the polls. And so they constantly appease them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But now those base supporters are getting a little antsy, if you think about it, right, Brandon, because the New York mayor, Eric Adams, and the New York Democratic governor, Hochul, are concerned that this is so out of control, the border, especially when it's at their doorsteps.\par \par \uc2 When you get a state that had declared that it would be a sanctuary state just a few years ago, New Jersey, saying, no mas, we can't deal with this, there is a division in the ranks there.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: There's a little bit of a division, but that division hasn't grown big enough.\par \par \uc2 I mean, when you have AOC that goes out that does a rally in favor of people coming to New York City, saying that there's a good idea for a tax on the American people to support these people in New York City, so there is a little bit of a chink in the armor, but it hasn't grown big enough yet.\par \par \uc2 And until that base changes its mind, until that base realizes that we have to do what's best for this country, not what's best for other countries or for other people from other countries, until they realize that we have to do what's best for this country, we're going to continue to see this problem.\par \par \uc2 This administration will not do anything until its base changes its mind.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I'm wondering if that base did get a little bit of a shocker though, Brandon, when \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  and \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2 , two liberals who were speaking to this group, were booed down, essentially shouted down.\par \par \uc2 I don't think they anticipated that. Do you think that was a wakeup moment or a short-lived one?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: I think it's very short-lived.\par \par \uc2 And what they're doing, and listening to all the rhetoric on Capitol Hill - - I spent an awful lot of time there -- listening to that rhetoric, what they're saying is, they're saying those were conservatives that were out there saying. That wasn't their base. That's what -- that's the rhetoric that's going around right now.\par \par \uc2 And that's what they would have everybody believe, even though, Neil, you and I both know that citizens of New York City, they're fed up with this. The base is in fact fed up with it. But until there's a lot more voices out there, this administration is going to continue doing what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 Remember, we could stop this tomorrow. There are ways to do it. This administration won't give us that policy that's necessary.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, I think it hits home when it is at home, though, when it is on the streets in New York City, just a couple of blocks from here and you have hundreds of migrants lined up.\par \par \uc2 I mean, maybe that is the moment.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Well, New York -- for New York City, it is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: They have reached the breaking point.\par \par \uc2 The Border Patrol, we have reached the breaking point. But that's New York City. That might be Los Angeles. But there's a bunch of other liberal cities. There's a bunch of liberal -- other liberal states. They haven't reached the breaking point yet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: And so the base still is where it's at. They're not going to give up.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you might be right about that.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230919ej9i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview with Rep. \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  (R-NY); Interview with Rep. \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  (D-CA); Fox News Polls Show Support Shifting in Key Voting Groups from Biden to Trump; White House Dismisses Concerns About Biden's Age; Former President Trump Expands Sizeable Poll Lead in GOP Primary Race; Ramaswamy's Unfavorable Rating Rises Among Voters in Latest Fox Poll; Ramaswamy Calls to Slash Federal Workforce by 75%; Ramaswamy Promises to Defend Taiwan Until U.S. Has Semiconductor Independence\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Lucas Tomlinson, Karl Rove \par \uc2 10,483 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Autoworkers are on strike, the president's son indicted, the House Republican leader launches an impeachment inquiry and struggles to find the votes to fund the government.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: I'm going to continue to just focus on what's the right thing to do for the American people. And, you know what, if it takes a fight, we'll have a fight.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Days from now, the government is set to run out of money. And hardliners are pushing back on a framework they say spends too much.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm disappointed with Kevin. These are a lot of good things, but on the spending, that's I can't stand (ph).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll discuss the revolt from the right, including calls for the speaker's job with the number three Republican in the House, New York Congresswoman \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then, while House Republicans debate the politics of an official impeachment inquiry, federal prosecutors indict the president's son on gun charges.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): Ironically, that's the one crime that he committed that you cannot tie \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  into.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): They've never connected anything to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So, it's not surprising that the Justice Department charges have nothing to do with the president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The historic indictment comes at a time when the president's reelection effort is encountering headwinds from rising inflation and gas price and now --\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A walkout by the nation's autoworkers.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Our auto workers help create America's middle class. They deserve a contract that sustains them in the middle class.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're joined by Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , a member of the president's 2024 national advisory board.\par \par \uc2 And our Sunday panel weighs on the challenges facing both parties.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a week away from the second GOP debate. Presidential hopefuls face a surge in polling from the frontrunner. We'll sit down with one of the candidates, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , to discuss his controversial plan to shrink the federal workforce by 75 percent.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Not a corporation, not a virus, not a three-letter government agency is going to defeat us.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 The race is on to avoid a federal government shutdown. Lawmakers have less than a dozen working days to pass a series of spending bills. House Speaker Kevin McCarthy working to get the most conservative members of his party in check, while simultaneously launching an impeachment inquiry into the president.\par \par \uc2 It's all playing as Biden's reelection effort faces concerns from within his own party about, plummeting poll numbers and his son Hunter's indictment on gun charges.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, House Republican \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  of New York and California Democrat \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  will join me to discuss the funding showdown.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turn to Lucas Tomlinson traveling with the president in Wilmington, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, President Biden returned here to his home in Wilmington to rest and reset after that tough week as you mentioned -- issues not likely to go away any time soon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Should Hunter get a pardon, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): President Biden voicing his support for the striking autoworkers, declining to do the same for his son Hunter one day after he was indicted on three gun charges, months after saying he did nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: It's the first time in U.S. history, workers from all three Detroit autoworkers went on strike, days after Biden predicted they would not.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don't think it's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The strike capping a challenging week for the president which included a new report showing inflation increasing for a second straight month, fuelled by increasing gas prices, now up to 50 percent from when Biden took office. Food is up nearly 20 percent over the same period.\par \par \uc2 The inflation report, out one day after House Speaker Kevin McCarthy launched an impeachment inquiry.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: These are allegations of abuse of power, obstruction, and corruption.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: A White House spokesman says this is all to appease McCarthy's Republican critics.\par \par \uc2 IAN SAMS, WHITE HOUSE SPOKESMAN: He's being attacked from his right and he's throwing them red meat.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: McCarthy hopes to probe paves the way for the House to pass a new budget by month's end, to avoid a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 The top House Democrat called it a three-wing circus. Many Republicans disagree.\par \par \uc2 REP. RALPH NORMAN (R-SC): The public deserves to know the facts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is due to return to a courtroom here in Wilmington in about a week to face those gun charges and more tax charges could be coming -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lucas Tomlinson in Wilmington, thanks, Lucas\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, House Republican Conference Chair \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2  (R-NY): Great to be with you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, let's start here, we have this possibility of a government shutdown days away, analysts say mostly of internal strife within the GOP.\par \par \uc2 "Politico", or excuse me, "Axios" puts it this way: Republicans have fallen into a state of paralyzed chaos with no obvious exit ramp in a government spending fight of their own creation.\par \par \uc2 Our brand new FOX News poll also puts congressional approval at 19 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, you guys run the House now. What are you telling the American people about the fact that we can't move a defense bill to fund our troops?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Well, we're in a very good place. I've been engaged in conversation with members, as well as Speaker McCarthy over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 We are working through this, and I'm optimistic that we will continue to move the appropriations process forward. And that includes the DOD appropriations bill but also includes potentially a continuing resolution to ensure that we do not face a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 But what's really important here, Shannon, is on behalf of the American people, we know inflation is one of the top concerns. Ninety-one percent of the American people are concerned about inflation. We have to rein in this reckless, out of control spending that we've seen under the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and that is what your most conservative wing is worried about. They say they made deals, they agreed to a budget deal, there were spending cap priorities, all those kinds of things. They don't think they're going to be honored in this new batch of spending bills.\par \par \uc2 You got \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , one of them, very vocal. He's threatening to call this motion to vacate, to force the speaker out, or at least force a vote on him.\par \par \uc2 If he doesn't what he believes was agreed to, which is cutting and fiscal austerity and, you know, dialing things back, we've got a meeting this week that was described by Congressman \uc2 Brian Mast\uc2  as saying that the speaker said to him, if you want to file a motion to vacate, then file the F-ing motion.\par \par \uc2 How tense are things within the party right now?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Well, I've chaired a lot of the conference meetings. I was actually chairing that meeting. It's our weekly discussions with all the Republican members. And it's --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is it an accurate quote?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: I'm not going to get into it -- I don't quote members in that meeting.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Because I want to make sure that I respect them. But Speaker McCarthy's support is strong within the conference. And he has said that he's going to continue doing his job leading this conference.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm confident we will work through this, and certainly, the majority of members and I believe the speaker will survive any type of motion.\par \par \uc2 But I don't think it's going to get to that, Shannon, because as I said, we are going through great productive conversations with our members including from the House Freedom Caucus, as well as the Republican Study Committee, and the broad Republican conference to work through this appropriation process, but while also standing up for fiscal responsibility and being good steward of taxpayers' dollars and reining in this out of control spending.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, all of this is going along the backdrop now of a formal impeachment inquiry. Just a few weeks ago, earlier this month, the speaker had said that this would go through a House vote. It wouldn't be the call of one person. It didn't.\par \par \uc2 Is that because you don't have the House votes?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Well, if you look at the president here, Speaker Pelosi started the House impeachment inquiry about a month before there was ultimately a vote. So, the speaker can call a vote at any time. That's up to his decision to do so.\par \par \uc2 The impeachment inquiry is an important step to take for legal purposes, to make sure that the House and Congress as an institution, as its apex of constitutional authority to make sure that we can get access to documents and frankly depositions that we know the administration is going to stone wall.\par \par \uc2 But it's important, Shannon, to take a step back, take a look at what House Republicans have uncovered. We've uncovered dozens of LLCs by Biden family members, including over $20 million, some of which came in while \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was vice president. So, this is about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and really answering valid concerns regarding corruption and