2 BREAM: OK, so what are the risks now for the GOP because the Hill says this, quoting a Senate Republican, it's a waste of time, a fool's errand. Even if this impeachment gets to Biden after an inquiry, there's no way the Senate controlled by Democrats would vote to convict, Bob?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Well, Senate Republicans and House Republicans are not getting along, and they don't agree on many things, including impeachment, and that's right. But can Kevin McCarthy even get the votes to pass it through the House? I mean, it remains to be seen, and that's why they didn't have a vote on the impeachment inquiry. Why? They didn't have the votes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Well, there's another fight that everybody's going to get blamed for too. Another Hill headline says this, lawmakers prepare for shutdown blame game. Karl, this never seems to go well for anyone. But it does generally seem that Republicans get blamed for these shutdowns?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER BUSH WHITE HOUSE ADVISER: Well, generally because Republicans are responsible for the shutdown. They seem to eagerly want it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There you go.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: So, yeah, there's a reason why they get blamed. And look, the American people demand that their government try and run itself in an appropriate fashion. And the fact that the biggest financial and business enterprise in the world, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  can out pass a budget in time and then ends up shutting itself down over things that are on the margin. I mean, we're talking about the difference between this and that. And the Republicans are going to shoot themselves in the foot in the run-up to the 2024 election if they continue to think that shutdowns are a great way to put themselves in front of the American people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But Olivia, you report here in D.C. I mean, there is that faction that says you made us promises. This was an exchange for voting you in as speaker, and we stand by real conviction that we need to scale back this funding level. These are things that you told us you would do. And now threats about, you know, the motion to vacate.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, POLITICO CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: Certainly, and, you know, this is a precarious position for the speaker to be in. I do think -- and I've been talking to sources, there's a group on the Hill with the Freedom Caucus and the Main Street, who are more rank and file, lean centrist, who have been talking, and they are getting close to a framework.\par \par \uc2 So there might be a way forward for Speaker McCarthy if they can get the Republicans on board. The question is, what is the top line? And I still don't have that information. There are some conservatives who say if it's near where the debt ceiling limit had been passed, they're going to oppose it, because they've been calling for cuts, and if they don't get those cuts, they're going to look like a hypocrite. So we're in a tough spot, but, you know, at least they're sounding optimistic that something will move forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so we have an escape hearing, which occasionally happens live on TV. That'll go on our end of your blooper reel. That will be very fun. We love it.\par \par \uc2 OK, so the internal fighting for the Republicans, but Democrats also have this internal conversation going on about whether they want somebody else to be running for president. Here is something from David Ignatius in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , and got a lot of attention this week. He says, if he and Harris campaign together in 2024, I think Biden risks undoing his greatest achievement, which was stopping Trump. So he says two liabilities, both age and keeping \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as vice president on the ticket.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN WALLING\uc2 , FORMER 2020 BIDEN CAMPAIGN SURROGATE: Yes, and I mean, the most amazing thing about that David Ignatius column is that 90% of it is in praise of what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has accomplished, mainly because of his experience, his wisdom, his age, his relationships in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 And I think, you know, you see a president that sees himself as one of the few people likely on the stage that can defeat an existential threat to the Republic, which in his mind is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And he did it just three years ago with the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  team and I think he's itching for that fight again.\par \par \uc2 It would have been interesting if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had not run again, whether the President would view a second term as something that he'd be interested in. But certainly, he views himself as the one person that can defeat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  again.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And Ignatius is trying to talk him out of that.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: He is.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Because he thinks that's going to be the backfire on him. OK, so to the issue of Vice President Harris staying on the ticket. Here's what a couple of top Democrats had to say when they were asked point-blank about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER, CNN HOST: Is Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  the best running mate for this president?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D) FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER: He thinks so, and that's what matters. She's the Vice President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That's President Biden's choice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Bob, not a resounding endorsement of the current Vice President?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: No, her poll numbers are not good and the President's poll numbers are not good. And this is a problem. I mean, Democrats, I think, are a little overconfident that they beat Trump four years ago and they're going to beat him again.\par \par \uc2 And listen, I think with Harris, though, we know this parlor game of speculation that maybe she'll be bounced. There's no chance they're going to bounce Harris from the ticket.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, I agree with you. But as you mentioned, tough poll numbers. I want to put this up new from our Fox News polling. These are places where President Biden is losing from key constituencies and President Trump is picking up suburban women, women overall, black voters, voters under age 45, Hispanic voters, I mean, Karl, those are warning signs?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Oh, terrible. And we've had lots of warning signs. Think about it, we have national polls showing that three out of every four Americans think that he is too old to be president already. And he's going to get older. Surprise. He's going to get older between now and the election. And two out of three -- two out of three Americans thinks he is mentally not up for the job. The idea that he won by 43,000 votes in three states with over 100 and -- significantly 160 million people voting and that he's in a better shape today than he was three years ago is, they're kidding themselves.\par \par \uc2 This is a disaster that is self-inflicted and two people who had the ability to tell \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  it's time to hang it up his wife and his sister who plays a major role in every one of his campaigns. What are they thinking, particularly after, you know, things like the Hanoi News conference? This is a disaster for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and there's some others, Maureen Dowd is among those who think that he's being overmanaged when it comes to things like the Hanoi press conference or others. She says this, "his staff reinforces the impression of a fragile chief executive by over managing him and white-knuckling all his appearance by publicly treating him as though he's not in control of his faculties by cutting him off mid-thought as though he's faltering and needs caretaking, they play into the hands of Trumpsters."\par \par \uc2 Kevin, what if he is just unleashed?\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Listen, I've always described the idea, let Biden be Biden, right? You know, he has the skill set necessary over 50 years in public life to deal with the press, to engage with the press. So I'm of the mindset, let it let him out, you know, let the bear loose. What we talked about during the \uc2 Obama\uc2  years. Because listen, I think he's up for the fight. You know, he's -- he just came back on a whirlwind tour of Asia, right?\par \par \uc2 We talked about that Hanoi press conference, our own Peter Doocy said, you know, he pulled an all-nighter, right, in advance of that press conference. And again, as he always says, and I'm going to get, you know, probably made fun of him for saying this again, but, you know, compare me to the Almighty, not the alternative. Or vice versa, compare me to the alternative, not the Almighty.\par \par \uc2 And again, these polls --\par \par \uc2 ROVE: They don't need to run the Almighty, they just need to run somebody who can string together two sentences and demonstrate competence.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: But these polls a year out to Karl's point, you know, don't make a lot of difference. And you compare them to the same conversations we were having about \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who was down with all those different groups of people in 2012. You know, he can win this and bring it back together.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yeah, look, \uc2 Obama\uc2  was down, but not as much as this guy, and nobody. Three out of four Americans did not say he's too old for the job, and two out of every three Americans did not say he's not mentally up for it. This is -- we are in a fundamentally different place with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and it is going to get worse for the Democrats, not better.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Olivia, how much of a headache is this for the White House? All of these polling numbers? And I know that they'll say he doesn't -- you know, that's not what he's dialed into. But now he also has to deal with the indictment of his son. It was a tough week?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Yeah, and I mean, like the polling about his mental soundness, where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's mental soundness is going up, steadily over the months, and his is going down, that's a tough look. David Ignatius' column was a gut punch, and now he's dealing with his son. And Maureen Dowd saying he needs to start answering for some of these questions, because you can't keep running from them.\par \par \uc2 But he's in a reelection, things are going to get intense, and he's going to have to figure out how to maneuver sort of these views that are increasing about him, and trying to disprove that he is up for the job.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, we've got more poll numbers we will dig into, panel, when we come back. We're going to leave it there for right now, don't go far.\par \par \uc2 All right, presidential hopeful \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  says he plans to fire 75% of all federal employees if he wins. Just one of his controversial positions we will ask him about when he joins us live, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Republican Presidential Candidate \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  is set to take the stage at the second GOP presidential primary debate in just 10 days. Brand new Fox News polls show him holding steady at 11% still in third place, but now just two points behind \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who's dropped three points since the first debate.\par \par \uc2 Ramaswamy made headlines this week by vowing to fire 75% of federal government workers by the end of his first four-year term if elected. He's also promised to eliminate several federal agencies, including the Department of Education and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Republican Presidential Candidate \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  is set to take the stage at the second GOP presidential primary debate in just 10 days. Brand new Fox News polls show him holding steady at 11 percent still in third place, but now just two points behind \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who's dropped three points since the first debate.\par \par \uc2 Ramaswamy made headlines this week by vowing to fire 75 percent of federal government workers by the end of his first four-year term if elected. He's also promised to eliminate several federal agencies, including the Department of Education and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss all of that and more is \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . Welcome back to Fox News Sunday.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Good morning, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's start here. President Trump has widened his commanding lead. I mean, that's 60 percent now. Many analysts say everybody else is just sort of playing for second place. You've said you would not accept the vice-presidential spot, so what's the point of your campaign now?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: So, look, the core focus for me, Shannon, is to take the America First agenda even further than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did, but to reunite this country in the process. And I can't do that from a number two position or a cabinet role. I can do that as the next president of the United States, especially reaching young people in this country, who are lacking in national pride.\par \par \uc2 And Shannon, I think the way that we're going to be able to revive this country is by answering even what it means to be an American today. We're in the middle of a national identity crisis. I have a vision for reviving the 1776 ideals that set this nation into motion. I think we can still do it. We're not working with a lot of time.\par \par \uc2 If my two young sons graduate from high school, if we're 10, 20 years into this and we haven't fixed it, I quite frankly don't think we'll have the same country left. But I think this is our window to get it right. That's what gives me my urgency. And I expect we will be successful. We came from 0.0 percent to where we are now. I think we're on track to win this election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So your profile is growing, but as it does, our polling also shows your unfavorables are up. This is the latest polling up 12 points since we pulled this in August. One recent opinion piece puts it this way. "Of all the descriptors attached to the \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , the 38-year-old political tyro enjoying a bizarre surge in the Republican primary race for second place, the most common one seems to be 'annoying." Why do you think as more people have gotten to know you your unfavorables are up too?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Well, look, we have been taking intense criticism, Shannon, over the last several weeks since I performed well on that second debate. And this is part of the process, so I invite the open debate.\par \par \uc2 The reality is many people are annoyed by my rise and believe that a 38- year-old is too young to be U.S. president. The fact of the matter is \uc2 Thomas Jefferson\uc2  was 33 years old when he wrote the U.S. Declaration of Independence. He also invented the swivel chair while he was at it, by the way.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think we need to revive that spirit. And I believe, Shannon, it will take someone whose best days ahead are still yet ahead in life to see a country whose best days are still yet ahead of itself. And I do believe that the United States of America can see our best days still yet ahead. But we can't just be running from something.\par \par \uc2 I don't criticize actually the radical Biden agenda that much because I think it's the wrong place to focus. Of course, there's a lot to criticize. But we have to offer a vision of our own, not just what are we running from? What are we running to, revive meritocracy, the pursuit of excellence, economic growth, free speech, open debate. These are basic values that most Americans still agree with, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 And that's why I'm confident that we have a chance to deliver a 1980-style, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 -style, moral mandate. That is how we'll unite this country. And I'm in this race because I think I am the single best positioned candidate to do it. And even though we've taken criticisms from basically nearly every other candidate that's been threatened by my rise, I'm not running against any of them. I am running for this country. That's the mission we're guided by.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to do a lightning round so we can get to some topics with you.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to talk about your decision or announcement that you would fire 75 percent of the federal workforce didn't go over well here in Washington as you might imagine, but maybe that's your whole point. You and I are both lawyers. You talked through the statutory and regulatory framework you believe would allow you to do this.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I'm having a hard time finding any scholars in that field who agree with you, but plenty would disagree, including Peter M. Shane, scholar in residence at \uc2 New York University\uc2  and a specialist in separation of powers law. He says, this campaign white paper you have on it is fantastical. Peter L. Strauss, professor emeritus of law at Columbia, said, it took bits of statutory law out of context while totally ignoring the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 So you got to know it's going to be tied up in legal framework and fights if you did get there. But what about the practical implications of this? I mean, people who can't get student loans processed or a background check for a gun? I mean, there's a reality to getting rid of a million federal workers.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Yes, so a couple of things, Shannon, on the legality of it, there are professors from Yale Law School to Columbia Law School that have carefully advised on this. And with due respect, I am probably the only presidential candidate whose books have been quoted by federal appellate court judges in their opinions in recent years. It turns out that presidents have been duped by that standard advisor class who said you can't fire these employees due to civil service protections.\par \par \uc2 The fact of the matter is those civil service protections only apply to individual employee firings, not mass layoffs. So I do think it takes a president with the first personal understanding of the law and the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, what about the practical implications?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: And the good news, Shannon, is the current Supreme Court. Yes, the Supreme Court agrees with me six to three on that, I believe. Practically speaking, this is a deeply practical policy. Take the \uc2 FBI\uc2  shutdown that I've proposed. That one turned the most heads. 35,000 employees at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , but I've been clear that we will keep 15,000 of the agents on the front lines. We will just move them to the \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  or to the \uc2 Drug Enforcement Agency\uc2  or to the \uc2 Financial Crimes Enforcement Network\uc2  at \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 , where they will have greater specialization and be even more effective at enforcing the law today than they already are.\par \par \uc2 So this is deeply practical, both for effectiveness as well as for reduction of cost and competitiveness. Department of Education, same thing. That 80 billion dollars put it in the hands of families. This will stimulate our economy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, let's talk about H-1B visas. On the issue there, you've said you would gut that program, which allows us to hire foreign workers for specialized tech and other jobs here in the U.S. Politico says this, that's the very system that you used in the past to hire high school foreign workers for the pharma company that built much of your wealth. You've called it a form of indentured servitude that can only accrue to the benefit of the company. So why did the company you start use that 29 times in the last five years?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Shannon, I believe the energy system in this country and energy regulation needs drastic reform too. But I still use water and electricity and turn on the lights. So the fact of the matter is I have an understanding of the regulatory apparatus because I have dealt with it as a CEO and an entrepreneur who's built multiple companies. What I think is we need to replace the H-1B lottery system.\par \par \uc2 That's what people need to appreciate. It's a lottery. Why on earth would you use a lottery when you could just use meritocratic admission instead, restore merit. There's also lobbying based provisions where companies who sponsor somebody, that H-1B immigrant cannot work for a different company unless they actually have a whole bureaucratic process to go through.\par \par \uc2 We have to gut that system, restore meritocratic immigration, which is skills, not just tech skills, but all kinds of skills to match the needs we have in this country, but also civic commitments to this country. Take the citizenship test on the back end. I say move it to the front end even to get a visa.\par \par \uc2 So yes, Shannon, I come from a place of understanding. And I've played within the rules that have been given to us by the government, but part of my job as U.S. president is to reform those rules to help all Americans. And I won't apologize for restoring merit.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: And debureaucratizing that legal immigration process.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I do truly want to try to do some lightning round on foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Because it's where you seem to be having to make the most clarifications. Let's talk about Taiwan. \uc2 Business Insider\uc2  has this headline. GOP presidential hopeful says he would let China invade Taiwan so --\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Wrong.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- long as the U.S. has got enough semiconductors. You've talked about us being independent on that front and at some point, that would dial back our commitment to defend Taiwan. Is that the message you want to send to China?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I want to be crystal clear, Shannon, because we've got to be -- everyone Americans should know this. Both political parties in the U.S. position right now is a one China policy towards China of strategic ambiguity.\par \par \uc2 I am the only candidate in either party who has been clear. We will defend Taiwan at least until we get semiconductor independence, at which point we resume the current posture of strategic ambiguity towards that island. That is honest. And here's how I'm going to lead our foreign policy, clear red lines.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. But -- so once you have that independence -- so once you have that independence, though, that we do on semiconductors, are you then signaling to China that we would not defend Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: No, I'm not. I am signaling that we go back to our current posture, the status quo accepted by both parties of strategic ambiguity. That's where we are now. I say resume that. But in the meantime, we need to upgrade our commitments.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: To say we will defend Taiwan. I've been crystal clear I am the only candidate in either party who has said it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. A couple of clarifications on Israel.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You have said that we would defend them. But Vice President - former Vice President Pence, who was with us a couple of weeks ago here, said that he was really worried that you wouldn't use military - are you - military defenses to defend Israel against Iran. He called it deeply troubling. He says, you've got to send the message to the Mullahs there that we will do what it takes to defend Israel.\par \par \uc2 Now, is your issue no boots on the ground or are you also saying no military equipment, drones, air strikes, those kinds of things?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: No, Shannon, you can look at my written statements on this. I've been crystal clear. And I'm disappointed in the other candidates for mischaracterizing it. I said we will defend Israel militarily in the case of conflict with Iran, but we would not want to put boots on the ground.\par \par \uc2 And any other candidate who disagrees with that position, I challenge them to be clear about how many American men and women, our sons and daughters, would they send to die in another war of the Middle East. I have heard crickets in response to that question.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: But the reality is, we will defend militarily. That's different than boots on the ground. And even Israel, the staunchest Israel advocates in Israel, do not have expectations of American men and women putting boots on the ground to invade another country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And I know you've said on the issue of aid, we would only withdraw U.S. aid from Israel once they tell us they no longer need it.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I can't imagine any country telling us they don't need U.S. aid, but - but I understand that's your line.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Actually, Shannon, that is my line. But the funny fact, the one leader who has ever said it was Bibi, the leader of Israel, in the mid- 1990s when he told us that they didn't need civilian aid anymore, which is why our relationship with Israel is so strong. We can be candid with each other.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Bibi is candid. So am I. That's why we'll have a great relationship.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, one last point, you talked about, and I'm quoting you, a broader comprehensive vision for disengagement from the Middle East. Do you worry about a vacuum that would create, the message that it sends there to very back actors in the neighborhood where Israel lives, who talk about, you know, wiping that country off the map. What is this idea of disengaging -- or the U.S. disengaging from the Middle East?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Well, this is my whole foreign policy vision is the number one threat we face is communist China. So, realistically speaking, and I'm a realist when it comes to foreign policy, we have to direct our attention there, not to the USSR, which doesn't exist anymore, and I think compared to the Middle East, communist China is a top priority for the U.S. to defend again. That's why our alliance with Israel is particularly important because we have to have a stable partner that ensures stability in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: That's why I want to lead Israel into Abraham Accords 2.0. And so I am a realist when it comes to foreign policy, assert American interests, but keep your eye on the real threat that is communist China today. And I think we should reckon with that and admit it or else we're going to make serious mistakes over the next four years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, we're only scratching the surface. So, please come back. Vivek, great to have you.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A reminder, by the way, Fox Business will be at the Reagan Presidential Library later this month, the second Republican presidential primary debate. Watch it all on Fox and Fox News Business on September 27th.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, special council Jack Smith is asking a judge to impose a gag order that would restrict what President Trump can say in some respects about the federal case against him in public, pointing to his heated statement on social media and the campaign trail. Our panel debates that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You know, a lot of times they say, well, they don't like Trump but they love his policy. No, I think they like me too.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL) AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The time for excuses is over. I don't see what we need a mulligan after 2024. We need to get the job done. I can tell you this, I will get the job done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former President Trump and Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  talking about polls and what's at stake in the 2024 election, both at events here in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with our panel.\par \par \uc2 All right, I want to start with some more polling because we asked people, does the word "corrupt" describe President Biden or Trump. The country seems pretty evenly divided on the issue of Biden. But Trump actually, he has a 14 point plus in favor of sounding corrupt. Bob, how does that play for him on the campaign trail? Is it already baked in for a lot of folks?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: I think it's totally baked in. And, you know, two out of three people want another choice. I think there will be a choice. That doesn't mean that choice is going to win. But a lot of people that I talk to are just begging. They - they - they don't want a repeat of this but both Biden and Trump have solidified their position in the party. And that's why they're going to, I think, both be the nominees.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, yes, we do hear the polls of people who say they don't want this and then overwhelmingly these are the two people leading their fields.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So we also asked the question of who has the mental soundness to serve. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Carlos referenced this. Only 36 percent of people think that he's got what it takes, 61 percent say no. And, Olivia, you mentioned this too, when you look at the trend, President Trump has been trending upward. People feeling he's more mentally sound to serve.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Yes. And so, I mean, that's sort of an interesting contrast when you look at the two presidents. And the corruption numbers, they're worse for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The mental soundness numbers, they're better for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And this is a debate we're going to be seeing. Some of the - just breaking down the Republican numbers for Trump's legal woes is interesting too because they seem divided. Some think it's going to help. Some think it will hurt. And some think it won't make a difference at all.\par \par \uc2 One of my colleagues wrote a story recently, and he said that I think about 38 percent of the Trump supporters say that they won't change their minds. So that's pretty strongly baked in supporters no matter what happens with these legal cases.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and with these legal cases, by the way, there has been a request filed by the special council with the judge who's overseeing one of the federal cases here asking for a bit of a gag order about what President Trump can say. And, you know, this is what he talks about on the campaign trail all the time. He knows it's working for him. Fox News reports this, "if approved by Judge \uc2 Tanya Chutkan\uc2 ," who I believe the president's also trying to get removed from the case, "the "well-defined restriction' would prohibit Trump from making statements regarding the identity, testimony, or credibility of perspective witnesses.\par \par \uc2 Karl, his team had said this is election interference. It interferes with his First Amendment rights. But this judge is pretty no nonsense and we'll see what she does with it.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes, absolutely. And, look, this is going to be one of the continuing saga of this campaign as he attempts to run while he is facing four legal actions. And already he has - the camp is saying that he is not saying things or appearing at things because -- giving interviews because they don't want to put him in potential jeopardy of saying something that causes a problem for him in a - in a - in one of these legal cases.\par \par \uc2 I'm with Bob, partly. People don't want this -- Biden versus Trump. If I were a betting man, and I'm not a betting man, I'd have to bet on Biden and Trump being the nominees, but I'd take the field against him. The party that figures out that a new phase ought to be its nominee is the party that is going to have the upper hand in the 2024 election. Right now we're aiming towards a race between two people whom every - who people distrust or dislike, and that's never a good recipe.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Kevin, No Labels is still sniffing around about a third party run, thinking that if these two are gridlock and if polls say that people aren't thrilled about either choice they want to offer another option. I know that's a -- Democrats feel like that's going to work against them if that happens.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Oh, it absolutely will. And you just have to look at the history of third party bids in this country. But, you know, to Bob's point, and it's an interesting one, listen, you know, Democrats are solidly united behind \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . You know, he's at 76 percent, I think, in terms of the primary voting universe according to \uc2 Morning Consult\uc2 . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's numbers are still up as well.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we're looking at these national polls through kind of partisan bifocals here. So, those numbers in terms of why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is down nationwide are because Democrats and independents are a part of those polls, and vice versa with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So, while they've got, you know, serious support from their party base, it is true, to Bob's point, that, you know, we don't want to see a rematch of 2020.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, with that in mind, who thinks we'd possibly get a third party ticket? I mean, No Labels is doing the work in the states to get on the ballots.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: They'll be on the ballot. They've got an excellent plan to do so. But I'm with Kevin, their parties typically don't have any impact in winning election but they have an impact on deciding who loses the election. Think about it, we had a big personality and \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  got 19 percent of the vote.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's a big (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Nothing - nothing in the Electoral College.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Zero Electoral College.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Zero. Right, the last two candidates to get votes in the Electoral College were in 1968, George Wallace, and Strom Thurman, 1948. Both of them explicit racists running for - to oppose desegregation in the South.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: And they were spoilers in that - in both races.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Who thinks it helps the - the Republicans?\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Absolutely. One hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes. Yes, it helps Republicans, yes.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: It's a shadow operation on behalf of Republicans, No Labels.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh. All right, well, they say they're not, but we've talked to them about that and they're always welcome to come back on the show.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a preview of my chat with the man called one of the most encouraging humans on the planet.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A quick note this morning. A brand-new episode of my podcast "Livin' The Bream" drops today. This week I sat down with Pastor \uc2 Max Lucado\uc2 . We talk about his new book "God Never Gives Up on You." Very thankful for that. Plus, you can hear all of today's program on the Fox News Sunday podcast. Download and subscribe at foxnewspodcast.com, or wherever you like to get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a wonderful week. We'll see you next Fox News Sunday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020230917ej9h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Thousands Of Autoworkers Picketing Across Three States; Senior Administration Officials Traveling To Michigan; Economic Impact Of Autoworkers Strike; Adviser Defends Biden Mindset Amid Domestic Issues; Jean-Pierre Answers Hunter Pardon Question; Republican Frontrunners Take Campaign To The Capital; Pence Brands Himself As A True Conservative; Former Afghan Soldier Finally Granted Asylum; Invictus Games Showcases Path To Rehabilitation; Demonstrators Protest Democrats Lawmakers While They Tour Migrant Processing Center In New York City; California State Legislature Passes Bills Focusing On Gay And Transgendered Minors; One Out Of Every Seven Dollars In COVID Relief Money Possibly Stolen According To New Government Report; Recent Poll Numbers Show President Biden's Approval Rating Underwater; Former President Trump Holds Large Lead Over Other Republican Presidential Primary Candidates\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Mark Meredith, Jacqui Heinrich, Rich Edson, Jennifer  Griffin, Benjamin Hall, Alexis McAdams, Bill Melugin, William La Jeunesse,  Lucas Tomlinson \par \uc2 7,984 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: And also, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  , who is very happy about his recent poll numbers.\par \par \uc2 Now, I got to get to Colin Bell, 102-year-old World War II veteran. He rappelled on the side of a building hundreds of feet. By the way, The Royal London Hospital. This was for charity. And he said, I don't frighten easily.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. That's it for us.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: No elevator?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Have a great night, everybody.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Hey, Judge, my wife called.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Hello.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And she said she is pro-shoe channel.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: We'll have a great time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Although, the magic mushrooms. All right. We'll see you. Thanks, Judge.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT", about 13,000 auto workers are off the job. The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  is striking against Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  , and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  simultaneously for the first time, in its 88-year history.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden weighed in today on the side of the Union.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith starts us off tonight with this. Good evening, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike is underway, but it is a targeted strike. Meaning, not every auto worker is off the job.\par \par \uc2 Still, labor leaders warn, unless their demands are met for better pay and better benefits, this strike could grow much larger within a matter of days.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No deal, no wheels! Union is strong!\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): With signs in hand and demand spelled out, tonight, at least 13,000 auto workers in Michigan, Missouri, and Ohio are off the job.\par \par \uc2 MALAK JOMAA, UNITED AUTO WORKERS: I'm making like 16 bucks an hour, and it's beating me up.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): President Biden says he supports the union's efforts.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Auto companies have seen record profits. Those record profits have not been shared fairly, in my view, with those workers.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): But some workers say, the president's support is too little too late.\par \par \uc2 Fox businesses Grady Trimble is in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: President Biden says he's the most pro-union president in history. You just rolled your eyes.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER DEVERES, UNITED AUTO WORKERS: If he was, where is he now? Why are we out here?\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): This weekend, the White House is sending two senior officials to Michigan to assist with negotiations. The trip coming one week after the president brushed off the impending strike.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN (text): No, I'm not worried about a strike until it happens.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Ahead of the strike, the union called for at least a 36 percent pay increase, less reliance on temporary workers, a shorter workweek with better retirement benefits, and protections for workers in electric vehicle era.\par \par \uc2 Auto industry leaders say they are offering workers a better deal. G.M. CEO \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA\uc2  , CHIEF EXECUTIVE OFFICER, \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2  : We have a historic offer on the table. The highest in our 115-year history. 20 percent gross wage increases over the life of the contract.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Ford, says it too will pay more, but to a limit.\par \par \uc2 JIM FARLEY\uc2  , CHIEF EXECUTIVE OFFICER, FORD: There is no way we can pay people $300,000 to work four-day workweeks.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: While progressives say they support the strike.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): These CEOs have given themselves a 40 percent raise, while making absolutely record profits.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Former President Trump warns the effort could backfire.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Electric cars are going to be made in China. The auto workers are not going to have any -- I'll tell you what, the auto workers are being sold down the river by their leadership.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Automakers say even just a few hours in, the strike is impacting some production lines, could also force some of these companies, Bret, to shut down other facilities. And the strike already coming at a tough time for unions, overall. Union membership in this country fell to a record low just last year. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They've asked initially for a 46 percent pay increase. You said, you have to be around for the future. If you raised wages that much, what would happen to the company?\par \par \uc2 BARRA: Well, again, it would threaten our ability to keep investing in strong products.\par \par \uc2 We need to get back to work, we need to make sure that we have the investment to make -- to win in E.V.s. And we're poised to do that, which is why every day is important.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The strike is on. Let's bring in \uc2 Forbes Media\uc2  chairman, Steve Forbes. Good evening, Steve. Your thoughts on this and where it's headed?\par \par \uc2 STEVE FORBES, CHAIRMAN, \uc2 FORBES MEDIA\uc2  : Well, where it's headed is we have an election year coming up, and the \uc2 UAW\uc2  figures, this is the time to maximize maximum pressure.\par \par \uc2 They've not formally yet endorsed President Biden, but this kind of labor strike, we're going to see more and more of the rest of the country because of the inflation of the last 2-1/2 -- three years, 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 And anytime you get those kinds of price rises, there is pressure for strikes and catch up pay. So, that's what you're going to see here.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know --\par \par \uc2 FORBES\uc2  : But also, the president came weighed in today, precisely, because he needs their endorsement, and the union figure they're going to get a much better settlement with him coming off the fence like he did today.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it's interesting. Steve, both the president and the former president weighed in kind of on the side of the union.\par \par \uc2 You know, when it comes to Ford, they are saying this transition to green energy and electric vehicles is costing them. You're \uc2 Forbes\uc2  had this piece that Ford earnings call reflected strong results, but the electric vehicle losses $4.5 billion. And so that puts the squeeze on.\par \par \uc2 FORBES\uc2  : Yes, it does. And also, if you get any kind of agreement like what the union is asking for, you're going to see in the next few years more and more production going to a non-union state. So, called Right-to-Work states in the South Tennessee and elsewhere. And so, it's going to be self- destructive.\par \par \uc2 E.V.s don't make money. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  's figured out how to do it, but the rest of the industry is not. And also, the union is well aware that E.V.s mean less jobs for traditional auto workers.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it. Steve, thank you.\par \par \uc2 FORBES\uc2  : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tech stocks had a big drop on Wall Street. They led the way. Dow losing 289. The S&P 500 fell 55. The NASDAQ was off 218. For the week, the Dow was up a 10th of a percentage point. The S&P 500 was down 16 hundredths. The NASDAQ was off four tenths.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike is just the latest event in what has been an incredibly bad week for President Biden. Analyst on the left and the right agree. It started with a bizarre performance at a news conference in Vietnam, then, the House Speaker Kevin McCarthy announced an impeachment inquiry. A Biden favored columnist essentially said the president should step aside, leading to more editorials calling him too old and his vice president unprepared.\par \par \uc2 And yesterday, a special counsel indicted the president's son on three felony gun charges.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the latest Fox poll shows the president has a 60 percent unfavorable rating. Only 40 percent of those surveyed rate him favorably.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris, very similar numbers. 60 percent unfavorable, 38 percent favorable.\par \par \uc2 38 believe President Biden did something illegal concerning his son's business dealings. That's up from March and December. 25 percent say he did something unethical. So, there you see about 63 percent total for those two. 34 percent believe he did nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich recaps a very newsworthy week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Should Hunter get a pardon, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The president hasn't answered a single question since returning to the U.S. as bad news piled up.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday, congressional Republicans opened an impeachment inquiry as new Fox polls show a steady increase in voters belief Biden is corrupt.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday Bidenomics takes blow with inflation rising for a second straight month. Now, 53 percent believing Biden's efforts to get inflation under control are hurting rather than helping -- jumping from 40 percent in April.\par \par \uc2 Thursday, the first son is indicted on felony gun charges. His attorney echoing complaints from Republicans in a letter to Capitol Hill "We actually agree that the department has not handle the case involving the president's son in an impartial manner."\par \par \uc2 ABBE LOWELL, ATTORNEY FOR HUNTER BIDEN: The U.S. Attorney's Office has known about this for years, no charges. When they found out about it and made a deal, it was for a diverted agreement.\par \par \uc2 What changed? Not the facts, not the law, but all the politics that have now come into play.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): And Friday, the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  go on strike, days after Biden said this.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don't think it's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): As Democratic leaders like New York City Mayor Eric Adams are lumping blame on the White House for the migrant crisis spiraling out of control today.\par \par \uc2 Biden's National Security Adviser says none of it is getting to Biden's head.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: I had the opportunity to participate in the president's daily briefing today, and he was dialed in on the key issues that we're confronting --\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Still, most answers about family issues are being deflected from the White House podium, except on the question of a pardon for Hunter.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So, I've answered this question before. It was asked of me not too long ago, a couple of weeks ago, and I was very clear, and I said no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): We will see the president once more today when he leaves for Wilmington this hour. But it's anyone's guess if he will talk to reporters who will be on the South Lawn waiting for him. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich, live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The latest Fox News polls reinforced an uncomfortable truth for all the Republican presidential candidates, except former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . They are playing for second place. The frontrunner and his closest rival are speaking tonight at the same event.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent Rich Edson has our democracy 2024 reports tonight. Good evening, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  are going to be on this stage this evening, just not at the same time. They will join several other Republicans who are pitching faith voters here at the \uc2 Family Research Council\uc2  summit in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  did so. Just a couple of hours ago on this stage, he did mention that auto workers strike. He said the federal E.V. push set the stage for it.\par \par \uc2 After his remarks, we asked Pence how he convinces evangelical voters to move on from Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He promised the government as a conservative in 2016. But what I want voters to know is he makes no such promise today. What I'm offering Republican conservative voters today is precisely what we ran on, how we governed, and frankly was defined my career throughout my adult life.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Pence urged Republicans to back a 15-week abortion ban. Trump has declined to support a nationwide ban. The former has a commanding lead in nationwide polls.\par \par \uc2 One that's only expanded since the GOP debate Trump skipped, and four indictments. Trump's opponents maintained, its early with four months until the Iowa caucuses.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: A lot of voters haven't made a firm decision. They may be leaning one way or another. So, I think this is very fluid. And I think someone's got to go out there and grab it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We have enough time to get it done. We're in the third quarter of the game, we're heading into the fourth quarter in about 30 or 45 more days.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  is also scheduled to speak here this evening. One of the candidates who's gotten some post-debate traction in the polls.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We are bringing young people along in droves. That's how we win this election in a landslide.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): Most of these speakers are scheduled to continue pitching evangelical voters this weekend. We'll be in Des Moines tomorrow at a Faith and Freedom Coalition event there. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich Edson, thank you, Rich.\par \par \uc2 Please join \uc2 Stuart Varney\uc2  , Dana Perino, and Univision's Ilia Calderon as they moderate the second Republican presidential debate on Fox Business, September 27th, from the Ronald Reagan Presidential Foundation and Institute in Simi Valley, California.\par \par \uc2 Special Counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  is proposing that the judge in the case against former President Trump in Washington imposed an order that he not make public statements that could, "present a serious and substantial danger of material prejudicing this case.\par \par \uc2 That from the special counsel about the former president. The filing says such statements would include those regarding the identity, credibility, or testimony of prospective witnesses, and disparaging comments about anyone involved in the case. Judge \uc2 Tanya Chutkan\uc2  has not signed such an order.\par \par \uc2 There is now a dramatic inclusion to a story we've been telling you for some time about an Afghan national who trained with U.S. special forces, but had all kinds of trouble getting into this country. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's such a good news for me. Of course, I can't believe. I can't believe that, and I'm so happy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Words cannot describe it. All you think is oh my god is this real?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: The U.S. intelligence officials say China appears to have suspended its surveillance balloon program. This follows that dramatic incident earlier this year in which the Chinese aircraft went over the U.S., eventually being shot down over the Atlantic Ocean.\par \par \uc2 Intelligence analysts believe Chinese leaders did not intend for the balloon to make that journey and reprimanded operators over the incident.\par \par \uc2 France's president says his country's ambassador in Niger is being held hostage at the French Embassy there. \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  says the ambassador and his diplomatic staff are being detained by the military junta that has seized power in that West African nation.\par \par \uc2 Tonight we continue our reporting on the story of an Afghan national trained as an elite special forces commando by the U.S. military, Abdul Wasi Safi was detained at the U.S. Mexico border almost a year ago. Remember that story? Now, a happy ending. Here is Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): It's a story Fox News first reported last December just weeks after a U.S. trained Afghan Special Forces soldier Abdul Wasi Safi made a harrowing journey through nine countries and three continents and crossed the border illegally into the U.S. attempting to flee the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and find his former U.S. military sponsors.\par \par \uc2 Now, less than a year later, on the anniversary of 9/11, Abdul Wasi Safi received news that he had been granted asylum.\par \par \uc2 ABDUL WASI SAFI, FORMER AFGHAN SPECIAL FORCES COMMANDO: I was shocked. I can't believe that because she showed me the paper, I told her like, really, it's mine? Like, really, my asylum is granted?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Wasi was detained by U.S. Border Police and handed over to Texas authorities, who placed him in a detention facility for five months. Despite the fact that he served for five years side by side with the United States and Afghanistan. His brother Sami was overwhelmed with emotion.\par \par \uc2 SAMI-ULLAH SAFI, FORMER U.S. MILITARY TRANSLATOR: I couldn't sleep. I cried so much when I heard that he was granted asylum. It's like, oh, my God, is this really happening?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Wasi can now apply for his old job.\par \par \uc2 A. SAFI: So, it's my big wish and my dream to join with U.S. military. I help this country because this country helped my country for 20 years. I came here, I saved my life. Now, I feel happy because I'm saved, my life is safe. My future is safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): Of the 73,000 Afghans who were brought to the U.S. by the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  after the withdrawal two years ago, about 34,000 applied for asylum. Most of them are still waiting, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jen, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Wounded American servicemen and women competing in the Invictus Games in Germany. Tonight, correspondent Benjamin Hall who was seriously injured while reporting in Ukraine presented medals to some of the athletes earlier today. He joins us now. Benji, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HALL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, great to see you too, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is a big deal for these athletes, right? And I'm sure a big deal for you.\par \par \uc2 HALL: Yes, very much so. You know, I think that when you go through, have some of the injuries that we've had, the thing you have to do is look for something to go to, a goal. For these athletes, it was getting here, it was competing at this level, this Olympic style level. And it was showing the world that no matter how they were injured, they can go and do great things.\par \par \uc2 Now, for me, when I was injured, I had a goal too and that goal was to get back to work and it was to get back out into the field and keep working. And this trip is the first time I've done that.\par \par \uc2 So, for these competitors, I know it was a huge moment. For me, this has been a huge moment. And I think it's one thing that should bring inspiration to everyone.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, when you look at these athletes, and they get over this hurdle, you personally know what that means. So, that connection as you're putting the medal around their neck is probably deeper than the average person putting the medal around somebody's neck.\par \par \uc2 HALL: It really is. And I spent the last week speaking to many of these people. And in fact, two of the American team were at the same place that I was recovering. We were both at the Center for the Intrepid in Texas.\par \par \uc2 And so, we have gone through the recovery steps together, side by side. And I think that you can look into the eyes of someone who has gone through something similar and you have an understanding and when you're surrounded by hundreds of them as we have been at the Invictus. There is a real sense of understanding which I think is really important.\par \par \uc2 So yes, we understand each other but the most important thing is that we want many -- we want other people to recognize this as well. We want other people to see that no matter what they're going through. If they're having a difficult time, they can pick themselves up and keep going.\par \par \uc2 So, Invictus is about winning medals. Yes. And the U.S. did very well. They won at least 120, maybe some more today, but it's about showing the world, it's about reminding people that no matter what you go through, you can go out and achieve great things.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Benjamin Hall at the Invictus Games in Germany. Benjamin, thank you. It's great to see you in the field and we'll see you down the road.\par \par \uc2 HALL: Thanks, Bret. Good to see you too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will have an exclusive interview with Saudi Arabia's Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  on Wednesday. It will be the Crown Prince's first interview with the major U.S. television network since 2019. SPECIAL REPORT will be live from Saudi Arabia Tuesday and Wednesday of next week. Tuesday's live show will feature interviews with numerous Saudi officials giving a behind the scenes look at the Kingdom.\par \par \uc2 A new melee at a democratic news conference over the migrant crisis in New York. We will bring you there.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Libyan authorities limit access to an inundated city to allow searchers to dig through mud and hollowed out buildings for 10,000 people missing and feared dead there. The flooding in Libya that followed Sunday's deluge in Derna is already blamed for more than 11,000 deaths.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's military says its forces have recaptured a village in the country's east after intense battles with Russian troops. The liberation of Andriivka would represent another game for the Ukrainians in the campaign to oust Moscow's troops from territory they had captured.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Australia. One of the big stories down under tonight, an Australian university promises to return several stolen artifacts to Italy if it can be verified they belong to that country.\par \par \uc2 Among the items a rare ancient jar the school purchased legally in 1984. A unit of art experts is assessing three items to determine original ownership.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Residents of New England are bracing for the first significant impacts from Hurricane Lee tonight, the storm is carrying a range of dangerous weather including strong winds, powerful surf to last through at least Saturday we're told the center of the storm is expected to stay east of the U.S. move into the Canadian Maritimes.\par \par \uc2 However, conditions in New England are expected to deteriorate this evening and be at their worst Saturday before rapidly winding down late Saturday and early Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 We have some extraordinary images to show you tonight from New York. This morning, members of the state's congressional delegation spoke about the migrant crisis there when protesters made their voices heard, really heard. Correspondent Alexis McAdams was there for all of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Close the border! Close the border!\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Tonight, chaos in front of the Roosevelt Hotel in New York City as protesters greeted Democratic lawmakers with blowhorns and backlash over the growing migrant crisis.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What message are you sending to the world? Break the law, come, we give you everything free. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , do you think that's going to stop?\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: More than a dozen members the \uc2 Congressional Hispanic Caucus\uc2  , including \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  , took a tour of the large migrant processing center. But their remarks mostly drowned out by the angry crowd.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADRIANO ESPAILLAT\uc2  , (D-NY): As you can see, this is a contentious issue. But we are all Americans and we all believe in the American dream.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: At least, 1,000 migrants have showed up in the city each and every month. But it's not just a New York problem.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Alexis, illegal crossings are skyrocketing here at the southern border. Border Patrol simply cannot keep up with the numbers of people coming across the border. Mass releases are underway, and some migrants are heading to sanctuary cities.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's no problem if I go to Chicago?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You can do whatever you want. You're free.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: This was a scene in San Diego yesterday where several hundred migrants were mass released by Border Patrol to a city sidewalk. Meanwhile, Arizona's Tucson sector has seen nonstop legal crossings this week, averaging around 2,000 per day, with FOX cameras seeing large numbers of single adult men from Africa crossing as migrants continue crossing illegally from all over the world.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And residents back here in New York City demanding answers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You don't know who's coming to these places. Molesters, murderers, you just let them in, OK. Meanwhile, an American sitting on the street doesn't have a dollar for a piece of bread? Whose fault is this?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (on camera): And Bret, breaking tonight just moments ago we're finding out that after weeks of ongoing conversations, the Biden administration and local officials here in New York have reached a deal to now house thousands of migrants out in an airfield in Brooklyn, New York. But a group that we just heard from, a bipartisan group telling us moments that they plan to file an injunction to stop that. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's quite something to watch. Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Critics are speaking out against a number of bills California lawmakers have passed focusing on gay and transgendered minors. They await the signature of Democratic Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  . National correspondent William La Jeunesse tells us what those measures would do.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: With a supermajority in Sacramento, Democrats wrapped up this year's session with a flurry of bills supporting gay and transgender rights for minors.\par \par \uc2 LORI WILSON, (D) CALIFORNIA STATE ASSEMBLY: Our bill specifically is regarding custody disputes during a divorce proceeding when two parents are in disagreement.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: One contentious bill requires judges to consider if a parent supports their child's gender identity or expression.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That means judges will have no ability to use their discretion.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Opponents say the bill means parents of faith and others could lose custody or visitation rights.\par \par \uc2 MARK TRAMMELL, CENTER FOR AMERICAN LIBERTY: What the legislature is trying to do is just trying to say, look, a child is not safe at home where they are not being affirmed by the gender identity du jour.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: The bill's sponsor insists California is not trying to separate children from parents.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No one is out to get your kids. No one is out to force your kids to be anything that they shouldn't be.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: While fewer than two percent of minors identify as transgender, it's a potent political issue.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT WIENER, (D) CALIFORNIA STATE SENATE: I'm proud of California for standing strong to support LGBTQ people.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Gay and transgender bills passed by lawmakers include one that mandates teachers take a class in support of LGBTQ students. Another makes it easier for students to see mental health counselors without parental notification. A third requires foster families to affirm a child's stated gender expression prior to placement. Another requires schools provide segregated, gender-neutral bathrooms. And districts can be fined if they remove LGBTQ books.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): Every state has its own culture wars, but as the saying goes, what happens California often doesn't stay here. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, or "Whatever Happened To?" segment on all those billions of your tax dollars that were supposed to go to COVID relief.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: House Speaker Kevin McCarthy says he has told his Republican colleagues to plan to stay in session until a measure is approved to fund the government. Members of the Freedom Caucus are resisting a continuing resolution.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: The difficulty was on the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nobody complained about the bill itself, but they wouldn't vote for the rule to even let us bring the bill up. So even that slows the process down further. We're trying to work through it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is the government not going to shut down, we can guarantee that?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I think you'd have to ask a few members of my conference. It's not my intention.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaker McCarthy says history has shown no one wins a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 In this evening's "Whatever Happened To?" report, we try to find out where as much as $135 billion of your tax money, your tax dollars, intended as COVID relief really went. Lucas Tomlinson has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: One out of every seven dollars in COVID relief may have been stolen according to new government report. The \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2  says from April 2020 until May of this year, fraudsters made off with up to $135 billion in federal unemployment aid, rapidly doled out during the pandemic. The Republican chairman at the House Oversight Committee wants answers.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM COMER, (R-KY) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: I am outraged. This is something we've been talking about for a long time. If you don't hold people accountable for crimes, they're going to continue to commit those crimes.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The report says the full extent of the fraud will likely never be known with certainty. A former \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  director says a lack of guardrails led to the unprecedented fraud.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, AMERICAN ACTION FORUM: In all of these cases, there was essentially no identity verification. We allowed people who were not previously eligible, self-employed, independent contractors, be eligible for unemployment insurance. And the result was protectable. Lots of people walked in, took checks, and left.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The White House says it will track down those responsible while blaming the previous administration. "When President Biden came into office, he inherited unprecedented fraud from pandemic relief programs. The president made clear the watchdogs are back." But the report finds both the Trump and the Biden administration failed to fix the loopholes which scammers exploit it.\par \par \uc2 LINDA MILLER, AUDIENT GROUP FOUNDER AND CEO: There's never been anything like the fraud we saw during the pandemic. It was really a once-in-a- generation, once-in-a-lifetime fraud event.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Congress approved nearly $5 trillion in COVID relief spending. President Biden promised to go after the COVID scammers.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My message to those cheats out there is this -- you can't hide. We're going to find you. We're going to make you pay back what you stole and hold you accountable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): The report says the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  has only recovered just a small fraction of the cash, just over $1 billion out of up to $135 billion that was stolen. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lucas Tomlinson at the White House briefing room. Lucas, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel President Biden's tough week, Winners and Losers, Candidate Casino, plus "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 2 in Detroit as a jury acquits three men in the last trial connected to a plan to kidnap Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  . There were found not guilty of providing support for a terrorist act and a weapons charge. They were the last of 14 men to face charges in state or federal court. Nine others have been convicted.\par \par \uc2 FOX 2 in San Francisco as city officials outline how they plan to use a $17 million grant from the state to combat retail theft. Meanwhile, the city of Oakland next door says it will not be getting someone funding because city officials missed the grants application deadline.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Dallas from FOX 4, our affiliate there. One of the big stories there tonight, new boots for Big Tex. The new design to outfit the iconic symbol came from a social worker and artist. Jessica Bonilla was the winner out of 300 people who entered the contest. Nice boots.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside of the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm just following my orders here. Staff, has anybody spoken to -- I ain't calling on you.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: President Biden did lie to the American people.\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN GOLDMAN, (D-NY): If \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  , the president's son, committed crimes, he should be held accountable for them.\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL, (D-MI): It is very important that the Biden administration pay attention to the issues that the workers are expressing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID IGNATIUS, "WASHINGTON POST": People feel uneasy about somebody who is 82 serving another four-year term.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Eighty is the new 40. Didn't you hear? Every time he beats the naysayers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it was a tough week. Pundits on both sides of the aisle saying President Biden had a not great week. And our polls reflect his approval, disapproval. It's underwater, that approval number, as of now according to the new FOX News poll. And you have "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  ," another opinion where it says "Democrats are crazy to insist only Biden can beat Trump. At cocktail parties and caucus rooms and on cable TV, one hears over and over that only Biden can defeat Trump, like an elderly Harry Potter who alone can stop Lord Voldemort. This is crazy. Democrats are deluding themselves when they tell themselves they have no alternative to Biden."\par \par \uc2 That follows David Ignatius, it follows other editorials. What about this week? We'll start there with our panel, Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," Bill McGurn, columnist for "The Wall Street Journal," and "THE FIVE" co-host Jessica Tarlov. Jessica, we've talked about this. At the end of this week, it was rough.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, CO-HOST, "THE FIVE": Not fabulous. And the workers' strike is not finished, and that is a big issue for President Biden. You see how many Democrats are going out there to join the picket line with the workers, like the Sherrod Browns of the world, and that will continue. And it's a key promise that he made that he was on the side of the workers in the worker economy here in America.\par \par \uc2 So I'm not denying this was not a banner week for the Biden presidency. I would note, though, that Peter Doocy, our own Peter Doocy, was on air talking about how he was working on this trip into the wee hours of the morning and contradicting this idea that he needed a nap. But any week that your son gets indicted, and you have those kind of poll numbers and seemingly friendly media has turned on you, it's not going to be one for the books. He still doesn't have four indictments and 91 felony counts against him. So that's a bonus.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mollie, listen, the week began with that news conference in Hanoi. And there's not a stage that the president has been on in recent days that he doesn't point one way and walk the other. We've all seen it. Everybody sees it.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": The age is just one of his problems that he is facing right now. And I think what was interesting, not just this follow-up "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2  " piece saying the Democrats should have a more active primary than the one that they're currently having, but that David Ignatius column. I think that's a name that probably doesn't mean that much to people throughout the country. But in Washington D.C., that is a reliable spokesperson for essentially the people who run this town. And when he was saying it's time for Biden to get out, that is something that a lot of people are talking about.\par \par \uc2 And I think a lot of the people who would like Democrats to win are worried not just about his age but about his record and his scandals. These were all things that they were able to keep under wraps the 2020 election, and they are signaling that they don't think they will be able to do it this time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill, to Jessica's point about the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and the strike that's ongoing, "Politico" writes it this way about the Chamber of Commerce, saying "The Cahmber of Commerce's message that strike and any ensuing economic fallout is Biden's fault could presage a rich vein of attack for other business groups and conservatives, especially if the strike stretches into weeks." Here's Biden on the need for a win-win.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Strong unions are critical to a growing economy, and growing it from the middle out and from the bottom up, not the top down. That's especially true as we transition to a clean energy future which we are in the process of doing. I believe that transition should be fair and a win-win -- excuse me -- for autoworkers and auto companies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is a possible this whole thing also backfires on the president?\par \par \uc2 BILL MCGURN, COLUMNIST, "THE WALL STREET JOURNAL": I think so. Look, part of the problem is that a lot of the subsidies he has for electronic vehicles are going to red states because they have lower energy costs, lower labor costs, and a lot of them are right to work. So I think there's a lot of problems.\par \par \uc2 And when we look back, however the strike ends, we're likely to see it as a dying gasp of the old ways, trying to get huge pay increases without any regard for the competition. So I think they're in a tough place. I think if they, quote, win and get the huge increase they want, I just think that means more future investment and more production will be moved to other states. So it's really not a good choice.\par \par \uc2 All right, it's an early trip down the road to Candidate Casino. And this is a place we go every Friday. But it's not going to be a place where we talk about the GOP primary. This question is, $100 bucks, it's President Biden of Vice President Harris as the presidential and vice presidential nominee at the end of this process. Jessica, first to you?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I put $95 on this duo running again, and the other $5 would go on someone else who's not 80 years old. That was my --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Mollie?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: I was right there with Jessica last week when we talked about this. But this week I'm radically different because of that David Ignatius column and otherwise. So I'm putting $50 bucks on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  running together, $25 on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  running but replacing Kamala, which would be tricky, but it looks like some people are starting to panic about that, and $25 on someone else.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Bill, $100 bucks?\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: I'm even more pessimistic than Mollie. I only put 40 bucks on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  . And I put \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  second for $25 -- $35, and \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  after her at $25.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that's interesting. And \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2  said $90 against President Biden being the nominee, that was last week.\par \par \uc2 One of the reasons we don't do the GOP primary right now is because of our new poll. Jessica, the former president has a massive lead right now, the choice for president. He is at 60 percent. He's up 50 percent on his newest arrival. This is when you are up against President Biden and the Republican candidate. And President Biden is being beaten, but the lead for President Trump is significant. He took to Truth Social saying, "Best poles ever, but rarely shown on television, leading by 50, 60 points. Just like they don't like showing our big rally crowds, they don't like showing our great poll numbers. We have rigged elections and rigged television." This television has shown your big election numbers. Jessica, it does seem like it's a breakaway, even up seven points after the latest indications.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's good news because he wasn't a fan of the FOX poll for a very long time. So now we have them on board, which is wonderful to hear. It is amazing to see with each new indictment he just gets stronger for the primary crowd. That isn't necessarily true in the general election. I understand it's within the margin of error right now, but \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  was up about four or five points on Barack \uc2 Obama\uc2  this time in 2011. I'm not that concerned about it.\par \par \uc2 But everyone else in the GOP primary is racing for the number two spot. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  seems to have the biggest increase from this. DeSantis continues to fall. But this is Trumps race. And it will be interesting to see once the trials actually begin how his standing is affected, if at all.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's do Winners and Losers. Mollie, first winner and then loser.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: My winner is coach Deion Sanders of the \uc2 University of Colorado\uc2  , my alma mater. He took our team that was one and 11 last year, and we have these two great games. He's planning on beating CUS, \uc2 Colorado State University\uc2  here tomorrow. So he is my winter.\par \par \uc2 My loser for the week is \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  , who had a lengthy career where he is ending with the bitter and angry and cynical look at his peers and voters, and has nothing really to show for it other than providing the blueprint for Obamacare.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK Bill, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: My winner is David Ignatius for what we discussed before. He said he was the first to say the quiet part out loud. My loser is David Weiss. I think he's the big loser of the Hunter indictment because the defense has a lot of good arguments going into the case. He could've brought this five years ago, and he tried to let him off with no charges. So it's very hard to defend.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Jessica, winner and a loser, wrap it up.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: OK, my winners were aliens in Mexican congress.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: There were two little bodies that came from Peru originally. It was supposed be 1,000 years old. It's obviously being disputed as everything with the alien kingdom, but I was excited to see the little guys.\par \par \uc2 And my losers were Jets nation, if that's what it's called. My husband grew up a Jets fan. It was a very bad night in the household on Monday, and, of course, for Aaron Rodgers, who I wish a speedy recovery to.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was tough to watch. Panel, thanks. Make it a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know that sound. That means it's Friday. It is Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Never forget, never forget, we never forget.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Our nightmare is finally over and the good guys won.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Far better that we're able to release a patrol dog than have to use lethal force.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This nation got a chance to see how government is supposed to work.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: Open a formal impeachment inquiry into President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Don't do it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We shouldn't take delight in other people's misfortunes.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don't want to contend China. We're all better off if China does well.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you and your partners like to mention how much money we make as if it's about thing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It would be great if we could just pick up the phone and call the Mullahs and say, hey, we want our Americans back.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The world's best tennis parent was banned from playing in the U.S. because he was vaccinated. And yet we're going to allow these folks into our nation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I guess people love comeback stories. I love them too.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't hurt for our locker room. I hurt for Aaron.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  , (D-CA): People say to me, why isn't she doing this or that? That's the job description. You don't do that much.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Before it was locked in stone it was worth at least raising.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's times for guys like me to get out of the way.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's some chance above zero that A.I. will kill us all.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don't know about you, but I'm going to go to bed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I hope A.I. doesn't kill us all.\par \par \uc2 Monday, SPECIAL REPORT, President Biden goes face-to-face with world leaders at the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  in New York. I'm heading to Saudi Arabia for another interview with a world leader, the Crown Prince of Saudi Arabia. We'll be live there Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Line up for FOX NEWS SUNDAY with Shannon Bream this weekend, House Republican Conference Chair \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  , California Democratic Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  , and Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and all this week, a busy week in news. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and