little dribble coming off the side of it. I don't know about you, Sandra, but at some point today there's a martini with my name on it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: There might be something like that in my future. I didn't know you were a martini guy. Did I know that? Sounds good, though. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: We'll see you again on Monday. Have a great weekend. THE STORY right now with Gillian Turner in for Martha. Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you guys. Good afternoon, everyone. I'm Gillian Turner in for Martha MacCallum in Washington. Today on THE STORY, Congressman Pat Fallon joins us as well as Brian Kilmeade and \uc2 Curtis Sliwa\uc2  on today's very heated protests around the migrant crisis in New York City.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Close the border!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: But first, President Biden now facing a very delicate moment. The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  for the first time in history are striking against the big three U.S. automakers all at once. Economists forecast the strike, if it continues, could end up costing the national economy $5 billion by month's end. This at a time 70 percent of Americans polled say they feel the economy right now is getting worse. This is a "real-time test" of Biden's economic agenda says the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 His call for higher wages for the middle class, unapologetic pro-union stand, his climate-driven push toward electric vehicles all centered in Michigan, a state he must win in 2024 to remain in the Oval Office. Dagen McDowell is standing by to join us with her reaction, but first we go to Fox Business Correspondent, \uc2 Ed Lawrence\uc2 . I believe he's -- there you are, North Lawn of the White House. Hi, Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Gillian, so far President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is sitting on the sidelines, but today he told the American people that the auto companies need to pony up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Record corporate profits, which they have, should be shared by record contracts for the \uc2 UAW\uc2 . And just as we're building an economy of the future, we need labor agreements for the future. It's my hope that the parties can return to the negotiation table to forge a win-win agreement.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE (voice-over): So, the \uc2 U.S. Chamber of Commerce\uc2  president delivering a harsh statement on this strike saying: "The \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike and indeed the summer of strikes is a natural result of the Biden administration's whole of government approach to promoting unionization at all costs. For the 94 percent of American private sector workers not in a union, the costs are stacking up." So, I asked this about 30 minutes ago.\par \par \uc2 Is the automaker strike and the contract impasse partly a result of the president's forced transition to electric vehicles?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: No. We don't believe that.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, the president tried to convince Americans in a speech yesterday that his policies would be better than that man. He highlighted the time under former President Trump during COVID when the country shut down, ignoring the pre-COVID economy from that president. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Our democracy is at risk because I don't think they do think it matters anymore. Because the country should know the facts. They should know the choice between Bidenomics and MAGAnomics.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So far, not buying what President Biden is selling, the president is losing, or within the margin of error on polls. The latest Fox News poll on the head-to-head matchup with all the leading Republican candidates.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE (on camera): And now, Gillian, we're starting to see overall inflation tick up a little bit as well as gas prices that are on the rise again back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. \uc2 Ed Lawrence\uc2  at the White House for us this hour, thank you. Let's bring in Dagen McDowell now, she's Co-Host of "THE BOTTOM LINE" on Fox Business. Hi, Dagen. Lots to unpack here. Let's start, I think, with the question that Ed asked the Press Secretary about E.V.s. Take a look at this tweet from J.D. Vance out today. He writes, "Random fact: Ford loses about $32,000 on each E.V. they sell. These things, even with the subsidies, are massive cost centers for the American car industry. Think gas-powered cars are expensive? That's in part because you're subsidizing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's donors." Think that's fair, Dagen?\par \par \uc2 DAGEN MCDOWELL, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK HOST: It's more than fair. Actually, Ford, based on my calculations, loses between $60,000 and $72,000 on every electric vehicle that it sells. And Ford, and \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , they subsidize these money-losing electric vehicles through the very profitable gasoline powered pickup trucks and SUVs that they sell. And what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done, and I don't mean to get off track, but the push is Americans will be forced to buy electric cars and trucks, and these automakers will be forced to make them. Period.\par \par \uc2 And electric vehicles are a job killer for the unions. Period. That the electric vehicle, each E.V. can be built with anywhere between 30 percent and 50 percent fewer auto assembly workers. That is a simple fact. It just takes a lot fewer workers to make an electric vehicle. And estimates from the \uc2 UAW\uc2  say that tens of thousands of jobs would be lost. I've seen estimates up north of 100,000 jobs lost. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's \uc2 EPA\uc2  has mandated the tailpipe emissions regulations in less than a decade. Two-thirds of all the vehicles produced by automakers must be electric to meet these new tailpipe emissions standards.\par \par \uc2 That is not a choice. And so, what Ford and et al have done is they are suckling at the teat of the federal government and taking subsidies top to bottom from particularly the Inflation Reduction Act and low-cost, billions of dollars in low-cost loans. There are up to $400 billion in subsidies or tax credits for people to ultimately buy these electric cars. The union, by the way, wanted the tax credits in the Inflation Reduction Act to only go, to be limited to only union-made cars.\par \par \uc2 So, the unions that are real pickle here, they're worried about the job guarantees that say battery factories that are being built across the South because those plants are not unionized yet. So, they're looking to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the White House for protection. But these jobs are in jeopardy. But again, inflation is up, the job market's tight, and they're putting the screws to these Detroit three automakers right now.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Dagen, before I let you go, I want to make sure I ask you about the politics involved in this strike. David Asman on our air earlier today said the fact that the strike went forward despite the president's very heavy- handed involvement indicates he has, indeed, lost the unions for now and forever. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: They're striking because inflation is hurting these workers, number one, and that's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And they're striking because their jobs are in jeopardy because of the electric vehicle mandate coming straight out of this White House. That's part of it. They have not endorsed him for the presidency. I think that that speaks volumes. But I will note that the original offer that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and Sean Fain wanted from the big three would have put the total labor cost at $150 an hour. That's what they were demanding. $150 an hour at a $40.00 an hour demand would put the total labor costs including health benefits at a hundred and fifty bucks.\par \par \uc2 Right now, they make $65.00 an hour including health benefits. At \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , it's $45.00 an hour. At \uc2 Honda\uc2  and \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , it's $55.00 an hour. But watch out with these Detroit three because again, if this whole electric vehicle thing goes south and there's a new regime comes in and changes it all, they're going to come looking for another bailout from the American people. We already shelled out $80 billion for Chrysler and GM just in 2009. We lost $11 billion on \uc2 General Motors\uc2  bailout.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Dagen, we got to leave it there, but thanks for taking time with us on this Friday. We'll look for you a couple hours from now ahead on FBN. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Still ahead, Congressman Pat Fallon joins us, as well as Brian Kilmeade and Joe Concha. This is the White House now weighs in for the first time since Hunter Biden faced his three-count indictment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Second one, would the president pardon or commute his son if he's convicted?\par \par \uc2 PIERRE: So, I've answered this question before. It was asked of me not too long ago, a couple of weeks ago, and I was very clear and I said no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. New reaction from the White House just a moment ago in the briefing room. Officials there spoke to reporters for the first time since the President's son, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , was indicted. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Can you tell us if the President has talked to his son since he started his campaign last year?\par \par \uc2 PIERRE: Yes, there's nothing new here, as I've been asked that question many times, I'm not going to get into private conversations that the president has with his family. Just not going to speak to it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Brian Kilmeade and Joe Concha are standing by to join me with their reaction, coming up next. First, though, we go to White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich at 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue. Hey, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Gillian. You know, we efforted questions to Karine, we were not called on today. It may have a thing or two to do with the rough week the president had. I mean, forget the rocky press conference that kicked off this week on Sunday, feels like ancient history now. This week blew -- or dealt a blow, rather, to the foundation of the president's campaign, Bidenomics, inflation rising for the second straight month.\par \par \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  went on strike, something the president said he did not think was going to happen. The migrant crisis spiraled out of control in New York; you had Democratic leaders there urging the White House to reflect on the federal government's responsibility for all of this. An impeachment inquiry was opened into the president. The president's son was indicted on three felony charges. And then, his son's attorney accused the Department of Justice, Biden's DOJ, of improperly handling this case. However, the national security advisor says none of this is getting to the president. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: You don't have to take it from me. You heard directly from the President that he's focused on delivering for the American people. So, that's what he's focused on. That's where his mindset is. I had the opportunity to participate in the President's daily briefing today, and he was dialed in on the key issues that we're confronting, and will continue to be as we head into the New York week next week.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But nerves are beginning to fray within the Democratic Party. You just had two op-eds in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and also the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  casting doubt on the viability of the Biden-Harris 2024 ticket. You also had top Democrats like \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and Jamie Raskin failing to issue strong defenses of the president and vice president in interviews following. But \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  says that all is good on the home front, listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIERRE: 80 is the new 40, didn't you hear? In 2020, he got the same criticism. In 2022, he got the same criticism. And every time, he beats the naysayers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Since he got back to the U.S., the president has not taken a single question from reporters, and later on today, he will head to Delaware for the weekend, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Jacqui Heinrich at the White House for us. Thank you. Let's go ahead and bring in Brian Kilmeade, "FOX AND FRIENDS" Co-Host, also Host of "ONE NATION" now at 9:00 p.m. Eastern on Saturdays. Make sure you DVR it or tune in live. And Joe Concha, Columnist for The Messenger, also a Fox News Contributor. Gentlemen, great to be with you on this Friday.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Great.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Brian, let's start with where \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  left off in the briefing room, she said that she's not at liberty to comment on personal conversations that the president has. Is that a fair defense in light of the fact that President Biden has essentially made his son a public figure by involving him in official White House events and travel all across the world?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I think he lives in the White House when he's not on a line getting \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  out in Malibu. And I do think it is very fair to bring up Hunter because Hunter is a major player. He's at major every event and it's all about how Hunter's business links with his dad or doesn't, and that's the investigation. So, if you're going to pull back and say he doesn't talk about his family, that doesn't really apply. You know, if you want to bring up his granddaughter, Navy, who he doesn't talk about, that might be, well, OK, let's not talk about that. But Hunter is right in the eye of the storm.\par \par \uc2 And now, he is avoiding press because of Hunter. He's also now avoiding press because of the imminent impeachment and the investigation there. And now, he's got to worry about his own party. And now, he's going to worry about the border. Sooner or later, you need a press secretary that leans forward and says, this is where we stand -- and she doesn't. She plays defense and never really answers a question.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Joe, Abby Lowell, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's attorney, in a statement says that really, you know, the real danger in all of this is not \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , who illegally possessed a firearm for 11 days. The real danger in all of this is David Weiss, the Special Prosecutor. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: That's just amazing. And I think sometimes, we're taking our eye off the ball a little bit in terms of this indictment and it being about the gun charge and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . The bigger story here that House Oversight has been looking into now ever since they took office earlier this year is did the president actually profit from Hunter Biden's overseas business dealings? Was he involved with them? Well, we've heard phone calls for example Where he sat on a phone 20 times on speakerphone not to talk about the weather by the way, and that's, that's been the excuse.\par \par \uc2 He's gone to dinner with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business associates. And again, these are happening in places where U.S. foreign policy is crucial: Ukraine, China, Russia, Kazakhstan. I could go through the list. So, that's the bigger story here. But I would say, Gillian, this has been the worst week this presidency has seen since the U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan two years ago, right? I mean you unpack the last week on top of the Hunter Biden indictment, we have the unprecedented auto strike that could cripple the economy, particularly in places like Michigan and Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is rising again. We got that report this week, yet the administration still continues to twerk over how wonderful Bidenomics is working. On the U.S. southern border, we see Bill Melugin's reporting, an unmitigated humanitarian national security disaster. We're seeing thousands of people in cages in Arizona. And then, these unforced errors, like when the president said he was at ground zero the day after the attack, he wasn't and that's an insulting lie to all those 9-11 families.\par \par \uc2 And again, Jacqui brought it up before, the David Ignatius column in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  which undoubtedly got the White House's attention because Ignatius is an ardent supporter of the president and he says that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  shouldn't run because he's too old and that sent shockwaves to the White House. I could guarantee you that, because they're used to getting hit from the right; from the left, not so much. And those calls are growing now, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Brian, there are these two alternate realities here in Washington right now. The Democrats say these charges against \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  are proof that everybody is equal in the eyes of the law, Republicans say, it's actually the opposite. This is proof now that there are actually two systems of justice.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I'm brought to what James Comer said: this is the least serious out of all of it. Joe had nothing to do with the gun charge. The rest is where the interest is. And now, they're going to look to get James Biden's bank accounts and they'll get Eric Sherwin in. He was the guy who had all the money and handled all the non or payment of taxes however you want to slice it. So, we'll see where this goes, but maybe somebody on the left knows where this is going to go. And that's why it looks like they're looking to push Joe out and take Kamala with them.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Quick last word to you, Joe. Latest A.P. poll reveals only six percent of Americans across the country say that they are very confident in the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Wow. I mean, can you imagine hearing that sort of number even five or ten years ago? And that just shows you how politicized the perception is of the Department of Justice overall. So, all institutions it seems these days, whether we're talking government overall or media, I could go down the list, are taking a huge trust hit at this point. I'll leave it here, Gillian. The president today, on his official public schedule, he received the daily briefing shortly before noon, and then there was nothing else on that schedule except another flight to Delaware. He has spent more than 40 percent of his presidency on vacation, and that's another reason why trust in institutions are so low, because we have a president that simply doesn't seem to be very interested in working very hard.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Gentlemen, got to leave it there. Thanks for taking time with us on a Friday.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Go get him, Gillian. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Happy Friday. Thanks, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, ahead this hour, former New York City mayoral candidate, \uc2 Curtis Sliwa\uc2 , join us on the meltdown happening today over migrants in New York City. Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Protect the city! Protect the country!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK):\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. House Speaker Kevin McCarthy got into a testy exchange with a reporter who accused House Republicans of launching an impeachment inquiry into President Biden without any evidence. Here's how that went down. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Do you believe when they said the president went on conference calls? Do you believe that happened?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That's what the testimony says.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: OK. Do you believe the president went to Cafe Milano and had dinner with the clients of Hunter Biden who believes he got those clients because he was selling the brand?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That's what the testimony says.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: OK. Then I go back. Do you think the president lied that he, when he said --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He said an impeachable is lying an impeachable offense.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, you want to know, I'm not saying impeachment. All I'm saying is I would like to know answer these questions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, let's bring in Republican Congressman Pat Fallon, he serves on the Oversight Committee. Sir, this reporter who was questioning the speaker is not the only person, you know, repeating that line of no evidence. Take a listen to some of your Democratic colleagues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN GOLDMAN (D-NY): There is no evidence at all to support an impeachment inquiry based on what we know. And every single time you hear a Republican say the Biden family, just know that's because it does not include \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . If it did, they would say \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): Republicans are engaging in a kind of an evidence- free, fact-free impeachment inquiry that has nothing to do with high crimes and misdemeanors.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Congressman, what do you say to your colleagues there?\par \par \uc2 REP. PAT FALLON (R-TX): Gillian, did I just see Adam Schiff say that the Republicans are engaged in evidence-free inquiry? Wow. I want to talk about the -- it's just laughable. Listen, Gillian, I'm on the Oversight Committee, and then we're just kind of spearheading this investigation and we have troves of evidence. I know that's the talking point that the left wants to use to convince middle America there's nothing here and this is merely a revenge tour, but it's not the case at all. I mean, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the one that told the American people in 2019 that he had no idea what his son did and he never met with any of his business associates.\par \par \uc2 We know that was an outright lie. So, why did he lie? Why did \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  accept the job with \uc2 Burisma\uc2 , the energy giant in Ukraine, for a million dollars a year and their CEO, \uc2 Mykola Zlochevsky\uc2 , was being investigated for corruption. And then \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , after his son asked him for help, went to Ukraine and withheld a billion-dollars-worth of aid, or at least threatened to, unless Victor Shokin, the prosecutor that was investigating \uc2 Mykola Zlochevsky\uc2 , got fired. And he did get fired, even though Shokin was doing a great job. So, that's one of several instances where there is actually damning evidence.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Your colleague, James Comer, leading the probe has produced a whole lot of evidence that doesn't look great for President Biden. It's also true, sir, that he's got an egg on his face in a couple of these instances getting ahead of the evidence. Right now, we're waiting to see if the White House is going to turn over these, or the archives are going to turn over these unredacted e-mails then Vice President Biden sent under a pseudonym. If those are released and don't produce the evidence that your committee hopes it does, is somebody going to acknowledge that reality?\par \par \uc2 FALLON: Oh, sure -- I mean, Gillian, I just want to get to the truth and to justice. And if the Bidens, if we find evidence that exonerates the Bidens, then we will move on. Selfishly, I'd love to see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  be the Democratic nominee for president and on the ballot in November 2024, but I'm not elected to go to Congress to do what I personally want to do. I'm elected to follow my constitutional oath. And the fact of the matter is, the seven times that we have subpoenaed bank records, we always find more money that was funneled from foreign entities or foreign individuals to the Biden family. I believe the preponderance of the evidence shows that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was acting as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's back man, and we all know what that is; and that they were smart enough not to be that stupid to have the money go directly to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Congressman we got to leave it there, but thank you so much for taking time with us on this Friday. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 FALLON: Thanks, Gillian. God bless. Take care.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Thank you, sir. Well, there's also this chaos erupting in New York City and Manhattan after Democratic lawmakers tore a facility that they say they plan to use to house asylum seekers in the future. We've got former New York City mayoral candidate, \uc2 Curtis Sliwa\uc2 , with us, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKERS: Close the border! Close the border!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's not even about the migrants, it's about the people who've been here, the homeless. The homeless -- the homeless who've been here, people on the street who don't have them. Well, it can't go to nobody, talked to nobody on the streets in New York\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: for legal migration, not illegal. There's a tremendous difference, and \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  you don't get it. You don't get it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Who knows who's coming into these places. Molesters, murderers, his let them in, OK. Meanwhile, an American that's sitting on the street doesn't have a dollar for pizza bread?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, that was Midtown Manhattan earlier today. 