achusetts, former member of Congress from Illinois taking on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Republican primary. They all clamored for this kind of engagement and doesn't get it. Because, you know, a party exists to support the leader of the party, which right now is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He's not just the Commander in Chief. He's also the leader of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 So I think, you know, this is the last kind of throes of a campaign in real distress. Robert Kennedy is at the lowest point in terms of polling that he has been. So this is a last ditch effort I see on his part for relevance in the primary process.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: David, there is also another parallel to be drawn with Republican candidates. Tim Scott is sort of haggling with the \uc2 RNC\uc2  about debate qualifications right now. But he is not, you know, burning the house down in the same way that RFK Jr. seems to be doing.\par \par \uc2 AVELLA: Parties can set their rules on their nomination process. What's interesting about this is you had \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2  who wrote profiles and courage.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AVELLA: Maybe Robert Kennedy could write profiles and cowardice. You have a president where two thirds of Democrats don't want \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to run again, you have Speaker Pelosi who won't formally endorse \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for the vice presidential nomination again, and yet not one credible Democrat will step up and take on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So to that point, Kevin, I want to read you this from a \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  op-ed out yesterday. This is an opinion piece if I didn't say that. "At cocktail parties and caucus rooms and on cable TV, one hears over and over that only Biden can defeat Trump like an elderly Harry Potter who alone can stop Lord Voldemort. This is crazy any moderately popular person could beat Trump in a general election."\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Well, I mean, I love the Harry Potter reference off the bat. But listen, you know, I think one of the fundamental tenants of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's thinking through 2020 was, he alone could be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And I think one of the indications early on with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is successfully really, you know, building the Republican image in his brand. He's far and away the Republican leader.\par \par \uc2 At this point, I think it's a call to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , to take that on. And I think he views himself as one of the few people in the country as the incoming president that can defeat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and all polls. At this point indicate that it would be a close match, but that he would be successful with that and never --\par \par \uc2 TURNER: But can you name a Democrat, that is not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , that could be Trump in general?\par \par \uc2 WALLING: I think the Vice President could beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I think \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  could be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I think Josh Shapiro, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , we all have -- we've got a great bench of Democrats coming down the pike in terms of governors, senators, that could beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and -- or whoever the Republicans put up at that point.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: David, I'm betting, obviously don't want to talk for you, but I am betting you would really like to see a \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  run against former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 AVELLA: Let's just stop and say every Republican right now running for president is competitive with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Look at the surveys. Every one of our potential nominees isn't a head to head or beating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And why is that partly because of this effectuation the Biden have with Bidenomics. And what is so flawed about their thinking is the majority of Independent voters who will decide this election when they hear the words Bidenomics, they think of wasteful spending and inflation.\par \par \uc2 And yet, you have a White House and a team 