ll right. There's a new push now on Capitol Hill to cap credit cards. This is Americans are burning through limits like never before. Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back now. Take a look at this. That's US credit card debt topping a trillion dollars for the first time. Now there's a push on Capitol Hill to cap the interest rates that Americans pay. The big banks though, unsurprisingly, are fighting back. Fox Business correspondent Gerri Willis joins us. She's got a live look at the story from New York. Hey, Gerri.\par \par \uc2 GERRI WILLIS, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Gillian. That's right. With consumer credit card debt as you say at an all time high of $1 trillion and interest rates on that debt approaching 25%. One Republican is talking about capping those rates. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): What my bill would do is cap credit card interest rates, Harris, at 18%. No more than that. No hidden fees, no backdoor fees. More and more people have to put on their credit card their monthly rent payments, they got to put food on the credit card because of inflation. We ought to give working people real relief.\par \par \uc2 WILLIS (voice-over) The Census Bureau study earlier this year found more than 35% of households using credit cards or loans for basic needs. Bankers say government price controls would have unexpected downsides. The \uc2 American Bankers Association\uc2  telling Fox Business that "creating a cap on credit card interest rates has been debated because it would reduce access to credit for those who need it most."\par \par \uc2 In fact, according to the organization's own research, a cap would result in the loss of cards for 65 million subprime borrowers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I like that saying about how credit cards can be like power tools. They're really useful or dangerous depending on how you use them. I think that ends up being really the best advice, is more about a personal habit of, you know, yes paying it off if you can.\par \par \uc2 WILLIS: Credit card rates look like something that two parties can come together on. A similar bill was introduced by Democratic Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and Representative Ocasio-Cortez back in 2019. We reached out to a number of Democratic lawmakers so far the only one to respond. California Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  who said that he supports capping credit card rates fast to look at Hawley's bill before weighing in. Back to you, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Gerri Willis in New York for us, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 That's THE STORY of Friday September 15, 2023. But as always, the story goes on, Martha. We'll be right back here Monday at 3:00. I'm Gillian Turner, "YOUR WORLD" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230916ej9f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Rep. Nancy Mace (R-SC); Hurricane Lee Targets New England; Hunter Biden Under Fire; Interview With Rep. Jason Smith (R-MO); President Biden Voices Support For UAW Workers; Autoworkers Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Alexis McAdams, Nicole Valdes, Jacqui Heinrich, Grady Trimble,  David Spunt, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,959 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 STRIKERS: We are the union, mighty, mighty union!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, as you can hear, the strike is on; 13,000 U.S. autoworkers are now off the job. That number could get a lot higher. A 40-day strike against \uc2 General Motors\uc2  back in 2019, for example, costs nearly $4 billion.\par \par \uc2 So anyone is trying to guess on how long this could go. We're told the talks are going to resume tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 For now, we have got FOX team coverage with Grady Trimble in Wayne, Michigan on those picket lines, and Jacqui Heinrich at the White House on what's on the line.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. Glad to have you. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Let's go right to my buddy Grady Trimble following all of this in Michigan, as we now get close to concluding the first full day of this strike.\par \par \uc2 How are things looking there, Grady?\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, I can tell you that the automakers were disappointed that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president, Shawn Fain, was not at the main negotiating table today.\par \par \uc2 I spoke with \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2 , the CEO of Ford, yesterday, and he told me: We have put a generous offer forward. We're working on a historic deal, while the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , he says, is focused on a historic strike.\par \par \uc2 And now that strike has happened. Here's what \uc2 GM\uc2  CEO \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 : The strike was completely unnecessary. We're ready to get to the table. Again, we have a historic offer on the table. We want to get to those last final issues. I'm extremely frustrated and disappointed, and I think the \uc2 UAW\uc2  leadership needs to get to the table, because we need to get this resolved.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: I'm with Shantel Johnson, one of these striking workers.\par \par \uc2 So they say the offer has been generous, your employer, Ford, as well as \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , 20 percent wage increases, cost of living pay bumps. Why is that not enough?\par \par \uc2 SHANTEL JOHNSON, UAW MEMBER: It's just we do a lot of hard work in here and we feel like we just deserve a little bit more.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people were OK with maybe the last offer that they put up on the table. But, like I said, we only could stand behind our big bosses down at -- up at the big house. They're -- if that's what they say they want to do, we only can stand behind them so far.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: This is your first time striking. You have been with Ford for 12 years. Let me ask you about the strategy, 13,000 workers to start, several thousand of them at this plant, as well as a \uc2 General Motors\uc2  plant in Missouri and a \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  plant in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 But your union says they could ramp this up and strike different plants, depending on how negotiations are going. What do you think of this approach?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Honestly, I think this approach is going well, for this to be my first strike. I think, like I said, we do a lot of stamping over here at our department for a lot of other \uc2 Ford Motor Company\uc2  businesses, buildings.\par \par \uc2 So I think that, eventually, all of the plants will be shut down, depending on how long they take to come up with an agreement.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: In other words, what you're saying is shutting down one or three plants could shut down an entire network?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Correct. Correct. Yes, we do a lot of stamping over here for Dearborn and sometimes Lavonia. So, as far as our stamping being down, it's going to really affect a lot of those businesses also.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: All right, thanks for your time.\par \par \uc2 Neil, you might have been hearing the honking horns behind me. That's been going on throughout the day. We know that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president is going to be joined in just about 45 minutes at a rally with Senator Bernie Sanders, a lot of other Democratic lawmakers voicing their support for these striking workers -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You had a lot of great questions you were asking that striking worker, but one thing stands out that I'm just curious what you're hearing.\par \par \uc2 Already, one of the big automakers had already proposed a 20 percent pay raise, and they sort of sloughed that one off. I mean, obviously they're hoping for far more generous numbers.\par \par \uc2 How likely are they to get it, from what you're hearing?\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: It's possible, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Some of the workers I have talked to said we'd have been happy with a 20 percent pay raise if it came with other improvements to benefits like health and pension. Shantel, who you just heard from, matter of fact, joined after Ford dissolved its pension program for workers. So she has a 401(k), she told me. She said she'd really like to see a pension.\par \par \uc2 And so for some of these workers, it's not just about increasing pay. They'd also like to see improvements to benefits as well. And that is what they say they're picketing for.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for that, Grady Trimble.\par \par \uc2 How the White House is handling that and all of this and trying to prepare for getting involved in this.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich there -- Jacqui\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this strike is bad news for the president, who said just a little over 10 days ago, I believe, that he didn't think a strike was going to happen and he was not concerned about it. The president, who calls himself the most pro-labor president in history, has still not gotten the endorsement of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , which traditionally backs Democrats and backed Biden in 2020.\par \par \uc2 So, today, he made clear that he's siding with the workers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Auto companies have seen record profits, including in the last few years, because of the extraordinary skill and sacrifices of the \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers.\par \par \uc2 Those record profits have not been shared fairly, in my view, with those workers. The bottom line is that autoworkers help create America's middle class. They deserve a contract that sustains them in the middle class.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The strike puts nearly one in 10 of America's unionized autoworkers on strike to pressure \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford and Chrysler-owned \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  to deliver huge wage increases, 36 percent over four years, among other demands.\par \par \uc2 It could push up already high prices and drive buyers toward foreign automakers and also add stress to inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Chamber of Commerce president writes: "The \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike and indeed the summer of strikes is the natural result of the Biden administration's whole-of-government approach to promoting unionization at all costs. For the 94 percent of the American private sector workers not in a union, the costs are starting to stack up, from increasing consumer costs now for new cars to sudden loss in business for those in related industries like auto suppliers, restaurants and caterers, whose customers are now on strike."\par \par \uc2 This was the White House response to that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE