ery different snapshot at that.\par \par \uc2 But we will see. Hopefully, cooler heads and maybe calmer heads resolve all of this.\par \par \uc2 Tom, good seeing you again. Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Go ahead. Finish your thought.\par \par \uc2 MAOLI: Good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 No, I was just saying, I think it will get resolved.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime here, more charges could be coming \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's way, and all of this as Republicans are moving pretty fast on an impeachment inquiry.\par \par \uc2 Jason Smith, the guy who runs the House Ways and Means Committee and will be a key player in that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: CAVUTO: All right, taking a look at three counts we already know on gun possession and worse for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , but conditions that could get worse for him in the weeks and months ahead, potential extra charges here.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from David Spunt following all of this -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, as we look forward into the future, we see what \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  may be charged with.\par \par \uc2 It's possible he may be charged with tax crime. And that's not just us speculating. It's because special counsel David Weiss said so in a court filing from last month in August, that the tax charges that were part of the original plea deal that fell apart are still on the table, and Hunter could be charged in either California, where he lives, or Washington, D.C., where he used to live.\par \par \uc2 But this indictment, as of yesterday, is about a gun. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  faces those three felony counts. Two are false statements. The third is possession of a gun. According to the special counsel's office, back in 2018, Hunter lied on a federal forum to purchase a gun when he said he was not addicted to any stimulant, narcotic drug or controlled substance, when in fact he knew that statement was false.\par \par \uc2 The second charge is a false statement made to a person who worked at the gun shop in Delaware, and the third, Neil, is actually owning the gun when unlawfully using illegal substances. Now, last night, Hunter's attorney, Abbe Lowell, criticized special counsel David Weiss and the overall charges, and he said Weiss is someone who caved to pressure by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ABBE LOWELL, ATTORNEY FOR \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN\uc2 : Number two point, this office has never brought a charge like this against anybody. When they are bringing this charge, it's either because somebody has had the gun in the commission of a crime, they have bought multiple guns, they are a straw purchaser for somebody else, or they're a felon in possession.\par \par \uc2 That -- none of that is true about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Now, the Justice Department put out this release yesterday that added up the amount of time for each charge. It's up to 25 years in prison and a significant amount of money, $250,000, for every charge.\par \par \uc2 But since Hunter's not a prior felon, unlikely he would see anywhere near that. And also to note, there's still no first appearance scheduled in Delaware for this case. We're waiting to have a judge assigned and waiting for his initial appearance in Delaware. That has yet to be scheduled. That's why you haven't heard anything on that front about him in court yet, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Understood, David. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 Now to the guy who runs the House Ways and Means Committee. Very happy to have Jason Smith with us.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that additional charges will be filed against Hunter?\par \par \uc2 REP. JASON SMITH (R-MO): I sure hope so.\par \par \uc2 If you go on based what the \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistle-blowers told our committee, the Ways and Means Committee, and also what additional \uc2 IRS\uc2  employees have said in our most recent testimony in the last week, they all believe that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  should be charged with several felony tax crimes that ranged from 2014 to 2019.\par \par \uc2 And that was more than 19 months ago that the \uc2 IRS\uc2  tax investigative team set before Department of Justice and recommended it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, you're a crucial player, one of three tasked with leading this impeachment probe, inquiry, whatever you want to call it. What will you be probing? What will you be looking at to take it to the next level?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The next step that we're looking at is we -- for one, we have asked for numerous individuals to come forward. We have sent over 16 letters since June 22, both myself, Jim Jordan and Jamie Comer combined, ask for documents, but also asking for answers to select questions.\par \par \uc2 Right now, I'm focused on the Department of Justice Tax Division. I think it's very crucial that they come in, answer numerous questions based on what the road map that the \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistle-blowers laid out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So is it your sense -- because I know there was some skittishness among some of your colleagues to initiate a probe or whatever you want to call it, because it would look like you're too zealous and all of that.\par \par \uc2 Did \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's indictment on these gun charges give you cover? I mean, I know there are a little apples and oranges, I grant you, but that it seemed to take the heat off you or some of you in this impeachment effort.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I just look at Hunter Biden's gun charges as being some of the weakest crimes that he could be charged with.\par \par \uc2 I -- and they're actually the only crimes that it appears that he could be charged with that aren't affiliated with anyone else in his family. And so that's the lowest-hanging fruit that the Justice Department was able to lay out there to try to get the attention off of them, I believe.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But many have been concerned that David Weiss, looking into all of this, wasn't even going to go that far, and now indications that he might be looking at some of these very issues.\par \par \uc2 What are your opinions now of David Weiss?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I will tell you, I have great concerns about David Weiss because our two \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistle-blowers highlighted a meeting on October 7, 2022, that they were in where Mr. Weiss told them that he wanted to bring charges in Washington, in California, and was denied by people above him.\par \par \uc2 But then after we released this information with the whistle-blowers on June 22, you saw letters that came to Jim Jordan and to Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  that said the other way around. However, just in the last week, Neil, we have had four different witnesses, two \uc2 FBI\uc2 , two \uc2 IRS\uc2 , that came in, and they confirmed what the \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistle-blower said.\par \par \uc2 That, in fact, prosecutor Weiss said that he had to go higher up in the food chain to get permission before he could bring any charges against \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . And that is completely counter what the attorney general of the United States, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , has testified before Congress, saying that the prosecutor has complete and total authority.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Chairman, there were reports that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had called a number of key Republicans to pursue this impeachment effort. Did he ever call you?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: He hasn't. And he has my number. And he's called me. I have not talked to the president in several months.\par \par \uc2 This is about getting the right information to the American public. The American public deserves answers.\par \par \uc2 The fact that we have seen now that the president of the United States has lied, we have seen that the president of the United States was part of selling his own brand, according to what Devon Archer referred to as his own brand, to help \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business, the fact that his family has received more than $20 million that we have tracked to 20-plus different shell corporations for their foreign business dealings, and the fact that his family members have been treated differently.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you don't have -- you don't have unequivocal proof that the president is part of the -- saving all of those millions of dollars. Are you saying that you have clear proof that he has and he did and he does?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What we have proof is that clearly he was setting down with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and his business associates.\par \par \uc2 We have testimony showing that the president did phone calls.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I understand that's \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and his business associates, sir. I apologize. I wasn't clear.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you seem to link the president unequivocally to that, and I didn't think we had pro of that.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I think that there's a lot of information that we're looking at that is connected to him, but...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But that sounds like smoke, but not fire.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: This is the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 The reason why we're doing the impeachment inquiry now is the fact that the Biden administration has been obstructing our investigation from day one.\par \par \uc2 This is the first time in the history of Congress that a special counsel was ordered just to obstruct a congressional investigation. That should alarm all Americans.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, again, I just want to be clear, because when you were mentioning the millions of dollars and this Hunter Biden situation, you seemed to indicate that the president himself was a beneficiary of that.\par \par \uc2 You're not so sure?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What I said is, is, there's been $20 million tracked by our \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistle-blowers of Hunter Biden and his associates and his family members.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, and the family members are and do include \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or not, that you know of now?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I'm not going to get the cart before the horse. We will get to that at some point. But, Neil, I will be happy to share that with you in time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, because a lot of people, as you know, a lot of your colleagues have already linked that. And it sounds like we're not quite at that stage yet, right?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I'm not going to get the cart before the horse, but I'm telling you right now that what we have been seeing is extremely alarming, that the president of the United States' son has received more than -- his son and his son's affiliates have received more than $20 million from China, Russia, Romania, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't smell right, and it's not making people happy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will follow it very closely.\par \par \uc2 Chairman, thank you for taking the time. Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You too, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime here, we have got some nasty weather to deal with in a hurricane that's been, well, sticking around forever, but right now getting very close to hitting some very populated areas.\par \par \uc2 The very latest on that with Nicole Valdes -- Nicole?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Neil, winds are already starting to pick up all across New England, as Hurricane Lee slowly moves north, taking aim at Nova Scotia, but the impacts here are going to be plenty.\par \par \uc2 We will talk more about that here coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just call Hurricane Lee the storm that will not go away. It's massive, a slow-mover, and headed to Maine right now, already declaring a state of emergency just in case.\par \par \uc2 We're on it right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, it might not be hitting land, but it will have an impact on land around the north, Northern New England, well up to Nova Scotia. It's a massive storm. I'm talking about Hurricane Lee.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Valdes following it all in Scituate, Massachusetts -- Nicole.\par \par \uc2 VALDES: Neil, the winds you see right now are truly just the beginning, as Hurricane Lee is still approaching the New England area.\par \par \uc2 But I will tell you, the peak of this storm is really going to be felt here tomorrow morning as that hurricane inches closer here. Now, I want to point out that the state has really tried to take preemptive action, not only getting all their resources in place hours ahead of that storm, but really trying to focus in on preparing for the worst, hoping for the best, especially after we have seen a lot of video come from areas that have already started to feel what Hurricane Lee can really produce.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this video showing rough surf in Bermuda. That is absolutely a major threat to places like Scituate, Massachusetts, where I'm standing here today. That rough surf could produce some coastal flooding.\par \par \uc2 So, Governor \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2  now says she's got about 50 National Guardsmen on stand by, with high-water vehicles ready to navigate the potential floodwaters that may come into play with this storm, hopefully not needed, but ready if so, as well as the Red Cross and utility workers spread out throughout across the state.\par \par \uc2 She says she's gone ahead and taken action to try to be in the best position possible ahead of the anticipated impacts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. MAURA HEALEY (D-MA): I declared a state of emergency in Massachusetts. I do this because it's necessary to get assets in place before anything lands here in Massachusetts. It puts us in the best possible position to be able to respond in the ways that we need.\par \par \uc2 This includes both positioning equipment and personnel. We also have sent to the federal government the formal request for an emergency declaration under the Stafford Act.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VALDES: Now, of course, this is not the first time this state has been needing to prepare for a flood event, and actually not the first time this month.\par \par \uc2 Just a few days ago, 11 inches of rain flooded neighborhoods across the state of Massachusetts. The video you saw earlier showed impacts from a nor'easter back in 2014, a storm that will produce -- really, Hurricane Lee, I should say, will produce some similar really impacts that this area is used to seeing during those nor'easter, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So, quite an interesting next couple hours we're going to be seeing here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I would imagine, Nicole. Thank you for your great coverage, my friend, and be safe.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Valdes on that.\par \par \uc2 By the way, FOX Weather is indeed your hurricane headquarters for coverage of all of this and any of this. I always say that we might forget about this storm. Of course, they have been all over it and reminding us, we're not out of the woods here. You can stream it on your favorite connected device, always on, always free. It is remarkable.\par \par \uc2 When I first heard about that and got it myself, now it's glued to me. It's like I know what barbecue conditions are going to be three years out. I exaggerate, but you got my point. They're very good at what they do.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is very good at what he does. It's what made him the world's richest man. But \uc2 Walter Isaacson\uc2 's book on him right now shows him warts and all, even some of the crazy things he does. Is it about getting attention or just because he's crazy?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We had a lot of buzz on my conversation with \uc2 Walter Isaacson\uc2 , who has just written a great book on \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But one of the issues that came up from that FOX Business chat, which we're going to play a little bit for you here, is just the oddity of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , very guy -- rich guy, very smart guy, but kind of weird. And that kind is part and parcel of the DNA of the guy. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALTER ISAACSON\uc2 , AUTHOR, "ELON MUSK": He really has all these personalities. And, at times, he is totally crazy. And he will admit it. He will almost cackle about his craziness.\par \par \uc2 And then he might go into a dark, brooding period. But, there, you see that picture. He takes risks. Even at his birthday party, he gets a blindfolded knife thrower.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, that was a little weird.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: And he's put down balloons between his legs, or the sumo wrestler.\par \par \uc2 But we used to be a nation of risk-takers. We used to -- and no matter where you came from, whether it was on the Mayflower or you crossed the Rio Grande. People would take risks. And now we have become a nation filled with more referees than risk-takers, more people telling you, no, you can't do it, it's not safe, or regulators, than people who are willing to innovate.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that crazed risk-taking gene, page after page in my book helps explain why he's getting rockets into orbit and us into the electric vehicle age.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I take nothing away from that. As you write yourself, as Shakespeare writes, the best are molded out of their faults. So he's got a lot of faults. To what extent did that mold him?\par \par \uc2 But those faults or whatever you want to say seem to go back to a rocky relationship with his dad. And his father was -- seems like a real SOB, but I'm just wondering, why does he react the way he does?\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: He would turn dark whenever we talked about it, sort of a sad look coming over his face.\par \par \uc2 And he was beaten up as a scrawny kid, Elon was. Not any social graces then or now. And his -- the scars, though, were worse that came from his father. He had to stand in front of his father once after a really bad -- being beaten up on the playground. And his father berated him for an hour-and-a- half and said, you're stupid, you're at fault, took the side of the people who beat him up.\par \par \uc2 And so that produced a lot of demons in his head. And all successful people, I think, have some demons. The question is, which demons do you travel -- do you channel into drives?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He can be pretty abusive. You had written that he lacks the empathy gene. And others have said of that.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering, in the two years, I believe it was that long, that you were tracing and following him and seeing this play out, whether you saw it enough for yourself. They say, you know, this is not a nice guy.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of times, he -- that's an understatement to say he's not nice to the people around him at times, especially when he's mission-driven. And in the book, you will see dozens of anecdotes, dozens of tales where he will be walking at the -- say, the base in South Texas where they're launching Starship, and suddenly he will get angry and order up a surge and really ream people out.\par \par \uc2 But there are times he's inspiring. There's times when he's funny, times when he's really caring. And he would say and does say in the book that, yes, he sometimes lacks what we would call empathy, but it's partly because, if you care too much about pleasing everybody around you, you're never going to fulfill your mission.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 Did he ever reveal to you someone he really liked for president? There was a time when it was DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I'm not sure whether that flips too.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: I don't think he's settled on a candidate.\par \par \uc2 He spends a lot of time making political pronouncements.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: But in politics and everything else, he's rather mercurial.\par \par \uc2 There will be times in the middle of the day, he's in a good mood, he tells me that we need to have a center in this nation, we need to help support moderates, people on Republican and Democratic side that can come together.\par \par \uc2 You're showing him with Obama there. He was a big fund-raiser for Obama.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: So he will talk about the need for bringing center together.\par \par \uc2 Then there will be times late at night, when he's particularly angry at somebody for dissing him, and it may be a Democratic politician, it may be whatever, and he gets dark. So I don't think his politics are easy to pigeonhole.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He was a rock star to a lot of the people on the left with his very energy-efficient, environmentally safe and beautiful cars.\par \par \uc2 And then he started espousing some of these political views, and the same people who said great things about and turned on him. How did he feel about that?\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: Well, he got very -- once when he was at a wilderness camp as a little kid, he used to get beaten up. They took all his food. Next time he went, he was a little bit bigger. And he said, I just learned to punch people in the nose as hard as I can. And even though they beat me up, they'd remember that I had punched them in the nose.\par \par \uc2 So he's pugnacious, pugnacious, so much so that it can be a bit shocking. So he has done that, especially when Elizabeth Warren says, you're dodging taxes. He ends up not only punching back, but he ends up exercising options and paying, I think, more tax than any human has ever paid to any person, any government in history.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: But that gets them all set up and riled up.\par \par \uc2 So he will get pugnacious politically, but I'm sure he will do it to people on the right, as well as the left.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The other stuff that's come out and all this personal stuff, and the demon mode and all of this, and how he lacks the empathy gene, and his rocky relationship with his father, and the fights back and forth, and all this baggage, he didn't get back to you on this and say, this isn't at all what I thought?\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: Oh, no, he knew what I was going to write. I mean, we talked about all of these things.\par \par \uc2 He's not very happy talking about his childhood. It makes him choke up.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: But there's -- we talked about all these things in the book.\par \par \uc2 And I think he wanted something that was almost brutally honest, and he's not objected. I have seen the things he's posted in the past week, and he will comment on things but he has not -- knock on wood -- decided that -- to go ballistic on anything.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So he's OK with you revealing that the world's richest man cuts his own hair?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: You should look at his hair. Sometimes, it looks like it's cut by a Korean barber who's about to be executed.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is he really concerned that humankind has a shelf life on it?\par \par \uc2 ISAACSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And when you ask about, is he empathetic, does he care about -- is he nice to the people around him, he's more empathetic to humanity, if you know what I mean, than maybe the five or six people who might be in front of him on a factory floor.\par \par \uc2 And he has three great missions that he's had since he was in college. One is to get us into an era of sustainable energy, including electric vehicles. The other is something could happen to this planet, to our civilization. We're the only consciousness we know of in the universe. We got to become a spacefaring civilization. And that's the adventurous part of us.\par \par \uc2 And, thirdly, he read Isaac Asimov as a kid. He worries about artificial intelligence and robots being dangerous. I used to think that some of those, these are my missions, were kind of the pep talks you give your team or the pontificating you do on podcasts. But over and over, I heard him talk about it and relate things back there.\par \par \uc2 I really think that this is part of this deep motivation of a man-child who thinks of himself on the stage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: An impressive stage, as \uc2 Walter Isaacson\uc2  put out. Whatever you think of the guy, he is the world's richest human being, and he has overcome many difficulties to become that success, warts and all, like a lot of successful people.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, the controversial exchange right now in New York just a few blocks from here earlier today concerning a migrant crisis that got loud and, yes, nasty.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, very hard to hear everything that was going on there, but Alexandria Cortez was there with people like \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2 , liberal Democrats, who were trying to say they want to look after the migrants who were here, take care of them.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of people in that crowd were not hearing it, as both sides went at each other.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams was in the middle of that, kept her calm and cool through that.\par \par \uc2 But now things are a little bit better, but I don't think that they were expecting this, were they?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I don't think the congressmembers were expecting it either, right? We're in a Democratic city, and they probably were expecting more of a warm welcome. But we heard from lots of frustrated New Yorkers who came with their yelling, their screaming chaos, and blowhorns.\par \par \uc2 Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Close the border! Close the border!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Their message there, close the Southern border. That was a scene outside of the Roosevelt Hotel in New York, protesters interrupting those members of the \uc2 Congressional Hispanic Caucus\uc2 , Neil.\par \par \uc2 More than a dozen Democratic congressmembers, including AOC, did tour the hotel, which is the main processing center for migrants. So, as we know, it is one of the busiest areas that people see walking by with lines of migrants outside on a daily basis. New Yorkers came to the hotel today to demand answers, saying they're for legal immigration.\par \par \uc2 Tensions boiled over, though, as the surge continues to show really no sign of slowing down, at least 1,000 migrants showing up in the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2  each and every month. Congressmembers tried to talk about their plans to help, but you really couldn't hear much.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADRIANO ESPAILLAT (D-NY): The American dream will not be bullied into submission today. We will continue to fight for the American dream. We will not be bullied.\par \par \uc2 We will not be pushed into a corner. We will fight for these commonsense solutions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Yes, and I think they thought it was going to be a quick press conference, talk about what they saw inside, talk about what they plan to do.\par \par \uc2 But New Yorkers say they're sick of the finger-pointing from the mayor looking at the White House, the White House pointing back to the city, and they just want to know what's going on and what the plan is, because you can check it out over my shoulder here in Midtown Manhattan again. There's migrants just lined up outside.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of questions and a lot of concern -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, we could see all of this again. Alexis, great coverage.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams on that.\par \par \uc2 Well, don't say that South Carolina Congresswoman Nancy Mace hadn't warned you about the possibility this kind of thing was spreading deep into blue states and that within those blue states there would be rage. She's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Close the border! Close the border!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the bluest of cities, in the bluest of states, a reminder that this migrant issue that has now gone way beyond Texas into a number of sanctuary cities and states, it's alive and well.\par \par \uc2 And, for Democrats, it could prove a major headache.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Nancy Mace joins us right now in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, I just wondered, with these developments here, whether we have exposed a clear weakness among Democrats who up to now have been kind of in lockstep with each other on operation ignore this if they can.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now you have New York Mayor Adams, of course, among those saying, Mr. President, you got to do something, the Democratic governor in New York, Mr. President, you got to do something, Governor Newsom out in California, you got to do something.\par \par \uc2 What's going on?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes. No, it's interesting.\par \par \uc2 And it's -- surprisingly, it's all these sanctuary cities that would say that they welcome illegal immigrants or the ones that do not want them in their cities. And I would -- I commend every single border governor who is taking these illegals crossing the border and taking them to other cities, especially sanctuary cities, especially blue cities, so they can understand that the president's administration, the president's policies are what are causing this problem.\par \par \uc2 And it's not just the people coming across the border. We all know the fentanyl crisis is at sky high. I have known multiple people in South Carolina who have died from fentanyl overdoses just this year. I didn't even know what fentanyl was 10 years ago. It's also about terrorists that are just freely walking across the border.\par \par \uc2 The numbers we're seeing this year are historic and it's damaging our country. We need to have laws that we follow.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think, Congresswoman -- I know you have been trying to address this and this impeachment inquiry against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , that you're open to that.\par \par \uc2 You and some of your colleagues have said that we can walk and chew gum at the same time. I get that. But this is a potent issue, this migrant issue, the economy.\par \par \uc2 MACE: It is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And now with a worker strike going on, potent issues.\par \par \uc2 Do you think, even with the best of intentions, that focus on that impeachment at this time is distracting from these bigger concerns?\par \par \uc2 MACE: I think that's always a risk, because the broader public is going to see a tit for tat. They're going to see this as a wash on either side, because there are investigations on either side.\par \par \uc2 And it takes from the focus of issues that matter. We have inflation. I mean, we just had our district work period. I went to the grocery store with my kids. It cost almost $500 for a week's worth of groceries. No junk food, by the way, Neil, for a week. And that -- just families can't afford...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's unfortunate right there, yes.\par \par \uc2 MACE: I know. Well, it's $2,000 for a family of four for a month of groceries without even going out. That's unaffordable.\par \par \uc2 It's women's issues. It's abortion. It's immigration. These are top issues facing America.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But they don't come up, Congresswoman, in this.\par \par \uc2 MACE: No. No.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: When you were on CNN the other night, you said, I -- "You don't do that by making Republicans and moderate districts walk the plank on abortion..."\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: "... walk the plank on women's issues, walk the plank on birth control, walk the plank on impeachment. Well, like, those are all reasons why we will lose next year if we go down that path."\par \par \uc2 Are you afraid of the party getting MAGA blindsided?\par \par \uc2 MACE: I am very concerned, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you today I'm about as frustrated and jaded and angry as I have ever been. I have tried to show some -- a framework and a road map on how to handle women's issues, particularly in the era post-Roe, being a victim of rape, working on rape kit legislation, working on birth control legislation, working on commonsense issues that bring everybody together.\par \par \uc2 And absolutely nothing has been done on these issues. And, instead, it's just all these floor fights, and we have not done anything for hardworking blue-collar Americans who can't afford to put food on the table, who can barely make ends meet, put gas in the tank. It's very difficult for them.\par \par \uc2 And I'm -- we're going to have to ask ourselves at the end of the year, Republicans and Democrats alike, what have we done for our country and our countrymen.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So by going after those who are more moderate on this issue and don't want to necessarily punish moderate members who might not down the road, Congresswoman, vote for impeachment -- and I'm getting ahead of myself here.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes. We're still early in the process.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They would be -- they would be -- they would be targeted, though, wouldn't they?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, they will be targeted on all the issues, whether it's the impeachment inquiry or women's issues.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you, abortion is going -- people are going to vote on abortion next year. And -- yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, no, within your party, there is a possibility that becomes the single issue, this impeachment thing, you're with impeachment or you're not.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right, it could be.\par \par \uc2 But there are a lot of other issues out there that we haven't done anything on. And as a suburban woman...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So they shouldn't be punished if they go that route, you're saying? I'm not...\par \par \uc2 MACE: No, they shouldn't be punished.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACE: I mean, nobody should be punished for the vote that they take for their own districts.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley, who right now is doing very well in the polls, still trails \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by a lot. His lead in your state is incredible. Is he the nominee?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, it's still very early in the process, but it does look that way.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Unless other candidates start dropping out, he -- I believe he's going to be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, thank you. Sorry, I wanted to jump on some of these issues.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Nancy Mace.\par \par \uc2 Now, I mentioned Nikki Haley. She will be my special guest tomorrow on my weekend show, "Cavuto Live," get her views on this race and some of these issues that we just raised with the congresswoman, all of that coming up at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 For now, here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230916ej9f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President's Son Faces Up To 25 Years In Prison; Hunter Biden Indicted On Three Felony Charges; Hunter Attorney: Charges Barred By Diversion Agreement; Democrats Criticized Republicans On Lack Of Inquiry Vote; German Foreign Minister Visits Texas And Washington; \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  and Three Large Car Manufacturers Yet To Reach Agreement On New Contract As Possible Strike Looms; \uc2 NASA\uc2  Says Recent UAP Sightings Present No Evidence of Extraterrestrials Visiting Earth; Congress Holds Hearing On Continuing Remote Work For Government Employees; Convicted Murderer Alex Murdaugh Appears In Court For Financial Crimes Trial; Hunter Biden Charged With Unlawful Possession Of Firearm; President Biden Losing To Former President Trump And Other Republican Presidential Candidates In Recent Poll\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, David Spunt, Shannon Bream, Chad Pergram, Jacqui  Heinrich, Grady Trimble, Jonathan Hunt, Aishah Hasnie, Jonathan Serrie \par \uc2 7,798 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: I want to -- I want to give kudos to these -- the Belgian Malinois Yoda. Yoda is the one who subdued Cavalcante. And he was part of the BORTAC K9 unit. And he was a significant force in the takedown.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: All right. Well, that's wonderful.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Hello, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Hello, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, I was guessing seven nuts for the squirrel. And I was really hoping you went into my hour too.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh. How many -- how many nuts can you fit in your mouth, Bret?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Oh, no!\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: What!\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You guys are sick!\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's really great to have this back.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Come on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's really great to have this back. I like wall nuts. Thanks. Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Hey, I didn't ask for it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT", President Biden's son, Hunter is facing federal charges tonight. The special counsel investigating numerous allegations is charging him with lying on a gun purchase form, lying to a gun shop employee, and possessing a firearm when unlawfully using illegal substances.\par \par \uc2 This is the same gun case for which prosecutors elected not to charge \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  as part of a now defunct plea deal several weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 Biden's attorneys still contends that agreement is binding. We have complete coverage tonight.\par \par \uc2 We begin though with correspondent David Spunt at the justice department with the latest. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 After weeks of intense pressure, Special Counsel David Weiss gave the green light to go ahead on charges, and ultimately, a grand jury approved charges against the son of the sitting president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  facing two false statement counts, which are felonies. Also, a third count of possessing and owning a gun, another felony.\par \par \uc2 According to the Special Counsel's Office, back in 2018, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  lied on a federal form to purchase a gun in Delaware when he said that he was not addicted to any stimulant narcotic drug and any other controlled substance, when in fact, he knew that statement was false.\par \par \uc2 The second charge is a false statement made to a person who worked for the gun shop in Delaware. The third is actually owning the gun when he unlawfully used illegal substances.\par \par \uc2 The charges Bret, could put him behind bars for 25 years if you total them all up.\par \par \uc2 Swift reaction on Capitol Hill from House Oversight Chairman \uc2 James Comer\uc2 , who says the justice department has plenty of other larger crimes to pin on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMES COMER\uc2  (R-KY): The list goes on and on. You've never heard me say anything about gun charges. So, again, that's the one crime he's committed that you cannot tie to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Democrats say today's indictment is nothing to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): We shouldn't take delight in other people's misfortunes. But we have to have a rule of law. And when people commit crimes, then, the consequences follow.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Both \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and the DOJ hoped to avoid this day when they met in court in Delaware in July.\par \par \uc2 The plea deal fell apart in a very public way, Bret, when the government would not guarantee that Hunter would be immune from any future prosecutions.\par \par \uc2 Hunter's attorney, Abbe Lowell, took aim at the special counsel. In a statement this afternoon to Fox News, he said in part, "Hunter Biden possessing an unloaded gun for 11 days was not a threat to public safety, but a prosecutor, with all the power imaginable, bending to political pressure presents a grave threat to our system of justice."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Bret, I'll end with this is may not be the only indictment for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . Special Counsel Weiss indicated in an August court filing that those tax charges that were part of that failed plea deal may be filed in an indictment in either California where Hunter lives or Washington, D.C. where he wants lived. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt at the Justice Department. David, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): Where are the charges on human sex trafficking, FARA charges, money laundering, tax evasion?\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): We've never connected anything to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So, it's not surprising that the justice department charges have nothing to do with the president.\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC SWALWELL (D-CA): I think that shows that we still have an independent rule of law, and nothing they have said has amounted anything.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of reaction today. Let's get some analysis. Now, joining us, Fox News chief legal correspondent, anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" Shannon Bream, and criminal defense attorney, Philip Holloway.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, obviously, the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  lawyers, Abbe Lowell, in particular, is saying, hey, wait a second, this is part of the deal we agreed to. We think that, that first deal is still binding here.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Yes. And that has been a position that they are sticking to. They say that this is something that parties were fully informed about. They had consent. They decided what they were going to do, and they were going to move forward.\par \par \uc2 But remember, back in July, the judge started asking questions about that, and many other parts of what they had put together, and never gave a blessing or signed off on this.\par \par \uc2 So, I would expect -- and Phillip can talk to this better than I can that as just -- as a defense attorney, you are not going to allow anything to slip through your fingers that may be of possible help to your client. That seems to be the argument they're going to stick with now. but there is so many more things now looming with this indictment actually in place, and how far they want to go with that, and what success they'll have remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. Phillip, what about that to Shannon's point?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP HOLLOWAY, CRIMINAL DEFENSE ATTORNEY: Well, she's exactly right. A -- the good defense lawyer is going to try to do everything possible to get the very best possible result for their client.\par \par \uc2 In this case, the very first way to defend it, because you cannot defend it on the facts. The facts are clear, you're going to have to try to establish, if you can, that, that diversion agreement was a binding contract.\par \par \uc2 In other words, was it a quid pro quo? Was that a meeting of the minds? Was it something that is enforceable? Just as a regular contract would be? And that's going to be a question, first off for the federal judge who is going to be assigned in this case.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, guys, they're going to be trying to have a dialogue with Weiss and his team. They're going to try to see if there's a plea bargain that can still be had.\par \par \uc2 I'm a bit skeptical, because we were told over the last couple of months that the parties did try to revive some kind of a plea deal, before we got to this point, and they just came to loggerheads.\par \par \uc2 So, it appears that we're either going to be headed to a trial or potentially a whole series of pre-trial motions about the enforceability of that agreement.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Phil, let me just follow on this. And that is there a lot of people calling for other things, the FARA charges, other investigations. But just dealing on these charges, it's still very serious what he's facing, right?\par \par \uc2 HOLLOWAY: Oh, yes. He is looking at potentially up to 25 years, I think. And this is the lowest of the low hanging fruit. What remains to be seen is whether or not Weiss is going to go for something a little bigger. Maybe is he going to look into some alleged financial crimes, things that might involve other people, perhaps, even the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 But this one is serious enough, because it goes back to almost five years ago, a gun was found in a dumpster near a school. And if that doesn't create a dangerous situation, I don't know what does. It's -- it could have been a potential tragedy if the gun had gotten into the wrong hands near that school.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOLLOWAY: So, we need to know why it took so long. Why did it take almost five years right at the statute of limitations to bring this case?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the politics of this Shannon, obviously, are all over the place. Our friend, Jonathan Turley, writing, "Biden's Break-the-Glass Option: Pardon Hunter, Withdrawal From the 2024 Election: Biden could bow out to the election without admitting as many on both sides are saying that old age has taken its toll on his mental and physical capacity. He would end his political career with an act as a father, which would some would -- which some would condemn, but most would understand."\par \par \uc2 I mean, there's all kinds of things that could happen. The White House is not talking about pardons.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And of course, we remember the moment not long ago that our Mark Meredith put that question to \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , would the president, if he needed to, pardon his son? She didn't sound like she left any room there and said no, repeatedly, that, that would not happen.\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind too that there are so many other optics in play here. The House Judiciary Committee says that Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is going to testify next week. They are certainly going to have questions about this, about the length of how this came together, that plea deal falling apart.\par \par \uc2 And while -- yes, now that there's an indictment, there could be conversations about a plea deal moving forward. I agree with Philip. I mean, it's unlikely we would go there.\par \par \uc2 But Bret, think about where that leaves us. That could be that the president's son in the midst of a campaign year, could be in the middle of a trial. At the same time, the man who looks like he could be the GOP nominee and former president is also in a courtroom. It's going to be a political year, like we've never seen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kind of a unique political year. I would say that's right. Shannon, Phillip, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: With the folk's cover.\par \par \uc2 HOLLOWAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The three House committee chairman who will lead the impeachment inquiry against President Biden briefed their Republican colleagues today. This comes as the House Speaker faces increasing pressure from conservatives.\par \par \uc2 Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): House Speaker Kevin McCarthy is trying to stay focused on impeaching the president for alleged wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The American public wants to know and that's what impeachment inquiry provides.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The length of an inquiry is unclear.\par \par \uc2 COMER\uc2 : It's hard to put a definitive timeline. I'm an impatient person. I hope that we can wrap this up as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Democrats are now criticizing Republicans for not voting to formalize an impeachment inquiry. Yet, Democrats initially resisted an official vote for the first impeachment of former President Trump before voting later on.\par \par \uc2 Now, Democrats insist the House vote.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARY GAY SCANLON (D-PA): At a certain point, you have to in order to proceed. But obviously, you don't just declare that you're going to impeach someone because you don't like their politics.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): But pursuing impeachment failed to impress conservatives who want to oust McCarthy. McCarthy hammered naysayers at a closed-door meeting.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I don't walk away from a battle. And you know what, if it takes a fight, I'll have a fight.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): McCarthy loyalists said the Speaker torched his opponents.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is there any f-bombs or any --\par \par \uc2 REP. DON BACON (R-NE): There may have been one. So, he is irritated and I think that's what I heard.\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA (R-CA): The Speaker said, look, if you want to make a motion to vacate the chair, bring it on.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): McCarthy rankled conservatives for saying Republicans must pass a one or two-month interim spending bill to avert a shutdown, which doesn't immediately cut spending. Democrats relish the GOP in fighting.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Well, we're focused on fighting for everyday Americans. The extreme MAGA Republicans are focused on fighting each other.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Jeffries said the GOP is now like a three-ring circus. One ring for impeachment, another for government funding, and another for the Speaker drama. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is that. Chad Pergram, live on Capitol Hill. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A Georgia judge has ruled former President Trump and 16 others will be tried separately from two defendants who are set to go to trial next month.\par \par \uc2 19 people are facing charges of trying to illegally overturn the state's results in the 2020 election. Prosecutors had sought to try all of them at the same time.\par \par \uc2 Our brand-new Fox News poll is filled with bad news for President Biden and good news for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The lead for the former president among Republican candidates has grown seven points since last month. Now standing at 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has lost three points in this poll. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  is on DeSantis's heels at 11 percent. 61 percent of those surveyed feel President Biden does not have the mental soundness to serve. Only 36 percent believe he does.\par \par \uc2 The president trails former President Trump, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , Nikki Haley, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , in head-to-head matchups, all are within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says he has led the country out of its great economic crisis. The president making the comment the day after the latest data showed increasing inflation and soaring energy prices.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jackie.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 The White House has a way of pulling out economic data that works for their message. For instance, arguing that real wages are up relative to before the pandemic, despite data showing that inflation has outpaced earnings growth for the middle class. So, team Biden is trying a new messaging strategy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Even though we've had 19 straight months of unemployment under four percent for the first time in American history, even though we have the lowest inflation rate of any major economy in the world.\par \par \uc2 All they do is attack it.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): In what the White House framed as a major economic address, President Biden delivering no new policy but a new strategy; showcased the other side as worse.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: For all the time they spent attacking me and my plan, here is what they never do. They never talk about what they want to do.\par \par \uc2 Republican given us a failed plan a trickledown economics, it didn't work.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Biden framed MAGAnomics as extreme, pointing to the congressional battle over the debt limit that ended with a downgrade in the U.S. credit rating, driving home the message that despite poll after poll, showing voters aren't happy with his handling of the economy, America is doing better than the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's not an accident. That literally is our economic plan in action, Bidenomics in action.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But new Fox polls show the president losing ground with key constituencies compared to the fall of 2020. Support among suburban women down 12 points, black voters down 10 points, and Hispanic voters down five.\par \par \uc2 Support moved to former President Trump's column up 13 points with suburban women and seven points with both black and Hispanic voters. After op-eds in The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  and New York Times cast doubt on the viability of Biden-Harris 2024, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  gave a notably weak defense.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER, CNN ANCHOR: Is the Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the best running mate for this president.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): He thinks so. And that's what matters. And by the way --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Do you think -- do you think she is the best running mate, though?\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: She is the vice president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But Hillary Clinton seem to get the memo showcase the flaws of the alternative?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: He's old. Yes, that's right. But look at what he's gotten done. And then, if that's not enough for you, look at the alternative, a wrecking crew.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The president is back at the White House. He did not take any questions from reporters today. But he did quip at the end of his event. I wish I had a chance to take all of your questions, but I'm going to get in real trouble if I do that. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Jacqui Heinrich, live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks we're up today because of optimism. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  may pause its interest rate hikes that meant the Dow gaining 332 today -- big gain. The S&P 500 finished ahead 38. The NASDAQ jumped 112 today.\par \par \uc2 Germany's foreign minister is here to talk about aid for Ukraine. Her meeting with American officials and what's next with the U.S. relationship. That is coming up. And later, if the truth is out there. Does \uc2 NASA\uc2  know about it? And would \uc2 NASA\uc2  tell us? A new report on UFOs from the space agency is just out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON, ADMINISTRATOR, NATIONAL AERONAUTICS AND SPACE ADMINISTRATION: If you asked me, do I believe there is life in a universe that is so vast? That it's hard for me to comprehend how big it is? My personal answer is yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER German Foreign Minister \uc2 Annalena Baerbock\uc2  is visiting the U.S. this week, she's talking about aid for Ukraine, as well as subjects such as technology and green energy. She joins us this evening. Madam Minister, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 ANNALENA BAERBOCK\uc2 , GERMAN FOREIGN MINISTER: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to talk to you first of all about where you all see the situation in Ukraine. As you look at a map of the controlled territories and the back and forth between Russia and Ukraine, how does Germany look at this, and what's happening on the ground in your neighborhood?\par \par \uc2 BAERBOCK: Just before I arrived in Texas, two days ago, I was in Ukraine, with a train ride overnight going there. And it was my first time there, I've met schoolchildren who haven't been in school for a year. I've met children who have been deported to Russia.\par \par \uc2 But I also always meeting people, brave men and women who are defending their freedom, because they want to live in a free world as we do as well.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Madam Minister, this is Rich Edson in Fox News, filling in for Bret here very briefly. But when you look at the situation that's ongoing in the ground right now and what more the West needs to do, what more the United States needs to do more than you need to do, where do you see that situation shaping up?\par \par \uc2 BAERBOCK: We have to keep up our efforts to support Ukraine military, so they can defend themselves. Free the people behind this mining belt (PH), which you have shown on the map, where people are hiding since 1-1/2 years in their bunkers, because they are afraid of going out, being afraid of being raped by soldiers or tortured.\par \par \uc2 So, therefore, Germany, Europe stepped up with its military support as the U.S. did. Because we are not only helping to defend Ukraine, the brave people of Ukraine, we are defending the democracy worldwide.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: There's been criticism in the United States of the Biden administration for not being quick enough with providing armaments, not providing more armaments, more deadly armaments. And there's been criticism of the United States that the European governments haven't moved quickly enough as well. What do you say to that criticism?\par \par \uc2 BAERBOCK: Look, we all believed that there would be never war again in Europe. But Putin lied, not only to us, he lied to the whole world. And his goal was to destroy Ukraine within a week, he wanted to destroy also the European peace order. He wanted to destroy the Charter of the United Nations.\par \par \uc2 And because of the unity between our European partners, but also between the U.S. and Europe, in many, many countries around the world, we stop this brutal invasion from Putin.\par \par \uc2 And we will stand with Ukraine as long as it takes. Because we are defending not only Ukraine as I said, we are defending the rule of law, the freedom in the world.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: What does the \uc2 German government\uc2  and the West see as the end game here? How does this conflict end?\par \par \uc2 BAERBOCK: With liberty and peace in Ukraine, and we will support Ukraine as long as it takes. Because if Putin were to win this war, what sign would that be for other dictators in the world, like Xi, like the Chinese president?\par \par \uc2 So, therefore, Ukraine, has to win this war. Freedom and democracy have to win. And we will stand with Ukraine as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Madam Foreign Minister, do you have any message for the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 , which is now considering another aid package to Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 BAERBOCK: It's not only about Ukraine, it's not only about the freedom in Europe. It's about us in the world. It's about the U.S. Because Putin is fighting all the democracies worldwide. And therefore, we have to stand united, we are stronger than this brutal aggression of Putin. And united, we can stand for that.\par \par \uc2 And I've been speaking to many senators, also of Republicans today, and it's really overwhelming. How many people in the U.S. are also thinking how they can support?\par \par \uc2 I totally understand this question about inflation, rising food prices. We have the same discussions in Europe as well. But we always have to ask our self, what would happen if we wouldn't support Ukraine, and the price would be so much higher?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: OK, and we're going to send this back to Bret Baier now, by the way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks for Rich Edson that has taken the baton there, as we had some technical difficulties.\par \par \uc2 Madam Minister, you were in Texas, you met with the governor of Texas, you said that it's important to go other places in the U.S. besides Washington. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 BAERBOCK: Because politics is about the people, the normal people who do work in a grocery store. And there are normal people who go at 6:00 in the morning to work and come back at home after two shifts to care for their children.\par \par \uc2 And I really wanted to see the diversity of your wonderful, of your beautiful country. And Texas is a state I think, where you can see all the progress in the future state, which is still standing with one lake in the oil field, and then the fossil fuels and with the others, the investing in green technology, like being the biggest wind state in the United States.\par \par \uc2 And as we are moving forward globally, with future production of green energy, I think we can really learn from each other. And there are 150 companies in Texas providing more than 50,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we can also show there, if democracies are working together in the field of economy, it makes us all stronger.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Madam Minister, we appreciate your time and taking questions from two interviewers. And we thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BAERBOCK: Thank you, all the best and thanks for having me on the show but also in your beautiful country. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you. We will have an exclusive interview with Saudi Arabia's Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  on Wednesday. It will be the Crown Prince's first interview with a major U.S. network since 2019.\par \par \uc2 SPECIAL REPORT will be live from Saudi Arabia on Tuesday and Wednesday. Tuesday's live show features a number of interviews with Saudi officials giving a behind the scenes look at the Kingdom. We're excited to bring this to you. And again, the big interview Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The clock is ticking toward a major strike against the country's big three automakers. We'll have a live report.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, we are counting down the hours for the walkout deadline for Detroit autoworkers unionized employees who are threatening to leave jobs at Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , if their demands are not met. FOX Business correspondent Grady Trimble is at Ford headquarters in Dearborn, Michigan, tonight. Good evening, Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. With less than six hours until the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  contract expires, a strike at at least one of the Detroit three automakers looks increasingly likely. The latest offers from Ford and \uc2 General Motors\uc2  include a 20 percent bump in pay over the four years of the contract with additional cost of living increases. But that's a long way from the 46 percent pay raise the \uc2 UAW\uc2  initially requested. Ford, G.M., and \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and Chrysler maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  say they are negotiating in good faith.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIM FARLEY\uc2 , FORD CEO: I know the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is playing for a historic strike of all big three at the same time. We would rather have a historic deal to make history.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: How hopeful are you that you will be able to reach a deal before the deadline tonight?\par \par \uc2 MARK REUSS\uc2 , \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2  PRESIDENT: I'm very hopeful, and I will remain hopeful until 11:59, because I believe that that's the best outcome for all of our employees, our company, and, frankly, the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: \uc2 GM\uc2 's president, \uc2 Mark Reuss\uc2 , says 40 percent of vehicles in the U.S. are made by companies with union workers. So a bad deal for the automakers or a drawn out strike could raise car prices. So far, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has rejected every offer, and its president, Shawn Fain, says its workers are ready to strike. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  says its plan is to have workers walk off the job not at every factory at all three companies at once, but at specific, targeted auto plants.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN, UNITED AUTO WORKERS PRESIDENT: The big three can afford to immediately give us our fair share. If they choose not to, then they are choosing to strike themselves, and we are not afraid to take action.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: We also know President Biden spoke with the president of the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and the leaders of the big three automakers about the status of the negotiations. Unless there is a major breakthrough in the next few hours. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  will announce at 10:00 eastern tonight which plants it will picket. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Grady Trimble outside Ford headquarters in Michigan. We will monitor this. Grady, thanks.\par \par \uc2 No aliens -- that is the headline tonight from \uc2 NASA\uc2 's long-awaited report on UFOs. The space agency says it does not know what the objects are that we have seen on video in recent months, but it says there is nothing to indicate nonhumans are involved. That contradicts whistleblower testimony we heard this summer in Congress. Chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt takes a look tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON, NASA ADMINISTRATOR: The \uc2 NASA\uc2  independent study team did not find any evidence that UAP have an extraterrestrial origin.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Fourteen months after \uc2 NASA\uc2  commissioned more than a dozen experts to look into what the government now terms unexplained anomalous phenomena --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh my gosh, dude.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Wow! What is that, man?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Look at it fly!\par \par \uc2 HUNT: -- incidents like this -- those experts have reached a definitive conclusion that more study is needed.\par \par \uc2 NELSON: We don't know what these UAP are. But we're going to try to find out.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The biggest difficulty, according to this panel of leading scientists, is that they don't know what they don't know.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPERGEL, UAP INDEPENDENT STUDY TEAM CHAIRMAN: With anomalies, that's really the case we're in. We're looking for things we don't understand. So if you want to find something strange in a haystack, you better know exactly what hay looks like.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: But the \uc2 NASA\uc2  administrator made a promise to the American people.\par \par \uc2 NELSON: Whatever we find, we are going to tell you.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And that promise has been welcomed by Ryan Graves, one of the former \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  pilots who testified at a congressional hearing this summer on his experience of unexplained anomalous phenomena.\par \par \uc2 LT. RYAN GRAVES (RET), FORMER U.S. NAVY PILOT: Of course, we don't know where they're actually from, what the origin of these objects are. Their nature. But that's really why \uc2 NASA\uc2  is getting involved, so that they can employ their scientific tools and their expertise to better understand if there is life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (on camera): Now, \uc2 NASA\uc2  officials say they want to move the UAP debate from, quote, conjecture and conspiracy towards science and sanity. But science and sanity tend to take time. So we'll all be waiting a while longer to find the truth that is out there. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The coronavirus pandemic's legacy of working from home has some lawmakers irked. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We go into buildings in this bizarre realm called Washington, D.C., and just look at the doors. There is nobody there. It's not working.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A tropical storm warning has been issued for the coast of Massachusetts as hurricane Lee heads toward the region. The warning includes Cape Cod, Nantucket, and Martha's Vineyard. Millions of people in New England are bracing for the category one hurricane. It's expected to bring damaging winds, heavy rain, flooding, and power outages there.\par \par \uc2 There is a renewed push tonight for federal government workers to return to their offices. The pressure is coming from both sides of the aisle. One senator even says workers who moved away during COVID and are still being paid as if they live in Washington are guilty of fraud. Here is congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Congress is divided over work from home as federal buildings in Washington continue to sit empty.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We've heard reports of V.A. employees who are doing their work from a bubble bath.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Today lawmakers grilled agencies like the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  on their remote work policies. According to DHS, 85 percent of its workers nationwide report on site more than 50 percent of the time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What assurances do you have that folks are totally engaged in their work when they are at home?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They can be distracted, so I want my supervisor to be engaged. He can't monitor what they are doing on the Internet.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: This summer, the White House asked all federal agencies to bring workers back to the office after a \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2  report found 17 of the 24 federal agencies use an estimated average 25 percent or less of their headquarters building's capacity. The report further broke down how much that's costing taxpayers. Federal agencies spend about $5 billion annually to lease office buildings nationwide, and $2 billion to operate and maintain them whether they are used or not. But House Democrats, who themselves worked remotely up until this year, defend telework, saying it attracts candidates in a competitive job market and increases productivity.\par \par \uc2 REP. MAXWELL FROST, (D-FL) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE: Seventy percent of federal employees reported that telework had improved productivity at their agency a great deal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, the GOP-led House tried to tackle this issue earlier this year by passing a bill that would require federal agencies to reinstate those pre-COVID telework policies, but it went nowhere in the Senate. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former South Carolina attorney Alec Murdaugh was in court today for the first time since his sentencing for killing his wife and son. He faces charges of stealing settlement money from friends, colleagues, and clients. Correspondent Jonathan Serrie reports tonight from South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Wearing an orange prison uniform and shackled at the waste, convicted murderer Alec Murdaugh was back in a South Carolina courtroom. The judge set a February 27th start date for his financial crimes trial in the case of Gloria Satterfield, his longtime housekeeper who died from injuries after falling on his property back in 2018. The disbarred low country lawyer is accused of pocketing millions of dollars in settlement money owed her family.\par \par \uc2 CREIGHTON WATERS, SOUTH CAROLINA STATE PROSECUTOR: This is about allegations that represent an assault on the state judiciary. And because of that, they need to be answered.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: As Murdaugh's attorneys prepare to defend him against state financial fraud charges, they are trying to overturn his March 2nd double murder conviction in the 2021 shootings of his wife and son. They are asking for a new trial based on disputed allegations that the clerk of court attempted to influence the jury. But the defense team confirmed Murdaugh plans to change his plea in a federal finance crimes case when he appears before a U.S. district judge next Thursday.\par \par \uc2 JIM GRIFFIN, MURDAUGH ATTORNEY: We will be filing a formal plea agreement probably tomorrow where he has agreed to plead guilty to all the pending charges.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: An attorney representing some of the victims of Murdaugh's alleged financial schemes believes he knows why the defendant would cooperate with the feds while he fights the state.\par \par \uc2 ERIC BLAND, FINANCIAL CRIME VICTIMS ATTORNEY: Then Alec Murdaugh goes from state prison to federal prison, to Club Fed, where it's much easier and safer to serve your time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (on camera): Eric Bland says he has no doubt Murdaugh will spend the rest of his life behind bars. The question is whether he serves time for murder, financial crimes, or both. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan Serrie in South Carolina. Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden's claim that he has led the country out of its economic crisis, and brand-new FOX polls.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM COMER, (R-KY) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: That's one of about a dozen crimes that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has committed. And ironically, that's the one crime that he committed that you cannot tie \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to.\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC SWALWELL (D-CA): It sounds like a prosecutor appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , kept by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to investigate his own, has found that there is no wrongdoing that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did. So, great.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2 , (R-SD): The initial treatment when the charges were brought seemed to be very unusual. This story is not done yet, and I think there is a lot of smoke there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hunter Biden charges, charged today, and here they are. Put them up on the screen. There are false statement in the purchase of a firearm. That's 10 years if convicted. False statement information required by federal firearms dealer, five years. Possession of a firearm by unlawful user because of drug use, it's 10 years.\par \par \uc2 The Hunter Biden attorney, Abbe Lowell, says this, "As expected, prosecutors filed charges today that they deemed were not warranted just six weeks ago following a five-year investigation into this case. The evidence in this matter has not changed in the last six weeks, but law has, and so has MAGA Republicans improper and partisan interference in this process." And obviously Republicans have a lot to say about all of that today.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Jason Riley, "Wall Street Journal" columnist, senior fellow at the Manhattan Institute, "THE FIVE" co-host Jessica Tarlov, and Tyler Pager of "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." You look at this, Jason, and what do you see in these charges? A lot of Republicans say hey, listen, where's the other part of this? Is it going to continue?\par \par \uc2 JASON RILEY, COLUMNIST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Well, what I see is what we should have seen months ago when the Biden team of lawyers was trying to strike a plea deal, which would have essentially made him immune to future prosecutions of his overseas dealings. And that's what we really want to know with respect to 2024 and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's role in all of this, if in fact, he has had a role. So he was sort of getting a sweetheart deal before. He is not any longer, or so it seems. But an indictment is not a conviction, Bret. We have to see how this plays out.\par \par \uc2 Here's the problem going forward for the White House. They want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be of the nominee. They want to run against him and claim that he is unfit for office because he is corrupt. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  keeps complicating that narrative for Democrats, and that's the real problem going forward with this indictment.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Tyler, your thoughts on this and what it portends, maybe, for the inquiry that's now happening on Capitol Hill?\par \par \uc2 TYLER PAGER, "WASHINGTON POST": Yes, look, the White House was well- prepared for this indictment to come. Special Counsel David Weiss filed court papers earlier this month signaling that this was coming. But obviously, the day that it lands is a different day at the White House. The president found out, was briefed by senior aides just before leaving the White House to give an economic speech in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 And as you said House Republicans are hoping that this is just the tip of the iceberg, that this gives more momentum to their investigation into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . But as we heard from members of Congress, this particular has no link to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Republicans are trying, they have not yet presented evidence that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  benefited at all from \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business dealings, but I think this is part of a larger effort by Republicans to really tie the president to his son and to some of his son's struggles with addiction and the like. Whether or not they are successful in doing so I think remains to be seen. And the impeachment inquiry, as we know, is just getting started.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And obviously Comer and Jordan have a long list of things that they tie directly to then Vice President Biden, and we will see where the money trail goes.\par \par \uc2 Jessica, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " opinion has "2024's Field of Nightmares. In running Biden for reelection, Democrats are making a fateful bet that the successful management can simply continue through two sets of risks, the high stakes of next election in which a health crisis or just more slippage might be the thing that puts Trump back in the White House, and the different, but also substantial stakes, of another four-year term."\par \par \uc2 You add this to the other write-ups. You add this to the "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " and David Ignatius. You add this to the new FOX polls out tonight that shows President Biden losing not only to former President Trump, but other candidates in the Republican field who are trailing the former president by some 50 points, actually. But there you see the matchups if you are voting for president right now. How do you see that, Jessica?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, CO-HOST, "THE FIVE": Well, it's going to be close, as all elections, or recent elections, have been. That is the expectation, and that's how Democrats are running this election. You can't take any vote for granted, especially when you look at the margins in 2020, and considering who it was that Democrats were able to pick off from the Republican side to win, namely white suburban voters, increase the Gen Z turnout, and then also a big increase in Asian-American voters.\par \par \uc2 It's going to be tight. All the polls are within the margin of error, which is where the exception is. But obviously some memo went out that there should be a lot of op-eds and a lot of reporting this week about concerns over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's age. And I will point out, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was interviewed yesterday about it. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was interviewed this morning about it. Jamie Raskin was interviewed about it this afternoon. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was the only one who gave a full throttled defense of the entirety of the Biden-Harris ticket. And that is something that certainly is sending a bit of a smoke signal up, at least to me as an analyst, saying what is going on? Jamie Raskin and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  do know how to answer a straightforward question. Why did they not defend Vice President Harris in the way that you would expect?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was striking. I agree with you. Former House Speaker Pelosi I think was asked three times about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as vice president. And it was, it was weak.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It was a dodge.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, saying it's the president's choice.\par \par \uc2 But Jason, if you look at the GOP field, and I mentioned it here about the polls, our latest poll has the former president well up in the polls. If he is not in the race, Governor DeSantis leads -- there you see the 60 percent. Over 13, and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has gone down. In this poll, if the former president is not in the race, but he is in the race, DeSantis is at 33, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  at 31.\par \par \uc2 Here is another one. Favorable opinion since the debate, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has gone from 78 to 82. He wasn't in the debate, Jason, but his favorability has gone up and DeSantis has gone down. What do you make of this GOP race?\par \par \uc2 RILEY: Yes, this is exactly what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted. He wanted a crowded field. And that is what he has right now. You have people who have been in the race for months still polling in the mid to low single digits, won't get out and let Republican voters consolidate around a Trump alternative. And so \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is benefiting from this, as we would expect him to.\par \par \uc2 And we'll see if the people still running in the Republican nomination have second thoughts about what they should do. But right now, the crowded field is benefiting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and that is what we saw in 2016, and that's what we are seeing again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, we'll see who makes the stage the next debate September 27th with Dana and Stuart and Ilia. Thank you, panel.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally tonight, today's throwback.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Two-hundred-nine years ago, Francis Scott Key penned "The Star- Spangled Banner." The anthem was originally written as a poem titled " Defense of Fort McHenry" after he witnessed the British bombardment of the fort during the War of 1812. The attack proved unsuccessful. The sight of a lone American flag triumphantly flying at sunrise inspired Key to write the famous patriotic lines. It was adopted as the National Anthem in 1931. There you go, a little history.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we'll look at whatever happened to COVID relief money. Remember that? Please remember, you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast. Plan to join us next week. I will be in Saudi Arabia Tuesday and Wednesday and feature my interview with Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230915ej9e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Autoworkers Strike Imminent?; Interview With Gov. \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  (R- ND); Interview With Rep. Nicole Malliotakis (R-NY); Hunter Biden Charged With Gun Violations\b0\par\par\uc2 Chad Pergram, Charlie Gasparino, Kelly O'Grady, Hillary Vaughn,  Jacqui Heinrich, Andy McCarthy, David Spunt, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,817 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, well, you knew it was coming. Some wondered whether it would ever come. Today, it did, three gun charges filed today against \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  by special counsel David Weiss.\par \par \uc2 The charges could carry a maximum penalty, in terms of time behind bars, of up to 25 years. That is unlikely. But, again, this entire scenario with a plea bargain that sort of fell apart a little more than a month ago also seems very unlikely. But it is what it is.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt at the Justice Department with more.\par \par \uc2 Hey, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, you mentioned those three felony charges.\par \par \uc2 The first two are false statements surrounding the purchase of the gun. The final count, the third count here, third felony count, is actual possession of the gun. We got this word just several hours ago from special counsel David Weiss, who also happens to be the U.S. attorney in Delaware, who was just negotiating a plea deal with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  back on July 26 that fell apart famously in court.\par \par \uc2 This all comes from a 2018 time period where \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  lied on a federal form to purchase a gun, when he said he was not addicted to stimulant, narcotic drugs and any other controlled substance, when, in fact, according to the indictment, he knew that that statement was false.\par \par \uc2 The second charge is that false statement made to an employee of the gun shop in Delaware. And the third is actually owning the gun when unlawfully using illegal substances, as you mentioned, 25 years total for count one, up to 10 years, $250,000 fine, up to five years for count two, same fine, up to 10 years, same fine for count three.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and DOJ really hoped to avoid this day, Neil, as I mentioned, but this plea deal fell apart in a very public way when the government, back in July, refused to guarantee that, after signing the deal, Hunter would be immune from any future prosecutions.\par \par \uc2 His attorney wanted it. DOJ said, sorry, that's not how it works. Now, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's attorney, Abbe Lowell, is pushing forward with a diversion agreement, part of the original plea deal that would help Hunter avoid jail time. Lowell says it's valid because it was signed by both parties. The special counsel says it's void, especially now we have this indictment.\par \par \uc2 We just got this lengthy statement from Abbe Lowell. Want to read just part of it. He says: "\uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  possessing an unloaded gun for 11 days was not a threat to public safety, but a prosecutor with all the power imaginable bending to political pressure presents a grave threat to our system of justice. We believe these charges are barred by the agreement the prosecutor has made with Mr. Biden, the recent rulings by several federal courts, and this statue is unconstitutional, and the facts that he did not violate that law, and we plan to demonstrate all of that in court."\par \par \uc2 Neil, very important to point out, this today is dealing with the gun charge, the felony gun charge. Remember, part of that plea dealt with two misdemeanor tax violations. David Weiss indicated that he could refile and possibly bring an indictment on those tax charges in either Washington, D.C., or California, where \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  lives. Stay tuned -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How likely -- I'm sorry, David.\par \par \uc2 How likely is that part of it to address again the tax issues?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Well, David Weiss said it in a court filing.\par \par \uc2 The special counsel's office, to be more accurate, in a court filing from August 13, just read it a few moments ago..\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: ... said that it's likely that the tax issue or the tax violations would go to trial and the proper venue would be in the District of Columbia or California.\par \par \uc2 Now, is it possible he doesn't bring those charges? It's possible, but the fact that it was mentioned is quite telling, and it's also possible Hunter Biden could face other charges we're not aware of. David Weiss is a special counsel. He can go wherever he wants. He can file wherever he wants, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, David, thank you. Great reporting, as always.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jacqui Heinrich at the White House, where, Jacqui, it almost sounds like operation ignore so far on this. What's the latest?\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That's right, Neil. It continued in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 The president just wrapped up a major economic address. He did not mention the news that had just broke at the very beginning of his remarks. And at the end, he only really alluded to the news saying: I wish I had time to take all of your questions, but I'd get in real trouble if I did that," not directly mentioning \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , but certainly a reference to all of our burning questions that we have still been waiting to get answers on.\par \par \uc2 Also worth noting that the vice president ignored a question on the indictment as she headed to New York. Officially, the White House is referring all of our questions to the White House -- excuse me -- to the DOJ and to \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's lawyers, who have already weighed in, as David Spunt mentioned.\par \par \uc2 And, really, the only concrete answer that we have gotten about how the president might handle his son's case as it continues to evolve came in the press briefing back in July. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: From a presidential perspective, is there any possibility that the president would end up pardoning his son?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: No.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Well...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I just said no. I just answered.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: For as hands-off as the White House is making out the president to be, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's attorneys have suggested in court that Biden would be incredibly hands-on.\par \par \uc2 Back in August, Politico reviewed some 300 pages of communications between prosecutors and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's legal team as they drew closer to bringing this gun charge, in which Hunter's now former attorney, Chris Clark, warned that if the DOJ were to charge the first son, his lawyers would put the president on the stand.\par \par \uc2 They write -- quote -- "President Biden now unquestionably would be a fact witness for the defense in any criminal trial." Clark also pushed for that plea deal, arguing that a trial would create political and constitutional chaos that would pit the president against his own Department of Justice, writing -- quote -- "This of all cases justifies neither the spectacle of a sitting president testifying in a criticism trial, nor the potential for a resulting constitutional crisis."\par \par \uc2 In White House statements and interviews, the president has treaded lightly on this issue. He has repeated that he loves his son. However, in the past, he has drawn criticism for saying that he's confident his son did nothing wrong. His critics pointing to that as an improper signal to the DOJ not to bring a charge.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the landscape has evolved since then. Charges were brought, the plea deal was arranged, then fell apart, and now we are here. And the White House is trying to continue to keep the president's focus and the American people's focus on what he's doing to govern and separate all of this business with his son to something else, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He can't do that that forever, though, right?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Well, they're going to try.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There you go.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jacqui, great job.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Appreciate that, Jacqui Heinrich.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to John Yoo, the former deputy assistant attorney general. We have also got Katie Cherkasky here, former federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 Guys, thank you.\par \par \uc2 John, if I could begin with you. I know two of these counts are punishable by up to 10 years in prison, the third one up to five years upon conviction. That, I would imagine, would be unlikely. But what do you think?\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: I agree, Neil. I don't think it's likely that we're going to see Hunter Biden spend 25 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, usually, the Justice Department tries to treat pretty toughly people who lie to the government. These are called process crimes. They're sometimes seen by people as unimportant. But they're actually the cornerstone of our justice system because we have got to rely on witnesses and people to tell us the truth in order to have an operating system of justice.\par \par \uc2 So I think the Justice Department is not likely to offer \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  a sweet plea deal again. They might even go to trial, but, if Hunter is smart, he might try to plead this down to some jail time in order to, right, avoid anything like a 25-year sentence.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think, Katie, he's looking at any jail time? I'm taking leaps here, but I'm just curious to get your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 KATIE CHERKASKY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Well, there's so many different paths that this charge can go down, because, essentially, at this point, we're at square one. There's been an indictment issued. It is still possible that Hunter could cut some sort of deal with the DOJ.\par \par \uc2 It is possible that he decides that he wants to press forward to trial. It's also possible, as Mr. Lowell, Hunter's attorney, has alleged, that the plea agreement is revived somehow, the original agreement. That's what Hunter's side is arguing at this point. So it's really hard to say, but I think, ultimately, there's a possibility that he will in fact face jail time for these offenses if they go straight to trial.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, we do know guys as well -- and, John, I will ask you first about this -- that his attorneys have indicated they would fight any additional charges filed against their client. But how likely is that additional charges could be?\par \par \uc2 YOO: First, we should recognize that this gun charge really has almost nothing to do with what should concern us as members of the public and the Justice Department, if it's really looking out for the public interest, is, was \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  engaged in influence peddling?\par \par \uc2 Was he receiving broads from overseas? Was he somehow bringing his father into it, whether he was vice president or after he was vice president, whether there's any obstruction of justice at the Justice Department now in this very prosecution while Biden's been president.\par \par \uc2 None of this, I think, is touched by the gun charge. And I think, as you just heard in the report earlier, the U.S. attorney, the special counsel has said in court that he's conducting that. I think it would actually be incredible if the U.S. attorney were not conducting further investigations and possibly thinking about bringing charges about all those other issues, which, again, have nothing to do with this gun charge.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Katie, what's fascinating here is, this was almost wrapped up and tied with a bow back in July when what was supposed to be a perfunctory plea deal sort of fell apart before a judge who was simply asking very simple questions, and it all unraveled.\par \par \uc2 It makes you think, had that not unraveled, none of this would be going on, right?\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: Well, that's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 And I think plea deals are not final until the judge signs off on them. And we have to remember, in this case, the judge had several different contentions with this deal. One was that the deal provided Hunter the sweeping immunity for all future offenses, including potentially all these finance offenses and foreign agent offenses that some people think may very well be on the table.\par \par \uc2 And in addition to that, she took issue with the fact that the deal required her to be a fact-finder. So the deal was really bunk on two grounds, according to the judge, who's the ultimate decision-maker in that respect.\par \par \uc2 But you're absolutely right. This could have been wrapped up. What's also stunning is that this is a crime of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's own making. He published this information in his book regarding his drug use and the timing of such just when he bought that gun. So, certainly, it's all quite spectacular what we have been seeing play out from this.\par \par \uc2 But it's far from over.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: John, let me ask you a little bit about that plea deal that fell apart here. It fell apart because, obviously, the two sides, presumably, before the judge would have agreed on the broad parameters of a plea deal, obviously prosecutors not too keen on how it ended up, but you could just also start saying, all right, what happens next if they try to cop a deal on this, or can they?\par \par \uc2 YOO: Yes, that's a really interesting point you have made, and actually ties in interestingly with the impeachment investigation, because the more the prosecutors offer \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  a sweet deal, the more it looks like he got away with something, the more the House is going to investigate the Justice Department and ultimately President Biden for interfering with the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Because the more a Hunter gets away with stuff, the more it looks like there's been obstruction of justice within the Justice Department. And so the ironic thing here, Neil, is that the incentive now is for the Justice Department to be tough on Hunter in order to deflect the impeachment investigation that's now been launched in the House.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just wow.\par \par \uc2 Guys, thank you both very, very much.\par \par \uc2 We are going to be getting more reaction to this. Again, on Capitol Hill, it was fast and furious, even from the former President himself \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  weighing in on these matters a short time ago. We will bring you up to date on that.\par \par \uc2 A quick peek at the corner of a Wall and Broad right now. This is one of the best days we have seen in about a month for the Dow Jones industrials, sprinting ahead despite a stronger-than-expected, by that, I mean a more worrisome-than-expected wholesale inflation report.\par \par \uc2 But we had an IPO today, an initial public offer, of something called Arm. That is a British chip design company. It debuted today and came out the gate a lot stronger than folks had thought. Suffice it to say, the stock ran up today better than 14 percent. Not bad for a new offering, the strongest of the year.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street interpreted that as a sign, the market is back, the water is fine, dive in. And, today, they did.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the indictment coming down, then the Capitol Hill reaction, as far as lawmakers gathering together to speak out about it, that's just starting and it's going on as we speak.\par \par \uc2 Hillary Vaughn on Capitol Hill with more.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, House Democrats we talked to have really basically used this indictment of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  to point out that they think it shows that President Biden is not showing favoritism, is not using his position to give his son special treatment, because this indictment has come down.\par \par \uc2 They're also pointing out at every opportunity from House Democrats we have talked to this afternoon that he was indicted by a Trump-appointed prosecutor, and the fact that President Biden kept him in his position shows that he is letting the system of justice play out for his son.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): I don't think people should applaud the system when it works for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , but then try to tear the system down when it works for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC SWALWELL (D-CA): President Biden has credibility that he believes in equal justice under the law, and he allowed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's prosecutor to stay on board and investigate his own son. And you're not going to see one Democrat here losing their minds, saying we need to burn down the whole government because a president's family member is being investigated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Republicans also are not overjoyed by this indictment. They think it's too little and too late.\par \par \uc2 They also say they will not be satisfied until \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is charged for everything that they think he is guilty of.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): Where are the charges on human sex trafficking, FARA charges, money laundering, tax evasion? I think there should be a lot more coming.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): That's one of about a dozen crimes that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's committed. And, ironically, that's the one crime that he committed that you cannot tie \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to. So we will see what happens with the other 11 or so crimes that the president's son's committed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And, Neil, Republicans are still insistent that their impeachment inquiry continues, that this indictment has no impact either way on that.\par \par \uc2 They want to continue their investigation into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and President Biden's potential ties to those business deals and other activities -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for that, Hillary Vaughn.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we have Congresswoman Nicole Malliotakis on all of this, sits on the House of Ways and Means Committee.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 REP. NICOLE MALLIOTAKIS (R-NY): Well, I think this is just the tip of the iceberg. I mean, we have said all along that we knew that there were multiple crimes that had been committed.\par \par \uc2 And, quite frankly, I don't think these charges would have come forward today had our committee been exposing what was going on in terms of influence peddling, the fact that there were 20 shell companies that received $20 million that went to nine members of the Biden family.\par \par \uc2 That is truly what we are having this impeachment inquiry about to determine, what did they do in exchange for that money? And I just think that this has woken up the DOJ and says, we have got to do something here to try to divert attention, but to show that we're being serious, because, all along, they were cutting a sweetheart plea deal for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  as it related to those other crimes.\par \par \uc2 