nomic policies that allow us to win the cold war with a strong economy and through innovation.\par \par \uc2 That's what we should be focusing on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, that impeachment stuff...\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: But, instead, every day that these -- every...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That impeachment stuff, and what they're doing right now to start an inquiry, whatever the pressures on Kevin McCarthy to do so, you think distracts from that?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I think, absolutely, they should have the inquiry...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: ... because we need to find out the answers to that.\par \par \uc2 But we just have to be careful that that's not the goal, because any time that we're focused on the past, we're not focused on the abysmal record of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on the economy, inflation right now choking every American family and energy prices in August up again.\par \par \uc2 And, at the same time, it's always on page 18, when he's having an assault on the U.S. energy policy, which is driving this inflation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know whether you will make the debate next week -- or the week after, I should say. It could be close.\par \par \uc2 Where do you think you stand?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, we have cleared every hurdle, except the national poll, and we're on track to get there as well. So we look forward to being a part of it.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, in the states where we are, there will be people on the debate stage that are behind us in states like Iowa and New Hampshire. And so we're continuing to fight forward to that. And we know that our message is getting traction with the people that are actually going to be voting in January in those early states.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In Iowa, it's interesting. You picked up a little bit of support. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost a little bit of support. So, everyone kind of jostled a little.\par \par \uc2 You would need a big shift right now to improve your chances. You have heard from those who say they want to see a great weeding out of the field, or an alternative challenger to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if it comes to that, that the party has a better chance.\par \par \uc2 Do you subscribe to that, that you have got to really do well in Iowa to get going?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I think Iowa and New Hampshire, when those two states are done, 98 percent of the Electoral College votes will still be left.\par \par \uc2 Those two states, those citizens on the ground, they come to events, they hear you one, two, three, four times, they're seeing all the candidates. That's why some candidates are falling in Iowa, because they have seen them more. Others like ourselves are going up.\par \par \uc2 We have got more days ahead of us between now and January since when we have launched our campaign. We think those states are the ones. Those voters that are paying attention, they're the ones that actually are going to decide who moves forward, not national polls, not clubhouse debate rules.\par \par \uc2 Those voters are going to decide. And, with those voters, we're getting traction.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think that a lesson from the first debate, Governor, was that you're too quiet, you're too level-headed, or you're not spastic? You obviously maybe didn't have a lot of lattes going into that, because you weren't firing off or ripping someone.\par \par \uc2 But the ones who got the press were doing that, not across the board, but that you need to bring in the octane, and then the rap against you is, you don't have it. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, we have got it. We can bring it. Maybe you didn't see, Neil, I won the award for the best presidential debate ever standing on one leg.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you did. Yes, you did.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Of course, blew my Achilles 24 hours before.\par \par \uc2 So, we're going to have a lot less pain, a lot more energy in the next debate when we're there. And, of course, we have got a lot -- we have got a lot to say about a lot of things, because you look at the record in North Dakota, where our economy is actually just on fire here, on track to have the highest GDP in the nation, educational scores top of the list in the country.\par \par \uc2 And we have cut our budget by 27 percent, still kept all the trains running on time. And then, of course, we have got an-all-above energy strategy, and we're feeding and fueling the world.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: So, if you want -- if you want the economy of the country to work, we know how to make it work.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, on the octane thing, you don't even want to know the half of what people say about me, Governor.\par \par \uc2 So thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Very good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Likewise. Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 We're going to continue our discussion with the candidates. Nikki Haley will be my special guest on my Saturday show 10:00 a.m. Eastern time on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, I will bring you up to date on that potential strike that could be a little more than seven-and-a-half hours away. That means that all of those autoworkers, close to 150,000, will be out -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: We are the unions, the mighty, mighty union!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They are already prepared to go on strike.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They know what their assignments are. They know what gate they will be at. And they're ready to go.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: What do we want?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If called upon, we will be ready to do whatever it takes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We can't make our money, they don't need to make they money, I mean, simple as that. Man, I feel like everybody need to go on strike at the same time, and we just need to shut them all down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that could happen in about seven-and-a-half-hours.\par \par \uc2 The way things are going right now, 146,000 \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  are poised to do just that at midnight tonight if the two sides can't reconcile what we're told are gaping gaps between the pay offered by the auto companies and some of the benefits and pay that are wanted by the workers.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O'Grady following it for us out of Los Angeles -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: That's right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I mean, tensions are really running high. And I think you can hear that from the clip that you just played. So the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  claims that the offer from the three major automakers out of Detroit -- Detroit, rather, are -- quote -- "completely unacceptable."\par \par \uc2 So, right now, it's looking like this strike is at least -- one of the automakers is inevitable right now. I do want to give you some context, though, first on what they're seeking. So the union was demanding up to a 40 percent pay bump over the next few years.\par \par \uc2 There are reports that they may have given a bit on that to something more in the mid-30 percent range. They're also looking for a 32-hour workweek and some other changes to health insurance and pension benefits. If there is no deal by midnight, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  says it's ready to strike, but it will only walk out at targeted plants.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN, PRESIDENT, UNITED AUTO WORKERS: We will strike all three companies, a historic first, initially at a limited number of targeted locations that we will be announcing.\par \par \uc2 Then, based on what's happening in bargaining, we're going to announce more locals that are going to be called to stand up and strike.\par \par 