il. And it's going to be a huge issue during the political campaign. It could lead to a recession. It'll be interesting to see how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  kind of threads this needle, because, as you know, he's a Democrat. He's very pro-union, but he's got this inflation issue that he has to deal with.\par \par \uc2 And the Big Three weighing -- giving into these unions will only add to inflation. And that's a political problem for him. I mean, there's no getting around that. You could already see it in the polls. He keeps saying Bidenomics is making the economy better, but he keeps scoring very low on an approval polls on the economy.\par \par \uc2 So I think this puts the White House in a real pickle. Obviously, they will cave into the unions, because that's what they do.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, we will see. And, to your point, I mean, a wholesale inflation report that was a lot stronger than people thought.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that's the level before it gets to us at the retail level.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Having said that though, it's interesting, because there have been a lot of generous deals for striking workers or those who threaten to strike, whether those at Chrysler, who, again, working through Shawn, were able to extract some very big raises for themselves.\par \par \uc2 We have seen American, United, some of these other airlines that have very hefty pay package increases for pilots, flight attendants, that sort of thing, many of them still to be ironed out. But that does get passed along. And you wish the best, hope the best for workers, but to think that it's just the companies paying it, we forget that.\par \par \uc2 So I'm just wondering what the spillover effect is here.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I mean, Americans are going to look at higher car prices, no doubt, just in the face of a strike, but even more potentially after one, right?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 . Don't forget the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  just got a great deal out of UPS.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Listen, a tight labor market gives some power to organized labor. There's no doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 But it also impacts consumers. And the question is, is now the time to be to be demanding this economically? I mean, and what is the spillover effect? Yes, they feel like they have the wind at their back because it's a tight labor market.\par \par \uc2 But if you're \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  here, you have got to be worried about union unrest, much more -- pay hikes coming in with all these unions, which these folks get paid pretty well to begin with, let's face it. This is not like coal mining in the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But your point about the president is a sound one. He can't not, not say good things about the union workers and that they deserve the best, and knowing full well that he's got to keep them loyal, right?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes, and he was trying to get the -- if you go back and read some of the subsidies that they were trying to throw at the big -- at the big car companies for E.V.s, he was trying to get them on board with this by giving them a backdoor subsidy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I mean, that's something that he's been trying to do.\par \par \uc2 So, clearly, he understands the situation here politically. But, economically, this is not a good thing, because I am telling you that, with all this labor unrest, that's going to lead to higher prices of goods during an inflationary time. And we are in -- we still have inflation here.\par \par \uc2 And let's just say the Fed starts raising rates again.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I mean, how is that going to -- I mean, that will impact markets. That will impact the economy.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is entering into very treacherous territory, so to speak.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Charlie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino.\par \par \uc2 None of that evidenced today at the corner of Wall and Broad, where, despite that stronger-than-expected inflation report, the performance of a new company that debuted today, \uc2 Arm Holdings\uc2  -- of course, it's a very big in the chip design business, a British concern -- did very, very well. And a lot of people are saying that that's the wind that's at their back, and they will worry about the inflation stuff a little later.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, Chad Pergram was sort of teeing up the fallout from the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  news today and what it could mean for the impeachment inquiry efforts on the part of many in the House. He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the House had already moved or started the at least initial things you have to do to move on an impeachment inquiry that many say could lead to an ultimate move to -- move to get the president impeached. It's not quite there yet, but then, of course, this \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  news.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram has more on how all of this is moving along on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 This is unprecedented for a presidential family member. And that's why there are so many questions about how this impacts the 2024 presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 It's possible questions about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  will dog the president on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): I think folks may want to politicize it, but, if anything, an indictment is a demonstration that our judicial process is proceeding under its current structures.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: This comes as House Speaker Kevin McCarthy is facing an uprising from conservatives who may want to show him the door.\par \par \uc2 Impeachment fails to impress those members. They're mad that McCarthy wants to pass a stopgap spending bill to avert a shutdown without immediate cuts. McCarthy hammered his opponents behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I am frustrated with the committee. I'm frustrated with some people in the conference.\par \par \uc2 We had the DOD appropriation bill yesterday. I couldn't put it on the floor. I don't have one complaint by any member of what's wrong with this bill. I'm going to continue to just focus on what's the right thing to do for the American people. And you know what? If it takes a fight, I will have a fight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Some conservatives do not believe McCarthy has done what he promised following the 15-round speaker election in January, the longest election for speaker since 1859.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy loyalists defended the speaker.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA (R-CA): What you have to do, and I would hope that you do, is question those individuals who never voted for the speaker, never explained why they would never vote for the speaker, and now would imply that somehow the speaker hasn't kept every promise he made.\par \par \uc2 You probably know that the speaker said, look, if you want to make a motion to vacate the chair, bring it on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: McCarthy says the only way to avoid a shutdown is to approve a 30- or 60-day interim spending bill, which simply renews old funding. The deadline is October 1 -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for that, Chad Pergram.\par \par \uc2 Again, more on this Hunter Biden being indicted on these gun charges and the ramifications that, where all of this is going.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt has more now from the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 Hey, David.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Now we wait to see what happens when it comes to potential other charges for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . Sure, this came out today that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has been indicted on these federal gun charges. There are three counts. He was indicted today for making two false statements, according to special counsel David Weiss.\par \par \uc2 He was indicted for making a false statement, a written false statement, when he said that he was not addicted to narcotics or other illegal drugs when purchasing a gun on a firearm. The special counsel says that he lied to an employee at the gun shop in Delaware where he purchased the gun. And then the third count is actually possessing the gun after knowingly having filled out no when he checked that -- whether or not he was addicted to drugs.\par \par \uc2 So it's important to point out, Neil, that the original plea deal that fell apart back in July, in a very public way, I may add, also included misdemeanor tax violations. The special counsel, David Weiss, indicated in an August 11 court filing that he was likely to bring up those violations again in the future, meaning potentially an indictment, and file charges either in California, where \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  lives, or the District of Columbia, where he lived prior in the 2014-2015 range.\par \par \uc2 So the bottom line is that the gun charge is just one indictment that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  may be facing, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How likely is that?\par \par \uc2 And David Weiss, of course, criticism, as you have reported better than anyone else, I mean, he was criticized for going slow, and now the move here, but then to pursue that later. What does your gut tell you? What are your sources telling you?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Well, it's important to point out that Weiss waited some five years.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: It's been five years since this gun incident. It was October 2018. We're coming up next month on five years since this gun was purchased and this form was filled out in Wilmington, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 Statute of limitations is next month, so he got this in right before the statute of limitations. With response to your question about the taxes, it's anybody's guess. However, Weiss made it a point to put in a court filing that the proper venue for future charges for the tax would be in California or Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 We know that 2014 and 2015 tax years are off the table because of the statute of limitations. But when you start getting into 2017, 2018, 2018, it's a possibility we may see that.\par \par \uc2 And, Neil, you also have the issue of -- or -- I don't want to say the issue, but you also have the element of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's attorney Abbe Lowell announcing today, taking direct aim at David Weiss, saying that he's a political prosecutor that basically caved to pressure and says that he's going to go forward assuming that this diversion agreement on the gun charge, which would have allowed Hunter to avoid jail time, is in fact valid, because it was signed by both parties.\par \par \uc2 DOJ says it's not valid because they indicted Hunter, but you have a very curious situation here where Hunter's team is operating as if this diversion agreement was valid -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That seems to ring a bell.\par \par \uc2 David, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: David Spunt at the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 To Andy McCarthy right now, the former assistant U.S. attorney.\par \par \uc2 And, Andy, it's very clear through the course of this broadcast, I found out just how clear, that Republicans are not entirely satisfied with just the Hunter Biden gun charges. I think one had said it's a very small start. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 ANDY MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think that's true, Neil, because it's the only charge that this prosecutor could have brought against Hunter in which Hunter's father is not implicated.\par \par \uc2 Everything else, the most important conduct in the case, all involves the sale of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's political influence. That is still out there, hasn't been brought, and, as David just pointed out, it's been slow-walked, so that even if he started to bring tax cases now, he made sure, Weiss did, that the three years in which \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was vice president in the Obama administration, those years are no longer open to be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 Charges arising out of that have now been time-barred.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So let's say we get -- these progress, and he is found guilty of one or more of these gun charges. Altogether, they could land him in the slammer for 25 years.\par \par \uc2 I think I caught you earlier saying that is very, very unlikely. But, again, the president is also his father. And as you also pointed out, if you have the power of the president of the United States to pardon your son and prevent him from serving years behind bars, that's a tough choice, right?\par \par \uc2 A. MCCARTHY: It wouldn't be a tough choice for me. I would do what I had to do to keep my sons out of prison. I would also resign after I did it.\par \par \uc2 But I would not underestimate what the Biden family is going through. I feel like I'm on the other political side, but this is a very wrenching human problem.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And if you think about it to that point, you have the power to prevent your son going through that. And yet, in doing that, in exercising that power, depends on when you exercise it, of course, all bets are off.\par \par \uc2 A. MCCARTHY: Yes, it's a tremendous abuse of power if it's done.\par \par \uc2 I'd also point out, Neil, that Hunter is not the only Biden family member who is implicated in this investigation. So, it's -- the fact that it's Hunter is a big deal, but it goes beyond him. It transcends him in important ways.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, that's a very human way of looking at it, Andy.\par \par \uc2 If I could step back, there was a great deal of criticism of David Weiss and how he's handle this, and how he let things go on and on and on. It seems that he reasserted himself here, but is it your sense that it's too little too late?\par \par \uc2 A. MCCARTHY: Yes, I think he's destroyed the case. The gun charges were a layup, and he tried to make those go away. The only reason we have gun charges today is because of a federal judge, not because of David Weiss.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that whole thing falling apart, the plea deal just weeks ago, that proved just so surprising. It's re-sort of shifted the grounds and the earth, hasn't it?\par \par \uc2 A. MCCARTHY: Well, it sure has.\par \par \uc2 They had a podium set up outside the courthouse, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 A. MCCARTHY: They were planning to have a press conference to announce that the whole thing was done and behind them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I remember. Incredible.\par \par \uc2 Andy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A. MCCARTHY: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You get the human view from you, the lawyer view, and it's very interesting.\par \par \uc2 Andy McCarthy following all of that.\par \par \uc2 We will stay on top of this as well.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, leaving you here with a Dow that was sprinting ahead. It's as if traders didn't care about any of this. They were more interested in money, money, money, money. Such is their life.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230915ej9e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 New York Crime; Heat Wave; Mental Competency Tests For Politicians?; Update on Health of \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 ; Migrant Crisis; Interview With Rep. \uc2 Nicole Malliotakis\uc2  (R-NY); Autoworkers Strike Imminent?\b0\par\par\uc2 Bryan Llenas, Kelly O'Grady, Adam Klotz, Chad Pergram, Jeff Flock,  Edward Lawrence, Alexandria Hoff, Charles Payne \par \uc2 8,226 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Labor Day may be over, but President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is facing a big labor problem, as the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  threaten to go on strike in nine days.\par \par \uc2 And with no deal in sight, could a strike send those already pricey vehicles even higher and create more inflation headaches for Americans and the president? A new poll finding the majority of voters feel the economy is worse than it was two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 To Alexandria Hoff in Washington, where the president is feeling the heat for downplaying the strike threat -- Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department said today it's keeping close watch on these talks. But, as you mentioned, there's only nine days left for the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  and Detroit's Big Three to agree to a new four-year deal. If they don't, the union's 146,000 members have voted to strike.\par \par \uc2 And the union's demands are admittedly bold. Members are seeking a 46 percent pay raise, 40 hours of pay for 32-hour workweeks, also for traditional pensions to be restored and cost of living increases. Workers argue that the companies have raked in billions on their backs.\par \par \uc2 And, according to Reuters, a \uc2 General Motors\uc2  executive said that the demands have -- quote -- "significant costs attached that would threaten manufacturing momentum." It's clear the two sides are still wide apart.\par \par \uc2 But while in Philadelphia yesterday for Labor Day, President Biden said he is not so concerned.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Mr. President, are you worried about an auto strike?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No, I'm not worried about a strike until it happens. I don't think it's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Says that he doesn't think it's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2 's president said he was shocked by this reaction. Quote: "He must know something we don't know. Maybe the companies plan on walking in and giving us our demands on the night before. I don't know."\par \par \uc2 This all comes at a time when Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  are dealing with some serious price competition from foreign automakers. At the same time, there's the Biden administration's push for electric vehicles, which are more simply assembled, therefore require less labor to produce.\par \par \uc2 So, while the president said that he does not think a strike is going to happen, other industry experts do, feeling that workers are encouraged by the strike in Hollywood and the recent win by UPS drivers. By the way, Charles, a 40-day strike by autoworkers in 2019 cost GM alone nearly $4 billion.\par \par \uc2 We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Alex, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the White House defending President Biden's handling of the economy, this as a new poll suggests that Americans just aren't buying it.\par \par \uc2 Want to bring in FOX Business' \uc2 Edward Lawrence\uc2  who's at the White House, with this part of the story -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Charles, Americans are just being bombarded with the successes of President Biden's administration.\par \par \uc2 However, advisers are picking and choosing the time frames, for example, of these successes. They're also hearing this on TV. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Today, inflation is down to 3 percent, unemployment the lowest in decades. There's more to do, but President Biden is getting results that matter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: They're desperately trying to change the poll numbers showing 63 percent of people see the economy as not so good or poor, while 37 percent see it as good or excellent.\par \par \uc2 Still, the White House economic adviser is telling voters to live in the real world.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, CHAIR, WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: I got to live in the real world, in the real history. And in the real world, real wages are beating prices. That is a very welcome trend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But people are living in the real world and see overall inflation up 16 percent since President Biden took office.\par \par \uc2 Food at home is up 20 percent in that same time. And now we have got another big union strike on the horizon, this one the \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: If we pass that September 14 date when the contract ends, will you then step in?\par \par \uc2 JULIE SU, ACTING U.S. SECRETARY OF LABOR: We're watching. I think it's premature to talk about what's going to happen afterwards. The contract has not expired, and the parties continue to talk to one another.\par \par \uc2 A tight labor market in which unions have the ability to demand better working conditions, better wages, we have seen historic gains in other industries like this, and we are hopeful that something can happen here too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But the last inflation indicator, the PCE inflation, actually rose.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at prices or wages, real wages, since President Biden took office, well, they're down about 3 percent, and Americans feel it -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Absolutely. Thank you very much, Edward.\par \par \uc2 So, from big labor threats to consumers still feeling inflation pain, how will this impact the 2024 race?\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in now economist Douglas Holtz-Eakin, along with Democratic strategist Laura Fink.\par \par \uc2 Doug, my man, let me start with you. There's a lot to cover here. Obviously, you do it every single day. No one's better. People are talking. I mean, the poll numbers aren't that great at all with respect to any economic data point.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, FORMER CONGRESSIONAL BUDGET OFFICE DIRECTOR: That's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 And I think what \uc2 Ed Lawrence\uc2  said at the end is the key. Overall, during the president's tenure, real wages are down. So they like to tout a very low unemployment rate. That means a lot of people in a job, but they're not getting ahead in that job. It's actually putting them further behind.\par \par \uc2 And on really sensitive items, food, energy, shelter, inflation is higher than the average. And we have seen gas prices up again this summer. None of that sounds like good news to consumers. They're making less at work and they have to spend a lot at home. So I'm not surprised about the numbers.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Laura, one of the things is, of course, we had a whole lot of stimulus under President Biden. And now we're starting to hear from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  that it's gone, that it's just about all gone.\par \par \uc2 And you're starting to feel it and see it in other areas as well. How concerned should the White House be, not necessarily about today's numbers, but the numbers that could be on the horizon?\par \par \uc2 LAURA FINK, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, I think you're always concerned about the economy if you're the president.\par \par \uc2 But I think the strong unemployment numbers, the fact that real wages are making a comeback, and we see with these wins by unions -- every single week, it seems, there's another union getting a big pay increase. Airline pilots increased their pay by 46 percent. UPS workers eliminated a two-tier system that treated workers like second-class citizens, got air conditioning in their vehicles, and got pay raises.\par \par \uc2 This increases the real wages as well. And with 71 percent of Americans approving of labor unions, they are supportive of the working-class community that is fighting for these hard-earned wages. They believe, as most Americans do, that these record-breaking profits from the auto industry and beyond should be shared with the men and women who are assembling those cars and who are working to put food on the table for their families.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I can see you didn't see Ford's last quarterly earnings.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FINK: Oh, I saw \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 ', though, and I saw that...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FINK: ... was up by a billion dollars over their estimates.\par \par \uc2 So they're doing pretty well, record profits.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Go ahead, Doug.\par \par \uc2 Not record profits.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead, Doug.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: So, these are big -- these are big -- these are big cost increases that will have to get passed onto consumers.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: And so this is Bidenomics in action. A narrow few of a favored classes get ahead. Everyone else pays higher prices. They go farther behind.\par \par \uc2 That's why it's not generally popular. You have got to have something that's broad-based, real growth for everybody, and they don't have anything like that. Everything they do picks winners and losers, and there are a lot of American losers out there who are not happy about it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Laura?\par \par \uc2 FINK: We know that, with that -- workers, I will say this. Workers that are not unionized benefit from the rise in wages because of the fundamental competition.\par \par \uc2 And, as an economist, my colleague would certainly know that. So unionized wages going up improves the lots of all workers.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: But those workers have to go -- they go -- have to buy things at stores, and they have to ship things with UPS and have to buy cars.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: And they're all going to be more expensive, and they're going to not be able to afford them.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FINK: ... raise wages...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Why is that getting ahead?\par \par \uc2 FINK: ... to do it, have retirements to do it. And...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: That hasn't happened.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FINK: ... are all important.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: It hasn't happened.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FINK: ... things like their prescription drug costs.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Laura, what do you say to people now?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FINK: There's no perfect economy. Fundamentally, to argue that this one is weak or that ultimately Republicans are going to do better, you're going to have to have more evidence.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Doug.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Among the things that the Biden administration does very effectively, as you just did, is to shift the time frames to be in their favor.\par \par \uc2 They haven't lowered anybody's prescription drug prices. Be very clear, zero so far. They haven't capped the out-of-pocket for our seniors, zero so far.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: They haven't built a solar farm yet. They haven't done any of the things they claim they have done, and people properly know that. But they want credit for it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, I will tell you what we have seen, though, Laura, is that the housing market is as unaffordable as every, right?\par \par \uc2 So a young family out there can almost forget about the American dream of owning a home. Cars are the most expensive ever that they have ever been before. We're talking about basic things that Americans want that they think is almost a birthright.\par \par \uc2 FINK: Yes. And...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hey, you know what? When I get out of school, I want to be able to get a job and buy a car. There's no cars under $20,000.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, Doug is right. Once these unions win -- and they will win -- those costs are passed on. That's economics 101.\par \par \uc2 FINK: Well, here's the other 101.\par \par \uc2 In order to tamp down inflation, you do need to raise interest rates. Paul Volcker knew that when he worked for \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  in the '80s, and now we see the same thing here.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But where did inflation come from, though? That's -- you brought up a great point. Where did that -- where did runaway inflation come from overnight from zero to 9 percent? Where did it come from?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: The American Rescue Plan.\par \par \uc2 FINK: But the other part of my point is that, in order to raise -- you raise the inflate -- you raise -- you fight inflation by raising interest rates. And that does have short-term pain.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's why you're seeing the numbers that you're seeing on Biden's approval on the economy. However, if you look just one tab down, his likely opponent, President Trump, has numbers that are just as dark.\par \par \uc2 So I think you sense the frustration in the American people and in -- among workers, and they're taking the fight into their own hands. And I think it's a good thing. It's a good thing to raise wages and preserve retirements for people working a life for a company.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: You can't have it both ways. You can't have it both ways. The low unemployment rate, the rapidly rising labor costs are part of an overstimulated economy that traces the American Rescue Plan, forced the Fed's hand for record increases in rates.\par \par \uc2 You don't get credit for the unemployment rate when it is a symptom of the problems created by overstimulus and bad fiscal policy.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FINK: Well, what does Biden get credit for? Anything? Does he get credit for averting the five strikes that...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You get credit for the old college try.\par \par \uc2 We got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 FINK: Well, what is the solution?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, I'd love to bring this up again, but we have run out of time for this particular segment. Obviously, it's a conversation that we will pick up.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, if you happen to hit the road this weekend, folks, you probably did notice something, that price at the pump. Well, now gas prices may be heading higher, the price of crude oil climbing sharply today, in part because of a spike coming from Saudi Arabia and Russia, both announcing, coincidentally, they're going to extend their oil production cuts until the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next: scorching temperatures blanketing the nation, delaying some back-to-school plans. How long will this heat wave last?\par \par \uc2 But, first, the Republican congresswoman who is leading the fight against the Biden administration over its handling of the migrant crisis. And now a top New York Democrat says she's right.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Malliotakis\uc2  is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The migrant crisis is extending from New York to New Jersey, as the Biden administration is looking to potentially house thousands of asylum seekers at the Atlantic City airport.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business correspondent Jeff Flock is there -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Charles, not exactly the busiest airport in America, but it could get a whole lot busier if some folks have their way.\par \par \uc2 It is one of 11 sites that the Biden administration has given to New York officials to try and alleviate that crush of migrants in New York City. As you know, literally, tens of thousands of them have been bused to New York from Texas.\par \par \uc2 Others have made their way to New York on their own, and the city is now just about tapped out. They're ready to cry uncle on this, saying they need to send them somewhere. This, as I said, one of 11 places that they potentially could go, along with an \uc2 IRS\uc2  office in Long Island, another Upstate New York airport.\par \par \uc2 No decisions have been made on it, but it really has -- well, it has gotten a lot of blowback here in Atlantic County, New Jersey. A lot of people think it's just not doable. And that includes the Democratic governor of New Jersey, \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2 , who is famous for saying New Jersey is a sanctuary state.\par \par \uc2 But he says, when it comes to sending maybe 60,000 migrants to his state, well, I just can't do that. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 PHIL MURPHY\uc2  (D-NJ): I don't see any scenario, Eric, where we're going to be able to take in a program in Atlantic City or, frankly, elsewhere in the state.\par \par \uc2 You need scale, enormous amount of federal support, resources that go beyond anything that we can afford.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: And, Charles, Republicans here say that the governor needs to do more than just say we can't do this. He needs to get President Biden on the phone and tell his ally that this is just a nonstarter.\par \par \uc2 Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JEFF VAN DREW (R-NJ): It is time for Governor Murphy not only to say that he's against it, to go to the president and say, this is unacceptable, I will not support it.\par \par \uc2 And it's time for this governor and all of us, if they try to do it, to block it from happening in any way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: And, Charles, it's worth noting the Atlantic City Airport is not actually in Atlantic City, but is in the town of Egg Harbor, New Jersey. That has a population of about 50,000 people.\par \par \uc2 The numbers they're talking about sending would be 60,000. Do the math on that, it's one way to grow your town, I guess, but maybe not the way local folks here would like -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, yes, it's not exactly the old gold rush. That's for sure.\par \par \uc2 Thanks a lot, Jeff. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 So, New York Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Nicole Malliotakis\uc2  has been protesting New York City's leaders, as well as the Biden administration, over the handling of this migrant crisis.\par \par \uc2 And the congresswoman joins me.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, one of the things that stands out to me is the infighting amongst the Democrats. So easy to wave the banner and quote the Statue of Liberty and the grand colossus and all these sort of things when it just feels like migrants are going into these small Republican towns in Texas and the Southern border.\par \par \uc2 Now, all of a sudden, there's a lot of infighting and it's kind of a marvel to watch.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NICOLE MALLIOTAKIS\uc2  (R-NY): Yes, look, this is a case of the Democrats' rhetoric, their sanctuary policies, their open border policies finally catching up to them.\par \par \uc2 They accused Republicans who wanted simply to have secure borders and have a process in place so we know who is coming in and out of our country to be racist, to be anti-immigrants. That couldn't be further from the truth.\par \par \uc2 I represent a community that is made up of immigrants, including my own parents. And the people in my community are saying, hey, we followed the rules. We did everything right. We never asked for anything. Why are we now being subjected to pay for the housing, the food, the health care, the education of people who are coming over our border?\par \par \uc2 They're doing so illegally. As a matter of fact, half to two-thirds of the individuals who are claiming asylum are being rejected in court when they finally get their court date. So, they are taking advantage of our country's generosity. And we cannot allow that to happen, nor is it safe.\par \par \uc2 And, as you know, and your network has been reporting, we have individuals who are being smuggled by ISIS. We have individuals that are being human- trafficked by the drug cartels. Those are the individuals who are taking advantage of this system. We can't allow this to continue. It's not safe. It's not sustainable.\par \par \uc2 And it's absolutely wrong what our mayor and our governor are doing to continue to incentivize it. And the fact that Schumer won't bring up our Border Security Act really is a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Over the weekend, former New York Democratic Governor \uc2 David Paterson\uc2  said that he agrees with you that the Biden administration is botching this. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. \uc2 DAVID PATERSON\uc2  (D-NY): Shout-out goes to Congressmember \uc2 Nicole Malliotakis\uc2 , who I think explains the whole situation and how it all happened better than anybody.\par \par \uc2 And she's talking about the fact that, had they planned for this prior to sending the migrants, how the Federal Park Service and all the -- a lot of the service agencies could have prepared for it in the states where the migrants were going to be sent. Instead, they didn't do that.\par \par \uc2 She also points out that these asylum hearings are not going in the favor of the migrants. They're about 50 percent. So, if you could calculate that from the very beginning, that would have been a reason not to allow that many people to come in. And I couldn't agree more.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow, high praise.\par \par \uc2 So, with both sides criticizing the handling of this crisis in New York, why isn't the Biden administration listening?\par \par \uc2 Why isn't some -- why aren't leaders in the Democratic Party doing something about this?\par \par \uc2 MALLIOTAKIS: Look, they're obviously doing this on purpose. They're trying to disrupt our society. They're trying to disrupt our nation.\par \par \uc2 They're actually causing great havoc within communities, because, as you mentioned earlier, there's a lot of infighting over this. I wish the other Democrats from New York would have the same common sense as \uc2 David Paterson\uc2 . If you look at our mayor, he continues to misinterpret the right to shelter law, continues to insist that New Yorkers have a responsibility to house all these individuals.\par \par \uc2 Our governor, she keeps asking for more federal funds to continue to incentivize these individuals. She even wants to cut the line for Section 8, when we have citizens waiting on the list for years, veterans, domestic violence victims.\par \par \uc2 This is her priority to give these illegal immigrants the first dibs on Section 8. It is outrageous. And I blame everyone from the president down to my mayor, who continues to incentivize this and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 . Where is \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 ? Here's a guy who who's been sitting on this legislation.\par \par \uc2 He talks about democracy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 MALLIOTAKIS: But he won't let the members who were elected to represent their states to vote on H.R.2, our Border Security Act. He needs to do that right away.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So that brings up the -- something that you have been sort of controversial recently, but it's -- we have talked about this in the past, Staten Island seceding, being its own entity, its own city, if it were.\par \par \uc2 You're now making a push for that right now, right?\par \par \uc2 MALLIOTAKIS: Well, I think we have to push for it, because the reality is, we pay a higher effective tax rate than any other borough in the city of New York. Yet we are being subjected to policies that we did not vote for.\par \par \uc2 We did not vote for the mayor. We did not vote for the governor. We did not vote for the president. Yet, combined, we are suffering as a result of these policies, particularly from the city of New York. And so I think a lot of my colleagues and also residents want to -- want this reconsidered.\par \par \uc2 There was a referendum 30 years ago; 65 percent said they wanted it. But the problem is, the state legislature and the city council have to be the ones to let us go. We often call ourselves a forgotten borough. When it comes to transportation, health care, the city doesn't give us our fair share.\par \par \uc2 But if it comes to charging us a higher effective property tax rate, or when it comes to putting these shelters in our district, then, all of a sudden, they remember where Staten Island is. And so it's a real double standard. And I don't think we should be taking it anymore. We should be moving forward with looking how we can secede from the city and self- govern.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Congresswoman Malliotakis, good stuff. Thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MALLIOTAKIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, a new poll signaling warning signs for President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  over his age. Is Republican candidate Nikki Haley's call for a competency test the answer? We're going to debate that.\par \par \uc2 And the issue of age extending to Capitol Hill, where the Senate is back in session and the health of Republican Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is back in question, right, Chad Pergram?\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  mum about his freeze episodes, but an update on what it's not from the Capitol attending physician.\par \par \uc2 More on that coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  back on the Hill after his latest freezing episode. We're going to have the latest there.\par \par \uc2 Plus, scorching temps putting back the school in a bind. When will this heat wave break?\par \par \uc2 We're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Mitch is back.\par \par \uc2 Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  returning to Capitol Hill, as the Senate gets back to business, this after another freezing episode. But he's not the only senator dealing with health issues.\par \par \uc2 To FOX's Chad Pergram with more -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Good afternoon, Charles.\par \par \uc2 There are questions about \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 , the California Democrat. And McConnell did not directly address his health issues today. This was McConnell's first appearance at the Capitol since freezing during a speech in Kentucky last weekend. Instead, McConnell spoke about the need to avoid a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Now, one particular moment of my time back home has received its fair share of attention in the press over the past week, but I assure you, August was a busy and productive month for me and my staff back in the commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: McConnell did not respond to two questions I posed to him in the hall. One, would McConnell update us on his health, and, two, does he know what's afflicting him?\par \par \uc2 McConnell did not respond. However, the attending physician for the Capitol, Brian Monahan, wrote McConnell a letter today. Monahan ruled out a stroke and Parkinson's, but Monahan did not rule anything in. It's puzzling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MARC SIEGEL, FOX NEWS MEDICAL CORRESPONDENT: Could it be related to the head trauma? That's not entirely off the table. It's not a concussion he's suffering from, but it could it be some kind of post-concussion syndrome that causes this?\par \par \uc2 I think what would help us the most at this point is, he's saying he doesn't have three major things that have come up. Well, what does he have? What is this?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats welcomed McConnell back to the Senate floor, glad to see their old sparring partner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Leader McConnell will speak after me here on the floor, and I'm glad to see him back and doing well.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICHARD DURBIN\uc2  (D-IL): I was here for the presentation by the Republican leader, and I'm happy he returned.\par \par \uc2 I told him it was great to see him back, and I couldn't wait to disagree with him. And I'm sure I will have that opportunity in the near future.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: McConnell also did not respond when asked by FOX if he's nervous the freeze could happen again.\par \par \uc2 McConnell is expected to take questions from reporters tomorrow -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hey, Chad, before I let you go, you understand the rigors of this job more than anyone else. You're there. It's great to welcome him back with open arms, but this is a challenge, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 This is -- he's got one of the most important positions in our government at a very critical time for our nation.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Well, this is why getting some questions into him tomorrow and seeing what he says, when we're able to ask him directly and see what he says.\par \par \uc2 This is why I posed these questions to him in the hall. It would only take five Republican senators to call for a special meeting of the Senate Republican Conference to kind of question his leadership and perhaps have a vote behind closed doors. We don't think we're there yet.\par \par \uc2 But, again, that's the question. What happens if he freezes again on the floor, especially if we get into a crisis situation later down the road, potentially dealing with a government shutdown, or maybe, maybe even an impeachment trial?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, we know the clock ticks really quick down there when these things happen.\par \par \uc2 Chad, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, with the new Wall Street Journal poll spelling trouble for President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  over his age, is Republican candidate Nikki Haley's call for a mental competency test the way to go? The debate is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Joe, no, a new poll from The Wall Street Journal showing a majority of voters think President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should sit out the 2024 race. More troubling, two-thirds of Democrats and 70 percent of independents think that he is too old.\par \par \uc2 To FOX's Peter Doocy with the latest -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And, Charles, White House officials are talking about something today that none of them can change, President Biden's age.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Why do you think it is that, in a Wall Street Journal poll, two- thirds of Democrats think President Biden is too old to run again?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Look, here's what I know. Here's what I can speak to. I can speak to that -- a president who has wisdom. I can speak to a president who has experience. I can speak to a president who has done historic -- has taken historic action and has delivered in historic pieces of legislation.\par \par \uc2 And that's important.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: We saw President Biden entering an event today in a mask -- here's some of the video -- because White House officials tell us he's going to do that after being exposed to the COVID-positive first lady.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Since the president was with the first lady yesterday, he will be masking while indoors and around people, in alignment with CDC guidance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But then no mask in this other video as he was leaving that event. Not sure why he would need one to go in that exact way, but not to leave that little corridor.\par \par \uc2 And officials are not telling us this afternoon yet what the plan is if President Biden winds up testing positive for COVID when he is in India at the end of this week -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thank you very much, Peter.\par \par \uc2 For Nikki Haley, age is the rage. The GOP presidential candidate wants mental competency tests as part of the deal. Remember this?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Anyone serving in D.C. -- I don't care if you do mental competency tests for 50 or over.\par \par \uc2 What I'm saying is, these are people who are making decisions on our national security.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, you're 51, right, Governor?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: These are people making decisions on what happens with our spending.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, would you advocate -- you're 51, still a whole generation younger than the present occupant of the White House and the former one.\par \par \uc2 Would you be open to that for yourself?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Absolutely. Everybody should be open to that.\par \par \uc2 And there's a problem if they're not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, is this the answer?\par \par \uc2 Let's get to read from Democratic pollster Doug Schoen and GOP fund-raiser Noelle Nikpour.\par \par \uc2 Doug, let me start with you.\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FORMER BILL CLINTON POLLSTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Charles, I think the best test you can get is with voters, not with so- called medical experts. There's no clear dividing line between competent and incompetent. I don't want to politicize our medical system, nor do I want a new category of expert involved.\par \par \uc2 That being said, presidents routinely reveal their medical information. Their doctors frequently do press conferences or press releases. I think the system works well the way it is. And if people feel \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's too old and he runs, then they should vote against him or vote for him if they feel he is, in fact, competent.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Noelle, I get where Doug is coming know. I don't know if we're necessarily talking about being an expert when you see, for instance, Senator McConnell or Senator Feinstein or it's occasionally President Biden.\par \par \uc2 But it's like the old thing. We hate the bums. We always say, throw the bums out, except our bum. We always vote them back in, because they bring us bridges to nowhere. And if that's the only solution, it feels like this is a new paradigm for politics.\par \par \uc2 NOELLE NIKPOUR, REPUBLICAN FUND-RAISER: Oh, Charles, I mean, good grief. This is just absolutely laughable.\par \par \uc2 There's an elephant in the room, no pun intended. We have got Feinstein. We have got \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . We have got -- before that was Fetterman. We have got it looks like the freezing \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , who I have a lot of respect for. But there is a problem here.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people cannot deny this. And I don't really think it is an age deal, as much as I do a health issue, because I work with a lot of major donors. I have got six donors right now that are in their 80s. They're fine. I mean, they are cognitive -- they're totally clear. They are supporting all kinds of races. They're in work every day.\par \par \uc2 You have got people like \uc2 Carl Icahn\uc2 . You have got \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2 . You have got a lot of elderly people that are making big decisions. And they're totally competent and totally fine. So, not -- I want to push back on the age thing as much as I am going to go forward with the competency deal.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 NIKPOUR: And all of us are talking about it. I mean, it's the big elephant in the room. Something has got to be done about it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, Doug, do you think there's any internal pressure, I mean, amongst the party themselves, when -- because I do believe that, even though we have rubber-stamped these elections, often people go in and pull the lever for the same person over and over again, the lack of enthusiasm is going to hurt the Democratic Party, I think, with President Biden, in part because of this issue.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Well, I'd be the first one to acknowledge that that's the case, Charles.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you run a candidate who is as old as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is and with his apparent infirmities, you're going to probably and certainly potentially suffer at the polling booth on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 But, to me, the American people, the electorate are the ultimate arbiters, not some medical panel. And take \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , who I have great respect for as well. I wouldn't know how we should judge his -- quote -- "competence." He's frozen twice. He gave a good speech today, and he has been an effective leader.\par \par \uc2 So I really don't want a doctor telling us who should be our Senate minority leader or indeed our president.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, we have got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 Noelle, Doug, great seeing both of you.\par \par \uc2 All right, folks, coming up: The heat is on and back to school for many kids is off, right, Adam?\par \par \uc2 ADAM KLOTZ, FOX NEWS METEOROLOGIST: Yes, you are absolutely right; 200 million people over the next couple of days will see highs up above the seasonal average.\par \par \uc2 And get this, Charles. A hundred different cities could see record-breaking highs in the next week. I will be tracking all of that coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Scorching temperatures putting back to school on the back burner for many schoolkids today.\par \par \uc2 To FOX Weather's Adam Klotz on when relief is coming -- Adam.\par \par \uc2 KLOTZ: Yes. Charles, intense heat really stretching across the country.\par \par \uc2 So far today, 19 records have been broken, including tying one in Washington, D.C., temperatures up to 99 degrees. These are our current temps across the mid-Atlantic, stretching back into portions of the Midwest, 98 degrees in Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 It's 89 degrees in New York City. But if you add in the humidity, you get your feels-like temperatures, suddenly mid-90s, 100 degrees in Baltimore, just millions and millions and millions of folks dealing with some real extreme heat. Heat alerts are currently in place across this entire region as this heat is going to stick with us here for a little while.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's not just the mid-Atlantic. I can take you back a little farther across portions of the Midwest, the Upper Midwest still incredibly hot, Des Moines, 98 degrees, 102 currently in Kansas City.\par \par \uc2 Do pay attention to this very defined frontal boundary, though. That is cooler air that's going to kind of settle its way in. You're now looking at the early morning lows, currently only into the '70s across portions of the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 A true cold front is moving in, and, suddenly, those early morning lows are going to be dropping down into the 50s. Occasionally, a few of these spots might be seeing the 40s. So, if you live back across the Plains or the Midwest, some relief ultimately is on the way. If you live on the East Coast, it's going to take just a little bit longer.\par \par \uc2 I do want to leave you with this. It's not about the heat, but it is our next tropical system, Charles. We have got a tropical depression currently sitting out in the Atlantic. It's currently forecast to ramp up to a Category 4 hurricane. Currently, it's not forecast to make landfall, but this is still a long ways off.\par \par \uc2 It's just the next system we're going to be paying attention to in a very busy tropical season that we're currently experiencing.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Adam, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more, by the way, folks, on this story and much, download the FOX Weather app or stream FOX Weather on your favorite connected TV device.\par \par \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  grounding all of its flights for a short time today due to some equipment problems.\par \par \uc2 To Kelly O'Grady at \uc2 Los Angeles International Airport\uc2  on what we're learning -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: It's great to see you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, more flight chaos coming out of Labor Day weekend. So, as you said, \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 , they asked the \uc2 FAA\uc2  earlier today to pause their departures nationwide. On social media, they shared this message -- quote - - "We are experiencing a systemwide technology issue and are holding all aircraft at their departure airports. Flights that are already airborne are continuing to their destination as planned."\par \par \uc2 Then, shortly before 2:00 p.m. Eastern, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  confirmed the grounding was over, and United updated everyone on social media, saying So,-- quote -- "We have identified a fix for the technology issue and flights have resumed. We're working with impacted customers to help them reach their destinations as soon as possible."\par \par \uc2 Now, as of now, 350 flights have been delayed in the U.S. I will note that equates to roughly 13 percent of all United flights. And I will note that is well above competitors today on a day after the holiday weekend, when, of course, many were expected to still be traveling.\par \par \uc2 It's unclear exactly what the technical issue that caused that stop, though sources do tell me it was not cybersecurity, thankfully. Instead, federal officials reportedly shared that United crews had been unable to contact airline dispatchers through their normal means.\par \par \uc2 Now, this does come on the heels of Transportation Secretary Buttigieg's speech on Friday touting the strides that airline travel has made this year. During today's stoppage, he took the social to share that he was -- quote -- "aware of the nationwide ground stop at \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  due to I.T. issues, and the Department of Transportation will make sure united meets its obligations to affected passengers."\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, technical glitches seem to become a bit of a trend this year. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  grounded flights nationwide back in January, again in June at D.C. area airports. And Southwest, if you remember, also experienced a tech-driven ground stoppage for an hour in April.\par \par \uc2 Charles, the fallout here at least doesn't seem too bad today, but I will say it would give me trepidation if I were flying anytime soon, especially with how much airline prices are.\par \par \uc2 I will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Absolutely. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Why disturbing new video we're about to show you after the break is raising new questions about the safety of New York City subways. How was a man able to strike a woman with her own cane over 50 times?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KEMPER, NYPD TRANSIT CHIEF: It's tough to watch. It's obviously concerning and shocking to us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Disturbing new video we're about to show you is raising new worries over safety in New York City subway stations.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Llenas has the latest from the newsroom -- Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, a warning to our viewers.\par \par \uc2 The video you -- we are about to show you is disturbing and shocking. It shows a 60-year-old woman being beaten with her own wooden cane by a man at a subway station in Harlem last Friday at 3:20 in the morning. The man pummels this woman more than 50 times in the head and body with the cane before taking off his belt and whipping her with it as well.\par \par \uc2 A witness tells police this started after the man may have been helping her carry something up the subway steps, when something dropped, causing an argument. That led to the beating. The victim was taken to the hospital in stable condition. The suspect is identified as 43-year-old Norton Blake.\par \par \uc2 He's been arrested nine times before. Police say they are confident he will be arrested again shortly. Today, at a press conference, the mayor and the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  touted their efforts in bringing down city and subway crime.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEMPER: That video that's circling around on social media, that's tough to watch. It's obviously concerning and shocking to us.\par \par \uc2 Look, we're down just about 4.5 percent overall in crime year to date. That 4.5 percent translates to 66 less crimes, major crimes, this year versus last year in the subway system.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: According to \uc2 NYPD\uc2  statistics, though, there have been 287 felony assaults on the city's subways this year through June.\par \par \uc2 That's the exact number of felony assaults through the same time last year. So while the overall crime numbers on the subway are down, Charles, the more heinous attacks continue to make headlines. Just last week, a 41-year- old mother was randomly shoved under the train tracks by a stranger.\par \par \uc2 He was arrested for felony assault. She fractured her leg -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Bryan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, that always raises the same question. What needs to be done to crack down on these kind of crimes in our crime crisis?\par \par \uc2 Former New York Police Lieutenant Joe Cardinale joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Joe, the common -- the most common theme here is that recidivism -- that they have been arrested X-amount of time. And you say, well, shouldn't they already be in jail?\par \par \uc2 JOE CARDINALE, FORMER \uc2 NYPD\uc2  LIEUTENANT: Well, they should be in jail, but the problem is, the mayor can tout anything he wants. He never challenges the DAs who are responsible for revolving door justice over here.\par \par \uc2 And I have spoken to you about this many times, and we have said it before. This is -- this criminal system -- justice system is geared for the criminals, not for the everyday citizen like this woman being assaulted. This is disgusting, all right? It's disgusting to watch. And you shouldn't have to have to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 And you have somebody standing by with a camera filming this and doesn't get involved because the -- in all fairness to the people that do want to help, look what happened to the good samaritan who did jump in. He's the one who got charged. So that's what everybody's afraid of.\par \par \uc2 They're afraid of getting charged before the criminals get charged.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARDINALE: And that's what's wrong with this. It needs to be revamped.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. No, it's tough to jump in when someone's wielding a cane like that and you're intimidated to begin with, but then it's a no-win situation if somehow you end up being the bad guy.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about this migrant crisis. It's also, of course, weighing on New York police resources. The New York Post reporting that a Venezuelan migrant who's been arrested six times in just two months for violent crimes has since been sent to Rikers Island. But why did it take so long?\par \par \uc2 CARDINALE: Because, Charles, they're not identified. We don't have anything in the system for them right now. We don't have their prints on file.\par \par \uc2 We don't have anything other than they were shipped over here, and we don't know. They weren't properly vetted. They were just sent over, and they're crossing the border. This is just a touch of what's coming across the border. You don't know what the criminal records are. So now you can go back, and I'm sure they can go check with Venezuela and see what his criminal past is over there.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CARDINALE: But the problem is, they're -- he's jumping. He got progressively worse. He started off with a minor crime, shoplifting.\par \par \uc2 And then he progressed all the way up to the felony assault. And why? Because he knows he can get away with it. He's never challenged. He's never been in the system. And over here, this is a criminal's paradise right now, over in New York City. They can do anything they want and not be challenged by the police.\par \par \uc2 They are challenged by the police.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CARDINALE: But they -- it stops there. It doesn't go to the courts. It doesn't go to the DAs, nothing. It just stops with the police.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Joe, I got a minute to go. I'm just going to -- real quick, you mentioned the \uc2 Costco\uc2 . June 28, he assaulted a security guard. July 7, he was arrested for attacking a journalist, July 31, arrested for violently attacking a woman in Midtown, August 21, arrested for menacing a weapon, and, of course, August 28 for stealing bikes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it was just anything and everything. It just -- it feels like a hopeless situation.\par \par \uc2 CARDINALE: It is, until we change the laws and the people that enforce the laws.\par \par \uc2 I'm not talking about the police department.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CARDINALE: I'm talking about DAs like DA Bragg. And we need to change this.\par \par \uc2 And the only way it's going to change is when it hits home for some of these politicians.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARDINALE: That's the only way it's going to change.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARDINALE: Until then, it's going to be business as usual and the cops are going to keep getting assaulted.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Joe, thank you so much. Always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Folks, you can catch me tomorrow and, in fact, every weekday, "Making Money" 2:00 p.m. Eastern time on the \uc2 FOX Business Network\uc2 , a really dicey time for the economy.\par \par \uc2 But, for now, here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230905ej950002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 August Jobs Report Divides Democrats And Republicans; Biden White House Touts August Jobs Report; 37 Percent Of Americans Approve Of Biden Economy; Lawsuit Over Former Vice President E-Mail Addresses; Ramaswamy Brushes Off Criticism From Republican Rivals; Defense Department Releases Videos; Lawmakers Address Economic And Military Challenges; Ohio Authorities Release Body Camera Video Of Fatal Police Shooting Of Ta'Kiya Young; Controversial California Bill Would Require Business Owners To Provide Trauma Counseling To Employees Who Experience Violence During Shoplifting Incidents; Some Still Hoping For Information On Fate Of \uc2 Malaysian Airlines\uc2  Flight 370; President Biden Touts August Jobs Report; Republican Presidential Candidate Nikki Haley Announces Support Of Term Limits For Members Of Congress\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, David Spunt, Mark Meredith, Jennifer  Griffin, Kevin Corke, William La Jeunesse, Griff Jenkins \par \uc2 7,634 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES HURT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: So, in Canada, drivers near Toronto were warned this week, keep their windows shut while driving around, after crates containing millions of bees fell off the truck.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Leaving them swarming around. There were 5 million bees buzzing around the roadway. But, of course, they are went back to their queens and everything was good.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Wow!\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: One of them stung Gutfeld. That's why he's in the hospital. True story. He got stung and he's really in a lot of pain.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: But not his --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank God.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Good evening. Welcome to SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT". The job market is continuing to cool down. Employers added more jobs in August than July. But the total for June through August is the lowest three-month total in three years.\par \par \uc2 The two parties have vastly different interpretations of the figures. Republicans say approves Bidenomics is not working. The president is touting what he calls historic job growth.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Bret, jobs were added and the unemployment rate rose because more and more people are entering the workforce.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden wants you to think that's all because of Bidenomics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: America is now on the strongest job creating periods in our history, in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): August's numbers, 187,000 jobs added. More than last month, but less than the 12-month average.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We recover all the jobs lost during the pandemic. We've added a million more new jobs.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The unemployment rate rose slightly to 3.8 percent. But traders see signs that interest rate hikes have worked.\par \par \uc2 TIM ANDERSON, MANAGING DIRECTOR, TJM INVESTMENTS: That's a great report for the Fed. Even the -- even the unemployment rate ticking up from 3.5 to 3.8 is really meaningless.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you and I have been a long around -- long enough to remember when four percent was considered full employment.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): President Biden's approval on the economy just 37 percent, well below 54 percent his first month in office.\par \par \uc2 TYLER GOODSPEED, FORMER TRUMP ECONOMIC ADVISER: Even as wage gains have crept up. They just haven't kept pace with inflation. And they're about six percent in real terms, adjusted for inflation, wages are about six percent below where they would have been had we continued on pre-pandemic trend.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Still, the read from the labor department is crisis averted.\par \par \uc2 JULIE SU, ACTING LABOR SECRETARY: It feels like all the dire predictions of how this, you know, where this economy was going have turned out not to be true.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Economists are in agreement this is a good report with an asterisk.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, PRESIDENT, AMERICAN ACTION FORUM: The way we've managed to keep the household sector going is they're spending more than they're earning. That can't last forever.\par \par \uc2 So, there are good reasons to be concerned about the staying power of the American family.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): President Biden decided today just to give prepared remarks in the Rose Garden. Questions can wait.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Have a good Labor Day weekend. God bless you all.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why is Americans living --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): When President Biden gets back from surveying storm damage in Florida in a weekend at the beach, summer is over, and an impeachment inquiry looms.\par \par \uc2 And here at the White House, they've got a whole staff of lawyers and comms experts standing by in a war room in the executive office building, waiting to comply or not with whatever Congress kicks over. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Peter Doocy, live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today after the jobs report. The Dow gained 116. The S&P 500 finished ahead eight. The NASDAQ lost three. For the week, the Dow was up almost 1-1/2 percentage points. The S&P 500 gained 2-1/2. The NASDAQ surged 3-1/4.\par \par \uc2 There is increasing scrutiny tonight over then-Vice President Biden's alleged use of fake names for e-mail accounts. A conservative legal group is suing, accusing him of using those accounts to discuss government business with his son, Hunter.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt has the latest tonight from the justice department. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Bret. Good evening to you. The group that is suing the National Archives tells Fox News today that the issue is not the fact that then-Vice President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  may have used different e-mail addresses with different aliases. They say the issue is that he discussed business with his son, Hunter. That's what they believe, even though he has said publicly many times that he never discussed business with his son, Hunter.\par \par \uc2 Now, a lawsuit filed this week by the Southeastern Legal Foundation is asking a court to force the National Archives to speed it up and turn over more than 5,000 records involving these three alleged pseudonyms. Robinware456@gmail.com, JRBware@gmail.com, and Robert.L.Peters@pci.gov.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY HERMANN, GENERAL COUNSEL, SOUTHEASTERN LEGAL FOUNDATION: We haven't moved up in the queue. In 14 months, we've been trying to get these articles to get these and put them out in the public. Maybe there's nothing in them. Maybe it's one of these nothing to see here.\par \par \uc2 I highly doubt that. Why else would you use an alias?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): It's not unusual for public figures including Republicans and Democratic politicians to use pseudonyms or fake names to e-mail or text to keep certain things private. It's gone on in Washington for years.\par \par \uc2 JAY CARNEY, FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Having alternate e-mail addresses for Cabinet secretaries and other high-profile officials makes imminent sense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Now, the National Archives responded to the legal foundation, indicating there are, "Approximately 5,138 e-mail messages, 25 electronic files and 200 pages of potentially responsive records that may help your requests."\par \par \uc2 Fox News has not independently reviewed any e-mails in National Archives' custody. But a sort with NARA tells Fox News, some of these e-mails may be covered under the presidential records act from the Obama administration.\par \par \uc2 Bret, there, of course, is a sense of urgency on the part of this legal foundation as U.S. special counsel David Weiss has indicated, he may charge \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  once again, refile tax charges against him in either Washington, D.C. or California. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David, on another legal front, and we covered it last night, what can you tell us about the judge sentencing two more of these high-profile members of the Proud Boys?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Sure. Another big day in federal court today. The first, Dominic Pezzola. He entered the U.S. Capitol. Originally, the government wanted 20 years for him behind bars. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appointed Judge Timothy Kelly gave him 10 years behind bars.\par \par \uc2 Another Proud Boy lieutenant, his name is Ethan Nordean. Government officials wanted 27 years behind bars for him. He received 18 years behind bars.\par \par \uc2 Now, Nordean and Pezzola were on the property of the U.S. Capitol on January 6th, 2021. Nordean did not go inside, Pezzola broke through barriers, rushed inside the building.\par \par \uc2 Nordean was convicted of helping plan the attack. Both men apologized for their actions. When Pezzola left Court after learning of his 10-year sentence, he held up his hand and shouted, "Trump, won."\par \par \uc2 Proud Boys' top leader \uc2 Enrique Tarrio\uc2  will be sentenced next Tuesday. That's expected to be a lengthy one as well. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt at the DOJ. David, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Republican presidential candidates are voicing their skepticism of \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , after his attention getting performance in last week's first debate. A particular interest is Ramaswamy's foreign policy positions. The candidate is taking it all in stride.\par \par \uc2 Here is correspondent Mark Meredith.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We're having fun in this race.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  is brushing off growing criticism from fellow Republicans that he is not qualified to be a front runner in the 2024 race.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Because I am the only candidate who was on that stage, who was a non-neocon.\par \par \uc2 God bless our great country!\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Ramaswamy, a 38-year-old biotech entrepreneur, who bills himself as an America First candidate has drawn outrage from the GOP establishment, including former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , ever since the first debate.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He's wrong on foreign policy, he's wrong on American leadership in world.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Former President Trump calls him a smart young guy. A sharp contrast to former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley, who is extremely critical of Ramaswamy's candidacy. She is outraged over his plans to cut U.S. aid to Ukraine, and this week blasted him for saying as commander in chief, he would not send U.S. troops to fight Iran if it attacked Israel.\par \par \uc2 Haley tweeting, "He should take his place beside AOC and the Squad and get nowhere near the White House."\par \par \uc2 Today, Ramaswamy fired back.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I have said that we would backed Israel fully militarily. But I've also said that I don't want our sons and daughters U.S. troops to die in that conflict.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Wow, that's a lot of water.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , meantime, remains off the trail, following Hurricane Idalia. Tomorrow, he'll meet with President Biden to tour storm damage. DeSantis says he is committed to helping Florida recover as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We're mobilizing all of our state assets and we will apply for whatever federal money is available.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): The DeSantis campaign has yet to say when it's going to resume its schedule. But one of the governor's biggest boosters, the PAC, Never Back Down, says it's changing plans and suspending door knocking operations in at least four states, including Nevada, one of the earliest contest.\par \par \uc2 Bret, we're still look to see when they make it back out on the trail, and whether or not a meeting actually happens tomorrow with President Biden or not. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Mark, thank you. Please join \uc2 Stuart Varney\uc2 , Dana Perino, and Univision Ilia Calderon as they moderate the second presidential -- Republican presidential debate on Fox Business, September 27th, from the Reagan Library Presidential Foundation and Institute in Simi Valley, California.\par \par \uc2 There is a new tool tonight for people who say they have seen identified flying -- Unidentified Flying Objects, UFOs, to report those sightings to the government.\par \par \uc2 The web site is designed to collect those stories and share the government's investigations with the public.\par \par \uc2 That's new. This comes as many in Congress are pushing for further disclosures after startling accusations from a former intelligence official about what he called recovered alien technology.\par \par \uc2 Chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin has details from the Pentagon tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Under pressure from Congress, the defense department has finally launched a web site to solicit videos and provide greater transparency about what it now calls UAPs, Unidentified Aerial Phenomena. One stop shopping for videos like this one.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Wow, what is that man?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Look at that flying.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Shot by two Navy F-18 Super Hornet pilots in 2015, on a training mission over the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 The web site for the All-Domain Anomaly Resolution Office or ARRO has finally been made public to comply with the 2023 National Defense Authorization Act.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: But what you see today is what has been declassified to date. ARRO is focused on the facts, taking in information, reviewing the facts, and then, when possible, you know, declassifying that information, making it available.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: High traffic crashed the web site last night. It's back up now, when this former Air Force intelligence officer presented himself as a whistleblower in July. The Pentagon pushed back on his suggestion they had evidence of aliens.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Do we have the bodies of the pilots, who piloted this craft?\par \par \uc2 DAVID GRUSCH, FORMER INTELLIGENCE OFFICIAL: Biologics came with some of these recoveries. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Were they, I guess, human or non-human biologics?\par \par \uc2 GRUSCH: Non-human.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Now, Deputy Defense Secretary \uc2 Kathleen Hicks\uc2  is overseeing the Pentagon's UAP investigation team. She will hold weekly meetings to make sure that any unexplained phenomena are not adversaries with technology that could pose a national security threat to the United States. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our "COMMON GROUND" segment on bipartisan cooperation and dealing with the growing threat from China.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. Mohammed Al-Fayed, former owner of the same \uc2 Harrods\uc2  department store in London, whose son was killed in a car crash with Princess Diana has died.\par \par \uc2 Al-Fayed spent the rest of his life mourning the loss and fighting the British establishment he blamed for their deaths.\par \par \uc2 Mohammed Al-Fayed was 94.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. congressional delegation in Sweden pledges strong support to that country's application to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 . The House Foreign Affairs chairman says he expects the accession of Sweden to take place in October once Turkey ratifies its request.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Rome. One of the big stories there tonight. \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  arrives in Mongolia to encourage one of the world's smallest and newest Catholic communities. It is the first time a Pope has visited the landlocked Asian country.\par \par \uc2 The trip comes at a time when the Vatican's relations with Mongolia's two powerful neighbors, Russia and China, are once again strained.\par \par \uc2 To some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT. It is time now for our Common Ground segment. Tonight, how congressional Democrats and Republicans are trying to work together to deal with the China threat.\par \par \uc2 Joining us are House China's Select Committee Chairman, \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  and top Democrat \uc2 Raja Krishnamoorthi\uc2 . Gentlemen, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 First, the common ground here is I think you both assume and can say that China is a threat. How grave a threat after you've been working on this committee together?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2  (R-WI): Well, in my opinion, I think it's our top geopolitical threat. No country has the military and combined economic power to challenge us in the way that China and more specifically the Chinese Communist Party does.\par \par \uc2 I think it's important, however, in our work, and I think part of the reason there has been a sense of bipartisanship is that Raja and I have been very careful to make a distinction between the party and the people of China with whom we have no quarrel and are often the victims of the Chinese Communist Party's authoritarianism and increasing totalitarian repression.\par \par \uc2 But what we're trying to encourage our constituents and our colleagues to understand is that this isn't just a distant over there problem for someone else to deal with in the Indo Pacific, it is a right here at home problem for us to deal with in terms of our sovereignty, the health of our economy, and how we defend American values, which are really, you know, we think transcendent values, human rights, need to be at the forefront of our strategy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Congressman Krishnamoorthi, how big a problem is the imbalance in economic trade? You know, we as a country, we sell them 150 billion, they sell us about 530 plus billion. And then you add in the stolen technology in there, it's about a billion a year.\par \par \uc2 So, how significant is that economic threat?\par \par \uc2 REP. RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI (D-IL): It's very significant. In fact, we were actually in Wisconsin, I felt like I was on a diplomatic mission as a Bears fan move -- going into territory a couple of days ago to be with Mike. But we had a field hearing at a factory that had absolutely been devastated by dumping by the Chinese company, their main competitor, who basically was dumping products at less than the cost to produce them.\par \par \uc2 And so, fortunately, both the Trump and the Biden administration, put tariffs in place that, you know, kind of level the playing field and allowed this company to come back. And now it's able to really thrive.\par \par \uc2 But we shouldn't have to do that in the first place. They shouldn't be dumping, they shouldn't be hacking, they shouldn't be stealing intellectual property. And yet, it's happening all the time. And that's why we have to protect our interests and our values, and work with our friends and partners and allies to do the same.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mr. Chairman, the administration, the Biden administration has done some outreach in recent weeks. You look at the officials who've gone out there, Secretary Blinken, mid June, Treasury Secretary Yellen July 8th, \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  on Climate Change July 18th. And then most recently, the Commerce Secretary Raimondo this week. And here's what she said coming back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMONDO\uc2 , COMMERCE SECRETARY: I pulled no punches in my meetings. They said the right things, and now we'll see how they react.\par \par \uc2 But on the narrow area of emerging technology that they have said, right, and they have a civil military fusion strategy. They are crystal clear about their intention. They're trying to get this technology to improve their military, and we have to do everything in our power to prevent that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  Josh Rogin writes this, reengagement turns out to be slow boat to China. Despite meeting with Chinese leader Xi Jinping in June, Blinken came home empty handed, the Chinese government wouldn't even agree to establish a military to military crisis hotline.\par \par \uc2 The most newsworthy part of Yellen's trip was that she inadvertently ate magic mushrooms. Although she said she didn't hallucinate and Chinese officials rebuffed Kerry's call for quicker climate action.\par \par \uc2 Before Raimondo even arrived, Chinese hackers broke into her e-mail account. Raimondo set expectations so low that her announcement of two new dialogues to manage U.S.-China Economic tensions might seem like progress. Unfortunately, it is anything but.\par \par \uc2 Point in reading all of that, presenting all of that, is that the outreach seems to be being rebuffed. Do you get that sense?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, that's my concern. And admittedly, I think I'm more skeptical of this renewed economic and diplomatic engagement than some of my Democratic colleagues, it seems that we slow roll defensive action just to sit at the table. And then when the Chinese agree to something, they either don't honor it, or they just sort of string us along in these -- in these talks.\par \par \uc2 However, I think one thing and I won't speak for Raj, but I think one thing that we would both support is the establishment of a military to military crisis communication channel so that we don't miscalculate our way into an kinetic confrontation in this South China Sea or across the Taiwan Strait.\par \par \uc2 No matter where you fall on this debate of engagement, less engagement, I think we can all agree that Congress should step up and legislate lasting solutions to this problem. So we're not bouncing wildly back and forth between different executive orders or different administrations, we have a very important role to play that I think can endure and transcend different administrations.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, on the economic front, Congressman, should the tariffs, the current tariffs on China remain in place? Or should they be lifted?\par \par \uc2 KRISHNAMOORTHI: I think that they should be in place, certainly for certain industries, where, you know, they're just not playing by the rules of the road, so to speak, there's economic aggression left and right.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I think in other areas, we have to see, but I think that these tariffs, you know, provide a space for our industry to thrive and to compete.\par \par \uc2 And so, in that regard, I think that they are necessary.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If there's a majority here or a bipartisan consensus or any common ground, when would Congress act?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, we're going to be back in session in a couple of weeks. And there we have an opportunity to act on the military competition line of effort, we'll be considering the conference version of the National Defense Authorization Act, we have critical votes coming up on defense appropriations.\par \par \uc2 So, when it comes to ensuring that we are putting hard power in Xi Jinping's path, so that he doesn't try and launch an invasion of Taiwan, which could cascade into a war that would make what's happening in eastern Ukraine right now seem, you know, timid in comparison. There we can act.\par \par \uc2 I think another area where Raja and I would like to see action based on our recent investigation is closing things like the De Minimis loophole whereby Chinese companies are allowed to ship things into our country without the scrutiny that they should be facing under the Uyghur Forced Labor Prevention Act. That's something we should be able to do by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, in my opinion, we should stop allowing American money to flow to China, particularly Chinese military companies that are building things like aircraft carriers, artillery shells, and fighter jets.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is it fair to say that they're -- you're singing from the same sheet of music here? That there is common ground on a lot of these things when it comes to China?\par \par \uc2 KRISHNAMOORTHI: Oh, absolutely. And, you know, one of the most interesting things that we've learned is that the CCP is counting on there are not being common ground between Democrats and Republicans. They love it when we are divided, when we are arguing with each other and fighting with each other.\par \par \uc2 And what they find very intimidating is when we actually come see the threat and the issues clearly and then act in unison.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I don't want to break up that bipartisanship, but the Bears play the Packers on September 10th. Is there a bet that you guys are laying down or?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I'm happy to wager something on T.V. right now. Perhaps some delicious spotted cow beer or some cheese curds made here in Northeast Wisconsin. You name the wager Raja and I'm with you.\par \par \uc2 KRISHNAMOORTHI: Never on -- never bet against a Bear. Including a Bret Baier. Chicago Bear.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There you go.\par \par \uc2 KRISHNAMOORTHI: I'm going to -- I'm going to put some dish pizza on the line here.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Perfect.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You heard it here. We're going to play this tape back on September 11th. Gentlemen, thank you very much for the time.\par \par \uc2 You can see all the Common Ground segments on my podcast Common Ground, find that and the all-star panel podcasts under The Bret Baier podcast at FoxNewsPodcasts.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2  or wherever you download podcasts. You can also see the whole extended discussion on the Fox News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, California retailers say state lawmakers are considering new legislation that will make the crime crisis there even worse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES GALLAGHER (R-CA): We are all gathered here today for one reason only to say enough is enough. We need to make crime illegal again in California.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, Ohio authorities have released body camera video of the fatal police shooting of Ta'Kiya Young. Police say the 21-year-old pregnant woman was suspected of shoplifting. Young's unborn daughter also died in that shooting. Correspondent Kevin Corke has this story. Good evening, Kevin.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Now, the video we are about to show you may be difficult for some to watch. We're offering that warning in advance. As you said, the pregnant 21-year- old had been suspected of shoplifting and confronted by a pair of police officers in an Ohio grocery store parking lot back on the 24th of August. But what happened next, say police, is why deadly force was used.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Get out of the car. Then get out. No, get out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Get out of the car.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Get out of the car.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Get out of the car.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Shots fired.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Shots fired.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORKE: In a statement by Blendon Township police chief John Belford, they are saying tonight, Bret, the officers called emergency services seconds after getting her out of the car. They attempted to stop the bleeding and offer aid. She, of course, died in the shooting.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, in another statement, Young's family and their attorney tonight are saying that the shooting was clearly a criminal act and they're demanding a swift indictment of the officer who was involved. Speaking of, that officer has been placed on administrative leave. We also know the other officer there has returned to duty since that shooting, Bret.\par \par \uc2 CORKE: OK, Kevin, we will follow this shooting, thank you.\par \par \uc2 If you are traveling over the Labor Day weekend, you have plenty of company. The \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  predicts this will be the third busiest holiday weekend of the year so far. The crush is expected to continue through Tuesday. The transportation secretary, though, says the system is ready.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: We are on track to be at or below the typical rate of cancellations over the last decade. So not only are we seeing the system rebound from the worst effects of COVID, but we are seeing things come more in line with what we would have regarded as normal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tonight's report on America's crime crisis looks at proposed law in California that business owners say will make the situation even worse. National correspondent William La Jeunesse tells us what that is tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Don't do that. Don't do that, ma'am.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Retailers in California are angry over lawmakers' response to a surge in shoplifting.\par \par \uc2 JASKARAN SAHOTA, SMALL BUSINESS OWNER: That means criminals can do whatever they want and Democrats, they're going to protect them, not us.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Hundreds of small business owners rallied Thursday to protest a bill that limits their ability to confront thieves and requires them to hire private security to stop crime.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: SB-553 allows all the shoplifters to come to our stores and grab whatever they need.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: The bill mandates a workplace violence prevention plan. Only security can confront shoplifters. Otherwise, Cal/\uc2 OSHA\uc2  can fine retailers $18,000 per incident. Businesses must log every threat of violence and provide trauma counseling if it happens.\par \par \uc2 JAMES GALLAGHER, (R) CALIFORNIA STATE ASSEMBLY: This is a terrible bill, a terrible bill that seeks to put all the burden on the business owners.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Supporters say it protects employees from having to confront shoplifters.\par \par \uc2 DAVE CORTESE, (D) CALIFORNIA STATE SENATE: It's not OK for an employer to take a rank-and-file worker and say, hey, if there is an intruder, we're going to deputize you to be the one to intervene.\par \par \uc2 VERN PIERSON, EL DORADO COUNTY, CALIFORNIA DISTRICT ATTORNEY: It's not enough that we decriminalize property crime, but now he is saying you can't stop criminals from stealing, taking your property.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: The D.A. is referring to voter approved Prop 47 which made property theft under $950 a misdemeanor.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I always tell our voters, you've got to read the fine print.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Passed in 2014, Prop 47 reduced the state's jail population, but it also increased property crime.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Rather than sending my kids through school to universities to get educated, I should teach them how to steal. Go to a store, grab $950 or less, come home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): Today the bill passed out of the Assembly Appropriations Committee, moving one step closer to Governor Newsom. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we look at "Whatever Happened To?" the missing \uc2 Malaysian Airlines\uc2  flight 370.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: FOX News confirms 400 U.S. troops originally deployed to the southern border in May will have their mission extended through September 30th. They were initially put there to deal with the anticipated fallout from the end of pandemic-related asylum rules.\par \par \uc2 In this evening's "Whatever Happened To?" report, how a simple barnacle could lead to vital information about the missing \uc2 Malaysian Airlines\uc2  flight 370. Remember that story? Here is correspondent Griff Jenkins.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Goodnight Malaysia 370.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The last communication with \uc2 Malaysian Airlines\uc2  flight 370, the evening of March 8th, 2014 -- 227 passengers and 12 crew aboard, never to be seen or heard from again. Among them, 51-year-old American businessman Philip Wood, leaving behind a grieving brother, seeking answers.\par \par \uc2 TOM WOOD, MH370 PASSENGER BROTHER: They are pretty sure that it crashed, then most likely it did. But I still want proof to be absolutely certain.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: More than nine years later, that proof still hasn't come.\par \par \uc2 FUAD SHARUJI, \uc2 MALAYSIA AIRLINES\uc2  FORMER CRISIS DIRECTOR: MH-370 is completely unprecedented. We do not know where the aircraft is. We do not know where the bodies are.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: \uc2 Malaysian Airlines\uc2  former crisis director says we may never know what happened as the official search was called off in 2017, Leaving only unproven theories of mechanical error, hijacking, or even pilot suicide. But now one scientist says barnacles, like those found on the plane's flaperon that washed ashore in 2015 could hold the clues.\par \par \uc2 GREGORY HERBERT, UNIVERSITY OF SOUTH FLORIDA: So the temperatures that these barnacles recorded from early in the drift when they first latched onto the flaperon debris right after the crash could tell us precisely where the flaperon was right after the crash.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Ultimately, he is confident one day we will find the missing plane.\par \par \uc2 HERBERT: The Indian Ocean is a very big place, but it's not that big. Sooner or later they are going to find it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): MH-370 remains one of the greatest mysteries of the modern aviation era. But despite new clues that could come from emerging science, the Malaysian government says it has no plans to restart another search mission. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Griff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have just learned in the past few minutes President Biden will not meet with Florida governor and Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  when he tours hurricane Idalia damaged tomorrow. DeSantis's office says the security needs would shut down necessary recovery efforts.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, about the hurricane, \uc2 FOX Corporation\uc2  has made a donation to the Red Cross hurricane relief efforts, continues to be an annual disaster giving program partner that enables the Red Cross to respond immediately to disasters such as hurricane Idalia, also the wildfires in Maui by providing safe shelter, hot meals, emotional support, and resources to aid in recovery. Here is how you can help. Visit RedCross.org/FOXforward, or you can scan the QR code right there on the bottom of your screen.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on what the latest jobs numbers may mean for the U.S. economy and the presidential race. Plus, Candidate Casino. We always go there on a Friday.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 29 in Philadelphia with the manhunt for a convicted murderer who escaped custody Thursday. Danilo Cavalcante was able to get past guards at Chester County Prison. He was recently convicted of first- degree murder in the stabbing death of his former girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at New York. One of the big stories there from our affiliate FOX 5, the governor's office announces all Long Island and New York state beaches will reopen this afternoon, Thursday. Dangerous rip currents caused by hurricane Franklin in the Atlantic led to the closure of three of those beaches. So that's good news.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: America is now in one of the strongest job creating periods in our history, in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 JULIE SU, ACTING LABOR SECRETARY: I think that this is a sign that Bidenomics is doing what we would hope that it would do and that people are starting to feel it in their pocketbooks, too.\par \par \uc2 TYLER GOODSPEED, FORMER TRUMP ECONOMIC ADVISER: Young Americans entering the labor force in part out of necessity, and with mortgage rates above seven percent, I think there is very little prospect for first-time home buyers to get on the housing ladder. And I think that does weigh on sentiment for younger Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Different thoughts. Meantime, the jobs report out today for August, Jobs up 187,000. Unemployment rate at 3.8 percent. What some people don't often see is, or report on, are the jobs revisions in each one of these monthly reports. You have got June from 185,000 revised now to 105,000, July 187 to revised 157. So those are going down as far as the job reports. Obviously both parties looking at it completely differently and how they talk about it.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," "Axios" political reporter Stef Kight, and syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 . You talk about the different visions, the prisms in which we look at the economy. Hugh, how do you look at it?\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: I look at it in January of 2020, Bret, versus today. The height of the Trump economy pre-COVID, unemployment was at 3.5 percent, the number of unemployed in the country were 5.8 million. And the interest rate was three percent. You could buy a house if you were a first-time home buyer.\par \par \uc2 Now those numbers are up to 3.8 percent to 6.4 million unemployed and, of course, seven percent mortgages. So I think when you go back to the Trump economy, pre-COVID, and compare it to the Biden economy after trillions of dollars of spending, Republicans are looking forward to running on that comparison. I think it's going to be in a recession next year. Economists are split.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, President Biden sees that differently, presents it differently. Stef, they say that the jobs are much better than what was created under the Trump administration. But you look at how people feel about it in the FOX poll. Biden has made the economy worse, according to 52 percent, better is 25 percent, Stef.\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, POLITICAL REPORTER, "AXIOS": This is going to be a huge issue going into 2024. And, of course, as we've discussed, we are going to see Republicans take their side of this story and make comparisons to Trump, and fill it all, what improvement we have seen in the economy under Biden. But the other side of this COVID really was -- had a huge impact on the economy. And having solid jobs numbers does matter, and Biden is going to be able to tout his successes like he did today. And it it's these kinds of numbers that if he continues to see them moving forward could have -- could sway some voters to see him as doing a better job on the economy. But this is going to be huge, and we've got a long way to go, especially going into 2024.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Byron, I want to talk about another topic, and this is presidential candidate, former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley speaking about all the incidents about age, recently.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Right now, the Senate is the most privileged nursing home in the country.\par \par \uc2 I am strongly in support of term limits in this country. I think that we do need mental competency tests for anyone over the age of 75. I wouldn't care if they did them over the age of 50. But these are people making decisions on our national security.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, Byron, she is talking about the Senate there and what happened, the freeze with Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , also Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 . But by going down that road, like she did, she is also obviously talking about President Biden.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Absolutely. And she is really also talking about former President Trump as well who is she is running against in the Republican primary. Governor Haley has made this age thing a generational change issue, a very big deal from her announcement speech on. And it does actually resonate with a lot of Americans who think there is something really weird about an 80-year-old man running against a 77-year-old man and 81-year-old leader of the Republicans in the Senate. Not too long the House leadership was 81, 82, and 83 years old. So there seems to be some sort of gerontocracy that we are living through at this moment. And I do think you are going to see candidates continue to push the issue of generational change because the problem is pretty obvious.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gerontocracy, I'm going to add that to the dictionary here on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 YORK: There you go.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, right now, it's Friday, so we take a trip down to Candidate Casino. Two different versions tonight. You have $100, first of all, to bet as we have been on the GOP primary. You've got $100, you've got to put them on the table. Hugh, where are you?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: I'm staying right where I was last week, Bret, with $40 on President Trump being renominated, $40 on Governor DeSantis getting the nomination, $10 on Nikki Haley, and $10 on the field. I think Governor DeSantis solidified my concern that President Trump is not out campaigning as much as he needs to be. And those numbers, false positive for him. So I'm sticking with my bet from last week.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Stef, place your bets.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: I'm going to keep $70 on Trump, and then I'm going to give just $10 to DeSantis. I will give another $10 to Nikki Haley and the last $10 I'm going to throw to \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  just because of how much attention he has generated following the debate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Byron?\par \par \uc2 YORK: I'm keeping former President Trump at $50, moving \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  up a little bit to $25, a good performance during the hurricane, Nikki Haley up a little bit to $15, good performance overall lately. And \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  down a little bit. I don't think he is going to wear well. And Tim Scott also down because he just seems to have disappeared.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, we're going to go around the horn. I'm going to do this for the cameras. Another bet, $100 in chips that President Biden is the Democratic nominee. Hugh?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Only $10 on President Biden returning as the nominee, $90 against that. I think the obvious infirmity is becoming the non-Republican elephant in the room. And I don't think voters are going to turn their eyes away after South Carolina primary when I expect the president to exit.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Stef?\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: I'm going to put $85 on Biden only because, despite the concerns around age, we have yet to see really another candidate step up to the plate as the alternative. I'll keep $15 in case someone does step up, but at this point in the race, I'm sticking with Biden as the nominee.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Byron?\par \par \uc2 YORK: I'm against Hugh on this, $80 for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It is so hard to get a president of your own party not to run for re-election if he wants to. But if he doesn't, I think \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the obvious choice. Although we should throw \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  in just for a little bit of excitement.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's do a super lightning, lightning round of Winners and Losers. Winner and loser, hugh?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Winner, SMU and Stanford and Cal who have joined the ACC, getting in the door in the great shakeup of realignment. The losers are, of course, the Cougar out in Washington State, Butch T. Cougar and Benny the Beaver at Oregon State wandering around in the woods of the pacific northwest. It's very sad for those two schools, all that's left of the PAC-12.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Stef, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: It's been a weird week, but I'm going to go with Biden as the winner because of his move to lower prescription drug costs which Republicans typically have a hard time messaging against. Loser, McConnell for his freeze.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Byron?\par \par \uc2 YORK: Winner, Mayor Suarez for seeing the handwriting on the wall and dropping out of the Republican race. Loser, Chicago Mayor Brandon Johnson. Car theft is skyrocketing there, and what does he do? He sues the car manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel. Make it a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know what that sound means -- "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That car is flying.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: You run away from the water, and then you hide from the wind.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is great, expressing her point of view.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Shame on you, ma'am.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Shame, shame, shame.\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS, (D) NEW YORK CITY: Don't sit in the bleachers and be a detached spectator on this full contact sport.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That one fan is out of control.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Upset at a couple people walking down.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A tough match. I'm lucky I'm a good sleeper.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The book is suggesting the president tells aides he is tired, and that that's why there have been so few public events before 10:00 a.m.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: That's a ridiculous assumption to make.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He was his old self.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The Senate is the most privileged nursing home in the country.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'll respect his wishes, but I would just say, will the real Slim Shady please stand up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This guy is on to something. Then he'll say something, and I'll say, oh my God. I attribute it to his youth.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Does President Biden want to limit Americans to two beers a week?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Where is this coming from? Maybe I didn't miss you so much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One week.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Biden (ph) will be with Shannon on Sunday. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight, fair, balanced, and unafraid. "THE INGRAHAM ANGLE" is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230902ej9100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Taylor Swift Tour Film Setting Box Office Records; Hurricane Recovery Efforts; Interview With Rep. Brad Wenstrup (R-OH); Trump Georgia Trial to Stream Live; \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  Under Fire; Job Gains Ease, Unemployment Rate Ticks Up; Holiday Travel Crush\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Kacie McDonnell Hosmer, Kat Timpf, Max Gorden, Mark  Meredith, Peter Doocy; Jeff Flock, Garrett Tenney \par \uc2 8,602 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, U.S. TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: This weekend is capping what TSA reports is the busiest summer travel period on record.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm going to Vegas to celebrate my sister's 30th birthday. She's doing it big, so we're going over.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I usually stay at home to stay out of the crowds and all the traffic.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We get together with our grown kids and our grandkids, and we just have a good time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, just have a good time, because the Labor Day travel rush is indeed on. Reports are, half of us are venturing out in any way we can, and no matter who or what gets in our way.\par \par \uc2 We have got FOX team coverage with Jeff Flock on how fliers are holding up, and Garrett Tenney on how drivers are paying up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. So glad to have you.\par \par \uc2 Let's begin with Jeff at Chicago's O'Hare, how it's looking for those trying to get out of town -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: If you're at home watching right now, good for you. You don't have to get on an airplane.\par \par \uc2 That's a good thing, although, I will tell you, Neil, it's probably going to be a pretty good weekend, nice and sunny here in Chicago at O'Hare and also around most of the rest of the country. I want to take you inside the American terminal here.\par \par \uc2 This is the TSA check-in right here, and, as you can see, pretty manageable at this hour, although it tends to heat right now.\par \par \uc2 What are the numbers? Well, you mentioned records. How about this? Twenty million people going to fly this Labor Day weekend. That's an increase of 14 percent from last year.\par \par \uc2 And good news cancellations of flights are down. Last summer, it was a disaster, but the cancellations have almost been cut in half, according to the secretary of transportation who was here in Chicago today, saying, yes, that's good news, but admitting there's also bad news in the area of near misses.\par \par \uc2 Those are on the increase, up 25 percent. And the secretary, in response to a question from FOX Business, said, yes, that's true, but we don't know why. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: We have seen a noticeable increase in serious close calls, and the only acceptable number of these is zero.\par \par \uc2 The truth is, there is no single cause or single issue that explains it. Sometimes, we have seen issues with pilots. Sometimes, we have seen issues with controllers. Sometimes, we have seen an issue with ground crews.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: And, Neil, I leave you with this, also numbers from the transportation secretary today.\par \par \uc2 He said, yes, delays are still happening, but they're not as a result of not enough air traffic controllers. He says they have already met their goal for the year in terms of hiring air traffic controllers.\par \par \uc2 But, as you know, some of the airlines and other people have blamed a lack of controllers for the problems at the airport. No flightmares today, though. I leave you perhaps with a picture of people happily going through the TSA line and off to wherever they need to go this Labor Day -- sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Very, very orderly, my friend. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Flock on that.\par \par \uc2 Garrett Tenney with what drivers are doing and those who don't want to fly and deal with any of the hassles maybe inherent there.\par \par \uc2 It's not necessarily worry-free, I guess, on the roads, Garrett. What are you noticing?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, the roads here in Chicago right now are packed, a lot of folks sitting in their cars, I'm sure maybe not as happy as they are there at the airport flowing through TSA PreCheck.\par \par \uc2 But this weekend is expected to be huge for travel, Neil. We are talking about more than half of all Americans who are expected to get out of town for Labor Day. That's according to WalletHub. But, this year, a whole lot of folks will be staying a bit closer to home with their travels.\par \par \uc2 More than 85 percent of AAA members in one survey said they will be driving to their destinations this weekend -- this weekend, and one big reason for that is inflation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AIXA DIAZ, AAA SPOKESMAN: We do anticipate a lot of road trips, especially a lot of shorter road trips, because, like I said, so many people are sort of wanting to get back into the fall routine. They're not wanting to travel as far away, but that means that local roads are going to be congested.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Gas prices certainly aren't helping folks either.\par \par \uc2 The national average for a gallon today is $3.81, which is a bit higher than a month ago and roughly the same as last Labor Day, which is why a lot of road trips this weekend are expected to be shorter distances than they may have been in the past.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARRY RICHARDSON, CONNECTICUT: I think they're disrespectful. They're just incredibly high. And for the last two weeks they have just been going up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: At this point, I just want to hop on the public transit, but not everybody has the time to hop on a public transit.\par \par \uc2 ALFRED CROTEAU, CONNECTICUT: I definitely am double-thinking whether I'm going to take some of those road trips, and do I need to use the car as much?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Now, if you want to avoid the worst of traffic, AAA says you should hit the road tonight or early tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And, remember, for Monday, it's likely going to be busy again, as families with kids head back home for school on Tuesday. So, unless you like an extended amount of family time together in traffic in a car, take off early if you want to avoid those headaches -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you for that, Garrett Tenney.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to \uc2 Gabe Saglie\uc2 . He is a great travel expert, a good help when it comes on these crowded flying times\par \par \uc2 Gabe, first to the flying, because that's what a lot of people are committing themselves to do in record numbers. We hear of a lot of delays, sporadic and others, but so far, so relatively good. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 GABE SAGLIE, TRAVELZOO.COM: Yes, listen, there's optimism, I think, as we head toward Labor Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 We know it's going to be packed this afternoon, if you have got to get out there, I'd say, or first thing Saturday morning looks good. I have got a flight at 7:00 p.m. on -- tomorrow, Saturday, so I'm trying to avoid that inevitable bump in traffic that we expect on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Sunday should be pretty easy. And then, of course, the volume picks up again on Monday. But, listen, the summer started off pretty -- in a pretty rough fashion, May, June. By July, things stable -- became a little bit more stable. Volume was certainly up. It was busy out there, Neil, most folks traveling internationally.\par \par \uc2 But I think, as we head toward this Labor Day weekend, we're expecting things to remain smoothly. The airlines have proven that they can handle this a little bit better now than compared to last year. And folks are certainly trying to get into one last vacation before we say goodbye to summer 2023.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There'd been a lot of attention at nation's airports about pilots, whether we had enough of them, and then this study that shows 5,000 are hiding medical conditions from the public.\par \par \uc2 And I thought to myself, what if a good many of them couldn't fly? We suddenly had a shortage of pilots. We have a problem with air traffic controllers, not in such a way that it's disrupting things, but it could. Do you worry, when we get after this holiday rush, that the next holiday rush, those could become issues?\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Yes, the next holiday rush will happen, as we know, toward November and December.\par \par \uc2 Now is the time to start booking those flights, Neil. But, yes, I think that, once we get over this Labor Day hump, we are going to be tightening the scope there on some of these issues around pilots. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  was also under scrutiny. They have now reached their 1,500 new employee goal, new hiring goal this year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: They still have another 18 to 24 months of training, of course, to go, but we're seeing some progress there.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, I think the pilot issue will certainly come under the microscope. And I think that the expectation that pilots will remain in hot demand over the next five to 10 years will simply be ramped up even more if some of these issues become too -- become reality, some of these concerns actually become a reality.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you surprised -- and you're a seasoned traveler, Gabe -- that's putting it mildly, of how much people have put up with and still crowd the nation's airports, still crowd these resorts?\par \par \uc2 You're lucky to find an available hotel room these days, of course, I know, depending on where you're going. But we're even seeing foreign travel that's up north of 60 percent. What's going on here?\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That seems to be a very focused public realizing, we will put up with all of that just to get out.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I think, last year, we predicted it was going to be a particularly good post-COVID year. This has certainly blown expectations even further. I mean, domestic travel took a bit of a hit this year. I think this is why this particular weekend is good news for domestic hotels and car rental companies and airlines, because domestic travel did miss the mark a little bit, if we look at summer.\par \par \uc2 Everybody was really looking at Europe and south of the border and the Caribbean, spending almost in unbridled fashion. That's what I saw when my family of five was making its way through Europe in July.\par \par \uc2 I think that there was -- there's almost this idea that we have got to really get out there and embrace travel, do it, put up with the potential headaches, put up with the potential delays and cancellations, because the hunger is still there. That desire is still very much palpable.\par \par \uc2 And I think that people are now -- will perhaps start looking at what that means for their budgets. Now that we have done the big-ticket traveling, let's now budget accordingly. That's why I think, after Labor Day, we will see a bit of a lull. It happens naturally, but I think people will sit back now, begin to readjust their budgeting, before we see a bump in the holiday season.\par \par \uc2 2024 could bode well for the domestic travel market all over again. People might actually pull back on some of those big-ticket international trips, maybe not travel as -- in such large windows, in such large groups. That could bode well for the domestic travel market.\par \par \uc2 But what we're seeing now, Neil, is, we have been using 2019, pre-COVID, as the -- that benchmark for the way that people travel.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: I think 2023 now becomes the new normal, the new travel benchmark. And we will start to use this as our ruler, if you will, around travel behavior as we move forward.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it, my friend. Travel safe.\par \par \uc2 Gabe Saglie\uc2 , the travel expert extraordinaire, the \uc2 Travelzoo\uc2  senior editor.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There is something to what he said, and the proof is in the pudding about the demand and the jobs and where the strength is.\par \par \uc2 Not surprisingly, in the employment report that came out today, among the biggest industries hiring and continue to higher, leisure and hospitality.\par \par \uc2 We will explore all of that after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the trend is our friend.\par \par \uc2 That is the way the administration is seeing it with the latest employment report out showing a gain of 187,000 jobs, although the unemployment report did uptick a little bit to 3.8 percent, still historically very low.\par \par \uc2 How the White House is playing that with our Peter Doocy, because that's where he is.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Neil, the jobs report was better than last month, but below the 12-month average.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden came out to basically say that he wants a W.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: America is now one of the strongest job-creating periods in our history, in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 We have recovered all the jobs lost during the pandemic and we have added a million more new jobs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: People really aren't feeling it though, 37 percent approval rating on the economy, per Gallup, way below the 54 percent he had on that issue after a month in office.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TYLER GOODSPEED, FORMER ACTING CHAIR, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Even as wage gains have crept up, they just haven't kept pace with inflation. And they're about 6 percent in real terms. Adjusted for inflation, wages are about 6 percent below where they would have been had we continued on pre-pandemic trend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden decided to stick to the script today. His Rose Garden event appeared to be set up for a Q&A, but it wasn't meant to be.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Have a good Labor Day weekend. And God bless you all.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Why are so many Americans living paycheck to paycheck?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Yes, off to the Oval Office. We haven't seen him since. We do expect to see him tomorrow stop in Florida to survey storm damage, before a weekend at Rehoboth Beach -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for that, Peter Doocy at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to my buddy Teddy Weisberg at Seaport Securities.\par \par \uc2 Markets liked this news, Ted, obviously, the market racing ahead, the Dow about 115 points, to start what is often a problematic month, September, I guess on the notion that, when the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  meets again, I think on the 19th or 20th of this month, one less reason to hike rates.\par \par \uc2 Do you buy that, Ted?\par \par \uc2 TED WEISBERG, PRESIDENT, \uc2 SEAPORT SECURITIES CORPORATION\uc2 : Well, I mean, this is the 800-pound gorilla in the room, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Are rates going up or is the Fed going to be on hold? And it's interesting, because the White House basically promotes job creation, and which, I guess, translates into a robust economy, and, on the other hand, the Fed is doing everything they can to put a wet blanket on the economy and slow things down to try to deal with inflation.\par \par \uc2 So, you kind of have folks pulling on the same rope from the opposite directions. I don't know what the end result is, but, clearly, the prospect of higher interest rates and the Fed continuing to be aggressive due to the interest rates is potentially a problem for the markets going forward.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know you have reminded me over these many years, Ted, that markets climb a wall of worry. There are plenty of worries, even new ones now, with some of these numbers and rates staying stubbornly high, hasn't really put a death blow to housing, to put it mildly, maybe quite the opposite.\par \par \uc2 Are you surprised by the resiliency of the consumer and just the strength of the underlying consumer part of this economy, two-thirds of this economy?\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: Well, I don't know if I'm surprised, but I would say, Neil, that, with all the unknowns that are out there -- and we always talk about markets hate unknowns.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: With all the unknowns that are out there, the market is actually hanging pretty good.\par \par \uc2 We had a very good first six months of the year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: Now, it is true that we had a handful of stocks that basically had the majority of those gains. We had a decent July. August was sort of good news/bad news, started off bad and recovered a little bit.\par \par \uc2 And now, of course, we're a bit down to the short strokes in terms of the end of the year and what will be the catalyst for the market. But all -- with all those unknowns, the market hangs in pretty good. But the -- and the economy hangs in.\par \par \uc2 And this is the problem for the Fed. Now, I'm not sure that 5 percent or 4 percent interest rates are terribly high. Yes, in terms of where we were a couple of years ago, when things were at zero, it's dramatically different. But there are folks that have benefited from these higher interest rates.\par \par \uc2 You know that you can get 5, 5.5 percent on short-term Treasuries. Money market funds were 4.5 percent, 5 percent. Two years ago, people were getting zero on their savings. Now they're getting paid. So, you don't have to come into the market to get some gains. You can get decent returns without taking on risk.\par \par \uc2 So, bottom line, I think the markets are kind of reasonably in good shape. The big question is going to be, what is the Fed going to be? What are they going to do at the next meeting? What are they going to do with rates? If rates continue to move higher, at some point, clearly, it's going to negatively impact the markets.\par \par \uc2 But for the moment, we seem to be in reasonably good shape.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Housing looks very strong, especially in the hot areas that were hot areas, that remain hot areas, maybe slightly less hot, but still hot.\par \par \uc2 Real estate is holding up. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: Well, I -- listen, Neil, I think -- well, I think it's very good.\par \par \uc2 I think the Fed is probably frustrated. Don't forget, it wasn't that many years ago when we had no inflation and the Fed was doing everything they could to create inflation, to get us to the 2 percent inflation rate. And now we're on the other side of the mountain.\par \par \uc2 Listen, they have a problem. You have fiscal policies and you have monetary policy. There's only so much that Fed can do. Inflation clearly is a problem, Neil. Just this week, look, a \uc2 Visa\uc2  and \uc2 Mastercard\uc2  announced that they're going to raise the fees that they charge merchants.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean? You know what that means. That simply means that the merchants are going to turn around and pass along those higher fees to consumers. What could be more inflationary than that? So we have kind of got this struggle going on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: thank\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: I don't know how it's going to end up, but if it stays the way it is and inflation continues to be a problem, the Fed is probably going to stay aggressive and continue to raise rates.\par \par \uc2 So, at some point, that's -- that will be a problem for the markets. But, for the moment, the economy seems to be chugging along pretty good.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: The Fed will do what the Fed will do, and, hopefully, the stock market will find something positive to look at for the last three or four months of the year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, so far, it has, so we will see how long that lasts.\par \par \uc2 Always good seeing you, Ted. Be well.\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: Thank you, Neil. And you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Ted Weisberg.\par \par \uc2 All right, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , he has become a sensation in the Republican Party. But you know what? He's also become quite the target. We will explain and explore -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That was Swift, Taylor Swift. She's been making a fortune off of these concerts, now a movie that's already making a good buck ahead of its October release on those concerts.\par \par \uc2 Go figure.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, it is a sign of respect or just intimidation, but \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  has gone from an also-ran to running into a buzz saw criticism because he's resonating in polls across the country.\par \par \uc2 Mark Meredith on why the other candidates are aiming for him now, and maybe in record numbers.\par \par \uc2 What's going on here, Mark?\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil. Good to see you. Happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 You're right. A lot of people are talking about \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . He got a lot of people's attention after the first debate. But now his fellow GOP candidates are ready to take back the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I have known him a couple of years. He's just wrong. He's wrong on foreign policy. He's wrong on American leadership in the world.\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R-NJ), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The fact is that Vivek says one thing, does another.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Ramaswamy says he welcomes the criticism and the attention. He says he's glad voters are responding to his America first agenda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We're having fun in this race.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that there is a real distinction. And I think that I am the only candidate who was on that stage who is a non-neocon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Former President Trump is praising Ramaswamy, but former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley doing just quite the opposite.\par \par \uc2 She says she is very critical of his candidacy. She's accused him of being a lightweight on foreign policy, especially after earlier this week, when he said he would not send U.S. troops to fight Iran if Iran were to attack Israel.\par \par \uc2 She says: "If he doesn't see a nuclear Iran as a threat to American security, then he should take his place beside AOC and the Squad and get nowhere near the White House."\par \par \uc2 This afternoon, Ramaswamy fired right back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I have said that we would back Israel fully militarily. But I have also said that I don't want our sons and daughters, U.S. troops, to die in that conflict.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: We're also watching what's going on South in Florida, where Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  remains off the campaign trail as the state recovers from this week's hurricane.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says he will meet face to face with the governor tomorrow, when he gets an up-close look at some of the storm damage left behind. And behind the scenes, one of the biggest boosters for DeSantis, the super PAC Never Back Down. Neil, says it's going to pull back on its door-knocking strategies in at least four states, including Nevada, which is an early contest state.\par \par \uc2 They say they want to focus on the first three. Obviously, they only have so many resources, and they want to focus them where they think they will do the most good -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Thank you very much for that, Mark Meredith, in Washington.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we will be covering the president's arrival in Florida tomorrow, meeting with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  on my weekend show beginning at 10:00 a.m. and monitoring those developments, how it's looking for the president, how it's looking for the governor, and of course, how it's looking for Floridians, who badly and desperately need the help.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, I want to go to Tom Dupree right now in another fascinating development when it comes to this political back-and-forth, this Georgia trial that apparently is now set. The president wants to push it further back, as you have heard, the former president, but now word that, when it is, it's going to be streamed live.\par \par \uc2 Tom Dupree again here, the former deputy assistant attorney general.\par \par \uc2 These things are piling up. We have got the added element here, Tom, of a streamed trial. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE, FORMER JUSTICE DEPARTMENT OFFICIAL: Well, you worry that at some point you're going to cross the line, if we haven't already, to a circus atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 I mean, just the idea of seeing the former president of the United States sitting alongside potentially more than a dozen co-defendants on a livestreamed trial is something that our nation has never even conceived of, let alone seen before.\par \par \uc2 We have seen that the former president's legal team is looking to basically separate himself from the herd and have a trial where he would be the only defendant, kind of cut himself loose from the other co-defendants, which I think is a smart move on his part.\par \par \uc2 Because the danger of being a defendant in one of these big, massive, multidefendant trials is that jurors may not be able to kind of keep separate the evidence that goes against one defendant and may inadvertently, erroneously hold it against a different defendant. Everyone kind of blends together and gets mixed up.\par \par \uc2 So it'll be fascinating to see how this all plays out, whether there's one big trial, multiple small trials, but, regardless, all of it will be livestreamed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, forget about whether that whole thing is delayed.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the back-and-forth continues on that. Mark Meadows, the president's former chief of staff, wants it moved to a federal court from a state court. How likely is that? And would the president and the others wish for the same?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Yes, he made some decent arguments. A lot of folks were saying, oh, this is frivolous, no chance of it. I don't share that view. I think he's got a chance of keeping it in federal court.\par \par \uc2 I think the likeliest outcome is probably that the federal judge does send it back to state court, but he's got a fighting chance here. If that federal judge decides to keep the Meadows case, I think it will substantially increase the likelihood that other co-defendants, including the former president, would likewise be able to move their cases from state court to federal court.\par \par \uc2 So, if that federal judge says, all right, I will hear Meadows' case, I think it will be like a magnet with iron filings, where you will see a whole bunch of the co-defendants in state court try to migrate their cases into a federal courtroom.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, judges and others have sort of warned the former president, be careful what you say on the stump -- I'm not saying it exactly right -- but that interferes or mars what's being done in these courts.\par \par \uc2 But he is a candidate for president of the United States, and can they dictate that sort of thing?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Yes, and talk about a fine line, because, on one hand, I mean, it is very common, at least in high-profile cases, for judges to issue these types of orders, basically saying, don't go trying your case in the press, limit your public statements, don't try to stir up passions and that sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 But, on the other hand, we have never had a defendant who's a major candidate for president of the United States. And he has a First Amendment right, among other rights, to speak about politics, to speak about his candidacy. And, as a practical matter, I think it is very difficult to try to completely separate his presidential candidacy from all these criminal proceedings.\par \par \uc2 That's what everyone is talking about. That's what the race, at least in part, is focusing on. So I think it's going to be a fine line. I suspect the president will probably make some statements or tweet out some comments that does cause the judge to say, hey, wait a minute, you need to rein it in a bit.\par \par \uc2 But I think the president is going to continue speaking his mind on this case.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We'll watch it closely.\par \par \uc2 Tom Dupree, have a safe weekend, my friend. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Thanks, Neil. You too.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we spent trillions of dollars on COVID relief and now reports that maybe upwards of $300 billion of that just disappeared. They don't know where it went. They have some suspicions.\par \par \uc2 Wait until you hear what they are -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They say the more you spend, the more you potentially waste.\par \par \uc2 So, in a program for pandemic relief that went into the trillions of dollars, probably not even worthy of a FOX Alert to say close to $300 billion worth of it disappeared. They can't find out where it went, but it seems like a good deal of it might have gone to nefarious sources.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Brad Wenstrup of Ohio joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, what do we know of this money and where it went?\par \par \uc2 REP. BRAD WENSTRUP (R-OH): Well, it's more about what we don't know, unfortunately. And it's been estimated, as you had up there, $280 billion that were pilfered through the programs that were intended for COVID relief.\par \par \uc2 And they also say it was about $123 billion that have been misused. And so it was used as not intended, if you will. But you're right. The more you hand out, the more money that goes out, the harder it is to track.\par \par \uc2 But the question is what kind of safeguards do we have in place? And do we have any on the front end, or are we only looking in the rearview mirror? And that's the big problem.\par \par \uc2 So with the Select Subcommittee for the Pandemic, this is what we're looking into. And so we have reached out to get questions, but -- questions answered. But our agencies have investigatory abilities. And we're hearing now that, oh, we just need more money to try and track this stuff down.\par \par \uc2 But they already have these abilities at Department of Justice, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , at the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , SEC, DHS. They all have abilities. And what we're trying to do is an after-action review and get some lessons learned. But the biggest problem - - one of the bigger problems that we had -- and this has come out from the inspector generals that talk about this -- is that we put in -- in Congress, we put in self-certification.\par \par \uc2 In other words, you just say that you qualify, and you're able to get these funds. I would tell you, Ways and Means Committee, Republicans were against that, but Democrats insisted on it. And, of course, that went through.\par \par \uc2 Another concern that's risen to the table has to do with the PPP program, where the Treasury has a list of people that you do not pay. And, unfortunately, that was not used in the PPP program.\par \par \uc2 So, some money...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, you're talking about the Paycheck Protection Program.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of the -- at the time, I think you acknowledged, and we were talking, I think, at the time, when COVID first happened, it was get the money out to the people who needed it as rapidly as possible. Officials and experts, according to this, sir, had said that a lot of this occurred going back to the Trump administration as the pandemic was unfolded.\par \par \uc2 I'm not blaming him any more than I am, by the way, President Biden at this time. But it was poorly designed in that there was no adequate check program for that. I assume things have improved since, or how would you define it?\par \par \uc2 WENSTRUP: Hopefully, things are improving. Certainly, the awareness is there.\par \par \uc2 But we're looking in the rearview mirror now, and we have to look forward. You're right. I don't blame Biden any more than Trump. This is an administrative type of problem that we have that should be nonpartisan. It's both sides of the aisle can say that we did rush this.\par \par \uc2 That was the feeling. If you remember, this was an unprecedented time, and this was an unprecedented amount of spending. But the problem we have is, how much of it is actually stolen and going to our adversaries. And we have got to have better guardrails in place going forward.\par \par \uc2 So we're looking into what was done, and not necessarily in a vindictive way. There may be some reasons to be vindictive, but we have had an economic attack on our country, and we have got to figure out where we went wrong, why we didn't do as well as we could have, why we're not able to recover much of the money that was taken out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 WENSTRUP: Those are all questions we need answered, so that we can correct the problem in the future.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I admire the fact you're trying to get to the bottom of that. So, the more this develops, Congressman, I'd love to have you back to see what you're uncovering.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, have a safe weekend, sir.\par \par \uc2 WENSTRUP: Yes, you do the same. Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, our attention focused on Florida right now, how they're trying to rebuild or more like regroup right now.\par \par \uc2 Max Gorden is in Florida with more -- Max.\par \par \uc2 MAX GORDEN, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Structures have been turned to twisted metal here in Florida's Big Bend region.\par \par \uc2 We will have the latest on the recovery efforts after Idalia slammed the state.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the president will be in Florida tomorrow touring damaged areas from Hurricane Idalia with Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Max Gorden is in Cedar Key, Florida, with how things are looking there -- Max.\par \par \uc2 GORDEN: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, the cleanup effort is well under way here on Cedar Key. Not only did we deal with tremendous storm surge, but also strong winds. Where I'm standing right now, there was a gas station canopy. That toppled over. It's now since been chunked up, and it's going to be carted away.\par \par \uc2 But now gas trucks have replaced this gas station. They're refilling first responders, fire trucks, police cars, as well as ambulances, to make sure that first responders can still get out in this community. When this first -- storm first struck, there were dozens of rescues that had to be made.\par \par \uc2 But, thankfully, the death toll from this storm has been significantly less than Hurricane Ian. Only one person has been confirmed to have died in Hurricane Idalia, according to Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . People here on Cedar Key, they're lucky that nobody died here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TAMI WILKES, CEDAR KEY HEALTHCARE: We have some businesses and some homes that didn't do very well, but, as a whole, I think everyone was very prepared.\par \par \uc2 This time, most of the residents left, packed, were ready to go and had their places ready and secured. So, for the most part, they did quite well. We do have a lot of work to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GORDEN: And there is a lot of work here to do as well, people mucking out their buildings, shops, stores, restaurants, their homes.\par \par \uc2 And energy is starting to get back on here. The lights are coming back on. \uc2 Duke Energy\uc2 , one of the power providers here, says that 95 percent of customers who had their power knocked out in the hardest-hit areas are going to have their power back on by late Sunday night, so some good news there.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, as you mentioned there, President Biden going to be on the ground here in Florida tomorrow, touring the destruction. He signed a major disaster declaration. That should free up some federal funding for folks here in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, FEMA requesting four billion more dollars for their disaster fund because there have been so many disasters across the country -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, almost nonstop, to your point, Max.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for that, Max Gorden.\par \par \uc2 GORDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, my next guest says the people of Florida need help, but, sometimes, you need to be focused on getting that help. Time is a wasting.\par \par \uc2 Dave Dodson, his name. Of course, he's very familiar to a lot of FOX viewers, has a great book out, "The Manager's Handbook: Five Simple Steps to Build a Team, Stay Focused, Make Better Decisions, and Crush Your Competition."\par \par \uc2 Once again, Dave, I'm trying to tailor this to the events in Florida. And you talk a lot in the book about key performance indicators and a way to track progress. I could just as easily apply it here and the challenge for president, working with the governor, to get help to these people, and quickly. How do you do that?\par \par \uc2 DAVE DODSON, STANFORD UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF BUSINESS: Well, that's exactly right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the concepts in the book, "The Manager's Handbook," the skills of trying to stay focused, are just as applicable whether you make baseball gloves or whether you're trying to deal with a hurricane that's happening in Florida.\par \par \uc2 And what's been really nice to see with this particular hurricane is that people do seem to be focused, and we are adopting the skills that are necessary in order to stay focused on, what are the key performance indicators?\par \par \uc2 Like, how quickly does electricity get put on? How quickly are the roads available, so emergency vehicles can get in and people can get back to their homes, so that you're not just sort of, like, running around trying to figure out whatever is the shiny object or the dumpster fire you're trying to take care of, but you have a clear plan and a clear focus.\par \par \uc2 And that's the difference between people like Sam Walton, who built \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , and people who are not focused and people who are not able to get things done.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I wonder too.\par \par \uc2 There's a great story in there, and one of my favorite things with your book is some of the fun people you quote, including \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , of course, who said, it really doesn't matter who gets the credit, as long as the job is done. I'm oversimplifying here.\par \par \uc2 But you're going to have a little bit of a competition between a Democratic president and a Republican governor, either taking a bow or trying to get it along and saying it couldn't get done without them.\par \par \uc2 Now, to be fair to both men, they have not played that game. But there is always the concern and the paranoia one has with the other that the other guy is going to get credit for doing something that he did. How do you avoid that?\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Well, Neil, maybe I'm idealistic about it, but I think, in the end, Americans are smart.\par \par \uc2 And they can see that there are some people who are really good at getting things done and some people who just are not very good at getting things done. And talk only lasts so long. And so I think, if you see a -- for example, a Biden administration that's working with DeSantis and they're getting things done, there's -- fine.\par \par \uc2 There's credit for everybody. I firmly believe that the people in Florida, for example, they don't really care right now. Whether they're a Democrat or Republican, they don't care who gets the credit. They want their lights put on. They want their loved ones found. They want to find out where their dog is.\par \par \uc2 They want to figure out how to get their roof fixed. They want to plan on insurance going forward. That's what they're looking for. And then the political party or the politician that is able to pull that off, they will get rewarded and they will get reelected.\par \par \uc2 But the person that's all talk and no action, that doesn't know how to get things done, which is, of course, what I talk about so -- about so passionately in "The Manager's Handbook," well, American people are going to figure that out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, sometimes, you can have a crowd of people all trying to rush at the same time. You have the president, the governor, and all their people and their people, and the mayors, and the councilmen and everything else.\par \par \uc2 I likened it to what you're talking about even with meetings, where you talk about choosing your attendees intentionally and not overdoing it. I'm simplifying it. But there is a risk that both sides could pile into this.\par \par \uc2 And you don't want that many cooks in the kitchen right?\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 And it may seem so sort of pedestrian to talk about how you run a meeting, but the effects actually are sort of volcanic, in the sense that we spend 40 percent of our time in meetings. And, interestingly enough, a survey which I reference in the book is that 50 percent of managers think that those meetings are either ineffective or highly ineffective.\par \par \uc2 That is an extraordinary waste of time. But now imagine you're the governor of Florida or you're head of FEMA, and you have got to assemble the right people there. Well, you can't run a meeting where you assemble 17 people there, of which 15 of those people are checking their e-mails.\par \par \uc2 You need the people there that are able to get -- get things done, that are -- have a purpose for the meeting. And you need to run the meeting in a way -- for example, as you know, I quote \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  a lot...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: ... in how he runs his meetings at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , that you run a very efficient meeting, so you get in and you get out, so people can get back to work, so they can, in the case of Florida, for example, get people back in their homes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Good concept.\par \par \uc2 I love Bezos, because he just walks out of his own meeting sometimes if they're dragging on.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Dave Dodson, all, all valuable information. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Dave Dodson, "Manager's Handbook," it can apply to any endeavor, even historic ones, like what's going on in Florida tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Something historical for Taylor Swift coming up here. You know she's made a princely sum on all of her concerts. Well, now some of them are going to come to a movie theater near you, $29 million in presales for something that will debut in October.\par \par \uc2 She's printing money, but she is changing an industry. We will explore -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR SWIFT, MUSICIAN: We're about to go on a little adventure together, and that adventure is going to span 17 years of music.\par \par \uc2 How does that sound?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I think she made, what, with this thing, a billion dollars off of that tour, The Eras Tour, and now it's coming to a movie theater near you, sort of a best-of concert feature that AMC reports has already gotten about $29 million in pre-reveal ticket sales.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that is unheard of, coming in October.\par \par \uc2 My guests aren't too surprised by this. Kacie McDonnell Hosmer joins us, the FOX Nation host. Kat Timpf is, co-host of "Gutfeld," "You Can't Joke About That."\par \par \uc2 No joking about this, Kat. This is serious dough. And it was just announced. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 KAT TIMPF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I wish that I was a Taylor Swift fan. I'm not, not a fan.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: I just really wish that I was into it, because the amount of passionate joy and love that these people experience whenever anything related to Taylor Swift at all comes up, like, maybe my life is lacking something. That's how I feel when I see all of this.\par \par \uc2 It's absolute mania, and I think that's amazing, amazing for her and amazing for everybody that feels that way. It makes me feel like there must be a hole in my own life of some kind.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I mean, she's no Adele, but I kind of understand what you're saying.\par \par \uc2 But -- I'm kidding here. I'm kidding.\par \par \uc2 Kacie, what do you make of it here? Because, obviously, tickets are hard to come by to see her concerts, and if you were so lucky, you or your parents or someone else paid through the nose for them. At least this is just a movie ticket and you get to relive it. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 KACIE MCDONNELL HOSMER, FOX NATION HOST: That was absolutely what I was going to say, Neil. People were taking out, what, mortgages to take their kids to these things.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's true.\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL HOSMER: Like $2,000 tickets. Everyone's like, ooh, lucky me, what a deal. It's like a \uc2 Groupon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I think about -- I like Taylor Swift. I mean, I can appreciate her. I technically grew up with her. We're similar in age, so you go through those same growing pains at the same times. But I think now, to Kat's points...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I did that with Lawrence Welk, the same thing.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But neither of you, that doesn't ring a bell.\par \par \uc2 But go ahead. Finish that great thought.\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL HOSMER: No, but I think it's like, I'm like at \uc2 Disney\uc2 . Like, at this point, I'm just like, you know what, no, I'm just going to boycott it. Because everyone's doing it, and I'm not going to do it for that reason.\par \par \uc2 But I love that she did give out $55 million in bonuses to all of her crew, everyone working on the show, the dancers, $100,000 to the truck drivers. I mean, she is doing it right. That's a good feel for her.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, she's -- no, she's very generous with her money.\par \par \uc2 But I don't like that, because then the crew starts asking, what are you going to do for us? And I said, well, processed cheeses are still a thing.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, leaving that aside, guys, before you go, I wanted to explore this story with you, because it's all the rage on TikTok right now, where a woman is saying she's asking men for their bank balance on the first date.\par \par \uc2 In other words, if they're poor schnubs, she wants nothing to do with them.\par \par \uc2 Kat, what do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Well, I think a couple of things.\par \par \uc2 First of all, I guess points for honesty, I guess, that these guys don't have to figure out later that that's kind of what she's looking for. But just...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But on the first date, Kat.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes. Yes. I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 And also, in terms of logistics, if the guy is really wealthy, he probably doesn't keep all of that in a checking account.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, that's a very good point. I didn't think of that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, maybe just a W-2 will do there.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think, Kacie?\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL HOSMER: Yes, he's like, wait, which one do you want to see?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, touche. Touche.\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL HOSMER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL HOSMER: Yes, I think it's crazy. She's 31, so you think about it. You're like, OK, what's her dating range pool? When you're at that level -- she has her own podcast, she has this huge notoriety.\par \par \uc2 Aren't most of her dates setups anyway? So don't the people setting you up with these friends of friends probably pre-vet them and know what they do and know that they have a house in Aspen and, like, Baker's Bay and all these places?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL HOSMER: But if you do a quick \uc2 Google\uc2  search, you could just search, I guess, single billionaires. You got \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  is single. Bill Gates is single. I mean, just to have your people reach out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what if they don't pop up on \uc2 Google\uc2 ? Then, no matter what these guys fling over, Kat, isn't going to be good enough, because they're invisible on \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I also am not -- can this really be true that she had relationships that started this way? And, like, who are these people that were OK with that?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's a good point.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: I don't think that -- I mean, they didn't -- I don't think they worked out. I'm not sure.\par \par \uc2 But I can't imagine anybody being OK with that and being like, this is the great -- the best way to start out a relationship. What a great foundation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Ugh. I don't know.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it starts with about -- it's about the money. It's about the money.\par \par \uc2 She's no Taylor Swift or Adele, is she?\par \par \uc2 Ladies, thank you both very much. It's a -- I guess a sad commentary on our times, but it is what it is.\par \par \uc2 All right, that will do it here.\par \par \uc2 The Dow, just to let you know, finished off the day up 150 points. We're going to explore the battle between an improving economy still looking OK, or so that's what we're told, and fears that it's not going to be OK for much longer. We will explore that and the president and the governor in Florida. A lot coming up tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Here's "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230902ej9100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Adds Two More Natural Phenomena To The World; White House's Favorite Electric Bus Maker Files For Bankruptcy; Democrats D.C. Councilman Says City Needs National Guard; In-Person Work Is Racist: L.A. Times: Remote Work Is Better For Minorities; A.I. Pets Could Replace Dogs And Cats; Driving Instructor Crashes Into Driving School\b0\par\par\uc2 Greg Gutfeld, Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Dana  Perino \par \uc2 8,293 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: There's just two candidates to have done so now. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We are keeping track of all of it for you as we get closer and closer to 2024. Remember to watch me and John Roberts every weekday 1:00 p.m. Eastern Time for "AMERICA REPORTS". THE FIVE starts now.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, I'm Greg Gutfeld along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters and she once rammed a ladybug who tried to cut her off, Dana Perino. THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 President Biden out west on a mission to save the planet from climate Armageddon. Joe touting his efforts to fight climate change during a three- day field trip. And looks like he got the senior discount on the Grand Canyon tour. The president appearing to get lost in the bushes and his thoughts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Grand Canyon one of the Earth's nine wonders -- Wonders of the World. Literally. Think of that. It's amazing. An enduring symbol of American to the entire world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's a wonder he's president. It's seven natural Wonders of the World, Mr. President. Eventually, gramps found his way to a T.V. camera, sitting down with \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  to ramble on more about global warming, and even provide some quality pest control.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2 , TWC METEOROLOGIST: Are you prepared to declare a national emergency with respect to climate change?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We already done that. Nationally, we've conserved more land. We've moved into rejoining the Paris Climate Accord. We passed a $360 billion climate control facility. We're moving. We it see it as the existential threat to America.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: So, you've already declared that national emergency.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, in practice, you have a bug on you.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Oh, thanks. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 So, you've already declared that National emergency?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Practically speaking, yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Too bad \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  wasn't around to shield Joe from the Hunter hurricane. Our very own Peter Doocy literally hopping the fence to get Biden's first comments on his son's business dealings.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: There's this testimony now, where one of your son's former business associates is claiming that you were on speakerphone a lot with them talking business, is that --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I never talked business with anybody, and I knew you'd have a lousy question.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Well, what do you -- why is that a lousy question?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because it's not true.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Thank you, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right. And it looks like the wheels in the climate bus are falling off. Joe and Kamala's beloved electric vehicle company is filing for bankruptcy despite all this, cheerleading.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're going to end up owning the future, I think, if you keep -- we keep doing what we're doing. We ought to be the single most significant suppliers of electric buses and vehicles in the world before it's over. Right now, we're running way behind China. But you guys are getting us into the game.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Judge, welcome back. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Thank you. It's good to be back at the table to rob some words from my fellow --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You have a delightful vacation glow.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, thank you. I appreciate that you noticed.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Looks like you must have had some fun, I'd like to hear more about it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Later.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: So, do you find it funny that he is on \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  after like, I don't know, what is it like 20 phone calls where apparently he was only talking about the weather?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, you know, I think it's interesting because even when I was in Europe, I was reading about Devon Archer, and all of the stuff that he started talking about in terms of Joe's being aware, not just being on a speakerphone, but being at dinners and other things. And it was rather stunning to me that nobody was talking about it in the mainstream media, but not so stunning.\par \par \uc2 But I think this whole thing with the electric vehicles and \uc2 Proterra\uc2 , the thing that gets to me is Elizabeth Granholm. OK, so she's the Secretary of Energy, correct? And she's a woman who had invested in \uc2 Proterra\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And aside from the ethical issues of her promoting \uc2 Proterra\uc2 . You've got Biden and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , specifically promoting a company that the Secretary of Energy has invested interest in, she exercises her stock options and then sells their stock 12 days later, after the government has invested millions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is about as conflict as anything I've seen, and I think it brooches on the issue of the criminality.\par \par \uc2 And you have to say to yourself, I mean, are these guys so corrupt that they're willing to do it in your face, and then the company goes bust? It's like \uc2 Solyndra\uc2 , we put all our taxpayer's dollars in it. And we make sure that the taxpayers the last person ever get paid back.\par \par \uc2 But having said that, I just want to say one thing, when I was involved and there were all these little cars. And I said to one of the car drivers, I said, are you guys all drive these tiny cars on the electric cars. And the man says to me, no, no, no, I hate electric cars. They burn, they go on fire all the time.\par \par \uc2 Now, that's just a comment from, you know, a cab driver in Malta. So, everybody knows that this electric vehicle thing is nonsense.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know, I heard that when Joe first got to the Grand Canyon, his first words were when did they put my face up here?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, because that would be a big, big monument, another Wonder of the World.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: He was about 800 miles south of where that would have actually taken place. I applaud Peter Doocy. I imagine that the president waved him over. And that's why the Secret Service let him crawl over a fence. I would say don't try that at home or around the Secret Service, unless you get the explicit invitation.\par \par \uc2 What I think is interesting is that now the White House will say the president answered this. Well, no now -- because now you just contradicted your press secretary who contradicted herself.\par \par \uc2 So, at some point, somebody is going to get a chance to actually ask President Biden a question where he's not surrounded by people saying, that's enough, that's enough, after one little question like that. So that's not good enough.\par \par \uc2 On the climate thing, Biden has spent a historic amount of money to try to deal with climate change. And he has a 57 percent disapproval rating on climate change, especially from the left, they're not happy.\par \par \uc2 So, the left has created a monster that they can't control. And they're not done yet.\par \par \uc2 This is an amazing thing I learned this week. So, the trucking industry, the manufacturers of the trucking industry are facing some really big regulations, they are coming down the pike January 1st, they have to go to this electric vehicle nonsense.\par \par \uc2 OK, maybe it's a worthwhile goal, however, get this. The manufacturers are saying you're doing it too much, too fast. For a diesel-burning engine, which is pretty clean, emissions are way down. It takes 15 minutes to fill up the truck, and that truck can then go 1200 miles. With these electric vehicles, these trucks takes six to 10 hours to charge and they can go 250 miles.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's amazing.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Do you think that's not going to increase prices for absolutely everybody, because everything that gets delivered is going to take so much longer?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That is crazy, Jesse, do you think he went on \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  because he loves it when Hunter makes it rain?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: He's never talking to his son about the rain, Greg or anybody.\par \par \uc2 You know who owns \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 , this big Democrat donor named Byron Allen, he bought it for a couple $100 million because he said that people are all going to die unless we warn them that they're all going to die.\par \par \uc2 So, this is just like the safest possible space that he could possibly go. And she asks him, when are you going to declare a climate emergency? Is that on as Jay -- what KJP would say is that's not top of mind. No one is out there clamoring when you're going to declare a climate emergency. And his answer was gobbledygook. He goes, we spent $300 billion on a climate control facility.\par \par \uc2 I would love to see this facility, what is that's going to control the climate? He doesn't know what he's talking about.\par \par \uc2 And then he just basically wolves off the entire million acres around the Grand Canyon for uranium mining. So, now we're completely dependent on uranium from the Russians and the Kazakhstan, these two countries that just happened to pay his son a lot of money and give him a \uc2 Porsche\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The E.V. bus deal is a pump-and-dump scheme. You had Granholm, you had \uc2 Al Gore\uc2 , you had Soros all pour money into this stupid electric bus company, and the buses never worked. They broke down all over the country. A lot of them caught fire. And then now it's a penny stock.\par \par \uc2 So, there needs to be an investigation there. How are you going to say I never talked to them about business? You were at dinner with a \uc2 Burisma\uc2  executive for an hour. OK. What do you talk about then? Did you talk about the weather for an hour, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? Or how about the other stationery from the vice president thanking Devon Archer for going into business with your son?\par \par \uc2 OK, so you go into business with your son and you don't know what the business is? Or how about the voicemail? Oh, yes. Read the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  article about the business that you're dealing with China. You're in the clear. Give me a call.\par \par \uc2 Come on. Jessica. Come on.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, come on, Jessica. I don't even know what that means.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Come one.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: I know what it means. Can I go?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: OK, great. If I want to talk about climate change, I'm going to go on \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  to talk about climate change. It seems like a pretty obvious place.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's so vain.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Not just me. I mean, anyone. Like, if Republicans cared about climate change, then they should also go on \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  to talk about climate change.\par \par \uc2 Now, to Devon Archer. So, the testimony and he wasn't under oath, but he still wasn't allowed to lie to them, was terrible for the Republicans. Jim Comer didn't even show up to think -- well, let's use real words than instead of laughing or saying come on.\par \par \uc2 He was asked if someone concluded from the 1023 form that you all think is so important that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was bribed, would you disagree with that? Yes, I would. Devon Archer said that.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What does he know about that?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, then he doesn't matter, then Devon Archer is either the crux of the puzzle that shows that this is a mob family or he doesn't matter.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, when he's in the middle of it, if he's in the middle of a dinner, then it matters. If it's something that he's not there, then it doesn't matter.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: OK. Viktor Shokin was not good for \uc2 Burisma\uc2  as I've been telling you, as has been widely reported as the entire Western world.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Why?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Because he wasn't investigating --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, he was.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, he wasn't.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: He was seizing their cars.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Then, what was he doing? What was Shokin doing?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'm so glad that you're back. OK. Hold on one second.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's not good.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Did Hunter ever asked Joe to take any official actions? Devon Archer, he did not.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: How would he know?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: They talk on the phone every day.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Also the calls which there are 20 calls over the course of a decade, he said weren't just the weather. He said they did talk about family and fishing.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That was a wink and a nod. Did you see the interview? Yes, we talked about the weather.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: You guys will say anything.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's what he said.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: There's a new oversight menu -- memo that's out today, which again does not explain what services were provided at the Biden.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: They didn't have any services.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Why is he getting the money?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: What did they do?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We're asking the same question.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I should have said this last week, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  which you said we run stands for the International Monetary --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We formed the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , it's our rich people.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Everybody funds the \uc2 IMF\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's our rich guys trying to get the prosecutor away from a company that we want to do business with.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I have been quiet on my boat.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, it's over. Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I think we should move on. I think we solved one of the world's most pressing problems by yelling. You know, it's an underrated art, just yell. That's all you got to do.\par \par \uc2 Up next, crimes getting so insane in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's America, it's got the Democrats talking about bringing in the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Sending the troops, Democrats are beginning to sound like Republicans as crime continues to explode in their cities, take D.C., which is on pace to have the deadliest year in two decades.\par \par \uc2 Shocking security footage just released shows the moment when one of Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  staffers was randomly stabbed while walking around in our nation's capital back in March. Police say the suspect had been released from prison just a day earlier.\par \par \uc2 And it was a bloodsoak start to this month, the surge of shootings in D.C. left 13 people dead in the first five days. Democratic D.C. councilmember thinks it's time the National Guard step in to restore order.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRAYON WHITE, COUNCILMAN, WASHINGTON D.C.: I have spoken with the D.C. National Guard. And well, I have to have more conversation with the chief police than I have today, and the mayor and my colleagues, we have a long way to get there. We just know that police alone is not getting it done.\par \par \uc2 We are clearly in a war zone. If you don't know you are in a war zone, to me, you haven't been here.\par \par \uc2 Now, government has to step up. Our police department has to step up. And our residents have to step up.\par \par \uc2 We're tired of this -- and enough is enough. Excuse my language but I want to be quite frank.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Wow. But that's what happens when it's all crime and no punishment. D.C. didn't prosecute 67 percent of those arrested last year, thanks to defund the police.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Greg, I think we're now reaching a point where people in these cities, Democrats are begging for someone to do something.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Forgive me if I sound a bit racist. But these stories seem to all look alike.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Meaning in each case, Trump offered a solution for it years ago, and was condemned in every case. In every case, he said something, like, you need more police, you need the National Guard. And because I call it -- what do I call it, the Trump containment disorder -- contamination disorder. You can't be seen agreeing with anything that Trump believes in, right? Because you can't be at a cocktail party and say, I agree with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It's how petty the media is, they'd rather argue against a good point than defend -- and actually defend the criminal, than be seen somehow on the same side as Trump.\par \par \uc2 The problem with these cities is a lot of the good people are going t