ar \uc2 GUTFELD: I do too.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It looks great.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We got scammed.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: OK, up next -- oh no, that's it. I hope you liked it. "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OH, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Time now for "ONE MORE THING." Judge.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, so I was away last week. If you're interested, I went on a cruise with my family. That's my son, Alex and Christine. They got engaged on the trip. And there we are with me and my son, Alex and Christine, and her mother, who came on this trip from Rome to Malta. And there's just part of the crew that went -- there's about 20 of us I think. And we all had a great time. And it was a cruise until we got to Malta, and then Malta, we stayed for a couple of days. They were going into the blue grotto. It's like everybody had a dock to get in there.\par \par \uc2 And as it relates to Red, Ted, and Stella, they were at a farm. And you see what that circle is? There were chickens on the farm. Stella posted herself there like three hours a day watching chickens.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Are there still chickens on the farm?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: There are still chickens on the farm. But it's great to be back.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Great to have you back.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: So, those farms are real. You know, when they --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Oh, when they send them away?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, that's good. Trooper had a good life then. Tonight, 11:00 p.m. Joe Machi, \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , Kat Timpf, Tyrus. Oh, and Kat Timpf relating her local Nick Gilder. He's not on but I just thought I threw that out there. Tonight, well, let's do this. Greg's Itchy Bear News. Now, just imagine that you're this bear. And imagine how good this feels. This is somewhere in a forest. Oh, look at that. Does that feel great or what? He's been going around all day in the forest looking for the right tree. He teach kids how to do the right -- he's there scratching.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, look at that.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, look at that.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Am I doing this right, mom?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know what? They might need a moisturizer or some better conditioner for their hair.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: They're cute.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Aren't they nice? And then they'll maul you to death. That's a real adorable part about bears. They're so cute but then they'll rip you to shreds. Much like Jessica, you're next.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Great. So, he's used to being a star on the court. But being a rock star was a new experience for Warriors guard, Steph Curry. He was invited on stage as a special guest during the Paramore San Francisco concert. He helped the lead singer Hayley Williams belt out the lyrics to their 2007 hit song of Misery Business. Paramore holds a special place in Steph and his wife Ayesha's heart. They bonded over the band's music. He knew every line which I was very impressed with. You go, girl.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Wow. That was -- that's a terrible "ONE MORE THING."\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It is not.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It is awful.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I agree.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: What?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Dana, show her what a good one --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You're going to love mine.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Does it involves a child doing something great?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: No, it's even better. Today is National Book Lovers Day.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I love books.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, it's a perfect day to remind everyone. You should support your local independent bookstore. Mine is Little Point Bookshop in Point Pleasant Beach, New Jersey. Last week, I got to interview Patti Henry -- Patti Callahan Henry. She wrote The Secret Life of Flora -- The Secret Life of Flora Lea, not book and -- The Secret Book of Flora Lea. Sorry. Excuse me. You made me some nervous, Greg.\par \par \uc2 Three fiction summer reads. I love this year. Demon Copperhead, Secret Book of Flora Lea, and The Covenant of Water. I highly recommend it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh, fiction.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: The best.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Give me some nonfiction, Jesse. Write about --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What is wrong with you today? You were just off your rocker today. Tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME," the January 6th --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Cover up.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I can't. I can't. I can't. I can't. I can't do it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Why?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I can't do it. I don't want to say why I'm not going to do it, but I'm just not going to do it. It's the right thing not to do it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Do you think that's a good tease?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's the best tease ever.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I'm definitely tuning in.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't know. It's so -- it's too far even for Jesse.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's too far for Jesse.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for us. Have a great night. See you.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Bye.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020230810ej8900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Russian Fighter Jet Hits U.S. Drone With Flare; Senate Holds Hearing On A.I. Concerns; Record Heat Wave; Gilgo Beach Murder Investigation; UPS Averts Strike; \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  Calls For Narrower Republican Presidential Field\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Keith Kellogg, Aishah Hasnie, Steve Bender, Max  Gorden, Ted Williams, Daron Shaw, Mark Meredith, Nate Foy \par \uc2 7,871 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Having a fit over Mitt. \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  calling on donors to drop the guys who can't win and get behind a candidate who can, or at least one who can take on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A Tim Scott donor here to say, look no further, why his guy is that guy.\par \par \uc2 And how a Dow that. The Dow Jones industrials notching on another gain today. That's 12 in a row, if it holds, in case you're counting. Now, it didn't hurt that UPS reached a tentative contract with 340,000 unionized workers. But will it hold?\par \par \uc2 We have got the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president here, and only here, who says it will, good deal, and he insists done deal.\par \par \uc2 "Your World" right now.\par \par \uc2 All right, welcome, everybody. Glad to have you.\par \par \uc2 Man, oh, man, what a busy news day. More on that big UPS deal in a moment and what could keep it together.\par \par \uc2 First to how the White House is responding to this news and so much more news.\par \par \uc2 Mark Meredith with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration and his reelection campaign, they are both talking a lot about the economy. And now it appears the White House is also breathing a sigh of relief that UPS workers have reached a tentative deal to avoid a strike.\par \par \uc2 We got a statement from the president. He said: "I applaud the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  and UPS for coming together, negotiating in good faith and reaching a tentative agreement today that will avoid a shutdown at UPS."\par \par \uc2 A report is also out today showing Americans are feeling more confident about spending their hard-earned money. The Conference Board releasing their latest results and finding, despite rising interest rates, consumers are more upbeat, likely reflecting lower inflation and a tight labor market. Although consumers are less convinced of a recession ahead, we still anticipate one likely before year's end."\par \par \uc2 Now, the timing here matters for the president because, of course, it will matter what it means for his reelection. He's still facing questions about his age, but, if it's a strong economy, it would almost certainly keep additional Democratic challengers at bay, and, for Republicans, this race would also be a bit tougher to navigate.\par \par \uc2 There's a new \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  poll just out today showing Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  still in a distant second to former President Trump, Trump now at 54 percent, DeSantis at 22 percent. Now, this is a national survey, not those early contest states like Iowa or New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 The DeSantis campaign is also facing some new questions about its long-term strategy. FOX confirming at least 38 staffers have been forced out. The campaign's manager writing today: "Following a top-to-bottom review of our organization, we have taken additional aggressive steps to streamline operations."\par \par \uc2 Those operations hit a bit of a snag, though, this morning when the governor's motorcade was involved in a four-car crash. The governor wasn't hurt, and he continued with his campaign events.\par \par \uc2 As for former President Trump, he is on the road today too, Neil. He's going to have a fund-raiser near New Orleans this evening and a rally ahead this weekend -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for that, Mark Meredith.\par \par \uc2 I want to pursue that story on DeSantis and this staff firing here, a third of that staff, what to make of that, with Daron Shaw, the FOX News polling team. We have got Doug Schoen, a former Clinton adviser.\par \par \uc2 Daron, first on this shakeup within the campaign, you don't want to make too big a deal out of something like this, but his campaign hasn't been officially running that long, and already a purge in the staff. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 DARON SHAW, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It's not a good sign when you fire a third of the people who are working for you. That's rarely a good thing.\par \par \uc2 You can make an argument that it's better now than later on, but the \uc2 FEC\uc2  filings that came out last weekend sort of confirmed this notion that they have burned through a lot of cash. And during this time when they have burned through a lot of cash, they have actually fallen in the polls at a time when Trump was being indicted.\par \par \uc2 So all of this stuff speaks to this sort of organizational questions, which is, did you build up too quickly? Did you burn through too much money without sort of a cohesive message about why you want to be president, why Republican voters should dump Trump moving into 2024?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The difference with some of the other former party luminaries who had big leads or look like the it guys, Doug, is they were the it guys at the moment.\par \par \uc2 Jeb Bush was that. Governor Pawlenty was that. And then they weren't. Throughout this race, it has been among Republicans \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Is there a difference there? Is this a reminder that the alternative to him might not be Governor DeSantis?\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FORMER BILL CLINTON POLLSTER: Yes, I think you're right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 First, the Trump lead is bigger than any lead that Governor Pawlenty or Governor Bush had, or Governor Walker, for that matter. This is a solid 30- point lead in the Monmouth poll, as in other polls. And Trump also appears to be getting stronger, if anything, based on the indictments that have come.\par \par \uc2 And I assumed, like most people, that he would in fact slip with multiple indictments, but that's clearly not the case. So Trump is solidifying his position at a time when DeSantis doesn't have a message and appears to be in freefall with a restive and declining staff as part of his campaign.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If I can switch, gentlemen, back to the original intention of our chat...\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... and that is the economy and the backdrop of the economy.\par \par \uc2 And, Daron, the one thing that you hear Republicans pound endlessly is the economy and inflation and all that. The White House counters, well, we're well off our worst levels, the 12 numbers in a row that show inflation improving, jobs are still strong and gaining. And they think this trend continues to Election Day and that Americans will feel it and show it.\par \par \uc2 They point to consumer confidence at a two-year high as proof that, whatever the Republicans are saying just ain't the reality. Is that clicking or no?\par \par \uc2 SHAW: I think it's not really clicking quite yet, Neil.\par \par \uc2 But the intent is to simply put themselves in a position where they're competitive on the economy, so they can turn to other issues. This isn't going to be morning in America, right, or even \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's 1996 reelection campaign, but if they can just sort of neutralize it.\par \par \uc2 It's fascinating. I would encourage your viewers to go out there and, if you get a chance to look at the consumer confidence reports, it varies greatly depending on whether you look at the five-year graph or you look at one-year graph. The one-year graph looks great, and I'm sure the White House will be championing that until their lungs are sore.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at the five-year graph, it's not so impressive.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're right.\par \par \uc2 SHAW: But I recall, in '92, when I was working with Bush, his economic message, nobody listened to it after about six months before the election. People tune you out. They're trying to recast things now, when they still have got an opportunity.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, because they would still have more than a year for that to register.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, Daron, don't steal my economic nerdy thunder here. It's all I have got, buddy, so just be careful with that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SHAW: I'm sorry\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you're quite right. You're quite right.\par \par \uc2 But, Doug, let me ask you a little bit about that. The administration is saying there is a difference between this president going into 2024 and, of course, \uc2 George H.W. Bush\uc2  and what he faced going into the '92 election. The recovery and the turnaround was actually happening then.\par \par \uc2 But there is, to Daron's point, that it wasn't being felt. The White House comes back and says, well, there's plenty of time for this to be felt, and that trend is our friend. Does that hold, you think?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Well, when you look at the poll numbers, Neil, as opposed to the economic data, there isn't a lot of reason to be confident, because prices are up, even if inflation is down.\par \par \uc2 Interest rates are up. There's the possibility of a recession. And when you look at the ratings the Biden administration gets, it's only about a third or so positive and 60 percent or more negative. Tough to turn that around and do what Daron said and neutralize it as an issue.\par \par \uc2 So far, it's a very strong negative and, so far, even with the better economic news, Neil, hasn't really improved at all. So it's time will tell with a healthy degree of skepticism.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, Doug, for not going knee-deep into the economic data like Daron did.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Well, I'm a pollster.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There you go.\par \par \uc2 No, you were both terrific, and I appreciate your insight here.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, be well and thank you again.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, at the corner of Wall and Broad, make it number 12, 12 days in a row for the Dow Jones industrials advancing, a level we have not seen achieved, that is, 12 up days in a row, since the earliest days of the Trump administration. So where does it go from here?\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, we might get a better idea. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  wraps up a two- day meeting, at which they are expected to go ahead and raise interest rates, at least by the market's bidding, the last time of this cycle, or so they hope. We will watch that.\par \par \uc2 Also watching \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  make a recommendation that has a lot of Republicans furious at him. They're, in fact, in a Mitt fit.\par \par \uc2 And I had to practice that a number of times.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I think what \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  is saying, time is running out for the long shots. And I'm wondering whether you believe that, that the more in the race, yourself included, the more it favors a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LARRY ELDER (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: How about giving the voters a say?\par \par \uc2 The first contest is not until January 15 in Iowa, for crying out loud. And let's remember, somebody named \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was dead in the water, dead man walking, until James Clyburn endorsed him on the eve of the South Carolina primary. And, all of a sudden, the guy that even David Axelrod said was Mr. Magoo-ing his way through becomes the party nominee and then becomes president.\par \par \uc2 So, please, lots of things can change between now and January 15, let alone between now and November of 2024.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so you have about a dozen Republicans vying for the Republican nomination, and \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  is afraid that, if some of them don't drop out, and soon, or get their donors to do so, and soon, then it's going to tee up a nominee named \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, some people might not have any problem with that. My next guest is saying, well, why are you searching for that guy? \uc2 Andy Sabin\uc2  says, he's got him. He's a big donor to Tim Scott, the South Carolina senator, joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 ANDY SABIN\uc2 , TIM SCOTT DONOR: Hey, Neil. Nice to be with you.\par \par \uc2 My friend Dick Grasso sends his love. You're his favorite.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, well...\par \par \uc2 SABIN: I will see him for dinner Saturday night.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Give him my best, the former chairman of the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you, Andy, about what -- what \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  was saying. I know you don't believe that it's your candidate who should pull out. Tim Scott is polling quite well, and he does have the resources.\par \par \uc2 But I think what Romney's getting to is that there are too many in the race, it's too crowded, it sort of tees up \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  all over again, picking off a large field.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that and that some, for the better part of the party, should go, should step down?\par \par \uc2 SABIN: No, I don't agree with him.\par \par \uc2 I think Tim Scott's the kind of guy to know him is to love him. You got to realize he started three months ago running. Look where he is now. He's third in Iowa, and the number two is cutting back his staff by a third. This guy is the real deal.\par \par \uc2 He understands what's going on. I had some major issues when I talked to Tim in the beginning. I have met him a bunch of times. Abortion, and no Republican can be elected to national office unless they believe in a 15- week abortion window.\par \par \uc2 And his view on Ukraine, where I built three orphanages and 130 ambulances. He understands defense and he understands energy. He's surging right now. And everybody I talk to tells me the same thing. They're looking for somebody, and I think Tim's the man.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, judging by the polls, Andy -- and you're a lot closer to this than I will every be -- it's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's race to lose.\par \par \uc2 Now, that can change, and polls change. At this point in late 2015, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was registering less than 1 percent. Jeb Bush looked like the guy. I can go through history and campaigns on both sides of the aisle where those polls were fleeting.\par \par \uc2 So, do you think there's something to what \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  is saying, that if the pursuit is to stop \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you need a lot of these candidates exiting the race and focus on one or two who have a chance at doing just that?\par \par \uc2 SABIN: Yes, I agree with Mitt.\par \par \uc2 I think they're going to drop out of the race because they're going to run out of money. It's simple. They do not have the resources to continue, so that's why they will drop out. And when they see people like Tim, who everybody likes, everybody -- nobody has anything bad to say about this guy.\par \par \uc2 I went to a \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  dinner at -- in Boca Raton. This has been very early. And Reince Priebus says to me, he says: "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to say something. Nobody wants to listen to him. We're going to put him in a corner for 10 minutes."\par \par \uc2 And look what happened. He ended up being president. This guy is the real deal. He has nothing bad to say about anybody, except \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, that might work.\par \par \uc2 Then again, there are some controversies there. I didn't know if you knew this, Andy -- and I will replay a portion from my interview with Senator Scott not too long ago -- but I think a disqualifying issue could be his love of the movie "Rocky III." It's true. Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Let's go. I love "Rocky III."\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, there we go. I pity the fool who thinks otherwise.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Senator, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I pity the fool!\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, that's pretty good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: "Rocky III," not "Rocky I," "Rocky III."\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 SABIN: I -- one thing with politicians, I have been around this. I was at Reagan's inauguration. I think I'm a few years older than you. I'm 77, and you're what, 37?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I love you. You're back on tomorrow. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SABIN: People -- by the way, all politicians, what I have learned, all these -- and I probably backed 70 or 80 House candidates, all the senators.\par \par \uc2 They're entitled to change their mind. They're entitled to evolve.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SABIN: And nobody's going to say the right thing all the time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 But I really would have gone for the first "Rocky" motion picture. But I digress.\par \par \uc2 In all seriousness, what I think that illustrated, Andy, is, he has great sense of humor. I think that is in short supply today in all professions, present company accepted.\par \par \uc2 I don't know why we don't embrace more of that, because it shows more of their humanity. But what do you think?\par \par \uc2 SABIN: Yes, well, I agree. I mean, my mission in life is to save the planet and save humanity.\par \par \uc2 What I'd like to see Tim do, and I have told him this, in this field, I want 