eal both sides say will avoid a workers strike of more than 325,000 such workers.\par \par \uc2 I caught up with Sean O'Brien, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president, on how they got that thing done.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEAN O'BRIEN, GENERAL PRESIDENT, TEAMSTERS: We went out and talked to our members and listened to our rank-and-file members who have to work in these conditions, and we got direction and guidance from them.\par \par \uc2 We eliminated two-tier wage systems. We put air conditioners in trucks. We also improved pensions. We maintain health care here. We have done a lot of great work. We got UPS to agree \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  Day as a paid holiday.\par \par \uc2 We have made so many, so many improvements in this contract, all for the benefit of our members. We took care of the part-timers, driving that starting wage rate up, taking care of long-term part-timers, make sure they're rewarded and everybody was rewarded for their hard work, so tremendous victory. It's a tremendous victory for labor.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sean, I know a big issue was this two-tier wage system, so that it would be a little different for newer workers coming in than older workers already there.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if that's been ironed out, but I'm told that it still exists. And I wonder if that's going to rankle members when it comes to voting among membership, I believe August 3. I think it concludes August 22. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: So, once this contract is ratified retroactively, there will be no two-tier wage system, formerly known as a 22.4, which was a concession that was given to by the last administration.\par \par \uc2 We have leveraged our position of strength, and the company has agreed to make everybody regular package car drivers and pay everybody and treat everybody accordingly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, another part of this, and it grew in importance just with the weather outside and how hot it is, was that all new UPS trucks would be air-conditioned.\par \par \uc2 I was surprised here that they weren't already. But that doesn't mean that existing trucks will be air-conditioned. So how did you sort that out?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: So, in the meantime, the existing trucks will be equipped with two fans. They're going to modify the cargo areas with more ventilation.\par \par \uc2 So it's a short-term fix, but we made history by getting the company to agree every single new vehicle that's put into existence will have air conditioning.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sean, you had kept saying that UPS could well afford to make some adjustments and more conciliatory moves on behalf of workers, that its profits had gone up 140 percent, I believe something to that effect, since the last contract.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, only that resonated with some management types, who feared that this would lead to a long-term chafing among workers, whether they were striking or not. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, I think by what we achieved at the bargaining table, a couple of things have happened.\par \par \uc2 First and foremost, we're taking care of our members that go into work every single day, especially the part-timers. This is going to improve their lives tremendously, but it's also going to add stability to UPS' work force.\par \par \uc2 When you're paying people a livable wage, when you're paying people for the hard work that they do and treating them with respect and dignity, they're going to retain employees. So I think it's a win-win for both of us, but, more importantly, for our part-timers.\par \par \uc2 This is the first time ever that our part-timers, in some instances, based on their years of service, will be getting larger wage increases than full- timers. So, this is historic.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A lot of people, when they see a deal reach and one with generous benefits and wage hikes that you helped lead, that someone's going to pay for this.\par \par \uc2 And it might be customers of UPS, that they might be seeing higher package rates in the future and right through the holidays. What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Look, it's like anything else. We can't predict what UPS is going to do as far as their pricing, but UPS has given generous stock options, more than they should have.\par \par \uc2 So maybe they take a look at their executive compensation. Maybe they take a look at their dividend returns and maybe adjust accordingly and not hurt the consumer.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The company seemed to want to avoid a disastrous strike, because, obviously, these kind of things are problematic for both sides here.\par \par \uc2 But some interpreted the company quickly caving on a lot of this, but that the better part of valor was to settle it now because business is booming right now and they didn't want to jeopardize that. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, I'm not sure what their motive was, but I know we pressured them tremendously.\par \par \uc2 While we were doing two weeks of practice picketing, we have had a great media strategy exposing the unwillingness of UPS to reward part-timers and the full-timers, especially in light of making $100 billion. So I think the pressure was being felt not only from internally in UPS, but also the general public.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We're told that the growing role of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and its own fleet of trucks, planes, other modes of transportation to get packages delivered was a very big pressure point for management, the pay and all the benefits you talk about for senior leaders at the company notwithstanding, that it was the specter of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  benefiting in a vacuum where a strike could go on.\par \par \uc2 I believe, a quarter-century ago, that one went on for a little more than two weeks. And that is what helped drive this deal. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, I think UPS was looking over their shoulders to see who would take the 