ed that house inside and out, actually ripped it apart. In the end, they looked for a lot of things, such as?\par \par \uc2 TED WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, they're looking for physical evidence to tie Heuermann to the killing of the Gilgo Beach Four.\par \par \uc2 Neil, one of the questions in this case is, could Heuermann been identified as a killer early on in this investigation? And I can tell you, I believe that the Suffolk County Police Department there may very well have dropped the ball.\par \par \uc2 It was in 2010 that that department was in some turmoil. And if we go back to 2010, Neil, there was a gentleman by the name of Dave Schaller. He comes home in the middle of the night and he finds his friend Amber Costello locked in a bathroom. And on the other side was Rex Heuermann trying to get into the bathroom.\par \par \uc2 What happened was, Dave Schaller and Rex Heuermann got into a fight. Rex Heuermann, who was 6 feet, 6 inches tall, weighing over 300 pounds, left the area.\par \par \uc2 Shortly thereafter, Amber Costello was found dead. She is one of the alleged victims of Rex Heuermann. But the key to all of this was that Dave Schaller went to the authorities right away, Neil, and told the authorities what he had observed. He gave them the physical makeup of Heuermann . He also told them about the truck that he was driving.\par \par \uc2 And guess what, Neil? It looks as though the police department there dropped the ball and didn't follow up on that phase of the investigation, which was significant and could very well have brought this man to justice a lot sooner.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, there's a lot we won't know about that, to your point, Ted.\par \par \uc2 I did want to ask you about this fear of other victims and that, much like the story around Ted Bundy, where there are other cities, other women, other corpses, that that could be the case here, and that evidence to that might have been found in the home. What do you think?\par \pa