ts all the way down to Charlotte, Music City, Little Rock, out towards Dodge City.\par \par \uc2 And notice St. Louis getting to 109, where it's just dangerous to be outside. The only relief that you're going to find on the map here, Neil, is that red streak, the higher elevation of the Appalachian Mountains. If you are on the valley, along the coast or into the interior Ohio Valley, into the Plains, it will feel like you are into the triple digits, and then some, for days to come.\par \par \uc2 These are temperatures here in the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2 , New York City, we haven't felt since August 10 of 2021. So it's been a considerable amount of time since we have felt temperatures like this, since we have had an excessive heat warning.\par \par \uc2 Looking at New York City, this stretch goes from Thursday, Friday, Saturday. Here's the good news. By Sunday, we get a cold front that comes in, brings some relief for the Northeast. We will be dipping back in the mid 80s. But look at the rest of the country. It's bottled up. This heat continues, and we're seeing all-time streaks be -- continue to be broken, Phoenix 110-plus for three weeks straight.\par \par \uc2 Tucson, Arizona, hasn't seen temperatures below 100 in the afternoon for 40 days straight. Those type of streaks continue. So, Neil, not only is this pushing into new areas like Kansas City and St. Louis, but we're seeing the longevity of this heat wave continue to expand in areas like the Desert Southwest.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just remarkable.\par \par \uc2 BENDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Steve, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Steve Bender following all of that.\par \par \uc2 Also following a hearing going on, on artificial intelligence. In fact, I still think it's going on. And it gets heated at times.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie with that in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 This is all about regulating A.I. And, sometimes, it can feel like it's going so slow that some lawmakers on the Hill are now calling it a moonshot. Coming up, I will tell you how close we are to landing on the moon.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, they had a lot to talk about it. I don't know whatever comes of it.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill following the great A.I. debate and debate and debate.\par \par \uc2 Aishah, what is going on?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And debate and debate.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: I was going to say the same thing.\par \par \uc2 It just feels like they're always just talking about it. That's exactly what's going on right now. So, Chairman Blumenthal, actually, before this hearing got started, told FOX that he is getting the feeling now that Congress could be moving towards perhaps creating an independent agency that would then oversee this emerging new technology.\par \par \uc2 And, today, he and ranking member \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  are holding a Senate judiciary hearing on A.I. And both of them really want to move on regulation ASAP. And one of the reasons why, Neil, is because we have got this big presidential election around the corner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): Let me ask each of you what you see as the immediate threats to the integrity of our election system.\par \par \uc2 DARIO AMODEI, CEO AND CO-FOUNDER, ANTHROPIC: My mind goes to misinformation, generation of deepfakes, use of A.I. systems to manipulate people or produce propaganda or just do anything deceptive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So that is actually the CEO of \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 . It's an \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 -backed A.I. start-up.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  is also one of the seven leading tech companies that just met at the White House last Friday to announce their voluntary commitments on sharing, on testing, developing safe A.I. technology.\par \par \uc2 But here's the thing. Lawmakers admit whatever commitments these companies make are vague and unenforceable. So, Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has pledged to incorporate some amendments related to A.I. in this upcoming defense bill, which is expected to move through the Senate later this week.\par \par \uc2 And he's also adding that there will be that last, third and final briefing on A.I. for all senators again before they get going on some kind of regulation. But, again, we're still waiting, just talking.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And talking and talking.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Aishah, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: You got it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill\par \par \uc2 By the way, from the A.I. threat to the Russian threat, I don't know if any of you have seen this, some new video coming in from a Russian jet hitting a U.S. drone with a flare. It was over Syria. The drone returned safely, but badly damaged.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant General Keith Kellogg with us right now.\par \par \uc2 General, they're just up in the ante, aren't they?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. KEITH KELLOGG (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Neil. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 It's like, what did the great Yankee catcher Yogi Berra say, it's deja vu all over again. They have done it in Syria. Before that, they did it in the Black Sea with a Russian fighter and one of our drones. The Chinese have done it as well.\par \par \uc2 I remember what Mike Tyson once said. Everybody has a plan until you punched in the mouth. Maybe it's time we change our plan. Instead of just doing what we have done after we have had one of these incidents and issue platitudes, oh, it's very unsafe and they shouldn't be doing it, I have got a pretty good solution, and it does work.\par \par \uc2 Why don't we just arm the Reapers and arm the Predators? You can put a AIM Sidewinder missile on it, let -- tell everybody we're doing this, tell them we're changing the rules of engagement, and if -- approaches an aircraft, your aircraft approaches a drone, then let the operator make the decision to shoot it down or not.\par \par \uc2 And then you tell the Russians, look, or the Chinese or the Moldovans or anybody, look, don't mess around with our drones. This is the way it's going to be. We have done it before. It works. They just need to up the ante.\par \par \uc2 The next thing you're going to see, Neil, if this is not done, we're going to see one of our drones is shot down. And then if you start escorting the drones with F-16s or F-35s, then you have really raised the ante. Is it risky? Of course it is. But you have got to raise the game, the level of the game, so they will understand that this is just unsatisfactory behavior, and we're going to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But we don't, General. And I wonder if they break that into their calculus.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes. Oh, of course, they do, Neil. They realize that we're not going to change our rules of engagement because we're so risk-averse that we're not going to do it, and we need to do it.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I put a lot of the blame on the chairman of the Joint Chiefs and the secretary of defense and their recommendations to the commander in chief, to the president. But, ultimately, it's the president of the United States. He's commander in chief under the Constitution. He can make the decision to change the rules of engagement.\par \par \uc2 I saw it done. I saw it done with President Trump in the Gulf when we changed the rules of engagement. We also did the same thing when we worked in Syria, and that's one of the reasons we put the TLAM missile attack on the Al-Said (ph) Air Base, where the Russians had launched the sarin nerve gas attack on civilians in Syria.\par \par \uc2 We did the same thing when we attacked and hit the Wagner pro-military group, the mercenary group that the Russians had in Syria when they attacked a special operations base. We changed the rules of engagement. We told the Russians we were going to do it. They didn't listen, so we hit the \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2  with artillery and close air support, killed about 200.\par \par \uc2 You have got to up the game. You have got to tell them, this is just not satisfactory. We're not going to accept it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, because they're not changing their game plan, to your point.\par \par \uc2 General Keith Kellogg, always good seeing you, my friend. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, at the corner of the Wall and Broad, as I told you, day number 12, 12th gain in a row for the Dow Jones industrials.\par \par \uc2 A big test for the markets tomorrow, with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  expected to hike interest rates and how it will digest all of that. Some numbers out of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  that were better than expected. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  stock flat, \uc2 Google\uc2  in after-hours trading up about 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 We will see how it all falls out tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 "The Five" right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230726ej7p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  Rebrands \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ; UPS Strike Imminent?; Supreme Court Code of Conduct?; Interview With Sen. \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  (R-LA); Gilgo Beach Murder Investigation; Interview With Pentagon Press Secretary Patrick Ryder; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Holding Big Lead in Early States; Interview With Fmr. Gov. Asa Hutchinson (R-AR)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Kelly O'Grady, Griff Jenkins, Jeff Flock, Grady  Trimble \par \uc2 7,869 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right. We are less than a month away from the first big debate.\par \par \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  in New Hampshire this hour, he's holding a town hall. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  is hosting a fund-raiser tonight in Iowa, as new FOX Business polls show that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is running away with it among GOP voters in two of those early states.\par \par \uc2 What Republican presidential candidate Asa Hutchinson makes of it all and why he isn't intimidated by it or the former president. He's here.\par \par \uc2 And so is Griff Jenkins on what GOP voters are signaling in those early states, and FOX Business' Grady Trimble on the key issues impacting those states.\par \par \uc2 All right, happy Monday to all of you. Glad to have you. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to it.\par \par \uc2 And, for that, we go to Griff Jenkins sort of chronicling what's going on right now, as we tick away the month to the first debate.\par \par \uc2 To you, sir.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey. Good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And, look, front-runner Trump has a commanding lead, no other way to put it, in South Carolina, in Iowa. But, Neil, he has yet to commit to taking the GOP stage in the first debate that will be right here on FOX.\par \par \uc2 And, today, one of his rivals is calling him out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think I want Trump in the debate. And the reason is, I think the American people deserve to know what their options are.\par \par \uc2 And, really, it's offensive for a candidate to say they're not going to be on stage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Now, here are the six candidates that have met the \uc2 RNC\uc2 's polling and fund-raising qualifications to take that stage. You see them, Trump, DeSantis, Ramaswamy, Scott, Haley, and Christie.\par \par \uc2 Noticeably absent, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . And back-of-the-pack candidates like Miami's mayor says there's plenty of time left.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS SUAREZ\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We see that shuffling already happening. We see people dropping and other people emerging. So time is going to be our ally. Getting on the debate stage is essential. We feel confident that we will be there, and there's five opportunities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Now let's get to FOX Business' latest polling.\par \par \uc2 Haley is in second place behind Trump in the Palmetto State, but it's a 34- point gap, you see there, 48 percent to 14 percent, DeSantis in third at 13 percent. Tim Scott rounding out the top four at 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, out in the Hawkeye State, 46 percent of Iowa's GOP caucus-goers back, Trump DeSantis at this distant second at 16 percent, Scott at 11 percent, Ramaswamy at 6 percent.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , who you mentioned campaigning in New Hampshire today, says about these polls that all it shows is that the American people simply know the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and not the rest of the candidates yet, but the debates will go a long way in overcoming that -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we shall see. Thank you, Griff, for that.\par \par \uc2 Griff Jenkins.\par \par \uc2 Let's go right now to Grady Trimble. He's been digging into what these polls and the issues that are dominating this race and what folks are worried about, particularly in the Grand Old Party. I mean, after all, this is a battle to become the head of the Grand Old Party.\par \par \uc2 Grady, what are we finding out?\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It is, Neil.\par \par \uc2 The GOP presidential hopefuls, as Griff mentioned, they're hitting the trail in the early voting states. And our new FOX Business polling shows us that voters really want to hear their plans on the economy. Our polling focused specifically on Iowa and South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 So, GOP caucus-goers in the Hawkeye State, to start, say economic issues top their list of important issues, way ahead of immigration, social issues and foreign policy. And, in South Carolina, even more Republican primary voters, more than half, say the economy is their number one issue.\par \par \uc2 Griff touched on the fact that former President Trump has a sizable lead in both states. But even the candidates who are lower in the polls say there is reason to be optimistic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm consistently polling at third nationally. And that's ahead of even the first debate, when many people of -- in this country haven't yet gotten to know who I am.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: The other guys are spending lots of money. We haven't spent any. The accountant in me is saving it. That's why you're not seeing the numbers move, because people are on vacation, their kids are out of school.\par \par \uc2 Once Labor Day hits, you're going to see it go. And that's when the time is to hit it hard.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Now, our polling is for Republican primary voters and caucus goers. But President Biden clearly sees the economy as a top issue in 2024 for his party as well.\par \par \uc2 He and his economic advisers have been touting Bidenomics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, CHAIR, WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: When this president came to office, his main goal was to get families and businesses to the other side of that crisis and to set them up for a strong recovery. Check. That's been done.\par \par \uc2 We see it in action. His -- his -- the second part of that plan is to make the investments of Bidenomics to reverse decades of trickle-down disinvestment and reinvest in America, so that growth -- that growth momentum can keep going.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: But the economy under President Biden has largely been characterized by inflation. Real wages are down about 3 percent since the president took office.\par \par \uc2 Even as prices fall, many Americans still feel they're falling behind. That could be part of the reason. It could also signal an opening for those Republican candidates going forward -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, Grady, very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, to Asa Hutchinson right now, the former Arkansas governor, presidential candidate. He hopes to make that debate stage. Let's see where things stand.\par \par \uc2 Governor, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thanks, Neil. It's great to be with you today. Super coverage on this important election year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So how are things looking for you in making that debate? I know it's important to you. On a couple of different criteria, getting 1 percent of the vote, 40,000 distinct donors, where are you?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, first of all, we have met the polling criteria.\par \par \uc2 And the Rasmussen poll that came out the last -- I believe today, had us at 4 percent nationally. And so we're going in the right direction the polls. And we have met the criteria for the debate on the poll numbers.\par \par \uc2 Now, you also have to have 40,000 unique donors. We're not there yet. We have got 30 days. We're doing all the things that need to be done to get there. It's Asa2024.com. And that's how you can make sure that I'm on the debate stage. It is important, as your coverage points out, because everyone wants to see the contrast between the candidates.\par \par \uc2 They want to learn how the distinctions are. And they're starting to get focused. So I expect to be there. We're working hard to make sure we're on that debate stage.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's likely that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won't be there, or so he says. There's no reason for him, he says, because he leads by a country mile. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, he's got his issues.\par \par \uc2 I mean, whenever you look at the pending federal indictment that is as serious as they get -- you have got the potential of another one. He's got a lot of things he has to focus on. And so whether he's there or not, he's going to be covered. Do we want to have a commander in chief that does not know how to handle classified information and our nation's secrets?\par \par \uc2 So I will take it either way. He will either be there on the first or the second debate. But he's got a lot of conflicts right now and a lot of things on his plate. Ultimately, the voters are going to look at this and say, while the economy is the most important issue, winning is an important issue as well in November to reverse Biden's policies.\par \par \uc2 And, to do that, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not the right one to lead us to victory in 2024.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, a lot of the Trump folks come back and say, depending on the poll, they are winning. They do perform well head to head against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , not all, but some.\par \par \uc2 So they say, if that's your argument, then you're an even deeper doo-doo because you don't even compete with the president. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, as -- you know, I have been a governor of Arkansas for eight years. We have balanced the budget.\par \par \uc2 But you're right. I haven't focused on a national image for the last eight years. I focused on running the state and doing my job. And so now we have got to get that message out, my view of a pro-growth energy policy, how we need to move toward a balanced budget, use my background as head of -- undersecretary at Homeland Security in charge of border security.\par \par \uc2 I know what needs to be done. So, as that message gets out, I expect we will continue to rise in the polls. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you're right, he is as high as they get. But there's going to be external and internal pressures.\par \par \uc2 And I don't see that lasting over the long term. And that's where it's an unpredictable and wide-open race.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Governor, I'm curious. I mean, there will be pressure again, should you make the debate, for all those who are there to eventually support whoever wins this thing, whoever gets the nomination.\par \par \uc2 Are you on board with that?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, I mean, I have always supported the Republican nominee.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you're looking at a trial next May, before the Republican Convention, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is convicted of serious federal charges, I can't support that. Now, I will support the nominee of the party, but I don't expect that to be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: By that time, as you know, sir, it's distinctly possible, with the primaries we will have already gone through, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , again, if things stand where they are -- and you're quite right -- that can change a lot, and history proves it does change a lot -- but he could seriously amass all the delegates necessary to be the Republican nominee going in to that trial, if they can't push it back.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that? Then he is the nominee.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, first of all, it's a disastrous scenario for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 You're putting the caucus voters and primary voters in a position of not knowing the outcome of a very serious criminal case when they're casting their ballot. That's unfair for the whole process. And are we going to be talking about Trump's legal cases or are we talking about fundamental issues that they care about?\par \par \uc2 South Carolinians and Iowans, they don't put Trump first. They put the economy first as to the number one issue. So that's the challenge. And that's where I have said anybody that put the country first would actually step aside here. They have so much to deal with, and it's not fair to our democratic system.\par \par \uc2 But we will deal with it. We have got to beat him at the ballot box. And that's going to take the voters to start thinking about who can lead, not just the Republican Party at the top level, but who's going to help bring up their congressional seats and win the Senate seat, because that's all an important part of changing from Biden's failed policies.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It sounds to me like you would support any other Republican running except \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Is that accurate?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: I would certainly support the nominee of the party. There is that challenge with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, not if it's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: I will support -- I'm going to be on that debate stage, and I will support the nominee of the party, because it's not going to be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. But if it were?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: They will ask me that question on the debate stage as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: But that's a scenario we don't know about. That's the challenge.\par \par \uc2 We can't support, we can't nominate somebody who's potentially convicted of a felony.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. They call that being a tease, Governor, so touche on you on that.\par \par \uc2 Asa Hutchinson, very good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: All right, thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, nothing to tease here, just to let you know that stocks at the corner of Wall and Broad are on a tear right now, the Dow, the 11th straight day we have seen gains. You would have to go back to early 2017, the first weeks of the Trump administration, to see that type of performance here.\par \par \uc2 The Dow is already at its highest since the early part of 2022, and, this time, it was taking the other market averages along with it, growing optimism that, yes, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is likely going to hike rates when it wraps up a two-day meeting on Wednesday, but that might be it for a while. That's the hope, and earnings coming out a little bit better than expected.\par \par \uc2 But that's the drill.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, trying to find out whatever happened to that soldier who crossed into North Korea. What's his status? Do we know what the North Koreans say -- are saying? What are we hearing from them?\par \par \uc2 The read from Brigadier General Patrick Ryder, the Pentagon press secretary on that, so many other big foreign issues that are dominating the headlines.\par \par \uc2 He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right it's been a little over a week right now since Army Private Travis King just ran into North Korea. Haven't heard much from him since.\par \par \uc2 We're told right now that there are no new communications with North Korea over his status.\par \par \uc2 But let's get the latest from Brigadier General Patrick Ryder. Of course, the general is the Pentagon press secretary. We're happy to have him.\par \par \uc2 General, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Thanks very much, Neil. Glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What can be said of the status of Travis King, sir? Anything?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Well, at this point, Neil, what we're doing is working very closely with our colleagues throughout the government, to include State Department, maintaining close contact with United Nations Command there in Korea, to gather information to determine the whereabouts of Private King.\par \par \uc2 At this point, our top priority is his well-being and his safe return home. But we're going to have to continue to look into this, continue to gather information. And, certainly, we will keep people updated on that front.\par \par \uc2 I will say that we know that his friends and family clearly must be concerned, as are we. And so we're going to continue to stay on this until we have more information.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So there's no talk of a hostage swap or anything like that, working through China, anything?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: At this stage, what we do know is that the United Nations Command, via long-established mechanisms to communicate with the Korean People's Army, had delivered a message notifying the North Koreans that we're seeking information.\par \par \uc2 We know that they received that message, but still don't have any updates to provide in terms of Private King's whereabouts.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. So it does raise a question, because, in the background of this, as you know, General, they have been getting a lot more militant. That is the North Koreans. They're testing many more missiles, even a space project or a race with the South Koreans here.\par \par \uc2 We have sent another ship over to the South Korean area. So it does look like the ante is being upped there. How would you characterize things now?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Well, I think it's very important to put all of this into broader strategic context, which is, as you see countries like North Korea conduct these types of provocative acts, the missile launches that you highlighted, it's important that we also send a clear signal of strength and support to our allies in the region and that we're going to be there for them.\par \par \uc2 And so you have heard the term extended deterrence, and that's more than just words. It's deeds. And so by us conducting these types of exercises, these kinds of port visits, it clearly demonstrates to our allies in South Korea, to Japan and elsewhere in the region that the United States is going to be there as a strong member of this important alliance in the region.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If I could switch to Ukraine, sir, the Russians are saying that a terrorist drone attacked Moscow, more deadly strikes on Odesa.\par \par \uc2 They say Ukraine is behind this, and we're accepting it and countenancing it. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Yes, so first of all, when it comes to these types of reports about drone attacks outside of Ukrainian territory, I will certainly let the Ukrainians talk to that.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you that, from a United States standpoint, from a DOD standpoint, our focus is 100 percent on providing the Ukrainians with the security assistance they need to defend their country inside their sovereign borders. And that's going to continue to be our focus.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, General, I know we have committed another, I think, $400 million in an extended military aid package to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 We are -- given far more than all the other \uc2 NATO\uc2  nations, I believe combined, and then some. And it comes amidst polls in this country that show Americans increasingly evenly split on support for this war and support for the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 Does that worry you?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: I think, again, you have to look at the big picture here and the important message to the international community that we're not going to allow an autocratic leader like Putin invade his peaceful neighbor and get away with it with impunity.\par \par \uc2 If that were to happen, the ramifications not only for European security, but international security are much, much bigger. And so, as we have done from the beginning of this invasion, we have been standing side by side with the Ukrainians to ensure that they have the capabilities they need to defend themselves and also working with the international community.\par \par \uc2 It's not just the United States. I think everyone sees the potential implications here if we don't support Ukraine. So, we're going to continue to work closely with them and ensure they have the capabilities needed to make a difference on the battlefield and, most importantly, to defend their know.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Much has been made of the \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2 , of course. And it's General Prigozhin who has tried to seize Moscow, then stopped. Then he was going to be placed in Belarus and left alone. That does not appear to be the case.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2 , which we were told was going to be disbanded, General, does not appear to be disbanded at all. If anything, there are reports that its ranks are strengthening and that it might be planning other attacks beyond Ukraine. What's going on there?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Well, I think at this point, we're watching to see what happens next.\par \par \uc2 I think it's important to note that, when it comes to Ukraine, the \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2  has essentially ceased to exist as an effective military organization conducting operations in Ukraine. But, that said, it's a transnational criminal organization, so it definitely bears close watching.\par \par \uc2 As you know, the United States has levied sanctions against Wagner and countries and individuals that support that group. So, they do continue to pose a threat. So it's something we're going to keep a close eye on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, is there any truth to the talk, General, that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is very leery, almost scared of Prigozhin and keeps a distance, but keeps an eye on him?\par \par \uc2 But he -- in any other situation, under almost any other condition, he would be locked in a jail cell. And he's not.\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Yes, that's really a question that President Putin can only answer.\par \par \uc2 And I'm not going to get into discussing the internal security concerns or issues -- yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, do you think \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has reason to look over his shoulder, that he has reason to be nervous?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Again, I'm not going to speak about what his particular concerns are.\par \par \uc2 I will say that the international community has certainly sent a clear message that it's not going to tolerate this type of belligerent behavior against a peaceful neighbor. And I think that the Russian government has some serious challenges ahead of it, challenges of their own making.\par \par \uc2 This is a war of choice in Ukraine. Nobody compelled President Putin to invade Ukraine. This war could end tomorrow. But, for whatever reason, they have made these decisions, which, again, certainly presents significant challenges for them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: General Patrick Ryder, thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Thank you very much, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime here, we're getting more information the alleged Long Island killer, the Gilgo Beach serial killer, as he is known.\par \par \uc2 Reports of a soundproof room at his home that wasn't a soundproof room, more like a vault, but it's raised eyebrows and questions. Could he have murdered any of his victims in that house?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We're told police could be done searching Rex Heuermann's home in Long Island by tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 It's what they might have found in the accused killer's home today that raised eyebrows and concerns.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the dig quite literally continues in Rex Heuermann's former home in Long Island on Gilgo Beach. He's the accused serial killer.\par \par \uc2 And in going through the home inch by inch, they found what looked like a soundproofed room. We're later told it was a vault. I'm not sure the distinction here, but concerns that, whatever you want to call it, that room might have been used for one or more of the alleged murders.\par \par \uc2 Pat Brosnan joins us, the former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  detective, Brosnan Risk Consultants founder and president, much more, a very good read on things like this, because he can step back and piece things together.\par \par \uc2 So, Pat, help us piece together, this idea of the soundproof room and/or vault, whatever you want to call it. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 PAT BROSNAN, FORMER \uc2 NYPD\uc2  DETECTIVE: First of all, good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I'd rather characterize it -- and it's horrifying -- as a tomb, because it may very well have likely been a tomb. As I'm sure you recollect, the last communication June 6, 2010, at 3:11 in the morning from Shannan Gilbert's phone came from Massapequa Park.\par \par \uc2 And there was at least two and as many as three of the phones from the victims taken from them as -- ostensibly as trophies, and I believe as trophies. And at least two of those phones had multiple pings from Massapequa Park to the same cell towers.\par \par \uc2 So, as terrifying as it is -- and it is terrifying -- soundproof, call it a vault, call it a bunker. I call it a tomb.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Another thing that's come up -- and maybe you can help me with this, Pat, is -- and what they were looking for oftentimes were trophies he allegedly collected from his victims and their families, telephones, articles of clothing, jewelry, notepads, down to photographs he would even pass along to their families.\par \par \uc2 That -- they look for that kind of stuff. But they're piecing together a pattern that's copied in a number of murders, right?\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 But I have to say, Neil, he's added an addition -- additional layer of horror that I personally am not familiar with, even on B movies late at night, where he has called on numerous occasions separate victims' family members and said horrible things to them from the victim's phone.\par \par \uc2 So, you understand, the victims are missing. So when their caller I.D. surfaces on a brother or a sibling or a parent, they're going to snatch that phone and have false hope that they're actually in communication with their loved one who's gone missing. They have no idea that it was the murderer of their loved one calling and taunting and teasing.\par \par \uc2 This is sadistic, sadistic stuff, horrible, horrible, really, really bad guy. And I'll tell you what.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, another thing that has come up, Pat...\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: He's in for it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, finish that thought. I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: Well, he's in for it, to the extent that he's going to learn that his DNA from the pizza crust, OK, is going to chase him across time and space, because it's like this perfect, perfect sophisticated compass.\par \par \uc2 And it's going to point investigators what, are very skilled, exactly in the direction of where there may very well be other victims, because I will tell you what. He did not take a 13-year vacation, no way, from June 6 of 2010 with the Shannan Gilbert murder.\par \par \uc2 I believe that he was active during that period, and I am reasonably certain that we will see that to be true, that the facts will bear that out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, a lot of these -- the folks who have been investigating it have compared it to the Ted Bundy investigation that spread to many other locales, went far beyond the initial victims thought.\par \par \uc2 They're already talking about a condo he rented in Atlantic City, in Las Vegas, where he was...\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... and these other states and all.\par \par \uc2 Do you suspect that there are a lot more victims?\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: I suspect and I am reasonably convinced, for a number of different reasons.\par \par \uc2 First of all, a 13-year vacation from the urges, the savage urges that compelled him to engage in the murder of young ladies, it just doesn't vanish; 13 years is a lifetime. I believe he was busy. I believe he also is very arrogant. And he believed, mistakenly, as it turned out, that he was invisible and invincible.\par \par \uc2 And you saw how surprised he was when detectives grabbed him in Midtown. He thought all his duplicitous behavior Neil, fake phones, disposable phones, fictitious e-mail accounts, fictitious and duplicitous online escort accounts, he thought, as they often do, that they were smarter than the collective intelligence of all the investigators.\par \par \uc2 And guess what? Dead wrong. Dead wrong.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Dead wrong is right. And we're still in the early going of all of this.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: You better believe it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Pat Brosnan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, keeping track of a drama developing on Capitol Hill, where a lot of Senate Democrats who sit on the Judiciary Committee want to get sort of a new code of conduct, an ethics bill, if you will, for members of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Not all Republicans are buying it. They say they're up to something else far bigger, far more sinister -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, this went along party lines right now, a crackdown on the part of Democratic members of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  to say, the free trip thing and not reporting on a thing, with a host of Supreme Court justices, it's not going to fly. So we're going to come up with an ethics bill there.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  joining other Republicans to say that that was not the answer or the solution. Kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 Senator, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): You too, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This ethics bill, Democrats are saying it would just hold Supreme Court justices to the same ethics standards that I suppose you have in the Senate and the House supposedly has there. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: I voted against the bill for a number of reasons.\par \par \uc2 Some of the proponents of the bill have said that the Supreme Court needs a code of conduct. That's not accurate. The Supreme Court has a code of conduct. You can go to uscourts.gov and read it. The Supreme Court amended it last spring, and it is talking about amending it again.\par \par \uc2 Number two, I think the legislation brought by Senator Whitehouse is unconstitutional under Article 3 in our separation of powers doctrine. If you read cases like U.S. v. Klein, U.S. v. Nixon, Buckley v. Valeo, Collins v. Yellen, I don't think that the Congress has the power to write a code of ethics for the Supreme Court any more than the Supreme Court has the right to write a code of ethics for Congress.\par \par \uc2 Number three...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So the Supreme Court should police itself and its own behavior. That's what you're saying.\par \par \uc2 What if it doesn't, though? I mean, the same could be said, you're quite right, about if the House doesn't do that or the Senate doesn't do that, then who does do that? But what do you think?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, they -- you ask, what if they don't? They are. They are policing themselves.\par \par \uc2 They have a code of conduct. Now, some of my colleagues don't like...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Will that code of conduct change -- will that code of conduct change the way some trips were not reported or gifts were not shared or publicized?\par \par \uc2 I mean, do you know that? Not that it would matter if you could say, like to your point, that you can't do that with another branch of government.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, it already has changed.\par \par \uc2 It was changed last spring. Now, if you take -- if you accept food or expensive lodging or a private airplane trip, you have to disclose that. So it has changed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, with those disclosures, especially concerning Justice Thomas and a couple of others that go before some of these event procedures, what happens then to justices who might violate that in the future?\par \par \uc2 Does the Supreme Court have the power to say, you have to go?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: I don't know the answer to that. I just believe that, under the separation of powers doctrine, Congress can't regulate the internal affairs of the court.\par \par \uc2 And I also believe, Neil, that, how can I put it, sometimes Congress is like high school, except no one graduates. There are members of Congress that, if you do something they don't like, they will try to hurt you. And I'm not saying this is true of all of my colleagues, but I believe that, for many of my colleagues, this legislation is not about ethics.\par \par \uc2 It's about the Dobbs case on abortion and Biden v. Nebraska and student debt, and it's about no racial quotas in admissions. I think some of my colleagues are angry at the Supreme Court, and they want to hurt this court. That's why, if they could, they would expand the court. So they can't do that. So, instead, they're going to try to destroy it from within. That's my opinion.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Understood.\par \par \uc2 Do you personally, though, Senator, have any issue with some of the things we have heard from a couple of justices who did get very expensive trips and very nice gifts? I mean, would you -- does that personally offend you in any way?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, under -- I have read the code of conduct, and I think it's a good code of conduct.\par \par \uc2 And you're supposed to disclose an expensive trip. Sometimes, justices haven't done that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: When it's been called to their attention, they have filed amended disclosures.\par \par \uc2 We're supposed to disclose that sort of thing as members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: True.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: I do it, but, if I forget something, as I have a number of times, I file an amended disclosure report. That happens all the time.\par \par \uc2 But if you're asking me, do I think there are members of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  who are bought or could be bought with a vacation, the answer -- the short answer is no and the long answer is hell no, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. All right, Senator, very good catching up with you.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  of Louisiana, the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , Federal Court Subcommittee ranking member, among others.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, it's looking like a messy UPS strike that could be close to sidelining better than 300,000 workers.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Flock on that.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Clock's ticking, Neil, absolutely. One week to go.\par \par \uc2 I'm at one of the eight UPS hubs, the one here in Philadelphia. Back in a moment, give you the full story, at least what we know up to this point.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, a UPS strike that could wreak havoc on the economy and a big one that could be just days away.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Flock following it all in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 What's going on, Jeff?\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: It would be a whale of a strike, Neil.\par \par \uc2 That's because these folks employ about 34 -- or 340,000 people