all of that.\par \par \uc2 To Dave Dodson, the knowledgeable professor, the Stanford School of Business lecturer, much more.\par \par \uc2 Dave, this could be a big strike, to put it mildly, the 300,000-plus affected, on top of writers and actors striking as we speak, other pilots considering actions like that, flight attendants. I mean, this could be the summer of a lot of big strikes, and the impact would be swift, I'd imagine.\par \par \uc2 DAVE DODSON, STANFORD UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF BUSINESS: Oh, yes, for sure.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you may not know this, but I actually had the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  working for me at one point, and Sean O'Neill was the person the other side of the table. Those are very different times. I wasn't staring down as a CEO of 3.5 percent unemployment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He's the president of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , but go ahead.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 I wasn't staring down 3.5 percent inflation -- unemployment, inflation inflation, trying to battle inflation, presidential election. Management is in a very different situation than when I was in there. And unions could not have picked a better moment in time to be negotiating these contracts.\par \par \uc2 They're also seizing on another issue here, which I think management is, by and large, completely missing,and it's one of the things that I talk about in my book, which is that for about two or three generations, we got our identity around the bowling league and our church and our service clubs, along with our company and our union.\par \par \uc2 Well, most of that's gone away. And what Sean O'Neill, the president of the union, and other unions are doing is, they're saying, we're going to give you a place of identity. And, by the way, that's how you get people out on the picket line is, you say, we're like-minded. We have a shared goal.\par \par \uc2 And what Sean O'Neill wants people at UPS to think is, I'm first a teamster and I'm second a UPS employee. And if he succeeds at that, UPS is in trouble.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And if all these other strikes are to be galvanized at the same time and go on for a long time, then what?\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Well, then I think you're going to have two issues. And the first one is inflation.\par \par \uc2 I'm not worried about inflation with the UPS strike. Nobody's going to change their prices with a strike that might end any day. But if the unions are successful with the UPS strike and they're able to demonstrate to workers at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and at \uc2 Apple Computer\uc2  and at Trader Joe's that they can add value, in addition to what I was talking about earlier, which is that sense of identity, then you're going to see inflation.\par \par \uc2 You're going to see inflation as a result of wages going up and this wage- price spiral. Now, that's not a reason to not pay people fairly, but it is going to be an outcome in the economy that we have to be prepared for.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Professor, thank you. Hope none of that comes to materialize here.\par \par \uc2 Dave Dodson on those developments.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, did the world's richest man just flip a lot of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  folks the bird? No, he just said, bye-bye, birdie. There is a difference -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's bye-bye, birdie, at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . What happened?\par \par \uc2 Kelly O'Grady in L.A., what happened here?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, this move isn't totally surprising, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  ad revenue was down 50 percent since Musk took over, and he's been telegraphing that he's aiming to create this everything app that he's been calling X. So, yesterday he shared this. I don't even think I can say that he tweeted it anymore, but he says -- quote -- "Soon, we shall bid adieu to the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  brand and gradually all the birds."\par \par \uc2 So, all of a sudden, we have got a new logo. And, remember, the letter X has been on just about everything Musk has touched for the last couple of decades, right? X.com was the original name for \uc2 PayPal\uc2  back in 1999. His new A.I. venture is xAI.\par \par \uc2 So what can we expect from this iteration of X? New CEO \uc2 Linda Yaccarino\uc2  shared this. She says -- quote -- "X is the future state of unlimited interactivity centered in audio, video, messaging, payments, and banking."\par \par \uc2 So it kind of sounds a bit like the Chinese app \uc2 WeChat\uc2 , if you will. Now, here's the thing. Trying to be everything all at once, though, can confuse the consumer and perhaps the advertiser as well. And so I will leave you with this, Neil. The businessperson in me is saying, wow, you're throwing away 15 years of brand equity that you just paid $44 billion for. This rebrand better work.\par \par \uc2 But, for now, what this will be just isn't super clear. I mean, Neil, do I even still say I'm tweeting? Do I say I'm X'ing? Still a lot to be figured out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're right. What are you doing?\par \par \uc2 All right, thank you for that, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O'Grady.\par \par \uc2 Kim Serafin, entertainment journalist, on what she makes of this.\par \par \uc2 Kim, what does it all mean?\par \par \uc2 KIM SERAFIN, ENTERTAINMENT JOURNALIST: Yes, it's very interesting.\par \par \uc2 I was just going to say the same thing, because you do have the CEO now saying it's going to be this global marketplace, but was it X'ed or was it tweeted? Was it posted? We don't know. I mean, it's all confusing right now.\par \par \uc2 I just checked on my app. It still has the blue bird there. So that hasn't changed yet. I guess some desktops have changed. They're trying to rebrand this. Exactly. There have been some advertiser losses. A lot of people are going to Threads, which is the other new social media network right now. So I think there's a lot of people who are just looking for something new.\par \par \uc2 And they're trying to change it into not just a platform where people are sharing ideas and having conversations. They want to have this banking platform and video and audio and all of these other things added to it. So maybe just the X is totally changing everything, rebranding it to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , Model X. His son is named X. So...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I was -- yes, I was thinking that. He does have a fondness -- yes, he does have a fondness for X.\par \par \uc2 I'd be remiss if I didn't mention the success of "Barbie." You had been saying it would be a big success. It was. It certainly helped "\uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2 ." If you just look at the traffic this weekend at theaters, over $300 million, that drew a lot of people in, a record weekend. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SERAFIN: Yes, Barbenheimer, I mean, this is saving the box office. "Barbie" is saving the box office, $162 million.\par \par \uc2 There are so many records. Let me make sure I get them straight, $162 million, biggest opening of 2023 so far...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SERAFIN: ... beating "Super Mario Brothers," beating "Spider-Man: Into the Spider Verse," also the biggest opening weekend for a movie directed by a female, beating "Captain Marvel," beating "Wonder Woman."\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SERAFIN: It is also the biggest combined weekend total since the pandemic, biggest combined, but, even more importantly, the fourth biggest combined weekend ever.\par \par \uc2 And that is more important because those other weekends were dominated by superhero movies, like "Avengers" and "Star Wars" also. So, when you have these two movies coming together, both beating huge expectations, but $162 million, $82 million, beyond expectations.\par \par \uc2 And just think about the money this is creating and just bringing -- reenergizing the box office. I think we needed that. I think a lot of people were writing off the box office, writing off movies, dealing with the actors strike right now, the writers strike.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SERAFIN: So there's a lot of other things going on in Hollywood. I think Hollywood needed this, for sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You mentioned that actor and writers strike.\par \par \uc2 And we could be looking in the future some delayed openings, if not significantly delayed. Are you worried about that?\par \par \uc2 SERAFIN: Well, it will affect promotion. I mean, right now, actors can't go out and promote movies.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SERAFIN: So, like, they had the "Haunted Mansion" red carpet in Disneyland last weekend. It was kind of the first red carpet.\par \par \uc2 And they had Mickey Mouse and \uc2 Disney\uc2  characters walking the red carpet because you couldn't have actors walking the red carpet. So you have that effect. We don't know what's happening with the fall film festivals yet. Will there be red carpets there? Because a lot of people are expecting this to stretch out for a long time.\par \par \uc2 You do have halts in a lot of production. "Wicked," "Deadpool," "Beetlejuice," a lot of these productions have had to stop until things get settled.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure. We will watch it closely, as we always do.\par \par \uc2 Kim, you're an encyclopedia.\par \par \uc2 Kim Serafin, the entertainment journalist extraordinaire.\par \par \uc2 It worked out for theaters that said, we're going to pair this and see if people go watch both. They did in droves. So, it's giving Hollywood some other ideas going forward. We shall see.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230725ej7o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Sen. \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  (R-NC); Interview With Rep. \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  (R-WI); Interview With Fmr. Sen. Joe Lieberman (I-CT); Interview With Presidential Candidate \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 ; Trump Sent Target Letter in January 6 Probe; Dangerous Heat Across the Country\b0\par\par\uc2 David Spunt, Janette Nesheiwat, Marissa Torres, Brandy Campbell,  Steve Harrigan, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,183 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hi, everyone. This is your captain speaking.\par \par \uc2 We certainly do again apologize for the situation of being very hot in the back there. This is the best that our cooling is going to occur while passengers are on the aircraft.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: And it got worse. Chaos on the tarmac, as passengers stuck on a flight from Las Vegas to Atlanta for hours were overcome by the heat as the temperature soared to 111 degrees in the cabin with no air conditioning.\par \par \uc2 Several passengers passing out with some in need of oxygen, this as more than 140 million Americans as we speak are under either a heat advisory due to those sweltering temperatures out West or air quality alerts from the ongoing smoke that is now back to filtering in from those Canadian wildfires up north.\par \par \uc2 And we have got FOX team coverage with Brandy Campbell in Miami on the hellish heat wave, Steve Harrigan in Atlanta with more on that flight from hell, and meteorologist Marissa Torres on if there is any relief in sight. Not immediately.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 My thanks to my friend Charles Payne for filling in so ably yesterday.\par \par \uc2 First now to Brandy Campbell on the weather and the heat wave. Let's go to Miami, where she is -- Brandy.\par \par \uc2 BRANDY CAMPBELL, FOX WEATHER MULTIMEDIA JOURNALIST: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Look, 14 states are currently under some form of heat alert right now, so it is hot out here. Phoenix hits its 19th straight day of temperatures of at least 110 degrees. Here in the Miami area, we have been under heat alert since the Fourth of July. And, yes, it is the Sunshine State, and you do expect to feel the heat here, but people here are telling me this just feels different, and they're not used to the heat that we're feeling now.\par \par \uc2 Now, one resident that I spoke with, he mentioned he's been living here several years, and he's not even used to seeing these heat alerts. Here, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I remember seeing the alert on my phone, and I hadn't seen those since I lived in Texas. So, definitely, it was kind of a first for me seeing it here.\par \par \uc2 Usually, it's a flood advisory. I mean, it doesn't concern me. It's just I avoid going outside, not because I'm worried about something happening, just because it's miserable to be outside in that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: All right, now, we haven't had as many afternoon storms to really cool us off, but, today, we did get one, and it worked.\par \par \uc2 And our team for FOX Weather tells me, going forward, we could see more of that relief coming up in Florida -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Fingers crossed. All right, Brandy, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Steve Harrigan right now at Atlanta's \uc2 Hartsfield-Jackson International Airport\uc2  on some incidents that were just and remain over the top -- Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That's right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 It was supposed to be a routine flight from Las Vegas at 1:00 p.m. there to Atlanta here. Instead, they got backed up 13 planes deep. And, inside, one of the passengers was a FOX News producer, Krista Garvin. She was on board, and she said after about two hours of waiting on the tarmac with a temperature of 111 degrees outside, the air was very minimal inside that cabin, and many passengers began to have some real medical difficulties.\par \par \uc2 Emergency personnel eventually came onto the plane after it was brought back to the gate. And Krista says passengers were given a choice, stay on the hot plane or perhaps face days' wait to get a new flight.\par \par \uc2 Here's Krista:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KRISTA GARVIN, FOX NEWS D.C. FIELD PRODUCER: You're telling us that we have multiple medical emergencies on the flight. The plane is still -- cannot get any cooler than it is.\par \par \uc2 It's still hot, and we're sitting on the plane, and you're telling us you have a choice to either sit here and get possibly sick, watch all this go down, or not get to your destination for days. I mean, people have jobs. We have lives. Like, we need to get back to where we're going. And you're basically telling us, sit here on this hot plane or you're not getting to where you need to go.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Delta acknowledged at least one passenger was treated by medical personnel for heat-related ailments. They said the flight was eventually canceled.\par \par \uc2 Delta issued an apology, rebooked and offered compensation for what people went through that day -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Incredible. Steve, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Now to the other problem, all that smoke that's back from the Canadian wildfires, not as bad as before, but 70 million Americans are in its path.\par \par \uc2 Marissa Torres has more, FOX Weather meteorologist.\par \par \uc2 Marissa, what are we looking at?\par \par \uc2 MARISSA TORRES, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: We're looking at heat, we're looking at air quality problems.\par \par \uc2 And we will get to the smoke in a moment, but it is billowing down from Western Canada with the active wildfires there, the heat that you guys have been talking about all compliments of this dome of high pressure that's been set up across the West, right over the Southwest.\par \par \uc2 And this is going to be at least, as we can see, over the next 10 days that we're going to be dealing with this very oppressive, dangerous heat. Those current temperatures, you're noticing a lot of triple digits across Texas, 107 over in Dallas. You have Albuquerque, New Mexico at 100 degrees, over in Phoenix, now up to 112.\par \par \uc2 But it even stretches into the Lower Mississippi Valley and also into portions of California, where we do have those heat advisories. Now Phoenix, as Brandy Campbell alluded to earlier, is historical heat. It's dangerous, it's oppressive, but it's historically persistent, in the sense that we tied the record yesterday, 18 days in a row where we reached 110 degrees or hotter.\par \par \uc2 Today, we actually went beyond that threshold. And we're continuing on again. For the next 10 days, at the very least, we expect to continue to be above 110 degrees. And this is a look at the forecast, where you can see we're going to be getting really close to 120 by the time Friday comes along.\par \par \uc2 You look at the heat alerts, you got 70 million folks currently suffering from this dangerous heat, excessive heat watches that have now erupted over Central California there, excessive heat warnings all the way in through the Southern Plains, over into the Lower Mississippi Valley, across Louisiana, into Mississippi, into Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 And we're yet again talking about the potential for record highs on our Wednesday in places like Roswell, New Mexico, at 111. Houston at 100 degrees is what we're forecasting. Now, it is monsoonal season in the Southwest. It kicked off mid-June. This is the time we should be seeing some of these thunderstorms, and we could see some storms develop that could produce some very heavy rain and severe wind gusts, and that could also prompt that flash flood concern, especially in through Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Tucson actually finally got some rain yesterday, some monsoonal moisture, well under half-an-inch, but, still, it's the third latest that that monsoonal moisture has arrived. So it was notable still.\par \par \uc2 In terms of air quality, Neil, you brought this up. We do have the concerns, those air quality alerts stretching out of the South, the mid- Atlantic, well into the Northeast, into the New England area. You take a look at that unhealthy marker, that red, Atlanta down to Macon, Georgia. Unhealthy impacts everybody. So you should all be careful, maybe keeping it inside, especially limiting physical activity.\par \par \uc2 But you're noticing everybody's kind of dealing with moderate to unhealthy, especially for sensitive groups. With the smoke tracker, you will notice that yellow starts to really phase out of the picture as we head in towards Thursday. That's a good sign. It means that everything is starting to ease out of the way.\par \par \uc2 And then also that rain day in the Northeast, Neil, it also did help kind of filter out some of the smoke particulates in our atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Man, when it keeps coming at us, it keeps coming at us.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Marissa, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 So, how to protect yourself in the middle of all of this?\par \par \uc2 Dr. Janette Nesheiwat joins us, FOX News medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 So, Doctor, a lot of people, you don't like this no matter what their health. There are some simple things we can do, but other things that a lot of people don't know we can do. Help us out.\par \par \uc2 DR. JANETTE NESHEIWAT, FOX NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, so some things that all of us need to do, and, despite your age, whether you're young or old or healthy, athlete, is, number one, hydration. And when -- and I'm not just talking about water. When you go outside and it's hot and you're sweating, you lose electrolytes.\par \par \uc2 So you want to replenish those lost electrolytes, not just with water, but, for example, coconut water, milk, believe it or not, or fruit-infused water so that you can get back that sodium and that potassium and that chloride that you lost from all that heat and from the sweating. So that's number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, if it's hot outside, especially when it approaches 90 degrees Fahrenheit, try to stay indoors in air conditioning. If you have to go outside, for example, if you work outside, you want to take breaks. You want to try your best to limit your exposure to the extreme heat. And, again, you want to make sure that you're staying hydrated, also proper clothing outdoors, wearing a hat, wearing loose clothing, sunblock, sunglasses, that sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 But, ultimately, the best thing to do with these extreme temperatures is to stay indoors until the temperatures go down, especially because, Neil, our body is like a human engine generating large amounts of heat. You have got your heart pumping blood. You have got your digestive system breaking down foods, and your kidney is filtering.\par \par \uc2 And so this all creates a lot of heat. And the body best functions when it's about 70 degrees Fahrenheit. You go above that, that's when we start to see problems. That's when we start to see symptoms like lightheadedness, dizziness, nausea, heat, cramps, fatigue, that sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, those are some of the classic symptoms you look for.\par \par \uc2 NESHEIWAT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But a lot of people don't know if they're outright dehydrated. Some of those would indicate it, right?\par \par \uc2 NESHEIWAT: Yes, absolutely. That's a good point you make.\par \par \uc2 And by the time you start to feel that extreme thirst, you're already behind the eight-ball. That means you're already on your way to dehydration. And that's why it's so important, again, if you feel any of these symptoms, to get indoors right away, because dizziness, headache, fatigue, lightheadedness can progress to mental confusion, to seizures, to coma, that sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 So you want to get indoors, you want to hydrate, you want to cool down, maybe take a cool shower. I had a patient not long ago. He was a young, healthy person out on the beach having a good time, drinking alcohol, and he lost consciousness. He had a seizure. He bit his tongue. Fortunately, he's OK.\par \par \uc2 But these extreme heat temperatures can impact, again, anyone, just like as we saw an \uc2 NFL\uc2  player, Marion Barber, who passed away, unfortunately, last year, year of a heat stroke.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. We forget that, even the most healthy.\par \par \uc2 NESHEIWAT: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Doctor, words of wisdom, all. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 NESHEIWAT: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In the meantime, this other big development today. Here we go again.\par \par \uc2 Federal prosecutors have told former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that he is a target of their probe into his loss of the 2020 election, as well as looking into the events surrounding January 6.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the very latest from David Spunt -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil. Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 A target letter is usually sent at the end of a probe or toward the end of a probe. It gives the target a chance to come in and speak to a grand jury. The former president says he received a target letter from special counsel Jack Smith through his lawyers on Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 Trump took a personal attack of Jack Smith. He said: "Deranged Jack Smith, the prosecutor with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's DOJ, sent a letter" -- again, it was Sunday night -- "stating that I am a target of the January 6 grand jury investigation and giving me a very short four days to report to the grand jury, which almost always means an arrest and indictment" -- end quote.\par \par \uc2 Now, Smith was appointed special counsel last November by Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . He already indicted Trump in a separate probe. That was the classified documents probe last month. In fact, there was a hearing today with Trump's attorneys and special counsel's attorneys to go over how to handle classified documents. A judge has not yet set a trial date.\par \par \uc2 Now, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is indicted in this January 6 probe that includes attempts to turn over the election, it would be his third indictment since April, the second one by the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Reaction from his defenders, swift.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): That's absolute (EXPLETIVE DELETED). Yes, that's my reaction. This is the only way that the Democrats have to beat President Trump is to arrest him, smear him, charge him with ridiculous charges.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: But, Neil, Democrats wasted no time capitalizing on this news.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PETE AGUILAR (D-CA): I don't think it's surprising. We expect that the target letter and the work that the grand jury continues to do will meet that threshold of seeking the facts and holding people accountable, no matter where they are and no matter who they are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants the trial in the documents to probe to happen after the 2024 election. The special counsel wants it in five months.\par \par \uc2 We just got word from the courtroom down in Florida federal Judge Aileen Cannon, ironically appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , said she would rule promptly on a trial date, but has not made a decision yet -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: David, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In case you're counting -- and David touched on it -- this is the third major series of indictments the former president could be facing.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, those challenging him for the Republican presidential nomination are wasting little time discussing it. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  is one of them. He joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. Republican presidential candidates have had an opportunity today to respond to this latest targeting.\par \par \uc2 We're hearing from the former president, that special counsel Jack Smith has gone ahead and called into his role in the January 6 attack on Capitol Hill. There's a lot of details we don't know. We don't how this will all pan out. We do know it's the third time we have seen something like this.\par \par \uc2 Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  is here.\par \par \uc2 Vivek, what do you make of this?\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Look, I think it would be a disaster if this did proceed to an indictment.\par \par \uc2 I just think we are setting a terrible precedent, Neil, of the party in power using police force to indict its lead political opponent in the middle of an election. And I think that this one is even more serious in its consequence than the prior indictments because of Section 3 of the 14th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 I think this is what's actually motivating them in this case. That says that if somebody who has sworn an oath of office is now actually convicted on a count of either, let's just say, insurrection or rebellion -- those are the words used in the 14th Amendment -- they're then ineligible for office.\par \par \uc2 And I think that would be a disaster for the country to literally eliminate a lead political rival in the midst of an election. And I say that as someone who is running against Trump. So it would be easier for me if Trump were eliminated. But that's not the outcome that I want to see, because I don't think that will be good for the country, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Nevertheless, that's very different, Vivek, no offense to how you sounded days after the attack on the Capitol, when you said or tweeted, I believe: "What Trump did last week was wrong, downright abhorrent, plain and simple. I have said it before."\par \par \uc2 You went on to say that: "Trump's egregious behavior blinds us from seeing that big tech's cure is worse. Makes for a winning game for China in the long run" when you talked about whether they were fairly dealing with this, fairly dealing with both sides on this.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering what changed.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Actually, nothing changed, Neil. I have been very consistent at every step, including in the video that I put out today, that I would have made different judgments than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did on January 6, 2021. I will remind you that I'm running in an election against him.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, no, no, at the time, you said what Trump did was wrong, what he did then was wrong.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Absolutely, he was wrong.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You were unequivocal.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: And, Neil, this is -- and I'm unequivocal today that there's a difference between doing something wrong, as he did, versus doing something that is illegal.\par \par \uc2 And I think the dangerous abuse of power in this country, Neil, is converting every bad judgment, every wrong behavior and wrong judgment into something that's criminal when it's politically convenient.\par \par \uc2 So, I respectfully disagree with you, Neil. I have been crystal clear and consistent on this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But this is potentially than just a bad judgment, isn't it, Vivek? This is something more than just a bad judgment, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, if even prosecutors pursuing this case are right that he galvanized a crowd to do what he did and what they did, does he bear some responsibility for that, as the leader of the country, the commander in chief?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: On the facts as we have them, I don't think he should bear criminal responsibility, and I think that that would be a disastrous precedent in this country.\par \par \uc2 I think what really fueled January 6, Neil, was a year of censorship, systematic censorship, telling people they couldn't talk about COVID lockdowns, telling people they couldn't talk about the Hunter Biden laptop story on the eve of a presidential election.\par \par \uc2 I think that's a big part of the pent-up frustration that showed up on January 6. And as somebody who is running to lead...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you really believe -- now, most Americans -- many Americans who were troubled by that, Vivek...\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Neil, if I may. Neil, if I may.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, seriously.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But do you really believe people who were troubled by that -- and you're quite right. Many, many were.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But they didn't storm the United States Capitol.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Neil, I think that what happens in our country is that, if you tell people they cannot speak, that is when they scream.\par \par \uc2 If you tell people they cannot scream, that is when they tear things down. And as somebody who wants to lead this country forward -- and that is why I'm in this race -- I think it's important that we confront that reality, acknowledge the systematic suppression of information and people's voices that led to the frustration that boiled over on January 6, if we are to actually move forward as one nation.\par \par \uc2 I do not want a national divorce, Neil. I'm in this race because I think I'm going to be able to lead this country forward differently than any other candidate in this race.\par \par \uc2 But part of that involves acknowledging the legitimate grievances of Americans who have systematically been told, frankly, to shut up, sit down, and do as they're told. And I reject that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But Americans don't then systematically go to the Capitol and storm it.\par \par \uc2 You're quite right. There was rampant frustration with the system. But people don't always express it that way. You don't believe, in retrospect...\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: And that was wrong. And that's exactly what I said, and I say it today.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, I know what you're saying. No, no, I know what you're saying, that that is not criminal behavior. You don't think it's criminal behavior on the part of the president.\par \par \uc2 Should it disqualify him from running for president again?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I think it should be an issue that the voters weigh when they make their decision.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I'm asking you. I'm asking you. Do you think it should?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I'm making my case to the people, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I do not think it should disqualify him legally from running. I think it should affect the decisions that voters make at the ballot box, because that's how we do things in the United States of America. The people of this country decide who leads them through a democratic process in our constitutional republic.\par \par \uc2 So should voters take into account when deciding? Absolutely. And I'm making my case in the same election that Trump is. And I intend to win, Neil. But I want to win by convincing voters of why they should vote for me, not by having the federal police state eliminate my competition. That's why I stand on principle.\par \par \uc2 And I say the same thing today that I have at every step of the way. You know what? Right after January 6, I published it in The Wall Street Journal, making the case that censorship was responsible for what happened. Three advisers to my company resigned. So, yes, it has been at considerable personal risk too, Neil, that these are touchy subjects, but I believe it is important to speak the truth without apology.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 But you have also been on record, Vivek, you have also been on record as saying that you would pardon the president should he assume -- or you assume office. Do you still stand by that?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I would because I think we -- I do.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Because there are a lot of people who do not like what went down...\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I don't...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... and don't think you're in a position, should you become president, to go ahead and pardon him.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I don't -- I don't like what went down, Neil, but what I really care about is moving our country forward.\par \par \uc2 And I think that it is an awful precedent in the United States to set the conditions for tug-of-war between political parties weaponizing our justice system against their political opponents.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you would have a lot to pardon for him, Vivek.\par \par \uc2 As a President Ramaswamy, you would have a lot to pardon him for, three cases, maybe a fourth. That could build with Georgia and his alleged role there trying to influence that election. Would you pardon him for all of those?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Well, the Georgia case has not been brought.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're right. You're quite right.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: But for the two indictments that have been brought, as well as the facts as they're known about January 6 -- and, Neil, I'm a guy who responds to facts and matches them to the law.\par \par \uc2 But for the two indictments that have been brought, I have committed -- you're correct -- that I would pardon him. And I think that that is in the national interest to move forward as a nation.\par \par \uc2 One of the people I take inspiration from, actually, is \uc2 Nelson Mandela\uc2 , the way he actually reunited a nation that was badly divided. If his task was so great, and he was able to achieve it, I think the next U.S. president can rise to the occasion to bridge the divide between this country as well.\par \par \uc2 But it is going to require putting principle over self-interest, and that's what I'm doing as a candidate. There are other candidates in this field that would like to see Trump eliminated. I want to win this election, but I'm not one of them that wants to win that way. I want to win by convincing the voters.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. We will see what happens with this case and if anything happens on Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Vivek, very good seeing you again. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Good seeing you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I want to give you a quick look. It's not only here, folks. In Rome -- it's nighttime now -- but man, oh, man, was it hot there today. I think it got north of 107 degrees. And let's just say, after all of that, they're not saying grazie -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sick of the same old choices? Well, they're talking about a third- party option. They have talked about that before, but why what's happening with the No Labels folks has a certain West Virginia Democrat getting a lot of attention.\par \par \uc2 We're on that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): We're here to make sure that the American people have an option.\par \par \uc2 And the option is, can you move the political parties off their respective sides? They have gone too far right and too far left. If that movement can move, but they can't be done -- that can't be done unless they're threatened.\par \par \uc2 I have never been any race I have ever spoiled. I have been in races to win. And if I get in a race, I'm going to win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, how many potential third-party candidates have said that over the past?\par \par \uc2 Some very successfully getting double digits. Of course, we could remember back with \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  getting 19 percent in 1992, yet not a single electoral vote. We could also remember what was going on in countless elections in between and after where at least the third-party candidate was influencing votes in key states, for example, \uc2 Ralph Nader\uc2  in Florida tilting a race that many argue \uc2 Al Gore\uc2  would have won had he not been siphoning votes away.\par \par \uc2 So we could go on and on about this, but let's go to Senator Joseph Lieberman, the former presidential -- vice presidential candidate, what he makes of this. Very involved in the No Labels movement, the co-chair.\par \par \uc2 Senator, once again, people are looking, could it happen? And many say, yes, I'm open to it, but I think it's a wasted vote. What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 FMR. SEN. JOE LIEBERMAN (I-CT): I tell them that if they're as dissatisfied with the two major parties today and as angry with the two major parties as they are, maybe the best way to change that and try to get those parties to come back from the left and right toward the center and common ground and try to solve our problems is to support, one, the policy agenda we put out yesterday, which is bipartisan. It's\par \par \uc2 not about us and them. It's about we, the people. And the second is if we decide to run a third ticket, a bipartisan unity ticket to support that ticket, because other than that, the two major parties seem to have been working hard at shutting out the majority of people who are not to the left and the right, but really in that broad middle ground where they just want the country to do better than it's doing now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Senator, one of the things I have heard is that the germ for all of this, the catalyst for this was if we have the same race we had four years ago, in other words, if it were \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  going at each other, then No Labels would submit a candidate.\par \par \uc2 Then the name that most often comes up is the West Virginia senator. But what if it's not that? What if it's a different race? Would No Labels stand down or what?\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: It depends.\par \par \uc2 And, incidentally, that's why we say that the effort we're making now to get on the ballot of all 50 states with a third line is an insurance policy. Like any insurance policy, in many ways, you hope you don't have to use it, but it's there if you do.\par \par \uc2 And we know that we're probably not going to be able to make a sensible decision about this until next year, certainly after the Super Tuesday primaries in March, in which case we would make the decision after that. It may be that one or both of the candidates who are now leading, President Trump, Biden, will dominate the nominating process and decide earlier.\par \par \uc2 But when the field becomes clear, then we will decide whether we have a constructive role to play by running a bipartisan unity ticket. We will do it if we think we actually have a chance to win, which I think we might, because the public distaste for the two parties and, frankly, for these two candidates is so great.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're talking about \uc2 Jon Huntsman\uc2  as well, former Utah Republican candidate for president as well.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: While I have you, Senator, I'd be remiss if I didn't mention this controversial remark out of \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She since dialed it back and about attacking Israel. But I want you to react to this, in case some folks at home have not heard it. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): We have been fighting to make it clear that Israel is a racist state, that the Palestinian people deserve self- determination and autonomy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A racist state,that's what Israel is. She's dialed that back somewhat. A lot of Democrats, a lot of Republicans are furious. What about you?\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Well, I thought the statement was offensive and offensive to everybody.\par \par \uc2 It's just not the American way. And, of course, it's not based on the fact about life in Israel. Israel is a great democracy, a rule of law country. The Israeli Arabs have tremendous rights, equal rights in Israel. Christians have more in Israel than in any other country in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 So, I -- this is part of too much undisciplined talk, which takes our country down. And it really hurts us. And I'm grateful that the Democratic leadership of the House immediately rejected this. But it was said in a way that makes you worry. And I think everybody's got to think a little bit about it.\par \par \uc2 It was divisive, and I hope we don't hear anything like it again about Israel or any other people or any other allied country that really is a democracy and full of equal opportunity and equal rights under the law.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Joe Lieberman, thank you very much. I always enjoy our chats.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Thanks, Neil. I do too. Have a good day.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you be well.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, here's the kind of clout, if you think about it, that Wisconsin Republican \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  has. He wanted to meet with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  didn't waste a nanosecond to do just that. What came of that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER, PRESIDENT, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 : If we don't stand tall right now, we are all going to be in trouble. We are all going to be in jeopardy of being replaced by machines.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): And if what we end up having is A.I.-written scripts, we're all going to get accustomed to less thoughtful entertainment. And the writers are saying, let's have some check on that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, but A.I. is moving along fast, Congressman. It's scary how fast it's moving along, you know?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: It is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: It's in my district, the heart of Silicon Valley.\par \par \uc2 And it's going to have a lot of great impacts, but we have got to make sure, ultimately, it's a human being with the final say.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: For the say -- for the time being, it is. It's -- artificial intelligence is really the crux of this whole battle going on between actors and writers who are taking on the major studios right now.\par \par \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  really wanted to step back and get a good read on all of this from everybody. He not only was able to sit down with \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , the aforementioned congressman, but \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . You might have heard about that latter fellow.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Congressman, very good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Both sides say this A.I. thing is accelerating quickly. I know \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  had argued maybe we should pause it right now. When you met with him and the congressman, what did you learn about any hope of a pause?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2  (R-WI): Well, I think the fact that Elon himself has now entered into the A.I. race, launching xAI hours...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: ... before our conversation suggests what I felt from the beginning, which is that the pause is impractical.\par \par \uc2 I don't want to discount the concerns over A.I. Certainly, I'm hearing those from constituents, and it's important that we take those concerns seriously and we develop a framework for using A.I. ethically. But if we pause, only one party benefits, and that's the Chinese Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 And the one thing we know for certain is that the Chinese Communist Party will use this technology for evil in order to turbocharge their Orwellian, genocidal, totalitarian surveillance state, and then export that model around the world. So we don't have the luxury of pausing. We in the free world at least have the opportunity to wield this technology for good, not for evil, as the CCP will do.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're talking about the Chinese Central Party.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you a little bit about this pause thing, because you're quite right, China is not pausing. Then the issue becomes the fallout, the economic fallout. You have all of these actors and writers who are picketing right now, because that's their biggest fear, that A.I. is going to replace them and they're going to be streamed right out of those jobs.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, the honest answer is, we don't know fully the impact it's going to have on the American economy.\par \par \uc2 I suspect there will be a lot of industries that are disrupted, but there are other industries in which the overall effect is going to be to create many better jobs, higher-paying jobs. I think the reality we have to confront is that A.I. is here. It's advancing rapidly.\par \par \uc2 So we can't simply ignore it or pause or just pretend we can go back to the status quo pre-A.I. So what we need to do is develop an ethical free world framework for A.I.'s use, be sensitive to the disruption in various industries, but ensure that we dominate this technology going forward.\par \par \uc2 And I feel that, if we do, ultimately, on balance, that's going to be positive for the American economy and, again, create new and better and higher-paying jobs and opportunities for a lot of Americans out there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think, while I still have you, sir, of this young soldier who is being detained in North Korea?\par \par \uc2 He walked right into North Korea. What should we do?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, I have only heard press reports of this. I haven't gotten an official briefing. We're hoping we're going to get something.\par \par \uc2 But, obviously, anybody who's familiar with the case of \uc2 Otto Warmbier\uc2  knows how dangerous this is. We're dealing with a fanatical regime, an enemy regime, and so our heart goes out to his family. I'm hoping to get more information so that we can ascertain what we can do to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 One thing I have said for a long time is that, additionally, people that make the case for cooperating with China say that our interests are shared when it comes to stability on the Korean Peninsula. But my view is that the Chinese regime, in many cases, enables the depravity of the North Korean regime, and that we need secondary sanctions on certain Chinese banks and businesses that provide an economic lifeline to the North Korean regime.\par \par \uc2 So we will see what's happening in this case, but it's time for us to step back and assess our overall economic relationship with China and, by extension, North Korea.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you. Good catching up with you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime here, I told you about the hot weather here and all throughout Europe.\par \par \uc2 In France, it's so bad right now -- it's been over 100 degrees for days back and forth -- they're already worried about the Olympics next year. Of course, they're hosting them. And they're wondering what to do if it gets like the way it is now next year when they're hosting -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in case you're counting, we have had seven up days in a row for the Dow Jones industrials, a lot of it buoyed by steady-as--he goes, Goldilocks kind of economic numbers, not too hot, not too cold.\par \par \uc2 The latest retail sales report, though it was up for the third month in a row, it was rather tepid, but that about suits investors, who seem to think now, seven days in a row, the type of a run-up, consistent run-up in the markets we have not seen in the better part of two years, that maybe something's going on here.\par \par \uc2 Hitha Herzog is a retail watcher, H Squared Research. She's very good at sort of picking apart what the consumer is thinking or not thinking.\par \par \uc2 And, Hitha, I mean, they're being more selective, as you were very prescient to seize on years ago. But they're playing it carefully, and so carefully that, even on the high end, those who would be drawn to expensive vehicles, like a \uc2 Ford\uc2  F-150 electric vehicle, they just slashed the price of that by $10,000 to woo people in. What's going on here?\par \par \uc2 HITHA HERZOG, RETAIL WATCHER: A couple of things are going on, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So you're absolutely right. The consumer is being much more careful about where they're spending their money. But the consumer is also working from home more. So that means there are more options for them to go shopping, and they're being very selective of where they want to go shopping.\par \par \uc2 So there was an interview with one of the executives at Simon Mall, and they were saying that now, in the suburbs, they're really trying to target those customers out in the suburbs, trying to make experiences for them, so that they will come into the mall and actually not only just go shopping, but spend their money there, in addition to the fact that small businesses, Neil, are really starting to ramp up e-commerce sales.\par \par \uc2 So, according to the Data Catalyst Institute -- it was a report that I worked on -- about 61 percent of small businesses spent more than $10,000 on advertising digitally across all certain platforms. So when you have that, compared to e-commerce sales increasing by 76 percent compared to 2019, people are just buying more online, and they're going into the stores more to either pick up or spend that money.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what I do notice, even in the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Prime day sales last week, that they're spending, and it was surprisingly up, even adjusting for inflation year over year.\par \par \uc2 But it was a wide swathe. It was more inexpensive than expensive items. That isn't too startling, I guess. But what did you make of the breakdown of what people are buying and the stores or the retailers benefiting?\par \par \uc2 HERZOG: Well, it's really interesting, Neil, because it wasn't just \uc2 Amazon\uc2  that benefited from \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Prime Day.\par \par \uc2 What we're seeing is this ripple effect. So there was a direct to consumer CEO of a company, right, direct-to-consumer brand, right? They were saying that their business grew tenfold from just the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Prime Day promotions, right, that they had in conjunction with \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Prime Day.\par \par \uc2 And they weren't selling just on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . This was sales on their Web sites too. Do so this ripple effect is not just having a massive impact on how people are buying on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , but also the smaller companies are seeing this benefit to it.\par \par \uc2 So the point is, yes, it looks like retail sales are slowing a little bit, but the consumer is remaining resilient. And I think -- to wrap it all up, I think the consumer is taking a little bit of a pause, and we're going to see that ramp up a lot more in Q4, because I think everyone's just saving their money just to spend it as we get closer to the holiday.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Good stuff, Hitha. Thank you again.\par \par \uc2 HERZOG: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, if at first you don't succeed when you're not pleased with the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , kind of do the exact same thing and see if you can get away with it.\par \par \uc2 Why Senator \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  isn't pleased with something the White House is pulling. He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: These borrowers are owed this relief, yet Republican elected officials, including those who had no problem with the government forgiving billions of dollars in business loans, are still objecting to, including their own constituents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, technically, the Supreme Court rejected that last effort of the part of the administration to forgive upwards of half-a-trillion dollars worth of student loans.\par \par \uc2 Now it's a smaller, more bite-sized package at about $39 billion, but it grows exponentially in the years ahead, and so CBO has ruled.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2 , the North Carolina Republican, kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 Senator, what do you think of this second crack at the apple here?\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC): It's in many respects worse than the first one.\par \par \uc2 The fact of the matter is, the $39 billion -- you're right -- it's going to be much more than that. It ultimately gets up to about a half-trillion dollars. I don't think it's putting the resources in the right place. And I think comparing student loan forgiveness to what we did to keep our economy afloat doing cares is an apple and an orange.\par \par \uc2 It probably speaks to the lack of valid arguments that the White House has for this policy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm sure they're well aware at the White House that this is an issue or they hope it's an issue that will politically resonate.\par \par \uc2 But I can just as easily imagine many for whom this will tick off voters all over again, especially those who have paid off their college loans, or those not going to college at all. Why should they be funding this? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: I think you're absolutely right. That was the narrative just shortly after the president passed this by executive order in the past.\par \par \uc2 He needs to keep in mind it is unfair for people who worked, who got a degree, got a job, paid off their loans to suddenly create this, I believe an entitlement, that maybe there are things that we should do to provide some sort of a safety net.\par \par \uc2 But why don't we start earlier by making sure that young men and women who are going to college are getting educated in a field where they can pay down their debt, understand the economic consequences, have colleges be more forthcoming with the cost of an education and the likelihood that you could ever pay a loan back?\par \par \uc2 These are fundamentals in financial literacy that I think are at the root of the problem.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I was talking to a Democratic congressman not too long ago, Senator, who started questioning Republicans' consistency on this issue to say, who are they to say this is the major you should have or the moneymaking potential to pay off your loans, focus of study that you should have, that that's not their call.\par \par \uc2 What do you say?\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Look, Neil, when someone is sitting down thinking about going to school -- it took me 17 years to get my four-year degree. I paid along the way.\par \par \uc2 When somebody is sitting down, they need someone across the table explaining them the consequences of their action. If they pursue a degree that has a market value of maybe $40,000 or $50,000 a year, and they're spending $200,000 to actually get the degree, do the math. There's no way that you can pay it off.\par \par \uc2 I think we have to be honest with people. I want everybody to pursue whatever they want to, but they need to do it with a full understanding of what the financial consequences are as they pursue a career. So this is just more a matter of being realistic.\par \par \uc2 And I think there's a role to be played by colleges, by high school counselors, a number of other people to not set these future students and employees up for failure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Senator, great catching up with you. I apologize for the truncated time here.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  of North Carolina of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , much, much more.\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 All right, one last peek at the corner of Wall and Broad. It's not only hot outside. It's very hot at the corner of Wall and Broad, seventh straight day for the Dow. A lot of people are saying, the momentum is there, more buyers are coming in.\par \par \uc2 You never like to jinx these things, but there are a couple of constructive developments we're going to be exploring on FOX Business tomorrow, which, if you don't get, you should demand. But you do get it, and you do love it.\par \par \uc2 So, here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230718ej7i0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Miami, Florida, Mayor \uc2 Francis Suarez\uc2 ; Biden Administration's New Student Debt Relief Plan; Interview With Rep. Michael McCaul (R-TX); Bridge in Crimea Attacked; Deadly Flooding Strikes Pennsylvania; U.S. Sending More Jets, Warships to Middle East; Secret Service Wraps Up White House Cocaine Probe; New York Police Update Serial Killer Investigation; Interview With Fmr. Gov. \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  (R-MD); Hollywood Actors Strike Goes On; Heat Wave\b0\par\par\uc2 Hillary Vaughn, Alex Hogan, Katie Byrne, Jacqui Heinrich, Nate  Foy, Molly Line, Marissa Torres, Robert Ray, Bill Melugin, Charles Payne \par \uc2 8,895 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Getting overheated like that, you start to get tired, irritable, angry. You're not thinking clearly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I will tell you, when I walked off the plane, I couldn't breathe. It just, like, stopped me from breathing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's burning, man. It's like -- it's like a burning heat.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's absolutely perfect. I'm like a lizard. I could soak this up all day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It feels like I'm like a little pig, like a rotisserie chicken in \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , just, like, around, you know?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: FOX on top of a heat wave showing no signs of letting up and union workers rising up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 And this is a long, hot summer. More on those union uprisings in a moment, but first to the heat, nearly 85 million people on alert as forecasters warn these hellish conditions, well, they could drag on for weeks.\par \par \uc2 To FOX Weather's Robert Ray in Columbus, New Mexico, with the very latest - - Robert.\par \par \uc2 So, Robert is frozen.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting, as we came into it, you heard one person saying he could soak it up like a lizard, and another one saying they feel like a roasted pig. So I think that describes almost all of us, especially the latter. The bottom line, though, is it's a really deep, deep situation. It's covering the entire country.\par \par \uc2 And we will get back to that later, as Robert -- we unfreeze Robert. Interesting that he got frozen on a heat wave story.\par \par \uc2 But let's talk about the heat in Hollywood, because that is really building up big time, the strike there by actors now in its fourth day. We're going to take a live look outside of \uc2 Netflix\uc2 's headquarters. This is in Los Angeles, folks, where one former studio chief is now warning of a total collapse of the industry if this strike is not settled by the start of September.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Bill Melugin in Los Angeles with the latest on the standoff -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Charles. Good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 As you can see behind me, the picketing under way here outside of \uc2 Netflix\uc2  headquarters in Hollywood. This is the first time in over 60 years that Hollywood writers and actors are on strike together at the exact same time.\par \par \uc2 I will step out and give you a live look. This has been going on for several hours now, and these strikes have essentially completely shut down Hollywood. They're expected to cost billions of dollars in losses, but the strikers say it's worth it. It has to happen.\par \par \uc2 Both of these unions want more compensation. They want residuals for streaming content. They also want assurances that artificial intelligence is not going to replace them. And then take a look at this video. California Congressman Adam Schiff, he was out here this morning on the picket lines joining these strikers. He says he represents a lot of the folks who are striking.\par \par \uc2 He shared their concerns about artificial intelligence and says, because he knows a lot of these people, he knows how hard they work, and he wants them to get a good deal. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): All too many people back in the Congress think that everyone who works in this industry is some multimillionaire celebrity, when the reality is, these are folks that are just trying to put bread on the table, keep a roof over the head, provide for their family, work enough so that they can qualify for health insurance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And as these historic strikes continue, the former CEO of Paramount predicts that, if this goes on into the fall, he says, the entire Hollywood industry could completely collapse.\par \par \uc2 Now, as for the other side of the table when it comes to these negotiations, the alliance representing these production studios, they are calling the demands being made by these unions behind me -- quote -- "unrealistic."\par \par \uc2 So, as it stand right now, in the short term, it does not look like we are close to getting a deal, at least in the oncoming horizon, Charles. So we will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Bill, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, there's some dire warnings out there about this strike, but it's not the only one. Now, aside from this Hollywood actors and screenwriter strike, the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  threatening a strike against United Parcel. That would be at the end of the month. \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  pilots, they appear to have walked -- set to walk off the job, this after talks have failed to reach an agreement.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  says it's prepared also to strike against the Big Three automakers when its contract expires in mid-September.\par \par \uc2 So, from your favorite TV show to packages getting to your home, consumers, you're about to feel the brunt of all of this, but to what degree?\par \par \uc2 Let's ask. Economist Steve Moore with me now.\par \par \uc2 Steve, the screen actors, the strike, that's $3 billion, UPS, if it's a week, $7 billion. And, of course, we know the airlines, that kind of thing. I mean, it sounds like it's going to be an avalanche of economic pain across the board.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: Yes, it sure could be.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, I'm hooked on "The Lincoln Lawyer," so I got to have that, my fix.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MOORE: And what am I going to do if we don't have that show or "Better Call Saul"?\par \par \uc2 So this has been going on for months in Hollywood and we will see whether this gets resolved. But you make a really good point, Charles, which is that there is labor unrest in a lot of industries right now, including package delivery, the airlines.\par \par \uc2 And what's going on, I think, is that the unions are flexing their muscle here. They have got a president in the White House, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who will just genuflect to whatever they want, and that's giving them more bargaining power. And so we will see.\par \par \uc2 Now, to take the labor side on this for a minute, let's face it, because of Bidenflation, prices are up 16, 17 percent since Biden came into office. So workers are feeling the middle-class squeeze, thanks to these policies in Washington.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Isn't there some irony, though? I mean, you look at the new contract for \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  workers, a 40 percent raise over the next few years.\par \par \uc2 Listen, that's pretty good money. And you talk about the wage spiral, which was sparked in part because this administration paid people not to work.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All of a sudden, you had this wage-price spiral that they haven't been able to contain since then. Where does it stop? Because the irony of it all is, the more people are getting paid, the more there's inflation, the more they need to get paid.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, I couldn't -- again, you said it very well, Charles, and you get this cycle.\par \par \uc2 And this is exactly, by the way, what happened in the 1970s, when you had very high inflation. Then workers and unions demanded big, big wage increases, and then that fueled the inflation, and you had a cycle that lasted many years. Let's hope we don't repeat that cycle right now.\par \par \uc2 But the other problem is, look, we have got an economy that is showing some signs of improvement, finally may be showing some signs of some growth. And now, if you see these strikes, for example, with respect to the package delivery, with the airlines...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: ... this could cause havoc for the economy as we go forward.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hey, I got a minute to go, a little less.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But, Steve, the UPS strike is -- sorry -- the -- the UPS strike is interesting, in the sense that the union has already said to President Biden, don't intervene, do not intervene at all.\par \par \uc2 Those -- the unions are big backers. They're big supporters and they raise a lot of money for President Biden. What kind of corner does this back him into?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, I would say that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to genuflect to the unions. That's what he did, remember, with the rail strike earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 We're seeing this all over the country. Look at how the governor of Pennsylvania vetoed a bill that he had supported when the teachers union said, we're not going to support you if you do this. So this is an example where the unions, which are the number one money source of the Democratic Party right now, exert incredible impact on the party, even when it's against the interests of the nation as a whole.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Steve Moore, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Thanks, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, let's get back to the temperatures rising story.\par \par \uc2 And I want to go back to FOX Weather's Robert Ray, who is in Columbus, New Mexico, on this heat wave that's blanketing the nation -- Robert.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT RAY, FOX WEATHER MULTIMEDIA JOURNALIST: Yes, Charles, hot weather creating technical issues a little earlier. My apologies, but, yes, greetings from Columbus, New Mexico, on the border here.\par \par \uc2 It is scorching in the desert, and it is scorching for so many people from California to Florida. Well, I spent the entire past weekend in Texas. Take a look at some of this video, just striking, as the sun beating down on El Paso, and that border town seeing 30 days-plus of temperatures above 100 degrees.\par \par \uc2 But I started my trip in Galveston, Texas, on the coast, and we look at some of the people on the water that are trying to cool off, except for, unfortunately, the Gulf of Mexico waters are very warm right now. And that's not going to bode well for tropical storm season, when those systems begin to form.\par \par \uc2 And then San Antonio, the Alamo, and people on the River Walk trying to enjoy that beautiful city, the hotels, the restaurants, spoke to some of them over the weekend about how they are dealing with this incredible heat. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And I'm drenching in sweat with my water bottle. Stay hydrated.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It feels like football practice in the middle of August. That's what it feels like. It's hot.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Business during the day is really slow. At nighttime, that's when everyone comes out. It's back to normal. But, definitely it slowed down a lot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAY: Yes, it does.\par \par \uc2 And people are trying to stay out. And if you are out, yes, you got to have a brim or some kind of hat and a lot of sunscreen and stay hydrated, for sure. Nearly 100 million people in the throngs of this incredible sun, and especially in the Desert Southwest right now, just unreal, Vegas hitting records, El Paso.\par \par \uc2 Today, alone, over 50 records, Charles, could be struck down as far as high temperatures. Charles, I'm doing a cross-country trip. I started in Florida last Thursday. I will be in Santa Monica Pier, California, on Thursday. And all I have been experiencing is the incredible heat, but yet the glory and magic and beauty of this country -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Absolutely. You're in some great places, especially the River Walk.\par \par \uc2 Hey, keep cool, Robert. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, how long is this sweltering heat going to last? This is a question everyone's asking.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to FOX Weather meteorologist Marissa Torres on what exactly we should expect.\par \par \uc2 Give us the layout, Marissa.\par \par \uc2 MARISSA TORRES, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, not a lot of good news. I mean, we're locked in a pattern. We have been here for about two weeks, and we're looking at it continuing on. So, Robert Ray just to the west of El Paso in Southern New Mexico, no matter which way Robert goes, to the west, to the east, to the north, he is stuck in these heat alerts, 85 million Americans stuck in these heat alerts that span from Southern California into Nevada, across the Desert Southwest, all the way into the Lower Mississippi Valley.\par \par \uc2 When Robert finally gets to the coastal areas of California, he will finally feel that cooler ocean breeze, but it is locked in, this heat. You have 118 million Americans currently experiencing those 90-degree temperatures or hotter.\par \par \uc2 But that's not the full story, right? Because we know, when we factor in that humidity, it's the heat index that truly matters in this excessive, very dangerous heat. So, the humidity, the air temperature making it feel more like the triple digits, areas in Texas feeling like 110 right now.\par \par \uc2 Want to point out Phoenix, it looks like, officially hitting 110. The humidity not so much a factor in the Desert Southwest like Phoenix. That's important, and I will show you in a reason, but this is the heat we're dealing with.\par \par \uc2 Phoenix is spectacular, in the fact that we're now seeing historical heat that has now lasted 18 days where it's been 110 or hotter, the potential records for today from Austin into Roswell into Flagstaff, Arizona, there. You take a look into tomorrow, it's a very similar story. It's getting a little monotonous, Roswell, New Mexico, expected to hit 111 there, Phoenix expected to hit 117, and into Wednesday. Albuquerque, New Mexico looking at 102.\par \par \uc2 So these are some of the records that we're forecasting, but a lot of 111s across the board from Arizona into Texas and New Mexico. Now, Phoenix, I pointed you out in particular because you look at some of the longest stretches that Phoenix has seen 110 or hotter, and it's not something they do frequently.\par \par \uc2 In fact, we were at 17 days as of today. But the fact that I just saw 110 on the thermometer means we have now hit 18 days in a row officially, which would tie the record from 1974. You look further into the future, the 10- day forecast shows temperatures easily stretching between 110 to almost 120 for the remainder of the week into the weekend into next week.\par \par \uc2 You add that up, you're talking about 28 days of temperatures reaching that 110 threshold or hotter, which is historic on so many levels. It is monsoon season for the Desert Southwest. Unfortunately, a lot of this moisture looks like it stays primarily in the higher elevations, not quite making it into places like Phoenix, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Marissa, wow. Thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, folks, we're expecting New York State Police to provide an update on the arrest of the Gilgo Beach murder suspect, a case that is captivating the nation. That press conference moments away, and we will take you there live.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, the 2024 field could get even bigger, this as West Virginia Democrat Governor -- Senator, rather, Joe Manchin headlines a No Labels town hall in New Hampshire in the next hour, alongside former Utah Republican Governor \uc2 Jon Huntsman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, is a third-party ticket in the future?\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in FOX News' Molly Line on what's going down -- Molly.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY LINE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hello.\par \par \uc2 Well, No Labels bills itself as this national movement born out of frustration with America's political parties and also extremes on the right and on the left. And the organization, this group, is unapologetically prepping for a potential bipartisan third-party presidential bid in 2024.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, they're holding a town hall today up there in the first- in-the-nation primary state of New Hampshire featuring Joe Manchin, the Democrat from West Virginia, and Governor \uc2 Jon Huntsman\uc2 , former governor of Utah, a Republican.\par \par \uc2 No Labels is promising, essentially, a commonsense approach. And they just have released a policy booklet by that very name touting centrist positions. Former Congressman Joe Lieberman, Democrat-turned-independent, is a founding chairman.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FMR. SEN. JOE LIEBERMAN (I-CT): We have made very clear that we're not in this to be spoilers. We're in this to give the majority of the American people who feel that the major two parties are failing them a third choice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINE: No Labels as working to secure ballot access all across the country.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Party faithful are doing a bit of hand-wringing over this. Former Alabama Senator \uc2 Doug Jones\uc2  is among those joining a bipartisan effort to thwart a third-party move that could siphon away some votes and hamper President Biden's reelection bid.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FMR. SEN. \uc2 DOUG JONES\uc2  (D-AL): It looks like they will be a spoiler in favor of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and that will be the biggest threat to democracy that we have seen since January 6.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINE: Charles, they have been pushing back, No Labels has been pushing back on this idea that there are spoiler.\par \par \uc2 Lieberman vows that if polling shows that No Labels will ultimately help either major party candidate, then they won't get involved. There's even an insurance policy that they have published that they have put out that talked about the conditions under which they would need to see to actually put a candidate, a ticket forward, and they have yet to commit to actually do so -- Charles, back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Molly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, when it comes to a possible unity ticket, my next guest, well, his name keeps popping up, former Maryland Republican Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , who's the chairman of An America United, also honorary co-chair of No Labels.\par \par \uc2 All right, Governor, is it something that you're considering?\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2  (R-MD): It's really not something that I'm considering, Charles, but I totally understand the frustrations that led to a group like this.\par \par \uc2 And, first of all, I have been involved in the organization because I really believe in commonsense, bipartisan solutions and reaching across the aisle to get things done. It's what I did as governor, Republican governor in the bluest state in America.\par \par \uc2 But I think this is really in case of emergency break glass situation where the real intent -- my focus is on trying to get the Republican Party back on track and coming up with a nominee that I believe can win in November and that can get us back to a more Reaganesque big tent party.\par \par \uc2 But, look, there are about 70 percent of the people in America that do not want \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be president. And yet it looks as if, at this point in time -- we got a long way until the primaries. But it looks like they're -- that's a likely scenario.\par \par \uc2 And 59 percent of the people in the country say they would consider another alternative, which is an unusual -- we have never really had a situation like that in this country. And somewhere between 44 and 49 percent of the people in America are now registered independent, with only 25 or so being Democrats and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there's no doubt that more people don't want to be actually completely aligned with either party. By the way, since 18 -- since I'm 18 years old, I have been a registered independent, so -- and I have voted for third party on more than one occasion.\par \par \uc2 L. HOGAN: Very good.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. So I understand where people are coming from on this.\par \par \uc2 L. HOGAN: Well, now everybody's coming to where you are, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I'm a trendsetter.\par \par \uc2 L. HOGAN: It's a growing movement.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I'm a trendsetter.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: When I hear Molly say that -- or Joe Lieberman say the centrist position, now, this is where it gets a little bit more murky, when you start to sell this to the nation.\par \par \uc2 You got to have an economic policy. You got to have a foreign policy. You have got to have a domestic policy. When do these things get decided?\par \par \uc2 L. HOGAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: How are they articulated?\par \par \uc2 L. HOGAN: Well, they started to do that articulating today, or they're about to do later today, in New Hampshire, where -- Joe Manchin and Governor Huntsman are both going to be there, along with a number of others.\par \par \uc2 And they're laying out a very detailed kind of policy agenda on 30 major issues in America where they -- where we believe we can find common ground. And, look, you don't have to agree with every single position in there.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 L. HOGAN: But at least they're actually talking about solutions to big problems, which is something that, frankly, the American people don't think that Republicans or Democrats are doing these days.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 L. HOGAN: And so this is a long way off, but I think that having more people involved in the discussion, actually sitting down and listening to people who you may disagree with is a good thing for American. And it's how I was one of the most successful governors in the country by working together to get solutions.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Governor, I have got less than a minute to go.\par \par \uc2 Let me squeeze this in, though, OK, because we heard that if there was a sign that they would tilt the election one way or the other, they would back out. I mean, this is sort of -- from what I have read, right now, the hypothetical third-party candidate would get 21 percent of the votes, 13 percent from Biden, 8 percent from President Trump.\par \par \uc2 If that were the case, former President Trump, would the No Labels candidate drop out?\par \par \uc2 L. HOGAN: Well, look, I think we're a long way from figuring that out, but the fact that they already start with 21 percent saying that they would consider -- they would vote for the third-party choice, that's much higher than \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  got after an entire campaign.\par \par \uc2 So they start out in a stronger position than any potential independent or third-party run has ever done. And I think they're only in it to win it. If we believe we can win the race, then we're going to put together strong candidates to do that.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  is one of those I voted for as well.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Governor, thanks a lot. Really appreciate it. I can't wait to pick up on this again as the presidential season heats up.\par \par \uc2 L. HOGAN: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But, right now, folks, I have got to go to this.\par \par \uc2 New York State Police, they're providing an update on the arrest in Gilgo Beach, of course, the murder suspect. The press conference, you can see they're setting up for it. It's moments away.\par \par \uc2 Nate Foy is in Massapequa Park. He's going to give us the latest from there -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Yes, that press conference is set to start right now from New York State Police, one of the agencies that has long been investigating this case, and specifically since a task force was formed by the Suffolk County DA's office. And they have really made a lot of progress on this, a 13-year cold case finally, hopefully coming to an end.\par \par \uc2 Behind me, you see the home of Rex Heuermann. He's the suspected serial killer who's charged with murdering three women. He's also the prime suspect in a fourth case, another woman who was killed, each of these women between the ages of 22 and 27, all of them accused of being sex workers, their bodies found on Gilgo Beach back in 2010.\par \par \uc2 But I can tell you, in the past couple of days, over the weekend, investigators took out some evidence from the home behind me that included over 200 guns, as well as a child doll. They have also been removing boxes and buckets of evidence and loading them into the trucks behind me. You can see they have shut down the road behind me.\par \par \uc2 Again, the New York State Police, Suffolk County police, the Suffolk County Sheriff's Office and \uc2 FBI\uc2  all working together to solve this case. And, coming up, we're going to have a press conference from the State Police as they explain their role in solving this investigation.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Folks, we will go right back to the presser as soon as it begins.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the White House just pressed, the Secret Service wrapping up its cocaine probe with a few -- few details. Jacqui Heinrich will give us the latest on that.\par \par \uc2 And after the Supreme Court slap-down, the White House defending the student loan work-around. So, is the $39 billion just the beginning?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: With Iran acting up, the Pentagon teeing up additional warships and aircraft to protect a crucial waterway.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the White House touting another China visit. What's going to come of that?\par \par \uc2 House Foreign Affairs Chair Mike McCaul is here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The U.S. announcing it will send additional F-35s and F-16 aircraft and warship -- and a warship to the Middle East in a bid to monitor key waterways, this after Iran's recent seizure and harassment of commercial shipping vessels.\par \par \uc2 FOX News White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has more on that story, also more on today's press briefing -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the administration confirmed it's sending the USS Thomas Hudner, a warship you mentioned, and also additional F-35s and F-16s to the Strait of Hormuz to bolster security in that area after Iran's recent seizure and harassment of commercial shipping vessels.\par \par \uc2 I asked NSC spokesman John Kirby what the final straw was in pushing the administration to take this action. Here's what he just told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: . They have become more bellicose, more active and more dangerous to peaceful maritime shipping in the Gulf region.\par \par \uc2 And the secretary of defense wants to make sure, as he should, that we have got the proper capabilities and assets and readiness in place to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: In May, the White House announced the administration would make a series of moves in response to Iran's actions, and this is the first that we have seen play out since.\par \par \uc2 It's not clear where exactly the fighter jets will be placed or how long they will stay in that region, but the Pentagon is putting the ball in Iran's court on how they react to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH, PENTAGON DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: We call upon Iran to immediately cease these destabilizing actions that threaten the free flow of commerce through this strategic waterway of which the world depends on for more than one-fifth of the world's oil supply.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Since 2019, attacks in the strategic waters have ramped up during times of tension between the U.S. and Iran.\par \par \uc2 And with the Iran nuclear deal effectively dead, the U.S. and its allies have been looking for ways to de-escalate, especially with relations deteriorating over the last year or so.\par \par \uc2 Another headline out of this briefing, Charles, you will recall that the Secret Service has announced they have closed the cocaine investigation without any suspects. And \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  told me just a couple of weeks ago that it was very important to the president to get to the bottom of this.\par \par \uc2 So she was just asked if he's satisfied with this answer from Secret Service, and this was what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There is hundreds of visitors that travel through this area where -- where the specific -- where the cocaine was found across that weekend.\par \par \uc2 And so I'm going to leave it to them for any additional information, but certainly not going to going to opine on the process here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So, the president apparently is not going to get the answer that he wanted that she said was very important to him.\par \par \uc2 But the White House is closing the door on this, saying they believe, after all, it was a thorough investigation. There have been, as you have noted, as we have noted many times, Charles, criticisms for the Secret Service not going forward with conducting interviews, especially of those 500 suspects to be whittled down -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, the paraphrase the Rolling Stones, President Biden won't get any satisfaction this one, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: No satisfaction.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Switching gears, folks, at least five dead, two missing after deadly flooding strikes Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 FOX Weather's Katie Byrne is in Upper Makefield Township. That's in Pennsylvania with the very latest -- Katie.\par \par \uc2 KATIE BYRNE, FOX MULTIMEDIA JOURNALIST: Well, Charles, crews are using a lot more equipment today in the search for 9-month-old baby Conrad and his older sister, 2-year-old Matilda.\par \par \uc2 They believe that the weather is helping them out with the search efforts today, and they're sharing these photos of the brother and sister as those searches for them intensifies. The fire chief says the fact that we don't have rain today, now they can use underwater equipment, air assets and canines on the ground.\par \par \uc2 We know five people are confirmed dead after rain submerged a busy road with feet of water on Saturday. Among the deaths, tragically, is the mom of those two kids. The family was driving to a barbecue when the road flooded and they got out to escape the flooding on foot.\par \par \uc2 The dad, his 4-year-old son and the grandmother all survived, but the mom, baby and toddler were swept away. Some survivors are saying they clung onto trees, waiting for crews to save them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELI WEISSMAN, FLOODING SURVIVOR: A gush of water just came down from 532, down a hill. As it was coming down, it was -- cars were, like, starting to float,. Just tried to survive, laying on our back feet, heading down, grabbing trees, grabbing vines, grabbing whatever we could to stay afloat.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BYRNE: Crews are expected to give an update any minute now, and we're expecting to hear from a representative of the family of those missing kids as well.\par \par \uc2 For now, we know the search is expected to continue until dark, and then they will reassess their plans for tomorrow -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Katie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The White House defending that $39 billion work-around on student loan debt, but is it legal?\par \par \uc2 And another top Biden administration official is visiting China. This time, it's top climate envoy \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 . So where does House Foreign Affairs Chair Mike McCaul fear this is all heading? We will ask him.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: To Russia now, where an attack on a key Crimea bridge left vehicle traffic at a standstill. Moscow blaming the blast on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 FOX News correspondent Alex Hogan has the very latest -- Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Russia is blaming specifically Ukrainian drones for this blast. And Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is calling for proposals to ensure the safety of this bridge.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , RUSSIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): This crime is senseless from a military standpoint, because the Crimean bridge is not used for military logistics since long ago.\par \par \uc2 It is also cruel because innocent civilians have been killed. Of course, there will be a response from the Russian side. The Defense Ministry is preparing adequate proposals.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 A. HOGAN: Ukraine officials -- Ukrainian officials, however, deny the involvement, calling it simply Russian provocation.\par \par \uc2 Explosions went off early this morning, killing a man and a woman whose now orphaned child was also in that car. She survived with serious injuries. An attack last October had temporarily closed this bridge as well. This is an extremely important corridor for Moscow, as it connects the annexed Crimean Peninsula to Russia.\par \par \uc2 And then, just hours later, Russia made some very big news, prompting global concern, after saying that it is walking away from the Black Sea deal. This is a deal allowing grain and other food shipments to leave Ukraine. It also protects the vessels carrying those food shipments.\par \par \uc2 Russia says it's going to prioritize its own shipments, although Russia has actually been shipping record volumes of wheat, all of this prompting a lot of condemnation from world leaders, saying that this is only going to create more of a global food crisis, especially for some of the world's most poor countries.\par \par \uc2 And in Kyiv today is \uc2 USAID\uc2  chief \uc2 Samantha Power\uc2 . She is there to announce that there has been an additional allotment of humanitarian aid from the U.S., as much as $500 million -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Alex Hogan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, the question, of course, is, what Will the impact of this have on the war?\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in chairman of the House Foreign Affairs Committee Texas Republican Congressman Michael McCaul.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  saying that there's going to be reprisals, there's going to be a response to this. What do you think? How far will this escalate, and what will the impact be on the war?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, let me say first I think the Ukrainian drone attack on the bridge between Russia and Crimea is very significant in terms of the counteroffensive.\par \par \uc2 This is really the supply line, if you will, of troops and weapons going from Russia through Crimea into the occupied territories of Ukraine. So that's a very significant development.\par \par \uc2 On the Black Sea grain deal, I believe that Russia for some time has been saying that they are not going to move forward with this agreement. And Charles, what does that mean for not just Ukraine, but the rest of the world? It means that, by doing so -- and it's very irresponsible, in my judgment -- they can -- you will see global famine, particularly in Africa.\par \par \uc2 It will also impact food prices in Europe, but also in the United States. This will have a direct impact on food prices really around the world. And I hope we can get our allies in countries like Turkey to help change Mr. Putin's mind.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, I mean, to that point, though, shouldn't -- shouldn't the United States, Ukraine, and our allies have seen this coming?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: I think so.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We had a year to prepare for it.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, you know me. I'm always for manufacturing in the United States.\par \par \uc2 I think we have got a great food supply here in the United States. We ought to be manufacturing here. We ought to be manufacturing semiconductor chips in the United States, looking at rare earth minerals, instead of China.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Instead of looking to China for these things, we ought to be doing it right here.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I think a lot of people will be surprised that rare earth isn't really rare. It's just sort of dangerous and harder to get to.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Chairman, I want to talk about the subpoena, that you subpoenaed the State Department. You want documents related to sanction policies toward China.\par \par \uc2 Today was the deadline. Have you heard back?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: No, we haven't. And we're going to move forward.\par \par \uc2 Look, this whole charm offensive with Beijing, I'm all for diplomacy, but to just get a meeting in the room, they have made concessions to not enforce export controls of, say, semiconductors to China. They have been not enforcing sanctions on human rights, like their violations against the Uyghur Muslims.\par \par \uc2 And they have also -- their drawdown to send weapons to Taiwan, they have not moved forward on that because they're trying to play nice with Beijing. But if you -- you know, in a negotiation, you need leverage and strength. You don't start in a weak position.\par \par \uc2 And then top all that with the spy balloon, the Cuba spy station, and now recently this hack in the State Department with their East Asian Pacific division prior to the visit of Secretary of State Blinken to Beijing.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 By the way, just to let the audience know, we have also reached out to the State Department, and we haven't heard from them yet. You talk about these visits. We got another high-profile visit, U.S. climate envoy \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  there to try to restart negotiations.\par \par \uc2 I mean, China is building a coal power plant every couple of weeks. They have got the most nuclear power in the world by far, and they're determined. You need energy to run an economy. And they're determined to make their economy the biggest in the world.\par \par \uc2 What does \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  think he's going to do, other than embolden China to be even more belligerent?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, he testified before my committee just the other day, and I asked him the question, why is China considered a developing nation under the United Nations?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Which they are using, Charles, to not come into compliance with the Paris agreement until 2060, 2060.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Chairman McCaul, I'm sorry, I have got to cut you off.\par \par \uc2 We're going to go to the Gilgo Beach presser.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: OK, thank you.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: ... search prior.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That -- I don't have that information.\par \par \uc2 Any other questions? Thank you all for your time.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK, that was pretty short, the Gilgo Beach serial presser, obviously very short.\par \par \uc2 We got there late, but, obviously, there wasn't a lot said, but the body language probably said a whole lot. This is a really ugly case, a cold case, took over 10 years to solve. Kudos to everyone involved.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, as we saw, that New York State Police didn't provide much detail, but I want to go to Nate Foy on what we did learn from the presser that just wrapped up -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Well, Charles, we learned that a New York State Police officer investigator was the first one to identify Rex Heuermann. That happened in February of last year.\par \par \uc2 It was one month after that that the task force was able to identify a car, based off a witness, a pickup truck that he previously owned, which then led to the collection of physical evidence through hair found on the victims' bodies, as well as a piece of pizza that he ate and then threw away, and also the discovery of burner phones, where he's accused of contacting each of these victims before meeting up with them and of murdering them, according to prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 So, the big takeaway is that the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  -- or the New York State investigator was the one who identified him first back in 2022, and then after that was the discovery of the pickup truck.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It is a remarkable story, though. Can't wait to see and hash this out and justice finally. Thank you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 So, after the Supreme Court takedown, the White House defending its new 20 -- $39 billion work-around on student debt relief.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Hillary Vaughn is on Capitol Hill with the very latest on that -- Hillary\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well, the Education Department is trying to cancel $39 billion in student loan relief for about 800,000 people. This relief is going to people who have student loans federally owned directly by the Education Department and are participating in income-driven repayment plans.\par \par \uc2 Biden's plan to let people off the hook from paying their student loan debt is happening while the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is taking on billions of debt and drowning in the interest of paying off that debt.\par \par \uc2 The Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget saying this: "We're on track to have interest be the single largest -- largest line item in the budget by 2051, larger than our two current biggest programs, Social Security and Medicare. With debt projected to shatter records in a few years, we're running off the rails at an alarming rate."\par \par \uc2 But Democrats say the billion-dollar forgiveness will pay dividends in other ways.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JOYCE BEATTY (D-OH): Dollars go right back into the economy when you are able to free up the debt that many of those college graduates have.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is making a huge investment in this country, because he understands the value of standing up for working-class people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And, Charles, the Penn Wharton budget model actually says the true cost of this loan forgiveness is closer to 10 times what the Biden administration is predicting, closer to $475 billion over the next 10 years -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow. Hillary, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 So is the work-around even legal, this after the Supreme Court knocked down Biden's original student loan bailout plan?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now on illegal ramifications is former deputy assistant attorney general Tom Dupree.\par \par \uc2 Tom, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 On the day the Supreme Court decision, President Biden came out and he promised that there will be some alternative measures or ways to get around it. And it's so interesting to me, because they never really talk about it in terms of legality. You just heard the clip, Tom. It's an investment. It's for working class. They use all of the key sound bites. But is it even legal?\par \par \uc2 And are we making a mockery of our three-tiered system of government?\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE, FORMER JUSTICE DEPARTMENT OFFICIAL: Well, those are all great questions, Charles.\par \par \uc2 And, look, you would have hoped the administration would have at least let a little more time go by, rather than literally days after the Supreme Court invalidates their original plan, they're back with Plan B. And I think their attitude is, look, it may or may not be legal, but we're going to try it and let the courts shoot it down, if they will.\par \par \uc2 I think they're going to have difficulty defending this in court, though, Charles, because the Supreme Court's reasoning in striking down the original plan was that Congress hadn't authorized the administration to enact a loan forgiveness program of this magnitude.\par \par \uc2 And what we're seeing with this new program, even though the administration is trying to frame it as different and narrower, we just heard it could cost almost as much, if not more, than the original program would have.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, Tom, I'm reading here from Miguel Cardona from the Department of Education.\par \par \uc2 "For far too long, borrowers fell through the cracks of a broken system that failed to keep accurate track of their progress toward forgiveness."\par \par \uc2 I mean, either you paid the loan or you didn't pay the loan.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Well, that's right. Either you did or you didn't.\par \par \uc2 And, look, what the administration here is doing is basically executing a huge wealth transfer from Americans who didn't attend college or who did attend college but paid off their loans to those who attended college but didn't pay off their loans. That's what's going on here. It's a political motive.\par \par \uc2 No matter how the secretary of education tries to dress it up as correcting old errors, that's what's really going on here, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, and what else is really going on, it appears, obviously, is that they're vote-buying. Say what you will. There's no doubt that the gambit paid off in the midterm elections, and maybe all of this is just fodder for the presidential elections.\par \par \uc2 But it is kind of frustrating, Tom. And I have got about a minute to go, the idea that this administration, time after time after time, did things that they knew were unconstitutional and to curry favor with the voters. At some point, does it weaken the judiciary, particularly the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Well, if nothing else, it consumes their time.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the Supreme Court just finished deciding this major, major case on the Biden administration's first loan forgiveness program. That required the justices to focus on the briefs, hear argument, devote a lot of their time to resolving it. And no sooner they did resolve it with a big stamp of unconstitutionality that the administration is right back up there with a new program.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I have no doubt that this new program is going to bring new legal challenges that I think are very likely to succeed. So it's like we went through this entire process, we got a decision, and now we're back at square one, and the litigation will begin anew.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, the litigation, of course, though, after the election, and they will say, hey, we went to bat for you yet again.\par \par \uc2 Tom, always appreciate your legal mind, my friend. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Thank you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, back to 2024.\par \par \uc2 My next guest is hoping that Lionel Messi will be the key to getting him on the debate stage; 2024 Republican candidate Miami Mayor Francis Juarez is trying to drum up donors by raffling off tickets to see the soccer star's game this Friday.\par \par \uc2 Francis Suarez\uc2  joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Golly, I saw people. They -- this is like the Beatlemania. It was -- people are going nuts over this. I'm glad David Beckham didn't fall off the stage, but I -- talk about the excitement down there in Miami, just, again, something else to be excited about in Miami.\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS SUAREZ\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It is.\par \par \uc2 It's an incredibly exciting time for us. Remember, we won a worldwide competition. You have to understand that. Barcelona, Messi's old team, wanted him back. The Saudis, I think, promised him billions of dollars, and he chose Miami.\par \par \uc2 And the reason why he chose Miami is because we have the best quality of life offering of anywhere on the planet. We have low taxes, low crime, and we have an innovation economy that's creating high-paying jobs. That is the envy of the entire world.\par \par \uc2 And, for me, it's really no wonder that he chose Miami. But it's a great value proposition. It's a great thing, not just for our city, but it's great for the country. This is going to boost ticket sales across the entire MLS.\par \par \uc2 I got to give a lot of credit to Don Garber, who is the commissioner, who helped put this together with Jorge and Jose Mas, who are the owners of the team, who came up with this creative way with a variety of other partners to make this deal work.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, we are going to give away two tickets to the game. And so we ask people to Venmo a dollar at Suarez 2024 to get me on the debate stage so that I can continue to talk about the greatness of my city and as a means of projecting American prosperity for a generation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: How are you looking so far right now? What's the count?\par \par \uc2 SUAREZ: It's looking great.\par \par \uc2 We raised in just 10 days about a million dollars hard, which, if you would -- and it's hard to do this, right, for an entire quarter. It would have been $9 million raised for the quarter. We started the quarter doing very, very well.\par \par \uc2 Political committees that are supporting our endeavor, I think the total was close to $14 million. I mean, that's a tremendous amount of fund- raising for a campaign that's only been in existence for about three weeks. So it's looking great. We feel confident that we're going to be there on the debate stage, so we can continue to tell the story of our success and how it projects to creating economic success in this country, which is what I really think people are concerned about.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Mayor, what are the requirements, the monetary requirements? I think you can have a certain amount of signatures. What are the requirements, so the audience understands?\par \par \uc2 SUAREZ: The requirements are simple.\par \par \uc2 You have to have 40,000 unique voters. I tell people all the time to go on my Web site, FrancisSuarez.com, and make a dollar contribution to meet that $40,000 -- 40,000 contribution requirement. Then you have to have 200 from 20 different states, and you have to poll at, at least 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I have said from the beginning that is a very reasonable threshold. I have abided by that threshold. I will abide by whatever rules a Republican Party establishes, because I do think, look, this time, even the time that we're getting right now, it's valuable.\par \par \uc2 And having an opportunity to talk about where you want the country to go should be given to people who meet those minimum thresholds.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I'm sure must have answered this question a million times. I haven't heard the answer, though.\par \par \uc2 Differentiating yourself from the governor, how much of the credit for Miami goes to you? How much goes to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SUAREZ: Well, I will give you an example.\par \par \uc2 I lowered taxes to the lowest level in history, and we grew 12 percent, which is four times what the state grew, and it's six times what the federal government -- I'm sorry -- what the federal economy grew last year, which grew 12 percent.\par \par \uc2 So I think that's a major issue. I have actually cut government spending in my time as a public official. I'm not sure any other candidate can say that they have cut a government, a public sector government. I cut it by 20 percent when I first got elected in 2009 to the council.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 SUAREZ: And we were on the verge of bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, I think I'm the only candidate that's created an ecosystem that has created high-paying jobs. That's something -- that's Miami's success story, right?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 SUAREZ: Miami is the one that's brought $3 trillion in assets under managed companies. And we have brought a 500 percent increase in our venture capital pipeline.\par \par \uc2 That has happened in Miami.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, and, listen, 10 seconds. It's Miami.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right? It's been a hip spot for a long time. It's not a bad place to visit.\par \par \uc2 Can you replicate it in Idaho? Can you replicate it in Minnesota? Can you replicate it around the country, real quick, yes or no?\par \par \uc2 SUAREZ: Yes. You can keep taxes low...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 SUAREZ: ... you can keep people safe, and you can lean into innovation anywhere in America.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: He chose Miami. You might be able to paraphrase that for your new bumper sticker, my man. I think so. Maybe work on that. I like that. He chose Miami.\par \par \uc2 Thanks a lot, Mayor.\par \par \uc2 Of course, you can catch me on "Making Money" 2:00 p.m. Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 Now here's "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230718ej7h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  Snubs Interview Requests In Hunter Biden Case; Secret Service Ends Probe After Inconclusive Results; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Affirms Support For Ukraine After NATO Summit; Controversial Amendments Threaten Defense Bill Passage; Severe Weather Expected To Hit Flood Ravaged Vermont; Cleanup Efforts From Destructive Floods; Scorching Temperatures In Southwest; Actors' Union \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  Joins Writers' Union Strike; New "Barbie" Movie Criticized For Depicting Map Which Shows China Controlling Disputed South China Sea; Lack Of Information Regarding Chinese Spy Balloon And Other Objects Shot Down Over U.S. And Canada Examined; House Republicans Suggest Secret Service Drug Test Individuals Visiting White House After Cocaine Discovered There\b0\par\par\uc2 Gillian Turner, Jacqui Heinrich, David Spunt, Mark Meredith, Chad  Pergram, Nate Foy, Max Gorden, Bret Baier, Jonathan Hunt, Aishah Hasnie \par \uc2 7,767 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR\uc2 ., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: So, they called another flight attendant who was in Detroit. He came to the airport with her passport. Things -- everything was saved, so they had a great honeymoon and bless their hearts and blessed Delta flight attendant.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Wow, Delta with some good P.R.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Do you think they sleep together?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: For now.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: My wife sent me things in our path forward might be us.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's it for us. SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Greg, everybody knows that \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2  has the best fries, OK? Thanks. Got to go. Bye.\par \par \uc2 Good evening from Washington. I'm Gillian Turner in for Bret Baier tonight.\par \par \uc2 Well, Secret Service has now concluded its investigation into the cocaine that was found inside the West Wing. We're going to tell you what they found.\par \par \uc2 And Hollywood actors are now going to join T.V. and movie writers out on the picket line. What this will mean for your family's summer entertainment options?\par \par \uc2 Plus, our Whatever Happened To segment on the infamous Chinese spy balloon.\par \par \uc2 Well, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has made a significant decision now about the investigation into President Biden's son. House Republicans are pressuring Garland to make multiple people involved in the investigation available for transcribed interviews. So far, Garland is not giving in.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt joins us from the Justice Department tonight. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Gillian. Good evening. We are waiting to hear what that decision is from the Attorney General. I'm told a decision has been made. There are still some discussions going on. And a response is being worked on. Perhaps, we're waiting for that to get over to Capitol Hill before we can actually see a copy of that written response to a request for the Attorney General to make roughly a dozen people connected to the Hunter Biden probe available for transcribed interviews.\par \par \uc2 House Judiciary Chairman Jim Jordan, Oversight Chair James Comer, and Ways and Means Chair Jason Smith wrote to Garland and the head of the \uc2 IRS\uc2  and Secret Service two weeks ago, asking them to make 11 people available, including David Weiss, the Trump appointed U.S. Attorney in Delaware, who's gone on the record in three different letters to Congress to say that he has been in charge of the Hunter Biden probe, despite claims from an \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblower who insists Weiss said the opposite.\par \par \uc2 But it was just three weeks ago, Gillian, when the Attorney General said this about David Weiss.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: I would support Mr. Weiss, explaining or testifying on these matters when he deems it appropriate.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): And Weiss did say in a letter to Congress, he will talk at the appropriate time.\par \par \uc2 Other two big names Weiss's Assistant U.S. Attorney Lesley Wolf and the U.S. Attorney Matthew Graves in Washington D.C.\par \par \uc2 According to the whistleblower, Weiss complained that graves office prevented him from charging \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  in Washington D.C. Weiss said, simply not true, writing in a letter earlier this week, "I was assured that I would be granted this authority if it proved necessary".\par \par \uc2 Hours ago, House Speaker Kevin McCarthy said the Attorney General must cooperate with Congress.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): He doesn't have really the ability to tell us whether Congress could find the answer or not. The American people deserve the answer of the truth.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): And a few weeks ago, the House Speaker floated the word impeachment but today when asked about impeaching the Attorney General perhaps, several Republicans backed off and lowered the temperature.\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA (R-CA): The need to impeach a cabinet officer depends on two things. One, a full due process investigation of the wrongdoing and two, a refusal by the president to dismiss somebody who serves at the pleasure of that president. So, we're a long way from that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): I mentioned the \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblower, there was a second \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblower who wants to remain anonymous. His attorney calls him Mr. X. He will testify before Congress next week. The week after that on July 26th Hunter Biden will enter his plea at federal court in Delaware, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: David, we're also hearing there are some updates in former President Trump's criminal case. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: That's exactly right, Gillian, two updates. The first is Fox News can confirm the former president's son in law \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , testified before a grand jury here in Washington D.C. This is the special counsel Grand Jury looking into efforts to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 We also know in the second investigation involving Special Counsel Jack Smith, this is the documents probe. We are told specifically that DOJ, the team doubled down on keeping the trial on December 11th.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his team wants it after the 2024 presidential election but Special Counsel Jack Smith's team submitted a motion just a few hours ago saying we want this to be in December, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, David Spunt at the DOJ for us this hour. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We're also getting new details now about the investigation into the discovery of cocaine at the White House inside the West Wing. The Secret Service's testing has been inconclusive. They have not been able to figure out where the drug came from or who brought it to 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith has the latest tonight from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): It is deeply frustrating.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): South Carolina Congresswoman Nancy Mace says she's dumbfounded the Secret Service was unable to determine who brought cocaine into the White House.\par \par \uc2 MACE: This is one of the most secure locations in the world with some of the best law enforcement officers in the world and they don't have any answers.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): For the past 11 days, investigators say they reviewed security footage, access logs and physical evidence trying to trace how narcotics got into the West Wing. But they say after both fingerprint and DNA testing came up short. The investigation is now closed.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the Secret Service says, "Without physical evidence, the investigation will not be able to single out a person of interest from the hundreds of individuals who pass through the vestibules where the cocaine was discovered".\par \par \uc2 Some lawmakers say investigators never went far enough.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): My question to them was have they drug tested this list of 500 potential suspects that brought an illegal substance of drug cocaine into the White House? Their answer was no and that they're unwilling to do so.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Democratic Congressman Jamie Raskin says mandatory testing goes too far.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): If there were a small amount of marijuana or cocaine found somewhere in the Capitol complex, we would not want a drug test everybody who works here.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm satisfied that the Secret Service in the White House are on top of it.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Still, some lawmakers believe more can be done to ensure other substances don't get past security.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Somebody needs to look at the procedures protecting the White House from top to bottom.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): House Speaker Kevin McCarthy says he does not buy investigator's explanation that there was a lack of evidence in this case, and Republicans are leaving the door open for a possibility of exploring this issue even further in the weeks and months ahead, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Thank you, Mark.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says Russian leader \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has already lost the war in Ukraine. The president said this during a news conference today in Helsinki, Finland. He doesn't think either that Putin will resort to using nuclear weapons on the battlefield. Good news.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich is in Helsinki tonight. Hi, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Gillian, Finland is in \uc2 NATO\uc2  and its neighbor Sweden is on its way. Although the timeline is a little unclear with Turkey's president still demanding follow through on certain reforms before he refers the vote to Parliament in the fall.\par \par \uc2 But President Biden's visit here was all about conveying to Russia that \uc2 NATO\uc2  is only growing stronger.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We would defend every inch of \uc2 NATO\uc2  territory. And that includes Finland obviously.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Biden capping off a three country trip to strengthen the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance with a stop and its newest member state in Russia's backyard.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Working in lockstep to set us on a stronger, safer and more secure path, not just for Europe, not just for \uc2 NATO\uc2 , but for the world.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Lauding Sweden's progress joining the alliance, Biden made clear Ukraine's path has a roadblock.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No one can join \uc2 NATO\uc2  while the war -- a war is going on, because that guarantees that we're in a war, we're in a third world war.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Contrasting with what national security adviser Jake Sullivan said yesterday, denying Biden believes the war must first end and saying we obviously retained flexibility.\par \par \uc2 Biden offered reassurance over Ukraine's timeline, especially with security pledges from allies proclaiming Russia has already lost.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: My expectation is you'll see that Ukraine makes significant progress on their offensive and that generates a negotiated settlement somewhere along the line.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The last American president to visit Helsinki made headlines when Trump side with the Kremlin over American intelligence when asked if Russia interfered in the 2016 election.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He just said it's not Russia. I will say this, I don't see any reason why it would be.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Biden not wasting an opportunity to show American leadership has changed.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: They've already interfered in American elections.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Also offering the alliance reassurance about America's future role, even as his own chances at a second term are unclear.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I absolutely guarantee there is no question there's overwhelming support from the American people, notwithstanding the fact that some extreme elements of one party, we will stand together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Biden also confirmed he's working on a prisoner swap with Russia to bring home detained Wall Street Journal Reporter \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2 . Biden said he is serious about freeing all Americans being illegally held and that would include Paul Whelan, who's been detained for more than four years, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Jacqui, thanks for the late night reporting from Helsinki.\par \par \uc2 There's also this, Ukrainian officials say their air defenses shot down 20 Iranian made drones fired by Russia today, most of those weapons targeted Kyiv. Wreckage fell on four districts of the Capitol, wounding two people and destroying several homes.\par \par \uc2 And a U.S. official confirms to Fox News that Ukraine has received those cluster munitions announced last week to great controversy. They have not yet been used though in the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 There are concerns tonight the Republican efforts to drive wokeness as they call it out of the military could jeopardize the entire defense budget bill.\par \par \uc2 Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram has details tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The American defense bill is of paramount importance in a world threatened by Russia and China.\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM COLE (R-OK): The NDAA is one of the most critical pieces of legislation the house considers each year.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): But the war on woke could imperil passage of the bill.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM MCGOVERN (D-MA): We're here debating social issues in a defense bill because the far right, the MAGA fringe, the Freedom Caucus, they are obsessed with these culture wars.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): House Speaker Kevin McCarthy took up the mantle of social conservative.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: We're watching the deterring of our -- of our military training. I think it harms us with our adversaries.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): There's a plan to prevent the Pentagon from paying for hormone treatment or gender altering surgeries.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This amendment will likely lead to service members leaving the military.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): There's also a proposal to end diversity programs in the armed services.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The American people I talked to back home don't want a weak military. They don't want to work military. They don't want rainbow propaganda on bases.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Inclusion of such white hot issues could make it hard to pass the bill. Democrats say it's a distraction.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: These amendments are disgusting. It's another example of the Republican Party not interested in governing for all the American people.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The most controversial issue, an amendment to overturn the Pentagon policy which allows service members to travel out of state for abortions. But even some Republicans believe that restriction goes too far.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, this really feels like meddling with women who are traveling out of state, women who are willing take a bullet for their country app. I hope we don't drop the ball.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Now, the House just approved amendments, ending the Helsinki Pentagon's abortion policy and barring the use of the military's health program for transgender treatment.\par \par \uc2 Also, Senator Tommy Tuberville talked today with defense secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  about his blockade on military promotions over abortion. And President Biden says it's, "Irresponsible and jeopardizing U.S. security". Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Chad, there was also we understand the dust up in the committee today between the members and the President's Climate envoy \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 . Can you tell us what happened?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: That's right, well, GOP Congressman Cory Mills of Florida, he sarcastically chided Kerry about how he got to the hearing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CORY MILLS (R-FL): I hope it wasn't too problematic for your operational team and your private jet to get here.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KERRY\uc2 , SPECIAL PRESIDENTIAL CLIMATE ENVOY: I don't own a private jet. I personally have never owned a private jet. And obviously, it's pretty stupid to talk about coming in a private jet from the State Department up here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Now, Kerry said he flies commercial. Kerry says the U.S. will not pay nation's hit by climate disasters, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, stocks are up today, the Dow gained 48. The S&P 500 finished ahead 38, NASDAQ jumped 220.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the historic heat wave is still gripping a lot of the nation tonight, we've got a live report coming out of Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 First, let's take a look at what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 53 at a Pittsburgh as a federal jury rules the man who killed 11 worshippers at a synagogue in 2018 will be eligible for the death penalty. Robert Bowers was found guilty last month of all 63 counts against him. Since then, he's expressed regret he didn't actually kill more people that day.\par \par \uc2 WFXD in Boston as a suspected serial rapist is ordered held on $50,000 this over new charges. 35-year-old Matthew Nilo was accused of sexually assaulting women in Boston 15 years ago. He has since been charged in connection with five additional tax on four different women during that same time period.\par \par \uc2 Plus, this a live look at Miami from WSVN, one of the big stories there tonight. A group of friends in Florida made history when they captured the largest Python ever found in that state, it was a whopper. Record breaking snake measures 19 feet long and it weighs 125 pounds.\par \par \uc2 For some of the folks in that group, this was their first time actually hunting for pythons, they just lucked out.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, officials in Vermont are expecting dangerous thunderstorms that could bring flooding, flash flooding, hail, even the threat of a tornado in the next couple of hours.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Nate Foy is in Montpelier, Vermont tonight. Hi, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Gillian, good evening to you. We're expecting that severe weather here in Vermont state capital in the next hour.\par \par \uc2 State officials also expect severe weather on Sunday. And with more rain coming, a lot of eyes will be on the Wrightsville Dam about three miles north of where we are.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday it was within one foot of overflowing, which would have drastically made flood conditions a lot of -- a lot worse here in Montpelier.\par \par \uc2 A lot of eyes will also be on the Winooski River that you see behind me.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, during the worst of it, water levels rose above the bridge where I'm standing. And that's why you see just a whole bunch of debris left on the bridge tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now, as Vermonters' brace for more rain over the weekend, state officials are saying they're expecting conditions to get bad, but not quite as bad as what we already saw in the past few days.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MORRISON, VERMONT PUBLIC SAFETY COMMISSIONER: This is not expected to be a repeat of Monday and Tuesday, but it will be dangerous in the areas that experience flash flooding.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Now, earlier today, take a look at this. Crews pulled the car out of a river in Barre, Vermont. Thankfully, Gillian, no one was inside.\par \par \uc2 State officials confirmed again today, as of this morning, nobody has been killed in Vermont as a result of this flooding. But there are a lot of homes with severe damage.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at our Fox News drone in the town of Barre as well. This is a live look tonight. Nearly every home in this community has a flooded basement. Many have first floor water damage as well. Some homeowners told us, they don't have flood insurance and we'll be relying on FEMA.\par \par \uc2 One person told us, this is five times worse than Tropical Storm Irene a dozen years ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We never had mud like this and our cellar is turned upside down. It's going to be a long recovery.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Yes, and that recovery, Gillian, will be delayed with more severe weather expected again tonight and through the weekend.\par \par \uc2 There are no active rescue operations happening right now. But state officials are preparing for more and they are ready to respond as the weather gets worse in the coming hours and days. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Nate Foy in Montpelier for us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The National Weather Service is surveying storm damage in the Chicago area in northeast Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Fierce winds there, ripped roofs from buildings down trees, and sent residents fleeing yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Confirmed tornado was spotted on the ground near Chicago's O'Hare International Airport. Weather team suspect there may very well have been others.\par \par \uc2 And this record setting temperatures are hitting out west. Las Vegas is forecast to reach 118 degrees over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Fox weather correspondent Max Gorden is there right now. Max, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 MAX GORDEN, FOX WEATHER FIELD CORRESPONDENT: Gillian, I can tell you, it is sweltering here in Sin City. 107 degrees. And right now, as we're on our way to 109, it really feels like a furnace out here. But this is going to be nothing compared to what we are expecting later on in the weekend.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, we're expecting to go to a height of at least 117, potentially 118 degrees. If we hit 118, that would be a new record high for Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 Now Clark County, Nevada has been opening up cooling shelters. Those will be open through Monday. Safe places for folks to go.\par \par \uc2 Officials advising people to stay inside if they can. But still, lots of action out here on the Las Vegas Strip. Take a listen to what some folks are saying about the heat.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're struggling. We're not used to this type of weather in Ireland, are we?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're sweating loads, yes!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's like the heat is been turned on and someone needs to turn it off.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Very hot. It is too hot.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You get out in it early, and stay out in it, and then you kind of accumulate to it, and you don't -- you don't realize it's getting hot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GORDEN: 84 million Americans under some sort of heat warning from the Mississippi Valley, all the way to the West Coast. This is, of course, part of a larger heatwave that is sweeping across the United States.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, back here in Las Vegas, officials telling people that if they have to be outside right now, make sure to hydrate and wear light loose clothing. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Stay safe out there. Max Gorden, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, T.V. and film actors now have voted to join union writers on the Hollywood picket line. We'll take you live in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, federal officials have approved the first over the counter birth control pill. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has cleared what is called Perrigo's once-a-day Opill to be sold without a prescription.\par \par \uc2 For the first time now, women will not have to get the medication from behind the pharmacy counter.\par \par \uc2 The company is slated to start shipping the pill out early next year.\par \par \uc2 The company that created ChatGPT is now under investigation by the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2 . The agency is looking into whether \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  tools have actually harmed the consumers they were built to help.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  -- FTC has informed the company it's probing deceptive practices that risk user's data security.\par \par \uc2 Also "BREAKING TONIGHT", they say the show must go on, but this time it's not going to, at least for a while.\par \par \uc2 Actors in L.A. have voted to walk off the job after negotiations with industry executives have broken down. Writers have been on strike since May, meaning the few productions that have continued are now likely going to come to a complete standstill. Chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt has those breaking details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 SAG\uc2  is on strike, baby, first time we've struck together since 1960.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hollywood is in crisis. Tonight, every film and TV studio effectively shut down now that the actors' union, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , has joined writers in striking.\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER, SAG-AFTRA PRESIDENT: We had no choice. We are the victims here.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The main issues for the actors are the same as they are for writers. They want a better and guaranteed share of income from the streaming of movies and shows. And they want guarantees over how A.I. might be used in future productions.\par \par \uc2 ADAM CONOVER, COMEDIAN: Most of those actors are making a middle class living. They are just trying to make their mortgage payment that month.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We just want a fair contract and a living wage.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The studios say the demands, particularly on revenue from streaming, are unrealistic given that many streamers are currently struggling to make a profit. Through their umbrella organization, the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 , studio executives accuse the actors' union of intransigence, saying, quote, "Rather than continuing to negotiate, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  has put us on a course that will deepen the financial hardship for thousands who depend on the industry for their livelihoods."\par \par \uc2 The joint strike actions mean the production of movies and shows and the promotion by actors of anything already made has now ended. And depending how long this goes on, it could cost the entertainment industry billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And as production shuts down at studios like the one behind me, it's worth remembering that the last times the actors' and writers' unions went out on strike at the same time was back in 1960. Leading the Screen Actors Guild then, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 . Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It sounds like a great summer to pull out a great new book. Jonathan Hunt, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: For sure.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Producers of the new "Barbie" movie are taking considerable criticism tonight over a map featured in the film. The movie has already been banned in one country over a propaganda map that gives China control over the South China Sea. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie is here to tell us what all the fuss is about.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Wow, this is the real world.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Trouble in Barbie land. As the world awaits the release of the most highly anticipated movie, China hawks on Capitol Hill are bursting Barbie's bubble.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They are willing to turn their eye to make a buck.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Good gracious, stand up for freedom.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The outcry is over this scene of Barbie standing next to a world map which appears to show the nine-dash line representing Beijing's illegitimate claim to almost all of the South China Sea. Vietnam banned the film in response, and the Philippines wants the map blurred before allowing it in theaters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Get that Barbie.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Republicans on Capitol Hill say this is the latest example of Hollywood kowtowing to Beijing to reach its 1.4 billion consumers.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2 , (R-WI) HOUSE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE: We have seen all these instances of Hollywood self-censuring or censuring in order to get access to the Chinese market.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: \uc2 Warner Brothers\uc2  is defending the map, saying it depicts Barbie's make-believe journey from Barbie Land to the real world. China is dismissing the controversy while some on the left think Republicans are making much ado about nothing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I guess Barbie might be a little too woke for them.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Whatever you believe, entertainment report Erich Schwartzel says China's influence on Hollywood isn't fantasy, and that's something movie goers can't escape.\par \par \uc2 ERICH SCHWARTZEL, "RED CARPET" AUTHOR: There is so much economic leverage that China has not just at the box office with these companies but in their broader business plans that I don't see going away any time soon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: -- an update from the Justice Department relating to our top story tonight. FOX News has obtained a letter in which the Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has agreed to negotiate with House Republicans about testimony from officials working the Hunter Biden probe. Tomorrow morning, Justice personnel will reach out to House Judiciary Committee Chairman Jim Jordan to talk about a timeline for U.S. Attorney David Weiss to sit down for a transcribed interview.\par \par \uc2 And coming up next, Bret Baier with our "Whatever Happened To" segment on the infamous Chinese spy balloon.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's take a look Beyond our Borders tonight. Tourists in central Athens huddled under mist machines and zoo animals in Madrid are fed fruit popsicles and chunks of frozen food as southern Europe is now bracing for a historic heat wave. The high pressure system has crossed the Mediterranean from North Africa.\par \par \uc2 Britain's state funded healthcare service is facing its longest ever strike. Tens of thousands of doctors across England have launched into a five-day walkout over pay beginning today.\par \par \uc2 And this, South Korea and the U.S. military are holding joint air drills. They have deployed one B-52H strategic bomber along with South Korea's F- 15K and American F-16s. Those drills were staged just a day after North Korea launched a missile test of its own.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders we are tracking tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It's been over five months now since the Chinese spy balloon was shot down off the coast of South Carolina, and now U.S. officials are beginning to learn more about that balloon's actual capabilities. In tonight's "Whatever Happened To" segment, my colleague Bret Baier takes a look at the ongoing investigation and why so little is still known about three other objects shot down by the U.S. and Canada in the wake of that incident.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Gillian. SPECIAL REPORT viewer Jack James asked, "Whatever happened to the efforts to recover the electronics from that Chinese spy balloon?" There are two known efforts to investigate. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is the main government entity looking into the balloon with the expertise of other government agencies, specifically focusing on that electronic payload. Congress has also pledged a bipartisan probe. It focuses on why the objects were not detected sooner. We'll dig into those investigations tonight and explain where they stand.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think it was more embarrassing than it was intentional.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden recently said China was not fully aware of its spy balloon's whereabouts.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don't think the leadership knew where it was or knew what was in it and knew what was going on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: U.S. officials recently determined, while the balloon had intelligence collection capabilities, it did not transmit information back to Beijing.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: We also took steps to mitigate the potential collection efforts of that balloon.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A senior Pentagon source confirmed to FOX News the device held specialized Chinese sensors. It also contained American made equipment, some of which is available for sale online. The U.S. has export controls in place to prevent China from accessing its more sensitive technology.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER, STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESMAN: We're not going to allow you to take U.S. technology and use it against us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The \uc2 Defense Intelligence Agency\uc2  has been examining the military- related equipment within the device. The DIA told FOX its analysis is still ongoing and pointed us to its report which details China's investment in reconnaissance equipment. The agency often makes certain reports public that explain how adversaries spy on the U.S. But administration officials have pushed back on any public release concerning the spy balloon's technology.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: I wouldn't expect -- and I have said this before -- that we are going to lay all that out for the public. This was an espionage piece of equipment, and we want to get a better handle on it, understand it for our own national security purposes.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I would say it's a matter for the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . It's not for the state department.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We asked the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for an update on its investigation. It pointed us to our February interview with \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  and said it has nothing further to add at this time.\par \par \uc2 What have you learned? Can you talk about that?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: Our technical folks, our lab folks, our counterintelligence folks who specialize in Chinese spying, are working hand in hand with our military and other government partners to analyze all the debris, and that work is ongoing. This balloon thing is just one piece of a much broader threat.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  also said it has no updates on the three smaller objects shot down over Alaska, Lake Huron, and Canada's Yukon territory.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Why do you think we don't we know what we shot down?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): I'm pretty sure we haven't been able to find, because they are rather small, we haven't been able to find everything.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Officials suspected they were likely research or recreational balloons. Search efforts for those ended soon after they began, with U.S. and Canadian authorities saying no debris was located.\par \par \uc2 We shot down, as a country shot down other things that we never really found out what those were. Were they balloons, too?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROGER WICKER, (R-MS): Yes. And, again, what looks embarrassing is that we shot down some toys, it seems.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Arizona Democratic Senator Mark Kelly and North Carolina Republican Senator \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  have legislation that would mandate tracking systems on high altitude weather and research balloons.\par \par \uc2 That Chinese move, how did you see it?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: It was clearly a system that they thought they could fly over the United States and we wouldn't notice. We're going to have to have a way where we can identify it and track it, because there's enough of these things that we can't be scrambling an F-22 to potentially shoot it down on a weekly basis.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amid calls to hold China accountable for its actions, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  met with Chinese officials in Beijing. His initial trip had been canceled over the incident. He told NBC News relations with China have since stabilized so long as its spy balloons stay grounded.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We said what we needed to say and made clear what we needed to make clear in terms of this not happening again. And so as long as it doesn't, that chapter should be closed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some lawmakers want to keep that book open, and have grown frustrated with the lack of information provided by the administration.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of people wondering, for all of the focus that we had on this thing, whatever happened to the fallout from it and what we learned from it? Where are those answers?\par \par \uc2 WICKER: There are a lot of questions left unanswered. What does it say about preparedness? What does it say about how seriously we take threats from China? What does it say about the way we responded and our capability of responding?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): President Biden has said he hopes to be meeting with President Xi again in the next several months. He plans to discuss the differences between the two nations and ways the U.S. and China can get along. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 If there's a story that has left you curious and you want to know whatever happened to it, you can let us know on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , or \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . We may just look into it and then get back to you by using your question right here on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the panel and the White House investigation into cocaine, plus China's growing influence in Hollywood, and the new "Barbie" movie. Plus, later, a very emotional reunion.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2 , (R-GA): It makes no sense to me whatsoever why they would not follow through on one simple task, and that is to drug test a list of 500 people that they have that are potential suspects for this.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 , (D-MD): Some people are saying everybody should be drug tested on the way into the White House. And these are the same people who are opposed to COVID-19 tests.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE, (R-SC): It's just interesting that every time there is something strange going on with the president, Biden or his family, or anything regarding his administration or the White House, no one can ever seem to find an answer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, welcome back. Time to bring in the panel. We have FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, Ben Domenech, editor at large for "The Spectator" and host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" right here on FOX News Radio, and Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico." Thanks, everybody, for being here.\par \par \uc2 Olivia, Nancy Mace is right, this is a very frustrating end to this whole debacle. But it is also a fallacy to think that the Secret Service has the authority to drug test 500 people, many of whom probably don't even work for the federal government.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": Yes. House Republicans weren't blown away, pun intended, by how the Secret Service has handled this. They walked in hoping to get answers. They came out and they were saying that they were very disappointed.\par \par \uc2 The White House does do drug testing. I think the Biden administration has talked about it in terms of legalizing marijuana. They are for that, but they still test some of their staffers. As to whether they are going to be able to find it, they are not getting those answers. But it's just bad optics for the White House, right? There's someone who is talking about being serious, and yet, here you have a bag found in a locker and they can't figure out who did it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It is bad optics. But Juan, is it fair for Republican lawmakers to blame the inconclusive findings on the Biden White House? Is it true that they could have found the culprit if they were just tougher and more security oriented like Republicans are saying?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I don't think so. I think that this is on the Secret Service. If you want to say that they are somehow corrupt or that they are flawed that they said they did a thorough investigation, could not find DNA evidence. They looked at the tapes and were unable to specify any one person, given that there were 500 people just before the Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 But I must say, it's the most observed building in the world, I think, the White House, and with all the sensors and the cameras it's a little odd. The investigation took like 10 days, so people are going to have conspiracy theories. You can imagine that.\par \par \uc2 The one last thing I would say on this is I think a lot of Republicans tie it to \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and the fact that he is a drug addict, or was a drug addict. But the Biden family is not acting like they are guilty. I mean, if they wanted to, they could have said hey, OK, if it's his, he has got to go back to rehab. And this is a guy that people criticize because he's at White House dinners and he's on White House trips. If they wanted to get him out of the picture, this was the chance.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Ben, another big story we are covering this hour is coming out of Hollywood. We learned today that actors, TV and movie actors are going to join writers on this strike. Everybody is predicting this is basically going to bring movie production to a complete standstill now. President Biden, as you know, has long touted himself as the most pro-union president in history. Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I intend to be the most pro-union president leading the most pro-union administration.\par \par \uc2 I'm proud to be the most pro-union president in American history.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I promised you I would be.\par \par \uc2 I meant what I said when I said I'm going to be the pro -- most pro-union president in American history, and I make no apologies for it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Is it a coincidence, do you think, that this massive strike, historic strike, is happening on the president's watch?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I don't think it's a coincidence, but I do think it's a huge mistake, and it's rare that I would come on here and agree with someone like \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , but he said this is one of the worst timings of any kind of strike along these lines -- obviously, the head of \uc2 Disney\uc2  -- that he has seen, one that runs the risk of really setting back the industry in a lot of different ways.\par \par \uc2 Look, American pop culture is still one of our greatest exports. We are dominant in that area in a way that we are not in others. And so I think that that's something that's certainly an item of concern. It's going to set back production. And what did we get out of the last time that we had a significant long writers' strike and then talent strike associated with it? What we got was an enormous, ebullient rise of reality television, which I don't know about the rest of you but I think is one of the worst things that can possibly happen.\par \par \uc2 So we'll see what comes of this, but I do think that it's a very poorly timed matter. And one more thing on this. The idea that this is going to be able to in any way adjust the rise of A.I. as being a creative source for Hollywood is, I think, very dubious. Whatever they come out with at the end of this, I think it's going to be a band-aid, and I think the studios and others are going to turn to A.I. simply because it is a source for, frankly, enormous creativity if you use it right.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So related to that, Olivia, I'm going to go to you since you are wearing pink. I want to talk about the "Barbie" movie. Critics are blasting early release editions of the film because it uses a map -- let's pull it up on the screen so folks at home can see it. It essentially is a piece of Chinese propaganda that makes it look like China has full control over the South China Sea, which is disputed territory. And this is according to "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ," "An international tribunal at the Hague ruled in 2016 China had no legal basis for its claims over that territory." Beijing has yet to concede the ruling. So this is an instance, critics say, of Hollywood caving to the Communist Party's demands.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Yes, Vietnam has blocked it from even playing in their country just because of that one short moment in the film. And House Republicans are coming out and they are pointing out that this is a sign that the U.S. needs to be much more aware how propaganda being played in the U.S. There's about $4.6 billion in China's film industry. And so you have seen a whole shift over probably the last two decades of Hollywood trying to work with and cater.\par \par \uc2 Btu we are entering a new sort of phase of self-censorship. And that's what these Republicans that you are hearing from, from \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  who has been warning that this is appeasing the CCP to other Republicans, that this is what this is. It's broader than the Barbie movie. We saw it with "Top Gun Maverick." We have seen it with other big blockbusters. And so that's what they're warning about here.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Also, caving in this way gives American movie studios access to billions of watchers in China. So there is financial interests at stake as well.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thanks so much for joining us. We have got to leave it there. Learned a lot from talking to you, as always.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, it's a very special day. Two California orphaned black bear cubs siblings were rescued from the San Bernardino national forest. They have now been reunited with one another. They were found originally nearby their mother who was deceased. This after officials tracked their locations. The brothers, once they were reunited, immediately started vocalizing, re-bonding, and connecting with one another. Kind of an amazing story. The wildlife center team says they are now planning to move the bears to a larger outdoor space as they grow, and they anticipate returning those cubs to their natural habitat early in the new year as soon as they can. So we'll bring you that story once they move outside, keep you updated on their progress.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, Bret Baier is going to take a look at how gas prices are factoring into the 2024 presidential race already. Remember, if you can't catch us live, to set your DVR to 6:00 p.m. eastern time, 3:00 p.m. out on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Gillian Turner in Washington. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" begins right now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230713ej7d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Amazing Prime Day Begins; Vermont Flooding; Interview With Sen. \uc2 Deb Fischer\uc2  (R-NE); Senate Receives Briefing on A.I.; Consumers Bracing For Potential UPS Strike; President Biden at NATO Summit; Interview With Presidential Candidate Nikki Haley\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Bill Melugin, Ian Oliver, Aishah Hasnie, Jacqui  Heinrich \par \uc2 9,153 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Not if, but when, \uc2 NATO\uc2  leaders trying to figure out when to let Ukraine in. President Biden making it clear, not now, or at least not yet.\par \par \uc2 That is not sitting well with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , but it is with former U.S. Ambassador to NATO Kay Bailey Hutchison, who told us earlier exclusively on FOX Business that rushing \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership for Ukraine in the middle of a war isn't wise.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAY BAILEY HUTCHISON, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 NATO\uc2 : If we have one of our allies attacked, we will all be in a war.\par \par \uc2 And I think the president is right not to go that far, but I think what Ukraine is getting is very significant and very much more than most of the new allies have gotten, and that is to take away the two-condition requirement and make it one condition.\par \par \uc2 And the reason for that, Neil, is that they are giving so much now in interoperability right now, that it will be a smoother entrance for \uc2 NATO\uc2  accession when the war is over.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That is the view from our former \uc2 NATO\uc2  ambassador now. What about our former United Nations ambassador?\par \par \uc2 We will ask her, because Nikki Haley is here, and only here.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. Very happy to have all of you.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank my colleagues and buddies Edward Lawrence and Charles Payne and Sandra Smith for filling in so ably while I was out. That's why I had to rush back. I don't really rush, per se, but, anyway, I'm back, and glad to be back.\par \par \uc2 Let's go right to the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit right now and Jacqui Heinrich in Vilnius, Lithuania, with more -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  is not hiding his fury that he's going to end this visit without even a conditional invitation to join the alliance. He called that unprecedented and absurd and warned that Russia will use this as a negotiating chip, trying to block them out of a future bid for membership as part of the path to end the war.\par \par \uc2 The alliance issued a joint statement saying that Ukraine's future is with \uc2 NATO\uc2 , but the timing is just not right. I talked about it with President Biden's undersecretary of defense and new \uc2 NATO\uc2  member Finland's foreign minister.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAHL\uc2 , U.S. UNDERSECRETARY OF DEFENSE FOR POLICY: Ukraine has to meet the same standards that every other \uc2 NATO\uc2  member does, in terms of democratic reform, security sector reform, interoperability, which just means our militaries can work together. So there are conditions that every country has to make.\par \par \uc2 So the door is wide open for Ukraine, but there's still some steps left to go.\par \par \uc2 ELINA VALTONEN, MINISTER FOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS OF FINLAND: Based on the Washington treaty, you cannot accept a member who is actively engaged in a war, and as unfortunate it is for Ukraine, because it's not their fault. They are defending their country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: \uc2 NATO\uc2  is expanding to include Sweden, though, after Turkish President Erdogan dropped his block on the country.\par \par \uc2 It came as a surprise after Erdogan indicated that Turkey would first need membership in the E.U. But officials say President Biden wasn't concerned hearing that, because he and Erdogan spoke at length on Air Force One.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Looked like he was moving the goalposts, Erdogan, to the administration.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, U.S. NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: The president continued to focus back in on the core point that, from the point of view of Sweden being able to come into the alliance, the real issue was, had it been able to satisfy what it had committed to a year ago in Madrid?\par \par \uc2 The president made the point to Erdogan, yes, in fact, it had.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Now, what nobody is admitting is whether F-16s in fact had anything to do with that, even though Jake Sullivan did admit that Turkey's vote to let Sweden into the alliance is not on a fixed timeline. There's no set date.\par \par \uc2 And we just might see the sale of F-16s go through before Sweden is fully approved. Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 , who has previously blocked congressional approval of that sale, indicated that he is presently talking to the White House about getting this through, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for that, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich in Lithuania with the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 A person who is seeking that presidency joins us now, Nikki Haley, the 2024 GOP presidential candidate, our ambassador to the United Nations, also the former South Carolina governor.\par \par \uc2 Governor, Ambassador, very good to have you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thanks so much, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you make of President Biden declaring now is not the time to make Ukraine a \uc2 NATO\uc2  member, not essentially in the middle of a war?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I mean, I think he just made Putin's day.\par \par \uc2 Look at what is happening. They are missing the exact strategy that could end this war. Look -- if you just look at \uc2 NATO\uc2  -- and I know because I dealt with them at the United Nations -- they can get weak in the knees and you have to stiffen their spine. I had to do it many times at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The key is, there is no reason Ukraine should not be part of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . \uc2 NATO\uc2  has been a 70-year success story at keeping Russia out. They have been a 70- year success story that there's no been inner conflict of all of those countries. Ukraine has shown their military might, they have shown their power, and they have shown that they would be a fantastic asset to the alliance.\par \par \uc2 But, more than that, Neil, look, Russia has never invaded a country that's part of Ukraine -- a country that's part of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . They invaded Georgia. They invaded Moldova. They invaded Ukraine. Those were not countries that were part of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . This is when the alliance should have gotten together and said, yes, we're going to bring Ukraine in, because the way we end this war is for Russia to get out.\par \par \uc2 That's it. Russia needs to get out, and the war comes to an end. If they would have seen that the alliance was adding Ukraine in, it would have been the biggest point of contention for Putin to realize the only way to deal with this is to get out, because he doesn't want to deal with all of them. He hasn't done it in the past. And there's a reason for that.\par \par \uc2 We're missing a strategic point. That doesn't mean any cash. It doesn't mean we put troops on the ground. That doesn't obligate us to anything we're not already doing now. And, already, Ukraine is fighting a war, protecting the rest of the alliance anyway. So I think it's just -- it's a mistake.\par \par \uc2 I think they're weak in the knees, and I think Biden joined them in that weakness, and that's a shame. He should be rallying them to the cause to make sure that we get Russia out, so that we can end this war.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Not everyone agrees with that sentiment, including Kay Bailey Hutchison, our former U.S. ambassador to \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But when I had her on the -- on FOX Business, Governor, earlier, which, if you don't get, you should demand. But I'm sure you get it, and it's worked out well.\par \par \uc2 But one of the issues that we had raised was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's promise that, if he became president again, he could end the Ukraine war in a day. Now, the former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  said that only came at a great risk to sort of play to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The same issue was addressed with Ambassador Hutchison. I want you to react to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUTCHISON: I think \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is taking the right position, and that is we definitely need to help Ukraine win this war, but not with our troops on the ground. And I think that is the essential difference.\par \par \uc2 And I think it is important that we help Ukraine and then welcome them into \uc2 NATO\uc2  after that war is won, and when we have helped them win that war.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you know, Ambassador, what the former president meant by ending this in a day, what he was offering?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHISON: I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You -- what do you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  meant by ending it in a day?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I think it's ridiculous. He knows that he can't end the war in a day. Everybody else knows he can't end the war in the day.\par \par \uc2 He says these crazy things. It's not true. We have got a realistic situation our hands. The way we end the war is to get Putin out. It's not to appease Putin. The way we end the war is to be strong and work on our alliance.\par \par \uc2 When I worked on dealing with Iran at the United Nations, or when we focused on dealing with Russia with Syria, when we had to negotiate with the Security Council on getting the largest sanctions against the country in a generation with North Korea, we didn't do it from a point of weakness. We did it with a point of strength.\par \par \uc2 And it wasn't just strength from the U.S. I had to strengthen the spines of the Europeans, of our friends in \uc2 NATO\uc2  to let them know our goal is to prevent war. The only way you prevent war is with strength. And you have to take these dictators on. You don't run from them. And that's what Biden has done every step of the way.\par \par \uc2 He's run from Putin. That's what's dragged out this war for so long. Let's end this war, get Putin out. We can't sit there and run scared of every dictator, or else they're going to keep on running all over us.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Ambassador, where do you draw the line, then?\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , in his latest comments on TRUTH Social, his site, said that: "\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should not be dragging us further toward World War III by sending cluster munitions to Ukraine. He should be trying to end the war and stop the horrific death and destruction being caused by an incompetent administration."\par \par \uc2 What did you think of that?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Putin has hit rock bottom. We know because he's getting drones from Iran, he's getting missiles from North Korea.\par \par \uc2 They have lost 50,000 soldiers in the process. They have raised the draft age to 65. And he created this monster with the \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2 , which was a group that fought their dirty work for them in Ukraine, in Syria, in Africa. And that monster turned on him.\par \par \uc2 And when you take a strong guy like that, who thinks he's invincible, and you suddenly make him feel vulnerable, that's not when you take your foot off the gas. That's when you put your foot on the gas and make sure we end this war. This is the opportunity to finish it.\par \par \uc2 And you have the Biden administration running scared. \uc2 NATO\uc2  is doing what \uc2 NATO\uc2  does, which is get scared of their own shadow. You have to draw strength and show Russia, we're not giving up, we're not giving in.\par \par \uc2 We don't have to give money. We don't have to put troops on the ground. It changes nothing for us to have Ukraine be able to become a part of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . And, more than that, what I will tell you, Neil, is, when this is all said and done, Ukraine is going to be one of our best allies. They're going to have one of the strongest fighting forces, and they're going to be incredibly good for us, just similar to the way Israel is.\par \par \uc2 We have to see foreign policy and national security 10, 20, 30 years out, not tomorrow, not next month, not this next year. And that's been the problem with what's happened with these administrations, one after the other. And it's why Putin and China continue to wait out a president, because you don't have anyone leading it for the long haul.\par \par \uc2 We have got to do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, you have made it very clear that you think some of the comments that the former president has made, especially now on the Ukraine, were -- were reckless. I don't want to put words in your mouth.\par \par \uc2 But it does echo what I'm hearing from a lot of former Trump Cabinet members, who've said that his behavior is a little distracting. Attorney General Bill Barr had said that the Mar-a-Lago indictment is very, very damaging. \uc2 John Bolton\uc2 , his former national security adviser, with whom you worked, said that the former president could be a national security threat if he were to get a second term.\par \par \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  described him as dangerous, General \uc2 James Mattis\uc2 , his former defense secretary, that the president demonstrates a constant threat to our values. \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , on and on, called him divisive, immature, and cavalier, abusing his powers.\par \par \uc2 These are all people who worked with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Do you share their reservations about him getting in again?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Look, I worked for him, and we had a great working relationship. But the reason we had a great working relationship is, I told him the truth, when he was doing something good or when he was doing something wrong.\par \par \uc2 And he knows that about me. And so I think that it's important you always tell someone the truth. I have always told him the truth. I will continue to do that. I don't necessarily want to get involved in their grievances. What I want is to make sure that he knows, if he wants to be president again, get on the debate stage with us.\par \par \uc2 Let's show the American people the differences between all of us. I welcome him on that stage. I welcome the debate. This is about the fact that we have lost the last seven out of eight popular votes for president. We have to start doing better. We need to win a majority of Americans.\par \par \uc2 And the only way you do that is, you have a new generational leader that's not focused on the drama of the past, the negativity of the past, or the chaos, someone who's focused on the fact that China is our number one national security threat.\par \par \uc2 They are planning war with America. No one else is talking about that. And this is an issue of national security. So, I don't care about grievances. I care about making sure we protect American families. That's not happening right now. I'm determined to do it.\par \par \uc2 I have been a two-term governor who took a double-digit unemployment state with an executive and turned it into an economic powerhouse. I didn't negotiate with one country. I dealt with 193 at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . We have got to get our ship straight. We have got to start to make sure we right this ship, and we have got to make sure we do it now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think you have to break through in Iowa?\par \par \uc2 Because, right now, even though you're polling third in a lot of these polls -- it varies by state, I grant you -- no trouble raising money, $7.3 million in the latest quarter, $16 million raised since you launched your campaign, actually, almost $20 million, I guess, by latest count, so no difficulty raising money, but raising awareness of your campaign still seems to be a problem.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried that it's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who's running away with this?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: No, I'm not worried. I'm being smart.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have raised, between our campaign and supporting organizations, $34 million. A lot of others are spending millions of dollars to hit each other. This is not the time to be spending money. We're not spending any. That's why you're not seeing big movement in the polls. We know you do it when it counts.\par \par \uc2 I'm an accountant. I'm not a lawyer. It's all about benchmarks. It's all about goals. It's all about not peaking early. And at the end of the day, we just finished the North Country in New Hampshire. We have done 39 events. The New Hampshire paper said, Nikki Haley's campaigning the New Hampshire way. We are headed back to Iowa soon.\par \par \uc2 We continue to do so many town halls, answering every question, shaking every hand. I'm the last person to leave. We are not taking shortcuts. We're not doing rallies where you come in and you leave. We're going there, and we're making sure we're doing this the right way. We have got a good ground game, and we're going to spend the money when we need to spend the money, when it counts.\par \par \uc2 So I am very comfortable where we are. We had a great announcement with thousands of people in Charleston. We have been well-received in Iowa and New Hampshire. We have had a great fund-raising haul. We're being very fiscally responsible.\par \par \uc2 I hope your viewers will go to NikkiHaley.com and join us. But we're going to finish this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: And we're going to finish it strong, and we look forward to the debates that start in August, and we have made it to the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 You were mentioning Iowa. As you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has attacked \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , the governor, for not showing more support for him. He said that: "I opened up the governor position for \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , and, when she fell behind, I endorsed her big, did big rallies, and she won."\par \par \uc2 Of course, he's writing this on his TRUTH Social site, Governor.\par \par \uc2 "Now she wants to remain neutral. I don't invite her to events. DeSanctis," his word for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , "down 45 points."\par \par \uc2 That's how he responds generally to people who bolt or don't do what he likes. He has already said similar things about you when you decided to run, saying that: "Nikki has to follow her heart, not necessarily her honor."\par \par \uc2 How does that make you feel when you hear that kind of stuff and this latest diatribe against the Iowa governor?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: It's what he does. I mean, when he feels attacked or he feels threatened, he's incredibly thin-skinned, and he starts to lash out.\par \par \uc2 That's why I have always told him -- he can tell you, I have always said: "You're your own worst enemy. Don't do this."\par \par \uc2 But he has done that. \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  is literally...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What would he tell you? What would he tell you when you told him that?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: We had a great working relationship, because he knew I told him the truth. I always told him the truth, because I wanted him and America to do well.\par \par \uc2 And I have always been a truth-teller. I'm going to continue to tell hard truths to people, whether they want to hear it or not, whether they're Republicans or Democrats. \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  is the best governor in the country. You can look at her records. You can see it for itself. The idea that he attacked her because he thinks that she favors one candidate or not is silly.\par \par \uc2 And, at the end of the day, Kim is smarter than that. She's going to go and she's going to lead her state. She's going to go and do whatever she feels is right for Iowa and the country. And I respect that, and I trust her.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You had raised early on this idea that maybe we need, not a competency test, but a test for older candidates. I don't know where you drew that line, but do you still think it's a good idea?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Absolutely. We need term limits in Congress. We need term limits in every one of our agencies, where no one holds a position longer than five years, that we rotate them out.\par \par \uc2 And we need to make sure that we have mental competency tests for anyone over 75. And I don't care if it's for anyone over 50. But, Neil, these are people who are making decisions on our national security. These are people who are making decisions on the future of our economy, for our kids. We need to know they're at the top of their game.\par \par \uc2 We can't say that right now. And, by the way, look at...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But there are a lot of 55-year-olds, right? Governor, there are a lot of 55-year-olds who've lost their fastball. I lost mine years ago, by the way.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I have -- I have said...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But my point is, why should we draw that line at 75?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I just said, I'm fine if we do it for anyone 50 and up.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, we need to do it so that we, that the American people have confidence that government is working for them. That's the problem. The American people now feel like they work for government. We have got to turn this around. We have got to have some accountability.\par \par \uc2 What has Congress done for you lately? They have done literally nothing, whether it's our debt, whether it's our border, whether it's our education system, whether it's crime, whether it's national security. They haven't done anything. And they were part of the spending problem that got us into this. That's not the answer of where we need to go.\par \par \uc2 We need to start holding them accountable and making them not just get on TV and give talking points, but making them show results to the American people, because the American people are tired. They are dealing with the debt. They're dealing with the inflation that's happening. They're sick of not having transparency in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 They're tired of having crime on the streets. They don't want a lawless border that's turned every state into a border state. And they don't want to see China flying a spy balloon over our country or putting a spy base 100 miles off of our coast. This is an issue. We have got to start righting the ship, focusing, getting serious, because our kids deserve better than what they're seeing right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Governor, you have been very forthright about this and getting more in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's face.\par \par \uc2 I have seen similar kind of musings out of \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , who never said any of this stuff when he worked for \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  -- when he worked for the president. But now, of course, he said January 6 changed his mind.\par \par \uc2 What changed yours to, not go on the attack -- I mean, you're a little less direct than that -- but to question \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  more, knowing that you risk the wrath of his voters and supporters, who are pretty much in lockstep with him and make up a large part of the Republican base right now?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Neil, his voters and supporters and the American people want the truth.\par \par \uc2 I just speak hard truths. You have got some people who are mad that I don't love Trump 90 -- you know, 100 percent of the time. I don't love my husband 100 percent of the time. You have got some people who are upset that I don't disagree with him 100 percent of the time. I think he was the right president at the right time.\par \par \uc2 I call it like I see it. And so, look, I think he was the right president at the right time. I just think we need to move forward. Our country can't keep dealing with this chaos and this negativity. We have got to go forward.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Does that mean -- I'm sorry, Governor.\par \par \uc2 Does that mean he's the wrong president this time and, if Republicans nominate him as your leader and as your presidential candidate, you would not support him?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I have said, and I say it to every Republican candidate who's going to get on that debate stage, I will -- I will support the Republican that comes out of that.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, I want it to be me, but I'm going to support the Republican, because I don't believe that America can afford President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And we all need to remember that. The guys on the stage need to understand this is not about being personal. It's not about being petty. They have got to get over it.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, we need to make sure that President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is never president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you don't agree with the John Boltons of the world, who think that the president should withdraw from the race, much like Asa Hutchinson, because of all these issues, legal and otherwise?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I think he has -- he has every right to run, just like everyone else.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: And I trust the American people to make the right decision.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Ambassador, very good talking to you. Thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Thanks so much. Go to NikkiHaley.com and join us.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I thought you would squeeze that in there.\par \par \uc2 Always good seeing you, Ambassador. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, before we take a quick break here, a look at the corner of Wall and Broad, where the Dow was sprinting ahead better than 317 points. A lot of it is built on optimism about a key inflation report that is due out tomorrow that might set the tone and the tenor for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and whether it's inclined to keep hiking interest rates maybe at the end of this month.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. You are watching "Your World."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Who are we?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 !\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: What do we want?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Contract!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that is actually a lot of \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  players getting ready for what could be a full and nationwide UPS strike to take effect at the end of this month if they don't get what they want.\par \par \uc2 Sean O'Brien is here, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  general president, to delineate exactly what they want.\par \par \uc2 Sean, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 Maybe you can spell -- where does this stand now, and are you any closer than you were last week?\par \par \uc2 SEAN O'BRIEN, GENERAL PRESIDENT, TEAMSTERS: Thank you, sir, for allowing me to be on the show.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: We're not any closer. Talks broke down. UPS walked out July 5 at 4:15 a.m. The contract expires July 31.\par \par \uc2 We had negotiated hard, agreed on a lot of terms and conditions that were beneficial to our members, and we're 95 percent through the process, and all that's left now is economics. And we were focused on the full-time and part time wages. And UPS said they had no more to give, to these part- timers especially.\par \par \uc2 And it's embarrassing for a company this size and this successful not to want to reward the people that make them truly a success.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, they say they are rewarding the people.\par \par \uc2 We tried to reach a company spokesman. The best we could do, sir, was get a statement from them, saying that: "The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have stopped negotiating, despite historic proposals that build on our industry-leading pay. We're proud of what we have put forward in these negotiations, which deliver wins for our people. The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  should return to the table to finalize this deal."\par \par \uc2 So they're essentially saying to you, Sean, you're the problem, not them.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Yes, it's always easy to blame someone else. We have been pretty transparent through this whole process.\par \par \uc2 And UPS hasn't once got in front of a camera. They keep sending out these press releases, but their story is compelling, but highly inaccurate. They know what our members need. They know what we want. We have been stating our intentions for a year now, in hopes of trying to reach a deal. So UPS can say what they want.\par \par \uc2 But one thing's sure. Our message has been consistent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, for the time being, nothing is affected, right? There's no walkout just yet. All of this occurs on the first day of the two day \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Prime Day sale, for which -- meant a lot of your guys deliver a lot of those goods.\par \par \uc2 What do you tell folks at home who don't understand the details of what you're arguing over that they might not, down the road, get their packages, or worse?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, look, I mean, that's up to UPS. And if UPS doesn't reward our members, then they'd be causing the strike and the work stoppage.\par \par \uc2 But the general public has to understand that, through the pandemic, the toughest times, our members, the UPS men and women who deliver their packages and the unsung heroes, the part-timers that loaded those packages, were never rewarded. They were never compensated correctly. They went out there and risked their lives.\par \par \uc2 And we hear about all these post-pandemic shortfalls in society. The one positive thing, with e-commerce, especially UPS, they have seen 100 percent growth. They have made $100 billion. Their stockholders have been rewarded by 50 percent. There's been lucrative stock options given to CEOs, and our members haven't shared in any of those wins.\par \par \uc2 So it's time now, and it's up to UPS whether they want to do the right thing by their employees or not.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you worried that conditions over which you don't have control, and, for that matter, neither does UPS, if the consumer slows down?\par \par \uc2 We're seeing early signs, not necessarily ominous ones, that consumers are retrenching a bit, and that that could be a case of very bad timing for you folks.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Look, it's one thing that's driving e-commerce is everybody wants instant gratification. They want their packages yesterday, and I'm one of them.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: But the reality is, the one thing UPS is good at, UPS is always good at running their business and capturing volume.\par \par \uc2 Any shortfall in volume is not going to be long term. And once this issue is resolved, I'm certain that it will be business as usual.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So you're not worried about -- some people go so far as to say a recession is imminent. There doesn't seem in a lot of the data to indicate that.\par \par \uc2 But it would be a kick if, just as you're trying to nail down an agreement, we get more economic news that maybe the company is sensing will reinforce their notion that they can't be as generous, because the economy won't be as accommodating.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, I think the one thing to point out is that UPS has moved their earnings call twice already, and they have moved it to August 8, the midterm earnings.\par \par \uc2 So I don't know why they're not releasing their earnings, because maybe they're trying to hopefully get a deal and not release the profits that they have made so far. So that's something to take into consideration.\par \par \uc2 But, like anything else, if there's a recession, it always hurts working people. But, at the end of the day, that recession ends at some point in time, and we build back bigger, faster, and stronger.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 All right, Sean O'Brien, thank you, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  general president.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, we did put out these calls to UPS. They're always welcome to come on and give their side of the story. That statement was the best we could do for the time being.\par \par \uc2 All right, when we come back: Artificial intelligence is very big on Capitol Hill today. But how do you police something you really can't get a handle on?\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie following those developments on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Neil. That's right.\par \par \uc2 So senators are getting their first classified briefing on how adversaries like China might be using A.I. or might one day use A.I. against us. And wait until you hear some of these senators' worst-case scenarios. Scary stuff -- next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you're looking at a live shot of Montpelier, Vermont, a beautiful place, a very wet and nasty place. They have gotten more rain in the last two days than they have over the last couple of months.\par \par \uc2 And it ain't over -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we're from the government and we're here to help, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 's famous warning about any time Washington intervenes in something, but, this time, a bipartisan group of senators saying that they want to get a handle on this whole artificial intelligence thing and whether they have to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 They clearly are itching to do something about it, but what?\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill with more on that.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 What and when, that's -- those are the two biggest questions, especially when it comes to anything that needs to happen on Capitol Hill. When might they do something about it? So, today, all senators are getting their very first classified briefing on artificial intelligence. It's a big deal.\par \par \uc2 And they didn't really have a lot of questions walking in. We tried to ask them what they were interested in finding out, and that's because they really don't know a whole lot about A.I. yet. They're still learning about it. And they have definitely thought about their worst-case scenarios, though, especially when it comes to A.I. being used by adversaries against us.\par \par \uc2 Now, today, senators are hearing from the director of national intelligence, \uc2 Avril Haines\uc2 , and Deputy Secretary of Defense \uc2 Kathleen Hicks\uc2 . Now, back in May, Neil, Haines told senators that her department was working with industry experts to understand A.I.'s possible uses.\par \par \uc2 But then she also admitted they really didn't have their hands around the potential impact or the threat. Now, Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , on his way into this briefing, warned that Congress really has no control over what our adversaries, like China, are doing with A.I.\par \par \uc2 But he's most worried about losing the human element from wartime decisions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): Imagine a Cuban Missile Crisis where A.I. made a decision about how to respond, instead of the human element. It gives smaller nations that historically may not have had the wherewithal to develop certain capabilities that punch above their weight in ways that could threaten world peace in the case of, like, a rogue state like North Korea.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has introduced a framework for federal regulations that he says would maximize A.I.'s benefits while also protecting the American people.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Today's classified briefing is crucial, because, of all the issues A.I. will impact, national security may well be the most consequential. In the wrong hands, the danger of A.I. could be extreme.\par \par \uc2 But A.I. could also become one of the greatest tools we have for keeping America safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Now, Neil, we have heard some senators say that we could see regulation start to trickle out, maybe even in the defense bill coming up this month.\par \par \uc2 But then Leader Schumer has also hinted that it could be months before we see regulation -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Aishah, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Senator \uc2 Deb Fischer\uc2 , the Nebraska Republican kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of this, Senator, and the back-and-forth as to artificial intelligence? Some people are thinking there's got to be a pause, including some of the biggest boosters of it. China isn't pausing. So what do we do?\par \par \uc2 SEN. DEB FISCHER (R-NE): Well, Neil, what we were looking at today and listening to from our defense and intelligence people were really the threats that we face to our national security.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have to understand what our adversaries, what kind of capabilities they're exploring that are a threat to our homeland. And then we also have to be able to figure out, how are we going to defend against those?\par \par \uc2 So, to get the basic knowledge there to be able to listen, learn from the experts, from our defense, as I said, and our intelligence communities, I think that gives us a really good basis to be able to move forward on this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There are a lot of people who look at maybe Washington's good intention to do something, your party, the Democrats, but you don't know enough about it or the dimensions of it, so you're going to make things worse.\par \par \uc2 How do you respond?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: You know, that's a concern.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I can tell you that our tech people, the tech sector here in the United States, they're very excited about A.I. But then we need to know what kind of ramifications are out there with any kind of government interference with it. As I said, we need to learn more. We have to be able to figure out what A.I. is, first of all.\par \par \uc2 Today, we heard it's -- you know, you count the stuff in the past so you can predict the future. That's what A.I. is. A.I. is being able to look at your phone. You have it in your phone already with the facial recognition. But we're seeing huge advancements in A.I. now, where we look at generative A.I., and not just ChatGPT, but also to be able to be concerned about biological uses of A.I.\par \par \uc2 I heard \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  just before you came to me, his comments about losing the human interface with A.I. That's extremely important that we maintain the human connection, the human interface, because A.I. is only as good, is only as correct as the information that's out there.\par \par \uc2 It's that collecting the stuff in the past.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Well, that stuff better be right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator, seeing as you sit on the Armed Services Committee, very influential in foreign policy matters, I wonder what you make of the president's decision right now to hold off on pushing \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership for Ukraine, the gist of it, because we're in the middle of a war, and now is not the time to be doing that?\par \par \uc2 How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: We are in the middle of a war.\par \par \uc2 We're providing munitions, which is a whole 'nother issue that I have a deep interest in and deep concerns about. But we're helping Ukraine with their war. Now is not the time to offer them membership in \uc2 NATO\uc2 . When we see a conclusion to these hostilities, when Russia withdraws from the sovereign land of Ukraine, that's the time then to talk about admission into \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What about some of these more dangerous weapons, these cluster munitions and the type that the president could be considering? Others say that actually goes too far.\par \par \uc2 What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: The Russians have been using the cluster bombs since the beginning of this conflict.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainians also have used cluster bombs as well. That's one thing that we can send them. But, Neil, we have to look deeper into our own production capacity of munitions. We have been sending over a lot of things to help the Ukrainians help themselves in conducting their defense against the Russians. We don't want to lose our capacity to defend our country.\par \par \uc2 We want to make sure we have production lines up and running, that we are able to have a budget that recognizes the needs that we have for our own country, for our own security. We had a hearing this morning with General Brown, who is nominated for chair of the Joint Chiefs.\par \par \uc2 I brought up those concerns to him, and that's something I'm going to continue to focus on. One thing we have seen with Ukraine is, you can really move through a lot of munitions in a short period of time. And we don't want to be caught flat-footed here. We need to make sure our production lines are up and running.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Senator Fischer, great seeing you. Thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime, nasty weather, particularly in Vermont, getting deluged.\par \par \uc2 I mentioned a little bit earlier more rain in the last couple of days than the state has seen in the last couple of months, any two-month period, actually.\par \par \uc2 The fallout from all of that with Ian Oliver, who has more -- Ian.\par \par \uc2 IAN OLIVER, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: Neil, this has been a brutal last 48 hours across the Green Mountain State.\par \par \uc2 We had intense flash flooding over the last couple of days, followed by historic river flooding. Things are getting better here in Montpelier. We will show you the scene and talk about a forecast that features more heavy rain coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, how long does it take a half-a-foot of rain to start evaporating? They're trying to find out right now in Montpelier, Vermont.\par \par \uc2 That's where you will find our Ian Oliver, FOX Weather meteorologist extraordinaire.\par \par \uc2 Ian, how do things look there?\par \par \uc2 OLIVER: Neil, if you can believe it, a whole lot better than they did just earlier this morning, as you talked about, this five to eight inches-plus of rain in Vermont over the last 48 hours.\par \par \uc2 We saw intense and destructive flash flooding. And today, and really over the last 24 hours a transition, as all that water rushed into the valleys, into the creeks, the streams, and into the larger rivers across the Green Mountain State, including right here in the beautiful capital of Montpelier.\par \par \uc2 The Winooski River, about 100 yards ahead of me here, that jumped its banks. The storm of record previously had been Tropical Storm Irene. That was back in 2011. I was actually living and working here in Vermont, and I saw that. This, at least in Montpelier, was about two feet worse. The floodwaters earlier today when we arrived, it was about 7:30 a.m. or so, that's the waterline on this \uc2 Ford\uc2  Focus.\par \par \uc2 So, the dashboard, the steering wheel, the whole interior covered with dust and mud, that is likely a total loss. This did edge out Tropical Storm Irene. There's only one storm of record, the Great Vermont Flood of 1927, in the history books that exceeded this particular event. So you could have lived in the Green Mountain State your entire life and never seen something like what we have witnessed over the last two days -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Ian, good luck drying out there.\par \par \uc2 Ian Oliver in Montpelier, Vermont.\par \par \uc2 We're seeing strange and crazy weather across the country, particularly along the West Coast right now, where it's about landslides and missing homes.\par \par \uc2 The latest there from Bill Melugin in Rolling Hills Estates, California -- Edward (sic).\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 At last check, 12 homes here have been red-tagged, 10 have been destroyed, and those 10 are still moving as I speak to you right now.\par \par \uc2 Let me step out and give you a look at the scene here. This is Rolling Hills Estates here in Los Angeles County. This all began on Saturday when these homes begin to buckle in an apparent landslide. Those homes still moving now. The ground continues to buckle. The earth continues to give way. Some roofs are now on the same eye level as the houses' driveway.\par \par \uc2 Sixteen more homes have now been warned they too could have to evacuate. They are also under threat. And for the homeowners who already had all this devastation, it is obviously an incredibly tough pill to swallow. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WEBER YEN, CALIFORNIA HOMEOWNER: It's a lifetime of memories.\par \par \uc2 We all traveled around the world. We raised children, and there's a lot of childhood memories and families and all that stuff. That's going to be all gone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: As for what caused this, that remains unclear right now, though there has been some speculation about that very wet winter Los Angeles had, a lot of rain.\par \par \uc2 Some people throwing ideas out that possibly there was some sort of a ground fissure. The county says that the homeowners association is going to have to hire a geologist or a soil expert to come in here and officially find out what happened. There are obviously going to be questions about insurance. Will this be covered?\par \par \uc2 A lot of questions for those homeowners, but the L.A. County tax assessor says these homeowners will be eligible for property tax relief, though that's likely not the kind of relief they're going to be looking for here - - Neil, we will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bill Melugin.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for that, Bill.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Amazon Prime, you buying? Well, let's just say the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is watching.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so point, click, buy?\par \par \uc2 Well, that's in the eye of the beholder right now, as \uc2 Amazon\uc2  kicks off its two-day national Prime Day sell, where they said people will take advantage of some big discounts on some big and not-so-big items here.\par \par \uc2 But the average Prime Day spending is not expected to be as strong as it was last year, about $250, versus close to $400 a year ago. So what's going on?\par \par \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 , the former \uc2 chairman of the Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  under President Trump, kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 When you were in that position, was this among the type of things you followed, Kevin? I mean, this is a good barometer.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT\uc2 , FORMER CHAIRMAN, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Retail, consumer interest buying, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 It's something that we should pay close attention to, because a lot of times you see the news about these things ahead of the economic news. One of the other things in the White House that we had -- I think \uc2 Gary Cohn\uc2  was the one who arranged it -- was we had access to anonymized and aggregated real-time data from the credit card companies.\par \par \uc2 And so we knew how much people bought yesterday. If you wonder sometimes when I was on your show and I seemed to be really good at the retail sales number, right, we actually had some good real-time stuff.\par \par \uc2 And I think that that's one of the cool things now about the way the Internet is aggregating information, is that there's a lot more real-time information for economists to chew on. And so we're not quite always driving with our eyes in the rearview mirror like we used to be.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It is fascinating what you can glean from information in real time. And you guys were the first to pounce on credit card activity and that sort of activity.\par \par \uc2 And we're told that a lot of Americans not all, but a good many, are tapped out, and that is what is behind the slowdown or the slowing in consumer spending. Now, you wouldn't see it everywhere because flights are still packed, even if customers and passengers are still angry about how packed and delayed they are. But they're still packed.\par \par \uc2 There are hotels and resorts and demand is very, very high. But at stores, we're beginning to see a pattern where it's not quite as big as it was. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Yes, that's right.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that I have done over the last year is very carefully calculate the excess COVID savings, because everybody got all these checks in the mail.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: And then you can look and see, like, how much did they spend, how much did they save? And the excess COVID savings have actually, by my estimate, run to zero this month.\par \par \uc2 And so credit card debt is really high, interest rates are high, COVID savings is gone. And so I expect, as you said at the outset, that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is going to see a worse \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Prime Day than they had last year. And retailers across the board are going to start to see that too, because consumers are kind of running out of money.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know whether that lasts long. Of course, you know the Biden administration has been claiming the wind at their back are the steady improvement of jobs, 209,000 in the latest month, and they have added millions. And they see that trend continuing, maybe not at quite the pace it has been.\par \par \uc2 But what do you think of that? Because they say, what recession? No recession. Nothing like it in sight. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Well, if you look at the real wage data, right, which is the real measure of how people are doing, so are their wages going up at the same rate as prices, and the real wage data have shown very, very steady declines.\par \par \uc2 Average hourly earnings have declined, I think it's 25 months in a row now. And so what that means is that you have less money to buy stuff, and it didn't show up in a decline in real consumption because people had all that savings left over from the COVID shutdown. But now that savings is gone. And so, unless real wages start climbing, then consumption is going to have to follow south as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime, everyone's focused on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , one of the reasons why the Dow was sprinting ahead today.\par \par \uc2 And you have always reminded me over the many years I have been fortunate to know you that don't hone in on a single day in the markets. You're quite right.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But much of the optimism has been built on the notion that maybe we get a better-than-expected inflationary read tomorrow on the retail front.\par \par \uc2 That could give the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  maybe a pause, after a pause, at the meeting at the end of this month. Do you think they should pause, give it a little bit more time, or not?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: No, I think that core inflation is pretty much where it is today, where it was last fall, and so you have not really seen much response at all to all the tightening.\par \par \uc2 And, in part, I think it's because of the stronger-than-expected labor market that you're discussing. So there are a lot of inflationary pressures in the economy right now. I expect that the core CPI number is going to be 0.3 or 0.4 tomorrow, which is about what we have been seeing, means that the annual rate is about 5, about what we have been seeing.\par \par \uc2 It's possible that the year-over-year number is going to look better because we're losing some really big months from a year ago. And it's possible the top line is going to be OK because energy prices have started to come down.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: But I think the core is still going to put a lot of pressure on the Fed, would be my guess.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it, my friend. Always good catching up with you, Kevin.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 , the former \uc2 chairman of the Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That core inflation he refers to takes out volatile swings in food and energy prices, and then we all have to gas up our cars, for those who don't own E.V.s. We all have to eat.\par \par \uc2 But that's a way they get a sort of an idea how the actual inflation is going. And we're expecting an uptick on that front of about half-a-percent for the month of June. And that would follow about a three-tenths-of- percent increase in the overall number. That's the expectation. Always a surprise.\par \par \uc2 Here's "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230711ej7b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Flying Cars?; New App Threatening \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ?; New Alzheimer's Drug Approved; New Jersey Wind Farms Raise Concerns; Weekend Storms; Secret Service Conducts Investigation Into Cocaine Found at White House; Jimmy and Rosalynn Carter Mark 77th Wedding Anniversary; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Campaigns in Iowa; President Biden Touts Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 Kelly O'Grady, Mercedes Colwin, Marty Makary, Bryan Llenas, Rick  Reichmuth, Mark Meredith, Jonathan Serrie, Rich Edson, Charles Payne \par \uc2 8,542 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, jobs cooling in June, 209,000 created. That was below expectations. President Biden just saying, though, that his policies are working.\par \par \uc2 So why are folks, so many folks, still worrying? A new survey finds less than a third of Americans feel financially secure.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 The president making remarks right now. We will see if he's going to take questions at the end.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, to FOX Business' Kelly O'Grady with a lot more on those numbers today -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Well, it's great to see you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 And that's right. Listening to the president speaking just now, you would think that we got all good news in the jobs report today. And, in fact, I was reading his statement from earlier on the jobs data, and the first line, it said -- quote -- "This is Bidenomics in action."\par \par \uc2 But, Charles, you ask the average American how they feel with what they're bringing home when they have to go pay for groceries at the grocery store, and there seems to be a disconnect versus that rosy picture coming out of Pennsylvania Avenue.\par \par \uc2 So I want to dig into the numbers that we got today quickly. So the economy added 209,000 jobs in the month of June. That is the lowest addition that we have seen since December of 2020. It was significantly below expectations as well.\par \par \uc2 But if you listen to acting Labor Secretary Julie Su, she says it's good news.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JULIE SU, ACTING U.S. SECRETARY OF LABOR: This is very much a part of Bidenomics, is creating good jobs in every community across the country. We are definitely seeing job growth and wage increases. And in a tight labor market, workers have more power to choose, right, and to look for better working conditions, and we are seeing that ripple across the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: I also want to highlight that job growth would have been even lighter without a boost in government jobs, which increased by 60,000.\par \par \uc2 Now, the White House was really zeroing in on manufacturing performance. They're even calling it a boom. Now, sure, the manufacturing sector has net added jobs under President Biden, but if you dig into today's report, that sector was essentially flat by Labor Department standards, adding a mere 7,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 And there were a couple of other sectors I found interesting that either saw muted growth or even losses. Leisure and hospitality, right, that's been the strongest job growth engine over the past three years, added just 21,000, and retail lost 11,000 jobs. That really underscores the pain Americans are feeling as they consider pulling back on discretionary spending.\par \par \uc2 And, Charles, the last thing that I will leave you with is there was a FOX News poll that really underscores that the president is trying to sell Bidenomics to a nation of skeptics. It said 60 percent of those disapprove of the president's handling of the economy, so a very different picture than what he's been saying at the podium just now.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Kelly, thank you so much. Really appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 And I want to find out now what my next guests make up today's job support.\par \par \uc2 With me, Dave Dodson, author of the new book "The Manager's Handbook," and also lecturer at Stanford School of Business, Alfredo Ortiz, is with us. He's the president and CEO of the Jobs Creators Network.\par \par \uc2 And, Alfredo, let me start with you. Obviously, there's a disconnect. By the way, I will add, on the private sector side, 149,000, significantly below what I think Wall Street was expecting.\par \par \uc2 ALFREDO ORTIZ, CEO, JOB CREATORS NETWORK: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And, Charles, look, we're actually not too surprised of the numbers, because our national small business poll, which is really a forward-looking poll, because, remember, all these numbers are really backward-looking, our small business owners have not felt comfortable with where the economy was going for a few months now, especially concerned with inflation.\par \par \uc2 Two-thirds of our small business owners aren't feeling great about the economy. In fact, they think it's poor or fair, at best. And they're not looking at hiring. They actually have their hiring plans on hold, and they're not also looking to raise wages. So this issue of basically 90 percent of the Biden -- Biden term where real wages didn't keep up with inflation, this is going to continue probably going forward.\par \par \uc2 And that's a real concern when you have cost of groceries up 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 Year over year, I know real wages have been down like 26 months in a row.\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: That's right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Dave, how do you see the numbers and where do you think we go from here?\par \par \uc2 DAVE DODSON, STANFORD UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF BUSINESS: Well, actually, I don't think they're so bad, Charles.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Go ahead, Dave.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: But -- sure.\par \par \uc2 Charles, I don't think they're so bad. I mean, if a year ago I was on the show and I said GDP was going to be growing at 2 percent, unemployment was going to be about 3.5 percent, and inflation was coming down to 4 percent, we'd be calling that a win. And so we as a nation have to decide, do we want to have the economy growing fast or do we want to be slowing it down?\par \par \uc2 And the Fed has been saying we're going to try to slow it down. Well, this is exactly what happens when you crank up interest rates for as long as we have. So we shouldn't be particularly surprised by this.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, on that note, though, a year ago, two years ago, I certainly would have been.\par \par \uc2 Here's the wild card, $2 trillion in stimulus. That was really, for the most part, after the COVID emergency, that's what gave us this inflation scare, right? We hadn't even thought about inflation in this country for 40 years, an entire generation.\par \par \uc2 And that's been the wild card, Dave. And I look at these numbers, for instance. I'm concerned about what happens over the next few months, over the next year. Black unemployment is now at 6 percent. Two months ago, it was 4.7 percent. We can't have these numbers going up that fast. What -- can -- will that slow down?\par \par \uc2 DODSON: That was actually, Charles, the most alarming number that I saw was what happened with black unemployment.\par \par \uc2 And I have spent the day trying to figure out what is actually driving that and whether it's a blip in the numbers and it's just a flash of what happened in the last 30 days or there is some fundamental trend. The only thing I could think about is if -- when you break down the jobs report, you saw that where the real jobs were added was government, education and health care.\par \par \uc2 And what you might find is that the sectors that tended to employ black workers are the sectors that were deeply hurt, as opposed to the sectors that maybe the proportion of black workers was smaller. That's the best I could do, Charles. But I thought that was a really interesting number.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Alfredo, again, just trying to handicap. You talked earlier about how you look forward. Sort of your work is forward looking. Almost everything we react in this country is in the rearview mirror. I'm looking at the so- called excess savings, which I think is an oxymoron, at least in most American households.\par \par \uc2 Still, we were sitting on a lot, right? They said over $2 trillion. You can debate where we are now, but we're spending it fast. I mean, that's what we do, right? We're Americans. You give us money, we will go out and shop.\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: What happens when all of that goes away?\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: Well, Charles, that's exactly it.\par \par \uc2 You have the president going around crowing about how good the consumer spending is and how healthy and resilient the consumer is, because look at all that spending. They're doing great. Well, first of all, they're spending a lot more, but they're actually getting a lot less because of inflation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: Again, remember, inflation over his term is up collectively about 16 percent.\par \par \uc2 And so this is an artificial kind of high overall, if you think about it, because they're doing it all on credit. Consumers are spending. Yes, they are. But it's on credit. Credit is at record high, credit card use. And savings is at record lows. And so this is actually an artificial high. And Biden has nothing to really crow about.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, consumers have brought the credit, the savings rate up from 2 percent. It's in the mid 4 percent. Obviously, Dave, that's far too little.\par \par \uc2 Also, we saw mortgage rates rocket 5 percent in this last mortgage applications number. It just feels like really, really tough.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Where do you see us going from here? And you mentioned the Fed, Dave. I'm -- are you worried that \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  is going to go too far? That happens to be a major concern of mine.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Mine too, Charles.\par \par \uc2 And you and I have shared the same concern over the last six months. If you look at where the jobs were added, which I just mentioned were education, government, and health care, are you -- in what world does an interest rate of an eighth-of-a-point or a quarter-point higher change that hiring rate?\par \par \uc2 What we need to look at is are the sectors that are sensitive to interest rates. And those sectors actually did not add very many jobs. So I am praying that the Fed sits on their hands, stops playing the economy like it's a video game, and lets things sort of settle down, because, right now, I'm not saying that the economy is fantastic, but it ain't broken when you have got interest -- when you have got inflation coming down to about 4 percent and you have got GDP growth about 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 So let's not break it, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, well, I'm concerned that that's going to happen. And who knows. With the lag effect, it might already be in play.\par \par \uc2 So, Alfredo, where do we go from here? You have got more money coming in still through either from the fiscal side. States are sitting on a bunch of cash. Listen, some of these programs, you can talk about manufacturing coming back, particularly with the semiconductor chips.\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But, again, you got these two forces, where the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is trying to tamp down on the economy. Obviously, the White House wants to pump it up as much as possible going into the election.\par \par \uc2 How does that work out?\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: Well, it's not going to work out well, because the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , I think, is going to probably take -- the rest of the year, they will probably take at least another 50 basis points up there.\par \par \uc2 So we're going to have a problem related to that. But the problem, Charles, really is, when we do that, is you're really going to be stressing the community and regional banks, where almost 60-plus percent of small businesses access credit.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: They are already having a hard time. They're very concerned about accessing credit. This is only going to make things worse.\par \par \uc2 And, remember, when two-thirds of new job growth is in the hands of these small business owners, they're not going to hire. They're not going to want to hire. They're not going to want to invest in their businesses. So I'm really concerned that, going forward, we're probably going to look at least at a soft recession in first quarter of next year.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, let's leave it there. Thank you both very, very much. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Now to the battle for Iowa between the two front-runners in the GOP primary, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just wrapping up his remarks there just moments ago.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ' team is hoping for a new life with a secret weapon, his wife. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  calling her his humanizer in chief. Will it work?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASEY DESANTIS\uc2 , FLORIDA FIRST LADY: ... come after us as mamas. It's another thing when your policies come after our children. And that's when the claws come out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, that's Florida's first lady, \uc2 Casey DeSantis\uc2 , campaigning solo for her husband in Iowa, this as \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  calls her the humanizer in chief. We're going to get more on that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But, first, it was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  firing up the crowd in Iowa today.\par \par \uc2 To FOX News' Rich Edson with more on that -- Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Charles.\par \par \uc2 One of those early, busy states. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's courting farmers in Western Iowa right on the Nebraska border. Trump's pitching voters there in support of ethanol subsidies and cutting farming regulations. Trump's also focusing again on his closest challenger, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The former president again claimed his endorsement got DeSantis elected to the governorship in the first place.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I said: "Ron, let me tell you, if George Washington came back from the dead and he had the support of Abraham Lincoln, you can't get elected."\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Ron was losing badly. And I said: "All right, let's give it a shot, Ron."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Now, DeSantis' defenders have pointed out he won reelection in November in Florida by nearly 20 points.\par \par \uc2 Trump has maintained a significant lead over the Republican field, leading the second-place DeSantis by more than 40 percentage points. DeSantis says he has a deliberate strategy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think, of any Republican in the country, I have the best record of defeating the left on issue after issue. And we will be making that case over the next six or seven months.\par \par \uc2 I'm running to win in January and February. I'm not running to juice polling now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: DeSantis, unlike Trump, is committing to the first GOP debate in August.\par \par \uc2 The Florida governor says debates are an important part of the process, that he will be there regardless of whether Trump shows or not, and says he hopes all eligible candidates debate. Senator Tim Scott told FOX he'd be at the debate if he qualifies. Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  also said this week that he's attending -- back to you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, Rich, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 So is this the start of that head-to-head battle everyone's been waiting for?\par \par \uc2 Let's ask Democratic pollster Doug Schoen, along with The Hill's editor in chief, Bob Cusack.\par \par \uc2 Bob, let me start with you.\par \par \uc2 At some point, they have got to go head to head. The innuendoes have started to build up, and, obviously, President Trump, former President Trump, has been taking aim at \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  a lot more than DeSantis has, although it seems like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is starting to pick it up a little bit.\par \par \uc2 BOB CUSACK, EDITOR IN CHIEF, THE HILL: Yes, I mean, Charles, it's so early. A lot of people are on summer vacations and they're not focused on this race.\par \par \uc2 So media people who are writing off DeSantis, it's a little bit early. At the same time, his campaign sputtered at the beginning, and other than raising money, he hasn't had a lot of bright spots. So the debate is going to be so important. Whether Trump is there or not, DeSantis, there is going to be so much pressure on him to win that debate whether Trump is there or not.\par \par \uc2 And everyone, including Trump, is going to be gunning for him.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Doug, in that debate, who would be the wild card for you? I mean, would it be maybe \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , who, I don't know, to me -- you guys like football, right?\par \par \uc2 Remember they used to have the wedge breakers, right, the guy that run down, and the only job he had was just to wreck havoc, like, not tackle the guy with the football, but maybe take four or five people out? I have felt so far that \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  has been the wedge breaker, but could a debate propel someone like him out of the pack?\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FORMER BILL CLINTON POLLSTER: Yes, theoretically, it's possible.\par \par \uc2 I think somebody like a Tim Scott could break out, a Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: But let me just offer one point of clarification. I don't think \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  is the wedge breaker.\par \par \uc2 I think he's headed for one person, which is \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 . I think he's going to use the debate to try to take Trump out and effectively be an asset and ally of Governor DeSantis of Florida.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Oh, OK. He may be looking for a gig, then, if DeSantis wins.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Exactly. Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Or if he doesn't win, maybe renew a contract somewhere. Wink, wink.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, Bob, on that note, as we came in, I mentioned the first lady, Florida's first lady, \uc2 Casey DeSantis\uc2 , hitting a campaign trail, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  calling her the humanizer in chief, something akin to the role that \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  played for her husband, which was huge.\par \par \uc2 Do you think it can work?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: I think it can, but DeSantis is going to have to connect more. Barack Obama gave that big speech at the convention in 2004, and that was his springboard to 2008.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, she is an asset, without a doubt, but there's no doubt about it that I think he's going to have to connect more and really get his campaign closer to Trump over the next several weeks before the debate.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Bob, let's stop beating around the Bush. You got to tell me exactly what he's got to do. Connect is a good word, but I don't know what that means.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what does he have to do?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: I think -- well, number one, I think he should be doing more media appearances. He's picked that up.\par \par \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  is doing a lot of media appearances. And in a recent poll, Charles, he was only six points behind DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: And I didn't even know who he was six months ago.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: So that's impressive.\par \par \uc2 So I think it's more on personality than policy. And that's where I think he's got to look a little more comfortable in these diners, because when Iowa and New Hampshire and South Carolina, these early states, it's got to be one-on-one, where you're doing the \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , I feel your pain.\par \par \uc2 And unless you have that type of personality -- and we haven't seen it yet -- I don't think you can get to the finish line.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Doug, a lot of folks in the media are saying it's too early to write off \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and yet I feel like they have written off \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Like, here's what I hear, Doug. He's got a core group, but he can't raise that number, and he can't win in a general election. But I heard that some other time in, remind me, 2015-'16.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats, how worried are they that Trump will become the nominee?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Charles, increasingly worried about President Trump coming back.\par \par \uc2 He's over 50 percent in a number of the recent polls in the Republican primary. He's ahead in the most recent polls I have seen, the general election, by a point or two over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Biden's numbers are at about 40 percent, on the economy, 34, 35 percent.\par \par \uc2 So the Democrats are increasingly worried. And I believe, like you, Charles, that it is entirely possible that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could win this election.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I'm just -- I'm not sure why everyone's writing them off, but that's what experts do.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you, guys. 2024 GOP presidential candidate Nikki Haley, she's held two town halls in New Hampshire today, RealClear average showing her in fourth place in that state behind \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bob, how important is it that she gains traction here and she makes some type of breakout in these early states?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Oh, very important. I think it is for all of these people who are struggling past DeSantis and Trump, especially for her campaign.\par \par \uc2 Remember, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  did not make it to Iowa in a presidential campaign. You need money and you need momentum, and she needs to get it. I think she's a very good politician, but she needs to generate momentum real soon.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Hey, guys. You are two of the best. I'm glad we were able to have this conversation. Thank you both very, very much. We will talk again real soon.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And the question is, of course, what does Nikki Haley have to say about this uphill challenge? Well, you're going to find out tomorrow, because she's going to join "Cavuto Live" at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time. Make sure you check that out.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, former President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  and his wife, Rosalynn, they're marking their 77th wedding anniversary today. So that extends their record for the longest married first couple every, this as the former president continues to receive hospice care.\par \par \uc2 To FOX News' Jonathan Serrie with more on that -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well, the former president and former first lady are marking this milestone quietly at their rural home in Plains, Georgia, in the southern portion of this state.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Carter, who is 98, has been receiving home hospice was care since February. And, more recently, Mrs. Carter, who is 95, revealed she has dementia. Nevertheless, the Carter family says the couple have continued to enjoy their time together and visits with loved ones. The former first couple have known each other since early childhood.\par \par \uc2 The future president's mother was the nurse who delivered Rosalynn Smith. But at a larger, more formal anniversary celebration in 2021, Mrs. Carter revealed that, while growing up in Plains, she had no interest in dating boys.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROSALYNN CARTER\uc2 , FORMER FIRST LADY: I didn't know how to talk to them. I didn't want to go out with them. I used to tell my mother when the phone rang to tell them I wasn't in. Whoever it was, tell them I wasn't at home. And I wasn't. I was already out the door and down the street.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CARTER: And then along came \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 . And my life's been an adventure ever since.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: The Carter Presidential Library tweeted today: "Seventy-seven years ago today -- that 7/7 -- young \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  hit the jackpot when he wed the love of his life, Rosalynn Smith. Four 7's are pretty lucky, even if you're not in Vegas."\par \par \uc2 The Carters credit the success of their marriage to, first of all, their shared Christian faith, but also to a policy that they adhered through -- to throughout their entire marriage, which is to resolve any differences they're having before they go to sleep at the end of each day -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: What a beautiful love story.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, thanks a lot. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, at least 100 million Americans are bracing for more severe weather from New Mexico to Ohio this weekend. We're going to get you the latest on that.\par \par \uc2 And what is the White House saying now as the Secret Service probe into the discovery of cocaine in the White House continues?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thousands of thrill seekers taking part in today's Running of the Bulls in Pamplona, Spain, several runners taking hard falls, but no one, luckily, got gored. This is the first of eight scheduled runs.\par \par \uc2 And we're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The White House facing more questions today as the Secret Service probe into the discovery of cocaine continues.\par \par \uc2 Mark Meredith is at the White House with the latest -- Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Five days after the drugs were found in the West Wing, still no clear answers about who may have brought them on campus to begin with. But, tonight, the White House has been facing fresh questions about whether or not there were any security implications with these drugs coming into the White House in the first place.\par \par \uc2 While the president has yet to make any public comments on all of this, we had a chance to ask the national security adviser, Jake Sullivan, about what the impact this may be on his office. Here's what he had to say at day's White House press briefing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, U.S. NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: But, no, there was no issue with the Situation Room relative to this.\par \par \uc2 And then, finally, look, we have rigorous drug testing policies at the White House. We have rigorous drug use policies here at the White House. We take those extremely seriously. So we will let the investigation unfold. If it involves someone from the White House, the appropriate consequences will ensue.\par \par \uc2 If it involves some visitor who came in and left it, then that's a different matter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: A different matter.\par \par \uc2 But congressional Republicans say they have plenty of interest in what was going on behind the scenes. We have heard from both House and -- House Republicans and one Senate Republican who's demanding information from the \uc2 U.S. Secret Service\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service says it is aware of the congressional interest into what's happened here. However, because it's still an ongoing investigation, they're likely going to have to wait until that wraps up before getting back to lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 But it's clear these Republicans, Charles, are ready to probe this any further. And Secret Service says they believe this investigation will wrap up early next week. But they also warn it's possible we may never know for sure how those drugs got here to begin with -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Oh, boy. All right, thanks. Thanks a lot, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: You bet.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, more wicked weather on the way, 100 million Americans under severe weather alerts as we head into the weekend.\par \par \uc2 FOX's meteorologist Rick Reichmuth has a lot more on the outlook -- Rick.\par \par \uc2 RICK REICHMUTH, FOX NEWS CHIEF METEOROLOGIST: Charles, OK, so it's hot, it's humid, and this time of year, when you get that, you get these thunderstorms pop up.\par \par \uc2 We're seeing this kind of every afternoon, a lot of kind of popcorn variety thunderstorms, especially down across parts of Florida. But we do have some more organized severe weather. And, today, that worst of it is crossed the Panhandle of Texas and Oklahoma, Southeastern Colorado. That's the bullseye, mostly a large hail threat, although we could see a tornado or two.\par \par \uc2 And then just take a look at what happens here. This is Friday evening. We see tonight around 10:00 storms moving through much of Oklahoma, in towards Missouri, Kansas, big rain across parts of Florida again, especially in the afternoons. We will see that.\par \par \uc2 Then, throughout the day on Saturday, the main front continues to pull towards the east, and that becomes severe weathermaker tomorrow, a severe weather maker for Sunday a little bit farther across the mid-Atlantic. So we're kind of in this pattern where we're getting round after round of rain across parts of the Northeast on the weekends, so not good news for a lot of beachgoers across the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, Saturday, we have got storms across the South, still again on Sunday, but the bullseye, I have it a little bit more across the Southeast and in towards the mid-Atlantic, and then just kind of a rainy stretch. Not everybody's getting a washout of your weekend, but you're going to be watching here because from time to time we will see some big storms coming through.\par \par \uc2 And, because of that, we will watch for some flooding concerns as well. One other story, Charles, that we're watching. So we're into hurricane season. We're about a month and a week or so into it right now. And an update to the forecast just came out, and they are increasing the forecast for the amount of storms that we're going to see this year, up to 18, according to Colorado State, nine hurricanes, four possible major hurricanes.\par \par \uc2 And that's an increase in what they had forecast. We have two things going on right now in the hurricane that we're watching. One is El Nino that would generally bring down the number of hurricanes or tropical activity that we have, but we also have really warm waters in across the Atlantic. It's a battle between those two things.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the forecasters are giving the warm water in the Atlantic the upper hand here and saying we will see a little bit more of that. Charles, big point to know, we have got about 96 percent of activity for hurricane season yet to come, so a long ways to go.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow. We will brace for it.\par \par \uc2 Rick, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 REICHMUTH: You bet.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Meantime, new numbers showing the number of stranded whale incidents along the East Coast is surging, and that has critics voicing new wind turbine warnings and concerns.\par \par \uc2 FOX's Bryan Llenas is in Long Branch, New Jersey, with more details -- Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Well, look, this week the Biden administration green-lit the construction of the largest offshore wind farm in the U.S. That's up to 98 wind turbines hundreds of feet tall just 15 miles off the coast of the Jersey Shore.\par \par \uc2 The Ocean Wind 1 project, according to New Jersey Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2 , is one that will be a monumental win for the state, saying by 2025 it could power up to half-a-million homes and create 3,000 union jobs. But a lawyer representing three environmental groups is suing New Jersey in three state and federal lawsuits, arguing the wind project is being built in the migration corridors of endangered blue and right whales.\par \par \uc2 These organizations are suspicious of recent whale deaths they believe have something to do with wind farm site surveys and construction. The \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\uc2  reports 45 whales have washed ashore dead so far this year. That's a 164 percent increase from the deaths compared to the same time last year.\par \par \uc2 NOAA\uc2  says there is no scientific evidence that links whale deaths to offshore wind surveys. But these groups contend the Biden administration is rushing the construction of wind energy farms without knowing the exact impacts on ecological and marine life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRUCE AFRAN, ATTORNEY: No one has ever studied what happens when you stick hundreds of skyscrapers in the migration paths of oceangoing whales. No one knows what will happen. Will it destroy their life cycle? Will it eliminate their habitat?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: This is now the third wind farm approved along the Northeast as Biden races to reach his 2030 goal of powering 10 million homes harnessing the energy of wind.\par \par \uc2 They say it's an urgent need to combat climate change. Republicans like New Jersey's Congressman \uc2 Jeff Van Drew\uc2  say that these risks aren't worth the negligible benefits that could come in the fight against climate change.\par \par \uc2 And one other thing, Charles, they also believe that locals here haven't really come to grasp -- or come to terms with the fact that these are going to be permanent eyesores along the Jersey Shore. And that's a concern for people that live here and love the Jersey Shore -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I read about one in Brazil that broke, fell apart, and it costs $1.1 billion to fix it. That's another concern no one's thinking about.\par \par \uc2 Bryan, a lot of stuff there. Thank you so much, my friend. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Of course.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, now to that new app that is taking subscribers by storm. And now \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  could be taking \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  to court. Want to find out who's got the best case? We're going to discuss that.\par \par \uc2 Also, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  signing off on the first drug showing it can slow the progress of Alzheimer's. How big of a breakthrough is this? Dr. \uc2 Marty Makary\uc2  is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We have got some new hope in a battle against Alzheimer's disease, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  giving full approval to a drug for the first time in more than 20 years.\par \par \uc2 It's called Leqembi. It's designed to actually slow the progress of the disease, but there's still a lot of questions about the side effects and who will have access to the drug.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, FOX News medical contributor Dr. \uc2 Marty Makary\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, Dr. Makary, first of all, just the magnitude of this breakthrough. What does it mean?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 MARTY MAKARY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Look, it's significant.\par \par \uc2 This drug slowed the progression of Alzheimer's. We have never had a drug that slows the progression. This has been in development for about 30 years. And there's been a question, if you reduce the plaque level in the brain, does that help symptoms?\par \par \uc2 And, finally, we got the answer from the large randomized control trial. It does. But there is a black box warning. There are some complications people should know about.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Tell us about them.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Well, first of all, the black box warning says that you can have a severe or life-threatening event.\par \par \uc2 That warning is referring to, in the trial, 13 percent of patients who were on this new drug had brain swelling; 19 percent had a brain bleed, about double the number in the placebo group. And there were three deaths out of about the 900 people that took the drug. So people need to weigh the risks and benefits.\par \par \uc2 And, remember, this is for early pre-Alzheimer's dementia or early mild Alzheimer's; 75 percent of people with Alzheimer's do not qualify to get this drug.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Twenty-seven percent slow down over 18 months. For loved ones and for people who have to endure this, that is significant.\par \par \uc2 But there are also, from what I'm reading, concerns about the cost and who has access to it. What's the controversy there?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Well, first of all, Charles, I couldn't agree with you more. I had an aunt with Alzheimer's. It was very painful for all of us. It's a very brutal and cruel disease.\par \par \uc2 So any hope right now is positive. But the price tag is significant, $26,000 a year, on average. This is an infusion, like it's a monoclonal antibody like Regeneron was to treat COVID. And so the price tag is significant.\par \par \uc2 Now, Medicare will pay for about 80 percent of it, but still that leaves you with a $7,000 bill for the drug on an annual basis. You stop the drug once Alzheimer's has progressed to a moderate stage. But CMS, the Center for Medicare and Medicaid Services, budgeted to cover the drug, decided in the spring not to cover the drug, had a bit of a surplus as a result.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration touted this as a savings to Medicare Part B as a result of the Inflation Reduction Act. In fact, it was a savings related to a noncoverage decision of Medicare.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow. Wow. So that means this is going to be tough for people who -- the rich will be able to afford it, but everyone else will just be sort of watching from the outside in.\par \par \uc2 I got less than a minute to go. I have been watching this for a long time from the stock market point of view and I have watched the fortunes of \uc2 Biogen\uc2 , for instance, go up and down. How hopeful are you that, sometime soon, we will be able to significantly slow, if not prevent Alzheimer's?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Look, I'm very optimistic that we're going to be able to slow it down, not just because of this discovery that reducing plaque helps the symptoms with Alzheimer's, but also we're learning a lot about lifestyle changes, the role of food in the progression of Alzheimer's.\par \par \uc2 Chronic poor sleep has been identified by many groups, including my own Johns Hopkins research team, as a potential contributing factor to the development of Alzheimer's. So it is ironic, Charles. We will spend billions of dollars on a medication for Alzheimer's, but we have barely started to scratch the surface on what causes Alzheimer's, and those causes appear to be right in front of us in clear view.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. How about medical prevention? We need to start talking about that, a lot about it, across the board.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Makary, thank you very much. And, again, sorry to hear about your aunt. It is so painful for everyone I have ever talked to about it.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Thank you. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, forget the battle between these two billionaires in a cage. Try court. We have got the latest on that.\par \par \uc2 And who didn't want to be George Jetson, right, chilling out, flying around in that flying car? Guess what? We may be closer than ever, that this may be closer than ever to becoming a reality. And you're going to meet the guy behind it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Point, click, sue.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says competition is fine, but cheating isn't. And now he's threatening to sue his new rival, Threads, which just keeps boosting - - boasting new members.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business reporter Kelly O'Grady has the very latest for us -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Hi. Good to see you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I think we're at 70 million at this point, according to \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , as of this morning. And, listen, we're only on day two here, so this fight is escalating. \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's legal team is accusing Meta of not just creating a competitor, but stealing its intellectual property.\par \par \uc2 So, hours after Threads launched, they sent a cease-and-desist letter to \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , warning they plan to enforce their I.P. rights and that Meta must preserve all relevant communication. So \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's counsel is arguing the company intentionally hired former \uc2 Twitter\uc2  staff to rip off the platform.\par \par \uc2 They said this: "These employees had and continued to have access to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's trade secrets. These employees owe ongoing obligations to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and many of these employees improperly retained \uc2 Twitter\uc2  documents and electronic devices."\par \par \uc2 Now, Meta responded with a "no comment" to our inquiries, but I did speak to a number of legal experts, and they tell me this is going to be really tough for \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to enforce. They would have to prove that their own intellectual property, like the underlying programming code, was taken, because U.S. copyright law does not protect ideas.\par \par \uc2 Now, Charles, Meta has done this before, and it's worked out, right? They have copied TikTok with Reels, Snap with Stories. So my takeaway is this legal threat underscores \uc2 Twitter\uc2  sees Threads as a real competitor.\par \par \uc2 I will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, thank you very much. Yes, 70 million in 24 hours, that ain't -- that's pretty good.\par \par \uc2 So the question, of course, now legally is, does \uc2 Twitter\uc2  have a case? We're going to get to read from our legal eagle, Mercedes Colwin.\par \par \uc2 And I got to tell you, it -- one thing about Silicon Valley, though, they steal everything.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: If people knew that every great billionaire fortune in the Silicon Valley began with theft.\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES COLWIN, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But what do you think?\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: It's a big depends. I mean, there's a lot that was said in that letter.\par \par \uc2 So where is the proprietary information? Are there confidential documents? Are there confidential data? And Kelly's exactly right. It's very hard to take intellectual capital. These are former \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employees. Put them now at Meta and Threads. And you can't make that proprietary. It's their experience.\par \par \uc2 But if you safeguarded a program, if you safeguarded steps...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: ... then maybe you have a case.\par \par \uc2 So, it really is about what it boils down to. And, by the way, it's not enough about being proprietary. What did you do to safeguard it?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: I mean, think about where the laws have given that type of protection, if you safeguard a formula, if you safeguard a process in place, you patent it.\par \par \uc2 That's what we talked about before the segment started. Then, of course, the laws come in, and they will seek that protection and get it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So I'm thinking about a guy who worked at \uc2 Mattel\uc2 , worked for the Barbie company. They were making Barbies. He drew some stuff up. He showed it to his boss. It was fantastic, he thought.\par \par \uc2 The boss said, nah, nobody would ever buy it. Later on, he quit, went to another shop. They say, OK, they made the dolls. It was called the Bratz.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Took the world by storm. I think \uc2 Mattel\uc2  sued and won.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Well, it's interesting. It could be exactly what you're talking about. Is it proprietary because he created the formula, or the formula for these dolls, the designs for these dolls, while being an employee of one company, took those designs?\par \par \uc2 Those designs are proprietary to \uc2 Mattel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: They took it and then created Bratz.\par \par \uc2 But one thing to say, if this is in California, they don't like these restrictive covenants. It is illegal in California. So we will see if there's forum-shopping, if \uc2 Twitter\uc2  takes us in other locations other than California, because these restrictive covenants are illegal in California. Can't do it. Can't restrict people from working in other places and taking their intellectual capital and proprietary information.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I guess that's why it's so -- it's so -- it happens so much out there.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Another thing I have seen with these cases that are successful, it's almost a moot point.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You go to court and then like, four or five, six, seven years later, you win. Sometimes, a founder of a company will get some cash. It's a lot for them.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But the company that's taken it, that's stolen it has built an empire on it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, ultimately, this might end up being a moot point. If they're getting 70 million subscribers in the first 24 hours, by the time this is resolved, it might not even matter.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Charles, that's a great point. I mean, 70 million in 24 hours, that's huge, tremendous.\par \par \uc2 And, look, obviously, a lot of people are intrigued, a lot of the media attention this. They may just make that switch. We will have to see what happens.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Well, they have got two billion in \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . You just may click and you're on the new thing.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: By the way, I'm looking at all of -- there's five of them. They all look exactly the -- exactly the same.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: They do.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It's nuts.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: But there's no patent or protection on the look of it. We will have to see. And there's really no consumer confusion. It says clearly Threads. The other says clearly \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . One has a bird, the other one doesn't.\par \par \uc2 So we will have to see how it works.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, Mercedes, great seeing you.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, the future is here. That's right, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  giving the green light to start testing the first flying cars.\par \par \uc2 And we have got the guy that's making it happen next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I have been waiting for this since the 1970s. It's straight out of "The Jetsons," right?\par \par \uc2 A California based company receiving the \uc2 FAA\uc2 's first certification for a flying car. It's going to cost you $300,000. And preorders are starting to come in. They're soaring.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in a guy behind it, Alef Aeronautics CEO Jim Dukhovny.\par \par \uc2 And, Jim, first and foremost, congratulations. I was checking out videos of this thing, and the first thing that struck me, because I have been waiting for these for a long time, all these other flying cars have propellers everywhere.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is like a car, but you don't see the propellers, anything. So, talk to us about it.\par \par \uc2 JIM DUKHOVNY, FOUNDER AND CEO, ALEF AERONAUTICS: Sure.\par \par \uc2 So, thanks for hosting. So I just want to point out it's actually four founders of us. It's not only me. And the actually, people who build it is Constantine Kisly, Oleg Petrov, and Pavel Markin.\par \par \uc2 So there are propellers, as you can see, like, for example, in what you're showing right now. They're just hidden. They're hidden inside.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Oh.\par \par \uc2 DUKHOVNY: They're hidden inside, and they are protected by the mesh, right? So it allows, on one hand, airflow through. On the other hand, it creates a solid body, solid car body.\par \par \uc2 Hence, they are there, but it allows for the flight.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, right. It's a beautiful design. It's elegant. It's sleek. It's a lot smaller, again, than everything I have seen thus far that I thought would be first to market.\par \par \uc2 So, obviously, there's some other issues. You're taking in these orders, but the jurisdictions that allow this, are there any places that you can actually use a flying car right now in this country?\par \par \uc2 DUKHOVNY: Right.\par \par \uc2 This is why it's a preorder. So, we're still working through that. There are some jurisdictions where we qualify, for example, as an ultralight. It's still limited, right, in the \uc2 FAA\uc2  certification, which we received special awards and certification. It's very limited, and for mostly testing, R&D, exhibition and something like that.\par \par \uc2 There are some jurisdictions, but we're working on it. And, yes, there are some jurisdictions in the world where you can do it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DUKHOVNY: And we're working very hard so, in U.S., you will also be able to do it pretty soon.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let's talk about airplane mode, because I think that's, like, the - - one of the coolest features.\par \par \uc2 DUKHOVNY: Yes, so the -- one of the big reasons why we did not have flying cars for 100 years is because the energy density, energy efficiency.\par \par \uc2 The car itself is shaped to push you down, not up, and you need, like, good wings to push up to be energy efficient. With the modern car design, it can fly, and with the propellers inside, it can fly maybe for, like, five to 10 minutes. But, after that, there is no battery in the world which can support it.\par \par \uc2 What we had to come up with is, somehow, we needed wings, and we had to solve this problem with zero extra weight. And the only way Constantine, Oleg, and Pavel came up with doing it is actually turning the car, and now you have a biplane mode, a very efficient mode of flying, with zero extra weight from the ground car.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Hey, I got less than a minute ago. I'm asking for a friend. Will you be able to use one of these if you weigh more than 250? Wink, wink.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DUKHOVNY: So, the weight we actually -- calculating the base weight, is actually 250.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Really? OK. All right.\par \par \uc2 DUKHOVNY: So, we have one of our -- yes, one of our investors is very tall and with very wide shoulders.\par \par \uc2 And we got kind of, like, something close to his weight as the basis for this.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 DUKHOVNY: Keep in mind, if you increase the weight, you will decrease the distance.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DUKHOVNY: This is totally OK.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, Jim.\par \par \uc2 Hey, congratulations. It is so, so cool. I will stay on my diet, so I can get one of those soon.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: See you. See you soon, my friend.\par \par \uc2 DUKHOVNY: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And, folks, before you fly into the weekend, make sure you catch "Cavuto Live" tomorrow 10:00 p.m. -- 10:00 a.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 2024 presidential candidate Nikki Haley is going to be there. She's pushing hard, and she's gaining a little traction.\par \par \uc2 Now here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230707ej7700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Tiny Homes; Trump Campaign Rakes in $35 Million in Second Quarter; Future Democratic Presidential Candidates?; Holiday Violence; UPS Strike Imminent?; Middle East Unrest; Iran Tensions; Air Travel Delays\b0\par\par\uc2 Kat Timpf, Jeff Flock, Aishah Hasnie, Lydia Hu, Trey Yingst, Phil  Flynn,Rick Reichmuth, Jennifer Griffin, Madison Alworth, Jonathan Serrie,  Charles Payne \par \uc2 8,053 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's been terrible. It's been terrible. As soon as I landed, they just canceled my flights. Had to wait in this line for over -- about over two hours.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They have to cut some flights or something, because they just can't handle it. They just can't handle it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Delayed, delayed, delayed, delayed until the next day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would expect the airline to be a little more responsible, help us out a little bit better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Fliers on edge, as the nation's airports continue to be hit with delays and cancellations.\par \par \uc2 And now Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg feeling the heat with another 50,000 flights expected today.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 We have got FOX team coverage with Madison Alworth at Newark International airport on how fliers are holding up, and Jonathan Serrie at Hartsfield- Jackson airport in Atlanta, severe storms that could have those problems actually piling up.\par \par \uc2 We begin with Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I mean, here at Newark, things are a bit smoother today, which is welcome relief for Newark travelers because this is an airport that was hit the worst when it came to those delays and cancellation last week, especially if they were flying on United.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  , FOX has now confirmed that United is actually going to be pulling back some of their routes this summer from Newark Airport, in part because of the meltdown they experienced last week. They were not specific about just how many routes or flights will be cut. The company also handed out 30,000 frequent flier miles to travelers that were hit with those delays.\par \par \uc2 Travelers had to do things like sleep on the floor as they waited to see when they could get out. Now, Secretary of Transportation Pete Buttigieg says travel is back to normal. But one of the big problems, air traffic controller shortages, that is far from over.\par \par \uc2 A recent Transportation Department report warns that problems could persist for years because of a government-caused shortage of air traffic controllers that started during the pandemic and continued as we made our way out of it.\par \par \uc2 The agency does have plans to hire 3,000 over the next two years, but, for now, passengers are still struggling to get where they paid to go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We tried to leave yesterday and they canceled our flight.\par \par \uc2 I got up around 4:00 this morning worried because I want to be there for his first birthday. And I wasn't sure, so if I couldn't get on the flight, I was going to drive. I was going to drive (INAUDIBLE) miles.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm a little bit irritated. This will probably being my last flight flying United.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: Yes, people really upset with everything that is happening. And the good news is fewer delays and cancellations today. But many of the passengers, the travelers that we spoke to here, it's their second time to Newark because those initial flights were canceled.\par \par \uc2 When you look at cancellations and delays out of this airport today, Charles, we're looking at around 3,000 delays and around 200 cancellations at last check, so still by no means perfect, but much better than what we were dealing with this time last week.\par \par \uc2 I will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, let's call it a reprieve for now. Thanks so much, Madison.\par \par \uc2 Now to Jonathan Serrie at Hartsfield-Jackson Airport in Atlanta -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Charles.\par \par \uc2 As you can see behind me, long lines at the main \uc2 TSA\uc2  checkpoint, but they're moving rapidly. Of course, Atlanta being a major hub, much of the activity is actually taking place out of the concourse, as people simply getting off one plane and transferring to the next.\par \par \uc2 According to the tracking service FlightAware, more than 3,100 flights within, into, or out the U.S. have already been delayed today and more than 250 have been canceled. That's nationwide. Travelers are having mixed experiences. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was pretty good. It wasn't bad at all. No delays, no big bushes of people or nothing like that. It was pretty seamless.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We had to spend the night in the airport in Dallas. We went to some of their airport suites, just like a -- I mean, it looks like an office, really. We spend it there. Pretty cold. I mean, it wasn't the best experience, but it is what it is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Air travelers set a new record heading into the holiday weekend, with nearly 2.9 million passing through \uc2 TSA\uc2  checkpoints on Friday, up from 2.5 million on the same weekday last year.\par \par \uc2 And even that was above the pre-pandemic level set in 2019. But travel experts say airlines have not built back staffing and aircraft inventory fast enough to keep up with the resurgence in consumer demand. And the airlines point to a shortage of air traffic controllers.\par \par \uc2 Then you throw in the weather. Charles, this time of year here in the Southeast, you have pop-up storms driven by the hot weather, and also a cold front is pushing severe weather across the plains and the Midwest going into Friday, which is undoubtedly going to pose problems for many people returning from Fourth of July vacations -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Jonathan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of bad weather, hail pelting parts of Northern Colorado. That's on the Fourth of July, this as 61 million Americans remain under the threat of severe weather today.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in meteorologist Rick Reichmuth in the FOX Weather Center with more on that -- Rick.\par \par \uc2 RICK REICHMUTH, FOX NEWS CHIEF METEOROLOGIST: Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Yes, Colorado has been under the gun for a number of weeks, getting so much severe weather. And that is going to stay with us, kind of the same pattern for the next few days. Want to show you what's going on. Jonathan was just mentioning these summer-type storms. It's hot and it's humid really anywhere on the East.\par \par \uc2 Because of that, you get these storms that will pop up pretty much anywhere. Could see a storm. But severe threat today is going to be up around the Chicago area and then here right up across the Front Range, parts of Colorado, New Mexico, panhandles of Texas and Oklahoma. And these storms are going to be firing throughout the earlier evenings, earlier evening hours. They move a little farther off towards the east by the time we get towards tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But take a look at this. Today, along I-25 and just to the east of, that's where we could be seeing a tornado threat. We will certainly be seeing some pretty significant hail along with some of these storms, and I think the wind threat as well could be causing some damage from here. And that goes again up towards the Chicago area.\par \par \uc2 Then go into the day tomorrow, almost the exact same area, maybe slightly to the east of this, looking at a very likely threat for severe weather again. That happens again for us on Friday. Because of this day-after-day severe weather, it also means we're going to get a lot of moisture out of this, some areas maybe three to four inches here.\par \par \uc2 Want you to notice Kansas, where we have this moisture, we also have a really significant drought going on. So this is going to help the drought that we have going on in Kansas, but we're getting it a little bit too quickly. And because of that, we have a chance for some flooding. That goes on with us today. We have got it tomorrow and we have got it in towards the day on Friday.\par \par \uc2 One last thing. There is this cold front kind of begins to stall out across this area right across the High Plains. But you get the idea. Some cooler temps are going to be moving in here. It's probably going to be some good news for a lot of people that have seen so much hot weather already in the Central Plains.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at these temperatures tomorrow, North Platte, Nebraska, only 73, Minneapolis for the day on Friday 76, so a nice fresher break for some cooler air, but we still have all of these storms, especially across the High Plains -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It certainly will be welcome.\par \par \uc2 Thanks so much, Rick.\par \par \uc2 REICHMUTH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And now this, folks, an editorial in The Wall Street Journal taking aim at the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 It reads: "Flying the \uc2 FAA\uc2  's unfriendly skies. A new inspector general report tells you what Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg won't about air traffic controllers." There's a major shortage. It does go on to criticize the training procedures for new flight controllers now due in part to the pandemic. And it says, be prepared for these problems to continue for a few more summers.\par \par \uc2 So how fair is that criticism? I'm going to bring in travel journalist Francesca Page.\par \par \uc2 Francesca, what do you make of that Journal editorial? I mean, golly, it's going to take a couple of years to resolve this?\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA PAGE, TRAVEL AND LIFESTYLE EXPERT: Well, you have got to keep in mind that, aside from the bad weather, the airports were seeing impacts from reduced staffing at critical air route traffic control centers.\par \par \uc2 So going into the summer, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  knew that this could be an issue. The lack of staffing almost systemwide impacting air traffic on high passenger travel days coupled with weather is bound to create severe delays for travelers.\par \par \uc2 However, probably also not the most tactful move on the part of the \uc2 FAA\uc2  to then joke about passenger etiquette amidst all this.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: But the problem is also that it seems the transportation secretary didn't originally even appear to be worried about how to handle issues with flight disruptions, whether ultimately due to weather, 5G issues, or staffing, essentially leaving travelers with a state of what do we do and what should we prepare for as we enter into the summer?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I think around the White House, they call him cool hand Pete.\par \par \uc2 It's not just, though, the Wall Street Journal. Last week, the CEO of United really upset. He says, when it comes to weather, particularly in Newark, where they have a lot of operations, he says "The \uc2 FAA\uc2  has historically been able to manage without severe impact to our operations or customers."\par \par \uc2 It just feels just so irresponsible. And then I went over to the \uc2 FAA\uc2  's Web site to see, well, what does it take to become an air traffic controller? Well, it turns out 2023 application, that window is closed. And so if you wanted to be an air traffic controller and come to the rescue, that window is closed. You got to be a U.S. citizen. You got to be 31.\par \par \uc2 I'm looking at this and it doesn't seem like, OK, three years general work experience. You wonder how we have had this void this long and why it continues to this degree.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Absolutely, 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, ultimately, I think everyone is very concerned about what the summer ahead is going to look like. As I have mentioned, there's a number of factors taken into consideration. It's not just staffing. Obviously, the weather has been pretty terrible. I can speak from experience. I have had a lot of friends who've had gone through severe delays and cancellations over this past holiday week.\par \par \uc2 Look, for the most part, airlines are really trying to do what they can right now to keep passengers happy, which is not an easy feat. Please make sure you check your flight status before you get to the airport. And always sign up for airlines' free text alerts and download their apps for updates.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: If you're already at the airport when you get delayed or canceled, please head as fast as you can to the agent's desk and call up your carrier in the meantime to see what your options are.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Also, try and book direct flights as much as possible. The more legs an itinerary has, the more points where it can get messed up along the way.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Yes, we got to leave it there. Maybe bring a sleeping bag too.\par \par \uc2 Francesca, no, really great stuff. Really appreciate it. And people are very frustrated.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, so just as prices at the pump are coming down, a new threat from Iran in a crucial waterway could actually send them back up. Hear how the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  scrambled to take action.\par \par \uc2 And with talks between UPS and \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  breaking down, is the threat of a strike ramping up? And, if so, what does it mean for your home deliveries? We have got it coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Tensions overseas sending the price of oil surging, the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  saying that it was forced to respond to an Iranian vessel that tried to seize two tankers in the Gulf of Oman.\par \par \uc2 FOX News national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin has the very latest from the Pentagon -- Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well, shortly after 1:00 a.m. in international waters in the Gulf of Oman, the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  says it stopped two Iranian naval vessels, both trying to seize two commercial oil tankers off the coast of Oman in the Arabian Sea.\par \par \uc2 In the first incident, the Iranian Navy was approaching the Marshall Islands-flagged oil tanker TRF MOSS until the USS McFaul, a \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  guided missile destroyer, arrived on the scene. And the Iranian naval vessel withdrew. An MQ-9 armed Reaper drone and P-8 Poseidon maritime patrol aircraft accompanied the McFaul.\par \par \uc2 This video released by the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  shows the second incident three hours later, which involved a different Iranian naval vessel threatening this Bahamian-flagged oil tanker Richmond Voyager seen in the upper right-hand corner of the screen. Iranian personnel on board the Iranian vessel fired multiple shots at the Richmond Voyager, leaving these bullet holes in the hull not far from where the crew slept.\par \par \uc2 The USS McFaul intervened in both incidents, steaming toward the Bahamian oil tanker at maximum speed 20 miles off the coast of Muscat, Oman, according to a statement from the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  . The Iranian vessel was within one mile of the Richmond Voyager and was hailing the oil tanker to stop when the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  arrived.\par \par \uc2 Vice Admiral Brad Cooper praised the McFaul crew -- quote -- "I couldn't be prouder of the entire U.S. Naval force's Central Command team, especially the exceptional effort by the McFaul crew for immediately responding and preventing another seizure. We remain vigilant and ready to protect navigational rights in these critical waters."\par \par \uc2 Since 2021, Iran has harassed, attacked, or seized nearly 20 internationally flagged merchant vessels. In May, the United States increased the rotation of ships and aircraft patrolling the Strait of Hormuz, Charles, with partners following an uptick in Iranian merchant vessel seizures -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 So how big of a worry is this act by Iran? And how important is it to protect this crucial waterway?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss, oil expert \uc2 Phil Flynn\uc2  , also former Deputy Undersecretary of Defense Lieutenant General Jerry Boykin.\par \par \uc2 Let me start on the economic side with you, Phil.\par \par \uc2 Oil up 3.3 percent, starting to make a big move. That's West Texas Intermediate. And we know in the past how geopolitical risk can add a whole lot of money to the price of crude.\par \par \uc2 PHIL FLYNN, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: It sure can, and now maybe more than it has in the past.\par \par \uc2 And the reason why I say that, Charles, if you look at what we have done here in the United States over the last couple of years, we have tried to control oil prices by releasing oil from the Strategic Reserve. So our supplies are at the lowest level they have been since 1983. They're down almost 200 million barrels from the high.\par \par \uc2 And we really released that oil before we had a real emergency. And I don't know what's going to happen in the Strait of Hormuz, but I will tell you this. It is the world's most important oil choke point. And if that ever gets shut down, we're talking about 22 percent of the daily petroleum supplies that go from place to place could be taken off the market.\par \par \uc2 And if that happens and you have no oil to replace it, it could set the stage for a major price spike, and really the danger of an economic Shock is high.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: General Boykin, the part of Jennifer's report that really stood out to me are the bullet holes. They actually fired into this ship, oil tanker, where the crew slept.\par \par \uc2 At what point does this sort of provocation become not necessarily an act of war, but something that demands even further action?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. \uc2 JERRY BOYKIN\uc2  (RET.), \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2  : Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, Charles, thanks for having me on, first of all.\par \par \uc2 And as was said earlier, this is not new. There was one on the 5th of June that the Brits were actually there alongside the Americans and they wound up launching a helicopter. So what I see in this whole thing is the fact that we have not shown strength to the Iranians.\par \par \uc2 As a matter of fact, when we reentered the JCPOA and then began to lift sanctions, all that did was send a message to the Iranians that America is weak. And it's -- and that has been a major issue with regards to their thinking about how far they can go with the Americans in this kind of behavior.\par \par \uc2 And I think that we're at the point now where, if they do something like what we have seen them do before, which was they ran some of their patrol boats up very close to our ships, and -- but we never really took any action. And that was before \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  's presidency.\par \par \uc2 So I think it's time now. And everybody says, well, World War III. Well, it's going to happen if we don't take action, if we don't show some strength.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: General Boykin, let me go back to you again, because you mentioned the British, and I'm thinking about the HMS Cornwall, 2007, all those U.K. soldiers who were -- navy personnel were arrested, the humiliation of the great British empire, which once had the greatest navy in the world.\par \par \uc2 How much of this is just to humiliate the West and remind us again that maybe we do have feckless leadership?\par \par \uc2 BOYKIN: Well, that incident was one of the most embarrassing things that I have seen. And I spent 36 years in the Army, and that's one of the most humiliating things I have ever seen.\par \par \uc2 And you can go back to some of the POWs that came out of Vietnam and look at some of the things that they did when they were requested or demanded that they actually gave propaganda tapes and that type of thing.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 BOYKIN: And instead of doing that, what they did is they stood up and said, I support my country.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And I think that this generation, we have got to look at ourselves very closely and analyze why we have a generation today that doesn't understand the rules of warfare. And they -- and everything that you saw those men in that -- in those two patrol boats doing was against the code of conduct, which is how you comport yourself when you have been captured.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 All right, gentlemen, we got to leave it there. Phil, General Boykin, thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to Israel now. After two anti-government protests, there's a clash with the police at the main airport in Tel Aviv on Monday. Now folks are taking to the streets yet again today. What is this all about?\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst is in Tel Aviv at the very latest -- Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Charles, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Protesters have taken to the streets of Tel Aviv, Jerusalem (INAUDIBLE) and cities across Israel after a police commander in Tel Aviv was fired earlier today. He was forced to resign, and he says it was for political reasons.\par \par \uc2 These demonstrations have been ongoing for more than 25 weeks. And I want to show you the scene and tell you a little bit about why this began. There has been ongoing frustration from the Israeli citizens about decisions by their government to push forward judicial reforms that would change Israel's democracy forever.\par \par \uc2 Israel does not have a constitution. It relies on a system of checks and balances in order (INAUDIBLE) and the judicial reform would destroy that system. It would give the ruling party, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  , the prime minister's party, the Likud Party, the ability to appoint their judges. And then it would strip the Supreme Court of its ability to challenge the legislative (INAUDIBLE) in Israel.\par \par \uc2 And you can see that these protesters are willing to risk injury in order to push back against these proposal -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Always in the thick of it. That's really an intense situation there.\par \par \uc2 Hey, folks, did you hear about this? A crippling strike at UPS may be on after labor negotiations were called off. What would that mean for your package deliveries and, of course, the overall economy?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Online shoppers watching this one closely. Shares of UPS falling today, with a potential driver strike getting closer. Lydia Hu has more on the standoff after that.\par \par \uc2 Also, where's Governor Newsom? We will tell you.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, shares of UPS falling today with a potential driver strike getting very close.\par \par \uc2 Lydia Hu has more on that standoff after unions called off talks -- Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, hey there, Charles.\par \par \uc2 UPS and the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  are accusing the other of walking away from negotiations over a new contract. The current labor agreement for roughly 340,000 UPS workers expires at the end of the month, and UPS workers have already authorized a strike.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  saying this morning -- quote -- "Following marathon negotiations, UPS refused to give the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  a last, best and final offer, telling the union the company had nothing more to give."\par \par \uc2 Now, Charles, the union's negotiating committee unanimously rejected a package offered by UPS. Meanwhile, UPS says -- quote -- "The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have stopped negotiating despite historic proposals that build on our industry- leading pay. We have nearly a month left to negotiate. We have not walked away and the union has a responsibility to remain at the table."\par \par \uc2 Now, Charles, if a strike happens, it would be the first since 1997 for UPS, when workers went on strike for 15 days. It cost the company $850 million and sent some customers to rivals. A strike now would put millions of daily deliveries at risk. The Wall Street Journal reports that the UPS- Teamsters contract is the largest collective bargaining agreement involving a private employer in North America.\par \par \uc2 A strike would impact supply chains for many companies, and, as you mentioned, Charles, shares of UPS falling today more than 2 percent on this news.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 So, what would a strike mean for UPS, the customers and for our economy?\par \par \uc2 I'm going to bring in retail watcher Hitha Herzog.\par \par \uc2 Hitha, we really brace for these things, because that's the lifeline. And it might give us some insight also to what's going on with the consumer. How do you see it playing out?\par \par \uc2 HITHA HERZOG, RETAIL WATCHER: Charles, when the pandemic happened, there was a real bifurcation in the way consumers spent.\par \par \uc2 And there was pre-pandemic, where everyone went to the store, for the most part, got some stuff online. Then there was midst of a pandemic, where most of us shopped -- so what we are seeing now is the aftermath.\par \par \uc2 So, when you have a strike that's pending, this could really create a major supply chain issue. Think when we were having those supply chain gluts a year ago, a year-and-a-half ago. Think of that, maybe not as severe, but that type of version 2.0.\par \par \uc2 So you're definitely going to see supply chain shocks because of this.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: What about the consumer in general? Everyone's kind of confused right now what's going on, all the excess savings starting to dwindle a little bit, bifurcation between wealthy households and not-so-wealthy households.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  still hammering this economy. It's a big question mark for everyone.\par \par \uc2 HERZOG: Yes, the consumer certainly is starting to pull back, because we don't really know what is going to happen in the next couple of months.\par \par \uc2 But that doesn't curb the way that they still spend their money. They're still going to be online. So when you start seeing the consumer spend a little less and then you start seeing a pending strike happen with UPS, those two may not bode well for earnings for UPS.\par \par \uc2 Already, I think the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  , their contracts, their incomes account for about $30 billion in spending on UPS alone. And about 70 percent of employees at UPS are part of \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  . So this is a major thing, not just for earnings, but if you look at the company when it comes to earnings, when you start seeing the consumer pull back a little bit, spending a little less, maybe not buying so many things online, and then you have the strike, this could be really bad for the company.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Hitha, thank you very, very much. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right now, folks, to the crime crisis that's plaguing America's cities, violence upending July 4 celebrations across the country.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie is in New York right now with more on that -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Charles. It's absolutely horrific.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of families nationwide are grieving today, making funeral arrangements while Democrats are lashing out at Republicans, as the blame game continues and the battle heats up over gun reform.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you what the White House thinks about all of this coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Nine people, including two kids, were shot and wounded in D.C. late last night.\par \par \uc2 It's the latest incident in a nationwide wave of violence over the holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Aishah Hasnie is in New York with more -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 That was actually the result of a drive-by shooting in the nation's capital. And the mayor there, Muriel Bowser, basically telling reporters today they have a problem with too many guns and too many violent people out on the streets there, this as the White House today doubling down on their calls for gun reform, calling on Republicans in Congress to do something. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president continues to call on Republican lawmakers in Congress to come to the table and ban assault weapons and high-capacity magazines, to require safe storage of guns, to end gun manufacturers' immunity from liability, and to enact universal background checks.\par \par \uc2 Lives are at stake here, folks. Lives are at stake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Meanwhile, get this.\par \par \uc2 In Philadelphia, the progressive DA is outright blaming Republicans for a mass shooting that killed five people and injured five more, including twin 2-year-old boys, this despite the fact that the suspect there, 40-year-old Kimbrady Carriker, had a past gun conviction, and he was also carrying illegally.\par \par \uc2 Here's DA Krasner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARRY KRASNER\uc2  , PHILADELPHIA DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Pennsylvania's gun regulation is crap. It is time for a bunch of legislators who wear AR-15 lapel pins, it's time for them to quit or to get voted out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Charles, Baltimore is also reeling today. Two people are dead there, 28 people injured after shots rang out at a block party.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic governor, Wes Moore, a lack of solutions while he's facing his own criticism for taking two days to visit the crime scene. And down in Shreveport, Louisiana, somebody opened fire at a holiday gathering there last night, killing three, injuring six more, and three people are dead. More hurt in Fort Worth, Texas, after somebody fired shots into a crowd at a Fourth of July celebration.\par \par \uc2 Again, more and more calls for gun reform. The president also calling for this as the nation just reels from all of this violence -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Aishah Hasnie in New York City, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, what is driving the crisis?\par \par \uc2 I want to bring a former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Detective Pat Brosnan to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Pat, let me start with the Philadelphia DA, because I watched that press conference yesterday, and he quickly pivoted from the people who pulled the trigger and insinuated that members of the \uc2 NRA\uc2  , folks who own guns legally, go to the range occasionally, maybe hunt occasionally, never committed a crime in their lives, that somehow they abated the situation that happened in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK BROSNAN, FORMER NYPD DETECTIVE: Titanically unaware, titanically. I saw it.\par \par \uc2 That was a very convenient narrative for them. But they're ignoring the central focus. The bottom line, Charles, the essence behind this shooting is the fact that, in America, crime is no longer illegal. You heard it right. Crime is not illegal.\par \par \uc2 There's rogue prosecutors. There's compassionate release. There's defunding of the police. There's desecrated police departments. And on top of it all, they turn to \uc2 NRA\uc2  -lapel wearing pins, when the reality is there's 300 million guns, handguns, in America today. They're in mailboxes, they're in purses, they're in safes, they're in women's pocketbooks, they're in holsters.\par \par \uc2 Unless you devise a weapon, some insanely operational technology that somehow eradicates the weapons and somehow makes them not function anymore, you have 300 million guns to deal with today.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: That means not another gun is made between today and the rest of time, you still have 300 million.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. And, Pat, though...\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: And those triggers are being pulled all the time.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk about who's pulling the triggers, right?\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: What we need, instead of rhetoric..\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: ... is handcuffs. Correct.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I just -- I want to talk about that. Who's pulling the trigger and why there's so little criticism of the people who are committing these crimes, because, when they're not criticized, when they don't go to jail, you actually embolden more of the same.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: It's the height of madness.\par \par \uc2 It's the absolute height of madness. Everyone is blamed, with the exception of the felons who are pulling the trigger and murdering, in this last weekend, 17 mass shootings, 18 dead, 102 wounded. This is absolutely unbelievable. Crime must become illegal again.\par \par \uc2 A law that's not enforced is not a law. It's just some words on a piece of paper, just some narrative.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: The fact is, we no longer need more baseless rhetoric. We need handcuffs. And we don't need rogue prosecutors. Lock them up. Give them stiff sentences. There you have it, my 2 cents.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Yes. No, listen. I think the only time the media seem to care is if there's a white supremacy narrative, and that's the only time the shooter gets blamed and shamed. Other than that, it's the gun's fault. It's someone else's fault. We can't keep going this way, because you and I both lived through the '70s. And we know it could get a whole lot worse than it is right now. We pray it doesn't.\par \par \uc2 Pat, always appreciate your wisdom. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: My pleasure. Thank you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, we got some new numbers on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  's fund- raising, and it could be telling.\par \par \uc2 Plus, why California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  's campaign stops for Biden have some buzzing about a run of his own.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Biden campaign surrogate and California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  making a stop in deep red Idaho this weekend. But is he out batting for Biden or is he preparing his own bid?\par \par \uc2 With me now, the Hill national politics reporter Julia Manchester.\par \par \uc2 Julia, by the way, a lot of people are saying Newsom out there creating some buzz, but it feels like he's making himself at least the alternative if he needs to step up.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE HILL: I think you're seeing that, Charles, absolutely, with a lot of concerns being raised about President Biden's age and his mental acuity.\par \par \uc2 But if it's not that, I would also say that Governor Newsom is certainly setting him up -- himself up for a 2028 presidential bid, or it seems that way. Look, it's so interesting looking at 2024 and seeing that Democrats, besides the president, I think a lot of people are asking, who else would they have run?\par \par \uc2 But in 2028, we see that there are potentially a lot of options Democrats have. You have heard Newsom floated, Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  floated, Georgia Senator Raphael Warnock, Pennsylvania Governor Josh Shapiro.\par \par \uc2 They have a much deeper bench. So this could also mean that \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is essentially trying to get out ahead of what could be a crowded field five - - four or five years from now.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, but four or five years is four or five years, right?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: It is.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: One thing I -- one thing you got to give President Obama credit for is that he didn't wait his turn.\par \par \uc2 So let's talk a little bit more about 2024 and the potential. Hey, you know what? You mentioned Governor Whitmer. How likely is it that she might be considering this? Because we had Tudor Dixon earlier, who actually thinks that she might be preparing. Take a listen to what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON (R), FORMER MICHIGAN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: But, right now, we're seeing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  decline, and they don't want a \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  ticket to come out. And so I think there's a chance that they take \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  out at the end and slide \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  and \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  in there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You think a brand-new team at the last minute?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: I guess anything's possible.\par \par \uc2 But every indication I have gotten from Whitmer's team and those around her is that she is preparing to use her political capital to elect Democrats up and down the ballot, not only in Michigan, but across the country.\par \par \uc2 I think, in 2022, when \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  ran against Tudor Dixon, she certainly showed off her political muscles. And we saw not only Whitmer, but Democrats down the ballot in Michigan, which was once very much a swing state, and I would argue still a swing state, but we saw Democrats under Whitmer perform very well in 2024.\par \par \uc2 So from what we have seen, hearing from Whitmer, she wants to sort of, like, spread that political wealth around and help other Democrats run, particularly in Michigan, where they have an open Senate seat coming up.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Of course, it never hurts to have some favors to cash in on later.\par \par \uc2 All right, Julia, real quick, I want to get to this because now we're starting to get those second quarter fund-raising numbers in, and FOX is confirming that the Trump campaign raked in over $35 million from March to June, twice the amount brought in for the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 Impressive stuff. It looks like the donors are confirming what the polls are saying. It's hard to write this stuff off. Everyone's saying, well, it's early on the polls. What about the fact that people are writing checks?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I think the checks tell a huge story, Charles. And this shows that President Trump continues to have the strength of the donors. That being said, though, Republican sources I have been chatting to close to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  or other -- close to other candidates argue that we still are so early, that we still have to get through the debates, that once we get through the debates, we will have a clearer picture of what's happening, but no question President Trump is the clear front-runner.\par \par \uc2 He's the incumbent president. He has the name I.D. This is something else that those fund-raising numbers definitely also suggest. So it's going to be an uphill climb, and that's something we have heard DeSantis allies in recent days say, that it's not impossible for the Florida governor to win, but Trump is going to be difficult to take down.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Certainly.\par \par \uc2 Julia, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, tired of paying all those baggage fees? Well, one airline is betting that you would be willing to rent your clothes on your next trip. That's right, rent. This way, you don't have to pack -- you can pack light or not pack at all.\par \par \uc2 And the big business of tiny homes, the housing trend that's picking up, as more Americans are considering really sizing down.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Sky-high home prices and interest rates also rising. First-time buyers are turning to tiny homes.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Jeff Flock is outside some at Atglen, Pennsylvania -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Charles, I am surrounded by tiny homes. It has really become a craze in this country and actually around the world.\par \par \uc2 And it's got a lot more room to run, they say. Take a look at the number, about $4 billion worth of growth in the industry, the experts say, in the next two, three years.\par \par \uc2 I want to take you inside one of these tiny homes. This is Zook Cabins. There's a lot of people that build them.\par \par \uc2 You have been building them for a long time, Dave Zook. What's driving this now?\par \par \uc2 DAVE ZOOK, ZOOK CABINS: So, a number of things.\par \par \uc2 If you're an owner who has a little bit of land at the mountains or the lake, perfect for that. You want to go there, spend the weekend. If you're an investor and you want to get a good ROI on your dollars spent, you can get one of these and put it somewhere and rent it out by the night at $200, $300, $400 a night.\par \par \uc2 Or if you're an R.V. park owner, great. Perfect product for an R.V. park owner.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Take a look at the numbers. And as we continue to tour this, maybe, Thomas, take a look in the bedroom there.\par \par \uc2 The average -- or, I should say, the median tiny house between $30,000 and $60,000, compared to the average -- or the median regular house, well over $200,000.\par \par \uc2 ZOOK: Yes, for sure.\par \par \uc2 And we have got more of the conventional park models that are on the lower end of those numbers, like in the $50,000 to $60,000 range. And then we got product with lots of glass and modern that can go up to...\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Which is what I'm looking at right here. And this is about 400 square feet, right?\par \par \uc2 ZOOK: That is correct, 400 square feet.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: OK.\par \par \uc2 It's a craze, Charles. Simplify your life. They got everything you could want in here. And if you're looking out over a waterfall or something there, that's not a bad deal. It kind of beats an apartment in New York City, don't you think, sir?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Jeff, I got a couple of old shoe boxes I might turn into tiny homes. Thank you so much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 So would you live in one of these tiny homes?\par \par \uc2 Let's ask New York Post columnist Rikki Schlott, along with "Gutfeld," co- host and "You Can't Take" -- "You Can't Joke About That" author Kat Timpf.\par \par \uc2 Would you live in one of these, Kat?\par \par \uc2 KAT TIMPF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I mean, are there other kinds of places to live in Manhattan?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: It's so tiny. It's so small. I would love...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Without roommates.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Exactly. I would love to. But I don't own a home. I would love to at some point.\par \par \uc2 And when I talk about that, sometimes, people, which I shouldn't read my mentions, but I do sometimes -- they're like, you're doing well. What are you complaining about?\par \par \uc2 Isn't that the point? I don't know what level of money you need to make in New York to be able to purchase a place to live.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: A lot.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Oh, it's crazy. I'm not there.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I think the minimum is like a mil right now. Yes, I think the minimum is a mil.\par \par \uc2 The average rent is $4,000 for a one-bedroom apartment that's smaller than those houses.\par \par \uc2 But, Rikki, you're -- it is a craze. They say it'll be $7 billion by 2029. It's -- I would be uncomfortable with it, but what about you?\par \par \uc2 RIKKI SCHLOTT, THE NEW YORK POST: I mean, it makes perfect sense to me, considering that three in four Gen-Zers say that they'd like to buy a home before 2030.\par \par \uc2 And at no point in the near future will interest rates be going down. And I think a lot of people know that this is a way to get their foot in the door in the housing market and not be burning cash with rent every single year. So I think, unfortunately, that's what a lot of people have to kind of resort to.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Yes, maybe they should start with a tent, then an R.V., and then work your way up, I mean, if you're willing to be extreme about this.\par \par \uc2 All right, let's switch gears for a moment, a whole new meaning to packing light. There's one airline offering to rent close to passengers, so all you got to do is bring a toothbrush.\par \par \uc2 Rikki, you seem practical. Seem like something you would like?\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: Yes, that's not going to work for me.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: Definitely not. I'm definitely too particular in what I want to wear, but I think there might be some test cases. It reminds me a little bit of rent the runway as a concept.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: But I would say I wouldn't even trust the airlines to get my luggage to where I'm going, let alone to pack it for me. So, I'm not sure.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Imagine you get there and your clothes, like, yes, they don't have your size, me, or what do you think, Kat?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: I'm into it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: I find packing to be very stressful, and I'm not very good at it.\par \par \uc2 I will even ask my husband, what do you think? He's like, OK, you wear the same five shirts. That's all you wear. Why are you bringing 15 shirts? And where's your toothbrush?\par \par \uc2 So I think I would go with this if I had the chance and then just complain that I don't like how I look in every photo.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, no, but, Rikki, here's the thing. I mean, at least they're trying to be innovative, right? I mean, everyone -- listen, there's nothing worse than getting somewhere and your luggage doesn't get there.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you got to give them props for that.\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: Yes, but if you can't trust them to get your luggage there, I don't know how you would trust them to pick your luggage for you.\par \par \uc2 And they're also saying that this is for climate change. But if it's only nine garments per person, I mean, truly, how much can you be cutting down on the emissions?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I don't know, Kat. They lost me on the climate change part of it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, listen, if they had my size, if they had a bunch of suits my size, they'd be like, you know what, UPS it, my man, because you're weighing down the plane.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: I think it also might add anxiety to it. What if you spill something on it or something like that?\par \par \uc2 But it would make it a lot less stressful for packing. I get anxious about packing weeks in advance.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, it's just -- listen, I guess it's the world -- the direction we're going in, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, Rikki, we had -- this whole show has been about travel over the weekend, how stressful it is, how tough it is. \uc2 FAA\uc2  blames airlines. Airlines blame \uc2 FAA\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: I mean, I think it's a huge disaster, especially considering how much money we pumped into our airlines to keep them afloat during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And I think we have kind of created a state in which they're really not beholden to the customer experience, and a lot of us are struggling as a result.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 By the way, that started after 9/11, I hate to say it, when they jacked up the packing, how much you pay for bags, canceling your reservations. They make billions off of that. They always said they would take that back. All they do is increase it every time.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: They never do.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: They never do.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Whenever they say they will take it back, they don't.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, you know they won't.\par \par \uc2 All right, Rikki, Kat, thank you both very, very much.\par \par \uc2 And thank you at home for watching. Remember, you can catch me on FOX Business tomorrow and all weekdays at 2:00 p.m. Eastern on "Making Money."\par \par \uc2 The stock market is hot, but not as hot as "THE FIVE," which is next.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230706ej7500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Biden Promises Further Efforts to Provide Student Loan Relief; Supreme Court Blocks Biden Student Debt Relief Program\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Mark Meredith, Shannon Bream \par \uc2 11,756 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Cancel.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Student debt!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: When I say cancel, you say all of it.\par \par \uc2 Cancel.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: All of it!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Cancel.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: All of it!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Student loan relief is legal, and it is a promise from President Biden. We will never, ever, ever, ever stop.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So don't sit here and tell us to pay your bills. I need money to pay my bills.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We didn't come here to talk about the cancellation. We didn't come here to talk about it. We came here to demand it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, demand all they want. The Supreme Court has gone ahead and ruled against the president's loan forgiveness program. A lot of folks not happy about that.\par \par \uc2 But there are options, we're told, to make the payments more affordable or to come up with some other solution. We're expecting momentarily to hear from the president of the United States on that.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 To Shannon Bream, who has had a kind of a busy day, who can tell us exactly what went down today -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, HOST, "FOX NEWS SUNDAY": OK, so let's talk about the student debt relief loan program that the Supreme Court 6-3 said cannot move forward.\par \par \uc2 The chief justice, writing for the majority, had this to say: "We hold that the act allows the secretary," meaning the education secretary in this case, "to waive or modify existing provisions, statutory or regulatory, but not to rewrite that statute from the ground up."\par \par \uc2 And that's what the court found here. They said, essentially, they're waiving away $430 billion in student loans. The executive didn't have the power to do that. Now, dissenting Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  said: "The statute, as it's read, does give the secretary broad authority to relieve a national emergency's effect on borrowers ability to repay their student loans. The secretary did no more than that with his lawfully designated authority."\par \par \uc2 So, clearly, the two sides saw this very differently. The bottom line is that program, which the president had expressed doubts about it being legal, the speaker of the House had said it probably was not legal, today, the court, the Supreme Court, agrees. And it means if you have been in limbo and you have been waiting, you're not going to get it waived away based on what the president says he's going to do to try to help borrowers.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Hey, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Let's see what the president is going to do right now, the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: ... in this country who feel disappointed and discouraged or even a little bit angry about the court's decision today on student debt, and I must admit I do too.\par \par \uc2 Before I tell you the steps we're going to take, I want to talk about what we have been able -- I want to talk about what we have been able to achieve so far on student loan over the past few years. First, we made the largest increase in Pell Grants in over a decade, helping students from families who nearly all make less than $60,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 Then we fixed the so-called, with the help of the department, Public Service Loan Forgiveness Program, so that borrowers who the got into public service, such as schoolteachers, police officers, social workers, service members, they actually got the debt they were entitled to under the law.\par \par \uc2 Before I came to office, only 7,000 people had benefited from that program. Today, over 600,000 borrowers have received relief from that program. And it is still available. So many people more -- so many more people could be helped. And I encourage you to apply if you haven't already. You are still eligible. Go to studentaid.gov. It matters.\par \par \uc2 Third, my administration approved a program from the Obama/Biden administration on income-driven repayment plan. That's what it was referred to as. Back then, we set a limit. Student borrowers would pay no more than 10 percent of their disposable income to pay back their debt at any one time.\par \par \uc2 My administration is going to reduce that to 5 percent. It is now the most generous repayment program ever. No one with an undergraduate loan today or in the future, whether from a community college or a four-year college, will have to pay more than 5 percent of disposable income to repay their loan.\par \par \uc2 And that is income after you pay for the necessities like housing, food, and the like. A typical borrower is going to save about $1,000 a year. And if you keep up payments for 20 years without missing them, your total debt is forgiven after 20 years. That is what the program was before, but we just reduced it to 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 In addition to that, last year, I announced my student debt relief plan, a plan that was on the verge of providing more than 40 million Americans with real debt relief up to $10,000 for many borrowers and up to $20,000 for those who had gotten a Pell Grant.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 90 percent of their relief would have gone to borrowers, 90 percent of it, making less than $75,000 a year, and no one, no one making over $125,000 a year would qualify. This program was all set to begin. The Web site had been set up, applications had been simplified, so that it took less than five minutes to complete.\par \par \uc2 Notices had been sent out to people about the relief they were eligible for; 16 million people, 16 million people had already been approved. The money was literally about to go out the door. And then Republican elected officials and special interests stepped in. They said no, no, literally snatching from the hands of millions of Americans thousands of dollars in student debt relief that was about to change their lives.\par \par \uc2 These Republican officials just couldn't bear the thought of providing relief for working-class, middle-class Americans. Republican state officials sued my administration, attempting to block relief, including millions of their own constituents. Republicans in Congress voted to overturn the plan, I think every one.\par \par \uc2 I don't think I had any Republican votes for this plan. At the same time, think about this. They all supported the Paycheck Protection Program. Remember PPP, which was designed to help business owners who lost money because of the pandemic? It was a worthy program. But let's be clear.\par \par \uc2 Some of the same elected Republican, members of Congress who strongly opposed giving relief to students, got hundreds of thousands of dollars themselves in relief, members of Congress, because of the businesses that they were able to keep open. Several members of Congress got over $1 million.\par \par \uc2 All those loans were forgiven. Do you know how much that program cost? Seven hundred and sixty billion dollars. My program is too expensive? -- $360 billion more than I proposed in my student debt relief program. I was trying to provide students with $10,000 to $20,000 in relief.\par \par \uc2 By comparison, the average amount for given in the PPP, the pandemic loan program, average amount for given was $70,000. Now a kid making 60,000 bucks, trying to pay back his bills, asking for $10,000 in relief? Come on. The hypocrisy is stunning.\par \par \uc2 You can't help a family making 75 grand a year, but you can help a millionaire, and you have your debt forgiven? My plan would not only have been life-changing for millions of Americans. It would have been good for the American economy, freeing millions of Americans from the crushing burden of student debt.\par \par \uc2 More homes would have been bought. More businesses would have been started. More couples would have had the confidence to start a family. Millions of people would have felt they could get on with their lives. These Republicans blocked all that.\par \par \uc2 Now, in addition to the hypocrisy, some of these Republican Congress are shamelessly pushing to advance a bill in the coming weeks that gives hundreds of billions of dollars in tax breaks and handouts to the wealthiest Americans. They still haven't given up on making permanent the $2 trillion tax cut that they never paid for, never paid for, $2 trillion.\par \par \uc2 So let me be clear. Republicans in Congress, it's not about reducing the deficit. It's not about fairness in forgiving loans. It's only about forgiving loans they have to pay. Today, the Supreme Court sided with them.\par \par \uc2 I believe the court's decision to strike down my student debt relief program is a mistake, was wrong. I'm not going to stop fighting to deliver borrowers what they need, particularly those at the bottom end of the economic scale.\par \par \uc2 So, we need to find a new way, and we're moving as fast as we can. First, I'm announcing today a new path consistent with today's ruling to provide student debt relief to as many borrowers as possible as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 We will ground this new approach in a different law than my original plan, the so-called Higher Education Act. That will allow Secretary Cardona, who's with me today, to compromise, waive, or release loans under certain circumstances.\par \par \uc2 This new path is legally sound. It's going to take longer, but, in my view, it's the best path that remains to providing for as many borrowers as possible with debt relief. I have directed my team to move as quickly as possible under the law.\par \par \uc2 Just moments ago, Secretary Cardona took the first step to initiate that new approach. We're not going to waste any time on this. We're getting moving on it. It's going to take longer, but we're getting at it right away.\par \par \uc2 Second, we know what many borrowers will need to make their hard choices, which their budgets are being strained now when they start to repay their monthly loan payments this fall. We know that figuring out how to pay these added expenses can take time for borrowers, and they might miss payments at the front end as they get back into repayment.\par \par \uc2 Normally, this could lead borrowers to fall into delinquency and default, but without their financial security -- it would hurt their financial security. And that's not good for them or the economy. That's why we're creating a temporary 12-month what we're calling on-ramp repayment program.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is not the same as the student loan pause that has been in effect for the past three years. Monthly payments will be due, bills will not go out, and interest will be accruing. And during this period, if you can pay your monthly bills, you should. But if you cannot, if you miss payments, this on-ramp will temporarily remove the threat of default or having your credit harmed, which can hurt borrowers for years to come because the Department of Education won't refer borrowers.\par \par \uc2 And the reason why that will work, they won't refer borrowers who miss payments to credit agencies for 12 months to give them a chance to get back up and running.\par \par \uc2 Let me close with this. Our Republican officials say student loan relief is a giveaway to the privileged. You hear that loud now, the privileged. I love their concern for the privileged.\par \par \uc2 But I know who student loan borrowers are in this country. So do all you. It's a couple putting off having a child until they can find their way to deal with their debt. That's who they are. They're young, putting off buying their first home until they get out from under student loans.\par \par \uc2 Hope on the horizon, thanks to relief that I planned last year, today's court decision snatches it away from them. I get it. I get it. I hear this. It's -- and I'm concerned about it. But today's decision has closed one path. Now we're going to pursue another.\par \par \uc2 I'm never going to stop fighting for you. We will use every tool at our disposal to get you the student debt relief you need and reach your dreams. It's good for the economy. It's good for the country. And it's going to be good for you.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very, very much for listening. We're going to get this done, God willing. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Mr. President, why did you give millions of borrowers false hope? You dated -- doubted your own authority here in the past.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I didn't give any false hope. The question was whether or not I would do even more than was requested. What I did I thought was appropriate and was able to be done and would get done.\par \par \uc2 I didn't give borrowers false hope. But the Republicans snatched away the hope that they were given. And it's real, real hope.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Did you overstep your authority?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think the court misinterpreted the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Mr. President (INAUDIBLE) failure in Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 Mistake (INAUDIBLE) Afghanistan withdrawal, saying there was failure (INAUDIBLE) There was mistakes doing during the withdrawal and before?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: (OFF-MIKE) No, no, all the evidence is coming back there. Remember what I said about Afghanistan? I said Al Qaeda would not be there. I said it wouldn't be there.\par \par \uc2 I said we would get help from the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . What's happening now? What's going on? Read your press. I was right.\par \par \uc2 Thanks.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: So, the report is from the State Department, actually, about the withdrawal.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, a couple of things before I expand on this.\par \par \uc2 The president saying he is going to continue his effort to find relief for those who are shackled right now with student loan debt, to the tune of about $400 billion. The Supreme Court shot that down today in a 6-3 vote that he couldn't do it, that he was going to the Constitution and using something called the HEROES Act of 2003 that provides grants to college kids and the like to sort of wedge this move in, so that student debt could be forgiven.\par \par \uc2 But no less than \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  herself said, a president can't do that. It's up to Congress to do that. So, while the president was blaming Republicans for this sort of falling apart on him, it really started with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , and it was her arguments on the House floor that was used in the majority opinion to shoot this down.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, I just want to explain a couple of things. The president is talking right now about rejiggering this, so that student debt relief can still go forward, working through his education secretary, \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2 . Now, that plan will work through the programs that we already have, a student debt relief program we already have.\par \par \uc2 But some say it's sort of like moving a shell game here when it comes to what programs are available and what you can use. The fact of the matter is that the president constitutionally did overstep his bounds. You don't have to be on the right or left about this. And a lot of his people knew that it was a Hail Mary pass and it just didn't pan out.\par \par \uc2 But this was not a Republican pile-on here. This was a big concern on the part of many who said, what you do for some, you're not doing for all and loans that you're forgiving for some don't recognize those who don't go to college at all and don't get any help, those who are working and necessarily do not get relief to buy, let's say, a tractor on a farm or equipment that they need in a profession, if they're a machinist or electricians, that they were being sidestepped by kids who were falling behind on their debt and getting stuck with the bill.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, it failed, and failed miserably. And now the issue is, where do we go from here?\par \par \uc2 Mark Meredith right now at the White House with more on that side of the story.\par \par \uc2 Mark, what are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Well, I think the most notable thing to come out of the president's comments just a few moments ago is, what is the next steps for this White House and for those Americans that are wondering about their student debt relief?\par \par \uc2 Because the president says the administration is going to look to rely on something from 1965, the Higher Education Act, which, through the Department of Education, will start to make exceptions for some student loan relief.\par \par \uc2 But we don't know how that process is going to work. And it sounded like even the president admitted that's going to be a bit complicated. It's not like they're going to be able to say, starting August 1, this is what we're going to do. Instead, it sounds like they're going to have to set up some criteria. The Department of Education will then work through maybe some applications.\par \par \uc2 But all that is likely to be challenged again, as you can imagine. So, maybe this starts all over. And so for the people that are worried about their student loan repayments, those are still looking to kick into effect starting in October. That would be the first bills that be due. Interest would start accruing in September.\par \par \uc2 The other notable thing the president said moments ago is that they are going to use this so-called on-ramp, that the Department of Education is not going to report people who default on their loans or don't make those payments right away starting in October, that they're going to give people up to a year to get used to having to make payments again.\par \par \uc2 It almost seems like putting a -- extending this a little bit longer. I don't know if that would be challenged again by the states that have already brought these lawsuits. And the other thing that was also notable is that you mentioned, Neil, that the president seemed to really want to pick a fight with congressional Republicans, instead of the Supreme Court, because, if this had gone through Congress and then the president signed into law, it's likely we wouldn't have been here.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the president saying that this is hypocritical. And when he kept pointing out that this happened after the COVID relief money, that was money, of course, Congress had approved and earmarked and that the prior administration had signed into law. So this was a different -- this was done by the executive pen.\par \par \uc2 Republicans came out pretty clearly today ready to say that this president, like you said, may have overstepped his bounds. The Senate minority leader, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , saying this equates -- this is equal to socialism in his book.\par \par \uc2 And I thought polling was kind of interesting. This was the statement from McConnell. He said: "The president of the United States cannot hijack 21- year-old emergency powers to pad the pockets of high-earning base and make suckers out of the working families who chose not to take on student debt."\par \par \uc2 McConnell went on to say: "The court's decision today deals a heavy blow to Democrats' distorted and outsized view of executive power."\par \par \uc2 So, while Republicans are saying that they were simply trying to protect the role that Congress has here, the White House is ready to say that this is battle between the rich and the poor. The other question, Neil, how much of this is going to be an issue in next year's campaign? The polling was already mixed on this, where you had people who were going to benefit were likely much happier with the student loan forgiveness plan.\par \par \uc2 But those Americans who either maybe didn't go to college, they paid down their debt already, they took out private loans, maybe they weren't going to be as impacted by this and didn't feel that need. But the president also speaking to Jacqui Heinrich who was in the room. She was the first one who shouted the question about, did you set people's expectations up too high? Did you fail these people?\par \par \uc2 And he said, no, once again pushing the blame back on congressional Republicans. We will see if the American people feel the same way, that false hope -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Mark, this was going to forgive up to $20,000 worth of college debt for those earning up to $125,000 a year. Now, many when they started parsing through that were thinking, well, wait a minute, that's far from everybody, let alone people who weren't going to college or didn't fit the criteria to get this.\par \par \uc2 So he was angering a lot of people who said that this was unbalanced and unfair and set expectations and beyond just those who wanted their college loans or some of them forgiven to others who might be in training schools, vocational schools, who were not planning.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Yes, you're right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the White House had made it clear that there was going to be a certain group of Americans, but because of the income standards they set, that would benefit from this. And the president, I thought, was also really interested in taking a swipe at the relief that was provided to the businesses through those PPP loans back at the height of the coronavirus pandemic, which, again, Congress authorized, and then the president signed into law, this, again, being different.\par \par \uc2 This was done through the Department of Education, and then it was challenged to the states, but the White House seeing a reason here that they felt like this was going to stick with the middle class, that these middle-class voters were going to be energized by this.\par \par \uc2 And they are still providing these other options here. And I'm really curious what this Higher Education Act plan that the Department of Ed is going to roll out would really look like. Would it be the same number of Americans that would qualify? How quickly would the Education Department get people answers?\par \par \uc2 Because,if these loans are due starting in October, the first payments would be due, would people be basically waiting, oh, I will see what happens, or will they go ahead and make those payments without -- with -- over fear that this could impact their economic situation a little bit further, Neil?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you will just be going from the frying pan into the legal fire here.\par \par \uc2 Mark, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Mark Meredith.\par \par \uc2 I want to go back to Shannon Bream, who has been covering this, a great lawyer, but -- I'm not a lawyer, Shannon, but the one thing I found, his comparison to the Paycheck Protection Program and how we look after people under duress, that was born of a disease that was crippling the entire world.\par \par \uc2 Businesses were entirely shut down. They couldn't open their doors. There's a big difference between that and a student who falls behind his or her bills. And yet the idea that what was justified for them should be justified for these students, it just didn't seem to add up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And critics of that will say, these student loans were something that people entered into, hoping to get something in return. They signed on the dotted line.\par \par \uc2 Many borrowers will say, OK, with the interest and everything else, I ended up in a much bigger hole than I thought I was going to be. But those were done voluntarily. The situation we were in during the pandemic, others will argue,these were emergency lifelines for people who didn't want to find themselves in these situations.\par \par \uc2 I thought Mark made a great point here that the president, if he's going to try to go to Congress for any of this, really seemed to pick a fight. He said that, when he came up with this plan to suspend the student debt and wipe out and forgive some of the loans, that the GOP just couldn't stand the thought of helping the middle class.\par \par \uc2 So we know what the line is going to be. He's going to be out there on the campaign trail saying, this is the MAGA Supreme Court. Look what they have done this week. That's why I need another term.\par \par \uc2 Alternately, former President Trump is out there saying, look what has happened at the Supreme Court this week. You're welcome. I gave you three of these justices.\par \par \uc2 So it is something that clearly is going to be used on the campaign trail. The current president will say: I tried to get you this help. The court stopped me from doing that, but I'm going to find another way.\par \par \uc2 And, again, the court said today, under the HEROES Act, that that's all that's before us, what the secretary did was akin to rewriting the entire statute. It wasn't making a waiver or a modification, in the court's estimation, 6-3. They said the executive branch has gone too far.\par \par \uc2 So, if the branch, the executive branch now, the president tries to find another way to do this that also comports with today's decision, may end up back at the Supreme Court, but that's a long journey. In the meantime, we have got that run for the White House next year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, as I understand it now, he is going to start examining what opportunities he has through the Higher Education Relief Opportunities for Students Act of 2003, and maybe attach to that what he couldn't with the HEROES Act.\par \par \uc2 It just seems like a desperate move that he knows legally he has a little odds of succeeding.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But then he's able to turn around and say, I didn't give the false hope in that interaction there with Jacqui Heinrich, to say, I didn't give the false hope. I was trying to do something. And it's the GOP, MAGA, and the court, these are the people who are taking away the hope and taking this away from you.\par \par \uc2 If he tries a path with the Higher Education Act, that equates to the same thing. The court said something this sweeping and massive, getting rid of a mass debt to the tune of $430 billion, seems to be something that's more in the purview of Congress. So we will see what the take is, the new plan is under this different statute, but the court's reasoning may be the same.\par \par \uc2 If they think this is something that the executive branch doesn't have the power to do, we will have to see how this rolls out. And I would imagine, if it's similar to what the court just struck down, we will immediately see legal challenges to the new plan from the White House.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. And it was \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's argument against a president doing something like this that ended up being the galvanization point for the conservative justices, the six of them who voted to overturn this move.\par \par \uc2 We are waiting.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, by the way, very much, Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 At the White House, Education Secretary \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2  is probably going to expand on this idea to provide another venue to aid college kids and on their debt.\par \par \uc2 John Yoo\uc2 , the former deputy assistant attorney general, \uc2 U.C. Berkeley\uc2  law professor, also served as a law clerk with Justice Thomas, knows of what he speaks.\par \par \uc2 John, if you can explain to me, it just seems like another legal bounce here to go from hanging on a HEROES Act to hanging on something else a student debt relief program that wasn't geared or meant for this. It could meet the same fate, I'm not saying all the way up to the Supreme Court, but legally dicey.\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO\uc2 , FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: Neil, it will meet with the same fate as the case today.\par \par \uc2 You have to ask President Biden's administration, if you have got a good law that allows you to cancel student debt, why'd you waste everybody's time for the last year and not rely on the law in the first place? And the reason why is because, as the court made clear -- and I just want to say, the court is reminding us of very simple truths.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, you and I said that -- yesterday was, the Constitution is colorblind. Today, the simple truth is Congress has the power of the purse. The president doesn't have any constitutional power to spend money unless Congress authorizes it. You notice in President Biden's comments just now he never addressed the constitutional issue. He was making a pitch as if he was asking Congress to pass his debt program.\par \par \uc2 He never mentioned that the Supreme Court doesn't take a position whether a debt payment program is good or bad. All they're there to say is, it's Congress' job to decide whether to spend $400 billion in student debt relief.\par \par \uc2 So I think, if the Biden administration just tries it again with a new statute, the court will come back and say, again, we see no clear law that gives the president that kind of spending authority, and you can't stretch and twist the existing laws to try to get there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  was joined by justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  -- I think that makes up the total six -- that the president had gone a step too far and a legal level too far.\par \par \uc2 Speaking for the conservative majority, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  said that: "The secretary asserts that the HEROES Act grants him this authority to cancel," as you were pointing out, John, this $430 billion in student loan debt. We hold," he says today, "that the act allows the secretary to waive or modify existing statuary -- statutory" -- I apologize -- "or regulatory provisions applicable to financial assistance programs under the Education Act, not to rewrite that statute from the ground up."\par \par \uc2 Isn't what the president is thinking here rewriting a statute within a program that doesn't call for it?\par \par \uc2 YOO: Neil, you're getting really good at reading Supreme Court opinions, because that is the exact phrase that's going to come back and haunt the Biden administration when they try debt cancellation version 2.0, because the court is saying, we see nothing in any law that authorizes the cancellation of student debt.\par \par \uc2 The only thing we see is a law passed in '03, as you pointed out, that says you can modify, waive. And the whole point of that law was really -- I was around in the government at that time -- was, if you got called up for service, you wouldn't have -- and got sent to Afghanistan or Iraq, you wouldn't have to be paying your student debt until you came back.\par \par \uc2 But that statute doesn't say you can just eliminate the debt entirely. It just means you can suspend the payments, which it's very sensible, bipartisan, passed by large majorities.\par \par \uc2 So the Supreme Court essentially said, there's nothing in any of the laws we have seen that says you can cancel $400 billion. Anything that important, economically and socially important, we're going to say, go back to Congress and get a clear statement that you can do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me step back. And the president's reaction is, he wasn't happy. I don't know if he wasn't happy as much as he was with the affirmative action decision yesterday, where he had referred to the court as not a normal court. I think I quote him correctly.\par \par \uc2 Didn't say anything like that on this decision, just that he disagreed strongly with it. But this gets to be a common response and refrain from the White House these days on this obviously conservative court's series of decisions, three of which in the past week went against him.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of how he reacts? This is maybe more I want to get your view as a lawyer and knowing the system as you do. We do have three branches of government. We have to accept what each and the other does, sometimes not happily, but to say, as he did yesterday, that's not a normal court, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 YOO: Yes, I got this new book out this week called "The Politically Incorrect Guide to the Supreme Court."\par \par \uc2 And this is a great pitch for it, because what we do is, we trace the history of attacks on the Supreme Court. In the last two years, you have seen these unprecedented assaults on the institutional independence of the Supreme Court by progressives who are angry with decisions like Dobbs from last year about abortion, or gun rights from last year in Bruen, or now this year's case about affirmative action, and now student loans.\par \par \uc2 You have seen progressives propose trying to pack the court, add more members to the court, trying to cut back on the kind of cases the court's allowed to decide. Now you're having proposals for investigations of justices, trying to make it easier to get them to recuse from cases.\par \par \uc2 We haven't seen this kind of attack on the independence of the court since it decided Brown v. Board, and these kinds of attacks were launched by Southern segregationists, or since the New Deal, when FDR tried these attacks on the court that wanted to stand up for contract and property rights.\par \par \uc2 And so I predict, after these losses these last two years, you're going to see enhanced attacks on the court by progressives.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, to circle back to this -- and we're waiting -- there's going to be a White House briefing that will include the education secretary, \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2 , maybe expounding a little bit on what the White House plans to implement this idea of postponing or forgiving some student loan debt. Don't know where that's going to go.\par \par \uc2 But what was that issue here is the role of a president. He can propose. He can delay repayment. He can freeze payments, and not just by himself, but for acts of war or you're going off to war, I think, as you said, for the serviceman who was heading off to Afghanistan, what have you, but he can't start cutting it, right?\par \par \uc2 YOO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 And I think there's a difference how we should understand presidential power. Presidents have great power in waging war, conducting foreign policy, but when it comes to spending money, domestic programs, it's Congress' job.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it, my friend.\par \par \uc2 John, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 YOO: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: To the White House briefing right now, where they will outline what they plan to do next.\par \par \uc2 (JOINED IN PROGRESS)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: ... on the student debt relief this morning. And, as you all know, you just heard from the president.\par \par \uc2 And so before we get -- we go there, I just want to talk about a few things at the top, and then we will -- I will hand it over to our two guests.\par \par \uc2 First, we are deeply disappointed in the Supreme Court's decision today in 303 Creative which takes our nation backward in the fight for equality. This decision undermines the basic truth that no American should face discrimination for who they are or who they love.\par \par \uc2 And it's even more disappointing as we close out Pride Month today. It chips away at longstanding laws that protect all Americans against discrimination in public accommodations, including people of color, people with disabilities, and people of faith and women.\par \par \uc2 While the court's decision only addresses expressive original designs, as President Biden said today, we are concerned that the decision could invite more discrimination against the LGBTQ+ community.\par \par \uc2 And we know that, when one group's dignity and equality are threatened, the promise of our democracy is threatened, and we all suffer. The administration will remain focused on enforcing federal anti-discrimination protections, and the president will continue to call on Congress to pass the Equality Act.\par \par \uc2 Now, second, as we head into July 4, the Department of Transportation and the \uc2 FAA\uc2  are working closely with airlines to help minimize flight disruptions resulting from extreme weather. Airline operations quickly recovered from the weather earlier this week, except for \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 , which expects to return to normal tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But we will continue to keep a close eye on all of this. And we want to remind our air travelers to visit flightrights.gov -- again, that's flightrights.gov -- to see what you're entitled to if your flight is canceled or delayed.\par \par \uc2 Finally, as you know, we normally do the week ahead on Fridays, but because it's 4:30 on a Friday and going into a holiday weekend, we're going to send the week ahead through a pool note in a couple of hours before the end of the day, for sure.\par \par \uc2 But,before I move on, I want to acknowledge someone in the room who you all know very well and who has been a friendly face to all of you and to all of us. That is Ed Lewis. Ed is retiring today after 25 years as photographer for FOX News and 14 years at \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: In his career -- hi, Ed.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: In his career, Ed has covered every president since \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , right? Not to age you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: You look young, 21.\par \par \uc2 I know he is a friend to many of folks here and certainly many folks on my team. He always has a smile. Every time I see him, he always waves, smiles and gives me a thumbs-up.\par \par \uc2 So I give you a thumbs-up back to you, my friend. And I know many of you have seen him traveling carrying like 13 bags or something like that all at once.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: So, incredibly impressive.\par \par \uc2 So, Ed, thank you for being a joy to work with. I hope you are -- you will enjoy more time at the tee following your last day here. And we will truly, truly miss you. And thank you for just always being a friendly face. We do -- the team decided to get you something. So here you go.\par \par \uc2 Can you step away for a second or do I have to come back? I will come back.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Oh, and you're very welcome. Very excited.\par \par \uc2 OK, so now I'm going to turn it over to Secretary Cardona, who's going to say a few words about the plan that the president announced today after the. Supreme Court decision. And then, of course, Bharat, as you know, is here to take any questions as well.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary...\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL CARDONA\uc2 , U.S. EDUCATION SECRETARY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: ... that's for you.\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 In the last 48 hours, our country has been set back in terms of providing equity and access in higher education. And as you heard from the president, today, the Supreme Court ruled against more than 40 million working families.\par \par \uc2 Let's be clear about who would have benefited from the president's student debt relief plan. Nearly 90 percent of relief would have gone to borrowers making less than $75,000 a year; 20 million Americans would see their debts drop to zero, with 20 million more seeing lower payments.\par \par \uc2 This would mean fewer borrowers falling into delinquency and default when the payment pause ended. We're not talking about the millionaires who benefited from the billions in tax giveaways a few years ago. We're talking about low- and middle-income families recovering from the worst pandemic in a century.\par \par \uc2 I strongly disagree with the court's decision here. So, today I want to assure our students, our borrowers, and families across America our fight is not over. We're taking action.\par \par \uc2 The president, the vice president and I have put borrowers first from day one. We refuse to go back to the way things were before the pandemic, when a million borrowers defaulted each year and faced devastating financial consequences.\par \par \uc2 So let me tell you about the actions we're taking today. Number one, we're going to open up an alternative path to debt relief for as many borrowers as possible as quickly as possible. While we acknowledge the court's decision, we began the negotiated rulemaking process on settlement and compromise authority today.\par \par \uc2 More information on the new regulations will come out shortly. Number two, today, we are rolling out the most affordable income-driven repayment plan ever. This new plan is called Saving on a Valuable Education, SAVE. It'll cut monthly payments in half for all undergraduate loans.\par \par \uc2 It'll cut payments to zero dollars for millions of people making less than $33,000 a year. All other borrowers will save at least $1,000 per year. This plan will stop runaway interest, and it's not going to require you to pay more than you can afford.\par \par \uc2 And, number three, as the president -- as the president mentioned, we're creating an on-ramp for repayment for up to one year for those who need it. This means, although interest will accrue starting September 1, and payments are due starting in October, we will help borrowers who are struggling to make payments avoid harsh financial consequences, such as delinquencies and wage garnishments. Borrowers who can make payments should.\par \par \uc2 Again, I disagree with today's decision from the Supreme Court. Today, the court substituted itself for Congress. It's outrageous to me that Republicans in Congress and state offices fought so hard against a program that would have helped millions of their own constituents.\par \par \uc2 They had no problem handing trillion-dollar tax cuts to big corporations and the super wealthy. And many had no problems accepting millions of dollars in forgiven pandemic loans. Like, Senator \uc2 Markwayne Mullin\uc2  from Oklahoma had more than $1.4 million in pandemic loans forgiven. He represents 489,000 eligible borrowers that were turned down today.\par \par \uc2 Representative Brett Guthrie from Kentucky had more than $4.4 million forgiven. He represents more than 90,000 eligible borrowers who were turned down today. Representative \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  from Georgia had more than $180,000 forgiven. She represents more than 91,800 eligible borrowers who were turned down today.\par \par \uc2 I want all the students and borrowers to know President Biden, Vice President Harris and I will not stop fighting for you. You should be able to earn a college education without student debt blocking you from opportunity. That's why we're fixing a broken system.\par \par \uc2 We're protecting the next generation by increasing college accountability, fixing public service loan forgiveness, and going after predatory college, in addition to increasing Pell Grants. To date, we have approved more than $66 billion in targeted loan forgiveness. And we're not going to stop fighting.\par \par \uc2 We cannot allow the setbacks of yesterday and today to stop us from reimagining a better tomorrow, one in which all students from different income levels and racial backgrounds have access to affordable pathways to college and career.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Could you give us any details about how you plan to apply the HEROES Act -- I'm sorry -- the Higher Education Act?\par \par \uc2 It seems like, if the HEROES Act was on legally spurious footing, this might be even more so. And if you're more secure that this is a legal route to do it, why didn't you just use this in the first place?\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: We believe the Supreme Court got it wrong today. We believe the HEROES Act does give me the authority. The Supreme Court made their decision today. We accept that.\par \par \uc2 The Higher Education Act has a pathway, and we're going to use that pathway.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: But if you think that's a better way to do it, why didn't you just use that in the first place?\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: We believed that the HEROES Act pathway was quicker, and we had the authority to do that.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: The president said that this new path would take some time, that it will take longer. How long are you expecting it to take? And you also mentioned that more details will be rolled out. When will that be happening?\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: Sure.\par \par \uc2 So it is a regulatory process, and I will turn it over to my colleague Bharat in a second. But that process does take longer, as the president said, which is why we started with the HEROES Act.\par \par \uc2 We believed we had the authority and we were ready to act, and it was stopped through a lawsuit right when we were going to provide the debt relief. Do you want to comment on that?\par \par \uc2 BHARAT RAMAMURTI\uc2 , DEPUTY DIRECTOR, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL: Sure.\par \par \uc2 So, the Higher Education Act rulemaking goes through what's called the negotiated rulemaking process. That's actually different than your typical rulemaking process. As you all know, even a typical rulemaking process can take some amount of time. You have to do a proposal. It has to receive comments.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I don't want to get into the weeds here, nor do I want to risk this falling into the weeds here, but what they're quoting is this sort of take two on dealing with the student debt relief, the one that was struck down by the Supreme Court in a 6-3 vote earlier today, moving on from using the HEROES Act to sort of wedge that through, force that through, justification for providing this relief, to using something called the Higher Education Act, which might take longer.\par \par \uc2 But to hear the education secretary say, it would be an easier approval process and one that wouldn't run into these supposed constitutional speed bumps.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if that is the case. I'm betting \uc2 John Yoo\uc2  has some ideas, back with us, the former deputy assistant attorney general,\par \par \uc2 John Yoo\uc2 , they're going from one program or edict, if you will, to another to give this a shelf life. But, in the meantime, they're going to spread this out a bit. That itself spawn some legal challenges. But what do you think?\par \par \uc2 YOO: Good point.\par \par \uc2 I think there are several problems here. One is, this might have to go through what's called a rulemaking process, which may put this well into past the elections next year. They may not be able to get it done in time.\par \par \uc2 Two, from what Secretary Cardona said, it sounded to me like what the main emphasis is going to be is not cancellation of student debt, but allowing more generous repayment terms. For example, he mentioned, if you have income that's not very -- not enough to cover a certain percentage of the loans, maybe the payments can be stretched out, but it's not cancellation, as far as I could tell.\par \par \uc2 And that might be an important point, Neil, because what the Supreme Court said is that you don't have the power to cancel. The federal government gave out about $400 billion in loans and expects to be paid back. That's a decision of Congress. Congress has passed other statutes which do make it somewhat easier to pay back loans, but that's not to the point of cancellation.\par \par \uc2 The court said that's still Congress' decision, especially when so much money is at stake.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Could I go back -- and I was raising this with Shannon Bream -- this idea of comparing the PPP program, the Paycheck Protection Program that came about because of COVID, and it shutting essentially the world down, and all commerce down, and all businesses down that could have gone completely out of business had it not been for a means, a federal means, by which to keep them going, to keep their workers employed.\par \par \uc2 That was force majeure, or something unexpected that changed the world and seemed to be a good justification, not that many people are keen on government stepping in, but that's a good excuse and a justification for it.\par \par \uc2 In this case, students and those who took out these loans went in knowingly doing so, without any impending disaster like what we saw. It just didn't seem like a valid comparison, especially when the president was saying, what we did for these businesses, we can't turn around and not do for these kids. It just seemed goofy to me.\par \par \uc2 YOO: Let the record show I'm never going to disagree with Shannon Bream. She knows what she's talking about.\par \par \uc2 But I think there's some important differences here. First, it seems illogical to me to say, oh, since the Paycheck Protection Program was so sloppy and badly done, we should make student loans as sloppy and badly done too. That's just wrong.\par \par \uc2 Does that mean every federal spending program should be sloppy and badly done, so it matches PPP? But the more important, point, Neil, is -- you put your finger on it -- PPP was done during the middle of the COVID emergency, when we were trying to keep people alive because the economy was completely shut down throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 And so, of course, there are going to be mistakes. It was an emergency. And so, when we have an emergency, sometimes, it's more important to act quickly than to act perfectly. But, as you say, Neil, student loans don't have that feature.\par \par \uc2 There's no emergency during the time students take out the loans. They're - - actually, students, I found at Berkeley, are very rational about their finances and think hard about -- they're constantly changing credit cards to get the 1 percent better interest rate. I'm sure they think hard about the student debt they take on and whether it's worth it.\par \par \uc2 And so I don't think you have the same conditions, the same emergency they have with the Paycheck Protection Program. But, even if so, that still brings you back to the constitutional issue, is, those arguments, according to the Supreme Court, should be made to Congress.\par \par \uc2 If that's the case, if it's unfair to give businesses this out and not students, then make Congress take a vote. They're accountable in elections. But the president doesn't have the power or the purse. Again, a simple, clear truth, just like the one about the colorblind Constitution yesterday, today is, Congress makes all the spending decisions in our country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, you could also argue, for students who go, why were we robbed of this aid and looking at this relief, trillions of dollars of a relief that we provided after COVID, just the money that was lost or misappropriated or stolen or grabbed, whatever, it went into the hundreds of billions of dollars, which would have been the cost of this program, so students come back and say, we have wasted more than we're due here, so what gives?\par \par \uc2 How do you legally answer that?\par \par \uc2 YOO: I think -- I feel bad for students. I mean, they make a claim to be treated fairly.\par \par \uc2 Now, they haven't been paying any student loans since the COVID outbreak, so they have already gotten a benefit, until the Supreme Court decision today. But,again, these are the decisions you make to Congress, right?\par \par \uc2 Congress is the one that's in charge of taxes. They decide what to spend money on. And this is just another appeal that they make.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, John, I'm going to interrupt you. Our Jacqui Heinrich is addressing, I think, this issue.\par \par \uc2 Let's go back to the conference.\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: So, I will let Bharat answer that about the Bidenomics and the long-term impact.\par \par \uc2 But we're going to fight. We're going to keep fighting. And there were many other legal scholars that believed we had the authority under the HEROES Act. I mean, the HEROES Act makes it pretty clear I have the authority to modify or waive. The Supreme Court saw it differently. We're moving forward in another path and we're going to put the best legal argument forward to provide relief for borrowers.\par \par \uc2 That's our focus, and we're not going to stay off of that.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Do you think, though, that this court will see eye to eye with you? It's going to be the same court if challenged legally.\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: Right.\par \par \uc2 We're going to keep fighting, and we're going to put the best legal argument forward to stand up for borrowers and to keep fighting for affordable college.\par \par \uc2 Did you want to talk about the bigger plan (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 RAMAMURTI: Sure.\par \par \uc2 Well, I just want to add one thing on that question. It's -- we're just beginning the rulemaking process now. There's going to be time to craft the exact proposal under this new legal authority. One thing, of course, that we will keep in mind is how this plan is going to be received by the courts.\par \par \uc2 But what the president believes is that, if his lawyers, who are all highly qualified attorneys, tell him that a path is legally available, he's going to trust them, and we're going to put our best foot forward in court to defend it.\par \par \uc2 What I would say to borrowers is that we are taking action on multiple fronts to help them, in light of the Supreme Court opinion today. There's the 12-month on-ramp to repayment, which is going to be a real benefit to borrowers who may be having trouble getting started again on their repayment months, and who may have to skip a payment or two, because they need to get their financial situation together.\par \par \uc2 Previously, they may have been put into delinquency or default or seen a negative mark on their credit report. We're going to make sure that doesn't happen for the first 12 months. So, that's a real benefit to those borrowers.\par \par \uc2 And, as the secretary said, and I don't want people sleeping on this, this income-based repayment plan that we're rolling out today that was finalized is a huge deal. It is going to save the typical borrower $1,000 a year on their loan repayments. Somebody who is making $50,000, $60,000 a year is going to see a substantial savings and may even end up paying zero dollars in their monthly payment because of these -- this new program.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, one concern that people have had about the income-based repayment program in the past is that, if you are making these smaller payments, your balance would keep growing over time. One of the reforms we're putting in place is making sure that balance doesn't grow over time, even as you are making potentially a zero payment each month.\par \par \uc2 It is a big deal for both current borrowers and for all the future borrowers who are out there. Now, I want to be clear, that's not a substitute for debt relief, but it is a huge benefit for borrowers that is going to available coming this summer. And we encourage people to sign up for it.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: You talked about the timeline on this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. You have been listening to deputy director of the National Economic Council, \uc2 Bharat Ramamurti\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I was trying to figure out how it is you can extend payments or pay on some occasions nothing at all, and not eventually have to pay for that. I don't know what new math they might be using. Maybe it just escapes me, but he seemed to be sending the message that this relief that will be built in to help future borrowers and existing borrowers where they could even find a month where they're not paying anything or they save $1,000 a year, I can't tell whether that's a zero percent loan program.\par \par \uc2 Doesn't strike me as that. Or a low-rate program., period, that would mean they'd never be upside-down on those payments, that, if you skip a month or two months or you're paying less, that your overall debt isn't going up. Presumably, there is some rate attached to that debt which would add to that debt.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes,you have heard of these programs even when you buy a car, and they will extend it out over five years or seven years. Your interest costs are going to be substantially more than if you paid back sooner and stuck to the original terms of the loan. But, again, I don't know the math they used in here to come up with what could be a great miracle if they could pull that off.\par \par \uc2 But, again, I could be missing that.\par \par \uc2 I do want to look at the political implications of all of this, because the administration is clearing -- clearly saying now to student borrowers, future borrowers, we are with you. We are on your side. We know you got a big disappointment today.\par \par \uc2 We know that there are millions of you who were counting on this relief materializing, this $400 billion relief that we were looking, actually works at about four to $432 billion, and that this would be money well spent and money that was coming your way, up to $20,000 for a typical borrower, as long as he or she doesn't make more than $125,000.\par \par \uc2 Now that is not happening. So they're blaming Republicans. Republicans are saying it was an ill-conceived program from the start and often quoted \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  as the reason it should go nowhere, she, of course, the former speaker who had said years ago it's not up to the president to start cutting monies, that only Congress can decide.\par \par \uc2 Bob Cusack, the Hill editor in chief, is this a 2024 issue, as you see it being laid out here? The administration is not going to let this die quietly. They think they have a good issue here. Do they?\par \par \uc2 BOB CUSACK, EDITOR IN CHIEF, THE HILL: I think they do, but it depends, Neil, if they can really turn this frustration from people who wanted to the White House to win this ruling and turn it into votes.\par \par \uc2 You remember former President Obama would say, basically, don't get mad, just go vote. Well, the House and the White House and the Senate are up for grabs in 2024. So, Democrats, I'm sure, are going to try this legal maneuver. I don't think that's going to work.\par \par \uc2 And then it's going to turn into a campaign message: Give us the House, Senate and White House,and we will get this through.\par \par \uc2 And, Neil, let's be honest here. The reason why the White House did this administratively, they didn't have the votes in Congress. We have seen prior presidents do that. Obama did that on the dreamers and then the court pushed back. And other presidents have also tried this.\par \par \uc2 But, obviously, with this Supreme Court, it's not surprising that it didn't go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I am curious though, Bob, in light of this latest development, how the administration is playing it -- and I'm sure many of these 25 or 26 million borrowers who had applied for or automatically were approved for this loan forgiveness, they're angry.\par \par \uc2 This 26 million strong, let's say they're all upset that this isn't coming. But there are tens of millions more Americans who weren't going to be part of this anyway and many who weren't going to college or those who were, were faithfully paying their bills and their loans who are saying now, wait a minute, it ticks me off that he's catering to them.\par \par \uc2 So how does that play out?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: It's interesting, Neil. As you know, some issues favor Democrats, some issues favor Republicans.\par \par \uc2 This issue, both parties think they have the advantage, because that is the argument from Republicans. What about people who've already paid off their student loans? What about people who just didn't go to college? Why should they subsidize other people?\par \par \uc2 And, of course, Democrats think that this will help with the young vote and getting them out, but, at the same time, there's going to be such frustration, that maybe some of those voters are just going to be fed up with Washington and won't vote. That's the real concern of Democrats now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will see where it goes.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 People who have an active interest in this, Tim McGee. Tim is a 29-year-old student with loan, supports the student loan forgiveness program that the Supreme Court shot down today.\par \par \uc2 Tim, very good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Rikki Schlott not quite the same thinking here, New York Post columnist, against the student loan forgiveness program.\par \par \uc2 So, guys, you have very different views on this.\par \par \uc2 Tim, I began with you. You probably were hoping this forgiveness thing to be approved by the Supreme Court. It wasn't. What do you think now?\par \par \uc2 TIM MCGEE, SUPPORTS STUDENT LOAN FORGIVENESS: Thanks, Neil. It's great to be here.\par \par \uc2 I am disappointed with the Supreme Court's decision to turn it down. I'm a borrower myself. I have $24,000 in student loans. And a payment plan -- forgiveness like this would have made a huge impact on my financial stability and my life.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the pause over the last three years that we have had, I have been able to save around $10,000 to $12,000. And that was actually the down payment on a house that I just purchased, the house I'm in right now. So these kind of things have a real impact on individuals like me who are young working-class millennials trying to make a future for ourselves.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Were you counting on that money, Tim?\par \par \uc2 MCGEE: I wouldn't I was counting on it. I was skeptical of whether it would actually happen or not.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCGEE: But I was really looking forward to it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Rikki, your thoughts on this and whether the court, I think, you think, had done the right thing. There are many like Tim who say, no, no, no, this was the wrong thing.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI SCHLOTT, THE NEW YORK POST: Well, Tim, I'm completely sympathetic to your situation, and my biggest problem with this is that there's nothing restorative about it, and there's nothing to prevent the next generation of kids from doing the exact same thing and ending up in the exact same position.\par \par \uc2 And from my point of view as a member of Gen Z, I know a lot of us are turning away from college, myself included. I'm a dropout because they're - - it's prohibitively costly, and there's not a real clear benefit necessarily to all these degrees. In fact, a third of U.S. colleges produce classes in which half of their graduates make less than the average high school graduate.\par \par \uc2 And so I think this needs a deeper reform than what the Biden administration is trying to do with this Band-Aid.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Tim, much has been said that whatever relief we provide students and/or their parents, college tuitions, room and board just go up that and then some.\par \par \uc2 And college tuitions have been running at about three times the established inflation rate for the last 35 years or so. So it's a gaping wound that can't be solved by this decision. Does that worry you going forward?\par \par \uc2 MCGEE: It does. I honestly completely agree.\par \par \uc2 I think -- I wish the Biden administration and the other administrations would do more. Why are interest rates so high on the student loans? I wish they would do something to cap those interest rates and other things to help people.\par \par \uc2 A lot of the conversation has been around just student loans, but to help working-class Americans find other relief for other types of debt, credit card debt, mortgage debt, and those kind of things.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Rikki, from your vantage point, Republicans are working on a fix. Democrats are working on a fix for this, presumably anticipating this court decision.\par \par \uc2 And one of the fixes as to, we will only maybe look at giving loans to students who are going into moneymaking fields or have a good chance paying off, if you go into engineering or business or finance or in the medical field or computer science or that kind of thing, that that almost guarantees you will make enough money when you graduate to pay these loans.\par \par \uc2 If you go into philosophy or, dare I say it, journalism, you're out of there. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: I think that's a completely fair proposal.\par \par \uc2 I also think that there is going to be a market correction with young people that are looking at millennials who are struggling to make down payments, who are crippled by debt that, frankly, they were put in the position at 17 years old. They're four years out from being able to buy a beer, and they're able to sign away their down payments on their homes.\par \par \uc2 I think we're looking at the next generation and saying, I don't think we want to repeat history. And it's no surprise to me that there are one million fewer kids right now in college than there were pre-pandemic, because a lot of us are saying there's more than one pathway to success.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Tim, the administration seems to be laying out a plan, working through the Higher Education Act. I could get into the weeds, but I frankly don't understand it, Tim, so if you will forgive me.\par \par \uc2 But it just seems to be another means of trying to reheat this thing. I don't know how far it's going to go. It doesn't seem likely to go far, but what do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 MCGEE: Yes, I'm not an expert on the legality of a lot of this, but, to me, it does make a big impact on my vote that they're trying something.\par \par \uc2 I think something needs to be done about the situation. And so the option of just taking the Supreme Court decision and not making any progress forward, dropping this off, Biden made a promise to us as voters. I really appreciate that he's taking it a step further.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you would reward him on Election Day?\par \par \uc2 MCGEE: I think I would, honestly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 MCGEE: I -- if something came out of this and there was relief, whether that was interest rates coming down or something like that, that would be a big impact on me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What would happen, in your light, Rikki, if Republicans fail to come up with some alternative to deal with this?\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: I do think that a lot of people will vote for Biden as a result of the fact that he was caught trying.\par \par \uc2 But, unfortunately, I think that it's nothing more than a cynical ploy to kind dangle a carrot in front of people's faces and to toy with the fact that people are genuinely hurting as a result of a system that the federal government put in place. And we need a better fix than what the Biden administration is providing us.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And maybe this moment will provide that.\par \par \uc2 Guys, I want to thank you both. You argue very eloquently for your causes, Tim McGee, Rikki Schlott.\par \par \uc2 We will continue to follow what the administration wants to do, how Republicans react. We haven't heard from them on this latest proposal. We hope to.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, just putting this in perspective on -- we had a week and a quarter and a half-year wrap up with very bullish signs. So, if Wall Street is panicking about this, it has a funny way of showing it, all the major averages up smartly. Look at the Nasdaq for the first half of the year, up almost 32 percent, its best first six-month performance in history.\par \par \uc2 So, they're gung-ho about an economy that they think is going to continue to take off, student loans notwithstanding.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230701ej6u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  All-In On Debating \uc2 Ron Desantis\uc2  For Multiple Hours; Barack Obama Scolds Progressives For Attacking Media; Hunchback, Claw-Like Hands: Horrifying Model Shows Remote Worker Of The Future; Millennials, Gen-Z Unleash On Boomer Office Habits; People Have No Clue What CVS Stands For; Crazy In-Flation: Beyonce Concerts Blamed For Sweden's Inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeanine Pirro, Richard Fowler, Jesse Watters, Martha MacCallum,  Greg Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,582 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: President that's going to lift it up to close to 36 trillion over the next couple of years but who is counting?\par \par \uc2 Right now, the markets aren't worried about it and going into this Father's Day weekend, I don't want to trouble you about it. Just a reminder to dad that we're going to debt fast. Here's THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Hello, everyone. I'm Jeanine Pirro, along with Richard Fowler, Jesse Watters, Martha MacCallum and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  knows who he wants to face in the general election and it's not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . The Florida Governor hitting back after relentless mudslinging attacks from \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The pair have been duking it out over hot-button issues including migrant flights and Newsom's pathetic threat to throw him in jail.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis thinks it's time for Gavin to man up and get into the race for the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): You know what? Stop pussyfooting around? Are you going to -- are you going to throw your hat in the ring and challenge, Joe? Are you going to get in and do it? Or are you just going to sit on the sidelines and chirp?\par \par \uc2 So, why don't you throw your hat in the ring? And then we'll go ahead and talk about what's happening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But I guess Gavin likes being on the sidelines, here he is repeatedly ruling out a White House run.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you considering running for president in 2024 and 2028?\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): No. Not at all.\par \par \uc2 It's not my ambition. And it's not the direction that I'm leaning into.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: How many times is your phone ping a day people saying you need to get in this race?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I see where you're going with THAT.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm asking?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: No, and I'm not answering.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But that doesn't mean Newsom wouldn't relish the chance to debate his ideological foe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You will do a two-hour debate with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I'll make it three.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Three-hour debate?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Yes, maybe more with no notes. I look forward to that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, Martha, what is the motivation for \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: -- four-hour debate between \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . You know, this is very interesting. I mean, I love how he -- I always listen to, you know, politician's words very closely, right? It's like, it's not my ambition. It's not the way I'm leaning right now.\par \par \uc2 In other words, that could change at any moment, right? If \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  decides not to run, I think a lot of people wonder if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  might do a Lyndon Johnson, you know, might sort of at some point say, I've decided I'm not going to actually run again. That the signs are not in that direction right now. But I think there's every reason to think that that's a possibility that that could happen.\par \par \uc2 I think, in some ways, this is good for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , because it draws him out. I talked to a lot of people who say, where is he?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, they want to know why he's not out there more, why he's not doing more interviews. I know, he spent time in Iowa and New Hampshire, and he's doing that work, which is really important to do.\par \par \uc2 But I think a lot of folks, you know, like to see him drawn out and the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  issues have kind of suck the oxygen out of the room, which is happens all the time, right?\par \par \uc2 But he needs to find his way to get in and to make his mark. So, this kind of provided him a little bit of an opening to get in the conversation.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Greg, what do you think would be more interesting, a three-hour debate between DeSantis and Newsom or watching that -- or \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  going into a debate with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: Well, this is a good question, because this is like a boxing bill, right? You have the lower card.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: It's called the undercard. Undercard. A boxing fan, Greg Gutfeld.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right. You know, it's like the undercard. So, they're going to edit out that first part. Yes, they are because this is a talk show.\par \par \uc2 You got Gavin nuisance Newsom versus Ron the dissenter DeSantis. But it's really about the top card, which is Joe versus the volcano.\par \par \uc2 Although, Ron is at least being honest about it. He's thrown his hair in the ring, but Gavin is pretending that he's not running for president when we know he is, you know, he's all glory, no hole as we like to say.\par \par \uc2 But I think that Gavin is effective and that he's managing to run for president.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, he's managed to run for president without declaring his running for president. He's definitely not governing California. None of this stuff has to do with California. Who's running California? It's just like Joe with America, United States, who's running it? It's really strange.\par \par \uc2 I don't think Democrats actually run anything they're in charge of, but it's clear that he's on a P.R. crusade to erase any flaws or obstacles that are going to show up next year.\par \par \uc2 And around February 2024, you're probably going to see when Joe drops out, you'll see Gavin step in with the my pillow. And then he'll --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, (INAUDIBLE) undercard. What do you think? Do you think that Joe is going to drop out? I mean, and by the way --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I think -- I think Gutfeld needs a drink.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, and ribs. But you know, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 's wife came out and talked about something that happened between, something very tragic between her and her sister. And her sister ended up dying. And it's a very sad situation, but it's almost like let's get this all out because we're getting ready to run.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Here's what's happening. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  cut a deal. And the deal is Gavin's not going to primary \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And they're going to make \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  the heir apparent.\par \par \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  just has to attack DeSantis. Doesn't matter about attacking Trump. Biden's got that covered, he's arresting him.\par \par \uc2 They just need to run up DeSantis's negatives right now. And it never looked good when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was attacking DeSantis. Remember, he did it for about two years. And Joe was really old looking and DeSantis was young and competent in Florida. And it kind of backfired.\par \par \uc2 So, they're like, let's send a younger, more liberal guy out there to attack DeSantis, and let's attack them on issues that Joe can't handle, immigration and trance.\par \par \uc2 That seems to be the issue because when you're attacking DeSantis, DeSantis is then attacking Newsom, and he's not attacking \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, he's kind of drawing DeSantis's attacks away from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Pussyfooting around is a great line. And I'm glad you used it and use it in a humorous and casual ways that this guy's obsessed with me.\par \par \uc2 He's obsessed, everyone's leaving a state and he just dismissed him. But these two are on a collision course whether it's in two years, or six years, you got a big governor in a big state. They're young, they got nice- looking families. They're both energetic. They're both very combative. And sooner or later, they're going to rip each other's faces off.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, you know, given what's going on right now, Richard with Comer, and the House and the Republicans, and we heard about that, you know, that unclassified 1023. And now there's allegedly tapes, in addition to all the money that's flowing into the Biden family, you think there's a chance that Biden will step down? And this is part of a bigger plan?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: No, I don't think so. And I mean, we're not really sure. I mean, in an interview, he said he wasn't sure if the tapes were legit or not, but on this DeSantis issue.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You think he has to listen to them? I doubt.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: I don't know what Comer -- I don't know what Comer is doing, I mean, if he -- if he had it, he would have had a hearing about it but I digress.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, no, he couldn't get it from \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Like I said, if he had -- if they had the evidence, they would have had a hearing, but let's focus on this.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, he just got it from \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: All he does is do television interviews, and he hasn't had a hearing yet. But anyway, back to \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Because I think that's a very interesting story.\par \par \uc2 Jesse, I'm not sure we watched the same television show because last time I checked, the person dumping on \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , right? Every day. He's attacking \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . And it seems to be working because like tomorrow at this point, nobody knows who \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 And I think in this clip that we saw here, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was signing the state's budget. And what you find out in this state budget is that he's in infighting with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  once again.\par \par \uc2 In the budget, he vetoed a $20 million investment for the -- for the unit for the \uc2 University of South Florida\uc2 's nursing campus during a nursing shortage because the senators whose district that's in haste -- endorsed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He also vetoed $3.6 million for opioid treatment. He vetoed 17 drainage improvement projects all in the same budget.\par \par \uc2 He wants to make this fight about between him and \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , but it seems very clear, talking about not being able to manage somebody's state, it seems that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  seems more -- trying to win this primary more than I'm actually trying to govern the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Then why is everybody moving to Florida?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: I'm talking about the polls. I mean, clearly, he's --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: He's talking about people moving with their feet, not the polls.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Yes, listen, you can have this conversation if you want to. He's running for -- now he's a candidate --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And I will.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Right now, he's running for President of the United States, and he is losing to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by what, 20 points in the last poll.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: 30.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Even worse, right? And I always feel like, Judge, to be honest with you, he spent so much time talking about drag queens, maybe he wants to be one.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Who?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: And \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , he spent so much time talking about drag queens what they shouldn't do and where they shouldn't perform and where they should be and where they shouldn't be. Maybe he wants to --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: If somebody introduces an idea that you debate, they go, oh, well maybe, why are you so obsessed with this?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: To me? Because I've prosecuted pedophiles my whole life, I'm obsessed with them?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It is the dumbest argument.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: That's completely different.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Why? I was obsessed with them because I hated what they did.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Listen, I think a drag queen and a pedophile are two completely different things that we can all agree on.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I will agree on that, absolutely but it doesn't mean I'm obsessed with them.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: The idea of (INAUDIBLE) drag shows.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Who said what?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I don't think you should be accusing anybody.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, coming up next --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I don't think you should be accusing anybody.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Anybody of what?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You know what I'm talking about.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: I don't know. What are you talking about?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Everybody knows.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, coming up next, Barack Obama -- all right guys, Barack Obama thinks liberals are a bunch of snowflakes.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Are you booing Billy Joel?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, someone has to.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, no, no, no. OK. So former -- we'll get to that later, former President Barack Obama is scolding liberals who don't like it when journalists ask tough questions, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have tended, at times on the progressive side, to tip into kind of a scolding social etiquette police and virtue signaling. There have been times where reporters are asking questions that don't fit the accepted narrative or are inconvenient for progressives around certain issues.\par \par \uc2 And just by raising the question, or pointing out facts that don't fit the neat narrative, we'll jump on him and say, you know, why are you, you know, aiding those who are attacking us?\par \par \uc2 And I do think it is important for us to not fall into that trap.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But it looks like President Biden isn't taking the advice of his former boss. Listen to what happened when the president got asked this inconvenient, in Obama's words, question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why did the Ukraine and \uc2 FBI\uc2  informant file refer to you as the big guy, President Biden? Why is that term continuously applied?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Why do you ask such a dumb question?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Why do you ask such a dumb question? Richard, what kind of warning is former President Obama sending to the president and to his own party, about the way that they are shutting people down if they don't like the narrative or the question that they've been asked?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Listen, I think this was a very good actual episode, and was a good chance to listen to the president sort of lament on his time at the White House, as well as his time post the White House.\par \par \uc2 And throughout the episode, he talks about this idea of the entire political system and how we are all engaged in this -- in this polarization where we listen to the loudest folks in the room and often forget the middle.\par \par \uc2 I think at this particular clip, what we're taught what he's talking about, he's right. I'm not sure this was the best answer from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , because I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's answer could have been look, you know, my son is a private citizen. The house -- the House Oversight Committee says they have this evidence, I would urge them to if they have it, and they say they've got all these facts to have a hearing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They could have just said, of course, I'm the big guy can't you tell?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: No. Because I don't think -- I mean, listen, I think there's been a lot of conjecture from this committee. This committee spend more time like I said in the last segment, they spend more time on television and actually in hearing rooms, actually subpoenaing people, actually asking the tough questions.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the president had an opportunity to call this committee out and said, listen, if you say you have this evidence, have a hearing, I'll come down there, my son will come down there, we'll testify.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Sorry, but we all know they're locked up in the back drawer of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , they're not touching it. So, they'll never going to get to that stage where they can have hearings, because they can't get their hands on any of the evidence because the laptop has been sitting there for years. And it's very comfortably off the -- you know, on the very backburner of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: But Martha, to be fair, we've seen this movie play out before, this happened a couple of years ago when there was this whole thing about Benghazi. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  went down there and testified for 17 hours, and nothing came of it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Of course, it didn't.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Right? There was like all these crimes happened. She did all this stuff with Benghazi.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: They had a 17-hour committee where she testified.\par \par \uc2 But she testified for 17 hours, so my point here is they have this evidence, send out some subpoenas and have a hearing, end. And stop going on television.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me back around to the topic here. You know, Jesse, it made me think of the moment this week when \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  just shut down this EWTN reporter who said, you know, what do you say to parents who are a little bit concerned about their daughters playing sports with biological males, and that they might get hurt in that situation? We know that there's a young woman who got a concussion and a neck injury playing volleyball with a biological boy.\par \par \uc2 And she just like -- she said, are you suggesting that trans children are dangerous? Like not -- let me answer your question, she just shut him down with a judgment on him and didn't answer his question.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes. So, the Democrats are just bullying reporters. And now the reporters are scared to ask questions. And if you're scared and you're working, you get hurt. That's what they say in football. When you play scared, you get injured.\par \par \uc2 And that's what all these reporters are doing. They're all 30 years old. They're all looking for friends. They're all trying to build a career. And if you get ostracized by \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , then you're going to get scared and everyone's going to look at you funny at the next briefing.\par \par \uc2 One second, Richard, we'll get to the hearings right now, we're doing depositions and we're doing subpoenas. It's kind of difficult when there's a cover up going on at the highest level.\par \par \uc2 You haven't seen the subpoena? Oh, you didn't see the six million that they took from China that they lied about? Let me finish.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Barack Obama have to understand this. Democrats aren't fun anymore. And Obama's a guy, guys don't get offended when we're around other guys. It's only when the girls come around.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Don't call us girls.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We pretend that we're offended so we look sensitive. Michelle said the same thing. She's like, you know what? Our daughters and their fr