lic lands. OK, and we have a similar problem and it has to do with forest management.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has policies that are in direct conflict with one another. The first is that they say they want to restore and preserve old growth and mature forests.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the Department of Agriculture has declared a wildfire crisis and they're using money from the infrastructure bill in the IRA in order to do deal with large swaths of forest lands.\par \par \uc2 Where you do not have this problem is on privately owned land because people who own their own land figure out a way to keep it a little bit more intact.\par \par \uc2 Wildfires can occur naturally, of course, but if you are actively managing your forests, then you can prevent some of these things. The left has never wanted to do that.\par \par \uc2 Let's say that you have a project where you want to make sure that these old-growth forests that you deal with the underbrush. But one of the things that happens is the environmentalist will throw every single thing that they have in its way to prevent you from actually doing that. And then what do you have? You have a problem that not just rural America that is dealing with this now, but because we have built so far into, it's called the WUI. The wildlife -- wild urban interface.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: WUI.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, WUI. Then you have more and more people being affected by it. I have to work on the healthy forest legislation. So, it's all coming back to me and I will shut up now. I love that bill.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But you know what, Dana -- the point that Dana makes a great point, and it has to do with forest management. And we first started talking about this one that were wildfires in California, and \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  was blaming everything, but you know, any kind of forest management.\par \par \uc2 And yet, we're at a point now, where I think 49 percent of Americans believe climate change is caused mostly by human actions, the lowest reported apparently in many years.\par \par \uc2 So, why the Democrats -- why do they keep pushing climate change over something that could have been prevented?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR\uc2 ., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it's good to be back around the table and being in this conversation.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think it's hard to -- I don't -- I don't -- I think the politics of this can go probably goes both ways. And I don't disagree, the forest management issue is one that Democrats probably and Republicans, if you and the majority in the House, have perhaps a forest management bill that Dana reference, should be perhaps one of the priorities.\par \par \uc2 We can -- we can and should debate what causes these occurrences. I have an opinion, a theory of the case that some of this -- a lot of this is manmade, but I'm open to the debate.\par \par \uc2 But what is harder to debate is the frequency, the increasing frequency of these occurrences. And what's even harder to debate about that is that it's clear that our climate is changing for some set of reasons.\par \par \uc2 Few facts Dana pointed out, things that have happened out west for a while. The West is running out of water, it's scarcer than it's been. Fires are seemingly everyday occurrences out west, it's hard to buy home insurance in Florida, because insurance companies are reluctant to sell home insurance less at a prohibitively high price because of -- because of the frequency - - increasing frequency of hurricanes.\par \par \uc2 What's happened here in Canada, I hope that they learned from this, it's disconcerting and concerning on other levels, because this is the beginning of their wildfire season, this thing is going to suppose -- according to the meteorologist will last deeper into July and maybe even early August.\par \par \uc2 So, this may unfortunately what we're dealing with today and yesterday, we could find ourselves 