Yelp\uc2  or anything?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, I don't do that on \uc2 Yelp\uc2 . But I got to tell you, it's something that struck me. It's like everybody you know wants to give good reviews, and you're right, they're meaningless. But it kind of reminds me of picking a jury, right? You pick a jury -- everybody is against crime, right? Everybody is all against crime. But you have to pick the juror who's going to have the stamina, the hutzpah or whatever to convict someone. And that is how I like in this.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: You think that's meaningless, the jury selection?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Jesse, you --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, no, no. What I'm saying is a lot of people will say yes, yes, crimes are bad, but then they don't go the -- to the end route and convict.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Got it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: See, I believe Jessie is petty enough to go online and destroy a business because his french fries were cold. Am I right?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I will go on air and do that. I have a show, and I can do bad critiques on air. I just give five stars to make stuff go away.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Ah, very good, very good. You know what, though, Dana, we've talked about this before. I hate people who go public with complaints before handling it privately. Like, they go on social media and they trash somebody or they trash a company, and it's like, well, they might have been able to help you with your service, or if it's an airline, but that people like that feeling.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Or your loved one who lives with you asks you to go on your own \uc2 Twitter\uc2  which has more followers and they will complain about the company. And you're like, I'm not going to do that. I'm not going to do that. But I also think the bigger problem here are the people that give fake one-star reviews.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Because that is a thing. And they try to destroy businesses. And then, you can't --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, that's not --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, it happens all the time. And then you try to appeal to \uc2 Yelp\uc2  and say this person, you know, is a disgruntled neighbor down the street, and they're trying to ruin my business. That's the bigger problem.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You're absolutely right. That's why I think this is bogus and I'm going to write a review about this show. You suck, Gutfeld. "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's time now for "ONE MORE THING." Harold.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: A United States Naval Officer flew across the world 30 hours and surprise his daughter at her graduation. Douglas Hernandez was returning from deployment in the Middle East and hadn't seen his family since last July.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: He coordinated with the staff at UNLV to arrange a surprise for his daughter, Pamela. You can see the look on her face. All those -- I said, he traveled 30 hours. It was well worth it. Congrats to him, to her, and the family.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I love that.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, that's great. OK, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: How could he see him? All right --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I missed that.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Tonight, 11:00, Jamie Lissow, \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , Todd Piro, Kat Timpf. It's going to be great. And now it's this. Greg's World Record News. Oh, get ready for this. That 3-year-old Labrador-German Shepherd with the name Zoe, the longest tongue on a living dog. They got it and used dead dogs. I think that would have been inappropriate because you have to dig them all up and stretch their tongues out.\par \par \uc2 But look at that tongue. It must be really hard to run around with a tongue like that. I know the feeling. So, anyway, keep your -- keep your mind out of the gutter, Dana. This is a wholesome family program. And anyway, take that, Gene Simmons.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know what? Can you imagine what it's like when he drinks out of a water bowl? There's got to be water all over the place.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Messy.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Very messy. OK, criminals are now thinking outside the box, really inside the box. The guy in Florida puts a box on his head to steal phones in a Florida store. And then he's dumb enough to take the box off his head, revealing his face to the security camera. Eventually, they found him drinking with his buddies at a nearby liquor store. And he was arrested and charged with grand theft burglary, criminal mischief, cocaine possession.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's definitely the cocaine. And you're on "HANNITY"\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, I'm on "HANNITY" tonight.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, I want to wish a very special happy birthday to Michelle Frazzetta. She is the best hairstylist in the business.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Happy Birthday!\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Ever.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: She takes care of my hair every single day.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And she's beautiful.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Super talented, super spunky. We love her.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: She keeps yours in a box, right?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, that's right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's the same box that guy had on his head. Tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME," we made Johnny go breathe some dirty air and interview people in New York about the smog. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY BELISARIO, FOX NEWS PRODUCER: New York City has new crack pipe vending machines. You think this is all from everyone smoking it up?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Smoking weed?\par \par \uc2 BELISARIO: Oh, there you go. You got that from the vending machine?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, I got this in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, Dana?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You know, I don't have enough time but I was -- I will be watching everyone's shows.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, Dana is on my show.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, I'm on his show.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, that's it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next. Hey, Trace!\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020230608ej6700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Sen. J.D. Vance (R-OH); \uc2 Martha Stewart\uc2  Wants to End Remote Work; Ukrainian Dam Destroyed; Interview With Gov. \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  (R- NH); \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  Enters Presidential Race\b0\par\par\uc2 Gerry Baker, Keith Kellogg, Jacqui Heinrich, Alexis McAdams, Neil  Cavuto \par \uc2 8,445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 June 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: The antagonist, the Trump slayer, that is how \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  is being portrayed, as the former New Jersey governor enters the Republican presidential primary.\par \par \uc2 But as Christie is jumping in, another Republican is officially out. New Hampshire Republican Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  says that he will not be making a presidential run, while warning, a crowded field only helps \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and could doom the party in 2024 if they're not careful.\par \par \uc2 Governor Sununu is coming up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. Glad to have you. I'm Neil Cavuto. My thanks to Sandra Smith for her hard work yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Now to Alexis McAdams in Manchester, New Hampshire, on this Christie announcement, and more to come, I guess -- Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it's a crowded field already. And, as you mentioned, the former Governor of New Jersey \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  has officially thrown his hat into the presidential race. Just about 30 minutes ago, he filed the official campaign paperwork to enter the race for the White House for the second time.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , Neil, says he has what it takes, if anybody does, to take former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on, on the debate stage. I went around here in New Hampshire to talk to voters to see what they think about this. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm not a particular fan of him, but I'd like to hear what he has to say.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER WRIGHT, NEW HAMPSHIRE VOTER: I think it's too early to tell. I think everyone has to kind of put out what they're doing and put forward what their aims are and then go from there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: So, in just a few hours, the former New Jersey governor expected to launch his campaign on stage here at a town hall at the New Hampshire Institute of Politics.\par \par \uc2 Christie's been spending more in New Hampshire recently, and this will be his second town hall in the state so far this year. He focused his 2016 campaign in New Hampshire, but that didn't go so well. He came in sixth in the state's primary and then dropped out of the race and later endorsed Trump.\par \par \uc2 Since then, though, they're not really buddies anymore. Christie has slammed the former president for everything from January 6 and for claiming the 2020 election was stolen. Christie now joins a growing field of GOP candidates.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I guarantee you, at Mar-a-Lago, they break out the champagne every time there's a new candidate, and they would love to have 15 or 20 candidates.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And if the deadline was today, Neil, Christie would not qualify for the \uc2 RNC\uc2  debate coming up in August. But if he does make it to the debate stage, he said he's ready. He's ready to pretty much rip Trump apart, and that could really help other candidates.\par \par \uc2 So, even if he doesn't make it to be the presidential nominee, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , he could be helping the other people along the way -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Well, one thing I know about \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  is, he's probably happy that this next fellow chose not to run for president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 I'm talking about New Hampshire's very popular Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , who opted out of a presidential run. Kind enough to join us now.\par \par \uc2 Governor, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2  (R-NH): Good to see you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I mentioned Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , of course, who's placing a lot of his bets, as you know, in your state, less so in Iowa. If you had run, decided to run, that would have complicated his efforts. Did he send you a thank you note?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No, look, I -- Chris is a great friend. I think he's going to be a great candidate.\par \par \uc2 They're all friend, I mean, all the candidates. There's about a dozen folks on stage at this point, getting a little crowded, but they're all friends. And I have talked to them all over the past 24 hours. And, again, we're just getting under way.\par \par \uc2 So everyone has a shot, whether it's in New Hampshire and Iowa, to prove their case, retail politics, connect with voters on a very personal level and ultimately show what they got. Can they give a punch and take a punch on the stage?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You could have made an argument that you would have a good shot at winning your state, but you still opted not to run. Why not?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Well, look, when you look at where Trump is in the polls, the field is separating. He's separating himself from the field for all the wrong reasons.\par \par \uc2 He does not represent good Republican values. He represents the past. He wants to relitigate the past, and that's not what America is looking for to win in November of '24. He cannot mathematically win in November of '24. So, right there, we got to find another candidate and use Iowa, New Hampshire, and some of the earlier states to really see who has the muster, who can deliver on a good presidential run.\par \par \uc2 And my ability, if you will, my skill is hopefully getting more independents, getting more younger voters back onto the team. It has to be a team win. It doesn't have to be the \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  show. And so, again, I just think that I can be a much more influential, and for the benefit of the party and ultimately the nation, kind of doing my own thing on my own time, being a little more -- as I said yesterday, a little more unleashed in terms of what I can say and how I can say it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Your father was instrumental in supporting George H.W. Bush, and he served because of that and helped cement a victory and carry him to the presidential nomination. He became his chief of staff.\par \par \uc2 Do you plan to endorse any of the candidates before New Hampshire?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes, that could happen. That very, very well could happen.\par \par \uc2 Look, if I find a candidate I really like and want to get behind, and they can really carry that message to a winning -- the winning momentum, not just through the primary, but through '24, and help other candidates, that's exactly who I'm willing to get behind.\par \par \uc2 I couldn't tell you who it is right now. I don't think anybody really could, because we're still so, so, so early on, but, no, if the mood strikes me, just like it would strike you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Governor, you just said that you opted not to run because, right now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is so far ahead in the polls. I don't know whether that was one of the reasons you opted not to run, but, right, now, he is very far ahead of the polls.\par \par \uc2 So, if he were to win, would you support him, or would you, going right before New Hampshire, say the former president included is among the people I would consider to endorse?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No, I'm -- I will not endorse former President Trump during the Republican primary process.\par \par \uc2 Look, that guy is all about litigating yesterday. He's not delivering what is about tomorrow. And I get it. He's playing this victim card. But you don't elect someone president or to be your nominee to play the victim card. If he could win in November of '24, different prospect.\par \par \uc2 But the fact of the matter, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won't even win Georgia. And if you're a Republican candidate that can't even win Georgia and would actually cost us other seats, then, no, we have to take a hard pass at that, because what it means is, if you support Trump and he gets the nomination, Biden ends up the winner.\par \par \uc2 I don't even know if Biden can finish out the second term. So what happens then? You now -- we now got \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as president? Well, thank you to all the Trump supporters out there. You have just given \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as our next president. That's the math. It's not about whether you agree with policies and...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you don't know that. You don't know that, right? Seriously, you don't.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I mean, he's up in the polls a lot. And you're quite right. There is a risk that he could, getting the nomination, lose the general election.\par \par \uc2 But I remember, back in 2015-'16, around this time, most people thought Jeb Bush would be running away with it. We know what happened. So are you misreading these polls?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No. No, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 There's no way \uc2 Don Trump\uc2  can beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in November. That was proven in 2018, when we lost the House. That was proven in 2020, when we lost everything. That was proven in '22, when we should have had 53 or 54 \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seats, and we lost those. We lost governorships because of President Trump and his message. It doesn't carry.\par \par \uc2 He's a three-time loser going on to carry this party to a fourth-time loss. We cannot have that. Thank you for your service. This is not personal. This is just about winning. We cannot govern. We cannot get this country back unless we have winners up and down the ticket. And he clogs that up. He's about his message, not the Republican message.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you, Governor, if he is the nominee? Who do you vote for?\par \par \uc2 Given a choice between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if that were the final, what would you do?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Let me tell you, if he -- yes, look, I tell you, if he's the nominee, all my efforts are making sure we don't lose Senate seats again, we don't lose governorships again.\par \par \uc2 Do you know there are folks that lose school board seats because they have to kind of explain away the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  thing? We need to be about, again, draining the swamp. Didn't happen under him. Limited government. Actually being fiscally disciplined, not adding $8 trillion to the deficit, which he did, actually getting border security, which we heard a lot about, but he didn't -- accomplishment.\par \par \uc2 So we have to be about the things that Republicans can be very successful on, in limited government and individual responsibility, not about just litigating yesterday. That's not about the future. America wants somebody that talks with some inspiration and talks about the future, delivers on what they're going to say. And we have a lot of great candidates to do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You sound like a pretty effective attack dog in yourself.\par \par \uc2 And you have a great sense of humor. I mean, right now, it's going to be left, I guess, to \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  to be that attack dog. And he's more than happy and willing to do that. The other candidates don't seem quite as inclined to go that route. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes.\par \par \uc2 No, you're absolutely right. And it is not \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , the attack dog. I mean, Chris is great, don't get me wrong, but any candidate that's standing on that stage down by 40-plus points in the polls and not attacking former President Trump for his failures and what he has done to this party doesn't deserve to be on that stage.\par \par \uc2 And I mean that really sincerely. This is not a vanity contest to run for vice president. This isn't a chance to get up there and sell books. If you are not passionate about running for president and defeating the guy that's standing ahead of you and defeating the guy that's holding this whole party back, then get off the stage, clear the field.\par \par \uc2 And if I can be a voice in doing that as we hit November, December with other folks that are across this country that I think are going to have that resonating message, either stand up and fight to be the president or stop wasting our time, because there's too much at stake when it comes to the Republican Party and the opportunity to really get this country back.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, to circle back to what I asked you near the beginning, whether you would endorse a candidate who isn't \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  before the New Hampshire primary?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes, I might. Yes, I very well might.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I will see where it all goes and see who stands out and who connects. My thing is about retail politics, who connects with people on an individual level, not so much on the policy. And if they can do that and get people excited about getting on the team and talk about things that win in November of '24, then that's a candidate I will be happy to get behind.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so let us know when that happens.\par \par \uc2 But, \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , it's always good seeing you. Thank you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Thank you, buddy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, how do you think the White House is responding to all these developments? The president has someone looking over his shoulder right now, and the last name is Kennedy.\par \par \uc2 Let's go right now to Jacqui Heinrich at the White House with more -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, if Neil, if Republican candidates aren't attacking former President Trump, Biden's campaign certainly is.\par \par \uc2 They think that his reelection will be pretty easy if Trump is on the ballot, so easy, in fact, that we haven't seen him yet on the campaign trail. More on that soon.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let's say the race starts, get to New Hampshire, get to South Carolina -- it looks like South Carolina will be first -- and Robert F. Kennedy scores pretty well. Doesn't have to win.\par \par \uc2 But it could be akin to what we saw in 1968, where \uc2 Eugene McCarthy\uc2  didn't win New Hampshire, but he did well enough to push LBJ out of the race and for the former president then to acknowledge, I'm not running again.\par \par \uc2 So, the irony will be that other party heavyweights will enter at that point, not Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. \uc2 DENNIS KUCINICH\uc2  (D-OH): Well, the reality is going to be, respectfully, Neil, that Democrats are going to be looking for the person who can win in November.\par \par \uc2 And I think that as we move towards the primary season, it will be abundantly clear that \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr\uc2 . is the only Democrat who can win in November.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Dennis Kucinich\uc2  himself a former presidential candidate, a popular U.S. Congressman, et al, now saying Robert F. Kennedy is the guy who's going to be our nominee for the Democratic Party in 2024.\par \par \uc2 I wonder how that's being digested at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich would know. She's there.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 It would be an understatement to say that the White House is paying no attention to \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr\uc2 . They are focused entirely on the general election. The president might not be hitting the road anytime soon, but the campaign is certainly getting his campaign on the road for him in the form of billboards around Washington, D.C., in some battleground states, and also an ad campaign on the debt ceiling deal.\par \par \uc2 Campaign manager Julie Chavez Rodriguez says: "These ads are a powerful reminder of the president's ability to get things done and to rebuild the middle class and our economy at a historic pace while preventing MAGA Republicans and their extreme agenda from unraveling our progress."\par \par \uc2 Notably, the ads plan to highlight programs the president saved from MAGA Republicans, despite GOP leadership making clear from the outset in those deal negotiations that these programs were never in danger of cuts, including Social Security and Medicare, veterans benefits.\par \par \uc2 The ads will also tout historic unemployment lows, 13 million jobs created under President Biden. The campaign told FOX that events will happen eventually, but the initial plan has been to let voters see Biden being president. So he's going to use the administration to get that message out starting in July.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The bipartisan budget agreement the president secured protects these investments that you just heard me speak of.\par \par \uc2 Members of his administration will fan out across the country later this month for a second installment of the Investing in America Tour during the congressional recess around July 4.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: As Biden faces criticism for holding no events since announcing his reelection bid six weeks ago today, the DNC is also taking heat for not opening the primaries or holding any debates.\par \par \uc2 Twitter\uc2  founder \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2  added his voice to those calls, but no sign that is going anywhere anytime soon. As for interviews with candidate Biden, nothing to read out on that front. As we often hear over here, the campaign signaled that they're going to rely heavily on social media influencers and a network of surrogates, citing a fragmented media environment, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Hmm. That was probably an understatement.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jacqui Heinrich following all of that.\par \par \uc2 I wonder what Don Peebles makes of this, a very powerful Democratic Party player, the former Obama fund-raiser, \uc2 Peebles Corporation\uc2  founder and CEO.\par \par \uc2 Don, it's always great to have you. Thanks for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 DON PEEBLES, FORMER OBAMA FUND RAISER: Glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me ask you first about the RFK Jr. threat, if you call it that. He's garnering 20 percent of the vote in polls right now. If you add \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2 , that's close to a third of the Democratic vote that wants to vote for someone other than the president of the United States. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 PEEBLES: Well, I think it's indicative that about a third or more of the Democratic Party members want to vote for someone other than this president.\par \par \uc2 So the danger for President Biden is if someone else who would be a much more credible candidate gotten to the race, a \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2 , for example, or \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , who has flirted with the idea of running previously.\par \par \uc2 Those types of candidates would get that 30 percent, plus more, and I think it would put the president in a difficult position, and that is what they're hoping doesn't happen by not having him engage with anyone.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We all go back to 196. At least I do. I was a kid. But I do remember the significance of \uc2 Eugene McCarthy\uc2  doing so well in New Hampshire in the primary that year that it forced LBJ out of the race, and he opted not to run for reelection.\par \par \uc2 Now, we live in different times, I grant you, and I know South Carolina will kick things off for Democrats this go-round. But I do wonder, if we get close to the polls and the key primary in other states, and they're still looking like they are now, do you expect others will be tempted to join in now?\par \par \uc2 PEEBLES\uc2 : Yes, I think others are tempted to join in. I think there is a lot of support to encourage one of the two candidates I just mentioned to get into the race, because people -- think about this, for example.\par \par \uc2 The person that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can beat is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Imagine a circumstance where he is not the Republican nominee. I think that would indicate that President Biden would be far less competitive against an alternative candidate. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tends to polarize the election.\par \par \uc2 Another type of candidate would be very dangerous for the Democrats. And I think that's what they're struggling with right now. And keeping him sidelined, I mean, makes him even more rusty.\par \par \uc2 And the Republicans are not going to be gentle with him, whoever that is. And, certainly, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won't be gentle with him.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But Republicans might not be too gentle with themselves if it's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I mean, you might have heard a little bit earlier New Hampshire Governor Sununu, who is opting out of the race, saying, if it's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he essentially wants nothing to do with the race.\par \par \uc2 PEEBLES\uc2 : Yes, I don't think that the majority of the Republican Party thinks that way right now.\par \par \uc2 I think that they will unite against whoever the nominee is. But there's a long road for President Trump to get the nomination again. I mean, if you look at \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , if he debates \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I think it may be a little different.\par \par \uc2 I ran into a former Republican presidential candidate today over lunch who did very well in the 1980s, and he thinks there will be a surprise, and he thinks Christie will be very competitive. And I think that is -- I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to have to answer for a lot of things.\par \par \uc2 So I think we're a long way from determining who the Republican nominee is. Trump certainly has the advantage, and I think we are in a circumstance where the Democratic Party has a president that they don't really want to see run for reelection. And that is a very dangerous situation for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Was that successful Republican kind of the 1980s, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PEEBLES: No, it wasn't. But it was one who had -- he didn't get the nominee, but was very competitive.\par \par \uc2 I think -- and, by the way, I run into Democrats. I had lunch with one of the top Democrat fund-raisers around the country, and they were thinking in terms of who else is out there. We have got to be able to build a stronger candidate. Take nothing away from President Biden. I think it's time for somebody else.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will watch it.\par \par \uc2 Don, always good seeing you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PEEBLES\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Don Peebles.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we have been talking about \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr\uc2 . He will be back with us this Thursday, get his sort of lay of the land here, what \uc2 Dennis Kucinich\uc2  was telling us, that they have a game plan, they have a financial plan, and they have a lot of people who are planning on him not only being a threat to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but beating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We will find out a little bit more what the candidate himself thinks on this show on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, more details coming out on that horrific Ukrainian dam explosion. Republican -- or, I should say, Moscow and, of course, the governor of Ukraine trading barbs on this, on who's to fault for all of this, but it is a mess. And for those in Southern Ukraine, it could be a painful mess that could go on for years -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: She's the queen of everything your home. But she doesn't like people continuing to work from home, says it's killing our culture and killing our work ethic and killing our productivity.\par \par \uc2 Martha Stewart\uc2 's strange message that has some people howling.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: We have seen the reports that Russia was responsible for the explosion at the dam, which, I would remind, Russian forces took over illegally last year and have been occupying since then.\par \par \uc2 We're doing the best we can to assess those reports, and we are working with the Ukrainians to gather more information, but we cannot say conclusively what happened at this point.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, just take a look at this damage with this attack on this dam, cascades of water pouring down and how the Russians said they weren't behind, as Ukrainians say they were.\par \par \uc2 Let's put it this way. A body of water the size of the Great Salt Lake in Utah is now streaming down in the southern part of the country and flooding it and could be the case now for months, some fear years to come. And the difficulties are just being compounded right now by the finger-pointing back and forth on both sides.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to lieutenant General Keith Kellogg, of course, the former national security adviser to \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 General, the magnitude of this is startling, and the impact on Ukraine just as they were making inroads, it would seem, in this counteroffensive, maybe too early to tell, startling. What do you make of it all?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. KEITH KELLOGG (RET.), FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, well, look, the Russians did it. I mean, if people don't believe the Russians did it, I will give you a -- I will sell you a bridge in Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: The reason they did that, though, and the reason why they're flooding the Dnipro River, is when you look at -- and I'm absolutely convinced this -- when you look at the counteroffensive that the Ukrainians were preparing to launch in full against the Russians, they were heading in the south from Kherson towards the Sea of Azov.\par \par \uc2 And if you took a ruler and drew that straight line, that would show you where they were heading to. And the reason they were heading to -- in that direction was, that would cut the Russian forces in Ukraine in half, and they could pivot left and they could pivot right, the Ukrainians, and fight a war of maneuver.\par \par \uc2 And they would beat the Russians at that. And it will also seal off Ukraine. What that water -- body of water now does, that creates a huge obstacle for the Ukrainians to try and maneuver against the Russian forces to the south. That's where the Russians were most vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 I think, still, the Ukrainians, when they go on the offense, will still have the advantage. It may take a little bit longer than they want to do it, because they can actually move that offensive north. But this shows the real desperation of the Russians. But the Russians did it.\par \par \uc2 And they did it for an operational reason. They don't care about people. They don't care about the damage was done to the environment or the people that lived in the city of Kherson or even in the people in Crimea, because that's where the majority of the water comes from. It goes to Crimea and helps them out.\par \par \uc2 So the Russians are just playing a game if they say they weren't involved. It was all designed for a military purpose. They have succeeded right now. I don't think they will succeed for the long term, but they're clearly afraid of the offensive in the south that will actually, I think -- if it succeeds, will defeat the Russian army in Ukraine. While\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: we're talking about Ukraine, General, I don't know what you make of a couple of the prominent Republican presidential candidates -- I know prominent, of course, distant seconds and below to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for the time being -- but saying that maybe we got to rethink our strategy in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Among them is \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , whom I chatted earlier today, who's saying that it's time to make major concessions to Russia, so the U.S. can move on to important matters like China. This kind of mirrors a lot of what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was saying, even though he dialed that back, that this was not indeed a border dispute, as he originally claimed.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But he has not dramatically done a 180 on that. What do you make of some of these rising candidates' responses to this?\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes, Neil, I think they're wrong.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think defeat of Russia is a strategic defeat, and it helps us. But until we can strategically, they, the Ukrainians, will help us defeat the Russians strategically. Then you have got two opponents you're going to be facing, the Chinese and the Russians. Let's take the Russians off the table.\par \par \uc2 And the Ukrainians have the ability to do that. But I think that just shows a real lack of strategic thinking moving forward with this. The Russians invaded. They're on the precipice of being defeated. Their forces have been mauled, their front-line units.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, the Russians are -- Neil, the Russians bringing tanks out of storage that were put into storage in 1980. I mean, these are tanks that have been in 42 years, the T-55 tank that was taken out of the inventory, 200,000 soldiers of the Soviets -- I'm sorry -- the Russians presumably dead and many, many were wounded. Their front-line units have been mauled.\par \par \uc2 Let's take them off the table as a strategic adversary. Then we can pivot to China. But for anybody to actually think you can actually placate Russia, it's a huge mistake. The only thing that Putin understands, Neil, is power, raw power. And we have an ability to help the Ukrainians defeat that.\par \par \uc2 And the center of gravity for Putin is his army. My frustration with this administration, we haven't given the Ukrainians everything they have wanted. They wanted the ATACMS. They didn't get them. They wanted good aircraft, like the F-16s. They're not going to have them. The M1A1 tank is still in Germany.\par \par \uc2 It hasn't been delivered, much like the Brits have delivered the Challenger and the Germans delivered the Leopards.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: We still haven't done it.\par \par \uc2 So I think we have helped them to a degree, but not as much as we should have.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: General, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime here, I did mention \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . He had even more compelling views on China, says that we have to get economically tough with China, even if it hits us hard in the U.S. of A -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  says it is time to fight the Chinese fire with fire, not military fire, business fire. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The reality is that we can make these sacrifices if we know what we're sacrificing for over the long run.\par \par \uc2 That is this thing we call America. And I think that the secret in geopolitics is, it's when you're most willing to make a sacrifice that you probably won't have to make one at all. What I have said is, we will cut the cord from China unless the CCP dramatically reforms its behaviors, including turning companies into geopolitical pawns, including requiring data of U.S. users for companies to enter the Chinese market.\par \par \uc2 That's the kind of reform I'm going to demand. Xi Jinping, Neil -- I think you may know this well -- has a tougher hand than we do right now, if we have the spine and courage to open our eyes up and actually see it.\par \par \uc2 So, I predict...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I definitely under -- I under...\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: ... if I'm sitting across the table from Xi Jinping, he will fold.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I understand that, Vivek.\par \par \uc2 I guess what I'm trying to say is, to play tough with China, it boomerangs back on us.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're quite right there are a lot of people and a lot of kowtowing going on right now with China. And there's a concern among a lot of U.S. businesses and titans, from \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , to \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 , who all say to a man that it would be risky and boomerang, to the degree that it hurts us just as much.\par \par \uc2 You don't agree with that?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I disagree with that. I disagree with that. First of all, I think they're making it out to be harder than it actually needs to be, because it serves their self-interest to say so.\par \par \uc2 I would -- and this is where I depart from Trump a little bit. I would reopen trade relationships with Japan, South Korea, India, Southeast Asian countries, Australia that put us in a strong position, from supply chain perspectives and otherwise, to actually stand with conviction across the table from Xi Jinping.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it's impossible to bring all of that to the United States on day one. I love bringing it to the United States, but we need to fill the gap with other Pacific trade partners, even Brazil, Mexico, and other trading partners too. But, if we do that, I think this becomes a lot more achievable than some of the self-interested business leaders that you named make it out to be to say that we can't cut that cord from China.\par \par \uc2 Then we have a strong hand in our possession. Then we stand with a spine across the table from Xi Jinping. I predict he will fold. But, even if he doesn't, we're ready to cut that cord, to think on the timescales of history, instead of on the timescales of two year election cycles or a quarterly earnings report.\par \par \uc2 I do think it's a national security risk for the United States to be dependent on our enemy for our modern way of life. Even when Reagan took on the USSR, they weren't putting the shoes on our feet or the phones in our pockets. That's the real risk we need to wake up to on the foreign policy grounds.\par \par \uc2 And I think I'm the leading candidate in the race who both understands it and has a very clear plan to deliver independence from China in a way that minimizes harm to Americans, but is also, as you said, Neil, honest with Americans, as I will be, that there's some short-term inconvenience we may incur, but that's what's worth it for actually having a long-run future for our next generation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  saying, essentially, it's worth the sacrifice paying higher prices, maybe a lot higher prices, for a lot of goods that come from China.\par \par \uc2 Are we up to that?\par \par \uc2 Gerry Baker, The Wall Street Journal editor at large.\par \par \uc2 Gerry, what do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 GERRY BAKER, HOST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL AT LARGE": Thanks for having me, Neil.\par \par \uc2 As I listened to that, it occurred to me that, when you know you don't have the slightest chance of actually being in the Oval Office, you can say just about anything you like, really, to get attention and to get -- to adopt the most extreme and, frankly, implausible positions. And that's the position that \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Look, I have got a lot of respect for Vivek. He's a very smart guy. He's opening up a lot of really important debates in this Republican primary, and I think that's really good. And he's taken on woke corporations better than anybody else. And I think that's all to be commended.\par \par \uc2 This idea of essentially a kind of economic blockade of China by the United States is, let's be honest, fanciful, to the point of lunacy. I mean, it's -- the U.S. and Chinese economies are integrated to a very great extent. You have talked about -- you raised with him some of the issues, obviously, some of the companies that do business in China, the concern they would have, the financial situation we have.\par \par \uc2 Remember, China is a huge holder of U.S. Treasuries. And the impact of a kind of China -- ban on Chinese -- U.S. companies doing business in China, what the Chinese might retaliate with, with that is, of course, extremely disturbing.\par \par \uc2 And on top of all that, Neil, I might say, he talks about opening up trade with other countries in the region, Japan, Korea, maybe India, others. Well, that's not something that a lot of people in this country are going to like.\par \par \uc2 Do we really want to say to American farmers, you're going to have to face competition from a lot of those East Asian companies or from a lot of U.S. companies, where -- U.S. manufacturing, which is coming back in some ways, do you want to face new competition from companies in places like Korea and India and Japan, which are traditionally -- have been traditionally quite anti-competitive in the way they behave?\par \par \uc2 So, look, it's a good way to raise the focus on the question of what kind of a relationship we want with China, a strategic adversary, but an economic -- certainly an economic partner, and we need to revisit that relationship.\par \par \uc2 But the idea that we could just simply ban American companies from doing business there is for the birds.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, if you think about it, though, Gerry, and I don't know what the answer is, what he's saying is, maybe China knows we won't do that presently in this environment.\par \par \uc2 And so China gets more bellicose and more provocative, knowing that the economic option, the business, the sanctions option is one that we're not about to consider because of the ramifications for us, like you said, with the Treasuries, which was an excellent point, and they know it, and they want to take full advantage.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAKER: I'm not sure -- but I'm not sure, Neil, what you achieve by shooting yourself in both feet in order to send a message to another country. It just seems so -- there's no need to do it.\par \par \uc2 Look, the U.S. is facing a long-term strategic challenge from China, no question. It needs to radically reorient its defense posture to make sure that it's much better equipped to deal with those challenges. It needs to improve its network of alliances.\par \par \uc2 It's done -- gone some way towards doing that, but it needs to do much more in the way that challenged the Soviet Union with the -- with \uc2 NATO\uc2 , with those kind of alliances in the Cold War. We have got a new cold war. We need to do something similar with China. There's all kinds of things we need to do, absolutely, to protect United States' interests from the threat from China.\par \par \uc2 We can do that. Oh, and, by the way, we also certainly want to make sure that our companies are not working with the Chinese government in ways that could be helpful to them. So, and we don't want -- this applies to technology and areas like that. We don't want the Chinese being able to listen into what we do. We should certainly take measures on that front.\par \par \uc2 But you can do all of this, you can make these really -- this really important strategic shift without, as I say, essentially, shooting -- I said shooting myself in both feet. You would be shooting yourself through the head with China if you do this approach.\par \par \uc2 So it's not necessary. It's good rhetoric. It's a good way to focus the topic on the strategic challenge from China, but it doesn't make any economic sense.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, next time, tell us how you really feel, Gerry.\par \par \uc2 It's always good to see you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Gerry Baker, Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Very restrained, for once.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, indeed. Indeed. I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal editor at large, Gerry Baker.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, \uc2 Martha Stewart\uc2  generating some controversy, not on that Sports Illustrated cover that's gotten a lot of global attention, but on doing home -- work from home. She says, enough is enough. Get back to work.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, the domestic diva says you can be a diva in your home. You can decorate and cook and do all of that. But if you're using it as a place to remotely work, get remotely and quickly back to work.\par \par \uc2 She's saying that it does more harm than good and thinks it's dangerous if we continue this whole half-time, sometime working from home.\par \par \uc2 I want to get the read from Danielle DiMartino Booth, what she makes of this.\par \par \uc2 Danielle, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE DIMARTINO BOOTH, FORMER FEDERAL RESERVE ADVISER: Neil, I'm right there with Martha.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, it was \uc2 Spotify\uc2 . Today, it was Reddit. We have seen people come out and do these quiet interviews that say, I don't have to work. I just have to kind of punch in the clock, punch out from my house. I can go do whatever I want, play a round of golf. Doesn't really matter.\par \par \uc2 Productivity was revised seriously downward. Big surprise that productivity was revised downward. But you know what? There's something to be said for higher productivity, making more money for your employer, having a better career trajectory yourself when you're inside of an office environment collaborating.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, though, for a while, we were being very productive through COVID. And I'm wondering if a lot of that just sort of petered out, and now people, some, not all, are taking it advantage of it.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that message that she's sending? Because I think that cuts to what she's really saying.\par \par \uc2 DIMARTINO BOOTH: And, again, I think that this is the whole point.\par \par \uc2 I have read not one or two, but a handful of interviews of people saying, I'm being paid quite a large amount of money, and I'm really not working. Just seeing the revenue levels of Silicon Valley companies, the profitability of Silicon Valley companies come down, and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  having to lay off 32 masseuses, who wouldn't want to go back into that office to go get a massage, Neil?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DIMARTINO BOOTH: I mean, that wasn't how it worked when I was first on Wall Street years ago.\par \par \uc2 But I think she's absolutely right, and there's something to be said for what our founding fathers envisioned. And that's great work ethic. That is part of the American ethos. And I think that we have wandered away from that to our detriment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, but our founding fathers, A, they didn't have masseuses back then, and they didn't have computers, so they could write all this stuff at home.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But leaving that aside, obviously, more companies are going this route, but there's a stubborn plurality of workers in this country, good or bad, who are not budging on this, hence the 10 million or so jobs that go begging.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 DIMARTINO BOOTH: Well, those jobless -- excuse me -- those job opening numbers are about the only ones that remain as optimistic as they are.\par \par \uc2 Other sources from \uc2 Indeed.com\uc2  suggest that there are much fewer job openings out there. And the fact that 91 percent of the U.S. population is living in a state where continuing claimants, the number of continuing claimants is rising as of last week's data, we're in full-blown recessionary territory when it comes to jobless claims.\par \par \uc2 So, you know what, Neil? The last chapter on these stubborn people has yet to be written. I hope they have got a lot in savings.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow. All right, Danielle, you're almost as tough on this as \uc2 Martha Stewart\uc2  herself.\par \par \uc2 Danielle DiMartino Booth. You do not mess with Danielle. You probably don't mess with \uc2 Martha Stewart\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Elizabeth Warren with Republican J.D. Vance of Ohio with a common goal: Stop these bank crises from happening by going after the bankers themselves who cause it -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, J.D. Vance said he would be a different type of senator, living true to form, saying that he could carve out some bipartisan moves to deal with issues that have been a big problem, like the banking problems we have seen, working with Elizabeth Warren -- that's right, Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren -- on an effort right now to avoid future bank collapses by going after the source and the reason behind those collapses, that many of them get too far and go after the executives who got them going too far.\par \par \uc2 Senator Vance kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 Senator, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH): Thanks, Neil. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Explain how this would work, Senator.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes, so, the basic idea is that, if you think about the \uc2 FDIC\uc2  taking a bank into receivership as effectively implicitly putting taxpayers on the hook, it allows the \uc2 FDIC\uc2  to go and claw back compensation that executives paid themselves as they were running their banks into the ground.\par \par \uc2 This is specifically motivated, Neil, by the \uc2 SVB\uc2  situation, where you had a bank that really was terribly mismanaged, a CEO paying himself millions of dollars in bonuses in the weeks and years running up to that collapse. And yet, when the taxpayers got put on the hook, they had no recourse.\par \par \uc2 And what I'm really worried about here, Neil, is not revenge. I could care less about the \uc2 SVB\uc2  CEO at the end of the day. What I do care about is whether we have a banking system where the small banks are playing by a different set of rules than the big banks. Everybody knows a community bank in the state of Ohio, they're going to suffer the consequences if they get put into receivership.\par \par \uc2 But if you can pay yourself fat bonuses by running yourself -- your bank into the ground at a big bank, that's a problem. And it's a problem we got to fix.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Senator, what happens, though, if it starts a selling contagion among bank stocks, which we have seen sporadically over the last few months?\par \par \uc2 Would you be just as tough on managements of those banks that were sort of caught up in the whirlwind?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, I think we need to be tough on the banks, Neil, because it makes these financial institutions ultimately more cautious.\par \par \uc2 The big problem in the American financial system is that we have socialized risk and socialized downside, but we have privatized reward. So if these CEOs know that they're going to benefit from what is effectively a bailout, whether it's the uninsured depositors or even the shareholders and executives of the banks, they're going to take more risk and they're going to expose our financial system to more danger in the process.\par \par \uc2 That is a bigger problem than just this piece of legislation. But this legislation does fix one tiny part of that issue.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, whatever happens to these small-, medium-sized banks -- and I get where you're coming from and where Elizabeth Warren is coming from, sir -- but I do wonder about banking itself, and that we -- after the meltdown, we were told that no bank could be too big to fail.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, they're getting bigger, those banks, and it's possible that they will get bigger still, at the expense of smaller, medium-sized banks. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, it's a huge problem, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We really can't overstate this enough, that one of the things that makes our country special, unique among the advanced economies is that we have this really robust, small and medium-sized banking ecosystem. The problem is, because of that implied bailout, because the big banks know they're going to get bailed out by the taxpayers, but these smaller and regional banks don't have the same promise, it makes it harder for those guys to do business.\par \par \uc2 It increases their overall cost of capital. It sometimes even means they're sharing more of the regulatory burden, even though they're not the ones who are going to ultimately benefit from that bailout. So we have to do that. We have to protect that system.\par \par \uc2 Look, some of these small banks are making loans to customers and to small businesses that big banks would never touch. It's a core part of American entrepreneurship to preserve our banking ecosystem. And I think this is one legislation that solves that problem, or at least makes the problem a little bit better.\par \par \uc2 One of the things we're going to have to tackle, though, Neil, long term is this implicit promise of bailout and the fact that it's there for the big guys, but it's not there for the little guys.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. And if you know that, so you're backstopped, you will go ahead and do a lot of things, because you know that support is there.\par \par \uc2 Senator Vance, very good to see you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 Separately, we did reach out to Elizabeth Warren to explain why she is working with the good senator on this. We have yet to hear back, but we're always -- we're always hopeful, fair and balanced, as always.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc