 President Biden's Oval Office Address. What could he be talking about there?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Did you just find out about that when you read that?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Five minutes ago. Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. So, there is this lovely story about this high school principal -- enjoy your donuts -- getting the shock of her life when she went to make her breakfast. She walked down to find her entire senior class sleeping in the floor of her kitchen in a living room. And this was like the surprise of her life because they came out of every nook and cranny. This is the senior class at St. Andrews in Middletown, Delaware. They pulled off this prank with little help from Mrs. McGrath's husband who helped them sneak into the house 1:00 a.m., wait until she woke up, surprised her. And this video has gone viral. It's been viewed 32 million.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Is that like a good thing?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What if she came in the kitchen naked?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I mean, that would have been really --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What if she was doing something even more embarrassing?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: With her husband.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: I love that.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Or somebody else.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Or her wife. I mean, who knows?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We don't want to judge.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: That is my house most weekends, kids sleeping over. So, earlier this week, a Hawaiian tourist drove her car into what she thought was a puddle. It turns out it was down the boat ramp into a harbor while following her GPS. She said she mistook the sea for a puddle. Luckily, good Samaritans came to her rescue and keep telling her get out, get out, get out, and she did. The same thing happened actually the week before to someone else.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, what do you know?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Oh, that's mine.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: That's you?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Wait, there's something in common with these videos.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What were they?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't know, Jesse. I can't see it.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Someone connect the dots.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's water, I believe. That's water.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I think it's water. Yes, it's definitely water is the common denominator.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We'll see you guys back here on Monday. Have a great weekend, everybody.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020230603ej6200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Businesses Struggling to Hire Teen Summer Workers; Tropical Storm Arlene Churns in Gulf of Mexico; Interview With Former U.S. Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 ; China Tensions; President Biden Set to Sign Debt Ceiling Deal; \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  Expected to Announce Presidential Bid; Stocks Surge on Strong Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 Charles Payne, Lydia Hu, Katie Byrne, Rick Reichmuth, Jennifer  Griffin, Chad Pergram, Rich Edson, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,207 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 June 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: A rally based on a job recovery that looks far, far stronger than anyone thought. We had stocks racing, the Dow up more than 700 points on a jobs report that pretty much stunned everybody, 339,000 more of those jobs.\par \par \uc2 And the fact of the matter is that it continues a churn here that is surprising those who thought, by now, we'd be looking at a recession. Anything but. The administration is crowing about this, but we're stepping back, taking a look at a jobs report that normally would have people fearful the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is going to be inclined to start hiking rates again in the face of all of this.\par \par \uc2 But, again, that was not a worry for the markets, maybe because, this time, with a strong jobs report like that, there is a lot less worry about a recession and a lot more hope that we can dodge one altogether.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto, because you have got that juxtaposed against Republican candidates crisscrossing battleground states, talking about how awful things are. So there is a bit of a disconnect. So we're following the principal ones and what they have to say, not only about the economy and where we're going, but that debt ceiling package.\par \par \uc2 By the way, relief that we even have a package and that we avoided default also fed into this rally today.\par \par \uc2 But let's go to Rich Edson in Pella, Iowa, how that is going down there -- Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 You Have got a couple of Republican candidates in Iowa today. Many more coming tomorrow for Senator \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2 'S Roast and Ride in Des Moines, about an hour drive here from Pella.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is also going to be at that Roast and Ride event. He's finishing up a full day of campaigning in South Carolina today. The governor was courting voters in New Hampshire and Iowa over the past couple of days before that. He's making the case that Republicans need a two-term president. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would only get one more term.\par \par \uc2 Trump argues he could turn the country around in the first six months of another term. DeSantis says that's nonsense.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Don't let anyone tell you they can do this in 24 hours or in six months or anything like that. This is going to be trench warfare.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Trump wrapped his Iowa trip last night with a town hall on Hannity. He's largely focused his criticism on DeSantis, though last night broadened it out to some of the other Republicans, asking, what's the purpose of some of them getting into the race, like former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , former Arkansas Governor \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 , who he calls Ada Hutchinson.\par \par \uc2 We caught up with Asa Hutchinson here in Pella, where he responded.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think it means that he's taken my candidacy seriously, so I take it as a high compliment, and there's probably going to be more of that as time goes on as we continue to rise in the polls.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Hutchinson is going to be at that Roast and Ride tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 He also welcomed some of the other Republican candidates to this race. You have got former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , North Dakota Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  all going to be announcing that they are entering this race next week -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Rich.\par \par \uc2 Rich Edson following all of that.\par \par \uc2 Amie Parnes joins us right now, the chief White House correspondent. senior political correspondent.\par \par \uc2 I'm wondering, Amie, looking at all of this, what you make of the running around the country, these Republican candidates, of course, talking about how bad things are economically. We're going to get into this more with Doug Schoen, who polls people on these issues, but this notion that maybe they have to redirect their strategy here.\par \par \uc2 What if we're coming out of what we're coming out of and that the job backdrop is stronger than these candidates are acknowledging? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 AMIE PARNES, THE MESSENGER: Well, I think that they're going to have to make inflation an issue. I think that a lot of Republicans feel like they have President Biden on his heels on that one particular issue.\par \par \uc2 They can kind of point to that. So I think they're going to continue to kind of say that a lot of Americans aren't comfortable with where they are right now, and I can make it better.\par \par \uc2 But they're going to have to make the case. I know President Biden is sort of waiting for that, for them to sort of pounce on that, and he has a response about how the economy has continued to get better. So I think you're going to hear a lot of this push and pull, but they're going to have to first compete against each other, which will be the toughest part here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amie, when I look at some of these numbers for the former president, he leads nationally. I don't know how it sorts out in the individual battleground stage, but he leads there as well.\par \par \uc2 Iowa is an area and a place that folks like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  think they can make up for lost ground. Even the former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  has said much the same. How doable is that? Because we forget that these are done a caucus at a primary at a time.\par \par \uc2 PARNES: I think it's very doable.\par \par \uc2 And I point to the 2008 race all the time on the Democratic side, where everyone expected Hillary Clinton to be the front-runner. Again, they expected her to be the front-runner in 2016. And then along come these outsiders, like a Barack Obama or Bernie Sanders.\par \par \uc2 I think you're going to see -- I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is kind of positioning himself to be the underdog, to make the case. When you talk to his people, they tell you it's very early. He just got into the race. He's just introducing himself to people right now, and he is going to be able to climb in the polls right now.\par \par \uc2 And that's what you're seeing him do. In Iowa, he's closer than he is nationally, so he's speaking about his record in Florida and how he was able to win by 18 percentage points. He's pointing to the fact that he has this six-week abortion ban. It's popular with evangelicals there.\par \par \uc2 I think you're going to continue to see him make that case, particularly in Iowa. He's not talking so much about that in New Hampshire, where it isn't as popular.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will follow all of that, Amie.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Doug Schoen on this.\par \par \uc2 And, again, we're talking about how the markets are up better than 701 points, the fact that we have this job surge going on, and the administration has been pointing that out. One thing that's interesting, Doug, is, if you go back to the, it's the economy, stupid, then are we characterizing it stupidly?\par \par \uc2 That there are pockets of weakness, no doubt, that there is inflation, no doubt, but jobs remain strong. The administration still pushing that and still saying, when all is said and done, we have gained more jobs for the American people in about 28 months than any president has in a four-year period.\par \par \uc2 That's what they're pushing. That's no doubt tonight what the president will be saying. Is it changing the game plan?\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FORMER BILL CLINTON POLLSTER: Well, it could, Neil.\par \par \uc2 This is one jobs report. Admittedly, we have had a couple of better-than- expected jobs reports, but this is one jobs report that is really very much contrary to what we all expected. That being said, I would go back to what Amie said.\par \par \uc2 There's still inflation. There are high interest rates. People are having a tough time making a go of it. So I'm not ready to pronounce the issue of the economy a Democratic win issue. But it's certainly the case that it feeds the narrative that the president and his team has been trying to advance.\par \par \uc2 And if there is, as we anticipate, at least a temporary pause on interest rates going up this month, it will provide further impetus for that narrative.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, that probably was one of the things that was involved in the market run-up today and these 13 million jobs that have come back since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office.\par \par \uc2 Now, many cynics, Republicans particularly, can say, well, that's a snapback from people who are getting back to work after COVID shutdowns. There's an element of truth to that, no doubt. But the administration is trying to point out that you can't have a recession with this many jobs added. And they're going to keep pounding that.\par \par \uc2 Perception is not reality. Reality is what you see happening in the jobs. The reality is the comeback you're seeing happening in the markets. Will that stick? Because I can remember, with \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , even though we were coming out of the recession during that year, in 1992, it was too little, too late for \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , and he ended up losing, even though, in retrospect, the economy really was turning the bend.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Well, certainly, there is a chance, Neil, that you are absolutely correct.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the ratings until today that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's gotten on the economy have not been good. They have been very, very alarming for the Democrats. But, that being said, I participated, as I know you know, in '96, when we were able to take the good economic news for \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , based on his bipartisanship on the debt and the deficit and the budget, and make a case of both unity and progress that...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that was really undebatable back then, if you think about it. No matter where you were in the politics then, that was an uphill battle for \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2  to have to face him in that environment.\par \par \uc2 Here, it's open to debate, as you said. I mean, the jobs picture is strong, but the inflation picture is pretty worrisome. And you mix that with people who are not returning to work, a lot of them are reluctant to do that for a variety of reasons. So it's not a slam dunk.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering, for the candidates now who worry about a delayed impact of this type of news, that do they say, all right, well, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's got another 18 months or so to see this stick and gain ground and gain traction. He's got time, not something that George H.W. Bush had going for him back in 1992.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, two things. There's a primary here, and Republican primary voters have no patience for or sympathy for the Biden administration. So, the Republicans like DeSantis and Trump and the other six or seven candidates will campaign stridently and strongly against the Biden administration through the nominating process, certainly through Iowa, New Hampshire, South Carolina, and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 But you make a good point, Neil. If things start to change, and if Biden does one of the other things \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  did, which was to advance the bipartisanship that was built into the debt ceiling legislation, and make the case that he is unifying the country and growing the economy, then it could be tougher for the Republicans than they would anticipate as they woke up this morning.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, and because both parties can play that, and working off the Kevin McCarthy success and say, it's all him and we're bipartisan and we can do that.\par \par \uc2 And yet you have hard-liners who say, no, no, no, no, there's nothing bipartisan about it. So the back-and-forth continues.\par \par \uc2 Doug, thank you very much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime, we are exploring this connection between Wall Street and Main Street and what's happening in Iowa and New Hampshire and in South Carolina, a special "Cavuto Live" tomorrow beginning at 10:00 a.m., where we crisscross with the candidates and who is saying what when it comes to being the better shepherds of this economy.\par \par \uc2 More than a few of them are saying that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is too old, too feeble, and they're going to be referring to a certain video again and again and looping it to emphasize their point. Will that work? Because video and pictures and images can sometimes give you the sort of sense of reality. And is that happening to one \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 When we come back, do you think he will be debated if he enters this race, as he's expected to do next week, for what he did eight years in the bluest of states as a Republican governor, or for what he did and didn't do and the friendship he had and then suddenly didn't have with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Or is it going to be over something very silly and stupid, like his weight?\par \par \uc2 Fat chance he will be treated fairly. We will explore.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don't know why people are doing it. They're at 1 percent. Some are at zero. I hear \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 's coming in. He's at...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (BOOING)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: He was at 6 percent in New Jersey, which is -- I love New Jersey, but 6 percent approval rating in New Jersey. What's the purpose?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It wasn't that long ago where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  thought very, very highly of \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 . But, all of a sudden, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  throwing some zingers his way, he's persona non grata.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things we're exploring here, regardless where you feel politically or personally, one thing I noticed that's been a trend -- and maybe it was illustrated in this \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  tweet, where he does, obviously, an inflated picture of the former New Jersey governor, saying that, "\uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  needs to run, not for president. He just needs to run."\par \par \uc2 Now, fat jokes are common here. I sometimes get one or two myself.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, I'm fat.\par \par \uc2 Now, my point here, though, is that this has come up a lot in \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 's career. Now, I'm a New Jerseyan. He was governor for two terms in New Jersey. I think you can arguably say he was a pretty effective governor.\par \par \uc2 We can say that, obviously, he capped property taxes, better than 400 vetoes to stop tax increases of any type in the state, widely touted as a promising presidential candidate, but always with a reference back to the weight, like this "TIME" magazine cover, the elephant in the room, the famous beat shot.\par \par \uc2 If he were a thin governor at the time, would that shot have even materialized? I personally doubt it. So, my point here on this is, get \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  on all the stuff you might not like, or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  might not like, or how he was a friend then he wasn't a friend, how he questioned \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and then, all of a sudden, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  questioned him, the timing of his presidential entrance and whether he can make a difference, but don't make it about the weight.\par \par \uc2 And I feel very, very strongly about that, because I just hope we don't go off course this election year. It might be too late for that.\par \par \uc2 But Sarah Bedford joins us of The Washington Examiner.\par \par \uc2 And, Sarah, part of my concern here with this \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  attack line is, it's invariably about the weight, how he looks. And I notice and I hope we live in a country where we don't let the color of someone's skin affect our coverage, but it seems like it's open field if they have a lot of skin.\par \par \uc2 And that's what bugs me here. But that is very clear in this attack line. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 SARAH BEDFORD, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Yes, it's certainly not something new to \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 . He's been dealing with scrutiny of his weight throughout his time in public life.\par \par \uc2 But it certainly is the sort of thing that should be off-limits in a primary. It wasn't that long ago that there were certain standards of decorum in politics. You didn't go after people's kids, for example. You didn't go after their wives.\par \par \uc2 A lot of that has been thrown out the window over the past 10 years or so. And so while it's a place that candidates shouldn't go, I would expect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  probably to go there. He had no trouble in the 2016 primary getting really personal with his opponents, including Christie. I mean, he made fun of the appearance of Ted Cruz's wife, called her ugly.\par \par \uc2 He mocked some of the more harrowing elements of Ben Carson's really tragic upbringing as a young child of a single mother. So, I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has shown he's willing to cross those boundaries. That's one of the reasons why there is some fatigue with him among voters. But I don't expect that any of the other GOP candidates will really go there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I'm all for, to your point, Sarah, going after someone on inconsistencies.\par \par \uc2 In the case of Christie, he was competing with him, bowed out of the race, supported \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , then unsupported him. And, of course, the January 6 thing, that did it. And, of course, the two have never seen eye to eye since. We know he's going to be the attack dog here.\par \par \uc2 But all that's well and good to get into those substantive issues, but this is the last bastion of prejudice I just don't like to see. I dare say a Winston Churchill couldn't survive in this environment. Even Grover Cleveland, the President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to emulate as the first president to serve two non-consecutive terms, he was a pretty big guy.\par \par \uc2 Now, I don't know if his weight was an issue back when he was president twice, but it's very clear that, if \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  gathers some steam, it will be an issue now.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: It could be for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I mean, you're right that there's a whole lot of other problems with \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 's candidacy that didn't even exist the last time. He had a pretty disappointing run, and he has a lot more baggage now, and he's even further past his so-called political prime, when he was coming off the governorship.\par \par \uc2 So I think that there's a lot of fodder there for candidates who want to go after him. The media also doesn't have a lot of credibility to bring up the weight issue either, because we have been told for the past countless cycles that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's obvious health problems are untouchable, that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's health problems in 2016 couldn't be mentioned, that every day that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's in public, basically, we're not to question his physical fitness for office.\par \par \uc2 So, even in the context of health precluding someone from doing the job of president well, I think \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  is clearly more able-bodied than our octogenarian class that we have in power right now. I don't expect it'll be a big issue.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you mentioned the big issue here.\par \par \uc2 The fact of the matter is, he did handle the state through Sandy. A lot of people probably remember maybe that was the year he should have run, but, you know, push charter schools, the same kind of things that Republicans are pushing now, and, again, for my money, looking back at it, two terms of being a very successful governor.\par \par \uc2 So, debate that, have at that. It's ancient news to a lot of people. Now, it's been 10 years, or close to that, when all of this was going on and he was breaking all these precedents. But I'd hate to see it defined through the prism of someone you don't like because you think they're fat.\par \par \uc2 I just think, by that metric, a lot of people would be passed over, and a lot of people, dare I say it, could never become TV anchors. But that's a whole 'nother story.\par \par \uc2 How personal do you think this gets?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: There's a lot of personal animosity between Trump and \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  that has nothing to do with policy.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they had a very public falling out during the last year of Trump's presidency.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: And so, even when there isn't that bad blood, Trump has shown a willingness to really go for someone's weak spots, and that's just an obvious low-hanging fruit one for Christie.\par \par \uc2 But add to the fact that you have that personal animosity that runs so deep, it could get really nasty. The only thing that could really shield \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , though, is that he's not considered a top rival to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and he's really busy going after \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . He might be too distracted to go that aggressively after \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , at least at first.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, we will see how it all falls out here.\par \par \uc2 But, normally, if you're going to criticize someone for being fat, you better make sure that you are an Adonis yourself.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, if you're not, maybe don't go that way. That's why I never tell people, eat the salad, because I don't like salad.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very -- you're the best, Sarah. I appreciate it, in indulging our fixation on this stuff.\par \par \uc2 I hate it when we do that. It so bastardizes the process, trivializes it. We are better than that. A lot better.\par \par \uc2 All right, when we come back, is our country now the better for a debt deal that's done and ready to be signed? What if I told you, not according to one ratings agency that still could pull a surprise on us? I'm all over that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Debt storm solved, now a real storm coming.\par \par \uc2 The first named one of the hurricane season, it just started this week. What's going on off the Gulf Coast or could threaten the Gulf Coast?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, debt deal done. It looks to be signed by the president in short order. He's going to address the nation tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now, I hate to interrupt the party and the market party, and everyone delighted about that. We got all of those unknowns out of the way. Talk that \uc2 Fitch\uc2  investors, the ratings folks, still have us on a negative credit watch, which means they could still downgrade us.\par \par \uc2 That is exactly what happened back in 2011, when we avoided defaults and all of that, but still saw S&P downgrade our debt from AAA. We haven't recovered from that one, but now we could be facing this.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill on how all this is going down -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, ratings agency \uc2 Fitch\uc2  says it's still keeping the government on negative watch. As you say, the agency does not like brinksmanship over the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are thundering over scoring 46 yeas for the bill last night. Meantime, Republicans could only cough up 17.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): More votes because the bill beat back the worst of the Republican agenda. This exercise was a -- basically, this was an exercise in where the American people were at, and they're much closer to where we are than where they are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: There was a higher percentage of GOP no's in the Senate, compared to the House.\par \par \uc2 Many Senate Republicans embraced the bill the House approved in April.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): I did not like the fact that it had a date certain it would end and not a dollar amount on our debt ceiling raise.\par \par \uc2 And if you give somebody a date certain or a time limit, what are they going to do? They're going to pack as much as they can into that period of time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The GOP may have scored cuts, but it's a shadow of what the party wanted. Democrats seized on that point.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PETER WELCH (D-VT): I think negotiating early, negotiating middle, negotiating late wouldn't have made any difference whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 The fact is, from what McCarthy and the House Republicans started with, compared to what the outcome is here, it's a whimper.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The president will not sign the bill today. Prepping the bill takes time.\par \par \uc2 Clerks at the Capitol must copy the bill onto parchment paper. Why parchment? It's a custom dating back to the Declaration of Independence once all 13 colonies signed -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the ink doesn't disappear, does it? I mean...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: That's right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Where is that deal?\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you. A couple of the more raucous members of the Freedom Caucus not at all pleased with this deal, but going so far, a couple of them at least, to call back the speaker. How serious is that?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Well, there's a vocal minority, but they lack the votes.\par \par \uc2 Ken Buck\uc2  wants to have a conversation about this next week. Never before has there been an effort to oust a speaker mid-Congress. There's an old saying. If you come at the king, you better not miss. The next signpost for Kevin McCarthy comes in September. That's when Congress must fund the government -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it, my friend. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And good luck with the parchment thing.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram, hasn't he been phenomenal in this whole thing? Just incredible.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, you have heard a lot about, in the Senate, most of the Republican senators were very uncomfortable voting for this. A Good many did, but one did not, said that this was fine. We will ask Oklahoma Senator \uc2 Markwayne Mullin\uc2  why he was a yes on that and why he thinks the better part to valor is to move on, they got what they wanted, and Republicans should be grateful.\par \par \uc2 So, we will follow that tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, I want to go to Jennifer Griffin, because some startling developments that are quickly going to make you forget the whole debt ceiling mess with now a renewed China mess -- Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Neil, they say they're simply Chinese tourists here to see the Northern Lights. The \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  thinks otherwise.\par \par \uc2 I will have the details coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Boy, with China, you take what