BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. DAN SULLIVAN (R-AK): Any spy balloon, Russian Bear bombers or this new reporting of suspected Chinese spies in Alaska, this is another wakeup call that we are in a new era of authoritarian aggression led by the dictators in China and Russia.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  director, \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 , says the \uc2 FBI\uc2  opens a new Chinese government espionage case in the U.S. every 12 hours -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's getting pretty scary.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 Want to go right down to the former defense secretary of the United States under President Trump, Dr. \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 Secretary, I'm just wondering. These incidents or the revelations over them keep happening, and we're grateful if we get a handshake with a Chinese official. It just seems sort of lopsided and weird.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 , FORMER U.S. DEFENSE SECRETARY: Yes, and Neil, it's not new, as Jennifer said.\par \par \uc2 Look, when I was secretary of the Army in 2018, I visited our sites up in Alaska and was told stories of similar type of incidents, where so-called Chinese tourists were found outside the gates of our military installation. So this has been going on for years.\par \par \uc2 And, as was said, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  opens up an espionage investigation every 12 hours. It's just nonstop.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what were the tourists doing?\par \par \uc2 I'm sorry, Doctor, but what were the tourists doing there? How did they explain they were there?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: It was not explainable, frankly. And I didn't go into that much detail at the time.\par \par \uc2 But they were taking pictures and said they apparently got lost. Now, how you get lost in the middle of Alaska...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: ... when you're talking about single roads, it just is really astounding.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But these threats of brazen activity is not new. We could talk about the balloons, other incidents of balloons, even fresh activity going on as we speak, this very, very provocative action the part of a Chinese fighter jet over the South China Sea with an American plane, more and more of these with abandon.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: The bad behavior is increasing.\par \par \uc2 Look, we're in this new era of global competition with the Chinese. Their aims are to dominate the Indo-Pacific and eventually the world and install their values, their norms to not have democracy, all those types of things. So we need to be pushing back.\par \par \uc2 We're going to continue to see Chinese spy balloons, espionage, theft of our intellectual property, police stations, all these things go on and on and on, and we just need to take it very seriously. And that's why, on the economic front, I like what we're doing with regard to exports and looking at a version of strategic decoupling, some strategic derisking, and other things to make sure that we are not aiding their military buildup.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But we know that we have this economic relationship, and it seems we're very reluctant, save some sanctions on certain companies here and there, to disturb that.\par \par \uc2 Now, I understand why. Obviously, if you punish China economically, it does boomerang on us and goods from China become a lot more expensive. But aren't we at the stage now, Secretary, where we have to prepare the American people for that, that this is so bad, we might have to endure that to deal with this?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And, look, the Chinese have a grand plan. We know that, in 2015, they put forward, for example, a China -- Made in China 2025, which said explicitly that they want to dominate in 10 key sectors. And the way they're going to go about doing that is through stealing our intellectual property through espionage and through other things.\par \par \uc2 That's why I think we need to look at a strategic decoupling of the really top-tier items. Think about 5G, semiconductors, high-end jet engines, space items, things like that, and then derisk a next tier of items down where we cannot afford to be dependent on the Chinese for our economic security and prosperity.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will watch it closely.\par \par \uc2 Secretary, good catching up with you, as always.\par \par \uc2 So we're looking at that.\par \par \uc2 We're also looking at this hurricane, or tropical storm, for the time being, the first named storm of the season. We begin near the top of the Alphabet here. This is called Arlene for the time being. Where she goes, how she churns, and how bad it gets through the Gulf of Mexico, we don't know.\par \par \uc2 But we will get a better idea right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Always excited for my Saturday show, but did you hear about this, a Florida professor known as Dr. Deep Sea breaking the world record for the most consecutive days underwater without depressurization? How would that go?\par \par \uc2 Now, he's closing on day 100. And he's going to join me tomorrow morning on "Cavuto Live" 10:00 a.m. Eastern time from under the sea and tell us what the whole experience was like, what he wants to do with this. That is a long time. I just wonder how you get all discombobulated from that. We shall see. Looking forward to that tomorrow at 10:00 a.m.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, I always look forward to having Rick Reichmuth on. He can make sense of the weather and the craziness from very hot New York today.\par \par \uc2 We have got this storm churning off the Gulf Coast. So, the hurricane season, storm season is on, I guess, right, Rick?\par \par \uc2 RICK REICHMUTH, FOX NEWS CHIEF METEOROLOGIST: Right, it is. And I can't wait to watch your interview tomorrow. That's going to be fascinating. Amazing experiment that the guy's doing.\par \par \uc2 You're right. We got Tropical Storm Arlene. This is a storm. Normally, we see storms move kind of north or to the east, this storm actually not going in that direction at all. It's going to move off to the south here. As it does, it's going to hit some really hostile winds and break it apart.\par \par \uc2 So we don't have any real significant concerns from our first named storm of the season, other than some enhanced rainfall amounts across parts of Florida that's had a really rainy couple of weeks, and so maybe some localized flooding from that.\par \par \uc2 Now let's talk about the hurricane season, which started this week, on Thursday, June 1, day one of hurricane season. And there's a few things that we're watching for this year to see if we see some more activity. One of them is, you're going to hear us talk a lot about El Nino. El Nino is where we get warmer waters here up across the coast of South America, and we have that in place.\par \par \uc2 We're likely -- not fully in effect yet, but we're probably going to see a significant El Nino event. When that happens, it makes for some really hostile upper-level winds. Hostile upper-level winds just break apart storms as they're trying to form. So, generally, you would say this is probably going to be a less active season than usual.\par \par \uc2 All that said, so this warm Pacific dimension brings -- strongly suppresses storms. But when you have a warm Atlantic, that strongly enhances storms. And the Atlantic is also, Neil, really warm right now, well above average. In fact, overall, our entire ocean basins cumulatively are the warmest that they have ever been, but including that there in the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 So, now we have got one force that should oppose any development or much development, one that's going to enhance it. So what does that do for this season? For right now, we're just saying kind of right down the middle. We will see which one of those forces wins throughout this season, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Rick, you are scary smart. You know, there's smart, and then there's scary smart.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know your stuff. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 REICHMUTH: Yes, you bet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Have a good weekend, my friend.\par \par \uc2 REICHMUTH: You too.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Rick Reichmuth on that.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Katie Byrne right now, the FOX Weather multimedia journalist, because we got all of these different temperatures all over the country. In the Northeast, they have been dealing with the heat. I don't know for how long.\par \par \uc2 But what can you tell us, Katie?\par \par \uc2 KATIE BYRNE, FOX MULTIMEDIA JOURNALIST: Well, it's sticky out here. It's so hot that some kids in Philly actually have an early start to the weekend.\par \par \uc2 The school district decided on an early dismissal for about 90 schools, because either they don't have A.C. or need electrical updates. Here in Philly, we are over 90 degrees. It has some people soaking up the sun today, because they know there's a big temperature swing coming for us.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, it'll be 20 degrees cooler. So, when you look at the hot spots in the Northeast today, where the heat's already broken records, a number of them broken, Baltimore, Poughkeepsie, New York, and Harrisburg, Pennsylvania, among the hottest, in the mid-90s.\par \par \uc2 Here in Philly, we have inched towards our record. The number to beat is 94 degrees. At last check, we were at 93. And I'm feeling a breeze right now, so I'm not sure that we're going beat it, but I don't want to jinx anything -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Man, oh, man.\par \par \uc2 All right, try to stay cool through all of that, Katie Byrne.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, you're not imagining it. When you go to restaurants, you go to stores, not too many kids working, not too many teenagers taking advantage of all these jobs that are available. And it got us wondering, why is that?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So many jobs for kids, teenagers, and the like, but so few taking them up on it.\par \par \uc2 Lydia Hu who has more on that phenomenon in Seaside Heights, New Jersey -- Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 New Jersey is expecting to see tourism levels that surpass -- this year that surpassed the levels we saw back in 2019. So that's great news for the state, but it's putting some pressure on businesses and destination cities like Seaside Heights, because the businesses tell us they don't have the seasonal workers that they need.\par \par \uc2 So, now New Jersey is trying to help, the state, that is. It's one of a handful of states that's enacting some changes to child labor laws. They are letting teenagers, 16- and 17-year-olds, now work longer weeks, up to 50 hours in a week, to try to give some extra boost to the businesses that need the help. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARIA SALTZMAN, MARKETING DIRECTOR, CASINO PIER: We need people to work long hours. So it definitely helps us, but we kind of still need those -- the 14- and 15-year-olds be able to work longer hours as well.\par \par \uc2 However, the 16-plus, it's fantastic that they can work even longer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: And so that manager here says it's starting to help.\par \par \uc2 But the critics also have concerns here. They say changes to the labor laws that have been made, it could undermine the protections that have been put in place for minors. But, of course, the proponents say, hey, it serves a purpose. It helps the businesses, and it also gives the kids a chance to make some extra money, which maybe they could spend on places like the Jersey Boardwalk.\par \par \uc2 Maybe they could play an arcade game like this. I'm going to go for this lion here, Neil. Let's see if 