ery fragile, particularly the men, the young boys, the young teenagers.\par \par \uc2 And I don't understand it. There are jobs out there. You look at these TikTok videos, everybody's sporting some new gear.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Everyone wants to have their nice fit. Everyone's like, hey, check out what I have got.\par \par \uc2 So they want things. And I just -- I can't figure it out. I really can't.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know what's so weird too? And, I mean, you and I were brought at a time we had to get a job, whether we wanted to or not.\par \par \uc2 And now the draw, these eye popping hourly rates. I mean, there's a fast- food place by me, I think they're close to 20 bucks an hour, and they can't find -- they can't find workers.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And it's...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I'm just thinking, in my day...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... when Abe Lincoln was president, that was a slam dunk, and it's not happening. I don't know why.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I didn't even know the term -- what the term minimum wage was. I know I was earning it, but I had never even heard of it. I just -- the only term I knew was wage. Wage. I got money.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I went in, I flipped some burgers, and they gave me money for doing it.\par \par \uc2 And then, at the end of the week, I went down and I went to Delancey Street and I went to 116th Street, and I haggled for new clothes to go to school.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But I bought them with my own money.\par \par \uc2 And you're right, the money that these kids are making right now is exponentially more they could make. Do things. You're not going to college. You're not doing anything.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: This is really the mind-boggling part that I can't -- I cannot connect, because you do want things. Everyone's on social media sporting all their new stuff, which, by the way, I'm not sure how everybody's getting it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's true.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But they figured something out maybe we didn't, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, but I do distinctly remember my boss would gouge me. I worked at fast food. I worked at a hamburger joint. I was a waiter.\par \par \uc2 But I always said, all right, even at the hamburger joint, if you're not going to pay me enough money, I'm just going to make up for it by eating all your product.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you're going to lose money on me.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But even that is not a draw.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And even the seaside place that you just saw in the previous piece, that's a fun place for a kid to work. No takers.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I remember going down there when I was in high school. It was a lot of fun. And, listen, I worked at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 , right? I was on the Quarter Pounder grill, and I would make so many extra Quarter Pounders that they had no choice but to let me take them home, Neil.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I had a strategy, my man.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I pulled that trick. I did pull that trick.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But we have these 10-plus million jobs that go wanting in this country. So maybe it's a bigger phenomena than just kids.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, although the labor participation rate for 25-to-50-year-olds finally got above the pre-pandemic level.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We're still a long ways from where the record was, but nowhere near.\par \par \uc2 I'm talking -- I mean, think about this for a moment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quick.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Participation, 59 percent all the way down to 36 percent. Something is wrong with that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it's amazing.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, we will get through it, buddy. But I don't know how.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We will.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know how.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We will.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm going to eat. All right, that's all I'm going to do.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230603ej6200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 House To Vote On Kevin McCarthy And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Agreement; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Focused On Debt Deal As 2024 Campaign Heats Up; \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Campaign In Iowa; \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  And \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  To Announce Presidential Bids Next Week; Wray Speaks By Phone With Lawmakers On Document Demand; Marines Partied With Teenagers Before Brawl; Five Minors Charged With Felony Assault; \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  And \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  Meet With Chinese Business Leaders And Communist Party Officials In China; Chinese Government's Handling Of Protests In Hong Kong Possible Precursor To Chinese Incursion Into Taiwan; New Labor Law In California May Put Goat Herders Out Of Business; Some House Republicans Criticize Debt Ceiling Package Reached With White House; Republican Presidential Primary Field Continues To Grow\b0\par\par\uc2 Gillian Turner, Rich Edson, Brit Hume, Peter Doocy, Chad Pergram,  Lydia Hu, Bret Baier, William La Jeunesse \par \uc2 7,800 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Put your kids in jail, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Wait a minute, it's your turn.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you, Judge. It is my turn. Tonight, Charlie Hurt, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , Dana Perino and Tomi Lahren. And then, we're going to have to toss it over to Gillian Turner.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Hi, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I'm here -- I'm here. Hi.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Thanks, guys. Good evening from Washington. I'm Gillian Turner in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Well, the Republican presidential field now is about to get significantly bigger with new announcements coming from \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, American business leaders really want to do business with China despite very tense and rapidly deteriorating relations between Beijing and Washington.\par \par \uc2 Plus this, tens of thousands of goats could be put out to pasture with extreme prejudice if new kind of weird California law is enforced.\par \par \uc2 Before it's possible relief over the debt threat, the House is going to vote tonight imminently on the debt extension deal to avoid a historic default on federal obligations. A lot of staunch conservatives are against this agreement, which means Democrats will definitely be needed to get the measure across the finish line.\par \par \uc2 Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill tonight. Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Gillian. The vote come sometime after 8:00 tonight, expect broad bipartisan support. Lots of Democrats will vote yes, albeit with reluctance.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 STEVE COHEN\uc2  (D-TN): When Trump was president, they approved every extension of the debt relief. And yet, when Biden's here, no, this is extortion. But we have to deal with it. It's for the benefit of our country and the world's economy and I will vote yes.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Some conservatives upbraided House Speaker Kevin McCarthy for forging the deal. \uc2 Garret Graves\uc2  negotiated the pack for the GOP. He's tired of the right torching the deal and the speaker.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 GARRET GRAVES\uc2  (D-LA): Some trust was lost. I'm not going to lie, there really was. I'm really offended. Now, they put themselves in a situation to where these historic winds they bought themselves into a no vote on the most substantial progress that we've ever made, ever made.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): GOP Representative \uc2 Ken Buck\uc2  tells Fox he wants to talk about ousting Kevin McCarthy for negotiating the deal next week. But McCarthy is not worried.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Look, everybody has the ability to do what they want. But if you think I'm going to wake up in the morning and be ever worried about that, it doesn't bother me. If someone thinks they have the right to do it, call the motion.\par \par \uc2 But I think you look at what we've been able to accomplish together through this, we've been more successful.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): After tonight's vote, the plan goes to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: How long we can -- you think is reasonable to give (INAUDIBLE) before this has to get done?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I can tell you what I hope happens is that those who have amendments, if given votes, will yield back time so that we can finish this Thursday or Friday and soothe the country and soothe the markets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): GOP Utah Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  says, "He was proudly profoundly disappointed in the deal". He accused House GOP leaders of, "deception". Lee could slow down the bill in the Senate, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill for us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Meanwhile, President Biden is said to be putting in the time working behind the scenes on the debt package. Tonight, we take a look at how the president at least so far doesn't appear to be putting in a lot of time on the 2024 campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy has that story for us tonight. Hi, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Gillian, as we speak, President Biden is aboard Air Force One flying West. We have been told he is going to address the vote on the debt deal. At some point tonight, we just don't know if it's going to be next to the plane or in a statement.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'll be landing in Colorado tonight.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): But not to campaign. He's giving a commencement address while waiting on the house to move on the debt deal.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: God willing by the time I land, the Congress will have acted.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Biden allies tell Fox the light campaign schedule is because he doesn't have much of a primary but he does.\par \par \uc2 MARIANNE WILLIAMSON\uc2  (D), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The founders had wanted to say, had to have been a lawyer, had to have been a governor, had to have been a senator, had to have been a congressman. They would have, they didn't for a reason.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): So, there are primary candidates but the party isn't planning on any primary debates.\par \par \uc2 JAMES VAN DER BEEK, ACTOR: So, the DNC is openly ignoring bypassing the will of the people, why? This is a democracy of the people by the people for the people.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Billionaire investor and Democratic Party donor \uc2 Bill Ackman\uc2  is calling for a Biden challenge from the CEO of America's largest bank, \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 's \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 . Tweeting, Jamie can beat Biden in the primary and real \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the general election but he needs to start now and build name recognition among the broad electorate. He will easily raise billions of dollars from Democrats and Republicans to fund his campaign. And he knows how to build support.\par \par \uc2 For now, Dimon is not a no.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 J.P. MORGAN CHASE\uc2 : I love my country. And maybe one day I'll serve my country in one capacity or another. But I love what I do.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): We know President Biden has hosted private campaign fundraisers but his events since announcing have all been in an official capacity.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Mr. President, how's the reelection campaign going? But we haven't seen you on the campaign trail yet.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You haven't.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Not an official campaign rally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): And no, Mr. President, we haven't. There have been official remarks about White House policies at home and abroad. And of course, he interacts with some of the folks that attend these official events.\par \par \uc2 But so far, no events in early states, no visits to early states and nothing from the president about what he would do with a second term since that online video announcing the launch more than a month ago, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Peter Doocy at 1600 Pennsylvania for us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this uncertainty over the outcome of the debt deal on Capitol Hill had stocks down today. The Dow lost 135, the S&P 500 fell 26, NASDAQ was off 82.\par \par \uc2 The Republican presidential field is about to expand again. Former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  and former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  are both planning to toss their hats into the ring next week officially along with South -- excuse me, North Dakota Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  continues his Iowa tour with four stops. Former President Trump also in the caucus state. Let's bring in Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson. He joins us from Pella, Iowa tonight. Hi, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, good evening, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 And Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has just wrapped up the third of those four stops today here in Iowa. The governor has been stressing his record in Florida saying that makes him the conservative to take on President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): When the governor says he's going to do something, that's our cue. We know he is going to act and he is going to come bring something down the pike.\par \par \uc2 And so, as president, I pledge to be an energetic executive that will take on all the big issues that we face head on and won't look back.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): DeSantis has been offering more subtle criticism -- launched his first tour as a presidential candidate.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Leadership is not about entertainment. It's not about building a brand.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): DeSantis's pitch to voters, the next Republican president needs two consecutive terms, former President Trump would only get one.\par \par \uc2 He also says Republicans need to end the culture of losing, another dig at the former president. DeSantis only alluded to Trump during his speech though he let loose with reporters after the rally, addressing Trump's criticism of Florida's COVID policies.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: He used to say how great Florida was. Hell, his whole family moved to Florida under my governorship. Are you kidding me?\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): The Trump campaign responded, "\uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is not a serious person. His poorly conceived speech was light on substance and was crafted to appease establishment Never Trumpers who were looking for a swamp puppet that will do their bidding".\par \par \uc2 Other Republican candidates have taken a different tone.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): People are starving for something optimistic. They want to have an optimistic positive conversation.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): The Republican field is growing, sources familiar with the announcement tell Fox News, Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  will open his campaign next week in Iowa, former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  will do so in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): DeSantis has been offering more subtle criticism of the former president, did so just here in his comments here in Pella. He said Republicans should have 55 senators, not the 49 they do now after the former president was criticized for playing a pretty outsized role in the Republican primaries ahead of those midterm elections, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Rich Edson in Pella for us this evening. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring on Fox News Chief Political Analyst Brit Hume. Brit, let's pick up -- lots to unpack, but let's pick up with \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 . Seems like he's expended an inordinate amount of energy recently kind of eking out this position for himself as a never Trumper, calling the former president all kinds of names everything from a coward to a Putin puppet.\par \par \uc2 But take a look at this latest RealClearPolitics average gives him one -- just one percent of the GOP vote. And then Monmouth yesterday gives him a 47 percent unfavorability rating among Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Kind of begs the question, is he a serious candidate? Is he running because he thinks he can somehow clinch the primary nomination or is this just a platform for him to continue to hammer Trump?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: No, politicians have a way of thinking that they should be president and can be president. So, he's going to take his shot I guess.\par \par \uc2 I got to say, Gillian, that this Republican field is growing so big now that if they have a debate, they might have to hold it in a stadium, not to accommodate the crowd, but to accommodate the candidates.\par \par \uc2 So, it's going to be fun to see how that works out. I hope it doesn't end up in one of these deals where you have -- you know, they have the -- you have the regular group, and then you have the undercard, separate tiers of debaters. That didn't work very well last time, which hopefully we don't have it this time.\par \par \uc2 And for Christie, one wonders what he's up to. One thing that he will do, that some of the other candidates may be hesitant to do, he will as he has take on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  directly. That can be effective.\par \par \uc2 It was effective when he took on \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  in the 2020 campaign, and in the end, of course, -- and I think he did real damage there on the 2016 campaign, I should say.\par \par \uc2 And the problem is that the benefit of it did not accrue to Christy, it accrued to other candidates. I think, which is the risk you take when you take on a candidate who's no well ahead in the field directly.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Speaking of taking on former President Trump directly, as you just mentioned, let's talk about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Because that's what everybody, everybody politics, experts both sides of the aisle has been saying he needs to do, it sounds like he is starting to do that in Iowa. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: If you say Cuomo did a better job with COVID than Florida did, first of all, that's not what he used to say. This is like new, like, six months ago, he would have never said that, right?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Remarkable, to a certain extent, right?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, it's -- well, it's remarkable in the sense that he's now referring directly to at least the things that Trump has said. He hasn't really attacked the former president directly by criticizing him or his record directly. But he's going about it the way he is, which is not a full frontal assault, but maybe that will come. But there are risks to that, as I mentioned.\par \par \uc2 And you know, DeSantis is we're getting our first real glimpse of him, Gillian. I mean, we -- you know, we've seen him in Florida, we've seen in here, we've seen in there, but the nation as a whole is just getting a chance to see him now out on the campaign trail at some length he was on last night (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 I've watched most of his speech last night and so, you know, we're getting a full -- a fuller glimpse of him. I don't think he's particularly exciting as a candidate. Trump is always exciting, at least to his supporters, and he's always entertaining in the primary season, who knows what voters in Iowa and New Hampshire will go for this year?\par \par \uc2 Do they want exciting and primary campaigns past? That is what -- that is what the voters had wanted. They wanted somebody to inspire them and make them feel that emotion of hope and an excitement about the candidate -- about the candidates in the campaign. Whether he can do that remains to be seen or whether voters really want that remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And if they can't be inspired? Are they going to settle for at least being entertained? I guess that's another question. Brit, we got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Maybe. OK. Thanks, Gillian. Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You too. Thanks, Brit.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the competition between the House Oversight Committee Chairman and the director of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  heating up over a document that alleges wrongdoing by President Biden.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country tonight are talking about. Fox 5 in Atlanta has a video the shocking car accident, see here. This happened last week in southern Georgia. That vehicle hit the ramp of a tow truck, sales through the air, then comes to a stop in the upright position on the road.\par \par \uc2 The Georgia State Patrol says the driver survived thankfully but was hospitalized with some pretty serious injuries.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Fox 32 in Chicago, the City Council there votes to spend $51 million on undocumented migrants. A whole lot of people were bused from Texas all the way to Chicago without any warning or explanation.\par \par \uc2 The Salvation Army Social Services took in some of those folks but a whole lot of others were left to sleep on police station floors.\par \par \uc2 Plus, this, a live look at L.A., one of the big stories there tonight from Fox 11. 83-year-old Al Pacino is going to become a father for the fourth time, that according to his reps. The announcement comes just a few weeks after his good pals there, Robert De Niro welcomed his seventh child at age 79.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, Alabama's Governor \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2  assigned new legislation prohibiting transgender women from joining all-female college sports teams.\par \par \uc2 The state had previously banned transgender athletes from participating in female youth sports that's grades K to 12. The new law though banned students from playing under a different gender identity from their born gender, even if they've already undergone hormone treatment.\par \par \uc2 Well, the chairman of the House Oversight Committee and \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  are still at odds this hour over a form that alleges President Biden engaged in a bribery scheme while serving as vice president.\par \par \uc2 There was some progress if minor made today but not enough for the GOP.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt has the latest from the Justice Department tonight.\par \par \uc2 Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Gillian. Good evening.\par \par \uc2 The high stakes phone call took place at 1:30 Eastern Time, about five hours ago. \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 , told House Oversight Chairman James Comer, he can see the document he's requesting. But there is a catch. He has to view it in a secure location at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  headquarters. He's not going to get it in person on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 It's a standard \uc2 FBI\uc2  form called a 1023. It's a used across the bureau when someone comes to an agent and wants to make an allegation or give a tip.\par \par \uc2 A 1023 may prove true, it may also not. This specific document allegedly contains a claim that then-Vice President Biden accepted a bribe for a policy decision.\par \par \uc2 Comer said in the statement this afternoon, "We have been clear that anything short of producing these documents to the House Oversight Committee is not in compliance with the subpoena."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has previously indicated it would not physically turn over the document because, Gillian, it could expose sources and methods and important information.\par \par \uc2 Comer says he wants it without that stipulation. He says he's going to move forward with the threat of holding \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Wray in contempt of Congress.\par \par \uc2 We just got a statement from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  want to read part of it, "The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has continually demonstrated its commitment to working with the committee to accommodate its request from scheduling briefings and calls to now allowing the chair to review information in person."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is committed to cooperating with the committee in good faith. Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. David Spunt at the DOJ for us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Juveniles are facing charges tonight in the alleged attack on a trio of Marines over the weekend in California. Now, we are learning brand new details about what may have precipitated that assault.\par \par \uc2 Chief Correspondent Jonathan Hunt has new details from Los Angeles tonight. Hi, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Gillian. Not so often with these kinds of incidents. There appears to be more to this than meets the eye in the first video that was made public. It seems that the Marines may, in fact, have been drinking with and even wrestling with the teens before the all-out brawl began.\par \par \uc2 And in the one video we have seen and studied closely, the three Marines are walking away from the crowd, when one of the girls in the group appears to strike one of the Marines in the back of the head and that marine then rushes the group, possibly targeting the girl.\par \par \uc2 Watch this very closely.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (voice over): You see there that the beatdown begins after that one Marine rushes into the crowd. The three Marines are then brutally punched and stomped by the group of teens, which numbers in the dozens. And the local sheriff says whatever started the brawl, the five teens four male, one female arrested last night will be charged with felony assault with a deadly weapon.\par \par \uc2 SGT. MIKE WOODROOF, ORANGE COUNTY, CALIFORNIA SHERIFF'S DEPARTMENT: We have sought out the probable cause that has led to the arrest of these five individuals. Plus, most likely there will be more to come. But now, it will be in the district attorney's hands and we are strongly confident that they will see this through for justice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (on camera): Now, given the new evidence that the Marines may have been partying with the teams before things got out of control, there is a possibility that they may too, face at the very least disciplinary action.\par \par \uc2 The 1st Marine Expeditionary Force at Camp Pendleton where the three are based says it is "supporting" local authorities in the investigation. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Jonathan Serrie in L.A. for us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, why some American CEOs are remaining stubbornly bullish when it comes to working with China, even as relations between the countries are strained deteriorating quickly.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Bret Baier has our "WHATEVER HAPPENED TO" segment. He is going to take a look inside Hong Kong's pro-democracy movement that's standing up to China's Communist rulers.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The \uc2 NASA\uc2  panel investigating unidentified aerial phenomena known to some of us as UFOs held its first public meeting today in Washington.\par \par \uc2 The 60-member group has experts in all kinds of fields from physics to astrobiology. It was formed last June, just about a year ago to examine UFO sightings and other data collected by everyone: civilians, government agencies, even the private sector.\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2  says it's not leaping to any conclusions just yet. But it is also the reality that they're currently actively investigating over 800 cases.\par \par \uc2 Also, this tensions between the government of the U.S. and China continue to escalate by the day. Some American businesses are remaining bullish when it comes to cooperating with Beijing, even partnering with Chinese companies.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business correspondent Lydia Hu tells us why tonight from New York. Hi, Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Gillian. As Chinese officials rebuffed an invitation from the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  to meet with Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  earlier this week, American business titans are embraced. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  CEO \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  are separately in China meeting with business leaders and Communist Party officials.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: These CEOs really should be allowed to speak for themselves and for their travel. There's never been some sort of fiat by the White House or the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  that American business executives can't travel to China and can't conduct business as they feel it is appropriate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: Now, since arriving in the communist nation yesterday, Musk has reportedly met with China's ministers on commerce, industry, and foreign affairs. He also shared a lavish 16 course meal with the CEO of a major Chinese battery supplier. According to Chinese authorities, Musk reportedly said he opposes decoupling the U.S. and Chinese economies. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  did not immediately respond to our requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  CEO \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  also in Shanghai this week for the bank's annual global China summit. Dimon is reaffirming commitment to business there, saying the bank will remain in China and calling for real engagement to resolve ongoing issues between the nations. But some Republican lawmakers call for decoupling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CARLOS GIMENEZ, (R-FL) HOUSE SELECT COMMITTEE ON CHINA: We need to stop funding the instrument of what could be our eventual demise. China makes no secret they want to be the dominant economic power and the dominant military power in the world by 2049.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: Now, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has been unusually quiet on his social media platform, \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , while he has been in China. And that's perhaps because \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is mostly blocked in the country. Gillian, back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Lydia Hu in the New York newsroom for us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HU: You got it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You may remember that back in 2019 millions of Chinese students protested efforts by the Chinese party government to impinge on Hong Kong's separate governing system. Tonight, my colleague Bret Baier investigates whatever happened to that pro-democracy uprising.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Gillian. The protests we saw in Hong Kong nearly four years ago have all but died out. Meanwhile, China's power in the region has increased. Tonight, we look back at the events that led to the unrest in Hong Kong and what that might mean for independence in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Spring of 2019, demonstrators take to the streets protesting legislation that would allow criminal suspects to be sent to mainland China for trial.\par \par \uc2 GORDON CHANG, CHINA EXPERT: Throughout the century, Beijing has tried impose stiffer and stiffer controls over Hong Kong. The protests that followed the extradition bill in 2019 were a watershed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: By summer, the demonstrations made headlines around the world, with millions protesting China's actions. Former Hong Kong chief executive \uc2 Carrie Lam\uc2  eventually suspended the extradition bill. Demonstrations continued, calling for her to step down as Beijing pushed ahead with a new idea, quelling the protesters in the name of national security.\par \par \uc2 CHANG: The 2020 national security law has rightly been called the end of law in Hong Kong. That legislation is so vague, it gives Beijing the power to do whatever it wants. And Beijing has fully implemented that law in ways that have ended freedom in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That legislation became law in the summer of 2020. Chinese leaders said it was necessary to for controlling the protest.\par \par \uc2 It was at the end of that that this real crackdown happens.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIEN LAI: They essentially use the national security law combined with a very harsh COVID restrictions that they put in place in Hong Kong to crack down on the protests.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sebastien Lai's father is one victim of the new law. Jimmy Lai has been imprisoned since December 2020 over his role in the pro-democracy movement.\par \par \uc2 When was the last time you saw your dad?\par \par \uc2 LAI: The last time I saw my dad was at the end of 2020.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And have you talked to him.\par \par \uc2 LAI: Doing something like this, for example, would put me in trouble with the national security law, so I haven't been back to Hong Kong ever since, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hundreds were arrested under the new law, including Jimmy Lai. He faced charges for his participation in protests. Others stemmed from his tabloid "Apple Daily" which off was critical of China. That business was raided and shut down.\par \par \uc2 This national security law after it was implemented, how did Hong Kong change?\par \par \uc2 LAI: They started mass crackdowns, arresting many people. At that time, we had around 900 people working at "Apple Daily." The first crackdown they sent 200 policemen ran into the office building, took people's laptops, hard drives. And the second time, because "Apple Daily" kept publishing, the brave reporters kept publishing, they sent 500 people. And that was the end of it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jimmy Lai's national security trial is scheduled for later this year. He faces life in prison and is among the nearly 1,500 known political prisoners detained in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 CHANG: We have seen the number of arrests in Hong Kong increase from the 2019 protest, and we are not sure that we know the full numbers about that, because there are reports of people being secretly detained. But whatever is the case, China is now fully in control of Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, U.S. lawmakers are calling Hong Kong Taiwan's wake-up call.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: We watched them shut down newspapers and freedom of press that have been there for decades.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: After Hong Kong fell and then Ukraine got invaded by Putin and Russia, Taiwan is very nervous, and they should be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You live in Taiwan now?\par \par \uc2 LAI: That's correct.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you say that that's accurate? It's a wake-up call?\par \par \uc2 LAI: Yes. I think once they cracked down in Hong Kong, everybody in Taiwan very quickly realized that that was no longer going to be a valid choice.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: China has less influence in Taiwan than it does in Hong Kong. The U.S. positions on the regions also differ. The One China Policy only acknowledges Beijing's position that Taiwan is part of China. The U.S. views Hong Kong as a part of China, but the U.S. policy holds that Hong Kong is an independent customs and economic territory.\par \par \uc2 CHANG: China is attacking not only our democracy but democracies in general. So we need to defend this principle of self-governance, whether in Hong Kong, Taiwan, or the United States.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Experts believe it will be a long road before Hong Kong is able to regain some independence.\par \par \uc2 CHANG: As long as Xi Jinping rules China, Hong Kong is lost.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is international pressure even something that you have seen work before?\par \par \uc2 LAI: International pressure in showing -- in shining a light on these cases, on my father and the story of Hong Kong, is paramount.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Jimmy Lai met with lawmakers during his last trip to the U.S. in 2019. He called support against China's aggression. Four years later, his son Sebastien has met with those same lawmakers recently, continuing the fight for democracy in Hong Kong and freedom for his father. We'll follow what happens with Jimmy Lai's case and other efforts by China to impede on freedom there in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Gillian, we will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: An important report there, Bret. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, why tens of thousands of goats in California could soon be headed to the slaughterhouse unnecessarily because of a new and highly unusual law there. Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: A highly unusual law being floated in California right now could end up sending thousands of goats to the slaughterhouse completely unnecessarily and putting goat herders there out of work. National correspondent William La Jeunesse explains tonight from Oceanside, California. Hi, William.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The goats are very versatile in the sense that a lot of what we have is on mountain sites and slopes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Oceanside, California, 200 goats hired by \uc2 San Diego Gas and Electric\uc2  clear brush under power lines ahead of fire season. The livestock are a popular ecofriendly alternative to bulldozers or chemicals. This could end, however, under a bill backed by the California Labor Federation that turns goatherders into hourly farm workers, paid time and a half even when they are not working. But former assemblywoman \uc2 Lorena Gonzalez\uc2 , who sponsored the law, says it's about protecting workers.\par \par \uc2 LORENA GONZALEZ, CALIFORNIA LABOR FEDERATION: To make sure that the workers who are associated with it aren't being abused, that they're not being taken advantage of, that they in fact are being treated fairly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are all about fairness. We don't want to lose these people.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Johnny Gonzalez owns 2,000 goats. Most companies like his bring herders here on visas, typically from Peru, and are paid about $4,000 a month, plus room, food, and phone. But if the union bill goes through, their pay will jump to $14,000 a month, more than most can afford.\par \par \uc2 TIM ARROWSMITH, WESTERN GRAZERS: By January 1 of 24, if we cannot fix the current legislation, we will be forced to sell these goats to slaughter and to the auction yards, and we will be forced out of business and probably file for bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: And in a month this canyon is going to be dry and brown. And a wildfire would rip through here in a matter of minutes. So land owners spend about $1,000 an acre to have the goats clear the vegetation. They don't complain. They show up every day on time and sober. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: William La Jeunesse in California for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM MCGOVERN, (D-MA): A week from default we have been rushed back to Washington because Republicans decided they wanted to waste months playing games with the government.\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM COLE, (R-OK): For all the posturing on the other side of the aisle, I would remind everyone that House Republicans are the only ones had who have taken up concrete action to avoid default.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL BURGESS, (R-TX): With the passage of this legislation, Republicans are putting America back on a sound fiscal footing.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RAUL RUIZ\uc2  (D-CA): This bill is not about fiscal responsibility. It is about extreme GOP pushing their extreme agenda.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Time now to bring in our panel. We've got Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," Vince Coglianese, WMAL Washington talk radio host and editorial director at "The Daily Caller," and Meredith McGraw, national political correspondent for "Politico". Thanks, everybody, for being here. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 Meredith, let's start with McCarthy. He went on this sort of last ditch media blitz today to try and corral the remaining holdouts from his caucus. He delivered this closing message that seemed to be this is historically humongous savings. We have never seen anything like it before. Take a quick listen. I want to get your reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: Here is the largest savings in American history, the unshackling of finally wasteful Washington spending getting rescinded. Do you realize of all the congresses in the past, you can add up all the money that they rescinded and add them together, this is higher than that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It's almost infinite the savings are so great. Is that reality or is that an exaggeration?\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH MCGRAW, "POLITICO": Look, nobody is exactly thrilled with this deal, left or right. And McCarthy is really trying to stave off potential Republican members maybe voting over his speakership, and members of the hard right who are really dissatisfied with this deal that's been cut.\par \par \uc2 And both McCarthy and Jeffries are trying to really sell this deal to Republicans and Democrats. McCarthy is saying that this new deal has work requirements, spending cuts. But even the CBO said that those new work requirements could actually end up causing even more spending in the long run. So, right now it seems like they are confident that they can get this deal passed, but it's going to be a lot of trying to sell it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Mollie, the Republicans no's seem to be saying today straight up they feel McCarthy got outsmarted by Biden, or outmaneuvered by Biden. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": Right. I mean, part of the problem for the whole Republican caucus is that Kevin McCarthy did such a good job of getting all the Republicans united to pass this bill that really had a lot of things that made even the fiscal conservatives happy. These are people who really do recognize that what we are doing with spending is very bad for the country. And the end result was that there was a compromise that is much less good than what they voted on together as a caucus. And so it's reasonable for the fiscal conservatives, the people who are concerned about the financial health of the country, to be so upset.\par \par \uc2 But on the other hand, Kevin McCarthy had a very difficult job. He had a president who was refusing to negotiate. He did get that guy to negotiate. He did bring \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to the table.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Though the White House insists there was no negotiating, any at every point, blah, blah, blah.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Obviously, there was, and there obviously were some concessions. I think the big complaint from a lot of Republicans is that McCarthy settled to too soon, that he had a bit more leverage and he could have extracted a bit more. But overall Republicans do have some reason to be happy.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Vince, the consensus seems to be that for both sides of the aisle, that the gist of all of this has been negative for both sides politically speaking. It has showed up fissures in the Republican Party and Democratic Party. I think there is a lot of fatigue amongst Americans who felt like the debt ceiling was not something that they had on their bingo card to worry about in 2023. They sort of expect Washington politicians to take care of it and let them know when it's done.\par \par \uc2 VINCE COGLIANESE, "THE DAILY CALLER": Right. But if you hate how expensive everything is now, blame the government spending and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's printing of all of this money, and that's why we are at this limit.\par \par \uc2 Now, I have to say, the American people in polling were showing that they wanted the debt ceiling raised, but they also wanted spending cuts. That was the majority position. And you had Republicans who had \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  over the barrel. He was refusing to negotiate, up until a couple weeks ago. The reason he sprints to the table, he doesn't want this outcome where he bypasses the debt ceiling, which means Republicans had immense leverage, and this is the only leverage they have all year to sustain any spending cuts whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 This is not a spending cut plan. What these two men signed on to will raise the debt by $4 trillion over the course of the next two years. And that's the optimistic version if the economy doesn't go south. That's why people are upset right now that McCarthy went along with this without getting any meaningful change.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So much savings, you can barely imagine what the country is going to do with it.\par \par \uc2 COGLIANESE: Yes, it's immense.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Panel, stick with us, because we're going to be right back. Coming up next, how large is the Republican field going to get, and is the DNC going to actually stage a debate? We'll look at the road to 2024.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: We must put an end to the culture of losing that has infected the Republican Party of recent years. Not in Iowa, not in Florida, but in way too many places.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT, (R-SC) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: People are starving for something optimistic. They want to have an optimistic, positive conversation, as long as it's anchored in conservative principles, and you have a backbone.\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE CHAIRWOMAN: We need to remember that at the end of this, after a bruising primary, after everybody is fighting to get the nomination, we need to come together as a party and focus on winning the White House.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. We're back with the panel now. Mollie, you heard Tim Scott there. He said people are starving for anything positive. But is that true? It seems like voters keep sending more and more partisan lawmakers to Washington ginned up for a fight.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: I think Republican voters want Republicans who will actually get stuff done. They got a taste of this during the previous administration, instead of just promising voters that you'll do something, actually delivering on that. And so as they are thinking about who to nominate, they really want to know who is someone who will get the agenda done rather than what you see a lot happening in D.C., which is people conspiring for how to not accomplishing things.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: On the Democratic side, Meredith, the biggest liability outstanding for the president right now seems to be his lagging approval ratings and perceptions about the state of the country under his leadership. Take a look at this latest Gallup poll, finds U.S. mood remains glum with only 18 percent of voters satisfied with the state of the nation. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MCGRAW: I mean, across the board, President Biden is struggling in the polls when it comes to the economy, his handling of immigration, his approval ratings. He is really in a tough place with his polling right now. And that's going to continue to be a drag on him. It's been interesting to see \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr\uc2 . and Marianne Wilson poll higher than we have seen some of the Republican primary challengers to Trump poll. And I think that's sort of an indication of some of the weaknesses that President Biden has had when it comes to his own party's approval.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Vince, it looks like \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  -- on the Republican side now, looks like \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  and former V.P. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  are getting in the race. Game changers, not really doing much for you?\par \par \uc2 COGLIANESE: I don't think \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is a game changer here in this conversation, as much as he wants to be. All these presidential candidates want to be.\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 's role is going to be interesting because I don't think he is jumping into this race to become president of the United States. I think he is jumping in to try and kamikaze \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , because he has expressed pretty forthrightly that he doesn't think that the other candidates are going to be as tough on Trump as he needs to. And you heard Brit Hume even say earlier this hour that does seem to be the role that he may play on this stage to try and attack Trump. But in the process, he will take himself down.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Mollie, take a listen to this former legislative affairs director to President Trump, Marc Short, earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT, FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF TO VICE PRESIDENT PENCE: A lot of us like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's policies when he was president. But we wonder, he has walked away from those policies. From a guy who championed the overturning the Roe v. Wade, he now says, hey, look we need to put abortion back on the negotiating table. For the guy who took on Russian mercenaries in Syria and canceled Nord Stream II, he now welcomes Putin invading Ukraine. From the guy who we think helped generate enormous economic growth, his policies is exactly the same as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So in essence, Trump isn't the same person he was when he was president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: What do you say?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Marc Short is a big fan of \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  who he worked for during the Trump administration. But I'm not sure that that argument works outside of the Washington, D.C. circuit. Namely, like you take, for instance, foreign policy. President Trump had a wildly successful foreign policy. The first time we had a president who didn't start a new war, who actually worked to end certain wars. He -- peace was breaking out all over. You had peace accords.\par \par \uc2 He does believe that the way we are handling the Ukraine conflict is not designed to end that as quickly as possible, that we should be seeking a peace agreement between these two countries before it spirals into World War III. That's something that a lot of Republican voters are desperately hungry for.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Panel, great chatting with you. Thanks so much. See you soon.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a very special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I love the country, ride out through the country roads and smell the new mown hay.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: A veteran's passion for \uc2 Harley-Davidson\uc2  is still riding strong at 90 years old. The machine has been part of Don Huffman's life from more than 70 years, he says. He served as a plane mechanic in the Air Force, received his first bike at just 18 years old, and he has been riding it ever since. He has now got this fabulous collection of about 30 bikes. He has retired all his most recent treasured bikes into a museum on his son's property where folks can go to visit. We wish him all the best with that.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, an energy watchdog is warning of widespread blackouts this summer. We're going to take a look at the current state of the power grid. Plus, why adding clean energy generators is becoming a long and very expensive process.\par \par \uc2 You can catch us right back here tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT at 6:00 p.m. Thanks for watching. I'm Gillian Turner in Washington. JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME with Mr. Jesse Watters starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230601ej5v00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Whistleblowers Testify \uc2 FBI\uc2  Retaliation Was Hostile; Vice President Does Podcast In Garage, Gets 3,000 Views In Three Days; Dem: Migrant Crackdown Will Leave Crops "Rotting"; Squad Members Demand $14 Trillion In Reparations; \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  Slams Remote Work As "Morally Wrong"\b0\par\par\uc2 Dana Perino, Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters, Tom  Shillue \par \uc2 8,228 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: We lost that genius, that incredible real estate mind this morning. Sam Zell gone at 81.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everyone, I'm Dana Perino along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters and Tom Shillue. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 So, the no good very bad week for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  just got worse, less than 72 hours after the Durham report revealed and discovered and proved that the bureau was biased against former President Trump, a group of \uc2 FBI\uc2  whistleblowers giving explosive testimony on Capitol Hill, the witnesses accusing the agency of unfair treatment and alleging retaliation.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say this is just further proof of what it calls institutional rot, and anti-conservative bias. And adding to their argument just before the hearing, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  yanked security clearances of two of the agents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN FRIEND, CLAIMS \uc2 FBI\uc2  RETALIATED AGAINST HIM: Working as an \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent was my dream job. My whistleblowing was apolitical, and in the spirit of upholding my oath. Nonetheless, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  cynically elected to close ranks and attack the messenger.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS ALLEN, CLAIMS \uc2 FBI\uc2  RETALIATED AGAINST HIM: My family and I have been surviving on early withdrawals from our retirement accounts. While the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has ignored my request for approval to obtain outside employment.\par \par \uc2 GARRET O'BOYLE, CLAIMS \uc2 FBI\uc2  RETALIATED AGAINST HIM: I'venever swore an oath to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . I swore an oath to the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 REP. KAT CAMMACK (R-FL): Do you believe that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is purposefully hostile to you for that reason to keep agents from speaking up?\par \par \uc2 FRIEND: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Yes, no question.\par \par \uc2 O'BOYLE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Chairman Jim Jordan warning the whistleblowers that they would face heavy attacks from Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): We've talked to over two dozen whistleblowers. People have come to us, we've interviewed several of those. And today, three of them. Three of those brave whistleblowers, and the lawyer who represents them will tell us their story. They will tell us what happened, what they saw, and then what happened to them because they were courageous enough to report it to Congress.\par \par \uc2 I just want to tell you guys, get ready. Get ready, because these guys are going to come after you. You know they are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, Jordan was definitely on to something, Democrats did bash Republicans for even holding the hearing, and trying to undermine the credibility of whistleblowers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 STACEY PLASKETT\uc2  (D-VI): On Police Week, watching House Republicans jump to lay the founding nation to defund law enforcement. My colleagues on the far right are on a mission to attack, discredit, and ultimately dismantle the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . This is defund the police on steroids.\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE WASSERMANN SCHULTZ (D-FL): You're engaging in the self- promotion of your new book that's about to be released, and what great timing to be on T.V. and in Congress right before your book tour starts.\par \par \uc2 REP. SYLVIA GARCIA (D-TX): In Texas, we would just say that this is just a lot of hot air blowing here and it isn't a whistleblower.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: This is one of the most heated passionate hearings I have seen on Capitol Hill in a long time. We've watched the whole thing this morning that both sides came loaded for bear. Jesse, get your initial thoughts.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: The primary allegation was that there was a cash incentive program with the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . So, the more investigations a field office opens up, the more cash bonuses are given to the head of the field office.\par \par \uc2 So, that's why you're seeing this flurry of investigations, a flurry of arrests. That's why so many January 6 prosecutions, so many parents are being investigated. Catholics are now being investigated, and it explains why.\par \par \uc2 Anybody who even went to D.C. on January 6 was investigated. They were investigated for money but they were investigated.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  handed over to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  every single transaction that was made by people that flew into D.C. for January 6.\par \par \uc2 Not only that, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was taking officers off child porn cases and making them hang out in the parking lots of parent-teacher conferences and write down their license plates.\par \par \uc2 They made the Betsy Ross flag or a bumper sticker that said to 2A terror symbol. So, now you understand why the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says white supremacy is the number one threat in the country, because it's a predominantly white country. And you could make money, a fortune investigating everything, a flag waver or a small business owner, a Trump supporter, a parent, anybody that can be considered -- wait, you can open up an investigation and get paid for that.\par \par \uc2 So, it's a very perverse incentive, and then they got retaliated against because they tried to blow the whistle correctly up the chain of command. And then they like sent him to California and then locked all their belongings in a storage unit and one family, \uc2 FBI\uc2  family was left homeless for a week. And they said the \uc2 FBI\uc2  will crush you if you speak out against them. And that's why a lot of people are saying the \uc2 FBI\uc2  needs to be dismantled.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You'll have more tonight on "PRIMETIME".\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We're going to have both of these whistleblowers and Jim Jordan tonight at 7:00.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Don't say I never promote your show.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I will always do it.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: How could I forget?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I will always do it. Judge?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: You know, it's interesting, not only did they bump up these cash bonuses, and we -- remember with the Veterans Administration, they pumped up the numbers and said they were doing these great jobs when they really weren't helping veterans, but they made these fake stats so that they could make more money.\par \par \uc2 Whenever you put money in with the federal government, you're making a mistake based on the numbers of cases or bonuses for arrests or callers. It's just that it's inconsistent. It's a bad intersection.\par \par \uc2 But it's worse than that because what they did was they pumped up the numbers, not just for the money. But if they were doing something that they call the white supremacist investigation, and they had four people they were interviewing, it wasn't that one case, it was then four cases. So, they could bump up the stats and say, Yes, white supremacy, it's the biggest problem in America today.\par \par \uc2 And what they did to these whistleblowers is really -- it's frightening. Because by the way, there's a Whistleblower Protection Act, I have no faith in it, to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 But what they did to these people, to these \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, and you know, it's the main \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, you know, the middle rung of them, they're doing a great job. They believe in this country. They're taking care to make sure that they follow all the rules and regulations. It's the upper core of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  that is corrupt, that's been politicized, that has been weaponized.\par \par \uc2 And so, they take these little people so to speak, and they made them homeless, they made one guy actually move his family to another town, and then forced him to relocate. And then as soon as he got there, they put them on unpaid, indefinite suspension. And he's homeless.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and it's worse, but we already knew that.\par \par \uc2 And one more thing, the congresswoman, I think her name is Plaskett. She is from --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Stacey Plaskett.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, Stacey Plaskett, she talks about how this is defund police on steroids. Are you kidding me? We had the Horowitz investigation, where Horowitz said the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was basically not doing its job, that \uc2 Jim Comey\uc2  was, you know, someone who lied and the affidavit was unverified and went on and on.\par \par \uc2 And then you have the Durham report saying that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  failed to uphold its mission and fidelity to the law. And this woman labels these people, as people who want to defund the police, the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And finally, it is -- it is simply too easy to say that a whistleblower who is saying something that is against his own interest, it's too easy for them to say, you know what, they're really inept, they were bad at their job.\par \par \uc2 Because how do you fight the system? How do you fight the institution of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ? You can't.\par \par \uc2 So, the incentive is against someone who wants to be a whistleblower and this is a perfect example of it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: One of the things that was revealed today -- I guess what wasn't really revealed to me because I didn't know that the whistleblower protections for \uc2 FBI\uc2  employees and agents is different from the rest of the federal government, which is something that could perhaps be looked at by the Congress.\par \par \uc2 Harold Ford Jr., the Democrats came ready today. They've got some pretty strong members on their side as well.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: They do. It's good to be back.\par \par \uc2 A couple of things, I think the report -- the Durham report is a troubling, troubling thing. Whenever the people, the American people lose confidence in a federal agency, particularly a law enforcement agency or have reason to and Mr. Durham uncovered a lot of that. That's something we need to take very seriously.\par \par \uc2 And I would hope that the committee might take a look. And if I were Chairman Jordan, I would invite director Wray who was actually praised in the report by Mr. Durham, saying he was taking big efforts to clean up some of the investigative excesses to give them a sense of what they've really done. And I think they could probably explore their whistle -- the whistleblower protections for \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents.\par \par \uc2 Two, there has been politics in every agency, the Department of Defense, education, ATF, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , every acronym there is.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: \uc2 IRS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FORD: \uc2 IRS\uc2 , they've been RS and DS forever. And it probably is a little bias somewhere along the way.\par \par \uc2 I think whistleblower should be protected in every agency. And one of the things that has to be done is you have to corroborate their stories. And while you corroborate their stories, they should not be punished. And it sounds as if, at least from some of the testimony you can read into that maybe there are those who believe -- who the whistleblowers believed that they had been retaliated against.\par \par \uc2 If I were Chairman Jordan, I'd call in those and I would ask the whistleblowers, will you sign a document to say we can look at your employee file because these are serious serious allegations.\par \par \uc2 Now, I don't like engaging, and Republicans are so bad, they're coming after Democrats. I thought it was a little irresponsible of the chairman to do those things to make that statement.\par \par \uc2 But if he has the evidence to back it up, then he should -- he should -- he should put forth the evidence.\par \par \uc2 A close where I started, I give back Director Wray a lot more credit than perhaps some not around this table but some are in the -- in the press, he did a lot to clean this up. And if you don't believe it, Durham says it in his own report.\par \par \uc2 I hope the \uc2 FBI\uc2  gets back to doing what they do best, which is protecting us against domestic and foreign crimes and threats and get back to the great work they did in Idaho, where they were able to hunt down that guy that killed those four people in their home.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You know, Tom Shillue, that was one of the things I was going to ask you about, Tom, is that in the whistleblower testimony, one of the concerns that they said they had is that all of this focus on domestic terrorism has really taken our eye off the ball from foreign enemies and terrorism.\par \par \uc2 TOM SHILLUE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I mean, it's unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And that we're back to like a September 10th attitude.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: We've seen it and we've seen him doing these raids and knocking on doors with the pro-life demonstrators and things like that, investigating Catholics and other issues like that. It's obviously rotten.\par \par \uc2 And I said to my friend the other day, I said something that kind of bothered him. I said they're going to get away with it, because they broke the law legally. He said, what are you talking about? How do you do that? I say they broke the law legally.\par \par \uc2 Like, we've seen it dramatized on television shows like "The Wire" where you have the mob, a lawyer who's doing work for a drug dealer, and we watched the way that he worked. He used the law to his advantage to essentially make this drug dealer legitimate. But he was a drug dealer. He was selling drugs to children. That's what this lawyer was doing. But you can't get them on it.\par \par \uc2 That's the way guys like Comey and McCabe operated. They knew they were above the law because they know what the law is. I think they broke the law. I don't like the word bias. They broke the law legally.\par \par \uc2 Now, the whistleblowers might be able to uncover some things. I don't think they're going to get the guys at the top. But some heads may be able to roll when you get these guys who are the inside giving information.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It is interesting that the cat apparently has \uc2 James Comey\uc2 's tongue. He used to love the media. I can't find him anywhere, we're looking.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Can I say one thing? For all the compliments that \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  is getting, who did he let go? He came in right after \uc2 James Comey\uc2 , Strzok, McCabe and the rest of them. What has \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  done other than say, you know, I'm going to do better?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FORD: You can't -- well, you can cherry pick the report if you want but --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, all right, all right. Up next, as President Biden slumps in the polls, the media is giving \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  a glowing profile praising his cathedral mind.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: The liberal media is desperately searching for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  replacement. And it looks like Mayor Pete is at the top of the list. The Transportation Secretary best known for overseeing disaster after disaster during his tenure is now getting glowing puff pieces from the press.\par \par \uc2 Check out this headline from Wired Magazine, "\uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  loves God, beer and his electric Mustang."\par \par \uc2 But wait, it gets even more ridiculous. The article praises old Pete for his, "Voluminous mind, his cathedral mind, the curious mind of Pete. His cabinet job requires only a modest portion of his cognitive powers."\par \par \uc2 But it gets more ridiculous with these questions, "Are there more ways that challenges of transportation speak to your spiritual side?"\par \par \uc2 And it looks like the media is praising Mayor Pete because the alternative is much worse. The White House has been trying to elevate \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , but then we ended up with this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LYNAE VANEE, CONTENT CREATOR: First question. Let's start with your favorite song of all time.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Oh, my favorite song of all time? I was going to say something from Bootsy.\par \par \uc2 VANEE: We love that.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I mean, I don't know if your viewers know Funkadelic.\par \par \uc2 VANEE: I mean, I am 28.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Scott Miley for sure.\par \par \uc2 VANEE: OK, OK.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: There's a range, I have a ranged but I'm a hip hop kid. That's how I grew up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, Jesse. We know the White House is trying to repair Harrison's image problem and put her out there more. But what was the goal of having Kamala do this interview with a small time YouTuber and wait, it got 3,000 views in three days.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Again, we're trying to reintroduce \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to voters. And what they don't understand is it's not about Kamala's personality, it's about her performance. We're not dating \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . We don't need to know what her favorite song is, her favorite salad dressing. We just want to know if she can perform because we're going to hire her if that's what they want. And that's not the kind of question you ask people you're trying to hire.\par \par \uc2 Now, Pete, I read this thing. Can you name a transportation secretary that's got a gushing puff piece like this? The answer is no, because this guy is running for president.\par \par \uc2 Look how many boxes this guy checks for a Democratic nominee. They say he's smart. He's a veteran. He lifts weights. He's in a gay relationship. He plays the piano, yet he also splits wood.\par \par \uc2 He'd not only likes cheeseburgers, he also likes impossible burgers. He loves God, but he really loves blacks, gays, women, and the poor. You can't check these many boxes. It's like a consultant came up with something and just overdid it.\par \par \uc2 I'm a little sick of Pete, to be honest with you, and especially the fact that I know he's got a electric Mustang. That's not -- that's not what I think you need Pete.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Dana, the author of this Buttigieg piece writes that he loves God, beer and his electric Mustang. But how about asking about the train derailment, flight cancellations and supply chain, his extended paternity leave? None of that comes in.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, I feel like this reporter at Wired needed an editor to say, go back and try again. And maybe the reporter himself was like this enamored, but they're -- just the total blind spot that they have.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is you only do a glowing profile or agreed to do a profile piece once you've done something significance. That's another life lesson. You don't do it when on the back of end of the East Palestine. You wait.\par \par \uc2 Now, the other thing is, the media keeps trying to make \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  a thing. The Democratic Party, the voters themselves, the grassroots of the Democratic Party don't want him as their thing.\par \par \uc2 And the media keeps trying to shove it down their throats that you are going to like him because it checks all of the boxes. And I think that he would be better off just like doing some great work, seeing how the travel season goes this summer, right? Because everybody's going to want to know, that's what people are going to want to know, how are the roads? How are the trains, how are the planes, and that's what's going to matter.\par \par \uc2 I do almost wanted to mention that I think I said this on Jesse's show last night. The White House is trying to be very, very sneaky on the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  reboot, because yes, that \uc2 YouTube\uc2  only got 3,000 views, right? But that woman that did the interview, she has 6.5 million likes on TikTok. And that's where -- they want you to think that they're only just doing \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , when it's actually a TikTok star that they're going after, while they still try to fight with the Chinese while the presidents in Japan at the G7.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Tom, they talked about the fact that the writer says they slowly became aware of Buttigieg's cabinet job requires only a small modest portion of his cognitive powers. And it appears now that Buttigieg beats Biden in Democrat voters pick of 2024, 23 to 18. What's stopping Pete from actually jumping into the game?\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Well, maybe he realizes as well, I agree with Dana, the voters don't like him. It's interesting, the two people we were talking about here was \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And they both were set up by the press as being the ultimate candidate. We never heard she's a perfect candidate. But the voters didn't like her either.\par \par \uc2 How about Robert Kennedy Jr. who's polling pretty well against the president, why doesn't anybody mention him? And you know what, a lot of red state people that I know, they like RFK Jr. and about the only thing he has in common with President Trump is that they were both banned from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Interesting. All right, Harold, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 FORD: So, I come from an era when you work for the president, you work for the pleasure -- at the pleasure of the president, you should behave like that.\par \par \uc2 If I were in the White House, I'd be disappointed that he would have done this, and the kind of attention that he's getting.\par \par \uc2 Number two, if I were the transportation secretary, I'd be focused on one, if I wanted to get out and get some press on my own, I'd want to get out and talk about some of the infrastructure successes and go into cities and states and talk about those things. That East Palestine trying to figure out, what can we do testing to ensure that people's property --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's what you would do, the fact that he's not.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, I can only answer the question how I would answer. I mean, so, I wouldn't be doing different things if I were him trying and if I were in the White House and say these are the things you need to be doing.\par \par \uc2 Now, he's chosen not to do that. And he shows him to do this. This is a really an error in politics where people think that having social media followers, getting on television is the only thing you need to do in order to be in politics. It happens on both sides of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 I give DeSantis a little credit, he at least is doing something. I disagree with a lot what he's doing with \uc2 Disney\uc2  and education here, but at least he's amassed a record that he's going to get out and campaign on.\par \par \uc2 Tim Scott the same way. Pence the same way. If you want to run for office, have an agenda, have a message, have a vision, have a platform, not a social media platform, but actually something substantive.\par \par \uc2 So, if I were in the White House, I'd be disappointed that my transportation secretary was --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And as former Congressman \uc2 Harold Ford\uc2 , are you disappointed in him?\par \par \uc2 FORD: I would, if I were the president of the United States, I would be disappointed.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You have an opinion, Harold.\par \par \uc2 FORD: True. I would much rather see the Transportation Secretary talking about those issues. The Energy Secretary talking about -- to me this is not the -- \uc2 Jennifer Granholm\uc2  said this is not what I would want my Transportation Secretary.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Disappointment is a strong word for Harold.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, I guess he went that far. OK, up next, a liberal city caving on migrants in school gyms after parents protests. But the problem is far from over.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 FORD: New York City backtracking and moving migrants out of some school gyms after parents and students took to the streets to express their outrage. Leaders there called it a temporary solution while they identified more potential housing options at the city. Sounds like it is running out of places to put migrants.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS, MAYOR OF NEW YORK CITY: In order to address an onslaught, you have to have places you can put people. New York City is the hotel capita, we're the hotel capital, tourism, visitors sporting events, graduations and it's a major economic engine for us. Almost 50 percent of those hotel rooms have been taken up by migrant asylum seekers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORD: And over in Florida, Democrats say that the state won't have vegetable pickers once Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  says immigration law goes into effect. It carries harsher penalties for transporting undocumented immigrants into the Sunshine State.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI FRIED\uc2 , FLORIDA DEMOCRATIC PARTY CHAIR: 800,000 migrants are going to leave the state of Florida. I don't know who's going to be doing the picking of the fields during the harvest.\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE WASSERMANN SCHULTZ (D-FL): I mean, you're going to have vegetables rotting in the fields, you're going to have construction sites that will lie dormant, or certainly will struggle to get workers to be able to help make sure that they can make progress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORD: That's just to my party. We should not have to -- we shouldn't say things like that. Dana, what's your reaction to this?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, here's the thing. If that is a concern, and it is, and I know, we hear it from farmers, too like they're having -- there's a big problem. But Congress has the reins basically, and the means and the obligation in order to pass laws to make that happen so that you can have people that are here that want to work legally, maybe it's temporary, maybe they want to come back and forth.\par \par \uc2 We just don't have a system like that. And until they fix the border, you're not going to have legislation that passes. So, we're still in this period where we're going to see how long can Democrats complain about every symptom of the problem without actually identifying the root cause of the problem, which is Biden's immigration policies and the fact that even \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  doesn't want to talk about the root causes of the Biden immigration policies.\par \par \uc2 50 percent of the hotel rooms in New York City are now taken up by migrants.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Astonishing.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Which may -- well, so where's Broadway? Where's the -- where's the union? Don't they want to complain, then nobody can afford a hotel room because of the sl0ws in supply and demand here in the city? Nobody can come and go shopping, or go to the sporting events like the mayor says.\par \par \uc2 So -- and the mayor is frustrated, and he's probably the most vocal, and yet he's still so mild-mannered about it. It's really interesting to see. I don't know how long this can go on. Because 50 percent will turn into 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 I understand they don't want to go to the schools and I applaud the parents for getting together and pushing back on that. But Eric Adams has a good question of where are we going to put them because border crossings aren't stopping at all. They're not slowing down.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Jesse, the Democrats are -- I think they're going to get even more aggressive on this issue like Adams. In your home city, Phila, they elected a new mayor who's cracking down, wants to hire more cops, more police officers in neighborhoods. Do you think other Democrats begin to take the position that Adams is taking on this issue heading into the election cycle?\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Depends how bad it gets. If you can keep sweeping it under the table? No. But if you're at capacity like we are in New York -- can you imagine who thought of it would be a good idea to put illegal alien men in schools? Is this the perfect Democrat school, OK, no security guards, CRT, no meat in the lunchroom, no grades, no honors programs?\par \par \uc2 And how dare you have Abraham Lincoln or George Washington be the name of the school? Because those guys were racist, white slave owners. And boys get to shower in the girls' room.\par \par \uc2 But special bonus, so do undocumented male illegal immigrants, some of which may be on the terror watch lists can be bathing, sleeping, and changing their outfits right next to chemistry class with your daughter or your son. That is perverted. And the fact that this got blocked by parents actually gives me hope.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Because parents didn't get together and actually protested not just on their phones, they were out there for 48 hours on the sidewalk. And that's what changed Adams' mind. And that really is the way we need to do things, not just with the schools and immigration with every issue.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Agreed, judge?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, it goes to show how important it is for Americans to rise up and be heard. This could have been a moment like what happened in Virginia with the school board. The parents are out and you know, they have the signs. I want my kids to go to a safe school, please. Just a question mark, background check anyone?\par \par \uc2 This is an evasion, not just at the border, but our schools. And the amazing part of this is you say to yourself, and I've been saying this forever and I'm tired of here when I said it, the who among these people that we have not vetted that don't even have an alien registration number?\par \par \uc2 Who among these people is in that gym where kids are supposed to play? And when our kids can't be in that gym, should they be playing stickball in the street somewhere in Manhattan? Should they be grabbed by someone else who's walking by?\par \par \uc2 And so, what the Department of Education does is they put out a response to parents' concerns. And the parents are saying number one, are asylum seekers that are in these gyms vaccinated? Asylum seekers, not illegal immigrants, asylum seekers who haven't made it to court haven't been determined to be asylum seekers.\par \par \uc2 They go through medical screening and they're offered vaccinations. They don't have to take them, but they're offered them. And this is my favorite. Do they have criminal records?\par \par \uc2 Asylum seekers have been screened by U.S. Customs and Border Patrol. Are you kidding me? We are sending them into the country on mass without an alien registration number where the courts are saying to Biden you cannot send them without at least giving them a court date and an alien registration number. And they're lying to parents and telling them, oh, border patrol did a run on them.\par \par \uc2 Hogwash, they don't even fingerprint them. They don't do DNA. They don't do it to the kids. And they let them out whenever they want.\par \par \uc2 And how about the five million that came in the last few years? The million gotaways, did they fingerprint them too? This is a bunch of lies and that's why the parents have to come out to be heard.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  who's so concerned about the schools, where the hell is she? She's in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Where's Randi?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Tom, I want you to build on those comments that were just --\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes. I don't even hear your question, Harold. I know -- I agree that the judge said it. Loudoun County, Virginia, it's the same thing.\par \par \uc2 The power of the parents, this is -- this is the new way that people realize they can approach politics. They can get out there in their neighborhoods. They can make a change. I think it's excellent.\par \par \uc2 And I think -- I think Karol Markowicz said this on your show that the -- I'm not impressed with the mayor because, you know, he is -- he's talking a good game when he talks about the president but he's not saying build that wall. He's saying just write me a check.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: So, it's about the money with him. He just wants to be bailed out.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Up next. It's not just California. The Squad is taking the push for reparations nationwide.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Why stop in California? The far left is now calling for a nationwide reparations payout. Squad member \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  introducing a bill that would give, are you ready? $14 trillion worth of reparations for black people who are descendants of slaves.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CORI BUSH (D-MO): America must provide reparations if we desire a prosperous future for all. 222 million hours of forced labor, which today would be at the value of over $97 trillion. When we can send money abroad to help elsewhere, we have to make sure because we're talking about helping with things that are happening now when we did not repair what happened before.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATERS: I am going to talk to you, Tom Shillue.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: 14 trillion or already what 33 trillion in debt?\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes. Where's Newsom on this? The thing is they -- he came out strong about the reparations until they started to be a reality. And we haven't heard from him, have we?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: He just called me somewhere.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes. He's hiding. Because anybody who's realistic about this knows it's a completely goofy idea. It's off-the-charts expensive. It's not even a good idea, to begin with. So, I -- you know, it's not a budget issue. It's a bad idea.\par \par \uc2 But they're not going to be able to pay for it. They can't pay for all the other stuff that's going on in California. So, no. It's a ridiculous idea.\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  wanted to propose it so he could run for president. And he thought, oh, they're never going to do anything about it. And now that they are doing something, he does want to run and hide.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: You know Congress, Dana. Is this going to get traction in Congress?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I don't care if it does. Because you know what? Politically, this is great for Republicans, right?\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Because under the Democrats' logic, one person presented a bill that would have reformed entitlement spending in the future. And that turned into all Republicans wanting to throw granny off the cliff and take away your Social Security and Medicare. So, I think turnabout is only fair play. So, all the Republicans can now say is that the Democrats want to do $14 trillion of reparations and just hang it on them.\par \par \uc2 Now, the White House has been very careful to say, you know, this is for Congress to decide.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: This is for Congress. Do you really think that your base is going to let you get away with that? For how long? And I just would love to see that if Biden were to debate in a primary, which probably won't, but he will have to debate in 2024, this would be a great question for him to have to answer.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: And he will say we will have a commission to study it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, and we know how that goes.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Judge Jeanine Pirro.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 , she's loud. She is, I guess, influential in The Squad. Do you --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: How many is in The Squad, four?\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Well, they added a guy.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, OK.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Does he get special privileges, by the way, if you're the only male in The Squad?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, he gets going to the ladies' room.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: So, will this catch fire at all?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No. 70 percent of Americans are against it. That's number one. Number two, I just listened to \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 , who by the way, \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  and \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2 , apparently, they voted against the resolution to honor police officers killed in line of duty. I mean, yes, they're real Americans.\par \par \uc2 But anyway, the -- she just said, \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 , we need what, was a $91 trillion dollars so that we can then send the money abroad. Wait a minute, I thought this was to repay you for slavery. Now, in a civil war, people are dying. That's not enough.\par \par \uc2 We need to get the money to send it abroad for other people. No, this is absurd. What it's doing is it's creating the anticipation just like Newsom did. He signed the Task Force, he signed it when it got back, and then he comes back and he actually said, you know, this isn't really all about money.\par \par \uc2 But what they're doing is they're creating division, a possibility of hope that it might happen, and then creating the division of us versus them mentality. They -- we are owed this, and it's ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Harold Ford Jr.\par \par \uc2 FORD: So, judge, will you -- we -- I think we all know. We have a history of redress in our country. And whether it's a government agency doing something wrong to an American citizen who had been entitled to money, we had it with the Japanese who were in turn wrongly during World War Two so, we agreed. Even when we talked about this with California, believe, Jess, I want to put words in your mouth that there was harm done through slavery.\par \par \uc2 Now, some of these proposals and plans here -- and we shouldn't allow presenters of this idea to distract us from the reality of what happened. And if indeed we should be thinking about some form of redress on the part of some people. Now, I would agree with Dana, $14 trillion or 90 -- well, I heard her say, 90, I think 97 trillion, Judge.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: That's not going to --\par \par \uc2 WATERS: 90 -- did you say 97 trillion? Really --\par \par \uc2 FORD: I think that's what -- I think that's what the congresswoman said that --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's what she wants.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, that's -- I know, it -- the bill say -- but whatever the case, we shouldn't let that distract from what we're doing. And she -- that these numbers, I'm not -- I'm not certain that if the numbers even arrived at with a mathematical equation that is justifiable. So, I hope is the person that sits in that chair, Thompson -- most of the time, Greg and I talked about education and business investment and figuring out ways to get guns out of certain communities where people have more guns and access to things that they really need, those are the things we should be talking about.\par \par \uc2 And if this conversation is the catalyst for something like that, as an instigation for something like that, then we'll be positive. But we should be rest assured we're not going to get to $14 trillion or for that matter, anywhere close to that. But we should also not deny that there's a history of redress in our country in compensating people for wrongdoing towards them.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. Including the Native Americans, I think.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Yes, ma'am.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: We can go back a lot.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: But how far are we going to go back? That's the question.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: We got to go forward now.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, history --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: History shows that slavery was our original sin, so.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: OK. Harold Ford Jr. Ahead. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is calling BS on the work- from-home revolution.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Billionaire, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , pulling no punches on what he thinks about the rise in remote workers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, \uc2 TWITTER\uc2  CEO: It's a productivity issue, but it's also a moral issue. People should get off the moral high horse with their work from home because they're asking everyone else to not work from home while they do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes, what do you think of that? Jesse, did you know that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  beeps a lot?\par \par \uc2 WATERS: It's amazing. It sounds like Gutfeld exclamation. I used to be a pro go into the office and I've changed positions.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Did you have?\par \par \uc2 WATERS: I think we should tailor it strategically to the industry. And I stand by that change of opinion.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes, interesting. But you're in an industry where you -- I mean you have to come in to work. I mean, I guess you could -- you could zoom in, but nobody wants to see that. You got to be here.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Only because I'm on a show with four other people.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's right -- that's right.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: I see, OK.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: If I could do seven, by myself alone in a garage or a truck --\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: -- you'd never see me again.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Well, Dana, here's the issue. He basically said it's not fair. He said it's a moral issue. Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think it's interesting that he said that. And part of it is the opportunity costs that are missed for people who are not here, right? And then there's -- the CEOs across the country are saying this. But his specifically saying that's morally wrong is that you have people in sort of the management -- executive management who are telling people they have to come back to the office while they sit in the Hamptons.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And that's what he's saying is morally wrong. And I would agree with that. There is a new group in the laptop class.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Right, yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: They could just sit on their laptop all day, go to the garage, hang out, go to the coffee shop, go to the park, and everybody else is schlepping it into the city back and forth. And part of that means that you have -- there could be things that you do like improving the infrastructure so that people can get in and out easier. But it's strange once you let people stay home for a while.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes, it happens.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And I get wanting to be there for the kids' school play, and the baseball games and everything. So, there might be some ways for flexibility. But he's not wrong about the executives demanding people being in the office when they're not.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: And that what -- is what was so bad about all the shutdowns, Harold, that people got used to it. And now they don't want to come in.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Right. I mean, I agree with -- Dana. I think his point -- the morale -- the moral point of this is you have a group of people who have a skill set and who have an ability to have a laptop, and more importantly, can work from it. He's pointing out the hypocrisy of it.\par \par \uc2 I think we've done it on this show the irony of people who say, we should get back to work, and people should wear masks and all these things, yet they want things delivered to them. They want things to cater to them. So, you know, he's a smart guy, as I've said, on many times on this show that he is the -- he's our generation, \uc2 Steve Jobs\uc2 . I'm 53 years old. He's a technologist that, in so many ways, is provocative, that is smart, that is willing to challenge the norms --\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: And for that matter, even challenges his own peers in his industry.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes. And, Judge, this is the thing that he's telling people, they got to come into work. But is he going to be able to do it? Can they demand people if they need those workers, and they say, I'm not coming in? You got to make a choice, right?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, we got all these new workers coming in, right? They're coming to work.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. But look, the way I feel about this is when you come to work and you engage with people in your line of work, you learn more. You know, you're kind of more on your game. You're a little more competitive.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, I think -- I think it's a good thing for me anyway, you know. And I think that people just want to stay home, know that they're distracted by other things and they're not totally focused on work.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: If you focused on work, you do your job, and then you go home when you're done.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: That's right. "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's time now one -- for "ONE MORE THING," Judge, then I did it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, all right. You know that I spent most of my life putting people in jail as a prosecutor and judge but I'm very proud of this. 30 California state inmates graduated from an apprenticeship program provided by the California prison industry authority.\par \par \uc2 It places -- it looks to place incarcerated individuals with employment upon finishing their sentences in jobs that go from healthcare facility maintenance to running commercial laundry facilities. 85 percent, folks, of the inmates who graduated from the program do not return to prison --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Love it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: -- within three years of being free. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's so -- maybe we need more of that. Absolutely. Harold?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Lords in the bus in business. Here's yet another example of why it's important to always keep your head on a swivel. Fisherman Scott Haraguchi was kayaking -- K -- kayaking off O-A-Ho when a tiger shark similarly came out of nowhere ramming and bodying his boat. If you look closer, you can see Scott taking a shark off with his left foot.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Wow. Oh, my --\par \par \uc2 FORD: He believes a shark likely mistook his kayak for wounded sale but got a mouthful of plastic. And a kick in the head, instead of a meal.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: How can he think that is a wounded seal?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes. and OK, really quickly --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Kayaking.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Jason Hardesty might be having more fun at work than we do. He's a \uc2 UPS\uc2  driver. He's in New Orleans. And every time he sees a dog, he takes a photo -- he takes a selfie and he posted for everybody on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, a goat.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: He has 134,000 followers. You can keep up with his adventures. OK. Also, don't miss "SHORT QUESTIONS WITH DANA PERINO." I have a "SPECIAL REPORT" with Brett Baier. And, Jesse, you're next.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: All right, wild police footage out of Iowa. Look at this police officer aiming his handgun at the driver telling him to stop, telling him to pull over --\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: -- he doesn't.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: God.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: So, he starts speeding through this industrial park. He's clinging to the roof for dear light.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: The guy runs over a ditch, officer falls, out ends up breaking a little vertebrae in the back.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: His guy says can we keep following? And he says go get them. And they got him.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: He's in the slammer for five years, Judge.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Excellent.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Tonight, at primetime, "THE FBI WHISTLEBLOWERS."\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right -- what's your name?\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: All right -- Tom, yes. Florida man plays pickleball everywhere. Dean Matt is going to play pickleball in 48 of the lower 48 states. He's halfway through. I hope you make it, Dean.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow, that's a lot of pickleball.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Look at him.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: A lot of pickleball.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, that is it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020230519ej5i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pressure Mounts On Lawmakers To Reach Debt Deal; United States Business Leaders Urge Negotiators To Reach Deal; Reaction To Durham Report On Alleged Russia Collusion; Critics Denounce \uc2 FBI\uc2  Handling Of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Probe; \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  CEO Testifies About Emerging Technology; Migrants Continue to Cross U.S. Border After Ending of Title 42; Democratic Presidential Candidate Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Interviewed On His Reasons for Running; New Chicago Mayor Brandon Johnson Draws Criticism For Comparing Death Of 13- Year-Old By Police To Shooting Death Of Police Officer; Biden Administration And Congressional Leadership Staffers Continue Meeting On Possible Deal To Raise U.S. Debt Ceiling; Special Counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2  Releases Report On Origins of Trump-Russia Collusion Investigation\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, David Spunt, Aishah Hasnie, Bill  Melugin, Mike Tobin, Ben Domenech, Katie Pavlich, Harold Ford Jr. \par \uc2 8,480 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Strike one.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: All right. And Tyrus, you're talking about yourself at these things?\par \par \uc2 TYRUS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: What? In my comedy shows?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: I talk about -- no, I talk about myself a little bit. I am self- deprecating, but I also enjoy jokes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: At his expense.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right.\par \par \uc2 GERALDO RIVERA, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT-AT-LARGE: One quick joke. One quick joke.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Watters, Judges, Geraldo --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Thanks for the comedy show, Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No joke. Hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'll wait for the joke. All right, Judge. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Coming up. We'll talk with former Attorney General \uc2 William Barr\uc2  about the special counsel report -- the Durham report on the Trump-Russia collusion allegations and that probe.\par \par \uc2 We will also speak with Democratic presidential candidate Robert F. Kennedy Jr. about his primary challenge to President Biden.\par \par \uc2 But "BREAKING TONIGHT", the White House meeting between the president and top congressional leaders wrapped up a short time ago.\par \par \uc2 House Speaker Kevin McCarthy says the two sides are still far apart on a debt ceiling deal. But the Speaker added, it would not be that difficult to get to a deal by the end of the week.\par \par \uc2 The president is canceling part of his upcoming overseas trip returning to the U.S. Sunday after the \uc2 G7\uc2  economic meeting in Japan.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has our top story tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening to you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 House Speaker Kevin McCarthy says there is a gap between the two sides in moving forward. But as the president leaves for Japan tomorrow, the White House is appointing Senior Adviser \uc2 Steve Ricchetti\uc2 , Legislative Affairs Director Louisa Terrell, and OMB Director \uc2 Shalanda Young\uc2 , to run point for the White House and talk to McCarthy's deputies.\par \par \uc2 And the pressure is on after treasury secretary put the deadline at June 1st or the days following.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're having a wonderful time. Everything is going well.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Biden with a message of reassurance, convening congressional leaders as the country creeps closer to potentially defaulting on its debt, with no agreement yet to cut spending and raise the debt limit.\par \par \uc2 Biden canceled plans to visit Australia and Papua New Guinea following the \uc2 G7\uc2  in Japan amid criticism he'd be out of the country for roughly half the time left to hash out a deal.\par \par \uc2 There are three fewer voices at the negotiating table now. McCarthy is saying, Biden agreed Schumer, Jeffries, and McConnell will take a backseat.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): And it is possible to get a deal by the end the week. So, the structure of how we negotiate has improved.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: After protests from congressional Democrats --\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): I'm sure that Speaker McCarthy would like to be the only show in town.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Leadership apparently conceded.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Default is a disaster. Full stop. And everyone understood that in the room.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But new sticking points are emerging. The White House assuring progressives, Biden will not give away the farm, voicing concern over the GOP push to add work requirements to some welfare programs.\par \par \uc2 And the White House is trying to limit spending caps to the next two years as Republicans warned they need 10 years of limits to get enough votes.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the country's debt to GDP ratio exceeds 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 Meaning, its bills exceed its assets. The only other time this happened was during World War II.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: How was it not a crisis when the country literally owes more than it's worth?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We should ask the Speaker this question. This is his job. This is his constitutional duty to move forward and get the debt limit done. That is a question for him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Today, dozens of CEOs urged negotiators to get a deal done fast. Saying, back in 2011, as the deadline approach, standard and poor's downgraded the country's AAA credit rating, which still has not been restored.\par \par \uc2 And even though a deal was cut two days before the default, it cost the economy about 1.2 million jobs and 17 percent of the stock market's value.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, we were told that Secret Service is investigating after a man who appeared to be drunk apparently accidentally walked into national security adviser Jake Sullivan's home last month was not detected by his security detail.\par \par \uc2 We're told he made no threats and left after Sullivan confronted him. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich, live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Here is some of the other headlines today. A woman who is suing \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  for sexual assault and harassment says the former New York mayor and Trump attorney discussed selling pardons for $2 million and plans to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Woman also says Giuliani refused to pay her. She is seeking at least $10 million damages. Julian -- Giuliani denies all the allegations.\par \par \uc2 Senators have passed a resolution rejecting police reform legislation in the District of Columbia. This follows a similar move in the House. President Biden is threatening to veto that measure.\par \par \uc2 And a discouraging financial forecast from \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  as well as the continuing debt concerns since stocks down today. The Dow lost 336. The S&P 500 was off 26. The NASDAQ fell 22.\par \par \uc2 Well, reaction is coming in to the special counsel's report on the Trump- Russia investigation.\par \par \uc2 John Durham\uc2  concluded the \uc2 FBI\uc2  should not have even launched that probe. He also pointed out the vast differences in how former President Trump and former Democratic presidential nominee \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  were treated by the Bureau.\par \par \uc2 But the pushback is loud. Correspondent David Spunt is at the justice department with the latest tonight. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, more than 24 hours after officials released that 306-page report, some of the names in that report are fighting back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): More revelations from the Durham report, including the claim that \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials suppressed an investigation into allegations of impropriety within \uc2 the Clinton Foundation\uc2 , leading up to the 2016 election in \uc2 the Clinton Foundation\uc2  matter. Both senior \uc2 FBI\uc2  and department officials placed restrictions on how those matters were to be handled, such that essentially no investigative activities occurred for months leading up to the election. The Clinton probe did not materialize.\par \par \uc2 Not the same fate for the Trump campaign and Crossfire Hurricane, the name for the probe looking at possible ties between team Trump and Russia.\par \par \uc2 Former deputy \uc2 FBI\uc2  director \uc2 Andrew McCabe\uc2 's name is mentioned 58 times in the report.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCABE\uc2 , FORMER DEPUTY DIRECTOR OF THE FEDERAL BUREAU OF INVESTIGATION: \uc2 John Durham\uc2  is wrong. I don't think this investigation was legitimate from its inception. And it continues to fuel a false narrative about alleged \uc2 FBI\uc2  malfeasance that continues to this day up.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: McCabe defends the bureau decision to open Crossfire Hurricane, around the time when candidate Trump said this about his opponent.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Russia, if you're listening, I hope you're able to find the 30,000 e-mails that are missing.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: But Durham wrote, "Senior \uc2 FBI\uc2  personnel displayed a serious lack of analytical rigor towards the information that they received, especially information received from politically affiliated persons and entities."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , under new leadership since 2017, in a statement, touted, dozens of reforms that even \uc2 John Durham\uc2  agreed would likely prevent a repeat of history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): And Bret, House Judiciary Chairman Jim Jordan invited \uc2 John Durham\uc2  to come testify before Congress next week. We're told those discussions are still underway.\par \par \uc2 Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David, moving another investigation, the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  investigation, there is new information from an attorney representing a whistleblower. What about that?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Yes, that's exactly right. Well, a few weeks ago, a - an attorney, a local attorney, representing an \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblower, came to say that his investigation was stalled.\par \par \uc2 We've confirmed via other sources. It's this high-profile investigation into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's taxes.\par \par \uc2 We're learning though, through a letter from the whistle blower's lawyer to members of Congress, the whistleblower and his entire team have been removed from the, "sensitive" investigation of the high-profile controversial subject at the request of DOJ. Our sources telling us that person is \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IRS\uc2  commissioner last month, said that nobody would be retaliated against for becoming a whistleblower.\par \par \uc2 And just in, Jason Smith, a congressman from Missouri told our Chad Pergram a little while ago that he specifically asked the commissioner about retaliating against whistleblowers, and he was assured that whistleblowers would not be retaliated against. Something we'll continue to follow. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, David. Earlier, we brought your reaction to the special counsel's report on the Trump-Russia collusion investigation.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we get perspective from former Attorney General \uc2 William Barr\uc2 , who put all of this in motion, choosing \uc2 John Durham\uc2  for this job.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General, thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 What's your take -- as you read this report, what did you take away from it?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR\uc2 , FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL OF THE UNITED STATES: I take away from it that it was a success in that -- its purpose from the very beginning was to get to the truth.\par \par \uc2 It was not launched as a criminal investigation and people compare it to the Mueller investigation or simply wrong. I asked him to get to the bottom of it, get his arms around the truth. If he found crimes, we could prosecute them.\par \par \uc2 But I think that's what he's done. He's dug into it. And I think he's gotten -- as close to the bottom as anyone is going to get to this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, the key takeaways, there was no actual evidence to support launching Crossfire Hurricane. And also, the Hillary Clinton campaign directly or indirectly funding and driving much of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 BARR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is that what's key to you?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Yes. Well, I think -- I think what this has shown is that the source and really two sources and drivers of this whole ugly episode, and that is \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's campaign, who launched a smear campaign that was based on getting Russian source -- Russian sources, to spread false information about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and peddle it to the press, and to the government, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And both the press and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  abandon any semblance of professionalism, and took up the cause with a vengeance. And I think the real story here from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's perspective is what an abomination this was, this so-called investigation.\par \par \uc2 You know, if it wasn't a witch hunt, it's a damn good imitation of one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As you can imagine, reaction has been all over the place. But pretty on other networks, pretty specific in the criticism of the report. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, CO-HOST, MORNING JOE, MSNBC: For years, millions of dollars, a record long investigation, and he has nothing to show for it.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, FORMER GENERAL COUNSEL, FEDERAL BUREAU OF INVESTIGATION: There was just this real lack of substance to what he was doing. This was trying to say that there's a big there, there, when, you know, there is no there, there.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER RODGERS, LEGAL ANALYST, CNN: This was a whole big nothing. They did not prove this deep state conspiracy because it never existed in the first place.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Let's not forget what the lie was. The lie was Russiagate. The lie was there was collusion. There was nothing to support it. And these people were implicated in spreading that lie and benefiting from it personally, and hurting the country with their conspiracy mongering.\par \par \uc2 My effort and Durham's effort was to look at that, which turned out had nothing behind it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, talk about coming up empty. Mueller came up empty. He took an old case against Manafort and resuscitated it. He prosecuted Stone for lying to Congress. But there was no collusion.\par \par \uc2 We went to look why -- what got this started? And what was behind it. And we have gotten to the answers.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is there something that stops short of being a success if you don't get a prosecution? He failed in the prosecution that he brought forward.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, there is other people that are mentioned here, but there is not an action against them in court.\par \par \uc2 BARR: Right. I made clear all the way through this thing. The first step is to find out what happened and get to the truth. And if crimes can be proven, which are very high standard, you have to prove intent beyond a reasonable doubt. There'll be brought.\par \par \uc2 But I've tried to -- I tried to explain to people as we went along that when you're dealing with people who make discretionary decisions that were within their authority, it's very hard to prove corrupt intent.\par \par \uc2 So, I -- you know, if there was a provable crime that would be brought, \uc2 John Durham\uc2  felt he could not prove criminal intent in these cases. But the important thing is shining light on what happened.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But what do you think comes out of that? I mean, is it up to Capitol Hill to dig into to force changes at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ? What comes out of this?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Well, I do think that the Hill can follow up on this and make sure that appropriate changes are made. I have to say that one of the things coming out of this is it's clear that the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the problem with came from the top.\par \par \uc2 This was not necessarily institutional rot, or problems that may exist today. This was Comey, McCabe, Strzok, driving this thing from the top. That is no longer the problem at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There may -- there are problems that have to be invest (PH), but that's not the problem. And I have to say that there have been some changes. \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2  and I put a lot of changes into effect as this thing has unfolded. And I think there is some more changes that can be made.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is interesting to listen to not only Andrew Weissmann, who was on the Mueller probe. But also, \uc2 Andrew McCabe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCABE: Yes, vehemently disagree with Mr. Durham's characterizations of what we did in the report. And it's very simple, he betrays a deep misunderstanding of not only what we knew at the time, but how we make these decisions.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: You have -- with \uc2 John Durham\uc2  is, like, it's a big, fat, nothing. And it reminds me of the sort of weaponization hearings that are going on where there also is to they're just falling flat on their face, because there is no there, there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And that's what you hear again --\par \par \uc2 BARR: Yes. Well, what the there, there is that this investigation was launched by McCabe, who directed that it would be brought immediately -- opened immediately, before they even interviewed the Australian diplomat.\par \par \uc2 And that turned out to be a nothing burger. And they then proceeded with this investigation, which they knew they had just received intelligence that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  campaign was spreading this information about Trump. And yet, they took this information at face value, they relied upon it, it was unvetted.\par \par \uc2 The things happened here that were a radical departure from how these cases are normally handled, and completely incompatible with the idea that this was an unbiased investigation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You've been critical of your former boss. His temperament, his style. But in this case, in this effort to go after him, you defend him. You think this exonerates him?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Well, I felt though, as I started learning more and more about Russiagate. And I've said, this is one of the greatest injustice has done to a presidential candidate and the president. And he was treated unfairly here. So, he is vindicated as far as Russiagate is concerned.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You stopped short there, are you --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BARR: Well, I've had my differences with him. I've also said that I feel like that he had great policies. There was -- he deserve a lot of credit for what he accomplished. And this was a grave injustice and the fact that he was able to accomplish a lot in the face of it, I think it was a great achievement.\par \par \uc2 I have other problems -- you know, I'm not supporting him for renomination but he was right on this and this is a vindication. He had it right from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, talking about the price of the Durham probe $6.5 million over four years, a couple days, the Mueller probe was two years plus, 32 million.\par \par \uc2 So, is this worth this time and effort? What is the benefit in the long term?\par \par \uc2 BARR: This goes to the heart of making sure that law enforcement power is not abused, interfere in politics. This is fundamental in civil rights.\par \par \uc2 I didn't hear anyone complaining when the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was being investigated for getting into civil rights groups and abuses back in the 60s and 70s. In fact, that was being cheered on by the left.\par \par \uc2 This was investigating a presidential campaign based on a lie. No evidence, not even dismissing all the exculpatory evidence.\par \par \uc2 I invite people to look at that report. It's great reading and the number of instances where the \uc2 FBI\uc2  made these decisions that are inexplicable, are breathtaking.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A couple of specifics. Lack of analytical rigor, apparent confirmation bias, and over willingness to rely on information from individuals connected to political opponents caused investigators to fail to adequately consider alternative hypotheses, and to act without appropriate objectivity or restraint. Although recognizing that in hindsight much is clearer, much of this also seems to have been planned.\par \par \uc2 BARR: Right. And it was driven from the top. There was a predisposition to do this. One of the -- one of the good stories here is that a number of \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents throughout this process would say, hey, wait a minute, what are we doing here and try to slow things down and point -- and they were brushed aside, they were reassigned.\par \par \uc2 And this thing was just driven from the top as one agent in London said, when they were trying -- when they were opening the investigation, you know, this train is coming down the tracks and people have to get out of the way, it's being driven from the top.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you concerned about the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the DOJ, looking at it now?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Yes, so I have been concerned about both institutions for a long time. And I do think that you know, that there are more people in those institutions that are that are willing to engage in politics.\par \par \uc2 But I still think the center of gravity in those institutions among the professionals are still people who can check their politics at the door and do a good job.\par \par \uc2 Another theme in this report, is how the Justice Department National Security Division kept on trying to inject some sanity into this thing and fought hard for it, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you see this report. And then you see the Hunter Biden laptop and the 51 signatories to this letter that is Russian disinformation, which then candidate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  uses in the debate. I mean, it seems to fit this pattern.\par \par \uc2 BARR: That was -- yes, it was. It's the same pattern, absolute lie. And then getting political hacks in the case of Russia-gate, where some of the talking heads we see on other cable channels to endorse it. And that's what happened on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , it was an absolute bare faced lie, and people knew it was a lie. There was no question that was his laptop.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, it hasn't stopped, in other words?\par \par \uc2 BARR: No. And, you know, I think one of the problems is that some of these channels now, these people are implicated in the big lie of Russia-gate. They're implicated in the big lie of the laptop.\par \par \uc2 They have been, you know, the political operatives that have been peddling this stuff to the American people, some of them have won prizes, and then they celebrate the civil servants who are responsible for the, you know, the horrible invest -- so-called investigations that were run and all the breaches of their duties.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to ask you two more things, one is a 70-page complaint that was filed in state court in New York on Monday. This is someone who worked for \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  who alleges not only sexual assault and harassment, but that she has evidence that he tried to sell pardons for $2 million apiece, which he and President Trump would split. Do you think that that's possible?\par \par \uc2 BARR: You know, I'm skeptical about that. I don't -- I don't think \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  would do that. I hope he wouldn't. But I don't know.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally, to follow up when you said you weren't endorsing the former president in this run, he is up pretty much in every poll that you look at significantly, obviously, this race is at the beginning.\par \par \uc2 But now you have this Durham report, in which what he was saying is backed up by what's in these pages. Do you think that helps him? And do you think he's going to win the GOP nomination?\par \par \uc2 BARR: I think it helps him in the short term. My own view is that he will not win the nomination. And I have nothing personal against him and there's no -- you know, I have no antipathy toward him at all. But I just don't think he's the right leader for the Republican Party going forward.\par \par \uc2 I think people who want to restore America should look for a big victory that brings strength into the Congress and allows us to achieve some fundamental changes. And I don't think he's capable of delivering that kind of victory.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you think he would lose to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BARR: I think he has the best chance of losing. But even if he won, my concern is that he would wipe out Republicans in the Congress because of the vast turnout that would occur just to vote against him.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think he has -- you know, he's very good at stopping the progressive march back in 2016. But I don't think he has the skills of unifying the party and persuading people on a particular program and getting it done through Congress.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Attorney General Bill Barr, we appreciate the time.\par \par \uc2 BARR: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime, today, Congress is considering new rules for artificial intelligence. A top tech executive spoke to lawmakers today about some of the potential dangers which range from A.I. taking your job to machines essentially trying to take over the world. No joke, concerns about A.I. in full view today.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie is on Capitol Hill tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM ALTMAN\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 OPENAI\uc2 : I think if this technology goes wrong, it can go quite wrong.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  CEO \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2  says it is time for Congress to regulate artificial intelligence, the man behind ChatGPT testifying for the first time on Capitol Hill warning lawmakers that A.I. could turn scary and be used to design novel biological pathogens hack into computer systems or even interfere with elections.\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): I just want to know what you're planning on doing it -- doing about it.\par \par \uc2 ALTMAN: I think this is an area where hopefully the entire industry and the government can work together quickly.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Quickly is the key. For the most part, the U.S. is behind other nations like China when it comes to rules and investment around A.I. Altman suggested lawmakers form a new agency to license and govern A.I. companies like his, set safety standards and require independent audits.\par \par \uc2 But he wouldn't tell Fox how soon he thinks A.I. could become self-aware if guardrails are not put up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think a lot of folks at home are wondering?\par \par \uc2 ALTMAN: I think there's a huge amount of speculation on that question. I think it's very important that we keep talking about this as a tool, not a creature.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: At the very least, Altman admits A.I. will destroy jobs but lead to better ones, and that Congress needs to protect those. A.I. is already testing, like when it was used to clone pop stars and create a hit.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): Who owns the right to that A.I. generated material?\par \par \uc2 ALTMAN: We think that creators deserve control over how their creations are used.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): So, Bret, for the past few weeks here on Capitol Hill, Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has been talking with industry experts like \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and crafting some safeguards when it comes to A.I. but still unclear if we could see some legislation before the August recess even though there is bipartisan support for seeing something done on this, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it all. Aishah Hasnie live on the Hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll take you to the southern border for a live report there.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Robert F. Kennedy Jr. joins me live to talk about his primary challenge, President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Customs and Border Protection sources tell Fox News drug cartels and smugglers are changing their tactics now that pandemic related asylum rules, Title 42, no longer in effect along the southern border. Tonight we get an update on illegal crossings now that that rule is history.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bill Melugin has the latest tonight from Brownsville, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): New security video obtained exclusively by Fox News shows a large group of migrants crossing illegally into private property in Eagle Pass Texas as a Border Patrol boat watches from the river.\par \par \uc2 A National Guard soldier can be seen opening a gate for the migrants. The Texas National Guard tells FOX that soldier is not one of theirs. They say she is a Missouri soldier working for the federal government.\par \par \uc2 In Ajo, Arizona, Border Patrol reports encountering three huge groups of more than 200 migrants each in the days after Title 42 expired. Sources tell FOX these migrants came from all around the world. And in the Rio Grande Valley, Texas DPS says a trooper encountered this group of 30 Chinese nationals walking down a road near Roma, Texas, after they crossed illegally.\par \par \uc2 Despite illegal crossings being down sharply, mass releases continue as capacity remains an issue in some Border Patrol sectors like El Paso. That's where migrants are still camped out on city streets after release.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): We thank God and the \uc2 United States government\uc2  for welcoming us with open arms. Fully grateful.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: El Paso's Democratic congresswoman blames a federal judge for overcrowding after he blocked Biden administration efforts to mass release migrants without court dates.\par \par \uc2 REP. VERONICA ESCOBAR, (D-TX): That has meant that our processing facilities in El Paso are seriously overcrowded.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, last week, a 17-year-old unaccompanied minor from Honduras died while in \uc2 HHS\uc2  custody while living in a federal facility in Pinellas County, Florida. Today, the sheriff there announced their investigation found that that minor died of an epileptic seizure. The sheriff says he suffered from seizures, had that medical condition, didn't have his meds on him, but told nobody at that facility that he had that medical condition. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin along the border. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has some competition, not entirely smooth sailing to another nomination from the Democratic Party. Robert F. Kennedy Jr. is challenging the president for a chance to be at the top of the ticket in 2024 and he joins us tonight in studio. Sir, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR. (D), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thanks for having me, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are recent polls, we have a poll out where you are at 19 percent. There are other polls you're hovering around 20, 21. That raised some eyebrows in political circles. What do you think is working for you as far as topics that people are getting on to?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: I think people really want to have a solution for what's happening and just attention to the fact that the middle class in this country is collapsing. In 1970, 62 percent of income in this country went to the middle class. Today 42 percent. In 1970, 29 percent of income went to the super rich. Today it's 50 percent. And that was aggravated, of course, by the pandemic. We created a new billionaire every day, which was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's pandemic lockdown, which created 500 new billionaires. We shifted $4 trillion in wealth from the American middle class to the super rich. And we shut down 3.3 million businesses.\par \par \uc2 Those trends were continued by the Biden administration, and I think a lot of Americans want somebody who is really going to start listening to the middle class, understanding the desperation that so many Americans feel and that is going to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about President Biden do you hear from Democrats? Is there real concern about his nomination, his ability to win? What are you hearing on the trail?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, I have a long kind of friendship and relationship with President Biden, so I'm not -- I'm not criticizing him or attacking him personally. But I think people want to see him out of the White House, and actually talking to Americans, going to town halls, the president says that the economy is humming along. But that's not what I hear when I'm out in western Pennsylvania, Ohio, New Hampshire, Florida. I see people really living in a state of desperation that I never thought I would see in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, there is some pushback in the chattering class about this candidacy. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " said "His name is Kennedy. His campaign is pure Trump. He offers a variation on the Trumpian theme of trashing government and science while stoking fear and resentment. He decries the $100 billion cost of keeping \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's out of Europe, a fraction of the nation's military budget. This sounds awfully much like America first and nothing at all like the new frontier." What's your response to something like that?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: On the Ukraine war, listen, I think we're in the Ukraine war for the right reasons. Americans supported it because of our humanity, because we are a good nation. We are a compassionate nation. My oldest son went to the Ukraine, joined the special forces, fought in the Kharkiv offensive as a machine gunner, and risked his life for the Ukrainian people out of compassion and admiration for their valor.\par \par \uc2 But the objectives of the war have changed, and they've changed from Washington, D.C., and driven by the neocons in the White House. President Biden himself has said that one of our objectives is to depose \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . His Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  has said that the objective of America in the Ukraine is to exhaust the Russian army, to degrade its capacity to fight elsewhere in the world. That is the opposite of a humanitarian mission. That is a mission that is going to use hundreds of thousands of Ukrainian young kids as cannon fodder to achieve a geopolitical objective or aspirations of the neocons. And we have made Ukraine now a pawn in a proxy war between two great powers to achieve our objective, the vanity objectives.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So you think a peace deal could be reached?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: The Russians offered a peace deal at the beginning, and in fact when Zelenskyy campaigned in 2019, he is guy who is a comedian and actor, no political experience. But he won because with 70 percent of the vote because he campaigned on a peace platform which was signing the Minsk accords.\par \par \uc2 And the United States went in there and we undermined that agreement. And we then encouraged and provoked them to have a full scale war with Russia, which is something we wanted, but the Ukrainian people clearly did not want.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I know you are not criticizing the president directly. He does get a lot of criticism about his age. Most recently on MSNBC he was asked what Fortune 500 company would hire an 82-year-old CEO? Why should they hire you? And this is what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have acquired a hell of a lot of wisdom. I know more than the vast amount of people. I'm more experienced than anybody who has ever run for the office. And I think I have driven myself to be honorable as well as also effective.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So why should Democrats turn the page and, say, choose you?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, the Democratic Party at this point has become the party of war, which we have never been before. We've sent $113 billion to Ukraine, and the entire budget of \uc2 EPA\uc2  is $12 billion. The budget of CDC is $12 billion. And 57 percent of Americans could not put their hands on $1,000 if they have an emergency, 25 percent of Americans are hungry. We have a crisis right here in this country and I think a lot of Americans are feeling like they are tired of the forever wars. They are tired of this being a warfare state abroad and a surveillance state at home, and they want to get rid of the surveillance here, the wars abroad, close the 800 bases, or many of them that we have abroad. We are spending $1.1 trillion a year on a stealth bomber that costs $1 billion and can't fly in the rain.\par \par \uc2 We should take that money and build schools in this country, invest in infrastructure, create jobs here. Our real national strength is going to come from economic power and a robust economy and a robust middle class, and not from projecting military power abroad.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We appreciate you coming in. We'll follow the campaign. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Bret, thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, Chicago's mayor begins his term with a comparison that has a lot of people raising their eyebrows.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chicago's new mayor is already making some waves with comments from his inaugural speech yesterday. He compared the death of an armed 13-year- old killed by police to the killing of a 24-year-old police officer by teenagers. Senior correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  is in Chicago tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Chicago police salute in dress uniform, paying respects to Officer Areanah Preston as she is carried into a suburban funeral home. The 24-year-old officer was ambushed on May the 6th by a group of teenagers on a violent crime spree. Preston fought back, was shot and killed. As services begin for her final rest, her memory is dragged into the political arena.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BRANDON JOHNSON, (D) CHICAGO: I, Brandon Johnson.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Chicago's new progressive mayor Brandon Johnson compared her death to that of Adam Toledo, the 13-year-old whose death caught on police bodycam. An older gang member handed him a gun. Toledo ran from police, tossed the gun away in the same moment that he turned toward the officer and was killed.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: The tears of Adam Toledo's parents are made of the same sorrow as the parents of Officer Preston's parents.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Chicago Alderman Silvana Tabares tweeted that Chicago's mayor's comments comparing the deaths of a gangbanger and an officer were wrong. Wirepoints is a conservative think tanks in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 MARK GLENNON, WIREPOINTS FOUNDER: That's where I'm most concerned, is that the police are going to get further demoralized by these kinds of things. We are already at risk of having them stand down.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: When Johnson was still mayor elect, rowdy teens mobbed Chicago's Millennium Park, trashed property, smashed windows, and set cars on fire. Johnson stopped short of condemning the rioters, tweeting in part, "It is not constructive to demonize youth who have otherwise been starved of opportunities in their own communities."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): Johnson had backed the Defund the Police movement. Now he says too many Chicagoans fear for their safety, and he promises to add 200 detectives to the police force. Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  in Chicago. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The Korean War began with a surprise assault by North Korean troops crossing the 38th parallel. There are new insights into the politics and planning behind the attack. It's all part of the story we tell in my new documentary series on FOX Nation "The Unauthorized History of the Korean War." Here's a clip.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A highly trained and well-equipped North Korean army swarmed across the 38th parallel to attack unprepared South Korean defenders.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: On the 25th of June, 1950, with the backing of both the Soviet Union and the People's Republic of China, North Korea unleashed its offensive. For decades, scholars have debated who was responsible for authorizing the invasion. Today, the historical record is clear.\par \par \uc2 KATHRYN WEATHERSBY, GEORGETOWN UNIVERSITY: Before the documents in the Russian archives were available to scholars, we didn't know who -- exactly who made the decision to attack South Korea. The Russian records show very clearly that the Soviets were in charge of making that decision because it potentially could involve the Americans coming back onto the peninsula.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can watch all five episodes of "The Unauthorized History of the Korean War," and all the unauthorized histories streaming now on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 China's Defense Ministry\uc2  says it is prepared to resolutely smash any form of Taiwan independence. It says recent increases in U.S. cooperation with Taiwan are, quote, "wrong and dangerous." This comes amid reports the U.S. plans to accelerate the sale of defensive weapons and other military assistance to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on reaction to the debt ceiling talks and all that we heard about \uc2 John Durham\uc2 's investigation. And later, "Tuesday Tweets."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: The president and the speaker are the keys to the deal. Whatever the speaker and the president can agree to that we know we'll get a signature.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: It's unfortunate we are where we are, but the good thing about it is Republicans always look to find a solution.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: We don't have much time, but default is just the worst, worst alternative. And the -- having a bipartisan bill in both chambers is the only way, the only way we're going to avoid default.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Debt talks at the White House today. The Treasury Secretary says the country is looking at an economic and financial catastrophe if they don't get it done by June 1st. GOP and Democrats drawing red lines, saying staff level talks continued this week. According to aides, they have centered on several subjects on which Democrats and Republicans may be able to find agreement. Among them, clawing back unspent COVID-19 funds, speeding up the permitting process for energy projects, capping spending and imposing stricter work requirements on some government programs. But negotiators are divided on the details.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, start there, Ben Domenech, editor at large for "The Spectator," host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio, Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com, and \uc2 Harold Ford Jr\uc2 ., former Tennessee Congressman, co-host of THE FIVE. Just have the wide shot today. We have so many things going on in the studio today. You think progress. You think it's going to happen?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR\uc2 ., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: I think it happens. It's unfortunate we have to go through these cheap thrills of the short-term politics they all play. I hope they all realize the world is watching. And when we think about us losing standing and ground, when countries are willing to look to China help negotiate things or, for that matter, are not looking to the U.S. first, these are the kind of things that add to it.\par \par \uc2 I do hope if they do negotiate a deal, and I think they will, what they ought to makes as a part of it is any time we do a tax cut or a spending bill, there's a clause in every one ever these bills that says if you vote for this bill that means you automatically vote for debt ceiling increase if the projections don't pan out, because the Democrats will say their bills don't put money on the debt. Republicans will say theirs don't. Let's put a clause in there that says you automatically vote to raise the debt ceiling so we don't cause this kind of unrest and potential chaos in markets in America.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaker McCarthy managed to get his caucus to vote for this bill, so they have got something in their hand. But if they come back, they negotiate it, if they water it down a little bit, he could lose some of those votes, and it could be a Democrat pass.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I reached out to McCarthy's office before coming on the show tonight, and they say on the issues of the gap, that they are completely far apart in every single policy issue. So they must agree on the fact that they need to raise the debt ceiling. They're not working on the policy differences there.\par \par \uc2 It's not abnormal to appoint a negotiator for these debt ceiling negotiations. Mnuchin was picked as treasury secretary under President Trump to do this. But it is unusual to do this this late in the game. And the fact is that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should be negotiating directly with Speaker Kevin McCarthy if he actually wants to get this done on time before they get to the brink. They have allowed staff to do it, but it's too late in the process. And President Biden, quite frankly, has been talking about how urgent this is but not actually acting like it's urgent, going away for the G-7 --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But changed his trip on the back end.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Hardly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He's not going to Australia and something else. Ben?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Hardly changing it.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I just think that the Democrats and the White House completely botched this. I think they did not expect that McCarthy was going to be able to hold his people together. And I think that they are now negotiating essentially on the territory that McCarthy wanted them to be negotiating on, which is a telling comment in this Washington. So many people looking back, I think, underestimated what Kevin McCarthy was going to be able do when he actually got that gavel. He has proven them wrong. Now the White House is coming in his direction. I think a deal will get done, but they will get something out of it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, extensive interview with Bill Barr, former attorney general, about the \uc2 John Durham\uc2  report. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR\uc2 , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: It was a success in that its purpose from the very beginning was to get to the truth. It was not launched as a criminal investigation, and people who compare it to the Mueller investigation are simply wrong. I asked him to get to the bottom of it, get his arms around the truth. If he found crimes, we could prosecute them. But I think that's what he has done. He has dug into it, and I think he has gotten as close to the bottom as anyone is going to get to this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Republicans are saying, why isn't there more? Why aren't more people held accountable? Democrats are saying there's nothing here. They are looking at the same 300 pages.\par \par \uc2 FORD: I said last night this is troublesome. For anyone that believes that someone should not be held accountable for this I think is not being honest about what government should do and how people believe their relationship to their government is.\par \par \uc2 Two things I liked about it was positive. First is that \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 , and I think Mr. Durham acknowledges, has cleaned up some of the investigative excesses here, which is a positive. And two, I hope the \uc2 FBI\uc2  gets back to doing what it does best, which is protecting the nation against domestic and foreign threats and crimes. That's what they should be doing. That's what they do best.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Katie?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: This 306-page report if full of things that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  did to break protocol to get to where they wanted to do. But it's really simple. In August of 2016, Peter Strzok, who was in charge of this investigation, was texting with \uc2 Lisa Page\uc2  about stopping \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from becoming president and they used the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to do it. The White House has been asked about this. Jake Sullivan is now the national security adviser who started pushing this narrative on behalf of the Hillary Clinton campaign.\par \par \uc2 And what the Durham report also said is, yes, these reforms have been implemented. But unless the \uc2 FBI\uc2  gets back to an ethos of protecting the American people, away from politics and back to the Constitution, then it will be, quote, fruitless for the efforts to change the trust that the American people no longer have in the institution.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, it was interesting to hear Barr talk about McCabe now weighing in, \uc2 Andrew McCabe\uc2 , and then Peter Strzok weighing in, these characters that are a part of this report are now commenting on it.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: None of these people should be taken seriously. This report documents repeatedly the norms, the rules, the regulations that they broke in order to make this case happen on a partisan basis. It is such an irony of the Trump era of politics that all the people who used him as a threat to the norms and rules and expectations for our government are the same people who violate all those things in order to try to bring him down.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly, Robert F. Kennedy Jr., impressive in his pitch?\par \par \uc2 FORD: He is. And his message seems to be maturing, the reason he is running. If he can stay with middle class and the forgotten man, I don't know how you don't debate him if you're President Biden.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's true. Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, it's time for "Tuesday Tweets." First up, James asks "How and when do the debt ceiling talks end?" The treasury secretary says June 1st, so I bet in the next couple of days we will hear something. Next Tyler tweets "I enjoyed your Unauthorized History series. Especially like those on Vietnam and Korea. Would you consider doing one on every war the United States was involved in?" Sure. Yes, we'll give it a shot. Check out "The Unauthorized History of Socialism" part one and two. It's on FOX Nation. It's really worth the time, really worth the time.\par \par \uc2 Last up, Paul says, "Hey, Bret, are you attending the PGA championship this weekend? What's your prediction?" Unfortunately, I'm not. I like Jason Day. Just won a tournament. But I really want to see \uc2 Jordan Spieth\uc2 , the Grand Slam at the PGA. So we'll see.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, our "Common Ground" segment features two senators, Democrat Tim Kaine, Republican \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2 , discussing their bipartisan legislation to disrupt fentanyl trafficking. We got a lot in this show.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME starts right now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230517ej5g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Pilot Strike Possible?; Debt Talks; Interview With Yuma, Arizona, Mayor Douglas Nicholls; Title 42 Ends\b0\par\par\uc2 Gary Kaltbaum, Chad Pergram, Nate Foy, Jacqui Heinrich, William La  Jeunesse, Bill Melugin, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,401 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, you are looking live at the Southern border right now.\par \par \uc2 Tens of thousands of migrants have been massing on the Mexican side, so far not as many as feared getting into the American side, while, in the U.S., a federal judge is blocking the administration from releasing migrants without a court date, this as we're learning of a migrant minor who officials said died while in federal custody.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 We have got you covered on all of this with Bill Melugin in Brownsville, Texas. We're also in Tijuana, Mexico, where you will find William La Jeunesse.\par \par \uc2 We begin with Bill -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Neil, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 This week alone here in Brownsville, thousands upon thousands of people crossed illegally. And, as a result of that, Border Patrol here over capacity and they have been conducting mass releases. Take a look at this video we just shot here a few hours ago in downtown Brownsville, showing large numbers of recently mass released migrants walking around the downtown area near a bus terminal.\par \par \uc2 You will see some of them carrying manila folders with their DHS paperwork. There is an NGO down there as well who helps these migrants out with their travel documents. A lot of them are Venezuelans.\par \par \uc2 We caught up with one Venezuelan man who was released earlier this morning. Here's what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): Title 42 ended yesterday. And my message? It doesn't matter. You can make it to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Last night, as the clock hit midnight and Title 42 expired, we were with a group of four GOP senators who were getting a tour of the area from the Border Patrol Union.\par \par \uc2 They had said there was an expectation that there were 22,000 people across the river in Matamoros getting ready to cross the river. That did not end up happening. The senators essentially got a tour of the area that the Texas National Guard had fortified with razor wire, and they didn't really see any illegal crossings there last night.\par \par \uc2 We will show you what we did see in Matamoros, however. Take a look at this video. We sent a cameraman over there. There were migrants who started gathering after midnight after Title 42 expired. It looked like they were going to cross. But you will see, on the U.S. side of the river, the Texas National Guard was pulled up. They had barbed wire out. They were on a megaphone yelling: Don't cross. You're not going to be let in.\par \par \uc2 One guy did try end up getting into the water and swimming over, but he was not let in. So, Neil, we did not end up seeing that big surge that we were anticipating last night after Title 42 dropped.\par \par \uc2 And back out here live, I can tell you it's been slow today as well. Behind me in recent days, we were having enormously long lines of migrants lining up after they crossed illegally. We are seeing very few of that today, largely in part because of what the Texas National Guard has done. They have shown up. They put barbed wire all over the place, soldiers all over the place.\par \par \uc2 And they are physically not letting people through, so a significant slowdown here in Brownsville, at least for now. We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, Bill, for that.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to William La Jeunesse right now. You will find him in Tijuana, where thousands have got -- up to 26,000 weren't told, waiting for this moment -- William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, San Diego is very different than Texas.\par \par \uc2 And, in fact, this is the most secure area along the border. Not only do we have one fence. We actually have two. But what you got here, you have people that have camped up along the side of the fence here on the Mexican side. Some have gone over, some have gone under the fence or actually sewer grates periodically here because of the mountainous terrain.\par \par \uc2 Down in the middle there, you have got about several hundred migrants. You can see them with the tarps along the side of the fence there. And then, Brian, if you see this group over here, they're looking for food and power, right? So, everyone has a cell phone. There's no generators in there.\par \par \uc2 This is kind of a no-man's land. It is U.S. territory. But, nevertheless, people are in there. And the Border Patrol has been coming by on a daily basis and taking 300 to 400 of these migrants out and taking them into processing. And yet, every day, it gets replenished.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have been here for three days. We have seen the vans come up and pull people out, and yet they continue to come. Now, about a mile away from here, I want to show you something else, people actually coming legally, individuals who used that CBP One app, right? So, right now, we're taking in about 250 individuals who are entering legally for their asylum interview.\par \par \uc2 They will get legal status and a work permit from the government. The DHS wants people to use that app. But, as you can see out here, a lot more people are coming in illegally and entering the U.S. system that way. We have spoke to one man from Russia, and here's what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's people you love. There's your friends, there's your family, the rest of your family. And, but, at the same time, there's a government that does everything possible to erase your existence.\par \par \uc2 So I cannot -- like, as a human being, I cannot stand by that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: And, now, the video you're seeing now are basically buses from ICE going to a hotel in San Diego.\par \par \uc2 And these are the mass releases that Bill is seeing in Texas as well, individuals who are picked up here at the border who get that credible fear interview and maybe they see a judge, maybe they haven't, but they're being released in the United States, most on a humanitarian parole. Some may have an ankle bracelet, but the women and children are not. And they're being released in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And that is part of the incentive, right, when you see those images and say, listen, you got 26,000 immigrants here in these shelters. What are they going to do? Are they going to wait for five months on an app? Or are they going to come down here and get picked up and then ultimately be released?\par \par \uc2 Neil, there has to be some certainty to an outcome if you have any deterrence. And, right now, the images shows it's worth it, frankly, to show up and enter illegally.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: We will see if that changes -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. And do you know app to begin with? So we're going to explore that a little later in the show.\par \par \uc2 William, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a lot of border towns are getting overwhelmed, if you just look at the staggering numbers, when we're told that 60,000 have sort of huddled to the Mexican border, whether you're talking on the Arizona-Mexico border or certainly what we have been seeing in Texas, to a degree in Tijuana, south of San Diego.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read on how these towns can get overwhelmed very easily, very quickly, so much so that the mayor of Yuma, Arizona, said, we have got a national emergency here, Mr. President, wants the president to declare just that.\par \par \uc2 Doug Nicholls with us now.\par \par \uc2 Mayor, good to have.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR DOUGLAS NICHOLLS (R-YUMA, AZ): Thanks for having me on again. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How are things in your town? Maybe you could tell us because you have got the eagle-eye view.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLLS: Well, it's a little bit chaotic.\par \par \uc2 Border Patrol in the Yuma Sector has been at near record numbers of interdictions. Yesterday was 1,550 people that they interdicted. They're over capacity by a significant amount. And they are starting releases onto the street, so not releases to NGOs that can help them, but our NGOs are overwhelmed also, so that they're just releasing them onto the sidewalk, essentially.\par \par \uc2 We had 100 and -- 100-plus or minus this morning, and then this afternoon we're looking at about another 150 in the area.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Incredible.\par \par \uc2 You're referring to non-government organizations.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLLS: That's right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And we are also learning separately, Mayor, that a number of holding facilities -- I don't know if this applies to your area as well -- are simply overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 They have been stretched to, at a minimum, 125 percent of capacity. And so they get transferred to other facilities that quickly get stacked to that degree.\par \par \uc2 How do you handle that?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLLS: Well, I don't know. That's a really great question.\par \par \uc2 This is uncharted territory, as far as I'm concerned. Usually, what you have is, you have the FEMA show up, and they manage disaster-related human needs, and that's not happening.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, I'm wondering. For your residents, they knew this time was coming, and this moment with Title 42 going away was coming, just as those trying to get into your community knew this was coming.\par \par \uc2 How are they dealing with this? I mean, the numbers, the kickoff, we're told, and to hear the administration tell it, a lot tamer than they thought it would be, but just to maintain this, we call it progress when it's under 10,000 a day. Those are record levels, no matter what you think of it. So what are they saying now?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLLS: The people of Yuma are just greatly concerned.\par \par \uc2 So, up to this point, there really hasn't been a tangible impact to the day-to-day resident here in Yuma, now that we're getting releases to the streets, and as those numbers will grow, only because there's few transportation options outside the community that aren't charter-type of arrangements.\par \par \uc2 So that number is going to grow just because people can't get on to their ultimate destination. And that's what people are concerned about, because, as humans, we all have basic human needs. And if they don't have those resources -- capability to obtain those resources, then we end up with camps.\par \par \uc2 We end up with people living on the street begging for food. And it's not a situation that we should be in. This is something we could have prevented as a country, and we haven't.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It could cut both ways, I guess, for you, Mayor.\par \par \uc2 But, if you will indulge me, this federal judge has blocked the administration from releasing more migrants unless they have a court date. A good many of them don't. And, even if they do, they get one that's sometimes years away.\par \par \uc2 How does that complicate things for you?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLLS: Well, so what that did is, it stopped kind of what would be a fast pass process that DHS was trying to put in.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLLS: Like a 15-, 20-minute interview with a DHS official, and then they were going to be released.\par \par \uc2 So that has been stopped by that court order, at least for the next couple of weeks. But they have got thousands of people that they're trying to process and get out anyway, so it's just exchanging one for another at this point.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Mayor, best of luck to you. I cannot even imagine the Herculean task ahead of you and your residents.\par \par \uc2 Doug Nicholls, the Yuma, Arizona, mayor.\par \par \uc2 We want to get a look at the legality, certainly, of what the judge is proposing to do and whether that will constitutionally mean trouble down the road here, in other words, that there will be a battle back and forth, much as there was with the implementation and the continuation of Title 42.\par \par \uc2 On that, our legal eagle Tom Dupree, the former deputy assistant attorney general.\par \par \uc2 Tom, at the very least, there does seem to be confusion about these rules and who has to have what, what kind of paperwork has to be out there, confusion not only in the eyes of this judge over someone who might or might not have a court date, but even down to the implementation of this app.\par \par \uc2 A lot of legal messes they have yet to clear up.\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE, FORMER JUSTICE DEPARTMENT OFFICIAL: That's absolutely right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 It's an incredibly complex area of the law. And it's particularly complex, given that you have situations where these federal judges are basically coming in and overseeing immigration policy in the United States, which is hard. I mean, it's not the way the framers drew it up. It's not the way the system should work.\par \par \uc2 It really is the presidential administration that should be taking charge of this and making sure that everything gets managed appropriately, but that's not happening. And, as a result, you see these lawsuits being filed and you see these federal judges come in and really doing their best to effectively manage on a day-to-day basis how our immigration system is functioning.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know what's interesting too, Tom, a lot of new things are being tried here. It's too early to tell whether they will succeed or not.\par \par \uc2 But, already, you have a lot of human rights advocates saying that this is unfair, this notion that we're going to set up these migration hubs across the Western Hemisphere, in Latin America, in all points Southern Mexico, what have you, and the nature by which we transfer migrants and allow some to come in, special asylum cases.\par \par \uc2 I believe we stick to those numbers of 30,000 a month if you're coming from a Nicaragua or a Haiti or a Cuba or Venezuela, but a separate list of rules for other migrants who might just be as much claiming asylum, but who are not allowed to go to these processing centers. I mean, that is a legal mess.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: It is.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's arbitrary. It can be irrational in places. And I think the overarching problem here is that we are not doing a good job of challenging migrants who are looking to come to this country to use legal processes, legal ports of entry, to follow the rules.\par \par \uc2 Instead, we're incentivizing people to enter unlawfully, because, even if they don't immediately get granted asylum or get granted legal status in the United States, our system is set up so that then they don't necessarily have to return to their home country, but they can remain in the United States during the pendency of their legal proceedings.\par \par \uc2 And so I think we're inadvertently creating an incentive for people not to follow the rules and to -- not to use the legal channels that we have created to determine who should be allowed entry into the United States.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This is less a legal question than a practical question, I guess, Tom, but one of the issues here is like an honor system, where you're trying to break into this country, and they stop you or tell you to turn around.\par \par \uc2 You could try again, but if you actually do break into this country and you have disobeyed that, you have to wait five years to do so again. And I'm just wondering, first of all, it works on the honor system. Secondly, if you have gone to the trouble of getting to the border and you got this close getting across the border, you go back, you're not going to wait five years.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Well, that's the thing, is that I think a lot of times we design these legal rules with kind of a blind eye toward human nature.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's similar to when a court date would be issued for someone to basically say, we're going to let you provisionally into the country, but we want you to return for your court date in a year or two. A lot of people just never return earn for their court date in a year or two...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: ... because they don't want to be potentially captured and removed from the country.\par \par \uc2 So I think we need to do here a better job of aligning human nature, human incentives with what the law is requiring.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. Can you just imagine, if we're doing this at a rate of 10,000 a day, and it might go up to 15,000 or more -- hopefully, they keep that down -- that is just an insurmountable fiasco in the making.\par \par \uc2 Tom, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tom Dupree following all of that.\par \par \uc2 This is such a big issue here. We're going to look at the magnitude of it and the effect on all the folks of this country, particularly not on those border communities, but how we police this going forward, Because, again, we could be talking about 10,000 migrants a day coming at a rate that's going to pick up above the 1.2 million who are already here only five months into the year.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be looking at this in much more detail 10:00 a.m. Eastern time tomorrow. You're seeing them already spring into action this week, the Texas National Guard joining the Joint Task Force Lone Star efforts on the ground to handle the surge of these migrants.\par \par \uc2 The commander of that operation, by the way, Brigadier General Matt Barker, will be my special guest tomorrow on "Cavuto Live" at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time. We're going to explore this from levels and angles you have not seen with the latest drone technology and shots and how they're dealing with this in these border communities that go way besides these border communities.\par \par \uc2 I'm talking as far north as the greater New York metropolitan area. More on that tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 First to Jacqui Heinrich on how the White House is dealing with a lot of this now -- Jacqui\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, we haven't heard from the president on this major issue. We didn't hear from him yesterday before Title 42 lifted at midnight.\par \par \uc2 We have not heard from him today. He just ignored reporter questions, even after his administration admitted that this week a migrant child died in U.S. custody and a Florida judge struck down a key part of their plan to manage the post-Title 42 world.\par \par \uc2 We will tell you what we know coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, after three years, through two administrations, finally, Title 42 is no more, the first full day without it, and the administration peppered with questions all about it. Don't know if any of those questions are answered.\par \par \uc2 But I know Jacqui Heinrich could know. She's there at the White House -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, officials say there's not been rise in illegal border crossings since Title 42 was lifted at midnight, although overall encounters do remain at record highs. The administration admitted in court that holding facilities are over capacity in eight of nine Southwest border sectors, and they expect to have 45,000 people in custody by the end of this month.\par \par \uc2 And they're now scrambling to adjust after a judge sided with Florida's attorney general, who sued to stop the administration from releasing these migrants into U.S. communities without proper processing or any way to track them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: What's the backup plan now?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So, let me just say on the ruling that you just laid out to me, so, look, the way we see that, it's sabotage. It's pure and simple. That's how that reads to us.\par \par \uc2 ICE announced that it is adding thousands more beds. But, look, Republican elected officials continue to play games here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: A memo uncovered in the Florida legal proceedings indicates that mass releases was part of the plan that they now say they can't execute since at least last year.\par \par \uc2 The administration says they're going to fight this ruling in court, but, today, they're under more pressure after admitting that one migrant child died in U.S. custody on Wednesday. Honduran officials are now calling for an investigation, saying 17-year-old Angel Eduardo Maradiaga Espinoza had been in the U.S. for about a week, when he passed away in a shelter for unaccompanied minors in Safety Harbor, Florida.\par \par \uc2 How many, though, like this child are right now in the care of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is unclear.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Can you tell us how many migrant children are presently in U.S. custody?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I would refer you to the \uc2 HHS\uc2 . I don't have -- don't have those numbers in front in me.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The Web site has not reported any data on this since January, and they're typically reported once a month.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I expect the \uc2 HHS\uc2  to provide that data.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So the president has still not spoken to the press since Title 42 was lifted. Officials today could not say whether he was aware of the death of that migrant child that happened in U.S. custody.\par \par \uc2 But when this exact thing happened under former President Trump in 2019, Biden tweeted saying that this was unacceptable, not who we are and all of us to stand up and speak out, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Remember that very well.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, this is not just an issue for border towns. I have been saying communities across the country, particularly those who deem themselves sanctuary cities, are dealing with this, including New York City, now taking in upwards of 1,000 migrants a day. With Title 42 gone, that could climb substantially.\par \par \uc2 Nate Foy with more on that side of the story.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  wrote a letter to President Biden today asking for more federal resources to deal with this crisis that the mayor's office here in New York City admits it does not have a solution for, as more migrants arrive here every single day, including two buses from Laredo, Texas, this morning.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this. You mentioned the city expecting about 1,000 migrants every single day now that Title 42 is over. The groups we saw this morning, a mixture of men, women and children.\par \par \uc2 Now, normally, in New York City, these families would be guaranteed their own private space with a bed, a bathroom, and a kitchen. But now, because the city is so overrun, Mayor Adams is suspending portions of the city's right-to-shelter law. And that's because they just can't keep up with the staggering numbers that we're seeing here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 As of Wednesday night, the city said they had 39,400 migrants in its care, that number now likely over 40,000. Over 130 hotels over the past year have been turned into shelters. Some migrants right now are being housed in gymnasiums.\par \par \uc2 And Mayor Adams predicts things are going to get worse, not only for migrants, but also for New Yorkers. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS (D-NEW YORK, NY): We could potentially get thousands of people a day in our city. This is just -- is wrong for those who are coming here, like Commissioner Castro, and it's wrong for New Yorkers who are here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Neil, this comes one day after the mayor sent two buses of migrants to neighboring Orange County, New York, and that was after county leaders declared a state of emergency, pushing back on the mayor's plans to do exactly that.\par \par \uc2 But back out here live, Mayor Adams is also asking President Biden for more help from the federal government. City officials confirm they are considering every possible solution here, Neil, and that includes housing migrants on residential streets in New York City.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They had also talked about setting up tents in Central Park. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 FOY: There are reports of that, Neil. We have not been able to independently verify that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 FOY: But, right now, they are just housing migrants wherever they can find any space.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing. All right, Nate Foy.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Nate, in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Teresa Kenny knows of what Nate just said, because the overflow has the city now sending a lot of these migrants to communities, most of whom don't get a heads-up, including this Orangetown, New York, supervisor, Teresa Kenny.\par \par \uc2 Supervisor, it's very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 There was no heads-up for you, no warning that migrants were coming and that you had to deal with them, right?\par \par \uc2 TERESA KENNY, ORANGETOWN, NEW YORK, SUPERVISOR: Thanks, Neil, for having me.\par \par \uc2 I just -- yes, I would like to describe it as a sneak attack. I think it was very intentional. I got a call from Mayor Adams on a Friday afternoon to say that they were implementing this new program where they were going to be sending migrants to hotels around the state and that Orangetown may or may not be considered one of the -- they had a location, but might not.\par \par \uc2 Within hours of that call, I found out that there was a local hotel that had hundreds of mattresses outside, and it was most definitely a plan to start housing migrants in one of our local hotels.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. Now, you're going to be appearing in court on Monday to try to get this restraining order to prevent this from happening. But it's an uphill battle, right, because the mayor and others are dealing with this.\par \par \uc2 They say they're overwhelmed, so they want you to share in that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you're not a sanctuary town, so why is it on you to do something?\par \par \uc2 KENNY: We actually ran to the court on Tuesday. The buses, we got a call they were coming on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 But we had previously, anticipating this was going to happen, had run to court. And this is a zoning issue for us. It's a local hotel. It's not zoned locally to be used as a shelter, certainly not one for New York City. So, essentially, the mayor tried to come in overnight and open a homeless shelter right in our town.\par \par \uc2 So our zoning prohibited enough that the judge issued a temporary restraining order. It was scheduled to be back on for Monday, but, actually, at the request of the hotel who we got the restraining order against, has actually asked to put it off until May 30. So we have a little bit of reprieve on the migrants showing up, the buses showing up.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We have reached out to the mayor, Supervisor, and he does get back they can't arrange something for the time being. But hope springs eternal. Maybe we will.\par \par \uc2 But part of the argument for this, and one of the things he touched on yesterday that struck me -- he has since dialed it back -- was this notion that maybe New York state itself could be a sanctuary state. Now, I'm oversimplifying it here, and, to be fair, he did not say that, but he hinted at the fact that, I'm a sanctuary city, the biggest city in the country among them, and that in my state that this is a way to make this more acceptable to communities.\par \par \uc2 What did you think?\par \par \uc2 KENNY: The reason we are not a sanctuary town or county is because we just don't have the resources or the -- to handle a large influx of migrants, basically, homeless people.\par \par \uc2 The hotel he was going to take over is going to take -- in a little hamlet in Orangetown was going to house 340 single adult males in a residential neighborhood. There was no thought put into this by the mayor at all, no input from the local officials, even with regard to the migrants. Is this an appropriate location for them?\par \par \uc2 It was just no thought. And I don't know if it was an act of a desperate person, but it was really -- and it was really a sneak attack. It really intentionally told us at last minute so that we wouldn't have time to go in and stop it. We fortunately did. Unfortunately, Orange County did not.\par \par \uc2 And so the 30 migrants that were supposed to be coming to us apparently got bussed up to Orange County the following day.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 Well, keep us posted. But, Supervisor, obviously now, with Title 42 gone, you could be facing this quite often and consistently back and forth. So we will keep an eye on that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, keeping an eye on what's going on in those debt talks. So far, no talks among the formal parties today. We're told their underlings are making progress, but a stunning report out of \uc2 Zillow\uc2 , the housing folks, who say, we default, mortgage rates will skyrocket.\par \par \uc2 Try a minimum of 8 percent -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you ready for a pilot strike?\par \par \uc2 Well, how about three different pilot strikes from three different airlines? It could happen. And if you thought the last travel meltdown was bad, this one could get really bad.\par \par \uc2 We're on it -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the standoff is on.\par \par \uc2 And if you think things are bad now, with them even hinting at the fact that this could go on and on, the folks at \uc2 Zillow\uc2 , who do anything related to housing, say, we get a default or no deal, we could be seeing mortgage rates skyrocket to close to 8.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram following that and everything else going on in Washington as to whether that is even remotely possible -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  warned that the government could run out of cash by mid-June. The CBO called this a significant risk. The White House said the talks were productive, but House Speaker Kevin McCarthy disagreed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Look, we have a short time period. If this were staff meetings happening on February 1, I'd call them productive.\par \par \uc2 I don't think there's enough progress for the leaders to get back together. I don't know having certain ones in the room that it's even productive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: President Biden and congressional leaders canceled a meeting today. We expect a meeting Monday or Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 FOX is told the president isn't planning to postpone his trip overseas next week if the talks are going well.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: The president and the four congressional leaders will meet early next week. When we have a date, certainly, we will share that with all of you. Staff will continue to meet. They're going to meet today and over the weekend, so that will continue.\par \par \uc2 The meetings have been productive over the past few days, and leaders wanted to continue before they regrouped.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The White House refused to give a deadline to forge a pact to avoid default.\par \par \uc2 There's growing concern that a lack of action could spook the markets.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: So, if there's no progress in the debt ceiling talks, are you worried about a default? Are you worried about the markets, Mr. Speaker?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The secretary of the Treasury, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , could enlist help from banking CEOs to talk to lawmakers about the consequences of default.\par \par \uc2 On their radar, moderate freshman Republicans from New York who helped the GOP win the House -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for that, Chad Pergram, following all of those developments.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, stocks finished mixed today, having a down week. But the fact of the matter is, they are dealing with this debt stuff, focused more on the strength of the economy and how banks are doing, and, for now, not seeing that there could be a kablooey in the works here, if that's such a thing.\par \par \uc2 But, believe me, if something were to get screwed up, kablooey.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What if?\par \par \uc2 What if we don't get a deal? What if it's a really delayed deal? What if we default? Well, there are all these scenarios that are out in the markets, most being largely ignored, as the markets hold their own. But one prospect is that interest rates could skyrocket. We told you about that.\par \par \uc2 Another has it that \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  over at \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  is holding regular meetings with his senior staff to play out the possibility of a default, which he says isn't entirely out of the question.\par \par \uc2 What does Gary Kaltbaum think? He's with us right now, a great read of the markets and market history.\par \par \uc2 Gary, you don't have to get a default to get a bad sort of series of events. We learned that in 2011, where we avoided it, but we still had our credit downgraded. A lot of folks are saying something like that in this environment could happen. How would the markets react to that?\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, three words come to mind, and it's cost of capital, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And if cost of capital skyrockets from here, which it has already has over the last year-and-a-half, would not be good news. Look, I believe the economy is pretty fragile right here. Numbers are not bad. I'm amazed that, frankly, the markets have held up with a crash in the regional banks. But from here, affordability for houses, forget about it.\par \par \uc2 And the borrowing costs would just be off the charts for consumer and business and leave no doubt that it would be -- put a crimp into the economy. And I suspect the markets would be quite unhappy too.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What would the markets do -- if I think it was \uc2 Michelle Bowman\uc2 , a voting member of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , I believe, who said, if she had her druthers, she would support further rate increases because, in her eyes -- and others have said kind of the same thing -- it's strong enough to warrant maybe a couple of more hikes.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Well, they better be careful.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think they're watching the market and saying, oh, the market's OK, it's taken all this. But I look at numbers, Neil, and valuations are up in the trees. Earnings have been down year over year, sales not so great. They go a little bit too far, I think the markets can turn the other way, and pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 Just -- I would just be real careful. And let me give you one little thing, and this is really important. The 10-year yield is on the 3.5 percent. The Fed's at 5 percent. They're way above the real market, the free markets. They go too far above, the markets are going to come in, and something to watch really closely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, we are sort of like the world bastion, the safe place to invest money. You can argue that with the markets these days, the stock market, but certainly it's been that way for a dollar, our Treasury notes and bond.\par \par \uc2 What would happen if we defaulted?\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: If it lasts a while, that's where trouble lies. If we default for a little bit, a couple of weeks, and I think that's -- for me, that's worst-case scenario.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Because we got an election next year, and I think these people worry about that more than anything.\par \par \uc2 If it lasts a while, all heck could break loose. The yields can skyrocket. The dollar, who knows which way it goes? I have seen where this turmoil and the dollar goes up, so who knows? But leave no doubt there is an intangible called confidence, and there'd be a huge loss of confidence. It would be headlines every day, and I suspect a big haircut in the market, and trouble just all across the board.\par \par \uc2 It's the worst possible scenario. It's amazing they're taking it to the fourth quarter on this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, and almost in overtime, to your point.\par \par \uc2 Gary, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: By the way, when we talk about default, the most common fear, at least in the markets, and a worldwide interest here, is, if we do not make good or we don't have the money to pay interest to bondholders and Treasury note holders the world over, that would constitute a default, no matter how long it lasts.\par \par \uc2 You have got serious problems down the road.\par \par \uc2 Stick with us, because we have other issues want to raise with you. We don't think it's going to happen just yet, but a number of airline pilots are going out on strike, or at least letting it be known that they're seriously considering that. So what happens if we see that?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, if you break into this country, sometimes, we don't force you to walk back. We will fly you back.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, better known as ICE, is conducting these repatriation flights. You're seeing video of this earlier today in Texas. This is part of the division that facilitates the transfer and removal of noncitizens, oftentimes using commercial airlines, chartered flights, to say, if you're here and you shouldn't be here, we're going to fly out of here.\par \par \uc2 And the process has begun. And it will no doubt be picking up steam in the weeks and the months ahead now that Title 42 is no longer in effect.\par \par \uc2 All right, something that is in effect, and not if you are illegally here, but if you're legally wanting to fly here or anywhere here and go anywhere else, you could be looking at serious disruptions. At least three major airlines and their pilots are hinting of going on strike this summer.\par \par \uc2 No indications as to when any of them might pounce on that, but it's a realistic threat, so much so that we thought we'd get Francesca Page here, the travel journalist extraordinaire, to help you through this.\par \par \uc2 Francesca, what do you do? How likely is this?\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA PAGE, TRAVEL AND LIFESTYLE EXPERT: So, these protests really come after years of flight complications spurred by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Now, much of it comes down to stalled contract negotiations after four years, which include requests for changes both to work rules and their scheduling systems. Now, airlines like American, United and Southwest are trying to negate any worries for travelers, especially with the upcoming summer season, about potential roadblocks for their summer vacation plans.\par \par \uc2 United flights, for example, have said that they will operate as planned while pilots exercise their right to -- in quotes -- "distribute information and picket while off-duty."\par \par \uc2 Here's the thing. As of now, according to United, they aren't really deeming this as a strike. This is because federal law bar airline worker -- federal law bars airline workers from striking unless they explicitly are allowed by the \uc2 National Mediation Board\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, in transparency, the unions aren't a hundred percent sure about the impact yet this will have on travelers moving forward this summer.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm just wondering, then, how does it affect flyers right now? Because, obviously, it's a busy travel season.\par \par \uc2 Say what you will about the economy. Planes are packed. Airports are stuffed full. And, of course, hotels are experiencing the same. How disruptive will this be? What do you do?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: We don't really know.\par \par \uc2 But we know what else is kind of in the mix with this. Politically speaking, President Biden has announced the launch of an updated version of the government's airline customer service dashboard at flightrights.gov.\par \par \uc2 Now, the idea is to provide travelers with more transparency into airlines' compensation policies, while including airline guarantees for additional compensation, such as cash miles or travel vouchers. Now, the administration essentially plans to propose a new rule later this year mandating all U.S. airlines to compensate passengers with meals, hotels, taxis, vouchers, et cetera, and rebooking fees, whenever they are the ones deemed to be blamed for cancellations or delays.\par \par \uc2 So I think the real question here is, while you might have travel insurance, does it cover strikes at all? And the larger question, what does travel insurance really cover at all at this point?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you have to wonder about that.\par \par \uc2 I want to get you back on this, because -- especially if this does materialize. Hope it doesn't.\par \par \uc2 But, Francesca, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Stepping back from all of this, have you ever gone on a date, just didn't work out, you go on another date, still not working out? How about going on 600 dates, 600 different women, and you're still striking out?\par \par \uc2 The a guy who's been experiencing that and is still looking for love, he's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I guess we have all had our bad first dates, but try 600 bad first dates.\par \par \uc2 Well, my next guest, Joey Batista -- he goes by Joey Bats -- has chronicled this journey for him. He's a good looking, smart guy, seems like he has a great sense of humor. But, for some reason, the 43-year-old New York cafe owner said that he's never really been able to follow up on those 600 dates with maybe a second date.\par \par \uc2 I don't know what the problem is, but Joey can help us out.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 JOEY BATISTA, SAYS HE'S GONE ON 600 FIRST DATES: Thanks, Neil. I appreciate it. Love being here. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I love the story, because you're very self-deprecating about it, but, for some reason, it's not worked out for you.\par \par \uc2 But 600 dates is a lot of dates anyway. But what happens? What falls apart?\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, I don't know that anything falls apart. I mean, it's really just the first date. For me, I'm just -- I'm a small-town boy. I got to the big city, and I was just a social guy. So I was just all of a sudden just going on all these dates. It was exciting and meeting new people. So I was -- I just kind of kept rolling.\par \par \uc2 And I did eventually meet someone and we were together for about a year- and-a-half. It didn't work out. And then the business started. And now I don't have any time to meet anybody.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh.\par \par \uc2 But, I mean, obviously, you got 600 initial yeses, right?\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Yes. That's correct, yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 So, what stopped a second one?\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: It was very rare that I'd go on a second date.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Why?\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: I guess I -- that's a good question. It's a great question you're asking me.\par \par \uc2 I just -- I have been married before, and I don't want to lie to myself. If I feel like I feel a certain way about someone, I will invite them out on a second date, and it's just very rare that that happened.\par \par \uc2 And I don't want -- it's not one-sided. There are times when I was interested in her and she wasn't interested in me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: That's fine. So, I just...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, those 600, it's not all these -- the women telling you, forget it, second time, no?\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A lot of times, it was your call.\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Exactly. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what is that telling you here?\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Well, I think that, because of my ex -- mediocre ex-marriage relationship has put me kind of in a position where I'm really happy by myself.\par \par \uc2 So, for me to introduce somebody into my life, I got to -- it's -- I got to feel a certain way about it. And I don't want to lie about it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, you're right.\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: I don't like the idea of settling. I would just rather...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How long were you married? Do you mind my asking, Joey?\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Well, it was together for 10 years, married five.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. All right.\par \par \uc2 Now, you have got a \uc2 Match.com\uc2  commercial. You're featured in that, so, obviously, you have become even a bigger cult sensation. You're all over The New York Post. Everyone's talking about you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So what's happening now with some of your newfound notoriety? No doubt it will explode after this appearance. But what's been going on?\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Well, I mean, I'm just really busy with my business, and I know that this will probably impact the dating side a little bit, but I'm really focused on what I'm doing now anyway.\par \par \uc2 So, like, even -- just like I said earlier, I'm doing a lot less dating now because I'm just so dedicated to it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Whereas, before, I just had a regular engineering job. I would get home from work, and I didn't have to think about my job. I just went, let's go. Happy hour. Let's go, you know, evening dates.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So you run the cafe. It's your cafe, right?\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Yes, we have a few locations, but I'm the owner. I'm the sole owner.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, so you're loaded. You have got money coming out the yin-yang, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Not yet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: I'm hoping soon, but that's the plan, Neil.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But now I understand you have a wonderful mother. She might be a wee bit protective.\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Very, very, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So has she burst a lot of these dating bubbles, in other words, you were making progress, and she tells you, no, no, no, that's not the one for you?\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Well, I'm glad you asked that.\par \par \uc2 So, she's super -- she's just as social as I am, or I'm as social as she is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Everybody loves mom here at the cafe.\par \par \uc2 But I tell them it's because you haven't dated me yet. If she finds out you're dating me, everything changes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: So, yes, very protective.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, she's protective. No one's good enough. No one's good enough for her son, right?\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Typical mom. Typical mom. Yes, you're right.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Do you ever try to sneak a date with someone, have them never meet your mom?\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Yes, well, I mean, I'd only really want to introduce someone to my mom if I thought she was someone worthy of introducing, just like I'd introduce to any of my friends.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: If I bring you to -- like, I'm in one of my friends' bars right now.\par \par \uc2 If I bring a woman here, it's because I'm enjoying my time with her. And she's going to meet a lot of the people that I'm friends with. So, otherwise, we will probably start somewhere where I don't know anybody. And if it doesn't work out, it doesn't work out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you have a good attitude about it.\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I have a feeling you will let mom know if you find the one, and then that will be quite an interesting development.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You got to let us know how that works.\par \par \uc2 But you have a great -- a great attitude about this, 600 first dates. So, that -- that alone says you're doing something right.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, you...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: A lot of my friends are like, how do you even...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: How do you even have the time? I'd be tired.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: But, you know, I was a sales guy, too. So...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This is a family show, Joey.\par \par \uc2 BATISTA: So, I have no problem...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This is a family show. All right.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you my, friend. Good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 Here is "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230512ej5c0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Booming Migrant Surge As Title 42 End Looms; Eight Dead At Texas Migrant Center From Vehicle Crash; Jean-Pierre Defends White House Priorities; Two Young Sisters Killed In Shooting; Biden To Meet With Top Congressional Leaders; Democrats Warn Default Will Stop Social Security Checks; Biden To Host Lawmakers Tuesday; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Faces Low Approval Numbers; Task Force On Slavery Reparations In California Recommending Cash Payments To Some Black Residents; Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  Pushes For $17 An Hour Federal Minimum Wage; Some Colleges Again Considering Standardized Test Scores For Admissions Policy After Coronavirus Pandemic; U.S. Border Patrol Prepares For Migrant Surge After Ending Of Title 42\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Bill Melugin, Jacqui Heinrich, Casey Stegall, Chad  Pergram, Brit Hume, William La Jeunesse, Aishah Hasnie, Alexandria Hoff \par \uc2 8,201 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: And then, gave the guy signed shoes after the game.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: Wow.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Really, really great moment.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD J.R., FOX NEWS HOST: Sport 42 (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: Bravo. I love this.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, he dropped 42, it looks like. Yes. Tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME," RFK Jr. says the CIA killed his uncle. He's not on the show.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I think he's on "HANNITY".\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes. But it makes it look like he I on the show.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's a good -- teas though for him.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's a great story.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: But you got three seconds.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I did a podcast with \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 . Everything will be OK. You can check it out whenever you get podcast.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right. That's (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, good job.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right. That's it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next. Hello, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Everything will be OK. Thanks, Greg.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Historically, low polling numbers for President Biden. Brit Hume will break it all down for us.\par \par \uc2 We have new details on that mall shooting in Texas that left eight people dead. And California takes a major step toward paying billions in slavery reparations. We'll take you there live.\par \par \uc2 But first, "BREAKING TONIGHT", with just days to go before the end of pandemic-related asylum rules at the southern border, there are alarming new numbers tonight about immigrants at the border.\par \par \uc2 The governor of Texas is now launching a new tactical force there, while leaders here in Washington point fingers and do little else at least right now.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE". Jacqui Heinrich is at the White House, with the president's slumping approval data, and the latest on the expiration of Title 42.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with correspondent Bill Melugin along the border in Brownsville, Texas. Good night -- good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Texas Governor Greg Abbott made a major announcement today, saying, he is going to be deploying several 100 elite Texas soldiers down here to the southern border, equipped with riot gear in an effort to block any mass migrant crossings into his state.\par \par \uc2 He is doing this as we are now just three days away from the end of Title 42. And already, we are seeing record-high illegal crossings.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): President Biden's over border policies is going to cause a catastrophic disaster.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Today, Texas Governor Greg Abbott, announced the formation of a new elite border response force, made up of hundreds of Texas National Guard soldiers, who he says will deploy to hotspots and repel mass illegal crossings.\par \par \uc2 ABBOTT: They will be deployed with the equipment and tools that will be needed for them to be effective. That includes boats on the water. Night vision, as well as riot gear.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Illegal crossings at the southern border are skyrocketing ahead of Title 42's expiration on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 And new video from the Fox drone team shows one of the biggest groups of migrants we have ever seen. After they crossed illegally into Brownsville, Texas. Most of them, adult men in a line almost as far as the eye can see.\par \par \uc2 Border Patrol reports that over the weekend, in a 72-hour span, they made more than 26,000 migrant apprehensions. A staggering average of almost 8,800 per day.\par \par \uc2 And now, some Democrats are joining Republicans in sounding the alarm, Kansas Democrat Congresswoman \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 , penned this letter to DHS Secretary Mayorkas today. Writing, in part, "We now face a doubling of illegal crossings at our southern border by some estimates, exacerbating the current humanitarian and refugee crisis."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, eight migrants are dead in a dozen others are injured in Brownsville, after police say the driver of an SUV ran a red light lost control and crashed into them in front of a migrant shelter.\par \par \uc2 Police say the driver, George Alvarez is a local with an extensive rap sheet. He is now charged with eight counts of manslaughter with bail set at $3.6 million.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, Border Patrol reports that last week alone, they detected almost 19,000 known gotaways at our border.\par \par \uc2 Just to put those numbers in perspective for your viewers, that's equivalent to roughly a full capacity \uc2 NBA\uc2  arena, sneaking across our border and getting into the United States without capture in a single week. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hard to fathom. Bill Melugin, along the border. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Less than two weeks after announcing he will run for a second term, President Biden is experiencing the worst poll numbers of his first term, and frankly, of any president at this point in their presidency.\par \par \uc2 The president is also grappling with the massive immigration surge, we just heard about, expected later this week along the border.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich is live on the North Lawn, with details. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 As officials warn of up to 12,000 illegal crossings per day, when Title 42 is lifted later on this week, the White House is calling on Congress for comprehensive immigration reform. But not the bill that House Republicans will vote on later this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Given all that House Republicans have had to say about our plan, you would think they'd have some grand alternative. But think again.\par \par \uc2 -- two, would be a disaster for border security and a Christmas morning gift for human smugglers.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The White House says President Biden would veto the proposal, mandating Border Patrol hire and train 20,000 agents, upgrade existing technology, and resume border wall construction because it "does nothing to address root causes of migration, border patrol, hire and train 20,000 agents upgrade existing technology and resume border wall construction because it, "does nothing to address root causes of migration, reduces humanitarian protections, and restricts lawful pathways, which are critical alternatives to unlawful entry."\par \par \uc2 Biden didn't elaborate Monday, announcing a proposal coming later this year to compensate travelers for canceled or delayed flights.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: As we approach Memorial Day this weekend, and -- Memorial Day weekend, I'm rushing it -- and a busy summer travel season, I'm here to talk about steps my administration is taking to make air travel better for all Americans.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The president's success on issues most important to voters laid bare in a new Washington Post ABC News poll, his approval rating dropping to 36 percent overall. With a 30 percent drop in support among Black people compared to two years ago. And he's trailing former President Trump on both mental sharpness and physical health to effectively serve as president. Even the former chair of the DNC sounding the alarm.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE, FORMER CHAIRWOMAN, DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL COMMITTEE: The coalition that elected \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with the historic numbers that we saw in 2020. That coalition right now is fragmented. That should concern them.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But the White House indicates it does not.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Is the president really focused on today's biggest issues, when everyone in here is asking about Title 42, the debt ceiling, mass shooting over the weekend, and we didn't hear the president talk about any of those things.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Regular American families -- everyday families are thinking about Memorial Day Weekend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So, the White House denies any disconnect as the president talks about things like meal vouchers for air travel disruptions, while the press secretary emphatically calls on Congress to solve issues like the border, the debt ceiling, and gun violence.\par \par \uc2 And even though the President campaigned on being the uniter-in-chief, tonight, he is holding a movie screening. He's signaling low expectations for tomorrow's pivotal meeting with congressional leaders with an event on Wednesday, slated to hit Republicans on their proposed budget cuts, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good, Jacqui. You know, tonight, The New York Post is reporting that it was banned from the president's only event today, as federal prosecutors were told are nearing a decision on possibly charging \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . Why the ban?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Well, that reporter who was not allowed into the event, Bret, often asks \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  questions. So, one can really only guess. But this has been an ongoing issue with this press office, limiting who can attend, by issuing event credentials.\par \par \uc2 Now we can tell you that The Daily Caller was also initially denied a credential, but they were allowed in eventually, after their report are pushed back.\par \par \uc2 And the White House, overall, has never explained why or how they determine who is and is not led into these things. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. We should point out, the previous administration had their own lists, sometimes, that didn't get in, and we've been fighting for access all along. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Another tragedy in Texas left eight people dead just north of Dallas. We're learning more about the victims of a gunman's attack at an outlet mall, Saturday.\par \par \uc2 He was killed by a police officer, who had been responding to a separate call. Senior correspondent Casey Stegall. is in Allen, Texas tonight with the latest. Good evening, Casey.\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening.\par \par \uc2 We are also learning tonight that two young sisters were among the victims - among those killed in this shooting, and their mother is in the hospital, listed in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL (voice over): Fourth grader Daniela Mendoza and her second-grade sister, Sofia Mendoza, attended Cox Elementary School. And today, in a letter to parents, the district superintendent says, in part, "Words cannot express the sadness we feel as we grieve the loss of our students. Please hold them in does a family close to your hearts."\par \par \uc2 This, has investigators trying to determine the motive with a 33-year-old Mauricio Garcia's attack for allegedly opening fire here at the crowded Premium Outlets of Allen outdoor mall, Saturday afternoon. Officials are looking at ties he may have had to white supremacy groups.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, a spokesperson for the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  confirms that Garcia did enter the armed forces back in 2008, but was released within weeks without completing initial training.\par \par \uc2 When the shooting began here, -- yes, Saturday afternoon, frightened shoppers hid and ran for safety. A police officer at the scene on an unrelated call, quickly responded, shooting, and killing the suspect.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When we were running, the shot got closer to us, like really, really close and I thought they were running towards us since we were all running around. And there are more people running around just.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Finally, tonight -- today, the Texas House advanced a bill that would raise the minimum age to buy a semi-automatic weapon from 18 to 21 years old, but passing it through both chambers of the Republican- controlled legislature will likely be an uphill battle.\par \par \uc2 Governor Greg Abbott in the past has called raising the age unconstitutional, though a handful of states have enacted similar laws. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Casey Stegall, live in Allen, Texas. Casey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 As mentioned before, President Biden is scheduled to meet with congressional leaders from both parties tomorrow to talk about the economy, specifically, about raising the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 This comes as he refuses to consider a bill, specifically, that has passed the Republican-led House to address that debt ceiling threshold and cut government spending.\par \par \uc2 Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill tonight.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Democrats sounding debt ceiling alarm.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Default would mean the checks would stop from Social Security, from the military, and to much more.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The administration warning of dire consequences.\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , UNITED STATES TREASURY SECRETARY: That would be permanently higher borrowing costs for Americans, for buying a home, buying a car --\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The public scared that the government could run out of cash.\par \par \uc2 DONNA HORNE, RESIDENT, FLORIDA: If we keep spending money the way we're spending it, you know, we worry about our country going bankrupt.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE BELLINI, RESIDENT, OHIO: It gives me the heebie-jeebies. It would be ridiculous apocalypse.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: A bipartisan bicameral conclave on the schedule Tuesday at 4:00 p.m. just after the markets close.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and House Speaker Kevin McCarthy huddle for the highest stakes meeting yet of their careers.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: The meeting in the Oval Office with the four leaders tomorrow, I think, is an important thing. I think that shows the American people how important it is.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The president and congressional Democrats, insisting on a clean debt ceiling hike.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): We are in lockstep with President Biden.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But most Senate Republicans are now in lockstep with McCarthy, and his bill to hike the debt ceiling and shave spending. More than 40 Senate Republicans vowed to filibuster President Biden's clean debt ceiling demand.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MIKE LEE\uc2  (R-UT): If any 41 senators don't want something to happen, they can effectively stop that legislation.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: It's all about the math. It takes 60 votes to crack a filibuster. There is talk of postponing the debt ceiling deadline to buy time, but that requires a vote.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE LAWLER (R-NY): Kicking the can down the road is not going to change the construct here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): A warning sign treasury bills ripening in June hit a record yield. The reason investors demand a higher return when there's a chance the government could default. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hey, Chad, quickly, is there optimism at all, that this meeting tomorrow is going to end with something concrete?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Very doubtful. Maybe in the next couple of weeks, but not there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed ahead of key inflation reports. The Dow lost 56. The S&P 500 finished ahead two. The NASDAQ gained 21-1/2, entering it into a bull market.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Brit Hume joins us on President Biden's plummeting poll numbers and the politics of the debt ceiling. First, here with some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 FOX 5 in New York, as communities near the city threatened to go to court to block a plan to put migrants in suburban hotels.\par \par \uc2 The supervisor in Orangetown, New York, says she and other officials in Rockland County were not consulted about the move, which she says was almost an ambush.\par \par \uc2 FOX 13 in Tampa, as Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signs legislation, banning Chinese citizens from purchasing land in Florida, unless they are also a U.S. citizen, or permanent resident.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis highlighted concerns about China making what he called a concerted effort to acquire farmland throughout the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Portland, from our affiliate FOX 12. One of the big stories there tonight.\par \par \uc2 An Oregon third-grader discovers a tooth belonging to a Columbian mammoth that lived about 10,000 years ago. It happened while Jeremiah Longbrake was playing in a creek at his grandmother's house.\par \par \uc2 An archaeologist at the \uc2 University of Oregon\uc2  has confirmed Jeremiah's discovery.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look, "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FORMER CLINTON POLLSTER AND ADVISER: This poll has to send shockwaves. He just announced for presidency. You're supposed to go up when you announce, not down.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS, ABC NEWS HOST: This poll is just too brutal for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE, ABC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It kept me up and I thought they should wake up and look at those numbers.\par \par \uc2 If they're unable to make this campaign from the bottom up and talk to people where they are, it's going to be a struggle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's one poll, but it is raising some eyebrows in democratic circles. ABC Washington post poll at this weekend. President Biden's job approval 37 percent, this is registered voters here. Disapproved 56 percent.\par \par \uc2 How about this one? 2024 Democrat primary preference. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , 36 percent, someone else, 58 percent. Just two markers in that poll. There are others that also got some attention.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some perspective on all of this. Joining us, Fox News Chief Political Analyst, Brit Hume. Brit, good evening.\par \par \uc2 You know, we say it's one poll. It does track slumping approval ratings, our Fox poll showed that recently. But it really did get a lot of attention this weekend, just a few days after President Biden launched his reelection effort.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, it got a lot of people's attention and no wonder it is kind of striking. That number that you saw, the approval number is about five points below his average job approval rating in the RealClearPolitics average, which we often pay attention to. So, it's possible it's an outlier.\par \par \uc2 It's also equally possible that the chickens have finally come home to roost on all these issues where things are going so badly in the country. The economy, the border, you know, inflation, worries with the national debt and all the rest of it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, I had left over worries about the consequences of Afghanistan, the ongoing war in Europe and so on.\par \par \uc2 So, it's possible all this is now finally catching up to him, to Mr. Biden, at the worst possible moment.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and you know, there are also questions in this poll about his ability to do the job, concerns about his age. You had MSNBC do an interview just last week where they asked what Fortune 500 company would hire an 82-year-old CEO.\par \par \uc2 So, those issues seem to be hitting home, especially with independence.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, I think, look, his senility and age are obvious. And it seemed to me it's been just a matter of time before people said, wait a minute, this guy's going to be 82, he'd be 86 when he finishes his term. It's too old.\par \par \uc2 You know, people have elderly relatives. Some people are elderly, like me. I'm too old for the job by far. And if I am, I know he is.\par \par \uc2 So, I think people see that, which is why you see even within the Democratic Party, people really don't want him to run. . So, it'll be interesting to see if this poll holds, Bret. It'll be interesting to see, you know, this poll has him losing to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That surprised me that he would be losing to Trump, the guy he beat already.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, it'll be interesting to see what the Democrats do if these numbers hold.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, the electability question there, you see at six points with registered voters, seven with adults, the former president winning substantially.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn to the debt ceiling politics and all of this back and forth ahead of this meeting at the White House. And there's not a lot of hope that something's going to be resolved tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But your thoughts on where this stands.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, the striking thing to me is that the tables have now been completely turned on this. The administration and Democrats in Congress clearly thought that the Republicans would not do anything on the debt limit, would not be able to do anything on the debt limit, and couldn't pass an extension of the debt limit. And they could therefore blame them as the deadline grew closer and anxiety is about some kind of default grew stronger.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans then turned around and did it. They passed an extension of the debt limit. They're the only body who has. They're the only body who's really done anything.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it was accompanied by some spending restraint that Democrats don't like. But nonetheless, it does raise the debt limit.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's up to, it seems to me, you'd look at the Congress and say, well, now it's up to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Well, you don't hear anybody in the Senate moving to act on the debt limit. And I think that's because Senate Democrats can't pass an extension of the debt limit without the kind of spending restraint that the Republicans want. They don't have the votes to do it.\par \par \uc2 So, they're considering ramping up the rhetoric on, oh, what a terrible thing it'd be and the economy would collapse and the Social Security would stop and all the rest of it to try to -- to try to renew the momentum they thought they once had. But they've lost it, I don't see how talking about it like this gets it back.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and you think the leverage is all with the House Republicans now?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, they've