d. The lawyer for the Marine veteran involved says his client did not intend to kill Jordan Neely. Neely's family calls the Marine's statement not an apology. Over the weekend police arrested 13 people for fighting with police officers in New York, and officials are now looking for several people accused of storming city subway tracks as a part of protests over Neely's death.\par \par \uc2 Also in New York, closing arguments are complete in the civil trial brought by the advice columnist who says then businessman \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sexually attacked her in a department store in the mid-1990s. The former president has denied the allegations by E. Jean Carroll who is suing him for defamation and battery. He did not attend the trial, but lawyers played part of his deposition to the jury. Carroll is seeking compensatory and punitive damages. The jury will get the case tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 California's task force on slavery reparations is recommending cash payments to some black people living in the state of California. It's also promoting a formal apology for slavery. But there are major questions about how much money should be spent, who gets it, and where it comes from. National correspondent William La Jeunesse takes a look tonight from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are here today for mission. It is reparations.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: That political football is now in the hands of Democrats in Sacramento, caught between an economic downturn and a base of loyal voters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If you are running for office, do not think you are going to win your election if you don't have a concrete plan for reparation.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: The California Reparations Task Force is recommending the state pay black residents who can show they descended from any African American relative living in the U.S. prior to 1900, slave or not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Reparations is now. Pay the debt, cut the check.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Black Californians could receive up to $1.2 million each. Payments include $13,000 a year for health discrimination, $2,300 annually for over-policing and mass incarceration, $3,300 a year for housing discrimination.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am so tired of my people suffering.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: The panel also wants eligible blacks to receive subsidized mortgage payments, free college tuition, guaranteed annual incomes, automatic increases in a minimum wage, and interest-free loans to black- owned businesses. That's unrealistic for some but a moral imperative to others.\par \par \uc2 REP. BARBARA LEE, (D-CA): Reparations are not a luxury for our people but a human right long overdue for millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 LARRY ELDER\uc2 , (R) RADIO TALK SHOW HOST: Reparations is the extraction of money from people who were never slave owners to be given to people who were never slaves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): The report goes to Governor Newsom and lawmakers in July. And with the state facing a $23 billion deficit, the task force said it's willing to accept a cash down payment with the promise of more in the future. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it. William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  says there will be political consequences for Democrats who do not support his push for a $17 an hour minimum wage. Sanders' last effort to get the wage up to $15 an hour failed right before last year's midterm elections. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie tells us where things stand tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2 , (I-VT): There will be political consequences.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: An ominous warning from Vermont Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  to his caucus over a fight to raise the federal minimum wage to $17 per hour over a five-year period.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: This is not a radical idea.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The bill has little chance of reaching 60 votes to overcome a GOP filibuster, especially after seven Democrats and one independent rejected Sanders' previous attempt at a $15 wage to a COVID relief package before the midterms, including then Democrat \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  who caused an uproar with a thumbs down and curtsy to vote no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Ms. Sinema? Ms. Sinema, no.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Calling another vote could create heart burn for Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  who wants to protect vulnerable senators like Sinema, \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2 , and Joe Manchin, who all faced tough reelections in Republican leaning states.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: We are going to push it as quickly and as hard as we can.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But Michael Hardaway, former senior adviser to Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , says moderates have nothing to worry about.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL HARDAWAY, FORMER SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL ADVISER: I think he is trying to drive the conversation.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And that Sanders is rallying the left ahead of a presidential election.\par \par \uc2 HARDAWAY: Even if there is not going to be a bill passed through the Senate, someone has to keep it out there for perception, for media, for public sentiment purposes, and that's really Bernie's job. It's really about building energy on the left as we also move toward a presidential election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And, Bret, Senator Sanders will hold a mark-up hearing on his bill next month. In the meantime, we reached out to Leader Schumer to find out if he, perhaps, anticipates holding a vote on this bill this Congress. We did not hear back. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live on the Hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we begin our series on education in America with a look at how the coronavirus pandemic affected children across the country. This comes as students finish their SAT college entrance exams this past weekend amid questions about whether to submit them. Here is correspondent Alexandria Hoff.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Good morning, girls and boys.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Mornings at William Ramsay Elementary School kick off with a burst of enthusiasm. These third graders were kindergartens when COVID-19 hit. Some younger students had no experience in a classroom before this school year.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ROUTHOUSKA, WILLIAM RAMSAY ELEMENTARY PRINCIPAL: We had a lot more work to do with them to catch up. Those that we needed to support the most were those that started virtual.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Older students haven't had as much time to catch up. Last year average SAT scores were the lowest since 2016 when the test format changed, that's according to Best Colleges, with the pandemic resulting in a wave of new testing policies. The National Center for Open and Fair Testing found that more than 1,700 colleges or universities have waived SAT and ACT requirements for admissions this fall. But some admissions prep experts say submitting scores anyway may give applicants an edge.\par \par \uc2 COLLEEN PAPARELLA, D.C. COLLEGE COUNSELING PRESIDENT: I still recommend that they proceed as if they are going to send test scores, because all things being equal, a better candidate will have test scores.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: MIT is among a few schools now reinstating SAT/ACT requirements, writing, quote, "Our research shows standardized tests help us better assess the academic preparedness of all applicants."\par \par \uc2 And while college admissions are years away for these elementary school students, there is an urgent demand to address social skills and emotional learning.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN CUMMINGS, THIRD GRADE TEACHER: Students have expressed during the pandemic that they were feeling hopeless and that they were feeling isolated, alienated, still to this day comes up a lot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): That teacher, Lauren Cummings, said that structure is something that kids crave, and because of a return to it, they have made strides. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In part two of our series tomorrow, the debate over whether arming teachers would make schools safer.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a massive surge of migrants heading for the U.S.-Mexico border leads to a lot of talk but not a lot of action so far in Washington. We'll talk about it all with the panel after a quick break.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT, (R) TEXAS: We are being overrun by our own federal government.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We are doing everything that we can. Congress has failed to act.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT, (R) TEXAS: President Biden is laying down a welcome mat to people across the entire world, saying that the United States border is wide open.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE DEMOCRATIC CAUCUS CHAIR: We want order at the border, at the same time do it in a compassionate way.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: We have to correct the lies that smugglers tell vulnerable migrants. They think they're coming and they'll be able to stay, and that is just unequivocally false.\par \par \uc2 CHAD WOLF\uc2 , FORMER ACTING HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: They've been removing about 50,000 folks a month on average under Title 42. Guess what, on Thursday that stops. They're going to put everyone in Title 8 removal proceedings, which means they are going to release them into American communities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That all happens Thursday as Title 42 expires. The Border Patrol Chief Ortiz has been putting out these updates from his folks along the border. And this one, 10:52 this morning, "Past 72 hours, 26,382 apprehensions, about 7,400 got-aways, 164 pounds of marijuana, 83 pounds of meth, 66 pounds of cocaine, 11 pounds of fentanyl, five pounds of heroin, $12,200 seized, one firearm, three sex offenders, one wanted felon, one gang member. Great work over the weekend." That's 72 hours.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , Vince Coglianese, WMAL Washington talk radio host, editorial director at "The Daily Caller," and \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 , national political correspondent of National Public Radio. Mara, we have been dealing with these numbers, we've been dealing with these stories. There hasn't been a change policy-wise, so here we come to Thursday.\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: Yes, and so the administration is trying, belatedly many people say, Republicans and Democrats, to get out the message, no, the border isn't open. You have got Republicans saying yes, it is, because that's their attack line on the Biden White House.\par \par \uc2 But they are trying to do a couple things. They have surged some National Guard to the border. They are going to have more facilities. They are actually making it so if you get apprehended crossing the border illegally you can't try again in five years. Whether that's going to be enough to stop the message that migrants are hearing, which is Title 42 is over, it's easier to cross, that's the message they are trying to counter act. And look, it's very hard to say you want to be more welcoming and humane and at the same time say don't come.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: That's just really hard to do. And they have never figured out yet a way to do it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But the bottom line, Hugh, is the politics of this. Even though the administration continues to say Congress is not acting, Congress is not acting. Really, it's President Biden, it falls on his doorstep when this surge happens.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: If you have the executive authority to forgive student loans, you have the executive authority to do whatever you need to make the country secure, which is make the border secure. And he has thus far taken a pose, struck a position of a hard no on anything having to do with enforcement. And I believe that's why he is at 36 percent. Age figures into it, too. But Americans understand you can't take 4 million people in a year. It's not acceptable in any national security metric. The last year President Trump half, half-million people came in without permission. This year 4 million, 72,000 a week. That's an Ohio state-Michigan game. And every week another group of people come in, and they're mostly look like young men who are going to take jobs and are going to take up space and are just not good for the country to allow in in a wave.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin, Vince, has been using different ways to look at numbers, because numbers everybody glazes over. But you talk about stadiums and somebody going to an \uc2 NBA\uc2  game or a Ohio State-Michigan game, it's coming over every day.\par \par \uc2 VINCE COGLIANESE, "THE DAILY CALLER": It's a mass of humanity. It's a lot of drugs. It's a lot of death. It's a lot of sex trafficking, it's a lot of human trafficking. It's the cartels making tens of the billions of dollars a year who have been able to turn the Biden administration into a great business asset for their illegal ways. And it's an administration that has no interest in actually enforcing the border. It's a lot of lip service if what we're hearing.\par \par \uc2 Even the deployment of these troops, sending these marines and soldiers to the border is not for border enforcement. It's for bringing people into the country. They're going to be tasked with working at the warehouses and dealing with paperwork. It has nothing to do with actually enforcing our border which, you saw Susan Rice wrote in a personal, in a handwritten note just published in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " about a week ago prior to her leaving the White House. She said it is our generosity to foreign nationals that is bringing them into the country. That's the problem.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is Senator Sinema from this weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. KYRSTEN SINEMA, (I-AZ): The Biden administration had two years to prepare for this and did not do so. And our state is going to bear the brunt. And migrants will be in crisis as soon as next week.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Have you talked to the White House directly about this?\par \par \uc2 SINEMA: Yes, I have.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What's the response?\par \par \uc2 SINEMA: It has not been adequate.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: I spoke with Senator Sinema, I think within the last two weeks. I spoke with Leader Schumer also within the last two weeks. There is not a day that goes by that we are not urging Congress to pass reform.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime, Mara, Associated Press, "Immigrants waiting 10 years in U.S. just to get a court date. Backlog stems from a change made two months after President Biden took office when Border Patrol agents began the now defunct practice of quickly releasing immigrants on parole. Until then, the migrants in question won't even get an initial court appearance on the books, though they can live and work in the U.S. After that their case will work its way through the U.S. immigrant courts, a process that takes about four years amid backlogged, and that reached 2.1 million cases in January up from 600,000 in 2016.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, and those are from people who are going tomorrow the, quote, legal process.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: They wouldn't be here unless there was also -- there are all those things you just mentioned. But there is also a real demand push. We have a labor shortage in this country. These people are finding jobs. They are not just sitting around. And the answer, and you know I get a lot of flak for saying this, is comprehensive immigration reform where we have strict border security --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But every time we go down that road, the comprehensive, they can't handle it.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: It never works, I know. It never works. Congress can't do it. But there is a solution to this. This is not a problem lurking for a solution. There is a solution. Strict border security, more legal immigration to meet the needs and to fill labor shortage in the U.S., and then something that you do about the Dreamers.\par \par \uc2 COGLIANESE: I'm sorry, there are laws on the books, though, to address the labor demand for illegal immigrants. The Biden administration has to enforce them go. Go after companies who are exploiting child labor and illegal labor and enforce the law and you can bring that magnet down.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And very quickly, can they go around the edges on Capitol Hill and get something that effects --\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: No. There is no fix. The president has to use his executive authority. If you can forgive billions in student loans, you can close the border.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Panel, stand by. Up next, the president's sinking poll numbers and his meeting tomorrow with congressional leaders amid the debt ceiling fight. And later, a 100-year-old veteran pumping iron toward the century mark.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There's not a Fortune 500 company in the world looking to hire a CEO in his 80s. So, why would an 82-year-old \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  be the right person for the most important job in the world?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Because I have acquired a hell of a lot of wisdom. I know more than the vast majority of people. I'm more experienced than anybody who has ever run for the office.\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , POLLSTER: There's a lot of roadblocks here. And look, everyone sees his age, and everyone sees the question of, can he really go a second term here?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some doubts in Democratic circles. Here is "The Wall Street Journal," though. "Biden faces rough month as debt talks, potential border surge loom. Following the campaign launch, Mr. Biden's team declined to release his initial fundraising totals. People familiar with the early fundraising said the president didn't surpass the amount raised first 24 hours of Mr. Biden's 2020 campaign when he brought in $6.3 million from about 97,000 donors, his campaign said at the time."\par \par \uc2 Back with the panel. We don't know. It's one poll, but it seems to track with others, Hugh?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: It does. Gallup came in at 38 percent approval, so the ABC- "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " poll of 36 does not sound like an outlier. You got the money quote. It's the one I had written down. I have a hell a lot of wisdom and I know more than the vast majority of people. That's not how one runs for the presidency. And I asked myself, what does General Secretary Xi, what does \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , what does the Ayatollah Khomeini think about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? And I think they think he is infirm, because that's what I see, that's what I hear. And I think American people coming to the recognition he really can't do this. And I'm expecting an LBJ 68 exit sometime next year.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You are?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Yes, because I don't think can he do it. And I don't think his wife is going to let him do it. And his friends certainly should be counseling not to do it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " just dropped this opinion, this editorial, "Why is President Biden afraid of news conferences?" Mara. "President Biden hasn't dropped the microphone. He appears to have lost it. Mr. Biden is turning into a news media evader. It's harmful to his presidency and the nation. The past 100 years, only Presidents \uc2 Richard M. Nixon\uc2  and \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  averaged fewer news conferences than Mr. Biden. So far in 23 Mr. Biden has done zero solo news conferences. He did conduct two joint news conferences in which the president and a visiting foreign leader faced the media together. It should not take a visit from Canadian Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  or South Korean President \uc2 Yoon Suk\uc2 -yeol for the American public to hear the president answering urgent questions from a free press." We are always asking for more.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, we're always asking for more. The White House doesn't see that there is anything in it for President Biden to have news conferences. They say he answers lots of questions when he is going to the helicopter. And they also say it doesn't really matter that he gives local news interviews when he is going out to talk about a bridge that's being funded by the infrastructure law.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You've covered all kinds of administrations --\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, of course he should give more press conferences. The fact that only \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  gave fewer than Biden is not a good sign. But they were both reelected.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Friday, he did say that he was giving a major press conference, but then it was MSNBC interview he was referring to, and the White House cleaned that up. Vince, your thoughts on all this?\par \par \uc2 COGLIANESE: Yes, I thought that head fake Friday was just rubbing the press' nose in it. I know Biden didn't mean to. I don't think he actually knew what he was doing next later that day. He said it was a major press conference. It was an MSNBC interview.\par \par \uc2 I think the White House press office knows that he is a liability when he is speaking openly. And he's a guy who spent a career just saying whatever is on his mind, often getting in trouble for that, but now in his later years it's getting worse and worse and worse. And the last thing they want him is unscripted moment, so much so that we saw during that bilateral with the South Korean president that he had that written out question from an "L.A. Times" reporter that included a pronouncer for her name and a picture of her face. It was as much handholding as they could possibly do without actually holding his hand at the lectern.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The other thing that was striking was the head-to-head with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the former president, in this poll at least. In the registered voters Trump is up six, and that's a big electability question for a former president that everybody said this is the strategy to win election again.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: The key thing is energy, and the executive wrote that in "Federalist 78." \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has energy on top of energy. Some people don't like the energy, but you can't deny that it's there. President Biden has almost no energy, and the schedule every day reminds us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you sense, though, Mara, that there is angst inside the Democratic circle?\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To the point where somebody is going to run?\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Those are two very different things. Is this nervousness about Biden's age? Absolutely. I would say it's sometimes almost hysterical. But whether he is going to be primaried is a completely different question.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously Robert F. Kennedy Jr. is.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, but I mean primaried by somebody who could really take him on and be a very formidable opponent. We don't see that. There is no deep ideological rift in the party that would lead to that kind of a challenge. Mostly the challenge would be he is too old. And so far nobody has been willing to do it, mostly because they are pretty happy with the things he has done. But there is nothing they can do about the age problem. They can only hope that he looks exactly like he did in the State of the Union every time he appears in public, which was pretty vigorous for him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We shall see. We've got a lot to talk about. Panel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't want to watch life go by, and that's why I get up and do my exercises.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A hundred-year-old veteran is pumping iron this year with the golf bench pressing 100 pounds. Werner Schlaupitz served on the frontlines in the Philippines during World War II. He credits his nutrition, active lifestyle for his longevity. We wish you well. Get the 100 pounds. He's close, 90.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME starts now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230509ej5800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Bernie Sanders Pitching Higher Minimum Wage; Voters Favor Trump and DeSantis in New Poll; Biden Administration Wants Airlines to Pay Passengers; Border Fears; Interview With Rockland County, New York, Executive Ed Day\b0\par\par\uc2 Bob Maginnis, Lydia Hu, Daron Shaw, Jonathan Serrie, Aishah  Hasnie, Jacqui Heinrich, C.B. Cotton, Bill Melugin, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,245 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ED DAY (R), ROCKLAND COUNTY, NEW YORK, EXECUTIVE: Rockland is not going to stand idly by while your administration, which boasts itself as the sanctuary city, diverts busloads of undocumented individuals to our county.\par \par \uc2 It is only draining taxpayer resources from the families who are already here. This is incentivizing illegal immigration. And it does nothing to support our infrastructure or the hardworking citizens we are elected to serve.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, a very angry Rockland County executive, who is here, by the way.\par \par \uc2 As The New York Post put it today, suburban warfare is on. He's angry at New York Mayor Eric Adams for shipping migrants to his community and, by the way, a community that isn't friendly to this sort of stuff or even had a remotely heads-up on this stuff. He -- he will be joining us, I should say, in just a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 This, of course, 72 hours ahead of Title 42 going away at the border and already prompting some nightmarish scenarios at the border that could get worse as the days drag on.\par \par \uc2 The very latest right now on all of this from Bill Melugin in Brownsville, Texas -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Border Patrol says, in just a three day span over the weekend, they made more than 26,000 migrant apprehensions. That's almost 9,000 every single day. We haven't seen these kind of numbers since the Haitian crisis under the bridge in Del Rio two years ago.\par \par \uc2 And, remember, just last week, the White House claimed that illegal immigration is down 90 percent. That is absolutely not the case whatsoever. Case in point, take a look at this video our team just shot right here in Brownsville late yesterday afternoon, one of the biggest single groups of migrants we have ever seen crossing the border illegally, hundreds upon hundreds of them gathering in a line for processing, almost all of them single adults, most of them adult men.\par \par \uc2 Not seeing a whole lot of families or little kids anymore. Brownsville the hot spot on the border right now, as the Rio Grande Valley Sector is dealing with thousands of illegal crossings every single day. Then, yesterday, tragedy here in Brownsville as well. You can see this video where eight migrants died and 10 others were injured after an out-of- control speeding driver plowed into them as they were standing in front of a migrant shelter here in Brownsville.\par \par \uc2 That driver has now been arrested. You can take a look at his mug shot right here, police I.D.ing him today as George Alvarez, a local with an extensive rap sheet. He is now facing eight counts of manslaughter. He's got a $3.6 million bond. And police are still trying to determine what kind of a motive, if any, there was here, in terms of whether or not there was any intention behind this crash.\par \par \uc2 Then we will take you out to El Paso, the other end of the state, where the city is also being inundated by illegal crossings. This is video of several hundred migrants who pushed their way through a gap in the border wall, illegally entering the United States and are gathering and waiting for Border Patrol processing over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 But not everybody is willing to wait like this. Last video to show you, take a look at this drone video from the same area in El Paso. This is a group of migrants who decided, no, we're not going to wait for Border Patrol. We want to get into this country. These are evaders. These are runners.\par \par \uc2 You can them walking right past a National Guard vehicle right now. They're not stopping. They're right by interstate 10 in El Paso. And they just go walking off into neighborhoods. And you will notice there's no Border Patrol immediately there to stop them because so many agents are taking up doing processing right now.\par \par \uc2 Very few of them are out there on the front line. So that's why the got- aways numbers are getting so high. And, Neil, back out here live, when we talk about those got-aways numbers,they are staggering. Border Patrol says last week alone there were more than 18,000, and just over the weekend there were more than 7,000.\par \par \uc2 So a huge amount of people are sneaking into this country without ever being captured, while a lot of our border agents are busy on the front lines doing processing and paperwork. And it's expected to get a lot worse when Thursday hits.\par \par \uc2 I will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I can imagine.\par \par \uc2 Bill, great reporting, my friend.\par \par \uc2 To Chris Cabrera right now, the National Border Patrol Council vice president.\par \par \uc2 Chris, it's accelerating. That, we know. What do you think it'll look like come Thursday?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CABRERA, VICE PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: I think, come Thursday, it's going to be the biggest we have ever seen.\par \par \uc2 And I don't know if it's going to just top off there. I mean, more and more people are mobilizing to get come across. And our administration, our government's not doing anything to discourage people from crossing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Chris, I had a chance to catch up with Texas Democratic Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  on this very issue. He has his own numbers he's thinking about here and what we could be looking at in the days ahead. This is from the congressman earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you expect, once Title 42 goes away, that there will be this throng of thousands, tens of thousands maybe, making their way here? Some have predicted 13,000 to 15,000 per day by the time this thing is gone. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): Well, right now, we're seeing about 8,500 to 8,700 a day.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: I have seen some of the numbers in the different Mexican states. There's way over 152,000 that I know of.\par \par \uc2 There's a huge number of individuals that are staging to come over at the U.S. There are some policies that I think will be effective, but, in my opinion, a little bit too late.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The argument for going ahead and letting Title 42 go, Chris, is this sort of a perverse argument. Well, the numbers are rocketing even with it in effect, the irony being, well, they're rocketing because soon it won't be in effect.\par \par \uc2 But what do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: Yes, I agree with Mr. Cuellar.\par \par \uc2 It's too little, too late. They have had quite a while to get a handle on this, especially when it was one or two areas in Texas that we were dealing with. Now it's spread out all across the Southern border. It's spreading across to the northern border and even to some of our coastal areas like Miami.\par \par \uc2 It's not going to take long for us to breach the 100,000 mark after May 11. It's going to be one of the most notable times I have seen since I have been in the Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, and you have seen a lot, Chris.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much. We will see what happens, Chris Cabrera on that.\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This is extending now to be a battle between cities and counties, even counties that are not sanctuary counties.\par \par \uc2 Rockland County found out the hard way that doesn't necessarily mean that illegal immigrants can't still come your way, at the behest of the mayor of New York City, Eric Adams.\par \par \uc2 We have C.B. Cotton here with more -- C.B.\par \par \uc2 C.B. COTTON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, local politicians are vowing to fight this.\par \par \uc2 One county issuing an emergency order in hopes of halting the plan. The county executive that's bracing to receive the migrants accused New York City Mayor Eric Adams of engaging in human trafficking. Also, another person saying that Mayor Eric Adams hasn't been able to answer the most basic questions. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TERESA KENNY, ORANGETOWN, NEW YORK, SUPERVISOR: We still can't get an answer when and who's coming and if they have indebted and if they have criminal records. And, yes, my residents are concerned. Rightfully so, they're concerned.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Friday, Mayor Adams announced his plan to bus 300 single adult male migrants on a voluntary basis to hotels in Rockland County and the neighboring Orange County.\par \par \uc2 In response, Rockland County declared a statement of emergency in effect for at least 30 days. Politicians there say the move prohibits hotels from housing migrants without a license and could cost any hotel in violation $2,000 per person per day.\par \par \uc2 But Mayor Adams' team says their plans still aren't changing. So far, New York City says it's received more than 60,000 asylum seekers. And the city says this new plan should help with the expected influx following the end of Title 42.\par \par \uc2 Adams says the federal government hasn't helped the city. Now his team says surrounding areas need to do their share. A spokesperson for the mayor writing, in part: "We need other elected officials around the state and country to do their part and emulate the humane and compassionate approach New York City has taken over the past year."\par \par \uc2 The other counties set to receive around 60 migrants said Mayor Adams has agreed to delay sending them as they figure out logistics, but they say his office didn't offer a timetable -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for that, C.B. Good reporting.\par \par \uc2 Rockland County cops, for their part, are deployed and ready to act, we are told, if Mayor Adams goes through on this and tries to get more migrants to the quiet suburban area that is not a sanctuary county, at that, a point that has been pounded again and again by Ed Day, the Rockland County, New York, executive, kind enough to join us now.\par \par \uc2 Executive, very good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So, you were stunned by this.\par \par \uc2 DAY: Hi, Neil. How are you?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You had no heads-up or warning, right?\par \par \uc2 DAY: This -- none whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 We had to figure it out ourselves. And even when we figured it out, the mayor's -- the mayor and the mayor's office were not giving any details. We had to figure out through investigation exactly where this was going to be. We figured out what hotel it was.\par \par \uc2 We got information that told us how many people were coming. They consistently refused to tell us when they were coming, so it left us no choice. We have no idea what's happening. And I resent the fact that, somehow, Mayor Adams thinks he has a hold on humanity and being -- and treating people well.\par \par \uc2 He's doing these folks no favors. We actually have a very humane course of action here. We have great connections with our local not-for-profits. They were at my press conference today, take -- treating people right.\par \par \uc2 What the mayor is doing right now is taking people, trying to have New York City homeless shelters outside New York City, in direct violation of zoning laws. The areas that he has cannot even legally put these things up. So, it's to a point now it's really absurd.\par \par \uc2 And this whole issue about how many are taken on, here's what we got. We have a situation where the mayor of New York decided he wanted -- he wanted a sanctuary county. And he got it. And now he realize he bit off more than he can chew and he can't handle this.\par \par \uc2 He wants to take these poor folks and dump them wherever, in 35 miles north of New York City. I don't think Mayor Adams knows what it looks like up there. It's just utterly ridiculous. It really is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Have any of them made their way to your county?\par \par \uc2 DAY: To the best -- well, it's a very good point, Neil. We have had people who have come to our county. We are caring for people up here, because people who've ended up in the New York City area have family and friends, some in Rockland.\par \par \uc2 They have been here. What that has turned into is 1,000-plus children in the East Rockland School District, a 35 percent increase in child foster care. This is an unmitigated disaster. We are at a breaking point. We are the -- we were deputized by the state to handle housing in Spring Valley because they couldn't handle it.\par \par \uc2 Things are really bad to begin with. Sending in 340 people now is about as cruel as it can get, because there's nothing that's going to be here for these folks. And it's -- that's not humane. It's a political reaction to a failure. That's all this is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, the way to get to and from New York City to you and your beautiful county is through Route 87, of course.\par \par \uc2 So, do you have police, do you have security officials watching for buses? How would they be identified? How would you know?\par \par \uc2 DAY: Neil, I'm a former police commander. I know exactly what -- what has to be done and how it could be done.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you and also the people of Rockland that we are making our very best effort to know whatever movements are in real time and react appropriately. That's as much as I'm going to say.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But as you know from past experiences of people being shipped all over the country under different systems, I should stress, it's oftentimes in the middle of the night, when no one's looking.\par \par \uc2 DAY: I -- we're well aware of that. And there have been some sleepless nights to some people up here. We will just let it go at that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I bet.\par \par \uc2 DAY: We're not going to get out-thought out by the mayor and his people.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, there was, just to confirm, Executive, no warning, that you had no heads-up from the mayor's office?\par \par \uc2 We ourselves have reached out to Mayor Eric Adams. We have not heard back. Hope springs eternal. Maybe we will. But there was no hint of this to come?\par \par \uc2 DAY: No.\par \par \uc2 The only hint they got was the town supervisor in Orangetown, who you had on before, she got a phone call from the mayor, and, basically as a courtesy call, basically. And she confirmed it's a courtesy call. It's a done deal. That's the bottom line.\par \par \uc2 The pointed question that was asked of the mayor was very simple: Are these people vetted? He didn't know. What kind of plan does that tell you? You don't know. You have no idea who these folks are. There may be 340 beautiful, wonderful people. I have no idea. But you don't know, and you're taking them and sending them to another county?\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, how dare you? You have no control over this county. I can't come to New York City and bring people there and say, guess what? I'm going to fill a hotel up in New York City because I want to. It's utter nonsense, utter nonsense. We are independent of New York City.\par \par \uc2 And, frankly, New York City got a billion dollars, a billion dollars to fix this problem. And they're still complaining, and they're still reacting, as opposed to taking a plan and doing something appropriate that does the right thing for these people who are in a very bad situation.\par \par \uc2 He's making it worse, not better. What's going to happen if they come here? They're going to stay here. They're not going to go back anywhere. So they are going to keep on growing and growing. And our lifeboat that we have that is there for the protection of people in need, like any other lifeboat, it works only when it's up until capacity.\par \par \uc2 Once it goes over capacity, it tips. Not only do the folks who you added on go, but the people who were there. I have not-for-profits right now who are livid over what's been going on here, because, with all due respect to the mayor, he doesn't know what he's doing. He has no clue what's going on up here in Rockland.\par \par \uc2 He didn't ask. His people didn't ask. I mean, how do you implement a plan and just say, oh, we're going to come up? We're going to come up. When we ask, when are you going to come up, oh, we can't tell you.\par \par \uc2 That's not...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How do your residents -- how do your residents feel about this?\par \par \uc2 DAY: They are absolutely angry, as -- and that's reflected in the way I'm reacting right now.\par \par \uc2 It's -- if the mayor lived up here in Orangetown, in Orangeburg, near the hotel...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 DAY: ... and this happened, he would be up in arms.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It is incredible, just out of the blue.\par \par \uc2 Ed Day, I'd like to keep track of this. You can update us on that, but this dropped in your lap, and it's like a grenade.\par \par \uc2 DAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will keep a close eye on it.\par \par \uc2 Ed Day, the Rockland County, New York, executive.\par \par \uc2 Again, there was no warning for this, no update on what could happen because of this, and the responsibilities that he and the county might have to deal with right now, whether they like it, over this.\par \par \uc2 We will have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, not exactly, we're from the government and we're here to help, but something like that.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration working in concert with \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  over at the Transportation Department now trying to say, you're stuck and your plane is stopped or delayed or outright canceled, help is on the way, with a little strong-arming from the \uc2 United States government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from Lydia Hu on that -- Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 That's right. The Biden administration announcing today it's going to propose new rules mandating airlines compensate passengers when the airline is responsible for a delay or cancellation. This proposal is similar to rules that are already in place in Europe and Canada, where airlines pay travelers cash when at fault for disruptions. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, U.S. TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: The \uc2 FAA\uc2  and Department of Transportation are doing our part, but airlines need to accept their fundamental responsibility to better serve passengers.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They matter most to middle- class families and people struggling to get the cost in the first place.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: But, Neil, the airline industry questions how this proposal will improve flight performance.\par \par \uc2 Airlines for America\uc2  is a trade association representing Delta, United, American, and others. It points out that most disruptions are caused by the weather, and they say these mandates would make airline tickets more expensive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS CALIO\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, \uc2 AIRLINES FOR AMERICA\uc2 : Careful what you wish for. The one thing you can be sure of is that costs will go up and there will be less flights, and the people most impacted will be consumers at the lower end of the scale.\par \par \uc2 It's going to have precisely the impact that the administration says it's trying to solve.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: Now, this plan follows months of finger-pointing between the federal government and the airlines over issues like shortages in air traffic controllers, lack of preparation for bad weather, and overbooked flight schedules, all of these issues being blamed for the poor flight performances.\par \par \uc2 Now, right now, it's unclear how much or under what circumstances exactly travelers would be compensated. Neil, there's also no clear timeline on when a rule could take effect, as the rulemaking process just getting announced, getting started today, and this could take months to unfold -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you, Lydia, on that.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to \uc2 Gabe Saglie\uc2 . He's a travel expert extraordinaire. I always rely on his expertise when, well, for example, when I'm stuck at a gate.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But anyway, Gabe, help me with this and when the airlines will make good on it, because oftentimes they can claim force majeure, right, I mean, situations beyond our control, the weather or what have you.\par \par \uc2 How does this change with that?\par \par \uc2 GABE SAGLIE, TRAVELZOO.COM: Well, that's a great point, Neil. And as Lydia just mentioned, that's what the airlines are saying.\par \par \uc2 Most of the flight delays we're seeing out there are because of weather and many more because the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is controlling the inbound or outbound flights based on how much volume they can handle. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  already signaling that this summer might be a tough one for them from a staffing standpoint, especially on East Coast airports.\par \par \uc2 So all of those are things that certainly would absolve airlines from having to pay out some of these fees. But, yes, if you look, this is modeled really after what we have been seeing in Europe now for several years, 2005, EU-261 forced airlines to compensate passengers, aside from just refunding them in certain occasions.\par \par \uc2 They have paid out millions of euros to date, and yet the increase in timeliness around flights has only gone up about 5 percent. So, at the end of the day, it's the airlines that are looking at having to shell out monies and fees that, as we just heard, could end up being passed on to the consumer in the way -- in the form of reduced flight schedules and more expensive flights overall.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I guess I'm just curious as to what changed here, Gabe. I mean, there had been an understanding that if you have to rebook a flight now, they would stop charging you for that, not across the board, but enough, that they would cover you for a hotel if it meant that you were stranded.\par \par \uc2 I don't know how many took advantage of that. And maybe get your food. A couple of Cinnabons could do it for me and close the deal. But what about where this goes and how they're going to stick to this?\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Neil, I think a lot of this is. being driven by the fact that the consumer now has become a heck of a lot more aware of the kind of pain that flight delays and cancellations can inflict upon them.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we saw that meltdown. Southwest owned it in December of last year. Tens of thousands -- or more than 60,000 flights canceled there. This summer was a rough summer. And, already, Neil, we're seeing TSA throughput numbers, in other words, the sheer volume of travelers going through TSA security these days, matching or surpassing 2019 pre-COVID levels.\par \par \uc2 So as we head toward the summer season now, there is now this incentive, I think, across the board to really get things straight, because, at the end of the day, if we see a repeat of what we saw last summer over the holiday season late in 2022, we could see a very angry constituency out there.\par \par \uc2 And consumers are now really sort of clamoring much more loudly around compensation and just being -- having these situations made good across the board. And the airlines are going to be, of course, in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Real quickly, though, it hasn't dented their business. I mean, planes are still packed. Airports are still crowded. Delays and cancellations are still more common than uncommon. Does anything really change here?\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Well, yes, I think it's going to be status quo. The goal here, I think, is just to give consumers a bit of optimism at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 But I don't think this is going to be resolved before the summer season, which is showing signs already of some strain in the system.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Because, again, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is concerned about staffing and airlines have already, in some cases, begun to cut back on their original summer schedule.\par \par \uc2 So stay the course, Neil. The Cinnabon does remain open until the end of the day, usually.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: But just be prepared to not be alone on those airplanes and in those airports.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, we're already seeing that, as you predicted, Gabe.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much on that.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In the meantime, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has a double headache to deal with right now, these latest polls that show him trailing both \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Governor DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 But that's just for starters.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich has more -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, it's a curious time for this proposed new rule that won't even be proposed until later on this year when it comes to air travel disruptions, when the president is not out front talking about this week's big issues, like Title 42 ending, the debt limit crisis, even the weekend's mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 So, do those factors play into this new polling that shows Americans disapprove broadly of how he's handling his job?\par \par \uc2 We will get into it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, ahead of debt talks tomorrow at the White House, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  once again saying it would be a huge hit to the U.S. economy if we default. As if.\par \par \uc2 Also saying there's a wide gap between the president and Republicans. Double as if -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Good for Don and Ron, not so good for Joe.\par \par \uc2 The latest on some poll numbers that have people shifting a little bit of their forecasts and strategies.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich has more from the White House -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Yes, Neil, this is just pretty bad news for the president, hitting a low that we have not seen, 36 percent overall approval rating amid overall concerns about his age, his mental acuity, these pervasive questions that have dogged this presidency.\par \par \uc2 The White House, though, is really just outright dismissing this polling, telling us that it's at odds with most polls on the approval rating, and they point to Democrat wins in the midterms while the president's average was lower than where it is now.\par \par \uc2 We're also getting a similar posture from the Biden campaign. They're telling FOX: "MAGA Republicans and their extreme agenda remain both dangerous and deeply unpopular and threaten our freedoms and the economic progress we have made under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . We will continue to focus on earning Americans' votes, addressing the issues they care most about, and again defying the conventional Beltway wisdom to win a second term."\par \par \uc2 But if team Biden is going to argue that he's out here addressing the issues that voters care most about, there really was not much evidence of that on his public schedule today. He took no questions on this week's big issues, like the looming debt limit crisis, the end of Title 42, even the weekend's mass shooting, after he announced a proposal coming later this year to compensate travelers for canceled or delayed flights.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Why is the president talking about meal vouchers for canceled flights and holding a movie night at the White House with all those things going on?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Because he's president. And there are multiple things that the president does. I think the airlines announcement that the president made is incredibly important to American families across the country. I hope you think so as well.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Is the president really focused on today's biggest issues when everyone in here is asking about Title 42, the debt ceiling, the mass shooting over the weekend, and we didn't hear the president talk about any of those things?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Regular American families, everyday families are thinking about Memorial Day weekend. They are. They are thinking about, how are they going to travel with their family, with their kids? They care about what the president said today. So that does matter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So the White House did not confirm reports that the president will talk to the Mexican president tomorrow ahead of Title 42 ending this week.\par \par \uc2 And from what we can tell, White House plans for this debt limit meeting may not really yield much progress. They have got a slated event for the president on Wednesday slamming Republicans on proposed cuts and how harmful they would be. So, if anything, that signals that maybe tomorrow does not yield a whole lot, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, not exactly an olive branch tossing there.\par \par \uc2 All right, thank you for that, Jacqui Heinrich, very much.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Francesca Chambers, USA Today White House correspondent.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk a little bit about that debt meeting tomorrow, Francesca. How much can we expect to get done?\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : Well, as Jacqui was just saying, Neil, the president has already announced that he will be giving that speech in New York on Wednesday, where he will tear into Republicans for the way that they would solve the debt ceiling crisis.\par \par \uc2 So that presupposes tomorrow's meeting and suggests that the White House doesn't expect much movement in these conversations. And the White House says that the president would be willing to have a separate conversation about spending talks.\par \par \uc2 But when I asked today when they would imagine that conversation beginning, they didn't have a firm answer. And, later, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , the White House press secretary, said that it would take place at some point in the future, without specifying when.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm just wondering, Francesca, if there's a middle ground here. Forget about what comes of these negotiations, but the proverbial punt.\par \par \uc2 In other words, push it back so that they can sort of regroup, think about this. Or is that even possible?\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: So what I have been hearing, Neil, from both Democrats and Republicans is that both sides are probably going to have to give a little to get a little on this.\par \par \uc2 But the question is, what is each side willing to give up in order to come to a compromise? So that's potentially one thing that we could learn after tomorrow's talks.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do the poll numbers -- I know they're fleeting, and, as you remind me, they're like snapshots. When snapped, they're shot.\par \par \uc2 But for the president right now, it's the first time he has trailed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by the degree he does, and now even \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . That must be lending to more frantic thoughts at the White House.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Well, another key number from that poll, Neil, was the economy in particular. Even though Americans seem to be feeling better about the economy than they obviously did during the pandemic, they would view Trump as having done better on the economy when he was president than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So that could potentially be a difficulty for President Biden if he does end up facing off against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the general election.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. How that economy is 18 months from now, anyone's guess.\par \par \uc2 Francesca, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Francesca Chambers following that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the push right now for a much minimum wage, Aishah Hasnie has more on that -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that's coming from Vermont Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, look, he's also pushing a 32-hour workweek. So, some folks out there on the left say this is just Bernie being Bernie. But we're going to dig into his comments made at the left and see what he's really saying here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  making some progressive ways and making that push for a higher minimum wage, in fact, a lot higher.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie has more on that on the fallout for the Democratic Party -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes, we're talking about $17 an hour. This is something that, of course, Democrats have often talked about, raising the minimum wage, but even Democrats have rejected a pretty significant hike in the past.\par \par \uc2 And, recently, \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  sort of took target at these Democrats when he issued an ominous warning. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): I don't think there's a state in the country where people do not believe we should raise the minimum wage. I would hope that every member of Congress understands that.\par \par \uc2 And there will be political consequences, as Mary Kay said, if they don't.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: There will be political consequences, he says, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So perhaps this moment from 2021 is seared in his memory. This is \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  giving a thumbs down and a curtsy as she joined seven other Democrats in rejecting this request to add a $15 wage hike to the American Rescue Plan. That was in 2021.\par \par \uc2 This $17 wage hike has little chance of getting through as well. So the question is, Neil, why is Sanders saying that he's going to push this as hard as he can?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL HARDAWAY, FORMER JEFFRIES SENIOR ADVISER: I really don't honestly think it's problematic for moderate Democrats, because, when it comes to a vote like this, people expect Bernie to do this sort of thing, but people don't expect people like Joe Manchin or others to actually vote for.\par \par \uc2 It's really about building energy on the left as we also move toward a presidential election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: We have reached out to Leader Schumer's office to see if he anticipates perhaps that bringing this bill up for a vote. Haven't heard back just yet -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Bob Cusack with us right now, the Hill editor in chief.\par \par \uc2 No surprise this is coming from \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . You could even argue, I guess, Bob, this could rally the progressive base a little bit. This is a good fight. But for the party as a whole, it's not deemed a good fight. How do you think all of this sort of sorts out?\par \par \uc2 BOB CUSACK, EDITOR IN CHIEF, THE HILL: Well, Neil, I mean, polls show it's popular, and the minimum wage hasn't been raised in 14 years.\par \par \uc2 But, at the same time, if you raise it significantly -- and this would be more than doubling it -- Sanders last proposal was $15. And the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2 , which is nonpartisan, said that would get people out of poverty, but, at the same time, lose more than a million jobs.\par \par \uc2 So that's something that small businesses are lobbying heavily on this. And, as you were just discussing, this is not going to move in a Republican House. But \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  is the chairman of the Budget Committee, so he's going to push this very hard.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Joe Manchin would not. I would imagine a number of other Democrats would not. \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2 , seeking reelection, would not. So where is this going?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Well, it's something that I think it's going to be on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, some Democrats, like \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , think this is a political winner, and then others don't. So I do think this is something that, if Democrats had control of everything again, this would be a top priority. And that's what they did in 2009. They had the House and Senate and the White House, and they raised it.\par \par \uc2 So I think this is going to be a battle against small business versus unions, who are pushing this hard.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you and I know -- I mean, you're quite right to say that the federal wage itself has not been raised formally by the \uc2 United States government\uc2  in over 14 years.\par \par \uc2 But we, of course, are quite familiar with so many businesses, fast-food, restaurant stores that have upped their wage considerably way beyond the federal level. So I'm wondering if it carries the same impact.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: I don't think so, especially after COVID.\par \par \uc2 And you know, Neil, this labor market. I mean, unemployment is at historical lows.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: And that would be the argument against it, is that you're going to raise unemployment by giving people more money, but then some people are going to lose their jobs.\par \par \uc2 So it's a balancing act. And the Democratic Party has fought over this. \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  did not see eye to eye on this back in the day. So this is one that's not going to go away. But there's no compromise here. \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  is not interested in compromising, and that's why, really, I mean, nothing's going to get done, certainly not in this Congress, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know these poll are fleeting and changing all the time, but this latest one, the ABC News one, showing the president not only with the low approval rating, or the lowest of his presidency, certainly, but the fact that he loses to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by seven points, loses to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  by five points, that is a first.\par \par \uc2 Now, that could change, but it must get panic setting it.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes, and it's an interesting dynamic here, Neil, because, publicly, many Democrats are getting behind \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , including AOC, including \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, at the same time, these poll numbers are really bad. And it's not going to be the same dynamic in 2024. You could have a third-party challenge if it's Biden versus Trump. It's not just: I'm not Trump.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Biden's going to run on his record. And the economy, most people think, is not strong, and many think we're either in a recession or headed for one. So this is troubling for the White House. And Democrats privately are very worried about these numbers.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are those Republicans taking on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , including potentially \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , the former governor, who has said of the former president that he's unelectable, that he can't win a general election -- now you have data like this that indicates well, right now, he can.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes. No, he can.\par \par \uc2 He's been winning throughout 2023. 2022 was a tough year for the president. The midterm was -- with losses by the Republicans, were not -- were blamed on him. So now he's having a better year, and he's beating the president right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Bob, always great catching up. Thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bob Cusack.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, China and the United States are talking. Don't get the idea that it's buddy-buddy, but we're talking.\par \par \uc2 We will tell you what happened -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, not exactly breaking bread, but, these days, it's not exactly breaking chops anymore, for a day, level, a high-level meeting, whatever you want to call it, between a new Chinese player, a top American player.\par \par \uc2 What to make of it all with the retired Lieutenant Colonel Bob Maginnis.\par \par \uc2 Bob, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. BOB MAGINNIS (RET.), \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2 : Well, we're looking for stabilization the Sino-U.S. relationship, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And, today, with Qin Gang, the foreign minister meeting with Ambassador Burns, it's a start. However, the conditions laid out by the foreign minister are pretty stark. He said, basically, we have to abandon any idea of a Taiwanese independence, and somehow we have to stop efforts to suppress and contain China.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Chinese are watching us, Neil. Two weeks ago, we had South Korean President Yoon here. Biden gave him a Washington declaration that basically says, we will bring nuclear submarines to your ports just to keep them from going nuclear.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Marcos from the Philippines was here, and we reiterated the 1951 common defense pact. And the Filipinos are giving us a host of military facilities in the northern part of Luzon, which, of course, faces China. And we're bringing Taiwanese soldiers to this country. We're sending trainers to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 And, oh, by the way, the $16 billion in military sales of equipment, we're going to fulfill to the Taiwanese. So, all around, China sees to the east all sorts of problems. Plus, President Biden is about to go to meet with Kishida in Japan and to his counterpart in Australia.\par \par \uc2 So the entire region is really ramping up its militarization, which has, of course, got Beijing's attention, and they're not sure that this is the way we ought to go.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, well, at least talks have started here, albeit brief.\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will watch it closely.\par \par \uc2 Colonel, good seeing you again. I apologize for the truncated time.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the legislative session is ending in Florida. Apparently, that means that the governor of Florida has time to do something else -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Florida's legislative session is close to ending right now. You know what that means. Could mean the earth is sort of cleared for a presidential run on the part of the governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Serrie has more -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has appealed to the GOP base in Florida with high-profile cultural issues, including his ongoing feud with \uc2 Disney\uc2 . But former President Trump's continuing popularity with this same group could pose challenges to a potential DeSantis run for the White House.\par \par \uc2 ABC News has obtained video of a 2018 mock interview in which DeSantis, then running for governor, is asked to explain his past disagreements with Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): And frame it in a way that's not going to piss off all his voters. So what I do is, I do what I think is right. I support his agenda, in terms of what he's been able to do.\par \par \uc2 If I have a disagreement, I talk to him in private.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: DeSantis' political team called the report -- quote -- "another swing and a miss from \uc2 Disney\uc2 -funded ABC. The best their propaganda machine could do with the 2.5 hours of leaked footage further proof that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is unwilling to be anyone but his authentic self, no matter the politics" -- end quote.\par \par \uc2 While discussions like this are common in debate prep, the video illustrates the importance DeSantis placed on not alienating Trump's supporters, a concern that would likely drive his campaign strategy if he indeed runs for president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AUBREY JEWETT, \uc2 CENTRAL FLORIDA UNIVERSITY\uc2 : They may look at the past and say people who have tried to attack Trump in Republican primaries usually don't come out well. And so maybe they decide that they have to finesse this a little bit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: In other words, talk about things like electability or effectiveness in getting your agenda passed without directly attacking the former president -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 Daron Shaw joins us right now from the FOX News Decision Desk.\par \par \uc2 Daron, I don't think there's anything startling here, but for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , the most startling development is this recent poll that shows that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is indeed electable over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , that could change, more so than \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . That would appear to be, at least for the time being, a bigger worry, wouldn't it?\par \par \uc2 DARON SHAW, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I think the ABC poll is not idiosyncratic in this regard, Neil. It's been kind of a consistent finding throughout the late winter and early spring that they run about the same in a head-to-head matchup with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I was thinking about this. Unlike a lot of past runs, where there is a clear divide between, say, a liberal and a moderate on the Democratic side or a conservative and a moderate on the Republican side, there's not a lot of ideological differentiation here.\par \par \uc2 So I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is just struggling with, exactly what is the rationale to vote for him over Trump? And unless there's compelling evidence that DeSantis is more electable, that's going to be a -- I think we all sort of think that at some level, but is that -- is there really empirical support for that?\par \par \uc2 And not a whole lot as of now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That could have an effect far and wide among just the other Republican candidates either in the race or considering a race. I was mentioning \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  early, the former New Jersey governor, who said \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can't be elected. He might get the nomination, but he can't be elected.\par \par \uc2 Here's a poll that says, yes, he can.\par \par \uc2 SHAW: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 I think there's a lot of sort of implicit reasoning behind these polls. You were speaking earlier about the Biden numbers with the age, and there's sort of this implied logic that if voters don't -- if voters think Biden is too old, they won't vote for him. There's this implied logic here that, because Trump had trouble in 2020 and 2022, that he's not as electable as some of these other Republicans.\par \par \uc2 But I'm curious, and I know you are too, to watch, are these things actually going to bear out? There's sort of two or three steps of logic here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAW: And it's not clear to me that one necessarily follows the other.\par \par \uc2 So the electability argument is a big argument, but until there's some polls out here showing a gap where Scott or Christie or somebody runs ahead of Biden by more than two or three points and Trump is trailing, then what's the empirical basis for the claim? It's just sort of speculation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. And to your point, one poll does not a whole new trajectory make.\par \par \uc2 But you then have to start thinking of how their posture changes in debates. I mean, in the past, in 2016, they learned almost to a man and woman that it was a mistake to take on Trump. I cannot imagine that they're not going to take him on in a series of debates, if he participates in them, this go-round, and it could get nasty.\par \par \uc2 SHAW: Yes, I think we have talked about this dynamic before. The trouble for DeSantis is, I think he would prefer someone else to directly take on Trump and sort of draw out those arguments.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAW: But, at some point, it's not clear to me that the others aren't going to train their fire on DeSantis, at which point...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, good point. Divide and conquer, right.\par \par \uc2 SHAW: ... when does DeSantis make a decision to really go after Trump?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 All right, Daron, great stuff. Wish we had more time. Unfortunately, we don't.\par \par \uc2 Daron Shaw of the FOX News Decision Desk, focusing on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, remember, that big powwow at the White House to try to deal with debt, even though it's unlikely they will come to any sort of agreement. But it is a start. What comes of it is anyone's guess. It's our big issue tomorrow, not only on FOX Business, but on FOX News.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, let's take you to "THE FIVE." Thank you.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230509ej5800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 DeSantis Too Focused on Woke Issues?; Trump Deposition Video Released in E. Jean Carroll Trial; Migrant Surge Expected; Interview With \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2  President Austan Goolsbee; Hot Jobs Report; \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 's Coronation Set For Tomorrow; Adele Celebrates Birthday\b0\par\par\uc2 Greg Palkot, Charlie Gasparino, Bryan Llenas, Griff Jenkins, Peter  Doocy, Ashley Dvorkin, Dion Baia, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 9,090 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Indeed, rumor has it that there's something much bigger than the monarchy going on this important day.\par \par \uc2 I'm done with my British accent.\par \par \uc2 It has nothing to do with the slightly less famous Brit celebrating a coronation tomorrow, but a certain other, much more consequential Brit, supposedly, and a global treasure, at that, celebrating a birthday today. That's right, today.\par \par \uc2 Did you think I forgot my saintly siren of song? How could I forget someone like -- you see, I do that all the time, incorporating songs. And if you don't know them, well, in the next hour, you're going to hear a ton of them. But it's happy birthday time for Adele, yes, Adele, the singer Adele, 35 years young today.\par \par \uc2 When she was born 35 years ago this day, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was still president. Maggie Thatcher was still prime minister. That's when "Beetlejuice" was on the big screen and roll-up jeans and high-tops were all the rage.\par \par \uc2 Little did the world know then that a pop sensation was about to take it by storm and capture the hearts of millions, including yours truly.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We broke it first on FOX Business, the dust-up with one Adele, only my favorite singer on the planet.\par \par \uc2 The bad news is, in the middle of October, we could run out of cash in this country. The good news is, about the same time, Adele will release a new song.\par \par \uc2 Look what it did to the markets today. They're calling it the Adele rally. OK, maybe they're not, but I am.\par \par \uc2 I did pick up the phone one time and she answered, hello? Hello? Hello?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, thank you, Adele.\par \par \uc2 Well, hello to you too, Adele.\par \par \uc2 He is a big story. Now, let me be clear, not like Adele suddenly quitting a promising singing career in her prime big story. She is in her own luscious league.\par \par \uc2 So are you a Taylor Swift fan? Well, who isn't, right? By the way, she's no Adele, but I digress. Willie Nelson, the Rolling Stones, the Beach Boys, but no Adele. Where's Adele?\par \par \uc2 The Adele thing, where do you stand on this? Can we utilize Adele in some sort of a peacemaker role here?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you worried that Adele is angry at you?\par \par \uc2 We didn't even get to talk about Adele.\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. TULSI GABBARD (D-HI): Adele.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Maybe next time, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Call Neil, Adele.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Call Neil. She's not. She's not, but I'm sure I'm just missing the call.\par \par \uc2 Hey, I'm in the control room. Has Adele called?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: She's not. OK, fine.\par \par \uc2 And this is Kevin Fitzgerald, my producer. I told him to use my name. Apparently, he did four times. Didn't work. She said: "Thanks for reaching. As it turns out, this isn't possible. Best, Benny."\par \par \uc2 That's fine, Benny. Just pass it along to Adele. I'm sure it'll be a different reaction.\par \par \uc2 Happy birthday, Adele. Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 This makes some people uncomfortable, maybe because I'm old enough to be her father or that I am happily married man almost 40 years. My wife has no idea on, though, so I'm just going full throttle.\par \par \uc2 But, in all seriousness, one of the things I just want to do, we live in an age where we're shouting and screaming at each other, people are cursing at each other. So why don't we celebrate a British invasion, of course, with the coronation of King Charles tomorrow, and, of course, celebrating a singing legend, an angel, if you will, named Adele today?\par \par \uc2 So you're going to have to bear with me here as we step back from all the nastiness in the world and just have fun with a singer who makes us have fun. And look what she did today. At the corner of Wall and Broad, Adele single-handedly ignited a stock market rally.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know there are those of you out there saying, Neil, this had nothing to do with Adele. This had to do with a strong jobs report and the fact that banking pressures eased. Blah, blah, blah, blah. This was all Adele turning 35 today.\par \par \uc2 So we're going to be covering this on all angles here, because I figured a lot of you at home were figuring we should cover this from all angles, even though not a one of you had advised me to cover this on all angles.\par \par \uc2 But my old buddy Dion Baia is back with me. And he is on top of these phone calls we're waiting for from Adele and her people to confirm not only that she's coming on the show, Dion, but that she just wants to talk to us.\par \par \uc2 But you know better than I.\par \par \uc2 DION BAIA, FOX NEWS AUDIO TECHNICIAN: Yes. We have a line open right now. We're waiting for the call. The line works.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIA: So, any time it comes in, and we're ready to add phones need be. In case I get a wrong number or a pizza order or something, we will be good to go to transfer her right to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, what we're waiting on, just to be clear, is her to get in here to acknowledge the fact that we obviously pointed out the significance of this day on FOX Business, which, in the past, she didn't know anything about it.\par \par \uc2 But I advised it. And now, of course, she gets FBN. So that's good news, right?\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Yes, absolutely. And if you don't get FOX Business, as we always say...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: ... you should demand it.\par \par \uc2 And she's demanded it. You have demanded it, and we have got it. And now we're all good to go. And I'm just waiting right here just in case, any second, this phone could ring, and it could make your day. Could make your weekend.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It could -- well, it could...\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Make your year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Our lives, both our...\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Make your marketing...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, our lives.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There are a lot of you, by the way, who are letting me know about the rally that had nothing to do with Adele. Whatever.\par \par \uc2 And there are a lot of other people saying, this obsession with Adele is a little over the top, Cavuto. And, by the way, nothing you say indicates that she really is the world's greatest singer.\par \par \uc2 She really is the world's greatest singer. So I don't know what your people are talking about.\par \par \uc2 But Charlie Lankston here, The Daily Mail femail editor. We have got Ashley Dvorkin, FOX News entertainment correspondent extraordinaire.\par \par \uc2 Ashley, I end it with you, begin with you, on how one woman could move the markets the way she did today. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY DVORKIN, FOX NEWS ENTERTAINMENT PRODUCER: I mean, it's Adele. It is a huge celebration.\par \par \uc2 And I am so happy to celebrate this day with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 DVORKIN: All those highlights you were playing from the show before, I feel like I was part of a few of those, but really big day...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You were.\par \par \uc2 DVORKIN: ... a birthday. Yes, a really big day, her birthday. And I feel like she's taking -- she probably has great plans, taking a break from Weekends With Adele, the Vegas residency, having a weekend for Adele.\par \par \uc2 So I'm happy to kick it off with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Charlie, I know everyone is focused on King Charles or whatever tomorrow, and that's great. But she is not performing, along with quite number of other British pop stars and musicians, for the king tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Lionel Richie is great. Love Lionel Richie. But what's going on with that?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE LANKSTON, FEMAIL EDITOR, DAILY MAIL: Well, we do understand that favorite human ever, Adele, was asked to be part of the coronation celebrations, but that she turned down the opportunity.\par \par \uc2 The same can be said of Elton John, \uc2 Ed Sheeran\uc2  and a number of British artists, including my favorite, the Spice Girls.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Why? Why did they all turn it down? Yes, they all turned it down. Why?\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: Well, I think right now, even though Charles has really done a great job in the first few months of his monarchy, far better than a lot of people thought he was going to, the royal family is a difficult brand to be aligned with right now.\par \par \uc2 There is so much scrutiny, speculation, controversy, that kind of overshadows the monarchy at every turn, because of Prince Andrew, because of Meghan and Harry. And I think all of these celebrities know that, no matter what they do, no matter how they perform, there is going to be some kind of negative connotation.\par \par \uc2 And people like Adele, \uc2 Ed Sheeran\uc2 , who's just finished a big court case, they just don't want to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 Ashley, when you look at this, and the entertainers here today, of course, to see big celebrities in concert, it's very expensive. Adele's been in Vegas, of course, booking out all these shows that could go into the weeks, months, dare I say years.\par \par \uc2 And I'm wondering if America has caught her fancy now and she doesn't want to go back.\par \par \uc2 DVORKIN: Well, yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 She extended her residency, and she's going back in June, June through November, so there's going to be a lot more dates for people to attend. And she announced that she's going to be doing a concert film from that residency. So, if you can't get there, you can watch it. You can still be part of all the excitement.\par \par \uc2 So I definitely think so, especially with the success of the Las Vegas show.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm curious too, Charlie. Obviously, you -- Britain has turned out some of the greatest musicians of all time. And it seems that every generation knows one.\par \par \uc2 And I joke here about whether Adele is the latest example of that or should we go -- can go on. But they're focused on the king tomorrow. And, that, I get. It's his coronation. But if you think about it, he's starting his career in his 70s. Adele was an international rock star at 19.\par \par \uc2 Do you think the fact that she's not appearing is because he's simply jealous?\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: Oh, I don't think that at all. I think the truth of the matter is, Charles...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you do. You took a while to answer.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, you took a while to answer. I think you do think that, but go ahead.\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: Look, do I think that Charles has the same singing talents as Adele? Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 But I think the reality here is that Charles has been waiting for this moment for his entire life, and nothing and nobody, even Adele, is going to stop him from enjoying every aspect of this moment. So, he's not sitting there worrying about who's going to perform at his concert.\par \par \uc2 What he is worrying about is the fact that tomorrow needs to go off without a hitch, without drama, without Harry causing a scene. That's what he's focusing on. And then, when he gets home, he can put on some Adele and maybe have a little bit of a happy cry over how well it went.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 Ashley, Adele is now 35. She could run for president in this country. There's that minor detail that she wasn't born here. I'm working on changing that, but that's a separate matter.\par \par \uc2 But I'm wondering. We think of her as a kid starting out. We think of a lot of the musicians today as kids starting out. And I'm wondering, to get back to what we were saying a little bit earlier about so many big names not performing for the king tomorrow, whether we're overreading that, or that it's a whole different crowd now with this king versus his mother.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 DVORKIN: Yes, I think it is. It's a different audience. It's a different generation of artists.\par \par \uc2 And I think, especially for them, as we were saying before, maybe just not taking part avoids the whole situation in case they were concerned about image or what -- if it does make a statement one way or the other, and also being so busy.\par \par \uc2 I mean, everybody can say scheduling conflicts for the most part, world tours, residency, a slight break. So a lot of them are also very busy with their own schedules, and maybe that's just an easier way to go about it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I think you're right about that. Maybe we're overreading all of this.\par \par \uc2 But I'd like maybe to get a final take from you, Charlie, on what we can look forward to. I know the birthday is almost running out here in the United States for Adele, but, of course, all eyes turn to the coronation tomorrow. You think it all goes smoothly?\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: I really do.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's going to be such a special and historic occasion. Neil, you and I have talked in the past about that amazing pomp and ceremony, which is what...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: ... the U.K. does so well, what the monarchy does so well.\par \par \uc2 And you can expect to see all of that tomorrow and through the weekend. There's going to be history. There's going to be tradition. Charles is going to be dressed in his finery. I think it really is going to be an event for the ages. And it's one that most people will only get the chance to enjoy in their lifetime on this one occasion.\par \par \uc2 So everyone really should enjoy that, embrace that. Even if Adele won't be there, it will still be something to remember forever.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, it's going to be grand. I mean, it is almost as grand as Adele's Vegas show, but we will see. That's tough competition.\par \par \uc2 Ladies, thank you both very, very much.\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm getting pelted with a lot of e-mails here, where a lot of people are saying, Neil, you can't be serious. You are comparing this to the royal coronation and that this could be even more significant?\par \par \uc2 Yes, I am. Yes I am. If you have any doubts about that, you can go ahead and catch her in concert or listen to Dion waiting at the phone for her to call in, which we're probably going to hear very soon, Dion.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Well, you have added a second phone because she is -- has a residency in Vegas. And we'd forgotten, or at least I had forgotten that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIA: So we have added a second line just in case. So we are all ready for her to call in and chat with you about her birthday and everything going on across the pond.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm a little disquieted by the fact that the phone has not rung at all. I mean...\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Well, you know, it's a very private line.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: And I have gotten some wrong numbers for, like, different kind of takeout places, but, at the moment, I'm trying to just feed through that...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: ... and tell people, you can't get a pizza here. You can't get any Chinese food or whatever.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure. Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: So, aside from that, we have someone now screening the calls, so, as soon as these darn things ring, I'm going to be ready.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So she would answer like, hello, right?\par \par \uc2 (PHONE RINGING)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, OK, that could be. That could -- keep us posted. Keep us posted.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Hello? Hey. No. No, you can't get a Bakewell tart here. No.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: All right. Sorry. Wrong number.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Did not work out exactly...\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Sorry about that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, no, please, please, please, please.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Sorry. At least we know they work.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Please.\par \par \uc2 All right, well, maybe, from rolling in the deep to stocks just rolling in the green, a lot of you were shocked to see, all right, why were we up so much? Could have been that jobs report. Could have been the fact that people are saying we might have a steady stream here with the Fed holding at bay.\par \par \uc2 Or why don't we just call it obvious? It's Adele's birthday. Markets are celebrating worldwide.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that big jobs report we were telling about, 253,000 jobs added in the latest month.\par \par \uc2 And this continues the spate of activity that's surprising some folks and led to, obviously, a big rally at the corner of Wall and Broad. Would not have been possible, as you know, without Adele. But that's a separate matter.\par \par \uc2 To Peter Doocy on how the administration is responding to all of this -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And, Neil, there's been a lot of bad headlines this White House has had to deal with over the last few weeks. So with this jobs report, they are trying to shine a light on what they see as a good one.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Inflation is now down 40 percent since last summer. It's come down the last nine months in a row. We obviously have more work to do, but we're trending in the right direction, and I think we're making real progress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden and Vice President Harris spent part of Cinco de Mayo buying tacos and churros just after he boosted the new White House line about the debt limit, that China and Russia will use a default to their advantage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You know, this is becoming an issue in other countries. What's the United States going to do? Are they really fooling around with not paying their debts?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And then there's this.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, President Biden tried to skirt shouted questions by promising to take more time with reporters later on today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And so I think we have got a lot of work to do. I'm -- and I'm doing a major press conference this afternoon. So I love you all, but I'd like to ask you to leave, so we can get down to business.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Well, here we are. It is this afternoon, no major press conference, no minor press conference either. So, a quiet week for the president is going to end with no Q&A -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for that, Peter.\par \par \uc2 To Austan Goolsbee right now.\par \par \uc2 I haven't had a chance to congratulate him on this. He is now the president of the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2 . He's a voting member, as such, of the Federal Open Market Committee, the folks that determine interest rates, and the folks who have just raised interest rates for the 10th time in this cycle to around the 5, 5.25 percent neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 Austan, very good to have you. And belated congratulations.\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE, PRESIDENT, FEDERAL RESERVE BANK OF CHICAGO: Yes, old friend, thank you for having me back.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, it's good.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, Adele sends along her congratulations, but I digress.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'd be curious. I mean, were you among those members who said, yes, raise it another quarter-point?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I was. It was a unanimous decision.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: So, I was in that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You get a good jobs report like this -- and there's no other way you can look at this jobs report. I know they talk about the revisions of the prior months. Fact of the matter is, it was a stunningly good jobs report.\par \par \uc2 So, now the question is, is it so good, it puts pressure on you and your colleagues to keep that interest rate spigot moving?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I mean, a little. That's partly -- as you know, before the banking stresses began, the economy was looking quite hot.\par \par \uc2 And inflation is above where we want it to be. It hasn't come down as fast as we wanted. And the job market has been by far the strongest part of the economy. So you got to take that into account. At the same time, we know that credit conditions like the ones we're seeing now in the past have been correlated with recessions, credit crunches, kind of have done the tightening work of monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 So, in our phrase, as you know, data dependence, we got to be data- dependent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: We got to watch a lot of indicators, not just the unemployment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Because I have already seen -- I don't know how much stock you put in things, because you guys decide it, not the markets.\par \par \uc2 There seems to be a higher expectation now that we will see a hike in June. Too premature to say that?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I think way too premature to say that.\par \par \uc2 As you know, Neil, even before I was in this job, I was not a fan of the Fed paying that close attention or primary attention to what the stock market says.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I think the real economy is what we all got to be watching, and it's way too premature to know what to do with monetary policy, especially because we're up, we're down on this credit, the issue of the -- of how our credit condition is going to be and what's going to be the fate of our regional banks in the immediate term.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Those are going to matter a lot.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's interesting, because that does seem to change the equation a bit here, what's going on with these regional banks and \uc2 First Republic\uc2  and all these others, the mergers and everything on PacWest.\par \par \uc2 A good day, then it's a bad day, then it's a questionable day. With a backdrop like that, does that give you pause on raising rates, that you could make that situation worse?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes, well, it has to give you some pause.\par \par \uc2 I gave a speech before the -- before this last FOMC meeting where I said, even if you don't think that we should use the interest rate as a way to fight off financial crisis, which I don't think you should -- I think you should use your prudential and regulatory tools to do it -- things like that, even if they don't spiral into a crisis, they are correlated with banks raising their lending standards...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: ... as they're trying to raise more capital. There's just going to be less to go around for business expansion.\par \par \uc2 And that's likely to slow the economy. And, for sure, you should think, take that into account. And so that's what we -- we all got to keep an eye on that part of credit conditions, because, as I view it, that's kind of doing the work of monetary policy. If you feel like the economy still very hot -- the unemployment rate is 3.4 percent -- the -- whatever tightening that you're going to need to do, it's got to be taking into account of what -- the banking system's impact.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me ask you a little bit about the debt ceiling debate back and forth.\par \par \uc2 We're told -- Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  has said that June 1 is sort of like the cutoff period. At that point, we don't have any more money under the couch cushions and we could be on the brink. Do you agree with that, that June 1 is the time by which this has got to be settled?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I don't know the exact dates.\par \par \uc2 I know what's -- this is the -- the revenue that comes in is very lumpy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: April 15, a bunch of revenue comes in. June 15, supposed to be revenue come in. So there is a balancing act about what the exact date is.\par \par \uc2 Rather than get into that, I would just say, look, is the banking stress and crisis not enough? Like, do we really have to do this? Chair Powell said it best when he said, look, there's no -- he's not going to get involved in negotiation. They have to raise the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: You can't -- we can't default on Treasuries. That's the safest asset in the world. I mean, that would be a mess.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I can remember, Austan, there were times -- I think we were talking then -- about in 2011, when you were with Barack Obama.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, of course, we had our debt downgraded. We avoided a default, but it was all the craziness prior that prompted that slash in our AAA credit rating.\par \par \uc2 Some are saying the same thing could be poised to happen now. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Well, I don't know if it was the crisis or if it was the fact that I left the government, but two hours after I left the government was when we got downgraded.\par \par \uc2 As you look now, this -- to not be flip about it, this is a nightmare. This is -- do we really want to pay more in higher interest costs and have financial crises or big recessions or cast doubt on the full faith and credit of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 ? I don't know how they're going to sort it out. That's to be -- Congress and the president, whatever, they just need to sort it out.\par \par \uc2 Like, we -- this is not something that we can play games with. What is going to happen?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But we have played games with it again and again.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I guess what I'm asking here is, what's a little different now is Kevin McCarthy and Republicans trying to push an immediate quid pro quo. We don't touch the debt ceiling or raise it until we have got some spending cuts.\par \par \uc2 Now, in the 2011 experience, if memory serves me right, we got some budgetary commitments, promises to address these issues, but not tied immediately to the debt ceiling hike. So that's a big difference. I'm just wondering how you see it settled.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I don't -- you know far more about the politics, Neil, than I do.\par \par \uc2 And the Fed is not a political position. So, however they sort it out, whether -- whatever the negotiations, whatever the details, they just need to do it before we're going to default on Treasuries. Because, what, are we going to pay the -- the military has got to be paid.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: The veterans benefits, Medicare, Social Security.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it really does not make sense to do this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, you're right. You hope cooler heads on both sides prevail.\par \par \uc2 But, Austan, while I have you, I just want to get your thoughts. I know, with all the banking stuff going on, you can't address individual banks, I get that. But the irony of this situation is that bigger banks seem to be getting bigger.\par \par \uc2 Could you have envisioned at the time of the meltdown and coming out about institutions considered too big to fail, that now we have a \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  that now accounts for about one out of five deposits in this country? And I just wonder whether that's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes, well, look, there is a cruel irony to that.\par \par \uc2 There is another side of it, though, which is you will remember, when we talked, it was 2007-2008-2009, as we went into the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Our fear was that the biggest banks are the ones that everyone's afraid of and the bailouts and the rescues, so that they don't collapse.\par \par \uc2 It is a little bit of testament, I think, to the re-regulation of the financial sector that we had these biggest financial institutions build up their capital. They got some of the riskiest activities out of the biggest banks, so that now it's not just an automatic financial crisis when financial institutions start having trouble.\par \par \uc2 There is money going from...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, boy, if you have a stock market freefall -- if you have a stock market -- I'm sorry to bump on that, Austan.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: No, it's fine.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But if you have a stock market freefall, that's the capital for a lot of these guys too.\par \par \uc2 So when that starts swooning, it's sort of like everyone heading to the exits at the same time, right?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 Yes, look, these issues of too big to fail, but also of just bank concentration in general, they have been with us for a long time. There's been a trend for 30, 40 years trending against the smaller and the regional banks. But issues like deposit insurance and how they're going to be treated, that's got to -- Congress has got to sort that out.\par \par \uc2 The Fed, for sure, cannot make decisions like that on its own. It's the American people and the policymakers that got to decide that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Finally -- I said that that was the last question. I lied, but not intentionally, Austan.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you're coming to this position the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , very influential position, as someone who is more sensitive to the knee-jerk reaction, jobs go up, rates go, that you are going to have a different point of view about how you go about your job.\par \par \uc2 Is that true?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Well, I take that as a compliment. That's what I aspire to. There are some people who view that as an insult, but I'm going to take it as a compliment.\par \par \uc2 I believe that the Federal Open Market Committee is, in this century, the world's greatest deliberative body. And it is a committee. And there are people with a lot of different views.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: And I come to it from a different perspective.\par \par \uc2 But I have been really impressed. The seriousness with which everybody takes their job is exemplary.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you should be proud of that.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations again, Austan Goolsbee, the president of the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Want to take you right now to the border. Griff Jenkins is there.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, we have had thousands of migrants continuing to stream across the border here in Brownsville.\par \par \uc2 And Secretary Mayorkas just visited where I am standing. We got an exclusive interview with the Border Patrol chief, Raul Ortiz.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, this is what happens when you try to take Ukrainians' flag away. A Russian diplomat learning the hard way, getting punched in the face. This new video of yesterday's scuffle at a summit in Turkey just coming in.\par \par \uc2 Back in 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just days away from Title 42 essentially expiring, with little hope that it can be resurrected or extended.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from Griff Jenkins at Brownsville, Texas -- Griff.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Hey. Good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And you can hear a loud bus passing behind me. They're transporting migrants out. And we have seen more than 2,000 migrants every single day I have been here, Neil, exactly at ground zero in Brownsville, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Let me take you up to our sky drone and show you Camp Monument as we have been covering it here. You can see the buses coming and the migrants lining up upon the levee. And we had secretary of DHS, Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas, along with the Border Patrol chief here, just visiting to see these operations ahead of Title 42.\par \par \uc2 But here's the thing. When we talk about a calm and preparation before the storm, the storm is already coming here. We have had more than 8,000 migrants every single day border-wide across the U.S. And that is really the situation we were in last year when we were breaking records.\par \par \uc2 I want to show you, Neil, some exclusive footage we shot with our FOX Flight Team early this morning before the sun was up. You can see the thermal imaging. On the bottom there is the U.S., the groups of migrants highlighted with the heat signatures on the other side, Matamoros.\par \par \uc2 They're coming in large groups of two or 300 all day, every day, 24/7. And this is the with Title 42 in place. Now, as I mentioned, we had Secretary Mayorkas here with Chief Ortiz, and he doubled down, Mayorkas, saying that, once Title 42 lifts, under this Title 8, which is the mechanism, they're going to expel people who do not qualify for relief under asylum, saying that the consequences were going to be -- quote -- "more severe" than anything we have seen before.\par \par \uc2 So we will find out with time whether or not the words match the actions.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we got an exclusive interview, Neil, with Chief Ortiz, and I asked him a little bit about what we can expect to see next Thursday when Title 42 lifts. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAUL ORTIZ, U.S. BORDER PATROL CHIEF: I fully expect us to see, come May 11, 9,000 or 10,000 apprehensions a day. That's not something that we're not prepared for.\par \par \uc2 If you get upwards of that, it really starts to stress the resources that we have available to us. But I do -- I am confident that the men and women out here are doing everything they can to prepare for those increases in traffic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And the men and women in green, the Border Patrol agents, are exhausted and tired, Neil, after two years of this border crisis, but they are ready and motivated to deal with what comes.\par \par \uc2 Now, Chief Ortiz also talked to me about what's happening in Mexico. We know there are tens of thousands of migrants in the pipeline making their way up from Mexico's Southern border to our Southern border. Only time will tell how bad this is going to get -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bryan (sic), thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 We will be updating you on what's happening at the border and the fear that's now materializing affecting it every day tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. on our weekend show.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, want to update you on a judge releasing video from former President Trump's deposition in that rape case brought on by writer E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Llenas has more in New York -- Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, look, this is the first time we're seeing video of former President Trump's deposition from back in October. For more than five hours, Trump was questioned at Mar-a-Lago by attorneys representing E. Jean Carroll, the magazine columnist.\par \par \uc2 Now, she accuses the former president of raping her in a dressing room of a New York City department store in the mid-'90 s. And Carroll is suing Trump for defamation and battery in a civil case. The nine-member jury watched 47 minutes of this video deposition this week.\par \par \uc2 And in this deposition, Trump dismisses Carroll as a -- quote -- "whack job" and flatly denies the allegations.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It didn't happen. And, by the way, if it did happen, it would have been reported within minutes. That's a very busy store, by the way, and checkout counters and everything else, and I would be in there.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's the most ridiculous -- it's the most ridiculous, disgusting story. It was just made up. But I know nothing about her. I think she's sick, mentally sick.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Now, despite continuing to say Carroll is -- quote -- "not my type" and that he would -- quote -- "never be attracted to her," during the deposition, Trump is shown a photo and mistakes Carroll for his ex-wife, Marla Maples.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: You say Marla's in this photo?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's Marla, yes. That's -- that's my wife.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Which woman are you pointing to?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Here.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: That's Carroll.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Oh, is that...\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: The person you just pointed was E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Oh, I see. Who is that? Who is this?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Your wife.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: And the person -- the woman on the right is your then-wife, Ivana?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't know. This was the picture. I assume that's John Johnson. Is that...\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: That's Carroll.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: ... Carroll? Because it's very blurry.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Now, Trump will not take the stand in his defense.\par \par \uc2 Carroll's lawyers, Neil, have called 11 witnesses, including two other women who accused the former president of forcibly touching them in unrelated incidents.\par \par \uc2 Now, closing arguments are set to begin Monday -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bryan Llenas, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, a billionaire, usually Republican backer, named \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2 , who is very impressed with one Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , but says he's spending way too much time talking about things that are woke and not nearly enough time talking about Americans who are broke.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): What happens in the future? Look, people will -- we will get on that relatively soon. I mean, there's just -- you either got to put up or shut up on that as well, so we will see.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, everyone keeping an eye on Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . It's just a matter of when, not if he is going to run for the White House, we're told.\par \par \uc2 But Republican megadonor \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  says you got to change what you're doing if you want to get a chance. I'm paraphrasing here. But Thiel is worried that the governor has spent way too much time talking about things that are woke and not the economic issues that are the bread-and-butter ones that can win or lose elections.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino has a far better take on all of that than I do. He joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, what's going on here?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: My far better take is only because I speak to a lot of DeSantis donors, people that meet with him, who meet with him, and here's what they're telling me.\par \par \uc2 He's a lot more explicit about running in these private conversations. He's telling people he's planning to run, that he's putting together a team. Obviously, he leaves himself a little wiggle room when he says planning, because you can always change plans, right? So that's one aspect.\par \par \uc2 The other aspect is his message. And I think this is not a guy that has been doing this for a long time, right? He's been running on a state level. What they're recommending, and they're recommending this both in private and just broadly in public, is that he diversify his message.\par \par \uc2 It's been a lot of \uc2 Disney\uc2 , which isn't so bad, but you kind of played that card already. He ventured into the abortion debate, which a lot of Republican consultants say you didn't have to go there, and limit abortion even further from where it is in -- at Florida right now. Just you're not going to get that many votes from it.\par \par \uc2 The Trump people are not going to 