1,500 troops will come down here and assist in a transport and administrative role, because, when you have these surges, logistics are a big part of trying to get it under control.\par \par \uc2 Let me take you to our sky drone, though, and show you where we are. This is Brownsville, ground zero for the migrant surge happening right now. What you're looking at, Neil, is Camp Monument. That's what Border Patrol is calling it. The blue and white tent is where migrants wait. And then along this road here to the left side of your screen, the buses pull down and they get the migrants, which are coming in droves.\par \par \uc2 More than 2,100 migrants were in this camp right, where I'm standing in the last 24 hours. And, usually, they have 200 or 300 at most in that area. We have also been down on the river, Neil. Let me show you some video now. You can see migrants.\par \par \uc2 About 90 percent of them, by the way, are Venezuelan. With the split screen, you can see the Texas DPS officers, the Texas National Guard, Border Patrol trying to deal with. On the other side, you're looking at Matamoros and women, children getting on these rafts, crossing dangerous currents to get there.\par \par \uc2 And they are coming in unprecedented numbers here. But yet, at the same time, we have got the White House press secretary, Jean-Pierre, yesterday saying illegal immigration was down 90 percent, then doubling down, even accusing our own Peter Doocy of being dramatic in his questioning. Here's what she said. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I'm going to answer. So I was speaking to the parolee program. As you know, the president put in place a parolee program to deal with -- to deal with certain countries on ways that we can limit illegal migration.\par \par \uc2 And we have seen, the data has shown us that it has gone down by more than 90 percent. That was what I was speaking to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: I don't know, for the life of me, Neil, what data she was looking at, but let me show you CBP sources exclusively sharing with FOX News the fiscal year to date of people who have illegally crossed our border and are illegally in the country and encountering our Border Patrol officials.\par \par \uc2 It's up 136,000 this fiscal year compared to last fiscal year. Last fiscal year was the highest ever on record, with more than 2.2 million on the year. Now, back out here, I just want to show you that drone shot one more time. This is the levee that goes down to the river. This is a hot area where they think they're going to have this upwards of 10,000 or more migrants coming across in nine days, when Title 42 lifts.\par \par \uc2 But make no mistake, so that we're very clear about the definition of what the border officials say is an illegal crossing and illegal immigration, it is when you come across, particularly between ports of entry, without authorization and land on our shores. At that point, we have got to deal with you.\par \par \uc2 Just in the last hour, I saw on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  a tweet from CBP directly, Neil, that said, if you are coming illegally, you cannot stay, and that most people who apply for asylum will not be successful. So they're sending the message, but, apparently, the message isn't understood at the White House and the consequences don't match the words.\par \par \uc2 So the border officials here don't believe anything's going to change as we get closer and closer to that May 11 date, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for that, Griff.\par \par \uc2 And, again, let me emphasize something that Griff just pointed out. We got our numbers from the U.S. Border Patrol. Of course, they answer to the secretary of homeland security. And the fact of the matter that, over the last 10 days, we have seen 73,000 migrants cross the Southern border. That is the last 10 days.\par \par \uc2 But extrapolating on this, we have seen more than 22,000 people apprehended in just the last 72 hours. We can take that down, guys. This is just coming in now. And we have had better than 16,895 so-called get-aways or got- aways.\par \par \uc2 So that really puts this in dramatic perspective. And these are numbers coming in just the last few minutes. And, after Title 42 ends, some fear that we could be seeing upwards of 13,000 illegal immigrants trying to cross the border per day, per day.\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy at the White House on how the administration is responding apparently, we're told, with upwards of 1,500 troops for that day.\par \par \uc2 Peter, what's the latest?\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Neil, some of the 1,500 troops on this 90-day-long deployment are going to start arriving as soon as next Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 And the administration is starting to explain how exactly this advances a promised more humane immigration system.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Trump ordered 5,200 U.S. troops to fortify the border. At the time, Democrats and former military officials and officers came out against that, saying they were being used for political reasons.\par \par \uc2 How is this different?\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Yes, so, really, my focus here is on talking about what we have been asked to do and what we're doing.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, DHS felt that there was a need for the Department of Defense to assist.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The Pentagon is saying the troops are going to be armed for self- defense, not for law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 But this order does seem to conflict with Secretary Mayorkas' testimony last month that the border is secure.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: If the border is secure, as the administration has said, then why would we need to send 1,500 active-duty U.S. troops down there?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Because we need more work. We need -- more work needs to be done. You have heard from DHS, you have heard from the State Department on what we're putting in place to deal with -- to deal with the border once Title 42 lifts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Giving an order to send active-duty troops anywhere for any kind of a mission is a big deal. Nothing from President Biden about this today. He has not been seen at any public events. He's not been seen on camera, period.\par \par \uc2 And the White House told us they had a lid for the day at 2:22 -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A lid for the day, just to explain to folks back home or those who won't -- don't follow this nearly as close as you, to mean he's done, right?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That means that he is done. We -- the press pool that has been assigned to come here and take video or take still pictures of him or try to ask him questions can stand down until tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I'm going to incorporate a lid on my day here. We will see how that goes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Although it might be forced on me.\par \par \uc2 Peter, always good seeing you. Thank you, my friend, at the White House.\par \par \uc2 That's pretty good. It's a nice lid for the day.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Jon Anfinsen right now. He is the National Border Patrol Council vice president. What he makes of all of this.\par \par \uc2 Jon, when I look at some of the numbers -- and you know these far better than do I -- but they're expecting quite the surge once Title 42 goes away in nine days, even these figures of up to 13,000 a day who could be flooding the border. What are you looking at?\par \par \uc2 JON ANFINSEN, VICE PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: Frankly, we're trying to figure out how we're going to get through this.\par \par \uc2 Last month, we were averaging something like maybe 6,000 a day. But, like you mentioned, just in the past 10 days that's jumped, 7,200 or so a day, 7,300. And we're already basically on par with our worst months last year.\par \par \uc2 So we have always been afraid that, when Title 42 goes away, we're going to see repeats of that Haitian migrant camp that we had in Del Rio. And like Griff was showing down there in Brownsville, I mean, that's sort of the beginning of this.\par \par \uc2 So, we really don't have any solution from DHS, other than to just process more, I suppose. But agents are frustrated. And we're right back where we were at our worst last year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I wonder, Jon, these 1,500 troops, we already know from Mexico that they weren't too keen in the idea of our sending troops down to deal with all the drug and related issues at the border.\par \par \uc2 They probably won't be greeted any more favorably with this news to deal with this surge. Do you anticipate problems?\par \par \uc2 ANFINSEN: No worse than it's already been.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANFINSEN: The government of Mexico already is just not cooperating.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, we will take whatever help we can get, but I don't know that it's going to change a whole lot, because I don't know how much worse it could get in terms of government -- Mexico's help. It's just not making a difference.\par \par \uc2 And while now these numbers are starting to skyrocket, we're still -- on the side, we're still dealing with people running through the brush, getting away from us, pursuits, bailouts. We just had a bailout into a school campus in Brackettville, Texas, just this afternoon. It's just going to keep happening.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So the troops that will come down, how will they assist or work with your guys?\par \par \uc2 ANFINSEN: It's different every time they come down. A lot of it has to do with logistics. Sometimes, they're there just to sort of maintain a presence in a particular area to maybe deter people from crossing there.\par \par \uc2 Other times, they have been posted along the river to help with rescues, so they will help out wherever they can. But it's going to change. It's going to basically be different from sector to sector. But we will see. Every pair of hands that we can get is an improvement.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, in the meantime, have we got any updates on this Mexican fugitive behind these five murders? Apparently, he had been deported and re-deported and deported after that at least four times.\par \par \uc2 Do we know how that happens or how it keeps happening?\par \par \uc2 ANFINSEN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, this is an unfortunate side effect of just not having enough Border Patrol agents on the ground and the technology to be able to detect them. People will get discovered maybe crossing the border or somewhere in the interior, and they get deported, and then they try again and again and again until they get in.\par \par \uc2 So, who knows when he finally made it across again. But this is what happens. They cross illegally, especially the people that have this type of criminal history or this deportation history, and they're going somewhere in the interior of the United States, and we don't know about them until something bad happens.\par \par \uc2 So, this is the frustrating part for agents, because we see who we do arrest and the criminal histories of some of them, so we know more is getting away. And this guy is a perfect example of that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is it fair to say he's back in Mexico or hiding still on this side of the border?\par \par \uc2 ANFINSEN: I would -- I'd be very surprised if he tried to stay over here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANFINSEN: But, at this point, he's -- maybe he's trying to go back to Mexico to blend in.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jon, thank you very much. Sorry you have to deal with all of this, but it's going to get worse, as you said, and you're going to be busier, if that's remotely possible, Jon.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, draw your attention to the corner wall of Broad. We had a sell-off today. I know 367 is a lot of points. It's a little bit more than a percent, but it had been a whole lot worse, and all on bank fears again. Remember when we rescued \uc2 First Republic\uc2 , or at least threw it into the arms of \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Well, that only worked so long, amid fears that other banks are in trouble as well, or could need help again as well. And it's conjuring up images of 2008-2009 -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Boy, it just drags on and on and on.\par \par \uc2 I'm talking about banks. And just when we think we have come over the worst of it, and we had our second biggest rescue just yesterday, along come fears that there are other regional banks in trouble, PacWest the mentioned named today, but there could be others vulnerable to depositors bolting and trying to protect their money in bigger money center banks.\par \par \uc2 Add to that concerns right now that the debt ceiling isn't getting any closer or at least closer to getting resolved. More on that later with Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, for now, the anxiety was palpable at the corner of Wall and Broad. And we saw it up close and personal. Let's just say it was a lot uglier earlier.\par \par \uc2 Connell McShane has more -- Connell.\par \par \uc2 CONNELL MCSHANE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it was worse earlier, but you could feel that sense of nervousness or anxiety kind of coming back to Wall Street today after what was really, at the end of it, just kind of a one-day sigh of relief after \uc2 First Republic\uc2  was purchased by \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Today, we saw the selling resume, especially for those regional bank stocks. In fact, we just found out moments ago that the Senate Banking Committee is going to have a hearing on all the banking issues on Thursday of this week in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And those worries have been combined with the looming Fed decision on interest rates and more signs that just the economy slowing down, including the new data that came out showing job openings falling to the lowest level in almost two years.\par \par \uc2 Now, you mentioned PacWest. Yes, that did lead the sell-off today alongside Western Alliance, which is another regional bank, another regional lender. And that is seemingly a sign that investors are looking at this as, what's next? Which bank might fail next?\par \par \uc2 What's worth noting, though, about both of these banks, PacWest and Western Alliance, both of them recently had posted relatively strong earnings, which you might think would calm investor concerns. But, clearly, with those stock price declines, that was not the case today.\par \par \uc2 Now, the other issue hovering over this market, indeed, is that debt ceiling, Speaker McCarthy, President Biden, everybody in Washington, and the discussions or lack of discussions over whether to raise the debt ceiling. The cost of ensuring government debt, that spiked higher today on concerns the Treasury could be running out of cash as soon as next month.\par \par \uc2 So, that's there. And the backdrop for all of this is the interest rate environment with \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and still higher interest rates expected. Chairman Powell possibly will raise rates again tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But then you're hearing more and more from investors there are real questions about whether the Fed maybe should pause after that. There's also the expectation the chairman would comment on all this banking turmoil at what will be a closely watched news conference, Neil, tomorrow afternoon, and then again a Banking Committee hearing on all these issues set for Thursday -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it, my friend. I appreciate that, Connell McShane.\par \par \uc2 So, believe it or not, we have shifted in just the last 24 hours, if you will, worrying about inflation and just how long the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  can keep raising interest rates -- tomorrow, they meet, as Connell pointed out -- to growing concerns maybe we're slipping into a recession or things are dramatically slowing down.\par \par \uc2 The latest jobs figures show a dramatic decline to 9.59. These are the so- called job openings, 9.59 million. Now, we would envy that a little more than a couple of years ago. But the fact of the matter is, it's the third down month in a row, and it's well off our highs of close to 12.7 million, little more than, as I say, 2.5 years ago.\par \par \uc2 So what does that portend for the economy, and are businesses really beginning to tighten up in this environment?\par \par \uc2 Alan Knuckman is with us, Bullseye Option chief market strategist, Gary Kaltbaum, Kaltbaum Capital Management.\par \par \uc2 Let me get your sense, Alan, as just a trader looking at this. People are now all of a sudden saying, we have got a real slowdown to worry about. And the Fed and whatever it does tomorrow, I assume a quarter-point hike, that isn't even the issue anymore, concern that it's not only succeeded in doing what it wanted to do, but it could be overdoing it. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 ALAN KNUCKMAN, MARKET STRATEGIST: Well, I do like you putting this in perspective.\par \par \uc2 And perspective tells us that, the last time we talked about this -- and, the last time I said, this is not 2009 all over again. This is an isolated incident. We're 5 percent above where the market was at that particular time. So the market is not that concerned and actually moving positively since our first bout.\par \par \uc2 Now, these are banks that broke themselves. And it's also a technology story, because people have the ability to move their money very quickly at any time. And the big boys, if you looked at their earnings that came out a couple of weeks ago, they made billions and billions. And they have actually added billions and billions of assets.\par \par \uc2 This just happened to be someone chose not to hedge themselves properly. And that's not a hard thing to do if you're a bank, if you want to do it. But \uc2 SVB\uc2  chose not to, and they suffered because of that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm just wondering if there are other shoes to drop in that banking arena then, Gary, because, to me, it looks like slow-moving train wreck.\par \par \uc2 It's certainly nothing at the speed and just the awe-inspiring kind of drop-off we had during the meltdown, very different. But this just goes on and on. It's like a drip, drip sort of torture. Like, which bank will be next? Whose depositors will bolt next? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Unfortunately, I think there's going to be more shoes to drop.\par \par \uc2 And it's simple. A bank is a bank because of their depositors. And if the depositors leave, that's party.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Neil, \uc2 Apple\uc2  just announced a high-paying savings account, and guess what happened? They took in a billion dollars in the first (AUDIO GAP) days.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: And just remember, this is for riskless income investments. People do not want to know their money's at risk. They don't want to know it has to go to another bank.\par \par \uc2 And now you have a situation where \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  sat and waited until \uc2 First Republic\uc2  was basically out, got it for a song, got guarantees on the real estate and pretty much guarantees the big banks now are just going to sit on the sofa waiting for the next shoe to drop. And all you have to do, just watch stock prices.\par \par \uc2 If the stock prices continue to plummet for some of these small to midsized names, the market's going to come for them, the depositors are going to leave, and \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 , which now 17 percent of all deposits, will end up at 25 percent one day. Good for them. They're doing the right thing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Alan, let me get your views on the Fed and the decision tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Most seem to be in the camp it's a quarter-point hike. Obviously, everyone's waiting to hear the Fed chairman's statement afterwards and whether he's done for the rest of the year. Do you think he will be done for the rest of the year?\par \par \uc2 KNUCKMAN: Maybe. I think we're very close to being done. We're a lot closer to being done than we are to the start of this cycle.\par \par \uc2 But let's remember, the stock market, the S&P is only down 15 percent from its all-time forever top. And we have had this enormous rate increase road for the last year. So I look at as a positive. It's what the market didn't do. And we're more off the bottom than we are off the top, when you look at the S&P.\par \par \uc2 And Gary is correct that this is natural selection. If you're a bad bank, you're going to get swallowed up by someone that knows what they're doing with money. And this is technology. \uc2 Apple\uc2  is a perfect example of that. People now have that choice with the click of a button to get that yield, risk-free yield of 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Neil, you have got to enjoy this. This is a very unique time in market history. But, again, when it comes back to it, I'm all about what the market is doing and not doing. We have earnings season right now halfway through, and 80 percent of the companies have beat; 75 percent have beat on revenue, and we're actually seeing revenue growth, while earnings decline.\par \par \uc2 So, companies, stocks are doing very, very well. If you want to get into those banks, the KRE, Regional Banking ETF, it's 40 percent off its February high, and the P/E of it is down to seven, seven.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KNUCKMAN: So those banks as a basket are inexpensive, but there is individual stock risk if you're going to take that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I want to thank you both.\par \par \uc2 By the way, he was referring to P/E, a price/earnings gauge, that judges how much a stock could be worth or the minute in that. And it's seven, of course, the price at about seven times earnings. That's pretty, pretty good, enticing, or so it would logically go. We shall see.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the read from Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2  on this big powwow of the House and Senate leaders at the White House discussing the whole debt situation and maybe coming up with something. Here's the thing about \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , though. He thinks the idea of just enlarging this group isn't going to help.\par \par \uc2 He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, these Hollywood writers aren't writing. They're striking.\par \par \uc2 And they could be striking for a long time over benefits and pay they say are just not up to speed. And a lot of late-night comedy and variety shows, they're in reruns -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now we know why President Biden has invited some congressional leaders, along with Kevin McCarthy, the speaker of the House, to the White House in a week. It's what his Treasury secretary told him, that we're on the brink of running out of dough, period, by June 1, less than a month from now.\par \par \uc2 Now, Republicans aren't buying all of that, but, right now, no one wants to take any chances. So talks will move on. As to whether there will be any progress, well, we will raise that with \uc2 John Thune\uc2  in just a second.\par \par \uc2 Right now to Chad Pergram to lay out where we stand -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL PRODUCER: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The government could be unable to pay its bills in less than a month, but no one is budging from their steeled positions. In fact, they may be digging in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Given the limited time Congress now has, it is clear that the only practical path to avoid default is for Congress to suspend the debt limit without conditions.\par \par \uc2 During his meeting with the leaders, the president will discuss initiating a separate process to address the budget in appropriations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is not committed to moving a clean debt ceiling bill just yet. That needs 60 votes. But he could use the House debt ceiling bill as a vehicle.\par \par \uc2 Schumer could strip out the GOP language and replace it with spending cuts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): We're going to wait until our meeting on the 9th, and then we will make a determination. After we pass a clean debt ceiling bill and get the debt ceiling bill move the debt ceiling move way back, then we could use this bill for a proper discussion of the appropriations and budget process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  will attend the White House meeting next week.\par \par \uc2 However, McConnell will not engage with his old Senate pal \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He leaves the talks to the president and the speaker.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: What exactly are you for? Are you for the House Republican bill?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I think what the House has passed makes a lot of sense. There is no solution in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: House Democrats are attempting parliamentary gymnastics. They want to go around McCarthy and force a vote on a clean debt ceiling bill.\par \par \uc2 The maneuver is called a discharge petition, but it needs 218 votes. Democrats only have 213. As we say, it's all about the math -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, that looks like pretty dicey math right there.\par \par \uc2 Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 To Senator Thune right now, the Senate minority whip.\par \par \uc2 Senator, always good to have you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You think inviting senators, or at least extending this beyond the president and the speaker, is essentially a waste of time, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 THUNE: Well, I guess what I would tell you, Neil, is that, in the end, anybody that's in that room other than the speaker of the House and the president of the United States probably is somewhat extraneous to what needs to be done here.\par \par \uc2 This has to be a deal that's negotiated between Speaker McCarthy on behalf of the Republican majority in the House of Representatives and the president, who ultimately would have to sign a bill into law.\par \par \uc2 The Senate's role, obviously, would come into play if and when there's a deal that would have to be voted on in the Senate. But, until they reach agreement, I think, for all intents and purposes, the Senate really is -- we're observers to this at the moment. We will have a role at some point, but, for now, it's got to be -- but it's got to be a two-way conversation between the speaker and the president.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, Senator, I caught your remarks earlier when you were speaking to the press with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and what have you that that seven out of 10 times we have been in this sort of situation, there have been some budgetary adjustments.\par \par \uc2 But this is one of those rare occasions, maybe by design, sir, where Republicans in the House pushing this want an immediate quid pro quo. We raise the debt ceiling, these spending cuts go into effect. That's never been done. That's what has the rankling that you're getting from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Where's the middle ground on this?\par \par \uc2 THUNE: There have been -- the Budget Control Act in 2011 came out of conversations around this topic and ultimately led to spending reductions.\par \par \uc2 What the House is trying to achieve here...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But it didn't immediately. It didn't immediately, right, Senator?\par \par \uc2 I guess the only reason why I'm getting into nuance like this is, that's where Democrats say, no, no, they're trying to play a whole different game here.\par \par \uc2 How -- what is the game, or, if you want to call that, what's the procedure going forward that you think would work?\par \par \uc2 THUNE: I think that sitting down and actually negotiating out something that would represent meaningful attempt to address the debt itself -- and what they want is simply just to increase the debt, keep on spending, keep on borrowing, keep on taxing.\par \par \uc2 And what I tried to point out today, Neil, is, if you look at the next 10 budget years, every dollar we borrow, 50 cents of that is going to be used to pay interest alone.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 THUNE: And if you look at the amount that we're paying for interest, in 2010, we paid a little under $200 billion a year in interest on the debt.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, we paid $475 billion just in interest on the debt. That's a 142 percent increase in that short amount of time. This isn't sustainable. We have got to do something to address the debt. And what House Republicans and what Senate Republicans supporting them are saying is, look, this is a divided government.\par \par \uc2 This -- it requires negotiation. You want to increase in the debt limit, let's do something about the debt and incorporate some spending reforms and some budgetary restraint as part of that process.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, where Democrats say this is a nonstarter, Senator, is this idea in setting discretionary spending levels at 2022 levels, say that it's not going to happen, limiting spending growth to no more than 1 percent a year, not going to happen. This is from \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even talk of clawing back $100 billion in unspent COVID money, not going to happen. Now, that one was a reasonable request, because the money is unspent. It just makes me wonder about whether any deal could be had before June 1. And you afraid that, if both sides are stuck in their positions here, we're going to default?\par \par \uc2 THUNE: And that -- that doesn't need to happen. It can't happen.\par \par \uc2 And for that not to happen, the president has got to step up to the plate and do what 65 percent of the American people want him to do. And that is negotiate spending restraint; 58 percent of Democrats agree with that.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at some of the things -- you mentioned it. I mean, clawing back unspent COVID dollars, why is that hard? And that yields immediate savings. There are dollars you could save right today. They're talking about work requirements in some of these government programs, particularly for able-bodied adults without children.\par \par \uc2 I think that's a sensible reform. Talking about permitting reform to make it easier to get energy projects sited and permitted in this country and start lowering the cost of energy, make us less dependent upon foreign energy. Those are all, I think, reasonable, should be bipartisan items that, as they sit down at the table, they ought to be able to negotiate out.\par \par \uc2 Now, I, for one, think we also ought to have the spending restraints in there in the future, the things that the House has talked about. But there are things on which there ought to be agreement. But the fact of the matter is, they cannot, one, ignore it. And that's an unsustainable position to say, we're not going to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 Or, two, come to the table and say it's a clean debt limit, or else. That's not where the American people are. That's not where the country is right now. We're $31 trillion in debt and spending almost half-a-trillion a year on interest on payments on the debt.\par \par \uc2 We have got to start drawing some lines here and start getting this situation under control.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 THUNE: And that starts with these negotiations between the president and the speaker.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm so old, Senator, I remember when it crossed a trillion dollars debt with \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , and I thought that was such an incomprehensible number.\par \par \uc2 THUNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I'm much older than you, Senator, so I'm just wondering, what the heck?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 THUNE: You're not a lot older, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's all right. It's all right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're just handling it better.\par \par \uc2 But, Senator, thank you very, very much. We will see where this goes.\par \par \uc2 THUNE: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, man, I think we are getting close to something.\par \par \uc2 THUNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2  on that.\par \par \uc2 This debt, if you think about, this trillion dollars when \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was there, right, and now $31.4 trillion, likely with all the added spending that's tucked in both of these measures that both parties have, trillions more, that could send it up to $45, trillion $50 trillion in 10 years. Go figure.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, go figure. Hollywood writers on strike and some of your favorite late-night shows, minus "Gutfeld," affected by not running shows, running reruns. "Gutfeld" is live, though. Go figure.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, a lot of Hollywood writers right now aren't writing. They're striking. It's over pay, benefits, that sort of thing. And it's already prompting a number of late-night television shows, variety shows to stall production, if not halt it entirely.\par \par \uc2 How long this goes on is anyone's guess.\par \par \uc2 William La Jeunesse following it from Culver City, California -- William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, seven studios in New York, 10 here in Los Angeles, this is what you see, some 11,000 screen and TV writers going out on strike.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is here. \uc2 Sony\uc2  is around the corner. So, what is it all about? Well, let's go to the sticking points. Writers want a 16 percent across-the-board raise. That's for streaming, network and cable TV, and higher residuals for the number of viewers.\par \par \uc2 Studios offering 9 percent, but no residuals for streaming. Unions want six writers for a pilot, one writer for every subsequent episode, and 13-week contracts. Studios say no minimums, no contracts, and the freedom to pay writers on a day rate.\par \par \uc2 Finally, writers say artificial intelligence cannot touch a script or a rewrite. Studios only saying, that's something we will talk about. The talks come as cord-cutting and streaming wars over subscribers have studios hunting for profits.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HANDEL, ENTERTAINMENT ATTORNEY: THE companies say, look, we're spending billions of dollars to build out these platforms. It's just a very tight economic position.\par \par \uc2 The Writers Guild response is, if you're going to spend billions of dollars to build worldwide enterprises based on our content, we need a fair piece of that, and we need to be able to sustain careers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Impact on viewers, late-night talk shows, except for "Gutfeld," all canceled. Soaps will be next to disappear.\par \par \uc2 Many popular series already have episodes in the can, but the fall shows usually start production in June. Those are likely dead. What you will see more of is news, sports, reality, game shows, reruns, and hits from Europe and Asia.\par \par \uc2 Now, the last writers strike lasted 100 days. This one could be longer, Neil, because both sides are far apart. The writers want, collectively about $400 million, studios offering $86 million.\par \par \uc2 Back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The last time, it didn't involve streaming or any of these new technologies, to say nothing of social media. So this sounds like something that, to your point, could drag on for a long time.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, Neil, it doesn't hurt just the writers, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: There's 10 people -- for every writer here, there's 10 people catering, the florists, the production crews you name it, the prop houses.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: They're all going to get hurt if this continues -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, William, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Now I understand what's going on there. All right, that makes sense.\par \par \uc2 There is another strike that could happen in the airline industry, and this one could be a big one, and this one could disrupt you. And it has nothing to do with any late-night comedy. It could be a real daytime horror show.\par \par \uc2 We will explain and explore -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: American airline pilots have voted to strike. That doesn't mean they're going to strike, but it does mean that 15,000 pilots could walk. We just don't know where this stands right now.\par \par \uc2 But our next guest does, Captain Ed Sticher -- Ed Sicher, I should say, the \uc2 Allied Pilots Association\uc2  President.\par \par \uc2 Captain, I apologize for screwing that up. It's very good to have you, sir.\par \par \uc2 Where does this -- where does this stand right now?\par \par \uc2 CAPT. ED SICHER, PRESIDENT, \uc2 ALLIED PILOTS ASSOCIATION\uc2 : Thanks, Neil. I appreciate you having me on.\par \par \uc2 Look, we have been negotiating for over four years now. We're still not at the end of this process. This is the first step in that process of a strike. I'm hoping, I think all of our passengers are hoping it doesn't get to that. But that's where we're at right now, right at the beginning of the summer, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, what are the issues here, Captain?\par \par \uc2 SICHER: Well, our issues right now are basic scheduling reliability, quality-of-life issues, force productivity issues.\par \par \uc2 These are the kind of things that leave us stranded in airports when we're scheduled out to the limits of our duty day. These are the kind of things that leave our passengers stranded out in the airport. We don't want a repeat of last summer, when this operation was stretched so thin, the slightest disruption just sent ripples through the system, leaving our passengers stranded all over the place and our pilots in the same boat.\par \par \uc2 We have got record number of our pilots being flown in, in days off, being restricted from trading trips. All of these issues, these are the issues that we're at a loggerheads with.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So you say you hope to resolve this, but the airline -- and we did contact \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They gave us this statement, sir, saying that: "We remain confident that an agreement for our pilots is within reach and can be finalized quickly. The finish line is in sight. We understand that a strike authorization vote is one of the important ways pilots express their desire to get a deal done. And we respect the message of voting results. Importantly, the results don't change our commitment to distract us from working expeditiously to complete a deal."\par \par \uc2 Where are you -- where are you on that?\par \par \uc2 SICHER: Well, Neil, I have got this question for you, sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 SICHER: If we have been working for four years and proposing win-win solutions at the table for over four years, how is it that management has been expeditiously working to solve this issue?\par \par \uc2 It's up to the CEO, \uc2 Robert Isom\uc2 . He could solve this in a minute if he wanted to. But don't tell me that, after four years, they're working expeditiously to close this out, because it just doesn't add up. And I don't think the public believes it either.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, could this disrupt the travel season? Could your members just go out and walk?\par \par \uc2 SICHER: Absolutely. Sir, I -- absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, in February...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: When would that be, then, Captain? When would that be, Captain?\par \par \uc2 SICHER: Well, it'll be a repeat of last summer if things continue the way they're going.\par \par \uc2 And you remember last summer...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SICHER: ... with the record cancellations, record delays. We don't want to be there. We don't have to be there.\par \par \uc2 We have got some proposals at the table that we have researched quite thoroughly. Look, we fly the airplanes. You want to know how to make a reliable operation, talk to our pilots. We have been speaking about win-win solutions now for years.\par \par \uc2 Last summer, our CEO, \uc2 Robert Isom\uc2 , and I made an agreement when our trip trade system melted down. That allowed us a more -- I guess a more effective utilization of our pilots over the Thanksgiving and Christmas holidays. And we sailed through those holidays. The pilot cancellation rate was zero.\par \par \uc2 Look what happened across town, Neil, to Southwest during that same time frame.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I remember.\par \par \uc2 SICHER: These are the kind of solutions that we have at the table, and we're just befuddled as to why management won't agree to them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, well, we will be watching it very, very closely. Hope to get management on here to talk beyond just issuing a statement.\par \par \uc2 But, Captain, keep us posted.\par \par \uc2 Captain Ed Sicher, the \uc2 Allied Pilots Association\uc2  president. Hopefully, they can avoid that kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, days ahead of the coordination of \uc2 King Charles\uc2 , we're learning of a man arrested outside Buckingham Palace a short time ago with a suspected weapon. London police said that a controlled explosion was carried out as a precaution outside Buckingham Palace after the man was arrested on suspicion of possessing an offensive weapon.\par \par \uc2 We're told that \uc2 King Charles\uc2  and Queen Camilla were not in attendance at the palace at the time. Everything's all right, but, obviously, some skittish developments days ahead of the coronation. We will keep you posted.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And with apologies to Billy Joel, who still does sold-out concerts at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 , good luck finding New Yorkers to stick around to even watch him in this environment where so many are bolting the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2  and other high tax states.\par \par \uc2 Fascinating study in today's New York Post that outlines it all, that the state's adjusted gross income continues to shrink year in and year out as people bolt. And some of the biggest beneficiaries are states, well, like Florida, not too surprisingly.\par \par \uc2 Zane Tankel isn't too surprised by this, the \uc2 Apple-Metro\uc2  chairman and CEO. He's a restaurant powerhouse, even though he isn't a pound overweight.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, anyway, Zane, that's a weird one to me.\par \par \uc2 I can't -- I can understand the high tax rate, but a lot of these numbers don't even include 2022, where it really accelerated. But it's uncanny. And all these states that deal with high taxes and all of that, the revenue loss is substantial, and it's building.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 ZANE TANKEL, CHAIRMAN AND CEO, APPLE-\uc2 METRO, INC\uc2 .: Well, I think, with every loser, there's a winner.\par \par \uc2 So as the wealthy evacuate or bolt, as you say, for the Florida, Texas, et cetera, I have been tracking this very carefully. It wasn't news, unfortunately, to me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: I think there's opportunities. Who feeds the masses? Who feeds the people who are left here in New York City that need to have value?\par \par \uc2 So we have two for $25. Two people can eat for $25, late-night appetizers, half-price appetizers.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you're talking about your Applebee's and other restaurants, right?\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: Yes, I'm sorry, our space.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And so their business is strong. A lot of people don't know this, but that's where you make your coin.\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: I apologize.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, please.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, your restaurants are crowded. You're still dealing with that. But you need that hook to get them, right?\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: You need that hook to get them. And value is the hook. And what I'd like to think and what I talk to our people out all the time is, let's give four-star fine dining service at Applebee's pricing.\par \par \uc2 So the casual dining space, which I won't name all my competitors, I think there's huge opportunity for the folks who can't afford to pick up and move to another state because of their tax issues. The wealthy, yes, $39 billion, that's a lot of infrastructure, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, real -- if this continues at this pace, and people still bolt a lot of these high-tax states, a lot of the high-tax states come back and say, well, it's moderating.\par \par \uc2 I haven't seen numbers to show that. But play that out for me a few years from now.\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: Well, that's a really good question. I wish I was all that smart, but here's how I'd play it out.\par \par \uc2 It took years to get here, started probably in the de Blasio administration with demonizing the rich, tax the rich. They will pay for everything. What nobody recognized was, the rich don't have to stay. The rich can afford to pick up and leave.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, they can move. And they have.\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: Sure.\par \par \uc2 And they have. And the folks that are left behind is the working -- are the working folks, the people we feed. And somebody has to feed those people. And that's value, value with great service.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But to those folks, are they beginning to cut back on things, maybe not order the appetizer, not order the dessert?\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: And that's why we try and push value more. You're right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: We can tell, Neil, you and I have talked often, by what they order from the menu on how well they are.\par \par \uc2 Are they ordering high-priced steak and ribs and things, or are they ordering mozzarella sticks and chicken fingers? You can -- but they still have to eat.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: The good news is, you cannot stop eating. It's a habit you get into at birth, and you never kick it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, yes. Yes. No, I hear you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So you see this phenomenon that's playing out in states like California -- and we have seen in other big blue states where the taxes are high -- you don't see that unless they change their policies, right?\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: And that's going to take forever.\par \par \uc2 Neil, you -- one of your earlier guests said, it's all in the numbers.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: And it's really simple.\par \par \uc2 So, as they lose the money, the income for infrastructure, they got to pay more, just like the banking crisis.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And it gets worse. It gets worse.\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: You got to pay more for it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: Interest rates going up, all the rest of it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. All right. Well, I have lost my appetite now because of this.\par \par \uc2 Zane, thank you very much. Good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 TANKEL: Thank you, Neil. Always my pleasure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Zane Tankel, a very successful restauranteur.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230502ej520002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Fast Food Weight Loss Plan?; IRS Hiring More Workers; Economy Adds 236,000 Jobs in March; San Francisco Murder Investigation; Afghanistan Report; Holiday Travel\b0\par\par\uc2 Lydia Hu, Claudia Cowan, Lucas Tomlinson, Rick Reichmuth, Jeff  Flock, Christina Coleman, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,932 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: And they're off, Americans out for the Easter holiday and Passover by road and by plane in what could be a preview to the upcoming summer travel season, already signs of trouble, from a shortage of air traffic controllers that could cause some travel chaos in the air, to gas prices spiking on supply concerns that could make things a lot worse on the ground.\par \par \uc2 And we are all over it with Christina Coleman at \uc2 Los Angeles International Airport\uc2  on how things are shaping up, Jeff Flock in Chicago on those gas prices that keep going up, and meteorologist Rick Reichmuth booth on if the weather will be holding up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto on a busy news day, as Americans get to take to the road in the skies, but not without some trouble along the way.\par \par \uc2 To Los Angeles, where you will find our Christina Coleman at LAX -- Christina.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA COLEMAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, fortunately, there have been no reports of any massive flight cancellations or delays today, good news because it was a much different story during the winter break. Now, air travel days ahead leading into Easter weekend matched pre-pandemic levels, with many people taking to the skies or hitting the roads during spring break.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  forecasts more than 51,000 flights for today. Those numbers are expected to drop slightly tomorrow, then increase by a little more than 6,000 between Saturday and Tuesday. Many passengers told me that they felt confident that they would get to their destinations on time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Were you guys worried about cancellations, delays, any of that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No. Had no problems whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was worried about it, but it didn't happen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We had built-in time to prepare for that. Just caught Ubers and had to get to a cruise ship and get back. We have plenty of time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: But some airlines are still facing looming challenges, including staffing shortages. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  is also dealing with a 30-year low in the number of fully certified air traffic controllers, due in part to attrition, according to the president of the \uc2 National Air Traffic Controllers Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In an effort to prevent delays and cancellations amid this problem, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is giving airlines flexibility to reduce its operations with fewer flights at New York area airports during the peak summer travel period.\par \par \uc2 So far, \uc2 JetBlue\uc2  and \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  already have plans to cut some of its flights out of New York this summer. Airline experts are encouraging travelers to book their summer flights early.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HENRY HARTEVELDT\uc2 , ATMOSPHERE RESEARCH GROUP: The fact that the airlines are now reducing flights in and out of New York means fewer flights and fewer seats, and that means fewer low fare seats. So don't wait for that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Now, despite these issues, demand for summer travel is still expected to be strong -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Christina, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, if you're thinking of travel on the road, you're going to have some problems as well, like higher gas prices.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Flock traveling the roads in Illinois to spell it all out -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, we have been driving all over the Chicagoland area sampling gas prices. They are all largely in excess of $4 a gallon. We're in Niles, Illinois, now, where the \uc2 Costco\uc2  station is located, gas.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, in Niles, they have something called the Leaning Tower of Niles. That's actually a one half scale replica of the Leaning Tower of Pisa. And it's just something I thought you might like to see.\par \par \uc2 As I said, it's right next to the \uc2 Costco\uc2 . And look at the lines, cars packing the area just before the pumps there. I want to check out the price over there. But, first, let me tell you what the national average is. That is $3.58 right now. It was up 3 cents since yesterday, up about a penny a day in the last week, a lot cheaper than it was this time last year.\par \par \uc2 But people say this is where prices are headed, over $4 a gallon for the national average, Ohio really getting hit hard. They have spiked, up 7 percent just in the last week. And in terms of the most expensive gas in the U.S., well, that would be the usual suspects, California, Hawaii, and then Illinois at $3.95 the average.\par \par \uc2 Back live here, maybe you see, this is the \uc2 Costco\uc2  line. Multiple gas pumps, but they're all filled. And maybe we move around here. We can see just how long these lines are. The price, I'm told right here, now at \uc2 Costco\uc2  $3.99. That's up about 10 cents in the last couple of days. And that's the cheapest gas you're going to get in the Chicagoland area, which maybe explains why so many people are in line to do it.\par \par \uc2 That's the latest, as we tool around, burning up some gas, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. Thank you, Jeff. I like the Leaning Tower of Pisa, by the way.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, if you are thinking of heading out, it doesn't look like the weather, so far from what we can see, is going to slow you down.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Rick Reichmuth, the FOX chief meteorologist, and what his charts are telling him -- sir.\par \par \uc2 RICK REICHMUTH, FOX NEWS CHIEF METEOROLOGIST: Jeff makes me so nervous every time he's driving and talking.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 REICHMUTH: He does such a good job, but I'm scared.\par \par \uc2 I tell you what. He's got good weather for his driving and most of the country is looking really good. And I just say that. We have had such an incredible series of the snow out across the West and all the severe weather, all the tornadoes the last couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 We have got a break for a few days. We have one little trouble spot. Let me just quickly show you our current temps. You get the idea. Here in the central part of the country, temps are really rebounding. And we have a big warmup coming here across the Central part of this country. But this comes in Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday of this week, areas from the Southwest through the Northern Plains, Great Lakes, and then areas of the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be seeing temperatures well above average. Temps are going to be 70s and 80s and spring is going to spring really quickly. That also means there's going to be a lot of rapid snowmelt in some spots, might cause some flooding. But we have got one little trouble spot, Neil, and this has been the case really for the last number of days.\par \par \uc2 Flooding concerns continue here across parts of the Deep South because of all of this consistent moisture. And we have got to get through tomorrow, really, for this start to push all the way off the coast. And we get a little bit of a break here at the start of this coming week.\par \par \uc2 But take a look at this. This is rainfall still to come throughout the weekend. Texas, you're about at the end of it. We have got still a little bit here down across parts of Mississippi and Alabama, Panhandle of Florida and then the Carolinas.\par \par \uc2 That's out of here by Sunday. But take a look at this. This is your Saturday. The West Coast, your great. Reno, 62 degrees right there at Lake Tahoe. Lot of snow there that is going to be melting, which is going to fill those reservoirs, and that's good news.\par \par \uc2 And take a look at this. This is your Saturday. And if you are maybe heading out for a Sunday morning, Easter morning service, we have got some beautiful conditions for so many people. Some rain late in the day here across parts of the High Plains. But, overall, Neil, we are catching a break, and so many people in the U.S. desperately need it. We have got it coming finally -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And they're finding it thanks to you, Rick. All right, thank you my friend.\par \par \uc2 Rick Reichmuth following all of that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, you're beginning to get a pattern that there are going to be some inconveniences along the way and some troubles along the way, whether this is a preview of coming attractions for the rest of the summer.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Francesca Page, the travel journalist extraordinaire.\par \par \uc2 What's your read, Francesca, when you look at those traveling by road or air, what they're in for this summer?\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA PAGE, TRAVEL AND LIFESTYLE EXPERT: Well, I'm getting asked a lot of these questions right now, and there's actually a few valid concerns when it comes to travel, particularly if you are flying, outside of just delays and staffing shortages that we're dealing with right now, especially as we kick off this holiday weekend and head into the summer travel season, between inclement weather and traveling abroad to places like Mexico, for example.\par \par \uc2 Now, my advice, as always, is to protect yourself and ensure your trip. There's travel security memberships like Medjet that can evacuate you for a number of safety threats and/or if you get moved -- you can get moved home, or if you get hospitalized.\par \par \uc2 And travel insurance has limitations, so memberships particularly like that are worth looking into. But there are a lot of concerns, obviously, right now with the summer ahead in terms of these cancellations and staffing shortages.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, on the cancellation part, I mean, we have just heard about American and \uc2 JetBlue\uc2  that they're cutting back flights to some areas, and sometimes with not a whole lot of warning.\par \par \uc2 What's a traveler to do if this happens to them.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Well, right now, to try and soften the problem, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  are saying they're going to waive some of their slot rules at airports in New York and D.C. from May until September.\par \par \uc2 It's also going to allow carriers to turn in up to 10 percent of the slots they hold for takeoffs or landings at JFK and La Guardia Airports, as well as \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and D.C. And the agency will also let airlines cut 10 percent of their approved operating timing at Newark, the idea being to give airlines more wiggle room to reduce operations during peak summer travel.\par \par \uc2 Now, keep in mind that fewer flights means less congestion and fewer delays. But it could also mean that travelers may have to pay more in airfare. So you want to be aware of that if you intend to travel this summer.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, I know people have been seeing these protests going on in France. They think, maybe I will skip that trip.\par \par \uc2 Others are worried about their safety in places like Mexico. How do you advise people concerned about that?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Well, we know that the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is predicting a summer season ridden with a lot of delays. That goes for both within the nation and also international travel.\par \par \uc2 And they expect these overall delays particularly to be on the East Coast. The best thing you can do, and I say it always, is to track your flights, keep up with the news, make sure you know what's going on, and plan your trips well ahead of time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Thank you for all that advice, Francesca. Have a great weekend yourself.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In the meantime, the week isn't ending very well for the administration over this whole Afghanistan thing. In fact, some people wonder, why didn't they release the report today, where it could kind of get buried in the news?\par \par \uc2 But the finger-pointing toward the Trump administration, over the top.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, going to the White House right now and looking at the timing of the release of that Afghanistan report yesterday that points the finger at really the Trump administration for the problems that ultimately bedeviled our exodus there.\par \par \uc2 But it makes you think as well that maybe it would have been a better idea to do it today. It could have gotten lost before the weekend. Not so. Too late.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Tomlinson with the latest -- Lucas\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS PENTAGON PRODUCER: Well, Neil, former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  blasted this new White House report on your show yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 , FORMER U.S. DEFENSE SECRETARY: It was a terribly planned and executed withdrawal from Afghanistan. It was very hasty. And not only did we lose brave Americans there. We left others abroad, and it really hurt our international reputation.\par \par \uc2 There's no -- there's little disagreement about that, except, I guess, from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: President Biden's approval rating has never recovered from the hasty withdrawal from Afghanistan, as you can see here, Neil. It has been underwater ever since.\par \par \uc2 Inside the 12-page White House report, this tacit admission -- quote -- "We now prioritize earlier evacuations when faced with a degrading security situation."\par \par \uc2 President Biden had promised the evacuation would not look like the fall of Saigon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There's going to be no circumstance where you see people being lifted off the roof of an embassy of the United States from Afghanistan. It is not at all comparable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The report does not mention that President Biden's top commanders warned against pulling all U.S. forces out of Afghanistan -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for that, Lucas, very much.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Tomlinson at the White House.\par \par \uc2 To Victoria Coates right now, the former deputy national security adviser.\par \par \uc2 Victoria, what did you make of this and the timing of this?\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA COATES, FORMER SENIOR DIRECTOR, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: Oh, Neil, it just thinks to high heaven.\par \par \uc2 And this notion that somehow the Trump administration bound the hands of the Biden administration doesn't pass the smell test. If you want to bind a subsequent administration, you pass something as a treaty through the Senate. They -- we didn't even try to do that, because with -- the agreement and the discussions we had been having with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  didn't rise to that level.\par \par \uc2 So their claim is just false and it's a dereliction of duty. They're trying to abdicate their responsibility for this mess. And, of course, it did look exactly like Saigon. And the president lied to the American people about it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, from what I can understand, the administration has laid out this theory that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Trump administration et al had recognized that it was time to pull out. They didn't give an exact timetable, but that there was no good staying there.\par \par \uc2 How that leapt from the total, total confusion and the rush and the rapid rise of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and the tumbling out of all these people, that doesn't quite jibe.\par \par \uc2 COATES: No.\par \par \uc2 And I think we all saw the polling, which went back about 10 years, that there was broad bipartisan support for getting out of Afghanistan. And I think that certainly guided President Trump's feeling that if the majority of the American people want this, you want to try to find a way to make it so.\par \par \uc2 But the approach he took was conditional with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , that they had to meet certain benchmarks, and if they didn't meet those benchmarks, he was going to come after them. And what's really remarkable here is the Biden administration has taken something that should be politically very popular and turned it into political poison.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Lucas' point that the favorability ratings have not recovered in the almost two years since this happened shows you it has really stuck with the American people and it is not sitting well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They also said -- this is from the Biden administration -- that a lot of this rapid acceleration of \uc2 Taliban\uc2  advances in Afghanistan, it was happening under the Trump administration as well. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 COATES: No, it really isn't, because we maintained our position at Bagram Air Force Base.\par \par \uc2 And I was over at the Department of Energy at the end, where some of the discussions were going on. But I did go to Al Asad Base in Iraq with President Trump Christmas of 2018, and he grasped immediately the significance of that facility, what we had paid for it, what we could do from it, the way we could protect our people there, including the president of the United States was safe flying into Iraq and going there.\par \par \uc2 And he would have gotten immediately from then-Secretary -- acting Secretary Miller the importance of this thing. So, no, this -- that's just inaccurate as well. Again, they're trying to shirk their responsibilities, and it is just a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, finally, I will give you their final pitch, which was to say no one could have seen something like this deteriorating as quickly as it did. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 COATES: Well, Neil, I mean, this is all coming from Admiral Kirby.\par \par \uc2 And you and I are old enough to remember Baghdad Bob at the outset of the invasion of Iraq. And I think we have got Kabul Kirby now, that he's basically saying whatever he can to try to make things look like they're OK. And it's not OK. This -- there were things that we could have done. We could have been much harsher with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We could have maintained our defensive positions and gotten our -- both all of our people and all the people we needed to get out, our friends and allies who had worked with us, and not left them to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will never know. History is history here, but we can't reinvent it, to your point here.\par \par \uc2 Victoria Coates, the former deputy national security adviser.\par \par \uc2 You saw it in this piece we were looking at, going back to Mike (sic) Esper, the former defense secretary. He is back with me tomorrow on my Saturday show to talk about some of these latest revelations and how our military moves forward, again, \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  joining me 10:00 a.m. Eastern time tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, more information coming out on the killing of the Cash App creator Bob Lee in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Claudia Cowan has more on that -- Claudia.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA COWAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And, Neil, Bob Lee's murder early Tuesday morning is now fueling calls for change from some of his friends fed up with violence in the city.\par \par \uc2 I will have the latest on this story as police continue the search for a killer.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Two hundred and thirty-six thousand more jobs added to the U.S. economy, that's the good news. The bad news could be, well, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  can still hike interest rates because the economy can endure it.\par \par \uc2 Can it? We're on it -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, it got a lot of attention because who it was and where it all happened.\par \par \uc2 A well-known, iconic Internet sensation, a guy who created the Cash App who was killed in San Francisco, supposedly in an area where that kind of thing rarely, if ever, happened. We're getting more details on exactly what was behind the death of Bob Lee right now from Claudia Cowan -- Claudia.\par \par \uc2 COWAN: Well, Neil, it is just heartbreaking to see video now of how Bob Lee spent the final moments of his life early Tuesday morning desperately trying to get help.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this surveillance footage obtained by The Daily Mail. It shows the prominent tech executive right after he had been repeatedly stabbed in the chest staggering along the sidewalk, bleeding, trying to flag down a driver or doorman, to no avail.\par \par \uc2 Reportedly, a kitchen knife with a four-inch blade was found in a nearby parking lot. But police have not confirmed if that was the murder weapon and have remained tight-lipped about the investigation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SGT. ADAM LOBSINGER, SAN FRANCISCO POLICE DEPARTMENT: This investigation is still in the early stages. Because of this, we are not commenting on evidence, nor will we speculate on the circumstances surrounding this horrific crime.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COWAN: Some of \uc2 Lee\uc2 's friends have launched a \uc2 Change.org\uc2  petition to seek justice for Bob Lee and make San Francisco safe again.\par \par \uc2 The petition calls on city leaders to take stronger act against violent offenders and to improve public safety. Lee had recently moved to Miami. His friends say he didn't feel safe living in San Francisco anymore. He called it -- quote -- "a cesspool."\par \par \uc2 But he had to come back last Friday for a business meeting, and then he extended his trip to visit with friends. His murder has drawn attention to a rise in violent crime, including four stabbings in four days, even in nicer parts of the city like Rincon Hill, where Lee was attacked.\par \par \uc2 At a community meeting last night focusing on public safety, police Chief Bill Scott hinted that an arrest could come soon. He said the department is getting good developments in this case, as the mayor and DA promise to hold those responsible fully accountable -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Claudia, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, if you're noticing a pattern right now of crimes being committed in areas where we didn't see them all that often in the past, you're not imagining it. Just consider this. This is not the first attack in an affluent area.\par \par \uc2 Remember Eleanor Bowles? We all remember that name. She was stabbed to death back in December in a very wealthy Atlanta suburb that didn't see too much crime after the assailant tried to steal her car. There was the case of a woman who was robbed at gunpoint just last year at her affluent Chicago neighborhood, or \uc2 Deloitte\uc2  consultant Michelle Go, who was killed after being pushed onto a subway track in Times Square.\par \par \uc2 Again and again, it's happening in areas where it didn't happen that often in the past. What are we to make of this new direction in crime?\par \par \uc2 Nicole Parker, the former \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent with us now.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of all that, Nicole?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE PARKER, FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: First of all, thanks for having me, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I think this is such an important topic to be discussing. I focused a lot on working violent crime for the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and when we look at Mr. Bob Lee, he's a father. I want to offer my condolences to his family. He left behind two young daughters.\par \par \uc2 I read on the Internet about him. He seemed to be a wonderful, kind human being who was really making a difference in this world and made a strong impact. And I think a lot of us are hearing about this because he had done so many amazing things, many new developments in the tech world.\par \par \uc2 Sadly, there are so many others that we don't necessarily hear about, and every life matters. And, again, I want to offer my condolences to his family and to all victims families, who -- their loved ones are victims of violent crime. It's very, very serious.\par \par \uc2 He had moved to Miami, Florida, because he had a feeling that he needed to get his family to a different community. From what I understand -- I have many, many friends in San Francisco, in the Bay Area, and they say that city has completely changed.\par \par \uc2 The fall of San Francisco, I think, can be attributed to a number of things. There's a fentanyl crisis going on. I know that, with COVID, many people that were working in offices in San Francisco are -- many of them are working from home. It's caused a lot of the -- some tough economy to happen there.\par \par \uc2 Some businesses have shuttered. There are less people coming into the city to work. And I think people are, frankly, scared in many instances. The defund the police movement is never a good thing. We're seeing that nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Any time that these major cities have chosen to cut funding to police, it causes problems. The vilification of law enforcement and the disrespect for law enforcement nationwide is crippling many major cities. And I hope that this is an instance that we can look and figure out what's going on in this country, because I can tell you, cutting law enforcement does not help reduce your crime rate.\par \par \uc2 The -- when people don't feel like they're held accountable and they're not going to be held accountable for committing crimes, they're repeat offenders. Many of these violent criminals are many times repeat offenders is what we have realized. And we don't know that the circumstances regarding this situation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, we do not know what exactly happened here. You're quite right.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But we have seen wave after wave of these kind of violent attacks, and they certainly caught people's attention, simply because the video is available and people see, oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 And I have always argued -- and I know we have discussed this -- that the reticence of people returning to work has nothing to do with the new post COVID world. It has everything to do with crime. And until that's addressed, I don't think this changes.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: And I think that this is not just happening in San Francisco. This is happening in many of the largest cities across the country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: The fact that he came to Miami, Miami is known to be tough on criminals, and they support their law enforcement. And there's something to be said for that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. Too early, but we will see.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: There are systems that we track crime. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, good points all, Nicole, very good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 We're watching breaking news, including what happened today. Of course, markets were closed in this country today, but we got a big jobs report out that kind of confirmed billionaire investor \uc2 Ken Fisher\uc2 's view of the world that things aren't getting worse; they're actually getting better -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, what if a good economic came out, but no one really was around to celebrate it?\par \par \uc2 Well, the markets were closed today. We got word that the employment picture picked up in the latest month, 236,000 new jobs. But there was the small issue of the market segment being closed, although they did have a bit of a minor reaction in other curious ways.\par \par \uc2 Lydia Hu on all of that -- Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Neil. That's right.\par \par \uc2 Today's jobs report showing that job growth slow, which is what the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  wants to see. But there are other signs, like a low unemployment rate and rising wages, which means the Fed is likely to continue to increase interest rates come May.\par \par \uc2 The labor market added 236,000 jobs last month. That's slightly below estimates. The unemployment rate also ticked down to 3.5 percent from 3.6 percent the month before. And while we saw -- we saw jobs added in leisure and hospitality, consistent with how Americans are spending money now, this report also shows job losses in various sectors, you can see here, from manufacturing, construction, and retail as well.\par \par \uc2 The pace of wage growth also slowed to 4.2 percent over last year. That's down from 4.6 percent in February. But wages, they're still not keeping up with inflation, which is running at 6 percent.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: What really worries me is where we're headed right now. I think that this -- that the euphoria we have seen in the markets over falling inflation, I'm not so sure we're going to continue to see that.\par \par \uc2 I think you're going to see inflation sticky in the area of 4, 5, 6, 7 percent over the months ahead.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: Now it's expected that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will be reluctant to back away from its campaign to hike interest rates until there's more clear indication that inflation is coming down.\par \par \uc2 That means all eyes are going to be on the next round of inflation data that is set to be released next week. For now, Neil, about two-thirds of investors that are tied to the Fed's benchmark rate expect to see a quarter-point hike come may -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yikes. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 All right, Lydia, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Lydia Hu.\par \par \uc2 Billionaire investor \uc2 Ken Fisher\uc2  has torn apart this number. I spoke with him earlier. He kind of likes the general -- well, the general economy he sees behind it. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEN FISHER\uc2 , FOUNDER, \uc2 FISHER INVESTMENTS\uc2 : I studied this one, and, 