ong growth market for loans -- propels the economy. People don't borrow money. Businesses don't borrow money to not spend it.\par \par \uc2 And the growth from there propels. The fact is, people expected -- uniformly, the bears expected loans to plummet in the post-SVB environment. The data post-\uc2 SVB\uc2  does not show that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you're talking about \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 . And when that went under and then Signature, and there it was concern about Republic. Some feared a bank contagion.\par \par \uc2 Still, \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  has been saying of late that, because of this bank disruption, the odds have climbed -- I hope I'm paraphrasing him correctly -- for a recession.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: So what I would say, as I said about the Fed, for example -- and I have great respect for \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  -- but what I would say is, watch what \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  does, not what \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 And the fact is, loan growth isn't imploding. As long as loan growth isn't imploding, we're not going to get to that recession.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, I know you're not a fan of the Fed in general, but the odds are -- I was just looking this morning -- 60 percent think that we are going to see another quarter-point hike in May. Are you in that camp.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: When you say I'm not a fan of the Fed, Neil, you're prone to understatement.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the fact of the matter, as you know, I'm a big critic.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. Well, I'm trying to use language that families can hear. So...\par \par \uc2 FISHER: I think they can hear Chauncey Gardiner, but I don't know.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FISHER: The fact is that, as the Fed has raised Fed fund rates, which has raised 90 day T-bill rates, that has not translated into increased costs for the base upon which banks lend.\par \par \uc2 And as I have said on your show numbers of times before, as long as that cost for banks' deposit base remains low -- and, mind you, there's some things that have gone on there that are worth paying attention to -- but as long as it remains very low, this lending isn't going to stop. The economy is going to be fine.\par \par \uc2 What the Fed does is largely superfluous. And might they increase rates this, that or the other? Sure. Because, basically, they don't know what they're doing and they react and they're not very good. They would do better to listen to Chauncey Gardiner.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Remember his famous line? He liked to watch.\par \par \uc2 All right, \uc2 Ken Fisher\uc2  on that, not too worried about the economy falling to a recession.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, should you be worried about these thousands of \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents being hired? We got a few more details as to what they're going to do and who they will be going after -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you just have a little over a week right now to file your taxes, if you haven't done so already.\par \par \uc2 But, this week, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  has been detailing a little bit more about what it plans to do with that $80 billion it's been granted to hire more agents, more people to take phone calls and the like, and the promise at this point that it will not be going after the $400,000-and-under crowd in terms of increased audits and the like.\par \par \uc2 It's targeting rich guys and big corporations.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Douglas Holtz-Eakin is here, the other former White House -- the former \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  director.\par \par \uc2 Doug, first off, on this plan, a lot more agents...\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, FORMER CONGRESSIONAL BUDGET OFFICE DIRECTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... presumably in the hope of spending all this money to raise a whole lot of money, more, money in tax revenue from people, the rich, presumably, corporations secondarily, who aren't paying their fair share. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Well, the claim from the CBO was that you spend $80 billion, you will raise another $180 billion in tax revenue. We shall see.\par \par \uc2 There are two big things going on here, as you mentioned. One is better customer service. Hire lots of customer service reps, give them better technologies, presumably have your whole \uc2 IRS\uc2  experience be so pleasant that you might pay twice, I don't know, but big customer service push.\par \par \uc2 And then audit, in particular, large corporations, large partnerships, wealthy individuals, and high-risk -- quote -- "emerging" issues, things like crypto. So the claim is you're not going to get anyone under $400,000. They're not going to aim at them. But it's hard for me to imagine you won't get hit in the crossfire.\par \par \uc2 You can have a relatively $200,000 person makes a crypto investment, and if that's an audit flag, they're going to end up in the net. And it does raise the question, how are they going to know who's really under $400,000 and who's just lying about their income to avoid taxes?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: So that's not answered in this report anywhere.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you have to be very careful the way they parse their language. There must be some very smart lawyers there as well, because they're not saying that the ratios will change. In other words, people will still get audited, whether you're under or over $400,000, especially if you're under.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In fact, that crowd makes up nine out of 10 people who are screened by the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , because that is where the bulk of the money is, because that's where most people are.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So that's not going to change. So people who think, OK, I will get a pass because I'm, let's say, under this $400,000 figure, that is not the case.\par \par \uc2 What they're promising is there won't be an increase in audits of that bunch.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you buy that?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: It'll be hard to tell, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: They claim the audit rates will not go up for those under $400,000, but, as I said, there are some other things they're trying to do, like crypto and other emerging issues, that may apply to those under $400,000.\par \par \uc2 I don't see how they avoid raising the audit rate if they're not -- if going to go after those.\par \par \uc2 So I take it as a statement that we're not after the middle class, but the money is in the middle class in America. That's the reality. And if they want to raise a lot of money through \uc2 IRS\uc2  enforcement, they're going to have to touch the middle class to do it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. We will watch them closely and see what the details are...\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... when they fan out with those agents and start going after folks who they feel maybe have not -- have not paid their fair share. So we will watch that.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we do always here put out calls to the powers that be, the Treasury secretary, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 . We have not heard back from her office. We did put out similar calls to the \uc2 IRS\uc2  commissioner, Danny Werfel. They are looking at the possibility of joining us at some date in the future. So hope springs eternal.\par \par \uc2 We want to be fair and balanced with everyone. Remember, there's no yelling or screaming on this show. No one cares what my opinions are, so I don't offer them. And there is no, no mean bone in my body. Well, it's a big body, so maybe there's one or two hiding in there.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have a lot more coming up. In fact, I was so fascinated by this case, I kept telling my crew, you have to book this guy. He is losing weight like nobody's business eating only \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's food. And I said to myself, self, this is someone I have to talk to.\par \par \uc2 And I will -- next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MAGINNIS, FAST FOOD WEIGHT LOSS PLAN: Can these nuggets of chicken quicken my fat being stricken? It seems like they might.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: I'm eating three meals a day. I'm eating half of whatever the meal is that they give me. That's it.\par \par \uc2 So it's got tomatoes and lettuce. It's like a salad inside of a sandwich. People who are worried about, am I getting enough fruits and veggies, there you go. And last time I checked, a french fry is a veggie.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, if a McDonald's french fry is a veggie, then count me in as a vegetarian.\par \par \uc2 I have been following Kevin Maginnis very closely. He's now on day 46 of a weight loss journey that's all \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's all the time. It's a 100-day mission, 100 straight days where he eats nothing but \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 , I believe three times a day.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, let him explain it to you. He joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: Neil, thank you so much. Glad to be here. I appreciate you having me on\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, are we getting you before dinner? Is that your dinner, or what's going on?\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: So this is going to be lunch today.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: I have got the Quarter Pounder with bacon. I have got my medium fries, apple pie, a little bit of water to wash it down. We should be good.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, you have been eating this way for 46 days, right? We will get into the details. It's not all that at one sitting, I should say, but how much weight have you lost?\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: So, 35 pounds down, 46 days in, and everything has improved, cholesterol, triglycerides, everything better.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, explain how you do it.\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: Super simple, just three meals a day, everything in half. You can have it if you halve it.\par \par \uc2 So I cut all the meals in half. And McFlurry, shakes, don't be bitter while I'm eating my \uc2 Apple\uc2  fritter and losing weight. As long as I cut it in half, it's all good.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So do you really go every day? If you're always cutting everything in half, you could take a day off and just eat the other half at home? Or what do you do?\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: So, normally, it's that meal that I have, like this half, it'll be lunch, and then I will have the other half for dinner.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, I see.\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: I waited on lunch today to have this conversation with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There we go. Well, I'm honored.\par \par \uc2 Now, your wife was seeing the progress you were making, and through no nudging on your own, she joined you, right?\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: That is absolutely correct. And she's about 10 days in, five pounds down. She says it's the most weight she's ever lost that fast, outside of having a baby.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, a lot of people are going to hear this and say, wait, this can't be healthy, he can't be doing it right. I know it's all about portion control, I get it. But you're invariably going to have some people saying, well, this isn't a healthy way to do it, eating fast food.\par \par \uc2 What do you say?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: I say, let -- let's follow along. Let's find out, because, again, had blood work done at the beginning, had it done at 35 days, I will have it done along the way.\par \par \uc2 Every way you can measure somebody, my health is improving, triglycerides down over 100 points, cholesterol down over 50 points. Our body can process the food when we don't overload it. It's the -- my wife used a great analogy the other day. She said, if you're putting gas in the car and you just kept pumping, gas would spill out on the ground.\par \par \uc2 But our bodies, if we overdo it, it overflows onto our hips. So if I'm not overloading my body, it's actually pulling from those fat reserves, burning off everything I put into it. So there's no -- it's -- my body can handle the processed food as long as I'm not overloading it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, are you overloading on the same stuff? I know \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  has a pretty vast menu, but you can't keep repeating Quarter Pounders, right?\par \par \uc2 So how do you vary it up?\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: So, luckily, I have -- they have been very, very gracious in allowing me to create new meals along the way.\par \par \uc2 So, this morning, for example, I had hash browns with sausage gravy, a McCrispy chicken patty with cheddar cheese -- or with American cheese on top of it. So I have made up my own menu items, like the McKevin, like the McMelody along the way.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: And it's -- there's tons of choices.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Have the McDonald's folks been in touch with you? They know what you're doing? I mean, you're a walking billboard for them.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: Yes. They're -- no contact from corporate whatsoever at this point.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Really?\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: I think -- I think...\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: They're loving it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you have a great sense of humor. Obviously, you're dealing with -- this is a diet, after all.\par \par \uc2 Do you have hunger pangs? Do you have days where you say, screw it, I'm not going to go a half, I'm going to eat the whole thing?\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: First three days, there was some of that. But your body acclimates, and because I'm not depriving myself of anything, I get to have anything I want, from the McFlurrys, to the shakes, to the Big Macs.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: So most people diet, diet, diet, binge. I have no need to binge, because I'm enjoying every single meal.\par \par \uc2 And because I wait in between, I seek the heat before I eat, meaning I wait until I feel that hunger pang.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: Three times a day, I'm hungry, but I'm not hungry throughout the day because my body's processing what I have put in it.\par \par \uc2 And then I'm able -- when I feel that heat again, that's my signal to go ahead and eat.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, doing something right. Keep us posted. I'd love to have you back, Kevin. For some reason here at FOX, they thought I should be the guy to talk to you. I don't know why that is.\par \par \uc2 But I wish you well, my friend, and we will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230407ej4700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Far-Left Democrats Calls TikTok Ban Push Racist And Xenophobic; White House Turning Into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  's Defense Attorney; L.A. School Strike: Teacher Dance, While Parents Scramble For Childcare; Dem City Pins Auto Thefts On Cars, Not Criminals; Professor Clinton: Hillary Announces She Will Tech Foreign Policy Class\b0\par\par\uc2 Greg Gutfeld, Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Dana  Perino \par \uc2 8,165 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 March 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DAVE DODSON, PROFESSOR, STANFORD SCHOOL OF BUSINESS: -- year after year when we could have made modest adjustments in the 70s, 80s and 90s. There's no -- there's absolutely no plan in place to fund those entitlements. So, we are going to be right behind France.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, I'm afraid -- I'm afraid you're right about that. Professor, thank you very much. We'll explore that more tomorrow. THE FIVE is now.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, I'm Greg Gutfeld along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters and a rain gutter is her waterslide, Dana Perino. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 If you have concerns about China, shut up you racist xenophobe. Far-left Democrats defending the Chinese-owned app TikTok, as its CEO gets hauled before Congress and testifies for the first time.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers are looking to ban it over national security risks. There are damning reports that the social media giant has been spying on journalists, scooping up the personal data of 150 million American users and pushing political propaganda. It's a rare bipartisan effort. But a member of the squad would rather make it about identity politics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMAAL BOWMAN (D-NY): Why the hysteria and the panic and the targeting of TikTok. Republicans in particular have been sounding the alarm, creating a red scare around China, let's not be racist towards China and express our xenophobia when it comes to TikTok.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have got no swag. That's why they want to ban TikTok. Republicans have got no swag. That's the problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: TikTok CEO trying to convince Congress that the company is not an agent of the Chinese Communist Party. But that didn't go well.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHOU ZI CHEW, CEO, TikTok: I have seen no evidence that the Chinese government has access to that data. They have never asked us, we have not provided.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ANNA G. ESHOO\uc2  (D-CA): You know what, I find that -- I find that actually preposterous.\par \par \uc2 CHEW: We don't sell data to any data brokers.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 FRANK PALLONE\uc2  , JR. (D-NJ): You don't sell to anyone?\par \par \uc2 CHEW: We don't sell data to data brokers.\par \par \uc2 PALLONE: I didn't ask you about data brokers.\par \par \uc2 REP. NEAL DUNN (R-FL): Has ByteDance spied on American citizens?\par \par \uc2 CHEW: I don't think the spying is the right way to describe it.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM WALBERG (R-MI): Do ByteDance employees in Beijing have access to American data?\par \par \uc2 CHEW: We rely on global interoperability and we have employees in China. So yes, the Chinese engineers do have access to global data.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: TikTok has repeatedly chosen the path for more control, more surveillance and more manipulation. Your platform should be banned.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: But what Dems really fear the most is that a TikTok ban could end the gravy train support from younger voters. Democrats from the top on down turn the -- turn to the app to reach Gen Z.\par \par \uc2 Remember Beto putting the lotion on the skin ahead of the midterms? And worse, a band would probably ruin Lori Lightfoot new career as a karaoke singer.\par \par \uc2 So, Jesse, I think this is like an important moment. Because this -- it's like the hearings like a hotel flashlight that shows you who's on the payroll and who isn't.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, looks like the squad member got taken to dinner by a few TikTok lobbyists.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's so obvious.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, dinner after dinner after dinner. And I'm sure there's more where that came from.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't matter to me if they banned it or not, because Biden's going to get blamed. Republicans aren't going to get blamed. All the young people that are on it are just going to blame the White House.\par \par \uc2 Now, I signed up for a TikTok account because I thought that was the thing to do to promote the show. And I go on and I enter in all my demographics, and then they asked me personal questions about my likes and my dislikes.\par \par \uc2 And all of a sudden, I log on and I'm flooded with the exact same video. 30-year-old white moms dancing to hip hop in kitchens. Apparently, that's what I love. And they know me and I don't like that because we spy on other people through our platforms. \uc2 Google\uc2  , \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  , the Chinese are allowed to do that. That's our territory.\par \par \uc2 But the political correctness thing, that's what really bothers me, because let's just say in World War II Bowman was around, he was going to give Truman hell for save Nagasaki, you don't want to bomb Nagasaki because they're going to call you racist against Japanese.\par \par \uc2 You don't want to let that woke ideology enter into national security because then that actually puts lives in danger.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, you know what's interesting, Dana, people are talking about the spying. They're talking about the data, but the thing -- it's a psy-op and we're in a way that China -- the company can amplify stories and videos that manipulate the mindsets of the people who use it. And that's when you're seeing, you know, teenage girls with depression and suicide boys who are no longer articulating with the opposite sex, preferring instead to look at these TikTok videos. Isn't that a bigger problem if you have kids?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, I thought one of the most interesting parts of the hearing was where he had to explain to everybody why his kids are not allowed on TikTok.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And China doesn't allow our apps in China. OK, so do we have to allow theirs in America? I don't know. I don't think the Biden administration has done a very good job of articulating the national security threat. One of the best questions yesterday at the White House briefing was from Grady Trimble from Fox Business. He asked, well, is the President for banning TikTok or having it sell off. But just last week for St. Patrick's Day, he made a St. Patrick's Day video on TikTok.\par \par \uc2 So, is it do as I say, not as I do? Or what does -- what does the president actually want people to think?\par \par \uc2 And then when you look at what has happened in the last week or so, in the media, there's been a defensive TikTok. Well, what happened? Let me give you a couple of numbers.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, TikTok spent $270,000 on lobbying, not that much. OK. Last year, 5.3 million. So, they know that they have a way to try to do that. So they brought their influences there -- influencers there, and somehow they got to Jamaal Bowman, but that's take -- that's a lot of money to spend to allow China who is undertaking a genocide, which we'll hear more about today when the China committee has their primetime hearing.\par \par \uc2 I do think that it is time for Americans to take one moment and say, wow, bipartisanship is still possible in America. That was a pretty good hearing today.\par \par \uc2 And the members of Congress were actually pretty prepared on a technological issue, which they're usually not.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, you know, Jessica, it's kind of it is -- it tells you the seriousness that people take this that actually left and right can get together and talk about it, but it must bother you that there are people on your side that always use race as an intimidation tactic, they don't want you to -- they don't want to debate so they just call you a racist. I'm tired of it, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: It bothers me mostly that you bring it up, I could live with it if it was just out there in the ether, but that I have to talk about it every day.\par \par \uc2 I was glad that he was so far out on a limb by himself in this particular defense. And it should add that \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  is on his side about it. So, it's not just a couple of squad members, plus, an important free speech organization.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Not anymore.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: The reputation has definitely been degraded for sure but it's still a something. It's not a nothing organization.\par \par \uc2 I thought the hearings were great. I still think the younger members showed up the older members a bit. But it was nice to see that Republican and Democratic members both knew what they were talking about and went for different angles.\par \par \uc2 You know, sometimes they just like hit the same thing. But it seems like everybody had a little piece of the puzzle. I don't know if it was bipartisan coordination, which would be incredible. But I was impressed by that.\par \par \uc2 One of the outstanding issues and I should note, so the only social media that I have is \uc2 Twitter\uc2  . And I use that to stay in touch with the news. I'm very scared of social media generally.\par \par \uc2 And I was talking to a tech journalist who actually has a separate phone for TikTok. She's aware of the fact that she needs to be on it, but doesn't want it to be anywhere near her other data, doesn't let her kids on it.\par \par \uc2 But one issue that I don't think was well enough addressed by the we want to ban it folks is the five million small businesses that are on TikTok. And this is the issue as well with like \uc2 Amazon\uc2  marketplaces, you need to make sure that you're taking care of the people whose livelihoods are dependent on this. And that isn't the 23-year-old influencers that are, you know, roasting tomato and feta on a one sheet and then pouring it into something. Those are people who won't survive off of this without it.\par \par \uc2 And I realized TikTok wasn't around a few years ago. So, nature of the business there will be something new. I hope it is an American -- why can't we make American TikTok?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, it's the big old \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, but dancing.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, you could -- you could absolutely move from TikTok to some of the American social media platforms.\par \par \uc2 But the amazing part of this is that I didn't hear a lot of discussion today. And by the way, I was thrilled that it was bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 I mean, as an American, I looked at them testifying, and I said, isn't that great? The Democrats and the Republicans are agreeing on something.\par \par \uc2 But you know, the dangers, their biometric identifiers, I mean they are exploiting us. And this guy who comes in, he was evasive, he was misleading. I think he thought -- someone said he wasn't prepared. No, I think he thinks that we're stupid, that ByteDance is an answerable, that the parent company TikTok is an answerable to the CCP, that the CCP can't just come in and say to ByteDance, you know, I want to see everything that's on there.\par \par \uc2 And then when you kind of join it with what else is going on, you know, the China, the CCP just had a spy craft going over our intercontinental ballistic missile sites. You just have Putin now meeting with \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  talking about a new world order.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's not a surprise that they would use this particular medium to get information on us to be able to spy on what our kids do, and be able to know everything about what's going on. And while we're at it, we ought to look at \uc2 Google\uc2  , we ought to look at our own social media site.\par \par \uc2 And these people who respond with its racist to go -- to be against the Chinese, it just shows you how shallow they are. They're not interested in looking into what the problem is. And he doesn't even talk about the Uyghurs or the concentration camps, or, you know, the genocide, as Dana mentioned, that's going on in China.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's just everything is through the lens of racism. Well, you've got to give that up. You've got to deal with the facts, because racism doesn't mean anything anymore. It doesn't mean anything to me anymore when someone says someone else's racist.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, I think this was a good day for America. I think with the continuing hearings on China, people will hopefully start to recognize how dangerous China is to the United States.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know, the downside to banning TikTok, no libs of TikTok.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And you know what? Maybe that's why the Dems want it gone. Because the libs of TikTok has done more to expose wokeism on social media than almost --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: On \uc2 Twitter\uc2  too, so let's take it over.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, no, but I mean, it's -- the best stuff comes from TikTok. The crazy screwed-up teachers.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I don't follow her.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: With more piercings and a bait and tackle shop, I love it.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, Hunter's got a brand new powerful defense attorney, it's the Biden White House.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: The White House acting more like Hunter's defense attorney. Questio