h about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and actually focus on his own state, because now you have all of these parents who had to scramble to get day care for their children. Some of the children -- and children love their teachers, so they're like, well, OK, we'll go help you. Like, we're going to be there.\par \par \uc2 This is the greatest reason to have school choice because if you're a parent right now like, watching what's happening to your school system knowing that there's $4.6 billion of COVID relief funds that were meant to go to schools that aren't going there, hey, I'd be willing to give the support staff a raise. Maybe they deserve it. That'd be fine. Like, let's talk about that. But to do this is really pouring salt in the wound to parents who have one, watch their children have a major learning loss from COVID, and now basically have to scramble to try to get care for their kids.\par \par \uc2 And they know that their kids aren't going to school at a time when most urban centers do not have kids reading, writing, or doing math at grade level.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, Greg, Dana mentioned, the raise is part of this which is what it's about. So, this is for support staff like bus drivers, teachers' aides, people who work in the cafeterias, they want a 30 increase, and have already been given a 23 percent increase. So, it does feel like we should be able to get a solution here and get kids back in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I mean, if they want more money, you should be funding the student and not the school. Because whenever the money goes to the school, we don't know where it goes. And the COVID relief is the perfect thing. It's time for tough love for the schools and that is when you're seeing it happen across the country, you know, states are voting to fund this -- fund the student instead of the school. So, the student can actually go with the money and the school get -- that forces the schools to compete for that money.\par \par \uc2 You said something about kids love their teachers. They used to, right? I bet everybody here could name their favorite teacher growing up. I named it immediately. It's still in my head. Mrs. Benoit, second grade. I could actually probably go through first through eighth grade. Everybody could do that. And somehow these unions did a really rare thing. They destroyed the reputation of something really, really heroic. They turned it into a punch line.\par \par \uc2 Imagine if there was like a union -- a national union of grandparents and they were really mean and scary, you know, and they would go on strike and they were off -- and it was like -- it was like, instead of being sweet and lovely like your grandparents, that's exactly what this is. Like, you look at the teachers unions, they're the exact opposite of how you think your teachers are. They seem angry. They seem like they're -- that they don't like you, that they don't like the kids. Every time you see them on Zoom, they seem --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, Zoom.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, cynical. They sound like me.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: So, Jesse, I'm consumed what -- by the optics of -- the idea that you would do this, which I think you should stand in solidarity with the bus drivers and people who make school possible. But why dance?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I think they can dance.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Like, that's what they did.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Because if they're going to play music and you're outside in the rain -- and you got to give them credit for protesting in the rain, that why shouldn't they be able to dance? What -- are they just supposed to hold the stupid signs and walk around in a circle like idiots? That is the worst. And that wouldn't be good television if they did that. This is better television if they're dancing.\par \par \uc2 And if they want to make themselves feel alive and show their spirit, and if they want to dance like that, that's fine. What I didn't like is the head of the teachers union went to a suite to a Clipper's game. That I don't like. Now, you shouldn't be -- first of all, if you're going to go to a Clippers game, you got to be in the nosebleeds. You don't want to be in a suite if everybody else is out there making 25,000 a year, the support staff. The custodians --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And don't wear yellow circle.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes. And Steph Curry drops 50 points. So, it's a good game.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's a great game.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: But it's not a good look. So, I'm fine with the decorum of the dancing but not the in-suite action at the Clippers game.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Got it. Well, at least it was Clippers and not Lakers. Because Lakers would be in everybody's face. Judge, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I think I disagree with everybody at the table. I don't like the idea that the teachers, and there are 35,000 of them are protesting in solidarity, which means that a thousand public schools are closed because the teachers have decided not to come in.\par \par \uc2 Now, I get it. The custodians, the bus drivers, and all the other people are not coming in. And if they want 30 percent instead of 23 percent, God bless them if they want to strike. But you know what this shows you more than anything about where the teacher's hearts are. We always talk about the fact that we have a favorite teacher. I want teachers to have a favorite class. I want them to have favorite students. I don't want to hear that the historic lows in reading and math.\par \par \uc2 And the president of the teachers union that you were just talking about, Jesse, says it doesn't matter if the kids don't know timetables, at least they learned about BLM riots in 2020. And now, when we get reports that IQs are down in the last decade by I think it's 10 percent for the first time in decades, she says -- this is the president of the teachers union. She says, there's no such thing as learning loss. Well, that's not too smart because part of the COVID money is to address learning loss. Part of the coveted money is to make sure that these kids can at least be addressed or have addressed their own academic losses.\par \par \uc2 And you've got the mayor who's 400 miles away. You've also got \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  was too quiet. That's because the \uc2 United Federation of Teachers\uc2  has given their approval. They are a Democrat-funded organization and they give their money to the Democrats. And so, everybody's in agreement. Who stands in solidarity with the kids? That's what I want to know.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I have a solution. You take the billion dollars L.A. spends on the homeless. You take a little bit of that and you give it to the custodians.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, I don't have any problem with them getting more money.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: There's money available. And it is interesting, I recently found out that most of these schools don't even know what has been allocated for them.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Right.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: There's such an information gap problem. OK, so coming up, cities under fire for blaming cars instead of criminals for surging auto thefts.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Try to follow this liberal logic. Car thefts are exploding in Democrat-led cities and instead of locking up the criminals, these politicians are blaming the cars. Milwaukee, a carjacking capital, is trying to sue \uc2 Kia\uc2  and Hyundai for making their cars too easy to steal. And 23 state AGs are blaming these automakers as well saying this. "It is well past time that you acknowledge your company's role and take swift and comprehensive action to remedy it."\par \par \uc2 Greg, this is pretty good.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Let's be honest, these cars are asking for it going out -- going out like that. You know, you're just begging to be assaulted. And you're definitely not going out with your top down. But it's so funny, you notice people always seek -- jump to a