ition, what his successes were during his first term as president, and just really address that issue that concern that many Republicans have.\par \par \uc2 And as for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , I think he needs to also follow your advice there of not listening to the swamp voices about what he should do, but really trusting his gut understanding that he should not be trying to distance himself so much from President Trump because of how much Republican voters do like those Trump policies.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, and Chris, to Mollie's point, then I'm going to get to a statement Trump made today, but to Mollie's point on DeSantis. I didn't see the entire interview. But I do know that he, when he talked about Putin today, he said, yes, I think he is guilty of war crimes. And I'm not saying that's a bad thing to say at all. But it does kind of begin to shift him over toward the GOP establishment, especially given what Xi and Putin just pulled off of their meeting and a new meeting scheduled in Beijing. Your thoughts on the Ukraine angle, which I did not address tonight?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BEDFORD, EXECUTIVE EDITOR, COMMON SENSE SOCIETY: Well, it's unless you can explain the difference between somebody committed war crimes and the just interests of the United States. Of course, it's just for the United States to try to stop war crimes. But as always, in U.S. interests, when you're quoting, World War III, that's something as not all been considered here in Washington, DC, as some of the people like DeSantis and a lot of Americans are actually wondering.\par \par \uc2 So, you don't have to actually say, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is a good guy. You can sit back and say, obviously, this is a bad man. But is it in the American interest to take boys from Iowa and girls from New Jersey and to get them potentially embroiled in a massive and escalating war in Ukraine, because that is the kind of danger we're playing with here. And DeSantis, I think, at least so far, has done a good job of threading that needle.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: And Mollie, I want to get your reaction to the statement President Trump released today criticizing DeSantis. It gets to something I hit on in the Angle, he said he is for a Republican, an average governor. Ron was a big lockdown governor; other Republican governors did much better than Ron. And because I allowed them this freedom, never closed their states. Mollie, do you think anyone in America thinks lockdowns when they hear \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Not very many people would think that because even while he did have some shutdowns early on, in the COVID pandemic response, he was kind of known for resisting that and he had so much success. It's one of his things that he's probably if he runs, he will be running - touting his great success and how he handled the response to the COVID pandemic much better than other governors did. And that's just another thing I want to say, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  should focus on what a good governor he is and how he's shown such leadership and how other Republican governors should be, but you know he should not backtrack.\par \par \uc2 He had some good statements on the Ukraine war that matched with what the average Republican voter and even average American really thinks about the Biden, McConnell foreign policy that we are being subjected to right now. When he kind of catered to that beltway establishment swamp mindset. I think that worries a lot of people that he won't have the courage of his convictions to stay strong. I mean, it was - he's been pretty nuanced in both ways. So, we'll see how it goes. But he does need to show courage under fire from how the beltway establishment is going to react to him and not adopting the failed foreign policy that they have been pushing for decades\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Chris, look, Trump has the experience with obviously these foreign leaders. He knows what it's like to face down \uc2 NATO\uc2 , sets