e GOP is so stupid generally the professional class, these are the only two candidates who are running in the populist lane, so it's going to be up to them to try and figure out who's going to be the - who's going to be the nominee. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's got the excitement, but DeSantis I think does have a better team.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mollie and Chris, great to see both of you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, you've invited me to speak here and I've been heckled nonstop. And I'm just asking for an administrator.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That's an administrator.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Your racism is showing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Your advocacy, your opinions from the bench, land as absolute disenfranchisement of their rights. It's uncomfortable to say for many people here your work has caused harm. Has caused harm. Has caused harm. And I know that must be uncomfortable to hear.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, the snapping of the fingers again. Now tonight we have an update for you on those hideous attacks on a Trump appointed judge. Now let's start with Tirien Steinbach. She's the Associate Dean-lit of Diversity Equity Inclusion at Stanford Law School, who not only allowed this abuse to happen, but she jumped into engage in it herself.\par \par \uc2 Now the school revealing today in a lengthy 10-page memo that the dean has been placed on leave, though it's not clear whether it was disciplinary or voluntary. Now as for those students who engaged in this disturbing behavior. Here's where I'll remind you of how bad it got.\par \par \uc2 Now Judge Kyle Duncan wrote in The Wall Street Journal that as he entered the classroom, one protester screamed, we hope your daughters get raped. So, what will happen to the future lawyers of America? Nothing. The memo from the cowards at Stanford said the students will not be disciplined because administrators sent conflicting signals about whether what was happening was acceptable or not. Well, apparently Stanford students need to be told by professors, whether it's OK to hope an invited speaker's daughters get raped.\par \par \uc2 And at a Senate Armed Services Subcommittee, we learn not just that the Pentagon is doling out six-figure DEI salary jobs, that they're working with doctors who are now suggesting that seven-year-olds may be able to make decisions on gender affirming care. Plus, another day goes by without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's indictment in that hush money case. So, is this over? Former Acting Attorney General \uc2 Matt Whitaker\uc2  has insight\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It is a long past time for Republicans to start auditing how all Pentagon dollars are spent. Our troops aren't served by bloated budgets. They keep getting these giant increases in the defense budget. Well, why are they going to change.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, how many times have I said that stop giving a blank check to the Pentagon because when we don't demand answers on where the money is actually going, we get situations that are horrific like this where Pentagon doctors say, we need to make it easier for military youth to get so-called gender affirming care, including puberty blockers. Now, these doctors are also saying that we can include children as young as age seven, in the decision-making process. But of course, that's just the tip of the iceberg.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is the story of a soldier who operates your nation's Patriot missile defense systems. It begins in California. With a little girl raised by two moms, I needed my own adventures, my own challenge. And after meeting with an army recruiter, I found it a way to prove my inner strength and maybe shatter some stereotypes along the way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How did we go from be all that you can be to cartoons? Why is everything a cartoon today? Look, I'm grateful for anyone putting on the uniform male or female for our freedoms, of course, it's amazing. And just like the woman you just saw, but there is no need to make DEI a driver in recruitment, or a central part of our military training. The Army missed its recruiting goals we've reported on last year by 25 percent, as our enemies like China grow stronger, and have the largest standing army now in the world.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ERIC SCHMITT (R-MO): I want to ask each of you, have you heard, is there any intelligence that you've heard that Communist China is somehow intimidated or deterred by our DEI initiatives? I'll take that as a collective no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now is man you just saw, Missouri Senator \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2 . Senator, good to see you tonight. Now, Biden is asking for a Pentagon budget increase, and it goes now to $900 billion. So, my question is, how do Republicans continue to just rubber stamp the kind of spending, given what we just laid out, what they're actually doing within the ranks of our own military?\par \par \uc2 SCHMITT: Well, I serve on the Armed Services Committee, Laura, and we were asking some really tough questions today. I mean, it goes beyond. First of all, the Biden Administration, from the very beginning has injected identity politics, it's really divisive ideology into everything. I mean, for grants for schools with CRT, and it's now made its way into the military. The military has been this apolitical place where it's a true meritocracy, where people from the humblest beginnings can achieve great things, no matter your skin color, your creed.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have ticker tape parades for people who serve this country. That's what the military is all about. This divisive ideology is hurting recruitment. They're asking for $114 million. Laura for just programming for DEI, six-figure salaries for DEI consultants in the military. This stuff has no place. You know, we need a military that stands ready to defeat China to - it's - you mentioned it, they are building islands in the South China Sea. They're fully weaponized. Nobody in Communist China is deterred by all this DEI training, we need a lethal fighting force and focused on that as opposed to this sort of woke politics, the Biden Administration is hellbent on injecting everyone.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, I think it's actually--\par \par \uc2 SCHMITT: Including the military.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator, I'm going to go further. I think it's hurting recruitment. I think all of this kind of woke stuff is hurting recruitment, the vaccine mandates that hurt recruitment. But if it's not anything different than like a college campus, and the way kind of these issues are addressed, then a lot of people would say, I could do that somewhere else. So, it's not the military I thought I was going to enter, but one of your Democrat colleagues did have a potential solution to the military's recruitment troubles. Check this out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TAMMY DUCKWORTH (D-IL): We need to also talk about other ways to expand that recruiting pool. This year, I'm introducing the Enlist Act, the Enlist Act will aid the services recruitment efforts by allowing a highly skilled and motivated individuals to succeed in the military. Who would be the people who could do this, DACA folks, Dreamers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, is this the workable solution? Is this the way to rebuild our military, senator?\par \par \uc2 SCHMITT: No, we need - I mean, this is just insanity. I mean, it's just one, it's a parade of terribles one after another. Look, we have incredible men and women of our armed forces. We need more of them. The way that we're deterring more and more people from joining the military is with this divisive woke politics that's found its way again, in every crevice of society. Now, thanks to the Biden Administration. The military is no place for this.\par \par \uc2 We have to be ready for the threats that we face around the world including China, specifically China, and this kind of stuff deters recruitment. It makes it difficult for retention, we've heard from servicemembers Laura, who have come forward and said they're uncomfortable with this. Dividing people by oppressor and oppressed, the totem pole of grievances has no place in our military.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, senator, thank you for pursuing this. We're going to stay on this that this should not be funded.\par \par \uc2 Everything seems set up for Manhattan District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  to bring the long awaited liberal hero, of course, he was going to be the one to take down Trump. But there were just a few problems with this. Not only was his plan was predicated on flimsy legal arguments, he was relying on a porn star and a perjurer. And now his reliance on \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  is coming back to bite him. Cohen said under oath that Trump asked him to pay off Daniels with his own personal funds from a home equity line of credit to avoid any money being traced back to him. But in a newly unearthed letter from February, 2018, to the \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2 , Cohen's attorney relayed something far different, saying, quote, "Mr. Cohen used his own personal funds and that neither the Trump organization nor the Trump campaign was a party to the transaction and neither reimbursed Mr. Cohen for the payment directly or indirectly."\par \par \uc2 Wow. Here now is former acting attorney general during the Trump administration, \uc2 Matthew Whitaker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW WHITAKER\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP ACTING ATTORNEY GENERAL: Hi, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Matt, is this an exculpatory document that could change the debate here and perhaps change where this case is going?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Yes, it's good to be with you tonight. I think it does two things. First of all, it gives Monday's witness, Robert Costello, a lot of credibility because that's what he's saying and has been consistent throughout. And now \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  has been on both sides.\par \par \uc2 And as you know, prosecutors love to ask, were you telling the truth then or are you telling the truth now? I think it really is giving the prosecution team pause when they think about putting this not only in front of the grand jury and trying to get them to vote to indict President Trump, but ultimately in front of a jury trial and what those 12 citizens will think with this evidence.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, the sense is that if something happens, it will happen early next week. And this buildup, I think, is also working in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's favor in the court of public opinion. I had a couple of Democrat friends of mine from college who were messaging me, going, oh, my gosh, the Democrats are going to screw this up. They always take it a step too far. We saw it with Mueller. We saw it with the ridiculous impeachments, and now we're seeing it again. I think in the end this ultimately is not a smart move. Forget the case, it's obviously ridiculous. But politically, I don't think this plays well at all.\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: No, this plays into the way that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been portrayed as a victim of the left's pursuit of him. And I think it makes him stronger in his base's mind's eye. And so I think you're absolutely right. The left is going to overplay this. This case is so weak to start out that it's only going to get weaker. And the fact that \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  is contorting himself into a pretzel to try to even bring charges I think demonstrates just how bad this idea is for the left to try to prosecute Trump under this set of facts, which have already been rejected by the Southern District of New York and the \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Does either the Georgia case or the classified documents case, apparently how certain questions were answered, do either of those, very quickly, pose serious legal trouble to Trump, in your mind?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: No, I don't think they do. In all of these situations I think they are always taking the facts and circumstances and using the worst case scenario against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  instead of taking the objective facts and circumstances and applying the law to it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Matt, great to see you tonight, as always. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 And Fauci is ready for yet another close up, and an L.A. teacher's strike looks more like a dance party. Raymond Arroyo has it all on his book tour. "Seen and Unseen" next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for our "Seen and Unseen" segment where we reveal the stories behind the headlines. And for that we turn to FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo author of the new soon to be bestselling book -- you go ahead, Raymond. You can say the title.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: We're going to do that later. We've got to get to this.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, all right.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I've got to show you this Dr. Fauci documentary.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, all right. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You remember back in 2021 that \uc2 Disney\uc2 + Fauci doc. He has got another one now, this time courtesy of the taxpayer and \uc2 PBS\uc2 's "American Masters." This is a show dedicated to American artists, Laura, you know, Ella Fitzgerald and Martha Graham. Now the guy who gave us the Fauci ouchie is being featured as a cultural hero, and Mrs. Fauci says it's hard living with a megastar, I mean a bureaucrat.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE GRADY, DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 'S WIFE: And I also get to see lots of positive feedback that he gets from people.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You're one of my heroes.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Yes, good to see you. Take care.\par \par \uc2 GRADY: You would think that some of the gratitude was a positive thing. It doesn't feel positive. It feels exposed. You feel so exposed. That just doesn't feel good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Then may I make a suggestion. Stop inviting documentary crews to shadow your every move and into your home, and maybe it won't be so exposed. My goodness, they're like the Harry and Meghan of D.C.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How about use the treadmill.\par \par \uc2 Now wait a minute. Last year, Fauci said that he didn't see himself, I thought, as a celebrity or a cult figure.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: I'm a physician. I'm a scientist. I direct a very important institute. I don't think about it in terms of being a cult person and everybody idolizing me. That's just a reflection of what is going on in society and really has very little to do with what I am and who I am and what I do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Really? The \uc2 PBS\uc2  documentary, Laura, furnishes us with some pictures of Fauci's home office. Look at this. Who has portraits of themselves and pillows of themselves and dolls of themselves and sketches of themselves on their desks everywhere? Either he is the greatest patron of the arts we've ever seen, or he has got the biggest ego, bigger than the vaccine's myocarditis rate, OK. This is like lifestyles of the rich and infamous.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I do have a book cover of myself behind me.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I do have a few pictures of myself.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I know, but that's a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestseller. You don't have it all over the house. This guy has got paraphernalia about him everywhere.\par \par \uc2 And Laura, in L.A., public school teachers are on a three-day strike demanding that the lowest paid public school workers receive a 30 percent salary increase while 500,000 kids were stuck at home. The teachers were line dancing in the streets.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's just like the healthcare workers dancing, you remember that, when they had the white coats.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The difference is we gave these people, the taxpayer, $4,626 million in COVID relief. The school board offered them a 23 percent increase. They won't take anything but 30 percent. I'm sorry, you cannot hold these kids hostage any longer. They went 18 months without schooling. That's enough.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And they are bad dancers.\par \par \uc2 Raymond, the teachers abandoning kids because they don't care about the kids. It's all about them and the teacher's unions, I mean. It's a good segue, I think, to your new book, which is going to be a huge hit. It's a children's book called "The Unexpected Light of Thomas Alva Edison." A lot of people think they know about \uc2 Edison\uc2 's life. It's just out. But tell us why you end up calling him the patron saint of home schooling. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I don't know why he is not regarded as the patron saint of home schooling. Look, Laura, this series, it's called turnabout tales. It's about crisis points in these young lives of historic figures. In the case of Edison, he was thrown out of school at eight years old. The schoolmaster said he was addled and couldn't be taught. His mother said you are wrong. My son has more intelligence than you do. She took him home. She home schooled him and allowed him to read deeply and experiment, learn with his head and his hands. That was the last formal schooling he ever received.\par \par \uc2 This is the man that gave us the microphone, motion picture cameras, lights, alkaline battery. It's an incredible story of never giving up on a child and how a parent's devotion can tend a light that can burn clear into the next century. And that's what we have in \uc2 Edison\uc2 . It's a great story.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And I know this from my own experience is not every child fits into the same square peg, round hole kind of deal. A lot of kids don't learn the same way. And they're more tactile, and they can learn much better that way and be --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: \uc2 Edison\uc2  had ADHD, they think now, Laura. So this is a book of hope for those who have been cast aside or told you can't or you're not good enough.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Raymond, Raymond, I know -- it seems like you're always on book tours. The unexpected light of the spider with the wise men tour.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Oh, stop it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Honestly, I've never seen anyone on a book tour so often. But you're on a book tour and you're going to my favorite place, The Villages, where you and I have had so much fun.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Which is where I'm going to check out your next retirement home.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That's on Saturday with \uc2 Barnes & Noble\uc2  of The Villages, Jacksonville, I'm in New Orleans on Monday, the Reagan Library next Wednesday. All the details are at RaymondArroyo.com. And if people care about history and you want an inspiring tale for Easter, this might be the one for your family.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Raymond thank you. Best of luck out there. And say to all my future friends at The Villages.\par \par \uc2 First whales, now dolphins. So where are the save the planet fanatics? They are actually causing mass maritime death events along our eastern seaboard. Commercial fisherman and New Jersey Mayor Kirk Larson has some thoughts next. So stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: More on these strange stories of dead whales washing up on the east coast, nearly two dozen since December alone. And now a New Jersey town just witnessing a different horror, a mass dolphin stranding event.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A group of dolphins just turned and ran straight in for the beach. I'm looking through the binoculars, and I noticed that there were six of them that were flailing on the beach and realized that they weren't going to get back out again. So I called the police.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When my officers arrived, it was determined that they found eight between 51st and 52nd.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So why did they beach themselves? In a press release the stranding center says they share the public's sorrow for the loss of the animals and they hope that they will inform them as to the dolphins' cause of death. Onlookers, however, came with increasing speculations about the sonar surveying for wind farms going on off the beach.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My first take is maybe the wind turbines that they put offshore.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The fact that they won't stop the testing for the windmill farms is really, it's really sad to everybody in our -- actually not say. We're mad. We are mad they are not stopping it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Another person angry that these projects aren't stopped is Barnegat Light, New Jersey Mayor Kirk Larson. He has been a commercial fisherman since he was 12 years old. Mayor Larson, these windfarms also pose a danger to fishermen, do they not?\par \par \uc2 KIRK LARSON, (R) MAYOR OF BARNEGAT LIGHT, NEW JERSEY: Thank you for having me, Laura, on a cold night here in Barney Light. These dolphins it's something new to us here. We have been hearing one or two here and there, but I have never heard of eight of them at a time coming to the beach like that. A couple of years ago here in Barney Light, we had two whales here one year. So it's getting to be a problem and very expensive to clean up the beaches also. So it's just a shame that these whales, this just keeps happening, and nobody wants to find out the reason why.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Where is the federal government on this? Where is the Interior Department? Where are the maritime experts working with an Interior, and I guess the Energy Department because they are pushing these massive windfarms in the Atlantic Ocean and in the Pacific Ocean that are going to take up lord knows how many miles of space. They're giant. They obviously make that very loud whirring sound. And we know whales and dolphins operate by their own sonar communication. So the sonar that is thrown off by this testing looks like it very well might be the cause of their mass stranding. It's spooking them and they are moving into a beach area instead of out toward the ocean. What else could it possibly be at this point? Does anyone have any other explanation?\par \par \uc2 LARSON: No. And they are supposed to do some mammal testing here on some of this, I've heard, but they have not done it yet. I think they are waiting -- I think they are in a hurry to get these windmills up, really, before the tax credits run out.\par \par \uc2 But it would be nice if they did some studies on these whales. The commercial fishermen are seeing them offshore. They have a video of one out of Point Pleasant the other day of thrashing around in the ocean like he was going to die any minute. That was offshore. So we are only seeing the edge along the beach there where the mortalities happen.\par \par \uc2 I know in the commercial fishing industry, Laura, we can't even drop a net in the water without some kind of a plan --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 LARSON: -- something to get our -- we do get a certain amount of takes on turtles. We have done a great job in the industry staying away from turtles. And we wish everybody would do the same, be held to the same standard if we could, you know.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That would be nice.\par \par \uc2 LARSON: It doesn't seem to be. It doesn't seem to be.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is insanity. The greeniacs and the save the whale people, where are they? They're nowhere to be found. I guess because green energy trumps animal rights. Mayor Larson -- and fisherman's rights.\par \par \uc2 LARSON: Well, I think --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mayor Larson, we have to go, but we're going to be following this story. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Kamala drops a line for the ages. That is the Last Bite next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, we just don't understand why people are giving \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  such a hard time, trying to push her off the ticket in 2024. Because once again, today she dazzled us with her oratorical skills.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: During Women's History Month, we celebrate and we honor the women who made history throughout history, who saw what could be unburdened by what had been.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wow. Forget my Angle advice. Trump and DeSantis, with her on the ticket, don't have a chance.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020230323ej3m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hunter Biden Countersues Computer Shop Owner; Sen. Tom Cotton (R- AR), Is Being Interviewed About China, Wanting To Be Sort Of The Power Broker Of Diplomacy On Ukraine-Russia War; Some \uc2 SVB\uc2  Employees Blame Remote Work For Failure; New York Lowers Test Score Standards After Pandemic\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, David Spunt, Jonathan Turley, Joe Concha, Jimmy  Failla, Madison Alworth \par \uc2 7,958 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 March 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: The Irish Prime Minister is not in Ireland. He's here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: He's here.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: To meet with the President and the Vice President. So, \uc2 Leo Varadkar\uc2  is not there enjoying the celebration, so probably hoist a pint or two here. We got some good Irish pubs in D.C.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The Guinness looked delicious, didn't it John? You don't have to find yourself one.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: There's nothing like a fresh Guinness.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Indeed. All right, John, great to be with you this week and Happy St. Patrick's Day to all of you. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: John Roberts. We'll see you again on Monday. "THE STORY" starts right now with Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, guys, thank you very much. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum at Fox News headquarters in New York, breaking right now on the story. New revelations in the Hunter Biden saga as the president's son sues for invading his privacy by copying and sharing what was on a laptop, while still trying to thread the needle to not admit that the laptop is actually his.\par \par \uc2 Biden's new ish, more aggressive legal team, led by former Clinton Attorney Abbe Lowell now turning tables as we also learn new leads on the Biden family money trail, and zero in on the remaining unknown Biden who appears to have received $70,000 from the Chinese electric company in a deal with apparent ties to the Biden family enterprise. So, Jonathan Turley and Joe Concha, both standing by on this St. Patty's Day but first we go to Justice Correspondent David Spunt, who gives us the very latest this afternoon on a Friday from there on this case. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. You mentioned the word aggressive, it's clear \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's legal team has upped the ante a little bit over the past few months. But this counterclaim today, and it is a countersuit against this computer repair shop owner make some interesting observations and interesting questions including the actual questioning of who owned the laptop. I want to go ahead and read this specific part. It's a footnote, it says: "This is not an admission by Mr. Biden that Mac Isaac, the shop owner or others in fact possessed any particular laptop containing electronically stored data belonging to Mr. Biden. Rather, Mr. Biden simply acknowledges that at some point, Mac Isaac obtained electronically stored data, some of which belonged to Mr. Biden."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voiceover): Now, the countersuit, 42 pages, and alleges that, that computer shop owner, John Paul Mac Isaac improperly took Biden's information from the laptop, made copies distributed to multiple people. Mac Isaac says Hunter never retrieved his laptop and he's done nothing wrong. Now, Mac Isaac sued Biden earlier this month, today is merely a response. According to the lawsuit filed by Hunter's team today, "No matter how they came into his initial possession, Mac Isaac improperly access files that he admits were 'none of his business' even though he was never given permission by Mr. Biden to access or review any data of Mr. Biden's.\par \par \uc2 I'll continue, Mac Isaac knew or should have known that the data he possessed and shared contain private and confidential information and content, including sensitive and private photographs and video of Mr. Biden, and that Mr. Biden had a reasonable expectation of privacy in this data and Mac Isaac's conduct would expose Mr. Biden's personal life and an egregious violation of Mr. Biden's right to privacy." It also goes on, Martha, McIsaac has continued his invasion of Mr. Biden's right to privacy by publishing a book and making media appearances which discuss or include data about Mr. Biden obtained by Mac Isaac.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): I'm at the Department of Justice, DOJ has been investigating \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  for at least five years since 2018. And still, no word if any charges will come down, if charges won't happen, but perhaps there will be some new information over the coming weeks and months. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting angle here today. David, thank you very much. So, with that, we bring in Constitutional Attorney Jonathan Turley, a Washington -- George Washington Law Professor and Fox News Contributor. Jonathan, good to have you here. I want to get to the big picture, but just a quick thought on this, does this lawsuit by \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's team: A. prove that this is indeed his material? And, you know, I mean, what else are we learning from this entire thing from Mac Isaac?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it's like Alice in Wonderland, it gets curiouser and curiouser, because this, this is one of the weirdest filings I have read in some time, because he's telling the court, this may or may not be my laptop, and these files may or may not be my files, but I'm egregiously injured by the invasion of my privacy. It's very hard to follow this chain of logic and this filing in the absence of saying, yes, I dropped off the laptop, did not pick it up. And it contains authentic e- mails.\par \par \uc2 So, they're trying very hard to make out a case without the key elements in my view. I mean, this is like complaining that someone it may or may not have stolen my car. You know, the police will usually say, well, could you pin that one down a bit? You know, is your car missing? And it's -- that's what is so weird about this. Now, they may be trying this type of equivocation because he is being criminally investigated. And they don't want to admit that these files are clearly his. But it's getting a bit long to be playing sort of Hamlet in all of these filings of debating whether it is or is not his laptop.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's interesting because they claim that 90 days -- Mac Isaac says, you know, I tried really hard to get in touch with him to give it back to him for 90 days. Biden's side, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  side is saying that there's a one-year clause in Delaware, personal property can't be given away to someone else, or exploited in any way for, for one year. How does that hold up to you, Jonathan?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Yes, but -- yes, that is itself a little bit of as a wicked turn to it. Because the Delaware law that I looked at after the filing talks about someone having a year to claim their property, he's still not claiming the property. He's still equivocating whether this is his property or not. And I think a court is going to be somewhat curious as to what the status not just of a \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is in this case, but the computer itself.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's going on almost two years. The other thing is that, you know, this is not the only laptop that he says, might have gone missing, you know, some of these files contain, for example, an exchange where he says that he thought Russian intelligence agents took an earlier laptop after one of his all night, sort of, party things. So, I suppose the, the Russians are also being warned that they could be injuring his privacy by stealing his laptop if that's true.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Before I let you go, Jonathan, I just want to get a quick thought on this. Let's put up this money trail that is basically the argument that James Comer is making on the Oversight Committee is that this shows that $3 million went from this Chinese company, CEFC, and then it went through a couple of steps and ended up in the pockets of at least three members of the Biden family. And then another, who is an unknown got $70,000, at least unknown so far. Then, I just want to remind everybody, James Geller, who's in the middle of this chart here as a conduit, James Geller is the one who wrote the email that outlined a number of individuals, including Hunter, and it said, you know, plus 10, held for the big guy.\par \par \uc2 So, it raises the question: is the big guy, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and is the big guy, the unknown Biden on this chart? And I also want to pull up this Hunter Biden text to his daughter, Naomi, when they were sort of arguing back and forth, and this came out of the laptop as well. He says, "I hope you all can do what I did, and pay for everything for this entire family for 30 years. It's really hard. But don't worry, unlike pop, I won't make you give me half your salary." So, from a legal perspective, Jonathan, all of this looks bad, OK. But, but is the Oversight Committee doing a good job of actually creating a link between this money and these Biden family members?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: I think it is. They're trying very hard to establish whether there was influence peddling here, which I believe there's ample evidence to show that. The Biden's had been long accused of influence peddling. But part of that investigation is to see where this money went, having Hallie Biden's name pop up in a SAR of a suspicious transfer of money internationally, is a concern. But the big, as for the big guy, a witness has already said, that was clearly \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And I think that this circle is tightening around the president.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jonathan, thank you very much, Jonathan Turley joining us today.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, have a great weekend. But joining me now is Joe Concha media and politics columnist for the hill, and Fox News Contributor. Of course, Joe, I want to start by playing this James Comer, Chair of the Oversight Committee, who's digging into all of this was on earlier today on Fox on America reports and he said that people have come to talk to the Oversight Committee, who are suggesting that some of this happened, not only after he was vice president, but before. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): Something that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was actively involved in, throughout the last year of his vice presidency, we've had several people come in and say that, in fact, even some of the transfers to that Robinson Walker company happened during 2015 and 2016, at a time when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was very much vice president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Joe, what do you make of the newest developments in this today?\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I would think that journalists out there would be asking lots of questions of Mr. Biden, who isn't very accessible, but the press secretary or spokespeople or even Democrats for that matter, and we are seeing either silence on this or whatever coverage is applied to this story -- and it's a big story. It's mostly to defend the Biden somehow. But the questions are obvious. When you look at that breakdown of the $3 million that went to Rob Walker, $610,000 to Hunter Biden; $360,000 to the President's brother James Biden; Hallie Biden, $25,000, and not just one lump sum, but multiple payments.\par \par \uc2 She was a guidance counselor working in Delaware at the time and was dating \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . What do those three people have in terms of on their resumes that would warrant that sort of payment regarding a Chinese Electric Company, just like in Ukraine for \uc2 Burisma\uc2 ? What qualifications did \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  have in terms of receiving the kind of money he did? We're talking seven figures here, multiple seven figures, we still don't know what services were provided in the situation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right, that's the question. You know, what was the business? What was this functioning business that this money was going to? Comer says they're having a very hard time figuring that out, but I will point out again, it's very difficult to trace money from one place to another place, and Comer is saying that it appears that the money flowed through this chain that he's watching. This is \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , when she was asked, why is the President not answering questions are huge things going on? You've got this Russia taking down our drone, you've got the banking crisis, and you've got a lot of questions about this. Here's what happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: This is coordinated. That's true, he's answered questions. It's not -- I hear you guys, I hear guys.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Really, that's funny.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: It's the \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  playbook where Psaki used to say as Press Secretary, the President answers questions from reporters all the time. There is very little evidence of that. And again, if this was \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 ., \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , who were receiving these sorts of mysterious payments, what do you think the media coverage would look like? Remember the apocalypse in certain movies that I'm referring that that's what it would look like at this point, 24/7, Martha, instead silence of the lambs at this point.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joe, thank you. Joe Concha this afternoon. Good to have you here, Joe.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Happy Patrick's Day.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You too. All right. Thank you. So, President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  is really elevating his role in so many aspects of what is going on around the globe. Now, he's getting ready for a big meeting with that man, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , and it's going to happen on Monday. What are they going to talk about? What does she want from him? Senator Tom Cotton standing by.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we've been watching some disturbing scenes that are unfolding right now. This is a live camera in Paris. Late in the evening. Very large protests and some vandalism that has been happening. Some fire in the streets as well we've witnessed in the streets of Paris. This is all happening because \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  has raised the retirement age from 62 to 64, because they have similar issues with the funding of these programs and the threat that it could run out.\par \par \uc2 Same problems that we have here in many ways, and it has caused a lot of unrest in the streets. There was no vote in parliament on this. That's what has outraged these protesters. But it was done to prevent tougher actions that might have to be taken to keep these funds fluid. But you can see that there have been some clashes with the police. After sanitation workers went on strike, garbage is piling up in the streets of Paris. Police have arrested hundreds of protesters out there. We're going to keep -- we've got a live camera on this, as you can see, and some pretty interesting action in the streets there. So, we'll go back to that if there is more breaking news from Paris this evening.\par \par \uc2 So, China's president, \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , fresh off brokering a deal between Iran and Saudi Arabia that really moved the U.S. and Israel to the backburner in the Middle East in many ways. Now, he wants to handle the situation between Russia and Ukraine. He's got a meeting on Monday in Moscow with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . And his foreign ministry reportedly telling Ukraine that Beijing is "concerned about the war spinning out of control." And they have conveyed that to Ukraine as well.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Arkansas Senator Tom Cotton, member of the Armed Services Intelligence and Judiciary committees. He's also an Iraq and Afghanistan war veteran.\par \par \uc2 Senator, good to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 It's been fascinating to watch, because we know that China is trying to dominate the globe, really on the economy, their military. And now, they also want to be sort of the power broker of diplomacy in the world that appears.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): Yes, I'm afraid that's the case of last few years, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has put a quick kick me sign on the back of Uncle Sam.\par \par \uc2 This just another lesson when America recedes from the world, we don't get peace and tranquility. We get chaos and war, and adversaries acting against our interests.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  are anti American dictators. They didn't need \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to give them overlapping interests. But \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has given them opportunity to exploit America's receding from the world.\par \par \uc2 You know, you look in China, for instance, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came into office saying, China is not a competitor of ours, and what do we get? I'm sending a spy balloon across America and rattling sabers against Taiwan or in Russia.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  spent his first year in office appeasing \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , and what do we get? We get the first or the biggest land war in Europe since World War II, and we get them downing our aircraft over the Black Sea.\par \par \uc2 This kind -- these kinds of weak actions just provoke and beget more aggression from our adversaries. And now, you see \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , striding across the world stage, trying to establish himself as a broker of peace among countries.\par \par \uc2 It just gets back to one of the most basic rules of foreign affairs. Either you run the show or the show runs you.\par \par \uc2 We've been used to running the show for many decades. But under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , we're increasingly let -- letting others run the show and let it run against us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, that's exactly what \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2  told David Ignatius, essentially. Let's put this up on the screen. "Over the longer run," this is \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2  quoted in this piece. "Beijing's emergence as a peacemaker, changes the terms of reference in international diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 The United States is no longer the indispensable power in the region." "China has in recent years declare that it needs to be a participation -- a participant in the creation of the world order."\par \par \uc2 And I think a new world order is the way that could be described. And when John Kirby was asked, is the president going to speak to \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  anytime soon? Here is what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: There is no call scheduled. And while the president has made it clear, he wants -- he looks forward to another opportunity to speak with the President Xi, we're not actively engaged in the -- in the logistics of setting that up right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Yes. Again, this is an example of what happens when you recede from the world. Barely two years ago, former President Trump had helped broker peace, for instance, between Israel on the one hand, and Bahrain, and the United Arab Emirates and other Arab and African nations.\par \par \uc2 We are hoping, two years ago that we'd soon get a peace agreement between Israel and Saudi Arabia, brokered by the United States.\par \par \uc2 Instead, under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , what we received is a normalization agreement between Saudi Arabia and Iran.\par \par \uc2 All these countries around the world that prefer to work with America that have been long standing partners of America, like Saudi Arabia, like the United Arab Emirates, like Bahrain, or Qatar, or Jordan, or Egypt have security needs. They have threats that they face, primarily from Iran.\par \par \uc2 Those security needs don't go away when America goes away, or when the Democratic Party turns its back on long standing allies, because they don't like the fact that some of these countries oppose President Obama's nuclear deal with Iran or that they work with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Those security needs will simply be filled by other countries, our adversaries like China.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator Tom Cotton, always good to see you, sir. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the warning that was sent by \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , about their work from home policy. They had concerns that it might lead to lower productivity. And gee, look what happened? Just months before the bank collapsed.\par \par \uc2 Shark Tank investor, \uc2 Kevin O'Leary\uc2  on whether it's a bigger sign of more problems across the sector and the economy to come.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN O'LEARY\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, O'LEARY VENTURES: 44 percent of our employees now across our Venture portfolio work remotely, and they aren't coming into the office, period.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Silicon Valley -- \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 's parent company filing for bankruptcy now, as we learn that remote work may have played a role in this historic collapse.\par \par \uc2 The bank itself admitted in their annual report last night that it could face "negative effects of a prolonged work from home arrangement."\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  reports, "when \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  imploded last week, most of its 8,500 staff were still working remotely. Some people worked from Miami, some moved to Las Vegas or a cabin in the woods and did the digital nomad thing.\par \par \uc2 One former banker said, and, "Long after Wall Street ordered their bankers back to the office, the California-based lender's chief executive, \uc2 Greg Becker\uc2 , at times worked from Hawaii."\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in, O'Leary Ventures chairman, \uc2 Kevin O'Leary\uc2 , who you know, of course, from Shark Tank.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Kevin, it's great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 I'm curious what you -- what your thoughts are on this bit of editorial -- or not editorial, but their report from their annual report where they admitted, this might be a problem for productivity.\par \par \uc2 O'LEARY: It has no merit. It's a bankrupt idea, and it's a falsehood. This bank blew up as a result of a negligent board. And idiot management. It's that simple.\par \par \uc2 There's many, many institutions in every sector of our economy -- there is 11 sectors, most of these companies have today found that up to 40 percent of their staff are not planning to return. Doesn't matter what sector and including financial services.\par \par \uc2 The reason is that the people realized they're going to have a better lifestyle, a lower cost.\par \par \uc2 But including jobs like accounting, logistics, and compliance. These used to be in cubicles in the basement of operations anyways, and these employees are now enjoying a much better lifestyle. So, you find that some portion of your staff, regardless of what company you're in, are effectively working remotely.\par \par \uc2 And that's the technology we have, obviously, you've had a whole generation and two cohorts of university recruits that have come out have never worked in an office before. So, this is a permanent change in our economy.\par \par \uc2 This excuse for \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  is for lack of a better word and excuse --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, but there could be a link between management and being able to oversee your employees and productivity if you -- I mean, they said in their own words, imbalances between work and home life. You know, you say people are seeking balance, but they are saying there could be imbalances in this.\par \par \uc2 I'm not saying that they're trying to excuse the other very fundamental things that obviously were out of whack here. Their lack of recognition that the interest rate environment had gone against them, and that they needed to get help right away.\par \par \uc2 But they are sort of, I think, pointing to something that the other banks are trying to work against these big banks, which are now bailing out the other banks, which is that every -- that there's a lot more oversight of employees.\par \par \uc2 O'LEARY: Well ask yourself a simple question for the CEO of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 . Did he have personal interactions with 8,500 people in person every day? Of course not. It's been done online for the entire existence of that bank. Has been around for 40 years.\par \par \uc2 He might meet with 15, 20 people a day at best. And if he wanted to go in the office, and by the way, maybe he should have, and his executive staff it might have been useful, but they were incompetent managers.\par \par \uc2 It had nothing to do with where they are working. They just worked poorly wherever they were. They were just a disaster. And look, we'll solve this through litigation.\par \par \uc2 But the real issue you're talking about you got two big issues there. Should we bail out all these other banks? First Republic? No, it's a zombie bank now.\par \par \uc2 No one's going to put any money there. The brand is radioactive waste, it should be allowed to die an orderly death and the assets will go to other places. It's not a bad thing that poorly run banks go to zero and the bigger question being debated now by investors and depositors, do we really need regional banks anymore? When they started 50 years ago, yes, you did, or 100 years ago, you rode your horse to the bank. But now everything's done online, 95% of transactions in banking are done online. I don't think we need all these little regional banks with really bad balance sheets and idiot managers.\par \par \uc2 It's time to do a little soul searching. So this is not the reason that \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  went to zero. They went to zero whether that CEO was in his office or not, that didn't matter. He was a very bad manager.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It's a great point. And, you know, you have to -- you don't really have capitalism if some businesses can't fail or banks can't fail, right? I mean, there's no process if everybody gets bailed out. \uc2 Kevin O'Leary\uc2 , thank you very much. Great to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 O'LEARY: Take care, thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. So since we now know the impact of learning from home, speaking of that, was abysmal. Let's lower the standards for proficiency so that the kids can meet them better. That's one state's bright idea to cover up the education crisis that our children are experiencing all across this country because of the COVID lockdowns. Why former Education Secretary \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2  says beware this trend is spreading.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Low student test scores caused by pandemic-related learning loss will now apparently set a new lower benchmark, a new normal for what is passing in New York State. The Albany Times Union reporting that the state's Education Department is changing what the standard for kids to achieve proficiency in math and English language arts tests in grades three through eight. The New York Post pointing out, in other words, the achievements of kids who just suffered nearly two years of school shutdowns and the joke of remote learning, they write, are all the state bureaucracy will expect for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in former Education Secretary \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2 . Betsy, always good to have you with us. This is a frightening trend. If you'll allow me to read one more part of this report from the Albany Times Union. Last year, some schools posted shocking results. In Schenectady, New York, no 8th grader was proficient in math, and the scores for the 3rd through 8th grade tests were much lower in 2022 than they were in 2019, a result, no doubt, of the absence of in learning for the first year since the COVID pandemic. And then, they basically go on to say that the policy now there is learning loss between 2019 and 2022. In some ways, we don't want to keep going backwards. So for New York, we're saying the new baseline becomes 2022. So let's just lower the standards across the board so that maybe we can meet them, Betsy.\par \par \uc2 BETSY DEVOS, FORMER EDUCATION SECRETARY: Yes. Martha, this is the Democrats plan for combating learning loss. Lower expectations, lower the bar, hide it from parents, don't measure anything, and just keep on doubling down on the failed policies that they've continued to promote. This is happening not just in New York but also in my home state of Michigan, where just last week the Democrat legislature and the Democrat governor repealed the 3rd grade reading law. They're about to go after the A to F grading for schools. This is their solution. Just keep on lowering standards, lowering expectations.\par \par \uc2 And I recall how often President Bush talked about the soft bigotry of low expectations. This is blatant bigotry and it is blatantly deceptive to all kids involved.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to go back to what you just mentioned, because there was a law or standard in Michigan, as you say, that you had to be able to read by third grade. It's a very good measure because obviously you don't want any student to go further than that. They're going to feel so lost in fourth and fifth grade if they can't read. Now that they are changing that standard, they're going to allow kids who can't read to advance to fourth grade. How is that going to fix the problem? How won't that child just feel frustrated and more nervous?\par \par \uc2 DEVOS: Well, exactly. And Michigan is among a number of states that have had this law on the books and that it's been working well to catch kids before they move on into that period of learning where they have to be able to read to learn. And there have been plenty of remedies to catch up but the Democrats, their solution is just do away with it and pretend it's not really a problem.\par \par \uc2 Well, we are failing these kids by continuing to move them on and give them a false perception of what they can actually do and make them more frustrated. It's no wonder that eventually a lot of these kids end up dropping out of school and there are so many tools to actually help them learn to read, starting with giving the parents the opportunity to choose a better venue or a place that's actually going to help them learn.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. If they can pull the kid out and get them into another program through choice where they will make sure that they can read. I mean, to have kids in the country not be able to read is such an enormous problem. If you look at Arkansas and other places, Iowa, they're doing in Arkansas, the governor is adding tutors to the program for any child who can't read. Options for them to get extra help. Extra help is what they need, not lower standards.\par \par \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2 , thank you very much. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 DEVOS: Thanks, Martha. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So where President Biden stands in a matchup with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brand new numbers on this in the polls as we head towards 2024? \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chair \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  weighs in next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. You know what? Everybody is fascinated with these polls. They keep coming out. And take a look at this one, former President Trump pulling further ahead of Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in a potential 2024 showdown, expanding his lead by eight points in the new Quinnipiac poll over last month. You can see up there, the next closest contenders on this particular poll, Nikki Haley and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  at five and three and they're in single digits, as you can see. But it is early going here, so let's bring in \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 . Ronna, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 There's a head to head that they did as well. The former President Trump at 51%, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  at 40%. These two are now going at each other pretty good. And \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  isn't even in the race yet. What do you make of the increase for Trump polling that we've seen in a couple of places this week?\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , CHAIRWOMAN, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE: Well, I think it's March madness, right? We're going to get a whole bunch of polls. This is going to continue throughout the primary season. I think the numbers that I found really interesting from these polls were where they matched them with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  still isn't breaking 50%. His unfavorable is at 54%, so we have a very unpopular president, which means whoever our nominee is has a really great chance to take back the White House, especially as we're seeing banks failing, soaring inflation like you just talked about, kids with massive deficits, an open border, a Fentanyl crisis. Republicans have a great shot to take back the White House, win the Senate with a great map, and then keep the House in 2024.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's show those polls that you just mentioned, Ronna. These are the head to heads between Biden and Trump. Biden at 49, Trump at 45, and then Biden beats DeSantis by one point. These are roughly in the margin of error here, so pretty tight races. They're both -- both sides are under 50% here, Ronna.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yes. But he's a sitting president, Martha, right? So Biden controls the agenda. He owns this inflation, he owns the border crisis, he owns Fentanyl, he owns what's happening across our country and he's under 50%. And his negatives are so high and we haven't even started making our case, right?\par \par \uc2 So every day he gets to make his case. He has the media on his side, the legacy media. He doesn't talk to the American people, and he's still underwater. I think this bodes very well for our Republican nominee. What I want to say to everybody is we want our nominees to make sure we don't take so many chips out of each other that we can't compete against Biden. We have to come out of this primary intact so that we can go up against the Democrat.\par \par \uc2 And I know there's going to be some gloves off, but we've got to remember the bigger prize here, which is winning the White House.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you do see that scrapping already. President Trump going after \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , calling him a rhino, aligning him with people in the party that he feels are not conservative enough. Would you tell the former president to pipe down and to not shoot within the tent? I mean, I don't know if you're going to get him to do that --\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Listen, there's going to be back and forth. But at the end, as long as we unite around the nominee and remember that Republicans want us to win, and they want us to be united, and we can't win if we're not united. We're going to need Republicans and Independents to win the White House. So at the end of this process, we all need to rally around whoever the voters choose to be our nominee if we're going to beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And that's what I'm focused on as head of the \uc2 RNC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  wants to clarify the criteria to get into the primary debates. Do you have anything that you can share with us about what percentage people would have to be at in order to make it onto that stage late summer?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yes. I've talked to Vivek. We're going to put the criteria out relatively soon. We don't want to get ahead of ourselves, especially as we're looking at polling criteria. We want to make sure that what we put out is accurate. But we're talking to any candidate who is looking at the White House and talking to them about what that criteria should be. We're not going to blindside anybody.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Always good to see you. I'm sure we'll be talking a lot more as we move forward in this quickly approaching 2024 environment. Thanks, Ronna.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Have a great weekend. So, what better way to start your St. Patrick's Day than with Jimmy Failla and how the holiday normally associated with booze may be sobering up? Not sure that's such a great idea.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So I haven't seen this happening so far on the street, but some say that New Yorkers are ditching the booze for a sober St. Patrick's Day with higher demand, more bars and restaurants serving non-alcoholic drinks. One bar owner telling the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , "There are hundreds of spirit free products now. Five years ago, none of these things existed. We're going to ask Jimmy Failla about what he thinks about the benefit of those kinds of products in just a moment. But first, to Madison Alworth live from a New York City bar where they're drinking the real thing from what I saw earlier, Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Martha. Those drinks have been flowing but I will tell you, they do stock Heineken zero here. I'm going to crack it for you during this set. Let's see if I can do this. Ah, there we go. Cheers. So they stock this drink because it's zero alcohol. The owner tells me it's really popular during the month of January, but because of this explosion of people that are sober curious, they say people are buying it into this holiday as well.\par \par \uc2 The reality, though, is a lot of Americans are going to be drinking beer and taking shots at St. Patrick's Day, and spending a lot of money as well. $7 billion will be spent celebrating this holiday. I want to bring in the bar owner here to talk about some of that money because he's been selling a lot of Guinness, Kenny.\par \par \uc2 So, Kenny, East End Bar and Grill, how many kegs did you stock of Guinness and how many Guinness pints are you going to sell today?\par \par \uc2 KENNY BOWEN, OWNER, EAST END BAR: We stocked ten kegs for today. Judging by the crowd, hopefully we don't run out, but it's about 1,600 pints that we expect to go through today.\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: All right. Tell me real quick, how many pints do you normally sell on a typical day?\par \par \uc2 BOWEN: Probably about 50 to 60.\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: OK. So quite a big growth. Thank you so much, Kenny. So not only is it St. Patrick's Day, it's also March Madness. So people spending money on beer, also spending money on bets. We're expected to bet $15.5 billion on this holiday. That's up from $3 billion last year. All around, just having a good time. I have my friend, John, here who's going to play us out. John, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 All right, there you have it. It's March Madness, it's St. Patrick Day, and we're drinking nonalcoholic beer. Martha, I'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I love the sound of bagpipes. Thank you very much, Madison. Let's bring in our favorite Irish guy, comedian Jimmy Failla, host of Fox Across America and Fox News Radio and Fox Nation. Do you have some Irish in you?\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FAILLA, FOX HOST: Yes, there's a percentage. I'm predominantly polish and Sicilian, and we have like a quarter, like a little chunk of Irish.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Who was that person?\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: We're still trying to get our name. We don't know. It was the mailman was transferred right after I was born. Nobody had blonde hair in my house except me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nobody can't figure why.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Yes. The guy got transferred. But yes, happy St. Patrick's Day. A couple of things, I want -- your viewers need to know that I did ask to be the one reporting from the bar but you didn't trust me. I just want her on record. I said --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We didn't trust you to drink the Heineken Zero.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Can I give you something on that? Two things, OK? One, obviously I'm not surprised that alcohol has increased at the same time it's decreased. Why? It's going up because half of the people doing extra drinking aren't even St. Patrick's revelers. The world's just a mess. People want to drink. But the reason nonalcoholic beer is becoming more popular, OK, especially on St. Patrick's Day, because they're the ones that film the viral videos you're going to watch on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  later. For real, I want you to think about that.\par \par \uc2 The reason you go on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  tonight and see a guy with no pants on singing McNamara's band is not because someone got drunk. It's because someone stayed sober, OK? If you're drunk, you get on stage and sing backup. If you're sober, you get likes, and that's who these people are.\par \par \uc2 I would say 90% of the people drinking nonalcoholic beer today are drinking tomorrow. They do it the next day.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, that's interesting. The only time that I really drank nonalcoholic beer, there were three times in my life when I did that, and all three of them I was pregnant for nine months. And after that was over, I was. OK, see you.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Well, you did better than my mom. Give yourself that. I only drank nonalcoholic beer once. It's when I didn't have time, I didn't want to get drunk, but I really wanted to pee. That's what I get. You could just give me some nonalcoholic.\par \par \uc2 No, but I'm not surprised. I think the whole spirit of St. Patrick's Day, the communal vibe of St. Patrick's Day, especially if you're in this area with the parade and everything, it starts young. We were going to it in high school, and it was just like a bender. And I love it. It's such good, positive energy to be around, so how could you not like it?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It is. I started my day going to mass at St Patrick's. It was enormous crowd there. Lots of firemen, lots of police officers, lots of members of the military, lots of bagpipes, a huge Irish wolfhound. It was a beautiful day in New York. I was like, this is an amazing way to start St. Patrick's Day. And on my way back, there were a lot of people flooding into some of the Irish pub --\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Oh no. They (inaudible) hard --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It was only very early in the morning. One other thing I want to ask you about, apparently, that there's a trend that's pretty disgusting sounding to me of eating high protein dog food in order to get your protein uptake. I don't even like -- I have two dogs, I don't even like to really smell their dog food all that much. I cannot imagine eating it.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: This is insane. They always name diets. Like, I'm on the keto, I'm on the caveman. Like, yo, I'm on the 101 Dalmatians, bro. Nobody should be taking advice of any kind from TikTok. These are either broken people or smart people preying on broken people, because they introduced this into the video vernacular. And some people will try this now but this is idiotic.\par \par \uc2 So what are you going to do? You're going to eat 600 grams of protein. Next time you go to the gym, someone's going to throw a tennis ball and you're going to chase it and roll over? You're going to turn into a dog, stop it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jimmy, thank you. Happy St. Patrick's Day.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Hapy St. Patrick's Day.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: To that one little sliver of the Irish --\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Quarter of the family.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- postman who's in your family. Good to see, as always. Thank you, Jimmy.\par \par \uc2 So, on a serious note, and really an inspiring note as well, the episode that dropped today of the Untold Story podcast is really special.\par \par \uc2 It is the conversation that I had with Fox war correspondent Benjamin Hall, also at the State Department for us, and his wife, Alicia, who sat down for the very first time and talked about what this was like for the two of them, what it was like for her and what the impact has been on their marriage and on their relationship. It is a really inspiring conversation with two really wonderful people. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALICIA HALL, BENJAMIN HALL'S WIFE: It was lucky we were such a good team, is that because he was so effortlessly positive. That reverberated onto me and as a result, reverberated onto the children.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: We have gone through the worst, the worst thing that can happen, and we came through with flying colors. And were there together every step of the way. We haven't disagreed on anything. We agreed. We listened to each other. We heard what the other wanted to say and went to it together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, this is just an amazing story, and thank God it has such a positive ending for Benjamin and for Alicia, and for their beautiful children. He's also just constantly like, darling, you look so beautiful. You're so wonderful. I mean, I just want to say give me some of that. It's all fantastic. And it's called "Sacrifice and Survival." Do not miss the show. It is Sunday night on Fox News. Watch this documentary that evening.\par \par \uc2 Happy St. Patrick's Day, everybody. Great to have you with us. I will see you back here on Monday. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230318ej3h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 SVB\uc2  Collapse: A Warning Sign Of What Is To Come; \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  Is The Largest US Bank Failure; Biden Administration Drains Strategic Petroleum Reserve; Democrat Lawmaker Uses Hearing To Call A Witness A White Supremacist Instead Of Talking About Issues; Lower Hiring Standards Put Us All At Risk; South Dakota Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  Vetoes Central Bank Digital Currency Bill; Our Media Has Been Pushing Race Hatred For Years; Student Punished For Standing Up For His Beliefs\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson \par \uc2 7,442 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Tucker is up next and always remember, I'm Watters and this is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2  , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 We are starting to get a better, a more precise sense of what it means when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  brags about the strongest and most equitable economic recovery in modern history. What it means is it could be time to buy gold and stockpile food. Oh, there's a deal on Russian steel case, let me go pick up a pallet. Just kidding, sort of.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, some of the biggest banks in this country -- Wells Fargo, Bank of America, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  , and \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  collectively lost more than $50 billion in market value in one day. That's quite a hit.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, those banks still exist, and you can't say that for \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  . As of this morning, \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  or \uc2 SVB\uc2  has gone under completely. That makes the second biggest bank failure in the history of this country, and that's significant.\par \par \uc2 SVB\uc2  financed nearly half of all venture-backed healthcare and technology companies in the United States. It also apparently held significant cash reserves for some of the biggest cryptocurrencies, and it's now gone.\par \par \uc2 Federal regulators have renamed it and taken it over and that means an awful lot of people lost an awful lot of money and no, most of that money was not insured, no matter what they tell you. The \uc2 FDIC\uc2  only guarantees bank deposits up to $250,000.00 and according to some reports, more than 90 percent of all deposits at \uc2 SVB\uc2  exceeded that and it's unclear whether those people will ever see their money again.\par \par \uc2 In fact, when customers showed up at \uc2 SVB\uc2  's branch in Manhattan today to get their deposits back, managers called the police.\par \par \uc2 So, what we have here is a 1929 style bank run and that's not a good sign for anyone. The question is whether the people who run \uc2 SVB\uc2  saw it coming. The CEO, a man called \uc2 Greg Becker\uc2  , apparently sold more than $2 million in bank stock over the last two weeks.\par \par \uc2 According to the site, "Unusual Whales," several other high-level employees of \uc2 SVB\uc2  , including Chief Marketing Officer \uc2 Michelle Draper\uc2  , Chief Operations Officer \uc2 Phil Cox\uc2  , General Counsel \uc2 Michael Zuckert\uc2  , all sold significant amounts of stock in \uc2 SVB\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 Did those employees know their bank was in trouble? We don't know and once again, where were the regulators? They're supposed to prevent this. Once again, we don't know, and the business press who is supposed to be telling ordinary people what's happening with business, apparently, nobody noticed anything.\par \par \uc2 In fact, as with the \uc2 FTX\uc2  crypto scam, you may recall, self-described financial experts in the media were busy promoting \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  as a great investment that would last forever.\par \par \uc2 The formerly great, but now quite embarrassing "Forbes" Magazine, in fact, named \uc2 SVB\uc2  to its list of America's best banks, not once, but five years in a row. And of course, inevitably you saw this coming, here is professional BS artist/dumb person, \uc2 Jim Cramer\uc2  of CNBC telling his viewers to buy \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  even though it was in the process of going under. This is last month.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIM CRAMER\uc2  , CNBC: \uc2 SVB Financial\uc2  , this company is a merchant bank with a deposit base that Wall Street had been mistakenly concerned about.\par \par \uc2 SVB\uc2  is the old \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  , recently bought one of our favorite research firms Moffett Nathanson and it's become less dependent on private equity and venture capitalist offerings.\par \par \uc2 Wait a second, those dried up last year, they could come back.\par \par \uc2 Long-term private equity and venture capital, they're not going away, being a banker to these immense pools of capital has always been a very good business.\par \par \uc2 SVB\uc2  's nearly 40 percent rally this year is barely a drop in the bucket\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: \uc2 SVB\uc2  is great. If that guy ever endorses anything you're doing, move to the Canary Islands, change your name because disaster is coming, but just like \uc2 FTX\uc2  , all the geniuses told you to buy \uc2 SVB\uc2  , even when it was on the brink of total collapse. What explains that? Surely something does. We don't really know at this point. Hopefully we will someday.\par \par \uc2 What we can say tonight is that \uc2 SVB\uc2  , like the scammers at \uc2 FTX\uc2  , had quite the public relations department. We're not some greedy finance outfit that exists solely to generate cash for overpaid sleaze balls who run it. No, that's not us. We may be a bank, we've got the soul of an NGO. We care. We're saving the world and to prove it.\par \par \uc2 There's a whole page on \uc2 SVB\uc2  's website about their plans to "monitor and reduce our own carbon emissions." Yes, because you want a bank that cares about carbon emissions and of course, there's endless posturing because it's all free about diversity, equity and inclusion.\par \par \uc2 Marisa Phan, the Managing Director at \uc2 SVB\uc2  , wrote this on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  just three weeks before the bank collapsed, "Feeling inspired and energized after hosting an extraordinary group of pioneering and dynamic women in \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  's Female Founder and VC Tahoe Ski Retreat."\par \par \uc2 Adjectives are free. They use a bunch of them and then she attached pictures of the ski trip showing the group of these pioneering and dynamic female bankers having a great time because, of course, they're having a great time because few things are more empowering to oppressed female bankers than skiing in Tahoe.\par \par \uc2 And they kept this crap up to the very end.\par \par \uc2 As their bank began to slip beneath the waves, \uc2 SVB\uc2  posted this on its social media feeds: "As part of Women's History Month, we're celebrating founders of women led companies like Suma Wealth's, Beatriz Acevedo," a company that \uc2 SVB\uc2  described lovingly as "focused on closing the Latinx wealth gap."\par \par \uc2 It's the problem with Latinx. There's a wealth gap and there was a video, too. Of course, there was.\par \par \uc2 So, now that \uc2 SVB\uc2  has blown up and lost billions of other people's money in the process, we thought we would show you the video because, oh, we can't resist. Enjoy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BEATRIZ ACEVEDO, CEO AND CO-FOUNDER, SUMA WEALTH: My name is Beatriz Acevedo and I am the CEO and co-founder of Suma Wealth.\par \par \uc2 I was born in Tijuana, Mexico. I am a very proud border girl who had the privilege to grow up with the best of both worlds.\par \par \uc2 I went to school in San Diego. I went to \uc2 UCSD\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 I have a lot of scars from my days going across the border. I remember I got this memo and it just said that all my shows were cancelled, and it was a time where women, never men, were put into something they called their refrigerator.\par \par \uc2 If they felt like you had a lot of power, they would just put you there to cool you off for a while.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Unaddressed in the video is whether she's actually a capable banker. We hope so. We have no idea.\par \par \uc2 We know the guys at \uc2 SVB\uc2  were not capable bankers. They didn't seem to spend a lot of time banking or paying close attention because they had videos to make about dynamic, pioneering, glass-ceiling shattering women.\par \par \uc2 So, when interest rates were at historic lows, \uc2 SVB\uc2  bought more than 20 billion in long-term Treasury bonds and to be fair, they were encouraged by the government to do this. All banks were.\par \par \uc2 The problem was when interest rates went up as they were always going to, because they can't stay at zero forever -- did you know that? Well, when that happened, those bonds lost their value and that, of course, was a disaster for a ton of banks, but a true disaster for \uc2 SVB\uc2  and then a disaster for a lot of individuals who had their money there and companies that had borrowed money from \uc2 SVB\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 So, you can expect a lot of bankruptcies in the coming weeks due to this and then the countless downstream effects from those bankruptcies, all of them bad. This is a disaster, not just for the tech industry, people who we could easily make fun of, but for everyone, for all of us.\par \par \uc2 But the interesting thing is the Biden administration doesn't seem to care at all. They're not that interested because numbers, whether you're making money or losing money or doing something efficiently, you're doing it sloppily doesn't really matter as long as you're making videos about pioneering female bankers and as long as they get to ski in Tahoe. That's the real concern.\par \par \uc2 So, just today, Biden's top economic adviser, \uc2 Cecilia Rouse\uc2  , addressed reporters in the White House in the Briefing Room about what had just happened and she assured everybody that the bank run we're watching is an isolated incident.\par \par \uc2 No, it's not a big deal, it is just this one bank. It's not connected to anything else at all and the fact that this bank failed, just like the fact that \uc2 FTX\uc2  failed, has nothing to do with the fact that the people who run it spend a ton of their time doing completely irrelevant crap that had to do with politics and changing American culture.\par \par \uc2 Were you reassured by that? We should know that \uc2 Cecilia Rouse\uc2  has been thinking a lot about this. When she worked at Princeton, which is a university in New Jersey, she wrote her research agenda on diversity, equity, and inclusion. The title is, and we are quoting, "Diversity in the Economics Profession: A New Attack on an Old Problem." Because the color of your economists is essential. Their competence? Not so much. Just so you know.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, these are the consequences of that attitude. See South Africa, where they don't have electricity tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coincidentally or not, that is the very same topic, diversity in economics, that our Director of our National Economic Council, \uc2 Lael Brainard\uc2  , is also fixated on. Another genius.\par \par \uc2 When Brainard was at the Fed in 2021, she wasn't too concerned about future bank collapses due to the insane and reckless Fed policy she was overseeing. No, why worry about that because there's climate change? That's what she was really worried about, climate change: "As part of our prudential and financial stability responsibilities, we are developing scenario analysis to model the possible financial risks associated with climate change and assess the resilience of individual financial institutions in the financial system to those risks."\par \par \uc2 Just a pause for one second. Anyone who writes that way is a moron, M-O-E- R-O-N, moron. So, if you find someone in a position of authority, particularly economics, who cannot write a declarative sentence, be prepared for your banks to fail.\par \par \uc2 We should be honest that ESG and equity are only part of the problem here. Stupidity is a huge part of the problem. \uc2 SVB\uc2  and \uc2 FTX\uc2  for that matter are just two of several major players in the financial industry that have gone under in recent months. Are you noticing any of this?\par \par \uc2 There was the bank \uc2 Silvergate\uc2  , more than $11 billion in assets. There was a crypto broker, \uc2 Voyager\uc2  , a billion in assets, the crypto fund 3AC, $10 billion in assets, Block-fi and a number of others.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  customers reported they didn't get their paychecks as expected. The bank says that was just a computer glitch. We don't really know, but we're paying attention.\par \par \uc2 What is very clear is that we're witnessing something serious happening in the finance sector and in other sectors of our economy. We don't know where any of this is going. We're certainly praying for the best. We don't want to see hysteria. But if you're the Treasury Secretary of the United States, you would be very concerned. You'd be paying really close attention because that's your job.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  is not paying close attention. She's not worried about it because it's in this country. She's worried instead about funding the pensions of Ukrainian government workers. She flew to Kyiv the other day to tell us that, and as always, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  is highly focused on her core duties as Treasury Secretary. That would be equity, climate change and abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2  , US TREASURY SECRETARY: The Biden-Harris administration has made racial equity a centerpiece of our economic agenda.\par \par \uc2 Roe v. Wade and access to reproductive health care, including abortion, helped lead to increased labor force participation.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, and I feel the same way too, believe that climate change is an existential threat that absolutely must be addressed.\par \par \uc2 The message I bring you from President Biden is simple. America will stand with Ukraine for as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Okay. So, she is very upset about Ukraine and we don't have enough abortions and equity, equity, abortion, and Ukraine. Okay. Got it. Got it. Got it.\par \par \uc2 The children hour is over. It's time for the adults because we have actual problems, we might even call them systemic problems with our economy and so when you have those, you want smart, wise people with foresight and self- control and the ability to focus on things of actual consequence, not on the fashionable crap that occupies most of our time here on cable news, but in the long-term stuff that will ensure your country remains strong and prosperous when you are gone, which for most of these people won't be long because they're very, very old.\par \par \uc2 Where are those people? Where are the adults? Here you have the second biggest bank failure in American history, the biggest since 2008, the second largest failure in our nation's history and they're yammering on about racial equity. Really? Yes, they are because nobody in Washington at this point even acknowledges economics is real unless it can be tied somehow to climate change or racism.\par \par \uc2 Here is Congresswoman \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  of Saint Louis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CORI BUSH (D-MO): My Republican colleagues have unironically invited a for-profit think tank and oil titan whose expertise is in maximizing profits, especially at the expense of our Black, our Brown, and our indigenous neighbors' health, safety and well-being.\par \par \uc2 Last week, I joined Ranking Member Raskin and all of my Democratic Oversight colleagues in calling on Oversight Republicans to denounce White nationalism and denounce White supremacy in all its forms.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: White supremacy, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah. Yes, great. That's fine and we get peace and prosperity. You can handle some dumb people at the helm and it's kind of amusing. They're fun to laugh at, but if things start going south, the dumb people have to leave and they should be replaced by the smart, wise people who have some idea of how to fix things.\par \par \uc2 But those people have not yet arrived. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  just introduced a $7 trillion budget. Oh, a budget? That's funding, that's money, right? That should address the core economic concerns that every person can feel. Most people don't understand it. Everybody can feel it.\par \par \uc2 So, the $7 trillion budget mentions "climate change" 148 times. He mentions "equity," never defined, 63 times, "environmental justice" 25 times and cross-dressing, now known as transgenderism, eight times. Feel better? Are the adults back? No.\par \par \uc2 But it will add $20 trillion to the national debt over ten years and that means more inflation and more interest rate hikes and more bank failures. This could be very, very, very serious. We hope it's not. We hope it ends today, an isolated story, but it very much could be. But our leaders don't care. In fact, they seem to be doing everything they can to accelerate it, and that's not reassuring.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Pomboy is an economist. She founded MacroMavens where she is the CEO. She joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 So can you just give us your coolest, most attached assessment of what we're seeing? This seems like a big deal, is it?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE POMBOY, FOUNDER, MACROMAVENS: Well, I mean, I can't believe that you're not allayed by listening to Treasury Secretary Yellen. I mean, clearly, she's got the situation in hand.\par \par \uc2 I also am just stunned, Tucker, that you don't believe that diversity, equity and inclusion are the root of a sound financial system. I mean, come on, where have you been for the last year, Tucker?\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It is time the kids to be quiet, and like, let's get some real economist here, who aren't just like repeating "Atlantic" articles. This is insane.\par \par \uc2 POMBOY: It is absolutely insane. And in the layers of insanity, I mean, I can't even believe the videos you showed about that the people at \uc2 SVB\uc2  , you know, focused on the women's ski vacations and whatnot. It's just nonsense.\par \par \uc2 And you're right, what we're facing right now is really serious. I mean, we are on the brink of a 2008 style financial crisis and I'm not trying to be hyperbolic. In fact, you and I discussed this at length in our long-form interview several months ago, and at the time, the Fed hadn't even raised rates nearly as high as they have today.\par \par \uc2 And I was saying, look, you know, raise rates in record fashion on an economy toning record leverage at maximum speculation and expect no consequence.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this was clearly going to happen and now we're seeing the weak links in the chain break. You know, the areas where speculation was most rampant and most egregious are clearly coming down and they are doing so as they seem to do in all the three letter acronyms just like they did in 2008. You know, we're back to the three letter acronyms and there'll be a lot more of those.\par \par \uc2 And frankly, I think, you know, this is the unintended consequence of the Fed's monetary policy, layered with really bad fiscal policy on top of it, but essentially, they've been encouraging people take reckless risks for years, just because you had no alternative, you could get zero percent sitting in a T bill or having your money in the bank, or you could run out and you could speculate, and that's what they've done, and that's all coming back to bite them hard.\par \par \uc2 And we've got some major consequences coming at us, and I think it's going to devolve very rapidly, because of all the leverage that's been built up here.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That's exactly right. If you treated your children like the Fed has treated Wall Street, your kids would all be in rehab. You know, what they have done is bad. No doubt.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Pomboy. I appreciate your wisdom on this. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 POMBOY: Thank you, Tucker. Pleasure.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So if you want to know what's actually happening, ignore the noise. Oh, transgender, equity. Okay, fine. Look at the things that actually matter.\par \par \uc2 For example, what's of value in the United States? What do we own that actually has value? Well, at the top of that list would be something called the Strategic Petroleum Reserve and the Biden administration has depleted it faster than any other administration in history. They sent a lot of it to China.\par \par \uc2 And as they've been doing that they've been attacking the American energy industry, killing the Keystone XL Pipeline, for example, banning drilling on Federal land.\par \par \uc2 So yesterday, an actual energy expert called Alex Epstein testified that we should well actually keep our Strategic Petroleum Reserve and increase domestic oil production. And in response to that pretty sensible suggestion, \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  , you know what she did? She called him a racist.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BUSH: A philosopher who has previously espoused White supremacist views.\par \par \uc2 This is the witness the Republicans invited to discuss issues of energy security. He came here to promote fossil fuels, which we know are disproportionately harming and killing Black and Brown people.\par \par \uc2 Will my Republican colleagues condemn White supremacy and work with us to ensure this Committee engages in advancing good and reducing harm rather than perpetuating it? That is respect.\par \par \uc2 ALEX EPSTEIN, AUTHOR, "FOSSIL FUTURE": I have fought my entire life for freedom around the world including in Africa, including in Asia, including in India and I want everyone around the world to have the same opportunities that I do in the United States.\par \par \uc2 And so I make no apology and the idea that this is associated with skin color is despicable and race and skin color has nothing to do with ideas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, exactly.\par \par \uc2 Would you be able to respond as cogently as Alex Epstein? I couldn't. He's a philosopher and energy expert who wrote "Fossil Future" and we're happy to have him join us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Alex, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 EPSTEIN: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: And for your measured and very smart response to \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  . The whole thing made me worry, though, because her lack of seriousness was just so obvious. I mean, she doesn't, I don't think understand even what you're saying.\par \par \uc2 EPSTEIN: I mean, you know, I really spent a lot of time preparing for this hearing, looking at every detail of the SPR, oil security, thinking of every counter argument possible to see if I might be wrong about something. And then what they do is -- what do they do? They go to Kinko's and print out this poster of something I said in college that they misinterpret, because I said "Freedom is superior." And they think that's racist.\par \par \uc2 So this is really childish, and really embarrassing,\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, and also really dangerous. I mean, you said it in the clip that we played. You've been around the world, you've looked at how other countries have collapsed. If you have people making energy policy who are that frivolous and political, that partisan, like what happens?\par \par \uc2 EPSTEIN: Well, I mean, look at Europe, right? I mean, Europe, their response to the shale revolution, the greatest energy achievement of our time was to ban fracking. So they saw it and said, let's ban it, and look at them. They have this huge dependence on Russia. We have a global energy crisis. There are poor nations who can't get electricity, can't produce electricity, because Europe is buying up the natural gas.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this should be a wake-up call, and instead, these guys are using the exact same playbook.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's really distressing and I appreciate again, your coolness under pressure there. Alex, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 EPSTEIN: And I did, by the way, Tucker, I did offer them all -- I gave this