 forces in the Pacific, says America needs to do more to prevent a fight with China.\par \par \uc2 Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin has that story tonight from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Amidst growing tension with China, the U.S. and the Philippines carried out the biggest military drill since 2015. An attempt to send a message to both China and Russia.\par \par \uc2 Days after Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  stood next to his counterpart, who announced the U.S. would have greater access to four new military bases, including one in Luzon, just south of Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 The top commander of U.S. forces in the Pacific, Admiral John Aquilino, told \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , "The current environment is probably the most dangerous I've seen in 30 years of doing this business. Everything needs to go faster," he said. "Everyone needs a sense of urgency, because that's what it's going to take to prevent a conflict."\par \par \uc2 The Marines just opened a new base for 5,000 Marines on Guam. A bipartisan congressional delegation met Taiwan's president today after meeting its Parliament Speaker, Monday.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. has warned China plans to provide Russia with weapons, or perhaps the semiconductor chips, needed to keep fighting in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Chris Miller, the author of Chip War, argues semiconductor chips are the new oil over which future wars could be fought.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS MILLER, AUTHOR, CHIP WAR: There is no doubt that China has really stepped in, in a big way to bail out Russia, and help acquire the components that it needs to build military systems.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: 90 percent of advanced semiconductors are made in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: They're going to be still quite reliant on chips from Taiwan in five years' time. And I worry that we're not moving fast enough.\par \par \uc2