sted in running for president on the Republican side. Governor, thanks a lot for being here.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2  (R-NH): Hey, thanks for letting me in.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to talk to you about presidential politics in a minute.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're just coming out of this piece about East Palestine and what's happening there. You have the Transportation Secretary saying, essentially, I will get there when the time is right. I do plan to visit.\par \par \uc2 How do you think the administration has handled this?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Terrible, absolutely terrible. Look, I was an environmental engineer for about a dozen years. My job was cleaning up hazardous waste sites. I lived and breathed this. You got to have leadership on the ground.\par \par \uc2 These people are in crisis. They're scared, they have every right to be scared. There's a lot of unknowns. And to say, well, we have some people there. No, we need leadership, we need decision makers on the ground, calming the situation and being super transparent.\par \par \uc2 If we've learned anything, whether it's through COVID, pandemic good leadership, transparency is the foundation of public trust, and that's (INAUDIBLE) doing.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: On a federal level too. On a federal --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Oh, absolutely. Most importantly on a federal level. Look, so few people in America actually trust that Washington is paying attention to them.\par \par \uc2 This is the opportunity that they've missed. To say, we're on the ground, we're with you, hand in hand. Real leaders, whether it's the president, hopefully, whether it's \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 , whether it's his entire team, and staying there. Not just showing up for photo op, but staying there and actually talking to folks, listening a little bit.\par \par \uc2 So, they've missed a huge opportunity politically. But more importantly, they've missed a giant opportunity to actually get into showing up for a photo op, but staying there and actually talking to folks listening a little bit. So, they've missed a huge opportunity politically. But more importantly, they've missed a giant opportunity to actually give people a sense of confidence that the problem can be corrected.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, the split screen is easy for critics to really hit. And that is President Biden over in Ukraine, saying we are with you for the long haul against Russia.\par \par \uc2 And obviously, there's criticism that they haven't had a figure stand up in East Palestinian, they haven't had somebody really, you know, make a stand down at the southern border. How do you talk about that for your party, about the issue of Ukraine and supporting Ukraine against Russia?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Sure, look, so, a couple of things. First, I'm a big believer, you need to support Ukraine, and we need to win. The message it would send around the world if we supported Ukraine and kind of half dipped our toe, and then we backed out, our allies would lose confidence in us. I think China is poking at us, they would have a better sense of our lack of resolve. If something came down in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 It opens the door for China to put potential weapons in Russia. So, there's a whole series and cascades of bad ideas and bad issues that could come if you don't support Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But timing is everything in leadership. And being able to say, look, you know, we're going to delay a trip or we're going to move here. I'm going to kind of an America first guy, not America alone. But America first.\par \par \uc2 And folks, when they're struggling, and you have a major issue like this, it's just one town in Ohio. But boy, it is sending a reverberation of concern to all of America that the president isn't there, Buttigieg is -- I'll go when the time is right, what better time than yesterday? Right?\par \par \uc2 So --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Should they have gone there first, before the president made this thing in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Of course.\par \par \uc2 Well, it wouldn't have been an issue if they were on the ground on day one, right? If they were smart, and on the ground on day one, then, they would have been there, they could have listened. They could have had some transparency, restored confidence, and you could still make your trip to Ukraine and whatever it is.\par \par \uc2 So, again, I think they're just looking for a lot of flag waving as opposed to understanding how to solve problems. You got to know how these systems work and what's going on the ground and the anxiety that our average Americans feel to have leadership that understands how to fix it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of people don't know the state politics in New Hampshire. But right now, you have a state legislature that's 201, Republican 199 Democrat. And yet, even with the Democratic-controlled legislature, you've gotten some conservative things through. Taking off some taxes --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Look, yes. I love it. Always a tax cut to be had, right? So, I'm super proud of had -- this is my fourth term. So, I've had Republican legislatures, Democrat legislatures, we always get our conservative agenda down.\par \par \uc2 We've gotten school choice done, we've gotten -- just opening up a deregulation of the state. Live free or die. Right? It's not just for words, on a license plate. It's how we live.\par \par \uc2 It's local control, limited government, low taxes, individual responsibility, and not just saying it, but actually creating a metric that revolves around it. And that's what we are as Republicans, right? Those are the real conservative values.\par \par \uc2 People say to me all the time, do we need to change who we are in the Republican Party? No, let's just remember what we're all about. Limited government, local control. At the end of the day, it's how you balance a budget and have a surplus. It's how you kind of gain the trust of individuals. And most importantly, this aren't about us, the voters --\par \par \uc2 As a governor, I'll tell you, the voters are smarter than me. They know what's going on in their towns, let them have control, let them make the decisions. That's what the founding fathers really built this country on.\par \par \uc2 I think as Republicans and Democrats, we all have to kind of get back to what this country is all about. That federalistic (PH) type attitude. That's how you can solve a lot of problems.\par \par \uc2 We're super successful in New Hampshire and anything we do to share that model with the rest of the country, you're happy to do it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you ready to make an announcement on this show?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: On this show tonight? I'm in. No, no, no. Look, I'm definitely thinking about it. A lot of folks want us to run. A lot of folks are saying well, how do we make that New Hampshire model more successful for the entire country?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, what does Governor Sununu think of former President Trump and his run for reelection? How will Republicans handle a heated primary?\par \par \uc2 Our interview with The New Hampshire governor continues after a quick break?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Earlier I spoke with New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  about how he sees the presidential race, perhaps how he may fit into it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2  (R-NH): The race for president hasn't really started, no one's really thinking that way. Remember, we're just coming out of November of 22. And you and I are in this world, we're talking about all the time, but the average American, they're just dealing with inflation, they're dealing with how high housing prices, record debt, and at the household level, so people aren't really thinking there yet.\par \par \uc2 And look, my message to President Trump is we thank you for your service, great, but we're moving on. Americans are not in the mode of just settling, right? We're never going to say the next -- the best opportunity for tomorrow's leadership is yesterday's leadership, we want the next generation, we want the next big idea, we want the next piece of technology.\par \par \uc2 And so, it is in the American spirit to say we're going after the next thing and the next individual to lead our party and to lead this country.\par \par \uc2 So, it could be a variety of individuals, I just -- former -- the former president is kind of baked in, either you're with him or you're not, I don't think anything's going to get his poll numbers up. And as this race actually heats up, and people start paying attention, there's just going to be a lot of other options on the table.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And for the people who say the more candidates get in, if you choose to, the more the chances are that the former president shoots the gap and becomes the nominee.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: If we were to stay in. So, here's the key. If folks get in the race, I don't care how many get in, you got to have the discipline to get out. And that's what a lot of folks did not have in 16.\par \par \uc2 And kind of being the, you know, the referee if you will, of the first of the nation prime in New Hampshire, I don't mind pushing a few levers. And they're all great people, they really are all good people.\par \par \uc2 But if you're pulling it five, three, four, five percent as we're going into January of next year, you got to get out of the race. And I'll be the first one to stand up and say, hey, thank you for trying, you're out. We got to narrow this field and go forward.\par \par \uc2 And so, I tried to give folks a lot of confidence that this is not going to be a repeat of 16 where a diluted field has one candidate winning at 25 percent.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here's -- I think one of your constituents who tweeted in Varad, he said, hey, Bret, how about asking him why he's no better than third or fourth place in two of the most recent polls in New Hampshire? Or what makes the governor think a self-described pro-choice Republican has any shot in Iowa, South Carolina or any southern Super Tuesday state?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Am I even in? Am I in the poll? I don't know. I'm not running for president.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I know but in the \uc2 University of New Hampshire\uc2  poll, you're -- you know, at four percent but you're not running.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No, look, people aren't even running it. People aren't -- I get it if folks that are in this world and paying attention, everyone thinks it's game on. But even Governor DeSantis hasn't even announced yet and it's really him --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And what about the other point about the pro-choice?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Oh, I think look, I think the dynamic on pro-life pro-choice has drastically changed. First, let's start with Dobbs, right? Dobbs has changed everything. Some states, if you're really pro-life, then I guess those states will ban it. If you're pro-choice and I think ultimately you'll have folks that are -- well, in New Hampshire, I signed a 24-week rule. Some states have 20 weeks or 16 weeks or 15 weeks. So, we're all kind of in that area.\par \par \uc2 And then you'll have some really extreme left wingers that are pro- abortion. And it's -- nobody's for that. And you'll see that kind of stuff where they want to allow abortion up until the day of birth is terrible.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the most of us, unless you're one of these states that are fully banning it, that might be the way they want to go. Everyone else will be having a battle over, well, you allow choice to 16. And you allow choice to 20 or -- so, I think it's -- I think that it's all a little bit different now.\par \par \uc2 And so, there's huge opportunity. It's not just about a single issue. It's about who can inspire the party to be bigger, who can inspire those independents to come on board. If you can't get the independence, none of this matters, right?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, you're saying extremism is not going to win, number one, and you do believe a rise of the middle or middle right in your case may actually happen?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Oh, without a doubt, people are yearning for a new voice and leadership to -- again, to inspire them not yell at them.\par \par \uc2 We can all complain about the White House administration, we could sit here all day and complain about Biden, and it's an absolute disaster in the White House.\par \par \uc2 But as Republicans, we got that memo, I got it. Let's talk about what we're going to be for. Let's talk about understanding systems, whether it's mental health, opioids, access to health care, immigration reform, which we told we're going to get, we never got.\par \par \uc2 Bouncing a budget. Washington doesn't want to talk about it. Health care reform, we were told as Republicans we're going to get it in 17 and 18. We didn't get it.\par \par \uc2 So, there's so many things we can and should be getting done. And this excuse of, well, we don't have 60 votes in the Senate. Well, we'll probably never have 60 votes in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, make a deal?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Make a deal. Find the plan. Give a little to get a lot. I do it all the time, but you can still get great conservative wins if you have folks that know how to negotiate with the legislature.\par \par \uc2 And you need private sector experience top. Private and public sector experience. You got to have -- I'm a big believer you got to have to sweat out a paycheck or two in your life.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor Sununu, we appreciate the time and when you're ready, come back and talk to us.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: You bet buddy. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Concerns over interest rates sent stocks tumbling today, the Dow lost 697, the S&P 500 dropped 82, the NASDAQ fell 295.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, those artificial intelligence have an inherent bias. We'll take a look.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the surviving son of the lawyer accused of murdering his wife and youngest son takes the witness stand, we'll bring you there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A special witness today in the trial of a prominent South Carolina attorney accused of killing his wife and youngest son. The surviving son of Alex Murdaugh took the stand and his father's defense.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Charles Watson shows us tonight. Good evening, Charles.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES WATSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. The defense called Alec Murdaugh's eldest son Buster to the stand to paint a picture of a loving family who enjoyed spending time with one another.\par \par \uc2 During his testimony, Buster described his father as being destroyed after the murders of his mother and brother. He later went on to contradict the prosecution's assertion that Alex is heard on the video during an interview with investigators following the murders, saying that he harmed his family.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEX MURDAUGH, ACCUSED OF KILLING HIS WIFE AND SON: This is so bad. I did him so bad.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What did he say, sir?\par \par \uc2 BUSTER MURDAUGH, SON OF ALEX MURDAUGH: He said they did him so bad.\par \par \uc2 WATSON (voice over): His testimony also poked holes in the prosecution's theory that Alex killed his wife and youngest son with the missing shotgun and a missing assault style rifle similar to firearms investigators seized from the family property.\par \par \uc2 Buster said they often went hunting on the property so it wasn't unusual for weapons to go missing from the family's gun room. During cross, the state hammered away at its theory that Alex committed the murders to distract attention away from millions of dollars he was accused of stealing from his clients at his law firm and a mounting wrongful death lawsuit from the family of 19-year-old Mallory Beach who was killed in a boating accident involving Alex's youngest son Paul in 2019. Asked about his father's financial issues, Buster said he had no indication anything was wrong.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As far as you knew, financially the family was sound?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATSON: The defense also called a forensic engineer to the stand who reconstructed the ballistics on the Murdaugh property. He testified that a person about a foot shorter than Alex would have had to fire the murder weapon to follow the path of the bullets markings found at that scene. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Charles, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the justices on the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  are weighing arguments about whether tech giants \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  should be subjected to lawsuits over some of their content. Correspondent David Spunt has details this evening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: These nine justices now hold the power to change the way free speech is moderated online.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Your argument this morning.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: At issue Section 230 of the 1996 Communications Decency Act which shields social media companies from lawsuits based on what is posted and shared on third party websites and platforms. In 2015, 23-year-old Nohemi Gonzalez, an American college student visiting Paris, was killed in an ISIS attack. Her family sued \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  arguing all three recommended to their users extremist content from ISIS-related groups.\par \par \uc2 BEATRIZ GONZALEZ, PARENT: I think something good is coming now from this court. And as my husband say, we just seeking for justice.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The family argues an algorithm to send content to users was not protected by Section 230. In court the justices pressed lawyers to differentiate between whether big tech was recommending or providing harmful content online, and whether the algorithms were neutrally applied through thumbnails, small preview pictures.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE CLARENCE THOMAS, U.S. SUPREME COURT: The thumbnails, from what I understand, is based upon what the algorithms suggests the user is interested in. So, if you are interested in cooking, you don't want thumbnails on light jazz.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JUSTICE JOHN ROBERTS, SUPREME COURT: The videos just don't appear out of thin air. Those algorithms just be targeted to something.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Some justices suggested the courts should stay out of the issue for now.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE ELENA KAGAN, SUPREME COURT: We really don't know about these things. These are not like the nine greatest experts on the Internet. Isn't that something for Congress to do, not the court?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): A case with a similar theme involving \uc2 Twitter\uc2  will come before the justices tomorrow. The justices expected to rule by the end of the term, likely the end of June. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt outside the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There are growing questions tonight about artificial intelligence and what some humans contend is built-in bias. FOX Business correspondent Kelly O'Grady is in Los Angeles tonight with that. Good evening, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The A.I. known as ChatGPT is facing scrutiny after critics say the artificial intelligence provides one-sided answers. We decided to investigate asking the A.I. about a number of topics. What we found is a scramble to address bias concerns, something the company admits. ChatGPT adds disclaimers to appear more balanced. When asked to describe President Biden negatively, it responded, "I'm sorry, but as an A.I. language model I'm designed to remain neutral and impartial in my responses. It's not appropriate or productive to engage in negative personal attacks on individuals." When we asked the same for former President Trump though, the A.I. still caveats its response, but then says "I understand your request, so here are some potential negative descriptions of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  based on public perception."\par \par \uc2 These changes appear to be spot treatments, though, with contradictions potentially deep-rooted. When asked to write a poem praising Hispanics it did so without issue. When asked to do the same for Caucasians, it responds "I can't comply with this request as it goes against ethical and moral principles. It is not appropriate to praise or discriminate against individuals based on their skin color or ethnicities." Inconsistencies like this have tech experts issuing a warning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER PREWITT, INVERSION6 CHIEF TECHNOLOGY OFFICER: Not everything coming out of this platform is going to be true and accurate. You need to follow up on this, test things out, ensure that what is coming out of the platform is good, is accurate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: And when you ask ChatGPT if you should trust A.I. like itself, it admits the tech is only as unbiased as its creators. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kelly, thanks.\par \par \uc2 A wide swath of the upper Midwest bracing for historic winter storm. It's expected to bury parts of the region in nearly two feet of snow and bring blizzard conditions. The storm is forecast to dump heavy snow over parts of the Dakotas, Nebraska, Minnesota, Iowa, and Wisconsin. The National Weather Service describes it as an historic three-day winter storm that could be life-threatening enemy places and cause major travel disruptions.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the governor of Texas discusses the growing crisis along the southern border from his point of view.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 32 in Chicago as Mayor \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  says she may have misspoken when she said southside voters who do not vote for her reelection should not vote at all. Critics have called her comments disqualifying and dangerous.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Bourbon Street in New Orleans from FOX 8, our affiliate down there. One of the big stories in the big easy, the city celebrating Mardi Gras. Thousands gathering in the famous French quarter. Festivities began before dawn in some parts of the city. Fat Tuesday marks the end of Carnival season. Get your king cake tonight. Lent begins tomorrow with Ash Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Oakland Democratic Congresswoman Barbara Lee is running to replace retiring California Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 . Lee says the Senate needs her life experiences as a progressive black woman. Lee is currently serving her 12th term in the House. She enters a race already including fellow congressional members Democrats Adam Schiff and Katie Porter.\par \par \uc2 Texas Governor Greg Abbott is discussing ongoing strategies for dealing with the border crisis. He is calling it an emergency item for state legislators. Senior correspondent Casey Stegall reports tonight from Weslaco, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT, (R) TEXAS: Texas is stepping up.\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Texas Governor Greg Abbott was back in the Rio Grande Valley today hosting a roundtable discussion on how the state can continue to secure its southern border in what he calls President Biden's absence.\par \par \uc2 ABBOTT: This is an issue for the state of Texas to deal with. And we have deal with it only because the federal government is not doing its job.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: The governor was accompanied by his newly appointed border czar Mike Banks, a retired Border Patrol agent tapped to help curb crime and speed up construction of state's own border wall.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BANKS, SPECIAL ADVISER ON BORDER MATTERS: The governor talked about the miles that we want to put up. We want to be aggressive. We want to put them up because the traffic is going to go to the path of least resistance.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Leaders of both Texas DPS and Texas Department of Military Services were also in attendance. Both agencies play key roles in Operation Lone Star with troopers and National Guard troops deployed across the region to assist overwhelmed agents and communities.\par \par \uc2 STEVE MCCRAW\uc2 , \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : It matters. An unsecured international border has consequences, and we are seeing these consequences throughout Texas and we are seeing it throughout the nation.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Abbott has now classified border security and fentanyl as emergency items for the current legislative session. He is also asking the legislators for another $4.6 billion to fund border security.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL (on camera): Late this afternoon, the Biden administration proposing a new rule that would prohibit migrants from applying for asylum in the U.S. if they travel through another country first and don't apply there, or if they cross the American border illegally without making an appointment with CBP. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Casey Stegall along the border in Texas. Casey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A former Mexican law enforcement official once in charge of his country's fight against drug traffickers has been convicted of receiving bribes from the Sinaloa Cartel. Federal prosecutors in Brooklyn said \uc2 Genaro Garcia Luna\uc2  accepted millions of dollars in bribes from the cartel once run by Joaquin "El Chapo" Guzman. In exchange, he provided protection from arrest, safe passage for cocaine shipments, and tipoffs about forthcoming law enforcement operations.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on dueling speeches between the presidents of the U.S. and Russia.\par \par \uc2 First Beyond our Borders tonight. The head of the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2 's fifth fleet says Iranian attacks in the waterways of the Middle East and elsewhere in the region have the attention of everyone. Vice Admiral Brad Cooper says he has seen a rise in what he describes as Iran's malign activities over the last two years.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  meets with Greek emergency rescue workers, applauding them for their work following the deadly earthquakes that struck Turkey and Syria. Blinken toured the earthquake zone and pledged more financial assistance from the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Tokyo. One of the big stories there tonight, Japanese panda fans bid a teary farewell to their idols Xiang Xiang and super papa Eimei and his twin daughters. They were sent to China to swap their home at the zoo for a protected facility in the Sichuan Province. Hundreds of people took photos, some wiped tears with handkerchiefs and waved at a white truck carrying Xiang Xiang away as it slowly drove past them on its way to the airport, parting with pandas.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: With this agreement the United States and Russia, the two largest nuclear powers in the world, also send a clear signal that we intend to lead.\par \par \uc2 JENS STOLTENBERG\uc2 , NATO SECRETARY GENERAL: With today's decision on new START, the whole arms control architecture has been dismantled.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , RUSSIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): Responsibility for fomenting the Ukrainian conflict for its escalation and for the increasing number of victims lies entirely with the western elites.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Putin's craven lust for land and power will fail, and the Ukrainian people's love for their country will prevail.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden over in Poland with dueling speeches as Russia says they are not going to take part in this arms control, the new START treaty they signed on to. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " writes "Biden in Poland proclaims \uc2 NATO\uc2 's unity against Russia. Biden's speech came just hours after Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  delivered his own fiery speech, claiming that the west is plotting to destroy Russia. The dueling speeches highlighted a sharp contrast in worldviews, testing Biden's arguments that democracies will outrun autocracies. Biden did not offer any diplomatic offramps, repeating that it is Ukraine's decision as to when and whether to enter negotiations with Russia." This as Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  may be visiting Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Marc Thiessen, \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2  is senior political correspondent for "Axios," and Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com. Marc, former speechwriter, two speeches happening hours apart. What was your take?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": So \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  said that the west has been trying to turn Ukraine against Russia since the 19th century in the Austro-Hungarian empire. So apparently this wasn't \uc2 NATO\uc2  after all. It started much sooner than that.\par \par \uc2 As for Biden, look, he keeps saying that we will support Ukraine as long as it takes, which sounds very robust. But the problem is it's taking a lot longer because of his strategy, because he doesn't have -- or