ge problem that diplomatically this treaty was supposed to run out in February of 2026. They are going to have to try and renegotiate that while we are supplying Ukraine with weapons to fight against Russia. I don't think that Putin would be willing to come to the table to renegotiate any kind of agreement for accountability on that system.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that I heard over the weekend and with President Biden's visit was from President Zelenskyy who said if China enters this war, it will be World War III. Well, what is President Biden doing to prevent China from entering this war? Because so far when it comes to what they have done to the United States, whether it's the spy balloon or spying on universities, et cetera, the president's posture is just we're not really enemies with China. They are just simple competitors. So, he hasn't really given the American people much when it comes to strategy about how he is going to prevent America from getting embroiled in a longer conflict.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, about China, it could have been in the speech in Poland, could have mentioned it on this trip. He hasn't so far. Chinese, this is "The Wall Street Journal," "China's \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  plans Russia visit as Putin wages war in Ukraine. Beijing says it wants to play a more active role aimed at ending the conflict, and the people familiar with Mr. Xi's trip plans said a meeting with Mr. Putin would be part of a push for multiparty peace talks and allow China to reiterate its calls that nuclear weapons not be used." That would be great if they push for negotiations, but if they provide heavy weapons, lethal weapons, what does the U.S. do, Josh?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , "NATIONAL JOURNAL": Yes, it's a challenge because you want to keep China on the sidelines, but they are meeting with Russia. They have an interest in maintaining an alliance against the United States and our allies. So it's a tricky situation.\par \par \uc2 But I think despite