ean Hannity\uc2  starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020230221ej2k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 An Environmental Disaster In East Palestine; Biden Administration Needs To Answer Questions Right Now; Toxic Fallout From Train Derailment Continues; Democrats Vote To Let Illegal Aliens Vote In DC; Interview With Rep. \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  (R-KY); Lowering Hiring Standards Put Us All At Risk; Soros-Backed Prosecutor Dropped Felony Gun Charges Against Michigan State Shooter In 2019; Unfunny Comedian Celebrates Childless Existence; Brave Store Clerk Overpowers Armed Robber\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Janette Nesheiwat, Trace Gallagher \par \uc2 7,557 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 February 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I would go with a spicy chicken sandwich.\par \par \uc2 We have LaMarca from the control room. "What the hell, dude?" He just outed himself. LaMarca had the hiccups.\par \par \uc2 I'm Watters. This is my world\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 East Palestine, Ohio is not a huge place. It's a town of about 5,000 people not far from Pittsburgh. For many years, East Palestine was known as the place that produced dishes and cups and pitchers for America's hotels. The enormous ceramics works there employed much of the town.\par \par \uc2 That's all gone now. And predictably, East Palestine is a lot poorer for it. The median household income in the town is now less than $45,000.00. East Palestine is overwhelmingly White and it's politically conservative. More than 70 percent of the voters in the surrounding counties supported \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the last election.\par \par \uc2 That shouldn't be relevant, but as you're about to hear, it very much is.\par \par \uc2 Eleven days ago, 150-car train derailed in East Palestine, and when it did, it spewed poisonous chemicals onto the ground and into the surface waters. After the crash, the Ohio State EPA found evidence of butyl acrylate in the Ohio River, as well as in the creeks and streams that feed it. Now, this was a concern for more than simply East Palestine, given that the Ohio River supplies drinking water to about a tenth of the entire US population.\par \par \uc2 So this train derailment was an environmental disaster, not an "environmental disaster" like climate change is, but an actual environmental disaster, a disaster that's hurting actual people.\par \par \uc2 And then it got worse. Several days after the derailment, officials decided it was a good idea to set fire to the spilled chemicals. And the result of that decision was a giant toxic mushroom cloud over the entire region. You're seeing a picture of it on your screen right now.\par \par \uc2 So, given the evident crisis in East Palestine, how did the Federal government and the Biden administration respond?\par \par \uc2 Well, under Pete Buttigieg, the Department of Transportation, which responds to train derailments, took decisive action. Pete Buttigieg announced something called Transit Equity Day. That's yet another day that we celebrate race-based Federal funding, that, despite the train derailment, does not apply to East Palestine because the people who live there are the wrong color.\par \par \uc2 So instead, Buttigieg's DOT announced an $80 million project to improve the roads in Philadelphia and $24 million for the roads in Detroit, both of which vote Democrat.\par \par \uc2 And then Mayor Pete talked about perhaps the most pressing problem in this country, which is that we have too many White construction workers. Mayor Pete said not a single word about East Palestine, Ohio. And that's not because everything was under control there.\par \par \uc2 As the DOT was busy tweeting about Black History Month and transit equity, the Governor of Ohio announced that that controlled burn of chemicals from the derailment could potentially kill thousands. And that shouldn't surprise you because vinyl chloride, when burned, turns into phosgene. That was the single most deadly poison gas used during World War I, and vinyl chloride was one of the chemicals on that derailed train in East Palestine. Here's the Governor of Ohio:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. MIKE DEWINE (R-OH): Norfolk Southern Railroad is planning a controlled release of the vinyl chloride at approximately 3:30 PM. The controlled release of the toxic chemicals also has the potential to be deadly if inhaled.\par \par \uc2 Those in the red area, those in the red area are facing grave danger of death if they are still in that area. Those living in the orange area are at risk of severe injury, severe injury, including skin burns and serious lung damage.\par \par \uc2 To state the obvious, all of you need to leave as well. And I need to leave as well, too, so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Notice the tone and the chuckle at the end. That's Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  of Ohio, a great defender of Ukraine. And he's just announced that, well, thousands of people in his State could die. Clearly, he doesn't seem panicked by it. It's not a "hair on fire emergency." It's not like Kyiv is in peril.\par \par \uc2 So after that controlled burn of the deadly chemicals, officials told residents within East Palestine that if you live within a two-mile radius of the derailment, you had to shelter in place and keep your windows closed. Residents closer to the mushroom cloud were put up in a hotel.\par \par \uc2 Within days, they were all let back into their home without the Department of Transportation in Washington saying a word. As one hazardous material specialist put it, "The whole town may be unsafe as a result of this." Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SIL CAGGIANO, HAZARDOUS MATERIALS SPECIALIST: The railroad company is responsible for this and for these people who went back to their homes. Their homes should have been tested. Their homes should have been cleaned.\par \par \uc2 From the onset, these people were being marginalized in an effort to mitigate this. And at first, they're being told, you know, "Go get a hotel and keep your receipts and we will reimburse this."\par \par \uc2 East Palestine is a very poor community. Some of these people don't have the money to go get hotels and do all of this [bleep], and you know, all this stuff. This really looks like a nuclear winter. And well, you know, pretty much, yes, we nuked this town with chemicals, and this is what they're getting.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Now, imagine if this had happened in, well, the favored cities of Philadelphia and Detroit. Lots of poor people in those cities. Everyone feels for them. Everyone wants them to be safe.\par \par \uc2 Imagine at the same time this had happened in Washington DC, in say Georgetown. Well, the National Guard would be called in. There would be no mushroom cloud of toxic chemicals on the horizon. We can promise you that.\par \par \uc2 And of course, in both cases, if this affected the rich or the favored poor, it would be the lead of every news channel in the world. But it happened to the poor benighted town of East Palestine, Ohio, whose people are forgotten and in the view of the people who lead this country, forgettable. So no big deal.\par \par \uc2 The hazmat specialist in that video, Sil Caggiano explained to local media, that the substance, vinyl chloride on the train, that's not the only threat. There's also ethylene gas and we're quoting. "You're looking at five, 10, 15, 20 years down the line until you see potential long-term effects. This stuff seeks low lying areas. It could be in sewers, it could be in nooks, crannies and crevices." Of course, that's how poison gas works. Ask anyone in Verdun.\par \par \uc2 And that would explain the effect that the chemical weapons expert just outlined. It would explain why fish and animals are dying in the area without explanation. Of course, the explanation is very obvious.\par \par \uc2 One resident in East Palestine, says his foxes are sick now. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WKBN REPORTER: Taylor is an ODNR registered fox keeper. A couple of his foxes broke their legs trying to run after the initial derailment. One of his foxes even died.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR HOLZER, FOX KEEPER: Out of nowhere, he just started coughing really hard and just shut down, and he had liquid diarrhea and just went very fast.\par \par \uc2 WKBN REPORTER: Taylor tells me all of his foxes have been sick and acting different since the weekend. Some have abnormally puffy faces, including the one he's holding. He says they are not eating properly. Many are dealing with stomach issues and are acting lethargic.\par \par \uc2 HOLZER: This isn't how a fox should act. He is very weak and limp. His eyes are very like watery and weepy.\par \par \uc2 WKBN REPORTER: Some of the foxes are pacing rapidly in their pen, another sign they are not well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes. Pay attention to the animals always. There's a reason the phrase "canary in the coal mine" is a cliche.\par \par \uc2 So after all of this becomes public, that the water and the air in and around East Palestine, maybe in the region, could be contaminated, finally -- finally -- Pete Buttigieg and the Department of Transportation decided to weigh in: "Our Federal partners at \uc2 EPA\uc2  are on-site and monitoring indoor and outdoor air quality."\par \par \uc2 Air quality? What about water? Oh, water. What does that have to do with climate change? Well, it's the one thing you can't do without. After about two and a half days, you die. But they're not even checking the water.\par \par \uc2 They are only checking on the air a week after people were let back into their homes. And according to Mayor Pete, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  hasn't even screened 200 homes in close proximity to the burn, the burn that wasn't organic or accidental, it was authorities burning it. So they just don't care, actually. Could that be clearer?\par \par \uc2 While residents of East Palestine were inhaling toxic fumes, Mayor Pete was joking about the Chinese spy balloon his boss allowed to fly across the country. He's a funny guy. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, US SECRETARY OF TRANSPORTATION: I mean, if you look at what the American transportation systems have faced in the last two or three years, partly because of the pandemic, we've faced issues from container shipping to airline cancellations. And now we've got balloons.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 [LAUGHTER]\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So you're completely incompetent. Completely incompetent. There's never been a Cabinet Secretary this flamboyantly incompetent and this so obviously uncaring, almost to the point of evil, if we're being honest about it. And then a little joke again. Oh, that was Mayor Pete yesterday, nearly two weeks after the disaster in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Now, was this disaster predictable? Well, we're not sure. \uc2 Netflix\uc2  made a movie about it last year. A train derails in Ohio and spills toxic chemicals everywhere. Some of the locals in East Palestine were actually extras in that film, but no one saw it coming. It never could have happened, right?\par \par \uc2 Well, it is coming out now, and again, we're not exactly sure why this train derailed. But according to a report in "Jalopnik," the train involved in the derailment, \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 , lobbied Federal regulators for about a decade so they wouldn't have to improve their emergency brakes. The Biden administration, like the previous two administrations, didn't push the issue, and apparently the emergency brakes on this particular train failed during this incident.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, there's a lot of propaganda here. There's unions involved. There are a lot of people who will benefit from assigning blame to this disaster. So, we're not going to endorse any theory about why it happened.\par \par \uc2 We're only going to note that Mayor Pete, who's in charge of finding out why it happened, doesn't seem interested at all. And like everybody else in New York, Washington and LA, he's just kind of pretending it didn't happen and telling us little jokes.\par \par \uc2 But we are interested in why it happened and its effects, and for that reason, we're grateful to be joined right now by Nathan Izotic. He is a resident of East Palestinian who lives just two miles from where it happened, and he joins us now with his wife, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both so much for joining us. Are you concerned about being there right now?\par \par \uc2 NATHAN IZOTIC, RESIDENT OF EAST PALESTINIAN, OHIO: Yes, we are.\par \par \uc2 KELLY IZOTIC, RESIDENT OF EAST PALESTINE, OHIO: Very.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Very. I bet you are.\par \par \uc2 Are you seeing effects? I mean, it seems from our perspective, hundreds of miles away like there are dangerous chemicals in your town. Are you seeing the effects of that?\par \par \uc2 NATHAN IZOTIC: We are seeing them locally, inside of our bodies, what we're experiencing. Local fish in our creeks have died. Oily sheens and coloration in our water. Constant smell of burning plastics and chemicals in the air, issues with our dog vomiting, acting lethargic. It's scary stuff here, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, it's horrifying. It's horrifying. It's a nightmare. And again, if that had happened in Washington, DC, no one would be living there right now.\par \par \uc2 Is there any Federal presence, are you getting the sense that anybody to whom you send your tax dollars every year cares about what happens to you?\par \par \uc2 NATHAN IZOTIC: At this moment, unfortunately not.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: No. So there, there are no \uc2 EPA\uc2  officials in hazmat suits, no one from the Department of Transportation, you know, walking around trying to figure out how this happened, how to prevent it again that you have seen?\par \par \uc2 KELLY IZOTIC: Nobody is knocking on our door.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: How many of your neighbors are still there?\par \par \uc2 NATHAN IZOTIC: Our neighbor across the street and our next door neighbor, they came back on this past Saturday as well, when we returned back home and that was days after when they said it was safe to return and we still didn't feel comfortable.\par \par \uc2 And as soon as we got into the Ohio line, we immediately smelled the chemicals yet again. And since then, I now have the chemical burns in reaction rash on my face and my throat is getting irritated again and feeling very uneasy, very uneasy.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Man, I'm so sorry. And I'm just assuming looking at the numbers. I've never been to East Palestine, but it looks like a lot of people there probably don't have anywhere else to go.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN IZOTIC: That's just the thing. We actually just moved here last May and got a nice 14-acre property, a place to kind of call our final home, you know, and we're outskirts of the town where the incident happened. But in town, I mean, it's a very small-knit area. And a lot of them are lower class and low income.\par \par \uc2 And fortunately, we had a place to go on Sunday, whenever we got the two- mile evacuation order. But a lot of those families unfortunately didn't. And this should have happened on Friday, you know, whenever --\par \par \uc2 KELLY IZOTIC: When the initial crash happened.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN IZOTIC: When the initial -- it initially happened, and the chemicals were, for whatever reason not released or known, and in my opinion, and in my industry, there's a paper trail with anything, you know, especially when it comes to rail cars. There are SDS's, there are manifests and right, even if they didn't know, benefit of the doubt what happened then, it is a simple call to the producer, and they can have it to you in minutes. So there is a lot of fishy stuff going on.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: They know what's in the rail cars. Of course, they do.\par \par \uc2 Nathan and Kelly, thank you for joining us and Godspeed in East Palestine. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KELLY IZOTIC: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN IZOTIC: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So residents in town were told they could return to their homes shortly after authorities burned the toxic chemicals and created the poisonous mushroom cloud you have seen.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Governor of Ohio, \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 , says the tap water probably isn't safe to drink, but at the same time you can't drink the tap water because it's filled with poisonous chemicals, DeWine is telling people about Ohio, it is okay to go back home. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: I think that I would be drinking the bottled water and I would be continuing to find out what the tests were showing as far as the air. I would be alert and concerned, but I think I would probably be back in my house.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You've got to think it's possible for the Ohio Legislature to pass a law tonight forcing Governor DeWine to move to East Palestine indefinitely and just see how he does.\par \par \uc2 Drink your bottled water. Stay alert.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Janette Nesheiwat is the director of CityMD, she joins us tonight to assess.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, thank you for coming. How concerned would you be? Would you be eager to move to East Palestine tonight?\par \par \uc2 DR. JANETTE NESHEIWAT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Absolutely not, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 You know, there was a variety of chemicals on that train from isobutylene, ethylhexyl, acetyl, and vinyl chloride. And of course, the greatest concern was vinyl chloride because this is one of the most toxic poisonous chemicals on the planet, such that it is listed as a human carcinogen.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean? It means it has the potential to cause cancer, liver cancer, breast cancer, leukemias, blood cancer. And so on top of that, when you burn it, which is what they're doing now, a controlled burn. Then you have byproducts. Those byproducts can be lethal as well. As you mentioned earlier, phosgene.\par \par \uc2 This is a lethal chemical that has been used as a weapon in World War One, and it is even listed on the CDC website. They have an emergency preparedness protocol in case you come into contact with phosgene.\par \par \uc2 So all these chemicals, in the \uc2 EPA\uc2  that that we know of so far are only testing for vinyl chloride, but what about the ethylhexyl? What about the butyl acetate and all these other chemicals that are causing the symptoms like your previous guest has been experiencing -- headache, sore throat, irritation of the eyes, burning of the skin, difficulty breathing -- you know, the disease of -- you know, the death of the fish, the wildlife, the chickens, the pets, the animals.\par \par \uc2 So it is of great concern. We need to see FEMA out there on the ground, and we probably had a premature clearance by the \uc2 EPA\uc2  to return the citizens back into that community.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, it is not about climate, so they don't care.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Nesheiwat, great to see you tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NESHEIWAT: Thank you. Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Nick Sortor is a video journalist who is on the ground there in East Palestinian, Ohio. He has spoken to business owners and residents of the town. He joins us now\par \par \uc2 Nick, thank you so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 It makes me feel bad, we are not there.\par \par \uc2 NICK SORTOR, VIDEO JOURNALIST: Thanks for having me, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: But we are glad, that you are. What's your assessment of the town right now?\par \par \uc2 SORTOR: So what's going on, on the ground is a little bit different from what you might see in the media. The sentiment from the residents seems to be that they are super annoyed at how the Federal government, how the Biden administration in particular, is handling this situation.\par \par \uc2 You have the \uc2 EPA\uc2 , in particular, they won't talk to any of the residents. You have people that are right up there. I spoke with a small business owner. That was -- their business was right in front of where the explosion was, they can't get anything out of the \uc2 EPA\uc2 . The Federal government came in for a few days, took over their entire business, would not let them in, okay, but also wouldn't talk to them and wouldn't give them a plan, wouldn't tell them anything and that is exactly how it is going for everybody that's in this town.\par \par \uc2 So they don't know if they're going to be abandoned by the Federal government, and they don't know if their town is going to be wiped off the map and it is really sad when you're talking to these people, because they are terrified that this town that they grew up in, that they've lived in for their entire lives is going to be completely demolished by people having to leave. People scared to even come back to the town.\par \par \uc2 There is a myriad of people that do not believe the \uc2 EPA\uc2 's assessment that the town is okay to go back into, so they just haven't bothered, and that is a really scary thing to think that the Federal government cannot be trusted enough to be able to tell us whether or not it's safe to go in an area like this.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, they forced the COVID vaccines in the country, so I think they can't be trusted. But I'm just interested as a factual matter, is the \uc2 EPA\uc2  tracking where this cloud of chemicals went? I mean, it was a local problem, it became a regional problem. Do we have any information on the effects of those clouds of chemicals?\par \par \uc2 SORTOR: Yes, so Tucker, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  is not releasing a lot of the data that that they've supposedly been collecting. They were supposed to release data last week on water samples, right? Was the water supply contaminated? Was groundwater contaminated? They have not done any of that, okay? That's another thing that residents are complaining about a lot because they don't even know if they can trust their own tap water. They don't want to turn on their taps.\par \par \uc2 A lot of these people do have wells, but they have no clue if they're groundwater has been contaminated.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It is just beyond belief.\par \par \uc2 SORTOR: So, I mean, how do you live like that? This is America. This is a first world country. I mean --\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, or was. Nick Sortor, I sure appreciate that report from the scene in East Palestine, Ohio. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So for over two years now, we have been treated to endless lectures about democracy, I'm defending democracy. Well, if you wanted to destroy democracy, what's the first thing you would do? Let people from other countries choose your leaders? That's not democracy, of course, it is the opposite. But Democrats are openly trying to do that now. Let's let the residents of other countries vote in our elections. For real. More on that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: For decades, prominent Democrats have bragged in public we are going to open the borders so we could get more reliable voters. If you noticed this, then you're some kind of creepy bigoted great replacement theory wacko. Oh, you're crazy. Shut up, racist.\par \par \uc2 But it's true, they say it, and now, they're not even hiding it.\par \par \uc2 All over the country in Democratic-controlled areas, it has become common to allow illegal aliens to vote in elections. So foreign citizens, foreigners get to pick your leaders. How is that democracy? That's not democracy, that's the opposite of democracy. Well, that's tyranny of course.\par \par \uc2 San Francisco, Chicago, and New York City have already allowed this to happen. Now, it's become Federal. A hundred and sixty-two Democrats in the House of Representatives have just voted to allow illegal aliens to vote in Washington, DC elections. So you're a resident of DC, you're an American citizen, but your vote is canceled by a foreigner, a holder of a foreign passport who sworn an allegiance to a foreign flag gets to pick your leader.\par \par \uc2 Democracy? No. Not even close. Here's DC Delegate, \uc2 Eleanor Holmes Norton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELEANOR HOLMES NORTON\uc2 , WASHINGTON, DC DELEGATE: There is a long history in the United States of non-citizens being allowed to vote in local, State, territorial, and Federal elections.\par \par \uc2 DC residents, a majority of whom are Black and Brown are worthy and capable of governing themselves.\par \par \uc2 It is true Congress has absolute power over DC, but might does not make right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh, so if something happened in American history for a while, it's okay. Is that what you're saying, you moron? And yes, the citizens of DC and every place in America have a right to govern themselves. She is saying you should be governed by foreigners.\par \par \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  is a Member of Congress from the Commonwealth of Kentucky and we're honored to have him join us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you so much for coming on. How is this not the most obvious attack on democracy?\par \par \uc2 REP. THOMAS MASSIE (R-KY): It is an attack and after years of bleeding about Russian interference, the Democrats have now voted to let Russian nationals and Chinese Communists vote in our elections.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Eleanor Holmes Norton\uc2  was correct, but what she didn't tell you, Tucker, is you had to own land back then. In other words, you were presumed to be a citizen if you owned land back then to vote. I'm pretty sure she doesn't want to go back to those days.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: No.\par \par \uc2 MASSIE: And here is the crazy thing.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 MASSIE: There are at least -- right -- there about 50,000 foreign nationals in Washington, DC, and as you pointed out, some of them have a visa, but about half of them don't have a visa, they came here illegally.\par \par \uc2 And I asked the Ranking Member, Jamie Raskin, since DC allows people who are in jail and prison to vote, does this mean that an illegal alien who is in prison can vote in Washington, DC? And he went and say that they couldn't.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So we have -- this as of special concern now, not just because it is philosophically repulsive, but because we have tens of millions of illegal aliens, foreign nationals, holders of foreign passports, sworn allegiance to a foreign country living in the United States, and our elections are determined by tens or hundreds of thousands of votes. So if they can all vote, then we're done, like, that's -- why aren't people outraged by this?\par \par \uc2 MASSIE: Well, I am outraged, but obviously 162 Democrats aren't because they voted to allow it to happen. So it's just insane, really, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I would say, on this as on so many issues, a rare voice of sanity, \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2 , Member of Congress from Kentucky. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 MASSIE: Thanks, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So if you want to know how free your society is, you just ask a really basic question: Am I allowed to make fun of the people in charge? Can I mock them? Can I tweet that? I want violence, but with words? And if you can, it's a free country; if you can't, it's not.\par \par \uc2 So you should be concerned that comedians are under sustained attack in this country, sometimes physically, and we thought it'd be interesting to make a documentary about it, investigating Getting the death of comedy. It is for FOX Nation. Here is part of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVE CHAPPELLE, COMEDIAN: Ladies and gentlemen, make some noise for hip- hop history.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: A local man accused of attacking Dave Chappelle on stage says he was triggered by the comedian's jokes.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: \uc2 Netflix\uc2  employees walking out of the company's Hollywood office.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hollywood's biggest night overshadowed by an onstage slap.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS ROCK, COMEDIAN: Oh wow. Wow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And then one girl goes, we think you're not entitled to be making some of the jokes you are making.\par \par \uc2 DON LEMON, CNN ANCHOR: We want to talk about another comedian.\par \par \uc2 BIANNA GOLODRYGA, CNN HOST: Well, Kevin Hart's job hosting the 91st Academy Awards lasted just two days.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That was the most racist joke ever.\par \par \uc2 LEMON: And we won't tolerate jokes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't think we should encourage the most easily upset, the most easily offended.\par \par \uc2 WILL SMITH, ACTOR: . out your [bleep] mouth.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The most hypersensitive people in a culture to establish a cultural norm.\par \par \uc2 BILL MAHER, HOST, "REAL TIME WITH BILL MAHER": This war on jokes must end.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am not giving a TED Talk. My job is to be funny on stage.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We don't get angry at the person who is screwing us, we get angry at the person who is pointing out that someone is screwing us, and that's where I come from.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I like to stir the pot, bro.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is what it's like to be you? How do you get through life like this, man?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: If you can't make fun of the people in charge, you're not free. The documentary is called "The Death of Comedy." It's out tomorrow on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 So 20 years ago, no one would even dare to suggest applying affirmative action standards to airline pilots or air traffic controllers, but the Biden administration has mandated that and the inevitable result is millions of lives at risk, for real. It's not a rhetorical point. We've got examples and new information from within the airlines, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Commercial air travel in the United States has long been the safest in the world, so safe you never think about it. When was the last time a big jet crashed in this country? What? October 2001 -- decades ago.\par \par \uc2 But like so much else, that began to change immediately after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  became President. Biden imposed the principles of equity on the airlines, and that meant dramatically lowering hiring standards for pilots and for air traffic controllers and disgracefully, the airlines went along with it. Result: There have been several near disasters in aviation recently with hundreds of lives at stake in each case.\par \par \uc2 Last Wednesday, for example, a United flight was coming in for landing at Bush Intercontinental Airport in Houston. The aircraft was low enough the passengers could clearly see the runway. Right before landing, a plane on the ground taxied out into the path of the United flight. The pilot aborted the landing so dramatically that people on board were terrified. It was another near miss at an American Airport, but this one never made the news.\par \par \uc2 What's happening here? We wanted to know what is going on in commercial aviation. Clearly, something is, so we reached out to someone who has flown for the airlines for many years.\par \par \uc2 The following account was written in December by a veteran \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  pilot. Under its woke CEO, \uc2 Scott Kirby\uc2 , \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  has allowed politics and racialist ideology to trump safety concerns.\par \par \uc2 According to this pilot who works for \uc2 Scott Kirby\uc2  right now, those decisions have put the lives of passengers in grave danger: "(Name redacted) just told me about a B777 seven off Maui that almost crashed two nights ago. Both pilots became disoriented and pulled out of a dive 300 feet above the water pulling two and a half G's. I'm just home from Denver Training Center. There's some real horror stories out there about United, but management is hell bent on just ignoring what is going on. The investigation is still ongoing, but the Captain of the Maui flight was brand new. It was a new hire First Officer and my understanding is that we almost lost an airplane for no good reason. Both had been sent back to go through the four-week course."\par \par \uc2 "The new hire here on my fleet is a nightmare. It took him 50 hours to get through initial operating experience. Worse yet, talking to his instructor, out of his 25 landings in the simulator, 15 ended up in the dirt. Not one of them was on the centerline of the runway. They said his radio work was like that of a private pilot. He has no situational awareness."\par \par \uc2 "I'm jump seating to SFO right now. A United B777 First Officer is next to me. She said the training is totally inadequate for new hires. Her husband helps train them at United's in-house pilot academy, Aviate."\par \par \uc2 "He is constantly asking these kids who come in, has is anyone told you what this is? Many have no idea what they're getting into. They're hiring people straight out of high school now. Zero aviation knowledge or desire. It's just about the money and adhering to ESG."\par \par \uc2 And it's not just United, it is everywhere. \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2 ' in-house pilot training program called Destination 225 has dramatically lowered its standards. Many of its graduates wind up working for a charter company called Swift Air which was under contract from the Biden administration to fly illegal aliens around the country without the American population knowing about it, but other graduates wind up flying for \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Last year, a newly hired First Officer on a Southwest flight, formerly a flight attendant, hit the runway so hard on landing that a crew member was gravely injured.\par \par \uc2 So this is what it looks like in real terms, when you decide that identity is more important than aptitude in something critical like aviation. At some point, many people are going to die because of this. You should know that.\par \par \uc2 Last night, there was a horrible shooting on the campus of \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2  and once again, as is so often the case, we're learning that prosecutors failed to do their duty and protect the rest of us.\par \par \uc2 FOX's Trace Gallagher has a story for us tonight. Hey, Trace.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 The Michigan State shooter is identified as 43-year-old Anthony McRae. His father says McRae turned bad after his mom died a few years ago, and the father says he pled for his son to give up his guns.\par \par \uc2 But now we know the father didn't have to plead because in 2019, Anthony McRae was arrested and charged with carrying a concealed weapon without a permit, a felony punishable by up to five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 But at the time the Ingham County, Michigan DA was Carol Siemon. She was from the \uc2 George Soros\uc2  lineup of progressive prosecutors including George Gascon in Los Angeles and \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2  in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 So in 2019, DA Siemon ended the mandatory sentencing for felony firearms charges allowing Anthony McRae to plead down to a misdemeanor and serve a year of probation. Now, some argue that even with the mandatory sentencing guidelines, it would have been unlikely that McRae would have served five years and they may be right, but that's not the point because had he been convicted of a felony, McRae would have been prevented from owning a firearm when his sentence was up. Instead, the misdemeanor charge left him eligible to purchase and own a gun, which he did.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden and several Democratic lawmakers talked about tougher gun laws, none of them talked about increasing or enforcing penalties for gun crimes.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, DA Carol Siemon said that sentencing enhancements lead to dramatic racial inequity and is not linked to the goal of keeping people safe.\par \par \uc2 The families of eight Michigan State students might beg to differ -- Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Trace Gallagher -- the Great Trace Gallagher for us tonight. You can see Trace every night at midnight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So a well-known media figure has a new video out celebrating the fact that she doesn't have any kids. It is so cool to be childless, but it is actually having the opposite effect. It's really depressing. We'll show you who it is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's Valentine's Day and to celebrate the occasion, we want to show you a clip from something called "The Daily Show," now almost forgotten, but they put out this video and the point is to make you feel good about being an aging deeply unlikable woman who never had kids. It's cool to be childless. Go barren.\par \par \uc2 But actually, the video if you watch it produces exactly the opposite effect. It's super depressing to hold a day in the life of a childless woman.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA HANDLER, COMEDIAN: This is a day in the life of a childless woman. I wake up at 6:00 AM. I remember that I have no kids to take to school, so I take an edible, masturbate, and go back to sleep.\par \par \uc2 I wake up at 12:30 PM and get ready for a busy day of doing whatever the [bleep] I feel like.\par \par \uc2 I put on my most impractical and stylish shoes since I won't be chasing a child around the grocery store. I go to my fave spot in Paris to grab a croissant. I do a meditation sesh on the plane since I have no screaming kids, allowing me all the time in the world to become enlightened.\par \par \uc2 And that's a day in the life of a childless woman.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Narcissism, it makes you happy. No kidding.\par \par \uc2 Jesse Kelly is the host of "The Jesse Kelly Show" and a very wise man, actually. He joins us tonight. Jesse, does narcissism -- does living for yourself, when it's always about you, is that the road to joy and fulfillment, I wonder?\par \par \uc2 JESSE KELLY, HOST, "THE JESSE KELLY SHOW": You know, I wonder why you called me a wise man, Tucker, because I'm actually very stupid. You see, when I was a kid, I thought I was wise. I thought I was very intelligent. I did. You know why I thought that, Tucker? Because I had a mom who loved me and she was always, "Jesse, you're the smartest" and "Jesse, you're the best."\par \par \uc2 And then one day, I'm 18 years old and I got a 0.0 grade point average in college. I'm not making that up and I realized you're not actually smart. Everything you've been told is a lie, Jesse. You're actually quite stupid and you're going to have to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 That's what you see women like Chelsea Handler, feminists like Chelsea Handler going with now. They've been lied to by their society forever, that you could be a girl boss and you can do anything a man could do, which everyone who's ever seen a woman backup a vehicle knows that's not true, but either way they've been told that they should do career and don't do a family or anything like that.\par \par \uc2 And soon you're Chelsea Handler, soon it's Valentine's Day and your room resembles a dried up tumbleweed blowing down an old Western town and your Valentine's Day date for the 10th year in a row is a 10-year-old copy of Magic Mike and a half full bottle of Xanax and you're trying to pretend like you're happy, but you're not happy and it's actually not her fault she has been lied to by a country that has lost its way.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I believe that. It's just -- rather than -- I mean, there really is not an overstatement. A crisis of solitude. People are alone, man is not meant to be alone. Solitary confinement is a punishment, it is the worst punishment. But rather than dealing with it like obesity, they're redefining it as good. How dark is that?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: We redefined everything as good, Tucker, but you're right about this. We tell people that whatever choices you make, no matter what you do, no matter where you end up. It's all good. It's all fine. There are no goals. There is no achievement. There's no this or that.\par \par \uc2 It's all just a bunch of meaningless nothing. And now, we have a society full of people that are sad. They don't even -- a lot of people don't even know why they're sad, but they're sad. They feel directionless, because we've taken that from them. It's not just