perling, the White House economic advisor. Do you think that you are going to get any spending cuts from this White House before this debt ceiling issue is resolved?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, you have to. Otherwise there won't be a debt ceiling increase. Look, we've got four months to do this. You're going to tell me that you can't find waste in Washington right now? I could point out hundreds of ability. I had a great conversation with the President just last week. He said we continue to negotiate. I think we can solve this problem long before the deadline. Put a curve back down because our debt today, in the next ten years, we'll pay 8 trillion just interest.\par \par \uc2 This debt is larger than our economy by 20%. We can't find ways to eliminate some of that. That just means you're not working hard if you can't.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Debt larger than our economy by how much?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: 20%.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: By 20%. (Inaudible) running your own finances that way?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Nobody does and we should do it ourselves.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We'll see what he has to say. Thank you very much, Mr. Speaker. Good to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Still ahead from Washington, Kellyanne Conway and White House senior adviser Gene Sperling on President Biden's claim of creating millions of jobs in the first two years and what does he think about the need to cut some spending in Washington, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Happening right now, a manhunt in New York City after a shooting outside a Brooklyn high school. Police say that two teenagers and an adult were shot. They are all in the hospital in stable condition. The suspect took off and is on the run. No word how old the suspect is.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking today, President Biden in Wisconsin, obviously a battleground state he won by a slim margin back in the 2020 election. He's getting ready for a possible 2024 campaign, repeating this claim from his State of the Union Address last night that some fact checkers are taking issue with. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: We've created 12 million new jobs, a half a million jobs just last month. We've created more jobs in two years than any president's created in a single four year term.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So with that, we're happy to bring in Gene Sperling, senior advisor to the President. Gene, good to have you with us. Thank you for being here today.\par \par \uc2 GENE SPERLING, SENIOR ADVISOR TO THE PRESIDENT: Thank you. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So the President is on the road, going to Tampa as well, and two big issues that he's going to be addressing. One of them is the jobs issue. And I want to ask you about that. We had some pushback on this because some of his critics are saying that millions of those jobs are just jobs where people went back to the same job after COVID. And that actual job creation is much smaller than that, says the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . They say only 214,000 manufacturing jobs and 2.7 million overall jobs if you're talking about jobs that were created.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Well, that's not the standard for how we measure jobs created. When presidents inherit a downturn, you want them to have jobs created to come back. So, yes, there's no real disagreement on this. All of the jobs that were lost, they're not the same jobs, but all of the 10 million that were lost, or about 10 million, have come back. There is about 2.7 million additional jobs beyond that.\par \par \uc2 So there doesn't need to be a debate about this. It's 12.1 million jobs were created under his watch, and some of that reflects an absolutely historic comeback. It took -- it's often taken five, six years to get back. He got back everything and had 2.7 million jobs. There's no country in the world who's done that. And I know when \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  brought back some jobs in 1984. He said it was morning in America, people he probably was reelected because he brought back jobs.\par \par \uc2 But also think about it, when he said that unemployment was 7.5%. We've actually seen unemployment go to 3.4%. It has not been that low over 50 years.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let me ask you, I guess kind of a disconnect. I noticed the President said a couple of times last night, I get it. I get it. Like, I feel your pain. It reminded me of in the Clinton years. You've got 41% who say that they are not financially better off than they were before this president took office. You've got 58% who say they want someone else to run. If everything's so rosy, why are people not feeling this?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Well, I mean, this was a president who made his main theme yesterday, let's finish the job. He didn't say everything was perfect, but we have seen extraordinary comeback. I will tell you, I really believe this. If a Republican president had brought back every job that had been lost in one of the worst crisis we've ever had in just a year and a half, and then created 2.7 million jobs and have 3.4% employment, I think people would be trying to make room on Mount Rushmore.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But the polls are telling us that people don't match that. They're not feeling that they see inflation. They go -- everywhere they go, everything costs more and they see the spending as part of that. Last night, we got a laundry list, Gene, of new programs, more spending on top of the 5 trillion that has already been spent.\par \par \uc2 So I guess my question is, if we're going to keep throwing money at more programs, they talk about child care, nursing home care, all kinds of things on this laundry list, how does that help solve this problem?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: OK. You've said a couple of different things. Let me agree with part of it and disagree with the other part. I think you're absolutely right. It's still been a year of coming back from COVID. You have global inflation. Our inflation is coming down. It's only about 2% the last six months, but it's still too high and people see some key food prices high and gas prices have come down almost $1.70. But people might like them to be a little lower.\par \par \uc2 And so, we understand. I think there's different polls that show different things, but we understand people's frustration. What I disagree with you about is people's support of the president's proposals. The American Rescue Plan is why growth surged. It's why revenue surge and deficits came down.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's also probably why inflation --\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: No, I disagree with that. It's also why we have the highest health care coverage in history. It's why we have a child tax credit that has helped people.\par \par \uc2 And also, let's remember that after all the talk in the previous administration about Made in America, all of this, you are seeing company after company deciding that right now, in this economy, this is a time to commit to decades of employment and production.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. What we're seeing, though, and you know this well, you were seeing leaving (inaudible), leaving California, they're going to places where they --\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Martha, that is decision --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me -- I'll you talk. I just want to ask you another question.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: You often talk about what happens in the private sector. The private sector --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's very important to talk about the private sector.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: That's right. That's why it's 12.1 million jobs is great. That's why the first 4% of growth in two years, over two years since \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  is great. And that is why company after company is building semiconductors and manufacturing, advanced manufacturing all over the United States, red states, blue states, everywhere. Why? They're deciding because of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's policies building infrastructure, encouraging semiconductors, encouraging green manufacturing, that this is where they're going to make the future. They're not looking at two or three months.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me just say this. They're doing that very selectively, state to state, depending on places where they're going to get either a good deal from the government or from the state. But let me ask you -- I want to play something for you because I think --\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Manufacturing is coming back.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You said. You made your point.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: I'm not just saying that, those are the clear facts.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I have one more thing I do want to get to you before I let you go. Speaker McCarthy was just here. He says there has to be some spending cuts. This is now President Biden in 1995 talking about the need for fiscal discipline in the country. We didn't hear one word about cutting anything, any form of fiscal discipline last night. Here he is in 1995. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : When I argued that we should freeze federal spending, I meant Social Security as well. I meant Medicare, Medicaid. I meant veterans' benefit. I meant every single solitary thing in the government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Why doesn't he believe that anymore?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: First of all, when he came into office the deficit 3.3 trillion. Because the American Rescue Plan worked, because growth came back fast, because revenues surged, because we did not need another major multibillion dollar COVID package, deficits have now come down 1.7 trillion.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We also know a --\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Do you know the only thing -- Republicans have already called for three things that would increase the deficit? Get rid of the \uc2 IRS\uc2  investigations on well off corporations and individuals. That's 114 billion. They'll increase Medicare spending by 159 billion refueling the Medicare --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It doesn't answer the question that I asked you, which is why doesn't the President talk about physical responsibility?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: He did talk about fiscal responsibility and he said he --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, he cut spending in order to get the debt limit.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: First of all, we are not negotiating on the debt limit as Democrats did not do with President Trump. They agreed. We all should agree. You don't tie your budget priorities to a threat to savage the US economy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So cuts and spending.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: What I said -- I didn't say that. I said we're not doing a negotiation tied to the debt limit. They have an obligation to raise that. We are willing to talk with them about fiscal policy and what we can do. And I think when the President puts out his plan, you'll see 2 trillion in deficit reduction over the next.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's different than cutting spending though.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Well, what are you going to cut? Well, that's one of the things we'll debate. Do they want to raise the retirement age to 70?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Is there waste in the government that can be cut? Are there waste, wasteful programs, that can be cut?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: The President called for increasing our anti-fraud strike forces and said that would return ten to one. That is one thing we are doing. I'll tell you what we're not going to do. The Republican Study Committee, that's 160 of the Republicans in the Congress right now, called for raising the Social Security retirement age to 70 and the Medicare age to 67. Those are things the President is not going to do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, the things that President Biden in 1995 thought needed to be looked at. So, obviously, he's changed his mind in a very big way. So anyway, it's interesting and we'll see where it all goes.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Thanks for having me. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Gene Sperling, thank you very much for coming, and we do appreciate it. We thank you for coming.\par \par \uc2 So our live coverage from Washington continues with Kellyanne Conway and Juan Williams after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's look at let's get some reaction to what we just heard from President Biden's senior advisor and of the State of the Union, Kellyanne Conway, former senior counselor to President Trump and Fox News contributor, and Juan Williams, Fox News political analyst. Great to have both of you here today.\par \par \uc2 Juan, very long list of spending last night, even touching the idea of cutting spending is so sensitive. It didn't used to be in this country. What is going on? Why can't we talk about cutting spending ever?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Oh, no. I think people want to talk about cutting spending. I think you'll see that. Americans thing --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know he didn't mention it, not for one second last night.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, because I think we're in the midst of an argument over the debt ceiling, and what Democrats view as sort of extortionist. Unless you cut, we're not going to allow the debt ceiling to be raised when in fact there's I mean, we know that argument. The debt ceiling is about obligations to pay money already spent. But the thing is about spending. I think that's what you want to talk about. And I think that the President is open to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But if you don't make it something, you're never going to get it.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No. The question is, and I think Joe Manchin, the senator from West Virginia, is on to this, let's, in fact, set a real deadline to get something done. I think that's what the American people want, a functional government that actually addresses where you have excess in government, where you have wasted spending, where you have people who aren't paying their tax. Let's go in that direction.\par \par \uc2 The President spoke about that last night in terms of saying corporations, the very rich, should pay their fair share.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They pay 52% of every tax in this country.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You know how much money do they make? I think the 1% is like 90% of the income in the country, come on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. But they paid 52% of all the taxes already. How much do you want them to spend? What's would be -- what's fair?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: What's fair I think is for the American people to have this discussion. It's not up to me and you.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: The Democrats don't want the discussion, Juan. And you know that you're right about the tax liability, it's indisputable fact. In addition, when Republicans use one magic word, wasteful, to describe their agenda of cutting wasteful spending, America listens. They know that there are so many pet projects out there, so much fat in the budget, so many things in something called the Inflation Reduction Act, for which every single Democratic senator voted for it, even the ones who say they're centrist, they're moderate, they don't vote with the President.\par \par \uc2 Yes, they do. And they voted unanimously for an Inflation Reduction Act that does no such thing. What it did was toll new spending. And last night he added to that. I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  only gets away with this, and his poll numbers will only improve from the dismal place they are. Now, if we are so stupid that we believe everything he promised last night doesn't literally come with a price tag.\par \par \uc2 Look, Americans live with debt. They finance their education, their homes, their lives with debt. Debt is not a four letter word to many