ans say that they're not better off under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . In fact, 16% say they're better off. That is the worst indictment of our economy since 2008 and 2009 when were in true recession. Compared to 2020, January of 2020, right before the pandemic hit, 59% told Gallup they were better off.\par \par \uc2 Now it's 16%. I mean, you can't argue with the numbers if you want to mention the last administration. The way people feel, people's perception the economy is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's raw political reality. They don't feel more prosperous. And with respect to Gene Sperling, who sat here, two things.\par \par \uc2 Number one, the new metric is not job creation. The new metric is how people feel about their household finances. And he said every single job came back. All the mom and pops did not come back. Our small businesses in so many places are decimated. The blighted storefronts, they're not coming back.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Come on now. We have very low, historic low unemployment.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: That's not the metric.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, but I'm just telling you. No. And you know why people don't feel comfortable? We had three years of the worst pandemic in our history and it drove our economy into a pit. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has helped to lift it up. And in fact, when you look at it, you say to people, are you doing better? Hey, 2020 to now, guess what, Kellyanne, the big intervening phenomenon was the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And you know what, though, the curtailing of the pandemic naturally put many of those people back to jobs. It also increased the tax revenue, which allowed them to bring down the deficit. It wasn't any magic economic levers that were pulled by the Biden administration. It was the fact that you had things opening up again, and we had added $5 trillion in spending. So, yes, you're going to see that deficit come back a bit when the country starts to open up again, which, as you say, was a devastating blow. So