ebruary 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: So would I. Not an endorsement, but so would I.\par \par \uc2 That's all for tonight. DVR "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME."\par \par \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  is up next and always remember one thing, I'm Watters and this is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to Tucker Carlson Tonight.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, the 21st of January, American Intel Services watched, maybe slacked jawed, as a white helium balloon with an enormous payload swinging beneath it, lifted off from Central China and began to float east over the North Pacific on the way here to the United States.\par \par \uc2 Within a week, it arrived. On January 28, the balloon entered US airspace over Alaska. From there, it traveled over the Northwest territories of Canada and then it dropped back into the United States over Idaho.\par \par \uc2 By last Wednesday, February 1st, it was spotted by civilians in the skies over Southern Montana. A man staring out of the window of an office building in Billings saw what he thought was a UFO -- it was too small to be a moon, he said -- and so he began to take pictures, which soon appeared on social media and then almost immediately, the airspace over Billings over the entire town was shut down.\par \par \uc2 Commercial flights were diverted. F-22 Raptors arrived from a nearby Air Force base, along with refueling tankers and an AWACS surveillance plane. It was a big story.\par \par \uc2 So, it was at this point when the existence of an unauthorized Chinese military aircraft over the continental United States became impossible to hide or deny. At that point, officials around \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  finally decided to tell him about it.\par \par \uc2 Now, we don't know \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's reaction to this news, but we do know his response. Biden did nothing. He did not order the balloon shot down. He did not call for sanctions or punishment of any kind against the Chinese government that sent it.\par \par \uc2 It was not until this weekend when the aircraft had traversed the entire United States, including at least one classified nuclear weapons installation, that an Air Force jet finally pierced it with a sidewinder missile off the coast of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Debris was still fluttering toward the ocean when the administration began telling lies, many of them, about what had happened. That balloon posed no threat to American National Security, the Biden administration claimed. Why? Because the balloon never transmitted data back to China.\par \par \uc2 Now, how could officials know that? They never explained. Nor did they say whether the Chinese military would send one of its aircraft all the way to the US without a satellite uplink. Apparently, the Chinese just forgot. They're not very good at technology.\par \par \uc2 It was a nonsensical claim, but as far as we know, not a single reporter in Washington pressed the administration to explain that claim, and that is not really surprising. Reporters in Washington no longer dig, they transcribe and what they transcribe next is almost enough to make you cynical about the role of the press in a free society.\par \par \uc2 It turns out, and we learned this from unnamed officials on background, that allowing Chinese military aircraft to surveil your country, including your missile silos, is not the big deal the rest of us thought it was.\par \par \uc2 In fact, it's routine. It happens all the time. Trump did it, too. Quite a bit of it, actually.\par \par \uc2 At least three Chinese spy balloons flew over this country while \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was President and despite the anti-China rhetoric, he just let them pass. You didn't know that. Now you do. So, settle down. This is normal.\par \par \uc2 In other words, everything \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did while President is bad except this, letting Chinese spy balloons fly over the country. That's very good, and we're taking a lesson from him.\par \par \uc2 So, virtually every news organization in this country repeated that claim, including some who should know better and of course, as soon as credulous reporters wrote their dishonest stories, the Democratic Party and the television stations it controls repeated those stories as fact, which was the whole point. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SENATE MAJORITY LEADER CHUCK SCHUMER: They are saying we should have shot down the balloon the minute we saw it. I would use two words in answering these GOP criticisms. They are "premature" and they are "political."\par \par \uc2 KAREN FINNEY, CNN POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: This is why we voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , because imagine if this had happened under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-IL): The way in which the balloon was taken down allows us to retrieve the parts of the balloon to see what was actually in there.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, US SECRETARY OF TRANSPORTATION I'm just glad that there was no damage or threat to US aviation operations and that this operation took place was done in a very effective, excellent way.\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, MSNBC: Some of the statements from the Republicans, especially in the Senate, have just been asinine and these jackasses going out again, "I've got my shotgun. I'm going to shoot down the balloon." Seriously, again, they just -- they make clowns of themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COOPER: So, the balloon shut down the airspace over Billings, Montana, including the airport and as it moved east, commercial pilots said they were afraid of it. They're afraid of hitting it.\par \par \uc2 So, it is funny to see the Transportation Secretary who is in charge of all of this, tell us it posed no threat, no threat to transportation.\par \par \uc2 But the best is the dismissal of anyone who has concerns. It is those Republicans, again, with their moonshine and their shotguns, and their extra chromosomes, thinking it's okay to shoot down Chinese spy balloons, when every sophisticated person knows, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself knew, the best way to handle Chinese spy balloons, let them pass, again, just like Trump did.\par \par \uc2 Trump, Trump, he's good all of a sudden.\par \par \uc2 So, the question is, did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  let Chinese military aircraft float over the United States unmolested while he was President? Well, someone asked him. He says he didn't. In fact, in the interview yesterday, Trump said he had no idea there were any Chinese spy balloons over the United States when he was President, and he seemed to mean it.\par \par \uc2 But it wasn't just Trump. Trump's former National Security Adviser said the same thing. He said he never heard anything about Chinese spy balloons over the country and you would think that he would have heard.\par \par \uc2 Then the General in charge of NORAD, Glen VanHerck, had the very same reaction. "It's my responsibility to detect threats to North America. I will tell you that we did not detect those threats." So, somehow NORAD, the most sophisticated supposedly monitoring system in the world, missed those Chinese spy balloons -- two or three of them -- and by the way, so did the Director of National Intelligence, the man whose job it is to know America's secrets. He said he'd never heard of Chinese spy balloons over the country during the Trump administration. Here's former DNI John Ratcliffe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN RATCLIFFE, FORMER DIRECTOR OF NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE: Do you remember during the Trump administration, when photographers on the ground and commercial airline pilots were talking about a spy balloon over the United States that people could look up and see, even with the naked eye and that a media that hated \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wasn't reporting? I don't remember that either, because it didn't happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: "It didn't happen," said the former Director of National Intelligence. You can see why he thinks that.\par \par \uc2 So again, according to media accounts, at least one of these three Trump- era Chinese spy balloons floated all the way to Texas in the center of the country and yet inside, the \uc2 US government\uc2 , at the very highest levels, no one seemed to know that it happened. Well, how could that have occurred, really?\par \par \uc2 Well, the Biden administration had an answer. Speaking on background to its shills in the media, unnamed officials explained that these Chinese spy flights were actually never reported to President Trump or the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff or even to the Secretary of Defense.\par \par \uc2 Why weren't they reported? Because they weren't important enough. They were, "relatively minor territorial incursions."\par \par \uc2 Okay, so a Chinese military aircraft hovering over Texas more than a thousand miles from the Pacific Ocean is a minor territorial incursion? That's what they said. It's pure absurdity.\par \par \uc2 But once again, no reporter in Washington pushed back. They just repeated the lie and then, without warning, the White House changed its talking point, undermining the first lie.\par \par \uc2 Actually, they explained today, nobody knew the Chinese spy balloons were floating over America during the Trump administration. We didn't know. We just found out pretty recently. Watch the smarter, but more dishonest White House spokesman spin that tale.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET), COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS AT THE NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: I can tell you that we discovered these flights after we came into office and I'm not going to get into the specifics of how we did the forensics. I think you can understand that we're going to be careful about that.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: They're not going to get to the specifics of the forensics (Oh, the forensics), but the one thing they were really shocked by when they took control of the \uc2 US government\uc2  was how soft on China Trump was. They had no idea. He was letting these Chinese spy flights go over. They were shocked, as shocked as anyone would be. That's what they're saying. Of course, once again, they can't say more because it's classified. Wish we could, sorry.\par \par \uc2 So, what is the real story here? Honestly, we don't know. The Pentagon ignored our many questions. Of course, they did, because we're not going to transcribe them.\par \par \uc2 So, once again, the people who owe you the truth are lying to you. So, all tonight we know with certainty is that the Chinese military just flew an aircraft that reportedly contained explosives over the entire length of the United States and got away with it. That seems like a bad precedent.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  of Florida thinks it may be a bad precedent. He joins us tonight. Senator, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 Why all the lying about this? And what do you think is actually going on?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): Well, I can tell you they are categorically lying, they are misleading people. Okay, the fact that a balloon -- these balloons that the Chinese have been innovating for a while, they've used them a couple of times to circumvent the globe and the like. You know, people took their pictures and posted on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  about it, but they have never ever crossed the entire continental United States flying over places like where our Strategic Command is based.\par \par \uc2 These balloons -- look, a couple of things people are wondering about. Number one, in that space between where satellites operate and where a spy plane could operate, in that zone, these things become very useful especially if you have a lot of them, which is what it seems like the Chinese are trying to innovate. What do they have on them? The ability to take in real-time video, high-resolution photographs, potentially as well, capture electronic communications, and they can loiter, that's the advantage.\par \par \uc2 It's cheap. You're not looking for them because they move very slowly and they can Lloyd over a period of time.\par \par \uc2 So imagine, in the middle of a war, one of these things in the Pacific being able to take real-time footage real time and feed it back of what's going on, on the ground, and what is going on in the sea and so forth.\par \par \uc2 But the notion that somehow this has been happening all along, it's just not true. I mean, because people were spotting this thing with a naked eye and they were seeing it, they were calling in, they were --\par \par \uc2 I mean, they had to warn people in South Carolina not to try to shut them down on their own, you know, because they were visible to people.\par \par \uc2 They are just being dishonest with no reason. Well, the reason being is I think that they were caught off guard, and did not respond appropriately. And now they're scrambling for answers and to deflect blame.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: The Pentagon shouldn't be lying to us, right? I mean, a lot of Americans, I put myself in this category, I always admired the US Military, but if they become a political instrument for an administration, and they seem like they are, some of their leaders are, I mean, that seems like a disfigurement of the military and it is very discouraging to watch.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, and it's also in many cases, as you've already pointed out, these unnamed sources that go off the record and leak out these little pieces of information, so we don't know if they are political appointees or civilians or someone just trying to climb up the chain of command and be promoted.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: But at the end of the day, we're losing focus on the fundamental issue here, and that is that the Chinese continue to develop and innovate new capabilities, including ones that appear really rudimentary and pretty simplistic, but actually can be proven to be quite effective.\par \par \uc2 But the most important point is they felt bold enough to fly this thing entering over Idaho through Montana, the Midwest of the United States. I mean, literally cut a diagonal path right across the middle of the continental US, that's never happened before.\par \par \uc2 And then, you know, obviously exiting off the East Coast, and it felt like they didn't have anything to fear about it. And it was, in many ways, in my view, it was sending a message for the world. And that is, look, we can fly these things over airspace of the United States, and they can't and won't do anything about it. So what makes you think they're going to do anything anywhere in the world? They're a hollow superpower. That's the Chinese argument. That's the point they're making tonight to the world.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It does seem like an obvious dominance move.\par \par \uc2 So the Biden ministration, was saying, well, we only had a very narrow window to shoot it down off the coast of South Carolina, because then it would drift in international waters, at which point we were bound by something called international law.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the Chinese broke the law by flying it over our country. So, why would the Biden administration be caught up in the minutiae of international law, which is kind of a meaningless concept. Why wouldn't they shoot it down where they felt like it? It's our country.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, that's a great question, and one they should answer. I mean, the trajectory of it was pretty clear by early to late last week to early last week -- early last week too late the week before, you could tell the trajectory that it was coming, they were tracking it, they knew it actually went over Canada for a brief period of time as well, and that's also NORAD territory. So they had multiple options to bring it down.\par \par \uc2 Look, I'm not saying detonate this thing over a major US city or populated areas, this thinks the size of three city buses. If that lands in the wrong place, it's going to hurt people. But they had other options to bring it down.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, you know, the Chinese could have also moved this thing they didn't feel like they needed to. This thing probably has a self-destruct mechanism on it, that allows it to self-destruct if necessary. They didn't feel any need to do that.\par \par \uc2 So ultimately, I think that this to me, is just one more example of hopefully, look, it's a balloon but I hope it wakes people up to the fact that we are now engaged, and if the people don't realize it already, in a full scale geopolitical competition with the Chinese, and they're playing to win and we are over here messing around with a lot of silly stuff and back talk that isn't addressing the fundamental issue of the 21st Century.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, self-destructive and weak.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  of Florida. Great to see you tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Thanks, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So several years ago, we reported how the \uc2 FAA\uc2  had decided to hire air traffic controllers on the basis of equity criteria and not on the basis of who would be a good air traffic controller, and they are doing that with pilots, too.\par \par \uc2 So we were really shocked to learn there was yet another near collision between two commercial aircraft at a major airport. \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  was supposed to be in charge of all of this, he didn't notice. He was too busy celebrating Transit Equity Day. We've got details next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Mayor Pete is out there celebrating Transit Equity Day. Happy Transit Equity Day, by the way. Meanwhile, there has been yet another near disaster in a major airport.\par \par \uc2 FOX's Trace Gallagher has the story for us tonight. Hey, Trace.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: It happened in Austin, Tucker. This was a \uc2 FedEx\uc2  767. It was cleared to land in Austin on the same runway that a \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  737 was cleared to takeoff off.\par \par \uc2 The flight tracking system, FlightRadar says at one point, the planes were less than a thousand feet apart. Now, the \uc2 FedEx\uc2  plane was at an altitude of 75 feet before pulling up and avoiding the collision. But here's the deal, a 767 lands at 180 miles per hour. That is 280 feet per second, which means at one point the planes were less than four seconds away from colliding.\par \par \uc2 It is interesting that the air traffic controllers told the Southwest plane to abort takeoff and get off the runway, but instead, the Southwest pilot said negative and got airborne instead.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  is investigating, but we are still waiting for more information on that near miss that happened last month at JFK between a \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  plane and an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  plane also only about a thousand feet apart.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  has not commented on the near collision, though he did comment on Transit Equity Day, which was happening at the very same time, the planes in Austin almost collided. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: I love that image of the doors opening on the bus or the subway car representing doors opening in so many other areas of life, if you can get to where you need to be in an equitable way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Kind of comforting. Tucker, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Trace Gallagher with that report. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: You bet.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, there is really no institution in American life that had higher trust than healthcare, and now has lowered trust after a couple of years of COVID.\par \par \uc2 The entire sector has been gravely discredited for obvious reasons if you've lived here since 2020, but what is interesting is that very few people who work in the healthcare sector seem to understand this, and certainly no one has apologized.\par \par \uc2 We bring it tonight one notable exception. Kevin Bass is a medical researcher. We don't know what his politics are, he doesn't seem like a right-winger or anything at all but he just wrote one of the most remarkable pieces we've seen this year in "Newsweek" admitting that his entire business got wrong. It's time for the scientific community to admit we were wrong about COVID, and it cost lives.\par \par \uc2 God knows what the effect of that level of honesty will be on his career, but in the meantime, we're happy to have him join us now.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Bass, thank you for coming on and thank you for writing this piece, which I want to recommend everyone, totally nonpolitical, and I don't even know what your politics are. I assume you're liberal. But you're honest in this piece. Why were you moved to write this?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN BASS, MEDICAL RESEARCHER: Well, for many of the reasons that you discussed that, I realized that we have lost trust in this country as a healthcare industry from the ordinary person. Anybody who knows, anybody who is watching the media, anybody who is on social media, and is talking to ordinary people realizes just how angry so many ordinary people are.\par \par \uc2 And I felt that if I wrote a piece like this apologizing for, you know, I didn't set policy, but I certainly supported it and every single day, I certainly patted all my friends on the back for supporting it and for thinking of the right kinds of thoughts. I thought, if I apologized, then at least I was doing my part to make things better. And as we know, change starts one person at a time, so that's why I wrote the piece.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Amen. It's such an honorable and difficult thing to do. I was so very impressed by your piece for exactly that reason. If we're going to say change starts one person at a time, the first thing we should all do is apologize for what we've done wrong, but no one ever does.\par \par \uc2 Did you -- and again, we don't have time to go through all the different things, but you basically hit everything from masks to vaccines to the lockdowns. Are you worried, since you're a younger man, still in school, are you worried about the effect of this piece on your career?\par \par \uc2 BASS: Yes, absolutely. I'm saying something that's not going to be very popular. A lot of people don't understand this point of view. When you talk to especially a lot of older individuals, including some people I admire greatly for all sorts of other different reasons, including great scientists, they often don't understand what I'm doing.\par \par \uc2 And so -- but you know, some of the younger generation does and there are some -- there are many people within the healthcare industry who do understand. So I think if I just speak the truth, which is what I'm trying to do, just speak the truth, and I do so with a pure heart, that's what I'm here to do.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I wonder why this hasn't occurred, and again, for the third time, bless you for doing it, but I wonder why it hasn't occurred to senior people in this business, I mean, people who ran, I don't know, our medical agencies to do the same? I mean, that would just -- that 