w are they spending the money? Well, here's one of the things they're paying for.\par \par \uc2 (CLIP FROM CBS/RECORDING ACADEMY.)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Sponsored by \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 . Oh, a little devil worship.\par \par \uc2 So let's say you're a modern American who doesn't believe in the supernatural at all. You reject the ideas that have formed the basis of every society in all human history. But you think it's bunk. Good and evil does not exist in the absolute sense, even if you believe that, and some Americans do.\par \par \uc2 Is it really a good idea to mimic devil worship? Because what if it is true? Probably not going to end well, right?\par \par \uc2 No one is asking those questions.\par \par \uc2 Sam Smith, the performer there has gotten rich doing it. His latest music video shows him wearing nipple pasties and being urinated on in a men's room.\par \par \uc2 (CLIP FROM "I'M NOT HERE TO MAKE FRIENDS.")\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, they stormed Omaha Beach for that, trans ideology plus Satanism, popular entertainment.\par \par \uc2 Chadwick Moore is contributing editor at "The Spectator." He joins us to make sense of what we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 Chadwick, great to see you tonight. So like you kind of wonder like, what's the next boundary? What's the next glass ceiling to be shattered? Is it cannibalism or kind of where do we go from here?\par \par \uc2 CHADWICK MOORE, CONTRIBUTING EDITOR, "THE SPECTATOR": I think we're actually already seeing it, you know, like satanic themes in pop music have been around since like the 70s and 80s with Kiss and Motley Crue, and of course, whenever an artist needs to have a little controversy, they're like, let's just put on some devil horns and do satanic ritual, like Rihanna did or not Rihanna, Nicki Minaj did something similar in 2016.\par \par \uc2 It's so tired and predictable and so boring, but what is new, I think is Sam Smith is physically grotesque and that seems to be how he is getting attention. He didn't al