e and he began to realize, wait a second, this country is really unhealthy because pharma and food spend more than any other industries on lobbying, and it has lots of effects and he became a completely different person.\par \par \uc2 We had a fascinating conversation with Calley Means for a new episode of "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today." Here it is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CALLEY MEANS, TRUE MEDICINE: I've become convinced now and I'm working to change the biggest issue we face as Americans as we're getting sicker, fatter and more depressed, more infertile. And when you tie those issues and really unpack it, it's because of food.\par \par \uc2 So I sat across from Coke, and about 10 years ago, when they were trying to keep Coke spending on food stamps, as we talked about. And the playbook was it was very simple and it's not complicated. It's how do we rig institutions to trust? Who do people trust? And how can we pay them and get in front of them?\par \par \uc2 So we talked about the \uc2 NAACP\uc2 , it was in a transactional meeting. It's like, here's some money, you can call our opponents racist. People trust the \uc2 NAACP\uc2 , when they call someone racist, it shut down to debate and there was contemporary reporting in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " how the \uc2 NAACP\uc2  and that \uc2 Hispanic Federation\uc2 , they were out in force, and that was from White men sitting across the table from them, paying them money and giving them talking points.\par \par \uc2 It was very unsettling. It was very unsettling.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You were a witness to this.\par \par \uc2 MEANS: I put list as a junior person, put a list together, \uc2 Hispanic Federation\uc2 , NAACP, African-American pastors. We would call them, they are pay-to-play, and this is a playbook. This is like ordering a Big Mac, this is very transactional.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: In this specific case that you saw, just to be completely clear to the audience watching. The issue at the core of it had nothing to do with Civil Rights or race or anything related, correct?\par \par \uc2 MEANS: No, in fact, lower income Americans are getting decimated. There's skyrocketing diabetes rates among lower income children. A man in the lowest income bracket in the United States dies 15 years younger than a man, an American at the richest income bracket and that is almost entirely because of nutrition.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So if you're wondering how soda wound up on the list of foods that can be bought with food stamps. That's how. Amazing conversation. Calley Means. That's tomorrow on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is hiring by race everywhere across the entire Federal government, but you wouldn't think he would hire Judges that way because Judges, well, their only job is to interpret the Constitution. It doesn't matter what color they are.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing that and degrading the country, the judiciary by doing it and we've got the numbers to prove it. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So the point of diversity, equity, and inclusion, as you often heard is to wind up with a leadership class that "looks like America," and we think we should hire on the basis of merit, but that's kind of an appealing idea that people run the country should look like the country. That's not what it's about at all.\par \par \uc2 Because no administration has ever looked less like America just by the numbers than the Biden administration. It is not about making the administration look like America, because they are discriminated against certain classes of people who don't vote for them. Period.\par \par \uc2 And now it's provable. Jeremy Carl at the Claremont Institute just ran the numbers and this is in the Judiciary. He found that out of 97 Federal Judges confirmed under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the total number of White men, five, 22 are Black women.\par \par \uc2 So this is race based hiring. It's illegal, but it is also not about looking like America. It's about punishing people. And it's also incidentally or maybe not incidentally, producing nominees for Federal Judgeships who know nothing about the Constitution, like this person.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): Judge, on the far end, tell me what Article V of the Constitution does.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE CHARNELLE BJELKENGREN, SPOKANE COUNTY SUPERIOR COURT IN WASHINGTON STATE: Article V is not coming to mind at the moment.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Okay. How about Article II?\par \par \uc2 BJELKENGREN: Neither is Article II.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So she doesn't know what's in the Constitution, but she's going to be a Federal Judge whose job it is to interpret the law against the Constitution. Okay. It is degrading.\par \par \uc2 Horace Cooper is the Co-Chairman of Project 21. He's also a lawyer who knows what's in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Horace, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I'm not a lawyer, not even as -- I don't even have a successful background in academia and I knew what those were, so like that's pretty bad, right? Why is this not on the front page of "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ?"\par \par \uc2 HORACE COOPER, CO-CHAIRMAN, PROJECT 21: Well, it should have been, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 You know, I actually taught an Article I course all about the legislative process at \uc2 George Mason University\uc2  and it is important that people understand what the options, what the authorities, what the powers that our founders granted, our Congress, our Federal Legislature, and we had an entire course focused on that.\par \par \uc2 But George Mason, like other good schools wants quality graduates. We want talented graduates and we are interested in seeing to it that the people who leave with a Degree or Masters of the topic.\par \par \uc2 What we're seeing from the Biden administration, and which is what we're seeing, generally, from the progressive movement, is this idea that we just need to reshape the judiciary in a way under color. I use that term in a duplicative way, by pretending that we're making the Courts look more like America.\par \par \uc2 But the truth of the matter is, we're finding people, they aren't talented, they aren't highly skilled, but they know progressive vision just like the back of their hand, right?\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: That's the ultimate goal of what is happening here, not excellence, and America suffers, corporation suffers, defendants suffer.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, and it's just so unbelievably degrading, I think for the whole country. It's the opposite of excellence and I appreciate your saying so.\par \par \uc2 Horace Cooper, great to see you -- sorry, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: I was just going to say, we set up this independent judiciary as a way for every American, instead of self-help, which is the nasty way of saying, I'm not going to rely on the legal or the justice system.\par \par \uc2 We set up -- our founders set up this independent judiciary so that we can get people to voluntarily agree to bring their disputes there. I don't want the cardiologist to be chosen by his color. I don't want the pilot to be chosen by his race, and I don't want the judiciary to be chosen that way.\par \par \uc2 We need excellence and this administration doesn't want that.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Amen. Great to see you tonight. Thank you, Horace.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So really, at the core, no one says it but it's true. At the core of the Democratic program is race hatred. Obviously, the party of Jim Crow is still a thing, nothing never really changed it, it is just called equity now.\par \par \uc2 Florida State University\uc2  completely controlled by the left has just adopted a series of diversity, equity, and inclusion programs to promote bigotry openly, it castigates believing Christians for their Christian privilege. There is no Christian privilege in this country by the way.\par \par \uc2 The school also offers a racially segregated scholarship that bars White students from applying, which is illegal, but it's also race hatred. What's another word for it? There isn't one, it is race hatred. It is everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Chris Rufo, tirelessly, is on this. He broke this story. He is a fellow at the Manhattan Institute. He joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Chris, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 So I just have to ask you, once again, how is this, which is so obviously illegal under Federal and State laws, all 50 States, how is this allowed?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER RUFO, SENIOR FELLOW, MANHATTAN INSTITUTE: Well, it shouldn't be, but it is allowed because these are people who have power, they've expanded in the universities with these so-called diversity, equity, and inclusion programs and no one is challenging them from within the institution. And so that's why I'm doing this investigative series. I'm exposing it, we're going to put pressure on these folks because what they're doing is quite simple.\par \par \uc2 They are dividing Americans, or in this case, students at Florida State along what they call a matrix of oppression. They are designating some groups oppressors, other groups are oppressed, and they're creating special benefits and special punishments based on your identity.\par \par \uc2 It's wrong. We have them scrambling and the more we expose it, the more we put pressure on them, the more that we can stamp out this racialist ideology that is capturing all of our institutions.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, and it's just -- it is directly opposed to the America that normal people want to live in of all colors, by the way.\par \par \uc2 You're not prepared for this, I'm just throwing this at you. I just read today that you've been appointed to the Board of a College by Governor DeSantis in Florida, and in a minute thirty, can you sum up what it -- it is a very interesting story. Can you sum up what you're doing and what that is?\par \par \uc2 RUFO: Yes. I was appointed along with six other trustees to take over the New College of Florida. It's the smallest public university in the system.\par \par \uc2 On our first Board meeting we fired the President. We let go of the general counsel. We switched the Board Chair and we're going to implement top to bottom reforms of this university.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be bringing a classical Liberal Arts education and demonstrating it is time for conservatives to get tough. It's time for when we win elections to actually start changing institutions. And we're not just simply going to be the caretakers of left-wing ideology. We're taking action. Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is leading the way and I'm proud to be part of this movement.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So you're not waiting around for a report from the current administration at his college?\par \par \uc2 RUFO: No. We're acting very quickly and I think you're going to see dramatic changes right from the outset. And look, we're following the Governor's lead. This is a guy who has a bold vision, takes courageous action and, and really, the thing that I hope to demonstrate in this experiment, taking over this college is political courage because that's what we need as conservatives.\par \par \uc2 It is time to stop just doing white papers, talking to each other. It's time to take action.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You know, John Durham is going to do a report in the next 10 years, so just hold tight. Chris Rufo, it is great to see you.\par \par \uc2 Now, walls don't work, walls are racist, you can't have a wall but the Democratic Party is building one right before the State of the Union.\par \par \uc2 Also, we want to point out that tonight's transgender Satanism brought to you by \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: In January of 1972, two police officers, Gregory Foster and Rocco Laurie were murdered while on duty. Their assassins were members of a black nationalist group called the Black Liberation Army, a terror group. The wife of one of the slain officers spoke at the Rocco Laurie Memorial to honor the 51st Anniversary of those killings.\par \par \uc2 We wanted to show you part of what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ADELAIDE LAURIE, WIFE OF SLAIN OFFICER: It's hard to believe that it's been 51 years since Rocco and Greg were murdered. It was so senseless, too, you know.\par \par \uc2 Rocco was only 23 years old and Gregory was only 22.\par \par \uc2 My life was never the same again. People often say that time heals all wounds, but when you have a broken heart, that never heals.\par \par \uc2 But today, we're honoring the memory of Rocco. He was a hero and we need heroes to look up to today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We thought that was worth remembering.\par \par \uc2 So the Biden administration is so committed to democracy that they're building a huge wall around the Capitol, what was formerly called the People's House is now just their House and anyone who is not from there can get away and stop asking what goes on inside.\par \par \uc2 You're seeing pictures on your screen right now. You're no longer allowed in your own House because January 6th. We can make you hostile if you think about it.\par \par \uc2 It's behind those walls that Biden is going to read the State of the Union tomorrow, and you need to stay far, far away because it's not your country anymore. A small group people who hate are running it.\par \par \uc2 We've got a packed show tomorrow night -- Glenn Greenwald, \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , Doug Macgregor, Brit Hume -- a few of the guests who will be joining us right before the speech.\par \par \uc2 We are out of time for tonight, but we hope you have the best evening with the ones who love, doing things that actually matter and not talking about politics and we will see you tomorrow in the State of the Union Address.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020230207ej2600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Low Unemployment Rate; Interview With Sen. \uc2 Jerry Moran\uc2  (R-KS); Interview With Sen. \uc2 Mike Rounds\uc2  (R-SD); China's Spy Balloon\b0\par\par\uc2 Mark Penn, Dan Hoffman,Jacqui Heinrich, Susan Li, Jennifer  Griffin, Jack Keane, Griff Jenkins, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,480 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 February 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: It's violated U.S. airspace. It's violated international law. We communicated that back to the government of China.\par \par \uc2 We do continue to monitor the balloon. We do know that it is a surveillance balloon. It's moving eastward across United States, currently over about the Central United States.\par \par \uc2 Any potential debris field would be significant and potentially cause civilian injuries or deaths.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Once it's over a body of water, will you shoot it down?\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Again, right now, we're monitoring the situation closely, reviewing options.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Up, up and not going away, a suspected Chinese spy balloon flying high above the continental U.S. and now heading east, sightings over Missouri and Kansas, after hovering over several sensitive U.S. military sites, so serious, U.S. officials did consider shutting it down. No more.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  abruptly canceling his trip to China, China confirming, yes, it's ours, but blames high winds for carrying it all the way over U.S. airspace. Let's just say some people don't quite believe that.\par \par \uc2 We're all over it, regardless, with Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon on how the military is responding to all this, Griff Jenkins on Capitol Hill with growing calls for investigations into this, \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2 's \uc2 Mike Rounds\uc2  on how long the administration may have known about all of this, and our General Jack Keane on the new tension with China because of this.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Man, who saw this one coming? New signs that we might have seen it for quite a while. So why are we just finding out about it now and growing concerns about what we're going to do about it now.\par \par \uc2 To Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon -- Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Neil, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is speaking right now with reporters, taking questions at the State Department standing beside his South Korean counterpart.\par \par \uc2 He was asked just moments ago about the Chinese spy balloon. Here's what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We made clear that the president of a surveillance balloon in U.S. airspace is a clear violation of U.S. sovereignty and international law, that it's an irresponsible act, and that the PRC's decision to take this action on the eve of my planned visit is detrimental to the substantive discussions that we were prepared to have.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The balloon has left Montana airspace. Most recently, it was sighted over Northeast Missouri.\par \par \uc2 It was launched from mainland China. The Pentagon is not buying China's apology and explanation that this was some sort of civilian research balloon that was blown off course. Pentagon Press Secretary Brigadier General Pat Ryder says the spy balloon is maneuverable and has changed course. He would not say whether the Chinese government is still controlling its direction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RYDER: The balloon has changed its course. Right now, we assess that it'll probably be over the United States for a few days.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The balloon is flying at 60,000 feet, which is higher than most U.S. warplanes can fly. The U.S. does not fly spy planes or spy balloons over mainland China, according to a senior U.S. defense official.\par \par \uc2 There have been questions about why the military advised the president against shooting down the Chinese spy balloon. For now, one U.S. official said to me there's a lot of empty space in Montana, but there are also a lot of schools. It is not unpopulated.\par \par \uc2 What happens when this balloon, which is carrying heavy equipment and is the size of at least two school buses, lands on someone's home or on a kindergarten? The official added, if we shoot it down, the balloon and the equipment on board will plunge to earth at meteoric speed.\par \par \uc2 I'm told the possibility of American civilian casualties and collateral damage is the main reason the president opted not to shoot down the balloon for now. The White House and Pentagon still have options for bringing down the spy balloon when it is deemed safe to do so and would not rule out shooting it down perhaps once it is over a body of water.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials tell me they have mitigated the ability for this balloon to survey any sensitive sites, blocking it from doing so, and that they are learning a lot by monitoring the balloon -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, they continue to monitor it, but it is no longer able to pick up intelligence; is that what you understand? Is that what they're saying?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: It's not clear whether it can still gather intelligence, but there's no evidence that it is actually sending that intelligence in real time back to Beijing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I see.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: But what I have learned is that the U.S. has taken measures at sensitive sites to ensure that sensitive intelligence is not compromised as a result of this spy balloon.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Jennifer, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Griff Jenkins right now in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We do know that \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , the secretary of state, has canceled a planned trip to China. What was interesting about that, Griff, is that the administration did know about this, presumably, for some time. We're not aware of how long. That meeting was still on. So it was just canceled or put off today, right?\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, delayed, Neil. Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And, look, we learned one thing. And that is if there's anything that all of Washington can agree upon, it is that a giant Chinese spy balloon should not be floating across the continental U.S.\par \par \uc2 Now, Montana's Democratic Senator Jon Tester joined his Republican counterpart, Steve Daines, today demanding answers, saying in a statement this: "We are still waiting for real answers on how this happened, and what steps the administration took to protect our country. And I will hold everyone accountable until I get them."\par \par \uc2 Senator Daines, who sent a letter to Defense Secretary Austin yesterday, is blasting President Biden today on inaction. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. STEVE DAINES (R-MT): I think he believes it's like a Taylor Swift concert moving across America, some kind of a tour. This is not the Chinese spy balloon tour.\par \par \uc2 This is a threat to America. It's provocative for our enemies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And there is bipartisan outrage on the House side.\par \par \uc2 House minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  putting out a statement, saying: "The unauthorized incursion into American airspace by a foreign military air balloon, likely for surveillance purposes, is unacceptable."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, no shortage of a chorus of House Republicans calling for the balloon to be shot down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Why hasn't the Biden administration shot the spy balloon down? This is the first time in my lifetime I have ever seen anything like this. And it's very disturbing to know that it's still up there in the air near our military installations. The time is now. Let's get this thing out of the air.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And interesting, Neil, despite the Pentagon's assertion that Jennifer Griffin was mentioning that shooting this down could pose a danger to Americans, several members of the previous administration, including former President Trump, Vice President Pence, Secretary of State Pompeo, and former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley, all say shoot it down now.\par \par \uc2 Now, where does this stand? Well, meanwhile, Speaker McCarthy has called for the Gang of Eight to be briefed on the balloon immediately. That would bring together top House and Senate leaders, along with the heads of the Intel Committees, as we wait to see more on what happens with the State Department and Secretary Blinken and what steps he takes next -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, my friend, very much, Griff Jenkins in Washington that.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Senator \uc2 Mike Rounds\uc2 , kind enough to join us, the South Dakota Republican, on Armed Services Committee and Foreign Relations, among others said.\par \par \uc2 Senator, we do know that, it looks pretty likely this thing was going over South Dakota skies a, it had Montana. We don't know where it is now. How do you feel about that? What have you learned about that? Have residents in your state pointed to it or gotten an eye on it?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE ROUNDS (R-SD): I was advised yesterday that there was a possibility of sightings. That was an unclassified discussion.\par \par \uc2 But I can tell you that they apparently knew about it earlier. The real question, the first question, why would they ever let it get into our airspace in the first place? It should never have been allowed into the airspace.\par \par \uc2 We have the technical capabilities. We could have taken it out before it ever came in. I want to know why. Second of all, what happened to the chain of command here? Was there not someone along the line who had the authorization to make that decision? Did it have to go all the way through to the president of the United States under this administration in order to protect the airways that we have got?\par \par \uc2 Now, recognizing that this was only supposedly an observation balloon, the real question then after that is, is, how soon did they find out that it was only an observation balloon? And, along with that, who made the political decision that it should be allowed to enter, simply because they felt that it was harmless?\par \par \uc2 And what does that do on the political basis to the United States, in terms of how the rest of the world looks at us? Can you simply just walk all over us or, for that matter, simply invade our airspace and move on? Those are the questions.\par \par \uc2 But it starts with, why was it ever allowed into our airspace in the first place? We have the capabilities. It never should have happened.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't want to compare apples and oranges, Senator, but it does ring a lot like the early readings we're getting on the classified documents within the administration and how they were explaining them.\par \par \uc2 And, in this case, if they were aware, as they seem to be indicating, of this presence for some days, why all of a sudden is it today that they decided to cancel \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 's trip to China?\par \par \uc2 ROUNDS: I can't tell you for sure. We're going to ask that question.\par \par \uc2 But I think, along with it -- and it's close to the same question -- is, would we have ever been advised that this had occurred in the first place, if the incursion would have happened, and then it would have slipped back out again, like they have suggested it has happened in the past?\par \par \uc2 And would they have simply not even talked about it?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm jumping on you, Senator. And I don't mean to be rude, sir, but \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is addressing this very subject.\par \par \uc2 I want to listen in.\par \par \uc2 (JOINED IN PROGRESS)\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: ... that undermined the very purpose of the trip, including ongoing efforts, as I said, to build a floor under the relationship, as well as to address a very broad range of issues that are important to Americans, to Chinese, to the entire world.\par \par \uc2 So that is why I conveyed to Wang Yi, the senior foreign policy official in China this morning, that I was postponing the travel, and that we will look to see about doing the trip when conditions allow.\par \par \uc2 I'm not going to put a date or time on that, because what we're focused right now is on making sure that this ongoing issue is actually resolved. The first step, as I said, is getting the surveillance asset out of our airspace.\par \par \uc2 PARK JIN, SOUTH KOREAN FOREIGN MINISTER: With regard to your question about North Korea's illicit cyber activities, as you may know, after the \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we're still monitoring that.\par \par \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  did temporarily or briefly address this subject of why he declined a meeting to go to China to meet with his counterpart there.\par \par \uc2 And, again, Senator Rounds, it gets back to what you and I we're discussing. I'm sorry for rudely interrupting you, that it didn't make sense, because, if you're from the administration, you're saying you knew about this, were aware of this for days, and, presumably, just as outraged over that period as you were today to cancel a meeting with your Chinese counterpart.\par \par \uc2 I'm talking about the secretary of state. Why didn't you do so a few days ago? Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 ROUNDS: Yes, look, I would suspect that it's being canceled -- and, once again, we will have to ask him that question.\par \par \uc2 But I would suspect that it's been canceled because we found out about it. I think, if the incursion would have occurred, and they would have turned it around or maneuvered it out of our airspace again, we probably wouldn't have found out about it. And I would suspect that the meeting might very well have continued on.\par \par \uc2 But, once again, it comes right back down to, they knew it was in our airspace. They allowed it to happen. They made the decision not to take it out. And I just heard it -- I heard part of his discussion, but suggesting that they wanted it out of our airspace. He did not indicate, from what I could hear, whether we were going to take it out or if we were going to let it simply float on its merry way.\par \par \uc2 I most certainly would expect that we would never allow them to have that observation balloon back, one way or another. It'd be nice to be able to get our hands on it, to recover it, and to actually find out what the technologies were that were involved, if it was designed to actually collect data, or if it was designed to test our response capabilities.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will watch it closely.\par \par \uc2 Senator Rounds, it is amazing. A lot of unanswered questions, a lot of things that don't click.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 By the way, the \uc2 Chinese Foreign Ministry\uc2  did issue a statement earlier today when the first questions came up, what the heck is this thing? And they had said, no, no, this is an airship. It is from China. It is a civilian airship used for research, mainly meteorological purposes, going on to say that: "The Chinese side regrets the unintended entry of the airship into U.S. airspace due to force majeure. The Chinese side will continue communicating with the U.S. side and properly handle the unexpected situation caused by force majeure," in other words, events that are out of your control.\par \par \uc2 Not everyone is buying that. And a quick footnote on what we're talking about here. This is not just like a weather balloon. This is something very, very different. Weather balloons, for example, are not nearly this big. This is about five times to 10 times the size of such balloons. Typically, weather balloons, the best of them, the best of them, go up about 20,000 to 22,000 feet.\par \par \uc2 This one's at 60,000 feet. The highest commercial aircraft goes around 35,000 feet. So what is propelling this and powering this and operating this and getting commands regarding this is anyone's guess. And this gives you a good idea of the size. If it is the length or the width of three buses, then you could put it this way.\par \par \uc2 It is about 10 times the minimum size of, for example, typical weather balloons. Now, they expand once in the air when they're retrieving information. Hot air balloons, don't even get me going on hot air balloons, but I became an expert on it earlier today.\par \par \uc2 They're just a fraction of this and can only go a fraction of the height. The bottom line is, none of this adds up. And that's maybe why we're not getting all the details.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be exploring this in a great more detail tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time on my weekend show. We have got with us the former Defense Secretary of Donald Trump Mark Esper, \uc2 Chuck Hagel\uc2 , who served in that role under President Obama. We have got Congressman Mark Green of Tennessee, who sits on the Homeland Security Committee. He's the chair, in fact.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the big names we are going to have digesting what they're hearing and ask them whether they can buy what they're hearing and what other questions they want answered, and fast.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, General Jack Keane on the things that don't add up to him -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, they're very guarded with the information on this whole balloon thing about where it's going and where it is now.\par \par \uc2 It's in the mid-continental United States, we're told, but it's checked off a lot of states or areas in the meantime here, and a lot of them over pretty sensitive territory and military and other installations that even commercial aircraft cannot fly over.\par \par \uc2 So, this thing has been and for presumably a number of days. So we're on top of that.\par \par \uc2 General Jack Keane on the significance of all of this and where he thinks it's going.\par \par \uc2 First of all, General, what do you think this thing is? Certainly, it's more than just a bit meteorological balloon or something to the extent the Chinese have said it is, but it is very different than almost any others that we have -- we have been in contact with. So your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Well, it's a spy surveillance device that they have attached to it, probably a little bit more comprehensive than what they would fly on one of their spy airplanes.\par \par \uc2 But, lookit, I want the American people to understand we just didn't know about this yesterday. North -- what we have in this country is NORAD, which is the \uc2 North American Aerospace Defense Command\uc2 , and that defends Canada and the United States. And the surveillance to do that has a sophisticated suite of radar stations around the world and satellites.\par \par \uc2 And what are we defending against? Any penetration of Canadian or American airspace by missiles or aircraft. And, obviously, it's the nuclear threat that concerns us the most. So, Neil, when this balloon ascended to that altitude in China, and began to move, we knew it.\par \par \uc2 And we can track it the whole time. We have to track a missile coming out of China or an airplane coming out of China heading to the United States. Certainly, we can track a balloon, And we have been doing it for some time. And the reality is, we're completely justified to shoot it down based on a couple things.\par \par \uc2 We have been told they have done it before. So we know what the intent is. Number two, when it got close to Alaska and Aleutian Islands, began to violate our international space, shoot it down. When it got close to Canada, another opportunity, shoot it down, when it got close to the United States, traveling from Canada to the United States.\par \par \uc2 And I think we should have shot it down. I think where the national security team...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But we didn't. We didn't, General. We didn't do that, right?\par \par \uc2 So then it begs the question, why didn't we do that?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Where the national security team is at fault here is, their judgment is way off and their assumptions are off.\par \par \uc2 Number one, I believe they believed -- they thought this probably wouldn't become a matter of public consumption, which it has because people saw and witnessed the balloon up and began to question it. And, number two, their analysis is really misguided.\par \par \uc2 The analysis should have been that we cannot permit an adversary nation to violate U.S. airspace and, therefore, our sovereignty. That has got to be the conclusion. And the guidance the national security team should have come up with and the Pentagon participating in this, I want it shot down, but do it safely.\par \par \uc2 But the decision is, we shoot it down, and we do it safely. And it's a national embarrassment to have this device transiting the United -- the swathe of the United States, and not seeing the third issue, the geopolitical implications, Neil. This weakens us, not just with our adversaries, but also with our allies.\par \par \uc2 Can you imagine what President Tsai is thinking? She's had people whispering in our ear, we're going to be there for you if China does anything. And, here, China violates our own airspace, and we do nothing. It is displaying weakness.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what if we want to take a look at it, General?\par \par \uc2 What if there's something more than we know? By balloon or whatever standards there are, and even defense and spying, this is an unusual beast, I'm told. You're the expert. I'm not. I'm just conveying what people were telling me earlier on FOX Business, that it's unusual something that's flying this high...\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well, there's...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... and that maybe -- I'm just trying to understand what the wisdom was here, if any -- we want to take a look at this thing what it's up to.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well, we probably have a pretty good feel for that, given all the electronic means and eavesdroppings that we have in the United States at our disposal, highly classified, but it would make your eyes water, you know, what we can do here.\par \par \uc2 So, I got -- I think we have probably a pretty good sense of all of that. Whether we have to actually go through the hardware is another issue. The most important thing is that, one, stop it from continuing to do what it's doing. And we have failed miserably at doing that.\par \par \uc2 And now we have led ourselves to a major national security issue in front of the entire world, which simply should have been avoided.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. And it has not, to your point.\par \par \uc2 General, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Guys, if we could show up again the track of this thing. This is from earlier. Again, any images you're getting of this, you are not seeing live. But this shows a map across the country where it's now gone through the middle of the country, started going from Canada to Alaska and certainly into Washington state, and then here we go, and on and on.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is that it is trekked through at least 16 states right now. Kansas was indeed one of them.\par \par \uc2 Jerry Moran\uc2 , the Kansas senator, sits on the Senate Intelligence Committee. What his concerns are, we will be going into that a little bit later in the show.\par \par \uc2 We have also not lost sight of a bit of history that was made today in the labor market, a stunning 3.4 percent unemployment rate. You would have to go to the days of \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  to see an unemployment rate that low, 1969, to be exact, 517,000 jobs added.\par \par \uc2 Now, a lot of people are still scratching their heads, what is going on here?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Welcome to wicked weather, wicked cold weather.\par \par \uc2 You're looking atop Mount Washington, New Hampshire, where it's now minus- 40 degrees, with the windchill later on, minus-100. Either way, it's cold!\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, for those who were alive at the time, do you remember March around 1969?\par \par \uc2 We had about a 3.7 percent unemployment rate that had just tipped about 3.4 percent, which is kind of where we are in this latest report, and it stunned everyone. Now, what was going on with the jobs gain had a lot of people thinking, you know, this might be a perfect environment. We're getting our soft landing. Jobs are out there, even though some of the inflationary pressures seem to be easing at the same time.\par \par \uc2 So, is this a Goldilocks number?\par \par \uc2 Susan Li on the importance of all that data.\par \par \uc2 What did we learn from this report, Susan?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, well, I wasn't around in 1969. Neil, but I will tell you that a lot of people were surprised by the fact that you had almost three times estimates in the month of January.\par \par \uc2 So I sifted through some of these reports. And LPL Research says it's because it's because a university strike ended. So you had 75,000 government workers coming back into the labor force, also the fact -- and this was interesting to me -- \uc2 Comerica\uc2  says because inflation is still at 6.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 You're still seeing it, inflation, higher than wages, 45,000 Americans each and every month are now looking and employed in second jobs in order to meet inflation and put food on the table. Also, if you look at Silicon Valley, yes, we have heard about 50,000 job cuts across whether it's \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  or \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  or \uc2 Meta\uc2 , but those high-paying tech jobs come with very lofty severance packages that lasts for weeks, if not months.\par \par \uc2 And those are not counted on the bottom line as well. So I was just trying to sift through why we missed so badly when it comes to guidance there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I know you're tight with a lot of these executives and get the real skinny on them.\par \par \uc2 And yesterday at this time, Susan, when \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  announced earnings, there was an immediate disappointment that they missed estimates and that their stocks were mercilessly punished in after-hours trading, a lot less of that by day's end today. Why?\par \par \uc2 LI: Rethink, given that, look, we kind of knew that the bad news was priced in.\par \par \uc2 And you heard \uc2 Apple\uc2  at the end of last year talking about these China -- China production snags. That was kind of already built into the bottom line in the earnings reports. But, in terms of hiring, I just want to tell you what \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  told me yesterday in that interview that we had after the earnings release.\par \par \uc2 By the way, they're still going to see reacceleration in the iPhone later on this month. So there could have been a pull forward effect with those production snags, and they weren't making enough of those high-end iPhones to meet demand.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  told me that they are actually not hiring in certain areas. So, the world's biggest company says that they are slowing down in head count. That's going to at least filter into the jobs report at some point, don't you think?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know.\par \par \uc2 One thing that's interesting is, \uc2 Apple\uc2  avoided a lot of the layoffs that we have seen elsewhere. I think you brought up something interesting on FOX Business, which, if people don't get, they should demand.\par \par \uc2 LI: Excellent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But they saw you and know you, they got it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the point being that he was very cautious about overhiring in the boom, right?\par \par \uc2 LI: That's right.\par \par \uc2 He only grew head count by around 20 percent over the last few years. Compare that to \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , as it used to be called, where they doubled head count. Same thing with \uc2 Amazon\uc2  as well.\par \par \uc2 And just to get back to what I told you about these really expensive and some would say generous severance packages, I also spoke to \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  CFO \uc2 Ruth Porat\uc2 . We can bring up that quote as well. They actually hired 3,500 in the -- at the end of last year. Remember that \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  said that they're cutting 12,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 So there's this distortion effect that's taking place, where I think there's a lag effect in terms of when we will actually see these job cuts in these nonfarm payrolls.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I think it's an excellent observation. And we should remember too these are massive companies.\par \par \uc2 So, when you hear about a few thousand laid off, you have to remember, in percentage terms still -- every layoff is tough, but, in percentage terms, still very small.\par \par \uc2 Susan, great job, as always. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Susan Li.\par \par \uc2 Well, the administration wanted to crow more about the jobs report today and the signals it's sending for the economy, not so much about a -- well, a certain balloon.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich on all of that -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it's certainly taking the focus off of everything the president wants to talk about today, Neil, the jobs report, also his achievements since he took office.\par \par \uc2 We're about a week out from the State of the Union and an expected 2024 announcement right after that. So this balloon is looming large, they say, over all that news -- coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Focus on the booming numbers, not the balloon, that seems to be the administration's approach today, to focus on that stunning jobs report, and not so much on the mystery around that Chinese balloon.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich has more from Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the president wants to keep the focus on today's very positive jobs report as he heads into the DNC winter meeting, where he's going to tout the achievements from the past two years and also frame extreme MAGA Republicans' out-of-touch agenda as the biggest threat to continued progress under Democratic leadership.\par \par \uc2 The president refused to take questions this morning around the balloon and let the Pentagon give updates on that balloon. And the State Department announced that Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is canceling his trip to China because of it.\par \par \uc2 There's been no shortage, though, of critics sounding off. Former President -- or Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  tweeted: "Shoot down the Chinese spy balloon. Go ahead and send the secretary of state to China next week. Have Secretary Blinken look them in the eye and tell them, it better never happened again. That's peace through strength."\par \par \uc2 The White House apparently hearing that criticism and responding, telling FOX: "It was the strong recommendation by Secretary of Defense Austin, Chairman Milley, the commander of Northern Command not to take kinetic action at this time because of risk to safety and security of people on the ground. Instances of this activity have been observed over the past several years, including in the prior administration. We acted to make it very difficult for them to learn anything significant. As we have said, it does not pose a military or physical threat."\par \par \uc2 Now, we're told that this balloon is moving east, and it could be hanging out over the U.S. for several more days, meaning that it will be hanging over the president's State of the Union address next week, which is supposed to precede a big 2024 announcement. And, right now, all anyone can talk about is this balloon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Apparently.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jacqui, very much.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich on that one.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , the former Clinton adviser and pollster.\par \par \uc2 Mark, it's too early to tell, I guess, but what do you make of this and the potential fallout for the administration even beyond?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, look, I don't think the China balloon is going to balloon into anything really big here.\par \par \uc2 I think it is an embarrassment. It is kind of puzzling that we can't take some kind of action to kind of take it -- take the property or what have you or remove it.\par \par \uc2 But if I were the -- working with the president, and I had a choice between talking about a Chinese weather balloon and a 587,000-person jobs report that blew away estimates, I know I'd be talking about the jobs report. And that's what the president's doing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Indeed, and, of course, an unemployment rate down to 3.4 percent, the lowest since the early days of \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 , if people can conceive of that.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering, though. This happens oftentimes a lot. The administration has a day where you got something to hang your hat on, good economic news or better-than-expected news, and then something like this that looks a bit like more of an international embarrassment. There's a lot we don't know about it. I like to give the benefit of the doubt here.\par \par \uc2 But there's a lot that looks weird, the fact that we knew this was coming, if we take them at their word, and we didn't do anything about it. And then the question comes up, why didn't and haven't we done anything about it? Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, it's in the larger context of China policy, which is, how is the Biden administration treating China? Is it letting China walk over intellectual property, steal from our economy?\par \par \uc2 Or is it -- and possibly gear up for an invasion of Taiwan? Or is the administration successfully pushing back on China? And so I don't really think it's about the weather balloon. It's about the symbolism that China could invade our sovereignty so blatantly.\par \par \uc2 Many of us may remember -- whether or not we remember unemployment in Nixon -- under Nixon...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. You're right.\par \par \uc2 PENN: ... we may remember the U2 incident, when we had a spy plane over Russia, and Russia shot it down.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. And we forget the Gary Powers incident was a very good example.\par \par \uc2 PENN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Mark, I am curious about just where you see it going, because, obviously, we have seen sort of the octane and the tension between our two countries rising, with, of course, establishing a military base or expanding into the Philippines, of course, the bellicose talk in the South China Sea that the Chinese say is their sovereign territory, to say nothing of Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 So we have had these arguments back and forth on sovereign territories. It's the Chinese who look hypocritical here, though. So why aren't we crassly taking advantage of that?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, look, I think the administration rightly is saying, well, what's the appearance if we shut down this balloon, and, by the way, if it comes down on some us population center, and then we turn this thing into an international disaster?\par \par \uc2 So I think, given that choice, they're saying, look, let's just let it float across. After all, they can look at us through satellites anyway. Are they really getting anything additional from this balloon?\par \par \uc2 I think it's more an international embarrassment. I'm puzzled that Blinken canceled his trip. I thought it would have been more dramatic to show up in China and say, what the heck? Remove this balloon immediately, instead of canceling the trip, which looks really weak to me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So then, oftentimes, what administrations in the past have been scolded for looking weak, either when it comes to Russia, or when it comes to China, they then toughen up, and they then take economic matters in their hands, or we cut off economic ties, or we might punish China in one way or the other, just as they're coming out of this post-COVID funk.\par \par \uc2 So this could escalate, whether people think it's justified to escalate or not, but what do you think happens?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, look, China, people -- the American public has a fairly dim view of China's plans internationally, and they also have a fairly dim view of the administration's response to China.\par \par \uc2 Generally, in our polling, they show the administration being weak towards China. So, look, I think this is not really -- I do think this is, I hate to say, going to blow over. And I don't think it's going to become a big incident. And I don't think the administration wants it to be a big incident.\par \par \uc2 And so I think they will let it -- going to go. Now, if the Chinese don't pull it out within a week or get it out within a week, and we let it sit there for weeks, that's where this could grow into a real issue with, how weak is this administration?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And all the other stuff, I guess what you're saying, Mark, is hot air, right, I guess.\par \par \uc2 PENN: So to speak. We could go on, on this thing for a long time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We could. And, blessedly, we're not.\par \par \uc2 But you and I were the only ones who got the Francis Gary Powers analogy going back to 1960, but touche for you with that, because it's a very good parallel.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, my friend. Have a wonderful weekend, \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime here, there is a history of mayors and governors citing China for going one step too far. Even before this latest incident, quite a few have taken matters into their own hands by getting China out of their own towns and states.\par \par \uc2 I will explain and explore -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG GIANFORTE (R-MT): China is not our friend. They have been infringing our freedom. We're going to continue to make Montana a sanctuary for freedom. We don't need the Chinese Communist Party monitoring our students and our people.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): This was an easy one. This is an easy one where Virginia taxpayer money, along with federal taxpayer money, wasn't going to go to the benefit of a Communist Chinese Party-influenced company and a work-around arrangement for what federal law is.\par \par \uc2 Very easy decision for me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You are potentially denying your residents because it's China- connected? What do you say?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON BOCHENSKI (R), MAYOR OF GRAND FORKS, NORTH DAKOTA: Well, I think, obviously, there's going to be economic benefits for something like a corn mill.\par \par \uc2 And we went through a long review process. We finally got a letter from the Air Force just saying, this is a national security concern. And it was just time to say no, I mean, not in Grand Forks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know what's wild about all of these interviews over the last week or so?\par \par \uc2 They're all mayors and/or governors who have cut ties with very important economic Chinese contracts, to say, we don't need your stinking money, we don't need the jobs that you promise would come as a result, we're cutting ties with you entirely, because we find your behavior so egregious. And that's before this whole balloon thing.\par \par \uc2 Dan Hoffman joins me right now. Love having Dan on, the former CIA station chief in Moscow and FOX contributor.\par \par \uc2 Dan, to a man and woman here, because I have talked to several others who have been saying the same thing, don't trust China, don't trust the temptation of the money they offer to do business with them, and all but sell your soul to them. Then along comes issue like this. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 DAN HOFFMAN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, there just isn't any distinction between Chinese business and the \uc2 Communist Party of China\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There's a 2015 intelligence law in China which requires all of their businesses to work on behalf of Chinese intelligence. And it's not as if this is happening in a vacuum. China's mounting a full-court press espionage against us. They hacked into the Office of Personnel Management back in 2015.\par \par \uc2 They're aggregating big data through TikTok and running all sorts of espionage operations against our national security professionals and defense industry, and they're buying up our land near key military installations. And so you call it what you want, a 21st century Cold War, whatever it might be.\par \par \uc2 China's bringing the full force of their nation-state against our private sector, as well as our government.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There's a lot we don't know, a lot, to your point here, that doesn't add up here.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things I wonder, a lot of people saying, well, why didn't you shoot this thing down? Why haven't you shot this thing down? And maybe the administration is aware of more details on this than we know.\par \par \uc2 So even trying to give the better the doubt, it comes at a time when China's made a world issue with our country about provocative moves in the Philippines, about, don't you dare go to the South China Sea. That's our area. Don't go talking and lecturing us about militarizing islands there or threatening Taiwan. We have sovereign rights.\par \par \uc2 And here they blow through that like a Mack truck with this effort. And we don't say anything. It is, at the very least, a P.R. opportunity lost for us.\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: Well, it's a violation of our territorial integrity.\par \par \uc2 And even if the information or the intelligence that this balloon is collecting isn't of great value to China, what is the value is all the collection they're going to get about internal U.S. deliberation, the fact that we haven't done anything about this, the fact that we reportedly knew about this balloon before it entered our airspace and potentially back when -- before it was a threat, could have done something about it.\par \par \uc2 I don't know. Maybe you could -- we do jet refueling in midair. I have done those. I don't know why we couldn't have towed it away, rather than necessarily destroyed it. We could have towed it away, and then reaped all the benefits of knowing exactly what was inside the balloon.\par \par \uc2 But we were certainly within our rights to do something about it. And the fact that we didn't, I think, has to cause us concern. Now, imagine if the balloon were filled with weapons of mass destruction. What if our nonstate actor enemies, terrorists are taking a look at this and thinking this might be their next way to target our citizens?\par \par \uc2 This is just -- has to be of grave concern here. Whenever we don't take action on a threat way out left of boom, as we like to say at CIA, and allow the threat to materialize, that's a bad day.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What if they were counting on no one ever knowing about it? After all, this thing flies at 60,000 feet, and they were hoping no one would notice, maybe chalk it up to UFOs, that a lot of people are saying, wait a minute, maybe all those sightings in the past weren't UFOs. They were Chinese drones and/or balloons.\par \par \uc2 Who knows. I'm only jumping the gun here to raise the point with you, because, a few days ago -- as of this morning, for example, the \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  China was still on. And then the outrage over this, when it came to light, now it's off.\par \par \uc2 Odd, don't you think?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: Well, first, I think China actually was totally fine with us knowing about the existence of the balloon. It's a chess match, and they're probing us, and they got what they wanted.\par \par \uc2 I actually think Secretary Blinken should have gone to China and held China accountable in Beijing. I think the administration was at a loss for what to do. And maybe they thought this was the only option they had, to take that state visit off the table. But he should have gone. Hold them accountable for the genocide in Xinjiang province against the Uyghurs.\par \par \uc2 Hold them accountable for concealing the outbreak and severity of coronavirus and for this balloon caper. Do it in Beijing. I mean, that's a powerful message that you send, using the United States' soft power. That, after all, is the responsibility of the secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Dan, well put, my friend. Thank you for your service to this country as well.\par \par \uc2 Dan Hoffman, former CIA station chief in Moscow. He knows of what he speaks.\par \par \uc2 So does my next guest, the Kansas Republican Senator \uc2 Jerry Moran\uc2 , sits on the Senate Intelligence Committee, among others.\par \par \uc2 Senator, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 I don't know. if you were able to hear that whole conversation, sir, but a lot of things that just don't add up, right? I mean, the reluctance to, A, even share the information that the administration presumably had that this thing was coming our way, and then only cancel the secretary of state's visits to China when it seemed the whole world became aware of this.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JERRY MORAN\uc2  (R-KS): Well, first, Neil, thank you for highlighting this significant issue.\par \par \uc2 And I would suppose that there's this opportunity to say we need more information. And all of us are calling on the administration, the Intelligence Committee -- I'm a defense appropriator -- the Commerce Committee and the technology transfers, all -- we need more information.\par \par \uc2 But I don't think that's a good defense for this administration, when they haven't provided us anything to date. I was in an Intelligence Committee briefing twice this past week, as recently as Wednesday. This has never been a topic. I'm a senior defense appropriator. Never been brought to our attention.\par \par \uc2 I know that other members feel the same way, that this administration is keeping us in the dark, and then claiming that they're doing what they're supposed to do. Give us the information, so that not only members of Congress, but the American people can make the determination.\par \par \uc2 But, on first blush, on second blush and third blush, this administration has acted, reacted in a way that is too weak, not strong enough, not message-sending. And simply having a diplomatic spat over attendance by the secretary in China, that's a pretty minuscule thing when it comes to our response.\par \par \uc2 And when did -- when did the administration know? What did they do as soon as they found out about it? And we certainly, in my view, from what I know, at the moment, should not allow this to leave the United States without it being taken into custody in some fashion, whether it's shot down or whatever other appropriate steps we could take.\par \par \uc2 I also want to know, hugely important, have we disabled the balloon, so that it's incapable of collecting information? And it's now just sitting there, flying there. And we know, most recently, it keeps moving because Kansans are now calling me saying they have seen it in our state.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Now, they say that that is indeed what they have achieved, that it's not collecting that information anymore. I don't know how they would know that, to be honest, Senator.\par \par \uc2 You said something interesting in the beginning. Everything you said is interesting, Senator, but that point about, as recently as Wednesday, at an intelligence briefing, this issue never came up?\par \par \uc2 MORAN: It did not.\par \par \uc2 And, again, we know this balloon has been in the -- over the United States, in our sovereign territory for more than several days. And it's an Intelligence Committee issue. It's also a Defense Department issue. They are there to protect our country. And, clearly, we need more information.\par \par \uc2 But don't -- again, I work hard at trying to get the amount of money to the Defense Department to defend our nation. And those dollars are hugely important. And it is a mistake to think that, if we simply fund the department, that that alone is sufficient. If we don't have leadership that utilizes our capabilities, we have lost our opportunity to push back on China on this and other actions they will take.\par \par \uc2 So it's disappointing to me. We worked hard in this recent appropriation bill to get more money to the Defense Department to defend our nation. But it also requires more than equipment and personnel. It requires leadership.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well put. Senator, there's so much we don't know.\par \par \uc2 I just found it fascinating that, as of your most recent intelligence briefing, this issue never came up.\par \par \uc2 Jerry Moran\uc2 , the Kansas Republican, following that.\par \par \uc2 As he was speaking there, we're getting word right now that the Biden administration does plan to brief the so-called Gang of Eight next week on this spy balloon. That includes people like \uc2 Mike Turner\uc2 , the Republican, Mark Warner, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , Kevin McCarthy, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , to give them an update on all of this.\par \par \uc2 We will be exploring all of this tomorrow on my weekend show, "Cavuto Live." We have got \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  joining us, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's defense secretary, also \uc2 Chuck Hagel\uc2 , served that role under Barack Obama, and Homeland Security Committee Chair Representative Mark Green.\par \par \uc2 So many questions, so much to delve into. We will tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230204ej2300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Warns House GOP Poses Threat To Economy; Senators Tired Of Joe Biden Stonewalling On Doc Drama; New York City Mayor Praises Merchants For Hiring Private Guards; Liberal City Crime Solution: Hire Private Security; Robots Are Taking Over: AI Could Replace Millions Of Jobs As It Gets Smarter; Man's Best Friend: Dog Walkers Making Up To $120,000\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Dana Perino, Brian  Kilmeade \par \uc2 8,611 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: We're waiting for the video of that arrest, we're told it's fairly gruesome, no clear in detail since exactly when it is released. We're told sometime tomorrow, no way of knowing.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Hello, everyone. I'm Judge Jeanine Pirro, along with Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Dana Perino and Brian Kilmeade. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 A desperate and politically wounded, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is trying to change the subject after a terrible start to the year. The big guy's already bad poll numbers are sinking fast, and he faces an onslaught of investigations.\par \par \uc2 In his first major speech since losing the House, Biden says to hell with working with the GOP to make your lives better, he is looking for a fight, ditching the kumbaya talk and unleashing on Republicans who are trying to rein in his reckless spending.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is not your father's Republican Party. This is a different breed of cat as they say.\par \par \uc2 MAGA Republicans deliberately choose to inflict this kind of pain on the American people. Why? As I said, they seem determined to be the party of chaos and catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 And now these MAGA Republicans introduced another bill that's going to eliminate the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , going to eliminate it completely. The only guys are going to look at the complicated tax returns that people have and replace the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , which I mean, nice, no taxes, right? Except with a 30 percent national sales tax.\par \par \uc2 And let me be clear, if any of these bills somehow got past the House and the Senate, I will veto them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, wow. OK. Well, let me start with -- oh, but Joe isn't -- sorry guys, I was so stunned by that too. But Joe isn't going this alone, the White House seems to send Democrats the same talking points.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's a silly doggone idea. And like I said, over my dead body, we will fight. We'll fight every day until this thing goes down to defeat.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): What are the Republicans doing? Team extreme, the team consisting of extremists who are willing to detonate the American economy.\par \par \uc2 REP. PETE AGUILAR (D-CA): It feels like Republicans only want to have these extreme discussions when a Democrat is occupying the White House.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Dana, I'll go back to you again. Taking the offense when you're really in charge of an economy that is a disaster, gas prices are up 50 percent. And I won't even go through the list.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: The Republicans should just play that Cardi B clip over and over again. Like, how did the economy get this bad? Why is everything so expensive? How are people supposed to live? Because that actually has a lot of pull.\par \par \uc2 I have to say, I think the Democrats, they are crass, there are doing exactly what you would want to do politically to put them in a position to help him run for reelection.\par \par \uc2 He goes to Virginia today, that's an important state for them. It's pretty much a purplish state because Obama had done very well there. But Youngkin won, a Republican, so it's sort of purplish state, so they got to lock down Virginia.\par \par \uc2 He's doing a lot of these things before the State of the Union on February 7th, then you're going to see more of it. He's doing two fundraisers for the DNC next week.\par \par \uc2 So, the White House is doing everything that they need to do to put him in position to run. They are not letting him do a lot of interviews, you'll notice that because he's got the document issue that he has to deal with.\par \par \uc2 And they also prerelease so much of the speech, including punch lines and jokes, because they want those to be in the media at the print media. Because you're not going to necessarily get a clean soundbite.\par \par \uc2 And that's fine. They know that about him. But they also know, OK, what are the Republicans weak on? Let's say this for the Republicans, at least are talking about ideas. OK, let's talk -- can we not at least have a discussion about what we're going to do if Social Security runs out of money in 2033? That's just 10 years from now. And you're thinking about kids like Cleo and Jesse Jr. and your grandson and Cami (PH)? Like, I know that kids name.\par \par \uc2 And you know, we can actually have a conversation as Americans to say what would actually be a good way to preserve Medicare and Social Security going in the future. It's ridiculous to suggest that people are going to all of a sudden have a 30 percent national retail sales tax on top of all the taxes they're already paying. That's not what the proposal is, besides.\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy already said it's not going anywhere. Other Republicans have said it's not going anywhere. I don't fault them for at least having ideas. What I do fault is the media for not asking \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats, well, what is your idea?\par \par \uc2 Is your idea to just pass so much in spending, that you cause inflation and then look back and say this was all caused by the Trump tax cuts of 2017? That doesn't make any sense. Biden wants to run his last campaign and the media shouldn't let him do it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right. So I'll go right to you, Jessica. I mean, didn't \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  go on the biggest spending spree in American history?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: No. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually spent a lot more than him. So \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is personally responsible as the president for about 30 percent of the national debt. And I believe \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is (INAUDIBLE) down by 1.4 trillion since he's been in office.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: COVID money went out.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I was asked a question, I answered it. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is responsible for more of the national debt than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is, period. End of story. Those are what the numbers say.\par \par \uc2 We also had a good jobs report today or a good economic report. I know Brian, you spoke about it this morning on "FOX & FRIENDS". 2.9 percent, fourth quarter growth, 2.1 percent increase in consumer spending, those are good indicators.\par \par \uc2 When people are talking about all the layoffs, the layoffs are in one sector in tech. And basically they're right sizing the fact that they over hired in 2021, sorry and 2022.\par \par \uc2 So, they're going back to the mean there, they paid people way too much. Give people big fancy titles, they couldn't afford to do that.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm not for calling everybody a MAGA Republican. I know that was part of what he was talking about today. But he does have an economic record to run on. And he does have the fact that you have import representative so much so that people thought that he could be speaker, this \uc2 Kevin Hern\uc2 , who said, we have to make these -- we have to make hard decisions about what's going on with Social Security and Medicare, and he's part of a conference of 160 conservative Republicans who have said that they want to look at changing the retirement age. That's something that Nancy Mace, for instance, a Republican has said that's absolute nonstarter. McCarthy said that as well.\par \par \uc2 But get your caucus in line, if you don't want Democrats talking about the fact that there are people in your conference who are important to you, by the way, saying that we should raise the retirement age, people are going to pay attention to it and they're going to vote accordingly.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Didn't \uc2 Kevin Hern\uc2  get one vote for speaker?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: -- consensus that he was going to be a speaker of the House.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, of course not. But he's not --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, the truth is that when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left office, inflation was 1.7. Inflation under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is gone what, to 8.7, 8.9. I mean, come on. It's the highest in 40 years.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about the truth. And truth is that you've got a 41 percent, 50 percent increase in fuel, eggs, I don't have to tell you what Americans are going through and that the economy is great? I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: What you meant about Cardi B is the everyday person who goes shopping, that's what the economy feels like.\par \par \uc2 And to your point Dana on politics, this is my point with media, I just thought they can get some clicks on this morning from what I said.\par \par \uc2 My point this morning was two months after the election, which we're doing politics, if there was ever a time to lead the country and not your party. It's two months after an election, and you haven't said I'm running for reelection again.\par \par \uc2 So, just tell me your vision. You don't have to say, well, I wonder what Buddy Carter wants to do. Now, the FairTax might have some possibilities. But politically, it's not the strongest thing.\par \par \uc2 But it's one congressman who brings it forward, just like \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  brings one thing forward, and he grabs on to it. And I just said to myself, he's 80 years old, if you do want to talk about the economy, and you do want to say be honest about it, he still should be upset at the inflation side, he should just talk about energy and the decisions he's making to green the economy, he should talk about the things that are bothering him, the fact that the job market starting to go a little wobbly, and say it for his point of view, I'd say look, I want things to be a little bit better. I'm proud of what I've done. But I think he should get the big picture and compare it to other countries.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's strongest argument, I think, is if you compare our economy to other countries, we're doing good.\par \par \uc2 My point was you don't always have to make the Republicans the villain. I understand elections, but be the president for once. This is what I did. This is what's disappointing me. This is what's worrying me.\par \par \uc2 But I'll tell you, the big picture when I look around and I meet with European leaders, we're pretty much doing better than everyone except for maybe inflation with Japan. That was -- that was my point.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. All right, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: The economy is about feel. And the economy doesn't feel good for the American people right now because wages are down. And last year, stock market was down year to year. So, we're not making money as of last year.\par \par \uc2 And if you add the gas prices and nasty inflation and the fact they had to jack up mortgage rates, you can't afford to buy a house and people are not -- it's like renter nation.\par \par \uc2 There is good stuff. He had a good GDP number. He had stronger GDP numbers last year and there's consumer spending, the job numbers have been strong, but people are getting laid off in tech because we're preparing for a light recession.\par \par \uc2 Everybody knows there's going to be a light recession next year. I don't like when Republicans propose big, new economic ideas when they're not in power. I like having ideas. Just keep those ideas to yourself waiting for the campaign to start. Campaign is two years away,\par \par \uc2 I'm not showing a lot of skin right now, you're like a boxer. You know what I mean? All of a sudden, you're out here -- out here. And then he just lands shot to the body, 30 percent sales tax? Maybe that's a good idea.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's not.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: But I mean, he's opening up big, big gaping holes for Joe to attack. And then what's the other thing, this guy in Florida wants to slash entitlements? You don't do that. You don't say that. It's just not a smart idea.\par \par \uc2 When I had to deal with entitlements, tell everybody that's about 18 to 30 to start having babies, have a lot of babies, that'll help