l. The DOJ has reportedly prepared to obtain a warrant to search President Biden's Delaware house, the other one, had the president's legal team not giving consent. And the National Archives is asking all former presidents and vice presidents to recheck their personal records, except Dan Quayle, who tells Fox, he hasn't received an inquiry, but he's willing to check his stuff.\par \par \uc2 So Jesse, do you believe that Senator Warner is going to go as far as Senator Tom Cotton and refuse to confirm any ambassadors or any nominees until they come clean on what exactly was in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's stuff?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's the one thing that's going to bring the parties together, not being able to see this stuff they're supposed to see.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Are you surprise that Senator Warner was o exercised about that?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No, I'm not because these guys are entitled to do oversight. And like he said, during the Russia investigation, they got briefed -- they got briefed all the time. And then it leaked, but they at least got briefed.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, the DNI comes in, and this was a scheduled briefing. And she goes, you know what, I don't even know what the documents say. She's walled herself off from the documents.\par \par \uc2 So, it looks like the Department of Justice is the only one that has any idea what's in these documents. And that's not fair because the Senate is supposed to do oversight to see if there's some sort of intelligence compromise, and then try to mitigate that somehow. So they're being cutting out of this thing.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you're \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , and you have a box of documents that are out at the dining room table, you get a box of documents that are in the garage, and they have a kid's birthday party that day. And then he gets so banged up drunk, he stays out to 2:30 in the morning, text, Jill, hey, I lost my key to the house, can you open the back door so I can come in?\par \par \uc2 This is the same time he's getting bribed by Chinese diamonds. The same time he's fooling around with the stripper, and he of all people is losing his house key while these documents are sitting there just out in the garage, out on the dining room table.\par \par \uc2 If I was a senator, Republican or Democrat, I'd want to know exactly what was in those documents.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: If it's country first, but everything's been party first. That's what my big surprise was.\par \par \uc2 Dana, the other thing what Jesse said was kind of familiar. You know, who else doesn't want to know what's in those documents what they found?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Biden.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: President Biden. Do not tell me, let me be surprised.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It seems to me like they've kind of made this a mountain out of a molehill. Because if it is something from 2008, the actionable intelligence if it was really serious, it's probably not really that acceptable anymore.\par \par \uc2 And forget like going back to Dan Quayle. Dan Quayle is so squeaky clean, he doesn't have any documents. Don't worry about it.\par \par \uc2 If Biden had said initially, wow, that was wrong. I should have been more responsible. Gosh, you know what, but I looked at the documents, everything's fine.\par \par \uc2 Remember when they did the Mar-a-Lago raid, the media ran away with a story that he was selling nuclear secrets to the Saudis.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right, or the Russians.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And so, why does it -- why doesn't the media do that now?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I'm not sure.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I mean, aside from Jesse.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right. Jesse, your scenario, I'm like, I wonder where you get those docs.\par \par \uc2 Judge, right now, when you -- when you think about what's going on right now, the president said the exact opposite really what he should say, no regrets, no, there there. No, we got plenty of there.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, we've got plenty of there there. And to go off of the point that Dana you were just talking about, what it was Trump it was drip, drip, drip, drip. Every day was something else with the nuclear codes or Russia, wherever he was sending them to.\par \par \uc2 And then we also had the layout of declassified documents, these top secret documents, like a narrative, like for a school child, look, there they are, right on the screen.\par \par \uc2 OK. But when it comes to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , no layout, none of that stuff. But here's the difference.\par \par \uc2 First of all, according to Senator Ron Johnson in 2014 when \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and his partner Devon Archer were trying to get the \uc2 Burisma\uc2  contract, that's when an e-mail in that laptop contained information was something like, I don't know, 13,000 words or something. So unlike everything else, which was give me this, I'll see you that, I'll meet you here with this, you know, thing, this assessment of what was happening in Ukraine, that could possibly be from a classified document.\par \par \uc2 Here, there is good cause to make a connection between what documents were seized and whether or not they were at the same time consistent with what they were doing.\par \par \uc2 Now, I want to say one more thing. The job Jesse as Jesse just said is oversight. It has to do with the separation of powers. The fact that there is an investigation is not relevant. There was an investigation during the Russia investigation. Everybody was entitled to know what was going on then. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his administration are stopping everyone from having access to what's going on.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Jessica, is it worth it for the president to take this heat from his own party and from a bipartisan group? Is there something among the -- can you blame anyone for thinking that there's something so bad, it's worth taking the heat from his own side?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Sunlight is the greatest disinfectant. Talk about it more and talk to the American public more about how this was unintentional, that he has great respect for the process that's why he's cooperating, which is something markedly different from what president -- former President Trump did.\par \par \uc2 But no, you need as many allies as you can possibly have when you're heading into a reelection.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: -- strategy.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think everybody on both sides of the aisle has questioned the strategy, the press secretary is not being given enough at least to answer questions. Even John Kirby was --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Are you sure about that?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Have you spoken to her?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, but I know -- I know how to watch television, I can see her, I can see that she's struggling and that the principal is not making her life easier. Send someone else --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Maybe that's the message.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: The message is \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  can't have decent answers to these questions. No, they're not happy with the coverage of it. No one is happy.\par \par \uc2 I mean, when you have -- you know, as much criticism from Kelly O'Donnell, as you do from Peter Doocy, you know that you're doing something wrong here, right?\par \par \uc2 And so, Mark Warner and \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2 , these are longtime allies of President Biden, right? Going back decades, these aren't just new senators that popped up, you know, the other day like \uc2 Alex Padilla\uc2 , I don't really know him.\par \par \uc2 These are people who have been in the Biden camp for a long time. And you're pushing them to have to make comments that I'm sure they do not want to make and you have to explain to a group of senators who all follow the rules about going into a SCIF and return the documents how it's possible that those got out of there.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right. And also, and final thought on that, part -- one of 