R OF NEW YORK CITY: It is a wise strategy because I say this over and over again. Not only must the numbers reflect safety, but people must feel safe, people must feel safe. And we must make sure that businesses are safe in the city. And that's what the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  is doing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: This isn't the first time. There are plenty of business owners who had no choice but to hire private security. You may remember when I interviewed an armed guard hired by a Philly gas station right there, holding to that AR.\par \par \uc2 Judge, if you're the mayor, you probably want to say something like, we don't want store owners to have to hire armed security. We'd like the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  to be able to do their job.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Or one better. As the mayor, I know that you pay taxes. And I know that you work hard to even make -- you know, to make any money on your business, and you shouldn't have to hire your own security. But the truth is that when you defund the police to $1 billion as they did in New York City, and when the rules are so tough the police feel like they're going into a war zone with their hands behind their back, then the local businesses are left to hire their own security.\par \par \uc2 First, they started by locking down the items, right? Then they started by putting more cameras in and hiring more security. And then they had security escorts who were walking people to their cars because somebody gets shot or killed outside of a store. So, now, people are left to their own devices. They -- and the owners as well.\par \par \uc2 So, the stores are suffering. They're not getting their tax money's worth. And there's an increase in violence as the theft goes up. So, this is when people say I want to have a gun.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, it's hard to come back from COVID or a crime wave when you look like a third world country. When you have armed guards standing outside certain shops, certain shops can't afford this though.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Absolutely. Well, that's what I was thinking that it probably is a luxury that this store owner or the Philly gas station was making enough that they could afford this. These are people who need to make a living and rightly so. So, the answer isn't like, good job on you. The answer is I am focused on making sure that New York City or Philadelphia, wherever you're in charge of, is a city where you don't feel like the police are good enough or can get there in enough time to help you save your business from whatever theft is going on. So, yes, I thought it was a very poor answer or at least not a solutions-oriented answer.\par \par \uc2 And with the number of people who are not -- retiring from the police force early or not going into it necessarily because it is such a bad climate for police officers, a lot of them are going into private security, that that's another option. Now, you take your retirement and you go and maybe you can make more. Your pension certainly wouldn't be the same. But this is where people were headed if these things aren't dealt with.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And then when you try to hire more cops and train them, Antifa hides in the trees and shoots them in the belly.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes. And also -- so, the mayor never talks about the prosecution or the lack of prosecution. And we've talked about those on the show. The judge has explained it very well. If you're a police officer and you know that you're going to get those guys that beat up Adam Klotz, our weatherman on the subway, and you know that the next morning the prosecutor is going to let them go, then you think why would I do this job? Why am I here? And people feel demoralized.\par \par \uc2 And also, just to get -- do you know on the closing of the shops, there's a drugstore in -- I believe it's in Queens that is now -- it used to be 24/7. Now, it's only going to be open from 11 to 11:20 every day so that people can pick up their medicines. But that's the only thing that you're allowed to do. OK, so, that's happening in communities.\par \par \uc2 And also, just to get really political. If you think about Biden was saying about the Republicans on the economy, if I were a Republican, all I would say all day long is what a world. They defund the police and expect you to pay for private security. That's the world that the Democrats want. And just repeat it a hundred times even though they'll say we don't really believe that. But if that's the way that they're going to play on the economy, just do that on crime.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's a good idea. Brian?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Oh, private security in New Orleans. They might not do -- they were thinking about not having Mardi Gras because there's so few cops and they don't know if they can do it. So, now, they -- everyone's hiring their own including the professional sports teams. I know someone in New Orleans on a daily basis, certain amount of time -- a certain time of night you just don't get gas. If you don't have your car filled up, you can't do it. It's too dangerous.\par \par \uc2 If for this particular story -- these guys are getting 18 an hour. They're called patrol ambassadors. But as Curtis Sliwa texted me when we were doing this story this morning, who knows anything more about law enforcement than just about anyone, including \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , he says New York City is 4000 cops down. And the fact is, these ambassadors might be the best people in the world but they still have to call 911. So, it's back to the cops that weren't there to begin with.\par \par \uc2 So, that's why this is -- this is terrible. 56 percent of small retail businesses experience theft. It fell the last year. And 86 percent say that most of the time the crime leads to violence. All I want to do is open up a deli and I've got to wrestle to keep my -- to be able to sell my wife's potato chips. It doesn't seem like a good scenario.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Onion and vinegar, those are the best. \uc2 Wise\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: They are good.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Very good.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Onion and vinegar.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: But you do have to brush afterwards.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And floss.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Salt and vinegar?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Up next -- that too. I was thinking sweet onion. I think I can buy that too.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, onion and vinegar doesn't sound terrible.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It sounds pretty bad.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Pringles is much --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's very (INAUDIBLE) for sure.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We got to go. Up next, we tried to warn you, America, but it might be too late. The robots are about to take over.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: The power of artificial intelligence is getting pretty scary. It could upend life as we know it and make your job obsolete. We've warned you about ChatGPT and its human-like responses since it launched two months ago. It passed a \uc2 Wharton Business School\uc2  test, law school exams, and wrote a job application that was better than 80 percent of the ones humans could do.\par \par \uc2 British researchers are warning their Parliament that AI could "kill everyone and should be regulated similar to nuclear weapons." That doesn't seem extreme, does it? And Congress is keeping an eye on the next steps.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TED LIEU (D-CA): If it's left unregulated and unchecked, it can cause harm. We've already seen some of the harms its cause.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): But obviously I think it's something we need to pay close attention to.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): I think there definitely needs to be oversight. We have to take this very seriously. It affects all levels of government and certainly questions of privacy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Do you know what really bothers me about that, Jesse? Is that by the time they're talking about that, it's too late.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's over. This stuff is going to be here. Jesse Jr is going to be utilizing it. We have to figure out a way to make it work for us and not kill everyone's job.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Against us.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: We have to figure it out, right?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, that's my plan. And I'm not worried about it because it's like having a robot butler, all right. You take the brain and then you put in a body, maybe like in between C-3PO and R2D2, and they just travel around with you wherever they go. It's your pilot. It's your cleaning lady. Let's say I want to give a speech at a banquet. Hey print me out a speech for a banquet. A little flap comes down the belly, prints out a speech, I give a great toast, and that's done. And the thing drives me home. And if someone threatens my life on the way home --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: He shoots them.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: -- he can use deadly force. It's like Stephen Hawking with a gun, really smart, always there by your side, and it never goes to sleep. This thing can do my taxes, it could make my investments. I think everyone is going to get this thing. What you don't want are contact lenses that tell you what you're looking at and give you lots of Intel.\par \par \uc2 You don't want implants in your forearm. You ever see the thing that pops up. You don't want any implants. You want a little side buddy that goes with you everywhere you go, just a robot butler.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, everyone's going to have a side buddy.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: What worries me is that chat box or whatever it's called, got a C-plus on an exam. I mean, if we have virtual reality, can we get an A student here?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's early. It's early.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Yes. The other thing is this is like -- I'd love to regulate this. It's like saying, let's not use the car, it'll kill horse sales. You can't stop it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes. It's already done.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: This is called -- yes, this is done. We could just -- we could just hold on tight. My worry is it's going to out -- that we're going to teach it how to think it's going to outthink us, but they'll never get five robots to do THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Judge, something that's happening, I think in February, there is going to be the first ever case where the lawyer for the defense is going to be the ChatGPT guy. And they're going to use that and see how it goes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, one of the things that I was going to say is look, if you're worried about losing your job, the first thing you should do is get a job that is so unique to you that no one else can do it. So, for example you should be in a subjective line of work, right? So, this -- whatever you want to call it, your buddy, he can -- he can write a speech, he can give you facts, but he can't do something subjective like art and say that art is beautiful or --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME."\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, Jesse Watters, that would be exempted. But also the whole idea of you're being a judge. So, you can put an AI lawyer in front of me, but you still need a judge to be able to make that determination. You can't put an AI as judge out there.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: So, those are the rules.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Those are my --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, that's how she's going to regulate it. You have a young daughter. What do you think her future holds with AI? Can you imagine?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No. And we're going to be so ill-equipped to understand what's going on with them. You know how like every generation of parents as kids these days. This will be absurd levels of kids these days. But what freaked me out when I saw the ChatGPT stuff, if you combine that with that \uc2 Boston Dynamics\uc2  dog that's like climbing and dancing --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, that's what I'm doing with my little buddy.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That could be your private asset.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You put the AI 15 in the \uc2 General Dynamics\uc2  dog, and that's what I'm going for with my butler.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And also, because all Democrats want to defund the police and make you have private security, you're going to have private security from the robot dog.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Hey, do you like -- do you like Medicare?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And you know what? He will be your doctor too.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: The robot can do everything.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Get a splinter out of my thumb.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, "THE FASTEST" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Welcome back. It's time for "THE FASTEST." First up, you may have seen this viral video of what appears to be an Uber Eats delivery guy wandering onto a basketball court during a live game to deliver \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's and causing a timeout. It left the game announcers totally shocked.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There he is right there in the court.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, there he is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This guy is actually -- who's he delivering it to? The ref?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The ref said later. Give it to me later.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But it turns out it was just a prank. The university where it happened saying the individual was wearing a mic while someone filmed him and was done before internet exposure. Did anyone -- I saw this on SportsCenter?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I thought it was real this morning.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Yes, we did. So, I feel totally duped. But let me just say, is that funny? It was pretty funny but why would you walk in the court? That's when it gets serious..\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, that one -- if we have AI, that's what would happen. They would say, I have a mission --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Also, like, what if -- what if one of the players thought it was somebody who was like, maybe -- like, an unsafe situation and knocked him out. But I have a feeling if Greg were here, he would have definitely known this was a prank.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: He always tell --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: He can sense a prank from a mile away.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I mean, how do we know -- this guy get into the stadium to deliver \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You don't have a ticket.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I mean, you need a ticket.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thanks, Brian.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I was duped this morning. I was duped for 10 hours.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But you've redeemed yourself in the 5:00 hour.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes. Jesse?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No, I mean, I just can't believe Brian had to explain that one. Yes, you do need a ticket to get into the stadium.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Just -- and \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 ? You have that McDonald's at the game?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, it were internet exposure.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You get up pretty early.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I do.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: OK, up next, stuck in a dead end job that pays very little? Not if you're AI. Consider this. A guy make six figures just by walking dogs. Michael Joe says he went from making under $40,000 a year as a teacher to growing a dog on empire complete with an app and employees.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Good for him.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And he's not alone. The Times profiling others who are making bank by walking Fido. Judge, you're saying --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, so --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: How much you pay your dog walker?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I don't have a dog walker in New York, but when I was in Florida, some of the time, I would pay a