 I'm not selling the service.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Did your dog --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I'm not (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: What your dog needs to learn?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Sign language?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Everything. My dog has to understand that just my clothing is not chewable. That's what he does.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, the tie. I saw the tie.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: But it happens all that time.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: What color was that tie, Brian?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: It was magenta.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It was turquoise. You couldn't tell.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I do not -- yes, I did not know. I did not know. I just know it's hard to match. It will never be replaced.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Although I'll get you a new one.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: OK.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: We have to go, unfortunately. OK, "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's time now for "ONE MORE THING." Dana?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I'm honored to bring you this update. There was a very positive update about our friend and colleague Benjamin Hall. Benjamin is opening up in his new book Saved: A War Reporter's Mission to Make It Home. He dives into his fight for survival almost one year after our Fox News team was hit by Russian fire while reporting in Ukraine. Two of our colleagues, Pierre Zakrzewski and Sasha Kuvshynova, lost their lives in the attack. Benjamin was severely wounded.\par \par \uc2 In his first live TV interview, he read a passage from the book about the moments after their vehicle was hit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HALL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Improbably, out of this crippling nothingness, a figure came through and I heard a familiar voice as real as anything I'd ever known. Daddy, you've got to get out of the car. It was very badly injured. I'd had shrapnel in the eye and in the throat and I saw my own daughters, my young daughters, and they brought me back. And I found the strength, I opened my eyes, and I managed to crawl out of the car.\par \par \uc2 I was in this incredible team of doctors and nurses who helped all the wounded out of Afghanistan and Iraq, and they rebuilt me. They rebuilt me physically and mentally. They gave me such strength. And so, for me, this book is about being saved. It's been about being saved by all these people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's an incredible story. We are very thankful that Benjamin is on his way to recovery. I've heard the book is amazing. And you can preorder it now. It's called Saved and it hits the shelves on March 14th.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: It was amazing to hear that story. He's got a lot -- you know, we have two months till it comes out but preorder it now. But he was talking to Pierre for 45 minutes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I know.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I did not know that. So, they were going back and forth for 45 minutes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And the amazing thing is apparently I think it was Peter died during that period of time.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's a stunning -- it's got to be an incredible part.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I've heard it's an amazing read, so definitely pre order.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Oh, I'm sorry. I was just going to say I was really struck by how thankful he was for everything and no regrets about it and how proud he is as you should be of the body of work that he's done as a war reporter.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And you can hold your phone up to the screen, right there.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's so easy.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And I'll take you right to \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  where you can click purchase.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, Brian, "ONE MORE THING." All right. So, I got to give credit to Fox Sports. They do a great thing. They are as you know going to be hosting -- they will be -- I'll be at the Super Bowl. "FOX AND FRIENDS" will be there, so -- the weekend show and the radio show. But they are doing something ahead of time in Phoenix. Fox Sports host committee, Fox Sports and Fox are teaming together. They spent a day with the Purple Hearts home to renovated disabled West Valley, Arizona veteran's bathroom kitchen and more.\par \par \uc2 Great organization, great cause, helping out in the city which is hosting the Super Bowl. The whole city benefits in the Super Bowl. You don't have to go to the game. When they come in, everybody benefits. There's a whole bunch of things going on. But Fox is not waiting for the \uc2 NFL\uc2 . They're doing it on their own.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, that's great. Jessica?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Awesome. All right, so watch out dating apps. TikTok's latest trend called hashtag \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  dating, has ladies headed to their nearest \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  in search of love. The hashtag has already received over 3.6 billion views with many sharing success stories from their expeditions to the hardware store finding what they call husband material, while others weren't as lucky while lurking in the store. It's cute.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Good idea.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It is cute, right?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Jesse?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: How can I find a mop?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Go ahead, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Don't go chasing waterfalls, especially reverse waterfalls. Look at this reverse waterfall. Have you ever seen a reverse waterfall?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: What's going on?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Is that real?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Neither have I. It's a video out of Utah. No idea.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Bryce Canyon?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: But this is very rare. Also, tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME," was Hunter Biden an intelligence asset? Was \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  working for the CIA?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's a tease.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You never thought about that one before, did you?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, I never did. I never did. Did they hired people like Hunter? OK, I didn't know that men love diamonds as much as women do, but apparently some do. Rapper Polo G showcase his new diamond chain on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , which doubles as a functioning iPhone case. Just the pendant of the iPhone contains 500 grams of 14 karat white gold and over 90 carats of diamonds. The piece is estimated to be into the six figures. Would any of you wear this?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You would, Judge.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No. You'd be robbed in two seconds in New York. Are you kidding me?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: He probably have private security though because all the Democrats want to defund the police.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, that's right. That's right. All the Democrats -- the Democrats do that and it's just not right. Is it, Jessica? It's just not right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's not right.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: We're the worst.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Just real quick in the last 12 seconds. Coming up on "ONE NATION" Saturday, Tim Scott and Matt Taibbi will be on Saturday night.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's it for us.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next. Hey, Bret!\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020230127ej1q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Airlines Overbooking?; Border Battle; Republican Presidential Contenders; Merit Award Delays; Tech Layoffs; Interview With Rep. James Comer (R-KY); Document Drama\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Griff Jenkins, Jeff Flock, Daron Shaw, Lydia Hu,  Madison Alworth, Jonathan Turley, Aishah Hasnie, Jacqui Heinrich \par \uc2 8,881 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: What does the president mean when he said no regrets?\par \par \uc2 Because he's also said he takes very seriously the handling of classified documents. So, I'm unclear what he means about no regrets.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So, I'm not going to comment further from what the president has said yesterday. I think he laid out his thoughts. He was asked about it. He laid out his thoughts of whatever question he was asked.\par \par \uc2 I'm not going to get -- I'm not going to get into specifics or I'm not going to go beyond what the president has said.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, no regrets and still no real answers about the president's handling of those classified documents. The president himself wants this wrapped up quickly, but with a special counsel and now at least two House committees investigating, is that just wishful thinking?\par \par \uc2 We're going to talk to the guy leading up one of those House investigations, House Oversight Committee Chairman James Comer.\par \par \uc2 First, FOX team coverage with Jacqui Heinrich on the latest White House maneuvering and Aishah Hasnie Capitol Hill, where frustration, you might say, is building with Democrats and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to it with Jacqui in the thick of it on Capitol Hill -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 The president also said there's no "there" there, which is ultimately musing on the outcome of this investigation that his administration is broadly refusing to comment on. And then his no regrets comment also somewhat undermines his other statements that he takes classification laws seriously.\par \par \uc2 And whether he does is a question that Biden himself asked of someone else back in 1977, when he was a senator. Biden sided with Republicans to kill the nomination of President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 's pick for CIA director. Ted Sorensen had admitted to mishandling classified documents for a JFK biography.\par \par \uc2 And Biden asked in the confirmation hearing, does he respect and understand the rule under which he will serve? "Quite honestly, I'm not sure whether or not Mr. Sorensen could be indicted or convicted under the espionage statutes or fired from his White House job if that were the issue."\par \par \uc2 Those same questions are now being asked of Biden by the House Intelligence Committee, Chairman \uc2 Mike Turner\uc2  saying in his request for a national security damage assessment the mere discovery of classified documents in Biden's possession -- quote -- "would put President Biden in potential violation of laws protecting national security, including the Espionage Act and the Presidential Records Act."\par \par \uc2 But it seems this administration is tacitly trying to pin this on Biden's aides from when he was vice president. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports Kathy Chung, Biden's longtime executive assistant, confessed to associates that she's worried she may have inadvertently been involved in the moving or storing of classified material at the Penn Biden Center.\par \par \uc2 The paper talked to a person briefed -- key word, briefed -- on Kathy Chung's account, which would suggest that someone inside the government might be pointing fingers at Chung. Here's how Biden described it last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We found a handful of documents were failed -- were filed in the wrong place. We immediately turned them over to the Archives and the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So Biden is heading to Rehoboth this weekend, not Wilmington, where those documents were found. We hope to catch up with him on his way out to Marine One next hour, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Jacqui, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich outside the White House.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Aishah Hasnie right now at our Washington bureau here, because Republicans have been desperately trying to get some information from the White House, some damage assessment report that they have been waiting for better than a week.\par \par \uc2 No progress, I guess, right, Aishah?\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Not yet. Yes, they have been waiting for about nine days for that. They haven't heard anything from the administration yet, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And Republicans extremely frustrated, as they are also struggling to get really any information. In fact, FOX can now confirm that the Justice Department has sent a letter to Judiciary Committee members today. Here it is. This is acknowledging Chairman Jim Jordan's multiple requests for records and answers related to the Biden documents.\par \par \uc2 But the letter, Neil, does not say whether the DOJ is actually going to provide those records or reject his requests. They're hiding behind what they call the accommodation process. Let's read a little part of this.\par \par \uc2 It says: "Under this accommodation process, the legislative and executive branches have a constitutional obligation to negotiate in good faith to meet the informational needs of Congress while protecting the institutional interests of the executive branch."\par \par \uc2 So they're going to go through this process before they decide whether they're going to get give him anything. Meanwhile, intel Chairman \uc2 Mike Turner\uc2 , as I said, waiting for the damage assessment that he requested nine days ago, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And oversight Chairman James Comer also struggling with his records request, this one from the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 , which is now rejecting any accusations that the Penn Biden Center, that think tank, ever received any Chinese donations. They say it didn't happen. Judiciary Chairman Jim Jordan fed up. He's not buying it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): China gives big money, millions of dollars, to the Penn Biden center.\par \par \uc2 Then you have \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  gets elected president. We find these documents, these classified documents at the Penn Biden Center. And what's he do? He names the head of \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 , makes her ambassador to Germany. Then Pennsylvania is asked about the money from China. Oh, we don't know anything about the donations.\par \par \uc2 None of that makes sense either. So, yes, he's trying to deflect.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But all of the president's allies are coming to his defense, saying, you know what, he really shouldn't have any regrets.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FMR. SEN. \uc2 DOUG JONES\uc2  (D-AL): It seems that the president's team, whether it's mainly his legal team, Kaitlan, is handling these documents absolutely correctly. In fact, they may be overcorrecting, because that's who \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 Well, the president shouldn't have any regrets about how the documents have been handled.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: One tiny glimmer of hope here for Republicans is that the Secret Service has said that they are prepared to offer Congress a list of visitors to at least the president's Wilmington residence, but only if Congress requests it.\par \par \uc2 So we are waiting and watching for that to happen -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very much for that, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Jonathan Turley right now. Of course, you know Jonathan very, very well, \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  law professor, FOX News contributor, legal genius. Love having him on.\par \par \uc2 So, Jonathan, I was curious about why the delay in getting information certainly to a couple of these committees. You know, obviously, there's a concern about who needs what and when. But it seems like the deference is given to the special counsel, and he gets things first.\par \par \uc2 I could be wrong about that. But it does seem that committee chairmen, whether we're talking the Judiciary Committee or the Oversight Committee, they're just going to have to stew.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, this is a longstanding problem between Congress and the executive branch. When you have an active criminal investigation, the priority does go to that investigation.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice will refuse to turn over material that might damage its investigation or compromise sources or make it more difficult to reach out to witnesses. So this is not the first time that this has happened.\par \par \uc2 So the Department of Justice's position is fairly predictable. What is less predictable and, quite frankly, understandable is the White House position. For a long time, they said, we're just going to have to defer to the Department of Justice. And then the Department of Justice said, well, we haven't actually asked them not to answer questions. They're not limited in that respect.\par \par \uc2 And yet the White House is still doing that. And even when asked about the president's statement that he has no regrets, the White House referred to White House counsel. Well, why? I mean, first of all, White House counsel represents the presidency, not the president as an individual.\par \par \uc2 But it makes the president sound like he's a criminal defendant. Go talk to my counsel. He made this statement in public. He made it as the president of the United States. And yet even that question would not be answered by the press secretary.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jonathan, you have heard the president's there's no "there" there statement. We played it at nauseam here.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things that stands out is, he's essentially saying there's nothing to see here. But early on, we got indications that they did not want this to look like they were \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that they were fighting either the release of these or not cooperating with authorities.\par \par \uc2 But in so doing, we learned that the timeline -- and we're just showing it there for the Justice Department -- there was a significant delay from the time they first learned of these documents, 68 days, as a matter of fact, before this was parceled out.\par \par \uc2 So, obviously, the "there" there is very late there. And that's the problem or a part of the problem, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: It is.\par \par \uc2 And that's part of the problem also referring media to the White House Counsel's Office. The press secretary admitted that the White House Counsel's Office drafted the statement that has been widely ridiculed as false or at least misleading. It omitted the fact that there wasn't just one discovery, and they're referring people back to that -- to that office.\par \par \uc2 But among the things that are there is a special counsel, and it is incredibly daft for the president to say that he has no regrets. I mean, this is a standard as to whether you grossly handled classified evidence.\par \par \uc2 You have classified documents found in your garage, and you have no regrets? That's not exactly a positive point to raise when there's a special counsel in the field. I mean, that's right out of the Alec Baldwin school of criminal defense work.\par \par \uc2 You -- these are statements that work against your interest. You're trying to convey that you do take classified evidence seriously. That doesn't exactly fit the president's last comment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, when, Jonathan, you hear the president -- he was obviously getting a little terse yesterday to sort of wave off the criticism he has been getting on, that he's doing his job, he's doing a good job at this.\par \par \uc2 But when six out of 10 Americans polled on this subject don't like what they're seeing or hearing, doesn't that risk telling them, as this president did in the middle of inflation, that it's not a really big problem?\par \par \uc2 I mean, it chafes people who hear, wait a minute, it's a big problem. To me, there is a risk with just the way this is carried to the public, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: I think it is.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you want to show that you take classified material seriously, you can start by saying, I have some regrets that I had classified evidence stored with my Corvette. I mean, that would not seem a rather difficult point to make.\par \par \uc2 But the problem with the White House position is that there are so many questions here that they're refusing to answer. When you say that something is an inadvertent placement of classified evidence, you should explain a few other strange facts.\par \par \uc2 One is that these documents were divided and distributed to different points. Why? If they were inadvertent, why weren't they all together? Why did some end up in the garage? Why did some of the documents in the garage, apparently, at least one, end up in the library? That indicates that they may have been used for different purposes.\par \par \uc2 Now, the obvious point is that the president was working on a book that dealt with his background with Ukraine and China, which is the subject of some of these documents. If this investigation shows that the president did work off these documents, did see these documents, then all of these statements that he has made and this inadvertence defense takes on a much more sinister appearance.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jonathan, finally, much has been made of the fact that these are documents that go back five-plus years, right, to when he stepped down as vice president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And there is this idea of a statute of limitations that has come and gone now. So if there were any trouble to come up with this, the statute limitations is up, no crime, no foul. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: No, I think that's not an issue.\par \par \uc2 It's an ongoing violation. These documents continue to be grossly mishandled in the fact that they were being housed with a Corvette or in a closet. So I don't see how that statute of limitations would be a barrier. There are criminal -- there's a criminal case that can be made. The question is, will it, whether the president has some clear defense here, and whether some of these questions will be answered.\par \par \uc2 And, unfortunately, those answers are not coming from the White House. And few expect they will be.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Very interesting.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, always enjoy getting caught up on the legal questions. And I appreciate that, as do our viewers.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Meanwhile, just want to let you know, later on in the show, we're going to be speaking to the House Oversight Committee chairman, James Comer, what he wants to see, what he's not seeing, and how he's coordinating things over with the House Judiciary Committee, asking oftentimes for similar documents and material.\par \par \uc2 But we will get clarification on where they distinguish that momentarily.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, a big comeback for the markets today that turned a worrisome year, where we had a January indicator that looked like it was going to be very good, and then for a while we were seeing the Dow slip into negative territory. Now, thanks to today's advance of 330 points, all the major market averages so far this year are back to being up.\par \par \uc2 Some people interpret that as a sign of good things to come. We shall see. It's very early in the year, isn't it? But we just wanted you to know about that.\par \par \uc2 Also want to let about not all the news being great, especially right now for \uc2 Google\uc2  workers at the parent company, \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 . Thousands are bound to lose their jobs right now.\par \par \uc2 Madison Alworth is all over that -- Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it seems most days in 2023, we are seeing hundreds, if not thousands of workers losing their jobs, \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  being the latest.\par \par \uc2 But I have the full list of companies that have seen it, what this means for the overall job market and what it could mean for your job coming up after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it's a start on this whole debt standoff.\par \par \uc2 We're just learning right now that House Speaker Kevin McCarthy says he has accepted President Biden's invitation to sit down and discuss a responsible debt ceiling increase to address irresponsible government spending.\par \par \uc2 That doesn't necessarily mean that the president has signed onto the idea that he would attach spending concessions to increasing the debt limit, now at around $31.4 trillion. They don't want that to be part of it. But the fact that they're going to sit down and talk is a promising development. So we will keep you posted on that.\par \par \uc2 Also keeping you posted on the backdrop for this, where we have a lot of companies firing a lot of workers, \uc2 Google\uc2 , of \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  fame, the very, very latest to do so.\par \par \uc2 Madison Alworth has more on that -- Madison.\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 As the list of companies laying off employees grows, so do fears of a recession. The latest tech company added to the list, \uc2 Google\uc2 's parent company, \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 , laying off 12,000 people. The CEO told employees in an e-mail today that the layoffs are global and impact U.S. staff immediately.\par \par \uc2 In the announcement e-mail, CEO \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2  said -- quote -- "This will mean saying goodbye to some incredibly talented people we worked hard to hire and have loved working with. I'm deeply sorry for that. The fact that these changes will impact the lives of Googlers weighs heavily on me and I take full responsibility for the decisions that led us here."\par \par \uc2 Tech giants across the board are pulling back after years of hiring. This week, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  announced they are firing 10,000 employees and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is cutting 18,000 jobs. So far, around 50,000 people have been fired within the tech industry since just the start of this year.\par \par \uc2 The White House saying today they don't believe the problem will spread to other sectors.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Leading analysts have publicly stated that they do not believe the recent layoffs in the tech industry are indicative of trends in the broader economy.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, of course, when we hear that Americans are losing jobs, we certainly watch that very closely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: But analysts tell FOX this trend of layoffs is not only not going anywhere; it will also likely be met with higher unemployment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEER, MORNING CONSULT: 2023 is going to be a really hard year for workers. I think we will see downright layoffs, not just this reallocation, again, as companies are going to face higher borrowing costs, and the demand from consumers and for businesses will be significantly weaker than it was in 2022.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: When people that are fired aren't reallocated to new jobs, AKA hired at a new company, our unemployment rate goes up.\par \par \uc2 And that's why \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  President \uc2 John Williams\uc2  expects that unemployment will rise to 4.5 percent this year from the current 3.5 percent -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will watch it closely. Thank you for that, Madison.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Professor David Dodson, right now, the Stanford School of Business.\par \par \uc2 Professor, always good to see you, maybe not under these circumstances. But, right now, with these latest layoffs, as you know, at \uc2 Google\uc2 , we're looking at close to 60,000 tech sector workers who've been laid off or will likely be laid off. Now, that's still a small percentage of the overall technology work force, but it's picking up steam.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 DAVID DODSON, STANFORD UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF BUSINESS: Yes, and I actually think the number is going to be closer to 70,000, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, my take on it is this. First of all, I'm not -- what's missing in some of these announcements is, they always blame it on the general economy and worried about the recession. I got to tell you, Neil, I'm not buying what they're selling, because, if that were true, they would have been doing these layoffs last summer.\par \par \uc2 Since then, we have got GDP is back up, inflation is down, supply chain issues are getting worked out. I mean, there's less signs of recession today than there was six months ago. That's not why they're doing it. They're doing it because \uc2 Meta\uc2  laid off 11,000 workers back in November because they had lost their mind with all their projects.\par \par \uc2 And it's providing cover for tech companies to do what they really should have been doing all along, which is right-size. The problem in the tech sector is not about the global economy. The problem in the tech sector is that it's been just the era of drunken spending. And they're figuring out that maybe we don't live in a world where someone's latte in \uc2 Palo Alto\uc2  costs more than what most people pay at the diner for breakfast.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's a very good line. I'm going to steal that one, Professor.\par \par \uc2 It's funny. We also talk about what's changed in the marketplace, the market itself, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , of course, laying off better than 3,000 workers. Its stock has been under enormous pressure, with many people saying, the era for deals that Goldman sort of dominated just isn't there. The appetite isn't there.\par \par \uc2 Whether that's indicative of what's going on in the technology world, I had no idea, but that there is a delayed response to this, because even now, with these layoffs, Professor, we're looking at 10.4 million or so jobs that are going unfilled. Is that -- is that about to peter out?\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Well, I think what's happening, first of all, in the financial sector is, everybody hit the brakes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: So there's not a lot of deals and transactions happening. So that should be no surprise to anybody, because everybody's just waiting to see what's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 In the tech sector, I think there's going to be two significant implications as a result of what's happened since \uc2 Meta\uc2  did their layoffs in November. The first one is this, is it the V.C. funding world is going to change radically.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're talking about venture capital people investing in all of this, right?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Sorry, what?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're talking about venture capital people investing in all of this, right?\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Sorry, venture capital.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Where someone shows up with a PowerPoint presentation, they get funded for $25 million, those days are over.\par \par \uc2 The second thing that's going to happen is the employment market in the tech sector. And, Neil, what I know from firsthand experience is that most companies are struggling to try to get tech sector developers to come work for them. All these people will have jobs, believe me, because, in the tech sector across America, not in Silicon Valley, but across America, they are struggling for employees.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, and it's real. And it's getting pronounced too.\par \par \uc2 You know things are bad Professor when \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  ends the free coffee. That was the first sign that this is -- this was going to build up steam.\par \par \uc2 Always good seeing you, my friend, the Stanford School of Business, incredible professor, Dave Dodson.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, some incredible developments in the political arena. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the only announced Republican presidential candidate thus far, but indications from more than a few that more than a few will join in. Whether it gets back to what we had in 2016 is anyone's guess. But back then, that proved very helpful to one \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . What about now?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you probably think we're over delayed planes and canceled flights and waiting in an airport and nothing happening. What if I were to tell you, and very soon you will hear, it could happen all over again?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, just call it merit madness.\par \par \uc2 This -- it started in Virginia, where hundreds of kids found out that they had gotten to be Merit Scholars, which is quite an honor. You have to test very highly to be in that elite group. Never found out about it. But now we're learning, as we dig into this, that it might not just be those students and it might not just be in, well, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Lydia Hu has more on all of this -- Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Today, the \uc2 U.S. Department of Education\uc2  told FOX Business that the National Merit Scholars program it's not run by the department, so decisions about notifying students of recognition status, those are made at the local level.\par \par \uc2 And that's where we find calls now for terminations as well. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. WINSOME SEARS (R-VA): Telling you that these people should be fired. Don't apologize. Just go. You have done a wrong thing. You have wronged my child. I can't get that back. But you already have your education, and you're denying my child that ability.\par \par \uc2 We're not standing for it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: Now, at least 17 public schools across three Virginia counties have admitted they failed to immediately tell students about their recognition.\par \par \uc2 And the revelation has led other states to take action.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CADE BRUMLEY, LOUISIANA SUPERINTENDENT OF EDUCATION: It's prompted me to take calls within our own state and make calls within our own state to make sure that something like this is not happening within the state of Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: Now, Fairfax County Schools in Virginia has said before that the very suggestion that information about awards being deliberately withheld from students is wrong.\par \par \uc2 But, still, the Virginia attorney general is opening an investigation, looking into whether perhaps discrimination is behind the delay in notifying students. He believes that it may have disproportionately impacted Asian students -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Lydia, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 In Lydia's piece here, you might have caught Virginia's Republican lieutenant governor, Winsome Sears, talking about those who were behind this and not getting word out to kids who had gotten this special recognition. And she was so impatient with it, said, don't apologize for it. Just go. You're fired. Don't let the screen door hit you on the way out.\par \par \uc2 My special guest tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time on "Cavuto Live,." Can't wait to talk to her and can't wait for you to get to the bottom of this issue that now apparently goes beyond just Virginia.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, there's also this:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: We're doing all the things that one would do to get -- to get ready.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: But we're still trying to figure our way through it.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO THE UNITED NATIONS: Do I think I could do that later? Yes. But we are still working through things, and we will figure it out. I have never lost a race. I said that then. I still say that now. I'm not going to lose now. But stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so it's not just \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You have heard from a couple of prominent Republicans there, and more to come, we're told, who are also considering 2024 runs on their own, no matter what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  thinks of it.\par \par \uc2 To Daron Shaw right now, the FOX News Decision Desk genius, to help us through this.\par \par \uc2 Daron, immediately when I saw this, and I caught Bret's interview with Nikki Haley, to say nothing of what Pompeo was winking and nodding with us, we're going to have a crowded field, likely, won't we?\par \par \uc2 DARON SHAW, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, it looks like it.\par \par \uc2 I was just -- in advance of talking to you, Neil, I was pulling some numbers from previous election cycles. And, in 2000, there were 13 Republicans. Bush runs for reelect in '04, no real opposition, 2008, 12 in 2012 -- in 2012 -- excuse me -- 2012, 14. 2016, there were 17.\par \par \uc2 So I don't see how we're anywhere sort of short of 15 to 25 candidates, if history is any guide here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, then I start wondering -- you and I have talked this before, Daron -- that, in that that kind of situation, with a crowded field, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  famously picked them off.\par \par \uc2 And that could work to his advantage, couldn't it?\par \par \uc2 SHAW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Neil, you and I remember, I think on the Democratic side in 2020, everybody kept talking about lanes, right, and brands.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHAW: What's the candidate's lane? What's the brand?\par \par \uc2 And Trump has a brand and Trump has a lane. Trump -- there may actually be a lane called the Trump lane. And my guess is, is that Trump is probably the most obvious candidate to fill that lane.\par \par \uc2 But you're right. I mean, what it does is, it reduces the need to -- you don't have to get 50, 60, 70 percent of the vote in these Republican primaries when it's so split up, especially amongst moderates or non-Trump candidates. That creates the potential for Trump to sort of skate by with 25, 30, 35 percent of the votes.\par \par \uc2 And that's got to be kind of daunting for some of these kind of more centrist candidates, non-Trump candidates who are contemplating a run.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, as you reminded me, all you do -- have to do is get one or a couple more votes than the other guy, and you pick up that state, or at least the plurality, the biggest plurality of that state's delegates.\par \par \uc2 I am wondering. Republicans are not planning, like Democrats, to shuffle around