timate sort of example of the mood of the time, right? You're a little tired. You're yesterday's news.\par \par \uc2 And then, on the other side, you have got a lot of candidates who are hot right now. And 10, 15 years ago, the thought of some of these candidates running, we'd say, oh, they need more seasoning, they need more experience, they need to get their legs under them.\par \par \uc2 No one thinks that way anymore, right? I mean, you go when you're hot.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SHAW: So, candidates like Youngkin in particular, right, I mean, why wouldn't you run right now?\par \par \uc2 I mean, in -- by 2028, no one's going to remember how kind of new and fresh you were because you won in 2021 or 2022. So it doesn't seem to be a barrier anymore. So, I think you're going to get a lot of these people who ordinarily would wait their turn, they're not going to wait their turn, and they're looking to knock off a guy who was a -- you know, the ultimate fresh different kind of politician in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Not so much anymore. A lot of baggage there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you got to run when you're hot and run when the moment is with you.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very, very much, my friend. We will follow it closely.\par \par \uc2 Daron Shaw of the FOX News Decision Desk.\par \par \uc2 He mentioned \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , the governor of New Hampshire. He too is among those potential presidential aspirants. He too will be joining me on Monday on FOX Business, which, if you don't get, you should demand, because we have got some -- two hot new shows for you as well. I will have more on that later. But he will be a special guest of mine.\par \par \uc2 One of the stars of that network and this network as well, Jeff Flock, no one does these moving stand-ups better. But, right now, when it comes to airline delays, moving around walking is about the best that you have than waiting for a plane and waiting and waiting and waiting.\par \par \uc2 And, apparently, Jeff Flock, it's going to happen again, or it could, right?\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: More delays, perhaps, more cancellations, Neil? Yes, maybe you should walk to your destination instead. And there's also a new problem out at the nation's airports right now. What is it?\par \par \uc2 Well, I think it'd be best if you stayed tuned. After this commercial, you will find out.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we want to alert you right now.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is in a San Francisco courtroom. He is defending comments and actions he made going back to 2018 when he was talking about the possibility of taking \uc2 Tesla\uc2  private, said that he had financing for the deal.\par \par \uc2 Well, a lot of investors were pretty happy about that at the time. That never materialized. They sued him en masse. And this is in response to that class action lawsuit, that he misled those investors. And now they want him to pay up. He is on the stand right now defending that tweet and defending his actions, but saying that in no way did that indicate something he was looking to do, that he would do.\par \par \uc2 But the back-and-forth continues. We will keep you posted on that. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  stock, as you know, has careened, of course, facing greater global competition. They have had to cut prices to sell vehicles. I could go on and on. And then there's a separate \uc2 Twitter\uc2  battle.\par \par \uc2 But this is just focused on that from back in 2018. We'll let you know how his time on the stand goes.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, letting you know how things at airports are going, certainly better than they were during the holidays and with the Southwest flight delays and with the \uc2 FAA\uc2  scares and all of that. But the notion that we're over all of that or that it can't happen again, Jeff Flock here to tell you from Philadelphia, yes, it could -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Calm