sh.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 UNITED AIRLINES\uc2 : There are a number of airlines who cannot fly their schedules. And their customers are paying the price. They're canceling a lot of flights. But they simply can't fly the schedules today.\par \par \uc2 And maybe it's pilots. Maybe it's something else. Maybe it's technology. Maybe it's infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: And maybe it's all the above, Neil, a complex problem that sort of affects all of us.\par \par \uc2 And my experience with complex problems is, they don't have simple solutions.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But they do have a lot of finger-pointing, to your point.\par \par \uc2 Great job, as always, Jeff Flock, star of FOX Business, following all of those developments.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, you know things are getting pretty bad at the border, when agents are saying we're sick of the homeland security secretary. We'd love to take matters into our own hands.\par \par \uc2 It's not that simple, but Griff Jenkins is there at Eagle Pass with more.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 There's a reason rank-and-file agents have lost confidence in Alejandro Mayorkas. And wait until you see the tape we shot last night. That's coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, more trouble at the border, but this time focused on the guy who's supposed to be, well, kind of in charge of what's going on at the border, the homeland security secretary, Griff Jenkins, on some very, very, very, very angry patrol agents.\par \par \uc2 He's in Eagle Pass, Texas -- Griff.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: That's right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 They're not happy, they're frustrated and they're exhausted. But let me just quickly take you to our FOX sky drone right now. You will see they're zeroed in on two migrants who just crossed the river in the last 10 minutes and literally walked right on to a bus that had been staged here for the migrant crossings, which happen every hour.\par \par \uc2 I have never seen them just walk right on, the Border Patrol pulling them off to process them before they put them right back on. Meanwhile, let me show you some exclusive footage shot by our FOX cameras embedded with Texas DPS last night. In the span of six hours, Neil, we saw troopers stop three separate smuggling attempts.\par \par \uc2 You see here more than a dozen migrants were rescued there. That's why these agents are so frustrated with Mayorkas. And Border Patrol Council Union Vice President Jon Anfinsen says he supports the GOP calls to impeach Mayorkas. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JON ANFINSEN, VICE PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: It's just, they're ruthless, they're effective and, in many ways, they have better intelligence than we do, because, again, their resources are completely unlimited.\par \par \uc2 And when we are stuck indoors processing, they're able to do whatever they want.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And what they want to do is stop those that are known got-aways, the ones that don't want to be apprehended.\par \par \uc2 Since I have been on the ground, 14 days now, Neil, talking to you, there have been more than 7,000 known got-aways just in this sector.\par \par \uc2 Neil, we will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, my friend, Griff Jenkins, following all that ahead of so many others on this story.\par \par \uc2 Brad Coe joins us right now, speaking of what Griff was just speaking about, the Kinney County, Texas sheriff.\par \par \uc2 Sheriff, great to have you.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of this move on the part of some other Border Patrol agents -- for all I know, you might concur -- that enough with the homeland security secretary, he's got to go?\par \par \uc2 BRAD COE, KINNEY COUNTY, TEXAS, SHERIFF: Well, I guess he does have to go.\par \par \uc2 I sent him a personal letter about a -- two to three weeks ago demanding his resignation, because he is not upholding his oath of office to the Constitution. He had basically sworn an allegiance to the president and not to the American people.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It would be an uphill battle, though, right, Sheriff? I mean, the president seems to like him, wanting to stick around.\par \par \uc2 So what do you do?\par \par \uc2 COE: Well, it's going to be up to Congress.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COE: I mean, I'm just one voice here. So Congress is going to have to do something with him.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I'm just wondering, from your perspective, sir, was there anything that changed after the president visited the border a couple of weeks ago?\par \par \uc2 Did any sign show up that and, finally, there's going to be action?\par \par \uc2 COE: Nothing -- nothing here has changed. We're still up to our armpits in it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, like, this morning, they were talking about DPS having six pursuits last night. We have had three since 9:00 this morning. Between 9:00 a.m. and 1:00 p.m., we have had three pursuits come right through my town.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So what do you make of an idea that Texas Congressman Crenshaw had to get the military involved there, for no other reason than to focus on all the drugs and everything else coming through, to go after the smugglers, to go after those guys?\par \par \uc2 COE: Well, the governor has got to do something, because the federal government is obviously not doing it.\par \par \uc2 And part of our Constitution, of the Texas Constitution, is, we have a right to protect our border. When we joined the Union, that was part of the guarantee, that the Union would help protect the Texas border. And they're failing to do that. So now it's up to the governor. And if the governor can't fulfill, we're going to have to be doing it on our own as local sheriffs.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what happens, Sheriff, if the homeland security -- he stays, doesn't go, the president doesn't visit again, the vice president doesn't visit again, there's no follow-through on some of the promises they made?\par \par \uc2 We're already hearing that Mexico isn't sticking to everything it had agreed to, especially when it came to these trains that have migrants getting to the border as well. Then what?\par \par \uc2 COE: We're on our own.\par \par \uc2 When of all this started, Ted Cruz came to visit. And he basically was the first one that actually told us the truth and said, guys, you're on your own until we can win back Congress or the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So, now we have got Congress. How are we going to do it? How are we going to make this move to protect our border on a federal level, plus get the secretary out?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. We will watch very, very closely. You have the House in that particular case, Sheriff, not yet the Senate, certainly not the White House.\par \par \uc2 We will keep an eye on it, though.\par \par \uc2 COE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for all you and your men and women are sacrificing to deal with this. It's a Herculean task.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to James Comer on this, the House Oversight chairman, the Kentucky congressman.\par \par \uc2 Among his many duties, besides looking into certain documents from the president of the United States, is what's happening at the border.\par \par \uc2 And I know, Congressman, you have been keen on getting a hearing going on this very issue. Where does that stand?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): Well, we're going to have our first hearing on the crisis at the border.\par \par \uc2 This is the number one priority for House Republicans. We have heard the message loud and clear from not just the Border Patrol agents, and not just from the people who reside in the border states, but from law enforcement all over America, that we have got to 