rry to jump on you, sir -- to the Justice Department and working with them, or so it would seem.\par \par \uc2 How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 COMER: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, I -- it's not right. And the National Archives is going to pay the price for it. Look, they're an agency. They have created a lot of chaos here. There have been mistakes made. Apparently, there needs to be reform of how documents are transported from once a president or vice president leaves office to their place in the private sector.\par \par \uc2 There's a huge problem when you have two general -- two special counsels investigating the last two presidents, and yet they won't even do a briefing for the chairman and ranking member? Listen, I'm not trying to ask for anything political. We will bring the Democrats in.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We only have a minute, sir. And I'm very sorry about that.\par \par \uc2 COMER: Right. That's all right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the president is saying there's nothing "there" there, that what you're doing and all this idea and it's a subterfuge and the drama, there's nothing "there" there, that they're handling things well.\par \par \uc2 That's what the president says. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 COMER: I disagree.\par \par \uc2 Why hasn't the \uc2 FBI\uc2  been in there? Just because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's personal attorneys say everything's hunky-dory, that doesn't mean the darn thing to me. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  admitted he didn't even know they had those classified documents. So, he probably doesn't even need to comment on this.\par \par \uc2 We have got some lawyers who we don't know who are paying them. We don't know who their clients are.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 COMER: We don't think they have national security clearance. They're the ones in charge of this. And it's very unfortunate.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Sir, thank you very much. I apologize, with all the other breaking news, for the truncated time.\par \par \uc2 COMER: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: James Comer, the Kentucky Republican, oversees the Oversight Committee, probably the most important committee these days, with all these multiple investigations going on.\par \par \uc2 We will pursue that and the ramifications of what Mr. Comer just said tomorrow on my Saturday show 10:00 a.m. Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, here comes "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230121ej1k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  Flies Private To Avoid Travel Disasters; Traveling By Train Has Also Become A Nightmare; Outraged Train Passenger Tells Tucker About Her Travel Hell; Biden's Classified Files Keep Popping Up All Over The Place; Interview With Sen. JD Vance (R-OH); Government Transparency Is Essential For Democracy; Food Prices Soaring Across The Country; The Best And Worst People In The Workplace; The Left Wants To Make It Easier For Americans To Do Drugs\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Trace Gallagher \par \uc2 7,743 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Jesse, would you ever consider being a guest on "GUTFELD!." I have been a guest on " GUTFELD!." Subbed for Gutfeld as a host, but he tapes his show right at the right time that I do this show, and it is the best that I don't have to do that damn show anymore.\par \par \uc2 Watch Tucker. I'm Watters, this is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Breaking news and this is going to shock you. Yet more classified documents discovered in the possession of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the guy who has been lecturing you about classified documents for the past few months.\par \par \uc2 This time, the documents were stashed in a new location separate from the so-called Penn Biden Center, that magnet for Chinese money. We don't know exactly where they were stashed, you're not allowed to know.\par \par \uc2 The administration won't tell us. We are waiting tonight for the DOJ police raid on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's house.\par \par \uc2 We maybe waiting a while for that. We're going to have more details on that story in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, good evening, and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 So think back two years. It was two years ago that \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  of South Bend, Indiana, was in a very hard place, really. He was unemployed. He was pushing 40. He was staring down the barrel of a grim and non-productive middle age. For Mayor Pete, the back nine did not look promising. But then help arrived in the unlikely and doddering form of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Either because of his innate decency or because he has a nasty sense of humor, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  made \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  the Federal Secretary of Transportation, despite the fact that \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  had no relevant experience with transportation of any kind apart from occasionally riding a bicycle with a little helmet.\par \par \uc2 This was a huge promotion from unemployment to the White House Cabinet. And understandably, Mayor Pete was full of vim and high-minded ambition as he started his new job. But soon, very soon, came crushing disappointment. Like a homebuyer who discovers that his new house is riddled with termites, Mayor Pete soon learned that the Department of Transportation was on the verge of collapse.\par \par \uc2 As it turned out, the department had a very serious diversity shortage. Deadly serious. Imagine being adrift in an open boat in the Sargasso Sea and realizing you're down to your final tin of equity. You're about to run out of equity. That's where \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  found himself in January of 2021. That's not the kind of emergency a responsible Federal appointee could ignore. And fortunately for our nation, Mayor Pete did not ignore it. He took swift action.\par \par \uc2 Just months after taking office, Buttigieg located the core of the problem, which, as we now know, was racist roads. "There is racism physically built into some of our highways," Mayor Pete announced. And in his voice you heard the iron resolve of a man who has no tolerance for bigotry and asphalt. And yet, at the same time, you also heard a deeply sensitive man, a man who can feel the hate beneath the wheels of his electric vehicle where others might just hear road noise. Mayor Pete decided to stamp out this hate.\par \par \uc2 By November of that year, Buttigieg had ordered every freeway overpass in the Bronx expected for racism, and soon, he had preliminary findings. "If an underpass was constructed such that a bus carrying mostly Black and Puerto Rican kids to a beach in New York was designed too low for it to pass by," he explained, ". that obviously reflects racism that would into those design choices." Yes, obviously. It makes sense. Problem identified.\par \par \uc2 We should point out here, though, that Mayor Pete didn't actually tear down and rebuild these racist overpasses in the Bronx. Construction is not his thing. It was enough to call them racist and move on. That is his thing.\par \par \uc2 And really, it's core to the new mission of the Transportation Department, which at this point, can be summarized pretty simply, if a single trans woman of color has a bad day in this country, we as a nation have failed. With that credo in mind, Mayor Pete turned his attention to aviation.\par \par \uc2 Now, Mayor Pete, himself, flies private, so he didn't have a tactile, firsthand experience with our crumbling airports or crowded, stinking planes last refurbished in the Clinton administration. But he knew something more important. He knew that the \uc2 FAA\uc2  was beset by structural misogyny and transphobia.\par \par \uc2 And to fix that, Mayor Pete had the \uc2 FAA\uc2  announce that the aviation system called Notice to Airmen -- that's the system that keeps pilots up to date on their flights -- that system would change immediately.\par \par \uc2 Actually, the system itself wouldn't change. It would remain the same. But more important, the name would change. Going forward, it would be called "Notice to Air Missions." "Missions," not "Men." Missions. See the massive improvement there. Now we're getting somewhere.\par \par \uc2 So at the beginning of last year, Mayor Pete declared victory. 2022, he said, would be the year that, "Every transportation decision inherently is in many ways a decision about equity." In April of last year, Mayor Pete noted that ensuring equity and accessibility for every member of the traveling public is one of the department's highest priorities. So he was on it and the equity crisis was solved.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, our transportation problems were not solved. They had, in the meantime, become much worse. In fact, it reached a point, it is at a point, that you might experience on, say, a trip across Bangladesh.\par \par \uc2 Just today, in fact, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  was forced to order a ground stop for all air traffic in the United States. No planes flying in a continental country. First time that has happened since 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Now, the problem affected the newly renamed "Notice to Air Missions" system. More than 10,000 flights were canceled or delayed. How did this happen? Total stop on all air travel. Let's go to the Secretary of Transportation for his assessment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, US SECRETARY OF TRANSPORTATION: Now that the system is up and running, our primary focus is to determine that root cause. And I've directed the \uc2 FAA\uc2  to figure out exactly how this happened. The timeline, piece by piece, about what was known overnight going into last night and then coming out of it.\par \par \uc2 So glitches or complications happen all the time, but we can't allow them to ever lead to this level of disruption and we won't ever allow them to lead to a safety problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Okay. So I'm the Secretary of Transportation and there's no air transportation in the entire country of 350 million people. I have no idea why, but I'm going to, "direct someone" to find out because we can't allow this to happen.\par \par \uc2 Really? Thanks for telling us. We thought we could allow it to happen. Oh, we can't. You're going to direct someone to find out. Yes, sure.\par \par \uc2 But the important thing to know here is that the system that didn't work, the "Notice to Airmen" is no longer called "Notice to Airmen." So it may not actually function. You can't actually fly anywhere. You're earthbound, but you're not transphobic.\par \par \uc2 So air travel is a disaster under the man who is supposed to fix air travel. But it's not just air travel. Have you ridden on a train recently? Have you really? Have you ridden on a train? Well, on Monday of this week at 5:00 PM, passengers in Virginia got on an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  bound for Orlando. The car train. They're supposed to arrive by 10:00 AM on Tuesday, but they didn't.\par \par \uc2 Instead, they were stuck on that train for 37 hours because \uc2 Amtrak\uc2 , part of our transportation system, could not figure out how to navigate around a derailment. Some passengers called the police thinking they'd been taken hostage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMTRAK\uc2  ANNOUNCER: Once again, for those of you that are calling the police, we are not holding you hostage. We are giving you all the information in which we have.\par \par \uc2 We are sorry about the inconvenience. As soon as more information is available, we will let you know shortly. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We'd like you to know that we're not holding you hostage. We have all the information, "in which we have."\par \par \uc2 Excuse me, that's our transportation systems. It is probably not funny if you were caught on that train, Colleen McKenna was. She was one of the travelers who was stuck on a stinking \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train for 37 hours.\par \par \uc2 Colleen, I'm sorry to laugh. It must have been horrible. Thank you for joining us tonight. What was that like?\par \par \uc2 COLEEN MCKENNA: Yes, was honestly just extremely frustrating just because of the lack of information from \uc2 Amtrak\uc2 . I woke up about 6:00 AM on Tuesday, and we were stopped in North Carolina and I spoke with one of my attendants, and he just said that there was a derailment of a truck -- of a train in front of us, so we're going to have to take a detour. I have no idea when we're going to arrive in Sanford, my best guess is later on this evening.\par \par \uc2 So they just had no idea what was going on the entire time.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Now trains, and correct me if I'm wrong, but they're on tracks, right? They can't just veer off into the woods and get lost. I mean, they run on metal rails.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA: No. Exactly, yes.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So how could -- right -- so at least that's the way it used to work. How could a train not have "information" about where it's going?\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA: I don't know. At one point, we moved to South Carolina and we were stuck there for six hours. They told us that they needed to switch over crews and one should be arriving at around 5:30. Five thirty came around and we got an update and they said that they're unsure where this crew is, and they don't know when we're going to be here.\par \par \uc2 So the next update we got was around seven o'clock where they said it would be two more hours for them to get to us.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Were you heartened or confused when they announced on the PA system that they were "not holding you hostage"?\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA: I thought it was funny, but I did understand people's concern because there was no way off of the train. We were stuck in the middle of nowhere in South Carolina and there wasn't an option to go outside. So we were locked in, but I didn't feel unsafe.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Did anyone try to make a break for it?\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA: Not that I'm aware of, but it could have been possible.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, I have to ask. You don't have to answer this if you don't want because it's a sensitive question, but there's been a lot of concern about racism, misogyny, and transphobia in our transportation system. Did you notice any of those things on the \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train?\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA: I didn't. I was a little isolated on the train just because I had a private room. So the most interaction I got was when I was having my meals, but when I was around other people, I didn't notice anything significant.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Okay. Well, I think \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  is going to count that as a win.\par \par \uc2 So I appreciate your coming on. I'm glad you're off the train, Colleen. Glad you weren't held hostage. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA: I'm glad I'm here. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I am, too. See you.\par \par \uc2 So that's not the only transportation disaster, literal disaster that we've lived through that has really hurt the country in the two years under Mayor Pete's non-management of the Department of Transportation.\par \par \uc2 FOX's Trace Gallagher has actually been keeping track of them with pen and paper. He joins us tonight with the list.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Trace.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 And remember \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  is the Transportation Secretary who said that parenting twin boys is the most demanding thing he's ever done.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, he said that in the middle of a supply chain crisis that left a fleet of container ships stacked up in ports from New York to Los Angeles, with no workers to unload the cargo and no truck drivers to carry the cargo.\par \par \uc2 Then again, Secretary Buttigieg might had been right about the demands of fatherhood because at the very height of the supply chain crisis, the Secretary wasn't leading the charge. He was on a two-month paternity leave.\par \par \uc2 Then came a series of airline fiascos beginning last Memorial Day weekend, where 3,000 flights were canceled. The airlines blamed a combination of bad weather, bad planning, and a lack of pilots and air traffic controllers. The airline said it had nothing to do with mandatory COVID vaccines, which turned out to be untrue.\par \par \uc2 Then in mid-June, another 1,500 flights were canceled, a frustrated Buttigieg then met with airline leaders to get to the bottom of the cancellations. The next day, the Secretary's own flight was canceled, though he was reimbursed.\par \par \uc2 And by late summer, Buttigieg was apparently in no mood to deal with the demands of railway workers so he jetted off to Portugal. His flight was on time.\par \par \uc2 And while Buttigieg has been an outspoken critic of the airline problems, today's \uc2 FAA\uc2  problem belongs entirely totally to the Federal government. Buttigieg said several times today he is looking into the root causes, but if the border crisis is any guide, root causes is Federal code for no clue what to do -- Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I'm guessing transphobia. But you know, I don't have the inside scoop on this. We'll find out.\par \par \uc2 Trace Gallagher, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So believe it or not, there is yet another batch, a sheaf, we don't know how many, but there are some classified documents that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was holding on to illegally. Where were they found? The White House is refusing to say because you've got no right to know. That's next.\par \par \uc2 Plus, it's pretty impressive to do anything while hungover,. it's impressive to go to the beach. How would you like to play professional baseball? Keith Hernandez played professional baseball at the highest possible level for many years and from time to time, he did it hungover. He tells us how he did that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: This is a FOX News Alert: A new Trove or sheaf or bundle of classified documents has just been found by people who work for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , his lawyers, his aides, we don't know. Where were they found? We don't know that either.\par \par \uc2 All we know right now is the documents were not in the same location as the first batch of illegally retained documents. That'd be the Penn Biden Senator, the magnet for Chinese money that the discredited \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2  runs.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration, again, will not say who found these documents, why or where? But don't worry. Today, the White House Press Secretary insisted it's not a big deal. Relax.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: How can President Biden be trusted moving forward with America's secrets?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Because his lawyers, his team did the right thing.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But he had a closet with classified information and --\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: His lawyers did the right -- again, again, again, he was surprised that the records were there. He spoke to this personally. He was surprised that the records were there and when his lawyers found out and his team found out that they were there, they turned it over to the Archives and now it's being reviewed by the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Did you hear that? He did the right thing, because he's a good person, unlike the Orange Man. So we don't need to send the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to go raid his home and look through his wife's underwear drawer. Everything's cool. He's a good person. He did the right thing.\par \par \uc2 By the way, that's kind of a consensus view in Washington, a city that needs to change.\par \par \uc2 Harmeet Dhillon plans to try and change it. She is running for \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chair. She joins us tonight to assess what we're seeing now.\par \par \uc2 Harmeet, thanks so much for coming on. So he did the right thing. Is that a legal defense?\par \par \uc2 HARMEET DHILLON, FOUNDER, CENTER FOR AMERICAN LIBERTY: Not at all, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 What we are seeing here is actually a cover up of typical Washington proportions. In fact, it looks now like at least one of these troves of documents was identified before the Midterm Elections, but we're just hearing about it now, so it was concealed from the country to affect the outcome of an election.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, it looks like there have been at least now three instances of these documents being moved. The first is out of the White House in 2017, as the Obama and Biden administrations were vacating; secondly, into location number one; thirdly, into location number two.\par \par \uc2 And if the DOJ were serious about this, Tucker, they should be raiding all of Biden's homes and vacation destinations over the last several years to see, you know, rifle through the sofa cushions, see if there are any classified documents there because you can really have no confidence with this set of facts that we are at the end of the discovery of these documents.\par \par \uc2 And an important distinction here is that President Trump had the absolute authority under Federal law to declassify documents and also to keep documents for a period of time while he was looking through them for his Archives before turning them over to the National Archives.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has no such defense to that, and so I'm not buying any of this. And the other complicating factor here, Tucker, is that multiple members of the former Vice President's family have close and questionable ties to foreign governments, including China and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That's right.\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: At least one of these documents pertains to the Ukraine, and there are serious Federal penalties for giving access to foreign governments to these classified documents and a longer statute of limitations.\par \par \uc2 This is a major criminal investigation issue and brushing it under the rug by giving it to a recent United States Attorney holdover from the Trump era is no substitute for a Special Counsel, which is how Democrats have treated the Mar-a-Lago documents. So there is a lot to see here and we aren't at the end of it yet, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: He did the right -- if you discover I robbed a liquor store six years ago, and I returned the money to the cash register, I'm still guilty of a crime.\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: No harm, no foul in Washington apparently.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: He did the right thing. Yes.\par \par \uc2 Harmeet Dhillon running for Chair of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2