f people don't want to actually be forced to acknowledge that a lot of the money we've sent to Ukraine hasn't been well spent, that there's a lot of ways, there are a lot of probably very corrupt people who are getting rich off of it.\par \par \uc2 And let me throw another statistic out there for you, Tucker. The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , the department that allegedly is supposed to secure our border also does aviation security and a lot of other things, it receives $60 billion, half of the money that we've sent to Ukraine, where is it all going? How is it being spent? It's our right to know.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Amen. I would love to hear the rejoinder to that. I would love to hear the argument against what you just said. Russian agent.\par \par \uc2 Senator JD Vance of Ohio, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Thanks, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, Ukraine, like so many other big stories, like the death of George Floyd, like COVID plays upon people's best instincts and subverts them, their compassion, which is at the core of the American spirit, the care for other people. It takes that care and it twists it to dark ends.\par \par \uc2 You're seeing some of the nicest people in Washington make some of the dumbest statements because they've been in infected by this brain virus.\par \par \uc2 So Members of Congress who are again decent people are now trying to spend your money on a monument to a foreign leader in the US Capitol. That's insane. And of course, that foreign leader is President Zelenskyy of Ukraine who is dictatorial. It's not even a democratic country. He banned opposition parties trying to ban an entire Christian denomination.\par \par \uc2 The person leading this charge, we're sad to say is a really nice guy who is pretty conservative, actually, Joe Wilson of South Carolina, a Republican member. He has just sponsored legislation to display a bust of a foreign leader, Zelenskyy in the Capitol building.\par \par \uc2 The legislation he is putting forward would ensure that Zelenskyy's bust is on display, "in a suitable permanent location in the House of Representatives wing," so we can worship him daily. This is crazy. Let's hold people back.\par \par \uc2 So in addition to everything else, we have now a national egg shortage. So who to talk to about that? Well, of course our in-house chicken expert, who we've consulted repeatedly through the years will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: As we told you last night, the Biden administration is trying to ban gas stoves because climate means they can control everything about your life, but wholly unaddressed is the question of what you're going to cook on the stove because food prices are out of control, the price of eggs especially.\par \par \uc2 In California, the price of eggs is up nearly 500 percent since last year. Here's one report from San Diego.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's killed over 57 million birds.\par \par \uc2 KSWB REPORTER: If you've noticed eggs are getting harder to come by, more empty shelves at the grocery store, and much higher prices --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We paid over $7.00 for a 12 pack at once, and I said I'm not doing that again.\par \par \uc2 KSWB REPORTER: The bird flu started on the East Coast last March and the effects of that are now being felt here in San Diego.\par \par \uc2 This couple was one of many stopping by Hilliker's Ranch Fresh Eggs in Lakeside telling us all their grocery stores were wiped out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The egg market is all controlled by supply and demand. The government sends out a price every week, and we follow it.\par \par \uc2 KSWB REPORTER: Which is why we're seeing those prices soar in the store.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So we saw this story and we thought, who can we consult about the price of eggs in the first name that came to mind was Tiara Soleim, a chicken enthusiast, a longtime chicken expert working informally for this show, and someone whose life has been vindicated by the current news.\par \par \uc2 Tara, thank you so much for coming on tonight. So you have been keeping chickens famously for years now. Now there is an egg shortage, you must feel like the smartest person in America.\par \par \uc2 TIARA SOLEIM, CHICKEN ENTHUSIAST: You know, I will say, my heart goes out to all the chickens that lost their lives to this horrendous bird flu that's going on, but for like local chicken owners and like small farms, this is great for us, because now people are turning to us wanting our eggs, and finally respecting chickens like they deserve.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, so that must feel really good, because I know you've been on the show a number of times, we've always been grateful to have you and we've gotten a big response every time and most have been positive. Some of it has been mocking. I'm sure that you have been mocked by your family, friends, and neighbors for keeping so many chickens, for being a chicken enthusiast.\par \par \uc2 They're not making fun of you anymore, are they?\par \par \uc2 SOLEIM: No, they're actually coming to me and wanting my eggs and finally needing me for something and I'm like, oh, it's about time. You're welcome. I have a purpose now.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, and do they look at the chickens a little differently?\par \par \uc2 SOLEIM: You know, I feel like at this point, yes.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So really, I mean, the two themes I'm taking away from your life are self-sufficiency and love of animals. Is that fair to say?\par \par \uc2 SOLEIM: You know, yes, because finally, after the years of people making fun of my chickens and thinking that I'm weird that I have so many roosters and so many hens, I am now like, oh, told you so, chickens are finally having their glow up and I'm so grateful for it.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Now, but we should just say in the interest of animal husbandry and accuracy, roosters don't lay eggs, correct?\par \par \uc2 SOLEIM: No. So funny thing is, my hens sleep in a different coop and so right now we're in front of all my roosters. So, a lot of people will probably be like, oh my gosh, roosters don't lay eggs. No shit. But this is easier, and if you remember last time there's Bad Boy Halo from the very, very first time I was on. He's a right there in the center. He was a little star.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SOLEIM: He does his job well.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Can I say, not to get too in the weeds on the chicken question, but how do you keep roosters from killing each other\par \par \uc2 SOLEIM: Okay, so I'm a full believer in roosters are misunderstood and if you raise them -- hand-raise them, hold them, handle them, they actually are all very nice. So my six roosters coexist really well with my other six hens. So I think it's all the way you raise them.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That's fair. You know, I actually consulted someone with chickens after your last appearance, and we both agreed you are the chicken whisperer. Tiara, thank you so much for joining us tonight. Congratulations on your vindication, and good luck with all those eggs.\par \par \uc2 SOLEIM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SOLEIM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So we sat down with one of the greatest figures in American sports, Keith Hernandez, formerly of the Cardinals and the Mets had an amazing career as a first baseman. He won two World Series titles, but he was really the kind of athlete that you don't see today.\par \par \uc2 Here is a man who smoked cigarettes in the dugout, played some of his games hung over, and we asked him because we couldn't resist, how did you do that? How did you play at that higher level while living the life you were?\par \par \uc2 Here is part of the conversation from "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We read the stories about baseball players tearing it up at night and then playing the next day. How do people play at a high level with a hangover?\par \par \uc2 KEITH HERNANDEZ, FORMER PROFESSIONAL BASEBALL PLAYER: You can't do it. Make a habit of it.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: But you can't.\par \par \uc2 HERNANDEZ: People will catch up to you. Chicago was the place. Wrigley Field. One of the great things about Wrigley Field was that it's the same ballpark that when Ruth made the point, the homerun.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HERNANDEZ: So you're in that clubhouse, it was a tiny clubhouse, tiny, and you had to walk up the stairs and over the people, you could see the people, they throw beers at it. It was just fun. It was great fun. I loved Wrigley.\par \par \uc2 But when you sat in your locker, you go, gosh, darn Babe Ruth, Lou Gehrig sat here.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HERNANDEZ: Stan Musial, and all the greats, and here I am, in the same locker as them.\par \par \uc2 But getting back to your point, Russian division, you can go into a bar in Russian division at 11 o'clock and it's empty. You go one o'clock, it's packed, and it will go to four. Now, if you stayed out to four, don't go to bed. Don't try to sleep, just stay up.\par \par \uc2 I did it three times in my career, just because I had to do it, and I got out the first base. And I said, Lord, please, no ground balls today. No tough plays. I promise. I'll never do it again.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: How'd you play?\par \par \uc2 HERNANDEZ: I don't remember. I did good when I was tired or if I had -- I focused more if the say I had a headache or for whatever reason, I focused more. You can't let your team down. It's a team game. I can't -- I can't in my right mind go out in the field because I'm tired or I've had too many hungover and I'm holding the runner on. And I've got to come off the bad with the pitch , and if I slough it, and don't do that extra step when the ball gets