2 CARLSON: Not to laugh at other people's suffering. The question is, was that an accident or passive aggression? We'll let you be the judge.\par \par \uc2 Who thought it was a good idea to encourage people to use drugs in the middle of the worst drug epidemic in American history that's killing more people than any drug epidemic ever has, and is flipping our society upside down? Well, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  thinks it's a good idea.\par \par \uc2 Biden's DOJ is saying it may soon legalize what is calling Supervised Drug Consumption Sites all over the country where people are effectively encouraged with Federal tax dollars to use narcotics and die. They're going to decide on this in a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 Michael Shellenberger is one of the very few reporters in this country who has actually gone out to take a look at what this looks like firsthand, these modern opium dens. He's the author, of course of "San Fran-sicko." He joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Michael Shellenberger, thank you so much for coming on. So you've reported on this, what do you think of it?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SHELLENBERGER, AUTHOR, "SAN-FRAN-SICKO": Well, Tucker, good to be with you. I mean, I think we all saw the Biden family struggling with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's addiction and fighting for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's life for many years. Any of us who have had friends or family suffering from drug addiction, we want to fight to rescue them so they can recover from addiction.\par \par \uc2 What the Biden administration is considering doing is legalizing what are effectively addiction maintenance sites in the middle of all of our major cities, not just San Francisco, they want to spread them across the country. Naturally, they end up getting put in poor Black neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 So you're looking at two different approaches to addiction. One is fighting for our loved ones to recover from addiction, and another is to basically give up and give in to addiction, to give in to the disease of addiction, which I mean, every reputable addiction expert says is the absolute wrong approach. We know that you need to make an intervention.\par \par \uc2 These drug sites, Tucker, attract drug dealers, violent drug dealers working for the Sinaloa Cartel and drug users around the sites. That's why San Francisco, we got it shut down after just six months because it was such a disaster on the street.\par \par \uc2 But the Biden administration is actively considering legalizing these modern opium dens as one recovering addict refers to them, and making them possibly opened up in cities across the country.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So this is not a new idea. There's no social science to suggest it works. This happened all across Europe 30 years ago in Zurich, most famously and they were shut down because it destroyed people. So on what basis are they doing this? What's the motive? I don't understand.\par \par \uc2 SHELLENBERGER: I mean, it is really crazy. I mean, you have to remember also, there was a very active effort in the United States to shut down cigarette smoking. We actually got rid of smoking areas. We don't allow smoking, and now they're opening up fentanyl and meth smoking areas. It's all victimhood ideology, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 It is the idea that people that are addicted to hard drugs are victims of the capitalist system and they should just be enabled in their disease to self-medicate. It's a really twisted philosophy, but that is what we're seeing playing out now at the Federal level.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: But Marlboros are immoral. And at the same time, these same people are telling us that it is "dangerous" to use a natural gas stove in your home because of emissions, but they're not concerned about smoking meth. Like at some point, you have to wonder like, what what's really going on here? I'm wondering that, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 SHELLENBERGER: I mean, you know, you have to consider that it is part of a broader movement that thinks that Western civilization is a terrible thing, that we're founded on slavery and genocide, and so maybe when they start to do things that undermine civilization, there's a connection there.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I think you're right over the target on that, you know, don't have proof. But what's the other explanation?\par \par \uc2 Michael Shellenberger, who again, has done more reporting on this than "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ," and so we're always grateful to have you on.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SHELLENBERGER: Good to be with you, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: More news straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We are out of time tonight. It's Wednesday. What's going to happen for the rest of the week? It's like hard even to imagine. More documents from Biden?\par \par \uc2 We are interviewing one of our favorite new guests, Simon Tebow, who covers the White House for a bunch of different African news organizations and has been banned from meeting with the White House Press Secretary and -- well, while we're not going to ascribe motive. He is going to join us tomorrow night and tell us about it.\par \par \uc2 And we'll be back then, 8:00 PM.\par \par \uc2 Have the best night with the ones you love. Politics is not the most important thing.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020230112ej1b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Work From Home Vanishing?; Interview With Rep. Mark Green (R-TN); Interview With Rep. \uc2 Vicente Gonzalez\uc2  (D-TX); President Biden Attends Leaders Summit in Mexico\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Jacqui Heinrich, Gerry Baker, Aishah Hasnie, Mark  Meredith, Griff Jenkins, Kat Timpf, Peter Doocy \par \uc2 10,431 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: You are looking live in Mexico City, where President Biden and his Mexican and Canadian counterparts, they're all set to hold a news conference this hour.\par \par \uc2 The three amigos, as they're told, are meeting south of the border, but just how much was discussed about the border and the surge of migrants and drugs flooding into this country?\par \par \uc2 We're all over it with Peter Doocy at the White House on what he's hearing, Griff Jenkins on the border on what he's seeing, Texas Democratic Congressman \uc2 Vicente Gonzalez\uc2  on what, if anything, the summit is accomplishing, and the new chairman of the House Homeland Security Committee, Mark Green on the border hearings that he is promising.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. Glad to have you. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Busy news day and maybe some progress at the border. We just don't know the details.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from Peter Doocy at the White House -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And, Neil, I noticed looking through the list of things that the leaders are trying to highlight, the list that they sent us, things that they want us to see, electric vehicle charging stations at the border is listed above anything about migration, but there is a part about a virtual platform.\par \par \uc2 And that says: "This will give potential migrants the information they need to come from Mexico, the United States and Canada lawfully, making them less likely to rely on smugglers who lie about border restrictions in place and put their lives at risk."\par \par \uc2 President Biden's border trip has been completely overshadowed today by allegations that President Biden mishandled classified documents and by these accusations from Republicans that the itinerary in Texas was lacking.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JAMES LANKFORD\uc2  (R-OK): What he did not do is, he didn't meet with the Border Patrol union. You're talking that most pro-union president out there refused to meet with the union members that could actually tell him what were the real problems are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Later on this hour, we do expect to hear from President Biden and Trudeau and AMLO of Mexico. If he's going to address the documents or the border trip today, that would be the time. So we are looking out for that - - Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, my friend very much, Peter Doocy, at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Griff Jenkins right now in Eagle Pass, Texas, and what they're calling the sort of ground zero of this border war is being waged as we speak -- Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 It is ground zero here in the Del Rio Sector, more than 150,000 migrant encounters in the last 100 days. But when you think about, Neil, what the officials on the ground here, the leaders, the Border Patrol chiefs want to hear from this summit, from Obrador and President Biden, it's going to be that they will stop doing things like this.\par \par \uc2 Mexico issues 30-day visas to people who have the explicit intention of coming to illegally cross our Southern border. If Mexico would simply stop people from crossing their Southern border, we wouldn't have this problem.\par \par \uc2 It's unclear that we're going to be headed for that. If you go up in the sky and look at our FOX flight team, our drone shot, you can look at this sector, which has never been more open, despite what the administration says.\par \par \uc2 And when you think about the other things that the leaders here want to see as a product of these meetings is that there will be policy changes coming to simply stop the catch-and-release en masse policies that they have been doing and allow Border Patrol agents to get out of the asylum business of processing and transporting migrants and into the business they used to be doing and what they were hired to do, which is enforcement along our Southern border.\par \par \uc2 Now, let me shift to some video we shot with our drone just a few hours ago. This is exclusive footage, when three GOP female senators, Senators Blackburn, Britt, and Hyde-Smith, came here where I am under this bridge and were able to observe an actual migrant crossing while it was happening, a group of Cubans with an elderly grandmother struggling just to get across the water.\par \par \uc2 The senators were able to speak with the migrants as well about their plight, and they had this reaction to the president's trip and whether that accomplished anything. Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KATIE BRITT (R-AL): It took him 718 days to get to the border. It took me six.\par \par \uc2 You have got to come and you have got to see it. And then you have to ask for the real unvarnished truth.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): The president didn't see what we're seeing. He is not seeing how there are lives that are wrecked because the cartels are promising them something they can never deliver.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And just to reiterate, to drive the point home a bit, Neil, the pipe dream that all of the leaders here along the Southwest border are hoping for is that something comes out of this meeting between Biden Obrador that will close off Mexico as a transit city for illegal immigration, because it's never been this bad.\par \par \uc2 I was holding up this temporary visa. It's good for 30 days. This is a Nicaraguan woman named Patricia. On January 2, she was stamped in on Mexico's Southern border in Tapachula. She made it 3,000 miles and crossed on Sunday and discarded it, because she doesn't want the Border Patrol officials to know she has permanent -- or temporary status in that country, because she wants to come here and be released.\par \par \uc2 That's the fundamental problem. And, hopefully, something comes out of the meeting south of this border that will help to get that under control -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Griff, this is a dumb question my part, but you're always at the border. So maybe you would know, that there is a role that Mexico will play in these discussions, we're told, where, if you're coming in from Nicaragua or Haiti, you're going to try to cross into the U.S. without proper authorization, Mexico will take them back.\par \par \uc2 In other words, you have to go back to Mexico. But Mexico has to presumably honor that. Is there any concern that they don't and won't?\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Well, the concern -- and, obviously, the border officials are relieved and encouraged that this temporary agreement to take back, under Title 42, by the way, which is the temporary health policy, which could go away in a few months' time, to take back these migrants from countries like Venezuela that they started to do back in October, now Nicaragua, Cuba and Haiti.\par \par \uc2 But they're only taking a certain number per day. I believe it's 2,000 at this port of entry, 30,000 total over the course of a month. We know much larger numbers are still coming. And our border officials are still left with such large numbers with no option other than to release them. So it's a temporary fix.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And if Mexico announced they would take even more, that would be good, but it's not one that's a long-term fix. And the long-term fix is what the officials really want to hear about.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Griff, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 So what does my next guest think of that long-term fix? He has a leading role in all this.\par \par \uc2 I'm talking about Congressman Mark Green, the Tennessee -- the House Homeland Security Committee chair.\par \par \uc2 Always good to have you, Congressman, and congratulations on your new role.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARK GREEN (R-TN): Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I did want to explore that, because, as far as I know -- and I'm getting dribs and drabs of what the administration is planning to announce with Mexico.\par \par \uc2 But key to that is, Mexico's apparently agreed to accept a total of 30,000 deportees from these four countries, Cuba, Nicaragua, Haiti, I Venezuela. And \uc2 Lopez Obrador\uc2  has said that Mexico could entertain accepting still more. Now, Mexico has made commitments, maybe not as grand as this, Congressman, in the past, and not honored them.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried that it might not here as well?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Well, thanks for having me on, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you that 30,000 is just not enough, when you have got 200,000 people hitting our Southern border eight of the 12 -- of the last 12 months; 30,000 is just not enough. And it's interesting, because the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . -- if you look at the refugee policies of the \uc2 U.N\uc2 ., it requires that the next contiguous country actually take care of those refugees.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: They're passing them off to us.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, so that's not enough. AMLO has to do more. Honestly, all of the countries of Central America have to do more. And we intend to ask them to do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It is an important point, the way I see it, Congressman, but you know far more, that we are now, under the guise of asylum, accepting more from these countries where that issue comes up, again, Cuba, Nicaragua, Haiti, Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 But do you worry that asylum is used as a means of just allowing more in here, period, whether their cases are valid or not?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Absolutely, it's being abused, the whole catch-and-release program.\par \par \uc2 And you can look at Biden's interim final rule, which he attempted to push down on Congress and on the operations of DHS and the DOJ.\par \par \uc2 And all it does is feed people into the country. Everything that he's doing, from his budget, which precludes ICE from deporting people, to increasing the processors at the border to get more people in faster, everything they're doing -- and this app, I can just see it right now, the coyote advertisement that says, hey, we will even fill out the app for you for an extra fee.\par \par \uc2 I mean, everything \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing is getting people into the country. That's what they want. And it's wrong. It's hurting America.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm also getting the impression -- again, you're closer to this than I am -- that not everyone's on board.\par \par \uc2 Progressives are saying the administration is actually being too cruel here. They have denounced the plan on human rights violations, and that it doesn't allow the fundamental right of people who want to seek refuge in the United States, for fear of either their lives or their safety.\par \par \uc2 That's a wide, wide definition. Now, to be fair, the Biden administration has not applied here. But they're under pressure to apply it. And Mexico is applying it. We're told Canada is applying it.\par \par \uc2 Where do you think this is going?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Well, in violation of that human rights section of the U.N. Charter, which I mentioned earlier...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: ... the progressives want a totally open border.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has actually given it to them. I mean, he's facilitating exactly what they want. Perhaps the dog-and-pony show of his trip is a great campaign ad for 2024, but it certainly is not doing anything to appease those in his -- those of his moderates and the Republicans, the rest of America, who recognizes that, hey, 100,000 dead Americans and all the other negative impacts of this open Southern border.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The president has also made clear, didn't so much when he was at the border on Sunday, Congressman, say that Republicans are the reason there's not enough help, financial help, coming to the border, but that he was going to -- quote -- "take care of that."\par \par \uc2 We know he's going to be providing resources to border officials. But do you know anything more about that, in terms of manpower, more money for maybe not a border fence or what have you, but just beefing up protections at the border? What does he mean by that?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: My guess -- and that's all I can do at this point, because the department -- or the administration has not reached out to the House majority to talk about this.\par \par \uc2 But my guess would be things like pay raises, extra Border Patrol people and potentially some sensors at the crossing sites for narcotics sensing, those things, I hear Democrats talk about all the time. They are needed, and there's a potential for some bipartisan work to get that done.\par \par \uc2 But, no, the administration has not reached out to the new chairman of Homeland Security.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You have reached out in terms you want to see them. He has said that the border is not open. You disagree. You have wanted to call him before your committee. Where does that stand?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: So, very clearly, the border is wide open, and he can say that it's secure. Mayorkas can say that it's secure.\par \par \uc2 But 3.5 million people coming across, if you add the folks who are allowed in and then the got-aways, 3.5 million -- 21 states in the nation have populations less than 3.3 million, so a moderate-sized state coming into the United States every year under this administration, it's a wide-open border.\par \par \uc2 So any assertions otherwise is crazy. And the cartels are the one controlling the border. They put mass waves of people at the human crossing sites. And CBP has to thin the line in the rural areas. That's where the fentanyl is pouring across. I have seen it.\par \par \uc2 So any assertion by him -- and, yes, we will get Mayorkas before the before the committee very soon.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Congressman, I'd be remiss if I didn't follow up a little bit on what the administration had run into when it -- when the president came to the border.\par \par \uc2 Much was said how everything was cleaned up. They disavow any notion that they had anything to do with that. El Paso officials say that that didn't happen under them. Someone cleaned that area up for the president, to the point he never saw migrants. And those on both sides of the aisle had said it probably would have been a good idea for him to see migrants. He never did.\par \par \uc2 But have you gotten to the bottom of how things looked so different and why he didn't see any migrants in an area that was flooded with them little more than 48 hours earlier?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Well, this is the whole reason why he hasn't gone to the border before.\par \par \uc2 And it's because, when the president and, of course, Vice President Harris, why they won't go is because the cameras go when they go. So this trip, they had to sterilize that crossing site. As I understand it, the police actually...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But who sterilized it? They say they didn't, sir.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that's why I tried to find out who did, because you're right. Someone changed something.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like us that the local law enforcement -- and this is what I'm hearing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: I don't have confirmation from that law enforcement agency -- arrested the homeless migrants that were there and moved them out of the out of the space where the president was coming.\par \par \uc2 So that's what I have heard.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Mr. Chairman, congratulations again on your assignment.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You have your work cut out for you. We look forward to chatting with you...\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Amen.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... in the weeks and months ahead.\par \par \uc2 Mark Green of Tennessee, the House Homeland Security Committee chair.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, big news out of \uc2 Disney\uc2 , having nothing to do with its stock or what it's buying or not buying or what it's streaming or not streaming. Demand on the part of \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2  coming in, and the boss, to tell everybody, all Mouseketeers, all corporate types, four days a week, you got to be in the office.\par \par \uc2 How's that going down?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, at \uc2 Disney\uc2 , they're trying to get rid of the whole remote work trend by demanding -- and this is \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 's orders, the guy who runs the place -- that by, March 1, all workers at the Magic Kingdom and elsewhere in all capacities better be in the office four days a week.\par \par \uc2 With us now is Kat Timpf, the co-host of "Gutfeld." We have got Samantha Ettus with us, workplace trends expert, and economist Steve Moore.\par \par \uc2 Kat, begin with you, end with you on this notion of, all right, it's over, you got to come back to work at least four out of five days, and soon, beginning March 1. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 KAT TIMPF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I do understand how it could be difficult for some people who have been working from home for years at this point to not want to go back.\par \par \uc2 But, eventually, ultimately, it's up to those people. When it comes to accompany with there's a lot of creative work being done, from experience, I know that there really is no substitute for meeting in person and being able to share ideas in that way for most people.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, Samantha, apparently, it's not up to those people. He's saying now, it's up to me, and I have had enough of this. I'm going too far here to make the point. It's four days or bust.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just wondering, because other companies have already gone this route. A lot of investment banks and all are saying that they want that to be the case, five days, in fact, in their case. What do you think of this trend back to back in the office?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA ETTUS, WORKPLACE TRENDS EXPERT: Well, \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2  is running a very different company than he was when he left in 2020, right?\par \par \uc2 He came back to an entirely new world. It's like the Wild West of the work force. And I think mandating that people go back four days a week is a little bit out of touch with all the changes that have happened in the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 Workers simply are not adjusting. They don't want to go back. They want flexibility. And they want control over their work lives. So the repercussions are going to be many when you have a CEO coming in and mandating something like that in such a severe way. There's a lot of middle ground, and he is not going there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, it's been a while now since the worst throes of COVID, Steve. So, you could say this is about time. It's not his way of saying it's five days or bust. It's four out of the five days. You can decide, I guess, what fifth day you want to do and how you want to do it.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: Well, Neil, don't tell anybody but I'm doing this interview from home, so I'm not in the office right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, if we could kill his mic, please, guys, that'd be fine.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead, Steve. I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MOORE: So, here's my attitude about this.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, I did my homework for this interview, Neil. I actually looked at some of the economic literature out there about what it's finding in terms of people working at home remotely vs. working in the office. And, quite frankly, the evidence is mixed, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Some of the studies show that people can be very productive working at home. And other studies show that it makes a big difference in a positive way when they get back in the office. So what do I conclude from that, Neil?\par \par \uc2 It's up -- it's the prerogative not of the employee, but of the employer. I mean, I like what I think it was \uc2 Facebook\uc2  said to their employees six months ago. We're not going to make you come back into the office. But if you don't come back from the office, we're not going to give you a paycheck. So it's that simple.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the employers should decide this. And they should take all factors into account. But, certainly, COVID is over. And there's no reason that, for health reasons, people shouldn't be showing up at the office.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I do like to what Kat had to say, though, that this is different in this kind of creative business, where they do need to bounce ideas off each other. Maybe that is the kind of distinction you draw with \uc2 Disney\uc2 . I just don't know.\par \par \uc2 But, guys, while I have you, there's another interesting story that caught my attention. And it concerns a Mumbai-based company called Dream11 which is now paying up to 100,000 rupees, or about $1,200, a penalty, if you call a co-worker who's on vacation and bug him about work issues. You have to pay $1,200, and there's all hell to pay.\par \par \uc2 Kat, the gist of this is, when people take a vacation, according to this 36-year-old CEO who runs a company, it's vacation, and it is not a time to be bugged by fellow employees at work or anyone else. What do you think of this policy?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Well, I would have made some money off of this.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: But, at the same time, I really think that you also, when you're on vacation, don't really have to answer or look at your e-mail. So I'm not sure why it would be necessary.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Samantha, what do think of that?\par \par \uc2 A lot of people who don't do that, then they feel the friction when they return. Why didn't you answer us? We had an emergency at work. Here's a guy who's saying no such emergency. And if even if there is one, we're not going to bug you. And I'm going to fine anyone who does.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 ETTUS: Yes, I mean, this is a ridiculous policy, frankly, because, if you're asking for flexibility on one hand, right, you want flexibility with when you go into work, how you work, then you can't also have it both ways and say, but don't ever talk to me when I'm not working, because the whole point is that the work force has completely changed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 ETTUS: And so if workers are demanding flexibility, they also have to be flexible enough to answer an e-mail on the weekends or on their vacations.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, another thing too, Steve, you could look at this as being maybe a sign of the times, with all these devices and gadgets, and everything we have. You just have to expect that that's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 What was interesting is a footnote on this company. He allows mandatory vacation of one week a year. I'm thinking to myself, OK, that's fine. That would be precious. But if you have a little bit more than that, maybe it's a little less onerous. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 ETTUS: Oh, sorry about that.\par \par \uc2 I was just going to say, we live in an era now where, I mean, now there's unlimited vacation policies. I mean, the whole world has changed. And so having one week of vacation is...\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Extreme.\par \par \uc2 ETTUS: That's extreme.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, but if it's one week -- and it's not. I don't know the full details here, but that is routine.\par \par \uc2 But I'm thinking, Steve, then he might have a point, right?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, I was going to say, I wish I had a dollar for every time you call me when I'm on vacation, Neil.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you never answered me, remember? You never answered.\par \par \uc2 You were on a beach, and just: It's Cavuto. Hang up.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, but this is reflective, though, of this kind of new attitude of young workers.\par \par \uc2 And I hear this all the time from employers, that they kind of these woke attitudes about they're doing their employer a favor for showing up to work. And that's one of the reasons, I think, that we're seeing an alarming decline in productivity of workers, that there's an attitude, like, where's the transgender bathrooms and all these other things that are not really related.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know. I think that pendulum is swinging back.\par \par \uc2 I don't think, in this environment...\par \par \uc2 MOORE: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Kat, if that's right, then a lot of workers aren't going to be able necessarily to call the shots anymore, or as many shots. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes, I think that if it's a great job, then there will be other people who would want that job.\par \par \uc2 There are options for remote work, but there's a lot of places where you got to go in. And there will be plenty of people who are also willing to do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. Well, you're in here either live or remote or coming from a cave somewhere like Steve.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: I'm here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But it works. Somehow, it works.\par \par \uc2 Guys, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 We called Mark Meredith earlier in El Paso, Texas, to give us an update what's happening there ahead of hearing from the leaders of the three countries that are going to decide our border. He answered the phone, answered the call, and he's there right now -- Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, no remote work for me today. You're right. It's good to be with you between the U.S. and Mexico border here.\par \par \uc2 There are eight senators down here this week getting a sense of what's going on, on the ground, because they want to pitch a comprehensive immigration reform package. Neil, we have been here before. Is this time going to be any different?\par \par \uc2 We're going to take a look at that part of the story coming out of the break.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. You're watching "Your World."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, they're called the three amigos, but at least two of the players, the president of the United States and the president of Mexico, haven't been saying consistently friendly things to each other.\par \par \uc2 What to make of that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, not too far away from hearing from the president of the United States and of Mexico and, of course, in Canada, as they try to iron out a policy, not only on climate matters, but on border issues as well.\par \par \uc2 It's being closely monitored in Texas, particularly in El Paso, Texas. That's where you will find our Mark Meredith.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Neil, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, congressional delegations, they come down to the border all the time. But, this week, it's something a little bit different. You have got eight senators, a bipartisan group that have come down here to see what's happening on the side of Mexico, see what's happening here in the U.S. and all across the Southern border.\par \par \uc2 Here's why it's also notable, because these same eight senators are likely going to be the same people that at some point later this year are going to introduce a comprehensive immigration reform package that would tackle not only border security, but also the asylum backlog.\par \par \uc2 We had a chance to be on the ground with these eight senators as they saw firsthand what these U.S. Border Patrol agents are dealing with on a daily basis, not only in the conditions that they're working in, but the type of migrants that they're encountering daily.\par \par \uc2 They also witnessed just last night Border Patrol interacting with two men who claimed to be Chinese nationals. These two men, they didn't even speak English. But when the Border Patrol was trying to communicate with them, the only thing they needed to do was to do Google Translate to try and tell them what to do next.\par \par \uc2 Texas Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  says this is just a perfect example of why federal agents need all the help they can get.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): People are going to continue to come. And we understand why, because people want to come to the United States, but we want them to come in an orderly, safe and humane and legal fashion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Now, Senator Cornyn tells FOX that he was glad that President Biden was down in El Paso over the weekend getting a brief tour of what's been happening on the ground, the president meeting with both law enforcement groups, as well as humanitarian groups who have been struggling to keep up with the large amount of migrants that have been coming through.\par \par \uc2 Where we are here in El Paso, the situation is a little bit different from where we were even just a month ago, the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2  saying that migrant crossings in El Paso are down like 70 percent within the last few weeks. But there's a lot of concern that this is a temporary dip and that, as obviously we get later in the year, that we will see another surge of migrants.\par \par \uc2 As for President Biden, he was putting the onus on Congress, saying that they want to make sure that Congress will create some more money available to the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  to handle these needs. But the senators are also saying, it can't just be money. There's got to be policy changes as well. And that's where the disagreement, as you know way too well, Neil, is going to be whether or not we actually see a breakthrough or not this year.\par \par \uc2 And that's yet to be seen -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. And that's going to be a leap.\par \par \uc2 Mark, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Mark in El Paso.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Congressman \uc2 Vicente Gonzalez\uc2 , the Texas Democratic congressman, kind enough to join us now.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, what do you want to see out of these three gentlemen talking and presumably offering some relief at the border? We just don't know the details of it. We know Mexico is willing to accept more of the migrants and even this asylum plan the president has, maybe to add to that down the road.\par \par \uc2 But we don't know much, much else. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 REP. VICENTE GONZALEZ (D-TX): Yes, I think that's good that they're sitting down and talking. Even Canada might be able to take some refugees.\par \par \uc2 But I think Mexico could do a lot on their Southern border and create a system where -- to cushion the blow of just having tens of thousands of people walk into our northern border. Mexico is in complete control of the transit of migrations coming through their -- through the country.\par \par \uc2 So, clearly, they're an important person at the table. They're an important place. And I think that there's a lot that can be done if they have a meaningful conversation, and there's the political will to do it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, I know, in the past, you have looked at Mexico maybe doing more, but I do get a sense that there's a building tension that is certainly between the Mexican president, who skipped out on a summit last year that the president orchestrated.\par \par \uc2 So there's been a little tension between them. But it really manifested itself in comments he made at the beginning of all of this, Congressman, where he said of the president of the United States: "End with this forgetfulness, this abandonment, this disdain toward Latin America and the Caribbean," going on to say right now, "You, President Biden, are uniquely poised to improve life across a region beset by inequality," telling him that "You hold the key in your hand."\par \par \uc2 Now, the president pointed out that the U.S. has under Democratic and Republican administrations provided tens of billions of dollars in relief to the region.\par \par \uc2 But I found it interesting that the Mexican president either doesn't remember that or doesn't think that there is enough of that.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, clearly, those type of comments aren't helpful. But I think, through the -- our diplomatic corps and theirs, we can have real talks that are not just political rhetoric. The president of Mexico, President AMLO, loves to have his morning mananitas and talk -- and just profess to the Mexican public of whatever his political agenda is.\par \par \uc2 And, many times, the rhetoric that comes out of these morning speeches are contrary to the benefits of the country. And I think, hopefully, they are able to have some private conversations and come up with real proposals.\par \par \uc2 And I was -- I flew with the president to El Paso.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: We -- he got to see the border in person. We got to -- I got to discuss my Safe Zones proposal, which he was interested in and Secretary Mayorkas really was interested in.\par \par \uc2 And I'm -- and I'm actually -- I just got off the floor talking to my Republican colleagues across the aisle about it. We need to have new ideas. And, in fact, the laundry list of new ideas that are being proposed by the administration, one of them being that you either ask for asylum on your app, on your phone, or you do it in a safe country where you transit, I think that is a great idea that will take a lot of pressure off our Southern border.\par \par \uc2 I have migrants that show up...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, who puts the asylum requests as an app on the phone or responds to it on the phone?\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: The State Department would do that.\par \par \uc2 And...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I see. All right.\par \par \uc2 Let me go back to one other thing you said, Congressman. And you were among the few who had the opportunity to be with the president. In fact, you were right next to him. And a lot of people have been raising, why was El Paso apparently so cleaned up? I mean, any sign of migrants, there aren't any. It's like they disappeared.\par \par \uc2 And there had been talk that maybe Biden folks had cleared the streets or, at others' behest, had done so. They deny that.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Yes. That's preposterous.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I'm still looking at this before and after, Congressman, and confused as heck. Something and someone did that cleanup there. I mean, just the day before, there were migrants all over the place.\par \par \uc2 What happened?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Let me tell you, what happens on the border, people come in spurts.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: They don't -- it's not a consistent flow of migrants. I -- being on the border, I have seen days that we're just overwhelmed, and then I have had empty days.\par \par \uc2 I took a bipartisan group of members of Congress down a couple of years ago, and I took them to the river where I normally saw a regular flow of migrants. I wanted them to see it. And it was almost empty. There was no crossings that day.\par \par \uc2 And, later, I found out that it was because the cartels were celebrating a quinceanera across the border, and they said, today, we're not going to work and we're not going to cross anybody.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, don't you find it odd on the very day, Congressman -- I'm not blaming you. I'm just curious.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: I don't.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That on the very day the president of United States is coming, he's looking at this whole area, getting an assessment of it, trying to get a sense of what these border officials of dealing with, and migrants -- it was like a science fiction movie. They were nowhere to be found.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 No, they were there. They don't just disappear. They were there. They were at respite centers. They were...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Where? Where were they? Why didn't the president see them? I didn't see them.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, I'm going by cameras and aerial shots. They weren't there.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: The president went to the border. He went to the processing center. He met with stakeholders, with the county judge, with the mayor. He met with Border Patrol. He met with Customs and Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But he didn't meet with migrants. He didn't meet with migrants.\par \par \uc2 That's the source of the issue. And I'm just curious why.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Yes, but he met with the people who deal with the migrants that are coming across. He went to the locations where they cross._ CAVUTO: Wouldn't you want to see the migrants? Wouldn't you see what's behind this 2.5 million spurt of people across the people and hundreds of thousands more?\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: He knows the migrants look like. We talk to him regularly. Secretary Mayorkas is down there regularly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Of course he does. But the point of this trip, sir, was to see them for himself.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: The migrants themselves?\par \par \uc2 That, I don't think, is going to accomplish our goal. Our goal is to resolve the crossings. And that -- for that, we need to talk to people who have policy ideas, and that's the stakeholders, talk to Border Patrol, talk to our customs agents, talk to the mayor and the county judge.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And he did that. And touche. He did that. But I just find it odd - - and I understand what you're saying and defending the president. I get that.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Having just a warm body isn't going to...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The fact of the matter is, a lot of people said, you have to see for yourself, Mr. President, what we're dealing with here.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: And he did.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He goes down to the border, and he doesn't see the migrants that they're dealing with there.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Yes, he -- but he did see where they cross, the process that they go through, the respite center where they stay. He spoke to the churches. He spoke to the local governments.\par \par \uc2 He spoke to the important stakeholders that know what the problem is and also know how to fix it. So he did his job. And he did a lot.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I have no doubt. I'm not saying that he deliberately said, I'm not going to meet with migrants.\par \par \uc2 You said he spoke to everyone who deals with the migrants and what they have to do on a daily basis, hourly basis....\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: That's right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... to deal with this surge. And the president of the United States, who comes down there, doesn't see them. He sees all the people who deal with them.\par \par \uc2 I guess I'm still chafing at all this time...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wait a minute, sir. He went down there and didn't see them.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that was the whole point. It's like going to a Giants game, if you like the Giants, and the Giants aren't there, and you're in the stadium.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Yes, but this isn't a Giants game. This isn't a Giants game.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This is a migrant game with 2.5 million getting through and hundreds thousands more, right? You want to see them, right?\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: And he went to the place where they're getting through. He went to the place where they're getting through.\par \par \uc2 He talked to the agents that are processing them. He talked to the city, the churches, the local officials who are dealing with the issue. He went right to the location where the crossings are happening.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Congressman, you have seen the migrants. You have seen them again and again. You have seen the problem.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Well, I live there. I live there. It's very different. But I have brought members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You live there. You know it. Wouldn't it be in your interests to tell the president, while you're walking around with him, where the hell are the migrants?\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: I have brought members of Congress down on days where the migrants aren't there. And it's just the way it is.\par \par \uc2 It's ups and downs. We have surges that lasts for a week or a month or a day sometimes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But members of Congress are very different than the most powerful man on the planet, right?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You want him to see it, right?\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: That's one way to look at it.\par \par \uc2 All I know is that, when President Trump was coming down to my district, he never once went to visit migrants. I don't know why nobody was critical on him for that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't care whether it's President Trump or President Biden.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: But Biden is there.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I just find it odd. Do you think that it was an opportunity missed?\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: I find it odd that nobody talks about.\par \par \uc2 No, he didn't miss an opportunity. He spoke to us. He spoke to the Catholic Church. He spoke to the respite centers. He spoke to the Customs and Border Patrol. He spoke to the people who are dealing with the urgency of the migration coming across that border?\par \par \uc2 And, that day, and those few days, the surge was down. But in a week, it could be up or it could be down. This is fluid. And living on the border, I know, from one day to another, things change. We ship out 1,000 to 2,000 people a day from a surge location like El Paso.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: So, in one or two or three days, things change.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, to your credit, Congressman, to your credit, you have been saying that Mexico has to do something about its southern border.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you went to that southern border and looked at Guatemala and what it's doing or not doing to help out at that border, you would probably want to see migrants from Guatemala or any of these other countries, right?\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Well, I would want to know where they're crossing, who's processing them, how they're moving.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you would want to see them and how they're crossing, right? You would want to see proof of it, right?\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Well, he did see that. He saw where they're crossing. You don't have to actually see a human that's coming across the river.\par \par \uc2 You want to know where they're coming.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It would help. It would help, because there have been millions of them. It would help to see, well, how the heck are they doing it? Yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: But they're just not there every day.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Unfortunately, that's just not the situation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, sir, thank you very, very much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Vicente Gonzalez\uc2  of Texas.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we are going to hear how the administration plans to deal with this with its colleagues from Mexico and Canada. And we could get a good idea of that very, very shortly and how Republicans are responding.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie on that -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, House Republicans are setting the tone right now for this new Congress. They might take some advice from you on how to interview, as they are in for some pretty big battles ahead. More on that straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, moments away from hearing from the president of the United States. Of course, we're also going to have \uc2 Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador\uc2  of Mexico, Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  of Canada.\par \par \uc2 This is the 10th North American Leaders' Summit. The border and what to do about it was a big part of this summit, but not the only part of it. They're also talking about climate issues and how to improve their economies between the country and the countries.\par \par \uc2 As you know, the Mexican president was quite critical of President Biden just a couple of days ago, saying that the United States and, more to the point, his administration has not done enough to help out, and quoting here, "You hold the key, Mr. President," to which \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  responded, we have given you tens of billions of dollars. That's a pretty pricey key. I added the pricey key stuff to make that alliteration make sense.\par \par \uc2 So we will take it there when we get more information and the leaders at those microphones.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie, meanwhile, on Capitol Hill with some actions on the part of the new Republican Congress.\par \par \uc2 I guess they're making good, Aishah, on some of the promises they made, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Well, I can tell you this.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting, Neil, that the Senate is still out. And yet House Republicans have somehow managed to really irritate and upset a lot of Senate Democrats out there by the priorities that they are rolling out. A lot of battles are brewing right now between House Republicans and Senate Democrats, not to mention House Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So, in fact, right now, take a look at the House floor. Republicans are launching two new investigatory committees to begin oversight. Democrats are not happy about at least one that's looking into the weaponization of government agencies.\par \par \uc2 Also, Neil, outrage over bills that Republicans are rolling out aimed at spending. Last night, in fact, Republicans passed the Family and Small Business Taxpayer Protection Act. This is the \uc2 IRS\uc2  bill it aims to rescind $71 billion that was sent to the \uc2 IRS\uc2  to hire those 87,000 new employees.\par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats have already said they're going to block this one. And they're in pointing to a CBO analysis that shows the legislation would actually reduce revenue by $185 billion and instead add $114 billion to the federal deficit over a 10-year period.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHARLES SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): It's absolutely absurd. What the Republicans are doing, plain and simple, is helping their friends, the multimillionaires, the wealthy corporations who pay no taxes, from being audited. We're not letting it happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: House Republicans say, you know what, it goes both ways and they can kill any Senate bills, even those that are supported by Senate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KAT CAMMACK (R-FL): It goes both ways. They can't just ram a bunch of things down our throat, just like we can't to them. So there's going to have to be some give and take here. It's time to fight back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And, of course, we're talking about a budget battle that is also looming, Neil.\par \par \uc2 You know, as much as I do, that Democrats are warning of an inevitable shutdown in this GOP-led House because some of those concessions that Speaker McCarthy may to limit spending -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I did want to ask about that, Aishah, because tied to the -- that spending issue, they have married it to the debt limit issue.\par \par \uc2 So, in other words, what they seem to be saying is spending will be a lever and a shutdown will be a potential remedy. They're pretty -- they're pretty clear about that. I imagine that's not going down well with everybody.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: It's not. They have -- there have always been a small group of a conservative Republicans, even in the Senate, that have been open to a government shutdown, I believe even now.\par \par \uc2 You had Congresswoman Anna Paulina Luna on your show earlier on FOX Business, who said that she was open, willing to take on a government shutdown, as long as they could get spending under control.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: That is the goal. They feel like this is why they were elected, and they're willing to go along with that, if that's what's going to accomplish it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm glad you quoted FOX Business, because, if you don't get it, you should demand it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Fortunately, you work here so you get it for free.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Good stuff on there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But thank you very much. Great job, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: You got it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Aishah Hasnie following all of that.\par \par \uc2 Gerry Baker, star of FOX Business as well, on what he makes of what Aishah said, because so much I want to talk to you, Gerry, including what's going on in Mexico City right now.\par \par \uc2 But I did want to get your take initially on this Republican push to deal with the debt ceiling in a way that almost, to hear critics tell it, will foster a shutdown. Now, many have talked to me, I'm sure talked to you that is a legitimate bargaining chip, and we will get what we want and we will control spending, because we're ready to use it.\par \par \uc2 They don't think they ever will have to use it, but they're ready to use it. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 GERRY BAKER, HOST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL AT LARGE": Yes, well, it hasn't worked in the past, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We have recalled many occasions, several occasions, under President \uc2 Obama\uc2  when House Republicans kept this threat of not raising the debt limits as a way to reduce spending. In the end, they got the worst of all possible worlds. They didn't reduce the spending. And they got the blame. And they got the blame, the blame for bringing the government to the precipice of a default.\par \par \uc2 So, maybe they have got better tactics this time, who knows. Maybe the circumstances will be different. We are away -- we're some ways away from the debt limit raise. It's going to be several months away yet later this year. So maybe things will change.\par \par \uc2 But the politics of this have not worked out very well for Republicans in the past. And here's the larger point, I think, Neil. As you said, Aishah there, very interestingly, talking about this vote to essentially defund the additional $87 billion that the Congress passed last year for these additional 87,000 IRS employees to increase tax audits.\par \par \uc2 We know -- I mean, I think there's lots of people who will support that. And I think many people will think that's a very positive step, myself included. I don't think there's a lot of enthusiasm for a huge increase in the scale of the \uc2 IRS\uc2  in this country. And we know anyway that the money that they're going after, despite what \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said, is not going to come primarily from multimillionaires and billionaires, because there just isn't enough money to go around.\par \par \uc2 So it's a good measure in itself. Of course, the problem is, it won't become law, because the Senate majority, the Senate -- the Democrats have a majority of the Senate, and they will stop it from becoming law. And this is what worries me a little bit about this Republican-controlled Congress, Neil, is that they're going to be able to do very little in terms of practical positive measures that will actually help the economy, because, unfortunately, most of everything -- pretty well everything they propose will be blocked by the Democratic Senate or indeed by the veto by the president.\par \par \uc2 But they will be able to do potentially damage by doing things like opposing an increase in the debt limit and risking all of the financial problems and chaos that result from that. So they're in an interesting position. They can make statements as they can like on the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . They can do many of these kinds of measures that will have no practical impact, but will send signals to the country about what they stand for.\par \par \uc2 But they are in a position where they can really have an effect is potentially in a negative one, which is either in stopping government funding, precipitating in a government shutdown or refusing to raise the debt limit.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: And I think both of those have real political risks for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I just want to caution and add for our viewers, Gerry, of course, you are a huge fan of the \uc2 IRS\uc2  and you're not pointing the finger at them in any way, shape, or form when it comes to audits and all the rest.\par \par \uc2 No, I'm just trying to -- throwing that out there, Gerry.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: That's a pretty free -- that's a pretty free hit politically.\par \par \uc2 I don't think I'm going to get -- I think it's very important.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I hear you.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: No, but I think it's very important to make that point that -- no, I think it's very important to make that point that the Democrats claim that these \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents will be going after -- going after billionaires and millionaires, but we know that, first of all, billionaires and multimillionaires are very efficient at arranging their taxes in a way that ensures that they don't pay taxes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They have a SWAT team of accountants. Yes, you go where the money is. You go where the money is. And it's not with those -- right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes, that additional revenue -- exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: That additional revenue is going to come -- that additional revenue is going to come from small business owners and people who make a reasonable amount money, but are by no means wealthy.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 Gerry's opinions of the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , again, are Gerry's opinions of the -- I'm kidding. I'm just pushing that way beyond it's necessary.\par \par \uc2 Gerry, thank you very much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Meanwhile, I want to take you back to Mexico City right now.\par \par \uc2 We're moments away from hearing from the president of the United States, his Mexican counterpart, the prime minister of Canada.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui, what can we expect to hear?\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, for all of the big issues that this summit was supposed to tackle, it has been so far pretty light on solutions.\par \par \uc2 And they spent a lot of time, we're told, discussing the flow of migrants across the border, also the flow of deadly fentanyl across the U.S. border.\par \par \uc2 But the fact sheet that the White House passed out ahead of this summit that included deliverables really didn't have a lot of concrete plans, no major initiatives to tackle either of those problems. There were a lot of promises made on other issues, like climate and conservation, cutting methane emissions and food waste, improving supply chains, and also semiconductors.\par \par \uc2 But the biggest piece of the migration plan was an app that will make information available to migrants for legal pathways and also the creation of a new brick-and-mortar facility in Southern Mexico, an area a lot of migrants pass through where they can get those -- that information and those resources.\par \par \uc2 They're also going to counter xenophobia and produce balanced narratives, promote balanced narratives about migrants and refugees to ensure that they're better integrated with communities. But that's really not what the president's critics have been asking for.\par \par \uc2 They were talking about things like sending military resources to tackle cartels, designating cartels as foreign terrorist organizations. This is a far cry from that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Even the issue of drugs and stemming the flow of fentanyl, the only real plan described in this fact sheet was efforts to share information about the creation of fentanyl and the chemicals that go into it and also announcing a trilateral public health summit.\par \par \uc2 So it's not yet clear if President Biden has asked for anything specific from either of his North 