. So the President Biden obviously wants to try to sort of repair this, or at least that's what it looks like. And he came to Mexico for this summit. And he invited AMLO to the Oval Office to try to hash things out after his absence at North -- at the Summit of the Americas.\par \par \uc2 So it seems like that it's a bit of a salty relationship right now. And that might be why we didn't get a whole lot on that deliverable fact sheet as of yet. Now, they're still in the meeting, which is why we are late and waiting for this press conference.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But in the way of major new initiatives to tackle these big issues that have been vexing this administration, the flow of migrants across the border and the flow of deadly fentanyl across the border, not a whole lot that President Biden as of right now can come home with.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And we only have less than a minute left. So I will see these leaders pretty soon, but it sounds to me like Mexico's made a promise to do a little bit more to deal with these asylum cases, take more in, but they haven't always kept their word in the past, right?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: They haven't.\par \par \uc2 Also, the big issue that we have been focusing a lot on is, why was there no mention of the word cartels? Who controls the border, truly? Alejandro Mayorkas said just a couple of days ago on his way here that the vast majority of migrants who cross the U.S. border illegally are using smuggling rings. They are going through smuggling rings.\par \par \uc2 And that's a serious problem. You saw it play out when Mexican authorities arrested El Chapo's son just the other day in what's been described as a gift to Biden.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: It took 3,500 Mexican soldiers to take that guy down.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Good point.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So that just speaks to the strength that they have here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jacqui, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Back to you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We're still waiting to hear from the leaders.\par \par \uc2 Again, that big commitment on the part of Mexico to accept up to 30,000 deportees from a number of key countries where asylum comes up, and maybe to lift that beyond that, but the specifics are few, the hope many.\par \par \uc2 Here's "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230111ej1a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Mexico Asks Biden For Funds To Secure Border; Lawmakers Call On Biden To Take...\b0\par\par\uc2 Trace Gallagher, Jacqui Heinrich, Griff Jenkins, Aishah Hasnie,  Brit Hume, Molly Line, Alexis McAdams, Jonathan Hunt, Lydia Hu, Gillian  Turner, Jennifer Griffin, Amy Kellogg \par \uc2 7,724 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Mexico Asks Biden For Funds To Secure Border; Lawmakers Call On Biden To Take Action Against Cartels; Locals React To Belated Presidential Appearance; Migrants, Drugs, And Assaults Stoke Frustration; House Republicans Plan New Subcommittee; Justice Department Reviews Documents From Biden Center; Proposed Cuts Spark Internal Republican Strife; Damar Hamlin Released From Cincinnati Hospital; Husband Brian Walshe Charged With Misleading Police Investigation; University Of Idaho Students Returns To Campus After Grisly Slayings; Thousands Of Nurses At Two New York City Hospitals Go On Strike; \uc2 FDA\uc2  Approves New Medication To Treat Alzheimer's Disease; Congressional Commission Announces Recommendations For Renaming Army Bases Named For Some Who Voluntarily Served Confederate Army; Supporters Of Former Brazilian President \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  Violently Overrun Government Buildings In Brazil's Capital; Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  Assigns U.S.\par \par \uc2  Attorney To Review Potential Classified Documents Found At Penn Biden Center For Diplomacy; President Biden Visits U.S. Southern Border\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: And I wrote the forward. So --\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Oh, very good. Very good. There you go.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I guess I have time to go. Percy has a new skill. Check this out. He loves --\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Oh.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Wow.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Two ball in the mouth.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Three. That's a big one.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Dana, you can't do this to me.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And with no time left.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Balls, balls, balls.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Dana, it's you.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I know. I can't talk. Trace -- hi, Trace!\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: That's it for us.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Dana, great to see you. Greg, we apologize for Friday night. Thank you everyone. And good evening.\par \par \uc2 I'm Trace Gallagher, in for Baier, "BREAKING TONIGHT", President Biden is in Mexico City right now. He is about to meet with his Mexican counterpart at the National Palace. We'll bring you there in moments.\par \par \uc2 This comes after President Biden's first trip to the U.S. southern border, and the capture of a major drug kingpin by Mexican authorities.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich is in Mexico City tonight. Jacqui, good evening.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Trace.\par \par \uc2 Looming large over this summit is the role of cartels pushing migrants and drugs into the U.S. And the question of whether Mexico truly controls its own border. Biden's DHS Secretary admits the vast majority of migrants who cross into the U.S. illegally go through smuggling rings.\par \par \uc2 And Mexico tried to show strength with that high profile arrest. It's been described as a gift to Biden and a message that Mexico's approach to cartels called hugs not bullets, prevents bloodshed.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Tonight, President Biden receives a dignified reception in Mexico City for the summit with his North American counterparts to talk immigration and drug trafficking.\par \par \uc2 But just days before --\par \par \uc2 It took 3,586 Mexican soldiers descending on Sinaloa to capture drug kingpin Ovidio Guzman, a son of El Chapo. 30 people were killed, including 10 Soldiers. Dozens more injured.\par \par \uc2 Cartels forced down two Air Force planes and shot up a commercial airliner. And authorities confiscated 26 Barrett rifles, six machine guns, 26 long arms, nearly 600 magazines, bulletproof vests, and dozens of armored cartel vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Mexico says they're not trying to win a war, asking Biden to send help in the way of dollars, south of their border to address root causes of migration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARCELO EBRARD, SECRETARY OF FOREIGN AFFAIRS, MEXICO: If you want to reduce the migratory flow, there should be investment. We recognize that it is a different policy. For obvious reasons, to what the right is proposing.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Republicans in Congress urging Biden to send in U.S. military assets.\par \par \uc2 And I want to be clear, I'm not talking about U.S. troops, but I'm talking about cyber, drones, intelligence assets, naval assets, because these are paramilitary entity.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Other critics say Biden's not yet used other tools.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): If the president does designate them as a foreign terrorist organization, it empowers the United States to take certain actions against them that would begin to erode the power of the cartels.\par \par \uc2 Again, no new law is needed here.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But U.S. officials have signaled no such plans ahead. Instead, monitoring the rollout of Biden's new work visa program for migrants from Nicaragua, Venezuela, Cuba, and Haiti.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: By the end of this summit, we're not going to have a new agreement as a deliverable here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): A U.S. officials say there will be a robust conversation about how to stem the flow of deadly fentanyl across the U.S. border. But any efforts to get Mexico to take in more deported migrants will be complicated by ongoing trade disputes. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Jacqui Heinrich, live for us in Mexico City. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, many people who live along the border are not happy that President Biden spent so little time there yesterday and failed to see or talk to any migrants.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkins has that part of the story tonight from Eagle Pass, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Too little too late.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Del Rio rancher Laura Allen frustrated that it took President Biden nearly two years to visit the border\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LAURA ALLEN, RANCHER, DEL RIO, TEXAS: We're the voice trying to warn the rest of our country. We can't hold the line. Your government is not holding the line.\par \par \uc2 MAGALI URBINA, FARMER, EAGLE PASS, TEXAS: I don't think it's OK.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Eagle Pass pecan farmer Magali Urbina says Biden didn't even see the real crisis landowners like her face every day.\par \par \uc2 URBINA: He went to El Paso, everything's picked up, everything is clean, everybody is kind of like on their best behavior.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: The president spent just over three hours on the ground in El Paso, at the port of entry, walking the wall with Border Patrol officials and touring a migrant center, but witnessed no migrants firsthand.\par \par \uc2 Despite CBP sources, exclusively telling Fox News, there were 518 migrant encounters in the El Paso sector yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Hours after Biden landed in Mexico, a group of 164 migrants crossed in the Eagle Pass before dawn. Fox News also witnessed a group of migrants from the Dominican Republic, scaling a makeshift wall of shipping containers in razor wire.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): I want to thank President Biden for giving us this opportunity. We come from a country that's really poor and we're looking for work.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: This, as Border Patrol reports over the weekend, nearly 7,000 encounters across the entire Southwest border, the seizure of more than $118,000 worth of narcotics and the assault of three agents.\par \par \uc2 URBINA: I hope somebody does something. Whether it's the president or lawmakers, somebody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): And Trace, as migrants are being loaded in that transport van behind me right now here in Eagle Pass, over in El Paso, a bipartisan group of the U.S. senators, including Republican \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2 , Democrat Mark Kelly, and independent \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  are meeting with border officials and local leaders to discuss the migrant crisis. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Griff Jenkins, live along the border. Grift, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of live meeting, to take you back now to Mexico City. That is a live look at the room at the National Palace in Mexico City where President Biden is about to meet with the Mexican president, President \uc2 Manuel Lopez Obrador\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind, there is several things on the table to discuss, no pun intended there, and also, we will expect a dinner where the Canadian Prime Minister will also join.\par \par \uc2 This is the first time that a U.S. president has visited Mexico City since 2014. We'll bring you back there live when the event begins.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the House is back in session tonight. The chamber reconvened about an hour ago after a long and difficult week during which Republican Kevin McCarthy was finally elected Speaker just after midnight Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 Now comes the really hard part. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie is on Capitol Hill tonight.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): House Speaker Kevin McCarthy heading into his first big vote tonight with some Republicans still steaming over concessions he made to get the gavel.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): The rumors of potential backroom deals that were cut for the Speaker vote with a select few people that didn't want those to leak out, a faction of those 20. That's not OK.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: South Carolina Representative Nancy Mace, who was on the fence this weekend about voting for the rules package now tells Fox, she's leaning towards it after voicing her concerns.\par \par \uc2 But Texas Congressman Tony Gonzalez is still a hard no.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): The defense spending -- the cut in defense --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GONZALES: I think that's -- terrible idea.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Some worry, an agreement to cap discretionary spending at fiscal year 2022 levels in order to balance the budget will bleed into defense funding.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): We've got to get spending under control, but we are not going to do it on the backs of our troops and our military.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But Congressman Chip Roy, one of the hardliners who eventually voted for McCarthy tweeted Sunday, "Cuts to defense were never discussed", as part of any negotiations.\par \par \uc2 What Republicans are uniting behind is a new subcommittee to investigate the weaponization of federal agencies like the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and their relationships with social media sites like \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A source familiar with the panel tells Fox, Republicans will demand any e- mails or memos a White House may have sent to big tech.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): We're going do it vigorously. We're going to do it aggressively, because that's our job.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And Trace, tonight's rules vote will also be the first big task for GOP whip \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2 , who received some criticism last week over that ordeal over the speakership bid.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, he told reporters he doesn't believe this rules package is controversial, and he thinks it will pass tonight. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Aishah Hasnie, live for us on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today. The Dow lost 113, the S&P 500 fell three, and the NASDAQ gained 66.\par \par \uc2 Well, "BREAKING TONIGHT", the Justice Department is now reviewing potentially classified documents found at the Biden Center. This is from his time as the vice president.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press is reporting the information comes from the White House. And, of course, the Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is now saying, according to CBS News that they will be looking into this.\par \par \uc2 Again, this is the Penn Biden Center for Diplomacy. We know, of course, the brouhaha that came of the documents -- declassified documents found in Mar- a-Lago, accusing President Trump of bringing those documents in.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have potential classified documents being found at the Penn Biden Center for Diplomacy, dating back to when President Biden was the vice president.\par \par \uc2 We'll continue our coverage of breaking news as we get more information.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News chief political analyst Brit Hume. Brit, just kind of your take on the breaking news here, where potentially classified documents from former Vice President Biden now found at the Penn Biden Center for Diplomacy. You're take?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST (on camera): Well, we need to know whether they were simply potentially classified or actually classified. And if they were, indeed, classified documents found there, what kind of classified documents they were, how many there were, and all the rest of it.\par \par \uc2 So, it's early days on this. We'll have to see where it goes. Obviously, it would be embarrassing for this president, after the tremendous to do that was made, as you pointed out over the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's apparently holding classified documents down here in Florida, at Mar-a-Lago, to have -- to have done something similar himself. So, we'll see if that's what it ends up being.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Meantime, back to the news today, House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  said this about House dysfunction, Brit, and I'll get your response on the other side,\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): General concern is that the dysfunction that was historic that we saw this week is not at an end, it's just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Is it a fair point, considering what happened last week, Brit?\par \par \uc2 HUME: I think it's a -- it's something that many Republicans are worried about, too. They're worried that too much power to influence the majority has been -- has been to influence the Speaker, in particular, has been turned over to what is, after all about a 10 percent share of the Republican caucus.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's, you know, as it seems a valid concern, they found themselves in the parliamentary situation that you sometimes find in a democracy, where a very small number has controlling power in a particular moment. And that was exercise last week and up until the end of the week, and that vote and concessions were extracted from McCarthy, that do not strengthen his hand. So we'll see. But it's a legitimate concern.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And it's interesting to me, Brit, because I keep going back to, you know, the Reagan Defense Forum we had back in December. And we talked about the budget, and there's been a lot of talk about, military cuts there.\par \par \uc2 And my two big takeaways were the fact that military recruitment is way down, and military readiness is also down.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal, if we can put this on the screen said the following about this. And we're quoting here. "The pressure for defense cuts will be great. That's a terrible signal to send adversaries who are increasingly belligerent," and the adversaries are increasingly belligerent to be sure, Bret.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, I think that's, that's obviously a valid concern. And the reason, I think is this, that this is often forgotten in these debates over domestic spending, versus national security spending, which is this. There are all kinds of actors, state and local governments, private businesses, and so on, who affect our domestic situation here, the economy in particular.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to national defense and foreign policy, the federal government is the agency and really the only agency that has anything to say about that, and it is, arguably, its primary responsibility.\par \par \uc2 So, when you talk about, well, we're going to cut from here, we're going to cut from there equally, you have to be concerned that the -- that the federal government's principal responsibility may get hurt in the process.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's what a lot of Republicans and some others as well are worried about, as a consequence of this agreement in which it is said that they're going to hold spending at current levels you do that?\par \par \uc2 And inevitably, because of inflation and increasing needs, defense ends up getting cut.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Brit Hume, good insight. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 HUME: You bet.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Up next, to major development in the case of the \uc2 NFL\uc2  player who collapsed on the field last Monday night.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox Two in San Francisco where relentless rain causes flooding, mudslides, and strong winds for two weeks in the Bay Area.\par \par \uc2 13,000 Sonoma County residents are under evacuation warnings for severe flooding. Forecasters expect rain to continue throughout most of the week.\par \par \uc2 And this, a live look at Fort Worth, Texas from FOX4 and Atlanta from FOX5. The big story in both cities tonight, the Texas Christian University Horned Frogs prepare to take on the Georgia Bulldogs in hopes of winning their first national championship football game in 84 years.\par \par \uc2 Georgia hoping to defend its national championship title, kickoff 7:45 p.m. Eastern at SoFi Stadium right here in Southern California.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT, and we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF BREAKING NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Breaking tonight, just a week after suffering a cardiac arrest on the playing field, \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  safety Damar Hamlin has been released from Cincinnati hospital. Hamlin will continue his recovery in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexis McAdams is in Orchard Park, New York tonight. Alexis, good evening.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Trace, good evening. That's right. Tonight, Damar Hamlin is back home here in Buffalo recovering at a local hospital. This comes just seven days after he was on -- went into cardiac arrest rather and also was on a breathing tube.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this tweet. It was in just this afternoon. You can see the 24-year-old \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  safety tweeting out that the news saying I'm grateful for the awesome care I received at UCMC. I'm happy to be back in Buffalo. The docs and nurses at Buffalo General have already made me feel at home.\par \par \uc2 Doctors in Cincinnati, Trace, says Hamlin was often walking around, even watching Sunday's game including this play when Nyheim Hines caught that opening kickoff running 96 yards for that touchdown to give the Bills an early lead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. TIMOTHY PRITTS, SURGEON, UNIVERSITY OF CINCINNATI: We've learned this week that the Bills mafia is a very real thing. And we all feel the love from you all in Western New York. But you know, he watched the game on yesterday when the opening kickoff was run back, he jumped up and down and got out of his chair. Set I think every alarm off in the ICU in the process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Yes, an amazing recovery. According to the hospital here in Buffalo, Trace, Hamlin is listed in stable condition and continues to make significant progress rather. Back out here live, I can tell you was absolutely electric inside of that stadium yesterday. This is an emotional time for the fans and Hamlin will have a long road to recovery and will still undergo some tests, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And even though he went to Pitt University, you can bet that he's going to be watching the national championship game tonight. Alexis McAdams live for us to the Buffalo area. Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There has been an arrest in the case of the Massachusetts woman missing since last week. Her husband is in jail tonight.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Molly Line is in Quincy, Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOLLY LINE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): With a grin on his face, the husband of missing Massachusetts mom Ana Walshe heading to court today, charged with misleading a police investigation.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors told the judge investigators found blood in the couple's Cohasset home and that despite being on house arrest for a prior crime, Brian Walshe also made an unapproved trip to a \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  on January 2nd, the day after the mother of three vanished wearing a mask and surgical gloves. He paid cash.\par \par \uc2 LYNN BELAND, ASSISTANT DISTRICT ATTORNEY, NORFOLK COUNTY, MASSACHUSETTS: He's on surveillance at that time, purchasing about $450 worth of cleaning supplies that will include mops, bucket, tops, Tdex (PH), drop cloths, as well as various kinds of tape.\par \par \uc2 LINE: Over the weekend, as police scoured the property, they found a blood stained weapon.\par \par \uc2 BELAND: Blood was found in the basement area, as well as a knife which also contains the blood.\par \par \uc2 LINE: Brian Walshe pleaded guilty in 2021 to federal charges stemming from the sale of two fake Andy Warhol paintings. Police say a family member last saw 39-year-old Ana in the early morning hours on New Year's Day as she planned to take a ride share to Boston's airport for a flight to Washington D.C., where she worked during the week for a real estate firm.\par \par \uc2 Police say there's no indication a rideshare vehicle ever came and no record of her boarding a flight. Her husband didn't report her missing until January 4th. The same day her D.C. employer raised alarm bells. Walshe's defense attorney argued for his release.\par \par \uc2 TRACY MINER, DEFENSE ATTORNEY: We have consented to searches on his property. We have consented to searches of his cell phone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINE (on camera): Walshe remains held on $500,000 cash bail, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Molly Line, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Students at the University of Idaho are returning to campus for the start of the spring semester. This comes as a suspect in the brutal murders of four students sits in jail tonight.\par \par \uc2 The chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt is in Moscow, Idaho this evening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOLENE BROOKER: I feel like they have their angel wings.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Jolene Brooker (PH) and her son drove five hours just to pay tribute to the four University of Idaho students so brutally murdered in their home.\par \par \uc2 BROOKER: Knowing that my kids are angels, we can't see them would somehow warmed my heart within this very sad time.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The University of Idaho officially begins at spring semester Wednesday, and many students are returning for the first time since the November 13th killings when they left campus in fear with a killer on the loose.\par \par \uc2 Now they have the small comfort of knowing police say that an accused killer Bryan Kohberger, they have their man.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm just happy they caught someone. I was hoping they got someone before we all came back for spring semester.\par \par \uc2 BRYSON BUTTERFIELD, UNIVERSITY OF IDAHO STUDENT: It's just going to be look out for one another more. Take care of one another and be kind.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA JACOBSON, UNIVERSITY OF IDAHO STUDENT: I really hope that all the people that were affected by it can find healing.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Kohberger a 28-year-old PhD criminology student will make his second court appearance in Moscow on Thursday. It's a status hearing that is unlikely to reveal much new information or answer outstanding questions such as where is the murder weapon, did Kohberger dispose what would have been heavily bloodstained clothing during what police say was a circuitous drive he took from the crime scene to his apartment on campus at nearby \uc2 Washington State University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (on camera): There are no indications as yet from investigators that Bryan Kohberger had any previous direct contact with any of the victims although his vehicle movements and cell phone records suggest he may have obsessively stalked one or more of them from a distance, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Jonathan Hunt in Moscow, Idaho tonight.\par \par \uc2 Up next, New York nurses go on strike forcing some hospitals to adjust treatment plans.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Thousands of New York City nurses are on strike. They are demanding pay raises and more staffing. FOX Business correspondent Lydia Hu is in New York this evening. Lydia, good evening.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Trace. About 7,000 nurses across two hospitals in New York City left patient bedsides today, taking to the sidewalks to strike as labor negotiations broke down early this morning. The management here at the Mount Sinai hospital calling this strike reckless, and adding in a statement, quote, "Our first priority is the safety of our patients. We're prepared to minimize disruption, and we encourage Mount Sinai nurses to continue to provide the world class care they are known for."\par \par \uc2 But Trace, the strike is impacting patient care as hospitals also manage surging cases of COVID, RSV, and the flu. \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2  says it has moved the most vulnerable like NICU babies to other hospitals, postponed some elective surgeries and heart surgeries, and is also helping cancer patients find alternative locations for treatments.\par \par \uc2 And thousands of nurses are also striking at \uc2 Montefiore Medical Center\uc2  in the Bronx. There the hospital is also rescheduling all elective surgeries and postponing outpatient appointments. The sticking point, Trace, is that staffing levels, the union says, compromises patient care.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You are working through your lunch hour, sometimes not at all. I worked on a unit where we did 12 hours sometimes without a break.\par \par \uc2 If anything goes wrong, it's the first thing they want to do is reprimand you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: Now, Mount Sinai and Montefiore say that they've offered nurses a 19 percent raise to be paid out over the course of three years. But the hospitals say the nurses walked away from the negotiating table early this morning anyway. The negotiations resumed this afternoon at Montefiore, but they do appear to be at a standstill here at \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2 . The nurses' union says they're prepared to strike every day until they reach an agreement. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Lydia Hu live for us in New York. Lydia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A federal trial is underway for a man accused of ramming his rental truck into eight people on a New York City bike path. Sayfullo Saipov has pleaded not guilty to multiple charges of murder for the 2017 attack on Halloween. The Uzbekistan native's alleged plot is being treated as an act of terrorism after prosecutors say they found multiple notes revealing Saipov had pledged loyalty to ISIS. The trial expected to last three months. He is the first person to face federal charges that could result in the death penalty under President Biden's administration.\par \par \uc2 Well, the New Jersey middle school now teacher facing drug charges after allegedly overdosing on fentanyl in the classroom. Fifth-seven-year-old Frank Thompson was charged last week for the November incident. A school nurse treated him in front of students and gave him Narcan to reverse the overdose. Classes were temporarily moved to a different location while the room was sanitized.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we have more about a story we brought you on Friday. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has now approved new medication to treat Alzheimer's disease, but not everyone is happy about that. Here's correspondent Gillian Turner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DR. THOMAS WISNIEWSKI, LANGONE ALZHEIMER'S RESEARCH CENTER: Lecanemab is in a class by itself.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: In a major medical breakthrough, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is fast tracking the new drug to treat early stage Alzheimer's disease.\par \par \uc2 WISNIEWSKI: Recently published data suggests that it is associated with meaningful clinical benefits to patients and it also slows down the disease progression.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The drug hits the market later this month. The \uc2 Alzheimer's Association\uc2  applauds \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval as, quote, "The right decision," but blasts Medicare and Medicaid's refusal to help pay for it. "Without coverage of this treatment, people are losing days, weeks, months, memories, skills, and independence. They are losing time." The association estimates an average of 2,000 Americans 65 and older per day will progress from mild to advanced Alzheimer's without drug treatment.\par \par \uc2 Leqembi's approval, though, is not without controversy. A recent congressional investigation issued a scathing report on the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's rushed approval of another Alzheimer's drug from the same U.S. developer, \uc2 Biogen\uc2 , calling the review process highly atypical, and the $56,000 per year price tag for that drug unjustifiably high. Because of that price tag and concerns about efficacy, that drug fizzled on the U.S. market.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  insisted it remains, quote, "committed to the integrity of our drug approval process." Alzheimer's experts say Leqembi has shown more promise in clinical trials than competitor drugs, but the medical evidence is not clear on whether it will make a noticeable difference.\par \par \uc2 DR. SAM GANDY, \uc2 MOUNT SINAI HOSPITAL\uc2 : The future will tell us more about how patients in the current trial fare over longer periods of time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): There are serious risks from the drug, including brain swelling and bleeding. Leqembi's price tag will $20,600 a year. That's less than half the cost of Aduhelm, its predecessor, that was condemned by congressional investigators. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in West Virginia has upheld a state law that prohibits biological male student athletes who identify and present themselves as female from playing on girls' school sports teams. The court found that West Virginia's definition of biological sex for school sports is, quote, "substantially related to its important interest in providing equal athletic opportunities for females."\par \par \uc2 Up next, the U.S. begins changing the names of military installations in an effort expected to cost millions of dollars. And later, chaos in Brazil over allegations of a stolen election.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: A special grand jury in Georgia investigating whether then President Trump and his allies illegally tried to overturn his defeat in Georgia's 2020 election has finished its final report now. The jurors considered evidence and heard testimony from dozens of witnesses including high profile Trump associates and top state officials. A hearing scheduled for January 24th will determine if the final report will be released to the public.\par \par \uc2 After two years of politically and racially diverse congressionally mandated commission has completed its recommendations to rename Army bases and remove statues and art that celebrate those who voluntarily served the Confederate Army. The Pentagon has ordered of the name changes to be implemented by the end of the year. The price tag -- an estimated $62.5 million. The chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin is at the Pentagon tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Nine army bases will face name changes, including Fort Bragg, North Carolina named for Braxton Bragg, an ill-tempered slave-owning Confederate general who lost most of his battles and troops. Fort Bragg was named in 1918 during the Jim Crow period as an olive branch to southern families whose sons the military hoped to recruit. It's been home to the U.S. military's elite special forces and airborne units and will now be known as Fort Liberty.\par \par \uc2 Other changes include Fort Benning, named for the Colonel who led Georgia's secessionist movement, will become Fort Moore. Fort Polk, Louisiana, named for a general who owned more than 100 slaves, will become Fort Johnson, who received the Medal of Honor for actions during World War I. Fort Gordon, Georgia, will now be Fort Eisenhower, originally named for the member of the \uc2 Ku Klux Klan\uc2 . Fort Rucker, Alabama, named for a slave trader, will become Fort Novosel.\par \par \uc2 KORI SCHAKE, NAMING COMMISSION: He was a World War II Army Air Corps pilot, was awarded the Medal of Honor for action in combat, and not only saved the life of his own son, but his son returned the favor by saving his father when the father had been shot down. It commemorates the way so many military families are intergenerational.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The commission came in the wake of the police killing of George Floyd in 2020. Congress established the Naming Commission in the 2021 NDAA which then President Trump vetoed due to the presence of the commission.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): Bipartisan majorities in both houses of Congress then overrode Trump's veto. The Pentagon has until the end of the year to comply. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian authorities are trying to clean up and find out exactly what happened following violent protests during the weekend. It has to do with allegations of a stolen election. President Biden spoke with Brazil's new president on the phone today and conveyed support for his government. Senior foreign affairs correspondent Amy Kellogg has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 AMY KELLOGG, FOX NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Supporters of former president \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  on Sunday storming the Brazilian presidential palace, supreme court, and congress, and causing widespread damage in a three-hour right. The protesters are claiming their vote was stolen and that they are trying to, quote, save their country. But President \uc2 Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva\uc2  has been sworn in and is vowing to make those he calls, quote, fascist and fanatics, pay for their actions.\par \par \uc2 Outgoing President Bolsonaro watched the events unfold from Florida where he went 48 hours before his term was up. Some U.S. Democratic lawmakers are calling for Bolsonaro to be sent home to effectively answer for the violence wreaked by his supporters. President Biden has condemned the, quote, "assault on democracy in Brazil" with State Department spokesperson \uc2 Ned Price\uc2  saying today.\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE, STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESMAN: We condemn this violence. Violence is never appropriate. It's never the answer. Brazil's democratic institutions have our full support. As we always are, we are standing by for any requests for assistance from our Brazilian partners, from Brazilian authorities, whether those come through diplomatic channels, whether they come through law enforcement channels.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG (on camera): Bolsonaro reportedly hospitalized Monday for abdominal pain. Don't forget, he was stabbed in the stomach in 2018 while campaigning. Political violence not uncommon in Brazil, but Sunday's riot is said to have been the worst since the country overthrew dictatorship in 1985. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Amy Kellogg. Amy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden's visit to the southern border.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Ukraine is strengthening its forces in the eastern Donbas region in order to repel attacks from Russian mercenaries. The Wagner Group has been trying to take control of the areas' vast underground mines for months.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Edmonton, Canada. One of the big stories there tonight. Canada finalizes an agreement with \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2  to purchase 88 F-35 fire jets. The aircraft are expected to be delivered in 2026 and be fully operational within the fleet by 2034. At $15 billion it is the largest investment in the Royal Canadian Air Force in more than 30 years.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. And we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What do you think about the documents that -- the classified documents that were found at President Biden's library from his time at vice president?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2 , (R-GA): Oh my gosh, I think we need to investigate the Bidens nonstop. I've been very vocal in impeaching \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and hopefully we can get that done this congress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Let's bring in our panel, Jason Riley, "Wall Street Journal" columnist and senior fellow at the Manhattan Institute, Susan Page, Washington Bureau Chief at "USA Today," and Ben Domenech, editor at large for "The Spectator" and host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio.\par \par \uc2 A little bit of breaking news, Ben, we would like to get to with you, and I want to put this tweet up on the screen if I can. This is from Jim Banks. And he is saying, "How will the people demanding President Trump go to prison spin this?" What he's talking about is the breaking news that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has now assigned a U.S. attorney in Chicago to review potential, potential classified documents found at the Penn Biden Center for Diplomacy. And, of course, Jim Banks is referring to the entire world coming down on President Trump for the classified documents found, or potentially classified documents found at Mar-a-Lago. Your thoughts on this, Ben?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Obviously, there's going to be the normal accusations of hypocrisy. And I think that they probably have merit to them here. One of the things that I think is important is to understand what the content of these documents are. We have an overclassification problem in America generally. A lot of people used that, frankly, as a point to defend former President Trump, and I think it could potentially be a valid point here as well. You have a lot of things that end up classified that aren't nuclear secrets, which apparently are not included in these documents. But we have a lot more to learn about what actually went on here and what is included among them.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, interesting. I want to say this is what the White House said, Jason, and we'll get your response. "The White House is cooperating with the National Archives and the Department of Justice regarding the discovery of what appear to be \uc2 Obama\uc2 -Biden administration records, including a small number of documents with classified markings. The documents were discovered when the president's personal attorneys were packing files housed in a locked closet to prepare to vacate office space at the Penn Biden Center in Washington, D.C." Jason, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JASON RILEY, COLUMNIST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Well, I agree with Ben. We'll have to see how this plays out. But I wouldn't at all be surprised if the press treats this differently than they treated it under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We have seen that pattern, and I think it will continue here.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, it's interesting. And Susan, your take on this, because he's right. It all really comes down to how the media covers this, if this ignore it or not. Does it become the next version of the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  files? Does it become the border where nobody decides to cover it until sometime down the road?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": Well, I think given the context of the investigation into former President Trump's handling of classified documents, we're going to take this seriously. We're going to -- the mainstream press is going to cover it. There should be an investigation into what these documents are and how they came to be held in this locked closet. So let's take it seriously as the attorney general seems to be doing, and let's find out more.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It's interesting, because, Ben, when you talk about taking this seriously, this, if this was President Trump, would be a four-alarm fire across every other network and "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " and "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," et cetera. And I keep looking up at the screen, I'm looking for the coverage. I'm looking at the monitors where they have all the different companies up there, and I'm not really seeing big, bold letters across the bottom of the screen, breaking news, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: They need to have some time in order to get the talking points in order. But I think one of the things that we do need to understand about this is that when it came to the documents that were held by former President Trump, what you immediately had was open speculation that he was holding documents of the most important nature, things that could be a danger to American security and the like. I don't think you're going to see that kind of speculation around this. I think you'll probably see a lot of people dismissing it, oh, these were just heirlooms and keepsakes that ended up in the files as opposed to anything of significance.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Jason, nuclear documents, right? There was a lot of speculation on what actual documents were being housed in Mar-a-Lago. And, again, the speculation has been kind of low key in this case.\par \par \uc2 RILEY: Well, yes. And, of course, the overriding issue here was when that raid first happened in Mar-a-Lago, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hadn't declared that he was running for president. And so now the big complication there is that you have the Biden administration investigating a potential opponent in the presidential race. And so, yes, that's what sort of complicated things here. And that's going to be a big difference between what was going on there and what's going on here.\par \par \uc2 But I think your overriding point is right, which was that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was fundamentally covered differently by the mainstream media, and we're going to see more evidence of that.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. I want to, if I can, very quickly move on here because we want to talk about the border and the border encounters. If I can put up these border encounter numbers. This is very telling because you had the president visit El Paso this weekend. Look at the bottom righthand number there, 2,378,000. And yet, the president goes to El Paso, Susan, yesterday, and oddly, not a single migrant could be seen in this entire tour. It's just -- it's kind of amazing how that worked out.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Yes. It's his first trip to the border. It's an issue that the White House has not been eager to talk about for the past two years. It's one that's, clearly a problem at the border. You look at those numbers and you cannot deny we have a problem with undocumented people coming over the border. But hard to solve.\par \par \uc2 We now have the White House trying to strike a kind of middle ground, taking somewhat more aggressive actions, including some policies that President Trump had backed to try to get control of the border, and for that being very much criticized on the left. So this is -- he's talking about this issue today and tomorrow with the president of Mexico at the Three Amigos Summit in the hopes of striking some kind of deal that brings some control to the border, and yet, preserves the system that we have under law to let people apply for political refugee status if they think they're being persecuted, if they have a legitimate threat of being persecuted in their home country.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And you can put that on the screen, if we can, full. This is the bilateral, this is in Mexico City at the palace, and the third amigo, as you mentioned there, Susan, Canadian Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  has not yet appeared. This is between right now between President Biden and the Mexican president \uc2 Manuel Lopez Obrador\uc2 . And the concept here, and we're going to dip in if there's any news brought, the concept here of this whole thing, Ben, is that as Greg Abbott gave the president a note that reads, in part, quoting here, "Your visit to our southern border with Mexico today is $20 billion too late -- too little, and two years too late. Moreover, your visit avoids the sites where mass illegal immigration occurs and sidesteps the thousands of angry Texas property owners whose lives have been destroyed by your border policies. This chaos is the direct result of your failure to enforce the immigration laws that Congress enacted." Ben?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Look, I agree with Governor Abbott in so many respects here. This is something that the White House has attempt to the ignore. They have attempted to look away from it. They know they were going to get hit from the left as soon as they did anything that would actually work because of its associations with former President Trump. But they also, I think, are really in a bind when it comes to the situation in Mexico just given the level of chaos that that state is currently experiencing, the level of crime and fighting within both the cartels and outside of the cartels is something that is only increasing there, leading to increased chaos on the border, and also, I think is something that people seem to not associate with this. But so much of this is actually about the flow, not just of people but of fentanyl across the southern border into our communities across the country, killing so many Americans. This is something the White House has ignored for far too long, and one visit to El Paso won't make a difference.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: We've got about 30 seconds to wrap this up. Jason, your final thoughts?\par \par \uc2 RILEY: I think Ben nailed it there. This is an issue -- the progressive left wanted \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to basically reverse everything that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had done on everything, but particularly on immigration. And the problem is that some of these Trump policies were quite effective in dealing with illegal immigration. And the Biden administration is dealing with that reality right now.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Panel, thank you for your time. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day. A community takes the polar plunge to raise money for a young boy battling cancer. Five-year-old Quinn Waters, also known as Mighty Quinn, has shown his strength in a multi-year struggle with two separate brain diseases. Massachusetts water temperatures were in, oh, the 40s when his supporters raced into the bay. This is the third year of the polar plunge, and our best, of course, to Quinn Waters and his ongoing battle.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Trace Gallagher in Los Angeles. Please join me for "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT" at midnight eastern, 9:00 pacific. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230110ej1900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Lot Of Good Things Are Going On In The World; Tony Robbins On What People Can Do To Be A Better Version Of Themselves In 2023; Mom Of 12: We Received Backlash For Our Big Family; Don't Learn From Success, Learn From Humiliation; Mike Rowe: It Is More Important Than Ever To Show Americans Doing Real Work; In Search Of Happiness In 2023; "Mighty Ducks" Star Battled Meth Addiction; The Power Of Prayer\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Mike Rowe, Harris Faulkner, Jackie Ibanez \par \uc2 8,056 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's it for us tonight. DVR "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" and always remember, I'm Watters and this is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening, and welcome to a Special and Unusual Edition of TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT, a Happy Edition.\par \par \uc2 It's the New Year, as the New Year begins, 2023 with everything that's going on in the world, it is easy to feel, well, a sense of impending doom. You could spend five minutes watching TV or browsing social media and you would come up with a million reasons why this country is done. So despair is more tempting than ever, but it is not the whole story.\par \par \uc2 And because it's not, we are taking the next hour to show you the other side. Yes, this country has problems. We wouldn't have a very interesting TV show if it didn't.\par \par \uc2 But the truth is, this country also has an awful lot going for it, more than any other country in the world. Great people, deep natural beauty and a culture wide love of animals which matters. So even where there is tremendous suffering, all of that shines through. That's the gift.\par \par \uc2 Lauree Simmons is the perfect example of this. She rescues dogs across the country, including those that have been abandoned at the Southern border. We spoke to her back in November.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAUREE SIMMONS, BIG DOG RANCH RESCUE: When we got down to Eagle Pass down at the Rio Grande last week and saw hundreds and hundreds of starving and abandoned dogs left to fend for themselves from illegal immigrants that crossed the border with their dogs, then they get on buses and just leave them behind.\par \par \uc2 These dogs can't survive on their own and the National Guard and a friend of mine from Blue Line Moving who had gone down there to do a border cleanup had reached out to us and we were really shocked at just how big the problem was and how much these animals were suffering, and this couldn't come at a worse time, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 Shelters throughout the country are overflowing everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Big Dog Ranch Rescue is the largest no kill dog rescue in the country with our Palm Beach County location. We have 750 dogs there right now, we're overflowing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, since that interview aired, Lauree Simmons has raised $600,000.00 for her shelter, the Big Dog Ranch Rescue. That is the largest no kill dog shelter in the country. It is a good place to send your money in our opinion. She joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Lauree, thanks so much for coming on. So, I hope that what you're seeing in the response to that segment is a really deep love of animals who are in some ways the weakest among us, we can do whatever we want to animals, so it is a measure of our decency what we do to animals. It is a measure of the fact that Americans still love animals.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Tucker, we cannot thank you enough for airing this story and it has helped us with your extremely generous dog loving viewers.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : It is going to let us save so many more and also allow us to go back to the border.\par \par \uc2 We've currently got about 900 dogs between our Palm Beach County and Alabama facility, which all need medical care and this is starting off our New Year in a great way. And it's just really heartwarming to me to see how many people have reached out to help donate and adopt.\par \par \uc2 And it's because of all of you that many dogs will be loved and have a chance and a wonderful new home.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I think it's meaningful that you are a no kill shelter. I mean without judgment, there are a lot of different kinds of shelters, but I think people feel good about giving to a shelter that really is about dogs and about loving them and keeping them alive and not disposing of them.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : There are so many dogs still being surrendered because of inflation, the high cost of rent, fuel, groceries, everything and shelters are so overcrowded right now. So the need for rescue, the need for us to grow and save more is at an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 And we're just extremely grateful to all of you that joined our cause whether you donate, volunteer, adopt, and thank you so very, very much for getting this out because people need to know there are wonderful dogs out there that need homes, don't buy from a breeder. Spay and neuter your pets. Let's end this problem of dog homelessness and the dogs need us, and we're thankful for all of you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: They love us unconditionally. We should love them back. I think that's true. Lauree, great to see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Thank you for what you're doing.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 ; And happy new year and thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Happy New Year. Of Course.\par \par \uc2 So all the people we've interviewed over the years, which is a lot of people, one of the wisest, maybe unexpectedly, but it really stuck out was Tony Robbins and we probably shouldn't have been surprised by that because he is famously one of the happiest, most positive people in the country, an amazing speaker.\par \par \uc2 There is a reason he is famous actually, if you listen to him, here's a clip that shows what we're talking about.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TONY ROBBINS, LIFE AND BUSINESS STRATEGIST: You're telling me I failed because I didn't have the resources and I'm here to tell you what you already know, resources are never the problem. It's a lack of resourcefulness is why you failed.\par \par \uc2 Because the ultimate resources are emotional states. If you're creative enough, can you find the answer? Yes or No?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: If you're determined enough, can you find a breakthrough? Yes or No?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: If you're passionate and loving enough, can you get someone to help you, yes or no?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: If there's no way that you're committed, can you find the money even if you don't have it? Yes or no?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: So I said creativity, decisiveness, passion, honesty, sincerity, love -- these are the ultimate human resources and when you engage these resource, you can get any other resource on Earth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So honestly, we've never been to a Tony Robbins seminar. And so when we -- oh, you've got to talk to Tony Robbins, that man is insightful and that turned out to be true.\par \par \uc2 And so on this New Year's, we thought we would ask Tony Robbins, who would know what are three things that all of us can do in 2023 to improve ourselves deeply.\par \par \uc2 Tony Robbins joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Tony, thank you so much for doing this.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: My pleasure, Tucker. Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I'm completely sold on your insight into the human condition. So what can all of us do to improve ourselves this year, do you think?\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: I think the first thing you have to do is develop a compelling future. I mean, after three years of COVID, and people thinking if somebody breathes on, you're going to die and all the lies we were told, and then now you have young people today that are saying they don't want to have kids, because after all, you know that we're going to have in 12 years, the world's going to end, which of course, is totally false.\par \par \uc2 But when all these stories keep getting stacked, and then you worry about inflation, people get overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 So we need a compelling future. Compelling future means anybody can deal with incredibly difficult today, if they have a real compelling tomorrow, and a way to get there.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: That's not affirmations. That's not positive thinking. That's not, you know, setting New Year's resolutions that you break two weeks later, it means really coming up with a plan.\par \par \uc2 In other words for you to improve your life, there's lots of things we can't control, but if you focus on that, you're overwhelmed. There's plenty we can control in our minds and bodies.\par \par \uc2 So for life to get better, we have to get better. For things to change, we have to change.\par \par \uc2 And so my focus is showing people let's start with your energy, for example. I'm doing this a program, I started in COVID when everybody was stuck at home, I said, how do I reach people to help them? What gets in their way? It's travel, it's time, it's money.\par \par \uc2 So I put together this five-day challenge and I'm doing one more this year, January 24 to the 28th and what I do is two hours a day at no charge, there's no money involved at all, and from your home or your office with your friends or family or by yourself, I walk you through the tools that will make your 2023 effective.\par \par \uc2 So we start with your energy. If you're exhausted and tired, you're not going to have a great relationship, you're not going to build a great business. You're sure not going to push through the difficult times.\par \par \uc2 What are the emotions is we're just alluding to in that clip that will change you? If you're having creativity and passion and decisiveness, that's very different than if you've gotten used to the emotions now feeling overwhelmed and stressed. And just you know, stressed out as the most one, you see people are depressed.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if you saw it today, but there was a new report out by the CDC that said 45 percent of high school students they interviewed said they were so depressed and so disappointed and down in 2021 they couldn't participate in normal activities. One in five said they considered suicide. Nine percent of those surveyed actually attempted suicide. That's how insane it has gotten.\par \par \uc2 So we need a compelling future. We need to shift our emotions, create the shift in our bodies, then what are we going to do about inflation? Well, you know, \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2  was asked about it the other day and he said, here's what you do, invest in yourself. No one can tax it. No one can take it away.\par \par \uc2 If you get stronger and better, even if the dollar goes down and it shekels, if you're the best lawyer or a doctor or a podcaster or whatever you're going to do well and so we'll show people what they are doing matter. What do you do to make sure you have a good time in your relationship?\par \par \uc2 One of the things I love about you, Tucker is talking about have a good time with the ones you love at the end of your show, usually, after you've put out all of the things that we've got to deal with out there, but it's so true, that's the safe harbor for all of us.\par \par \uc2 So what are you going to do in each of those areas and at the end of five days, people now have a plan. They're not just excited about the future falsely or feeling overwhelmed or stressed by the future, they've really started to create a transformation.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That is absolutely right. You have to have a sight picture of where you're going, and once you do, you can be excited about it. It is so wise, and I'm not surprised, but I am grateful for that advice.\par \par \uc2 Tony Robbins, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: If people want to join us, they go to BecomeUnshakable.com. There is no charge, register yourself and then come join us for five days. Couple hours out of the day. We'll have over a million people from around the world there so, Become Unshakable.com.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Become Unshakable. Love it. Thank you. Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: Thanks for having us on. Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 So you just heard Tony Robbins say it, there is a lot of pressure on people not to have kids, oh, the future is so scary. Don't have kids.\par \par \uc2 Okay, you're getting that message every day constantly. We wanted to hear the other side. What's it like to have a really big family? We just spoke to a mom with 12 kids and she wants more. Why? Why does she want more kids? Maybe because they're great. We'll let her tell us after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh, there is a lot of anti-child propaganda everywhere. It's just in the air. In fact, our Treasury Secretary decided she was our Family Planning Secretary and she announced not so long ago that if you don't like the economy, just have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , US TREASURY SECRETARY: I believe that eliminating the right of women to make decisions about when and whether to have children would have very damaging effects on the economy and would set women back decades.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Okay, so that's what you're seeing everywhere -- on social media. That's the core assumption of the entire American media. It's all you hear.\par \par \uc2 But what's the other side? There are some people who aren't going along with it, who find joy and meaning in having children.\par \par \uc2 Veronica Merritt is definitely one of them. She is the mother of 12 children. She's now a grandmother. She said she wants even more children, and the question is, what is she finding in children that \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  hasn't?\par \par \uc2 We thought we'd ask her directly.\par \par \uc2 Veronica Merritt, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA MERRITT, MOTHER TO 12 CHILDREN: Hello.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: What why did you have so many children, Veronica Merritt?\par \par \uc2 MERRITT: Well, when I first started having kids, I was really young and I think that when I started, I didn't expect this to be a large family mom. I didn't really plan for it.\par \par \uc2 But as I started having kids, I really liked just seeing them together and you know, I had a really rough childhood, you know, and I was really sad. And I was really lonely. And when I started having a bigger family, I was like, wait a minute, you know, this is exactly what I needed. This is what I was missing.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I'm going to say it's not always perfect, but it's so awesome. It's like -- it's awesome seeing everyone together. It's awesome - - birthdays and holidays. I love showing them how special they are to me. It's hard to explain really, it's just something about having a large family that I wouldn't trade it for anything.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I love how you've said twice, seeing them together, their relationships with each other bring you such joy. It's not even about you. It's about them and each other. That's a cool observation.\par \par \uc2 MERRITT: Exactly. Yes, and because -- yes, no, you're right. And because, you know, like I said, I didn't have a lot of people when I was younger, I have one sister who wasn't really around that much. So I don't even really have a whole lot of memories with anybody. And I didn't want my kids to grow up without, you know someone else.\par \par \uc2 So like I had my firstborn and I waited until I finished school before I had another kid. And she used to tell me when she was little that she had wished I had had more between them because they're almost five years apart. And so I guess I took that to heart.\par \par \uc2 And so I had several back to back, and again, I didn't really expect to have so many but the more I grew my family, the more I loved it and I just accepted that I am a large family mom, and that's who I am and that's what I want to be.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, I love that. I mean, sometimes people -- I mean, life is complicated and messy, and families are complicated and messy, but the joy is more than enough in return.\par \par \uc2 I have to ask you, Veronica, how old are you?\par \par \uc2 MERRITT: Thirty-seven.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Thirty-seven. Well, you have done your part to repopulate the earth and to make people happy and I think you may be onto something. I love it, anyway.\par \par \uc2 Veronica Merritt, thank you so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 MERRITT: All right, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So one of the things we learned in 2022 is it is very hard to forecast the future, you don't really know what's going to happen. You think you do, but you're often surprised and that was of course the case for a lot of normal people over the Midterm Elections.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party really fell dramatically short. So, I guess that's bad. We think it is, but it's probably also a chance to learn and we'll give you perspective on what we learned, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JACKIE IBANEZ, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Welcome to "FOX News Live." I'm Jackie Ibanez in New York.\par \par \uc2 Russia says 63 troops are dead following a Ukrainian strike on a military facility in the Donetsk region. A US-supplied precision weapon was used which allows Ukraine to hit key targets. It's the latest setback for Russia, which has reeled from a recent Ukrainian counteroffensive.\par \par \uc2 According to Russian military bloggers, ammunition stored close to the facility exploded, which contributed to the high number of deaths.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, thousands of fans are paying their final respects to Brazilian soccer great, Pele. There is a public viewing at a stadium in his hometown. Before his funeral tomorrow. The 82-year-old died last week following a year-long battle with cancer.\par \par \uc2 He won three World Cups and helped popularize soccer here in the US during his time with the New York Cosmos.\par \par \uc2 I'm Jackie Ibanez. Now, back to the TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT Special.\par \par \uc2 Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, the Midterm Elections were a crushing disappointment for Republicans and conservatives and for basically anyone who looks around and says this isn't right. So, we're not quite sure what happened. But we're pretty sure we can learn from what happened anyway.\par \par \uc2 And that was the subject of a speech that we gave recently in Phoenix. Here is part of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: And the Midterm Elections taught me a number of new things. And I should preface my story of what I've learned by telling you that you never learn anything except through pain and humiliation. I wish that weren't true, unfortunately, no one has put me in charge of the rules of nature. So that is the law and it's unchanging.\par \par \uc2 But you never learn anything from succeeding. I have never learned anything from succeeding, from getting what I want. I've only learned from failure, and particularly from humiliation.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Midterm Elections were like a grave disappointment, I think, to every normal person, but they were particularly painful to me because I got them so completely wrong. Like, I've never gotten anything wronger in my life.\par \par \uc2 Like people are getting poorer, the country is getting more chaotic.\par \par \uc2 In a normal system, and the party in charge of deciding what we do next would be punished.\par \par \uc2 They're not even promising to make your life better. Like they're not even pretending -- oh, castrate your children, okay. Have some inflation. How about a war that we have nothing to do with? Yes, we want more of that. Yes, a lot more.\par \par \uc2 So like, there is no way a party that pretends that, you know, that that is going to make your life better, it is like not even -- it's not even up for debate. So it's like they're going to be destroyed, because they're not even trying.\par \par \uc2 And then they win and my mind was blown.\par \par \uc2 We actually don't know what the future holds, actually. And not to be morbid, but we could all get hit by a bus on the way out of this building. And I pray to God, we won't be, but we could be and that is a truth that we live with every day. And if we're wise, we never forget it.\par \par \uc2 We don't know what's coming. We control the moment we're living in now and that's it and barely even that.\par \par \uc2 We have less power than we think -- we can't even turn male pattern baldness, okay? No, it's true. It's true. Or add a single hair to the top of our heads in other words.\par \par \uc2 So to tell ourselves like, we've got it all figured out. We know what's going to happen. We are lying to ourselves. And when we say it out loud, we're lying to others and misleading them.\par \par \uc2 So we should start with the knowledge of our own limitations -- we are limited -- and that is the beginning of wisdom.\par \par \uc2 If you look back, and you're honest on your life, and the really sad things that happened, and they are real for everybody. If you're honest, you will see that it's only out of those, that happiness grew. That that was the foundation for something better, that all wisdom is accrued during those moments.\par \par \uc2 But in your victory, you lose a little bit and that's always true. But thankfully, the flipside is true.\par \par \uc2 So I have no idea how this is going to play out. I'm completely over my brief flirtation with pretending to predict the future. I've thrown my crystal ball away. I'm not going to read your palms. I have no idea what's coming.\par \par \uc2 But I am totally certain in my heart and I mean that we will look back in this moment and if you're not even conservative, if you just think what's going on now is really wrong and really destructive to the country that you love and imperiling dangerous to your children and grandchildren and future generations if you feel that way, I think most people do, you will look back on this moment of despair and confusion, what the hell just happened? No one really knows. It's bad. We know that. We don't really know how or why.\par \par \uc2 You will look back on this as the beginning of clarity. You will -- beginning of clarity.\par \par \uc2 This is when I really begin to understand what was happening and what I personally could do about it. I changed for the better.\par \par \uc2 Suffering causes growth. That is always true, and it's true now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So if you go on TikTok, you may have concerns about the state of the American economy. There are a lot of young white collar employees, marketing majors from Davidson, filming themselves at work. And it turns out, they don't do a lot. They take a lot of breaks. We're not being mean. These are their words.\par \par \uc2 Here's a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employee from San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TWITTER\uc2  EMPLOYEE FROM SAN FRANCISCO: Welcome to a day in my life as a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employee.\par \par \uc2 So this past week, went to SF for the first time at a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  office, badged in. Honestly, it took a moment to just soak everything in. What a blessing.\par \par \uc2 Also, I started my morning off with an iced matcha from the perch. Then I had a meeting so quickly scheduled one of these old pod rooms which were so cool, they're literally noise canceling. Took my meeting, got ready for a brunch. Look how delicious this food looks, oh, my goodness, I was so overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 Then made my way down to this like log cabin area. I don't know what this is, but it was really cool. I played some foosball and brands to kind of unwind a bit.\par \par \uc2 I found this really cool meditation room,\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Mike Rowe has spent his life reporting on work and in fact working. He saw that segment and reached out and said it's more important than ever to show the fact that a lot of Americans are doing real work.\par \par \uc2 In fact, he's got a whole show about this called "Dirty Jobs." It has been on the air for 20 years. He is also the host of "How America Works" on FOX Business. He joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Mike Rowe, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 When we did that segment, I thought of you banging out the script the whole time, this is the opposite of the kind of actual work Mike Rowe has dedicated his life to telling the rest of us about.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ROWE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK HOST "HOW AMERICA WORKS": Yes, you didn't tell me you were going to do that segment. had I known, I would have insisted on following it up with something. So thank you for the chance to do that now.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: Now, let me just say, I can't speak really for anybody, but me, but I would bet big that millions and millions of people jumped out of their chairs screamed words we can't repeat on your program, as a result of watching what you showed them.\par \par \uc2 And look, again, it's not about being mean, but our relationship with work, our definition of what a good job is, that's something we can control. And you're so right, portrayals of work shift with time.\par \par \uc2 And I think part of the reason "Dirty Jobs" has been on the air for the last 20 years is we are the antidote to what you just showed America. We go to places where there is no meditation room. There is no five-star breakfast to start the day. There is the bag house at the asphalt plant.\par \par \uc2 There are just example, after example, after example, 350 jobs we've done so far of people who are still doing things that look like work. So, we can talk about a ton of different things. But fundamentally, our country still needs to be reminded of what, forgive me, I'm just going to say it, what real work looks like.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, it's just interesting that what you're calling real work is provably real, because it's the work that if people stopped doing, we would all pay for immediately, like we would die in a lot of cases. There is no dispute like this is the actual indispensable work.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: Look, and it makes us angry to be reminded of that, because we don't like the idea that we're so reliant and so dependent on such a small and marginalized chunk of our workforce, but it's the truth.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: Look, this episode is about good news. So let me give you some.\par \par \uc2 Mike Rowe Works, my foundation evolved out of "Dirty Jobs" 15 years ago. With your help, and a lot of appearances on your show over the last however many years, we've raised over $6 million in Work Ethic Scholarships, and we've awarded that money to over 1,500 people who are now welding and steam fitting and pipe fitting and plumbing, and they are thriving, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 Again and again and again, we see that it is still possible to achieve something that looks like prosperity if you begin by mastering a skill that's in demand.\par \par \uc2 Shameless plugs. We're going to do it again next month. Work Ethic Scholarships at MikeRowWorks.org. We are moving the needle in a modest way, but it starts by tapping the country on the shoulder as you have done and reminding them that real jobs still exist. Eleven million of them are out there right now. The opportunities are everywhere. Don't let anybody tell you otherwise.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: And they should have higher status. I mean, I love what you said about these are marginalized people. That's exactly right. These are the people -- the guys who work at asphalt plants who make it possible for us to travel are marginalized and that should not be the case and thank you for everything you're doing to change that.\par \par \uc2 Mike Rowe, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 So what is the key to happiness? Since this is a segment, a show about happiness, we thought we'd get a little closer to that. We're about to speak to a man who was very unhappy as a young man and who is happy now and has learned a lot along the way. Our friend Johann Hari joins us after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, not to get too heavy or anything, but what is the key to happiness? Well, we thought we would ask someone who has learned that.\par \par \uc2 Johann Hari was very unhappy as a young man, he is happy now. What did he learn that made the difference?\par \par \uc2 Johann Hari is the author of a couple of fantastic books, including "Stolen Focus: Why You Can't Pay Attention and How to Think Deeply Again."\par \par \uc2 Johann Hari joins us now to explain the root of happiness.\par \par \uc2 Johan, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 JOHANN HARI, AUTHOR, "STOLEN FOCUS": Hi, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I love your story because you were so desperately unhappy, and now you have a lightness about you that suggests you're not, so what made the difference for you?\par \par \uc2 HARI: Well, I think there's lots of factors, but I would say to everyone listening, everyone watching, think about anything you've ever achieved, that you're proud of in your life, whether it's starting a business, being a good parent, learning to play the guitar, whatever it is that makes you feel good that makes you happy.\par \par \uc2 That thing that you're proud of required a huge amount of sustained focus and attention. You had to really concentrate on it for a long period of time. And we are living in a huge crisis of focus and attention.\par \par \uc2 And when your ability to focus and pay attention breaks down, your ability to achieve your goals breaks down, your ability to solve your problems breaks down. It's happened to me, it's happened to so many people who are watching.\par \par \uc2 You know, at the moment, the average American office worker focuses on any one task for only three minutes. For every one child who was identified with serious attention problems, when I was six years old, there's now a hundred children who had been identified with that problem.\par \par \uc2 So I think one of the key factors that is making us so unhappy is that we can't focus, we can't pay attention.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That's right.\par \par \uc2 HARI: So to get to the bottom of this, I ended up going on a really big journey all over the world. I interviewed over 200 of the leading experts on attention and focus, and what I learned from them is there is scientific evidence for 12 factors that can make your attention better, or can make it worse, it is doing that to your kids, and loads of the factors that can make your attention worse, have been hugely rising in recent years.\par \par \uc2 You know, though my book is called "Stolen Focus," because your focus didn't collapse, it has been stolen from you. But the good news, the reason why this is an item for the Happy Show, is that once we understand what those causes are, we can begin to get our attention back for us and our kids.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You can't be happy unless you're grounded. And being grounded means being focused, being able to take something in and fully experience it. So, I think you're absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 HARI: That's a really good way of putting it and you know, it makes me think about, I went to interview one of the leading neuroscientists in the world, an amazing man named Professor Earl Miller, and he said to me, look, there's one thing you've got to understand about the human brain more than anything else. You can only consciously think about one or two things at a time. That's it. It's a fundamental limitation of the human brain.\par \par \uc2 But what's happened is we've fallen into a kind of mass delusion. The average teenager believes they can follow six or seven forms of media at the same time. So what happens is scientists like Professor Miller get people into labs, and they study them, and they get them to think they're doing more than one thing at a time. And what they discover is always the same.\par \par \uc2 You can't do more than one thing at a time. What you do is you juggle very quickly between tasks. You're like, wait, what did Tucker just ask me? What is this message on WhatsApp? What does it say on the TV? Wait, Tucker, what did you just ask me?\par \par \uc2 And it turns out that juggling, which we're all doing all the time, is coming with a really big cost. It's called the switch-cost effect. It is having a profound effect on our attention of focus, but there is loads of things we can do to get it back. I will give you an example of one.\par \par \uc2 This is a case safe, anyone could order it. You put your phone in it, push the button, it locks your phone away for anything between five minutes and a whole day.\par \par \uc2 I go through dozens of things like that, we can do to get our attention back in my book "Stolen Focus." We don't have to live like this. We can get our attention back if we want to.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Also, if you live near a large body of water, you could just toss it in there.\par \par \uc2 HARI: A hundred percent. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Johann Hari, great to see you tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HARI: Great to see you, Tucker. Cheers.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Shaun Weiss was a child actor. In 1994, he started as the goalie in the film "Mighty Ducks." Here's a clip.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN "MIGHTY DUCKS" VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREG "GOLDIE" GOLDBERG, FICTIONAL CHARACTER: Miss Mackay, we're America's team here. Shouldn't we just be concentrating on hockey? May I suggest optional attendance.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I agree.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE MACKAY, FICTIONAL CHARACTER: Well, that's not a bad idea.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Call me Goldberg.\par \par \uc2 MACKAY: Goldberg. Yes, school will be optional.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Twenty-nine years ago. Well, since then, Shaun Weiss has had major struggles with drug addiction, but there is good news tonight that we want to celebrate, Shaun Weiss is about to hit three years of sobriety. He joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Shaun, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 SHAUN WEISS, ACTOR: Hey.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: In a country that is beset by addiction to talk to someone who's made it three years and come as far as you have is just is a wonderful thing. How did you do it?\par \par \uc2 WEISS: Well, I had a lot of help from a lot of people. I went to a residential treatment, and then after that, I've just been in sober living.\par \par \uc2 So basically, I did a 12-step program and that really is what changed my life, I started praying to God and trying to increase my strength and my relationship with God through prayer and meditation. And that was probably the biggest thing that did it for me.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You know, you hear that again and again. You know, a lot of people who go to rehab and you know a lot of people who then go again to rehab and again to rehab and then die of drugs or alcohol.\par \par \uc2 WEISS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: But the ones who make it as you have seemed to be the ones who decide they're going to appeal to a higher power.\par \par \uc2 WEISS: I think definitely. I mean, first of all, you've got to get God to return you to your sanity and that's a big -- you know, that's a big hurdle. I don't know who else can do it for you, if not your higher power. So that was definitely key for me.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So you're, I mean, you're an actor in LA. How hard -- a very secular world -- how hard was it for you to admit that you needed a higher power?\par \par \uc2 WEISS: It was difficult. It was difficult to look myself in the mirror when I was in residential treatment and come to terms with the fact that I had become insane. It's not an easy thing to admit yourself.\par \par \uc2 But my thinking had just become insane and my behavior become insane. And it was difficult to reconcile that, but that was sort of a major turning point for me. Because you know, it's impossible to remedy a problem when you don't know it exists.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: What you're saying could be applied across all kinds of different areas, not just addiction. But yes, no, that's it. It's hard to admit you're insane.\par \par \uc2 WEISS: Yes, it was difficult. And I'm not so sure that I'm sane now, but I'm definitely better than the guy that was, you know, using drugs on the streets of Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 And really, my message is, no matter where you are in life, if you decide you want to turn your life around, it is completely possible. And you know, literally the 180 that I've made in three years, it's difficult, but once you set your mind and your energy towards getting well, support is in abundance, people come out of the woodworks really to help you out.\par \par \uc2 And really that's been another boon that I've had is the love and support of the fans that watched those "Mighty Ducks" movies. So, that was really a blessing for me. I don't know where I would be without that.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Because people are thrilled to see someone get better. I mean, it's true. There is nothing better than watching someone get better. There's nothing more inspiring and hope giving, so I appreciate your telling your story here. Once again, Shaun Weiss, it's great to see you.\par \par \uc2 WEISS: It's great to be with you, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, you just heard him say it. There is no getting better without acknowledging you're not really in charge. Most people are too embarrassed to say that out loud.\par \par \uc2 Our friend Harris Faulkner is not embarrassed to say that out loud.\par \par \uc2 You watch Harris Faulkner on TV every day and we hope that you do. Wait till you hear her next. Really amazing. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Our friend, Harris Faulkner is out with a new book on a topic that transcends politics. In fact, it transcends everything else. It's a book about prayer. The book is called "Faith Still Moves Mountains: Miraculous Stories of the Healing Power of Prayer."\par \par \uc2 Now the country is getting more secular, so you have to ask how is it doing? Well, it's killing it.\par \par \uc2 This book just topped "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestseller list. So we want to talk to Harris Faulkner about it. She is of course the host of "The Faulkner Focus" on FOX. She's also co-host of "Outnumbered."\par \par \uc2 Harris Faulkner joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Harris, thank you so much for coming on tonight.\par \par \uc2 The book title suggests that prayer is real, it actually works.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's not just something you do to seem pious, it has like actual effects, is that the argument you're making?\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: It is and I make it with evidence because the divine assignment on my life, Tucker, is something that I want everybody to find for their own lives.\par \par \uc2 I don't prophesize, I'm obviously not a pastor, I'm a journalist. My divine assignment is to be a witness. And so through "Faith Still Moves Mountains," I tell stories of other people's miracles in their lives from some of the news stories that I've covered.\par \par \uc2 Lee County, Alabama Twister outbreak, eighty-something-year-old woman Ernestine survives the unthinkable inside of her prayer closet. All of the homes on her street razed to the ground to their foundations, but the tethered, windblown wet paint strip, tornado driven prayer closet survives.\par \par \uc2 And the EMTs show up and there she is praying mightily, "Lord, it is your will for me to go on with the life you've blessed me with. Lord, I'm so grateful. I'm here, and thank you for letting me survive." She prayed before, during, and after the storm.\par \par \uc2 There are so many stories like this.\par \par \uc2 And Tucker, you know, from interviewing people all over the country, people always share with you the bad news, but wouldn't it be amazing if they could share the good news? And that's what this is about. But news that we can use, because we all need to connect with something greater than ourselves to lift us up.\par \par \uc2 We are a country that is falling away with its faith. Gallup is studying this. \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  is studying this. By 2070, it appears there will be fewer than 50 percent majority Christian-Judeo in this country.\par \par \uc2 We're changing and look at what's happening around us? People don't seem happier for it, they don't see more hopeful for it.\par \par \uc2 So "Faith Still Moves Mountains" is based on what the Bible tells us about mustard seed faith, that faith that you have when it looks like everything is being torn apart or even when things are great.\par \par \uc2 Pray all the time, you know people are ready to make their New Year's resolutions. I love that that what you're doing is on, you know the second day of the year, we're going to grip poll, people are going to -- I'm going to eat fewer donuts and workout more at the gym. Why don't you make a permanent change in your life?\par \par \uc2 And God does not care what season you are in. He'll meet you right where you are. And when you lean into the Lord, he leans into you. And whatever your struggle is -- your addiction, your depression, whatever it is, you can begin to really work through it without saying, well by February 15th, if it's not fixed, I give up because isn't that what we usually do with our resolutions?\par \par \uc2 This is a resolute promise from the Lord to go forth with a better destiny than ever before.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: How are you living in the world that we both live in so forthright and unembarrassed to talk like this? I love it. But how do you do that?\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I am unapologetic and I was raised by a warrior. My dad was a combat pilot. And he and my mother have seen -- they had seen some pretty tough times, two wars. It wasn't clear if Dad was coming home.\par \par \uc2 I really do believe that that warrior spirit and that forever prayer filled mom's belief got so deeply into me that as I grew up and connected with that, it started to make me feel like I should tell the truth when people ask me: How do you get through tough times? How do you tell stories like, you know, disasters?\par \par \uc2 Because you know, if you see me, a news anchor, something bad is probably happening if I'm on scene. So, as a cub reporter on through now with a network, I pray. I always have.\par \par \uc2 We know unthinkable -- TMI -- too much stuff about some of our colleagues through years and years of being in the business. Why can't I share the foundational way that I get through things?\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You're free.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Why can't I tell people that I love the Lord? And by the way, why can't we be bold with our faith? You can't cancel us. This is the time of the year and everybody knows we've just gotten through Christmas, entering this New Year. Satan is skipping through the tulips right now.\par \par \uc2 The Satanic Temple of Illinois, just a few days ago, put its display. Well, the city led it, right next to the Menorah, the Christmas tree, the Cross, and then they came out and they did their -- well, look, it's a free country, you can do that.\par \par \uc2 You don't get to cancel us though. Don't move the Christmas tree, the cross and the Menorah. You don't get to do that. But if you want to worship something that is not going to edify you and lift you up, well, heck, don't blame the rest of us if we're going to be bold.\par \par \uc2 So now I'm going -- I'm getting invited to Mega Churches, which is so not the thing I ever thought I would be doing as a journalist, but we go and we talk about being bold in our faith.\par \par \uc2 This is the time to have an impenetrable weapon, a power of spirituality in our hearts and at our fingertips. It's called prayer.\par \par \uc2 Be bold with God and it is amazing what can happen in your life and don't keep it a secret, don't hide your light, shine brightly.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Amazing. Boy, am I glad we had this conversation.\par \par \uc2 Harris Faulkner, bless you and thank you for saying what you said.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Thank you my friend, Tucker. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, that put us in a much better mood on this New Year's, not all is lost. There are wonderful people making this still the greatest country there is. There are a lot of them and we are really happy to see them tonight.\par \par \uc2 We'll be back tomorrow night and every weeknight, 8:00 PM, the show that's a sworn enemy of lying, pomposity, smugness, and groupthink. Have the best night with the ones you love.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020230103ej1200001\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row